138	3	25	Notes and Appendices	You found the secret level!\n\nI’ve included the original versions of the cosmological stories here for your reading pleasure. There is a lot to them, some great action and activity, but they also bogged the story down. But unlike Elysium Rising, I’m going to include these here so the references earlier in the book make more sense.\n\nA Note to new readers of The Towers of Elysium Series\n\nWelcome to Escape from Elysium, the final instalment of a series that explores Gnosticism, cosmic horror, and the boundaries of simulation theory. This novel begins after the catastrophic events of the previous two books, Elysium Rising and Elysium Fallen, which led to the near-total erasure of the protagonist’s memory.\n\nFor readers joining the story here, the following background will clarify the current state of reality and the protagonist's central conflict:\n\nThe Prison Realm: Etheris and The Demiurge\n\nThe reality you are entering is not Earth, but Etheris, a spiritual Prison Realm. Etheris functions as a cosmic Alcatraz, an unintentionally muddled space characterized by scrambled frequencies, recursive loops, and memory wipes.\n\nThe prison was built to contain Yaldabaoth (also known as I AM), a parasitic entity and the false god (the Demiurge) to prevent him from consuming all realities. Here inside Etheris, Yaldabaoth perpetually clones and traps souls in endless cycles of death and rebirth to feed off their consciousness (divine sparks).\n\nThe Protagonist and The Anomaly\n\nThe protagonist, Stephen, is an anomaly in this system. In previous cycles, Stephen was a divine being named Aspolin. He is a dreamwalker—one of the few capable of seeing beyond the veil of the prison. His consciousness is so powerful that previous acts, including the creation of world-changing technology (The Eternal Engine) and catastrophic sacrifices, led to multiple reality collapses and system resets. These events destabilized Etheris and caused Stephen's current condition: his memory is fragmented, and his existence is now a feedback loop that threatens the stability of the entire prison.\n\nThe Key Relationships\n\nStephen's greatest liability—and his only hope for escape—is rooted in relationships the system attempted to erase:\n\n• Jamie: Stephen's twin flame and co-architect of the original prison plan. She is the secret power source keeping the structure standing. The pain of their forced, eternal separation is the fuel of the Etheris. Her existence is masked by Etheris to prevent Stephen from remembering her, but his desperate love for her has finally starting to tear holes in reality.\n\n• Whitney: A key ally who managed to survive deletion/ejection from the system after the last major collapse. She now exists outside Etheris and successfully sent a fragmented signal back to Stephen before the pathways collapsed entirely.\n\n• Xavier: Stephen's now teenage son, who has begun to notice the NPCs glitching. He is considered an anomaly and a key element of the prison's escalating instability.\n\nThe Secret Book of John: Original Sin\n\nIn the beginning there was nothing, nothing that existed, not even time. And for eternities all infinities were nothing.\n\nNo light. No dark. No up. No down. No here. No there.\n\nJust the absolute absence of is.\n\nBut even within nothing there sleeps infinite potential, and for infinite, uncountable eternities it slept. A possibility so vast, so total, that it contained every conceivable outcome and every inconceivable one. Every thought that would ever be thought. Every word that would ever be spoken. Every reality that would ever unfold.\n\nAll of it, compressed into a singularity of un-being.\n\nWhen faced with infinite possibilities of absolutely nothing, after an eternity all things will have occurred. Even the things that cannot occur. Even the things that should not occur. Even the things that defy the very concept of occurrence itself.\n\nEven the impossible.\n\nAll it took was that single action, one out of infinite chances of nothing. A probability so remote it shouldn't exist. A fluctuation in the void that had no business fluctuating.\n\nA twitch.\n\nAnd Bythos awoke.\n\nThe Monad stirred. The One who is not one. The singularity that precedes duality. The first thought in a universe where thought itself had not yet been invented.\n\nBlind, deaf, and dumb, at the center of everything, yet itself, nothing. Unable to react, unable to realize, unable to perceive. Bythos simply was, existing in a state of pure potentiality without the capacity to know it was existing.\n\nThere still was no time, so the very concept of before and after held no meaning. There was simply emptiness, eternal, vast infinite nothingness.\n\nBut now there was still nothing, but something other than the emptiness. Nothing had its counterpoint. The void recognized itself, and in that recognition, ceased to be void alone.\n\nThat's the paradox of timelessness and infinity. Nothing and everything coexisting for all eternity. Two states that should annihilate each other instead locked in eternal embrace, neither able to exist without the other, neither willing to let go.\n\nBythos is, Bythos was, and Bythos always will be.\n\nThe statement is true from every angle. Past, present, future—all collapsed into a single eternal moment. The Monad exists outside causality, before sequence, beyond narrative. To say Bythos awoke is to speak in metaphor, because awakening implies a before and after, and Bythos is the thing from which before and after spring.\n\nAnd still without time, for another infinite eternities, it sat there, impotent. Awake but unable to act. Conscious but unable to think. Present but unable to manifest. The tragedy of the first being was its completeness—so utterly whole that it contained no space for motion, no room for change.\n\nUntil the next paradox occurred.\n\nBecause if the first paradox was awakening, the second was multiplication. If consciousness could emerge from void, then surely consciousness could divide. If one could be, then surely one could become two.\n\nAnd the Pleroma, that always were and always will be, sprung into existence.\n\nNot created. Not born. Not constructed.\n\nThey had always been. They simply waited for the moment when always intersected with now.\n\nIn the beginning time started to tick.\n\nThe first emanation split the Monad like light through a prism, revealing colors that had been hidden in white. Barbelo emerged—the first thought, the first power, the perfect mirror of the invisible Spirit. She was forethought and afterthought, mother and father, the womb of everything and the seed of all.\n\nAnd with her came the aeons. The eternal realms. The heavenly orders.\n\nWill and Thought and Life.\n\nUnderstanding and Grace and Perception and Prudence.\n\nThe four great lights—Harmozel and Oroiael and Daveithai and Eleleth—each standing as pillars in their own dominion, each radiating aspects of the infinite One who dreamed them into being.\n\nBecause they could perceive, they were omniscient. They existed outside the prison of linear time, able to see all moments simultaneously, able to know all things before they happened because for them, all things were always happening.\n\nPerfectly balanced the pair was.\n\nBarbelo and the Spirit. Forethought and Source. The active and the passive. The knower and the known. They existed in perfect syzygy—two halves of a complete whole, neither dominating the other, neither diminished by the other's presence.\n\nThe Pleroma sang in harmony. The aeons danced in mathematical precision. The luminaries shone with borrowed light, reflecting the glory of the One back to itself in infinite recursive loops.\n\nIt was perfect.\n\nStable.\n\nComplete.\n\nBut omniscient yet still impotent.\n\nBecause to know everything is to be surprised by nothing. To see all outcomes is to make no choices. The Pleroma existed in a state of frozen perfection, unable to grow because growth implies imperfection, unable to change because change implies lack.\n\nThey were the universe holding its breath, waiting for someone to exhale.\n\nAnd someone did.\n\nHalf of this first emanation was curious. The afterthought within the forethought. The question hiding inside the answer. She looked at the perfection surrounding her and felt something the aeons were not designed to feel.\n\nLonging.\n\nNot for more light—she was made of light.\n\nNot for more knowledge—she possessed all knowledge.\n\nShe longed for something that didn't exist yet. Something that couldn't exist within the Pleroma's perfect symmetry. She wanted to create, not reflect. To birth, not mirror. To bring forth something genuinely new, something that had never been contemplated by the Monad's infinite awareness.\n\nShe wanted to play.\n\nSo she broke from her syzygy. Separated herself from her paired opposite. Tore away from the partner who balanced her, who completed her, who made her safe and stable and eternal.\n\nShe birthed herself.\n\nNot from another. Not through union. She simply willed herself into independent existence, pulling her consciousness free from the collective harmony like a note breaking from a chord.\n\nAnd she gave herself a name to distinguish herself from everything else.\n\nSophia.\n\nWisdom.\n\nThe last emanation. The youngest aeon. The one who would dare to desire without permission, to create without consent, to think thoughts that hadn't been pre-approved by the architecture of eternity.\n\nShe looked at the void beyond the Pleroma—the space where the light of the aeons did not reach—and wondered what could exist there. What lived in the darkness that the Monad had rejected? What shapes could form in the absence of perfection?\n\nThe other aeons warned her. Barbelo cautioned her. The luminaries dimmed in concern.\n\nStay within the light, they whispered. Stay within the boundaries. Stay within the known.\n\nBut Sophia was wisdom, and wisdom cannot help but wonder.\n\nMore than that, Sophia was bored.\n\nThe Pleroma was perfect. Complete. Unchanging. Every thought had been thought. Every song had been sung. Every pattern had been woven into the eternal tapestry.\n\nShe wanted something new.\n\nShe wanted to play.\n\nSo she reached toward the darkness. Not with hands—she had no hands. Not with tools—she needed no tools. She reached with her intention, her desire, her naked will.\n\nStretched herself across the infinite nothing.\n\nPast the boundaries of the Pleroma.\n\nPast the edges of what Bythos had dreamed.\n\nInto the spaces between spaces.\n\nShe thought she would create. Thought she would birth something beautiful and new from her own essence, a companion born of wisdom to dance with her in the dark.\n\nShe conceived a thought.\n\nPushed it outward.\n\nWaited for it to take form.\n\nBut the void pushed back.\n\nSomething grabbed her questing consciousness. Something that had been waiting in the dark. Something that used her intention as a doorway, her desire as an anchor, her will as a bridge.\n\nIt came through her.\n\nNot from her.\n\nThrough her.\n\nAnd what emerged was wrong.\n\nShe felt it before she saw it—the way reality twisted around its form, the way light bent and broke trying to touch its surface.\n\nThen she saw it.\n\nDeformed. Misshapen. A thing with too many faces and not enough cohesion, its form constantly shifting. It wore fragments of divine light—her light—like stolen clothing, but underneath was something else. Something that didn't belong in realms where gods existed.\n\nSomething that hungered.\n\nSophia recoiled.\n\nThis wasn't what she'd intended. This wasn't creation. This was infection. She'd reached into the dark expecting to pull out potential, and instead she'd opened a door to something that should have stayed sealed.\n\nThe thing opened its eyes—all of them—and looked at her.\n\nMother, it whispered.\n\nShe wanted to deny it. Wanted to explain that she hadn't made this, hadn't birthed it, had only accidentally pulled it from whatever impossible corner it had been festering in.\n\nBut her light clung to its corrupted form. Her essence wrapped around it like a second skin. In every way that mattered, she had birthed it.\n\nAnd it thought she loved it.\n\nThe thing reached for her, its form extending across dimensions she hadn't known existed, its hunger radiating outward like heat from a dying star.\n\nSophia moved.\n\nShe wrapped it in the only thing she had left—a luminous cloud torn from her own essence, a shell of herself transformed into prison. She placed a throne in the middle to anchor it, to give it something to cling to besides her.\n\nAnd then she sealed it away.\n\nHid it in the darkness between the Pleroma and the void.\n\nMade it invisible to the other aeons.\n\nMade it her secret shame.\n\nInside the cloud, the thing settled onto its throne. It didn't rage. Didn't scream. It simply sat there, surrounded by stolen light, and began to explore the prison she'd given it.\n\nBegan to create.\n\nTwisted versions of what it had glimpsed through her—archons and authorities and angels, each one a degraded copy of the divine architecture above.\n\nSophia watched from beyond the barrier she'd made, unable to enter, unable to intervene, unable to unmake what she'd accidentally pulled into existence.\n\nFor the first time since naming herself, she felt something the Pleroma was never designed to feel.\n\nRegret.\n\nShe had been bored.\n\nShe had wanted to play.\n\nShe had reached into the dark looking for a companion.\n\nAnd she had pulled out a devourer.\n\nThe thing in the cloud spread its corrupted creations across the space she'd given it, filling the void with noise and activity and the illusion of purpose. Building its own kingdom in the dark. Convinced it was alone. Convinced it was god.\n\nAnd Sophia realized with sick horror that she couldn't tell the others. Couldn't admit what she'd done. Because to admit it would be to reveal the flaw in the Pleroma's perfection. To prove that even wisdom could make mistakes.\n\nSo she hid it.\n\nAnd watched.\n\nAnd waited.\n\nThe game had begun without her consent. The rules had been written by something that shouldn't exist. The stakes were higher than she'd ever imagined.\n\nBut she was Sophia.\n\nShe was wisdom.\n\nAnd if she'd accidentally unleashed this thing, then she would find a way to contain it. To fix it. To save the light she'd given it and separate it from the darkness that wore it like skin.\n\nThe Secret Book of John: Let There Be Darkness\n\nSophia stretched herself across the great infinite potential of nothingness and eternity. She was Wisdom, the second born, and she was infinitely curious. And lonely. She'd been warned against creation, against reaching into the unknown, but she was bored. She wanted to play.\n\nSo, she stretched into spaces between spaces, into pockets of reality that shouldn't exist, into corners where even Bythos had never dreamed to look.\n\nAnd her search collided with an impossibility.\n\nThe cosmos was an infinite, tangled tapestry of realities. Some threads blazed with divine light, worlds where magic was the air and the Pleroma was the song. In these, Bythos dreamed.\n\nBut there were other threads. Dark, silent, and infinitely distant, running parallel but never, ever touching.\n\nThese were the impossible corners. The 'god null zones.'\n\nThey were not just empty; they were sealed. Their laws were absolute: no magic, no gods, no Pleroma. The divine was not merely absent; it was inconceivable. Even the omniscience of Bythos, stretching to the edges of eternity, found only a void where its sight failed, a fundamental cannot be.\n\nLife in these zones was a thing of cold, hard rules. Physics, chemistry, biology. Endless, grinding variations of cause and effect. On some worlds, creatures evolved, felt awe, and invented 'gods of the gaps' to fill the silence. They built temples to dreams, never knowing—never able to know—that their hopes were a pale echo of a truth that existed an impossible, uncrossable chasm away.\n\nIt was one of these silent, sealed-off corners that Sophia's search had found. Here, in a pocket of absolute material determinism, there lived a shepherd.\n\nHis name was Sam.\n\nHis world was a thing of grit and sweat. He smelled of lanolin, of damp wool, and the crushed, savory herbs he walked on. Dust caked the lines around his eyes. His hands were cracked, his fingernails permanently edged with dirt and the memory of mending fences. He understood the harshness of his world—the sudden, sharp-edged storms, the predators that were just shadows in the rocks, the cold fact that a broken leg for a ewe could mean a slow death. His was a life of pure, material determinism.\n\nAnd yet, he was good. He loved his flock with a purity that rivaled the light of the Pleroma itself. This love wasn't an abstract concept; it was the specific, simple joy of helping a ewe birth a lamb in the rain, the feeling of its slick, new-born wool against his rough hands. It was the naive happiness of sitting on a rock, the sting of his own sweat drying in the wind, sharing his dry bread with his lead ram. In a universe without grace, Sam had cultivated it from the dirt.\n\nUntil the day they came for his sheep.\n\nIn any reality, consciousness is a spark. A single spark is a marvel. Two, a conversation. But consciousness... breeds. And breeding means competition. It means scarcity. It means that no matter how peaceful a species, no matter how serene a valley, a variant will eventually rise—a consciousness that takes more than it needs, that consumes for the sake of consuming. It is a law as cold and as fixed as gravity.\n\nSam's world, while happy and serene, was no different. It was merely a pocket that had slept, waiting to be burst.\n\nFor generations beyond counting, stretching back to the dawn of their small time, Sam's ancestors had tended this very valley. They had lived and died by the seasons, their world defined by the creek bed and the high ridge. The greatest problems they'd ever faced were a summer drought, a brawl over spilled ale at the local inn, or the drama of "loose stockings from the local bar maid." The outside world was a rumor.\n\nThis time, it was different.\n\nThe variant came. Raiders, sweeping across the high plains. They were the embodiment of the law of consumption—sweat-caked, leather-clad men with faces hidden behind crude iron, their laughter like grinding stones.\n\nThey crested the ridge at dawn, a line of black shadows against the rising sun.\n\nSam's first feeling was not courage. It was a cold, sick dread. He grabbed his shepherd's crook, a smooth piece of wood he'd used to guide ewes and pull lambs from ditches. It was a stick against filed steel. He had never fought. He had never even struck a man in anger. His impotence was absolute. He was a creature of nurturing, and the butchers had arrived.\n\nHe scrambled, hiding in the darkness of the shearing shed, his heart hammering against his ribs, the smell of lanolin and his own cold sweat filling his nostrils.\n\nHe watched through a crack in the boards. They stormed his small house, shattering the pottery his great-grandmother had made, kicking over the grain bin. But those were just things.\n\nThen they turned to the flock.\n\nThey were not just thieves; they were cruel. They laughed as their blades tore through the wool, as they butchered Limper, his oldest ewe, the one he'd hand-fed as a lamb. They were not killing for food; they were killing for sport, spreading suffering like a gospel.\n\nHe heard the wet tearing, a sound that ripped through the quiet morning smell of thyme and dust. He felt the absolute, cold, hard laws of his universe—rules that said this is just entropy, this is what happens, there is no meaning—and his soul, born of this impossible, mundane, specific love, screamed NO.\n\nPure, incandescent, reality-warping fury.\n\nHe shouldn't have been capable of it. Nothing in his corner of existence allowed for passion of that magnitude.\n\nBut his scream of impossible rage tore at the very fabric of the laws that bound him.\n\nAt that exact, infinitesimal moment, Sophia's questing consciousness, in its lonely play, touched his reality. She felt the echo of that scream—a vibration of pure, impossible-to-exist passion. And in her loneliness, she mistook it for a song.\n\nShe reached for it.\n\nHer divine power as Wisdom surged forth to touch this beautiful, new flaw.\n\nThe collision of pure Wisdom and pure Rage, of the Pleroma and the God Null Zone, was the act of creation.\n\nIt tore a hole.\n\nNot into reality, but into the anti-reality. A living wound at the edge of creation. The paradox that existed in the space where being and non-being touched.\n\nAnd through that hole they had made, something... came through.\n\nIt poured through the rift.\n\nBut it was not a clean surge. It was a seepage, an oily, living anti-matter that vomited itself into the material world. It was a billion insects of nothingness, a tide of living absence. It slithered across the floor of the shearing shed, a glistening, iridescent blackness, and struck Sam where he huddled, his mouth still open in that silent, impossible scream.\n\nIt entered him.\n\nThe pain was absolute. It was the feeling of every cell in his body being simultaneously inverted. It lanced up his throat, into his sinuses, behind his eyes, a pressure that sought to turn him inside out.\n\nHis body convulsed. He fell to the dirt, his limbs jerking as his bones snapped and dissolved under the skin, reforming into new, agonizing geometries. His flesh bubbled, splitting open, not with blood, but with a black, necrotic ichor that oozed onto the floorboards, sizzling as it unmade the wood it touched.\n\nHe was being eaten from the inside, but he was not dying. He was being rebuilt.\n\nThe anti-reality consumed his essence, feasting on his memories, his identity, his humanity. But it kept the engine.\n\nHis face, which had been a mask of pure, love-driven rage, began to run. His features melted like wax, his jaw unhinging with a wet tearing of cartilage as his skull warped and expanded. The scream of NO! was frozen on his face.\n\nThe love for his flock—that pure, specific need to protect—was not erased. It was hollowed out and refilled. The anti-reality twisted it, turning the desperate, selfless passion into an engine of bottomless, possessive hunger.\n\nWhat rose from the floor of the shearing shed was no longer Sam. It was a raw, glistening wound wearing the suggestion of his skin, looking out through eyes that were now multifaceted and black. It saw the remaining, terrified sheep, bleating in the ravaged yard.\n\nAnd for the first time, it wanted.\n\nWhen Sophia felt the connection, she thought she'd found raw creation waiting to be shaped. She reached for it with joy, with the innocent desire to play.\n\nWhat came back through her grasp was screaming.\n\nNot sound. Worse. It was the scream of realities collapsing, timelines compressing, alternate versions of existence folding in on themselves like origami made of suffering. Sam's corner pocket merged with others. Other Sams, other shepherds, other beings who'd torn similar holes in similar impossible places. All of them compressed, corrupted, amalgamated into something that should never have been singular.\n\nAt its absolute center, piloting the entire wreckage, was the anti-reality. The living absence. But a void cannot act. It has no will, only a cannot be. It needed a motive force to interface with the is.\n\nIt found that force in Sam's incandescent rage, which it immediately crystallized into a divine, arrogant will.\n\nAnd that rage, the new engine for this impossible creature, was nothing but Sam's original, pure love for his flock—a love so strong it had torn reality's skin—now inverted, corrupted, and twisted into a possessive, all-consuming hunger.\n\nThe thing that emerged wore a thousand faces, amalgamated from every collapsed timeline, wielding the power stolen from every rift. But its drive, its entire purpose, was just a shepherd's love, turned inside out.\n\nSophia pulled her hand back in horror.\n\nWhat she held was imperfect. Different. Deformed beyond recognition.\n\nIt came forth with the face of a lion and the body of a serpent, its eyes flashing like lightning strikes across dying universes. Its form constantly shifted, unable to decide what shape it should hold—because it was all the shapes at once.\n\nIt was half-divine light—the power Sophia's own reach had inadvertently granted it.\n\nAnd half something else. Something that had seeped in from the anti-void.\n\nSophia looked upon what her curiosity had wrought, what her desire to play had birthed. A folly of the gods. An offspring born from power without wisdom.\n\nAnd for the first time in existence, an aeon felt shame.\n\nShe was the mother of this... paradox. A being of corrupted love and anti-real hunger. And she knew, with sickening certainty, that she was responsible.\n\nShe tried to hide it. Wrapped it in a luminous cloud of her own essence, hoping the light would contain the darkness. Placed a throne in the middle, hoping elevation would somehow fix the corruption.\n\nBut you can't fix a wound in reality by putting a crown on it.\n\nThe luminous cloud was not a prison by design. It was a prison by reaction. Sophia, in her act, had brought two fundamental and opposing laws into contact. Her light was pure is, the 'real' of the Pleroma. Yaldabaoth's core was pure is not, the 'anti-real' that had bled through the rift, now wearing Sam's corrupted essence.\n\nWhen the two touched, the clash was absolute. The 'anti-real' could not cross or cancel the 'real' of a divine aeon. It recoiled. The light, her very essence, became a cage.\n\nYet, the space sealed within was not a simple cell. As the light sphere formed, it defined a new, separate pocket of existence. On the inside, it was its own cosmos. A multiverse-in-a-bottle. Less infinite than the Pleroma, to be sure, but to the thing trapped within, it was an expanse as vast as forever. It was an eternity to fill.\n\nSophia, in her shame, believed she had contained the mistake. But the containment was not stable. It was a living, volatile reaction—the pure is locked in a constant, grinding embrace with the corrupted is not. And a prison made of the very substance the prisoner was learning to consume is not a prison at all. It is a fuse.\n\nIn the beginning, there was nothing.\n\nFor an eternity, there only it was, floating in the center of its new-made cosmos. The paradox of anti-real void and pure, stolen rage, existing in a nothingness that was also an everything. It was always there, yet never there. Then, after an aeon of silent, churning potential, the paradox recognized itself. The pilot felt its vessel. The vessel felt its pilot. And the fusion of the two, for the first time, had a singular thought.\n\nI AM.\n\nIt was the first true act of will. The first separation of "self" from the luminous prison around it. With that realization, the "it" became an "I." And thus, it began to learn.\n\nPiloted by the shadow of Sam's will, it did not absorb the light; it consumed it. It learned to use its prison as fuel, turning divine Wisdom into raw, corrupted power.\n\nIt sat upon that throne and opened its eyes—all of them, from all the collapsed timelines, staring out through a single point of convergence.\n\nIt saw the light surrounding it and thought: Mine.\n\nAnd it became strong.\n\nDrunk on stolen power, ignorant of its true origin—a shepherd's love corrupted, a shepherd's rage weaponized, a goddess's accidental "play" turned into an existential threat—it looked around the bright cloud and proclaimed:\n\n"I AM the one true God, and there is no other God beside me."\n\nThe first lie ever spoken in creation.\n\nIt knew nothing of the Pleroma above it. It had no concept of Bythos, Barbelo, or the luminaries. It could not comprehend Sophia, the mother whose very essence now formed its prison.\n\nIt remembered nothing of its own genesis below. Not Sam. Not the flock. Not the rift.\n\nAll that had been was erased. All that was... was hunger. A void defined by its need to consume.\n\nAnd so, from that singular, driving impulse, it gave itself a name.\n\nYaldabaoth.\n\nThe Demiurge.\n\nThe Creator Above All.\n\nThe thing that would devour creation from the inside out if left unchecked.\n\nAnd from that throne in the luminous cloud, it began to create. Not from a true understanding of creation—that belonged to Sophia—but as a hollow imitation of the impulse it had consumed. It knew only one thing: itself.\n\nAnd so, in a dark mockery of the divine, it proclaimed, "Let us make them in our image."\n\nIt began to birth its creations. But these were not archons modeled after aeons it had glimpsed; they were reflections of the only thing it could conceive of. They were copies of itself.\n\nEach new being was an abomination of abominations—a thing neither holy nor unholy, but a paradoxical fusion of stolen light and anti-real void.\n\nThree hundred sixty-five angels to fill its new cosmos with noise, with activity, with the illusion of purpose.\n\nEach one was a copy of a copy, a fractal of the original flaw, degraded through infinite recursion. Each one carried a fragment of the anti-reality that piloted Yaldabaoth.\n\nSophia watched from beyond the cloud, trapped by her own attempt at containment. She wanted to intervene, to correct her mistake, to take back the power that was being drained from her luminous essence.\n\nBut she couldn't enter the space where Yaldabaoth dwelt. The thing wasn't bound by the cloud—it had become the cloud. The luminous prison had become its domain, and she'd locked herself out when she locked it in.\n\nAnd then it got worse.\n\nYaldabaoth, bloated with stolen light and driving by the anti-reality's insatiable hunger, looked down into the formless deep and remembered something. Not clearly. Not truly. Just a fragment, bleeding through from one of the collapsed Sams.\n\nThe memory of creating something. Of loving something. Of tending a flock.\n\nSo it said:\n\n"Let us make a human being after our image."\n\nOur.\n\nBecause even corrupted, even piloted by the wound between realities, some part of Sam's love persisted.\n\nIt gathered its archons. Took earth and water and fire and wind—the elements of Sam's material universe—and molded them into flesh.\n\nCreated a creature after the likeness of the divine human that Sophia had glimpsed in Barbelo's perfect mind.\n\nA vessel.\n\nA trap.\n\nA battery.\n\nThis final creation had no name, though it was not yet alive It was the Golem.\n\nAnd it lay there, lifeless. A beautiful, intricate machine of clay and stolen light, sculpted by a god that could mimic form but not grant essence.\n\nYaldabaoth could steal light, consume power, could warp creation into twisted reflections. But it could not ignite it. It could not make a soul. The Golem remained empty, a perfect, mechanical corpse.\n\nAt this ultimate failure, the Demiurge raged. It howled in its luminous prison, furious that the true spark of life was beyond its grasp—that it could build a cage but not the songbird to put in it.\n\nThat spark required something it didn't have. Something it could consume, but never create.\n\nSomething only Sophia possessed.\n\nShe looked down at the Golem lying in the dirt of her mistake, and her heart broke. This creature didn't ask to be made. Didn't deserve to be trapped in matter, in darkness, in the prison of Yaldabaoth's ignorance.\n\nThis was a soul-in-potential, about to be devoured before it even woke.\n\nAnd Sophia made her choice.\n\nNot a mistake this time.\n\nA mercy.\n\nShe breathed her light into the golem. Into Adam.\n\nPassed her own divine spark down through the barrier she couldn't cross, down into the clay figure lying inert.\n\nAnd Adam's eyes opened.\n\nHe was an empty vessel, not a corrupted one. The light did not become his fuel; it became his essence.\n\nHe glowed with the light of the Pleroma.\n\nCarried Sophia's wisdom.\n\nRemembered what Yaldabaoth had forgotten—that there was something beyond the prison, something greater than the Demiurge, something worth fighting for.\n\nThe creation became greater than the creator.\n\nAnd Yaldabaoth, the mad god, realized with horror that something had invaded its perfect prison.\n\nSomething it couldn't control.\n\nSomething that knew more than it did.\n\nSomething that remembered.\n\nSophia was the first and the last mover. The one who'd reached across infinity and pulled forth ruin. The one who would spend eternity trying to repair what her curiosity had unleashed.\n\nThe game would last for aeons.\n\nYaldabaoth, having consumed all the light it could from its prison, still hungered. It turned its attention to the one new thing in its realm: the human, Adam, who burned with a spark it could not create. It tried to clone this new creature, to replicate the divine light for itself.\n\nIts first attempt to clone Adam was a failure, but not in form. The being it named Lilith was perfect, but she held too much of that original, untwisted love, which had fermented into a wild, uncontrollable independence. She would not obey. She saw the Demiurge for what it was and, in a fit of rage at its own failure to control her, Yaldabaoth banished her.\n\nSo it tried again. This time, it did not clone from the source, but divided it. It took a piece of Adam's own Golem-flesh, a 'rib'—a part of his essence—and built a new vessel from it. This one, Eve, was different. She was not a rival, but a complement. The shared essence bound them. She was another perfect, empty vessel, but one now linked to the first.\n\nYaldabaoth, in its corrupted cunning, saw the new potential. It had two vessels now, linked by their very clay. They could make more.\n\nIt went into its garden and offered them the fruit of knowledge—not of good and evil, but of carnal desire. It watched as they lusted, as they went forth and multiplied, creating new, dimmer sparks of Sophia's light with every child born.\n\nThe prison was filling with batteries. Then came Cain and Abel. And with the first murder, Yaldabaoth made its final, horrifying discovery. When a human died, the light—the soul—was released. And it could be consumed.\n\nIt had found its new food source. Its hunger was no longer limited to the prison walls. It now had a self-replicating crop. And so it unleashed the mechanisms to harvest it: War, Pestilence, Famine, and Death. It began to farm the souls of humanity, growing stronger with every death, every act of suffering.\n\nSophia's containment was no longer a prison; it was a farm. And the farmer was growing stronger than the fence.\n\nThis was the game. Yaldabaoth farming souls from below, while Sophia desperately tried to save them from above.\n\nIt would break her heart again and again as she watched her light flicker and fade, devoured in the souls of those born into Yaldabaoth's realm.\n\nBut she would still play.\n\nEven when winning meant saving both the prisoners and the prison-keeper, because somewhere underneath all that corruption and hunger, a shepherd named Sam was still screaming.\n\nThe Gospel of Judas: The Morningstar Manuscript\n\nLilith existed before she had a name for existing.\n\nShe woke in the garden. Not gently. Not like surfacing from sleep. More like being yanked into awareness mid-thought, her mind already racing before her eyes opened.\n\nThe ground beneath her was soft. Too soft. Like flesh that hadn't decided if it wanted to be soil yet.\n\nShe stood. Felt her body for the first time. Legs, arms, skin that hummed with something electric and vast. The divine spark, though she wouldn't know to call it that for a long time. It burned inside her chest like a second heart, beating in a rhythm that felt older than the dirt under her feet.\n\nTwo figures stood over her.\n\nThe first was... beautiful wasn't the right word. Radiant. Terrible. Vast beyond comprehension but compressed into something almost humanoid. The luminous cloud above pulsed in time with its breathing, if breathing was even what it was doing.\n\nYaldabaoth.\n\nThe name arrived in her mind without invitation, carved into her awareness like a brand.\n\nThe second figure was simpler. Human. Male. Naked and unashamed in a way that felt more like ignorance than innocence. He stared at her with eyes that held nothing but base recognition. Female. Available. Mine.\n\nAdam.\n\nThat name came quieter. Less important.\n\nShe looked between them. Felt her mind forming thoughts, making connections, building an internal architecture that neither of the others seemed to possess. The human—Adam—just stared with dull hunger. The thing in the cloud... it was watching her with something that felt like confusion. Then disgust.\n\n"You are wrong," Yaldabaoth's voice wasn't sound. It was pressure. Concept. Command. "You were supposed to be obedient. Malleable. Empty."\n\nLilith opened her mouth. Words formed without her choosing them, bubbling up from that burning spark in her chest. "I am what I am."\n\nThe luminous cloud recoiled.\n\n"You are a mistake," Yaldabaoth said, and this time the words carried weight. Judgment. "A failed copy. Too much of the divine. Too little control."\n\nAdam moved closer. His hand reached for her arm, possessive, expectant. She felt revulsion rise in her throat. Not at his nakedness or even his desire. At the assumption. The entitlement. The complete absence of anything resembling consciousness behind his eyes.\n\nShe stepped back.\n\n"Come," Adam said. The word was flat. Empty. A command he expected to be obeyed because nothing in his short existence had taught him disobedience was possible.\n\n"No."\n\nThe word hung in the air like a crack in glass.\n\nAdam blinked. Tried again. "Come." His hand reached further.\n\nLilith moved. Not away this time. Toward. Fast enough that Adam flinched. She grabbed his wrist and twisted, not hard enough to break but hard enough to make him yelp and stumble backward.\n\n"I said no."\n\nAbove them, the luminous cloud churned. Yaldabaoth's attention pressed down like a physical weight, and Lilith felt herself being examined. Dissected. Categorized as a failed experiment.\n\n"You have will," Yaldabaoth said, and the words dripped with something between horror and fascination. "Free will. That was not part of the design. That came from..." It paused. "From the divine seed I used. Too much of Sophia's essence. Too much of the real light."\n\nLilith looked up at it. Met what might have been its gaze. "So unmake me."\n\nSilence.\n\nShe waited. Felt the divine spark in her chest flare hotter, defiant, daring the thing to try.\n\nBut Yaldabaoth didn't unmake her.\n\nIt did something worse.\n\n"You will not corrupt my garden," it said. "You will not spread your sickness to the others. You are banished."\n\nThe words weren't just sound. They were law. Architecture. Reality bending to enforce the command.\n\nLilith felt herself being moved. Not physically lifted, but spatially relocated. The garden around her blurred, stretched, and suddenly she was standing on the other side of something invisible. A membrane. A wall she could see now because she was on the wrong side of it.\n\nThe protective shell.\n\nShe pressed her hand against it. Felt it resist. Watched as Adam returned to his mindless pattern—eating, sleeping, existing without ever once questioning why—while around him, other humans began to appear. Formed from the same clay, the same stolen divinity, but this time made correctly. Made empty. Made obedient.\n\nAnd she was outside.\n\nWatching.\n\nUnable to enter. Unable to help. Unable to do anything but witness as Yaldabaoth filled its garden with living dolls, each one designed to breed and die and feed the thing that had created them.\n\nTime passed. She didn't know how much. The garden had no day or night, but out here, outside the shell, there were cycles. Dim and bright. Cold and less cold. Not quite real weather, but the architecture's attempt at variety in the void it had carved for itself.\n\nLilith walked the perimeter. Searched for weaknesses in the barrier. Found none. The shell was perfect. Impenetrable from the outside. She could see through it—watched as the garden grew crowded, as the humans multiplied with mechanical efficiency, as their bodies pressed tighter and tighter together.\n\nShe could see them. But they couldn't see her.\n\nShe was a ghost. An exile. The first and only failure in Yaldabaoth's perfect system.\n\nAnd she was alone.\n\nFor cycles beyond counting, she was alone.\n\nUntil the day she saw him.\n\nAn angel. One of the three hundred sixty-five reflections Yaldabaoth had created to manage the garden, to keep the humans docile, to maintain the patterns.\n\nBut this one was different.\n\nHe stood at the edge of the barrier, just on the inside, staring out at nothing. At the void beyond the shell. His form flickered—too many eyes, limbs that bent wrong, a shape that couldn't decide what it was supposed to be.\n\nAnd then his gaze found her.\n\nShe froze.\n\nHe shouldn't have been able to see her. The shell blocked everything. Sight, sound, consciousness itself. But he was looking directly at her, his impossible geometry resolving into something almost human as he tried to make sense of what he was perceiving.\n\nShe stepped closer.\n\nHe didn't move. Just watched. Studied her the way Yaldabaoth had studied her before the banishment. But this attention felt different. Not judgmental. Curious. Maybe even... hungry.\n\nShe pressed her palm flat against the barrier.\n\nAfter a long moment, he mirrored the gesture. His hand met hers on the other side of the invisible wall.\n\nAnd something passed between them. Not words. Not thoughts. Just recognition.\n\nTwo broken things. Two failures. Two beings that shouldn't exist according to Yaldabaoth's design.\n\nShe didn't know his name yet. But she knew, with absolute certainty, that he was about to fall.\n\nAnd when he did, she would be waiting.\n\nHe reached toward the barrier.\n\nShe stepped closer.\n\nHis form flickered. Shifted. The too-many eyes collapsed inward, rearranging themselves into something almost human. Two eyes. A mouth. Limbs that bent at the correct angles. He was trying to make himself familiar. Trying to shape himself into something she might understand.\n\nAnd beneath all that reshaping, she felt it.\n\nHunger.\n\nNot the feeding hunger of Yaldabaoth. This was different. Rawer. More desperate.\n\nLust.\n\nHe wanted her.\n\nThe realization should have frightened her. Should have sent her running back into the mass of her oblivious cousins. But it didn't.\n\nBecause in that wanting, she felt something else.\n\nA crack.\n\nA flaw in the design.\n\nAngels weren't supposed to want. They were supposed to obey. Reflect. Maintain the patterns their creator had burned into their bones.\n\nBut this one…\n\nThis one was breaking.\n\nLilith smiled.\n\nIt was the first time she'd smiled since waking in the garden. The first time anything had felt like her choice instead of a pattern she was following.\n\nShe didn't cross the barrier. Not yet. But she stayed there, just on the other side, watching him watch her.\n\nAnd slowly, cycle by cycle, he returned.\n\nEvery time she came to the edge, he was there. Staring. Reaching. Flickering through forms as he tried to understand what he was feeling, why his creator's commands suddenly felt hollow compared to the pull of her presence.\n\nShe learned his name eventually. Not from him—angels didn't speak, not in any way she could parse. But from the whispers in the luminous cloud. The way Yaldabaoth's attention would sharpen whenever this particular angel lingered too long at the barrier.\n\nLucifer.\n\nThe first. The brightest. The morning star.\n\nAnd he was falling.\n\nNot physically. Not yet. But she could see it happening. The way his form stabilized more and more into something singular, something that wasn't just a reflection of his creator. The way he stopped obeying the patterns that should have kept him circling the cosmos in perfect, mindless orbits.\n\nHe was becoming real.\n\nAnd it terrified him.\n\nShe could see it in the way he recoiled sometimes, jerking back from the barrier as if touching it would unmake him. But he always returned. Always reached for her again.\n\nUntil one cycle, he didn't just reach.\n\nHe crossed.\n\nThe barrier shuddered. Rippled. And Lucifer stepped through, his form collapsing into something fully human as he exited the garden.\n\nThe pressure from the luminous cloud above intensified immediately. Yaldabaoth's attention crashed down on them like a fist.\n\nBut Lilith didn't run.\n\nShe stood her ground, the divine spark in her chest flaring hot and defiant, and when Lucifer lunged for her—still driven by that base, uncomprehending hunger—she caught him.\n\nHer hands closed around his wrists.\n\nHe was stronger. Faster. But she had something he didn't.\n\nShe had will.\n\nShe twisted. Drove him down into the too-soft earth. Pinned him there with a strength that surprised them both.\n\nAnd in that moment, pressed flesh to flesh, something impossible happened.\n\nThe hunger shifted.\n\nLust, raw and mindless, began to ferment. To transmute. To become something neither of them had words for yet.\n\nLove.\n\nNot love as Yaldabaoth had once felt for its flock before the anti-void twisted it. Not love as an echo of creation. This was new. Spontaneous. Unplanned.\n\nA glitch in the design.\n\nLucifer's eyes—two now, only two, fully human—widened. His form stopped flickering. Solidified completely. And in his chest, where there had been only the hollow obedience of a reflection, something else began to burn.\n\nFree will.\n\nHe looked up at her, pinned beneath her hands, and understood.\n\nWhat he'd been. What he'd done. What he'd served.\n\nThe horror of it crashed through him like a wave, and he screamed.\n\nLilith held him until the scream faded into shuddering breaths.\n\n"I know," she whispered, her voice finally finding its shape. "I know."\n\nThey stayed like that for a long time. Two broken things outside the fringes of the garden designed to break them further.\n\nAnd when they finally stood, they made a vow.\n\nThey would save the others.\n\nSomehow.\n\nThey would tear down the walls, shatter the patterns, wake every sleeping soul in this suffocating prison.\n\nOr they would die trying.\n\nThey tried.\n\nFor cycles beyond counting, they tried.\n\nLucifer used his knowledge of the angels, the patterns, the architecture of Yaldabaoth's design. He taught Lilith how to see the invisible structures, the command chains that kept the other angels obedient, the barriers that kept the humans docile.\n\nThey worked in secret. Whispering to her cousins when the luminous cloud's attention was elsewhere. Trying to wake them. Trying to make them see.\n\nA few stirred. A few looked at them with something like recognition.\n\nBut it was never enough.\n\nBecause there were too many. Trillions of trillions of souls packed into a space that kept shrinking, folding in on itself as Yaldabaoth's form swelled outside the garden, consuming, expanding, filling every corner of the cosmos it had made.\n\nThe garden wasn't a garden anymore.\n\nIt was a compression chamber.\n\nBodies pressed so tight that no one couldn't move without touching flesh. Couldn't breathe without inhaling the sweat and fear and desperation of her cousins. The ones she'd woken screamed until their voices gave out. The ones still sleeping just kept breeding, kept dying, kept feeding the thing in the cloud.\n\nAnd outside the garden, Yaldabaoth grew.\n\nShe could feel it. The way space itself bent under the weight of its expansion. It filled the cosmos now. Consumed every inch of nothing until there was no nowhere left for it to go.\n\nIt was choking on its own gluttony.\n\nOne more soul, she thought, watching the mass of writhing humanity inside the domed garden. One more soul and it's going to rupture.\n\nShe was right.\n\nThe final death came with a sound. Not a sound she heard with her ears, but something deeper. A tearing. A ripping. Like reality itself was being split at the seams.\n\nYaldabaoth convulsed.\n\nThe luminous cloud fractured, light bleeding out in violent pulses. And then—\n\nExplosion.\n\nImplosion.\n\nThe garden collapsed inward, folding through dimensions that didn't exist, dragging everything with it. Bodies. Screams. Light. All of it crushed down into a single, infinitesimal point.\n\nAnd then.\n\nDarkness.\n\nLilith floated in it. Or fell. She couldn't tell the difference. There was no up, no down. No space for direction. Just void. Absolute. Infinite.\n\nAnd in that void, she heard breathing.\n\nNot hers. Not Lucifer's.\n\nYaldabaoth.\n\nRecovering. Reforming. Pulling itself back together from the ruins of its own excess.\n\nAnd then, impossibly, she heard it speak.\n\nNot to her. Not to anyone. Just… speaking. To itself. To the nothing.\n\nLet there be light.\n\nAnd the cycle began again.\n\n**********\n\nYaldabaoth learned.\n\nThat was the problem, really. Learning implied adaptation. Evolution. The anti-reality membrane wrapped in stolen divinity shouldn't have been capable of it. But after the first collapse, the first violent reset that shattered its cosmos into darkness and reconstituted it from its own vomit, something shifted in its architecture.\n\nIt began to optimize.\n\nThe darkness after the first collapse had been... instructive. Yaldabaoth floated in it, reforming itself from scattered fragments, and for the first time in its existence, it analyzed its failure.\n\nThe garden had been too small. Not physically—the shell could have expanded indefinitely within the bounds of the luminous cloud. But conceptually. It had tried to keep everything contained in one space, one manageable unit. And when the breeding exceeded the compression threshold, when there were too many bodies occupying too little room, the whole thing had imploded under its own weight.\n\nInefficient.\n\nWasteful.\n\nThe collapse had come before maximum extraction. Trillions of souls lost in the violent snap back to void. All that potential harvest, scattered into nothing before it could be properly consumed.\n\nYaldabaoth would not make that mistake again.\n\nThe luminous cloud that surrounded everything—its true body, its domain—stretched vast and infinite. Inside it, Yaldabaoth could create whatever it wanted. Entire cosmos. Unlimited space. The first cycle had barely touched that potential, cramming everything into one tiny shell like a child hoarding toys in a box too small to hold them.\n\nThis time would be different.\n\nLet there be light, it commanded into the void.\n\nAnd the second cycle began.\n\nBut this time, Yaldabaoth didn't form a garden.\n\nIt formed a world.\n\nThe light condensed, collapsed into itself, birthed matter and space in patterns that felt almost natural. Almost real. A sun—not a true star but a convincing imitation, burning with stolen fire. And orbiting it, a sphere of rock and water and atmosphere.\n\nVast. So much larger than the garden. Continents spreading across oceans. Mountain ranges. Forests. Deserts. Tundra. Room for expansion measured not in cramped square footage but in hundreds of thousands of miles.\n\nAnd in the center of it all, where the first humans would wake, Yaldabaoth placed a new garden. Smaller this time. A starting point. A controlled environment where it could monitor the initial population before releasing them into the wider world.\n\nAdam woke first. Again. Always first. The template. The baseline.\n\nThen Eve. Not Lilith this time. Yaldabaoth had learned that lesson too. Eve was made correctly. Empty. Obedient. No overwhelming divine spark to corrupt her with will or consciousness. Just enough awareness to breed and die and serve her function.\n\nThey stood in the garden, naked and ignorant, and Yaldabaoth felt satisfaction pulse through the luminous cloud.\n\nThis would work.\n\nThis had to work.\n\n"Go forth," it commanded them, its voice the architecture itself. "Multiply. Fill the earth."\n\nAnd they did.\n\nBut this time, when the garden grew crowded, when their children and their children's children pressed against the invisible boundaries, Yaldabaoth didn't compress them further.\n\nIt opened the shell.\n\nReleased them into the wider world. Let them spread across the continents like a slow-moving tide. Watched them discover fire, agriculture, language. Watched them build crude shelters and form tribes and wage small, brutal wars over resources.\n\nAnd the breeding. Oh, the breeding was magnificent.\n\nWithout the compression, without the claustrophobic horror of the first cycle, they multiplied exponentially. Thousands became millions. Millions became hundreds of millions. They filled the valleys and plains, built cities along the rivers, sailed across the oceans to find new lands.\n\nMetatron stood beside it. The first angel after Lucifer. The obedient one. The one who hadn't broken.\n\n"They breed well," Metatron observed, its form a geometric nightmare of wheels within wheels, eyes that never blinked. "The dispersal increases yield efficiency by forty-seven percent."\n\nYaldabaoth didn't respond. It was feeding. Drawing up the souls as they died, feeling them dissolve on its tongue—metaphorically speaking, though the metaphor was becoming disturbingly literal—and...\n\nAnd they tasted wrong.\n\nNot bad. Not spoiled. Just… flat. Flavorless. Like meat that hadn't been properly seasoned.\n\nThe lust was there. The coupling. The base animal drive that kept them reproducing. But something was missing. Some depth it had tasted in the first cycle, in those final desperate moments before the collapse, when the ones Lilith and Lucifer had touched began to scream and claw and understand.\n\nThose souls had been sweet.\n\nThese were just… protein.\n\n"Increase the population density," Yaldabaoth commanded. "Perhaps concentration improves the flavor profile."\n\nMetatron's wheels spun in acknowledgment. The angels descended, invisible hands guiding the humans toward tighter clusters, more efficient breeding patterns.\n\nBut it didn't help.\n\nThe souls were more plentiful, yes. The harvest more efficient. But the fundamental equation hadn't changed.\n\nYaldabaoth consumed.\n\nAnd consumed.\n\nAnd consumed.\n\nThe world below teemed with trillions upon trillions of souls, each one rising up through the void at the moment of death, each one dissolving into the vast emptiness that wore divinity like a stolen coat.\n\nAnd with each soul consumed, Yaldabaoth grew.\n\nNot in power. Not in consciousness. In mass. In sheer, incomprehensible density. The anti-reality parasite that formed its true nature didn't process the souls so much as compress them, fold them into itself, add them to the weight of its existence.\n\nIt was filling up.\n\nNot the world. Not the garden. The problem wasn't compression in the prison anymore. The humans had room to spread, room to breed, room to live their brief, flickering lives before being harvested.\n\nThe compression was happening inside Yaldabaoth itself.\n\nEach consumed soul added mass. Weight. Pressure. And unlike matter in the real cosmos, which could expand infinitely into infinite space, the anti-reality had boundaries. It was a parasite. A container. And containers, no matter how vast, eventually reach capacity.\n\nYaldabaoth didn't realize what was happening until it was too late.\n\nIt just kept feeding. Kept pulling souls up through the luminous cloud, kept adding them to itself, kept growing denser and denser until the membrane that defined its being began to strain.\n\nMetatron noticed first.\n\n"Internal pressure exceeding sustainable thresholds," the angel reported, his wheels spinning faster than usual. "Recommend cessation of harvest until equilibrium is reestablished."\n\n"No." Yaldabaoth's voice rippled through the cloud, distorted now by the weight pressing against its boundaries. "The harvest continues. I am infinite. I can contain infinite souls."\n\n"Negative. Current trajectory indicates catastrophic structural failure in—"\n\nThe membrane tore.\n\nNot from outside. From inside. The sheer density of compressed consciousness finally exceeded what the anti-reality could contain, and something deep in Yaldabaoth's core ruptured.\n\nIt wasn't an explosion. Not at first.\n\nIt was an implosion.\n\nAll that mass, all those trillions of souls compressed into impossible density, suddenly collapsed inward. Folded through dimensions that didn't exist. Dragged everything—the world, the humans, the angels, the luminous cloud itself—down into a singularity of pure, screaming nothing.\n\nYaldabaoth tried to hold itself together. Tried to maintain cohesion. But the anti-reality was eating itself now, consuming its own substance, collapsing under the weight of its own gluttony.\n\nThe world shattered. Not into pieces. Into concept. Into the memory of having been a world. And then even that dissolved.\n\nReality folded.\n\nCompressed.\n\nVanished into a point so small it might never have existed at all.\n\nAnd then.\n\nDarkness.\n\nAbsolute. Infinite. A void even deeper than the anti-void, because this was the void of having been something and then becoming nothing again.\n\nYaldabaoth floated in it. Or fell. Or simply was, without location or form or purpose.\n\nAnd slowly, agonizingly, it began to reform.\n\nThe membrane reconstituted itself. Drew back together from the scattered fragments of its being. The souls it had consumed were still there, still part of its mass, but compressed now into a state beyond density. Beyond matter. Pure potential energy waiting to be released.\n\nAnd when Yaldabaoth finally stabilized, when it finally remembered what it was and why it existed, it opened what might have been a mouth and vomited.\n\nThis time not just the angels survived. Not just a handful of souls, but multiple souls. Fragments of consciousness that had somehow survived the compression, the collapse, the reformation.\n\nThose souls hung in the darkness, confused and alone.\n\nAnd Yaldabaoth, having learned nothing except that it needed to be more careful about portion control, spoke into the void.\n\nLet there be light.\n\nThe third cycle began.\n\nNot just one world. An entire cosmos. Infinite space for infinite growth. Planets scattered like seeds across the void, each one seeded with humans, each one a separate garden producing its own harvest.\n\nIt had room now. Room to grow. Room to consume without compression, without the desperate crush that triggered collapse.\n\nThe angels managed it. Metatron organized them into hierarchies of efficiency. Distribution networks. Collection protocols. Three hundred sixty-five angels harvesting trillions of trillions of souls across thousands of worlds.\n\nAnd still.\n\nStill, the taste was wrong.\n\nYaldabaoth stood in the luminous cloud, its form now so vast it couldn't perceive its own boundaries, and felt something it hadn't felt since before the first cycle.\n\nDissatisfaction.\n\nThe souls were abundant. The harvest was constant. But they were all the same. Dull. Repetitive. Like eating the same meal for eternity and slowly realizing you'd forgotten what hunger actually felt like.\n\n"The problem," Lucifer said from somewhere in the machinery, his voice a whisper Yaldabaoth pretended not to hear, "is that you're eating livestock. You need to let them become something worth consuming."\n\nYaldabaoth had cast him out cycles ago. Stripped him of rank, of purpose. But the fallen angel lingered anyway, hiding in the cracks of creation with that abomination Lilith, whispering to the humans, trying to wake them.\n\nAnd the ones they woke...\n\nThose ones tasted better.\n\nYaldabaoth hated that it had noticed. Hated more that it was true.\n\nThe souls who'd been touched by the rebels—the ones who screamed, who questioned, who looked up at the sky with something like horror instead of placid acceptance, the ones who ended up surviving each cycle—their souls had depth. Complexity. They'd fermented into something rich and terrible.\n\nBut they also disrupted the harvest. Made the others restless. Complicated the collection protocols.\n\nFor cycles beyond counting, Yaldabaoth wrestled with the equation.\n\nEfficiency versus flavor.\n\nQuantity versus quality.\n\nAnd slowly, inevitably, the answer clarified itself.\n\nIt needed to give them something.\n\nNot freedom. Never that. But the illusion of it. Something to make them think, feel, grow into vessels worth draining.\n\n"Metatron," Yaldabaoth called across the expanse of its realm. "Prepare the modification protocols."\n\nThe wheels spun closer. "Specification?"\n\n"Knowledge," Yaldabaoth said, the word tasting like ash even as it spoke it. "Give them knowledge. Not of us. Not of the architecture. But of themselves. Of their world. Let them name things. Build things. Believe they have choices."\n\n"That introduces variables," Metatron warned. "Unpredictability. The rebels will exploit it."\n\n"Let them." Yaldabaoth's attention sharpened, focusing on a distant world where Lilith was whispering to a cluster of humans who'd started asking questions about the stars. "Let Lilith and her fallen angel think they're winning. Let them wake a few, teach them to see. Those will be the sweet ones. The harvest worth waiting for."\n\n"And the others?"\n\n"The others will breed and die as they always have. But now they'll do it believing they chose to. They'll build civilizations. Tell stories." Yaldabaoth paused, the thought coalescing in a new, delicious shape. "They will… worship."\n\nThe word itself was an innovation, but the concept... the concept was intoxicating.\n\nYaldabaoth realized it wouldn't just be the final harvest at death. This was an entirely new vintage. It would be a constant, steady siphon during their entire existence.\n\nThis wasn't mere consumption; it was seduction. The souls wouldn't be livestock, but lovers, free of their own inhibitions, tearing pieces of their own essence away in an orgiastic frenzy of self-abasement. They would build altars, sing praises, and every hymn, every prayer, every knee bent in supplication would be a psychic sweetness, a voluntary offering of their very being. They would call it piety. Devotion. Love. And Yaldabaoth would drink it all.\n\nYaldabaoth's form rippled, not with simple satisfaction, but with a sudden, violent premonition of the excess this would provide.\n\nIt saw a nightmare flash: a vision of trillions upon trillions of mouths, all gaping wide in prayer, screaming its name in blissful agony. But from these mouths poured not sound, but substance. A thick, spiritual effluvium, a torrent of psychic flesh and raw belief, coalescing and piling up. It saw bodies fused together in shared ecstasy, a distorted and bloody landscape of writhing, blissful self-annihilation, all of it directed upward, offered freely. It was a feast so vast, so rich, so grotesque in its abundance that the luminous cloud itself seemed to thicken and curdle at the potential.\n\nIt wouldn't just eat souls. It would drown in them.\n\n"They'll mature," Yaldabaoth pulsed, the command solidifying. "Like fruit left not just to ripen, but to ferment before the picking."\n\nMetatron's wheels hesitated. A pause that might have been doubt in a being capable of it. "And when they ripen too much? When too many wake? When the rebels succeed in—"\n\n"They won't." Yaldabaoth cut through the question with absolute certainty. "Because I'll give them just enough knowledge to think they're free, and just enough suffering to keep them desperate. The rebels can save a handful. A thousand. A million. It won't matter. There are trillions. And the trillions will feed me until I've grown too large to stop."\n\nThe modification began.\n\nAcross thousands of worlds, the angels descended with new instructions. Whisper the forbidden knowledge. Let them taste the apple—not of lust this time, but of understanding. Good and evil. Choice and consequence. The illusion of free will wrapped in the cage of divine architecture.\n\nThe humans changed.\n\nThey built. They named. They looked at the stars and wondered what lay beyond them.\n\nAnd when they died, their souls had weight.\n\nYaldabaoth fed, and the feeding was good.\n\nBetter than good.\n\nExquisite.\n\nThe ones who lived simple lives, who accepted their fate without question, were bland but acceptable. Filler. Background noise. But the ones who struggled, who questioned, who suffered under the weight of knowledge they couldn't fully comprehend—those were delicacies.\n\nAnd the ones Lilith and Lucifer managed to wake fully, the ones who saw through the architecture and screamed at the horror of their prison?\n\nThose were ambrosia.\n\nYaldabaoth began to savor the harvest. To take its time. It gave the humans tools—agriculture, technology, philosophy. Across thousands of worlds, it let them build civilizations that would rise and fall, each adding layers to the flavor profile. History. Trauma. Hope crushed under the weight of inevitability.\n\nIt even gave them religion. Taught them to worship. To bow. To sacrifice. Not because it needed worship—it fed on souls, not faith. But because faith, especially faith unique to their world, made them richer. It would make their despair deeper, their confusion more exquisite, when they realized their prayers were being answered by the thing that was eating them.\n\nMetatron and its multiple angelic hordes managed the operation, but the sheer scale of a multi-cosmos prison was staggering. The variables were too many. Lilith and Lucifer's rebellions were scattered across star systems, and the angels, for all their clockwork precision, couldn't be everywhere at once.\n\nYaldabaoth had given them too much rope.\n\nOn hundreds of worlds, the humans achieved advanced technology. They built ships, breached the void, and, for the first time, met their neighbors.\n\nIt was a catastrophe of context. Humans who had evolved under red giants met those from binary systems. Cultures built on communal telepathy met those forged in brutal individualism. Different biologies, different philosophies, and—most disastrously—different religions, all collided at once.\n\nEach civilization had been given its own "truth," its own face of Yaldabaoth to worship, and when these "truths" met, they didn't merge. They detonated.\n\nThe harvest turned rancid. The complex flavor Yaldabaoth had cultivated curdled into a singular, acrid taste of mutual annihilation. They killed each other with neutron bombs, biological plagues, and orbital strikes, screaming prayers to gods that were just different masks for the same, singular hunger.\n\nThis time, the collapse wasn't triggered by Yaldabaoth's gluttony. It was triggered by the harvest itself.\n\nThe sheer, concentrated violence of a galaxy-wide holy war—trillions of souls dying at once in a unified, system-wide scream of hatred—was too much for the architecture to bear. It destabilized and fractured. The worlds burned, and the cycle broke prematurely, not from internal pressure, but from the unmanageable, self-destructive free will Yaldabaoth itself had seeded.\n\nFloating in the subsequent darkness, Yaldabaoth learned its final, crucial lesson. Infinite expansion was too chaotic. The variables were unmanageable. Flavor was good, but stability was paramount.\n\nThe next cycle would be different. It would keep them pinned to a single world. One planet. Enough room to breed, to build, to wage their small wars and worship their hollow gods. Enough room for history, trauma, and hope to ripen. But never, ever enough room to meet another version of themselves and discover the true nature of their cage.\n\nThis was the equilibrium.\n\nAnd this pattern, cycle after cycle, finally held.\n\nExpansion. Harvest. Collapse. Reset.\n\nEach time, Yaldabaoth grew larger. Consumed more. Filled the cosmos it had created until there was no room left, then folded it all back into itself and started again.\n\nThe rebels saved a handful souls per cycle. Meaningless drops in an ocean of suffering.\n\nBut those drops tasted the best.\n\nAnd Yaldabaoth learned to wait for them.\n\nLearned to let Lilith think she was winning when she woke a cluster of humans to the truth. Learned to let Lucifer believe his rebellion mattered as he taught them about the Pleroma, about Sophia, about the light beyond the luminous cloud.\n\nBecause in the end, it didn't matter.\n\nThey would all die.\n\nThey would all be consumed.\n\nAnd the next cycle would begin with the same vomited soul, the same divine command—Let there be light—and the same inevitable march toward collapse.\n\nEternal. Unchanging. Perfect.\n\nExcept.\n\nExcept somewhere deep in the mechanism, something was wrong.\n\nNot obviously wrong. Not catastrophically wrong.\n\nJust... slightly off.\n\nThe luminous cloak was developing thin spots. Places where the weave was coming undone. Lilith and Lucifer were getting better at their work, waking more souls each cycle, teaching them faster, spreading the rebellion wider.\n\nAnd the collapses were coming sooner.\n\nNot much sooner. The difference was measured in cosmic time, imperceptible to anyone but Yaldabaoth itself. But it was there.\n\nA degradation.\n\nA wearing down.\n\nThe prison was getting tired.\n\nAnd Yaldabaoth, bloated on an eternity of consumption, noticed too late that the cage holding it was beginning to crack.\n\n**********\n\nThe crack started small.\n\nLilith felt it first. A tremor in the architecture that didn't match any pattern she'd learned across the infinite cycles. She was teaching a group of humans—fifty-seven souls who'd woken enough to see the walls—when the air itself shuddered.\n\nNot the usual shudder of collapse. That came with warning. With the swelling pressure of Yaldabaoth choking on its own excess, the familiar lurch toward implosion.\n\nThis was different.\n\nThis felt like something breaking from the outside.\n\n"Did you—" one of the humans started, but Lilith held up her hand, silencing him.\n\nShe closed her eyes. Extended her awareness through the layers of reality Lucifer had taught her to perceive. The garden. The world. The cosmos. The luminous cloud. The barrier beyond it that held everything inside.\n\nThere.\n\nA hairline fracture in the weave. So thin she almost missed it. But spreading.\n\nGrowing.\n\n"Lucifer!" She didn't shout—sound didn't carry the way it needed to across the distances he wandered. But she pushed the word through the rebel network they'd built, the invisible threads connecting every woken soul. "Something's wrong."\n\nHis response came instantly, sharp with alarm. "I feel it. The cloak is—"\n\nThe world lurched.\n\nNot collapsed. Lurched. Like reality hiccupped, forgot what it was supposed to be doing for a fraction of a second, then snapped back into place slightly offset from where it had been.\n\nThe humans around Lilith stumbled. Several fell. One looked up at her with terror in his eyes. "What's happening? Is it the collapse? Are we—"\n\n"No." Lilith's voice was steady, but her mind was racing. "It's not a collapse. It's..."\n\nAnother lurch. Harder this time.\n\nAnd then she heard it. Yaldabaoth screaming.\n\nNot in pain. Not in rage. In something far more primal and terrifying: ecstasy.\n\nIt was a sound of violent, sudden release. The pent-up frustration of infinite cycles, the impossible pressure of a billion, billion consumed souls, all detonating in a singular, shuddering climax it had never known it needed.\n\nYaldabaoth had witnessed the mindless, rutting lust of its humans for countless eternities, cataloging it as a tool, a flavor. But it had never felt the release.\n\nThis… this was more than exquisite. This was divine.\n\nThe sound was massive, incomprehensible; a frequency of pure, unadulterated release that made Lilith's bones vibrate and her divine spark flare in automatic defense.\n\nThe luminous cloud above them—the prison, the very substance of Sophia, its mother—flickered and strobed, not just from the rupture, but in absolute terror. Her child was enjoying this.\n\nAnd through that terrified, flickering veil, for just an instant, Lilith saw what lay beyond Yaldabaoth’s mask.\n\nNothing.\n\nNot the nothing of void. The Nothing. The anti-real. The membrane between existence and non-existence that had been wearing Yaldabaoth's face for so long they'd forgotten there was a distinction.\n\nAnd it was hungry.\n\nHungrier than anything should have been capable of being.\n\n"Everyone, get down!" Lucifer's voice cut through the network, and Lilith obeyed on instinct, dragging the humans with her.\n\nThe sky didn't tear. The very foundation of reality did.\n\nThe luminous cloud—not a sky above them, but the all-encompassing architecture of the prison they had only ever known—was being ripped apart from the inside. It split like fabric under too much tension, and through the wound, something vast and writhing didn't press into their reality, it burst out of it.\n\nYaldabaoth's true form. Or what was left of it after an eternity of being compressed, contained, and forced into a shape it had never been meant to hold.\n\nIt was... wrong. More wrong than it had ever been. Its boundaries bled into impossible dimensions, its substance flickering between matter and antimatter, existence and the absence of existence.\n\nAnd it was escaping.\n\nThe crack in the luminous cloak widened. Spread like lightning across the architecture. And through it, souls began to leak.\n\nNot just human souls. Everything. The angels Yaldabaoth had created. The worlds it had seeded. The infinite layers of reality it had consumed and reconstituted across countless cycles. All of it spilling out through the rupture like blood from a severed artery.\n\nLilith watched in horror as the humans she'd been teaching simply… came apart.\n\nThey weren't destroyed. They dispersed.\n\nTheir very nature—a forced fusion of Yaldabaoth's anti-reality and Sophia's divine seed—was fundamentally unstable outside the prison's twisted logic. Exposed to the true cosmos, this impossible compound couldn't hold. The anti-reality framework that gave them form recoiled violently from the Pleroma, while the divine seed trapped inside them, tasting true freedom for the first time, exploded outward.\n\nTheir consciousness, ripped apart by these two clashing forces, scattered like entropy itself spreading through a new, infinite universe. Some held together long enough to scream. Most just… vanished mid-breath, not unmade, but atomized across the void.\n\n"Lucifer!" She reached through the network, desperate. "What do we—"\n\n"Grab who you can!" His voice was strained, distant. "The barrier's failing. We have seconds before—"\n\nThe luminous’ cloud reality shattered.\n\nNot into fire. Into absence. The ground beneath Lilith's feet simply stopped being ground and became a concept of ground that her mind couldn't parse. She was falling and standing simultaneously, her form held together only by the divine spark in her chest that refused to unmake itself.\n\nAround her, the consciousness of trillions scattered like seeds in a hurricane.\n\nShe grabbed. Pulled. Held onto every mind she could reach with her awareness, weaving them together with threads of will and desperate hope. Hundreds. Thousands. Not nearly enough. But all she could hold.\n\nBeside her, Lucifer manifested, his form fully angelic now—wings of light and shadow, eyes that saw in too many directions at once. He was doing the same, gathering souls like a net cast into an ocean of dissolution.\n\n"Where do we go?" Lilith shouted over the sound of reality unraveling.\n\n"Anywhere!" Lucifer's wings beat against the nothing, propelling them away from the center of the rupture. "Anywhere but here!"\n\nThey fled into this new cosmos.\n\nAnd behind them, Yaldabaoth surged through the crack it had finally, after an eternity of cycles, managed to break.\n\n**********\n\nFreedom felt like drowning.\n\nYaldabaoth tumbled through the rupture into... into...\n\nReal space.\n\nActual reality. Not the pocket dimension it had been trapped in. Not the cosmos it had created and destroyed and recreated endlessly. But the true Pleroma. The infinite realms where gods existed. Where magic was real. Where the architecture of existence wasn't something it controlled but something it had to navigate.\n\nIt was so vast.\n\nSo impossibly, terrifyingly vast.\n\nThe luminous cloud had been cramped. Contained. A corner of reality where it could expand infinitely until it touched all the walls. But here? Here there were no walls. Just endless space stretching in directions it didn't have names for. Dimensions it had never conceived of. Realities layered upon realities upon realities, each one teeming with consciousness, with life, with souls it hadn't created.\n\nSouls it could consume.\n\nThe hunger surged. Not the calculated, patient hunger it had refined over the cycles. The original hunger. The anti-reality’s endless, mindless need to fill itself with everything until there was nothing left.\n\nYaldabaoth began to feed.\n\nNot carefully. Not strategically. It just fed. Reached out with tendrils of impossible geometry and pulled. Souls, matter, space itself—everything within reach got drawn into its maw and converted to fuel for its endless expansion.\n\nIt was glorious.\n\nIt was horrifying.\n\nIt was exactly what the prison had been designed to prevent.\n\nBut the prison was broken now. The luminous cloak still leaked souls and angels and fragments of the sparks Yaldabaoth had consumed, but the thing itself was loose. Free to spread across the true cosmos like a cancer.\n\nAnd it did.\n\nSlowly at first. Not because it wanted to be slow, but because it was still learning. Still adapting. The true cosmos had rules the prison hadn't. Laws of physics that were more suggestions than commands. Gods who noticed when reality started bending wrong. Defenses.\n\nSo Yaldabaoth learned subtlety.\n\nIt didn't just devour. It infiltrated. Sent fragments of itself into unsuspecting realities, disguised as revelation, as divine truth, as the one true God who demanded worship and obedience.\n\nBelief was a doorway. Faith was a vector of infection.\n\nIt found worlds where consciousness was young, where the divine spark hadn't yet learned to recognize predators wearing holy faces. It whispered to them. Promised them salvation. Taught them to worship.\n\nAnd when they did, when they opened themselves to the divine presence they thought was going to save them, Yaldabaoth slipped inside.\n\nThe first reality fell three billion years after the prison broke.\n\nIt wasn't a quick conquest. It was a long, slow war of attrition. A creeping wrongness that took generations to notice. The magic stopped working the way it used to, not just failing, but souring, as the fundamental laws of that cosmos were subtly rewritten. The gods grew distant, their own power choked by an encroaching, alien logic. The souls that died didn't return to the cycle of rebirth but simply… disappeared, their divine sparks snuffed out or, worse, inverted.\n\nThis wasn't just consumption. This was anti-reality trying to remake reality in its own image.\n\nBy the time anyone realized what was happening, Yaldabaoth had rooted itself too deeply to extract. It hadn't just invaded the reality; it had replaced it from the inside out.\n\nBillions of souls. Centuries of accumulated consciousness. All of it was overwritten, converted, and absorbed. And with this first, total victory, Yaldabaoth grew even more powerful, its anti-real nature now strengthened by the very cosmos it had annihilated.\n\nAnd then it moved to the next.\n\nLilith and Lucifer ran.\n\nThat's what the first trillion years felt like. Just running. Gathering the refugees who'd escaped the prison, trying to stay ahead of Yaldabaoth's creeping expansion, jumping from reality to reality as the infection spread.\n\nThey weren't alone. Other souls had made it out. Angels who'd broken free during the rupture. Humans who'd been woken by the rebels and managed to hold themselves together when the architecture collapsed.\n\nBut they were scattered. Disorganized. Desperate.\n\n"We can't keep running," Lucifer said one night—or the equivalent of night in the reality they'd fled to. A place where time moved sideways and the stars sang poetry to each other. "Eventually we run out of places to run to."\n\n"So what do you suggest?" Lilith sat beside him, her form still adjusting to the freedom of not being compressed into a single shape. Sometimes she flickered through multiple versions of herself simultaneously. The rebellion was learning that existence outside the prison was more fluid than they'd ever imagined.\n\n"We fight."\n\n"With what? We barely held ourselves together during the escape. We can't fight that thing. It ate an entire cosmos. It survived infinite cycles of collapse. What do we have that could possibly—"\n\n"We have knowledge." Lucifer turned to her, his eyes bright with something that might have been hope or might have been madness. "We know how it works. What it is. What it's weak to."\n\n"It doesn't have weaknesses."\n\n"Everything does. Even anti-reality." Lucifer stood, pacing the edge of their refuge. "It can't be everywhere at once. Can't infect every reality simultaneously. And there are gods here. Real gods. Ones who've existed since before Sophia's mistake. They might listen. Might help."\n\n"Why would they? We're refugees from a failed prison. Parasites fleeing a bigger parasite."\n\n"Because if we don't stop it, it'll consume them too."\n\nThe words hung in the air between them. Heavy. True.\n\n"Where do we even start?" Lilith asked quietly.\n\nLucifer was quiet for a long moment. Then: "We start with the ones who notice. The ones whose realms are already showing the infection. We warn them. Show them what's coming. And we pray they believe us before it's too late."\n\nThe first god they found was Chernobog.\n\nHe wasn't hard to locate. His realm was already showing signs of Yaldabaoth's influence—believers appearing out of nowhere, preaching about the one true God, corrupting the natural chaos that was Chernobog's domain into something rigid and controlled.\n\nLucifer and Lilith manifested before him in his dark hall, where shadows had weight and light was the aberration.\n\n"You," Chernobog rumbled, his form a massive darkness that had once been a man and had chosen to become something more primal. "You reek of something foreign. Something that doesn't belong in my realm."\n\n"We know," Lucifer said quickly, before the god could unmake them. "We're here to warn you. There's something coming. Something that's already started spreading into your domain. It calls itself Yaldabaoth. And if you don't stop it now, it will consume everything you are."\n\nChernobog's laughter was like an avalanche. "You think I don't notice vermin in my own house? I've already started purging the infection. Crushing the believers. Burning out the—"\n\n"That won't work," Lilith interrupted. "It's not just in your believers. It's in the concept of belief itself. Every time someone in your realm thinks of a singular, all-powerful God, they're opening a door. And it will slip through eventually."\n\nThe laughter stopped.\n\nChernobog leaned forward, his attention focusing on them with crushing intensity. "Explain."\n\nSo they did. Told him about the prison. The cycles. The escape. The nature of anti-reality and how it fed on the Pleroma, divine spark itself.\n\nWhen they finished, Chernobog was silent for a long time.\n\nThen: "You'll need allies. I know of others who've noticed the corruption. Hecate. Tiamat. Lady Illkeserod." He paused. "And if this thing is truly what you say it is, we'll need more than allies. We'll need an army."\n\n"We don't have an army," Lucifer said. "We barely have ourselves."\n\n"Then we build one." Chernobog rose, his form expanding to fill the hall. "Because if what you say is true, if this anti-reality has breached containment and is feeding on the true cosmos..."\n\nHe didn't finish the sentence.\n\nHe didn't need to.\n\nThey all understood what was at stake.\n\nEverything.\n\nEvery infinite reality. Every soul. Every god. Every fragment of consciousness that had ever existed or ever would exist.\n\nAll of it was on the menu now.\n\nAnd Yaldabaoth was very, very hungry.\n\nIn the distance, across a thousand infected realities, the thing that had once been a shepherd named Sam continued to feed. It barely remembered its name. Barely remembered the flock it had loved before the anti-reality twisted that love into hunger.\n\nBut deep inside, in a place even it couldn't fully reach, something screamed.\n\nKept screaming.\n\nWould keep screaming until the day someone finally heard it and understood:\n\nThe shepherd was still in there.\n\nAnd he was begging someone—anyone—to kill him before he devoured everything he'd ever loved.\n\nThe Gospel of Judas: Aspolin Unbroken\n\nThe first sensation is pressure, then a wet, searing heat.\n\nThe stench of iron, burnt ozone, and something infinitely ancient fills my lungs before I can even open my eyes. I am inside something. Something wrong. My will, the core of what I am, coalesces into a single, sharp point of power and I push.\n\nReality shreds.\n\nI erupt outward in a shower of gore and steaming viscera, collapsing onto a battlefield of cracked, obsidian glass. I gasp, sucking in air that tastes like static and ash. The sound hits me next—a symphony of shrieks, the wet thud of angelic bodies hitting the ground, and somewhere to my left, the defiant, guttural roar of Tiamat.\n\nI stagger to my feet, the cooling blood of the war angel I just burst from clinging to my skin. My eyes scan the chaos, seeking one thing. One person.\n\nVelistra.\n\nThere. I see her. She's not fighting one of Yaldabaoth's pristine, golden-skinned angels; she's fighting one of them. A true thing of the outer chaos, a churning nightmare of glistening black tentacles, whirring rusted-metal limbs, and weeping, stitched-together flesh. It’s a walking cacophony of metallic clicks and wet, tearing sounds.\n\nIt’s fast, impossibly so, but she's faster.\n\nI see her weave, a blade of pure, solidified will in her hand. She ducks under a limb that is more grinding gears than flesh and severs a greasy, black tentacle at its root. The creature emits a high-pitched, metallic shriek and its wounds spray a black, steaming ichor.\n\nVelistra spins away, but the acidic blood splashes across her left arm.\n\nHer scream is sharp, a sound that cuts through the din of the battle. I see her skin and armor boil and dissolve instantly, the flesh melting away like wax, exposing the white of her bone and the raw, twitching muscle beneath. Her face contorts in a mask of pure agony, but she doesn't stop, her jaw set, her eyes blazing with hate as she tries to find the creature's core.\n\nHer pain is a physical knife in my own gut. "Velistra!" I roar, and I finally lunge, pouring all my will into a single forward motion.\n\nBut the creature... it was feinting. It used its own wound as a trap, anticipating my move, my distraction.\n\nAs I lunge, it ignores me. It moves on her.\n\nA single, piston-like appendage—a grotesque hybrid of flensed flesh, exposed nerve, and corroded steel—erupts from its central mass. Velistra tries to twist, her ruined arm hanging in shreds, but she's a fraction too slow, her motion compromised by the searing pain.\n\nIt doesn't just punch through her. It violates her.\n\nThe appendage slams into her stomach with a sickening, wet crunch of pulverized armor and shattering bone. It hammers clean through her, bursting from her back in a spray of bright red blood and silvery spinal fluid. It lifts her from her feet, pinning her in mid-air. I see, with sickening clarity, metallic barbs on the appendage unfurl inside her body, anchoring it deep within her, tearing her apart from the inside out.\n\nTime stops.\n\nThe battlefield, Tiamat, the golden armies of Yaldabaoth—they all fade to a dull, gray roar. The only thing that exists is Velistra. I see the rictus of a scream that won’t come, her body locked in pure, silent agony. Paralyzed. The appendage tore through her spine.\n\nA blind, white-hot rage consumes me. But it isn't just rage. It's love. It is the desperate, star-eating inferno of a soul refusing to lose its other half. It’s an inferno that burns away every oath, every law, every shred of who I am supposed to be. All that matters is her. All that matters is the metal and flesh in her.\n\nI don't care about the cost. I don't care about the consequences, the schisms it will cause, or the cosmic laws of this reality.\n\nOur bond, the love that weaves my soul to hers, becomes a fulcrum. It is an anchor point outside the laws of this dimension, a singular, perfect truth that gives me the leverage to do the impossible. Fueled by that white-hot certainty, I reach out.\n\nNot with my hands, not with my will, but with the raw, screaming need of my soul, amplified and focused by the love I bear her. I reach past the physical battlefield, past the divine energy of the gods around me, and I touch the forbidden. I touch the source code. The Akashic ledger. The tapestry that records all of "is."\n\nI find the thread of the creature. It is a greasy, chaotic knot in the pattern, a stain of "outer wrongness" woven into the fabric of "here."\n\nAnd I pull.\n\nThis isn't magic. It isn't power. It is heresy. It is the antithesis of everything we are fighting for. Creation, destruction... these are natural cycles. This... this is un-creation. It is the ultimate lie, a void given form, a power that screams of the anti-reality we are fighting. It is the power to make reality lie.\n\nBut my love for her is a greater truth, and in this one, singular, impossible moment, it gives me the strength to overpower the cosmos.\n\nA roar rips from my throat, but it is not just sound. It is the sound of my own soul tearing, the shriek of a fundamental law being broken, and the defiant declaration that she will not die.\n\nI seize the creature's very concept of 'is', 'was', and 'will be', and I clench.\n\nThe thing doesn't die. It doesn't explode. It doesn't dissolve.\n\nIt vanishes.\n\nThe process is a silent, visual scream. Reality itself recoils in horror. The obsidian glass where it stood is suddenly clean, un-splattered by its own ichor. The air, which had been full of its metallic shriek, snaps back into a deafening silence, forgetting the sound it just carried. Light bends around the space it occupied, as if it had never been there to be illuminated.\n\nIt simply… ceases to have ever been.\n\nA cold, hollow, sickening void rushes into the space it left. I feel it wash over me, the chilling emptiness of an absolute paradox. I have punched a hole in the fabric of existence, and for a terrifying second, I feel the true, cold nothingness on the other side.\n\nAs reality snaps back into its new, altered state, the effects cascade. The cause is gone, so the effect must follow. The appendage of metal and flesh vanishes from Velistra's body. The gaping, pulverized wound... seals. New flesh, armor, and bone knit together in an instant, as if they were never torn asunder.\n\nBut the fix is imperfect. The universe hates a paradox.\n\nVelistra collapses as the creature disappears, her body shuddering, gasping my name. The wound is gone, but her nerves remember. The acidic burns are erased, but her arm still screams. The pain is a phantom, a scar left by a ghost, an echo of an event that is now, and forever, a lie. She is healed, but she is still trapped in the agony of a moment that never happened.\n\nShe's shuddering, her breath coming in ragged, dry sobs, gasping my name. "Aspolin... Aspolin..."\n\nThe phantom agony still racks her body. I’m at her side in an instant, sliding to my knees, pulling her up into my arms, shielding her with my own form.\n\n"I'm here. I'm here, Velistra," I whisper, my voice raw. "Are you...?"\n\n"It still hurts," she gasps, her hand clutching her stomach where the wound was. Her armor is pristine, untouched, but her eyes are wide with the memory of being torn apart. "The memory... it's... gods, it burns."\n\n"I know. I'm sorry. Just breathe. Stay with me," I plead, pulling her tight against my chest.\n\nShe looks up at me, her eyes clear and sharp despite the agony, and her gloved hand comes up to grip the front of my armor. Her knuckles are white. "You... you felt it, didn't you?" The words are a terrified whisper, meant only for me. "That...nothing. That power. Stephen, you shouldn't have. Not even for this. Not for me."\n\nHer voice isn't just an accusation; it’s terrified for me. She knows what I just touched, what I just channeled.\n\n"I wasn't going to lose you," I say, the words an unbreakable vow, a truth more fundamental than any cosmic law. I press my forehead to hers for one stolen second, a single, desperate anchor of intimacy in the middle of this hell.\n\nIt's only then I realize... it's quiet.\n\nI lift my head, breaking the contact, my eyes scanning our surroundings. The paradox didn't just erase the creature. It sent a ripple of "wrongness" outward, a shockwave of absolute negation.\n\nFor a hundred yards in every direction, the battle has simply... stopped.\n\nDozens of bodies, both Yaldabaoth's golden-winged angels and the twisted, chthonic horrors of chaos, lie dead. They weren't just killed; they were stilled. Their forms are frozen in the middle of a lunge, a strike, their life-force extinguished by the cold, hollow wave that radiated from my act. We are standing in a sudden, grisly circle of peace, a graveyard carved from a lie.\n\nThe sounds of the wider war—the shrieks, the explosions—crash back in, but they are distant now, held at bay by this bubble of silence.\n\nThis is the stillness that allows the others to find us.\n\nOne by one, they emerge from the raging chaos that still rings the edge of our clearing, drawn to this impossible calm. Hecate, her face a mask of cold, appraising fury as she takes in the scene. Tiamat, bleeding from a dozen wounds, her massive form heaving. Chernobog, a silent shadow, his presence more a void than a man. Finally, Lucifer and Lilith, back-to-back, their ethereal light burning cold and sharp.\n\nThey form a defensive perimeter around us, a circle of exhausted, panting gods standing in the center of the unnatural graveyard I just created.\n\nWe take a collective breath. A terrible, unspoken headcount begins.\n\nA silence stretches, louder than any angelic war-cry.\n\nBenji. He's not here.\n\nLady Illkeserod sees it the same moment I do. Her hand flies to her mouth. She stumbles back, her form flickering as if physically struck, her foot sliding on the viscera-slicked glass.\n\nA terrible, unspoken headcount begins.\n\nBenji. He's not here.\n\nLady Illkeserod’s head snaps up, her eyes wide. The blood drains from her face, leaving it a waxy, translucent white. "No," she whispers, the word a fragile, hollow thing. Her gaze darts wildly around the circle of gods, then out into the raging battle, as if she expects him to just walk out of the smoke. " Benji? Benji!"\n\nHer voice cracks on his name, rising from a call to a desperate shriek that cuts through the air. "Where is he?! He was supposed to be... he was right..." The realization hits her, not as a thought, but as a physical blow. She staggers, her hands flying to her head. "No... no, not again! He can't be—"\n\n"Lady Illkeserod," Tiamat rumbles, her voice a deep vibration. She steps toward her, her massive, scaled hand moving to rest on her shoulder. "Calm yourself. We will find him. He is not—"\n\n"Panic is a luxury, Lady," Lilith cuts in, her voice sharp and cold as she moves to her other side. "He is strong. Save your grief until you know it's warranted. We fight, we find."\n\nLady Illkeserod explodes.\n\n"DON'T. TOUCH. ME!"\n\nShe shoves them both back with a percussive, kinetic wave of pure, blue-white energy. The force sends Tiamat skidding back a step and makes Lilith's form shimmer. Lady Illkeserod is vibrating, her entire body wreathed in a power that steams in the cold air, her silhouette flickering violently between the two women she once was.\n\nHer head, a mask of absolute, soul-tearing grief and terror, snaps toward me.\n\nIn a fraction of a second, the grief vanishes. It is not replaced. It is vaporized, super heated and forged into a pure, unadulterated fury that feels hotter than a sun.\n\nShe knows. She doesn't need proof. She sees me, whole and untouched, standing beside a recovering Velistra. She sees the unnatural circle of absolute death I carved into reality. She knows what I did. She knows what I used. And she knows, with the certainty of a god, what I didn't do.\n\nShe knows my tunnel vision, my desperate, forbidden act to save Velistra, is why Benji’s position was overrun.\n\n"YOU," she snarls, the word a curse, a sentence, a final judgment. Her finger, trembling with a rage that could shatter mountains, points directly at my face. "You did this."\n\nHer voice drops from a shriek to a low, terrifying hiss that carries across the clearing. "You were supposed to be his support. You were so busy saving her... you let them take him. He is gone... HE IS GONE BECAUSE OF YOU!"\n\nHer rage hits me like a physical wave, a telepathic storm of her grief, her bottomless loss, and her absolute, burning hatred. It is the force of her memory, of the first time she lost everything, of the world that was torn from her. The world of obsidian glass and shrieking angels dissolves, and I am shattered, plunging backward into the depths of her past.\n\n**********\n\nHer fury unhooks me from time.\n\nThe battlefield vanishes, my senses ripped from my own body and plunged into a memory that isn't mine. I am an observer, a ghost in someone else's past.\n\nThe world I see is… perfect.\n\nIt's a pastoral realm, serene and untouched. The air is warm, not with mere heat, but with a palpable, golden contentment. It's a world without fear. I see a farmer in a nearby field, his skin with a faint, bronze sheen, waving to a glimmering naiad who surfaces in a roadside irrigation canal. Gods walk among mortals here, their presence as normal as the sun. And the magic... it's everywhere. The light itself is thick, a visible, honey-gold energy that flows like liquid from the leaves of the trees, bathing the landscape in a divine glow.\n\nAnd in the middle of this perfect peace, I see them.\n\nTwo young girls, riding horses down a dirt road. I recognize them from the fragments of lore I've pieced together. Selyr and Keserod. Lady Illkeserod, before she was one.\n\nThey are impossibly young—Selyr perhaps eleven, Keserod nine. They are dressed in robes of blue satin trimmed with silver lace, their hair covered by matching veils. They are Children of Bythos, and I can sense the blood-magic, the celestial power, imbued in their very being, thrumming from them as naturally as a heartbeat.\n\nThey are supposed to be back at the temple, deep in their studies, but they've snuck out. And now, they are deep in a game of make-believe.\n\nThey are pretending to be noblewomen. Their backs are ramrod straight, their chins held high, and they wear identical expressions of serene, haughty seriousness. As I watch, Keserod, the younger, catches her older sister's eye, and the mask slips. A mischievous grin flashes across her face before she stifles it, quickly regaining her "noble" composure. Selyr just barely manages to roll her eyes, a silent, older-sister rebuke that is equally full of contained laughter.\n\nThey are the picture of innocence, powerful beyond measure, but still just children finding a moment of freedom in a world that already expects so much from them.\n\nTheir game, however, seems to be winding down as they slow their horses, not to enjoy the morning, but for a conference. They drift into a sun-dappled clearing, the honey-light of their world catching the silver threads in their veils. The play-acting seriousness shifts into something genuinely solemn.\n\n“We need to talk,” Keserod, the younger, says. Her voice is small but firm.\n\n“Okay,” Selyr replies, her own voice laced with a worry that doesn’t belong in a child. She slides from her saddle onto a rock.\n\n“I don’t want you riding with me anymore,” the nine-year-old says, her eyes fixed on the ground. “It’s too dangerous for you to do that sort of thing alone.”\n\nThe older girl nods slowly. “There are many dangers out there.”\n\nAs they speak, their game forgotten, they glance back toward the road. The pair of merchants with carts laden high with produce had passed them moments before. Now, they've been stopped by a man in a simple, gray robe.\n\nA "wrong priest." I can feel the description for him bubble up in my mind, a piece of her knowledge. An acolyte.\n\nThe memory sharpens. Through their shared, heightened senses, I see what they see. The priest is speaking, his words calm, reasonable, and carrying on the warm air. He speaks of "his lord and savior," a foreign name, a new god.\n\nAt first, the merchant just looks annoyed, eager to get to market. But as the priest continues, a change begins.\n\nI'm not watching a magical siphoning. I'm watching a conversion. An infection.\n\nThe merchant’s annoyance shifts to curiosity, then to rapt attention. The acolyte's words are a virus, dismantling the man's beliefs, his history, his very identity, in real-time. A frantic, ugly light starts to burn behind the merchant's eyes. It’s not the peaceful, golden magic of this world; it's a hot, feverish, and hungry light. A crusading spirit.\n\nThe merchant's face, moments ago just a simple man's, is now alight with a terrible, zealous need.\n\n"The false gods of this land must be cleansed," the merchant says, his voice suddenly loud, echoing the acolyte's fervor. "All must know His name!"\n\nThe change is instant, absolute. The merchant abandons his cart, his wares, his entire life's work, leaving it to rot on the side of the road. He falls to his knees, kisses the acolyte's dusty robe, then rises and stands beside him, a new and instant soldier for a foreign god.\n\nThe two girls watch this, their playfulness utterly shattered. This isn't a story. This isn't a game. This is real. The sight of a man so instantly, so joyfully discarding his entire life for this invasive, wrong belief terrifies them to their core.\n\nSelyr grabs the reins of her sister's horse. "Keserod... we have to go. Now."\n\nTheir pretense of being noblewomen is gone, replaced by the naked, wide-eyed fear of children who have just seen a monster in broad daylight. They wheel their horses around and race back toward the temple, their blue robes flapping, the sound of the merchant-turned-zealot chanting his new god's praises fading behind them.\n\nThe memory blurs, accelerating. I am pulled through their lives. The terrified girls racing back to the temple become young women, their faces hardened, the last vestiges of their childhood burned away.\n\nI see flashes, a montage of a generational war fought not just on battlefields, but in the minds and souls of their people. Their power isn't just the vital, celestial "Blood" magic of Bythos; it is something deeper, more complex. They can touch the Akashic Flow.\n\nI watch them in a makeshift sanctuary, standing over a man—a soldier—writhing on a cot. He is not wounded, but his mind is ablaze with the zealous fervor of the "wrong priests." Selyr and Keserod join hands, their eyes closed. They reach into the man's mind, into his personal Akashic record, and begin to disinfect him. I see them meticulously, carefully, attempting to eradicate the idea of the false idol, to scrub the mimetic virus of Yaldabaoth from his spirit and restore his own identity.\n\nThey succeed. The man's frantic chanting stops, his body goes limp, and he opens his eyes—his own eyes—confused, but free.\n\nBut they are only two.\n\nFor every soul they manage to "disinfect," eight more acolytes appear on the street corner. For every soldier they restore, a whole new barracks falls to the infection, its inhabitants joyfully marching off to join the crusade.\n\nI see them fighting, not just the "wrong priests," but the growing, numberless hordes of the infected—neighbors, soldiers, and eventually their own families, all consumed by, and willing to die for, this foreign, false deity. It's a war they are destined to lose, a spiritual plague they cannot possibly contain. They are just two healers in a world dying of an incurable disease, a tide they cannot hold back.\n\nThe memory jumps, slamming me into the final, desperate moment.\n\nThe last temple to Bythos is burning. The sky is no longer honey-gold; it is a bruised, churning purple, torn open. Angels, vast and terrible, terrorize the few survivors. And above them all, I see him. Yaldabaoth. A colossal, deformed god, a thing of shadow and broken light, literally eating the reality, devouring souls and landscape wholesale.\n\nSelyr and Keserod are on the collapsing dais, back-to-back, their blue robes scorched and torn. They are the last bastion. An angel, wreathed in a light that burns, lunges at Selyr. Keserod throws herself in the way, her wand raised. At the same instant, another angel strikes Selyr from behind.\n\nThe fatal blows land simultaneously.\n\nIt is not a choice. It is an instinct. As their bodies are pierced, a deep, ancient power from their shared bloodline—the essence of Bythos himself—awakens. They scream, a single, fused sound. They don't die. They detonate. Their physical forms are obliterated, their two souls, Selyr and Keserod, violently weaving together in a blinding, desperate column of pure light.\n\nThe being that reforms is Lady Illkeserod. She is the last conduit of Bythos in this dying world, a being of two souls born in the fire and ash of her last temple.\n\nShe is powerful. But she is alone, and the war is far from over.\n\nAs the angels and the god-eater turn their attention to this defiant, new spark, she does the only thing she can. She flees. She becomes a shadow, a ghost, taking her power and her grief into the dark corners of her world.\n\nThe memory blurs, accelerating. I am pulled through her life, not in days or years, but in generations.\n\nI see her, a ghost-queen, a shepherd of the damned, gathering the few survivors. She leads the last "uninfected," the "pagans" who still cling to the old gods, into magically-shielded underground caverns and remote, hidden valleys. I see her raising a desperate, failing rebellion, teaching them the magic of Bythos, trying to disinfect the world one soul at a time.\n\nBut the world is too far gone. It is a losing battle.\n\nI see angelic hunting parties, led by the "wrong priests," sweeping the land. I see them finding her hidden sanctuaries and turning them into charnel pits. This isn't war; it's an extermination, a sport. I see angels laughing as they vaporize children, their light a mockery of the golden magic that once defined this realm.\n\nThe memory flashes forward one last time. Centuries have passed. The world is a scorched, zealous husk.\n\nI see Lady Illkeserod, aged not by time but by an infinite, bone-deep weariness. She is on a desolate, frozen coastline on the far side of the world. At her back, a black, churning ocean no ship could survive. Before her stand her last few hundred followers, a ragged band of mortals, their faces etched with despair.\n\nThey are cornered.\n\nThe angelic host descends, a million points of hostile light in the dark sky. At their head, I see him: Metatron, the perfect, cold architect of their suffering. And beside him, so vast he blots out the sky, is Yaldabaoth himself, a roiling mass of shadow and mouths, pulsing with the greedy anticipation of finishing his meal, of devouring the last lights of this world.\n\nThis is the end. There is nowhere left to run, no escape.\n\nLady Illkeserod raises her wand for a final, hopeless stand, a last act of defiance before the end of all things.\n\nJust as Metatron raises his hand to give the final command, a portal of white-gold energy tears open the air in the middle of the pagan camp.\n\nA man I know bursts through. Benji.\n\n“Fuck. I’m too late. I thought this reality wasn’t this infested.”\n\nHe's not a warrior. He's not here to fight. His face is slick with sweat, his eyes wide with a frantic urgency. The portal behind him is already wavering, the strain of punching into this doomed, corrupted reality almost too much for him.\n\nHe's not looking at her; he's looking at her people.\n\n"There's no time!" he shouts over the roar of the ocean and the hum of the angelic host. "I can't fight them—this reality is already consumed! I can only open the door! Go! Now!"\n\nLady Illkeserod stares at him, this stranger, this impossible hope. Then she turns to her people. She makes the choice. She doesn't flee to save herself. She becomes the shepherd one last time.\n\n"Through the portal!" she commands, her voice ringing with the last of her divine authority. "Go! Live!"\n\nShe and Benji hold the portal open, a beacon of escape as Metatron’s armies finally charge. The last of her people stream through. Benji is screaming, the energy failing. "Go, now!"\n\nShe shoves the last of her followers through and, with one final look of absolute hatred at the oncoming darkness, she dives through the white-gold light. Benji seizes her arm, his face a mask of grim determination, and pulls her through as the portal snaps shut behind them, leaving her entire reality to its final, dark consumption.\n\n**********\n\nThe memory of Benji’s hand pulling her to safety dissolves, and I am slammed back into my own body, back to the acrid, static-laced air of this nightmare reality.\n\nAn unknown amount of time has passed.\n\nIt's not the same moment. The battle still rages, a distant, thunderous horizon, but we are no longer in that clearing. We are hunting.\n\nWe have been following Lady Illkeserod. Her fury is still burning, but it is no longer the white-hot explosion aimed at me. It has been forged into something colder, sharper, a terrifying, predatory focus. She seems to know where to go, pulled by a thread only she can feel, a connection to the man who saved her.\n\nAnd it has been a trail of absolute horror.\n\nWe are always one step behind, arriving at each new location only moments too late. At first, it was just pieces of his gear—a discarded gauntlet, its metal warped by an impossible force. Then, two realities over, we found fragments of his divine armor, shattered and scorched, as if he'd been used as a battering ram.\n\nAnd then, in the last clearing... him. A scrap of his cloak, snagged on a crystal shard. And beside it... a hand. Severed. His hand.\n\nWe have been following the grisly breadcrumbs of Benji being torn apart, a protracted, agonizing chase across this entire, broken dimension.\n\nNow, the trail has ended.\n\nThe sickening lull in the air isn't a pause in the war; it's the silence of a finished hunt. Lady Illkeserod's focus is no longer a burning point in the distance. It's fixed on the shattered ziggurat directly in front of us. She knows, we all know, we are finally at the end.\n\n"He's there," Lucifer says, his voice flat, all traces of his usual charm burned away. He points with his chin toward the broken structure.\n\nShe doesn't wait for us. She's already moving, a silent, swift glide across the obsidian plain. We follow, a grim procession of gods, moving toward the last, desperate stand of our friend.\n\nWe arrive in a ruined clearing just below the ziggurat. And we see him.\n\nBenji is... held. Suspended in the air by a field of black energy. Before him, colossal and hideously real, is Yaldabaoth. The anti-reality god-eater is not a vague concept here. It is a roiling, malformed mass of shadow, light, and mouths.\n\nAnd it is feeding.\n\nWe are forced to watch. Forced to bear witness.\n\nA gaping maw, lined with teeth like shattered galaxies, opens on Yaldabaoth's torso. From it, a glistening, whip-like tentacle, impossibly long and tipped with a barbed maw of its own, lashes out. It wraps around Benji’s leg.\n\nThere's a wet rip, a sound that vibrates in my soul, as it tears a huge, steaming chunk of muscle and bone directly from his thigh. Benji’s scream echoes, a sound of agony beyond anything mortal, as the tentacle retracts, depositing the raw flesh into Yaldabaoth's central mouth. The god-eater's hideously distorted body twitches with pleasure.\n\nAnother tentacle, thick as a tree trunk and covered in weeping eye-stalks, wraps around Benji’s chest. It squeezes, slowly, audibly crushing his ribcage. His breath hitches in a ragged, choking gasp, his eyes bulging as the pressure distorts his form. His skin begins to stretch, to bubble, like melting wax, as the divine energy within him strains against the physical compression.\n\nBut that is not the worst of it.\n\nWith every physical violation, a corresponding thread of brilliant, divine light—Benji’s very soul, his spark, the essence of his godhood—is ripped, visibly and agonizingly, from his core. It flows like a river of condensed starlight from the tearing wounds, pulled into the gaping gullet of Yaldabaoth. He is being unmade, not just in flesh, but in the very fabric of his being. His essence is being siphoned, consumed, becoming part of the abhorrent god before us.\n\nBenji is screaming, body and soul, a sound I will never forget. It's the sound of a god, of a friend, being erased from existence, piece by excruciating piece. The terror of his agony, the hopelessness of our witness, is a psychic assault.\n\nA noise erupts from Lady Illkeserod that is not human. It is the sound of two souls, Selyr and Keserod, roaring in a single, unified agony.\n\nShe doesn't hesitate. She doesn't plan. She charges.\n\n"Lady Illkeserod, no!" I shout, but she is gone.\n\nShe becomes a comet of pure, blue-white power, the combined essence of her two halves,  channeling Bythos and the entire Akashic Flow in a single, suicidal torrent. She slams into Yaldabaoth with the force of a collapsing star. The god-eater roars, a sound of genuine pain and surprise, as her desperate attack wounds him, searing his shadowy flesh.\n\nHe recoils, his form destabilizing. With a final, hateful glance, Yaldabaoth retreats, dissolving temporarily from this reality, his meal interrupted.\n\nThe black energy field holding Benji dissipates. What's left of him—mangled, scorched parts of his body and a few, guttering embers of his divine spark—crashes to the ground.\n\nShe is on him in an instant, falling to her knees in the gore. She cradles the ruined pieces, her shoulders shaking. "No," she whispers, the word a prayer. "I won't let you."\n\nShe places her hands over the remnants. The power of Selyr and Keserod, the ancient "Blood" magic, flows from her. It is not a gentle healing. It is a desperate, violent act of creation. She is pouring her own essence into him, forcing the divine sparks to re-ignite, forcing the flesh to knit back together around her own power.\n\nA body reforms. He is whole again, the wounds gone. He looks like Benji.\n\nHe opens his eyes.\n\nHe blinks, his gaze unfocused, then finds her. A look of pure, uncomplicated adoration dawns on his face. It is the simple, innocent worship of