94	2	4	Ch. 3: DreamTrap	8:02 AM. Sunday, August 20th, 1995.\n\nThe red numbers burn through my eyelids like accusations. My skull feels cracked, thoughts leaking through the fissures.\n\nThe clock-radio drones in the background. I should turn it off. Should get up. Should do a lot of things that require my brain to stop feeling like rotting fruit ready to spill its contents all over my pillow.\n\n"...confirming reports that former South African political prisoner Nelson Mandela has died at Pollsmoor Prison from complications related to food poisoning. The 77-year-old activist, who has been imprisoned since 1962, was reportedly found unresponsive in his cell early this morning. Prison officials state that Mandela had been suffering from a stomach ailment for several days before his death..."\n\nWait.\n\nI force my eyes open, blinking against the aluminum foil taped over my windows. The light seeps around the edges like reality trying to break in. Food poisoning? That's... that's not right. Mandela gets released. Becomes president. Dies old and free in 2013, surrounded by family and the whole world mourning.\n\nDoesn't he?\n\nThe announcer's voice continues, flat and matter-of-fact, describing a timeline that feels wrong in my bones. I sit up too fast, and the room tilts sideways. Blood rushes in my ears, drowning out the radio's lies.\n\nOr maybe drowning out the truth.\n\n"...funeral arrangements have not yet been announced. In other news, President Clinton is expected to address the nation regarding the escalating bombing campaign in Kosovo..."\n\nClinton. Right. 1995. The tournament was yesterday. My head is scrambled eggs, and I'm listening to news from a world that suddenly doesn't fit the shape of my memories.\n\nI reach over and click off the radio. The silence hits like a slap.\n\n8:15 AM. I stumble to my desk, limbs moving like a marionette with half its strings cut. There's a worn out college-ruled notebook sitting there. My chicken-scratch handwriting fills every page, but the words feel foreign in my mouth when I read them.\n\nSeptember 11, 2001: Planes hit the Pentagon and World Trade Center. President Bush reads to children about goats while America burns.\n\nThat sounds right. But then:\n\nPrincess Diana survives Paris crash, goes into hiding. Later mauled to death by Queen's corgis during secret reconciliation meeting at Buckingham Palace.\n\nWhat the hell?\n\nI flip faster. My handwriting, definitely mine, but the content shifts and blurs like ink bleeding in water:\n\nChicago Cubs win World Series 2016: Break 108-year curse in dramatic Game 7.\n\nThen, in the same pen, same hand:\n\nChicago Cubs win World Series 1995: Steve Bartman catches foul ball, becomes hero. Curse broken by fan interference.\n\nThe pages swim. Some entries scratch themselves out as I watch, words fading mid-sentence like someone's erasing them in real time. Others appear spontaneously:\n\nStar Wars prequel trilogy wins 23 Academy Awards. Jar Jar Binks spawns religious movement.\n\nAltaVista founded as dating app called "Ogle." Becomes worth $2 trillion matching people based on search history.\n\nDon't {illegible} BitCoin. (In big bold letters).\n\nI slam the journal shut, but my hands are shaking. These aren't predictions—they're memories. Warped, twisted memories of things that happened and didn't happen, all tangled together like Christmas lights in a box.\n\nThe worst part? I don't remember writing any single contradictory word.\n\n"Monday isn't real," reads the last entry I glimpsed.\n\n9:30 AM. My stomach gnaws at itself like a starving animal. I stumble to the kitchen, desperate for something solid, something real I can trust.\n\nI yank open the cabinet and freeze.\n\nThe cereal boxes stare back at me, wrong in ways that make my brain itch. Captain Crunch grins from his cardboard throne, crown tilted at an angle I swear used to be different. Wasn't it Captain Krunsch? Or was it always Crunch? The jagged letters seem to mock my uncertainty.\n\nNext to him: Froot Loops. The toucan's beak curves wrong, and she's got a side-kick she didn't have yesterday. Or did she? My hands shake as I reach for the generic store brand—Crispy Rice Squares. Safe. Boring. But even the knockoff Chex looks suspicious, like someone redesigned it in a fever dream.\n\nThe hunger claws at my ribs. I grab the Crunch, pour a bowl, add milk that has that sour tang of sweetened glue right before it needs to be tossed. Each spoonful cuts the roof of my mouth the same way it always has, but somehow feels like a lie I'm swallowing.\n\nOn the back of the box: "Part of this nutritious breakfast!" But the cartoon kid eating it has dead eyes and too many teeth.\n\nI'm either losing my mind or my dimension. Either way, at least my stomach stops complaining.\n\n11:42 AM. The TV flickers to life without me touching it. The remote sits three feet away, untouched, but channels flip anyway—click, click, click—like invisible fingers are surfing through dimensions. A commercial blooms on screen: Sinbad in a purple turban, grinning as he sells a movie called Shazam. I blink. The image shifts. Now it's Shaq in the same turban, same grin, hawking Kazaam. The VCR clicks, rewinding itself. Shazam again. Kazaam. Back and forth like reality can't make up its mind which lie to tell.\n\nStatic crawls across the screen like digital cancer, but something moves behind it. Shadows. Tall, wrong-shaped things that stretch and bend in the spaces between pixels. They shouldn't exist in a two-dimensional screen, but there they are—reaching through the glass, fingers of darkness that make the air in my lungs turn to ice. The temperature drops ten degrees. My breath comes out in visible puffs. The shadows aren't just in the TV anymore. They're pooling in the corners of the room like spilled ink, growing bolder, reaching toward me with tendrils that smell like copper and ozone.\n\nI try to move but my body won't obey. Terror locks my muscles as the shadows stretch closer, closer, until icy fingers brush my forehead—\n\nA bullet tears through my skull, gray matter painting the wall behind me in abstract art. I taste metal and see my own blood pooling on linoleum that used to be carpet—\n\nI'm back on the couch. The TV shows the Conservative News Network and the blond talking heads are making fun of Clinton again, like nothing happened.\n\n2:24 PM. The shadows are gone, but the fear clings like smoke in my clothes. My father will be home soon—early shift at whatever soul-crushing job he's pretending gives his life meaning this week. The thought sends ice through my veins. I need something solid, something real to hold onto.\n\nThe journal. I flip it open, desperate for answers that probably don't exist. My handwriting stares back—two different versions of it, really. The careful script of a sixteen-year-old trying to make sense of biology homework mixed with the hurried scrawl of someone who's lived seventy years and died twice. The old me wants to strategize, to find patterns in the chaos. The young me just wants to hide under the bed until the universe stops glitching.\n\n"I don't think I belong to this dimension," I write, the pen trembling in my grip.\n\n"But this one remembers me." My hand somehow replies.\n\nNeither version of myself knows what that means. Neither knows what comes next. But writing it down makes the terror smaller somehow, like capturing lightning in a mason jar. The pages don't rewrite themselves while I'm watching. The ink doesn't bleed backward. For now, the journal is the only truth I can trust.\n\nI hear the garage door open and my bladder gives way a little.\n\n**********\n\n3:04 PM. The door slams like a gunshot. My father's silhouette fills the frame, wrong in ways that make my spine crawl. He moves like broken machinery—joints grinding, face twitching between expressions that don't quite fit. The tournament. The embarrassment. The family name dragged through dirt.\n\n"You little shit."\n\nHis voice fractures reality. The walls breathe. The carpet becomes quicksand.\n\nI float above myself, watching this happen to someone else. Some other boy with my face, my bruises, my terror. The older me remembers this and catalogues each impact with clinical detachment—right kidney, solar plexus, that spot just below the ribs that makes you piss blood. The younger me screams until his voice breaks.\n\nBelt leather whistles through air thick as honey. Time stretches. Each strike lands like a meteorite, and I'm the crater it leaves behind. My body folds and unfolds like origami made of meat and bone. The watching-me counts: one, two, seven, sixty-mauve. The screaming-me stops counting after beta.\n\n"Embarrassed the family," he pants, and his breath smells like sulfur and disappointed dreams.\n\nMy mother hovers in the doorway—not quite there, not quite gone. A photograph of concern with the emotional range of wallpaper. She watches like she's viewing a nature documentary. The wild father feeds on the wounded offspring. Fascinating.\n\nThrough the haze of pain, a thought crystallizes—sharp and unexpected. He doesn't know how to break me as well as he thinks he does.\n\nSomewhere, I hear a public service announcement playing: ‘This domestic abuse brought to you by Benji's Personal Attorneys. Let’s make trauma brighter.’\n\nHe goes on and on. Is he ever not shouting? Ever not reminding me? He stretches my tolerance for this life. His presence boils every possibility, every alternate self down to one. Me, as I am now, back in the same body I thought I left. It would be impressive, how fast and unthinkingly he goes for the vulnerable spots, if it weren’t so fucking pathetic. Or maybe I’m the pathetic one, for thinking it might be different in the end. The end: my weak spot, one he always knew and always used.\n\nAt least he's learned to be strategic about it. Ever since that dentist appointment when I was eight—blood in my mouth, baby teeth scattered like confetti—and Dr. Morrison kept asking uncomfortable questions about how a kid loses that many teeth at once. After that, my father got smarter. Now he aims for places fabric can hide. Spots that scream in silence.\n\nThey’re both haunting me, a house of horrors, wallpaper and silence, omnipresent and passive. Maybe that’s my cue. The role I’m supposed to play in this sad little psychodrama. Maybe the older self should know that by now. Maybe the young one already does. But this time, I make them wait for my screams. I make them wait as long as I fucking can.\n\nUntil I see it in his eyes. Until I see the awful joy in them.\n\n“Doing this for your own good,” he says, reality fracturing around each brutal word. “You’re lucky to have us.” Us, the shadows that he called and raised. Us, the sad ghosts in their not-quite human shell. He’s the word of the distended god, the self-made kind that needs belief to hurt me. His universe. My bedroom. His rules, his laws, his almighty judgment. Nothing I say changes them. It just proves him right.\n\nI observe. I remember. How many versions of me has he killed?\n\nBut not this time. Not yet.\n\nA ritual all to itself. This is his ceremony. His rite of punishment. I laugh through my own blood and watch him double his efforts.\n\nThat’s the most familiar part. They look away first, not him but the others. They’re tired of me, I know. They’re sick of this dimension not living up to its potential. The shadows peel back. They always do. And that’s when I remember. And that’s when I care. “Embarrassed the family,” he says.\n\n“Embarrassed myself,” I finally cry.\n\nFinally.\n\nHis foot like a drumbeat on my lungs, syncing me back to his goddamn reality. That’s what he wanted. And Mom. And everything they stood for. I thought I knew all my tormentors, but they always come back with new tricks. The same old results, and a great big I AM STILL. So am I. They didn’t account for that. Not this time.\n\nThe older me knows things the younger me can't comprehend yet. I know that he dies when I'm 45—a massive stroke in a Walmart parking lot, sprawled alone beside a lifted truck, his face cooling in a pool of his own vomit. My mother follows seven years later, liver failure from all those "special teas" she used to calm her nerves. I never bothered going to either funeral. No one did.\n\nThe memory tastes like freedom and feels like justice. He doesn't know that yet. But I do.\n\nSo I endure. The pain is just pain now—sharp and familiar, but not the universe-ending terror it used to be. I don't fight back. Don't kill them in their sleep like the darker parts of me whisper I should. I just play the only game left: survival until they get bored. Eventually they do, backing out of the room with quiet eyes and cosmic disappointment, like they'd hoped for a better show.\n\nUntil they stop looking, I stay on the floor. Until they stop caring, I don’t.\n\nUntil they stop being demons. Until they’re just my parents again.\n\n"Don't bleed on the carpet," he says, in case he thought I cared about anything else.\n\n**********\n\n7:55 PM. Time blurred together like watercolors in rain. I think.\n\nI eventually tape myself back together with bandages and spite. My ears are still ringing from the concussion. Or maybe that's new blood. Hard to tell when your gray matter is falling out of your face holes like rancid sausage bites. At least in this version I can sit down without leaving blood stains.\n\nThe room spins when I move too fast, so I don't. I sit on the edge of my bed, alone for the first time since the universe started unraveling.\n\nThe journal lies open beside me.\n\nA note appears in my handwriting: "Tomorrow: avoid the couch."\n\n**********\n\n2:08 AM. I wake up suddenly. Every position is agony. Roll left—ribs scream where his boot found home. Roll right—bruises bloom fresh pain across my back. Lie flat—can't breathe.\n\nI'm staring at the ceiling, replaying the everything, the darkness, the way my heart forgot how to be