95	2	5	Ch. 4: Echoes of Ends	I brush my teeth. Put on jeans and a hoodie because it's Monday and that's what I always wear on Monday. The bruises on my body look worse in the bathroom mirror than they felt yesterday, but whatever. My backpack weighs about the same as usual. I grab my keys, check that I have my lunch money, and head out the door.\n\nSchool looks like school. Gray concrete, dying palm trees, the same dented lockers that have been here since the eighties. Kids walk around talking about weekend stuff—who hooked up with who, what movies they saw, the usual garbage. My head still hurts from getting kicked at the tournament, but not as much as it did yesterday.\n\nI see people stare at my bruised face in the hall, everyone moving at different frame rates. My friends are there too. Jamie. Becca. I wish I could just... just what? Be in one timeline long enough for them to notice me?\n\n"Hey, Stephen!" Jamie waves, bouncing with a preternatural energy even after staying up all night with me. "Oh my god, your face looks terrible."\n\n"Thanks," I say, hand trying to hide the one bruise it’s ok to show. "Really what every guy wants to hear."\n\n"No, I mean—" She steps closer. Vanilla and peaches. "It looks like it hurts. Are you okay?"\n\nBefore I can answer, Becca slides up next to us. "We heard what happened at the tournament. Everyone's talking about it." She's got this soft, concerned voice that makes me want to tell her everything's fine even when it's not.\n\n"Jamie was freaking out all weekend," Becca adds, shooting Jamie a look I can't quite read. "She made me call the hospital to see if you were admitted."\n\n"I wasn't freaking out," Jamie says, but her cheeks go pink. "I was just... concerned. Like any normal person would be."\n\n"Right. Normal." Becca smiles, but there's something sharp underneath it. "That's why you called me at midnight asking if I had Stephen's home number."\n\n"My number?" I ask Jamie, genuinely surprised. Why would she— "Oh, so that's why when I called you last night you were still awake and wanted to talk. I thought you just really needed help with studying for the test this morning."\n\nBecca lets out a snort of laughter. "Oh my god, Jamie. He called YOU for tutoring and you thought—"\n\n"Shut up," Jamie hisses, her face turning bright red.\n\n"I don't get it," I say, looking between them. "What's the big deal? You're good at Bio, I needed help. You seemed happy to explain the Krebs cycle all night."\n\n"It doesn't matter," Jamie says quickly, glaring at Becca. "The point is you're okay. You are okay, right?" She reaches out and touches my arm, her fingers lingering longer than they need to.\n\nBecca notices. I can tell because she immediately steps closer, close enough that our shoulders almost touch. "You should sit with us at lunch today. Just to make sure you don't, like, pass out or anything." She pauses, then adds, "I mean, sit next to us while we play Magic. You know, at our usual table."\n\n"That's actually a really good idea," Jamie says, but she doesn't let go of my arm. "I read that concussions can cause delayed symptoms. Someone should keep an eye on you."\n\n"Both of us could," Becca suggests. "Take shifts."\n\nThey're both staring at me now, waiting for an answer, and I realize I'm supposed to say something. But my brain feels like it's running through molasses, and all I can think about is how Jamie's hand is still on my arm and how Becca keeps biting her thumb, which she only does when she's nervous.\n\n"I'm fine," I finally manage. "Really. It's just a bruise."\n\n"Just a bruise?" Jamie's eyes go wide. "Stephen, you got kicked in the head. That's not nothing."\n\n"She's right," Becca agrees, but she's looking at Jamie when she says it, not me. "Head injuries are serious. You could have brain damage and not even know it."\n\n"Thanks for the vote of confidence," I say.\n\nJamie laughs—this bright, musical sound that makes something in my chest flutter. "You know what I mean. We're just worried about you."\n\n"We?" Becca raises an eyebrow. "I think you mean 'I.'"\n\n"What's that supposed to mean?"\n\n"Nothing. Just that some of us don't need to make everything about ourselves."\n\nThe tension between them is thick enough to cut with a knife, but I'm completely lost. Are they fighting? About what? They're both being nice to me, they never even talked to me.\n\nWait. That's not right.\n\nSomewhere in the back of my mind, the older version of me—the one with seventy-four years of hindsight—is practically cackling. You dense, stupid idiot, he whispers. They're fighting over you. How did you not see it the first time?\n\nBut that can't be right. I'm the guy who sits in the back of class and plays Magic at lunch with the other nerds. I'm the one who gets tongue-tied around pretty girls and has never went on a date until his thirties. Girls like Jamie and Becca don't fight over guys like me. They barely notice guys like me.\n\nExcept... they're noticing now. Jamie's hand is still on my arm. Becca keeps finding excuses to stand closer. They're both volunteering to babysit me through lunch like I'm some kind of prize worth winning.\n\nThis isn't how it happened, I think frantically. I was invisible. I was nobody. They were nice to me sometimes, sure, but they weren't... they didn't...\n\nThe old man in my head chuckles again. Maybe you were just too scared to see it. Maybe you spent so much time convinced you were invisible that you missed all the signs.\n\nMy heart starts beating faster, and not in a good way. Because if the older me is right—if I really was this blind the first time around—then what else did I miss? What other signals flew right over my oblivious teenage head?\n\nJamie is still talking about lunch plans, something about saving me a seat and how she's been working on a new green creature deck that could totally counter TJ's strategy. Becca jumps in with something about her blue control build and how she's been testing new counterspell combinations all weekend.\n\nThey're nerds.\n\nJust like me.\n\nThey know the meta, they understand card synergies, they've been spending their weekends theory crafting Magic decks. The realization makes my chest tight because I can see it now: the competition I'm watching between them isn't about who has the better deck or the smarter strategy. Becca keeps shooting Jamie these little competitive looks—the same look she gets when someone tries to counter her spells. Jamie's hand is still on my arm, staking her claim like she's tapping mana.\n\nThey're not fighting about Magic cards.\n\nThey're fighting about me.\n\nBreathe, I tell myself. This is a good thing.\n\nBut it doesn't feel good. It feels terrifying. Because if my memories are this wrong about something this important, then what else is my brain lying to me about?\n\n"Anyway," Jamie says, turning back to me with a smile that could melt Europa, "you should definitely sit close to us today. I brought extra cookies."\n\n"I brought homemade cookies, not some store-bought crap" Becca adds quickly, eyeing Jamie. "I made oatmeal raisin ones this morning. Your favorite, right?"\n\nHow does she know what my favorite cookies are? And when did Jamie start bringing me food?\n\n"That's really nice of you both," I say, because it seems like the safe response.\n\nJamie grins. "Great! Meet us in the courtyard. Usual table."\n\n"Our table," Becca corrects. "The one where we always sit."\n\nAs they walk towards Biology class—together but somehow competing even in their footsteps—I'm left standing there wondering what the hell just happened. Since when do girls fight over who gets to give me cookies? Maybe that concussion scrambled more than my short-term memory.\n\nThe 74-year-old me is practically screaming in the back of my skull: Just grab one of them and kiss her, you coward! Any one! Both of them! Stop overthinking and START LIVING!\n\nBut I'm already lagging too far behind, paralyzed by the impossibility of it