llapses. Older hands, scarred hands, reach for a cable that snaps with the sound of breaking bones. Thirty feet of free fall compressed into one perfect moment of terror. The ground welcomes me with concrete finality.\n\n"Stephen?"\n\nI'm face-down on my desk, test paper crumpled under my cheek, dark crimson fluids pouring from my mouth. Ms. Rodriguez stands over me, hand on my shoulder. The classroom spins like a carnival ride.\n\n"Do you need to go to the nurse?" Her voice sounds far away, underwater.\n\nI try to sit up but my arms feel like overcooked pasta. "I'm fine," I croak.\n\nBut when I blink and look again, Ms. Rodriguez is back at the front of the class, writing notes on the board like nothing happened. Like she never left her spot.\n\nJamie and Becca are both twisted in their seats, staring at me with identical expressions of worry. Whitney's turned around too, mouthing "What's wrong?"\n\nI point weakly toward the door and mouth "Lunch."\n\nThe test sits before me, still blank except for my name. Fifty minutes feels like fifty-four years.\n\n**********\n\n12:12 PM. Lunch. The courtyard is our kingdom—concrete tables under dying trees, where we gather like some discount version of Camelot. Everyone's already settled into their usual spots when I arrive, cards and lunch scattered across the table like tarot readings for nerds.\n\nTJ's shuffling his red deck, the one that's basically "burn everything and ask questions later." Morgan's got her white artifact spread, all gleaming chrome and mechanical precision. Becca sits across from me with her blue control deck, the kind that says "I will counter your hopes and dreams." Jamie's next to her with green—elves and forests and honest creatures that hit hard. Andrew's running some janky combo that works exactly once every seventeen games. Gil, James and Patrick round out the table with their usual random booster pack mix.\n\n"Saved you a seat," Jamie says, patting the bench beside her. "And brought cookies."\n\nShe slides a bag of Mrs. D's Soft Peanut Butter cookies to me. Becca immediately produces her own container full of oatmeal raisin.\n\n"Thanks," I say, taking one of each because I'm not stupid enough to pick sides. "These are really good."\n\nJamie beams. Becca smiles but shoots Jamie a look that could freeze lava.\n\n"Whose turn?" I ask, trying to defuse whatever's happening here.\n\n"Mine," TJ says, dealing out hands. "Royal Rumble rules. Last one standing wins."\n\nThe game starts normally enough. Lands, small creatures, everyone feeling each other out. But I'm distracted by the way Jamie keeps leaning over to "help" me organize my hand, and how Becca somehow manages to need my opinion on every single play.\n\n"So," I say, trying to sound casual while I play a forest. "Weird question, but... have any of you ever felt like you died?"\n\nGil looks up from his hand. "Every time I play against TJ's burn deck."\n\n"No, I mean really died. Like, actually stopped existing for a minute."\n\nThe table goes quiet. Andrew pauses mid-shuffle.\n\n"Dude," Morgan says slowly, "that concussion really scrambled your brain, didn't it?"\n\n"I'm serious. Saturday, when I got kicked... I was gone. Like, completely gone. And when I came back, I remembered... things."\n\n"What kind of things?" Jamie asks, her hand finding my arm under the guise of reaching for cards.\n\n"Flashes. Deaths that aren't mine. This morning in Bio, I was on some scaffolding, falling—"\n\n"Stephen." Whitney's voice cuts through my explanation like a blade. She's staring at me with eyes that have seen too much. "Maybe we should talk about this later."\n\nThere's something in her tone—a warning, maybe. Or fear. I follow her gaze and notice the shadows under our table are... wrong. Too dark. Too still.\n\n"Fine," I mutter. "Parking lot after school?"\n\nShe nods.\n\n"Maybe it's just your crazy dream imagination," TJ says, tapping mana for another Fire Bolt. "You know, like those stories about space elevators you were always going on about since sixth grade. What did you call it... Elysium Rising? Said if you ever got old enough to care, you'd write a sci-fi book and put us all in it."\n\nThe words hit me like a physical blow. Memories crash over me—the old man's voice, Lady Illkeserod's library, the weight of writing that book in another life. But then they're gone, slipping away like smoke, leaving me gasping.\n\n"I... what?"\n\n"Dude, you literally drew diagrams of orbital rings on your notebook covers. Don't tell me you forgot your own obsession."\n\nBut I did forget. Or I never remembered. Or—\n\nThe confusion must show on my face because TJ just shakes his head and eliminates Andrew with a perfectly timed burn spell.\n\nThe game continues. Andrew gets eliminated first when Morgan's artifacts overrun him. "Stupid white weenie bullshit," he grumbles, but he's grinning.\n\nGil falls next to TJ's relentless burn. "Death by a thousand fire spells," he announces dramatically.\n\nPatrick tries to stabilize with life gain, but Jamie's elves grow too big too fast. "Nature finds a way," she says, clearly pleased with herself.\n\n"That's Jurassic Park, not Magic," Morgan points out.\n\n"Still applies."\n\nMorgan's artifacts look unstoppable until Becca drops a Counterspell on her key piece. "Blue always finds an answer," Becca says sweetly. Too sweetly.\n\nTJ throws everything at Becca in a final kamikaze run, but she's got too much mana up. Another Counterspell seals his fate. "Mono-red is honest," he sighs. "Honestly dead."\n\nNow it's just me, Jamie, Becca, and Whitney. The real game begins.\n\nJamie and Becca eye each other like predators circling the same prey. Which, I'm starting to realize, might be exactly what's happening.\n\n"I attack you," Jamie declares, pointing at Becca.\n\n"Why me? Stephen's got more life."\n\n"You're the bigger threat."\n\n"Am I? Or are you just jealous that he liked my cookies better?"\n\n"He ate yours first," I point out helpfully.\n\nBoth girls turn to stare at me. Apparently that was the wrong thing to say.\n\n"I mean, chronologically. Not preference-wise. They're both equally—"\n\n"Just play your cards, Stephen," Becca says, but she's trying not to smile.\n\nThe battle between Jamie and Becca becomes vicious. Creatures clash, spells fly, life totals plummet. I try to stay out of it, but eventually I have to pick a side.\n\nI attack Becca.\n\nHer face falls for just a moment before she covers it with a smile. "Fine. Gang up on me. I see how it is."\n\nJamie grins triumphantly and pats my shoulder. "Good choice."\n\nThree turns later, Becca's eliminated. She gathers her cards with quiet dignity, but I catch her watching Jamie with something that might be resignation. Or calculation.\n\n"And then there were three," Whitney says.\n\nBut not for long. My black demon deck is powerful but straightforward. Black against white. Whitney's been playing the long game, and when she finally reveals her strategy, it's devastating.\n\n"I play Veilwalker," she announces, placing down a card I've never seen before.\n\nI squint at it. Beneath Whitney’s doodles of the letter J adorning the card, the art shows a figure stepping between realities, one foot in shadows, one in light. The rules text is strange: This card cannot be removed from the battlefield. If Veilwalker is ever alone, exile it and lose the game.\n\n"What set is that from?" Jamie asks.\n\nWhitney shrugs. "Custom card. I had it made."\n\nShe uses Veilwalker to exile my demons, leaving me defenseless.\n\nThen dead.\n\nJamie puts up more of a fight, but eventually even she falls to Whitney's otherworldly strategy.\n\n"Good game," Whitney says, but she's looking at me when she says it. Not at the cards. At me.\n\nThe shadows under our table seem to shift, pleased.\n\nAfter the others drift away to dump their lunch trays, Whitney catches me. "Four-twenty. Parking lot. And Stephen?" She glances around, making sure we're alone. "Stop talking about the visions. They're listening."\n\n"Who's listening?"\n\nBut she's already gone.\n\n**********\n\n4:20 PM. Afterschool. Patrick and Whitney catch me by the parking lot, and I'm not sure what to make of it. Patrick with his unshakable calm, the kind that used to annoy me until I realized how much of it I wanted for myself. Whitney, finally Whitney—the first year she's been open about it at school, the universe finally acknowledging what Patrick's known since 8th grade.\n\nThey ask if they can talk. I've seen them both plenty, but not together like this, not as a team. Patrick's hands are already fishing for his clove cigarettes. Whitney's got that nervous energy she gets when she's about to say something important.\n\n"Thought we'd gone nuts," Patrick says, lighting up. The smell of cloves and tobacco mixes with the Florida heat.\n\nThere's relief in his voice, but it's complicated relief—the kind that comes with more questions than answers.\n\n"It's not just us then?" Whitney asks.\n\nI look from one to the other. These two have been inseparable since forever—Patrick was the first person Whitney told when she figured out who she really was back in 8th grade. When her family kicked her out for it, Patrick's family officially adopted her to keep her out of foster care, but in reality we all took her in.\n\nShe couch-surfs between Patrick's place, Becca's house, and Jamie's—wherever feels safest on any given night. We all worked together to get her out of that Christian fundamentalist hellhole she was living in.\n\nWhen Whitney threw herself into Wicca and the occult, trying to find some kind of spiritual framework that didn't condemn her existence, Patrick followed her into it.\n\n"It's like we're haunted," Whitney says. "Not by ghosts, but... something else. Something that watches."\n\nHer eyes have that look—the same one she gets when she's casting circles or reading tarot cards. Like she's seeing layers of reality most people miss.\n\n"We thought it was just us processing Whitney's transition," Patrick adds, exhaling smoke. "You know, reality shifting because she was finally being herself. The universe adjusting."\n\nWhitney nods. "When I came out publicly this year, things started feeling... different. Not just socially. Actually different. Like the world was rewriting itself around the truth."\n\n"But then you started talking about your visions," Patrick continues. "Death flashes. Other timelines. And we realized we've been seeing weird shit too."\n\n"Shadows that move wrong," Whitney says. "Dreams that feel more real than waking up. Cards that shouldn't exist." She touches her deck protectively. "That Veilwalker card? I didn't make it. I found it in my collection one morning, like it had always been there. So I claimed it as my own and scribbled my J’s all over it."\n\nPatrick takes another drag. "Whitney thinks it's connected to her transition. That being true to herself somehow... broke something. Made her more sensitive to whatever's bleeding through."\n\n"I spent so many years hiding who I was," Whitney says quietly. "Living as Will, pretending to be someone I wasn't. Maybe that created some kind of... spiritual static. And when I finally stopped pretending, it cleared up enough for me to see what was always there."\n\n"The question is," Patrick says, stubbing out his cigarette, "are we seeing things that are real, or are we all just losing our minds together?"\n\nI think about the scaffold collapse, the taste of blood and concrete. About Ms. Rodriguez noticing me, then not noticing me. About the shadows under our lunch table.\n\n"It's real," I say. "Whatever's happening, it's real. The visions, the shadows, all of it."\n\nWhitney steps closer. "Then we need to figure out what it wants. Because something this big doesn't just happen. It gets summoned."\n\n"Or it escapes," Patrick adds darkly.\n\nThe three of us stand there in the parking lot, the Florida sun casting long shadows that seem to reach for us with intentional hunger. For the first time since Saturday's tournament, I don't feel completely alone with this weirdness.\n\n"We need to meet again," Whitney says. "Compare notes. Figure out the pattern."\n\n"Agreed," Patrick says. "But not at school. Too many ears."\n\n"TJ's," Whitney offers. "Plus he's the only one with a car if we need to go anywhere."\n\nPatrick nods. "Rich kid privilege has its advantages. And TJ's used to us weirdos crashing his place."\n\n"His Nana will probably just leave snacks and tell us stories about the old country," Whitney adds with a small smile. "Perfect for discussing interdimensional bleeding and shadow entities."\n\nI nod. For the first time in days, I feel like maybe I'm not going crazy. Or if I am, at least I've got good company for the trip.\n\n**********\n\n6:19 PM. Home. The sun bleeds out. My ceiling catches the stain, repaints itself red with light that's traveled too long to mean anything. I feel like I've traveled with it, a secret too old for my brain to keep. Shadows conspire in the corners of my room. I think they whisper. I think I'm hearing them even before they make a sound. I watch, I wait. The phone in the living room breaks the silence, almost breaks me. My heart jumps. I rush to go pick it up before my parents do. I hear a voice more haunting than ghosts.\n\n"It happened again," Whitney says, both of us holding memories too loud to forget.\n\n"How bad was it?" I ask.\n\nShe breathes on the other end, the quiet fear of someone who's already seen the ending. "Pretty bad." Her words are steady, but I hear what's under them. A shiver. A nightmare that made itself at home in her voice. "I didn't want to freak Patrick out earlier, but when you started talking about those death visions... I've been seeing things too."\n\n"What kind of things?"\n\n"Fire. But not like your scaffold thing. I'm always burning. Sometimes I'm tied to a stake, sometimes I'm just... consumed. Like the universe wants to punish me for existing." She pauses. "One minute I'm lighting a candle for protection, next I'm somewhere else. Watching myself burn. Feeling it burn. Screaming." The memory fills the silence. "Then I'm back, and the candle's still in my hand."\n\n"Jesus, Whitney. I'm sorry."\n\n"It's happening more often now," she continues, not like a victim but like a scientist observing her own trauma. "Like the universe keeps glitching every time I try to be myself."\n\n"Where are you now?" I ask.\n\n"Jamie's. Her parents are working late, so we can actually talk without someone listening." There's relief in her voice. "I can't exactly discuss interdimensional death visions at your place with your parents around."\n\nYeah. My fucked-up home situation isn't exactly conducive to cosmic horror discussions.\n\n"Stephen," Whitney's voice gets softer, more careful. "I know why you wear those baggy hoodies all the time. Those bruises weren't from the tournament, were they?"\n\nMy throat tightens. "Whitney—"\n\n"You don't have to say anything. I just... I know what it's like. Living in a house where you never know what's going to set them off. Where hiding becomes second nature." Her voice carries the weight of someone who's been there. "If you ever need to talk about anything—and I mean anything—you know I'm here, right? I've been where you are."\n\nI close my eyes. She does know. Better than anyone.\n\n"Thanks," I manage. "Really."\n\n"I'm bringing some stuff tomorrow night to TJ's. Oiji board, tarot deck, some ritual components and research I've been doing on dimensional bleeding, maybe some protection spells. Figure we should come prepared if we're going to dive into this weirdness properly."\n\n"That sounds—"\n\nIn the background, I can hear Jamie's voice getting closer—that distinctive giggle mixed with what sounds like her tripping over furniture. Then there's a crash, followed by Whitney's exasperated "Jamie, be careful!" and more giggling.\n\n"And here comes trouble," Whitney mutters. "I should probably go before she breaks something important."\n\n"Good luck with that."\n\n"See you tomorrow, Stephen. Whoever we are by then."\n\nThe line goes quiet, but I can hear Jamie's voice getting louder, more insistent about talking to me.\n\n"Is Stephen on the other line?" Jamie's voice comes through breathless now, like she just ran across the room. "Because if he is, I want to talk to him. It's important."\n\nWhitney laughs. "Jamie, no—"\n\nBut Jamie's already wrestling the phone away. I can hear the struggle, complete with more giggling and what might be a lamp getting knocked over.\n\n"Hey, Stephen." Her voice is warm and slightly out of breath. "So... about lunch today."\n\n"The Magic game? You played really well. I thought we made a great team against Becca there. Too bad Whitney came in with that card at the end."\n\n"Oh, I wasn't talking about the cards." There's something in her voice—satisfaction mixed with mischief. "Though I'm glad you noticed my... technique."\n\nI have no idea what she means by that. "Your deck construction was solid. Good mana curve."\n\nJamie laughs, and it's not quite the response I expected. "Stephen, you're so... focused on the technical stuff. But yeah, I definitely got what I wanted out of that game."\n\n"A win's a win, I guess."\n\n"Exactly. And now I get to sit next to you tomorrow at lunch, like Becca and I agreed. Winner's privilege." Her voice gets softer, more playful. "Unless you're really on her side?"\n\n"Sides? What sides? It's just lunch."\n\n"Just lunch," she repeats, and I can practically hear her smiling. "Right. Just the two of us. Sharing cookies. Sitting really close so I can... monitor you for concussion symptoms."\n\n"That's very responsible of you."\n\n"I'm a very responsible person, Stephen. I take care of what's mine."\n\nWhat's hers? When did I become hers? "I appreciate the concern, but I'm feeling much better—"\n\n"Good. Because I have plans for us tomorrow. And the day after that. And..." She trails off. "Well, let's just say I'm ver