l."\n\n"And some people," Jamie counters, her voice silk over steel as her free hand finds my other knee, claiming territory, "know how to catch you when you do." Her fingers squeeze like a vice.\n\nI'm starting to feel like a wishbone at Thanksgiving, and from the amused look on Patrick's face, he's placing bets on which girl's going to snap me first.\n\n"Jesus, you three," Whitney mutters from her corner, but there's amusement in her voice. "Get a room."\n\nPatrick laughs, low and knowing. "This is better than cable."\n\nTJ, finally picking up on the tension, grins like Christmas morning. "Well, this got interesting fast." He reaches for an empty beer bottle on the coffee table, spinning it between his palms. "Truth or dare, anyone? Or we could do this properly..." He holds up the bottle like a sacred artifact. "Spin the bottle. Like we're twelve again, but with better beverages."\n\n"Truth or dare," Morgan suggests with a wicked smile as she glances down at TJ.\n\n"Dare," Jamie says immediately, her eyes never leaving mine. "Spin the bottle. Let fate decide."\n\nBecca's hand slides higher on my thigh, possessive and warm. "I like that idea." She glances at Jamie with barely concealed challenge. "Assuming everyone's brave enough to follow through."\n\n"Oh, I'm not worried about bravery," Jamie replies, her nails starting to dig patterns in my wrist. The touch burns through my skin like a brand. "The question is whether everyone can handle the consequences."\n\nThe air between them crackles with unspoken warfare. This isn't just flirtation anymore—it's territorial. Final. Like they both know this moment will decide everything.\n\n"I'll go first," Becca announces, standing up to grab the bottle from TJ.\n\n"Actually," Jamie says smoothly, getting up faster, her hand shooting out to intercept Becca's with predatory speed, "I think we should let our gracious host decide." But she's already turning toward TJ with a smile that could melt steel or start wars, her fingers