ke everything I was afraid of becoming." I swallow hard, tasting copper and the memory of dying. "It said something to me—called me something that wasn't my name, but I can't... I can't remember what. Like my brain won't let me hold onto it." I press my hands against my head, frustrated by the gap in my memory. "But I remember what it was called. What it told me to call it. That fucking word that's been screaming in my head ever since."\n\n"Benji."\n\nThe word explodes like a gunshot, sudden and violent and completely unwanted. I don't know where it came from or what it means, but it's there, echoing in the hollow spaces between my thoughts like something alive and starving.\n\n"What?" Whitney gasps, leaning forward so fast she nearly falls off her cushion.\n\nI realize I've said it out loud. The name—if it is a name—hangs in the air between us like smoke from a gun barrel.\n\n"I don't know," I say, pressing my palms against my temples hard enough to leave marks. "It's been screaming in my head since I woke up after the tournament. Benji. Over and over, like a broken record player in hell."\n\nThe word hits the room like a physical blow. Even the shadows seem to recoil, then lean closer, drawn by something I can't begin to understand.\n\n"That's fucking dark, man," TJ says, his voice shaky. "But how do you know it's real? I mean, you were dying. Maybe it was just—"\n\n"Hallucinations," Patrick finishes. "Your brain shutting down, making up stories."\n\n"Because of this." I pull the polaroid from between the pages of my journal—sepia-faded and fragile as old promises. "I clung to this thing like it was the last fucking wooden door on the Titanic. Never showed my parents, never let them find it. Kept it hidden all these years because it's the only proof I have that any of it was real."\n\nJamie takes it first... "Holy shit," she whispers... "Look at your eyes. Even then... you look like you were fighting something. Like you were trying to hold on so tight." Her eyes flick up to meet mine. "You still look like that sometimes."\n\nBecca takes it with reverent fingers... "Jesus, it's the smile. It doesn't reach your eyes." She glances at me, her own expression pained. "You were trying so hard to pretend everything was okay, weren't you?"\n\nTJ leans forward to peek. "Dude, what the fuck is that? A trailer or a shipping container. That's some serious bottom-of-the-barrel shit right there."\n\n"TJ," Morgan warns, but she's craning her neck to see too.\n\n"No, he's right," I say with a bitter laugh. "We weren't just broke—we were fucking homeless most of the time. Always moving, always running from someone or something my dad owed money to. That trailer was the first place that actually felt like... like somewhere."\n\nPatrick takes the photo next, studying it with his usual intensity. "Man, what's all that junk scattered around? Looks like a tornado hit a junkyard."\n\n"Shit my dad probably stole from his temp jobs," I say, the words tasting like rust and shame. "Broken equipment, busted appliances, random metal crap he thought he could sell. Never did, of course."\n\n"Is that a shopping cart?" Morgan squints at the background.\n\n"Three shopping carts. Dad used them to haul water from the gas station down the road." The memory makes my stomach clench. "No running water in that piece of shit. Had to fill up jugs and milk cartons."\n\n"The whole place looks like it's held together with prayer and spite," Becca observes, passing the photo to Whitney.\n\n"Because it was. We were squatting there, actually. Some snowbird's abandoned trailer that Dad found and just... moved into. Said if nobody came looking for six months, it was ours by right."\n\nWhitney examines it like she's looking at evidence from a crime scene. "The desperation... God, living like that, no wonder your mom..." She trails off, understanding dawning in her eyes.\n\n"Did desperate shit to keep us together," I finish for her. "When you're that poor, that powerless, sometimes hurting the people you love feels like the only control you have left."\n\nThey pass it around like a piece of evidence from hell itself, each taking their turn to witness the tangible proof of just how far we'd fallen. Behind me in the photo, that ramshackle trailer squats against the Nevada wasteland like a monument to absolute desperation—surrounded by stolen debris, broken dreams, and the kind of grinding poverty that turns love into poison and hope into something too dangerous to feel.\n\n"When I came back—when they managed to jumpstart my heart—everything had changed. Suddenly my father was in the military and we were stationed in upstate New York. No memory of moving, no explanation. When I asked about Nevada, they laughed like I'd told the funniest joke in the world."\n\nA shudder runs through me as my father's voice echoes in my memory: Nevada doesn't exist you stupid little shit. Stop making up stories, you fucking retard. I can still feel the leather biting into my skin, hear the whistle of the belt cutting through air before it found its mark. But that wasn't the worst part. The worst part was what happened after, when he'd climb in and "apologize"...\n\nI blink hard, forcing the memory back into its cage. My hands start shaking.\n\nJamie notices immediately, shifting closer on the couch. Her warmth presses against my side, grounding me. "Hey," she whispers, her hand covering mine. "You're okay. You're here with us."\n\nI nod, not trusting my voice, grateful she can't see the images playing behind my eyes. Some things are too dark to share, even in a basement full of broken teenagers.\n\n"But it did exist. I had proof." I gesture to the photo making its way around our circle, my voice gaining fierce intensity. "One picture that I guarded like my fucking life depended on it. Because after what happened...I wasn't going to let them steal my memories too." My hands clench into fists. "I hid that photo in places they'd never think to look, moved it every few days, slept with it under my pillow sometimes. I was going to hold onto that piece of truth even if it killed me, because without it, I'd have nothing left of who I really was."\n\nJamie's moves her hands to my arm, possessive and warm. Becca’s hand slips from my knee, and for a second, I think she’s about to punch Jamie. But then her gaze drifts to Patrick. For once, he’s looking back—not just watching, but seeing her. She blinks like she’s surprised by it.\n\n"Up until that tournament two days ago when I got knocked the fuck out, everything was fine," I continue, my voice gaining momentum like a freight train heading for a cliff. "Like, my whole life for the past eight years was just... normal. I grew up as a military brat, my father retired from the Air Force in New York, we moved down here when I was in middle school. I met TJ in sixth grade, Patrick in eighth." I pause, looking around the room at faces that suddenly feel both familiar and foreign.\n\nMy eyes find Jamie's and I feel something twist in my chest—a horrible uncertainty I can't voice. How do I tell her that even she might be a false memory? That everything I thought I knew about my life, about us, might be some kind of elaborate lie my brain constructed to fill in the gaps?\n\n"But since that fucking kick, it's like I'm not even supposed to be here. I've got all these weird memories flooding back—shit I thought I'd forgotten, stuff that doesn't make sense. And then there are these fucking flashes. Like the ones I told you about yesterday.\n\nWhere I'm dying. Not just me, but all of us." My voice gets harder, more desperate. "Different ways, different timelines. Sometimes I see other versions of myself, other versions of all of you. Some better, some worse, all wrong somehow. And in every single one of those death visions, that fucking name is bouncing around somewhere in the background like a broken record."\n\n"Reality glitches," Patrick says. It should be a question, but he knows it's not.\n\n"The thing in the void—it's still there. Still waiting. Every time I die in my dreams, I see it again. And it knows that word. Benji. Says it like it's calling for someone who disappeared a long time ago."\n\nI look around at their faces, seeing my own haunted expression reflected back like funhouse mirrors. "Now I think it's coming for all of us."\n\n**********\n\nThe shadows stretch closer, patient as undertakers. The TV static swells, threatening to swallow us whole.\n\n"We have to figure out what it means," Whitney says suddenly, her voice fierce with the kind of determination that starts wars. "Benji. Whatever it is, whoever it is—it's the key to all this."\n\n"How?" TJ asks, but his voice is already resigned to impossibility.\n\nI stand up abruptly, needing to move, needing space to think. The room tilts like a ship in a storm, but I make it to the chair across from them.\n\nI close my eyes, feeling the weight of infinite possibility pressing down like deep ocean water. The name pounds in my skull like a second heartbeat, insistent and mysterious. "I don't know. But maybe... maybe if we can find the source, we can find our way back. To wherever we're supposed to be."\n\nWhen I open my eyes again, they're all staring at me like I'm a ghost they've just decided to follow into hell.\n\nMaybe I am.\n\nMaybe we all are.\n\nBut for the first time since waking up in this wrong dimension, I don't feel alone in the dark. And somewhere in the back of my mind, that name keeps calling—Benji, Benji, Benji—like a lighthouse in a storm I don't yet know how to navigate.\n\nI drop my head into my hands, suddenly exhausted. The confession has drained something vital from me, left me hollow as a broken church bell.\n\nTwo sets of muffled footsteps cross the shag carpet. I feel them approaching through the fog of memory and pain, but I don't look up. Can't. The weight of eight years of false memories presses down on me like a collapsed building.\n\nSomeone reaches me first—I sense rather than see a hand hovering uncertainly near my shoulder, hesitant, afraid I might shatter completely if touched wrong. But before that contact can happen, someone else moves like smoke, slipping around behind the chair. Arms wrap around me from behind in an embrace that's part comfort, part territorial claim.\n\nThe warmth hits me first—familiar but unidentifiable through the vanilla and peaches haze. Whoever it is presses against the back of the leather chair, breath warm against my ear, and suddenly the room stops spinning. My chaotic thoughts find their rhythm again, edges sharpening back into focus like someone just adjusted the lens on reality.\n\nI slowly open my eyes, blinking away the sting of confession and memory. As I stand up and turn into the embrace, Jamie's face comes into view as I the blurriness fades , her dark eyes intense with concern and something deeper. Her embrace tightens slightly when she realizes I'm looking at her, possessive and protective all at once.\n\nOver her shoulder, I catch sight of Becca standing there, her hand still half-raised from that aborted attempt to comfort me. The look on her face is bittersweet—a flash of disappointment mixed with something softer, more resigned. She knows she's lost something she never really had, but there's acceptance there too. She lets her hand drop slowly, takes a small step back, and manages a genuine smile even though it wavers at the edges.\n\n“Well, if we're picking teams,” Becca mutters under her breath to no one in particular, her eyes drifting toward Patrick, “might as well stop wasting time.”\n\nJamie's hug lingers, stretching beyond comfort into something hungrier. Her touch feels undeniably real, achingly good, and in that prolonged connection, electricity hums between us like a live wire.\n\n"You know," Jamie murmurs against my ear, her voice playful, "I think the universe owes me something after that interruption."\n\nBefore I can process what she means, her hand turns my face toward hers and her lips find mine. The kiss is quick but intense, tasting like beer and secrets and the kind of promises that get people in trouble.\n\n“BENJI! BENJI! BENJI!”\n\nThe TV erupts at maximum volume, some old kids' show blaring the name like an air raid siren. A cartoon dog with a red bandana bounces across the screen, barking the word over and over while cheerful music plays underneath like a soundtrack to madness. The dog pauses, stares straight at the screen, and smiles. It's mouth showing, not animated—human teeth. Then the audio distorts. Like it knows we’re watching.\n\nWe all scream. Jamie grabs me tighter, Becca stumbles backward, TJ dives for the remote. But the volume won't turn down, won't respond, just keeps blasting that name like the universe has decided to join our conversation.\n\n"BENJI! TIME FOR ADVENTURE! BENJI!"\n\nPatrick runs for the TV, and grabs the power cord, but when he does he realizes it's already unplugged. The massive screen keeps blaring that name like it's powered by something beyond electricity.\n\n"What the fuck?" he shouts over the noise.\n\nBecca appears beside him, grabbing a pool cue from the rack and shoving it into his hands. "Hit the back! Get inside and turn it off!"\n\nPatrick doesn't hesitate. He starts beating on the rear panel of the elderly television case, wood splintering under his assault until he can reach inside and rip at whatever's making the sound. Finally, mercifully, the TV goes silent.\n\nWe all stare at each other in the sudden quiet, hearts racing, ears ringing from the assault. Becca looks shaken, more than the rest of us—like the coincidence of that name has rattled something loose inside her. She drifts away to a corner of the room, wrapping her arms around herself.\n\nPatrick lets out a long, shaky breath, still holding the pool cue like a weapon. "Well," he says, his voice hoarse. "That was fucked."\n\nThe echo of that name hangs in the air like smoke from an explosion, and I can't shake the feeling that something just responded to my story—something that's been listening all along.\n\n**********\n\nThe adrenaline drains out of us like water from a cracked glass, leaving behind the kind of bone-deep exhaustion that comes after confessing your darkest secrets. We settle into the expensive furniture like survivors of some cosmic shipwreck, too tired to process what just happened but too wired to pretend it didn't.\n\nJamie curls against my side on the leather sofa, her warmth anchoring me to this dimension while we flip through my journal together. Whitney joins us, cross-legged on the Persian rug, her grimoires spread around her like protective wards as she traces patterns in my handwriting with reverent fingers.\n\n"Look at this entry from last week," Jamie murmurs, pointing to a page I don't remember writing. "You drew the same symbol Whitney has in her book."\n\nWhitney's eyes widen as she compares the sketches. "That's a binding sigil. For lost souls." Her voice drops to a whisper. "Where did you learn this?"\n\n"I didn't," I say, staring at the precise geometric lines. "I've never seen that before in my life."\n\nAcross the room, Patrick notices Becca still huddled in the corner, arms wrapped around herself like she's trying to hold her pieces together. He abandons his position by the broken TV and crosses to her with that quiet grace of his.\n\n"Hey," he says softly, settling beside her on the floor. "You okay?"\n\nBecca looks up at him with red-rimmed eyes, and something shifts in her expression. Not quite attraction—not yet—but recognition. Like she's seeing him for the first time as more than just Whitney's protective shadow.\n\n"That was intense," she admits, her voice shaky. "All of it. The story, the TV thing, just..." She gestures helplessly at the chaos of our evening.\n\n"Yeah." Patrick offers her his cigarette, and when she takes it, their fingers brush. The contact lingers just a moment too long. "But we're here. All of us. Whatever this is, you're not facing it alone."\n\nThere's something different in the way she looks at him then—like she's making a decision, choosing a new path forward. Her smile is tentative but genuine, and Patrick responds with that rare grin that transforms his whole face.\n\nWithout warning, Becca pushes herself up and wraps her arms around him in a hug that's both grateful and something more. Patrick stiffens for just a moment, surprised, then relaxes into it, his arms coming up to hold her steady.\n\n"Thank you," she whispers against his shoulder. "For being here. For caring."\n\nWhen they break apart, there's a new understanding between them, fragile but real. Becca glances toward the stairs, then back at Patrick.\n\n"Want to get some air?" she asks. "I could use a few minutes to process all this before I go home."\n\nPatrick nods, stubbing out his cigarette as he grabs her hand. "Yeah. Let's talk."\n\nMeanwhile, Morgan has migrated to TJ's recliner, perching on the armrest with calculated casualness. "So," she says, twirling a strand of hair around her finger, "that was some deep philosophical shit tonight. Really makes you think about the nature of reality and... stuff."\n\nTJ nods enthusiastically, completely missing the flirtatious undertone. "Right? I mean, if parallel dimensions exist, then technically Reagan could be president in infinite realities. That's some serious multiverse validation for conservative economic theory."\n\nMorgan blinks. "I... that's not exactly what I meant."\n\n"Oh, you're talking about the ontological implications? Because I've been thinking about Descartes' 'I think therefore I am' in relation to—"\n\n"TJ," Morgan interrupts gently, leaning closer until their faces are inches apart. "Do you want to get some air? Maybe talk about... non-philosophical things?"\n\nTJ squints at her like she's speaking a foreign language. "But why would we talk about non-philosophical things when we just discovered we might be living in a false reality construct?"\n\nMorgan sighs and slumps back in defeat. Even interdimensional horror can't overcome TJ's complete obliviousness to romantic cues.\n\n"Jesus." Her voice cracks like ice. Whitney's staring at the journal in my lap like it might bite her. "This isn't just echoes."\n\nThe words hang in the air, pregnant with possibility and terror. I feel something shift inside me—a tectonic plate of understanding sliding into place.\n\n"I got switched." My voice comes out louder than I am, louder than I want to be. It fills the basement, bounces off the paneled walls, settles into their fear like sediment. "When I was eight. When I died. I got switched into the wrong fucking dimension."\n\nJamie and Whitney look at each other first—that universal teenage telepathy that says is he losing it? Then they look at me, really look, and I see the moment when disbelief starts to crumble.\n\n"Switched how?" Jamie asks. Her voice is careful, like she's talking to someone standing on a ledge.\n\nI open the journal with hands that won't stop shaking. The pages flutter, reality shifting with each turn. "Look at this. These newspaper clippings... I don't remember putting them here. Some of them are dated before I even had this journal..."\n\nThey lean in, a conspiracy of broken teenagers trying to make sense of impossible things. The dead TV light suddenly flickers across the pages, illuminating words I wrote before I knew what they meant.\n\n"February 14th, 1989," I read. "Boy poisoned by Mother in Nevada. March 3rd, 1991. Car accident in Florida. July 7th, 1993. Fell off the roof trying to fix  antenna."\n\n"Those are all..." Jamie starts, then stops. Her face goes pale.\n\n"Deaths," I finish. "My deaths. In other timelines. Other dimensions. I've been skipping between them every time I die, but I always end up in the wrong one."\n\n"That's impossible," Whitney whispers, but her conviction wavers.\n\n"Is it?" I flip to another page. "Look at this. I wrote this three days ago, before the concussion. Before any of this started." The handwriting is mine, but the words feel foreign: The shadows are coming. They know I remember. Find the boy before he forgets himself.\n\nThe sound of footsteps on the basement stairs interrupts my train of thought. Becca and Patrick come down together, her hand trailing along his arm, both of them slightly breathless and grinning like they've shared some private joke. There's a new energy between them—electric and playful.\n\n"Did we miss anything earth-shattering?" Becca asks, still close enough to Patrick that their shoulders brush.\n\n"Just more evidence that reality is completely fucked," Whitney replies without looking up from the journal.\n\nPatrick moves closer to peer over my shoulder. His shirt is slightly rumpled, and I catch the faint scent of Becca's perfume clinging to him. "Who's the boy?" he asks, pointing at the cryptic entry.\n\n"Me. The version of me that was supposed to live. The one who never got poisoned, never died, never started skipping." I close the journal, meeting each of their eyes in turn. "He's still out there, in the right dimension. And I need to find him."\n\nThe silence returns, but it's different now. Charged. Electric. Like the air before a thunderstorm.\n\n"We can find him," Jamie says suddenly. Her voice is stronger than the rest of us, more certain. Beer makes her brave, but this is something else. This is faith. "We can find him. We have to try."\n\n"Jamie—" Whitney starts.\n\n"No." She cuts her off, leaning forward with intensity that makes the air shimmer. "You didn't see what I saw. When he was unconscious after the fight. He wasn't there. His body was breathing, but he wasn't there. Like something else was wearing his skin."\n\nWhitney nods slowly, understanding dawning in her eyes. "You died more than once," she says to me. Her voice cracks, and I want to reach out and catch the pieces as she stares down Patrick. "So have we ."\n\nThe words hit like a physical blow. I stare at her, seeing the truth reflected in her face.\n\n"What do you mean?"\n\n"The dreams, the death visions," she whispers, her voice barely audible over the hum of expensive electronics. "The ones me and Patrick have been having. Where we're different people, living different lives. Where we die in different ways." Her hands shake as she reaches for her beer, condensation making the bottle slip in her grip. "Now that you mention it, they've been getting a lot worse since Saturday. What if this is all connected? What if we've all been switched?"\n\nWhitney pauses, her eyes distant with something that might be memory or might be fear. "In some of my dreams, I'm... different. Not just dead, but..." She swallows hard. "Sometimes I'm still Will, but twisted—like a version of him that never questioned anything, never fought to become me. Other times I'm someone completely different, someone I've never been but feel like I should recognize."\n\nHer voice cracks slightly. "Sometimes I wake up and I can't remember which version is real—the me in the dreams or the me sitting here with you. And that terrifies me more than dying ever could."\n\nThe basement feels smaller suddenly, the walls pressing in like the space itself is responding to her confession. The broken TV flickers, sound cutting out for a moment before returning with a burst of static that sounds almost like voices whispering in languages we don't recognize, before dying again.\n\n"That's..." Morgan swallows hard, his face pale in the blue light. "That would mean we're all in the wrong reality. That maybe this version of Whitney, this version of all of us, we're just echoes of people who were supposed to exist differently."\n\nWhitney's laugh is bitter and broken. "What if the reason I feel so wrong in my own skin isn't because I'm not supposed to be here? What if it's because this skin was never supposed to be mine at all?" The words hang in the air like a curse, beautiful and terrible in their honesty. "What if I've been fighting to become someone I already am somewhere else?"\n\n"All of us together," Patrick adds quietly, his protective instincts kicking in as he watches Whitney unravel. "That's not a coincidence. If we're all switched, all displaced, then maybe we found each other because we're supposed to find our way back together."\n\nI look around the circle of faces—my friends, my anchors, the only things keeping me tethered to this dimension. And suddenly I see it: the same haunted look in all their eyes. The same sense of displacement, of not quite fitting in their own skin.\n\n"Find the boy," Jamie says again, smiling now with something that might be madness or might be hope. "Find the boy, and maybe we find ourselves."\n\nThe bottles come together in the center of the table—a toast to impossibility, to the kind of plan that only makes sense when reality has already given up making sense.\n\n"To finding home," I say.\n\n"To finding ourselves," Whitney echoes.\n\n"To finding the boy," Jamie finishes.\n\nThe glass clinks together, sacred and profane, the sound echoing off concrete walls like a prayer to gods we don't believe in but desperately need.\n\n*********\n\nThe clock on the expensive sound system reads 11:47 PM in angry green digits. Patrick glances at it and groans. "Shit, it's getting really fucking late," he says, reluctantly pulling away from Becca. "And it's just Tuesday. I've got an ass-load of homework to pretend to care about before tomorrow."\n\nThe spell breaks. Reality reasserts itself in the form of AP Biology reports and calculus tests and parents who'll ask questions if we stay out much longer.\n\n"We need more time for this," Whitney says, carefully gathering her grimoires. "I want to research some things, cross-reference what you've written with some older texts I have at home."\n\n"Friday," Jamie suggests, her hand finding mine again. "Sleepover Friday. TJ, can we use the basement again?"\n\nTJ brightens. "Hell yes. Mom and Dad are going to some charity thing in Tampa. We'll have the whole place to ourselves."\n\n"Perfect." Whitney stands, brushing carpet fibers from her black jeans. "That gives me three days to dig into this properly. Figure out what we're really dealing with."\n\nAs we prepare to scatter back to our separate lives, our separate beds, our separate dreams that might not be dreams at all, I catch Jamie's eye. She smiles—soft, secret, full of promises I'm not sure either of us understands yet.\n\n"Friday," she says, and it sounds like both a threat and a benediction.\n\n"Friday," I agree, moving closer for what feels like the natural conclusion to everything that's happened tonight. Jamie steps into my arms, her body warm and solid against mine, and for a moment the universe makes perfect sense. The hug stretches longer than friendship, deeper than comfort, teetering on the edge of something that might change everything.\n\nI'm leaning down, she's tilting up, our faces inches apart when—\n\n"Jamie!" Becca's voice is sharp but not malicious. She’s standing by the stairs with Patrick, his arm casually draped over her shoulder. "My mom's here. She's laying on the horn."\n\nThe moment shatters. Jamie pulls back, shooting Becca a look that could freeze fire.\n\nBecca just raises an eyebrow, a small, knowing smirk playing on her lips. "Sorry," she says, her tone making it clear she isn't. "Some of us have curfews to keep. Come on." She turns and heads up the stairs with Patrick, leaving Jamie looking after them with a mix of frustration and grudging respect for the power play.\n\n"See you tomorrow at school," Jamie says, turning back to me, her fingers brushing mine one last time—a promise that this interruption is temporary.\n\n"Tomorrow," I repeat, watching her go, realizing the battle for my attention might be over, but a new, more complicated war between all of us has just begun.\n\nFor the first time since waking up in this wrong dimension, I actually look forward to school tomorrow.	6