97	2	7	Ch. 6: Frequency Bleed	Friday arrives like a bad hangover I didn't earn. We all eventually arrive back at TJ's basement—his folks drove over to Tampa for some weekend golf tournament, leaving us with Nana watching an empty house and the kind of freedom that makes terrible decisions feel inevitable. Morgan had some Academic Decathlon thing in Orlando, so it's just five of us tonight, armed with contraband and the kind of adolescent confidence that could power a small city.\n\n"This is either the stupidest thing we've ever done," I say, watching Whitney arrange candles with the precision of a bomb technician, "or the most brilliant."\n\n"Those aren't mutually exclusive," TJ replies, taking a long pull from some imported beer he’s been drinking. He's got a whole case of the green bottles—probably lifted from his dad's private stash—and he's savoring each one like it's liquid rebellion. The empty bottle makes a satisfying thunk when he sets it down on the table.\n\nPatrick and Becca arrived together an hour ago, and they've been circling each other like planets with complementary gravity ever since. She keeps finding excuses to lean into his space—reaching across him for his clove cigarettes, letting her fingers brush his when she passes the lighter. He responds by doing that thing guys do when they're trying to be smooth: unnecessary demonstrations of casual strength, like hefting the scrap lumber we liberated from the new subdivision going up on Turkey Creek Road.\n\n"Careful with that," Becca says as Patrick arranges the plywood on top of TJ's pool table, her voice dropping to that register girls use when they want you to know they're paying attention to your biceps. "Wouldn't want you to hurt yourself."\n\n"I'll manage," Patrick grins, flexing just enough to make the point without being obvious about it. "But if I do get injured, you offering to kiss it better?"\n\nShe blushes and looks away, but she's smiling. "Maybe. Depends on where you hurt yourself."\n\nGod, they're painful to watch. But also kind of beautiful in that uncomplicated way that normal people navigate attraction—like they both read the same instruction manual and actually understood it.\n\nMeanwhile, Jamie slides closer to me on the couch. The shag carpet beneath our feet is thick enough to lose small pets in, a brown nightmare that somehow makes the basement feel both retro and eternal.\n\n"You know," Jamie says, her shoulder bumping against mine as she settles into the cushions, "we still have some unfinished business from Tuesday."\n\n"Tuesday?" I blink, genuinely confused.\n\nAt school we've been hanging out, sure, but always with the whole group—passing notes in biology, MTG at lunch, the usual herd dynamics that make any real conversation impossible. Tuesday feels like six months ago instead of three days. "What happened Tuesday?"\n\nShe gives me a look—not angry, exactly, but the kind of expression girls get when they're deciding whether you're being deliberately obtuse or just naturally dense. "Spin the bottle…"\n\n"Oh. Right." Heat creeps up my neck like spilled paint. "That."\n\n"That," she repeats, and there's something playfully dangerous in her tone that makes my stomach do complicated gymnastics. She traces a pattern on my knee with her fingertip, just the lightest touch through my jeans, but it feels like she's writing her name on my soul with electricity. "That concussion must have been really bad if you forgot about that."\n\n"I'm not—" I start, but my voice cracks like I'm thirteen again. "I mean, it's not that..."\n\n"Good," she murmurs, and somehow she's moved even closer, close enough that I can smell her perfume mixing with the basement's atmosphere of teenage rebellion. "Because we're here now, and I can give you that reminder..."\n\n"Jamie!" Becca's voice cuts through the moment like a buzz saw. She appears beside the couch with the kind of timing that suggests she's been watching us from across the room. "Can you come help me with something? Girl emergency."\n\nJamie's face flashes with annoyance, but she squeezes my knee once—a promise of unfinished business—before standing. "We'll pick this up later," she says.\n\nPatrick's been helping Whitney by carving symbols into the plywood with his grandfather's old hunting knife—crude lines that look more like the doodles of a disturbed child than ancient mysticism. But there's something about them that makes my eyes water if I stare too long, like trying to focus on an optical illusion that doesn't want to be solved.\n\n"Did you really get these instructions from some random BBS?" Becca asks as she slides back into the room, her voice pitched higher than usual.\n\n"BALG. Black Arts Liberation Guild," Patrick says, waving the dot-matrix printout like a flag of surrender. "F.B. Goatting knows his shit. This guy's supposedly summoned everything from minor demons to major credit card debt forgiveness."\n\n“If my parents find out I am so dead.” Becca keeps mumbling to herself.\n\n"If they try anything weird," Patrick adds to Becca with a grin that's pure trouble, "you can always crash at my place. My parents don’t care."\n\nBecca laughs, a sound like silver bells wrapped in mischief. "Is that your idea of a pickup line? 'Hey baby, want to flee religious persecution together?'"\n\n"Is it working?"\n\n"Maybe."\n\nMy head pounds—a bass drum keeping time with my pulse. The concussion from Saturday's tournament still feels like broken glass rattling around in my skull. Every flicker of candlelight sends jagged reminders dancing across my peripheral vision, and I can't shake the feeling that some of them are dancing back.\n\n"Stephen," Jamie's voice is soft, concerned, but there's an undertone that makes my pulse quicken in ways that have nothing to do with head trauma. "You sure you're up for this? You've been through enough weird shit lately."\n\nHer thumb traces lines across my knuckles.\n\n"Define enough," I manage, my voice rougher than I intended. The pressure of her hand helps ground me, keeps me tethered to this moment instead of drifting into the static that's been building in my brain all week.\n\n*********\n\n11:11PM We think things are ready. Whitney kneels in the center of everything, spreading her tarot deck out like the wings of some exotic bird. The cards look older than they should, considering it’s a brand new set with some cyberpunk fairy aesthetic. The edges are worn smooth, and the images seem to shift when I'm not looking directly at them.\n\n"Everyone needs to understand," Whitney says, her voice taking on that ethereal quality she gets when she's channeling something deeper than teenage angst, "once we open this door, we can't just slam it shut and pretend nothing happened."\n\n"Speak for yourself," Becca mutters, as she grabs at the cross around her neck. "My parents are going to murder me if they find out. And I mean literally murder me. Like, Old Testament smiting."\n\n"Your parents think Dungeons and Dragons is a gateway drug to Satanism," TJ points out, popping open another beer with the skeleton key hanging from his wallet chain. "Pretty sure they'd perform an exorcism if they find your Magic cards."\n\nThe basement grows colder as we settle into position. Not the comfortable cool of central air, but something that seeps up through the floor and wraps around your bones like wet cloth.\n\n“Shit.” Whitney stands up and grabs two large boxes of Morton's salt, the blue containers looking absurdly mundane in her hands.\n\n"Almost forgot. For protection," she says simply, and begins walking a wide circle around us, pouring salt in a steady stream. The white crystals form a perfect barrier about ten feet across, encompassing our entire ritual space. She moves with deliberate precision, like she's done this before, her bare feet silent on the shag carpet.\n\nOnce the salt circle is complete, she takes her place within it, and immediately the candle flames stretch impossibly tall, their tips nearly touching the drop ceiling, and then suddenly freeze—not flickering, not wavering, just perfectly still like someone hit pause on reality.\n\n"Is it supposed to do that?" Becca whispers, her eyes fixed on the motionless flames. Instinctively, she leans closer to Patrick, and he doesn't miss the opportunity to slide his arm around her shoulders.\n\nNobody answers because nobody knows. We're flying blind into whatever cosmic shitstorm we're about to unleash, armed with nothing but arrogance and instructions downloaded from a dial-up bulletin board at 2400baud.\n\nPatrick takes a long drag from his clove cigarette and exhales slowly, the sweet smoke mixing with the incense until the air tastes like a funeral home trying too hard to be exotic. "Well," he says, grinning like a man about to jump off a cliff, "I guess we're about to find out."\n\nThe temperature drops another ten degrees, and somewhere in the darkness beyond our circle, something that might have been laughter echoes off the paneled walls.\n\n**********\n\nThe chanting starts rough, our voices scraping against each other like rusted metal dragged across concrete. Whitney leads us through syllables that feel wrong in my mouth, words that taste like rancid spam dissolved in old dog piss and dumpster porno mags. The basement air grows thick, viscous, like we're breathing through honey mixed with blood and the last breath of dying things.\n\nThen reality doesn't just crack—it screams.\n\nThe sound hits like a freight train made of soggy popcorn and infant wails. I feel it in my teeth, in the marrow of my bones, a frequency that shouldn't exist vibrating through the foundation of everything that ever was or could be.\n\nThe walls begin to urinate—actual brackish-yellow fluid—something that might once have been Jägermeister but now carries the metallic tang of fresh wounds.\n\n"Oh fuck, oh FUCK—" TJ's voice cracks as he squirms around, but there's nowhere to go. His expensive Heineken bottle shatters in his grip, glass shards embedding in his palm and starting to crawl up his arm, and the blood that wells up is the wrong color—too dark, almost purple, like it's been poisoned from within.\n\nThe shadows don't just peel away from the walls—they molt like diseased skin, curling and snapping with wet tearing sounds that make my stomach lurch. Underneath, something writhes—organic impossibilities that hurt to perceive, angles that fold in on themselves while fleshy-surfaces exist in too many dimensions at once, creating optical illusions that feel like ice picks driven directly into my eyes.\n\n"Hail Mary, Full of Grace, eht droLLLLLLL—" Becca's voice stretches like taffy, each repetition becoming more distorted, more desperate. The rosary she pulled out of her pocket snaps, beads buzzing around her like bot flies looking to embed themselves in her flesh.\n\nJamie doubles over on top of my lap, her body convulsing so violently I can hear her spine popping. When she opens her mouth, what emerges isn't vomit—it's liquid darkness, black and viscous as crude oil mixed with tar and the space between stars. The substance defies gravity, flowing upward in spiraling ribbons that reach toward something I can't see but can feel watching us. The dark fluid splashes across my face, my chest, soaking through my shirt with cold that burns like frostbite.\n\n"It's in me," Jamie gasps between heaves, her voice wet and broken. "It's in me and it's hungry—"\n\nWhitney's transformation is the most horrifying. Her eyes don't just roll back—they invert, becoming perfect spheres of bloodshot white threaded with optic nerves that pulse with their own light. Her head tilts at an angle that should snap her cervical vertebrae, and when she speaks, harmonics layer beneath her voice like a chorus of the damned singing backup.\n\n"The membrane is thin here," she says, and each word drips with frequencies that make my eardrums feel like they're bleeding. "We can taste your dreams. We can smell your endings."\n\nPatrick tries to reach for Becca, but where she was sitting there's now something that writhes and shifts. His hand doesn't just sink into her—it disappears in her crotch up to his wrist, swallowed by flesh that ripples and quivers at it starts suckling on his arm. Through the distortion of what used to be my friends, I glimpse pulsing masses that stretch beyond the curve of space, lined with organic machinery that squelches wetly, breathing with lungs made of rusted metal and regret.\n\nBlood doesn't just run from my nose—it erupts, a crimson geyser that defies gravity and joins Jamie's dark vomit in a grotesque ballet twenty feet above our heads. The pressure in my skull builds like a over-inflated balloon, and I can feel my consciousness beginning to fray at the edges, unraveling like the intestines of a drawn and quartered criminal.\n\nThe fluids start sloshing over everything, forming a liquid shell that starts closing in— physically contracting like the womb of a living goddess trying to abort it’s passengers. The ceiling drops inch by inch, the shag carpet now the villi of an eviscerated bowl.\n\nMy consciousness tears loose with a sound like ripping silk.\n\nI'm suspended on silver threads that sprout from the center my hands, my feet, and my side—threads connecting me to the places where my friends used to be. I can smell their agony flowing through the connections like cyanide in my lungs, taste their screams like crusty mucus on my tongue.\n\nTJ's terror cuts through the silver thread like broken glass against bare skin, every jagged edge slicing through my nerve endings until I can feel my flesh being flayed from the inside out.\n\nPatrick's agony floods my mouth with the taste of being consumed alive—copper blood and acid bile, like swallowing molten metal while my tongue dissolves.\n\nWhitney's transformation sears my vision with light and fire and devastation, electric white-hot needles driving themselves directly into my optic nerves until I'm blind from the brightness of her endless burning.\n\nBecca's corruption assaults my nostrils with the stench of organic decay—pustules bursting in rhythm, filling the air with the smell of rotting meat mixed with sulfur and the sweet cloying scent of flowers left too long on a grave.\n\nAnd Jamie... Jamie's darkness doesn't touch my body at all—it reaches directly into my very essence, wrapping around my thoughts like cold fingers, whispering I'm alone.\n\nI've always been alone\n\nEveryone I've ever loved was just a beautiful lie I told myself to keep the emptiness at bay.\n\nBelow me? Above me? Direction has abandoned all meaning as the landscape contracts into impossibility, walls of breathing mucus pressing closer with each frantic heartbeat until the air itself develops a cardiovascular system, numbers of light pulsing in panic as space compresses like a cosmic fist clenching.\n\nMirrors multiply and shrink, each reflection smaller than the last—Victorian coffins, chrome boxes, medieval plague pits, a child's bedroom—all contracting until every possible death happens simultaneously in a space the size of a pinprick, dense beyond sanity, dense beyond the possibility of existing, a kaleidoscope of endings compressed so tightly that reality itself screams as it's crushed into something smaller than a heartbeat yet somehow still shrinking, tighter, more impossible with each fraction of a second that refuses to end.\n\nWhitney suddenly catches fire—not burning but becoming flame, her flesh turning to ash that reforms again and again in an inescapable cycle of death and resurrection. The dark shadows she emanates are wrong, casting light that moves independently and whisper secrets in languages that predate human consciousness. Her screams become a symphony, harmonizing with frequencies that make the basement walls bleed actual blood.\n\n"THE FIRE DOESN't STOP," she shrieks, her voice layering over itself in impossible harmonies. "I KEEP BURNING BUT I NEVER GET TO DIE—"\n\nA choir of centipedes with human faces erupts from the intestinal lining that was the carpet, their segments clicking in percussion as they vomit calendars showing dates that violate the laws of time—February 30th, the 13th month of Octember, years that count backward into negative numbers that hurt to contemplate. Their fleshy parchments dissolve before hitting the ground, becoming letters that rearrange themselves into colors I recognize from dreams I've never had, counting out feelings I've never heard but somehow know by heart.\n\nAbove it all, a voice speaks with the casual authority of entropy itself:\n\n"You came. You opened. We answered. Enjoy the resonance bleed."\n\nThe words don't enter through my ears—they carve themselves directly into my nerves with needles made of crystallized understanding I desperately don't want. The truth they carry is too large for my skull, too sharp for whatever sanity I have left, and I feel myself beginning to unravel like a poorly knitted sweater.\n\nThe fluid sack continues to contract, the membrane now close enough to touch. The blackness that Jamie has been vomiting begins to fill the shrinking space, mixing with the reality to create a soup of liquid darkness that rises like sewage. The silver threads connecting me to my friends stretch taut, then begin to snap one by one—first TJ's thread parts with a sound like a guitar string breaking, then Patrick and Becca's merged connection severs with a wet tearing noise.\n\nWhitney explodes into purity, abstractions dissolving into memes that scatter like startled birds, leaving only the echo of her final scream reverberating in dimensions I can't perceive. Her thread doesn't break—it simply ceases to exist, as if she never was.\n\nThe fluid creeps over my chest, then my neck, warm as blood and thick as tar. It tastes like everything I’d imagine loosing Jamie would when it touches my lips. Jamie's thread is the last to snap, and when it does, I feel a part of my soul tear away with it.\n\n**********\n\nStatic cuts through the silence like a scalpel through flesh. The hellscape flickers—once, twice—and then vanishes as something abruptly shuts the TV off.\n\n“Enough of that noise.” Says something as I my eyes start to open and focus on my surroundings.\n\nI'm sitting on a folding chair in what appears to be the waiting room of a particularly depressing DMV. The tonal whiplash hits me like a wet fish slapped across my ass—one moment I'm dissolving in Lovecraft and HR Geiger’s love child, the next I'm surrounded by flickering fluorescent lights humming overhead, casting that special brand of institutional pallor that makes everyone look three days dead. The walls are a creamy off-white that hopes to one day grow up and become beige.\n\nFuck. Everything is beige. Even the air tastes beige.\n\nA middle-aged demon in a cardigan sits across from me, clutching a clipboard and looking harried. His horns poke through badly thinning hair, and his reading glasses are held together with electrical tape.\n\n"Oh thank GOD," he says, visible relief washing over his otherworldly features. "You didn't run."\n\nI blink hard, wondering if my brain has finally snapped. "I'm sorry, what?"\n\n"Benji," the demon says, extending a hand that's mostly human except for the eyes embedded in each skin cell running up his arm. "Third Class Dimensional Interlocutor and Temporary Assistant to the Recursive Memory Department. Though honestly, the paperwork is killing me."\n\nI stare at his outstretched hand. "Are you actually a daemon wearing a cardigan right now?"\n\n"Technically it's a vest," Benji says, looking down at himself. "The cardigan's in the wash. Anyway, are you going to shake my hand or just gawk? I don't bite. Much."\n\nI shake his hand because what else do you do when an otherworldly being offers you customer service? It's surprisingly warm and only slightly sticky.\n\n"Stephen.” I finally squeek out “I think I might be having a complete psychotic break."\n\n"Oh, you're not," Benji assures me, consulting his clipboard. "Well, maybe a little one. But mostly you're just leaking." He looks up with something between amazement and concern.\n\n"Sorry about the introductory violence, by the way. You’re the first person who’s ever broken through without spontaneously combusting first. Real mess that last fellow”\n\nAfter flipping through a few more papers, he continues. “Our hypothesis is that people who break through the Veil either die immediately or get trapped in pocket hell dimensions for all eternity. The fact that you made it here at all is..." He pauses, tapping his pen against his teeth. "Well, it's either miraculous or catastrophically bad news. Possibly both. The Veil's compression rate is absolute garbage this cycle."\n\n"Introductory violence?" My voice climbs an octave. "My friends just turned into meat puppets and I watched myself die seventeen different ways! That was just the introduction?"\n\nThe fluorescent light above us flickers, and for a split second, I glimpse the centipede choir writhing behind Benji's chair. Their human faces mouth silent screams before the light steadies and they're gone.\n\n"Good news is they won’t remember much of it. At least while waking. But still, you have no idea how impossible this is," Benji continues, apparently oblivious to the momentary cosmic horror.\n\n"Usually, if we ever get to break through to your side, most people just soil themselves and start praying to Jesus. Can't even get through the preliminary intake forms." He shuffles through his papers with practiced efficiency. "But you? You're still here. Still conscious. Still remarkably un-soiled."\n\n"Should I be soiled?" I ask, then immediately regret the question.\n\n"Statistically? Yes. The soiling rate for first-time encounters with entities across reality breaches is approximately one-hundred point seven-green-zulu percent."\n\nHis form flickers. Now, he's a writhing mass of exposed muscle and bone wearing a trench coat made of flayed skin, moths with human teeth bursting from the eye sockets of severed heads that crown his shoulders like grotesque epaulettes. Next, he snaps back to the cardigan-wearing demon who looks like he'd rather be filing tax returns, as if the nightmare anatomy was just a glitch in whatever avatar keeps him looking mundane.\n\n"Okay, what the actual fuck is happening right now?" I demand.\n\n"Monday," Benji finishes. "Well, it's always Monday here. Time doesn't really work the way you think it does." He reaches into a pocket that shouldn't exist and pulls out a juice box. "Apple. Hope you don't mind. The vending machine's always been broken."\n\nI take the juice box because apparently my life has become a cosmic joke. "You're offering me a juice box."\n\n"Juice boxes are very soothing," Benji says seriously. "Also, the apple juice here tastes like childhood. I would think that most people find that comforting after their first reality fracture."\n\nThe straw is tiny and plastic and perfectly normal. The apple juice does taste like childhood—like old dog piss and the salty regret of lost teeth, like hiding in closets and praying he wouldn't find me. I drink it down anyway because why the fuck not at this point.\n\nBenji notices my grimace. "Not good?" He glances at his clipboard, flipping through a few pages. "Oh. Oh dear." His expression shifts to something approaching genuine sympathy. "Sorry. Should have checked the trauma index first." He reaches into another impossible pocket and produces a different juice box—this one purple. "Try this instead. Happiness flavor."\n\nI take it skeptically, but when the liquid hits my tongue it tastes like vanilla and peaches, like warm summer afternoons that never existed but should have. Like holding hands with a love that was always there.\n\n"Better?" Benji asks.\n\n"Much," I admit, and somehow that makes me want to cry.\n\n"Benji," I say slowly, "are you actually trying to help me, or is this some kind of elaborate torture?"\n\n"Oh, I'm absolutely trying to help," Benji says earnestly, his grin revealing teeth that are definitely sharper than they were a moment ago. "The problem is the Veil interferes with everything. Every tool I might try to give you will malfunction, every piece of advice will get corrupted in transmission, every attempt to make things better will somehow make them exponentially worse." He sighs heavily. "It's quite frustrating, actually. I have all this useful information, but the moment I try to tell you about..."\n\nOut of his mouth streams something like water, monstrous tiny fish swimming around and then exploding into bits of gore, revealing glimpses of corridors that extend forever, lined with filing cabinets that breathe. Out of one of the cabinets, a creature that might once have been human slides past, its limbs replaced with manila folders.\n\n"…part's over," Benji finishes saying, making notes on his clipboard with a pen that occasionally turns into a small, screaming face. "You should feel normal by the time you turn seventy-four."\n\n"Seventy-four?" I nearly choke on the happiness juice. "What the hell has happened to me?"\n\n"See?" Benji points at me with his writhing pen. "I was trying to tell you how to avoid problems. The Veil turned it into existential dread. Classic interference pattern."\n\nBenji's expression grows sympathetic, the way a doctor might look at someone with terminal stupidity. "You opened a door that was supposed to stay closed. Saw behind the curtain. Glimpsed the gears that keep reality spinning." He pauses, then adds with genuine concern, "How's your sanity holding up? On a scale of one to completely shattered?"\n\nThe fluorescent light flickers again. This time, I see myself hanging from meat hooks, my body opened like a flower of flesh and bone. Blood pools upward, defying gravity, forming words in a language I almost understand. Then the light steadies, and I'm back in the beige waiting room with a demon who looks like he moonlights at H&R Block.\n\n"You know what?" I say, crushing the empty juice box. "I think I'm remarkably okay with this. Which probably means I'm completely insane."\n\n"Good, good," Benji nods, making another note. "Acceptance is the first stage of interdimensional adjustment. Very important in your situation."\n\n"Which is?"\n\nHe looks up from his clipboard, and for just a moment, his eyes are infinite—galaxies spinning in sockets too small to contain them. "You're leaking between realities, kid. Bleeding through the cracks in existence." His grin widens. "And frankly? We're all fascinated to see what happens next."\n\nJuice runs between my fingers like something darker, something that shouldn't exist in a place this aggressively normal. "We?"\n\n"Oh yes," Benji says, his form stabilizing into something that's mostly goblin with a hint of middle management. "There's quite a betting pool going on upstairs. The odds on you surviving until tomorrow are really quite terrible."\n\n"Fantastic," I mutter. "Should I be flattered or terrified?"\n\n"Both. Definitely both."\n\n"Right then," he says, settling back in his chair with the air of someone about to deliver very bad news with practiced efficiency. "Let's talk about where you are, what you are, and why everything you thought you knew about reality is complete bollocks."\n\n"Oh good," I say, gripping the arms of my folding chair. "I was wondering when we'd get to the existential devastation part of this conversation."\n\nThe waiting room expands around us, walls stretching until they're lost in institutional beige distance. The fluorescent lights multiply, creating an endless grid of humming mediocrity that somehow makes the cosmic horror worse.\n\n"First things first," Benji continues, pulling out a red pen that occasionally writhes like a worm. "Earth—your Earth—isn't actually Earth. It’s a place called Etheris. It's what we in the business call a Prison Realm. Think of it as... cosmic Alcatraz, but with better cable television and significantly more existential dread. It was originally built to hold…"\n\nAs he opens his mouth to speak, his words become liquid horror—black tendrils of sound that flow like acid through my eyes and into my brain, eating through my thoughts like maggots through rotting meat. The syllables twist into shapes that shouldn't exist, each consonant a razor blade dragging across my consciousness while the vowels burn like molten lead poured directly into my skull. I can feel my sanity unraveling with each word, reality itself rejecting the information he's trying to convey, and for a moment I'm certain I'm going to die again, right here in this beige nightmare.\n\n"… resulting in a lot of dangerous consciousnesses. Broken souls. Reality glitches like yourself." He makes a note that seems to write itself backward. "The whole setup is unintentionally muddled—scrambled frequencies, recursive loops, memory wipes right after death.”\n\n"Wait, wait, wait." I hold up a hand. "Are you telling me I'm in cosmic jail? What did I do, forget to return a library book in another dimension?"\n\nBenji chuckles, a sound like wind chimes made of fingernails. "You? Absolutely nothing. In fact, you and everyone else in there is completely innocent. Unfortunately, in the process of capturing the main prisoner, some sacrifices had to be made." His expression grows somber. "A trillion trillion trillion other souls are enslaved inside as collateral damage. We've been trying to get someone on the inside to help, or at least mount some kind of rescue mission, but the Veil being the Veil..."\n\nThe walls shimmer, and for a moment I see them as they really are: transparent membranes keeping something vast and hungry at bay. Shapes move beyond them, pressing against the boundary like fish in a cosmic aquarium.\n\n"But I'm not—" I start.\n\n"Dangerous? Oh, you absolutely are now," Benji continues. "Not because you're evil, but because you might actually be able to do something about this mess. And that terrifies the administrators more than anything."\n\nBenji's grin widens, showing teeth that are definitely sharper than they were a moment ago. "You're what we call a Leaker. Most consciousnesses, when they die, get properly recycled. Slow absorption into a new body, new memories, clean slate. But you? You're like a cracked battery—all that psychic energy just hemorrhaging across dimensional boundaries."\n\n"That... actually explains a lot about my life," I admit.\n\nHe gestures with moth-wing fingers, and symbols trail in the air behind them. The symbols arrange themselves into a three-dimensional model that hurts to look at directly—overlapping spheres that pass through each other at impossible angles, connected by threads of light that pulse with each beat of my heart.\n\n"These are your timelines," Benji explains, pointing to the spheres with his writhing pen. "Should be separate, nice and tidy. But look here—" The pen traces a crack running through multiple spheres. "This is where you broke the system. Age eight, dying from poisoning. Except you didn’t just die. You somehow pulled yourself back. That trauma created a fracture that's been spreading ever since."\n\nThe model rotates slowly, and I can see how the crack branches out like lightning, touching dozens of other spheres. In some, I'm older. In others, I'm different entirely—sometimes not even human.\n\n"And see here," Benji continues, his pen moving to a larger sphere that pulses with a sickly light, “That was when you ripped that engine from the dream realm, destroyed reality, and then came out the other end along this pathway” as he traces the undulating line to a larger black hole at the end.\n\n"After that reset, we had hoped our fixes would have held. This is where you were supposed to die at age seventy-four. Hospice pod, watching the world rust around you, full of rage and disappointment. Another standard old age termination protocol."\n\nThe pen traces a line from that sphere to a much smaller, brighter one that seems to vibrate with unstable energy. "But instead of properly merging back into another body, your consciousness did... this. Created a weird pocket divergence that sprouted out of nowhere when your death-rage hit critical mass."\n\nHe taps the smaller sphere. "That's you now. Age sixteen, in a timeline that technically shouldn't exist, with all the memories of a seventy-four-year-old man who died angry. The system has no idea how to process you anymore. You're like a computer virus made of pure existential crisis."\n\nThe crack spreads further as I watch, touching more spheres, and some of them begin to flicker and distort. "It's still growing?"\n\n"Oh yes," Benji says cheerfully. "Every day you exist makes it worse. Fascinating, really."\n\nThere’s a bright flash, and suddenly I feel myself covered with shadows. There’s a dark flash and I’m back again.\n\n"The shadows you've been seeing?" Benji continues, making the model collapse back into his clipboard with a casual gesture.\n\n"Those are Suppressors, think of them like Jailors. Reality's immune system. They're supposed to keep Leakers like you from noticing the cracks, but your signal is so strong you're basically broadcasting on all frequencies at once."\n\nA suppressor materializes beside his chair—a humanoid shape made of television static and bad intentions. It reaches toward me with fingers that aren't quite there, and I feel a sudden urge to forget everything that's happening, to go home and watch TV and never think about any of this again.\n\n"That's... actually really tempting," I say, staring at the thing.\n\n"Now, now," Benji chides, and the suppressor dissolves like smoke. "We're having a conversation here. Very rude to interrupt."\n\n"And, the death flashes?" I ask, my voice smaller than I'd like. "Those are real?"\n\n"Bleed-through from other alternatives," Benji explains, flipping through his papers with practiced efficiency. "See, when someone dies, their consciousness is supposed to randomly attach to another living host in an alternate timeline. Most of the time, the dead consciousness slowly fades away with the existing living host not even realizing. Sometimes this shows up as people getting flashes of déjà vu. Some stronger folks dream all the time, but are actually temporarily incepting into these other realities. As I said, usually the transition is seamless. One moment you're getting shot in the head in Vietnam, the next you're suddenly eating lunch with your elementary school sweetheart in suburban Ohio, maybe with a sudden aversion to jungles or something."\n\nHe pauses, looking up from his detailed reports of my various demises. "But what happened to you has broken that whole system. Instead of fading away, these consciousness transfers are being captured and remembered. Not only that but your soul is jumping to variants of your self, and each flash, each vision is making you stronger and the destabilization worse. Every time you experience significant trauma, the barriers thin and you get glimpses of how you died in adjacent realities. Not only that, but your strength in dreaming is injecting these issues onto other people."\n\nThe pen traces through the list. "Let's see... scaffold collapse, car accident, angry artificial intelligence, consumption by younger self wielding bone knife—oh, that one's particularly unpleasant. The point is, you're not supposed to remember any of this. But here you are, collecting death experiences like trading cards."\n\n"So I'm basically a cosmic glitch?"\n\n"The biggest glitch we've ever seen," Benji confirms with something approaching pride. "You've turned the entire reincarnation system into a feedback loop."\n\n"Jesus Christ." I run my hands through my hair. "Why is this happening to me?"\n\nBenji looks up from his papers, and his expression softens slightly. "Because you died wrong, kid. Most people, when they reach the end of their natural lifespan, they fade gradually. Consciousness dims, memories blur, the transition is smooth. But you? You went out with a bang. All that rage and disappointment and unfinished business—it created a resonance that's been echoing across realities ever since."\n\n"Which death are we talking about here? Because I'm starting to lose track."\n\n"Actually, it was the one at eight years old," Benji says, consulting his notes with a frown. "Your parents dragged you to church relentlessly—not to mention the abuse you suffered from the priests there, who apparently liked sharing proclivities with your father."\n\nHis expression darkens. "That just shattered your conception of love entirely. Towards the end, you started to realize that love isn't poisoning your son, and that God wasn't love at all but hate. Pure, distilled hate. That did something to the mask that he was hiding behind."\n\nHe flips through several pages. "It was at this time when you started to hide, not just physically, but also mentally. You began creating vivid dreams, realities you could escape into. That realization, that rage, that sudden dream walking power—it kept you from fading out like you were supposed to. It stuck with you when you initially jumped over after that death that wasn't supposed to happen."\n\nBenji looks genuinely apologetic. "Huge apologies about that, by the way. Even the Jailers who were monitoring couldn't quite figure out what went wrong."\n\n"And then?"\n\n"That set you up for the timeline where you somehow captured what we call the Eternal Engine from your dreams, before managing to destroy our main prisoner in that timeline." Benji's voice drops to something approaching awe.\n\n"When you reconfigured reality in that final instant... well, let's just say it really screwed things up. The ripple effects are still propagating across dimensional barriers."\n\n"So this is all my fault?"\n\n"Oh, absolutely," Benji says cheerfully. "But in the best possible way."\n\nThe waiting room flickers, and for a terrifying moment I see what's really around us: an infinite bureaucracy of the damned, creatures in ill-fitting suits processing the paperwork of existence while something vast and tentacled watches from the shadows between the filing cabinets.\n\n"So I'm what, stuck in some kind of cosmic time loop?"\n\n"The prison was designed to contain one particular entity," Benji continues, apparently deciding to ignore my question entirely.\n\n"Something we call Yaldabaoth, though it prefers the name I AM. Bit of a megalomaniac, if you ask me. The whole recycling system exists to prevent it from consuming souls directly and gaining power to escape—it keeps souls in circulation, generates just enough spiritual energy to maintain the barriers without feeding the prisoner too much."\n\n"And I'm breaking that system?"\n\n"Like a bull in a China shop, yes." Benji grins with genuine enthusiasm. "It's fascinating, really. Most Leakers burn out after a few jumps. You've been bouncing around for millions of years now, and you're only getting stronger. The administrators are beside themselves."\n\nHe gestures toward a corner of the waiting room where several creatures in business attire huddle around a water cooler, speaking in hushed tones. They have too many eyes and not enough faces, and they keep glancing in our direction with expressions of cosmic concern.\n\n"Let me get this straight," I say slowly. "I'm in prison. For a crime I haven't committed yet. And I'm accidentally breaking the entire system just by existing."\n\n"That's the gist of it, yes."\n\n"And you're telling me this why?"\n\nBenji checks his watch, which appears to be running backward. "Because Lady Illkeserod—one of the resistance goddesses operating from outside the prison, and technically my boss—has tasked me with trying to figure this whole thing out. See, on the inside, all humans have a guardian angel assigned by Yaldabaoth. We're not there to protect you—we're quality control. Make sure souls don't die before they're ripe enough for harvest."\n\nHe pauses, his expression growing more serious. "I was originally supposed to be your guardian angel. But in one of your dream realities—some little bubble dimension somewhere—you called out to Lady Illkeserod at the exact same moment I was being formed in another timeline. That synchronicity allowed me to initially escape Yaldabaoth’s programming, gain actual free will instead of being his slave."\n\nHis grin returns, but there's something fierce behind it now. "Once I was free, I was able to help Lady Illkeserod and the resistance movement gain access to the prison systems. Technically, we're all out here on the outside while you're still trapped in there. This DMV?" He gestures around the beige nightmare. "It's a weird nowhere place that exists between dimensions—took considerable effort to stabilize it enough for us to meet."\n\n"So here I am, free of his influence and helping one of the Prison Architects figure out how to un-fuck this entire situation."\n\n"But officially?"\n\n"Officially, I'm not supposed to be talking to you at all. You're classified as a Category Five Existential Hazard. I should be running diagnostics on your consensual reality matrix and filing a report in triplicate."\n\nHis grin returns, and this time it's almost paternal. "But you're the most interesting thing to happen to this place since we locked everything down. And frankly? Lady Illkeserod thinks you might be our only shot at fixing this whole mess."\n\n"No pressure or anything." Oh, tremendous pressure actually. The fate of trillions of souls kind of hangs in the balance. I somehow hear his thoughts through all of this\n\nThe waiting room begins to destabilize, walls warping like funhouse mirrors. Through the distortions, I catch glimpses of my friends still unconscious in TJ's basement, their bodies perfectly still while something that might be time itself pools around them like spilled mercury.\n\n"Time's up," Benji says, standing and brushing imaginary lint from his cardigan. "But don't worry—this won't be our last chat. I have a feeling you're going to need all the help you can get."\n\nHe takes a step toward what I assume is the exit, then stops abruptly, snapping his fingers like he's just remembered something crucial.\n\nBenji rummages through his cardigan pockets like a man searching for his car keys in a burning building. The waiting room continues its slow collapse around us—chairs melting into abstract sculptures, ceiling tiles raining down like confetti made of institutional despair.\n\n"Oh wait!" Benji says suddenly, snapping his fingers. "Before you go, I was supposed to give you something. This liminal space we're able to meet in—it isn't inside the prison, yet it isn't outside either, but it's unstable as anything. I told Lady Illkeserod this wasn't going to work at all and it was a terrible idea, but if I go back and tell her I didn't even try, that would be quite a shame."\n\n"Right, where did I put those..." he mutters, pulling out increasingly bizarre objects. A stapler that screams when opened. A coffee mug full of liquid darkness. What appears to be someone's severed finger wearing a tiny wedding ring. "Sorry, interdimensional pockets are murder on organization. Ah! Here we go."\n\nHe produces a small obsidian stone, perfectly round and warm to the touch. It pulses with its own inner light, like a tiny heartbeat made of starlight.\n\n"Shadow tracker," Benji explains, pressing it into my palm. "Should glow when Suppressors are nearby. Or at least, it would if the Veil wasn't completely buggering up the frequency modulation."\n\nThe stone flickers weakly, then dims to almost nothing. Benji frowns and taps it like a broken flashlight.\n\n"Hmm. That's... that's not right." He examines the stone with the expression of a mechanic staring at an engine that's somehow running on hopes and prayers. "It should be blazing like a miniature sun in here. The Suppressor density alone..." He trails off, pulling out what looks like a cosmic multimeter and waving it around.\n\nThe device makes sounds like a dial-up modem having an aneurysm, then promptly catches fire.\n\n"Bollocks." Benji stamps out the flames with his sensible office shoes. "The Veil is worse than I thought. It's not just dampening the signal—it's actively inverting it. Like trying to use a compass in a magnetic storm while standing on the surface of a neutron star."\n\nThe fluorescent lights above us strobe frantically, and for a moment I see the waiting room as it really is: a tiny bubble of manufactured reality floating in an ocean of screaming void. The Veil—a membrane that looks like oil and television static had a particularly ugly baby—presses against the boundaries, distorting everything it touches.\n\n"Look, the stone will still work," Benji says with forced optimism. "It's just... severely compromised. Think of it as cosmic background radiation detector that can only pick up signals during leap years while Mercury is in retrograde."\n\nHe reaches into another pocket and pulls out what appears to be a piece of parchment covered in symbols that writhe when I'm not looking directly at them.\n\n"Stabilization technique," he explains, holding it at arm's length like it might bite him. "Breathing pattern combined with specific hand gestures. Should temporarily reinforce local reality when you're experiencing... episodes."\n\nThe parchment suddenly bursts into flames.\n\n"Oh, come on!" Benji beats at it with his clipboard until only ashes remain. "That was a perfectly good reality anchor! Took me three centuries to calibrate those sigils."\n\nHe stares at the ashes with the defeated expression of a man whose printer has jammed for the fifteenth time in one day. "Right. New plan. I'll just... I'll try to transmit the pattern directly."\n\nBenji places his hands on either side of my head. His palms are surprisingly cool, and they smell faintly of ozone and regret. For a moment, I feel something trying to download into my brain—geometric patterns, breathing rhythms, a sense of cosmic order that my mind desperately wants to grab onto.\n\nThen the Veil interferes.\n\nThe information becomes corrupted as it passes through the barrier, transforming into something like a recipe for chocolate chip cookies written in Sumerian and performed as interpretive dance. My head fills with the taste of seven, the sound of Tuesday, and instructions to breathe in patterns that would require at least four lungs.\n\n"Did you get any of that?" Benji asks hopefully.\n\n"I think so?" I lie, because the look of desperate optimism on his face is somehow worse than the cosmic horror. "Something about... breathing... and maybe jazz hands?"\n\n"Close enough!" Benji declares with the enthusiasm of someone grading their own test. "Jazz hands are definitely part of advanced reality manipulation. You'll figure it out."\n\nHe reaches into a third pocket and pulls out what looks like a fortune cookie made of crystallized starlight. "Warning," he says solemnly. "This one's important. More you poke, more it pokes back. That much should transmit clearly even through this bloody interference."\n\nHe hands me the cookie. The moment it touches my fingers, it crumbles to dust, but the warning echoes directly in my skull with crystalline clarity: The more you poke, the more it pokes back.\n\n"There!" Benji says triumphantly. "At least that one worked. Sort of. The packaging could use work, but the message is intact."\n\nThe waiting room gives a particularly violent shudder, and several filing cabinets phase through the wall like bureaucratic ghosts. In the distance, I hear something that might be an interdimensional PA system announcing the arrival of Entity Control on Platform Existential Crisis.\n\n"Time's really up now," Benji says, his form beginning to flicker more rapidly. "I'm being recalled to face a disciplinary review. Apparently, unauthorized contact with Category Five Hazards is 'grounds for dimensional reassignment' and 'a threat to the cosmic order.'" He makes air quotes that leave actual quotation marks hanging in the space around us.\n\n"Wait," I say, panic rising as the only semi-helpful entity I've encountered begins to fade. "What am I supposed to do with this information? How do I—"\n\n"Wing it!" Benji calls out as his cardigan begins to unravel into moths.\n\n**********\n\nThe waiting room implodes like a house of cards made of screaming.\n\nI slam back into my body with the force of a meteor hitting atmosphere, every nerve ending catching fire as consciousness crashes into flesh. The basement floor is cold concrete against my cheek, rough and real and gloriously mundane. I gasp for air that tastes like incense and terror, my lungs working overtime to process oxygen that feels too thick, too laden with possibility.\n\nMy friends are scattered around me like casualties of a war nobody remembers fighting. Naked. All of us, completely naked. Unconscious. Breathing, but barely—their chests rising and falling with the mechanical rhythm of people whose souls have temporarily vacated the premises.\n\nBut something's wrong. This isn't TJ's basement. The walls dirt, lined with clawing roots and mottled paintings, the shag carpet is missing, instead it’s a cold, moist concrete. The room’s proportions are all off. The ceiling is too low, angles are wrong, pressing down like the lid of a coffin. The corners stretch into shadows that seem deeper than they should be, and the familiar hum of the dehumidifier has been replaced by something that sounds almost like breathing.\n\n"Hey," I croak, my voice sounding like gravel in a blender. "Hey, wake up."\n\nNothing. Jamie lies curled in a fetal position in my lap. Her dark hair fans across the my legs like spilled ink, and when try and flip her over to check her pulse, her skin is cold as marble. But the pulse is there—steady, strong, reassuring.\n\nWhere the hell are our clothes? I scan the basement frantically, but there's nothing. No discarded jeans, no scattered shirts, no underwear—like we materialized here already naked. The ritual supplies are gone too. No candles, no BALG printout, no tarot cards. Just us, bare and vulnerable on cold concrete that feels like it's never been warm.\n\nI get up and go over to try to shake TJ awake, but his body is limp, but he feels as fragile as porcelain. His eyes move beneath closed lids, tracking something I can't see, and occasionally his mouth forms words in a language that doesn't exist. When I touch his shoulder, his skin is fever-hot despite the basement chill.\n\n"Come on, man," I whisper, panic creeping into my voice. "This isn't funny anymore."\n\nThat's when I notice the TV.\n\nIt's flickering with static, which shouldn't be possible since Patrick smashed it with the pool cue on Tuesday—the frame cracked, the cord still unplugged, the whole thing a monument to teenage violence against malfunctioning electronics. The screen shows nothing but white noise bleeding through the spider web of cracks, but there's something moving in the static—patterns that almost resolve into faces before dissolving back into chaos.\n\nThen I hear it: "We'll talk again. Bring snacks next time."\n\nBenji's voice, tinny and distant, crackling through speakers that shouldn't work. The words would make me laugh if I wasn't so terrified. At least some part of what happened was real.\n\nWhitney's body convulses once, violently, and black fluid leaks from the corner of her mouth before her breathing stabilizes. The fluid doesn't evaporate—it pools on the concrete, forming patterns that look almost like symbols before dissolving into ordinary liquid darkness.\n\n"Whitney!" I scramble over to her, but she's completely unresponsive. Her pulse is there but weak, fluttering under my fingers like a trapped bird.\n\n"Come on," I whisper, crawling from friend to friend, checking pulses, looking for any sign that they're still themselves. "Please don't be broken. Please still be you."\n\nPatrick's fingers twitch, and for a moment they're not fingers at all but something segmented and chitinous, like the legs of some cosmic insect. Then they're human again, pale and ordinary, but when I look closer, there are tiny puncture wounds in his palms, as if something with very small, very sharp teeth has been nibbling at him.\n\nThat's when Becca's eyes snap open—not gradually, but all at once, like someone flipping a switch. Her pupils are dilated to perfect black circles, and she stares at the ceiling with an expression of absolute serenity that's somehow more terrifying than any scream.\n\n"The membrane remembers," she says in a voice that's definitely hers but wrong somehow, like she's speaking through water. "The membrane always remembers."\n\nThen, without breaking eye contact with the ceiling, her hand shoots out and grabs Patrick's naked crotch with surprising gentleness. Her fingers curl around him with the familiarity of lovers, and a slow, satisfied smile spreads across her lips.\n\n"Becca?" I whisper, but she doesn't respond. Her hand remains where it is, and Patrick's breathing changes—not waking, but shifting into something that sounds almost like purring.\n\nThen she's unconscious again, nestled in her new home, but that smile lingers for a few seconds before her face goes slack.\n\nI sit back down, naked and shivering and more alone than I've ever been in my life. The basement feels too small now, like a cage designed to hold something that's outgrown its boundaries. Every shadow seems pregnant with possibility, every flicker of light threatens to tear open reality and show me things I'm not ready to see.\n\nThe walls start to shift—not dramatically, but subtly, like someone's adjusting the contrast on reality. The wood paneling ripples like water, and for split seconds I glimpse what's behind it: vast corridors stretching into infinity, lined with machinery that breathes and weeps lubricant that might be blood. Something moves in those corridors, something with too many joints and not enough skin, dragging itself toward whatever membrane separates its world from mine.\n\nThe static on the TV grows louder, and buried in the white noise I hear something else—not words, but music. A lullaby played on instruments that don't exist, in harmonies that make my teeth ache. The melody is familiar, like something from a childhood I never had, and it makes me want to close my eyes and sleep for a thousand years.\n\nBut I can't sleep. I have to stay awake. I have to watch over them.\n\nShadows begin moving at the edges of my vision—not the normal shadows cast by the flickering fluorescent light, but shapes with purpose, with hunger. They creep along the walls like living things, reaching toward my unconscious friends with fingers made of darkness.\n\n"No," I whisper, trying to crawl toward Jamie, but my limbs feel like they're made of lead. "Leave them alone."\n\nThe shadows pause, and I swear I feel them looking at me. Evaluating. Deciding.\n\nThen the darkness rushes in from all sides, and the last thing I see before unconsciousness takes me is the shadows flowing into Jamie like smoke being inhaled—pouring through her mouth, her nose, the corners of her eyes, until she's full of writhing darkness that moves beneath her skin like living tattoos. Her back arches, and for just a moment, she smiles with too many teeth.\n\nThe basement exhales around us, and everything goes black.\n\n**********\n\nI don't know how long I was out—could have been minutes, could have been hours. The basement feels different when I finally drag my eyes open, thick with a humid musk that reminds me of stale tuna mixed with the stench from the boys' locker room after PE, but wrong somehow. Sweeter. Stickier.\n\nEveryone else starts to wake up like swimmers surfacing from deep water—slow, disoriented, gasping for air they forgot they needed. One by one, consciousness seeps back into their bodies, and I watch their faces cycle through confusion, recognition, and a kind of dazed acceptance that probably should worry me more than it does.\n\nTJ sits up first, blinking owlishly at the basement around him. He's completely naked, pale skin goose bumped from the cold and glistening with a thin sheen of sweat that catches the fluorescent light. His hair is matted to his forehead, and there's something sticky on his cheek that he absently wipes away without seeming to notice.\n\n"Jesus," he mutters, running a hand through his damp hair. "Did we...? What the hell happened to our clothes?" He starts to look under the furniture and rummage in some of the clutter strewn around outside our summoning circle.\n\nI look around the basement, and it’s like we’re in the middle of nothingness. The salt circle of protection marks a perfect boundary: inside it, we're naked and disoriented. Outside our tangled mess of bodies within the circle, everything looks normal. It's like whatever happened was surgically precise, affecting only what was within the ritual space.\n\n"Did it work?" TJ asks, his voice thick with sleep and something else.\n\n"Define 'work,'" I say, still sitting cross-legged on the shag carpet, completely naked and trying not to think about the dampness I can feel between my legs, on my thighs, like I've been sweating in places that shouldn’t.\n\nWhitney emerges from unconsciousness next. Her eyes flutter open, unfocused at first, then sharpening as she takes in the scene. She sits up slowly, unselfconsciously naked, her growing chest pale in the incandescent light, and reaches for where her scattered tarot cards should be—but they're gone too.\n\n"The polyester," she says suddenly, her voice distant and dreamy. "The cosmic energies... they don't like synthetic materials. That's why our clothes are gone. The Veil rejected them."\n\nShe says it with such matter-of-fact certainty that nobody questions it, even though it sounds completely insane.\n\nBecca wakes with a start, immediately looking around frantically. Her face is flushed, chest rising and falling rapidly, and when she realizes where she's been sleeping—her head pillowed in Patrick's naked crotch, both hands holding his manhood for warmth—she jerks upright like she's been electrocuted. But not before I catch the satisfied smile that flickers across her face, followed immediately by a blush that spreads from her cheeks down to her chest.\n\n"My parents are going to kill me," she mumbles, but there's no real panic in her voice. Her skin has that post-shower glow, like she's been thoroughly... cleansed. "What time is it? How long were we..."\n\n"Gone," Patrick finishes, rolling onto his side and propping himself next to Becca. He's the calmest of all of them, naked and sprawled across the shag carpet like it's the most natural thing in the world. There's dried salt crusted at his temples, and his face look slightly rug burned. "We were gone somewhere, weren't we?"\n\nI nod, not trusting my voice to explain what 'somewhere' actually was. Because I remember. I remember all of it—the hell, the bureaucratic demon, the cosmic truth. But looking at their faces, it's clear they don't. For them, there's just a blank space where the terror should be.\n\nJamie is the last to wake, and when she does, it's with a gurgle that sounds more like the aftermath of something intense than surprise. Her dark hair is a tangled mess, stuck to her neck with sweat, and something gleaming between her breasts. Her eyes find mine immediately, and there's something in her gaze that makes my skin prickle with awareness.\n\n"Stephen," she breathes, and my name in her mouth sounds like a prayer and a promise all at once. She sits up slowly, making no move to cover herself, and the way she looks at me is... different. Hungry, maybe. Or satisfied in a way that makes my stomach do strange flips.\n\nShe moves toward me, completely oblivious to her nakedness, her movements fluid and purposeful. The basement air is thick with the scent of bodies—not unpleasant, but unmistakably intimate, like the aftermath of something that should have required consent forms.\n\n"I don't remember what happened," she continues, her voice dropping to a whisper that somehow fills the entire basement, "but I feel... changed. Connected. Like something important happened between us."\n\nHer eyes drift down from my face, taking in my bare chest, and I suddenly become very aware that I'm completely exposed. Heat rises in my cheeks, and I reach instinctively for a pillow from the couch behind me, clutching it against myself like a shield made of polyester and embarrassment.\n\n"Found them," TJ says from behind one of the recliners, his voice muffled. "They're all... piled back here. They smell fresh. Clean. Like washed and someone folded them."\n\nWe all scramble toward his voice like refugees fleeing a natural disaster, our dignity abandoned somewhere between cosmic horror and teenage mortification. The clothes are indeed neatly folded—which is weird because none of us are the type to fold anything, especially not in the middle of a demonic summoning. But there they are: jeans stacked on jeans, shirts arranged by size, even our underwear separated into tidy little piles.\n\nIt's almost more unsettling than finding them missing in the first place.\n\nAfter we're all dressed, we collapse back onto the furniture like survivors of some cosmic shipwreck. The couch cushions feel too soft, too normal after everything that's happened. Everyone looks drained, exhausted in a way that goes deeper than physical tiredness—like we've been wrung out at the soul level.\n\nI find the obsidian stone in my pocket and take it out carefully. It's not the smooth, warm talisman Benji gave me anymore. Instead, it's a dense lump of what looks like coal, rough and cold to the touch, all its cosmic properties apparently burned out.\n\n"Look at this," I say, holding it up for Jamie to see.\n\nAs I pass it over to