98	2	8	Ch. 7: Echo Maps	September slips by like a fever dream.\n\nThe first week after the basement incident, we meet every day. Urgent whispers in hallways, emergency telephone sessions in my bedroom, bbs sessions that stretch past midnight. We're addicts chasing the high of cosmic revelation, desperate to recapture that moment when the universe showed us its teeth.\n\nThose of our group who weren’t there quickly fade away. By the second week, Gil stops showing up. "I've got college prep," he says, but his eyes won't meet mine. James follows three days later, citing "academic priorities" and "personal mental health concerns."\n\nThe third week brings Becca's grounding. Her parents found something – ritual notes, occult symbols, evidence of our "satanic activities" hidden in her backpack. She swears she cleaned everything out, but somehow they knew exactly where to look.\n\nOctober arrives wearing the face of exhaustion.\n\nBy then, the shadows have developed an appetite for curious teenagers, and our lunch table feels like a support group for reality-allergic outcasts. We're still playing Magic: The Gathering, still going through the motions of normal teenage friendship, but the energy has curdled into something desperate and strained.\n\nGil, James, Andrew, and Morgan sit at the far end of the table now, close enough to maintain the illusion of group cohesion but far enough to avoid our conversations about dimensional bleeding and cosmic bureaucracy. Ever since I told them about Benji – about the basement horror show, the blood, the walls that screamed – they've been backing away like I'm contagious.\n\n"Your turn," TJ says, tapping his cards impatiently. His usual Reagan-era confidence has been ground down to nervous energy. He's been grounded since his mom found those BALG printouts, and his car privileges got revoked indefinitely. "Can't believe I'm stuck taking the bus like some freshman."\n\nJamie suddenly shifts uncomfortably beside me, her grip on my hand tightening. "TJ, I'm so sorry," she says, her voice thick with guilt. "I keep thinking about those printouts... When I was burning them at Becca's, your backpack was right there by the firepit. Some papers might have blown around in the wind, fallen into your bag..." She bites her lip, looking genuinely distressed. "I thought I'd checked everything, but I must have missed some. God, I feel terrible."\n\nTJ's expression softens slightly. "Jamie, it's not your fault. Mom was already suspicious. She was going through my stuff looking for reasons to ground me."\n\n"Still," Jamie continues, her eyes glistening with what might be tears. "If I'd been more careful... I should have made sure every single page was ash before we left."\n\nI squeeze her hand reassuringly, impressed by how much she cares about our group's safety. But something nags at me—hadn't TJ's mom found the papers folded neatly inside his Trapper Keeper, alphabetized? That doesn't sound like wind-blown accident papers.\n\nI draw a card, but my attention keeps drifting to the empty spaces where our investigation used to live. The conversations that stop when Gil walks by. The way Morgan takes notes for everything except our theories now.\n\nIt wasn't always like this. A month ago, the shadows were everywhere – dancing behind fluorescent lights, pooling in classroom corners, following us home like cosmic stalkers. The death visions hit me daily: construction accidents, car crashes, drownings, murders. I'd sketch them frantically during calculus, trying to map patterns that felt just beyond comprehension.\n\nBut the more we noticed them, the more they learned to hide.\n\n"So Benji confirmed it," I say quietly, leaning forward so only the core group can hear. "The shadows are suppressors. Reality filters. They're actively working to keep us from seeing—"\n\n"Can we not?" Gil interrupts from down the table, not even looking up from his cards. "Some of us are trying to maintain what's left of our sanity."\n\nWhitney's jaw tightens, but she keeps her voice level. "Just because you don't want to see it doesn't mean it's not happening."\n\n"And just because you want to see it doesn't mean it is," Andrew counters. He's been reading philosophy books again, wielding logic like a shield against anything that doesn't fit in neat academic categories.\n\nThe divide is getting wider. I can feel it pulling at us like gravity, dragging the skeptics away from those of us who still believe. Or maybe the believers away from those smart enough to run.\n\n"It's like they know we're watching," I continue, trying to keep the desperation out of my voice. "Every time we get organized, something goes wrong. TJ loses his car right when we need to follow a lead. Becca gets grounded right before we plan a cemetery investigation—"\n\n"Maybe that's just life," Becca interrupts, her voice carrying an edge I've never heard before. She's been different since her parents discovered our "occult activities" – colder, more distant. "Maybe we're seeing patterns where there aren't any."\n\nHer words sting more than they should. Becca was supposed to be on our side.\n\nJamie shifts closer to me on the bench, pressing against me under the table. "You know what?" she says, loud enough for everyone to hear. "Maybe this is good. Maybe we need to focus on high school stuff instead of... whatever this has become."\n\nI feel Becca's eyes on us, sharp and accusatory. There's history there I don't fully understand – some falling out between her and Jamie that happened right around the time Becca's parents found those ritual notes. Jamie was supposed to dispose of everything, but somehow Becca's mom found copies in her room.\n\n"You know what might help?" Jamie says suddenly, her voice bright with helpful concern. "Maybe we should tell our parents we're done with all this. Just to get them off our backs. We could still investigate privately, but if they think we've stopped..." She looks around the table with earnest eyes