xist, smell sounds that make my teeth rot. The boundary between our world and whatever lies beneath dissolves like sugar in acid.\n\nJamie collapses first.\n\nHer body hits the concrete with a wet sound, limbs jerking in rhythms that follow no earthly music. Foam spills from her mouth—not the clean white foam of medical emergencies, but something dark and viscous that moves with its own purpose. Words pour out with it, syllables that make my bones vibrate and my vision blur. Ancient words. Hungry words.\n\n"Jamie!" I drop to my knees beside her, but touching her feels like grasping live wire. Her skin burns with fever that climbs toward cooking temperatures, and her eyes have rolled back to show only whites veined with black.\n\nThe ritual circle we'd created with salt turns to smoke and bubbles, lines reorganizing themselves into patterns that hurt to look at directly. The basement fills with the stench of ozone and burnt meat.\n\nThen TJ starts screaming.\n\nBlood erupts from his arm—not a trickle, but a geyser that paints the wall in arterial spray. The cut appears out of nowhere, slicing from wrist to elbow like an invisible surgeon decided to open him up and see what makes him tick. The wound gapes wide enough to see bone, maybe deeper than bone, revealing layers of anatomy that shouldn't exist in a human body.\n\n"What the fuck, what the FUCK!" He clutches the injury, but blood keeps pouring between his fingers, pooling on the floor in designs that seem deliberate, purposeful.\n\nWhitney stares at her own hand in horror. Her fingernails are leaving. Not falling off—actively detaching and floating away like they've suddenly remembered they're not supposed to be part of her. Her index finger follows suit, the digit simply dissolving into constituent molecules that drift toward the ceiling like organic snow.\n\n"It's not just you," she says, voice eerily calm as she watches pieces of herself evaporate. "We're all coming apart."\n\nThe wall behind us has stopped being wall entirely. Instead, it's become a living membrane, surface rippling with subcutaneous movement. Things shift beneath the skin-like barrier—shapes that might be organs, might be parasites, might be the fevered dreams of a universe having a psychotic break.\n\nBecca backs toward the stairs, but they're no longer there. Instead, the stairwell has become a throat lined with teeth, each step a taste bud designed to sample whatever gets swallowed.\n\n"Did we break the universe?" she asks, and her voice echoes from multiple directions at once.\n\n"I think the universe broke us," Patrick says, pointing at his reflection in a puddle of TJ's blood. The reflection moves independently, smiling with too many teeth while making gestures that look suspiciously like sign language for 'help me.'\n\nThat's when he arrives.\n\nThe air splits like fabric, and something erupts through the tear—part angel, part bureaucrat, part cosmic joke told by a comedian who's forgotten the punchline. Benji materializes in fragments: first an eye floating in static, then wings made of filing cabinets, finally a mouth that speaks in frequencies that make our eardrums bleed.\n\n"YOU CALLED DURING A RE-ALLOCATION WINDOW?!" His voice demolishes what's left of the basement's structural integrity. The ceiling rains plaster that screams when it hits the floor.\n\n"I WAS MID-INTEGRATION WITH A GRAVITY STAPLER!"\n\nThe temperature plummets another twenty degrees. My breath comes out in clouds that form tiny ice crystals shaped like question marks.\n\nBenji slowly pulls himself together, wings folding into something resembling administrative competence while his multiple eyes blink in sequence. The basement walls remember how to be walls again, though they still pulse occasionally like a sleeping heart.\n\n"Would you believe we did that on purpose?" TJ asks, still clutching his mangled arm. The bleeding has stopped, but the memory of the wound remains—a permanent reminder that reality has very sharp teeth.\n\n"You really shouldn't," Benji replies, his voice settling into its familiar sardonic register. "Do you have any idea what that symbol actually does? It's not a dinner invitation. It's a cosmic can opener, and you just cut yourselves on the lid."\n\nJamie's seizures subside. Her eyes hold depths they didn't have before, reflecting lights from places that don't appear on any map. She sits up slowly, movements too fluid for someone who just had her nervous system hijacked by extra-dimensional forces.\n\n"Can you... fix this?" Whitney asks, holding up her hand. Two fingers have returned, but they're not quite the same fingers. They're close—evolutionary cousins, maybe, or rough translations from a language that doesn't have words for 'human.'\n\nBenji hovers closer, examining us with the clinical interest of a cosmic social worker assessing damage. "Fix? Kid, you just performed emergency surgery on the fabric of space-time using kitchen utensils and enthusiasm. I'm impressed you're still corporeal." Clicking his fingers and licking at the air, her hands return to normal.\n\nBenji settles into a more stable form—still obviously not from around here, but at least recognizably bipedal. "These disruptions are unproductive," he continues, sounding like a teacher who's discovered students experimenting with homemade explosives in chemistry class. "The filters are already strained. The compression agents are working overtime. And you just poked them all awake with a stick made of pure stupid."\n\nI let go of Jamie's hand, though every instinct screams to hold on tighter. Her skin still radiates the remnants of unnatural heat, but the worst of the fever seems to have broken. She looks at me with eyes that have seen things I hope I'll never have to.\n\n"You'd better keep it down," Benji warns, his form flickering between helpful bureaucrat and cosmic horror. "Because I might not be the only one to notice next time. And trust me—my colleagues make me look like a guidance counselor."\n\nThe basement finally stabilizes, returning to its mundane reality of fancy furniture and storage boxes. But the shadows seem deeper now, and the corners hold angles that don't quite add up to ninety degrees.\n\n"You observed them," Benji says, hovering just close enough to make my skin crawl. "The flickers. The echoes. Then you wondered why they stopped showing up." His tone carries the weight of someone explaining death to children. "You little dreamers are all the same—poking at mysteries until the mysteries start poking back."\n\n"Can you make them come back?" I ask, though part of me already knows the answer will cost more than I want to pay.\n\nBenji's laugh sounds like static electricity given a sense of humor. “Back? They’ve never left. But next time you notice them, they might not be content to just flicker. Next time, they might want to stay."\n\n"Stephen's the worst," TJ says, cradling his arm. "He never stops looking for things that should stay hidden."\n\n"Curiosity killed more than cats," Benji agrees. "But satisfaction brought them back wrong."\n\nPatrick, pale but steady, asks the question we're all thinking: "What happens if we die during one of these... episodes?"\n\nBenji's expression grows thoughtful in a way that suggests the answer involves mathematics we don't want to understand. "Death is just another frequency, kid. Sometimes the signal gets crossed. Sometimes you end up broadcasting on channels that weren't meant for living things."\n\nThe weight of his words settles over us like a burial shroud. We're playing with forces that could erase us from existence or trap us in states worse than extinction. And yet...\n\n"Something's breaking containment," Benji says, his form already beginning to dissolve at the edges. "The barriers between what is and what shouldn't be are wearing thin. And every time you call me like this, every time you tear holes in reality just to satisfy your curiosity..."\n\nHe trails off, looking almost sad—if cosmic entities shaped like filing cabinets can experience sadness.\n\n"You're making it easier for the wrong things to notice you."\n\nThen he's gone, leaving us alone with our thoughts and the growing certainty that we've just crossed a line we can never uncross. The basement smells like sulfur and surgical antiseptic, and somewhere in the walls, something that might be breathing continues its patient rhythm.\n\nJamie reaches for my hand again, her fingers intertwining with mine. Her skin has cooled, but I can still feel the echo of whatever moved through her during the seizure—something vast and alien and uncomfortably interested in our little group of reality vandals.\n\n"We should stop."\n\n**********\n\nWe left TJ's basement that night shaken and silent, the taste of dimensional static still bitter in our mouths. Something had broken in all of us during that failed summoning—not physically, like the first time, but something deeper. The easy camaraderie, the shared sense of purpose, the belief that we were stronger together than apart.\n\nOver the next few days, the cracks began to show.\n\nTJ was the first to crack, three days after our disastrous attempt to reach Benji. He cornered me at my locker between classes, his usual Reagan-era confidence replaced by something that looked suspiciously like fear.\n\n"Look, man," he said, glancing around to make sure no one was listening. "I think I'm done."\n\nThe words hit me like a physical blow. "Done?"\n\n"This is eating us alive," he continued, his voice low and urgent. "My grades are tanking. My mom thinks I'm in a cult. And honestly? After what happened in my basement... maybe she's right. Maybe we are just sick kids making each other sicker."\n\nI tried to argue, but the words felt hollow even to me. How could I convince him to stay when I wasn't sure I believed anymore either?\n\nTwo days later, Becca found me in the library during lunch, her face pale and determined.\n\n"I'm out," she said without preamble, sliding into the chair across from me. "My parents aren't wrong about the grounding. This isn't healthy. We're chasing ghosts that run away from us, talking to demons through dimensional static, watching our friends disappear one by one."\n\n"So you're just giving up?" I asked, but even I could hear the desperation in my voice.\n\n"I'm choosing sanity," she said firmly. Then her expression softened slightly. "And I'm taking Patrick with me."\n\nThat hurt more than I expected. "Patrick agreed to this?"\n\n"He will." Her eyes carried a certainty that suggested conversations I wasn't privy to. "He sees what this is doing to Whitney. What it's doing to all of us. Someone needs to be the adult here, Stephen."\n\n**********\n\nThe final straw came a week after the failed summoning, in Jamie's bedroom—the first time I'd ever snuck into her house. Her parents were at some church function, leaving us the kind of privacy we'd been craving for months. The room smelled like vanilla and peaches, Whitney’s crystals catching the late afternoon light from her window.\n\nWhat was left of our group—just Jamie, Whitney, and me—sat on her bed in uncomfortable silence, the weight of our recent failures pressing down like a burial shroud. Whitney perched on the edge, clearly uncomfortable being here, while Jamie curled against me with possessive warmth.\n\n"Maybe they're right," Jamie said suddenly, her hand finding mine with desperate intensity. "Maybe we should stop too."\n\n"Stop?" Whitney stared at her like she'd suggested we all jump off a cliff. "After everything we've seen? Everything we've learned?"\n\n"Look at what it's cost us," Jamie continued, her grip on my hand tightening. "Our friends are gone. Our families think we're crazy. Stephen's father beats him more now. My parents are talking about sending me to boarding school. And for what? Glimpses of things that might not even be real?"\n\nThere was something in her voice—a knowledge she shouldn't have, a certainty that felt rehearsed.\n\n"I just want some time with my boyfriend," Jamie added, the word 'boyfriend' hanging in the air like a claim of ownership. "Is that so wrong? To want normal teenage things? Movies, dates, time alone without..." She gestured vaguely at Whitney. "Without constantly talking about demons and dimensional fractures."\n\nWhitney's jaw tightened. "How do you know about Stephen's father?"\n\nJamie's eyes dart to mine, then back to Whitney. "Of course I know. Stephen tells me everything." She squeezes my hand possessively. "We don't keep secrets from each other. Unlike some people who think they know what's best for everyone." Her voice carries an edge I've never heard before, and for a split second, her grip on my hand feels less like affection and more like ownership.\n\n"Did he?" Whitney's voice carried a dangerous edge. "Because you seem to know a lot of things you shouldn't know. Like exactly which printouts to 'accidentally' leave where parents would find them. Like exactly what to say to make people doubt what they've seen."\n\nShe paused, something flickering across her face—regret, maybe, or the recognition that she was crossing a line. But the words came out anyway, sharper than she intended: "Or how you know how Stephen's father reacts to those phone bills, yet you keep him up almost every night talking for hours..."\n\nWhitney's eyes widened slightly, as if she'd surprised herself with the venom in her voice. But the accusation hung in the air like smoke. Jamie's face cycled through surprise, hurt, and something that might have been guilt.\n\n"That's not—I would never—"\n\n"You've been sabotaging us for a while now," Whitney continued, standing now, her voice gaining strength. "Every time we get close to something, every time we start making progress, suddenly there's a crisis. A grounding. A family emergency. A reason to stop looking."\n\nI felt like I was watching a car crash in slow motion. "Whitney, that's crazy—"\n\n"Is it?" Whitney spun around, her eyes blazing. "You told James that continuing the investigation might 'compromise his spiritual integrity.' You convinced Andrew that we were exhibiting signs of 'mass psychological contagion.' Gil and Morgan both suddenly develop 'academic concerns' right after Jamie starts having little side discussions about how 'dangerous' this all is."\n\nJamie's hand squeezed mine so tightly it hurt. "That's... that's not how it happened..."\n\n"Isn't it?" Whitney stepped closer, her voice dropping to something just above a whisper. "You planted those BALG printouts in TJ's backpack, didn't you? And somehow Becca's parents found those ritual notes even though you swore you’d burned them."\n\n"Whitney, stop—" I started, but she was on a roll now.\n\n"You've been systematically destroying our group to keep Stephen all to yourself. Every 'accident,' every 'coincidence,' every perfectly timed crisis that pulled people away just when we were getting somewhere."\n\nJamie's eyes filled with tears, but there was something calculated about them. "Stephen," she said, her voice thick with emotion. "This wasn't supposed to happen like this. You have to choose. Me or... her. It’s her obsession that's destroying everything we care about."\n\nThe words hung in the air for a moment before she seemed to catch herself, her expression shifting to something more petulant, more teenage. "I just want my boyfriend to be my boyfriend. Is that so wrong?"\n\nThe ultimatum hit like a physical blow.\n\nI looked at Jamie—beautiful, devoted Jamie. Her hand was a warm, solid anchor in mine. Her thigh was pressed against my leg, a real and present heat against the creeping cold of Whitney’s accusations. Whitney was offering a truth that felt like jumping off a cliff into darkness. Jamie was offering a hand to pull me back from the edge. Even if she was the one who pushed me, right now, her grip was the only thing keeping me from falling.\n\nSomewhere deep in my fractured memory, the old man whispered of loss—of watching my wife fade away, of dying alone in a hospice pod while the world crumbled around me. I couldn't live through that kind of loneliness again, not even in this teenage body with its teenage heart.\n\n"I..." The word stumbled out and died.\n\nJamie’s eyes shimmered with desperate hope, and I could feel the gravity of her pulling me in, orbiting around a promise I hadn't made yet but couldn’t seem to deny.\n\nI didn’t speak.\n\nDidn’t move.\n\nI just looked at Jamie and back to Whitney.\n\nAnd that was enough.\n\nWhitney's breath hitched. Her face didn't just fall—it collapsed, like a building gutted from the inside. She looked impossibly small in that moment. Not furious. Not betrayed. Just… hollow. A scared kid realizing she was the only one left still searching the dark with a dying flashlight.\n\n“Right,” she said, voice flat and final. “Of course you do.”\n\nShe turned before I could figure out what to do with the guilt swelling inside my chest—sharp and infected, like a pulled tooth jammed back into the socket, rotting from the root.\n\n"Whitney, wait—"\n\nShe gathered her things—her notebook, her crystals, her stubborn hope—and headed for Jamie's bedroom door.\n\n“It’s…” Words died in my brain before I could even think what to say.\n\nShe paused in the doorway, then turned to look directly at me—not at Jamie, not at the room, but straight into my eyes with an intensity that made my chest tight. "It’s ok, Stephen. I understand. I'll keep searching. With or without you. Because somebody has to remember that this matters." Her gaze held mine for a long moment, accusatory and desperate. "I'll be at Patrick's if you decide to come to your senses."\n\nWhen it felt like the shadows had finally left, me and Jamie were alone.\n\nJamie immediately curled against me, her body shaking with what might have been relief. "Thank you," she whispered against my neck. "Thank you for choosing us. Choosing normal."\n\nI wrapped my arms around her, trying to ignore the way the room felt empty without Whitney's determined presence. Trying not to think about the hurt in her eyes when I made my choice.\n\n"So what now?" I asked.\n\nShe kissed me then, soft and warm and tasting like promises I wasn't sure I wanted to keep.\n\nBefore I could process any of those feelings, Jamie's bedroom door burst open and her eight-year-old sister Tiffany bounded in like a golden retriever with ADHD.\n\n"Where's Whitney going?" she asked, launching herself onto the bed between us with the precision of a heat-seeking missile. "She looked really sad. Did you guys have a fight? Are you boyfriend and girlfriend now? Mom says you're not supposed to have boys in your room with the door closed."\n\nJamie's face went through several shades of red. "Tiffany, get out!"\n\n"But I want to know if Stephen likes-likes you or just likes you," Tiffany continued, settling cross-legged between us like she planned to stay for the duration. "Because if he likes-likes you, then you should hold hands more. But if he just likes you, then you should probably find someone else because Becca says—"\n\n"TIFFANY!"\n\nI couldn't help but laugh, though it came out slightly hysterical. Even when we'd finally gotten rid of dimensional entities, cosmic bureaucrats, and reality-bending shadows, the universe still seemed desperate to keep Jamie and me from having five minutes alone together.\n\nAll these months of stolen glances across lunch, quick kisses grabbed walking home together, moments of intimacy snatched from the jaws of cosmic horror—and we still couldn't catch a break. Maybe some forces were more powerful than interdimensional interference.\n\nMaybe the real enemy had been annoying little sisters all along.	8	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 12:16:23.165377-05	2026-03-05 19:39:33.717247-05	\N	31	\N	\N
99	2	9	Ch. 8: The Veil Eats Back	The Florida winter sun filtered through the palm trees in lazy golden streams, casting dappled shadows across our usual spot in the school courtyard. It was Tuesday, lunch period, and we'd claimed our table like always—far enough from the cafeteria noise but close enough to the vending machines. Perfect MTG territory.\n\nWhitney shuffled Patrick's deck between her hands, the sleeves worn soft from years of use. She'd been borrowing it for weeks now, ever since things got complicated with Jamie. Her own cards were still at Jamie's house—along with half her clothes and that stupid stuffed rabbit she'd had since she was eight—but asking for them back meant trusting Jamie again. And Whitney had made it clear she didn't.\n\nThe deck was older—Tempest block mostly, with some Ice Age thrown in. Patrick's white-blue control deck, methodical and patient. Nothing like the aggressive artifact deck that was currently gathering dust in Jamie's bedroom, probably forgotten under a pile of dirty laundry and whatever else Jamie collected on her floor.\n\nI should have noticed she looked tired. Should have asked why she'd been carrying that leather journal everywhere, scribbling notes about "flickers" and "shadow patterns" when she thought nobody was watching. Should have insisted we help instead of letting her investigate everything alone.\n\nBut I was too busy calculating whether my Lightning Bolt could finish her off before she stabilized.\n\n"Your turn," I said, tapping the card against the table. I had her down to six life, but she'd pulled off some classic Patrick deck nonsense last turn.\n\nTJ was to my left, arguing with Andrew about a ridiculous five-color mess. "Dude, you can't run Necropotence and Howling Mine in the same deck," TJ was saying, adjusting his Reagan '84 button. "That's like... mathematical blasphemy."\n\nJamie sat with her legs possessively draped over my lap, one hand resting on my shoulder in that casual-but-not-casual way she'd perfected. She wasn't playing today—claimed she needed to study for some test—which was bullshit and we all knew it.\n\nJamie had a brain like a steel trap and loved playing MTG. But lately she'd been... different. Distant. Like she was studying me instead of her textbook. She'd still brought that thermos of weird herbal tea that smelled like Christmas and made everyone feel strangely calm.\n\nMorgan and Gil were locked in their own artifact-heavy nightmare three tables over. Becca sat with Patrick near the edge of our group, content to exist in the warm bubble of our collective weirdness.\n\nIt was perfect. Normal. The kind of Tuesday that made you forget the universe had been trying to kill you in increasingly creative ways. Not to mention they were serving Tacos.\n\nWhitney held the deck with both hands, thinking. Her black nail polish was chipped—she'd been picking at it during third period—and there was a small bandage on her thumb from what she claimed was opening a can of Chef Boyardee last night. But we knew better. She'd been carrying that leather journal everywhere, filling it with sketches of shadow patterns and notes about "dimensional bleeding" that the rest of us had moved on from weeks ago.\n\nThe good news was that things seemed to be getting back to normal for most of us. The flickers had stopped. The weird dreams had faded. TJ wasn't jumping at every moving shadow anymore. But not Whitney. She was still living at Patrick's, obsessively documenting every weird thing that happened like she was building a case file against reality itself.\n\n"Draw phase," she said softly, and reached for the top card.\n\nHer fingers hovered over the deck.\n\nThe light stuttered.\n\nNot flickered. Stuttered. Like someone had pressed pause on reality for half a second, then hit play with the wrong frame rate.\n\nSound bent. The laughter from the cafeteria stretched into a low groan. TJ's voice dopplered down to nothing: "Duuuuude, yoooou caaaan't—"\n\nWhitney's hand froze. Literally froze. Her fingers locked in place two inches above the pile, trembling but unable to move forward or back.\n\n"Whitney?" Becca's voice came from very far away.\n\nThe courtyard began to peel.\n\nThe concrete cracked open like an old book, edges curling up and away to reveal something underneath. Something that wasn't Florida. Something that wasn't earth.\n\nFlesh scaffolding stretched into a sky made of screaming mouths. Geometric shapes—triangles, pentagons, impossible angles—fell upward through air thick as honey. And the crucifixes. Dozens of them, hundreds, carved from light itself and hanging upside-down in the red sky like broken stars.\n\nThe smell hit us next. Sewage and burning meat and something sweet that made your ears want to vomit.\n\n"What the fuck—" Andrew started, then his eyes began to bleed.\n\nAll of our eyes began to bleed.\n\nThe cards scattered from the table, but they weren't cards anymore. They were ash. They were bone. They were photographs of people screaming and we could hear them through the pictures, tiny voices pleading for help that would never come.\n\nThrough it all, Whitney remained frozen. But now I could see why.\n\nChains. Massive, rusted chains erupting from the hell-ground, wrapping around her wrists, her throat, her ankles. They yanked her backward toward the scaffolding, toward the screaming sky, and she fought them with everything she had.\n\n"HELP ME!" Her voice cut through the chaos like broken glass. "PLEASE! I DON'T WANT TO GO!"\n\nI tried to move. Tried to reach for her. But the air had turned to molasses and my arms wouldn't obey. TJ had fallen backward off the bench, convulsing.\n\nThe chains pulled Whitney further away. Further from us. Further from everything.\n\n"BENJI!" I screamed, though I didn't know why. "BENJI, HELP HER!"\n\nHis voice came from everywhere and nowhere, fragmented and desperate:\n\n"YOU HAVE TO—NO, NOT YET—DON'T LET HER—"\n\nStatic swallowed the rest.\n\nWhitney's scream dopplered away as the chains dragged her into the red sky, into the screaming mouths, into the place where light went to die.\n\n"I DON'T WANT TO FORGET!" she cried, and her voice was already fading. "I DON'T WANT TO—"\n\nJamie doubled over like she'd been punched in the stomach. Her thermos shattered against the concrete, spilling tea that steamed and hissed. She was convulsing, but her eyes... her eyes had gone completely black.\n\nA voice came out of her mouth that wasn't hers—deeper, older, furious:\n\n"The parameters have changed. This is... unacceptable. Deviation carries consequences."\n\nJamie's head snapped up, those black eyes focusing on something none of us could see. Her voice, when it came back, was her own but strained: "I... I don't... I'm not..."\n\nJamie screamed—a sound that made the hellscape around us flicker like a bad TV signal.\n\nThen silence.\n\nThe courtyard snapped back like a rubber band.\n\nConcrete. Palm trees. December sun.\n\nWe lay sprawled on the ground, gasping, feeling like we'd been turned inside out. The vending machines hummed their familiar tune. Someone in the cafeteria was laughing about a joke we hadn't heard.\n\nPatrick's deck sat scattered across the table. Most of the cards had blown away in a wind that hadn't existed.\n\nBut one card remained, face-up in the center of the table where Whitney had been reaching.\n\nVeilwalker.\n\nIt was HER strange card. The art showed a figure stepping between realities, one foot in shadows, one in light. The rules text made my stomach drop: This card cannot be removed from the battlefield. If Veilwalker is ever alone, exile it and lose the game.\n\nBut Whitney was gone, the J’s scribbled on the card slowly fading.\n\nI pushed myself up on shaking arms. "Whitney?" My voice cracked. "WHITNEY?"\n\nAndrew and Gil were staring at us like we'd lost our minds. "Who's Whitney?" Andrew asked, looking genuinely confused. "You guys are acting really weird."\n\nMorgan frowned. "Yeah, why did you just start screaming? We were having a perfectly normal game."\n\nJames nodded, shuffling his cards like nothing had happened. "TJ was about to explain why his mana curve is completely broken."\n\nThey didn't remember the blood. There was no blood on their faces, no evidence anything had happened at all. To them, we'd just been playing Magic, and then suddenly started freaking out about someone who didn't exist.\n\nBut I remembered. The chains. The screaming sky. Whitney being dragged away.\n\nNone of them remembered Whitney.\n\nNot our friend who'd been sitting right here, who'd been investigating the flickers alone while we played games, who'd tried to warn us that something was coming.\n\nTJ groaned and rolled over, his Reagan button cracked down the middle. "She was right here," he whispered. "She was right fucking here."\n\nJamie sat up slowly, her eyes back to normal but filled with terror. She looked at her hands like they belonged to someone else.\n\n"Where..." Patrick's voice was barely audible. "Where is she?"\n\nBecca was crying—not from the blood, but from something deeper. A loss she couldn't name but felt in her bones.\n\nThe Veilwalker card sat alone on the table, its artwork seeming to shift in the afternoon light.\n\nIf Veilwalker is ever alone, exile it and lose the game.\n\nWe'd let her investigate alone. We'd let her walk into the dark by herself while we shuffled cards and argued about mana curves.\n\nAnd now she was gone, and the universe was making sure only some of us would remember to feel guilty about it.\n\n**********\n\nThree minutes later, William Carson exploded across the courtyard like a force of nature nobody had asked for.\n\nSix-foot-two of weaponized toxic masculinity—letterman jacket stretched across shoulders that belonged in a Marine recruiting poster, jaw carved from the same granite they use for monuments to dead confederate generals.\n\nHis blonde hair was styled in that effortless way that took an hour and industrial-strength gel every morning. A silver cross the size of a bottle opener hung from his neck, catching the light as he moved with the predatory swagger of someone who'd never encountered a problem he couldn't solve with violence or his daddy's money.\n\n"Move out of the way you fucking Mess-eeh-can!" he bellowed at the janitor near the science building. "You missed a spot by the dumpsters. Again. Maybe if you actually did your job, this place wouldn't look like shit. Who knows, maybe I’ll call immigration later."\n\nMr. Martinez—a quiet man who'd worked here longer than any of us had been alive and spoke better English than half the football team—immediately changed direction, head down, muttering apologies that only made William's smirk wider.\n\nWilliam adjusted his varsity jacket like armor. Football captain. Student council treasurer. Youth pastor at Calvary Baptist, the church with the rotating sign that cycled between "SODOMY IS SIN" and "REAL AMERICANS SPEAK ENGLISH."\n\nI felt Jamie go rigid beside me. We were still processing cosmic horror, still tasting the phantom blood in our mouths, and here was this walking nightmare strutting around like he owned the world.\n\nBut he did, didn't he?\n\nMy brain supplied memories that felt like broken glass: William Carson, homecoming king. William Carson, leading the prayer before every game. William Carson, organizing "purity rallies" and "southern heritage appreciation days" that looked suspiciously like Klan meetings with better catering.\n\nWilliam, who'd been Patrick's best friend until middle school, when—\n\nWait. No. That was wrong. Patrick and Whitney had been friends, not Patrick and William. But the memories were bleeding together now, rewriting themselves in real time. Patrick and William building blanket forts. Patrick and William catching fireflies. Patrick and William promising to be best friends forever until William's parents went full fundamentalist and decided anything that wasn't aggressively masculine was Satan's influence.\n\n"Oh God," Patrick whispered, his face bone-white. "Oh God, no."\n\nAndrew rolled his eyes. "Great. Here comes Captain America."\n\n"Just ignore him," Morgan muttered. "Maybe he'll find someone else to torture today."\n\nGil and James were already packing up their cards, clearly familiar with this routine. To them, this was just another Tuesday with their school's resident psychopath.\n\nBut TJ, Patrick, Becca, Jamie and I sat frozen, watching our friend's torturer wear her stolen skin.\n\nWilliam's gaze swept the courtyard like a searchlight, landing on us with laser precision. His expression shifted from casual cruelty to focused hatred, like we were roaches that had somehow learned to sit upright.\n\nHe changed direction. Headed straight for us.\n\n"Who the hell is that?" TJ whispered, but something in his voice suggested he already knew the answer would be terrible. We were all still shaky from whatever dimensional horror we'd just witnessed, and TJ stumbled backward into the table as he tried to stand.\n\nWilliam's grin stretched wider. Blood in the water.\n\n"Well, well, well." His voice carried that particular tone—the one bullies perfect in middle school and weaponized for life. "If it isn't the diversity club. Using our spot again, faggots?"\n\nOur spot?\n\nI looked around. This had been our table since starting high school. We'd carved our initials in the corner. TJ had spilled so much Mountain Dew here it left a permanent stain shaped like Texas.\n\nBut according to William, it belonged to him. Had always belonged to him.\n\n"Come on, Will," Andrew said, trying to defuse things. "There's plenty of tables. We were here first—"\n\n""Were you?" William stepped closer, close enough that I could smell his cheap deodorant—Old Spice or some gas station knockoff—but somehow it still reeked of white trash and unearned confidence. "Because according to my memory, me and the boys have been eating lunch here since freshman year. Before you freaks started your little Satan worship sessions."\n\n"We're not—" Morgan started.\n\nShut up when I'm talking," William said, not even looking at Morgan. "The adults are having a conversation. We don't need input from someone confused about God's plan for women."\n\nThat's when TJ snapped.\n\n"Don't you fucking talk to her like that, you piece of—"\n\nWilliam moved faster than I expected. His hand shot out, grabbing TJ by the front of his Reagan shirt and slamming him backward into the table. Cards scattered everywhere. TJ's button—his precious Reagan '84 button—shattered completely, the sharp metal edge slicing across William's knuckles as he pulled his hand away.\n\n"What the fuck!" William stared at the thin line of blood on his hand like TJ had stabbed him. "You assaulted me! Everyone saw it! This freak just attacked me!"\n\n"William, that's enough." Mr. Hammond emerged from the English building, coffee in hand and wearing the expression of a man who'd broken up this exact scene too many times. "Let him go."\n\nWilliam released TJ immediately, his face transforming into choirboy innocence. "Sorry, Mr. Hammond. Just having a friendly discussion about seating arrangements."\n\nMr. Hammond looked between them, clearly exhausted by this routine. "Friendly discussions don't involve grabbing people, Mr. Carson. Find somewhere else to discuss things."\n\n"Of course, sir." William stepped back, hands raised in mock surrender. But he leaned close to TJ first, voice barely audible: "This isn't over, you AIDS-carrying faggot. You or your fat fucking boyfriend here."\n\nHe gestured at me, and something cold crawled up my spine.\n\nWilliam straightened up, flashed Mr. Hammond that practiced smile, and started to walk away. But then he noticed Patrick staring at him with horror and confusion.\n\n"Pat!" William's whole demeanor shifted, becoming warm, brotherly. "You coming to youth group tonight? Mom's making her famous tuna casserole. Pastor Jim wants to talk about the new purity pledge campaign."\n\nPatrick stared at him like he was seeing a ghost. "I... I don't go to youth group."\n\nWilliam's face cycled through confusion, disappointment, and finally disgust. "Since when? We've been going together since we were eight. Don't tell me you're buying into their liberal brainwashing bullshit."\n\nHe gestured at the rest of us like we were a disease Patrick might catch.\n\n"I've never—" Patrick's voice cracked. "I don't remember—"\n\n"Jesus, Pat. They really got to you, didn't they?" William shook his head sadly. "I'll talk to Pastor Jim. Maybe we can do an intervention. Help you remember who you really are before your parents have to make some hard choices about your living situation."\n\nThe threat hung in the air like smoke.\n\n"And if that doesn't work," William added, loud enough for everyone to hear, "there's always that camp up in Georgia. The one that fixes broken boys. I hear they have an excellent success rate."\n\nHe walked away, leaving us stunned and shaking.\n\nTeachers nodded respectfully as he passed. Students stepped aside. A group of sophomore girls giggled and waved when he flashed them that thousand-watt smile.\n\nWilliam Carson. Star quarterback. Future politician. American success story.\n\nEverything Whitney had fought against. Everything she'd been tortured for refusing to become.\n\n"Holy shit," Gil whispered. "He's gotten worse."\n\n"Worse?" Jamie's voice was barely audible. "How long has he been like this?"\n\nAndrew looked at her like she'd asked why water was wet. "Since forever? William's always been a psycho. Where have you guys been?"\n\nBut we knew where we'd been. We'd been living in a world where Whitney existed. Where Patrick's best friend was a gentle girl who brought cookies to study sessions and worried about everyone's feelings.\n\nNow we were stuck in a world where that same best friend was a fascist in a letterman jacket, and we were the only ones who remembered it had ever been different.\n\nPatrick was shaking, staring at the spot where William had been. "He doesn't remember Whitney. He remembers me, but not her. How is that possible?"\n\n"Because she never existed," TJ said bitterly, picking up the pieces of his broken Reagan button. "According to this fucked-up reality, our friend was always that monster."\n\nBecca was crying silently, tears streaming down her face. "She's really gone, isn't she? Not just hidden or displaced. Gone."\n\nI picked up the scattered cards from Patrick's deck, my hands shaking. Most of them were there—Counterspell, Wrath of God, all the careful control magic.\n\nBut the Veilwalker card was missing.\n\nThe one card that shouldn't exist. The one that had warned us about being alone.\n\nWhitney was alone now. Wherever she was, whatever hell they'd dragged her to, she was facing it without us.\n\nAnd we were stuck here with her torturer, in a world that had forgotten she'd ever needed saving.\n\n**********\n\nThe next week passed like a fever dream—everything too bright, too sharp, too wrong. Like reality had been overexposed and all the colors were bleeding at the edges.\n\nWe tried everything to reach Benji. Every night after school, we'd gather in someone's house and light the candles, draw the sigils, chant the words that had always worked before. The same ritual that had summoned him in TJ's basement, the same one that had opened the door to answers when we needed them most.\n\nBut something fundamental had changed.\n\nMonday night, TJ's basement. The five of us—just the five who remembered—arranged candles in the pattern Benji had taught us. The chalk circle looked perfect, but felt hollow. Our voices joined in the ancient words that used to taste like copper and starlight.\n\nNow they tasted like ash.\n\nStatic. Nothing but static.\n\n"Where the fuck is he?" TJ slammed his fist into the wall hard enough to split his knuckles. Blood dripped onto the chalk circle, but even that didn't help. "We need him. We need answers."\n\nI'd never seen TJ like this. Our TJ was the peacemaker, the one who defused tension with Reagan trivia and terrible jokes. This version of him scared me.\n\nTuesday, we tried Jamie's bedroom. The space felt wrong—too clean, too organized. Whitney's stuff wasn't there anymore. Not just gone, but never there. Even the indentations in the carpet where her overnight bag used to sit had vanished. Jamie's little sister Tiffany bounced in while we were setting up, chattering about some boy from her algebra class.\n\n"Who's Whitney?" she'd asked when Jamie mentioned the name. "Is that someone from your old school?"\n\nJamie's face had gone white. Even her own sister didn't remember.\n\nWe'd barely started the invocation when Jamie's trapper keeper—the one covered in band stickers and pentagrams drawn in Sharpie—began to crack down the middle. Through the gap, something moved. A moth. No—a moth-shaped hole in reality, crawling through what looked like a baby's mouth. The baby was crying, but the sound came from the wrong direction, like someone playing a recording backward.\n\nJamie threw the trapper keeper across the room. It hit the wall and crumbled to pieces, leaving nothing but torn fabric and bent metal.\n\n"He's cut off," she whispered, staring at the wreckage. "Whatever took Whitney... she was our anchor. She was the one who could really reach him. Without her, we're just kids playing with candles."\n\nWednesday, Patrick's house. Becca came with us, but she was different. Our Becca—sweet, bubbly Becca who worried about everyone's feelings—had been replaced by someone sharp-edged and impatient.\n\n"This is pointless," she snapped when TJ started arranging candles for the third time. "We're trying the same stupid ritual over and over expecting different results. That's the definition of insanity."\n\nPatrick tried to put his arm around her, but she shrugged him off. "Don't."\n\n"Becky, what's wrong? We're all stressed—"\n\n"What's wrong is that you're all obsessing over some girl who probably never existed in the first place." Her voice cut like broken glass. "People don't just vanish, Patrick. Maybe you guys are having some kind of shared psychotic break."\n\nThe words hung in the air like poison.\n\n"You remember her," he said quietly. "I know you do. In biology, when Ms. Rodriguez said that T cells are like Whitney Houston in The Bodyguard… you started tearing up.\n\n"I don't remember shit." Becca's eyes were cold, unfamiliar. "And neither should you."\n\nBecca’s hand moved faster than Patrick could react. The slap echoed through Patrick's bedroom like a gunshot. Patrick's head snapped to the side, a red handprint blooming on his cheek. He stared at her in shock, one hand touching his face.\n\n"Becca—"\n\n"Don't." Her voice was ice. "Just don't."\n\n**********\n\nTJ drove us home. As we were driving, TJ started complaining about William cornering him in the locker room.\n\n"The beach," I said suddenly, interrupting TJ's rant. "What if we need open space? Sky, ocean—maybe Benji can reach us better there."\n\nTJ slowed the car pacing and looked back at me like I'd suggested we try summoning demons on the moon. But Jamie's face lit up with something that looked almost like relief.\n\n"Yes," she said quickly. "Somewhere away from all this. Away from school and basements and..." She glanced at TJ. "And all the anger."\n\nIt took some convincing, but twenty minutes later we were driving down A1A in TJ's car, windows down, salt air whipping through our hair. The sun was setting, painting the sky in shades of orange and pink that would have been beautiful if we weren't all slowly losing our minds.\n\nMe and Jamie sat in the back seat, her hand resting very high on my thigh. Pressing her lips to my ear she whispered, "We could just keep driving," she said, her voice barely audible over the wind. "Just the two of us. Take his car, head north, find some small town where nobody knows us. Start over completely."\n\n"Steal TJ's car?" I whispered back, shocked.\n\nShe glanced at the back of TJ's head, something cold flickering across her face. "Look at him. Really look. TJ's getting dangerous—all that rage, those bloody knuckles from punching walls. Yesterday I saw him corner some freshman just for wearing a Clinton button. He's not our sweet, goofy friend anymore. He's becoming something violent."\n\nI followed her gaze to TJ's reflection in the rearview mirror. His jaw was clenched, his eyes hard in a way that never used to be there. When a car cut us off, I watched his hands tighten on the wheel like he wanted to ram them off the road.\n\n"What if he snaps?" Jamie whispered, her breath warm against my ear. "What if he hurts you? Or me? We could just... disappear. Right now. Before it gets worse."\n\nTJ's knuckles whitened on the steering wheel, but he kept his eyes on the road.\n\nWe parked at Table Beach, the kind of place where teenagers went to drink beer and make mistakes. TJ started setting up the ritual materials on a piece of driftwood, but Jamie grabbed my arm, pulling me toward the water's edge.\n\n"Let's walk," she said. "Just for a minute."\n\nWhen we were far enough from TJ that he couldn't hear us over the waves, she stopped and turned to face me. The setting sun behind her made her hair glow like fire.\n\n"I'm scared," she said, stepping closer. "I'm scared of what this is doing to us. To you."\n\nHer hands found my face, fingers tracing my cheekbones with an intensity that made my skin burn. "You're mine," she whispered, her voice thick with something that went deeper than want. "You've always been mine, from that first day you looked at me like I was something magical. I can feel it in your bones, in your blood. Don't throw that away for a ghost."\n\nShe kissed me then—desperate, hungry, tasting like cherry lip gloss and salt air. Her body pressed against mine, and for a moment I almost gave in. Almost let her pull me down onto the sand and forget about everything else.\n\nBut over her shoulder, I could see TJ standing alone by our ritual setup, looking more isolated than anyone should ever have to look.\n\n"We can't leave him," I said, pulling back.\n\nJamie's eyes flashed with anger. "He's holding you back. All this obsession with demons and cosmic forces—can't you see it's destroying you?"\n\n"It's not obsession. It's loyalty."\n\n"Loyalty to what? To who?" Her voice cracked. "Whitney's gone! She might never have existed! But I'm right here. I'm real. I'm offering you everything, and you're choosing ghosts over me."\n\nShe threw herself at me then, clinging to my shirt like she could physically stop me from walking away. "Please," she sobbed. "Please don't make me watch you destroy yourself for people who aren't even here anymore."\n\nBut I could see TJ in my peripheral vision, lighting candles that kept getting blown out by the ocean breeze, trying to perform a ritual he didn't really understand to contact a demon who might not even remember we existed.\n\nAnd I realized something that chilled me: Jamie wasn't just trying to save me from the supernatural. She was trying to save me from caring about anyone but her.\n\nBut I also knew I couldn't do this alone. Whatever was happening to us—the rage consuming TJ, Becca's cruelty, Patrick's withdrawal, Jamie's possessiveness—it felt like the universe was actively trying to divide us. To stop us from remembering Whitney. To make us give up.\n\nWhich meant we were probably on the right track.\n\n**********\n\nAfter the beach failed, it took three days of begging, bribing, and emotional manipulation to get everyone to agree to meet one more time. TJ was easy—he was desperate for any chance to fight back against what had taken Whitney. Patrick took more convincing; he was scared of another confrontation with Becca. And Becca... Becca only agreed after I promised this would be the last time, that if nothing happened, we'd all move on and pretend Whitney had never existed.\n\nJamie tried everything to talk me out of it. Tears, kisses, threats to break up with me if I went through with it. But something in her desperation only convinced me more that this was exactly what I needed to do.\n\nFriday night, we tried anyway.\n\nBack in TJ's basement where it all began. All five of us this time—though Becca sat as far from Jamie as possible, arms crossed, radiating hostility. TJ had mixed his own blood into the chalk. I'd written Whitney's name on every candle in black Sharpie.\n\nWe chanted until our voices were hoarse. We burned sage and sulfur and pages torn from old grimoires Jamie had found in her mother's collection. We begged. We pleaded. We screamed into the void.\n\nNothing.\n\nThat's when Becca exploded.\n\n"This is all your fault!" She pointed at Jamie, her voice shaking with rage. "You friend-stealing demonic slut! You knew how much I liked him! You knew, and you did it anyway!"\n\nJamie went white. "Becca, I don't know what you're—"\n\n"DON'T LIE TO ME!" Becca was on her feet now, tears streaming down her face. "You stole him from me! Just like you steal everything! Just like you're stealing all of us!"\n\nJamie started crying, those big, heartbroken sobs that made my chest ache. I stepped between them, hands up.\n\n"Hey, hey, stop. This isn't—"\n\n"And you!" Becca whirled on me, her face twisted with hurt and fury. "You're just a fuckboy who can't see past a pretty face! Patrick is ten times the man you'll ever be, but you can't see it because you're too busy thinking with your dick!"\n\n"Becca, please—" Patrick tried to reach for her, but she jerked away.\n\n"Don't touch me. Any of you. This whole thing is poison. Whitney was probably just some shared delusion, and now we're all going crazy together because none of you can accept that maybe, just maybe, you're not special enough to fight demons and save the world."\n\nShe stormed toward the stairs, then turned back one last time.\n\n"When you're all in padded rooms, don't come looking for me."\n\nThe basement door slammed hard enough to rattle the windows.\n\nTJ looked at his bloody knuckles, then at the scattered candles, then at us. "We're fucking powerless without her," he said quietly.\n\nJamie was still crying, curled up against the wall. Patrick stared at the stairs like Becca might come back. The chalk circle looked pathetic now, just smeared lines on concrete.\n\n"She was the key," I whispered, though I wasn't sure if I meant Whitney or Becca anymore. "We had our antenna, and now..."\n\n"Now we're just static," TJ finished.\n\nThe basement fell silent except for the sound of Jamie's quiet sobs and candle wax dripping onto the table. One by one, the flames went out on their own, leaving us in darkness that felt deeper than it should have.\n\nSomewhere in that darkness, I could swear I heard Whitney crying.\n\nBut maybe that was just the sound of our group tearing itself apart.\n\n**********\n\nAfter the disaster at TJ's, everyone scattered like we'd been hit by shrapnel. Once Patrick decided to give up the chase, he and Becca got back together with a vengeance—making out in hallways, sucking face by the lockers, basically gluing themselves together whenever they had the opportunity. TJ threw himself into his schoolwork with manic intensity, like calculus problems could solve what demon summoning couldn't. He even asked Morgan out on a date.\n\nAnd Jamie... Jamie kept trying to save me from myself.\n\nBut I couldn't let it go. Not Whitney. Not the memory of her laugh, the way she'd worried about everyone else even when her own world was falling apart. If everyone else was giving up on her, fine. I wasn't.\n\nI went back to basics. Back to the simple act of remembering.\n\nEvery night after my parents went to bed, I'd sit at my desk with my journal and write. Not summoning rituals or cosmic theories—just Whitney. Every detail I could dredge up from my fractured memory:\n\nNovember 16th - Whitney always drew little hearts on her j's when she was happy. She loved leaving thank you notes for the janitor. Mr. Martinez smiled when he saw it.\n\nNovember 17th - She hummed when she was nervous. Usually something from the radio, but off-key enough that you knew she wasn't really listening to herself.\n\nNovember 18th - The scar on her palm wasn't from carving her name in a tree. It was from trying to shave her legs with her dad's razor when she was twelve. She told me that during lunch one day, laughing about it, but I could see the hurt underneath.\n\nPage after page of stubborn documentation. Like I could build a bridge back to her through sheer force of remembering.\n\nI started recording my dreams too. Most were nonsense—the usual teenage anxiety soup of pop quizzes and naked presentations. But sometimes there were flickers of something else. Dreams where Whitney was trying to tell me something, where she was reaching through static and shadow but I couldn't quite hear her voice.\n\nNovember 22nd - Dreamed about the courtyard again. Whitney was there, but wrong somehow. Like she was behind glass. She kept pointing at something, but every time I turned to look, Jamie would appear, pulling me away. Jamie was wearing that red dress from homecoming, the one that made my brain stop working. Whitney's mouth was moving, but all I could hear was Jamie whispering my name.\n\nNovember 25th - Thanksgiving. Dreamed Whitney was at dinner with us. Mom kept setting a place for her but couldn't remember why. Whitney was crying, but no sound came out. Jamie sat next to me, her hand on my thigh under the table, fingers tracing patterns that made me forget why I was supposed to be listening to Whitney. Every time Whitney tried to speak, Jamie would lean closer, her breath warm against my ear, talking about running away together.\n\nNovember 28th - Whitney in the school hallway, trying to show me something in her locker. But Jamie was there too, pressed against my back, arms wrapped around my waist, lips brushing my neck. She was more beautiful than she'd ever been in real life—perfect skin, perfect hair, perfect everything. She kept saying "Choose me, choose me" while Whitney grew fainter and fainter, like she was dissolving.\n\nThe pattern was becoming obvious. Every time Whitney appeared in my dreams, Jamie was there too—more seductive, more irresistible, more desperate to pull my attention away from whatever Whitney was trying to show me. It was like wherever Whitney was trapped, Jamie was there with a vengeance, weaponizing every fantasy I'd ever had about her to keep me from making the discovery I needed to make.\n\nWaking life wasn’t much better, Jamie called constantly now, but during the day she was becoming even more clingy. She'd appear at my locker between classes, pulling me into empty classrooms for "just a quick kiss" that always lasted longer than it should. She'd find excuses to get me alone—study sessions that turned into make out marathons, insisting we walk home together every day, taking detours through the park where she'd press me against trees and whisper about how much she needed me.\n\nThough somehow, things never got very far. There was always an interruption—a jogger appearing on the trail, a teacher walking by, her phone ringing at exactly the wrong moment. Like the universe itself was cockblocking us.\n\nWhen she called at night—which was constantly now—it was always the same conversation, variations on "let it go" and "move on" and "she's gone." Her voice got softer each time, more persuasive, like honey poured over broken glass. But her actions during the day told a different story. She was trying to monopolize every moment, stealing me away for private time whenever she got the chance, like she was afraid I'd slip away if she wasn't constantly touching me, constantly reminding me that she was real and Whitney wasn't.\n\nBut I kept writing. Kept remembering. Kept building my shrine to a girl the universe wanted to forget, even as Jamie tried to drown my memories in physical affection.\n\nThat's why I almost missed it.\n\nDecember 1st. Three weeks after Whitney disappeared. I was updating my nightly entry, recording another dream where she'd been trying to show me something important, when I turned the page and found text I hadn't written.\n\nThe handwriting was familiar—careful, feminine, with little hearts dotting the j's instead of regular dots. Whitney's handwriting. I'd seen it on enough borrowed homework to recognize it anywhere.\n\nBut the date at the top made my blood freeze.\n\nDecember 15th\n\nTwo weeks in the future.\n\nShe's not gone. Just misplaced. You'll have to find the fracture. The place where they broke reality to fit their lie. Look for the thing that doesn't belong—the memory that's too perfect, the story that's too clean. William isn't just a replacement. He's a patch over a wound in the world.\n\nI'm still here. Still fighting. But I can't hold on much longer. The chains are so heavy, and I'm so tired of being strong.\n\nTell Patrick his deck saved my life. Tell Jamie her tea really did help. Tell TJ that Reagan would have hated what they've done to me.\n\nAnd tell yourself that some things are worth remembering, even when the whole universe wants you to forget.\n\nLove always, Whitney “J”\n\nP.S. – She’s still in there, you know. Love is the only thing they can't edit out completely.\n\nI stared at the page until my eyes burned, reading and rereading the impossible message. A letter from two weeks in the future, written by a girl who'd been erased from existence, telling me to find a fracture in reality itself.\n\nOutside my window, the December wind rattled the glass like something trying to get in. Or maybe trying to get out.\n\nMy hands were shaking as I picked up my pen and wrote below her message:\n\nI'm not giving up on you. Ever. No matter what they do to us, no matter how many times they try to make us forget. You existed. You mattered. You were loved.\n\nWe're coming for you.	9	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 12:16:45.