100	2	10	Ch. 9: Fracture Point	I've been tossing and turning for hours, but every time I close my eyes, I see those words scratched into my journal in handwriting that isn't mine: "She's not gone. Just misplaced. You'll have to find the fracture."\n\nTwo days since Whitney's message appeared on a page I know was blank, dated two weeks in the future, and my brain won't stop picking at it like a scab. Tell Patrick his deck saved my life. Tell Jamie her tea really did help. Except Patrick doesn't seem to care anymore, and Jamie thinks I'm losing my mind chasing ghosts that everyone else has forgotten.\n\nFinally, I give up on sleep and slip out of bed, bare feet silent on the stained carpet. The cinderblock walls of our cookie cutter 80’s Florida sub-division house carry sound like a cathedral, and Dad's passed out in his recliner just outside my bedroom door, the TV's shifting colors dancing on my foil blocked windows from the gap beneath my door.\n\nI hold my breath as the 486 boots up, that familiar electronic whine cutting through the silence like a saw blade. When he doesn't stir, I exhale slowly and settle into my chair, the modem's screech announcing another nightly seance with the digital dead.\n\nWith no idea what I’m even looking for I spiral down random rabbit holes.\n\nCASSANDRA'S SENSUAL MASSAGE - OUTCALLS ONLY.\n\nUFO SIGHTING OVER I-4 - MULTIPLE WITNESSES.\n\nLONELY? CALL 1-900-HOT-TALK.\n\nSHADOW PEOPLE IN YBOR CITY???\n\nASIAN FANTASY SEEKS GENEROUS GENTLEMAN.\n\nEach thread leads nowhere, digital detritus from people seeking connection or escape or something to fill the empty hours between midnight and dawn.\n\nI've been at this for hours. Digging through buried conversations, following breadcrumbs that crumble to nothing the moment I touch them. My journal lies open beside the keyboard, pages covered in increasingly frantic scrawl—notes about William's impossible existence, Whitney's surgical removal from reality, the shadows that seem to breed when I'm not watching.\n\nI should be sleeping. Should be letting this go, like Jamie keeps telling me to. Like the rational part of my brain screams every time I boot up this machine. But that note from Whitney, the one that appeared in my journal like a message in a bottle from a sinking ship... it won't stop scratching at the inside of my skull.\n\n"…some things are worth remembering, even when the whole universe wants you to forget”\n\nThe cursor blinks at me, mocking. Another dead end. Another BBS thread that leads nowhere. I'm about to give up, to admit that maybe Jamie's right, maybe I'm losing my mind chasing ghosts through copper cables.\n\nThen I see it.\n\nWHTNY_Jxdead.\n\nThe thread title hits me like ice water. My cursor hovers over it, trembling along with my hand. When I click, the timestamp makes my stomach drop through the floor: December 15th, 1995.\n\nToday is December 3rd.\n\nThe message sits there, glowing like a trap:\n\n"Don't go looking. But if you do… Whickham Park, west ridge. The trail you've never taken but remember anyway. Bring nothing red. Trust no one wearing your face."\n\nMy breath catches in my throat. It's her voice, threaded through those pixels like DNA through a crime scene sample. The way she fractures sentences, breaks thoughts into sharp little pieces that cut when you try to hold them. Nobody else thinks in punctuation wounds the way Whitney does.\n\nDid. Does. Whatever tense applies to someone who exists in the spaces between memory and denial.\n\nI stare at the date stamp until my eyes water. December 15th. Twelve days from now. Posted at 11:47 PM, which means... what? That she'll be alive then? That she'll have access to a computer? That time itself is just another broken toy in whatever cosmic playpen we've stumbled into?\n\nThe dot matrix printer kicks to life before I consciously decide to print. It grinds and whines like a mechanical animal in pain, each line materializing with the precision of prophecy being carved into stone. The sound echoes through the house, obscene in its volume, too much proof that impossible things can become paper and ink and real.\n\nFrom the living room, I hear Dad shift in his recliner, the leather creaking under his weight. Oh fuck. My heart hammers against my ribs as I freeze, listening for the sound of his feet hitting the floor, for the heavy footsteps that would mean another beating for "sneaking around like a little shit" when he's trying to sleep off whatever poison is keeping him upright these days. But after a moment, his breathing settles back into the rhythm of the unconscious—deep, rattling snores so loud they could wake the dead. Which, given what I'm dealing with lately, seems fitting. I exhale slowly.\n\nMy hands shake as I tear the perforated edges free. The ink is still warm, like it's been touched by fever.\n\nI need to call Jamie.\n\nI slip out of my room and ease around the corner to where the phone sits on the living room wall. Dad's still unconscious in his recliner, empty bottles scattered around him like fallen soldiers. I lift the handset with surgical precision and retreat back to my room, the spiral cord stretched to its limit as I dial her number.\n\nFour rings. Shit, what if her parents answer. Five. I'm about to hang up when her voice cuts through the line, but instead of sleep-thick confusion, there's something warm and dangerous in her tone.\n\n"Stephen?" A pause, then a soft laugh. "God, I was hoping you'd call."\n\nMy brain stutters. "You... were?"\n\n"Mmm. I've been missing your voice. There's something about these late-night calls..." Her voice drops to a whisper. "Something about knowing you're thinking about me when you should be sleeping."\n\nHeat crawls up my neck. "Jamie, I—"\n\n"I love when you call like this. Like you can't help yourself." There's rustling on her end, like she's shifting in bed. "What are you wearing?"\n\nThe question I asked her earlier bounces back like a boomerang, and suddenly the printout in my hand feels like it weighs nothing compared to the weight of her attention. "I... uh..."\n\nShe laughs again, softer this time. "You're cute when you're speechless. Tell me what you're thinking about."\n\nThis is it. This is the moment I should put the BBS message aside, should focus on the girl who's practically offering herself to me. But Whitney's words burn in my peripheral vision, and the rational part of my brain that Jamie keeps appealing to chooses the worst possible moment to malfunction.\n\n"I found something," I say, the words tumbling out before I can stop them. "On the BBS. A message from Whitney."\n\nThe silence stretches so long I wonder if the line's gone dead. When she speaks again, the warmth has drained from her voice like water from a broken glass.\n\n"Stephen."\n\n"I know how it sounds—"\n\n"Do you?" Her voice sharpens, all traces of seduction evaporating. "Because it sounds like you'd rather chase dead girls than talk to the living one who's practically throwing herself at you."\n\nThe accusation hits like a slap. "That's not... I didn't mean..."\n\n"We talked about this. After the journal, after the rituals that no one but us remember. You promised you'd let this go." I can hear her moving, probably sitting up in bed, the moment between us crumbling like ash.\n\n"This message, it's dated next week. She's telling us about some place in Whickham Park—"\n\n"Stop." The word cuts through the line like a blade. "Just... stop. Please."\n\nThere's a pause, and when she speaks again, her voice carries a different kind of weight. "Stephen, if you drop this right now, if you promise me you'll stop chasing shadows and focus on what's real... I'll give you something. Something I've been saving. Something sacred."\n\nMy mouth goes dry. The way she says 'sacred' makes it sound like an offering, a ritual exchange. "Jamie..."\n\n"My parents are going out of town next weekend with my sister. And I've been thinking... about boundaries. About crossing them." Her voice wavers slightly, vulnerability threading through the anger.\n\nThe printout crinkles in my grip. Whitney's impossible message versus Jamie's very real, very immediate sacrifice. The choice should be obvious.\n\nBut I made a promise to Whitney—to her memory, to whatever fragment of her is still fighting somewhere in the spaces between reality's cracks. I swore I'd find the fracture, and vows like that aren't something you break for convenience, no matter what they cost you.\n\n"The west ridge," I say quietly, reading from the printout. "Behind Whickham Park. You know where that is, don't you?"\n\nAnother long pause. When she speaks, her voice carries a different strategy—honey instead of vinegar. "You know, we could just sneak off into the woods at cross country practice. I think I know of a place that might afford us enough privacy to... explore things. Just the two of us. Forget about messages and ghosts and focus on something real."\n\nThe offer hangs between us like forbidden fruit, but I've already made my choice. "This message is really strange, Jamie. What if she's really trying to reach us?"\n\n"Then she's dead, Stephen." Her voice hardens again, the seduction cracking like a mask. "And dead people don't send messages through computer networks. They just stay dead, and the people who love them learn to move on."\n\n"If we don't find anything there, I'll drop it. I promise. But we could just pop over during Cross Country practice tomorrow. No harm, no foul."\n\n"Swear to me. Swear on something that matters to you that this is the last of it. That after tomorrow, you'll let the dead stay dead."\n\nMy throat feels tight. "I swear."\n\n"Good." She pauses, and when she speaks again, her voice drops to that whisper that makes my skin prickle. "When we find nothing, you're going to owe me. Big." Her voice drops to that whisper that makes my skin prickle. "And I have very specific ideas about how you can pay that debt that don’t involve any clothes.”\n\nHeat crawls up my neck. "Jamie..."\n\n"Shh. Do you hear that?" There's rustling on her end, then a muffled voice in the distance. "Shit, that's my mom. She must be getting water or something."\n\n"You should go."\n\n"Yeah, I should." Another pause, then her voice drops to barely audible. "Stephen? I've been naked this whole time we've been talking. And there are much better ways to spend a phone call when someone is... clothing impaired." She lets that hang in the air for a heartbeat. "When you're lying in bed tonight, trying not to think about dead girls... Think about what you could have been doing with this conversation instead."\n\nThe line goes dead before I can respond, leaving me alone with the dial tone and the slow realization that breathing has become manual labor. My underwear feels suddenly, uncomfortably restrictive.\n\nAs sleep finally claims me, a smell drifts through my room, of vanilla and peaches, sweet enough to make my mouth water despite everything. Through the gap in my curtains, something moves between the streetlights. I don't look directly at it, but I can feel it watching, patient and knowing.\n\nTomorrow, I'll hunt for ghosts in broad daylight.\n\nTonight, I dream of shadows that smell like forbidden fruit.\n\n**********\n\nThe Florida heat hits us like a wet slap the moment we step onto the track. It's that special kind of afternoon hell where the asphalt shimmers and the air tastes like melted rubber and teenage desperation. The smell hits me in waves—aged crotch sweat baked into decades old polyester uniforms, the sharp tang of fear-sweat that's different from exercise-sweat, and underneath it all, the earthy rot of the swamp that borders Whickham Park.\n\nCoach Henderson stands at the edge of the track, swaying slightly in a way that has nothing to do with the heat. His clipboard hangs limp in one hand, a thermos that definitely doesn't contain water in the other. His eyes are glassy, focused somewhere beyond us, beyond the track, probably beyond this decade.\n\n"Alright, you little shits," he slurs, not bothering to look at us directly. "Warm-up laps, then some... some knee-raises or whatever. Then trail running. You know the drill."\n\nHe waves vaguely toward the woods behind the school, then shuffles toward his favorite park bench—the one positioned perfectly in the shade where he can pretend to supervise while nursing whatever poison is keeping him upright.\n\nJamie presses against my side, her body vibrating with nervous energy. Her skin is already damp with sweat, but there's something else in the tension of her muscles, the way her red-polished fingernails dig into my arm. She's wearing those characteristic red Converse high-tops that she swears bring her luck, and her red JanSport backpack bounces against her hip as we start moving. The color should trigger alarm bells—Whitney's message specifically said bring nothing red—but when Jamie leans close, her breath warm against my ear, coherent thought becomes a luxury I can't afford.\n\n"You know," she whispers, "we could always just disappear into the woods together. Forget about messages and ghosts and focus on something more... immediate."\n\nHeat crawls up my neck despite the Florida humidity already doing its best to melt me. "Jamie, the message said—"\n\n"Forget the message." Her fingers trace patterns on my chest that make my brain stutter. "I keep dreaming about someone too, you know. But in my dreams, he's telling me to stop looking. To focus on what's real." She presses closer. "To focus on what's right in front of me."\n\nTJ falls into step beside us, his Reagan '84 shirt already darkening with sweat. He fidgets with his calculator watch, the ancient LCD display flickering in the harsh sunlight. "Anyone else think Coach is getting worse? Like, professionally speaking? I'm pretty sure that thermos is just straight vodka at this point."\n\nBehind us, Becca and Patrick run close enough that their arms brush with each stride. They're having one of their wordless conversations—the kind where entire novels pass between glances. Patrick's coolness never wavers, even in this heat, while Becca keeps stealing looks at us with something that might be concern or might be curiosity.\n\nThe printout burns in my pocket like a secret I can't keep much longer.\n\n"We need to go somewhere new on the trails," I say quietly, just loud enough for our group to hear. "Today. Now."\n\n"Go where?" TJ pants, already looking winded despite our glacial pace.\n\nI pull out the crumpled paper, smoothing it against my chest as we jog. "Whitney left us a message. On the BBS. From next week."\n\nThat stops everyone except Patrick, who keeps running for another twenty yards with before realizing he's alone.\n\n"What the fuck does that mean?" TJ whispers, glancing toward Coach Henderson, who's now sprawled on his bench like a passed-out drunk at a bus stop.\n\nJamie's grip on my arm tightens, but not in the good way. "Stephen, we talked about this—"\n\n"Just listen," I say, and read them Whitney's words, my voice barely audible over the sound of our breathing and the distant hum of traffic. When I finish, the silence stretches like a held breath.\n\n"The west ridge," Becca says finally. "Behind the park. There's... nothing back there. It just goes straight into Wickham Road."\n\nPatrick's eyes go distant. “I... there was a trailer. White and broken. But that's impossible. We've never...” He shakes his head violently. “Someone else's memories. They're not mine.”\n\nJamie's red fingernails dig deeper into my arm. "The fucking road is back there, and nothing else." She turns to me, eyes bright with something that might be excitement or might be fear. "But Stephen, we could just…I know a better place—north of the trails, lots of shade, private..." Her voice drops to that whisper that makes my skin prickle. "We could slip away somewhere just the two of us..."\n\nI try to focus on the message, on Whitney's warning about bringing nothing red, but Jamie's thumb traces circles on my wrist and my brain short-circuits.\n\nThe message said—, I try to say but nothing comes out.\n\nJamie leans closer, voice dropping to that whisper that makes my skin prickle. "I picked red on purpose. You noticed, right? The shoes, the nails..." Her voice drops. "I thought maybe if everything matched, you'd finally..."\n\n"Let's get on with it," Patrick starts. "The sooner we run the trails—"\n\n"…the sooner we can get back to more interesting activities," Becca finishes for him with a knowing smile and a giggle that makes Patrick's ears turn red.\n\nJamie sighs dramatically but doesn't let go of my arm. "Fine. We'll go chase your ghost. But when we find nothing—and we will find nothing—you owe me. Remember?"\n\nThe way she says 'owe me' makes my mouth go dry, and once again, Whitney's warning about red slips from my mind like water through my fingers.\n\nWe slip away one by one, then regroup at the edge of Whickham Park where the manicured grass gives way to scrub palmetto and twisted oak. The temperature drops ten degrees in the shade, but the air gets thicker, heavier, like breathing through wet cotton.\n\n"Last chance," Jamie murmurs, pressing against my side as we start down the trail. "We could still turn back. Find somewhere quiet. I promise I'll make it worth your while."\n\nThe trail—if you can call it that—winds deeper into the woods than any official park path should go. Spanish moss hangs like funeral shrouds from the branches, and the ground squelches under our feet with decades of accumulated leaf rot and standing water.\n\n"This feels wrong," Becca mutters, swatting at mosquitoes that seem to materialize from thin air.\n\nIt does feel wrong. The deeper we go, the more the forest closes in around us, until we're jogging single file through a green tunnel that blocks out most of the sun. The air smells like decay and secrets, like things that died and were never properly buried.\n\n"Still time to turn back," Jamie whispers behind me, her red shoes squelching in the mud. "Still time to focus on something real."\n\nThen we smell it—the unmistakable stench of poverty and desperation. Rusted metal, moldy fabric, the sour tang of cheap beer and cheaper cigarettes. It's the smell of giving up, of places where hope goes to die.\n\nThe clearing opens before us like a wound in the earth.\n\nThe trailer sits dead center, listing to one side on cinder blocks and broken dreams. It's the kind of white that was never really white, now stained yellow-brown with years of neglect and Florida weather. The aluminum siding is streaked with rust that looks like dried blood, and half the panels have pulled away from the frame, creating gaps that stare at us like missing teeth.\n\nBut it's the decorations that make my stomach turn.\n\nPlastic crosses hang from every available surface, sun-bleached and cracked. A ceramic Jesus lies shattered on the front steps, his painted eyes staring blindly at the sky. Wind chimes made from bent coat hangers and broken glass tinkle in the dead air, creating a sound like tiny screams.\n\n"Jesus Christ," TJ breathes.\n\n"Don't," Jamie whispers, her red fingernails digging into my arm hard enough to draw blood. "Don't say that here."\n\nThe windows are covered with yellowed newspaper, but I can see gaps where the tape has come loose, revealing darkness beyond that seems to move and shift when I'm not looking directly at it. The front door hangs slightly ajar, held open by what looks like a child's broken doll.\n\nThis is Whitney's childhood—or at least one version of it. The realization hits us like a physical blow, memories flooding back that feel borrowed from someone else's nightmares. This is where she learned that love comes with conditions and God comes with fists. This is the place she ran from, again and again, until her psycho white-trash parents finally made the running permanent by disowning their own child for the crime of being true to herself.\n\nExcept this trailer never existed. We know this. We've been in these woods before, and there was never anything here but trees and shadows and the occasional beer can. This is where Will grew up—the Will that Patrick remembers from his childhood, before he became William fucking Carson. He and his parents moved to Baytree after... after... How do we know this? The memories feel twisted, wrong, like someone else's photographs shuffled into our family album. Suddenly I can't tell what's real anymore.\n\nBut standing here now, breathing the stench of sanctified decay, I know we've been here before. All of us. In dreams, maybe, or in the spaces between memories where the real truth hides.\n\nThe air buzzes against our skin like static electricity, and somewhere in the distance, I swear I can hear the sound of a television tuned to nothing but snow.\n\nJamie presses closer against me, her whole body trembling now, all traces of seduction gone. "We shouldn't be here," she whispers.\n\nBut the door is already calling to us, and Whitney's message burns in my pocket like a promise we're not brave enough to break. I glance at Jamie's red backpack, her red shoes, her red fingernails, and finally understand why Whitney warned us about the color.\n\nToo late now.\n\n**********\n\nThe moment I touch the door handle, the world hiccups.\n\nIt's not violent—more like reality clearing its throat, adjusting itself around us with the casual indifference of a predator. The air thickens until breathing feels like swallowing honey, and that static buzz I've been hearing escalates to a frequency that makes my molars ache.\n\n"Holy shit," TJ whispers, but his voice sounds like it's coming from underwater.\n\nThe trees around us begin their impossible dance. The massive oak that was definitely, absolutely to our left now looms behind Patrick, its gnarled branches reaching over his shoulder like arthritic fingers. A cluster of pine trees shuffles to the right like they're embarrassed to be caught moving, their needles rustling with the sound of whispered apologies.\n\nJamie's red backpack flickers—red to blue to red again—like a glitch in a video game running on dying hardware. She notices and holds it up, staring at it with the kind of fascination you'd reserve for watching your own hand melt.\n\n"That's not..." she starts, then stops. "That's not normal, right?"\n\nTJ holds up his wrist, and his watch is ticking backward, the numbers rolling in reverse like time is having second thoughts. "Guys," he says, voice cracking. "Guys, this is really not normal."\n\nThat's when Becca points into the trees. "There," she says. "Look."\n\nIn the distance, moving between the shifting oaks, is Patrick. Same height, same way of holding his shoulders like he's perpetually at attention. Except Patrick is standing right next to us, staring at his own doppelganger with the kind of expression usually reserved for discovering your own corpse.\n\n"What the actual fuck," Patrick breathes, and his distant twin turns at the sound, waves cheerfully, then disappears behind a tree that wasn't there a second ago.\n\nThe trailer door slams shut with the finality of a coffin lid.\n\nWhen we spin around to look at it, the trailer is gone. We're standing on Whickham Road, except it's not the Whickham Road we know. The asphalt stretches in both directions like a black tongue, but it curves upward at impossible angles, defying gravity in ways that make my inner ear vomit. The familiar strip mall where we buy Slurpee’s has been replaced with buildings that lean at angles that shouldn't be structurally possible, their windows reflecting scenes that aren't happening outside them.\n\nThe 7-Eleven where we steal adult magazines now bears a hand-painted sign reading "Yesterday's Emporium: Specializing in Things That Never Were." The familiar red brick of the bank has been replaced with something that looks like dried meat, and the digital clock on its facade shows times that don't exist: 25:73, 14:91, 88:12.\n\nJamie presses closer to my side, her red fingernails digging into my arm. "This is our town," she whispers, but there's uncertainty in her voice. "Isn't it?"\n\nThe street signs are wrong. Instead of familiar names like Palmetto Avenue and Oak Street, they read things like "Remembrance Way" and "What Once Was Boulevard." The stop signs are upside down, and when a car approaches one, it doesn't stop—it accelerates backward until it disappears into the distance with a sound like screaming metal.\n\nTJ points to what should be Dragon's Den Comics, its sign twisted into 'Vaglunia Membranella.'\n\n“This is where we buy comics,” he says, his voice hollow as we watch the heroes on the covers move, trapped in tiny, repeating loops of action.\n\nThe people walking the sidewalks move with mechanical precision, their smiles painted on like masks. They nod pleasantly as we pass, but their eyes track us with the hungry interest of predators sizing up prey. A businessman in a crisp suit walks past us, but when I look down, I realize his torso ends at the waist—his legs moving independently below, connected by nothing but shadows and malice.\n\n"Beautiful day, isn't it?" A woman says as she passes, pushing a shopping cart. When she reaches for an apple from her cart, I see that her arm extends impossibly from her shoulder, joints bending in places where joints shouldn't exist. The apple in her hand is moving, squirming, and I realize with growing horror that it has tiny human features.\n\nThe smell here is all wrong too. The pizza place that should smell like oregano and melted cheese instead reeks like formaldehyde and oranges. But the dumpster behind it, overflowing with what looks like rotting meat, somehow smells like cinnamon and fresh baked cookies—sweet enough to make my mouth water despite my revulsion.\n\n"Where's the arcade?" Becca asks, her voice small and confused.\n\nThere—except it's not. The familiar neon sign now reads "Whispering Pines Funeral Services," but through the windows, I can see the games are still there. Sort of. The Pac-Man cabinet gapes open like a mouth, its screen showing nothing but static that occasionally resolves into what looks like screaming faces. A couple feeds quarters into Street Fighter II, but instead of Ryu and Chun-Li, the characters are anatomically impossible flesh sculptures that merge and separate with wet, organic sounds.\n\n"This is where we come to have fun," TJ says in a voice that doesn't sound like his own. When I look at him, his eyes are too bright, too wide, like someone turned up the contrast on his face.\n\nWe pass what should be our houses, and strangers wearing our parents' faces wave from windows with movements too smooth, too practiced. In TJ's bedroom window, a child who shares his features but not his humanity plays with action figures that march across the windowsill in perfect formation, their tiny plastic faces turned toward us with expressions of malevolent awareness.\n\nThe further we walk, the more wrong everything becomes. A mother sits on a park bench, cradling what looks like an infant. As we watch, she opens her mouth impossibly wide and the baby crawls inside, disappearing completely. Moments later, she hunches forward and the child emerges from beneath her skirt, clean and giggling, only to crawl back toward her mouth to repeat the process.\n\nTwo teenagers lean against a lamp post, their faces pressed together. At first it looks like kissing, but then I realize one is vomiting a steady stream of what looks like liquid starlight into the other's mouth, who swallows gratefully before returning the favor. They notice us watching and wave enthusiastically, the fluid still connecting their lips like a grotesque umbilical cord.\n\n"Holy Mother of God," Patrick breathes, then immediately claps his hand over his mouth like the word might summon something worse.\n\n"Don't," Becca whispers. "Don't say names here. Names have power."\n\nThe streetlights flicker in patterns that hurt to look at directly, casting shadows that move independently of their sources. The shadows reach toward us with fingers made of darkness, and I realize with growing horror that they're not random—they're purposeful, hungry, testing the boundaries of what they can touch.\n\nJamie's grip on my arm tightens, her red fingernails digging in so hard I can feel them through my shirt. But then I hear it—a voice that sounds like grinding glass and broken promises, speaking words that seem to come from inside my skull:\n\n"Her only job was to prevent you from coming here."\n\nJamie's entire body goes rigid. "We need to leave," she whispers, but now her voice is pure desperation, panic rising like floodwater. "This is all wrong. It wasn't supposed to happen like this." She's holding onto me like I'm the last real thing in the universe, her whole body trembling.\n\n"I'll be good, I promise. I'll do what I'm told. Please, just let us—"\n\nHer red fingernails dig deeper into my arm, and I watch in horror as they begin to smoke where they touch my skin. The red backpack on her shoulder starts to glow like heated metal, and her red shoes leave burning footprints in the impossible asphalt.\n\n"GET OUT!" Whitney's voice cuts through the air like a blade, seeming to come from everywhere and nowhere at once. But it's not directed at us—it's aimed directly at Jamie, sharp with recognition and fury.\n\n"THE RED! YOU BROUGHT THE RED!"\n\nJamie looks down at herself—at the red nail polish, the red shoes, the red backpack—and for a split second, her face flickers. I see two expressions overlaid like double-exposed film: the human Jamie, eyes wide with genuine confusion and terror, whispering "I didn't know, I swear I didn't know what it would do."\n\nBut underneath, bleeding through like ink through wet paper, is something else—red eyes gleaming with the knowledge that she knew exactly what the red would do, that she brought it deliberately, a beacon to guide the wrongness straight to us.\n\nBoth Jamies speak at once, their voices harmonizing in a chord of guilt and innocence: "No, no, no."\n\nJamie is reluctantly ripped from my arm.\n\nI can feel the red—a gradual erosion of certainty, like standing too close to the edge of a cliff made of memory. Each impossible sight we witness takes something from us, replaces a piece of what we know to be true with something that shouldn't exist.\n\nWe pass a storefront with mirrored windows, and for just an instant—a single, precious heartbeat—I see Whitney's real face reflected back at us. Not William's cruel features, but Whitney's soft brown eyes and the way she used to bite her lower lip when she was thinking. Her panicked mouth forms words I can't hear but somehow understand: "GET OUT. GET OUT NOW."\n\nThen she's gone, and the mirror shows only our own terrified faces staring back. Except...\n\nFour faces stared back. But there were only four of us. I counted again. Once. Twice. And still felt like something was watching from just behind the glass.\n\nThat's when I feel it.\n\nIt starts as a tug in my chest, like someone pulled a thread connected to my heart. Then it becomes a hollow ache, the kind of emptiness you feel when you reach for something that should be there and find nothing but air.\n\nSomething's missing. Someone.\n\nFuck, this is it. This is the fracture Whitney warned me about. Not a place, but a person. A seam in reality they're trying to stitch shut, and I'm the only one who can feel the thread pulling.\n\n"Where's..." I start, then stop. Where's who? The question feels urgent, desperate, but I can't complete it.\n\nThe others look at me with blank confusion. TJ tilts his head like a dog hearing a high-pitched sound. "Where's what?" he asks, and there's genuine puzzlement in his voice.\n\n"Someone was here," I say, spinning in a circle, scanning the impossible street. "Someone was with us. I can feel their absence like a missing tooth, but I can't..."\n\nBecca reaches toward me with concern. "Stephen, are you okay? It's just been the four of us this whole time."\n\nThe hollowness in my chest expands, threatening to swallow me from the inside. I can feel the ghost of someone's touch on my arm, can smell phantom traces of vanilla and peaches and fear-sweat and that indefinable something that was purely... purely...\n\n"No," I say, fumbling for my journal with shaking hands. "No, no, no. Someone was here. Someone real. Someone who..."\n\nI flip through the pages frantically, looking for a name, a description, anything that might anchor this dissolving memory. But the pages that should contain references to this missing person are blank, or worse—they contain references to empty spaces, to conversations where I'm apparently talking to myself, to moments where I'm described as walking alone when I know, I KNOW, someone was beside me.\n\nI grab my pen and try to write the name that's hovering just beyond reach. The ink seems to fight me, wanting to form other words, other names. I press harder, and just as the last traces of memory threaten to slip away completely, the smell hits me—vanilla and peaches.\n\nMy hand moves almost without conscious thought: J\n\nGone.\n\nA\n\nWhat are you doing.\n\nM\n\nThis is wrong\n\nI\n\nSTOP.\n\nE.\n\nThe moment the letters form, memories flood back—but not the ones I expect. Instead of teenage flirtation and nervous energy, I see flashes of something darker. Red eyes in darkness. Claws instead of fingernails. A mouth that opens too wide, revealing rows of teeth like broken glass. The sound of wings made of shadow. A succubus wearing the face of innocence, sent to seduce and destroy.\n\nBut then—beneath the demonic imagery, like sunlight breaking through storm clouds—I remember her. The real Jamie. The girl who played magic and made every day just that much brighter. Who listened to me ramble about dreams and death. Who offered me everything she had to give.\n\nThis was my fault. Not the coming here, but the remembering. My refusal to let her go was the final act of rebellion the prison could not tolerate. I had held onto love, and in response, it was showing me the true anatomy of pain.\n\nAround us, the impossible street wavers like heat shimmer. A man with eyes where his mouth should be nods pleasantly as he passes, but his smile has turned predatory. Somewhere in the distance, children sing a nursery rhyme in reverse while playground equipment made of what looks like human bones creaks in a wind that touches nothing else.\n\nThe name in my journal pulses like a heartbeat, burning against the page like a brand. The only evidence I have that someone real was supposed to be here—and my refusal to let them be erased.\n\nBut even as I watch, the letters begin to smoke and fade, and I know with cold certainty that I'm fighting a war I might not win.\n\nAnd the worst part? I can feel the universe itself turning its attention toward me, annoyed that one small human dares to remember what it has chosen to forget.\n\nThen, cutting through the impossible air like synchronized lightning, two voices scream in perfect unison—Whitney's defiant rage and Jamie's desperate love…\n\n"RUN!"\n\n**********\n\nWe bolt back down Wickham Road, hoping to make it back to the park, back to where this fucking nightmare started. But on the impossible street, our feet pounding against asphalt that feels like it's made of screaming faces. Behind us, the mechanical people turn in perfect unison, their painted smiles stretching wider until their faces split like overripe fruit. The sound they make isn't human—it's the grinding of cosmic gears, the death rattle of broken machinery trying to restart itself.\n\nBut there's nowhere to run to. Every street we turn down leads back to the same impossible boulevard. Every door we try is locked or leads to rooms filled with static and the smell of burning copper. The wrongness closes in around us like a fist made of screaming geometry, reality deciding we're a typo that needs to be erased.\n\n"This way!" Patrick shouts. But when we follow him around the corner of what should be the old movie theater, we emerge exactly where we started—standing in front of the wrong 7-Eleven.\n\nTJ spots something through the window—a radio, old and battered, sitting on a shelf that shouldn't exist in a convenience store. Without hesitation, he grabs a loose chunk of concrete from the crumbling sidewalk and hurls it through the glass. The sound is wrong—instead of the sharp crack of breaking glass, it makes a wet, organic noise, like tearing flesh.\n\nHe reaches through the jagged opening, ignoring the way the glass edges seem to writhe like living things, and grabs the radio. His fingers shake as he extends the antenna. Static pours out like blood from a wound, but underneath the white noise, something else writhes—voices speaking in languages that predate human tongues, frequencies that make our teeth ache and our vision blur.\n\n"There has to be something," he mutters, spinning the dial frantically. "Benji, you bastard, where are you?"\n\nBecca and I begin the chant we've used before, our voices small and desperate in the face of this cosmic wrongness. The words feel heavy on our tongues, like speaking through molasses, each syllable requiring effort that leaves us breathless.\n\nPatrick tries to draw Benji's sigil with his finger in the air, but his hands are shaking too badly. What appears are scratches and scribbles that seem to leak something dark and viscous—not quite blood, not quite oil, but something that makes my stomach turn. The symbols writhe and shift, rearranging themselves into configurations that hurt to look at directly.\n\nAt first, nothing happens. The static continues its assault on our eardrums while the mechanical people maintain their endless pantomime. Then, buried beneath layers of radio snow, we hear it:\n\nA breath. Choked. Strangled. Human but not human, like someone drowning in air; a sound that will haunt whatever dreams I have left—a low, warbling "nooooo" that stretches and distorts like rubber being pulled over bone. The word becomes a scream becomes a sob becomes something that no throat should ever produce.\n\nThe sound cuts off with surgical precision, leaving us in a silence so complete it feels like being buried alive.\n\nThe shadows around us begin to fold inward, collapsing their dimensions until they're not shapes anymore but pure pressure. They press against us from all sides—not touching, but being, existing in the spaces between molecules, squeezing reality itself like a cosmic fist.\n\nI can't breathe. None of us can. The air has become solid, viscous, fighting every attempt to draw it into our lungs. And underneath it all, I catch whiffs of something sickeningly familiar—that black ichor Jamie once vomited, the smell of rotting flowers that accompanied her worst moments.\n\nBehind everyone’s eyes, something ancient and hungry stirs, and when it speaks, it's in that same grinding voice:\n\n"Disobedience will not be tolerated. Disobedience must be corrected."\n\nThe world around us melts like wax under a blowtorch. The impossible street dissolves into fractals of screaming color. We're falling upward through layers of existence that shouldn't exist, each one more wrong than the last. I see forests made of screaming mouths, oceans of liquid regret, cities built from crystallized childhood fears.\n\nAnd then, in the center of it all, I see them.\n\nThe stakes.\n\nTwo of them, side by side, surrounded by flames that aren't fire but pure anguish given form. And tied to them—\n\nJamie. Both of them. One and the same.\n\nOn the left stake, the demon version writhes in chains that look like they're made from braided screams. Red eyes like dying stars, skin that shifts between human flesh and something reptilian, wings made of shadow and regret. She's magnificent and terrible, but when she sees me watching, her expression isn't the cruel satisfaction I expect. It's love—desperate, anguished love—mixed with the horror of what she's become. Her too-many teeth part as if to speak my name, and I see tears of liquid shadow streaming down her shifting features. Even in this form, even burning, she was falling for me. And she knows it's what damned them both.\n\nBut on the right stake—oh god. The girl who brought me tea and traced patterns on my arm and offered me everything sacred she had to give. Her mouth is open in an eternal scream that produces no sound, her eyes wide with the kind of terror that breaks minds and souls equally. But when those eyes find mine, the terror transforms into something worse: love and confusion and desperate pleading. Why is this happening to her? Why can't I save her? The questions burn in her gaze as clearly as the flames around her flesh.\n\nBoth sets of eyes—demonic and human—lock onto mine with identical expressions of love corrupted by cosmic punishment. They are one soul, split and burning for the same crime.\n\n"DISOBEDIENCE WILL NOT BE TOLERATED," the universe speaks with a voice like breaking planets. "LOVE IS THE ULTIMATE REBELLION."\n\nThe flames rise higher, feeding on hope and innocence and all the small kindnesses that make life bearable. Jamie is looking directly at me through the fire, through time, through the spaces between heartbeats, and I know with cold certainty that this is happening now, has always been happening, will always be happening.\n\nThe wrongness reaches critical mass.\n\nReality doesn't just implode—it inverts, turning inside out like a glove made of screaming nerve endings. The buzz of static from the trailer isn't just a sound anymore; it's the texture of my own dissolving flesh. Agony roots itself in my marrow, my skeleton vibrating with the modem's final, eternal screech. Every cell rebels, tricked into an existence it can no longer sustain.\n\nTime becomes elastic, stretching each second of torment into geological epochs. I experience every moment of pain individually, distinctly, with the kind of crystalline clarity usually reserved for the most beautiful experiences life has to offer.\n\nIn the background, threading through the symphony of agony like a silver wire through rotting meat, I can still hear Jamie screaming—in rage at her failure, in despair at her fate, both calling a name that isn't Stephen but something older.\n\nAnd as my consciousness frays into nothing, the last thing I perceive is the sweet, damning scent of vanilla and peaches.	10	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 12:17:05.060788-05	2026-03-05 19:39:33.717247-05	\N	34	\N	\N
101	2	11	Ch. 10: Dreams That Stay With You	I snap awake like a broken guitar string, notebook welded to my chest by cold sweat. The dream clings to me... no, not a dream. A place. Real as the taste of bile flooding my mouth, real as the echo of Whitney's scream still bouncing around my skull.\n\nThe trailer. The door that opened into hell. Patrick pushing us back as shadows with too many teeth reached for him.\n\nMy hands shake as I grab the phone. Jamie first—I need to know she made it out, that whatever I saw of her in there wasn't the last version I'll ever see. The busy signal hits me like a slap—that monotone drone that means someone else is already trying to reach out, trying to confirm they're not losing their mind alone.\n\nTJ. Busy.\n\nPatrick's number rings once, then that same connection death rattle.\n\nEven Becca's line is jammed.\n\nThe panic crawls up my throat like battery acid. We're all doing this. Right now. All of us clawing for each other through the phone lines like drowning swimmers fighting for the same piece of driftwood.\n\nMy legs feel like wet paper as I stumble to my computer, the ancient 486 humming to life with all the urgency of a sedated sloth. The BBS loads in painful increments, blue pixels bleeding across the screen like digital blood. My reflection stares back from the monitor—hollow-eyed, desperate, looking like I've been hit by a truck made of nightmares and regret.\n\nThe message board explodes with activity:\n\nMORTALKOMBAT_TJ: HOLY SHIT DID YOU GUYS SEE THAT\n\nPIXIE_BECCA: WHAT THE HELL WAS THAT PLACE\n\nJAMIE_SPARKLES: ARE YOU OK??? EVERYONE ALIVE???\n\nRelief floods through me seeing Jamie's handle. She's alive. She's real. She's not still burning. She got out.\n\nMORGAN_FREEMAN_NOT: wait what are you guys talking about?? you all sound insane\n\nGILLIGAN_87: Did I miss something?\n\nBLACKDRAGON_666: we all saw it. the trailer. the door. heard whitney screaming.\n\nThe responses split in two directions:\n\nANDREW_THE_GIANT: Seriously what trailer? You guys are freaking me out\n\nMORTALKOMBAT_TJ: The hallway that went nowhere. Jesus Christ the hallway.\n\nPATRICK_BATEMAN: She's still there. I know she's still there.\n\nJAMES_HETFIELD: Five people sharing impossible memories while reality says they're wrong? Either we're all losing it, or we need to seriously reconsider what's possible.\n\nMORGAN_FREEMAN_NOT: This is how horror movies start. Group hysteria. Shared psychotic episodes.\n\nI type with two fingers, hunt-and-peck style, because my hands won't stop shaking:\n\nBLACKDRAGON_666: morgan gil andrew james – do any of you rember a trailer behind wickham park?\n\nGILLIGAN_87: Dude I've lived here my whole life. There's nothing back there but the road.\n\nPIXIE_BECCA: Do you remember Whitney? She used to be called Will before she transitioned?\n\nA long pause. Too long.\n\nANDREW_THE_GIANT: What the fuck are you talking about, that asshole has always been Will?\n\nJAMES_HETFIELD: This is fascinating from a philosophical standpoint. You're asking us to remember someone who apparently never existed, while simultaneously claiming they did exist but as someone else entirely. It's like Theseus's ship paradox applied to human identity—if every part of a person changes, are they still the same person? Or in this case, did they ever exist at all?\n\nMORGAN_FREEMAN_NOT: James, this isn't a thought experiment. They're talking about real people.\n\nJAMES_HETFIELD: But that's exactly my point. What constitutes "real" when five people share memories that contradict observable reality? Descartes would have a field day with this epistemological crisis.\n\nJAMIE_SPARKLES: I was burning. I had no idea who I was. The pain wouldn't stop. But then I felt her, Whitney, she did something... pulled me back from whatever I was becoming. I can still feel her fingers on my arm.\n\nMORGAN_FREEMAN_NOT: Jamie what are you talking about? Pulled you back from what? And who's Whitney?\n\nGILLIGAN_87: You're talking like you were literally on fire. Were you on fire?\n\nJAMIE_SPARKLES: Yes. No. Something worse. Like my identity was being torn apart. Whitney saved me from forgetting myself completely.\n\nMORGAN_FREEMAN_NOT: This is getting seriously concerning. You're describing some kind of dissociative episode.\n\nThe divide becomes crystal clear. Those of us who went through the door remember everything. The others think we've collectively lost our minds. But there's something else—a curiosity bleeding through their skepticism.\n\nGILLIGAN_87: Look I don't know what you guys experienced but you're scaring me. And I don't scare easy.\n\nANDREW_THE_GIANT: You've been acting weird for weeks. All of you. Like you know something the rest of us don't.\n\nMORTALKOMBAT_TJ: Because we DO know something. Something that keeps getting erased.\n\nMORGAN_FREEMAN_NOT: Erased by who? The CIA? Aliens? Come on.\n\nBLACKDRAGON_666: this is going no where. lets meet before school? see if we can find that trailer in the woods behind track? i know how this sounds but something is happening to us. to reality.\n\nJAMES_HETFIELD: This is insane.\n\nPIXIE_BECCA: Maybe. But will you come anyway? Please?\n\nAnother pause. I can picture them on the other side of their screens, weighing friendship against sanity.\n\nGILLIGAN_87: I'll be there. But only because I'm worried about you guys.\n\nANDREW_THE_GIANT: Same. And if this is some elaborate prank I'm going to kick all your asses.\n\nMORGAN_FREEMAN_NOT: 7 AM. I'm bringing my Poloroid. If something supernatural is happening, we document it.\n\nJAMES_HETFIELD: I still think you should all check yourselves into Charter Behavioral. But... I'll be there.\n\nThe relief that washes over me is almost embarrassing. They don't remember. They don't believe. But they're still coming. Still choosing friendship over logic.\n\nA new message appears at the bottom of the screen. No username. Just... words:\n\n[~~~~~~]: DON'T LET THEM FORGET HER AGAIN.\n\nI stare at the screen until my eyes water. The message doesn't disappear. It just sits there, glowing like a threat or a promise.\n\nBLACKDRAGON_666: did anyone else just see that message?\n\nMORTALKOMBAT_TJ: What message?\n\nJAMIE_SPARKLES: See what?\n\nThe unknown message has vanished. Just empty space where it used to be.\n\nBLACKDRAGON_666: never mind. see you all at 7.\n\nI log off and sit in the blue glow, listening to the computer fan whir like mechanical breathing. Finding my way back to my bed, it creaks below me, settling into whatever passes for sleep in this fractured timeline.\n\nWhatever happened in that shared nightmare, whatever door we opened or crossed through—it's changing things. Reality is splitting along the fault lines of memory. Some of us remember the girl who was erased. Others have accepted the boy who replaced her.\n\nBut they're all still coming to meet us in the woods.\n\nMaybe that's what matters. Maybe that's how we pull the timeline back together—one confused, loyal friend at a time.\n\n**********\n\nSleep isn't happening. Not after what we just lived through. Not with the taste of that place still coating my tongue like ash and copper. I flip open my journal, pen trembling between my fingers, trying to capture every detail before it fades like most nightmares do.\n\nDecember 4\n\nThe trailer existed. We all went inside. The basement wasn't a basement—it was a spiral made of rotting wood and childhood memories. Patrick stayed behind. Whitney screamed. The shadows had teeth. But that's not the worst part. The worst part is what happened when reality decided to punish us for caring about each other. Becca reached for Patrick, just wanted to hold his hand. The universe saw that love and decided to make it literal. Their flesh merged like heated wax—fingers, then hands, then arms. They became one writhing mass of teenage longing turned cosmic horror. Still conscious. Still themselves. Still screaming in harmony.\n\nMy pen scrawls across the page, a desperate, frantic race to cage the nightmare in ink before it dissolves. I write until my hand cramps and the letters blur, digging the words into the paper as if they could anchor me to a reality that still makes sense.\n\nJamie was burning. Both versions of her. Chained to stakes side by side—the demon with shiny skin and shadow wings, tears of liquid darkness streaming down her face. And the girl who brought me tea, mouth open in silent screams, eyes pleading for rescue that will never come. Same soul, split in half, burning for the crime of loving me. The flames weren't fire. They were anguish given form, feeding on hope, on innocence, on all the small kindnesses that make life bearable. Through it all, she was calling a name that wasn't Stephen. Something older. Something that felt like it belonged to me but from before... before what? The last thing I remember: vanilla and peaches. Her scent. Even while burning, even while split between demon and saint, she still smelled like the girl who traced patterns on my arm.\n\nThe pen sputters, scratching a final, dry line across the paper before dying completely. I drop it, my fingers tingling, and stare down at what I’ve done. My breath hitches. It’s not just words. For who knows how long, my cramped hand has been mindlessly tracing the same shape over and over—spirals. A whole page of them, a vortex of ink pulling inward toward nothing.\n\nMy eyes lift from the hypnotic mess on the page, and that's when I see it.\n\nWhitney's deck box with the big monogrammed J on the front.\n\nBut it's wrong. The box is deep red instead of the faded blue-grey I remember, and there's something predatory about the way it catches the lamplight. Like it's watching me.\n\nI reach for it with two fingers, half-expecting it to crumble or burst into flames. Inside, nestled among cards that shimmer with an oily, wrong kind of light, is something that shouldn't exist.\n\nVeilwalker stares up at me, the artwork showing Whitney's face dissolving into static—half-there, half-gone, reaching desperately toward something beyond the frame. Behind her, two burnt stakes rise from tortured ground, their chains hanging empty, broken. Whatever was bound there has escaped.\n\nThe mana cost reads 3BB. This was wrong. Not black, it should be white. The rules text makes my throat close up:\n\n"When Veilwalker enters the battlefield, shuffle all exiled cards into your library. You gain 20 life. When Veilwalker is exiled, search your library for a card named 'The Boy Who Remembers' and put it into your hand. If you cannot find 'The Boy Who Remembers,' you lose the game."\n\nThe card stock feels organic under my fingers. Warm. Pulsing slightly, like it has a heartbeat.\n\nI set it aside and turn back to my journal, but the page I just finished writing on has... changed. Below my frantic documentation of the nightmare, in handwriting that looks like mine but isn't quite right, new words have appeared:\n\nShe's not gone. Just misplaced. You'll have to repair the fracture to bring her back. But first—find the boy. The one who remembers before the poison. Before the fear. Before father taught you that love comes with conditions.\n\nHe's still in there. Scared. Hiding. But he holds the key.\n\nP.S. - Don't trust those who remember her. They’re not real.\n\nThe ink gleams wet, refusing to dry. I touch it with my fingertip and it comes away blue, staining my skin like evidence of something impossible.\n\n**********\n\nKnock knock.\n\nMy entire body goes rigid. Dad doesn't knock. Dad enters—usually with a belt or a fist or at minimum the kind of rage that makes the air taste like battery acid and fear.\n\n"Stephen? Are up? I heard something.”\n\nThe voice hits me like ice water. Soft. Warm. Kind. I spin around so fast the journal tumbles from my hands, Whitney's wet warning slapping against the carpet.\n\nThe door opens and I see my father stands in the doorway, hands clasped behind his back like he's presenting himself for inspection. "Morning, son. Sleep well?" The words are right, but his timing is wrong—like he's never actually asked that question before and isn't sure when it's supposed to happen.\n\n"Just wanted to say I'm proud of you," he continues, leaning against the doorframe like we're best friends who share inside jokes and Saturday morning cartoons. "You've got this, son. Whatever you're going through... you're handling it like a champ."\n\nThe words don't compute. This is the same man who gave me a split lip for breathing too loud during his TV show. The same man who made me eat dinner off the floor when I spilled my plate. The same man whose very footsteps make my nervous system scream run, hide, disappear.\n\nI look down at my arms instinctively, checking for the finger-shaped bruises from where he grabbed me yesterday. Gone. I lift my shirt with shaking hands. The belt welts across my ribs, the ones that usually take weeks to fade. Vanished. My skin is unmarked, healthy, normal.\n\nLike they never happened.\n\n"I made pancakes for breakfast," Wrong Dad says, and his voice is honey and sunshine and everything my real father has never been. "Blueberry. Your favorite."\n\nBlueberry pancakes aren't my favorite. I don't have favorites because having preferences in this house is like painting a target on your back. But somehow this version of him thinks he knows me. Thinks we have the kind of relationship where fathers remember what their sons like.\n\n"Thanks..." The word sticks in my throat like broken glass. "...Dad."\n\nHe grins wider, and for just a moment—less than a heartbeat—his teeth seem too sharp. Too many. "Take your time getting ready for school. No rush. We're family, after all. I'll drive you today—maybe we can stop for coffee on the way. Father-son time."\n\nFather-son time. The words hit me like ice water. We don't do father-son time. We do survival time, damage control time, please-don't-notice-me time. But never this.\n\nThe door closes with a soft click. Not the slam that usually rattles the windows and makes the house flinch. A gentle, considerate close.\n\nI turn back to my journal, staring at the impossible message from Whitney. The blue ink continues to gleam, wet and alive, like it's still being written by an invisible hand.\n\nWhitney’s card lies face-up on my desk, her half-dissolved face staring at me with eyes that seem to move when I'm not looking directly at them. When Veilwalker is exiled, search your library for 'The Boy...’\n\nThe smell of pancakes drifts under the door—real pancakes, not stale bread and the musk of leather belts. They smell perfect. They smell like Saturday mornings in houses where children don't learn to sleep with one eye open.\n\nI want to vomit.\n\nThis is worse than the beatings. Worse than the belt, the screaming, the careful dance of making myself invisible. Because that father—my real father—is predictable. Known. I understand his rhythms, his triggers, his particular brand of casual cruelty.\n\nThis father is a stranger wearing my abuser's face, speaking in a voice that makes me want to trust him. And that trust feels like the most dangerous trap of all.\n\nI pick up Whitney's sketch from the journal, watching the ink smear under my thumb. She's reaching for something beyond the page's edge, her mouth open in what might be a scream or a warning. Behind her, those empty chains sway in an unfelt wind.\n\nFind the boy, the journal whispers in my handwriting that isn't mine. The one who remembers before the poison.\n\nThe pancakes smell delicious. Perfect. Like love tastes when it's not contaminated by fear. I should be relieved. I should be grateful. For once, maybe just once, I might get through breakfast without calculating escape routes or measuring the distance to the door.\n\nBut I've never been more terrified in my life.\n\n**********\n\nThe woods behind the track field feel like a graveyard for living people.\n\nWe huddle in a rough circle at 7 AM, the December morning still clinging to darkness despite this being Florida. The air is cool but deceptively so—I can already feel the humidity lurking like a patient mugger, waiting to ambush us once the sun gets serious about its job. Each of us clutches our damage like rosary beads, breath creating ghosts in the air. Fitting, since we're all starting to feel like we might not be entirely real ourselves.\n\n"My little sister," Jamie starts, her voice hollow as a church bell with the clapper removed. "She keeps insisting her birthday is in March. Not November. March. I showed her the photo from her party last year—November fifteenth, clear as day—and she just stared at me like I was trying to convince her the sky was plaid."\n\nTJ nods grimly, his Reagan/Gore '84 shirt looking particularly absurd against the cosmic horror backdrop. "My mom's been humming this song all morning. Some folk tune I've never heard. But when I asked her about it, she said it was from her favorite radio show... except the show she's talking about doesn't exist. Never has."\n\nPatrick's face is gray as old concrete. "Our house. The spare bedroom where Whitney stayed is gone. Not remodeled, not converted—gone. There's just wall where the door used to be. My parents act like it was always that way. When I pressed them, my dad started crying and said he missed 'the room that was never there.'"\n\nMorgan wraps her arms around herself, looking like she's trying to hold her sanity in place through sheer physical force. She showed up because we asked, even though she thinks we've all lost our minds. "Look, I don't know what you guys think you experienced, but..." She pauses, her scientific certainty wavering. "My dad called me by my sister's name three times yesterday. I don't have a sister. Never did. But he keeps setting an extra place at dinner and then staring at it like he's waiting for someone who'll never come. I thought it was just stress from upcoming winter exams, but..."\n\nGil shifts uncomfortably, his usual sarcasm replaced by genuine unease. "You guys are scaring me with this shared nightmare stuff. But..." He runs a hand through his hair. "I've been having these dreams about someone I know I should remember but can't. A friend who used to hang out with us. Someone important. And when I wake up, I feel like I'm forgetting something crucial."\n\nJames crosses his arms defensively. "This is all mass hysteria brought on by exam stress. But..." He hesitates, his philosophical certainty wobbling. "My car stereo keeps switching to a station that doesn't exist. Playing music for someone it calls... well, the name gets all staticky. And I found myself crying yesterday for no reason. Just... mourning someone I can't remember."\n\n"But yet, there's no fucking trailer!" Andrew suddenly explodes, his composure completely abandoning him. "You want proof? Fine!"\n\nHe bolts from our circle, crashing through the underbrush toward Whickham Road like a man possessed. We chase after him, branches whipping at our faces as we stumble through the pre-dawn darkness.\n\nAndrew reaches the road and starts jumping up and down like a deranged kangaroo, pointing wildly at the empty asphalt and scrub brush. "SEE? JUST FUCKING WHICKHAM ROAD! No trailer! No mysterious path! Just road and more road and some very normal, non-apocalyptic Florida vegetation!"\n\nHe's breathing hard, his usually perfect hair disheveled, sweat beading on his forehead despite the cool morning air. "There's no secret entrance to hell behind the park! There's no—"\n\n“Explain this” I pull the MTG card from my pocket—Veilwalker, still warm to the touch like it's been sitting in someone's fevered palm. "And these. Things that shouldn't exist but do anyway, like cold Florida weather in December."\n\nThings quiet down as we form a circle around a stubby candle TJ brought from Nana’s stash—one of those religious candles with a saint on it, though I'm pretty sure Saint Expedite wasn't traditionally invoked for demon summoning. The flame gutters in the wind, casting our shadows long and wrong across the dead leaves.\n\n"Benji," I say, feeling foolish and desperate in equal measure. "We need to try."\n\nThe chant starts quiet, hesitant, like we're teenagers again trying to convince ourselves we're brave enough to use a Ouija board. "Benji... Benji... Benji..."\n\nNothing happens except Gil looking like he's about to bolt.\n\nWe try again, louder this time, our voices cracking with desperation and the early morning cold. The candle flame dances but doesn't change. No static. No smell of burnt moths. No sarcastic demon bureaucrat materializing to explain why reality is having a nervous breakdown.\n\n"Maybe we're doing it wrong," Gil suggests after our fifth attempt, his voice pitched somewhere between hope and hysteria.\n\n"Or maybe he can't hear us," Morgan says, the scientist in her grasping for rational explanations even as rationality crumbles around us like a house of cards in a hurricane.\n\nJames shifts uncomfortably. "This is insane. We're sitting in the woods chanting at a candle like we're in some kind of... I don't know, discount séance or something."\n\n"Shut up, James," Patrick snaps with uncharacteristic venom. "At least some of us wiccans are trying something instead of just standing here philosophizing about how fucked we are."\n\nWe keep chanting until our throats are raw. Until the candle burns down to a puddle of wax and broken dreams. Until the hopelessness settles into our bones like arthritis.\n\n"This is bullshit," TJ finally explodes, kicking at the dead leaves with the fury of someone whose worldview has been fed through a cosmic blender. "Where the hell is he when we actually need him?"\n\n"Language," Becca says automatically, then catches herself. "Sorry. Habit. Though I guess if we're summoning demons, swearing is probably the least of our sins."\n\nWe try one more time, putting everything we have into it. Our fear, our desperation, our growing certainty that we're truly alone in this cosmic joke. "BENJI!"\n\nThe silence that follows is deafening.\n\n"Maybe..." Becca's voice is small, uncertain, like she's suggesting we rob a bank to pay for lunch money. "Maybe we need to offer something. Like... like a sacrifice?"\n\nWe all turn to stare at her. Sweet, church-going Becca who won't even say 'damn' without apologizing and probably confesses to having impure thoughts about Jesus.\n\n"What kind of sacrifice?" Patrick asks carefully, like he's defusing a bomb made of teenage desperation.\n\n"I don't know. A goat? People in the Bible were always sacrificing goats."\n\nThe absurdity of it hits us all at once. Here we are, a bunch of Florida teenagers standing in the woods at dawn discussing animal sacrifice like it's a reasonable Tuesday morning activity, somewhere between AP History and lunch period.\n\n"Becca," Jamie says gently, "where exactly are we going to get a goat?"\n\n"Pet store?" she suggests weakly.\n\n"Those are hamsters and goldfish," Andrew points out with the patience of someone explaining gravity to a flat-earther.\n\n"Well, what do you suggest?" Becca snaps, her composure finally cracking like an egg against cosmic horror. "Because I'm running out of rational explanations for why my reality is dissolving!"\n\nWe're about to give up—about to accept that we're truly, cosmically fucked—when the air starts to taste like zinc pennies and electrical storms.\n\nStatic crackles between the trees, soft at first, then building like a radio trying to find a signal through the dimensional static. A cough echoes from nowhere, raspy and wet, followed by the acrid smell of burning moths having an existential crisis.\n\nBenji flickers into existence, but he's... wrong. His usual bureaucratic confidence is gone, replaced by something that looks like pure, weapons-grade terror. His form keeps glitching—winged eyeball one second, sweating intern the next, sometimes both at once like reality can't decide what nightmare to serve us.\n\n"Jesus Christ, kids," he gasps, his voice distorted like a bad phone connection from the seventh circle of Hell. "You have no idea—they nearly—I wasn't authorized to buffer that—"\n\nThat's when Morgan and Gil completely lose their shit.\n\nMorgan, who's spent years explaining away every weird thing with scientific rationality, takes one look at the floating, flickering eyeball-moth-bureaucrat and lets out a shriek that could shatter glass. She stumbles backward, trips over her own feet, and lands hard on her ass in the dead leaves. She starts fumbling for her Polaroid camera then just sits there in shock.\n\nGil, meanwhile, has gone completely catatonic. He's standing there, mouth hanging open, making a sound like a broken air conditioner—"Uhhhhhh... uhhhhhh... uhhhhhh..."\n\n"WHAT THE EVERLOVING FUCK IS THAT THING?" James screams, pointing at Benji with a shaking finger. "WHAT THE HELL IS HAPPENING?"\n\nAndrew, has apparently decided the best strategy is to faint. He goes down like a sack of potatoes, out cold before he hits the ground.\n\n"Oh, come on," Benji says, his voice cycling through seventeen different frequencies of exasperation. "I'm not that scary. I'm middle management! I wear cardigans!"\n\n"YOU'RE A FLOATING EYEBALL WITH WINGS!" Gil shrieks, finally finding his voice. "FLOATING EYEBALLS DON'T WEAR CARDIGANS!"\n\n"Actually, I do. They're very comfortable. And they have excellent pockets for—"\n\n"SHUT UP!" Morgan scrambles to her feet, backing away from Benji like he's radioactive. "This isn't real. This is a shared psychotic break. We all need medication. Lots of medication."\n\n"Benji!" I interrupt, relief flooding through me despite the chaos. "What happened? Where were you?"\n\nHis eye-wing-face turns toward me, and I see something I've never seen from him before: fear. Real, bone-deep, cosmic terror that makes my stomach clench.\n\n"You shouldn't have gone there," he says, his voice breaking up like static electricity having a nervous breakdown. "That place—that door—something overwrote protocol. The system's... compromised."\n\n"What system?" Morgan demands, apparently deciding that if she's having a psychotic break, she might as well interrogate the hallucination. "Compromised how?"\n\nBut Benji's already shaking his head—or wing, or whatever passes for a head when you're a bureaucratic moth-demon having an existential crisis. "I can't—they're monitoring. I'm only here because—"\n\n"Tell us about…," Patrick interrupts desperately. "Is she—can we—"\n\nThe moment Patrick tries to say her name, an inhuman scream tears through the air. Not from Benji, but from everywhere—the trees, the ground, the sky itself, like reality just stubbed its cosmic toe. We all clap our hands over our ears, but the sound comes from inside our skulls, reverberating through our bones.\n\nGil promptly pisses himself. The dark stain spreads across his jeans as he stands there, paralyzed by terror.\n\n"Did... did Gil just..." James starts.\n\n"YES I PISSED MYSELF," Gil shrieks. "THERE'S A DEMON AND REALITY IS SCREAMING AND I PISSED MYSELF. ARE YOU HAPPY?"\n\n"No judgment, man," James says quickly. "I'm like two seconds away from joining you."\n\nBenji's form convulses like he's having a seizure. He tries again, mouthing what might be her name, but the scream returns, louder, like reality itself is rejecting the sound with the violence of an immune system attacking a virus.\n\n"Paper," he gasps. "Maybe—"\n\nTJ scrambles for his notebook, tears out a page with shaking hands. Benji tries to write, his form solidifying just enough to grasp the pen like he's trying to hold onto sanity itself. The moment ink touches paper, the page explodes into blood—actual blood, spattering across our faces like we're extras in a low-budget horror movie.\n\n"NOPE," Morgan says, wiping blood from her cheek. "NOPE NOPE NOPE. I'M OUT. I'M TRANSFERRING SCHOOLS. I'M MOVING TO ALASKA. I'M BECOMING A NUN."\n\n"Sign language," Becca suggests desperately, apparently the only one still trying to problem-solve through the cosmic horror.\n\nBenji raises his hands to spell out her name. His fingers snap backward with wet, audible cracks that sound like someone breaking twigs made of bone and regret.\n\n"OH GOD OH GOD OH GOD," James chants, rocking back and forth. Andrew is still unconscious, which honestly seems like the smartest response to all this.\n\nWe stare in horror as Benji cradles his broken digits, his form flickering more wildly now like a TV with bad reception.\n\n"She's not gone," he whispers finally, his voice barely audible over Gil's continued whimpering. "But I can't tell you where. If I try, they'll take me too."\n\n"They?" I ask. "Who’s they??"\n\nBut Benji's already fading, his terror pulling him back toward whatever dimension he calls home.\n\n“They don’t erase you. They repurpose you. Recode you into the story that fits.”\n\nWe stand in the circle, staring at the bloodstained paper, at each other, at Gil's wet jeans, at Andrew's unconscious form, at the growing certainty that we're in something much bigger and more dangerous than we thought.\n\n"So," TJ says into the silence, his voice hollow as a funeral bell. "Anyone know where to buy a goat?"\n\n"And maybe some adult diapers," Gil adds miserably.\n\nNobody laughs. But honestly? Given everything that just happened, the fact that we're not all gibbering in padded cells feels like a small victory.\n\nAndrew chooses that moment to wake up, takes one look at all of us and promptly faints again.\n\n"Okay, that's it," James declares, his philosophical composure finally kicking in with some practical application. "Gil, help me carry him. We're taking him to the nurse's office."\n\n"Is the school even open yet?" Morgan asks, checking her watch and trying to pretend she's not still shaking.\n\n"Building opens at 7:30 for early activities," Gil says, already moving to grab Andrew's legs while James takes his shoulders. "Nurse should be there by now."\n\n"I'll help," Morgan adds, gathering her scattered dignity along with Andrew's backpack. "Someone needs to come up with a rational explanation for why he fainted. Twice."\n\n"Food poisoning?" Gil suggests as they hoist Andrew between them.\n\n"Low blood sugar," Morgan counters. "More believable."\n\n"Just... don't mention the floating eyeball," James pants as they start moving toward the school. "Or the blood. Or any of this."\n\nThey disappear through the trees, Andrew's unconscious form swaying between them like the world's most awkward weekend-at-Bernie's scenario.\n\n"We'll catch up with you guys in first period," Morgan calls back. "Assuming any of us are still sane by then."\n\n"Wait," I say to TJ. "When did they start hanging out with us again? I remember them avoiding us after Whitney... after Will. They thought we were crazy."\n\nTJ frowns. "They never stopped hanging out with us. Did they?"\n\n**********\n\nThe silence after the others leave doesn't feel empty—it feels full. Full of the terror left behind, full of words we can't say, full of the growing certainty that we're insects who just realized they're living in a jar.\n\nEven TJ sits motionless, his usual nervous energy completely drained. His face has gone the color of old milk, and for the first time since I've known him, he looks genuinely afraid. Not teenager afraid—existentially afraid.\n\nI flip open my journal with shaking hands, looking for something to anchor me, something to make sense of Benji's broken warnings. The page falls open to fresh text in my own handwriting:\n\nDon't trust the ones who remember her. They're not all real.\n\nI didn't write that. I would remember writing that. Wouldn't I?\n\n"What does it say?" Jamie asks, her voice barely above a whisper.\n\nI read the words aloud, and they hang in the air like a curse. Jamie's fingers tighten around my arm—not comforting, but desperate, like she's drowning and I'm the only thing keeping her afloat. Her grip is almost painful, and for just a moment, I swear I feel something else beneath her skin. Something that moves wrong.\n\n"They're not all real," Becca repeats, her voice hollow. She's moved closer to Patrick, seeking comfort in the familiar. He wraps an arm around her shoulders, and she leans into him with the kind of trust that only comes from months of stolen glances and shared secrets finally becoming something real.\n\nNobody answers because nobody wants to think about the implications. Andrew, James, Gil, and Morgan—they showed up. They remembered fragments. They felt the holes in reality where Whitney used to be. But they weren't there for the nightmare. They weren’t at the trailer.\n\nSo how do they remember anything at all?\n\nI think about the Veilwalker card, still warm in my pocket: When Veilwalker leaves the battlefield, shuffle all exiled cards into your library. The deck has been shuffled. The game has changed. And maybe some of the cards that came back aren't the ones we lost.\n\n"TJ," I say quietly, "Morgan. How long have you two been... you know?"\n\nHe blinks, confused. "What do you mean?"\n\n"Dating. How long have you been dating?"\n\n"We're not dating," he says, but there's uncertainty in his voice. "Are we? I mean, I thought... didn't we go to the movies last week? Or was that..." He trails off, looking genuinely confused.\n\nBecca and Patrick share a look, and there's understanding there. They remember the awkwardness of trying to define what they were after everything started—despite being all over each other practically from the moment Becca realized she'd lost Patrick in that hellscape. The desperate physical need came first, the labels and relationship talks stumbled along after. They know exactly how confusing and disorienting TJ's uncertainty about Morgan feels, because they lived through that same "what are we?" confusion themselves.\n\nBut TJ can't remember if he's dating Morgan or not.\n\n"The shadows at the edge of our circle seem to pulse with each heartbeat," I mutter, not realizing I'm speaking aloud.\n\n"What?" Jamie asks, but her voice sounds layered—like two people speaking in perfect unison.\n\nI look at her, really look, and for just a moment I see both versions superimposed: the girl who brings me tea and traces patterns on my arm, and something else. Something with too-sharp teeth and eyes that reflect light like a cat's. The demon and the saint, existing in the same space, both real, both struggling for control.\n\nBoth of whom I'm falling hopelessly in love with.\n\nAnd somehow, I feel that they might be too—both versions, fighting for the same heart, wanting the same impossible thing.\n\n"Jamie," I whisper, "are you okay?"\n\nShe blinks, and her eyes are just brown again. Human again. "I'm fine. Just... tired. Everything feels like it's moving too fast."\n\nBut her grip on my arm doesn't loosen, and there's something desperate in the way she holds on—like she's afraid that if she lets go, one of her selves might disappear forever.\n\nWe gather our things in silence—the melted candle, TJ's bloodstained notebook, my journal with its impossible warnings. Every sound seems too loud in the pre-dawn stillness. Patrick helps Becca to her feet, and they share a quick, desperate kiss—the kind that says I need to know you're real, I need to know this is real.\n\nIt's almost painful to watch. The simple, teenage need in that kiss—a gesture of comfort that feels like it belongs to another world entirely. A world where love isn't a liability.\n\nJamie watches them with an expression I can't quite read—longing mixed with confusion, like she's seeing something she wants but doesn't know how to reach for. Then, suddenly, she turns to me and presses her lips against mine. It's awkward, desperate, like she's copying what she just saw without quite understanding the mechanics of it. Her kiss tastes like vanilla and something else—something like eggs and ancient.\n\nWhen she pulls back, her eyes are wide, uncertain. "Was that... did I do that right?" she whispers, and I can hear both versions of her in the question—the inexperienced girl who's never kissed anyone, and something older that knows exactly what it wants but has forgotten how to be gentle.\n\n"We should get to class," Patrick says, but his voice sounds mechanical, like he's reading from a script. "First period starts in twenty minutes."\n\nAs we start walking back toward school, I can't shake the feeling that we're being watched. Not by shadows this time, but by something closer. Something that knows us.\n\nJamie squeezes my arm again, and I try to ignore the way her touch now feels like both a comfort and a warning. "You're overthinking this," she says, and her voice is perfectly Jamie's voice. But underneath it, I swear I hear something else whispering in a language I don't recognize.\n\n"The ones who remember her now," I say quietly, thinking of Andrew's confusion, James's phantom radio station, Gil's dreams of someone important, Morgan's extra place setting. "The ones who shouldn't be able to..."\n\n"What about them?" Becca asks, but she's distracted, focused on Patrick's hand in hers.\n\nI think about the Veilwalker card again. About shuffling exiled cards back into the library. About gaining life and searching for 'The Boy Who Remembers.'\n\n"Nothing," I lie. "Just... we need to figure out who we can trust."\n\nJamie's fingers dig deeper into my arm, and for just a moment, I feel claws.\n\nThe school building looms ahead of us, normal and mundane and completely wrong. Students are starting to arrive, parking their cars, walking in groups, laughing about things that don't matter. They have no idea that reality is coming apart at the seams, that some of their classmates might not be real, that a girl they should remember never existed.\n\n"First period," TJ says, checking his watch. "You have Biology. You should probably come up with a story for why you look like we've been wrestling with demons."\n\n"Because we have been," Patrick says grimly.\n\n"Yeah, but Ms. Rodriguez won't accept that as an excuse for being late."\n\nDespite everything, I almost smile. Some things never change. Even when the world is ending, we still have to worry about attendance.	11	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 12:17:29.121648-05	2026-03-05 19:39:33.717247-05	\N	36	\N	\N
102	2	12	Ch. 11: Those Who Remember Her	The day has been a waking nightmare of impossible homework and Benji's static-laced warnings echoing in my skull. December 4th should feel like winter in Florida—cool but not cold, the kind of night where you crack a window for air that doesn't taste like sweat and stale farts. Instead, I'm hunched over my desk at 11:47 PM, trying to make sense of calculus that keeps rearranging itself on the page and a journal entry I've been avoiding for three hours.\n\nThe tapping at my window cuts through my exhaustion like a blade. Three soft knocks, deliberate and patient. Then silence so complete it makes my ears ring.\n\nWhen I look up, Jamie's face floats in the glass like a pale moon made flesh, her breath fogging the window in rhythmic puffs that spell out patterns I almost recognize. Her lips are moving, but no sound penetrates the glass. She looks like she's been crying—or like something wearing her face has been practicing how to cry.\n\nThe wrongness hits me in waves. No one comes to my house. Ever. My father's belt hangs on the bedroom door like a leather promise of violence, and even friends know better than to risk his attention. Then I remember—the father in this house isn't mine. Not really.\n\nWrong dad looks the same, sounds the same, even has the same scar on his knuckles from punching the garage door last spring. But this version asks about my day without demanding I justify my existence. This version bought me lunch and meant it as kindness, not control. Reality's been editing him all week, turning my personal monster into someone almost... human.\n\nBut how does Jamie know which window is mine? How does she know that the screen pops out if you push on the bottom left corner? The knowledge feels embedded in her movements, muscle memory she shouldn't possess.\n\nShe shouldn't be here. She's never been here before.\n\nHas she?\n\nMemory fragments scatter like startled birds. Jamie laughing in my room. Jamie curled against me on the bed. Jamie's hair spread across my pillow like dark water. But those aren't my memories—are they? They taste like someone else's dreams, sweet and artificial, like vanilla extract mixed with grave dirt.\n\nThe window opens with a soft click that sounds impossibly loud in the silence. I don't remember unlocking it. I don't remember opening it. But Jamie slides through with practiced ease, bringing the Florida night air and something electric that makes every hair on my body stand at attention.\n\nThe scent of her hits me first—vanilla and peaches. But then I notice that her eyes are too bright, pupils dilated wide enough to drown in. She's been running—her chest rises and falls with quick, shallow breaths. Or crying—salt tracks gleam on her cheeks.\n\n"I can't do this anymore," she says. Her voice cracks on the words, each syllable dripping with vulnerability so perfect it feels rehearsed. But underneath the trembling confession, something else purrs—a hunger that makes my pulse stutter and my mouth go dry with equal parts desire and terror.\n\n"The pretending. The careful distance. The way we dance around each other like we're made of glass."\n\n"Jamie, what—"\n\nShe crosses the room in three steps that feel choreographed, close enough that I can feel the warm vanilla and peaches embracing me.\n\n"I'm tired of being afraid," she whispers, and her hand finds my face, thumb tracing my cheekbone like she's memorizing the shape. Her skin is fever-warm, almost burning. "Tired of hints and maybes and what-ifs. I know what I want."\n\nThe rational part of my brain starts cataloging inconsistencies. Jamie doesn't usually climb through windows. Jamie doesn't speak in honey-thick declarations. Jamie definitely doesn't look at me like I'm prey and salvation wrapped in the same package.\n\nHer lips find mine before I can form words, and thinking becomes impossible.\n\nIt's not like the soft, tentative kisses we’ve had. This is desperate, hungry, like she's drowning and I'm air. Her hands tangle in my hair, pulling me closer until there's no space left between us.\n\n"I've wanted this," she breathes against my mouth. "God, I've wanted this for so long it hurts."\n\nThis isn't right, some distant part of my mind whispers. Jamie blushes when she hugs me in public. She doesn't devour people like she's starving.\n\nBut my body doesn't care about logic. Sixteen years of hormones and loneliness surge forward, drowning out every red flag my paranoid brain tries to wave. When she pushes me backward until my legs hit the chair, I let her. When she pulls me down to the floor with her, both of us laughing at the graceless tumble, I pretend the laughter doesn't sound wrong—too musical, too knowing.\n\n"You feel that?" she asks, pressing her forehead to mine. Our breathing synchronizes, quick and shallow. "That's real. That's the only real thing in this whole broken world."\n\nBut then her fingers work at pulling off my shirt with hands that somehow shake, and I'm drowning again. There's something almost ritualistic about the way she moves, like she's done this before... or practiced it in dreams that aren't her own.\n\n"I've watched you," she admits, voice low and honey-thick. "In class, at lunch, when you think no one's paying attention. The way you bite your pen when you're thinking. How you tap out rhythms on your desk during tests."\n\nShe kisses my collarbone, and I can feel her smile against my skin. "I've memorized you."\n\n"That's..." I start, but she silences me with another kiss, deeper this time. Her tongue traces my lower lip and that metallic taste gets stronger.\n\n"Don't think," she whispers. "Just be here. Just be mine."\n\nMine. The word should sound romantic. Instead, it sounds like ownership. Like a claim being staked.\n\nThen her hands map territories I didn't know existed, when she whispers things that sound like prayers or spells, I let myself fall into the sensation. My vision blurs at the edges, but I tell myself it's just arousal, just the overwhelming rush of finally getting what I've wanted since freshman year.\n\nWe move together like we've choreographed this dance a hundred times. Her skin is fever-warm, almost burning where it touches mine. She guides my hands to places that make her gasp, makes sounds that I want to memorize and replay forever—if I survive whatever this is.\n\n"You and me," she breathes, hair falling like a curtain around our faces. "We're meant for this. Meant to burn together."\n\nBurn. Why does everything she says sound like a threat wrapped in silk?\n\nI'm losing myself in her heat, in the weight of her above me, in the way she says my name like it's sacred. Everything builds toward something bright and desperate, and for a few perfect moments I forget about the journal entries that rewrite themselves, forget about friends who vanish from memory, forget about the growing certainty that reality is just a badly coded simulation.\n\nThis feels real. This feels right. This feels like the first honest thing that's happened to me since the concussion.\n\nAnd then, just as the world starts to fragment into pure sensation, she leans down, lips brushing my ear like a secret.\n\n"I've missed you so much, Aspolin."\n\nThe name hits me like ice water in my veins. That name... the one from the void, from an alternate time line full of Eternal Engines and frustration. The name that belongs to versions of me I'm not sure I want to remember. The name Lady Illkeserod whispered in a library made of living knowledge.\n\nI freeze beneath her, every muscle going rigid. The heat between us turns cold so fast it's like someone opened a door to winter. The taste in my mouth shifts from peaches to grave dirt.\n\n"What did you say?" My voice sounds strange, hollow.\n\nShe lifts her head, blinking at me with those too-bright eyes, confusion washing over her features like she's waking from a dream she can't quite remember. For just a moment she looks lost, almost fragile—like she doesn't know where she is or how she got here, like the Jamie I know from biology class is struggling to surface through layers of something else. But then the hunger returns, predatory and focused, and that brief vulnerability vanishes behind a mask of perfect, terrifying control.\n\n"I said I missed you," she whispers, but there's uncertainty now, like she's not sure she likes how the words taste in her mouth. "Why? What's wrong?"\n\nEverything I want to scream. But nothing is all the escapes as I exhale.\n\n**********\n\nShe shifts her weight, reaching for something with fluid, predatory grace. Her hand slides under the bed with practiced ease, like she's rehearsed this exact motion in this exact room a thousand times before. The movement is too confident, too knowing—how does she know what's hidden under my bed when I don't even know myself?\n\nWhen her fingers close around something metallic, I feel the change in the air—a shift from passion to purpose, from seduction to ceremony.\n\nThe knife that emerges is beautiful and wrong. Ornate silver handle carved with symbols that twist and writhe when I try to focus on them, making my eyes water and my skull ache. The blade catches moonlight from the window, throwing geometric shadows across the ceiling that form patterns older than language. It's the kind of weapon a demon would commission—too pretty to fear until the steel finds your heart.\n\nShe sits up, straddling me, the knife held loosely in her grip like an extension of her own body. Her eyes are still soft, still loving, still the same warm brown I've been memorizing in biology class. There's no malice in her expression, no hatred. Just inevitability, like she's reading lines from a script written before either of us were born.\n\n"I'm sorry," she whispers, and I can hear real sorrow threading through her voice—genuine regret that makes this somehow worse. "But this is how it has to be. How it always has to be."\n\nMy hand shoots up, catching her wrist just as she begins the downward arc. Her eyes widen in surprise, then narrow in concentration as something ancient and hungry looks out through them. For a moment we're locked there in perfect tension, her pushing down with inhuman strength, me pushing back with everything I have, the knife suspended between intention and salvation.\n\nTime crystallizes into perfect, horrible clarity. I can see every detail etched in diamond-sharp focus—the way the blade trembles slightly in her grip, the single tear tracking silver down her cheek, the goosebumps rising on her bare arms like braille spelling out secrets I don't want to read.\n\n"Jamie," I say, shutting my eyes against the sight of the blade.\n\nReality doesn't just hiccup; it feels like it's being forcibly deleted and rewritten around me. A wave of pressure makes my ears pop, and for a nauseating second, I feel a phantom vertigo, as if the floor has dropped out from under me. The scent of ozone and something burning, like an overloaded circuit board, floods the air. It's not a glitch; it's an edit, a violent correction to the code. My brain stutters, trying to hold on to the memory of the knife even as the universe itself tries to rip it away.\n\nWhen I open my eyes, her hand is empty.\n\n“Stephen?” and there's something different in her voice now—confusion bleeding through the certainty, like she's waking up from a dream she didn't know she was having. "I... what am I...?"\n\nShe's shy now, almost breathless, like she's just realized what we've been doing. A deep blush spreads across her cheeks as she takes in our naked bodies, the way she's still straddling me, the intimate space between us that suddenly feels both natural and impossible.\n\n"Oh God, that was amazing. I’ve never felt like that before," she whispers as she rolls off me with careful, reverent movements, settling beside me on the carpet with her skin still flushed and warm. She immediately curls into my side, nuzzling against my chest like she can't bear even an inch of distance between us.\n\n"This is real, isn't it?" she breathes against me, her voice full of wonder rather than shock. "This is actually happening?" Her fingers trace lazy patterns on my chest, mapping me like she's memorizing every detail. "I've dreamed about this so many times—exactly like this. The way you'd touch me, the way I'd feel brave enough to touch you back."\n\nShe lifts her head to look at me, eyes bright with absolute bliss. "I thought it would always just be a fantasy, you know? Something I'd daydream while in biology class, the room would empty, you’d come up behind me and…" She laughs, the sound soft and amazed. "But this... God, Stephen, this is so much better than any dream."\n\nShe laughs, the sound shaky and real. "I can't believe I did that. What if your parents had seen me? What if you'd said no?" She presses her forehead to mine, and when she breathes, I smell only vanilla and peaches—no sulfur, no copper, just the girl I've been quietly obsessing over since I first saw her.\n\n"But it feels right," she continues, eyes closed like she's afraid opening them will break the spell. "Being here with you, like this. It feels like something I've been waiting for my whole life without knowing it."\n\nThe sincerity in her voice cuts through every paranoid thought trying to surface. This Jamie—human Jamie—wants this as much as I do. Maybe more. Her hands shake slightly as they rest on my chest, not from demonic calculation but from genuine nervousness, genuine desire.\n\n"Stephen," she whispers, and my name sounds different in her mouth now—reverent instead of possessive, hopeful instead of hungry. "Tell me this is real. Tell me you want this too."\n\nI can feel the warmth of her body pressed against mine, the rapid flutter of her pulse where her wrist touches my neck. Everything about her radiates shy excitement and wonder, like she's discovering magic for the first time.\n\n"It's real," I tell her, and mean it completely.\n\nShe smiles then, radiant and nervous and perfectly, beautifully human. "Good," she breathes, settling more fully into my arms like she belongs there. "And since I’m here already…"\n\nShe curls against me, fitting perfectly into the spaces I didn't know were empty, I push away the memory of silver blades and ancient symbols. This moment—Jamie's shy laughter, her wondering touch, the way she whispers my name like a prayer—this is worth whatever supernatural price tag comes attached.\n\nEven if I'm falling for a girl who might not always be entirely human.\n\nFor now, this is enough. This is everything.\n\n**********\n\nDecember 5th, 7:15 AM. The fluorescent lights of the 7-Eleven buzz like dying insects, casting everything in that particular shade of sick yellow that makes even healthy people look like corpses. After Jamie snuck out last night, I call TJ to help get everyone together for an emergency meeting here instead of our usual spot behind the track—too exposed, too close to where Jamie might find us.\n\n"Guys," I start, then realize I have no idea how to explain this without sounding completely insane. "It's Jamie. Something's wrong with her."\n\nThe words land like a grenade in the cramped space. TJ nearly chokes on his coffee.\n\n"Define 'wrong.' Like, forgot-to-do-homework wrong or tried-to-murder-you wrong?"\n\n"The second one."\n\nBecca goes pale. "Jesus, Stephen. Are you sure?"\n\n"Ritual knife. Silver handle, carved with symbols that hurt to look at. Somehow it appeared under my bed, like it was there the whole time. "\n\nJames looks like he’s going to vomit. "Are you fucking serious?"\n\n"Serious as a goat at a satanic barbecue," I mutter glancing at Becca. "She climbed through my window, seduced me, then tried to sacrifice me to whatever cosmic horror she's working for."\n\n"This is worse than Whitney," Becca says, and I can hear the fear threading through her voice. "At least with her, they just erased her. This is... personal."\n\n"It started back at TJ's basement," I continue, watching their faces for recognition. "Back the first time we tried summoning something. I remember seeing the shadows came over her as she was vomiting everything out. It’s like they did something to her. But then the fucking trailer nightmare? It's like someone cranked the supernatural bullshit up to eleven."\n\nPatrick leans against a rack of beef jerky. "So what's the plan? Avoid her? Confront her? Call an exorcist?"\n\n"We watch her," I say. "But carefully. If she really is one of the Jailers, we can't tip our hand too early."\n\n"Jailers?" Andrew asks.\n\n"The things that keep us trapped in Etheris," I say, watching recognition flicker across their faces. "You know, the prison realm I already explained after our encounter with Benji? It's all in my journals, but half of you didn't want to go on what You called a cosmic rabbit hunt that's led to Whitney getting abducted and replaced with that asshole Will.'"\n\nAndrew has the grace to look sheepish. "Yeah, well, hindsight's twenty-twenty. Maybe we should've listened when you said reality was breaking."\n\n"The guards make sure nobody remembers between cycles," I continue. "Keep us compliant, keep us confused. And now one of them is wearing Jamie's face."\n\nGil grins with unhealthy enthusiasm. "So Jamie's basically a cosmic corrections officer wearing our friend's face?"\n\n"Pretty much. And she's using me as bait to find out what we know."\n\n"Then we use that," Patrick suggests. "Turn her game against her. If she wants to play seductress, let her think it's working while you gather intelligence."\n\nTJ snorts. "So Stephen becomes the group whore and the bait?"\n\nEveryone turns to look at me. Through the window, I can see students starting to trickle toward school, and somewhere among them is Jamie—or whatever's wearing her skin.\n\n"If that's what it takes," I say, surprised by how steady my voice sounds. "Besides, how hard can it be to seduce a demon who's already trying to seduce me?"\n\n"Famous last words," Becca mutters.\n\n**********\n\nThe day unfolds with eerie normalcy. Jamie appears at my locker between classes, perfectly human—shy smiles, nervous laughter, quick kisses that taste like strawberry lip gloss and nothing else. She radiates the kind of sweet awkwardness you'd expect from a girl who just spent her first night with her crush, blushing when we hold hands walking through the hallway like she can't quite believe this is real.\n\nWe test her throughout the day like scientists probing a specimen. At lunch, while we're playing MTG, Becca asks if she remembers any of Whitney's old White Artifact decks—the specific card combinations, her strategy for using Serra's Sanctum with Metalworker. Jamie rattles off the deck list like she'd played against it yesterday, even mimicking Whitney's frustrated sighs when her combos got countered. TJ follows up with an increasingly ridiculous Reagan impression, and she laughs at all the right moments, finishing one of his favorite catchphrases when he pauses for effect.\n\nPatrick corners her by the water fountains, asking about homework assignments from three weeks ago. She rattles off due dates and teacher preferences with photographic precision, down to Mr. Henderson's weird thing about blue ink versus black.\n\n"Remember when you helped me with that chemistry lab?" Morgan asks during study hall, setting what should be an impossible trap—they'd never worked together on anything. But Jamie just tilts her head, confused but not defensive.\n\n"Which one? I don't think we've partnered up this semester. You usually work with Gil, right?"\n\nNot once do her eyes flash reptilian. Not once does she smell like sulfur. When she kisses me goodbye after school, her lips still taste like the strawberry she's always used, and nothing more sinister than teenage affection.\n\n"She's perfect," I tell the group as we watch her walk toward the parking lot, backpack bouncing with each step. "Too perfect. "\n\n**********\n\nDecember 6th. The week unfolds like a fever dream of domesticity laced with attempted murder. Every single night, Jamie climbs through my window like clockwork—sometimes sweet and nervous, sometimes predatory and hungry, always ending with us tangled together until dawn. The strangest part isn't the supernatural seduction. It's how Wrong Mom and Wrong Dad not only allow it, but actively encourage it.\n\n"Jamie, you seem like a sweet girl," Wrong Mom says over breakfast the next morning, like teenage girls regularly spend the night in her son's room. "You should invite her to spend weekend, Dad and I are going out of town."\n\nWrong Dad just nods approvingly from behind his newspaper—the same man who would've beaten me unconscious for talking to a girl six months ago. Now he's practically planning our wedding.\n\nSomething in this universe is going terribly wrong, and it's not just the demon wearing my girlfriend's face.\n\nJamie shows up this morning with homemade chocolate chip cookies still warm from the oven, her smile so devastatingly sweet it makes my chest ache. "Good morning, handsome," she says, pressing the plate into my hands along with a kiss that tastes like vanilla and promises. "I made these just for you."\n\nI play my role perfectly—the devoted boyfriend, completely smitten, totally oblivious to any danger. "You're amazing," I tell her, and the scary part is how easily the words come.\n\nDecember 6th. 5pm.That evening at TJ's pool party, I watch Jamie hold Morgan underwater during what starts as playful roughhousing. I'm counting seconds now, watching Morgan's struggles shift from laughter to genuine panic. Bubbles stream to the surface as Morgan thrashes, and Jamie's grip tightens with methodical precision—execution technique disguised as fun.\n\nI dive in, wrapping my arms around Jamie's waist. "Babe, let her up. She's had enough."\n\nJamie releases Morgan instantly. For a split second, as her head turns toward me, the chlorine-laced air is cut by the sharp, metallic tang of copper, so faint I almost miss it. Then it's gone, and pure confusion flickers across her face like she's waking from a dream. "What? We were just playing."\n\nDecember 7th. She brings TJ homemade tea in a mason jar—deep crimson liquid that smells like botulism and rotting flowers. "It's for your allergies," she tells him with such genuine concern that even I almost believe her.\n\nTJ takes one sip and immediately doubles over, blood streaming from both ears. "What?" Jamie asks, tilting her head with perfect confusion as he stumbles backward, hands pressed to his bleeding head. "Did I use too much ginger? I'm still learning herbal medicine."\n\nThe switch is seamless—one moment she's a calculating predator, the next she's the sweet, scattered girl I'm still crushing on. It's the most terrifying magic trick I've ever seen.\n\nDecember 8, 8PM. She climbs through my window wearing nothing but my oversized hoodie, something different happens. She curls up against me on the bed, head on my shoulder, fingers tracing idle patterns on my chest.\n\n"I missed you today," she whispers, voice catching with raw vulnerability. "Sometimes I feel like I'm forgetting who I am. Like I'm watching myself do things I'd never do." Her voice drops to barely a whisper. "But when I'm with you like this, I remember. I remember how much I wanted this before everything got so confused. Like I’m really and truly free to be myself."\n\nThis isn't the demon playing a role. This is Jamie—the real Jamie—fighting to surface through whatever's wearing her skin.\n\nAnd the sad part? I'm falling for a demon wearing my crush's body, and she's falling for the boy she was meant to destroy. It should be a tragedy.\n\nInstead, it feels like the most honest thing in this broken reality.\n\n**********\n\nDecember 13th.\n\n"Any plans after these exams are over?" I ask Morgan, as we pack up our notes Wednesday afternoon. The home stretch of finals week. The question sounds casual enough, but we both know I'm really asking if she thinks we'll all survive whatever Jamie's planning next.\n\nMorgan corners me before I can escape, worry etched so deep in her expression it looks like it was carved there with a knife. "She's getting bolder," she says, pressing a stack of Polaroids into my hands with trembling fingers.\n\nThe photos show Jamie's reflection in the lab mirrors—normal in most frames, but in one split-second capture, something else looks back. Something with too many teeth arranged in concentric circles, eyes that reflect light like a cat's but in colors that don't have names.\n\n"She almost poisoned three people yesterday with that 'special brownies' batch," Morgan continues, voice dropping to barely above a whisper. "And I caught her putting something in the water fountains that made them bubble like acid. The janitor had to shut down the whole wing."\n\n"But she didn't succeed," I point out, shouldering my backpack and studying the photograph that shouldn't exist. "Every attempt fails. Every plan falls apart. She's either terrible at being a demon, or..."\n\n"Or what?"\n\n"Or part of her doesn't want to succeed."\n\nMorgan stares at me like I've suggested we invite Freddy Krueger to a sleepover. "Stephen, she tried to stab you with a ritual knife. She's been systematically attempting to murder our entire friend group."\n\n"And failing every time," I counter. "Or the real Jamie stops her. The girl underneath is still fighting, and I think she's winning."\n\n"That's a dangerous assumption."\n\n"Everything we do is dangerous." I start walking toward the cafeteria, Morgan keeping pace beside me like a worried shadow. "But this might be our only chance to save her. If we can strengthen the human side, maybe we can push the demon out entirely."\n\n"And if you're wrong? If you're just giving her more opportunities to kill you?"\n\nI pause at the cafeteria doors, watching Jamie through the glass. She's at our normal spot, playing MTG with Becca, TJ, and Patrick, her cards spread in neat little piles. Every few seconds her eyes flick up to scan the room like a predator checking for threats, but when she spots me watching, her face transforms—genuine smile lighting up her features, cards forgotten as she waves with the enthusiasm of someone who hasn't seen me in years instead of forty-seven minutes.\n\n"Then I die," I tell Morgan simply. "But at least I'll die trying to save someone I love."\n\n**********\n\nFriday, December 15. When exams end, the weight of another semester lifts from everyone's shoulders like a collective exhale. The hallways buzz with that particular energy that comes with freedom—three weeks stretching ahead with no homework, no tests, just the promise of sleeping in and Christmas morning and all the possibilities that winter break holds.\n\nJamie finds me at my locker, practically vibrating with excitement. "We made it," she says, throwing her arms around my neck with such genuine joy that for a moment I forget about Polaroids and poison tea. "Three whole weeks. Just us."\n\nDecember 15, 6PM. She brings chamomile tea in a thermos that smells suspiciously like almonds and peaches mixed with metal shavings. "For your insomnia," she says with perfect concern, settling beside me on the bed with the careful grace of someone who's claimed this space as her own. "I know you've been having trouble sleeping, even after all our... nightly activities." Her smile carries just a hint of something darker. "I brewed this especially for you. It should help you sleep deeply. Very deeply." The way she emphasizes the word makes my skin crawl, like she's promising rest that might last considerably longer than eight hours.\n\n"This isn't cyanide, is it?" I ask, remembering that mysterious gas leak reported in the chemistry lab last week—the one that left three students hospitalized and Jamie conspicuously absent from class.\n\nShe laughs, the sound bright and innocent. "Don't be silly. It's just herbs."\n\nSomething deep inside me—maybe love, maybe stupidity, maybe the reckless faith that comes with being a seventy-four year old trapped in a sixteen-year old body—makes me trust her with more than just my life. So I drink it. The liquid burns like acid going down, making my eyes water and my throat constrict, but I smile and tell her it tastes perfect. Her shoulders relax fractionally—another test passed, another layer of trust built between us like sedimentary rock.\n\nBut as I set the thermos aside, she catches my wrist. Her grip is gentle but her nails are just sharp enough to draw tiny crescents of blood that well up like ruby periods at the end of sentences I can't read.\n\n"You drank it," she whispers, her voice has that dual quality again—layered harmonies of girl and monster, human and something else speaking in perfect unison.\n\n"I trust you," I lie, and the words taste like deception and honey.\n\nShe cuts me off with a kiss that tastes like sulfur and desperation and something deeper—something that might actually be love wrapped in supernatural dressing.\n\n"I know you're playing me," she whispers against my lips, and there's something raw in her admission, vulnerable and sharp-edged. "Just like I'm playing you. But Stephen—" Her voice fractures for just a moment, and I swear I hear another name bleeding through, something that sounds like Aspolin spoken in the same breath, two identities colliding in her throat. "What if we're both losing the game?"\n\nHer fingers trace the line of my thighs with reverent care, mapping territories she's claimed and reclaimed night after night. "What if this is real? What if love is the one thing they can't fake or program or control?"\n\nThe question hangs between us like a prayer to gods neither of us believes in, and I realize she's right. Whatever we are—demon and dreamer, predator and prey, actor and audience—this feeling crackling in the space between our heartbeats is the most honest thing in our twisted reality.\n\n"Then we keep playing," I tell her, covering her hand with mine. Our fingers intertwine like they were designed to fit together, like some cosmic blueprint drew us for exactly this moment. "And maybe we both win."\n\nShe shifts closer, pressing her forehead to mine, and when she breathes I can smell Christmas cookies and sulfur, vanilla extract and something that might be brimstone or might just be the scent of both girl and demon falling in love.\n\n"I've been thinking about Christmas," she says, voice soft with the kind of hope that's dangerous in places like Etheris. "My parents want you to come over Christmas Day—we'll open presents with Tiffany. Speaking of which," she adds with a playful grin, poking my ribs, "please tell me you got her something. She keeps complaining that it's not fair I get to spend all my time with you instead of her. Says you're stealing her big sister away and she wants compensation in the form of Christmas presents."\n\nHer expression grows more serious, more wondering. "About what comes after winter break. Spring semester, summer. Next year. Then senior year after that. graduation, college..." She trails off, then laughs—a sound caught somewhere between joy and disbelief. "God, I'm planning a future with you. When did that happen?"\n\n"When you stopped trying to murder me quite so enthusiastically," I suggest glancing at the thermous, and she laughs again, the sound more human than anything I've heard from her in weeks.\n\n"Speaking of exams, I want to examine you more closely," she says, fingers already working at the buttons of my pants with the practiced ease of someone who's made this journey dozens of times. "All break long. Every day. Until I've memorized every inch of you."\n\nThe words should sound predatory, but they don't. They sound like promises, like plans, like a girl who's discovered that love might be worth fighting the demon in her head.\n\nLater, as we lie tangled together in the aftermath of winter's first real night of freedom, she traces patterns on my chest that feel like sigils, like protection spells, like someone writing her name on my soul in a language older than words.\n\n"I love you," she whispers against my ear.\n\n"I love you too," I whisper back.\n\nWe're both lying. We're both telling the truth. And somehow, in this fucked-up prison reality, that makes perfect sense.	12	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 12:17:49.042028-05	2026-03-05 19:39:33.717247-05	\N	38	\N	\N
103	2	13	Ch. 12: Beneath the Trailer	Christmas break should have been normal. Three weeks of sleeping late, dodging family obligations, and pretending we weren't all haunted by shadow-flickers and friends who might never have existed. For a two weeks, it almost worked. Yet here we were, going back to the scene of the crime.\n\nTJ spent most of Christmas break trying to work up the courage to kiss Morgan. She finally had to literally spell it out for him on New Year's Eve: "KISS ME. ON THE LIPS. NOW." Even then, he asked if she was sure, twice. Watching them navigate their new... whatever this is... would be adorable if it weren't so painfully awkward.\n\n"Are you going to hold my hand or just hover yours near mine like a nervous ghost?" Morgan asks as we trudge through the woods behind Wickham Park. Her camera bag bounces against her hip with each step, containing the Polaroid she borrowed from her dad's closet. She's trying to sound annoyed, but I catch the smile tugging at her lips.\n\nTJ's face goes crimson. "I didn't want to assume—"\n\n"Sweet Christ on a cracker, TJ," she mutters, grabbing his hand herself. "We've been dating for two weeks. I think hand-holding is covered under the girlfriend agreement."\n\nJamie snorts with laughter, and for a moment, everything feels wonderfully, desperately normal. Just six teenagers hiking through Florida scrubland, complaining about relationships and trying not to trip over cypress roots. The winter air carries the clean scent of dead leaves and distant rain, nothing more sinister than teenagers being teenagers.\n\nThen we crest the ridge, and the trailer sits there like a cancer on reality.\n\n"What the actual fuck," Morgan breathes, her scientific skepticism cracking like old paint. "That wasn't here before. That can't be here. It’s supposed to just be Wickham Road. I've mapped this area for my environmental science project."\n\nBut it is here. The same weathered aluminum trailer from our shared nightmare, squatting between pine trees that have grown around it like the forest is trying to digest something it can't stomach. Spanish moss drapes from branches that shouldn't exist this close to suburban Wickham Park, creating a canopy so thick the afternoon sun barely penetrates.\n\n"We shouldn't be here," I mutter, staring at the impossible structure. "The first time we came here, we almost died. The second time, Andrew nearly had a stroke when Benji materialized out of thin air. And now we're back because you pushed us, Morgan."\n\nShe flinches but doesn't let go of TJ's hand. "I know how this sounds—"\n\n"Do you?" Becca interrupts, her voice tight with barely controlled hysteria. "Because it sounds like we're risking our lives because you had a bad dream."\n\n"It wasn't a dream!" Morgan snaps, her scientific composure finally cracking. "I wish it was a dream. Dreams I can explain. Dreams follow logical patterns based on neurological processes and subconscious anxiety manifestation."\n\nShe takes a shaky breath, looking at each of us in turn. "Two nights ago, I was in my makeshift darkroom, reviewing photos from when Benji appeared. I was being methodical—looking for light anomalies, double exposures, anything that could explain what the fuck we saw through rational means."\n\nHer hands tremble as she continues. "That's when Whitney appeared."\n\n"Whitney?" Becca interrupts, confusion creasing her forehead.\n\n"See? That's exactly what I mean," Morgan says, her voice rising. "When you all kept talking about Whitney before, I thought you were crazy. I had no clue who you were talking about. Complete blank. Like trying to remember a word in a language I'd never learned."\n\nShe stares at the trailer, her scientific mind warring with what she experienced. "But there she was—translucent but undeniably real, standing between the enlarger and the chemical baths like she'd been waiting there all along. She spoke in this... layered voice. Multiple recordings of herself played simultaneously."\n\nMorgan's voice drops to barely a whisper. "She said, 'The journal. It's waiting in the place that shouldn't be. Behind the ridge where the trees remember pain. Find it before they erase me completely.'"\n\n"Jesus," TJ mutters.\n\n"When I blinked, she was gone. But the smell lingered—cloves and cigarette smoke." Morgan looks directly at me. "I've spent two days trying to rationalize what I saw. Stress-induced psychosis. Chemical exposure from darkroom fumes. Sleep deprivation. I checked everything—air quality, my psychological state, even possible hallucinogenic compounds in the development chemicals."\n\nHer grip on TJ's hand tightens. "But after she appeared to me, it was like a dam burst. All these memories came flooding back. Whitney in calculus class, asking if she could borrow my notes. Whitney at lunch, talking about some pagan ritual she'd read about. Whitney crying in the bathroom after her parents kicked her out."\n\nHer voice cracks slightly. "I remembered her. All of it. How could I forget someone who mattered that much?"\n\nBut even as she speaks, her voice wavers. Because the trailer door is breathing.\n\nNot like the PTSD-remembered nightmare where shadows damned another five of us to hell. The goddamn thing inhales and exhales in a rhythm that makes my pulse race to match it. The aluminum siding expands and contracts like diseased lungs, and with each exhale, the yellowed newspaper covering the windows flutters despite the complete absence of wind.\n\nPatrick stands closest to the threshold, drawn forward like iron filings to a magnet. He's been different since winter break—quieter, more distant, like he's listening to conversations the rest of us can't hear. Now he whispers Whitney's name in a voice that carries too far in the still air, each repetition making the shadows beneath the trailer stretch and writhe, reaching toward him with finger-like tendrils.\n\n"We shouldn't be here," Jamie says suddenly, her voice sharp with an edge I've never heard before. Her grip on my arm is painful, fingernails digging through my jacket hard enough to draw blood. "This is wrong. This is so fucking wrong. We need to leave. Now."\n\nShe's shaking, her usual confident facade cracking to reveal something underneath that looks like genuine terror. Her eyes dart between the trailer and the tree line, as if she's calculating escape routes or watching for something to emerge from the shadows.\n\n"Jamie, what—" I start, but she cuts me off.\n\n"You don't understand," she hisses, pressing closer to me. "Some doors shouldn't be opened. Some places shouldn't be entered. This is one of them. Whatever's in there, it's been waiting. And...I just can’t go back."\n\n"Waiting for what?" TJ asks.\n\nJamie's eyes flicker between brown and something deeper, darker—like looking down a well at midnight. "For you. For all of you. It's been patient, but patience has limits."\n\nThe clay sigil pulses against my palm like a living heart, the metal growing warm despite the cool air. We'd crafted it over Christmas break from the ashes of the black object that remained after Benji's talisman burned out, mixing it with clay and reforming it into something that felt protective.\n\nTJ makes a nervous joke about horror movie clichés, but his voice cracks on the punchline. The sound dies in air that feels too thick, too charged, like the moment before lightning strikes. Morgan's camera clicks once, twice, the flash creating stark shadows that seem to move independently of our bodies.\n\n"The windows," Becca whispers, pointing with a trembling finger. "Look at the windows."\n\nThe yellowed newspaper covering them shifts and writhes, but not from any earthly wind. Shapes move behind the glass—geometries that hurt to track with human eyes, folding in on themselves in ways that make my brain stutter and restart. The door itself is painted white, or was once, but now it's stained with handprints. Dozens of them. All different sizes, pressed into the wood from the inside, as if people had been clawing to get out.\n\n"The sigil's getting hot," I manage, my voice sounding foreign in my own throat.\n\nPatrick takes another step toward the trailer, his face slack with something that might be recognition or might be hunger. The shadows beneath the aluminum siding reach for him like desperate lovers, and I swear I can hear Whitney's voice calling from somewhere inside—not words, just the musical cadence of her laugh, distorted by distance and something that might be pain.\n\n"Patrick, stop," I call out, but he's already moving with the inexorable certainty of someone who's found exactly what they've been looking for.\n\nJamie's grip on my arm tightens until I can feel her pulse through her fingertips—too fast, too irregular, like her heart is trying to pound its way out of her chest. "Don't," she pleads, her voice layered with harmonics that make my bones ache. "Don't you fucking dare go near that thing.”\n\nBut Patrick's hand is already on the door handle, his fingers fitting perfectly into grooves worn by countless other hands. The metal should be cold, should be normal, but I can see steam rising from where his skin makes contact.\n\n"Whatever happens in there," I say, pressing the sigil against the door frame, "we don't let go of each other. We don't leave anyone behind."\n\nThe moment the sigil touches wood, the world screams.\n\nNot sound—something deeper, something that bypasses the ears and goes straight to the primitive parts of our brains that still remember when we were prey. The trailer door swings open with the slow inevitability of a coffin lid, revealing nothing but darkness that seems to have weight and texture, like black velvet made of absence.\n\nThe floor inside doesn't just collapse. It tears like wet fabric, reality ripping open along seams we never knew existed. Below, an impossible staircase spirals down into murky water, each step too narrow, too steep, existing in dimensions that make my eyes water to follow. The wood feels organic under my feet, less like construction material and more like the calcified remains of something that once lived and breathed and feared the dark.\n\nThe water below reflects faces that aren't ours—twisted, agonized expressions that mouth silent warnings we can't hear but understand in our bones: Turn back. Run. Forget you were ever here.\n\n"Jesus Christ," TJ whispers, his voice echoing strangely in the space that shouldn't exist. "This is like something out of that Escher print my mom has in her office, except..."\n\n"Except wet and terrifying," Morgan finishes, raising her camera with hands that shake so badly I'm surprised she can aim. "And probably violating the laws of thermodynamics."\n\nPatrick is already three steps down, drawn by love and loss and something else—something that wears Whitney's voice like a mask to hide its true nature. His feet find purchase on steps that shimmer into existence just before he needs them, the wood groaning under his weight with sounds that are disturbingly organic.\n\n"We're actually doing this," Becca breathes, more statement than question.\n\nJamie looks at each of us in turn, her expression cycling through fear, resignation, and something that might be pride. "If we're going to be incredibly stupid," she says, her voice steadying, "let's at least be stupid together."\n\nAnd we descend anyway, because Patrick's already gone, and Whitney's voice echoes up from the depths, calling us home to a place we've never been but somehow remember—a place where the water tastes of blood and childhood disappointment, where our deepest fears float past like carnival prizes we never wanted to win.\n\nThe moment my feet touch the impossible stairs, I know we've crossed a threshold we can never uncross. The wood beneath us pulses with the rhythm of a massive heart, and somewhere in the darkness below, something vast and patient settles back to wait for us to reach the bottom, where all our questions will finally be answered in ways we'll spend the rest of our lives trying to forget.\n\n**********\n\nThe first step down feels like stepping into a mouth.\n\nThe wood beneath my foot isn't wood anymore—it's something organic, yielding slightly under my weight with the texture of calcified cartilage. Each step exists in too many dimensions at once, my brain struggling to process stairs that curve back on themselves like a Möbius strip made of bone and memory. The flashlight beam fractures when it hits the water below, splitting into prismatic colors that spell out words in languages I don't recognize but somehow understand in the primitive parts of my brain that still remember being prey.\n\n"This is wrong," Jamie whispers behind me, her voice tight with something beyond fear. "We shouldn't be here. I can't... I won't let them take me again."\n\nThe water around our ankles is warm, too warm, with the consistency of syrup and the metallic taste of old pennies. It climbs past our shins as we descend, releasing bubbles that burst with whispered names—people we should remember but don't, friends who were erased so completely that only phantom grief remains. Sarah. Michael. David. Crystal. Each name makes our chests tighten with loss we can't explain.\n\nMorgan's camera flashes, and in the brief illumination I see things that shouldn't exist floating past us. My Thundercats lunchbox from third grade, metal rusted into patterns that look like screaming faces. When it opens on its own, something pours out—not maggots, but fragments of memories, pale and writhing, spelling out Whitney's name in the dark water before dissolving into rivulets that taste of copper and childhood disappointment.\n\n"Jesus fucking Christ," TJ mutters, then immediately claps his hand over his mouth as his words echo back wrong, chopped up and rearranged: Christ fucking Jesus. Fucking Jesus Christ. Jesus Christ fucking.\n\nThe echo doesn't stop. It multiplies, layer upon layer of profanity bouncing off walls that pulse like the inside of something's throat, until the sound becomes a chant, a prayer, a blasphemy that makes our teeth ache.\n\n"Don't talk," Becca whispers. "Don't touch anything. Just... just keep moving."\n\nPatrick moves ahead of us like he's sleepwalking, his feet finding steps that materialize just before he needs them. The liquid around our legs never getting deeper despite descending under the surface. Whitney's name falls from his lips in a constant litany, and each utterance makes the shadows stretch closer—not threatening yet, but watching. Waiting for something.\n\nJamie stumbles behind me, her breathing coming in sharp gasps. "They're holding back," she says, her voice layered with harmonics that make my bones resonate. "Something's keeping them from... but they want to. God, they want to so badly."\n\nThe walls around us begin to shift. What started as weathered wood planks now shows glimpses of something else beneath—surfaces that glisten with moisture, veined with networks of blue-black vessels that pulse in rhythm with our heartbeats. We're not descending into a basement. We're being swallowed, each step taking us deeper into something's digestive system.\n\nTJ's tenth birthday balloon floats past, deflating and re-inflating with breathing that doesn't belong to any of us. The latex is covered in bite marks—small, desperate teeth marks that speak of hunger and fear. When Becca reaches for it instinctively, her fingers come away slick with saliva that's still warm.\n\n"Don't," I try to say, but my words come out backwards: Anything touch don't.\n\nThe wrongness is accelerating now. Each step down makes reality stretch thinner, like plastic wrap pulled too tight over something vast and angular. A cassette tape drifts toward us, bleeding actual crimson that spirals through the water in helical patterns. When it bumps against Morgan's leg, it starts playing—our own voices, recorded conversations that haven't happened yet.\n\nI hear myself screaming. TJ begging for something to stop. Jamie's laughter mixed with sounds that might be sobbing or might be ecstasy. The tape rewinds and plays again, and this time the voices are different. Older. Talking about Red Hat portals to Sorhhinda and deaths we haven't died yet, in places we've never been.\n\n"I can't go back," Jamie whispers, her grip on my arm becoming painful. "Please don't make me go back. I won't survive it again. I can't be that thing again."\n\nHer skin ripples under my touch, something vast moving beneath the surface like a kraken trying to surface in too-shallow water. But she's fighting it, holding whatever's inside her at bay through sheer force of will.\n\nThe stairs beneath our feet shift like living muscle now, undulating in waves that force us to grab onto walls that feel like flesh stretched over broken glass. Our voices echo back to us in fragments, prophecies carved from our own fears: He's down there. She's down there. They're all down there, waiting for us to remember how to die.\n\nMy flashlight beam catches something moving in the water ahead—a child's rocking horse, its painted eyes weeping tears of rust, rocking back and forth with no rider. But when the light hits it directly, I see the small skeleton still strapped to its back, tiny finger bones gripping the reins, jaw open in a scream that echoes through dimensions.\n\n"How much further?" Morgan asks, her scientific composure finally cracking. Her camera hangs forgotten around her neck, its flash having revealed too many impossible things. "This defies every law of physics I know. We should have reached the water table by now. We should have hit bedrock."\n\nBut Patrick keeps descending, drawn by love and loss toward something that wears Whitney's voice like a mask. Each step takes him closer to whatever waits at the bottom, and we follow because abandoning him isn't an option, even as the darkness presses closer and our sanity frays like old rope.\n\nBy all accounts we should be miles under water now, but it rises only past our waists now, thick as blood and warm as fever dreams. Objects from our collective past float by like debris from a sunken childhood: Barbie dolls with too many joints, their plastic faces melted into expressions of terminal agony. A Big Wheel tricycle pedaling itself through the murk, its rider long gone but the seat still warm with phantom heat.\n\nJamie's breathing becomes ragged, her human mask slipping with each step down. "The barriers are weakening," she gasps. "I can feel them watching. Feel them wanting. They've been patient so long, but patience has limits."\n\nThe walls pulse faster now, in rhythm with our racing hearts. Symbols appear and disappear in the organic surfaces—geometric patterns that hurt to look at directly, equations written in scripts that predate human mathematics. The air thickens until each breath feels like drowning in syrup made of screams.\n\nAnd then, finally, we reach the bottom.\n\nThe spiral staircase opens into a chamber that exists in too many dimensions at once, its walls curving away into impossible distances while simultaneously pressing close enough to touch. At the center sits what might have been an altar in some other reality—a stone slab carved with symbols that shift and writhe when you're not looking directly at them.\n\nWhitney's journal rests on its surface like an offering to gods that should never be fed.\n\nThe journal breathes. Actually, visibly breathes, its blood-fused pages rising and falling like diseased lungs. The cover adorned with her signature J’s pulses with veins that weren't there before, dark arterial networks spreading across leather that glistens with moisture. Around the altar, standing water reflects nothing but absolute darkness, as if the surface itself has become a wound in reality.\n\nPatrick reaches for it first, his hands shaking so violently I think his bones might snap. The moment his fingertips brush the cover, the breathing stops.\n\nComplete silence. So profound it makes our eardrums ache with phantom pressure.\n\nThen the pages begin to turn themselves.\n\nWet sounds. Like skin peeling away from muscle. Like flesh remembering how to scream.\n\nAnd that's when the shadows stop being patient.\n\nThat's when Jamie's mask finally shatters.\n\nThat's when we realize we've walked willingly into the mouth of something that's been waiting to feed for far too long.\n\nThe journal opens to pages written in multiple hands—some in ink that looks fresh despite being underwater, others carved so deep they've gouged through multiple sheets. Words burn themselves into my vision: "The ones before me. The ones who broke. They don't erase you all at once. They fracture you, piece by piece, until even the demons forget your screams."\n\nNames appear and disappear across the pages like living things—scratched out violently, gouged so deep they've torn through to pages beneath, only to reappear in fresh blood somewhere else. Patrick's name bleeds through from other sheets, sometimes half-erased, sometimes written in handwriting that looks disturbingly like his own.\n\nThe water around the altar begins to churn, releasing bubbles that carry whispered fragments: Help me. Find me. Remember me. Whitney's voice, but layered with harmonics that suggest she's speaking from multiple dimensions simultaneously, her consciousness scattered across realities like shattered glass.\n\nAnd that's when Jamie moves.\n\nIt starts as a ripple beneath her skin, like something vast uncoiling under too-small flesh. Her bones rearrange themselves with wet popping sounds, joints bending in directions that shouldn't exist in human anatomy. When she lunges for the journal, her movement isn't human—it's predatory, serpentine, something designed by evolution that never touched Earth.\n\n"It's not for you—!" she snarls, and her voice carries frequencies that make our teeth ache and our vision blur.\n\nJamie's inhuman snarl cuts through the chaos like a blade through silk, but her lunge for the journal never connects. Instead, she crashes into something invisible, her body slamming against barriers we can't see with a sound like breaking glass wrapped in wet meat.\n\n"The containment protocols," she gasps, dark blood trickling from her nose. "They're still active. I can't... I can't touch it directly."\n\nThat's when reality detonates.\n\nThe air itself splits along invisible seams, revealing something vast and writhing beyond the wound—an eye the size of a cathedral, ancient and hungry and so alien that looking at it makes my brain stutter and restart. The pupil dilates with recognition, focusing on us with the weight of cosmic attention, and I feel my sanity begin to fray at the edges like old rope.\n\nThe walls don't just crack—they explode outward and inward simultaneously, revealing themselves as honeycomb chambers riddled with holes. Thousands of them. Millions. Each opening perfectly circular, ranging from pinprick size to gaping maws large enough to swallow a person whole. The surfaces writhe with this impossible architecture of apertures, and in each hole, I see us.\n\nDifferent versions of us. Endless variations trapped in recursive torture.\n\nIn the largest hole, directly above the altar, I see myself succeeding—grabbing the journal and running—only to watch as the book transforms in my hands, becoming a mouth that devours me from the inside out while I scream apologies to friends who are already dead in realities that never had the chance to exist.\n\n"GET THE JOURNAL!" Whitney's voice cuts through the chaos like a scalpel made of pure desperation.\n\n"PATRICK, GET THE JOURNAL AND RUN!"\n\nBut Patrick isn't running. He's reaching for the bleeding book, and the moment his fingers approach the cover, invisible forces tear into his flesh like razors made of pure malice.\n\nHis scream cuts through the chaos—raw, animalistic, the sound of someone being flayed alive. But he doesn't pull back. His arms stretch toward the journal even as something unseen shreds the muscle from his bones, peeling away strips of skin that hang like bloody ribbons. The protection around the book doesn't just hurt—it devours, consuming his flesh in spiraling patterns that reveal white bone beneath.\n\n"Patrick, stop!" I scream, but he's beyond hearing. His blood sprays across the journal's cover, mixing with the crimson already seeping from its pages. Where his torn flesh touches the leather binding, the book pulses brighter, as if feeding on his sacrifice.\n\n"The book can never leave," Whitney's voice echoes from somewhere beyond the holes in reality, layered with anguish and desperate warning. "It's the anchor. It's the chain. It's how they keep us here."\n\nPatrick's left arm is nothing but exposed bone now, tendons hanging like harp strings, but still he reaches. His fingers, stripped to skeletal remains, finally make contact with the journal's spine. The moment bone touches leather, the screaming protection field collapses, and he clutches the book to his ruined chest with arms that are more absence than presence.\n\nCorridors fold back on themselves in recursive loops that hurt to perceive. I watch TJ disappear around a corner that leads back to where he started, his panicked shouts echoing from directions that don't exist in three-dimensional space. Morgan's camera flashes frantically, trying to document impossibilities that no lens was designed to capture.\n\nThe journal's breathing stops entirely, and something vast in the darkness notices. Notices that its anchor is moving. Notices that someone has paid the price in blood and bone and stubborn love.\n\nPatrick turns to us, his face a mask of agony but his eyes bright with terrible purpose. Blood pours from his destroyed arms, but he holds the journal like it's the most precious thing in any universe.\n\n"I can carry it," he gasps through teeth gritted against pain that should have killed him. "But I can't leave with it. That's the trade. That's always been the trade."\n\nThe water around us begins to boil with increasing violence, releasing steam that carries the compressed screams of everyone who was ever erased. The sound makes our eardrums bleed, but through it all, Whitney's voice keeps cutting through with impossible clarity.\n\n"I'm holding the door!" Her words carry weight, substance, the accumulated mass of months spent clawing at the barriers between worlds. "But I can't hold it much longer! Patrick, you have to choose—NOW!"\n\nPatrick finally manages to clasp the journal to his chest, but the moment he does, invisible forces seize him. His body stretches like taffy, caught in a dimensional vortex that's pulling him in multiple directions at once. I watch in horror as his limbs begin to elongate, his torso twisting into impossible configurations while he screams through gritted teeth.\n\n"Someone take it," he whimpers, his voice distorting as reality tears him apart molecule by molecule. "Please, someone take it before—"\n\nHis words cut off as something snaps inside him with a wet, cartilaginous sound. Blood pours from his mouth, but still he holds the journal, even as the vortex shreds him like paper in a blender.\n\n"Patrick, NO!" Becca screams, lunging toward him with desperate fury. She grabs for his outstretched arm, but the vortex is tearing him apart at the molecular level. Her fingers only manage to rip away strips of flesh that come apart like wet paper in her hands, blood and sinew stretching like grotesque taffy before snapping.\n\nShe stumbles backward in horror, clutching pieces of him that are already dissolving, and loses her footing on the blood-slicked stone. As she falls, Morgan dives to catch her, but the dimensional storm catches Morgan's arm and drags her toward the vortex with a sound like reality tearing.\n\n"Morgan!" TJ screams, grabbing her legs as she's pulled toward the swirling chaos. Her shoulder dislocates with a wet pop, and she shrieks in agony as the vortex tries to claim her too.\n\nPatrick watches this unfold, his body stretching and contracting like a rubber band on the verge of snapping. Half his face is gone, torn away by forces that exist outside our understanding of physics, but his remaining eye fixes on me with desperate intensity.\n\n"TAKE THE FUCKING BOOK!" he roars through a throat that's more absence than flesh.\n\n"TAKE IT, YOU ASSHOLES, BEFORE I DIE FOR NOTHING!"\n\nWith his last coherent movement, he hurls the journal toward me. The book sails through the air trailing droplets of blood that hang suspended like ruby stars, and I catch it against my chest just as Patrick's body finally gives up its fight against the impossible forces tearing him apart.\n\nThe moment the journal leaves his hands, everything changes.\n\nThe shadows rush in like a black tide—not just darkness, but writhing tentacles of living void that pour from every surface with hungry intelligence. They reach for us with claws made of crystallized screams, trying to drag us into the same dimensional storm that's consuming Patrick.\n\n"RUN!" Morgan gasps, her dislocated arm hanging useless as TJ helps her to her feet. "The anchor's breaking! We have seconds!"\n\nThe shadows wrap around my ankles like liquid obsidian, burning cold through my jeans. Becca claws at the space where Patrick used to be, still trying to hold onto someone who's already been scattered across dimensions, sobbing his name as reality collapses around us.\n\nI grab her arm with my free hand, the journal burning against my chest like a brand. "He's gone! We have to GO!"\n\nThe dimensional fracture begins to close with Patrick's sacrifice complete, but not before the hungry dark tries one last chance to feed. Tentacles of living shadow wrap around our limbs, our throats, trying to pull us back into the throat-world where we'll become part of the feast instead of escaping it.\n\nWe fight our way toward the dissolving stairs, each step a battle against forces that want nothing more than to add our screams to the symphony of the forgotten. Behind us, the last echoes of Patrick's voice fade into the screaming mouth of something that's learned to hunger across dimensions.\n\nBut we're carrying Whitney's journal now. The price has been paid in blood and bone and stubborn love.\n\nAnd somewhere in the collapsing darkness, I swear I can hear Patrick's laughter—not pain, but triumph. He held the door just long enough.\n\nJust long enough for us to remember.\n\nJust long enough for us to run.\n\nThe shadows wrap around us like living chains, and I grab Becca's arm as she fights against me with animalistic fury.\n\n"I WON'T LEAVE HIM!" she screams, clawing at my face hard enough to draw blood. "WE CAN'T JUST LEAVE HIM!"\n\n"He's GONE!" I shout back, but she's beyond reason, trying to throw herself back into the collapsing vortex where Patrick's remains are being scattered across dimensions. TJ appears beside me, his Reagan shirt torn and bloodied, and together we drag her toward the dissolving stairs.\n\nMorgan stumbles up behind us, her dislocated shoulder making her movements jerky and wrong. Blood streams from the gash on her forehead, but her eyes burn with a fury I've never seen before. She turns back toward the chaos below and screams at the shadows with pure, scientific rage.\n\n"YOU ARE JUST A FUCKING SYSTEM!" Morgan screams, tears and blood mixing on her face. "You follow rules! You have inputs and outputs! I will find your variables! I will dissect you! I will turn you into a fucking data set and I will write the equation that UNMAKES you!"\n\nThe shadows recoil slightly, as if her rage has actual substance, actual weight in this place where emotion bleeds into physics.\n\nThat's when I realize Jamie's gone.\n\nIn the panic and horror, she simply vanished. We race toward the stairs—me and TJ dragging the still-fighting Becca, Morgan climbing one-handed behind us—and I look up to see Jamie's silhouette at the top of the staircase.\n\nFor a heart-stopping moment, she appears to be closing the trailer door. Her hand rests on the edge, and her posture suggests she's about to seal us down here forever. The demonic part of her mission finally taking control.\n\n"JAMIE, NO!" I scream, but then I see the rope.\n\nShe's tied climbing rope around her waist, anchored to something outside, and she's not closing the door—she's holding it open, wanting to dive back in after us. Her face is a mask of desperate concentration as she fights against whatever's trying to take control of her body.\n\n"The anchor's failing!" she shouts down to us, her voice layering with inhuman harmonics. "I can feel it breaking! CLIMB FASTER!"\n\nThe stairs beneath our feet begin to dissolve like sugar in acid. Becca finally stops fighting when she realizes the step she's standing on is turning to mist, and she scrambles upward with the rest of us, sobbing Patrick's name.\n\nJamie lowers herself into the collapsing throat-world, the rope burning through her hands as she reaches for us. Her skin ripples with barely contained alien geometry, but her brown eyes stay locked on mine with purely human determination.\n\n"Take my hand!" she screams, stretching toward us as reality folds in on itself. "NOW!"\n\nMorgan reaches her first, Jamie hauling her up with inhuman strength despite Morgan's dead weight. Then TJ, who grabs the rope and swings himself toward the dissolving doorway. I push Becca toward Jamie's outstretched hand, the journal burning against my chest like a brand.\n\n"I can't hold it!" Whitney's voice echoes from the collapsing chamber below, layered with agony and desperate love. "The door's closing! GO! GO! GO!"\n\nThe darkness rushes up behind us like a living tsunami, and I feel claws of crystallized shadow tear at my legs as I leap for Jamie's hand. Her fingers close around my wrist with crushing force, and she hauls me up through the dissolving doorway just as the entire structure begins to collapse inward.\n\nThe last thing I see before we reach the top is Patrick's face in the churning darkness below—still crying out in agony, still choosing love over survival, even as whatever remains of him are being torn into nothing by the screaming mouth of something that's learned to hunger\n\n**********\n\nWe burst into daylight like drowning victims breaking the surface, gasping and covered in swamp water that evaporates like mist the moment it touches real air. The sunlight feels like daggers after the organic darkness below, too bright, too clean, too utterly normal for what we've just survived. My lungs burn as I gulp down oxygen that doesn't taste of blood and forgotten screams.\n\nBecca collapses into the grass, her body folding in on itself like her bones forgot how to hold her up. The sobs come in waves—violent, body-wracking, each one tearing out of her like her chest is trying to turn itself inside out. Her hands claw at the earth as if digging could somehow undo the last hour, fingers tearing up weeds and dirt as if grief could be exorcised with enough effort. She chokes on her own breath, gasping between sobs that sound more animal than human. No words. Just the raw, primal howling of a heart that can’t take one more loss.\n\nThen she goes still.\n\nHer eyes snap to mine with laser focus, tears still streaming—but now there’s something else burning underneath. Rage. Weaponized sorrow. A need to blame before it drowns her too.\n\n"This is YOUR fault," she snarls, her voice raw from screaming and barely human anymore. "It all started with your fucking concussion and those damn death visions! If we'd never gotten wrapped up in your fucking delusions, we'd never have been here!"\n\n"Becca—" I start, but she's beyond reason now.\n\n“He stayed after class with me every week! He taught me how to shuffle Magic cards!” Her words hit like physical blows. "Patrick was good. Patrick was kind. Patrick actually gave a shit about people, and now he's been erased because you couldn't keep your dick out of whatever Jamie really is!"\n\nShe whirls around, pointing at Jamie with eyes full of violent fury. "And then you chose HER!"\n\nJamie takes a step back, her face pale but resigned, like she's been expecting this moment.\n\n"You fucking bitch!" Becca screams, lunging toward her with claws extended. "You stole Stephen from me! You dragged us all here! You sacrificed Patrick to your fucking evil gods!"\n\nMorgan intercepts her just before she reaches Jamie, wrapping her good arm around Becca's waist and holding her back with surprising strength. "Becca, stop!"\n\nJamie doesn't fight back. Doesn't even raise her hands to defend herself. She just stands there, utterly exhausted, her brown eyes flickering with something that might be guilt or might be relief that someone's finally saying what they're all thinking.\n\n"Let me go!" Becca thrashes against Morgan's hold. "She killed him! She killed Patrick and she made us watch!"\n\n"She saved us," I say quietly, but the words feel hollow even to me.\n\n"SAVED US?" Becca's voice cracks with hysteria. "We went in as six and came out as five! How is that saving us?"\n\nHer rage collapses as suddenly as it erupted, turning into uncontrollable sobs that shake her entire body. The fight goes out of her all at once, and she sags against Morgan like a broken marionette.\n\n"Why me?" she whispers through the tears, her voice small and lost. "I'm sorry, God, what have I been doing wrong? Why do you keep taking everyone I love?"\n\nWe have to guide her back to the car carefully, like she's made of glass. She lets us move her, unresisting now, just sobbing quietly and asking the empty sky questions that will never be answered.\n\nTJ opens the car door with shaking hands, his usual Reagan-era commentary nowhere to be found. Morgan helps ease Becca into the passenger seat, her own injuries making the movement awkward and painful.\n\nJamie walks behind us in silence, her footsteps making no sound on the d