rblind tests," TJ suggests, but his voice lacks conviction. "Different people see different patterns."\n\n"Or maybe," Andrew says, still grinning with the determination of someone who refuses to let reality kill his fantasy, "it's trying to tell us something about the cosmic significance of guys named Andrew. Andrew Carnegie, Andrew Jackson, Andrew Lloyd Webber—we're all connected by some kind of interdimensional brotherhood of awesome."\n\nTJ throws a pencil at him. It bounces off Andrew's forehead and lands in a jar of preserved sheep brains with a wet plop. "Dude, shut up. You're not the main character of this reality."\n\n"Says you," Andrew retorts, rubbing his forehead. "But what if I am? What if this whole nightmare has been waiting for me to step up and embrace my destiny as Andrew the Destroyer? Andrew the Magnificent? Andrew the—"\n\n"Andrew the Delusional," Morgan interrupts, but there's fondness in her voice.\n\nI look down at the journal again. The ink seems to shimmer like oil on water, and as I watch, the letters actually move—not dramatically, just a subtle shift, like someone adjusting the focus on a microscope. The smell of formaldehyde intensifies, mixing with something else that reminds me of burnt hair and ozone.\n\n"Look," Morgan whispers, her voice tight with something between wonder and terror. "It's writing itself."\n\nNew words bleed across the page in fresh ink, forming sentences that none of us have seen before. I read them aloud, my voice barely a whisper: "Reality is breaking down. Aspolin must remember the boy. You must find the boy."\n\nThe moment the words leave my lips, the classroom shifts.\n\nMs. Rodriguez's skeleton—the one that's been grinning at us from the corner since we walked in—is gone. In its place hangs a massive laminated poster of the human reproductive system, complete with anatomically correct diagrams and labels in Comic Sans font that somehow makes the whole thing more disturbing than educational.\n\nWe all see it. We all stare at it.\n\n"That wasn't there before," Morgan says quietly.\n\n"No," TJ agrees, his voice hollow. "It definitely wasn't."\n\n"Reality is breaking down," I repeat, the journal's words still burning on my tongue. The truth of it settles into my bones like cold water. "We're not even seeing the same words anymore, but we're still sharing the same... glitches. We're no longer in the same world. We're just... brushing up against each other's versions."\n\nThe room falls silent except for the distant hum of the heating system and the muffled chaos of lunch period in the hallway—lockers slamming, someone laughing, a teacher yelling about running in corridors. Normal sounds from a normal world that suddenly feels impossibly far away.\n\n"That's..." Andrew starts, then stops. The joke's gone out of him now, replaced by something that looks uncomfortably like fear. "That's completely fucked up."\n\n"Yeah," Morgan agrees quietly, unconsciously leaning closer to TJ, who still hasn't noticed. "That about covers it."\n\nThe end of lunch bell rings, but it sounds wrong—too long, too resonant, like it's echoing through dimensions before reaching our ears. We all look at each other, and I can see the same thought written across every face: if we can't even agree on what we're reading, how can we trust anything else we think we know?\n\nJamie closes the journal with a soft thud that somehow sounds final. "Well," she says, slinging her backpack over her shoulder, "this is either the beginning of something really interesting, or we're all about to get fitted for matching straitjackets."\n\nAs we file out of the classroom, I catch Andrew muttering to himself: "Anthony... Angelo... Aristotle... Definitely not as heroic-sounding as Andrew, though..."\n\nBut I'm not thinking about names anymore. I'm thinking about a book I apparently wrote but can't remember writing, and a word that feels like it belongs to me even though I've never heard it before.\n\nAspolin.\n\nThe word tastes like electricity and old dreams, and maybe just a hint of formaldehyde.\n\n**********\n\nMr. Hammond's voice drones like a broken air conditioner as he discusses The Catcher in the Rye, a book that technically shouldn't even be on our reading list anymore. He keeps glancing at the door like he's expecting Principal Martinez to burst in and confiscate every copy, muttering about how the state legislature thinks Holden Caulfield has "too much latent homosexuality" for impressionable Florida minds. The irony isn't lost on me—here we are, discussing a banned book about a kid having a mental breakdown, while I'm actively having my own psychological meltdown in real time.\n\n"Now, Salinger's use of stream-of-consciousness narrative reflects Holden's deteriorating mental state," Hammond says, pacing between the rows of desks like a caged animal. "Notice how his thoughts become increasingly fragmented, jumping from topic to topic without logical connection."\n\nI'm trying to take notes, really I am, but the words on the page keep shifting. My handwriting looks like it's been done by three different people—sometimes neat and controlled, sometimes jagged and desperate, sometimes in a script I don't recognize at all. The margins are filling up with doodles I don't remember drawing: spirals that hurt to look at, geometric patterns that seem to move when I'm not looking directly at them.\n\nBut it's more than just the handwriting that's wrong. Everything in this classroom carries the weight of impossibility.\n\n"The phoniness Holden perceives in the adult world represents his inability to accept the loss of innocence," Hammond continues, and something about those words—loss of innocence—hits me like a tuning fork to the skull.\n\nThe classroom dissolves.\n\nOne moment I'm staring at the chalkboard covered in Hammond's cramped analysis of Salinger's themes, and the next I'm sitting in a vast library that follows no earthly architecture. Shelves stretch upward into infinity, curving impossibly back on themselves like a mathematical equation made flesh. Books whisper their contents in languages that predate human speech, and the light has texture and weight, pressing against my skin like warm honey.\n\nAcross from me sits Lady Illkeserod, and she looks exactly as I described her in a book I apparently wrote but spent a lifetime trying to forget—tall and ethereal, with skin like polished moonstone and eyes that hold the depth of collapsed stars. She's wearing something that might be a dress or might be shadows woven into fabric, and when she moves, reality ripples around her like water disturbed by stones.\n\n"You're finally remembering," she says, and her voice bypasses my ears entirely, going straight to whatever part of the brain handles revelations and cosmic truths. "The collision is accelerating."\n\nThe name comes not as sound but as sensation—Aspolin—carved into my consciousness like a sigil made of screams and silk. It burns itself into my skull with the weight of absolute truth, and I understand with horrible clarity that this isn't the first time I've heard it. This is just the first time I'm allowing myself to remember.\n\n"This timeline is wrong," I say, though my mouth doesn't move in the library space. "They're all wrong. Becca and Patrick never got together, or even acknowledged each other’s presence. Morgan and TJ were never in the same classes. And Jamie..."\n\n"Jamie never existed," Lady Illkeserod finishes, her voice carrying the weight of cosmic sadness. "Not in your original thread. She's a creation of the collision, born from the spaces between realities. A demon wearing the face of what you needed most—hope wrapped in teenage love."\n\nThe vision shifts with dream-logic suddenness. Now I'm lying in a hospital pod, ancient hands clutching at sheets that smell like disinfectant and despair. I'm 74 years old, dying while dust storms rage outside a window streaked with grime. My reflection in the glass shows a face carved by disappointment—every failed dream, every compromise, every moment I chose comfort over courage written in the lines around my eyes.\n\n"This is what you chose then," Lady Illkeserod's voice echoes from somewhere outside time. "Safety over truth. Forgetting over remembering. You wrote their stories because you couldn't live your own. But stories have power, Aspolin. Fiction becomes flesh when enough pain bleeds into the telling."\n\nI see it now—the promise I made to scattered friends across decades of loneliness. Composite characters built from half-remembered faces in yearbook photos, kids I'd shared lunch tables with but never really knew, their names fading like old polaroids until only impressions remained. I'd taken these ghost-memories and breathed life into them, combined them with Whitney from our late-night gaming sessions and shared stories about feeling like strangers in our own skin—brilliant and funny and brave until the world's cruelty became too much to carry.\n\nI'd woven them all into Elysium Rising, giving them the adventure we never had together, the deep bonds that distance and time had denied us. It was fiction then—or so I thought. A way to honor half-forgotten friendships and create the family I'd never quite managed to build in reality, populating my cosmic horror story with the people I wished I'd been brave enough to really know.\n\nBut loneliness is a powerful editor, and regret makes even the faintest memories feel like lost love.\n\nThe hospice room dissolves into static, then reforms into the basement where we summoned Benji, then shifts again to show me writing furiously in journals that bleed ink like open wounds. Each vision layers over the last, creating a palimpsest of memory that threatens to collapse my teenage brain under the weight of lifetimes I shouldn't remember.\n\n"The revision was never meant to be permanent," Lady Illkeserod says, her form becoming more solid as my understanding deepens. "But you're choosing it. Every day you stay, every moment you let yourself love this Jamie who shouldn't exist, you're writing this timeline deeper into reality."\n\n"Stephen?" A voice from very far away, calling me back from libraries that span dimensions. "Stephen, you okay?"\n\nI gasp and clutch my desk, the plastic edge cutting into my palms like an anchor to consensus reality. Mr. Hammond has stopped mid-sentence, and every head in the class has turned to stare at me.\n\nThe classroom feels different now, like someone's adjusted the lighting or changed the air pressure. The motivational posters seem too bright, their messages about the power of reading written in fonts that hurt to look at directly. There’s a periodic table on the far wall of this English classroom that shows elements that shouldn't exist, chemical symbols that spell out words in a language I'm starting to understand despite myself.\n\n"That wasn't fiction," I whisper, the words tumbling out before I can stop them. My voice sounds hollow and strange in the sudden silence. "That wasn't a dream."\n\n"What wasn't fiction?" Mr. Hammond asks, approaching my desk with the careful posture of someone trying not to spook a wild animal.\n\n"Elysium Rising," I say, and the name tastes like electricity and old dreams. "It was real. She gave me that name—Aspolin—because she wanted me to remember I was more than just a prisoner."\n\nBecause that's what this is, isn't it? A collision of timelines that should never have intersected, held together by the desperate love of a dying old man who couldn't bear to let his friends go. In the reality where I died at seventy-four—where that concussion on the wrestling mat was just the final echo of a life already lived—none of this existed.\n\nAnd Whitney... Whitney didn't enter my life until I was twenty-three, when we met in some forgotten corner of the internet, bonding over late-night gaming sessions and shared stories about feeling like strangers in our own skin. She was brilliant and funny and brave in ways that made the rest of us look like cowards, but the world... the world wasn't ready for someone like her. Wasn't kind to people who dared to live their truth when that truth made others uncomfortable. She tried to stay, tried to carve out a space for herself in a reality that kept telling her she didn't belong.\n\nBut sometimes even the strongest souls can only carry so much weight before they break.\n\nI'd promised them all, in that other timeline, that someday I'd write them into a book. A promise I kept when I penned Elysium Rising, weaving their essences into characters who fought cosmic horror and bureaucratic demons. It was fiction then—or so I thought. A way to honor friendships scattered across time and geography, to give them the adventure we never had together.\n\nExcept now they're all here, in the wrong years, living the wrong stories, and I'm the only one who remembers how it should have been.\n\nThe weight of memory settles into my bones like sediment, and I know with absolute certainty that the boy who walked into this classroom is not the same one who's sitting here now. Something has been unlocked, or maybe something has been broken, and there's no going back.\n\nMr. Hammond crouches next to my desk, his face creased with the kind of worry usually reserved for students threatening to jump off buildings. "Maybe you should go see the nurse," he suggests gently. "You look pretty pale."\n\nI shake my head, though the motion makes the room spin slightly. "I'm fine. Just... tired."\n\nBut I'm not fine. I can feel Lady Illkeserod's presence at the edges of my consciousness, patient and eternal, waiting for me to make the choice I've been avoiding since that night Jamie climbed through my window. Stay in this beautiful lie where my friends are young and alive and together, or remember the truth of what I've done.\n\nThe bell rings, signaling the end of class. As students file out, chattering about homework and after-school plans, I sit frozen at my desk, staring at my notebook where I've apparently been taking notes in three different handwritings.\n\nIn the margin, in script I don't recognize but somehow know intimately, someone has written:\n\n"The author remembers his own story. The revision begins to unravel."\n\nI didn't write those words. But the ink is still wet, and somewhere in the space between heartbeats, I can hear Lady Illkeserod whispering:\n\n"Choose carefully, Aspolin. Some stories are too beautiful to survive the truth."\n\n**********\n\nThe following days blur together like watercolors in rain, each one bleeding into the next with a strange, dreamlike quality that makes me wonder if time itself is becoming unstable. But through it all, Jamie transforms in ways that should terrify me but mostly just make my chest tight with something between hope and heartbreak.\n\nIt starts small. Tuesday morning, she appears at my locker with a brown bag that radiates warmth and smells like someone's grandmother spent hours in the kitchen. Inside: a sandwich cut into perfect triangles with the crusts removed, cookies that taste like love instead of sulfur, and a thermos of soup that somehow stays exactly the right temperature even though she made it at dawn.\n\n"I learned how to cook," she says, her fingers brushing mine as she passes me the bag. The contact sends that familiar temperature fluctuation through my skin—human-warm one second, then a flash of heat that reminds me of standing too close to a bonfire. But it's gentler now, less like touching molten metal and more like fever breaking. "Well, I mean, I remembered how to cook. It's weird—like muscle memory from a life I never lived."\n\nBy Wednesday, she's mastering things that should take years of practice. I watch her braid her hair in the hallway mirror between classes, her fingers moving with impossible precision, creating intricate patterns that look almost Celtic in their complexity. When I ask where she learned it, she just shrugs.\n\n"I just figured this out," she says, but there's something in her eyes that suggests deeper knowledge, older memories bleeding through from whatever she used to be.\n\nThat afternoon, she starts showing up with elaborate origami cranes folded from notebook paper, each one perfect despite the fact that she swears she's never done it before. She leaves them in my locker, on my desk, tucked into the pages of my textbooks like love notes made of geometry and patience.\n\n"My hands just... know," she explains when I find her in the library during study hall, surrounded by a small flock of paper birds. Her fingers work without conscious thought, folding and creasing with mathematical precision. "It's like there are two sets of memories in my head, and sometimes they overlap in weird ways."\n\nDuring lunch on Thursday, she sits in my lap while we pretend to study for Ms. Rodriguez's AP Bio test. Her weight against me feels impossibly real—the press of her thighs, the way she tucks her hair behind her ear when she's concentrating, the small sighs she makes when she's trying to remember how molecular bonds work. But underneath the familiar teenage gestures, there's something else: a grace that's too perfect, movements that flow like water or smoke.\n\n"I hate biology," she mutters, tracing the human fertilization diagrams in my notebook with her finger, but somehow her doodles turn the scientific illustrations into crude stick figures with exaggerated anatomical features.\n\nShe looks up at me with mischievous eyes and a smirk that's pure teenage trouble. "Though I wouldn't mind some... hands-on study sessions. You know, for academic purposes." The way she says it makes my face burn, and I'm pretty sure the other students can hear my heartbeat from across the courtyard.\n\n"Says the demon who probably helped invent half these processes," I tease, and immediately regret it when her face falls.\n\n"I wasn't always a demon," she says quietly. "I mean, I was Jamie first. Before... whatever happened to me happened. But I’m also something much more ancient, at the same time. It’s hard to explain." She looks up at me with eyes that shift between warm brown and something deeper, more ancient. "Sometimes I remember being thirteen and crying because my mom wouldn't let me get my ears pierced. That seems so stupid now."\n\nBut it's not stupid, and that's what's eating at me. Because every day, I watch her become more herself—not just human or demon, but something new, something that combines the best of both. The girl who cried about earrings and the entity who could tear holes in reality, merged into someone who makes perfect soup and folds origami with hands that still remember how to summon fire.\n\nI brush a strand of hair from her face, and she leans into the touch like a cat seeking warmth. "It's not stupid. It's human."\n\n"Yeah." Her voice is small, vulnerable in a way that makes my chest ache. "I want to be human. Or... human enough."\n\nThe confession hangs between us like something fragile, and I realize that's exactly what this is—fragile. This Jamie, this version of her that's choosing humanity over power, love over duty, is as delicate as spun glass. One wrong move, one moment of doubt, and she could shatter back into whatever she was before.\n\nAround us, normal teenagers complain about normal things—pop quizzes, curfews, whether who's been sleeping with who at last weekend's party. Jamie watches them with an expression I can't quite read, something between longing and bewilderment, like an anthropologist studying a foreign culture she desperately wants to join.\n\n"Do you think they have cheerleading tryouts in college?" she asks suddenly.\n\n"Probably. Why?"\n\nShe shrugs, but there's something careful about the gesture, like she's testing the weight of the dream before fully committing to it. "I was thinking... maybe when we graduate, I could try out. I've been practicing." She demonstrates a small arm movement, something between a wave and a flourish that somehow manages to be both graceful and slightly otherworldly.\n\n"You've been practicing cheerleading?"\n\n"Don't laugh." Her cheeks flush pink, and for a moment she looks exactly like what she's becoming—a sixteen-year-old girl with a crush and dreams that feel too big for her skin. "I watched some late-night infomercials about cheerleading school. It looks... fun. Normal. Like something Jamie would do."\n\nI don't laugh. Instead, I kiss her forehead, tasting salt and something that might be cinnamon, or maybe just the residual magic that clings to her like perfume. "I think you'd be a great cheerleader."\n\n"Really?" The hope in her voice is almost painful in its intensity.\n\n"Really."\n\nThat afternoon, TJ finds her behind the gym, sitting on the concrete steps with her head in her hands. He tells me about it later, how he'd been looking for a quiet place to help Morgan with her "boyfriend-girlfriend dynamics tutorial" (his words, delivered with the obliviousness of someone who still doesn't realize he's the subject of the lesson), when he stumbled across Jamie trying to summon hellfire to light a cigarette she'd bummed from someone.\n\n"She was just sitting there, holding her palm out and concentrating until her face turned red," he explains as we walk to my car after school. "But nothing happened. Not even a spark."\n\n"I can't do it anymore," she'd told him through tears that smeared her mascara into dark rivers down her cheeks. "I can't remember how to summon hellfire. I can't remember the names of my superiors. I can't even remember why I'm supposed to hate you all."\n\nWhen I find her, she's sitting on our usual bench by the flagpole out front of school, staring at her hands like they've betrayed her. Her reflection in the glass doors behind her shows two faces layered over each other—the girl I'm falling for and something else, something with too many teeth and eyes like burning coals. But the demonic features are fainter now, like a double exposure that's slowly fading.\n\n"It's happening more," she says without looking at me. "The forgetting. Sometimes I try to remember what it felt like to have power, real power, and all I can think about is whether my hair looks okay or if you think I'm pretty."\n\nI reach over and take her hand. Her skin is fever-warm, but steady. Human. Real. "You are pretty."\n\nShe laughs, but it comes out wet and broken. "That's not the point. The point is that I was sent here to destroy you, and instead I'm sitting here wondering if you'll ask me to prom."\n\nThe question hangs between us like a bridge I'm afraid to cross. Because this is the moment, isn't it? The choice Lady Illkeserod warned me about. I can keep pretending this is all real, let myself fall deeper into this beautiful lie where my fictional friends come to life and the girl I love chooses humanity over Hell. Or I can remember that none of this should exist, that Jamie is a creation born from the collision of timelines, that every moment I spend loving her writes this false reality deeper into existence.\n\n"Would you say yes?" I ask.\n\n"Yes," she whispers. "God help me, yes."\n\nI lean over to kiss her, and she tastes like strawberry lip gloss and something darker, something that reminds me of smoke and honey and promises made in languages that predate human speech. Her hands tangle in my hair, and when we break apart, her eyes are completely human—warm brown, no trace of the otherworldly fire that used to burn there.\n\n"I want to graduate," she says against my lips, the words soft and fierce and desperate. "I want to go to homecoming and complain about calculus and maybe get into UCF or somewhere close here in Florida. Somewhere with you. I want to be normal."\n\n"Okay," I say, because what else can you say to someone who's choosing love over demonic power? What else can you say when you're choosing the same thing?\n\n"Okay?"\n\n"We'll figure it out. Whatever you're becoming, whatever you're forgetting—we'll figure it out together."\n\nShe smiles, and for the first time since I've known her, it reaches her eyes completely. No shadows, no hidden depths, just pure human joy. "You promise?"\n\n"I promise."\n\nAs we walk home together, her hand warm in mine, she hums a song stuck in her head—some pop ballad that's probably too simple for cosmic entities but perfect for teenage girls falling in love. The shadows cast by the streetlights seem to move wrong, like something ancient is watching us with approval or maybe just curiosity, but I don't look too closely.\n\nBecause Jamie doesn't notice them anymore. She's too busy being sixteen and human and beautifully, impossibly normal. And I'm too busy falling in love with someone who shouldn't exist, in a timeline that's probably a lie, feeling more real than I ever did when I was dying alone at seventy-four.\n\nMaybe that's enough. Maybe some truths are worth the price of forgetting, and some lies are worth believing.\n\nAt least until the bill comes due.\n\n**********\n\nWhile I'm falling deeper into this beautiful lie with Jamie—watching her braid her hair with impossible precision, fold origami cranes from muscle memory she shouldn't have, trace crude stick figures in my biology notes with eyes that promise things that make my face burn—the rest of the group is slowly losing their minds.\n\nBy that Friday, no one can't take it anymore. That's how we end up back in Ms. Rodriguez's AP Bio lab after hours, the sterile space feeling more like a confessional than a classroom in the failing light. The late afternoon sun slants through grimy windows at an angle that turns everything the color of old blood, casting long shadows that stretch across lab tables like reaching fingers.\n\n"This is driving me insane," I say, setting Whitney's journal on the central lab table between a microscope and a tray of scalpels that gleam too brightly under fluorescent lights. "None of you are seeing what I'm seeing, are you?"\n\nMorgan leans forward, her usual scholarly confidence replaced by something more desperate. "The shadows stopped multiplying in my bedroom three nights ago," she reports, voice barely above a whisper. "They just... sit there now. Watching, but not growing."\n\nTJ nods, absently straightening his Reagan/Romney '86 shirt. "Haven't had a run in with shadows since Monday. Not even a flicker. It's like someone turned off a radio that was always playing static in the back of my head."\n\nAndrew fidgets with a pen, clicking it in a rhythm that sounds almost like morse code. "My dreams have been boring as hell. Just regular anxiety nightmares about forgetting to study for tests. It's actually kind of refreshing."\n\nWe all turn to look at Jamie, who's been unusually quiet, sitting with perfect posture that somehow manages to look both graceful and slightly unnatural. She meets our eyes and smiles—a real smile that reaches all the way to her pupils.\n\n"I made cookies this morning," she says, wonder in her voice like she's announcing the discovery of fire. "From scratch. With my own hands. And they turned out... normal. Good normal. Not demon-enhanced or cursed or anything. Just cookies."\n\nThe relief in the room is palpable, like pressure equalizing after a long dive. But underneath it, something insidious lurks. The normalcy feels too perfect, too carefully constructed, like a movie set designed to look like a real room.\n\n"But the journal's still changing," I insist, opening it to pages that shift and blur when I'm not looking directly at them. "Look, this entry about fracture points appeared yesterday, and this map—"\n\n"What map, Stephen?" Morgan says gently, "We're all reading the same thing. The handwriting's consistent. Everything looks normal."\n\n"That's the problem!" I slammed the journal shut. The sound echoed wrong, too sharp, like glass breaking in a padded room. "Everything's too normal. Too stable. Like someone hit the reset button and we're all pretending we didn't notice."\n\nI pointed at the periodic table, where some of the symbols were subtly wrong, shimmering like heat haze. "Look at this! The elements are different!" Morgan blinked, her gaze following my finger, and then her eyes widened, just for a flicker.\n\n"Oh," she said, her voice soft, "they always were, weren't they? I guess I just never noticed." And as she spoke, the subtle distortions on the chart seemed to solidify into her perception, just as quickly as they'd been visible to me. It was like they were seeing the changes as they became the new truth.\n\nJamie stands and moves to the whiteboard with fluid grace, uncapping a red marker. "But maybe that's good," she says, beginning to draw what looks like a map. "Maybe we found our way back to something real."\n\nThe map shows familiar landmarks—our school, the downtown church, even the fucking trailer-that-isn’t-a-trailer in the woods that appears and disappears depending on who's looking. But she's added annotations: "fracture core," "dimensional anchor points," "reality stable but monitored."\n\n"How do you know all this?" Morgan asks, leaning closer to study the map.\n\nJamie pauses, marker hovering over the board. "I... remember. Not clearly, but it's like having a word on the tip of your tongue. The knowledge is there, just out of reach." She turns to look at us. "I think this is what I was really sent here to do. Not to destroy you, but to help you find the path back to Whitney."\n\nThe fluorescent lights flicker once, so briefly it might have been imagination, but in that strobing instant, every preserved specimen in the room seems to turn its empty eyes toward us.\n\nThe dead pig fetus seems to widen its grin imperceptibly. The reproductive system poster, twitches momentarily, revealing anatomical diagrams of organs that couldn't possibly exist in any earthly biology: spiral wombs that twist through dimensions, fallopian tubes that branch like fractals into infinity.\n\nAlmost normal.\n\nNormal enough to fool us for exactly thirty-six more seconds.\n\n**********\n\nThe lab door slams open with a bang that makes us all jump, our carefully constructed sense of normalcy shattering like glass.\n\n"Jesus Christ!" TJ yelps, nearly falling off his lab stool.\n\nBecca stumbles through the doorway, hair disheveled and eyes red-rimmed with exhaustion. She looks like she's been running, or crying, or both. For a heart-stopping moment, we all freeze—because this is exactly how the bad shit always starts. Doors slamming open. Someone appearing where they shouldn't be. Reality preparing to fold itself inside out.\n\nBut then she's just... Becca. Tired, traumatized, but undeniably human Becca.\n\n"Holy shit, you scared us," Andrew breathes, hand over his heart.\n\nShe stops in the doorway, taking in the scene—the five of us huddled around the whiteboard with Jamie's map, Whitney's journal open on the lab table, the general air of conspiracy that clings to us like incense. For a moment, she just stares, and I can see her trying to process what she's walked into.\n\n"You're back," Morgan says, genuine relief flooding her voice. We all move toward her at once, a collective impulse to comfort and protect that feels as natural as breathing.\n\nBecca lets us envelope her in a group hug that smells like vanilla perfume mixed with something institutional—disinfectant and despair. She's thinner than when she left, sharper at the edges, and when she looks at us, there's something haunted in her expression.\n\n"Yeah," she says, voice hoarse. "I'm back. Finally."\n\n"How was..." I start, then stop, because I’ve got no idea where she’s been.\n\n"Healing Through Traditional Values Institute," she says bitterly. "Three weeks of being told that my trauma was actually gender confusion, and that if I just embraced my feminine role properly, I'd stop having nightmares about people who never existed."\n\nThe words hit us like physical blows. Jamie's face goes pale. TJ makes a sound like he's been punched in the stomach.\n\n"Wait, what?" TJ sputters. "They sent you to conversion therapy? But you're not even—"\n\n"Gay?" Becca laughs bitterly. "Yeah, I tried explaining that. Told them I was in love with Patrick, that he was a boy, that we were planning to go to prom together. But since Patrick doesn't exist in any of their records, any of their photos, any of their reality..." She wipes her nose with the back of her hand. "They decided I'd invented a male persona to cope with my 'unnatural desires.'"\n\n"That's fucking insane," Andrew breathes.\n\n"They tried to convince me that Patrick was a lesbian fantasy I created to avoid dealing with my attraction to girls," Becca continues, tears starting to track down her cheeks. "That my grief was really just misplaced sexual confusion. They had charts, Stephen. Fucking charts about how trauma manifests as gender dysphoria in vulnerable teenage girls."\n\n"Charts?" Morgan's voice cracks on the word.\n\n"Diagrams. Flowcharts. 'Signs Your Daughter Is Confused About Her Gender Identity.' Shit like 'excessive grief over fictional male friends' and 'resistance to traditional feminine activities.'" Becca's hands shake as she mimics air quotes. "They said my love for Patrick was actually my psyche trying to become male to attract other females."\n\n"But that doesn't even make sense," TJ says, his face flushed with anger. "You've dated guys before Patrick. Not to mention your not-so subtle crush on Stephen."\n\n"Try explaining that to people who think psychology is just applied theology," Becca snaps. "They had an answer for everything. Danny was a 'practice boyfriend' I used to convince myself I was normal. My makeup and dresses were 'performance femininity' to hide my true nature. Even my fucking period was somehow evidence of gender confusion."\n\nJamie's hands are pressed to her mouth, eyes wide with horror. "They can't just... decide someone's gay and ship them off."\n\n"Watch them," Becca says darkly. "Especially