m. Seconds passed like drumbeats, amplifying the air of impending catastrophe. The room smelled of stale coffee and sweat so thick it seeped into our pores. In her corner office, the CEO paced in agitation, barking the occasional useless question. The COO, predictably, was nowhere to be found. That fact alone stoked my frustration; he was always conspicuously absent when things turned catastrophic.\n\n“Do you have this under control?” the CEO asked, her voice tight with desperation.\n\n“Yes,” I lied, not breaking my focus from the screen. She sighed and rubbed her temples, muttering something about how the COO had assured her this was my fault. Her misplaced trust felt like a lead weight on my shoulders, each ounce a testament to her naivete.\n\nTime crawled by, and each attempt that failed pushed us closer to disaster. The tension was suffocating, our nerves stretched thin as we cycled through every troubleshooting procedure in the book. I was exhausted, my mind a haze of command lines and configurations, but as I scanned the code again, something caught my eye, a single, buried entry.\n\nIt was small but insidious, a script tied to the COO’s credentials. I froze. Its ripple effects were derailing critical processes, blocking every fix we attempted. My jaw clenched as the implications hit me. This wasn’t an accident; it was yet another example of the COO’s reckless tinkering.\n\nRemoving it could solve everything, but the reality was undeniable: the COO was a ticking time bomb, and as long as he had access, this wouldn’t be the last disaster.\n\nI didn’t share my thoughts with the CEO. Instead, I made my choice. With a sharp keystroke, I deleted the offending script.\n\nTime stopped. The entire world held its collective breath.\n\nSlowly, like a giant rousing from slumber, the server whimpered back to life. The screen blazed with a green light, signaling the systems’ triumphant reboot.\n\nA digital heartbeat steadied as data flowed back into place, the precious lif