never existed at all. I delved deeper, scouring Reddit - a platform known for its deep dives and alternative discussions - but found nothing. Even Facebook, notorious for its endless debates and cat videos, held no mention of the Pointers.\n\nAt first, I thought it was some weird trick played on me by the Cybersecurity folks updating one of the porn filters on the firewall. However, when I pulled out my cell phone, I realized that the hundreds of links I thought had saved in Google Chrome were all gone.\n\nI continued my fruitless search as a sense of unease crept into my mind. How could something so widespread and widely discussed simply disappear without a trace? It felt unnatural, almost as if a force was deliberately wiping away their existence from the digital world. Determined to uncover the truth, I used every search trick in my arsenal, but it was all in vain. The Pointers had become elusive ghosts, vanishing as mysteriously as they had first appeared.\n\nI walked over to Mattias’s desk, figuring if anyone knew what was going on, they would.\n\nHis space was its usual chaos, a scattering of energy drink cans, sticky notes with cryptic scribbles, and a monitor wallpaper featuring a pixelated cat shooting lasers. He was hunched over, earbuds in, mumbling something to himself as he tapped away at his keyboard.\n\n“Mattias,” I said, leaning on the edge of his desk.\n\nHe pulled one earbud out, squinting at me. “What’s up?”\n\nI hesitated, unsure how to even broach the subject. “Do you remember that video we talked about earlier? The one of the Pointer disappearing?”\n\nHe frowned, tilting his head. “Pointer? That bug in the code that was causing a memory leak. We fixed that this morning.”\n\n“Not that, but, you know, the Pointers. The... things. We were just talking about them outside; aliens, government experiments, remember?” My voice trailed off as his expression shifted from confusion to irritation.\n\n“Are you messing with me right now?” he asked, spinning 