ing, or someone, made him forget? The alternative was far scarier: was I unexpectedly remembering something that wasn’t really there?\n\nThe clock ticked closer to 5pm when I finally stumbled upon a link that sparked hope of an answer: an anonymous post titled ‘Pointing the way?’ published around the same time I’d seen the video earlier, claiming to have witnessed strange people randomly showing up but then disappearing.\n\nThis supposedly occurred during Operation Blue Storm last month near Area 51 outside Las Vegas. The article spoke of strange sightings along Nevada highways leading to Groom Lake Air Force Base, prompting authorities to dispatch heavily armed military units across southern Nevada. In that moment, my heart pounded with excitement and fear as I realized just how close I was to uncovering the truth.\n\nDigging deeper, and there was nothing.\n\nRealizing my search was going nowhere, I packed up my belongings and headed home. My thoughts lingered on the Pointers and how the digital age constantly redefines the truth. Equally unsettling was how swiftly the office had rebounded from the previous night’s crisis. Normalcy had crept back in, the urgency fading into the hum of daily routines, as if nothing had happened at all.\n\nStepping into the cool night air, I felt the suffocating grip of the office finally release me, yet an unfamiliar weight settled in its place. The eerie sensation of being watched clung to me, a heavy, invisible shroud that sent shivers down my spine. The world outside felt warped, as if the disappearance of the Pointers and my fragmented, dreamlike experiences had twisted its fabric. A tingling coursed through my body, sharpening my senses and stirring an insistent whisper deep within: Don’t stop now. Keep digging.\n\nThe drive home was no reprieve, just another layer of tension. My hands gripped the wheel with white-knuckled intensity as the deserted streets stretched endlessly, the rhythm of flickering streetlights dissonant against the chaos pounding in my head. Every car that passed seemed to hold a knowing gaze, their headlights cutting through the fog of doubt clouding my thoughts. Fragments of the past few weeks replayed like a corrupted film reel, visions of the Pointers, vivid dreams, and the creeping sense that reality itself was unraveling.\n\nEven the air felt heavier, as though the night carried a sinister awareness, amplifying the silence of the slumbering city. It wrapped itself around me, suffocating yet magnetic, pulling me toward answers I couldn’t yet define. The Pointers loomed larger with every mile they faded into nothingness, their shadow both overwhelming and slipping through the cracks of my fraying sanity.\n\nReality stabilized in the chaos unraveling within me, the truth hovering just out of reach, undefined and unknowable, as precise as common sense. All I could do was drive, each moment both tethering me to the present and unraveling me further away from the unknown, waiting at the end of the beginning.\n\n**********\n\nOnce home, walking inside brought me back to reality. The warm aroma of my wife’s cooking wrapped around me, a mix of spices and comfort that briefly eased the tension in my chest.\n\n“Hey,” she called from the laundry room, “what’s with the grease stains on the sheets?”\n\nI froze mid-step, the question hitting harder than it should. Flashes of last night’s dream, the Eternal Engine, gears grinding and glowing in the dark; reality abruptly rippled through my mind. Could it somehow be connected? I forced a chuckle, masking the unease creeping up my spine.\n\n“Probably just something from tinkering in the garage earlier this week,” I replied, even though I couldn’t remember ever stepping foot in there.\n\nShe appeared in the doorway, holding the stained sheet like a piece of evidence. “You tinkering? Haha. This stain looks fresh,” she said, narrowing her eyes. “Sticky, even. Are you sure you didn’t have an ‘accident’ last night?”\n\n“Other than you