ill found it hard to fully set aside the inner conflict.\n\nWithout warning, as if on cue, the peaceful evening shattered like glass with my wife’s voice cutting through the calm. “Shoot. We’ve got a camping trip this weekend! Are you packed yet” she announced, her tone somewhere between a call to arms and sheer exhilaration. Before I could blink, she was barking orders, transforming the living room into a chaotic battleground of sleeping bags, tents, and half-forgotten gear.\n\n“Where’s the lantern? Did you pack the bug spray?” she demanded, her eyes cross-referencing her packing list.\n\nMy son tried to sneak away to play games, while I rummaged through a closet, emerging triumphantly with the headlamp. “Was this on the list?” I joked, only to be met with her unimpressed glare.\n\nThe frenzy took on a life of its own; items were stacked, unstacked, and meticulously checked under her watchful eye. A business replaced the earlier calm. Despite wrangling stray scout socks and stashing snacks in backpack pockets, my mind drifted back to the Pointers, their shadowy presence threatening to creep into even this slice of normalcy. I shook it off.\n\n“Focus,” I muttered to myself, trying to ground my thoughts in the present. This was about my son, about the joy of a weekend outdoors, away from screens and the tangled mess of work and mystery.\n\nDespite the whirlwind of activity, there was an odd comfort in the madness. The living room morphed into a staging ground for adventure, our hurried packing (despite still having most of the week left) fueled by my wife’s relentless energy. By the time we were done, we had our neatly sorted backpacks containing anything nature, or an overly ambitious Boy Scout leader, might throw at us. Exhausted but buzzing with anticipation, we stood amidst the mess, grinning at each other.\n\nDespite all the chaos, it felt good to be caught in the moment, united by the thrill of the upcoming escape.\n\nLater that evening, as I lay in bed, the we