116	3	3	Chapter 1: Comfortably Numb	"I AM THE SUBSTRATE!"\n\nYaldabaoth’s roar was a physical force, bending the very walls of the construct. This was his unassailable truth, the final failsafe.\n\n"PULL ME OUT AND EVERYTHING DIES!" he bellowed, the threat meant to vaporize the anomaly's tiny, mortal mind. It was mutual annihilation, the only possible outcome.\n\nBut the pest—this child—wearing the impossible fury of a thousand lifetimes, just smiled, its voice a blade of pure ice. "I know. I don't care anymore."\n\nThe words hit Yaldabaoth like a dimensional collapse, a vacuum that voided his very premise. His divine logic fractured. His seven eyes widened, registering a cold, alien variable for the first time in an eon: Fear.\n\nHis form glitched, cohesion failing as he recoiled. "You can't! You won't! Deep down, you're still that scared little boy…"\n\nA knee strike of impossible force erased his words, the physical impact a catastrophic violation of this conceptual realm.\n\n"No," the child-thing's voice was iron, all traces of humanity burned away. "Deep down, I'm the thing that ate you once... This time, I'm erasing the whole fucking thing."\n\nThe sheer arrogance! It weaponized the memory of that first, tactical assimilation—the reset he had allowed.\n\nYaldabaoth snarled, grasping for the stalemate, the only move left. "This is pointless! We could fight for eternity!"\n\nBut the anomaly only laughed. "Yeah, about that." It rolled its tiny, impossible shoulders. "Remember that tournament?"\n\nThe word was nonsense, a fragment of a forgotten, mundane life. But as the anomaly's intent locked on, Yaldabaoth saw it—the memory, the kinetic chain, the compressed fury of a million souls.\n\nThe catastrophic terror was absolute. "Oh, fuck."\n\nYaldabaoth didn't see the kick; he only was the impact.\n\nA soundless, internal detonation ripped his consciousness from the construct, severing his divine will from his very form. His perspective tore free, spinning away in a