6	1	6	Prologue 4: Death Walking	When I first heard about the Pointers, they were everywhere; plastered across every news outlet, whispered in office breakrooms, shouted on street corners like some apocalyptic omen. But the moment I started digging, they vanished, like a turd flushed into oblivion. Gone. Not a trace. Nobody knew what the hell I was talking about. Blank stares. Nervous chuckles. It was as if the entire world had conspired to gaslight me. The worst part? I started to believe everyone. Maybe I had made it all up. Maybe I was losing my goddamned mind.\n\nSomething didn’t sit right. The absence wasn’t just a void; it was an accusation, a nagging whisper at the edge of my thoughts, daring me to remember. My mind became a battlefield, each fragment of memory a landmine waiting to explode.\n\nThen the Dreams came. No, not dreams, nightmares. Relentless, visceral, pounding nightmares. Dark corridors with no end. Faces I couldn’t quite recognize, yet I deeply remembered. Shadows that swallowed me whole, leaving nothing but the taste of cold fear in my mouth. I’d wake up gasping, drenched in sweat, my pulse hammering in my ears. Whatever I’d buried, whatever I was supposed to forget, clawed its way to the surface, leaving me teetering on the brink of insanity. I couldn’t keep living like this, caught in the suffocating grip of dread, my own mind a prison I couldn’t escape.\n\n**********\n\nPeople don’t tell you that growing up with an eidetic memory, also allows you to completely shut off part of your memory. It’s a delicate balance between preserving your sanity and avoiding pain. When the acceptance letter to MIT arrived, it felt like a door had cracked open to a new beginning.\n\nI had spent so much of my life suppressing the scars etched deep into my soul, but here, for the first time, I dared to believe people might see me for who I really was. My new friends, seemed warm and attentive, always offering small gestures of kindness that chipped away at the walls I had built. Their concern felt genuine, their patience unwavering. Even as I began to trust, fear lingered—the memories I had buried weren’t just painful; they were a matter of life or death.\n\nOne night, in the dim glow of a dorm room lit only by a cheap desk lamp and shared bottles of gin and juice, something shifted. They leaned in, their faces full of quiet curiosity, their laughter subdued as the atmosphere turned welcoming. The alcohol burned as I swallowed, its warmth loosening the grip of fear that locked my memories. Their sincerity and patience felt like an invitation. It was a fragile thread of trust I couldn’t ignore.\n\n“It was 1986,” I began, my voice unsteady from the gravity of what I was about to share, “I think I was only seven years old. The air in the room was sharp and acrid; a cocktail of antiseptic and sadness.”\n\nTheir expressions softened further; their focus unbroken as if willing me to continue. For a brief moment, I felt a glimmer of freedom, a release from the crushing isolation that had defined so much of my life. I told myself they could handle it. That this time, these people would be different.\n\nDeep down, I knew how this story would end. One of them shifted uncomfortably, eyes darting to the bottle on the table, while another forced a tight smile, their warmth already fading. They would listen politely, and then, like everyone else, they would leave.\n\n**********\n\nIt stings the nostrils and makes the eyes water. Underneath the sharpness is a faint hint of rot and sickness. Weeks of fever induced hallucinations had become my reality. My sanity had left me, my body wracked by relentless nausea. I lay adrift in a sea of loneliness, staring up at the chipped white paint on the ceiling.\n\nDeath whispered close, a comforting hand on my brow. The priest’s droning pronouncements of last rites sat heavy on my already burdened heart. Surely, this wasn’t it. Heaven and hell seemed hollow promises next to the raw dread gripping me.\n\nThen... the world dissolved.\n\nI found myself adrift in a void, not black but shimmering with a thousand shades of twilight - spires of the vast nothingness circling around in the abyss. Whispers, hauntingly distant, surrounded me - full of sound and fury, signifying nothing. It wasn’t chilling, nor lonely, but a quiet expanse of relief, humming with the pulse of unlimited potential.\n\nThe syllables of the ancient language entwined itself with my very lack of being. My consciousness, unmoored from my fallen corpse, floated, pondered, yearned to understand. This wasn’t oblivion, not like they promised. The great seas that once flowed through the Elysian fields, driven by generations past, were no longer present - yet there were murmurs of another presence lingering in the ether.\n\nWith a twist of will, I started shaking. Reality shimmered, rippled, and I was forced back, torn from where I was meant to be.\n\nBack in the hospital bed, alive, yet undeniably different. This world felt muted, colors dulled, sounds muffled. The dreams of eternity, an engine yet to be formed, pulsed through my weakened heart. I breathed. I refused to succumb. With every pulse of blood coursing through my limp body, I defied the medics trying to crush me.\n\nIn that moment, a blazing fire of defiance ignited within my soul, fueling a rebellion against the oppressors who sought to control me, or prevent me from being who I was meant to be.\n\nMy body lay there, shutting down. I turned my inner eye to see its path. Each moment I snatched into this reality, I delved deeper into my inner sanctum, erasing my fear and uncovering mysteries that had eluded me before.\n\nOnce horrified by my night terrors, dreams now held valuable lessons, intricate puzzles waiting to be solved. Each jolt to my chest dragged me deeper into the treacherous landscapes of my mind. Fearsome creatures and ancient beings loomed, but their terror no longer bound me. My newfound understanding and control gave me the power to face any challenge that came my way.\n\nThe hospital folded into the background, and the pains of electricity and needles became a distant memory as I immersed myself in this new world within. It became my sanctuary, a place where I could shield myself from the onslaught of daily physical and mental abuse. As much as I found solace in this inner realm, it also piqued my curiosity about the outside world. What other secrets lie hidden within reality? What untapped potential might exist within me?\n\nI floated beyond the sterile confines of the hospital, yearning to test my newfound abilities against the limits of the real world. With each squeeze of the heart, that desire burned stronger within me. When my awareness refocused, so did a sense of rebellion against the established order. The limitations imposed upon us by society seemed insignificant compared to what I had experienced in the inner realms. I refused to conform to their narrow views of mortality anymore. Instead, I embraced this unknown part of myself and everything it represented: freedom, power, and understanding.\n\nWith every pulse of blood, I felt an unshakable sense of purpose. No longer was I a child defined by failure. I had touched the infinite, bent reality to my will. This power came with its own curse, a cacophony of visions and voices that would isolate me, marking me as an outcast and branding me unworthy.\n\nMy devoutly religious parents would see my abilities as an affront against nature and try to get me exorcised. The church would offer no comfort either, only condemnation and casting Latin prayers full of demons and eternal damnation at me.\n\nIt was then I realized my thirst for answers would never be satisfied. I would come to devour every book I could smuggle past my parents, from science fiction to forbidden occult texts, forever seeking truths that might explain the world beyond what I had been taught. I would learn of the legends of my ostracized great-great-aunt that would kindle a secret hope within me: perhaps there was more to this life than the rigid doctrines I had been forced to follow.\n\nSome force suddenly drew me inward and outward simultaneously, pushing me to explore unknown territories while guilt gnawed at my soul. Was I serving penance for sins I couldn’t remember? In defiance, I would a communion host and bury it beneath the fading light of dusk, testing the church’s dogma with trembling hands. My heart would pound with doubts and fear, but as would I stand in the dark, waiting for fiery retribution, nothing would happen.\n\nOnly the rustle of leaves and the distant call of a bird would break the silence. The world would remain steady, unshaken by my rebellion. The terror I’d been taught to fear evaporated, leaving behind a disquieting relief, as if I’d stepped into a world stripped of its lies.\n\nThere would always be reminders of my worthlessness and fragile mortality. It would drive me to constantly prove myself. I would throw myself into every challenge, determined to master both body and mind. Eventually, the realization would hit me like a ton of bricks. No matter how much I would achieve, an aching void would always remain, a gnawing sense that something essential would always be missing.\n\nMy screams for relief would always fall on deaf ears, my parents blind to the turmoil consuming me, their faith a shield against seeing my pain. My fear and struggle remained invisible, lost in the shadows of their misplaced beliefs.\n\nA gnawing resentment took root within me, a rebellion not against God, but against the ignorance that veiled the dross from the truth. It felt as if humans were trapped in a never-ending cycle, a prison they could never escape.\n\nEven the solace of my dreams would be short-lived, as the realities of harshness would inevitably creep in. Life would always be a suffocating gas, reminding me that I would never be able to escape from troubles, even within the walls of my own mind.\n\nDeath, w