12	1	12	9: Under the Stars	Friday. The camping trip was finally here, and the long-awaited escape from the relentless grind of daily life had arrived. My son and I had eagerly anticipated this camping trip with his Boy Scout troop and were envisioning serene moments under the stars and the crackle of a campfire to melt away the week’s frustrations. The day wore on, work refusing to release me from its grip.\n\nA last-minute meeting stretched late into the evening, leaving us scrambling to pack and hit the road. By the time we reached the forest, night had fully descended. The deeper we drove, the darker and more imposing the woods became, shadows from towering trees stretching across the winding road like silent sentinels. It was as though the forest itself was preparing to test us.\n\nArriving at the campsite, nothing but darkness and the distant glow of the troop’s fire greeted us. Armed with only flashlights and a burning desire to eat smores, we fumbled through the motions of setting up our tents, avoiding fire-ant piles and wrestling with stubborn poles. Despite the chaos, my son figured out a way to transform the struggle into yet another adventure. His antics were infectious even as I muttered about “something being packed wrong” under my breath.\n\nAt last, our tents were set up, joining the others already in place. We took a moment to catch our breath, soaking in the surrounding forest. We then headed over to join the others at the campfire, yet I couldn’t dispel the sense that we had transitioned into an experience that was far more profound than a mere camping trip.\n\nThe fire crackled with laughter and peaceful smiles as marshmallows browned and blistered over the snapping branches. I savored the moment, observing how flickering flames mirrored in the scouts’ faces under the quiet majesty of the stars above. The scent of hot chocolate mingled with the smoky tang of burning wood and the earthy aroma of the forest. It created an intoxicating incense, capturing a scene frozen in time. Voices wafted like a gentle melody as the hum of murmuring conversations and snapping embers grounded us in the night's stillness.\n\nThe once roaring fire popped and hissed, slowly dying into a bed of glowing embers that pulsed with a quiet intensity. Shadows danced across the scouts’ faces, the light giving everyone an almost spectral quality. Taking this as his cue, the scoutmaster leaned forward, his features bathed in the warm orange glow, voice low and measured, drawing everyone into the story.\n\n“Long ago,” he began, his eyes scanning us with deliberate slowness, “there was a group of scouts, much like yourselves, who came to this very forest. They were full of excitement, anxious to prove their mettle against the wilderness. The forest, as you’ve no doubt realized, has a way of testing those who dare to enter its depths.” He paused, letting the silence stretch, broken only by the occasional pop of a pocket of gas escaping the firewood.\n\nA gentle breeze stirred the trees, their branches creaking softly as though the forest itself was listening. The story seemed directed at me. I shifted slightly under the weight of it, the cool air unexpectedly feeling sharper against my skin.\n\n“One night, after a long day of hiking, the scouts decided to explore beyond the marked trails. They laughed off warnings of thick mists and confusing paths, confident they could navigate their way back. But as the darkness deepened, a strange sound followed them. It was faint at first — like a relentless hum — but it grew louder, resonating through the trees, vibrating in their very bones.”\n\nThe firelight caught in his eyes, reflecting an unsettling intensity. “They said the sound came from a clearing that seemed almost to be alive, a great and terrible thing, hidden deep within the forest. The scouts called the spot the ‘Power of Infinity,’ though no one knows if they named it or if it whispered its name to them. They described it as an empty space, alive yet not alive, shadows of hidden gears and tendrils moving in ways that defied logic, pulling them further and further from the trail.”\n\nA shiver ran down my spine as his voice dropped just above a whisper. “Some claimed the spot offered glimpses of what could be—future paths, unspoken truths, choices that had yet to be made. It demanded something in return.” He leaned back, his shadow stretching tall against the trees. “Those who saw it... well, they were never the same. Some returned, but their eyes carried a hollow look, as if a piece of them had been left behind.”\n\nThe fire flared briefly, as if in response, and he tilted his head slightly. His gaze seemed to lock onto me. “If you know nothing else tonight,” he said, his voice dipping into something softer, almost intimate, “know this: the forest doesn’t always let you leave the way you came. Sometimes, just sometimes... it’ll show you exactly what you need to see.”\n\nHis tone shifting. “Remember, no matter what you hear or think you see out there, you’re going to be okay.”\n\nThe words hung in the air, strangely heavy. Around me, the scouts were silent, their quiet filled with nervous tension. I sat frozen, the faint hum of the forest suddenly louder in my ears, an echo of something far too familiar.\n\nRetreating to the confines of my tent, I lay restless on the thin sleeping pad, the scoutmaster’s story weaving itself into the fabric of my thoughts. The idea of uncovering something hidden deep within the forest seemed ridiculous, yet the notion tugged at me with an almost magnetic pull. The stillness of the night outside only sharpened the edges of my unease, each creak of branches or a distant rustle adding weight to the tale. Eventually, weariness claimed me, but sleep brought little relief.\n\nMy dreams were tangled in thick fog and endless trees, the faint, rhythmic hum of a familiar yet unseen machinery threading through the silence — a sound both foreign and unsettling, as if the forest itself held its breath, waiting.\n\nSaturday morning arrived with the soft calls of birds and the rustle of leaves stirred by slowly awakening Boy Scouts. The air was brisk but invigorating, a reminder that we were far from the routines of everyday life. Everyone gathered around the camp kitchen, chattering alive with excitement for the day’s challenges—map reading, compass skills, and orienteering to earn their merit badges. Their eyes shone with excitement; their energy was infectious as they got ready for the adventure.\n\nThe morning unfolded in its normal camping rhythm; the boys weaving through the forest with maps in hand, their voices rising in bursts of triumph each time they reached a checkpoint. They navigated dense underbrush and uneven trails with a mix of determination and camaraderie, their laughter cutting through the stillness of the woods. Breaks were filled with lighthearted banter and shared snacks, the scoutmaster’s story from the night before lingering only as a faint memory to fuel their imaginations.\n\nThe forest was just that — beautiful, expansive, and full of life — offering nothing out of the ordinary to disrupt the day.\n\nThe sun bled its lingering light into the cooling sky when the cheerful voices of returning scouts trickled back to camp. Group after group reported in; laughter and triumph ringing through the clearing—until one group didn’t. Minutes dragged on until the scoutmaster confirmed my worst fear: my son’s patrol was missing.\n\nDespite the weeks of training and planning, none of my son’s patrol had reached several nearby checkpoints, not even the easiest ones.\n\nThe forest, once so vibrant, now felt oppressive. Shadows stretched long and claw-like, as though the trees were leaning in, watching. Bark twisted into strange shapes in the fading light, and the once-familiar trails seemed to dissolve into the gloom. Voices rose in clipped, urgent commands as we scrambled to organize a search party.\n\nFlashlights flickered on, their narrow beams slicing through the encroaching dark, while maps were hastily consulted. The boys’ names echoed into the stillness as we fanned out, but the forest swallowed our shouts, replacing them with an unsettling, suffocating silence.\n\nThe further we pushed, the more the woods seemed to resist us. Roots snagged at our boots, and branches scraped at our faces, snapping back like deliberate obstacles. The trail blurred into dense undergrowth, and even the faint rustling of leaves felt unnervingly deliberate, as if the forest itself had a will. My breath grew shallow, each step dragging us deeper into a labyrinth that defied reason. The air grew heavy, tinged with a sour rot that clung to the back of my throat.\n\nFrom the edges of perception, a sound — faint and fragile — broke through. A muffled voice, trembling and disjointed, sent a jolt of adrenaline through my veins. I surged forward, tripping and stumbling, following the faint cries until, finally, we found them.\n\nIn a clearing was the lost patrol, everyone standing nervously together; faces pale and caked with relief. Although their uniforms were dirty, liberation and terror melted together in a facade of bravery. One scout held a shredded map, his knuckles white, while my son’s voice cracked as he tried to explain what had happened.\n\n“We... we kept going back. No matter where we turned, we ended up... here.”\n\nHe pointed into the humming trees, hands trembling, and for a moment, none of us wanted to look. All of us were terrified to see what he was showing us. Something felt wrong. The shadows were stretched in angles too tightly. The forest lay unnaturally still.\n\nThe moment passed, and we headed back. Getting the scouts on their feet was the simple part; leaving was another story.\n\nThe path we thought we’d come from was gone, swallowed by darkness that twisted the landscape into something unrecognizable. Flashlight beams flickered across shapes that seemed to shift when we weren’t looking.