en things made sense, before the rot set in.\n\nWhile the company has never won awards for being the best and biggest, it’s been skating along for decades, all the recent troubles began with him. The COO. A hire made not for skill, but for proximity to power—a torrid relationship so close to the CEO it bordered on parasitic. From the moment he waddled into the office, his influence cast a shadow that seemed to warp everything it touched. He was chaos incarnate, over-promising miracles and under-delivering wreckage. Projects faltered under his mismanagement, while attrition soared as talented employees fled from his tantrums and micromanagement. It wasn’t just poor leadership—it was deliberate sabotage masquerading as incompetence.\n\nIt was the imbecilic Azathoth made incarnate, thriving on disorder. Every meeting became a theater of the absurd, with directives as incoherent as they were impossible. We were trapped in a cycle of firefighting his self-inflicted disasters while he racked up frequent flyer miles, dining on the company’s dime with the CEO, both laughing at our expense. His failures were never his own; blame was always deflected, credit stolen. He was a grotesque monument to vanity and ego, a testament to how far a man could rise on connections alone.\n\nThere was something deeper, darker. Whispers passed among the staff, half-jokes and half-truths, that he’d made some kind of deal, a pact with forces better left unnamed. It wasn’t hard to believe when you saw the way his presence seemed to drain the air from a room. Late at night, as I worked in the quiet, I could swear I heard sinister chanting echoing down empty halls, a sound too sharp, too cruel, to be human.\n\nThe CEO, blinded by her infatuation, turned a blind eye to the chaos, whether out of lust or fear, I couldn’t say. The partnership between them seemed almost unnatural, a bond forged in shadows. We were left to suffocate under the weight of his whims, struggling to keep the company afloat while they orchestrated its collapse with a perverse glee. Each day felt like an unwinnable battle against a force that thrived on tearing the company apart.\n\nThat would be neither here nor there, as I had a job to do, so I just pushed everything down, buried deep within my cold chest, the resentment simmering just beneath the surface.\n\nUnexpectedly, the world around me shifted without warning.\n\nIt’s about 11am and I’m in the middle of a call with one of our largest clients (one I originally landed), so don’t hear the wailing and screaming coming from the office. What I do first notice, is the rhythmic thudding—a seismic disturbance that vibrated through the floor and up into my desk. My heart races as I realize it’s the unmistakable earthquakes caused by the waddling COO.\n\nThe emptiness of the office truly was a portent, and I knew then that the rhythm of the day would be shattered, to be replaced by an ominous beat that seemed to foreshadow doom. When the door opened, it wasn’t just the COO who entered. A tiny HR rep trailed behind him, her expression unreadable and her presence foreboding. They moved without preamble, each step amplifying the tension in the air, and seated themselves in chairs, as though this office were their execution chamber.\n\nI tried to focus, to ground myself in the client’s words still crackling through my headset, but my instincts screamed louder. This was not a routine meeting. This was a reckoning. My palms grew slick, my throat dry as I scrambled to make sense of their presence. Annual review? No, bonuses were already in bank accounts; this felt worse, darker, like the air before a storm that didn’t just destroy but consumed.\n\n“I’m sorry. I’ve got something that just came up.” I said to the empty phone. “Sure, I’ll call you back in a few minutes.”\n\nI barely had time to end the call when the COO launched into his spiel. “You’re fired, you failure of a fuckstain,” he bellowed, his voice a guttural roar that seemed to shake the