ou really think something like that could explain it?” I asked, my voice laced with both curiosity and a faint sense of unease.\n\nHe shrugged, looking down at his notes. “I don’t know. But the timelines match up. And ever since they dialed back the experiments, people say the big Mandela Effects have slowed down. Now it’s just petty stuff. But... there are still glitches. Something else seems to be doing something.”\n\nA heavy silence filled the room, broken only by the faint whistle of wind outside. The shadows in the corners seemed a little deeper, the stillness of the house more pronounced. I cleared my throat, trying to shake the creeping unease. “What about those glitches? What kind of stuff are people noticing?”\n\nMy son pushed his chair back, walking over to his desk in the corner on the other side of the dining table. He leaned over his phone, the glow of the screen illuminating his focused expression. “Like here,” he said, pulling up a Reddit post. “People are talking about stuff that doesn’t make sense. Objects swapping places on their own. People noticing tiny shifts in their lives—Polaroids that changed, but no one else remembers. It’s like... reality glitching.”\n\nHe paused, his finger hovering over the screen as he read silently for a moment. “Some people think it’s more than just physics breaking down,” he said finally. “Something darker.”\n\nThe word hung in the air, and I straightened in my chair. “Darker?” My voice sounded quieter than I intended.\n\nHe nodded slowly, still staring at the screen. “Yeah. Some people think it’s... demons. Or entities. Not glitches, but something doing it on purpose. Messing with reality. Like opening doors we were never supposed to find.”\n\n“Demons. That’s... comforting.”\n\nHe let out a soft laugh, but there was no humor in it. “I know how it sounds. Crazy, right? But what if it’s not? What if humanity has been poking at something way beyond what we’re supposed to understand?”\n\nThe wind outside picked up, ratt