ergy from one of these alternate dimensions?”\n\n“Perhaps.” His fingers drummed against the desk, the wheels in his mind clearly turning. “It would explain why it seems infinite. If it’s pulling from a source beyond what we know, it’s not just creating energy—it’s borrowing it from somewhere else.”\n\nThe idea sent a shiver down my spine. “If that’s true... borrowing from another dimension could come with consequences we can’t even begin to predict.”\n\nHe gave a slow nod, his gaze still fixed on the screen. “Like tearing holes in reality.”\n\nHe tapped on another link, his tone shifting to one of urgency. “Check this out. There’s an interview with one of the IBM researchers. She says the computations are like accessing parallel universes—like, crossing over into them. She also hints that some of the anomalies might be things bleeding through from those universes.”\n\nI leaned over his shoulder, skimming the lines. “She doesn’t outright say they’ve succeeded, but if even part of this is accurate...”\n\n“Then it’s already happening,” he finished, his voice barely audible.\n\nMy stomach twisted. “That would explain the accounts of strange occurrences. Even what people are saying about quantum research labs having... incidents.”\n\nHe turned toward me, his expression grave. “Dad, what if this isn’t just science going too far? What if... I mean, if the Eternal Engine is connected, could it be part of whatever’s causing this?”\n\nThe thought hit like a jolt. “It’s possible,” I drawled. “If the Engine is tied to quantum mechanics—and it has to be, given what it’s doing—then it could be another piece of the puzzle. My dreams of the engine in the workshop go back decades. When did these things first start popping up?”\n\nHe opened another post, his excitement giving way to something more somber. “Folks seem to be saying sometime in the early 1980s. Wait. Here’s another post. A former IBM employee says she saw someone—or something—inside one of the labs. She described it as a figure just standing there, watching her, but it wasn’t anyone she recognized.”\n\n“What does she mean, ‘something’?” I asked, leaning in.\n\n“She says it didn’t look... human. At least, not entirely. She felt like it was studying her, but not in a way that made sense. Like it wasn’t supposed to be there.”\n\nThe room seemed to grow colder as the weight of her account settled over us. “If they’ve been messing with dimensions...” I began, my voice trailing off.\n\nHe finished the thought. “It makes sense that something might have crossed over.”\n\nI exhaled sharply, shaking my head. “What scares me isn’t just that these things might be real—it’s that no one seems to know how to stop it. If we’re tearing at the edges of reality, who knows what’s waiting on the other side?”\n\nHe gave a small, humorless laugh, glancing back at the screen. “Let’s just hope we haven’t already let it in.”	20	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 11:54:34.938251-05	2026-03-05 19:39:33.692739-05	\N	4	\N	\N
