d forward, clearly enjoying this. “The Engine came from a dream. Dreams don’t make sense. You don’t solve them by being all serious. You just... go with it. Do dumb stuff. See what happens.”\n\n“You want me to just wing it?” I asked, raising an eyebrow.\n\n“Yup.” He said. “You can’t crack it by thinking like a normal person. You gotta think like a YouTuber with zero common sense.”\n\nI set my mug down, a grin creeping across my face. “So, what, slap some peanut butter on it and hope it cures cancer?”\n\n“Maybe!” He laughed, pointing his toast at me. “On the other hand, it might make the garage smell like a Reese’s for the rest of our lives. Either way, it’s a win.”\n\nI shook my head, chuckling. “Alright, I’ll admit it—you might have a point. This thing hasn’t followed the rules so far. Perhaps it’s time to stop pretending it should.”\n\n“That’s what I’m saying!” He gave me an exaggerated nod. “Go crazy with it. Embrace the dream logic. Who knows? It could be you’ll accidentally invent a teleporter or, like, infinite bacon.”\n\n“To infinite bacon,” I said, raising my coffee mug in mock toast.\n\nHe grinned, holding up his miso soup cup. “To bacon pancakes.”\n\nPerhaps he was right. Trying to treat the Engine like a science project hadn’t gotten me anywhere. It was time to get weird with it.\n\nThe garage looked like a tornado had swept through a thrift store and dumped its spoils in random piles. Tools and junk were everywhere, pizza boxes leaned precariously on the workbench, and in the corner, inside its pristine glass enclosure, the Eternal Engine sat humming softly, like it had better things to do than power our entire house indefinitely.\n\n“Alright,” I said, clapping my hands. “Let’s get weird.”\n\n“Define ‘weird,’ Dad,” my son said, dragging a broken fan into the pile we were assembling. “Because if this involves glitter, I’m out.”\n\n“No glitter,” I promised. “Not after the Great COVID Christmas Cleanup of 2020.”\n\nHe smirked. “Good. Glitter’s basically craft herpes.”\n\nWe began gathering the most random objects we could find. The first contenders were a set of soggy paper bags from the recycling bin, still faintly smelling of Chinese takeout, and a bottle of stale craft mead that had been sitting in the fridge since last summer.\n\n“Why do we even have this?” he asked, holding up the bottle.\n\n“It was a gift,” I said defensively.\n\n“From who? Someone who hates us?”\n\n“Focus,” I said, grabbing the mead, taking a sip, then sloshing it over the paper bags. “We’re making history here.”\n\n“History of what? Bad decisions?”\n\n“Exactly.”\n\nNext, we added a broken toaster with its insides hanging out like a mechanical autopsy. I jammed a handful of toenail clippings and clumps of hairbrush trimmings into it for good measure, while my son contributed a spaghetti strainer and an old rubber chicken he’d found under the workbench.\n\n“Why do we still have this?” I asked, holding up the chicken.\n\n“For emergencies,” he said, dead serious.\n\nThe Eternal Engine sat quietly in the middle of the chaos, its surface gleaming like it was judging us. “Alright, big guy,” I said to it. “Show us what you’ve got.”\n\nWe stepped back, watching as the beer-soaked paper bags sagged into a damp heap. Nothing happened.\n\n“Maybe it’s broken,” my son said.\n\n“It’s infinite energy. It doesn’t break,” I replied.\n\n“On the other hand it might be bored. Do we need to sing to it or something?” He cleared his throat. “Alexa Play ‘Let It Go.’”\n\nBefore I could tell him to stop, the air around the Engine rippled.\n\n“Uh, Dad?”\n\n“I see it,” I said, my voice hushed.\n\nThe paper bags twitched. One of them folded in on itself, like it was trying to stand up. The toaster sparked violently, sending a shower of tiny firework pops into the air.\n\n“Okay, that’s...new,” I said, stepping back.\n\nThe rubber chicken unexpectedly let out a haunting squeal that seemed to echo from another dimension. My son doubled over laughing. “This is the best thing I