f the kitchen was a stark contrast to the eerie atmosphere of the garage, but something still felt... off. My wife sat at the table, her phone in hand, scrolling with a distracted frown. She glanced up as I entered, and her eyes widened.\n\n“What on earth were you two doing out there?” she asked, her voice a mix of curiosity and alarm. Her gaze flicked to my hands, which were smudged with grease and something darker I couldn’t identify.\n\nI hesitated, running a hand through my hair, trying to wipe things off and back to normal. “Honestly? I don’t even know where to start.”\n\nShe set her phone down, the click of it hitting the table louder than it should have been. “Start with what happened. You look like you just walked out of a war zone.”\n\nI sank into a chair across from her, the weight of the Engine’s presence still clinging to me. “We were... experimenting. With the Engine. It started off as a joke, just throwing random junk at it to see what would happen. But then it—” I broke off, searching for the right words. “It reacted.”\n\nShe leaned forward, her brow furrowing. “Reacted how?”\n\nI met her eyes, unsure if she would believe me. “It came alive. Not literally—well, maybe literally. It was like it had... a will. Like it was responding to us. The things we threw at it—they moved, warped. The whole garage felt... wrong.”\n\nHer expression shifted from disbelief to unease. “Wrong how?”\n\n“Shadows were moving the wrong way. The air was thick, buzzing. And the Engine... it was glowing, but not like normal. It was—” I gestured helplessly. “It’s hard to explain. It felt alive. Powerful.”\n\nHer face paled, and she folded her arms tightly across her chest. “You’re not joking.”\n\n“I wish I was,” I said, my voice low. “This isn’t just some weird battery. There’s something... ancient about it. Something I don’t think we’re supposed to mess with.”\n\nThe only sound was the faint hum of the refrigerator. She reached out and placed a hand over mine, her grip firm but 