re like exasperation. She sighed, rubbing her temples before breaking into a reluctant laugh. “Of course you did. The two of you can’t stop yourselves, can you?”\n\n“Not really,” I admitted with a sheepish smile. “But, hey, it’s better, right?”\n\nShe shook her head, still laughing, and gave the hood an affectionate pat. “It’s amazing. Thank you—for all the thoughtful fixes. Just... next time? Talk to me first. You know I hate surprises.”\n\n“Deal,” I said, holding up my hands in mock surrender.\n\n“Promise?” she asked, pointing a finger between the two of us.\n\n“Promise,” my son and I said in unison, exchanging a conspiratorial grin.\n\nJust like that, the tension dissolved, replaced by the warmth of shared accomplishment.\n\nBut as we climbed into the futuristic masterpiece for a test drive, a thought lingered in the back of my mind: This is only the beginning.	41	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 11:59:23.65491-05	2026-03-05 19:39:33.692739-05	\N	8	\N	\N
