ries, seemed momentarily entranced. Questions buzzed around, profound musings about warped space and stellar birth, but as answers stumbled, I realized something: in dissecting the cosmos, we risked losing its magic.\n\nI gently guided my son away from the artificial projections, directing his gaze to the Milky Way arching above. “Look at that,” I whispered. “Ancient civilizations must have found their stories in those stars. Heroes, creatures, gods—all written in light.”\n\nHe stared, the glow reflecting in his wide eyes. “But, Dad,” he said hesitantly, “how can I ever understand it all? It feels... impossible.”\n\nI put my hand on his shoulder, steadying him. “No one understands it all. That’s the beauty of the universe, it’s an endless mystery. We’re not here to solve it completely. We’re here to marvel at it and piece together what we can.”\n\nHe sighed, the weight of his struggles softening. “What if I fail?”\n\n“Everyone does, son. Even the greatest minds stumbled. Failure isn’t the end, it’s part of the journey. The real challenge is staying curious, even when it’s hard.”\n\nThe tension in his shoulders eased, and a small smile crept across his face. “I want to explore it all, Dad. I want to discover what’s out there.”\n\n“You will. Not because you ace every test or know every fact, but because you care. That’s what matters.” I said.\n\nWe sat there, bathed in the soft glow of starlight, the crackling fire behind us fading to embers. The vast universe loomed above, infinite and mysterious, but tonight, under its watchful light, it felt less daunting. For now, we were just specks in its grand design, small, curious, and filled with hope.\n\n**********\n\nThe next morning, as we left the smoldering embers of our campfire behind, I reflected on how this cosmic adventure of ours wasn’t just about navigating the stars, it was about finding wonder in the unexpected. Little did I know that wonder, as usual, would come wrapped in chaos.\n\nOur son headed back to university as we returned to our quaint Florida home, we were  welcomed with the usual return to normalcy. But something was off. The hum of the self-cleaning windows was conspicuously absent. The gentle whir of Roombas? Missing. Even the air itself seemed... suspiciously still.\n\n“Sweetheart,” I called, setting my backpack down. “Why’s there a layer of dust everywhere? Did the Roombas unionize while we were gone?”\n\nFrom the bedroom, my wife emerged, her hair still carrying the faint scent of campfire. Her expression was one part amused, two parts mischievous. “Let’s just say... they’re having a team-building exercise.”\n\nBefore I could ask for clarification, a deep rumble sent vibrations through the house. A moment later, the source of the chaos revealed itself: the Roomba Rumble.\n\nOur fleet of Roomba 9000s had descended into anarchy. One, its LED eyes flashing red, was locked in a vicious tug-of-war over the rug with another whose spinning brushes sputtered defiantly. Two more whirled in an endless waltz, tangled in what appeared to be a charging cable they’d mistaken for a dust bunny. And one Roomba, the apparent instigator, was attempting to scale the orchid stand like a robotic King Kong.\n\n“Didn’t I tell you three was too many? Our house is too small for all of them” I muttered, catching the orchid mid-plunge before it could become collateral damage.\n\n“But they were on sale!” she shot back, suppressing a laugh. “And they promised triple the cleaning power!”\n\n“Right. Triple the cleaning, triple the territorial warfare.” One Roomba rammed another, sending it skittering into a wall with a defiant beep. “I hope you’re proud of your little army.”\n\n“Oh, I am,” she said, crossing her arms as she surveyed the chaos. “But I think they need better leadership.”\n\nUndeterred by our presence, the Roombas continued their war. Then, with a pitiful whine, one sputtered out of battery and collapsed onto the carpet in a heap of exhausted servos. The room fell silent. 