hen use the ERB’s quantum properties to anchor its structure anywhere I could directly observe. The implications were staggering—distance and time would no longer be obstacles. The universe was opening its doors, and all I needed was the key to step through.\n\nDriven by my usual reckless mix of passion and curiosity, I set up my next experiment with the precision of a mad scientist possessed. Ignoring the nagging voice in my head telling me to start small, I grabbed the telescope my son, and I had used to marvel at meteors just hours before and rushed over to our warehouse. With wild determination, I aimed it at Mars, our distant celestial neighbor, my heart pounding with the thrill of attempting the impossible.\n\nThe setup was meticulous—alignment, calibration, and countless adjustments—and as the final piece clicked into place, I exhaled sharply. This was it. I activated the portal, watching with a mix of triumph and awe as a shimmering gateway opened to reveal the barren red landscape of Mars.\n\nI let myself I bask in success, just for a fleeting moment. Because then the fuckton that is reality hit—literally. The pressure differential between Earth’s thick atmosphere and Mars’ near-vacuum was catastrophic. The gateway transformed into a monstrous vortex, sucking everything in its path. Tools, papers, and even part of the lab’s wall vanished into the void as the portal raged uncontrollably.\n\nIn a desperate bid to stop the destruction, I sent a nearby robot through to destroy the structure. Sparks flew, the portal collapsed, and silence descended—broken only by my ragged breathing. When the dust settled, I opened my eyes to a scene of utter devastation: the once-pristine workshop lay in ruins, the wall partially collapsed, and shards of equipment littered the floor.\n\nDespite almost dying the only thought that rushed through my head: “I was the first person on MARS! I’d love to see the folks over at NASA’s face if they ever train one of their telescopes on that area and see paper and part of the wall. Try and explain that as just pareidolia.”\n\nThe thrill of my achievement soured, replaced by the crushing realization of how narrowly I had avoided total disaster. My heart raced as I questioned my own recklessness. Then, the sound of sirens grew louder, and dread pooled in my stomach. The authorities.\n\nFlashing lights painted the warehouse in ominous shades of red and blue. The first officer stepped into the chaos, his eyes widening as he took in the scene. “What in the world happened here? We got reports of loud noises, like an explosion.” he demanded.\n\nI plastered on what I hoped was a calm expression. “Officer,” I began, steadying my voice. “There was an accident. I... uh... stacked some boxes wrong. They fell and—well—chain reaction. It was a mess.”\n\nHis gaze narrowed as he surveyed the gaping hole in the wall. “Stacked boxes? This looks like a bomb went off.”\n\n“Yeah,” I said, nodding earnestly. “A really bad stacking job.”\n\nThe officer raised an eyebrow, clearly unconvinced. “So, you’re telling me boxes caused this?” He gestured to the destruction like a prosecutor presenting evidence in court.\n\n“Absolutely,” I replied, doubling down. “I’ve been meaning to get better at Tetris. Clearly, I need practice.”\n\nA younger officer stifled a laugh behind him, but the senior cop wasn’t amused. “We’ll need to file a full report. This kind of ‘box stacking’ can’t happen again.”\n\n“Of course,” I said solemnly. “I’ll hold a safety seminar for the team.”\n\n“You don’t really have a team, do you?”\n\n“No, sir,” I admitted, “but I’ll still make a PowerPoint.”\n\nAfter a tense inspection, they seemed to accept my bizarre explanation—or at least decided it wasn’t worth digging deeper. As they left, the younger officer muttered, “Worst game of Jenga ever,” earning a glare from his partner.\n\nWith the police gone, I surveyed the wreckage, conflicted. The experiment had proven the portal itself worked flawlessly, but the 