onnections.\n\nThough time-consuming, each step required precise observations and adjustments. Consulting Google Maps and RedShift night sky, I had to carefully calibrate my location based on what I saw - which was easier said than done with unpredictable weather patterns and cloudy skies, not to mention the trouble I ran into when I found myself crossing over the ocean (a makeshift raft helped here). I headed west to ensure I wouldn’t run out of night as I continued my endeavor.\n\nDawn creeped through the workshop’s window as the final connection locked into place, the nanites hummed with anticipation, their synchronized energy casting a soft glow around the portal. My hands trembled as I activated the remote ERB gateway, the shimmering light pulsing with life. I could hardly contain myself—I needed to share this moment.\n\nI raced home, bursting through the door and slamming it behind me. “Wake up!” I called, shaking my wife gently but insistently.\n\nShe groaned, burying her face in the pillow. “It’s too early for this,” she muttered.\n\n“You’ll want to see this,” I insisted, practically pulling her out of bed. Groggy and half-protesting, she followed me to the car. I drove like a man possessed, my excitement spilling out in bits of hurried explanation as she gave me skeptical glances from the passenger seat.\n\nWhen we arrived at the warehouse, her annoyance gave way to stunned silence. In the corner of the room, the ERB gateway shimmered, defying the constraints of time and space. Without hesitation, I took her hand and led her through.\n\nThe transition was seamless, the sensation fleeting, and instantaneously we were standing in the cool night air of Kyoto, Japan. Her eyes widened in disbelief as we stepped into her mother’s backyard, the familiar surroundings bathed in moonlight.\n\n“Come on,” I urged, pulling her around the house toward the front door.\n\nHer mother opened the front door. The shock on her face mirrored the awe we felt. “Ka-san!” my wife ex