humans opens a Pandora’s box we may never close. What if we create beings without rights, forced to exist solely for our purposes? Are we ready for that?”\n\nI paused, grappling with her words. Morgan had been the voice of reason since joining, tempering my ambitions with practicality. “Our intentions are noble,” I countered. “The clones wouldn’t be tools or slaves. They’d be fully human, treated as equals and contributors. We’d establish safeguards to ensure their rights and well-being.”\n\nHer expression softened, though doubt lingered. “Even with safeguards, have you considered their psychological and emotional toll? What if they suffer? What if they question their existence?”\n\nI leaned forward, meeting her gaze. “We don’t have all the answers, but we’ll do everything possible to create meaningful lives for them. Purpose, community, dignity, these will be our guiding principles.”\n\nA tense silence hung between us. Finally, Morgan exhaled, her shoulders relaxing. “Fine,” she said, her voice heavy with resolve. “But we proceed cautiously, with ethics and compassion guiding every step.”\n\nWith the Infinitron’s unparalleled computational power, our team of biologists and genetic engineers embarked on the controversial mission of creating human clones to sustain our off-world colonies. The ethical weight of this endeavor loomed over us, but necessity demanded bold solutions. Beyond mere cloning, we were able to leverage the brain-Infinitron-interface technology to create a simulated environment where the clones could rapidly grow and learn, condensing decades of knowledge and experience into just three hours.\n\nDespite our initial reservations, and to our astonishment, it worked.\n\nKey to this process was transparency. Within the simulation, clones were informed of their origins and purpose, gaining a full understanding of their roles in The S Corp’s vision. They knew they were clones growing up in a simulation and given full choice if they wanted to leave. Some