e found someone seated at a table in the walkway. A man with an air of quiet authority, dressed impeccably in a tailored suit and a hat that contrasted greatly with his dreadlocks and large heavy chains around his neck. My breath caught. The emblem on his hat bore an uncanny resemblance to the symbol of Lady I.\n\n“Apologies,” he said, standing and offering a polite bow. His voice carried a calm confidence that immediately set him apart. “It seems I’m a bit lost.”\n\n“No harm done,” I said, though curiosity gnawed at me. “Your hat—it’s intriguing. May I ask about the symbol?”\n\nHe removed the hat and studied it for a moment, his lips curling into a faint smile. “You have a sharp eye,” he replied, his tone both amused and guarded. “The emblem is not one many notice, let alone recognize.”\n\nI stepped closer, lowering my voice. “I think I’ve seen it before, if not the symbol itself, then what it represents.”\n\nHis expression shifted, his eyes narrowing slightly as if measuring my words. “Is that so? Perhaps you’re more attuned to the unseen than most.”\n\n“What do you mean?” I asked, glancing back at my wife, who was now watching the exchange with keen interest.\n\nThe man gestured toward the plush seat he had vacated, inviting me to sit. “You can call me R,” he said, his tone soft yet commanding. “I’ve been associated with this train for many years. It’s more than just a vehicle, it’s a passageway, a crucible for the strange and the extraordinary.”\n\nMy wife took the seat beside me, her curiosity as piqued as mine. “You mean the train itself has... secrets?” she asked.\n\nR inclined his head. “Not the train, per se, but the passengers it carries. This journey has a way of drawing out what lies beneath; dreams, fears, truths one might prefer to keep buried.”\n\n“And the symbol?” I pressed. “Does it have to do with Lady I and Lady A?”\n\nHis eyes flickered with recognition at the names, though he said nothing for a long moment. The silence stretched, thick with impl