es wrong?”\n\nA pause hung in the air before I answered. “Failure is always a possibility. But we’ve built redundancies, fail-safes, and response protocols to mitigate risks. No step of this journey was taken lightly. And while the space elevators are here as symbols and ways to jump off world into local space, they’re not the primary means of travel or communication. That’s what the ERBs are for.”\n\nPresident Rodriguez leaned back, his sharp eyes gleaming. “You speak as if the future is already written. Yet, here we sit, a room of wary leaders. How do you propose to temper the chaos this announcement will unleash?”\n\nI allowed a brief smile. “By guiding the narrative. Initial travel will be highly regulated. All those secret construction sites around the world that none of your spies ever were able to infiltrate. We’ve expanded the already existing ERB network hubs and elevator base stations into existing international airports. Think of it as just another terminal. Land at concourse A, transfer to the space elevator or ERB gateway, pop out at a gate on concourse C at another airport. Movement between Earth and space will mirror current air travel protocols. S Corp will lead, but we’ll do so with the world’s cooperation.”\n\nThe room buzzed with murmurs, questions flying fast. Some challenged. Others strategized. The skepticism of earlier moments softened into cautious optimism.\n\nLee folded her arms. “You’ve given us a map. Now we need the compass. What’s the immediate plan?”\n\n“We unveil the project publicly in three months, in April.” I said. “Before then, our task is preparation, global agreements, resource allocations, and public education. Unity will define our success.”\n\nWhile the delegates started to break off and talk among themselves, considering the impact of this revelation, I stepped away from where I was speaking, my gaze drawn back outside. Earth loomed below; a luminous orb suspended against the infinite void. It looked so serene, so unified, 