ai each day. You’ve got full control over who goes in and out of the airport. Correct? This is nothing more than a fancy airplane. Or are you telling me you’d completely ban aircraft in China because you weren’t the one flying each plane.”\n\nThe room shifted, skepticism giving way to tentative curiosity. The representative, ever cautious, raised an eyebrow. “And the costs? Surely you don’t expect nations to shoulder the burden alone.”\n\n“Precisely why S Corp is footing the entire bill,” I replied. “Each elevator’s value exceeds $150 trillion, an investment beyond the entire world’s capacity, let alone a single nation. But with shared governance, the returns, economic, technological, cultural, will transform humanity.”\n\nThe Chinese representative exhaled sharply. “You propose a partnership where we can neither buy nor ignore your influence.”\n\n“Correct,” I said simply. “You cannot buy the sky, nor can you bar humanity’s ascent. The choice isn’t to compete but to cooperate. Together, we can build a legacy that transcends borders.”\n\nA heavy silence filled the room as leaders absorbed the magnitude of the project. Then President Rodriguez spoke, his voice laced with grudging admiration. “You’ve thought of everything, haven’t you?”\n\n“Not everything,” I admitted. “But enough to start.”\n\nThe Chinese representative, after a tense silence, extended a hand. “If this is the future, let it be a shared one.”\n\nI grasped his hand firmly, a momentary relief tempering the weight on my shoulders. “It will be. For all of us.”\n\nBut as the handshake lingered, so did the quiet doubts. What if the cooperation faltered? What if humanity’s divisions proved stronger than its aspirations?\n\nNearby, delegates from New Zealand and the United Kingdom stood by the panoramic viewport, their gazes locked on the web of satellites and the sinuous arc of the space elevators stretching into the void. Their quiet awe was almost palpable, and it drew me toward them like a tide.\n\n“Quite 