lizing global economies?”\n\n“We’ve worked with leading economists and advanced financial models to ensure a controlled integration,” I replied. “The reserves will be released incrementally, tied to projects that directly stimulate growth and employment. This isn’t about flooding the market, it’s about targeted impact.”\n\nThe U.S. representative leaned forward. “You mentioned initiatives. What exactly are you proposing we do with this newfound financial windfall?”\n\nI laid out the framework. “Expanding Medicare for All to provide universal healthcare. Bolstering the Peace Corps both domestically and interplanetary to employ vulnerable populations. Constructing micro-housing across the country to eliminate homelessness. And, perhaps most importantly, revitalizing public education, offering completely free schooling from kindergarten through post-doctoral studies.”\n\nThe representative’s voice softened. “Free education? That alone would transform generations.”\n\n“Yes,” I said. “It would liberate students from crippling debt and empower them to contribute to society without financial shackles. These initiatives aren’t just dreams, they’re achievable today. Without having to service 40% of your budget on debt payments, that can be all reinvested directly back in the population. Not to mention, it then frees a considerable amount of the population to help drive value to the corporations who are the backbone of your economy. A highly educated workforce, low taxes, and freedom to expand to the stars.”\n\nNew Zealand’s delegate chimed in. “And how do you ensure these programs sustain themselves long-term?”\n\n“By making them self-sufficient,” I replied. “Public-private partnerships, innovative funding mechanisms, and reinvestments from the economic growth these programs generate. This isn’t a one-time fix; it’s a systemic recalibration. We know there is a huge market for private insurers, educational institutions, and construction firms. We don’t want anyone to be excluded or able to share in the growth and prosperity opening the solar system will provide.”\n\nThe U.S. representative was silent for a moment, then spoke with measured conviction. “If this comes to fruition, it’s a paradigm shift for our nation. But this isn’t a decision we can rush.”\n\n“Take your time,” I assured them. “We’ll provide any information or support you need to make the right choice. We’re also very aware of the power of lobbyists. I’m sure we could financially incentivize things as needed.”\n\n“This could change everything. We’ll give it the consideration it deserves.”\n\nI shook their hand firmly. “It already has. Together, we can ensure that change is for the better.”\n\nLeaving the U.S. delegation to ruminate on the bombshell I just dropped in their lap, I walked over to the Japanese and Korean representatives. The absence of North Korea and Russia hung heavily over the room, an invisible tension that underscored every move.\n\nI greeted them with a nod and gestured to the seats near the viewing deck. “Gentlemen, it’s an honor to have you here. Let’s talk about how we move forward, together.”\n\nThe Japanese representative wasted no time, his voice tinged with unease. “Let’s not ignore the glaring issue. These absent nations, North Korea, Russia, could leverage the wealth they’ve received through your land deals for military escalation. How do we safeguard against that? It feels shady like you’ve somehow broken the sanctions on them by providing them these funds. Funds no doubt being funneled directly into the conflict in Ukraine.”\n\nI nodded, understanding his concern. “Transparency and accountability are cornerstones of these agreements. Funds allocated must be tied directly to infrastructure and social programs, not military endeavors. We’ve implemented a monitoring mechanism that involves international oversight to ensure compliance.”\n\nThe Korean delegate leaned forward. “Even so, history tells us that such funds can easily be diverted. What happens when they defy these terms?”\n\n“Our agreements allow for punitive measures,” I said firmly. “If a nation violates the terms, S Corp retains the right to suspend further economic partnerships and resource access. In other words, being cut off from the elevators or ERB network. We’ve also developed technological measures, covert and overt, to track resource usage and deter misuse.”\n\nThe Japanese delegate let out a long breath, his skepticism yielding to thoughtfulness. “And if the Russians won’t end the conflict, or another conflict inevitably arises? You’ve unlocked massive wealth, and the ability to move people, materials, and weapons anywhere in the world. That’s bound to alter the regional balance.”\n\n“It’s a valid point,” I acknowledged. “But S Corp isn’t just an innovator in space. Behind the scenes, we’re working with peacekeeping organizations to monitor potential flashpoints. We’re also fostering collaboration between nations, showing that cooperation in space exploration can set a precedent for unity on Earth. We’ve got advanced scanning technology that can block completely the shipment of any item or material through our networks. Even drug mules will be out of a job. Not to mention our advancements in non-lethal, humane suppression systems.”\n\nThe Korean representative’s brow furrowed. “Cooperation? Even with nations that see each other as enemies?”\n\n“Yes,” I said. “Take this meeting as an example. Sitting together, finding common ground, this is the blueprint. Our space initiatives aren’t just about technology. They’re about creating shared stakes, shared successes. A conflict over Earth-bound rivalries diminishes everyone’s access to what lies beyond.”\n\nThe Japanese delegate crossed his arms, his tone softening. “You make a compelling case, but skepticism will linger. These mechanisms you’ve described—how do we know they’ll be enforced impartially?”\n\n“S Corp isn’t beholden to any single nation,” I replied. “Our neutrality is our strength. We operate with interstellar stewardship as our guiding principle. And we’re prepared to hold anyone, ourselves included, accountable if necessary. Because we do operate in the interstellar space we technically are incorporated as our own planet.”\n\nThe Korean delegate nodded thoughtfully, offering a faint smile. “It’s bold. But perhaps bold is what we need. This isn’t a time for half-measures.”\n\n“Precisely,” I said. “The stakes are too high to allow mistrust to derail progress. We’ve crafted some initial contingency plans for every scenario, from resource misuse to regional instability. Our goal is to prevent conflict, not exacerbate it. But to make sure those things work, we need everyone’s help and input here.”\n\nThe Japanese representative bowed deeply. “For the sake of our region, and our world, we’re willing to work with you. But vigilance will be key.”\n\n“Agreed,” I said, shaking his hand. “This partnership will only thrive through continued dialogue and collaboration.”\n\nMoments later, I noticed the Israeli and Iranian delegates standing nearby, their usual distance softened by the neutral setting. To my surprise, the Iranian representative leaned forward, proposing a regional working group to explore cooperation in space technologies, a bold suggestion that hinted at a willingness to move beyond entrenched division.\n\nI stepped closer, sensing the gravity of the moment. “Gentlemen,” I said, my voice calm but resolute, “you’re seizing an opportunity that history rarely affords. If space exploration can bring adversaries to the same table, imagine what else it might achieve.”\n\nThe Israeli representative met my gaze, cautious but contemplative. “We’re willing to discuss shared goals. But trust doesn’t form overnight.”\n\n“True,” I agreed. “But step builds trust step. This regional group could focus on mutual interests—space exploration, resource management, even disaster response. Let the results speak louder than history.”\n\nThe Iranian delegate 