t they don’t understand is that we’ve built something unbreakable. Infinite energy backed with a limitless network of infinite computational devices. Not to mention an inexhaustible army of robots being built by the Matter Replicators. Still, we can’t afford to underestimate them. Desperation makes people dangerous.”\n\nAcross the room, the holo-screen flickered to life, TJ’s holographic image materializing with his usual air of calculated calm. “We must acknowledge that this isn’t just a tactical battle,” he said, his analytical tone laced with concern. “This is ideological. We’re not simply fighting insurgents; we’re combating a fundamental rejection of S Corp’s vision for humanity. Like the crusades in the past, this has deep religious connotations. Both Christian and Islamic, even some Hindu extremists, have been using heavy spiritual imagery, painting as us the devil, in league with demons, and our technologies portals from hell.”\n\nThe nuance of his words settled over the room like a storm cloud. This wasn’t just about territory or cultural sovereignty; it was about belief.\n\n“Our objective remains clear,” Patrick said, his voice resolute. “Neutralize the threat while minimizing collateral damage. Our technology gives us the upper hand, but we need to adapt faster than they can.”\n\nGil interjected. “It’s not just about defense. There are innocent people caught in the crossfire, people we’ve sworn to protect. We need to show them we’re better than the heresy these terrorists peddle.”\n\nI nodded, my fingers steepled as I absorbed their arguments. “Then we escalate. Not with weapons, but with outreach. We must connect with local communities. Provide more aid. More opportunities. Education. Show them that S Corp isn’t here to dominate, it’s here to uplift.”\n\nTJ’s image flickered, his eyes narrowing. “You’re not wrong,” he conceded. “But goodwill hasn’t won any hearts in this war. In fact, it seems to have been doing the opposite. The rebels are heavily entrenched in the local media ecosphere and are able to spread misinformation faster than we can combat it, poisoning minds with lies about resource exploitation and government manipulation. We need to control the narrative, but without access to those channels, it’s going to be almost impossible to make any progress. It’s not like in the early days of S Corp when we controlled everything and could just lock everything down and keep communications controlled. This is what happens when anti-intellectualism runs amok. Free speech and do your own research crowd has distorted what people think is the truth.”\n\nGil’s lips curled into a wry smile. “A full-scale communication campaign,” he suggested. “Transparency. Highlight our successes. Show them how many lives we’ve improved. We drown their lies with undeniable truth.”\n\nPatrick’s brow furrowed. “But truth is never enough. These terrorists have belief, staunch denialism, and emotions behind their actions.”\n\n“Then we adapt,” TJ said sharply. “We dominate every battlefield, physical, ideological, digital, and spiritual. Crush their networks. Expose their leaders. If we leave any room for opposition, we risk everything.”\n\nTheir words coiled around me like thick smoke, creeping into every breath, every thought. This wasn’t just a battle of perspectives; it was a fight to keep from choking on the truth.\n\n“Can’t we just buy some of these folks out? Forge alliances with those who align with our goals. Local broadcasters, pastors and imams who need new books or something,” I said, my voice firm. “And for those who don’t... we leave no path to victory.”\n\nA message chimed on Patrick’s computer. “Fuck! The situation in Afghanistan just went to hell,” he began. “Intel confirms that a faction of the Taliban, backed by splinter groups and covert Russian support, is targeting our personnel and facilities.”\n\nThe words hit like a hammer. Our earlier strategy of offering independent groups and historically repressed religions, their own colonies on the remote moons and distant colony worlds, had brought an uneasy peace to Earth. Despite everything, it seemed some factions still chose violence over progress.\n\n“Most groups accepted the deal with open arms. It gave them an opportunity to start fresh, on their own terms, without interference or threat from the outside. Free to pursue their own things,” I said, my voice tight with frustration. “They left quietly, took the offer, and moved on. So why now?”\n\nPatrick’s jaw tightened. “People love playing the victim. Not everyone is going to be content to leave. Some stayed behind, bitter and vengeful, and now they’ve found allies willing to exploit that hatred. This isn’t random. It’s coordinated. Fuckers are given the world on the plate and they turn around and shit in your face, then complain they’re being oppressed because they’re not allowed to poop on you.”\n\nGil returned to the room, his movements brisk, a stack of reports in hand. “It get’s worse,” he said, dropping the papers onto the table with a heavy thud. “Hindustani extremists are targeting our supply centers and education centers. Blaming the destruction on S Corp at the same time. It’s calculated chaos.”\n\nPatrick skimmed the reports, his face darkening with every page. “This isn’t just retaliation,” he said, slamming a fist on the table. “They’re trying to dismantle us from within.”\n\nThe holographic image of TJ flickered on the screen, his analytical gaze scanning the room. “We need better fucking intelligence,” he said sharply. “This seems too coordinated to be chaos. They’ve got to be organizing, or have a deeper center of control somewhere. We need to know everything. Capabilities, leadership structures, alliances, everything. Understanding their network is key to shutting this down.”\n\nI nodded, my mind racing. “Partick and Gil, get your folks to prioritize intelligence gathering,” I said. “We need to know who’s pulling the strings, how they’re recruiting, and where the