ow do we counter this bullshit? We can’t just bulldoze over their concerns without deepening their resistance.”\n\nJames’s gaze darkened. “We need a two-pronged approach. First we need to poison them from within. As much as I hate to say it, the whole pacifistic approach might need to stop. We’ve got to cut the literal heads of these snakes, prevent the infection from spreading.”\n\n“And the second?” I asked.\n\n“Don’t ask,” he said, the word almost jarring in its softness.\n\nGil entered, his brisk stride breaking the tension. He dropped a stack of reports onto the table. “They’ve stepped up their attacks again, this time with more advanced munitions,” he said bluntly.\n\nAs James prepared to leave, he stopped at the door, his gaze lingering on me. “This isn’t just about S Corp,” he said, his voice quiet but heavy. “This is about the future of humanity. If we fail here, we lose more than this fight, we lose the ability for rationality to prevail and delusions to die.”\n\nJames’ words stayed with me long after he left. But I would have time to ponder them when I was dead. Another urgent message appeared on my screen. This time it was a report from the field—something dark, something chilling. The battle ahead wasn’t just political or ideological. It was existential.\n\nPatrick came running into my office and handed me the paper version of the report. A tangible emphasis of the seriousness of its contents - odd in this age of digital information. I had to take a double take and reread the report. North Korean scientists working with Russian nuclear researchers had forged a sinister alliance, creating a new nuclear device ominously named the “God Killer.”\n\nThe name alone sent a chill down my spine. The device wasn’t just a weapon, it was a herald of annihilation, capable of collapsing entire civilizations at the push of a button – it’s projected yield was 138 billion Mega Tons. More than all the combined nuclear devices humanity had ever made.\n\n“This has got to be