73	1	73	68: A Sorhhinda Sunset	Sunlight, filtered through lush alien foliage, dappled the wooden walls of Yukiko’s room. Outside, the whirring of hover scooters mingled with the trills of an unfamiliar bird, an avian concerto unlike anything Earth had ever offered. It was the middle of August, (according to earth calendars), and a five years since they arrived.\n\nNow a teenager, Yukiko stood by the window, her dark hair neatly braided, eyes fixed on the endless expanse beyond. Sorhhinda, once just a speck on the cosmic map, now felt as familiar as her own reflection.\n\nHer mother had risen to chief scientist, a role she filled with quiet authority. Her father, an ex-Japan Self-Defense Force veteran, had remained on Earth, taking a security post at the Vatican. Despite the distance, the ERB communication network and advanced holographic displays kept them connected, their conversations bridging the vastness of space with the ease of a whispered word.\n\nShe’d initially missed her elementary school friends on Earth, of course. But the thrill of being a pioneer, of learning about alien ecosystems in school, of collecting iridescent beetles in the emerald twilight, eclipsed the pang of separation. The ERB, a shimmering arch in the colony’s central plaza, brought a smile to her face. A flick of her wrist, a whispered password, and her best friend, Hana, materialized on the screen, giggling in her crisp school uniform.\n\nLife, for everyone in the colony, had blossomed under the triple sun of Alnitak. S Corp, the corporation spearheading the interstellar expansion, had promised paradise, and so far, it seemed they’d delivered. Yet, beneath the blueish veneer, anxieties lurked. The machines, marvels of Earth technology, seemed to hum a shade off-key lately.\n\nAnimals, once curious, now showed flashes of aggression. Yukiko had witnessed a bioluminescent scorpiolith take down a hover-drone with uncanny ferocity. And whispers about “The Incident.” A closed-door meeting between Colony Governor Tanaka and the S Corp executives, rippled through the housing blocks.\n\nOne humid afternoon, Yukiko decided to ditch classes and ventured beyond the colony’s protective dome. Seeing a new variant of a local butterfly, she took out her hand held scanner and chased after it. But soon the carefree laughter died in her throat when she stumbled upon a scene that tore down her carefully constructed utopia. A bioluminescent spider, bigger than any she’d seen before, lay feasting on the lifeless husk of a bio-gardener. Panic clawed at her throat. This wasn’t the playful menagerie she’d was used to. This was something else, something wild and untamed.\n\nAs Yukiko surveyed the gruesome sight before her, her heart pounded in her chest. The once serene and harmonious landscape had turned into a twisted nightmare, and she knew in that moment that she had stumbled upon something far more sinister than just the effects of malfunctioning machines.\n\nIn the distance, concealed within the shadows of an overgrown grove of trees, Yukiko spotted a group of figures clad in dark attire. Their movements were purposeful, their expressions hidden beneath hoods and masks. They moved with an eerie precision, each motion deliberate and calculated. Keeping her distance, she followed them back towards the center of town.\n\nYukiko’s breath hitched as she strained to hear the hushed conversation ahead. Shadows flickered across the plaza as the figures moved, their voices sharp with urgency. Watching as they worked on devices around the area. Snippets of their plan reached her in broken fragments.\n\n“...the network will collapse... I AM demands it... everyone is busy now with their daily work, no one ever watches the gateway. Shipments only come in a few times a week.”\n\nHer stomach twisted at the venom in their tone. I AM. The words buzzed in her mind, heavy with implications. Was it a code name? A rogue reverse AI? Whatever it was, the power of its suggestion was undeniable, and its intent catastrophic. Yukiko’s eyes darted to the devices scattered around the gateway’s base—small, innocuous-looking, yet bristling with purpose.\n\nThe ERB gateway loomed above them, its shimmering arch of energy pulsing faintly, like a heartbeat connected to the birthplace of humanity. If the terrorists succeeded, it wouldn’t just sever this colony from Earth—it would unravel the entire ERB network, dooming countless lives across the stars.\n\nHer heart hammered. She couldn’t let this happen.\n\nSteeling herself, Yukiko slipped a hand into her pack, fingers brushing past tools and supplies until they closed around the scanner flashlight. Not exactly a weapon, but if she cranked it to max intensity and angled it just right… it might be enough.\n\nHer heart pounded as she twisted the dial to intensive blast mode, its power meant for deep mineral scans, not tactical maneuvers. But desperate times called for desperate improvisation. Holding her breath, she aimed the beam at the smooth curve of the portal’s surface, angling it so the light refracted in a chaotic burst.\n\nA sudden searing flash exploded across the plaza—brilliant, erratic, and blinding. The terrorists reeled, cursing as they staggered back, hands flying to shield their eyes. The portal shimmered, its reflection amplifying the radiance into a disorienting cascade of fractured light.\n\nYukiko didn’t hesitate. She launched herself forward, weaving through the stunned figures, her breath sharp and focused. Sparks spat and died as her fingers flew over their devices—yanking wires, and removing connections with frantic precision. Her pulse roared in her ears, but fear faded beneath raw adrenaline.\n\nOne task. One mission. Stop the destruction.\n\nThe terrorists regained their composure, their curses cutting through the haze. Realizing their plot was unraveling, they turned and fled, their boots echoing against the stone plaza as they disappeared into the wilds beyond the dome’s protective barrier.\n\nBriefly, Yukiko stood frozen, her chest heaving. Relief flooded her, but it was fleeting. Her gaze fell on the gateway and noticed another sinister device still attached to its base, a strange machine pulsing with an unnatural rhythm.\n\nShe recognized it immediately. Her science class had just covered this; it was an energy destabilizer. It was a fairly common piece of tech, normally used by miners in conjunction with specialized ERBs to create localized excavation blasts, it worked by overloading and collapsing the quantum field anchoring the gateway. Examining this one, however, it wasn’t just a standard device. Its configuration was warped, augmented with unfamiliar technology that hummed with malevolence. It wasn’t meant to sever this gateway alone. Its energy ripples would cascade across the network, tearing apart connections system-wide. And worse, it could unleash something far more destructive, something Yukiko couldn’t fully comprehend but instinctively feared.\n\nHer hands hovered over the device, her breath hitching as panic gripped her. Disabling the other devices had been child’s play, almost made those countless hours she spent playing BombSquad Simulator 2000 worth it. This device was different—this was fused, tangled, and too deeply embedded within the ERB system to be disarmed. There was no way to surgically remove it without destroying the gateway entirely.\n\nLeaving the device intact wasn’t an option either. She could almost sense the destabilizer’s malicious design, its intent to spread chaos and destruction. If it wasn’t stopped, the ripple effect would annihilate the entire ERB network, leaving countless colonies and trillions of other lives at risk.\n\nFor all the greatness the human expansion had been so far, this single, sinister act would undo it all.\n\nHer mind spiraled through the grim possibilities, each more unbearable than the last. The gateway shimmered faintly, its energy vibrating beneath her fingertips. It wasn’t just a