ydration’s hydration.\n\nNext came the Argentinians. Their bakery smelled heavenly, which made the angry shouting in Spanish more jarring. “Frank! Look at this!” screamed Sr. Hernandez, thrusting a green empanada in my face.\n\n“Looks fine to me,” I lied, gagging at the algae funk.\n\n“It’s these raccoons!” Hernandez declared. “They’re sabotaging us, stealing yeast, ruining dough!”\n\nI sighed. “Hernandez, there are no space raccoons. That’s not a thing.”\n\n“Oh really?” He jabbed at a monitor showing grainy footage of a raccoon somersaulting through zero-g with a canister of yeast. “Explain ese… Sr. Janitor!”\n\nLong story short, the “raccoons” were an algae contamination issue in Hydroponics. I fixed it, only to get a call about the zero-g bowling alley.\n\nApparently, some jackass had bowled a strike so aggressively the ball ricocheted through the ventilation system and into the oxygen recycler. I arrived to find bowlers floating mid-air, swearing as pins spun past their heads. A cosmic cat was chasing the rogue bowling ball like it was God’s gift to felines.\n\nUsing duct tape and two vacuum hoses, I rigged a slingshot to yank the ball free. “One, two, three!” The ball flew back onto the lane with a satisfying thunk. Cheers erupted as the bowlers tumbled gracelessly back to solid footing.\n\n“Another crisis solved,” I muttered, wiping bowling-ball grease off my hands and ignoring the faint smell of burnt duct tape. My comm buzzed, interrupting what might have passed for a moment of peace.\n\n“Frank, pick up. We’ve got a situation,” came Pete’s voice—calm, steady, and with just a hint of his trademark sarcasm. That voice was the only reason I survived life on Elysium without losing my mind. Pete, my husband, and the best damn comms officer for S Corp, was the yin to my perpetually frazzled yang.\n\n“What now?” I sighed, already bracing for Pete’s mix of bad news and dry humor.\n\n“You’re not going to like it,” Pete said. “We’ve lost contact with Sorhhinda.”\n\nMy