stion. The flickering light of their cramped Mumbai kitchen glowed behind her, an all-too-familiar backdrop from a life Aditi couldn’t forget.\n\n“Amma,” Aditi whispered, her voice barely steady.\n\nHer mother’s face softened into a weak smile. “Meri pyari bitiya,” she said, her voice warm but strained. “How are you, beta? Did you eat?”\n\n“I’m fine, Amma,” Aditi replied, swallowing the lump in her throat. “What about you? How are things at home?”\n\nHer mother sighed, the sound heavy with weariness. “Beta, the air here keeps getting worse. Your papa’s cough hasn’t gone away. And Rahul, he’s hasn’t been home since you’ve left.” She hesitated, her voice dropping as though speaking too loudly might break the fragile connection. “When are we coming, bitiya? It’s getting so hard to live here.”\n\nAditi’s chest tightened, her heart twisting with guilt. Her mother’s words carried the weight of everything she couldn’t yet deliver. “Amma, the waiting list is very long,” she murmured, her voice faltering. “I’m trying, but it’s going to take some time.”\n\nHer mother’s eyes filled with unshed tears, and she gripped the edge of her sari like it was a lifeline. “Beta, we’re only waiting to see you. This house... it feels so empty without you. Especially since Rahul is…”\n\nAditi clenched her fists, her nails biting into her palms. She couldn’t bring herself to explain the truth—that the waitlist wasn’t just about bureaucracy. It was about caste, privilege, and money, all designed to filter out those deemed “unworthy” of the stars. To Elysium, her parents were just another statistic in the shadows of Earth’s decay.\n\n“I promise, Amma,” she said, forcing strength into her trembling voice. “I’ll do something. I’ll bring you and Papa here. Just give me a little more time. I can ask around if they can help you find Rahul.”\n\nAmma nodded, but her face betrayed the fragile hope behind her acceptance. “Beta, we just want you to be happy. But don’t forget everything you left behind here.”\n\n“I won’t, Amma,” Aditi whispered, her voice cracking. “I could never forget.”\n\nThe static on the hologram worsened, Amma’s image flickering like a ghost caught between two worlds. Aditi’s fingers hovered over the terminal, wishing she could reach through and hold her mother’s hand, erase the distance between them. But all she had was the cold, unfeeling glow of the light projections.\n\nWhen the call ended, Aditi stared at the empty display, her chest hollow, and her heart heavy. Outside, Earth continued to spin, its soft light taunting her with its unattainable closeness.\n\nShe turned back to her dorm, the equations on her tablet a temporary refuge from the promises that felt more impossible with every passing day. Yet her mother’s words echoed in her mind, soft and insistent: Don’t forget everything you left behind.\n\nLate one night, months later, in the quiet hum of Elysium University’s research lab, Aditi sat surrounded by the rhythmic thrum of machinery—a sound that usually lulled her into focused tranquility. But tonight was different. The sharp crackle of static from the antique radio telescope shattered the stillness, cutting through the usual calm like a siren in the dark, jolting her awake.\n\nShe shot upright, her pulse quickening as her fingers danced over the console. The erratic signal was unlike anything she’d encountered before—raw, jagged, desperate. Adrenaline coursed through her veins as she filtered the data, refining the garbled static into discernible fragments.\n\n“This is... I am... Janus gate jump... execute... Pluto…”\n\nThe voice dissolved into white noise, leaving behind an oppressive silence that seemed to thrum with unspoken danger.\n\nAditi froze, her heart pounding in her chest. Pluto. The name alone sent a shiver down her spine. It was the solar system’s edge, home to S Corp’s experimental Exo-planet colonization facilities. A place shrouded in secrecy and so isolated that even rumors struggled to escape.\n\nProtocol dict