e this is the right file?” Hiro asked, his voice a faint whisper. The screen was filled with diagrams in ancient Latin, their intricate notations spiraling into what looked like madness but hinted at genius.\n\n“Yes,” Aditi replied, her voice clipped but resolute. “The signal referenced Janus. These blueprints match the propulsion system specs—systems that shouldn’t even exist.”\n\nHiro leaned back, his chair creaking. “Latin blueprints for a faster-than-light engine hidden in an S Corp database. Makes total sense,” he muttered, shaking his head. “We’re going to get caught, you know.”\n\n“We won’t,” Aditi said, her tone sharp enough to cut through his doubt. “Not if you stop talking and finish the decrypt.”\n\nHiro sighed but refocused. “You’re lucky I like you, Aditi. Otherwise, I’d be halfway back to my dorm with a bag of stolen protein bars and no regrets.”\n\n“I’ll buy you a lifetime supply of protein bars if we live through this,” she quipped. Her eyes flicked back to the terminal. “Is that the location?”\n\nHiro pointed at the screen. “Far side of Elysium, near the Rome space elevator. Hidden launch facility. I’ve flagged a vessel... the Genesis.” He turned to her, the rebellious glint in his eyes muted by apprehension. “It’s operational.”\n\nAditi exhaled sharply. “Operational? And no one’s using it?”\n\nHiro shrugged. “Maybe it’s classified. Maybe it’s bait. Either way, if you’re thinking about doing something reckless...”\n\n“I am,” she interrupted. Her voice dropped, firm and deliberate. “We’re going to Pluto.”\n\nHiro froze, then pushed his glasses up the bridge of his nose. “You’re serious.”\n\nAditi stepped closer, lowering her voice. “Hiro, this isn’t just about some rogue signal. Something’s happening out there. Sorhhinda’s gone dark, the Red Hats are more organized than anyone’s admitting, and now there’s this...” She gestured at the blueprints. “...this technology, hidden from everyone. If we don’t figure out what’s going on, who will?”\n\n“And you 