ied.\n\nHow long had he been here? Days? Weeks? Time had become a blur, a suffocating fog through which he could barely see.\n\nThe door hissed open, and a figure stepped inside, cloaked in shadows. The man’s presence was unsettling, his voice smooth yet devoid of warmth. “Call me Nameless,” he said, his tone as blank as the name itself. He exuded an air of authority that demanded attention, his words cutting through the haze clouding Todd’s mind.\n\n“You want freedom,” Nameless said, his gaze piercing. “Real freedom. Not the lies they feed you, not the false hope you’ve clung to. I can give it to you.”\n\nTodd’s throat tightened. He wanted to believe—needed to believe—but half a decade of betrayal had left his trust in tatters. He glared at Nameless, his voice raw. “What’s the catch?”\n\nThe cloaked figure’s lips curled into a faint smile. “There’s always a catch, Todd. Freedom comes with a price. But it’s a price you’ve already agreed to pay.”\n\nThe words pierced through Todd like hot pokers. He stared at the man, his mind racing. For years, he had fought, raged, and failed. But now, an ember of defiance flickered within him—a stubborn refusal to let despair consume him. This might be his last chance to matter, to fight for something that went beyond his own broken ideals.\n\n“What do I have to do?” he rasped, the words carrying the weight of his fractured resolve.\n\nNameless stepped closer, his shadow stretching across the sterile floor like a phantom. “I AM”\n\nDarkness engulfed everything and Todd was no more.\n\n**********\n\nThe crypts beneath the Vatican were a twisting maze of ancient stone, each corridor heavy with the weight of untold secrets. Candles flickered in alcoves, casting dancing shadows that seemed to breathe with the darkness. Todd followed Nameless, the man’s steps echoing softly in the damp air.\n\nAt last, they arrived at an imposing iron door, its surface etched with symbols that pierced Todd’s skin and demanded blood as a key. It groaned