choing through the room. “You’re the goddamn monster here, Todd, not us! You’ve deluded yourself into thinking your violence is some holy mission, but all you are is a rabid dog pissing on progress because you’re too stupid to understand it. So go ahead, preach your hellfire and brimstone, but don’t you dare shit there and pretend you’re anything but a bloodthirsty asshole hiding behind a Bible you clearly don’t even understand!”\n\nTodd leaned in so close to the camera that the veins on his forehead looked ready to burst. “Justice demands the blood of the wicked! Your heretical abominations, the Eternal Engine, your unholy Infinitron, they’re bringing forth things no man was ever meant to see. I’ve seen them in my dreams, the writhing ones, the mouths that speak madness. They’re coming for you, S Corp. They’re already here! You will rot in hell where you belong!”\n\nThe screen flashed violently, images burning into our retinas: grotesque forms, writhing tendrils, endless rows of serrated teeth. My stomach twisted, my mind reeling as reality blurred and bent under the weight of those cosmic horrors.\n\n“You’re insane,” I snapped, my voice harder now. “You think you’re fighting for God? You’re doing the bidding of the Demiurge you fucking shithead. You’ve deluded yourself into thinking destruction is divine!”\n\n“I am the sword of the Lord!” Todd bellowed, his voice raw, cracked with a zeal that bordered on hysteria. “You can’t see it, can you? Your arrogance blinds you to the truth. You have opened the gateway to hell. And I will throw you and every one of your demonic fuckers into the abyss if that’s what it takes to save humanity!”\n\n“Listen to yourself. Do you not see what your fuckery has unleashed? I shouted back, the room buzzing with the static charge of our mutual hatred. “You won’t win, Todd. S Corp isn’t going to crumble under your cult’s insanity.”\n\nHis laugh was a dry, rasping cackle that sent a chill down my spine. “You think this is about winning? No, Satan. This is about judgment. And soon, you’ll all answer for your sins. The time of reckoning is upon you.”\n\nThe screen went black, his final words hanging in the air like the toll of a funeral bell. The room was silent save for the muted alarms signaling our crumbling defenses. I let out a slow, ragged breath, my hands trembling.\n\n“Jesus Christ,” muttered Patrick, breaking the silence. “That man’s a fucking lunatic.”\n\n“No,” I said quietly, my voice heavy with dread. “He’s worse than that. He’s the devil.”\n\nThe weight of the transmission hung heavily in the air, suffocating us like a lead shroud. Everyone stared at me, their faces pale and drawn, their eyes desperate for a plan I didn’t yet have. The silence stretched, broken only by the hum of monitors displaying the devastation Todd’s forces had wrought.\n\n“Get me Dr. Fucking On right now!” I screamed.\n\nPatrick looked over his security files. “Shit!”\n\n“What now?” I asked.\n\n“She’s gone. She and her fucky little department dissappared about the same time all this shit was going down. Get this. She planted a virus that attempted to scramble our communications between Europa and Pluto just before this occurred. Not only that, but we’re getting reports of sporadic explosions all across the colonies on Ariel, the moon of Saturn and her last known location.” Gil chimed in.\n\n“That fucking bitch! I knew something was off with her. Gaslighting all of us about the Red Hats not being a problem. She was fucking one of them.” I yelled.\n\n“This threat cannot be underestimated,” James finally said, his voice sharp enough to cut through the oppressive stillness.\n\n“The Red Hat rebellion isn’t just some meal team six zealots playing dress up lunatics anymore. Their coordination, their resources. it’s far beyond anything we anticipated. They’ve breached our defenses, and they’re using tactics we can’t predict. Something is wrong. Terribly, damnably wrong.” I scanned their faces.\n\n“We need answers, and we need them now. Plus we need to figure out who the fuck else is in on this. It’s like we’ve been invaded internally as well. See who else Dr. On might have been working with.” I sighed.\n\nGil, his face a mask of stoicism, said. “I’ll launch a full-scale investigation immediately,” he said. “If there’s a leak, I’ll find it. We’ll sweep every system, interrogate every weak link. Reinforcing our defenses is priority one. No stone left unturned.”\n\n“Fuck that. It’s too late.” Patrick, cut in, his tone brimming with urgency. “Gil, I respect your thoroughness, but plugging holes... the whole fucking ship is sinking. Investigation isn’t going to stop them. These people aren’t just fighting, they want a holocaust. This isn’t just insurgency. It’s fanaticism.” He turned to me. “We have to hit back. Hard. Fast. Crush them before they can escalate further.”\n\nMy fists clenched as Patrick’s words sank in. He wasn’t wrong, every instinct I had screamed for action. But before I could respond, TJ cut in, his voice calm yet tinged with a sharp edge of concern.\n\n“Patrick, escalating to full-scale war risks more than lives, it risks the moral ground we’ve built everything on. We’re not a corporation of violence, and you damn well know it. The Matter Replicators? We sold them as a way to eat meat without killing a single living creature. We abolished the death penalty everywhere and pushed for humane rehabilitation programs across the board. Hell, even the personal shields we developed have saved thousands from violence and murder. That’s who we are.\n\n“And don’t fucking forget Korea!” His voice rose, laced with frustration. “We stopped an Eternal Engine bomb, Patrick, a whole goddamn bomb, and no one died or got so much as a fucking paper cut. Not a single life was lost because we stood firm in our principles. And now you’re ready to throw that legacy into the dirt? Public opinion is already on shaky ground; people are scared, confused, unsure who to trust. If we’re seen as the aggressors, we might as well hand Todd the fucking keys to the colonies, because that’s how many will rally to his side.”\n\n“Public opinion?” Patrick spat, his face darkening. “Don’t you remember Order 66, or do the thousands of folks we round up and exterminated not count. Before. Hoo boy. Before we were fighting conventional means that had no way of hurting us. We had the power to be the good guys and we still reacted. Now. Now when they are out for blood you want to go back and play nice? These maniacs just wiped out our fucking Pluto facility, with tech we weren’t able to stop. You think optics are going to stop them when they’re blowing up colonies and painting the walls with infidel’s blood propaganda?”\n\n“Enough,” I snapped, silencing the brewing argument. “This isn’t just about containment or PR. We’re dealing with people who are brainwashed, clinically insane. Todd and his Red Hats aren’t just using conventional warfare, they’ve weaponized their belief system. It’s the Russia-Korea fucking genocide all over again. They think they’re on a divine mission to exorcise us from existence. Negotiating with them is like trying to reason with a wildfire.”\n\nGil nodded grimly. “So what’s the plan? We can’t just sit here waiting for the next explosion.”\n\n“First, we uncover how they were able to overcome our defenses,” I said. “If they have inside information, we need to find the source. Or worse, if they’ve got something that nullifies what we can protect, God help us all. Second,” I turned to Patrick. “We prepare for countermeasures. I’m talking total eradication. If they’re hell-bent on destruction, we’ll meet them with firepower that makes them regret stepping into our orbit.”\n\nPatrick grinned, his expression a mix of approval and dark anticipation. “About time. We’ll draw up plans for surgical strikes and leverage the ERBs to teleport explosives directly up their assholes. Let’s show them what real power looks like.”\n\n“While you do that,” I continued, turning to TJ, “we’ll work on the other front. I want a campaign to reassure the colonies, dispel the lies Todd is spreading. Crack down on dissent, and prevent any false information getting out. I’m talking full censorship with regards to any of the Red Hat shit. We need people on our side, confident, united. If they see us falter, it’s over. We also need to aggressively wipe out anything like graffiti or other calls to arms being sent through non-traditional means.”\n\n“And if people keep siding with the Red Hats?” Gil asked, his tone blunt.\n\nMy voice dropped, cold and unyielding. “We give them a chance to see reason. And if not. Then they’ll burn with the rest of them.”\n\nI let the weight of my words settle over the room. This wasn’t just about survival anymore, it was war. A war we couldn’t win by playing nice.\n\nAs the meeting wrapped up, the tension didn’t fade. My team filed out, their faces etched with determination and dread. I lingered, staring at the smoldering images of the Pluto outpost on the screen. How had Todd gotten this far? What was he using to bypass our systems, to infiltrate where no one else had ever succeeded? It felt like a game we didn’t know the rules to, a game rigged from the start.\n\nWhen I finally left the war-room, the air in the corridor felt heavier, almost alive with tension. My thoughts raced, a chaotic storm of strategies and fears. The shadow of Todd’s rebellion loomed large, monstrous, and incomprehensible. How do you stop a man who believes he’s a prophet, who thinks every death he causes is a sacrificial blood offering to his dark God?\n\nI clenched my fists, my resolve hardening. The answer was simple. You don’t stop him. You obliterate him. Completely.\n\n**********\n\nAs I walked through the dim hallway, my thoughts were a tangled mess of half-baked strategies and sheer panic. Each idea unraveled the moment I tried to hold onto it. My chest felt tight, like I was suffocating under the weight of uncertainty. How the hell do you save people who are dead set on destroying you? How do you fight something that’s already rotted its way into every corner of your existence?\n\nOut of the shadows, my wife nearly collided with me, her face pale and lined with worry. “Oh my God,” she said, gripping my arm like it was the last steady thing in the world. “What’s going on? Are we safe?”\n\nI rubbed my face, trying to scrape together some composure, even as her question clawed at me. “Safe?” I repeated bitterly, letting out a short laugh. “That’s a joke at this point. Safety is a PR slogan, not a reality.”\n\nHer eyes narrowed. “Kuso,” she muttered under her breath. “Don’t pull that cynical bullshit on me right now. Just tell me, are we okay, or do I need to start packing emergency bags?”\n\nI sighed, pulling her hand into mine to ground myself. “We’re not okay,” I admitted. “But we’re not powerless either. We’re rallying everyone we’ve got—the best minds, the best tech. We’re not going down without a fight.”\n\nShe crossed her arms, her expression skeptical. “Hontou?” she asked, the edge in her voice unmistakable. “You’re not just giving me the corporate spiel, right? Because I just checked, your ‘best minds’ still let that lunatic Todd bomb Pluto to hell and back.”\n\nMy jaw tightened, but I didn’t argue. She wasn’t wrong. “I know,” I said finally, my voice lower. “But we’re working on it. We’ve got to move fast, calm the colonies down, shore up defenses, maybe even figure out how the hell he’s breaking through. I’m going to announce a fireside chat. We’ll address this head-on. We need to get everyone on a war footing.”\n\nShe exhaled sharply, muttering, “That’s all well and good, but what about everyone? People are freaking out. And not just about Todd, about us. They’re starting to think we can’t handle this.”\n\nHer words hit me like a gut punch. “I know,” I said. “That’s why the communication is critical. We have to show everyone we’re still in control. We’ll ramp up security, send elite robotic forces to every colony. We’ll make sure they know we’ve got their backs.”\n\nShe gave me a hard look, the kind that cut through my bullshit like a scalpel. “And Todd? What’s the plan there? Smile and wave while he keeps blowing shit up? You gonna send him a fruit basket with a strongly worded note?”\n\n“Damn it,” I snapped, running a hand through my hair. “You think I don’t know how fucked up this is? I’m trying here. But Todd and his cult aren’t just terrorists, they’re brainwashed motherfucking lunatics. Reason doesn’t work on people like that. The only way to reason with them is after they’re 6 feet under.”\n\n“Then what does?” she asked sharply. “Because if you think you can keep playing defense, you’re going to lose. Fast.”\n\nI hesitated, the truth burning in my chest. “Its fucking order 66 all over again,” I said quietly, “We have to eliminate them. Completely. It’s an infection, a cancer we need to excise.”\n\nHer eyes widened slightly, but she didn’t flinch. Instead, she sighed, her shoulders sagging. “So that’s where we’re at,” she said softly. “Mass extermination. What a fucking world.”\n\n“We don’t have a choice,” I said. “They won’t stop. They’re not just trying to win, they’re trying to wipe us out. And I’ll be damned if I let them take everything we’ve built. They’re on a holy crusade to unleash genocide on us. If we don’t obliterate them, they’ll more than likely return the favor. We can try to bottle them up or suppress them. All that’s going to do is let them simmer, figure out better ways to hit back at us. No. They won’t see reason, so they can’t co-exist in the same reality as we do. I’m scared. I thought we had these ironclad security systems, things to keep people safe—that death, violence were a thing of the past. But this, this was completely unexpected.”\n\nShe stared at me for a long moment, her expression unreadable. Finally, she stepped closer, squeezing my hand tightly. “Alright,” she said. “Just... don’t lose yourself in this, okay? We’ve been through enough shit already.”\n\nI nodded, though her words hung heavy in the air. “Together,” I said, forcing conviction into my voice.\n\n“Yeah,” she said, her voice quieter now. “Together. But damn it, you better come back to me in one piece.”\n\nI kissed my wife goodbye, her grip lingering on my arm as if letting go would unravel the fragile thread keeping us tethered to hope.\n\n**********\n\nThe weight of the empire pressed on my cheek, each breath a struggle against the crushing knowledge of what lay beyond our perception. Cosmic horrors, truths too vast for comprehension, pushing my sanity to its limits. But I couldn’t falter. If I fell to the madness, so would everyone who depended on me.\n\nBy the time I reached the briefing room, the team was already gathered, their faces pale, their eyes flitting nervously to every shadow like they expected the void to reach out and snatch them. “Alright,” I said, my voice sharper than I intended, cutting through the oppressive silence. “We have one shot at this. We need a message that will reassure the solar system, rally our people, and send a clear signal to the Red Hats: we’re not broken. We’re not scared. And we’re sure as hell not going anywhere.”\n\nBecca, leaned forward, her hands shaking slightly as she scribbled notes. “We can’t sugarcoat this,” she said. “Most colonies have already seen the footage of Pluto. They know we’ve been hit. If we lie, we lose them.”\n\n“We’re not lying,” I said, the edge in my voice softening. “We’re framing it. Acknowledge the attack, yes. But make it clear that this is not the end. This is the moment we rise.”\n\nAndrew, frowned. “And if they ask what’s really going on? About Todd, about... whatever’s out there?”\n\n“We tell them he’s a mother fucking homicidal maniac hell bent on killing everyone and everything,” I said firmly. “They don’t need to know about the shadows lurking at the edges of reality. What they need is to believe that we have the strength to face the unknown and win.”\n\nFor hours, we wrestled with the words, stripping away the excess, sharpening every phrase until it was a weapon of unity. When the script was done, I felt the weight of its promise settle on my shoulders. This was it. This was what would keep us together, or tear us apart.\n\nWhen I stepped in front of the camera, the room fell silent. The lens stared at me, unblinking, and I stared back, forcing myself to inhale slowly, steadying the hammering of my heart. The broadcast began, and my voice cut through the static with a calm I didn’t understand.\n\n“My fellow citizens,” I began, my tone firm, measured. “Today, we faced an attack unlike any in human history. Our facility on Pluto, several outposts, and our colony Sorhhinda, a symbol of human ingenuity and resilience, have been struck. Millions of lives were lost or missing, and wounds have been dealt to our community. But let me be clear, this is not the end. This is the beginning of our fight.”\n\nI straightened, letting the weight of my conviction fill the room. “S Corp is not just a company. It is a family. And like any family, we stand together in times of crisis. For almost half a decade, we have built a future of progress and peace. We have stopped wars, fed the hungry, and brought light to the darkest corners of the interstellar space. And we will not let fear or fanaticism unravel what we have achieved.”\n\nMy voice grew stronger, rising like a rallying cry. “The Red Hats think they can divide us with terror. They are wrong. We will not bow to threats. We will not cower in the shadows. We will rise, stronger, united, unyielding. Together, we will show them that humanity’s light burns brighter than any darkness they bring.”\n\nI leaned forward, letting my words land like hammer blows. “This is not a time for despair. This is a time for action. To every citizen in our colonies, to every soul watching this broadcast: Stand firm. Stand proud. Because the future is ours, and we will claim it