ly turning their back on these fundamental aspects of themselves. Impossible. Not at the rate and spread that we saw.\n\nBecca and Andrew worked feverishly to draft an emergency decree; their pale faces bathed in the sickly glow of their screens. The decree banned the displaying Red Hat symbols, warning of the chaos they incited. It was a desperate measure to stem the tide, but desperation breeds fury.\n\nWhen the decree went live, the backlash was immediate. Protests erupted across the colonies, masses thrumming with rage. Fires burned unchecked, their smoke curling into shapes that seemed almost serpentine. Shadows writhed at the edges of the crowds, too fluid, too deliberate, things that should not exist. The chants of the rebels twisted and deepened, a guttural chorus that scraped at the edges of sanity.\n\nI watched the chaos unfold on the monitors, my breath caught in my throat. The colonies weren’t just angry; they were infected. Whispers from across the galaxy spoke of strange phenomena: voices emanating from darkened corners, entire rooms consumed by shadow, and figures flickering in and out of existence.\n\n“This isn’t just Todd,” I said, the words barely audible over the cacophony of destruction. “It’s something darker. Something ancient.”\n\nBecca turned to me, her voice trembling. “What do you mean?”\n\nI gestured to the screens. “Look at them. This isn’t rebellion, it’s corruption. Shadows don’t move like that. People don’t sound like that. Whatever this is, it’s feeding on their hate, twisting it into something monstrous.”\n\nHer silence was confirmation enough. The horror in her eyes mirrored my own.\n\nThe streets below churned with chaos. Fires lit grotesque silhouettes in the smoke, twisted figures that defied explanation. Walls scrawled with sigils radiated an oppressive aura, as though they were conduits for something vast and unholy. I blinked hard, trying to shake the creeping dread that whispered to me from the darkness.	80	ai_extracted	2026-