cabre display of dominance. The sight froze us in place, a visceral assault on our sanity. The Red Hats’ twisted ingenuity was a weapon we could barely comprehend. Anti-machine, they turned to the only technology they knew—complete organic bio-synthesis. Hybrid horrors of torture, pain, and flesh monstrosities.\n\nThe images we saw tricking in created a fear and revulsion never before known to mankind, we had no choice but to act. Each attempt to dismantle these abominations of nature, their barricades only seemed to strengthen their grip, their control tightening like a predator’s jaws. We searched frantically for a way out of this nightmare, the weight of responsibility crashing down. We were the last line of defense against a force beyond reason, a cosmic horror that threatened to consume us all.\n\nEven in that darkness, a flicker of hope endured. We knew that as long as light existed, shadow could be fought. Against the unrelenting tide of despair, we resolved to fight, to the bitter end, if necessary, not just for survival, but for justice, for humanity, and for the chance to reclaim what was slipping through our fingers.\n\nI called an emergency meeting with my top advisors to address the crisis at the gateways. “We need a solution that respects human life but also reasserts control over the hijacked ERBs,” I began, scanning the room. “Ideas?”\n\nPatrick, ever pragmatic, spoke first. “Deploy specialized robots to dismantle the blockades. They can molecularly separate the protestors without causing too much harm or suffering.”\n\nMorgan countered, “What about sedative gas? Something mild to incapacitate them temporarily, allowing us to clear the area safely.”\n\nJames frowned. “Gas sounds good in theory, but the risks are too high. If it backfires, we’re responsible for unintended casualties, and we can’t afford that.”\n\nI shook my head. “We can’t underestimate the Red Hats. No mater what we do to try to solve this, they’ll adapt, evolve, and hit us harder. Their goal is our complete destruction, and we’re fighting for more than just survival, we’re fighting for the future of humanity itself. We need to outmaneuver them without stooping to their level. You think they give a shit about their captives? They’d just as likely torture them as kill them. Anything we do is going to be fucked. But doing nothing is not an option.”\n\nPatrick’s voice was steady but edged with urgency. “So what the fuck do we do then?”\n\nMorgan leaned forward, her eyes sharp. “Psychological warfare. Deploy AI algorithms to create the illusion that their numbers are shrinking. Get our folks to do some submliminal messaging. Build out a brainwashing device or something. Confuse their ranks, sow doubt among their commanders. It could force them into disarray.”\n\nJames considered it. “With you and what army? Not like we have a whole lot of time. These fuckers are right on our doorstep, so first thing we really need to do is try and prevent this from spreading. If any of this fails, we’ll fuel mistrust and hostility, which could make things worse.”\n\nI nodded, acknowledging the risk. “Without wholesale killing everyone just for the fuck of it, information is our best weapon. If we don’t out-think them, we’ll lose. Let’s refine the AI plan, control the narrative, but carefully.”\n\nPatrick suggested harnessing public opinion. “We need to expose their vision for what it is, a world without progress or innovation. Becoming slaves to the flesh. Rally society by showing what’s at stake if they succeed. Fuck, just start broadcasting images from the places the Red Hats have taken over.”\n\nMorgan added, “You think these brain fucks care if they’re going to be tortured and raped when the Red Hats win? It’s like they’re welcoming bringing this shit on themselves. On the other hand, not all of the Red Hats are beyond redemption. Many joined out of fear or desperation. We maybe able to de-convert them. Especially if they realize what hell they’ll be in for once things progress.”\n\nGil leaned in. “Agreed, but we can’t ignore strengthening our defenses. Cybersecurity, network resilience, and intelligence gathering must remain priorities.”\n\nPatrick crossed his arms. “And espionage. If we can assassinate influential figures within their ranks, we might find openings to disrupt their command structure. Or at least fuck enough shit enough that the chaos prevents them from moving for a while. Give us some time to regroup and recover.”\n\nI nodded, weighing the options. “A multi-pronged approach it is. Psychological tactics to destabilize their command, a public campaign to shift sentiment, and fortified defenses to counter their escalation. But most importantly, we need to show the universe, —and the Red Hats, that unity, innovation, and hope are stronger than their organic chaos.”\n\nOur final solution was born of desperation. The plan relied on twisted robotic technology, outfitted with inhuman tools designed to tear through the living chains that held us captive. To subdue resistance, we deployed a potent sedative, numbing their minds into compliance. One side effect was that it was highly flammable, so there were bound to be a few little booms along the way.\n\nWhen the plan was set into motion, chaos erupted. The air filled with cries of agony, the metallic clash of the machines reverberating in a deafening symphony of horror. The scene was brutal, almost surreal, as the Red Hats’ grotesque barricades fought against dismantling with a fervor that defied comprehension. The fuckers held onto those portals until the last link fell. What should have been an easy battled stretched into weeks sometimes as they fought to the very last person. One by painful one, though we began to reclaim the gateways, though the sheer cost to our humanity was palpable.\n\nVictory brought no solace. It only underscored a grim truth: the Red Hats were far more than insurgents. Their twisted minds, fueled by unearthly abilities and an insatiable thirst for destruction, were beyond logic. Their ideology had become something monstrous, a force that didn’t just challenge us but tore at the fabric of reason itself.\n\n**********\n\nOur limited success at reclaiming colonies only served to intensify the Red Hat activities. Despite our security measures and network lockdowns, Todd was able to create and broadcast a podcast that became a monstrous force, its apocalyptic rhetoric infecting minds and fueling chaos. His broadcasts summoned a growing following, weaving rebellion and madness into society.\n\nFor S Corp, it posed a chilling dilemma: our commitment to free speech now clashed with the horrifying consequences of his words. Todd’s influence teetered on the edge of unleashing societal insanity, forcing us into a precarious balancing act, defending principles while protecting our citizens from descending into madness.\n\nWe deployed advanced network controls to completely eliminate Todd’s broadcasts across any and all S Corp systems, mirroring efforts used during critical crises. Even with all these fail-safes in place our efforts, Todd’s followers, possessed by a near-otherworldly determination, circumvented our barriers with terrifying ease. Todd’s words slithered through alternative channels like demonic whispers, spreading his influence as if powered by forces beyond human comprehension. Each evasion underscored the cosmic horror of his movement—an ideology bending reality and preying on the discontent buried within our fractured society.\n\nEvery passing day tightened the noose. Todd’s blasphemous broadcasts threatened not just rebellion but a spiraling apocalypse that would wipe humanity off the face of this reality. It became a race against time as we scrambled to counter his message while holding on to our own sanity. Even listening to the podcast by our hardened soldiers garnered him new recruits. Nothing and no where was safe. His podcast, and the movement it galvanized, came to embody something larger: a war of ideas, fueled by the raw power of belief. The Red Hats’ resilience revealed a festering darkness within humanity, forcing us to confront the unspeakable horrors lurking in ourselves.\n\n**********\n\nS Corp’s communication systems, reliant on the near-instantaneous precision of ERB technology, had always been a point of pride. Wi-Fi-based transmissions rendered outdated Earth-based radio and telecom systems unnecessary, or so we thought. Yet, as I stared into the digital abyss, tracking Red Hat movements like a grim chessboard of chaos, I couldn’t shake the sense that we’d overlooked something vital.\n\nThe hiss of the door broke my focus. Morgan stepped inside, her face shadowed with worry. “We’ve got a situation,” she said, her voice tight. Behind her walked Professor Nakamura, his posture stiff, his expression grim. He carried himself like a man accustomed to catastrophe.\n\n“Professor Nakamura,” I said, standing to greet him. “You don’t usually visit the war room. What’s happened?”\n\n“It’s about two of my missing students Aditi and Hiro,” he said, his tone grave. “We intercepted a weak distress signal. She’s in the Kuiper Belt, just beyond Neptune. Her ship’s systems are failing—life support is critical.”\n\nMorgan crossed her arms, her face a mask of concern. “She’s been missing for months. How did she survive this long? And why are we only hearing about this now?”\n\nNakamura exhaled sharply. “The Red Hat chaos made monitoring near-impossible. But this signal is unmistakable—it’s her.”\n\nI turned toward the display, my mind racing. “And we didn’t catch this because our network doesn’t monitor standard radio broadcasts,” I muttered bitterly. “We built the ERB system to connect everything instantly, and in doing so, we made ourselves blind to the simplest signals.”\n\nMorgan nodded grimly. “We’ve been so focused on efficiency, we’ve ignored the basics. Traditional radio could have flagged this weeks ago.”\n\nThe professor spoke up, his voice measured but urgent. “The signal’s fading. She’s running out of time. If we don’t act now...”\n\n“We’ll act,” I interrupted, the decision already made. “Morgan, assemble a rescue team. Use the ERBs to get them as close to her coordinates as possible.”\n\nShe nodded. “I’ll have a ship ready within the hour. We’ll outfit it with everything needed for deep-space recovery.”\n\nNakamura stepped forward. “Their courage shouldn’t go unnoticed. They risked everything infiltrating that ship, and now they’ve paid the price. We owe them a chance.”\n\n“We’ll get them back,” I said, meeting his gaze. “But this is more than a rescue. Aditi’s signal has exposed a critical weakness in our infrastructure. We’re too reliant on centralized systems. Once she’s safe, we’re overhauling our communications, traditional radio, backups, redundancies. We can’t afford blind spots.”\n\nMorgan turned to leave but paused at the door. “You realize this is just the beginning, right? If the Red Hats realize we’re making a move, they’ll react.”\n\n“They’ll react anyway,” I said. “We’ve already declared war by refusing to let them win. But this mission isn’t just tactical, it’s personal. Aditi and Hiro represent what we stand for: resilience, ingenuity, and hope. We won’t abandon them.”\n\nA few minutes later Morgan burst back into the war room, trailed by a group of technicians and engineers. Their faces were drawn with intensity, sweat glistening on their brows.\n\n“Listen up,” Morgan said, her voice cutting through the hum of the displays. “We’ve got a distress signal from Aditi’s ship in the Kuiper Belt. The priority is clear: locate her and bring her back safely. We’ll be using the ERB gateways to transport you as close to her coordinates as possible.”\n\nThe team nodded in unison, their determination palpable. One of the engineers stepped forward. “We’ve modified a ship for the mission,” he said, his voice steady. “It’s equipped for extended deep-space travel—emergency oxygen, advanced navigation systems, medical supplies, and enough rations to last us weeks if necessary.”\n\n“Good work,” I said, nodding. “Time is critical, and every second counts. Professor Nakamura has provided the exact coordinates, use them to triangulate your approach.”\n\nI turned to Nakamura, the gratitude in my voice unfeigned. “Thank you, Professor.”\n\nThe team responded with solemn nods, their resolve clear as they filed out to prepare for the mission. Their movements were deliberate, their purpose unwavering. Watching them leave, a knot of worry twisted in my chest. Aditi’s survival, the mission’s success, it all rested on their shoulders now.\n\nI turned to Patrick, “We can’t afford any mistakes. The Red Hats have proven themselves to be unpredictable, and if they catch wind of this mission...”\n\n“We’ll be ready,” he interrupted, his tone resolute. “I’ll ensure every contingency is covered. You have my word.”\n\nI appreciated his steadfastness and placed a hand on his shoulder. “The Red Hats thrive on chaos, Patrick. Stay vigilant. One misstep, and we risk losing more than just Aditi.”\n\n**********\n\nAs the days dragged on, the tension became suffocating. Each incoming message was dissected for progress, every signal a source of hope or dread. My heart raced with every update, praying for news of Aditi’s survival. The weight of waiting pressed down on all of us, the uncertainty gnawing at the edges of our resolve.\n\nStill, the flicker of hope remained. Aditi’s courage had brought her this far. Now, it was up to us to bring her the rest of the way home.\n\nEvery hour we didn’t hear anything was like an eternity. Finally the message we had been waiting for finally arrived. The comms crackled to life, Morgan’s voice breaking through with a mix of relief and urgency. “We’ve found them. Aditi and Hiro are with us.”\n\nA surge of joy coursed through me, but it was short-lived. The strain in Morgan’s tone told me this was far from over. “What happened, Morgan?” I asked, leaning closer to the console. “How are they?”\n\nThere was a hesitation, a beat of static, before she answered. “We’ve encountered hostile forces,” she said, her voice tight. “The Red Hats launched an attack on our ship. We held them off, but not without casualties.”\n\nMy chest tightened. The thought of my team under fire, of Aditi in danger, was overwhelming. “And Aditi?” I pressed, my voice edged with fear. “Is she...?”\n\n“She’s alive,” Morgan interrupted, though her tone carried a weight that hit me like a blow. “Unfortunately, Hiro didn’t make it, and she’s gravely injured. She took a direct hit during the attack. The crew managed to stabilize her, but barely. The ship’s taken heavy damage. We’re holding it together, but it’s fragile.”\n\nA wave of dread swept over me as I gripped the console, my knuckles whitening. I took a deep breath, forcing calm into my voice. “What’s your plan? How soon can you reach Elysium?”\n\n“We’re en route now,” Morgan replied. “Her condition is critical. We’re doing everything we can, but she needs advanced care. We have to get her to an AutoDoc as fast as possible.”\n\nI nodded, though she couldn’t see it. “I’ll have Patrick coordinate security for your arrival. We’ll clear the approach and ensure the med-bay is ready for immediate treatment.”\n\n“Understood,” Morgan said, her voice steady despite the tension. “We’ll make it. We have to.”\n\nWhen the comm line went quiet, I turned to Patrick, who stood at my side, his expression as grave as my own. “Patrick, we need to rally every resource we have. I want the landing zone locked down and secured. Mobilize medical teams, and prepare for worst-case scenarios.”\n\n“You’ll have it,” Patrick said, his voice firm. “The Red Hats won’t get within a light-year of her. We’ll ensure she gets home safely.”\n\nI placed a hand on his shoulder, the gesture brief but filled with meaning. “This isn’t just about Aditi,” I said quietly. “It’s about showing everyone, our colonies, the Red Hats, and ourselves, that we protect our own. No matter the cost.”\n\nPatrick nodded, his resolve mirroring my own. “We’ll make it happen.”\n\nAs soon as Aditi was well enough for a debrief, she recounted her ordeal with a calmness that belied the harrowing experience she’d endured. Stranded in the vast void of space, her hope dwindling, she had tuned into radio frequencies in desperation. What she found changed everything.\n\n“They’re using shortwave radio,” Aditi explained, her voice steady. “It’s old-school, over a century old. We overlooked it in favor of more advanced systems. They’ve exploited that blind spot to bypass our network controls entirely.”\n\nPatrick raised an eyebrow, leaning forward in his chair. “Shortwave? They’ve weaponized something that basic?”\n\nAditi nodded. “The simplicity is the genius. It’s untraceable by our systems. All our security measures are aimed at digital threats. We never accounted for something so analog.”\n\n“Aditi,” I asked, my voice calm but intent, “how did you manage to decode their transmissions?”\n\nA faint frown tugged at her lips as she glanced at the makeshift radio equipment on the table beside her. “It was mostly Hiro. It took trial and error.”\n\nPatrick’s gaze sharpened. “And what did you find?”\n\nHer smile faded, replaced by a somber expression. “Their broadcasts detailed plans to sabotage our main communication networks and disrupt supply routes to the colonies. It’s all about creating chaos, breaking us apart, weakening our unity. They’re organized, ruthless, and utterly committed. But the biggest thing was the sound patterns and their damn hats. Apparently there is something in the broadcasts that takes over listeners personalities completely. If you hear it, most people get corrupted by it. Both Hiro and I have had too many ear infections as kids so it muffled whatever frequency they were sending so we weren’t affected. We didn’t see this much because with the AutoDocs most people got these issues remedied.”\n\nI gritted my teeth. The pieces clicked into place. “What about the hats?”\n\n“That’s where it gets weird. Apparently, they’re just normal hats with a little extra aluminum foil stuck inside of them as a throwback to old conspiracy theories. The problem is our ERBs. The hats amplify the resonance the ERBs are putting out. Combine with some shortwave broadcasts in the area, and suddenly wearers become beacons that transmit the sound patterns wherever they go. They become walking infection vectors. It’s why they’ve been so violently guarding the portals with human sacrifices.”\n\nPatrick leaned back, his arms crossed. “This changes everything. We need to reexamine every assumption we’ve made about their tactics. If they can exploit something this simple, what else are we missing?”	81	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 12:10:35.467287-05	2026-03-05 19:39:33.692739-05	\N	16	\N	\N
82	1	82	76: Expanding Outwards While Falling Inwards	“We did it. We actually fucking did it.” A triumphant sigh escaped my lips as the final flicker of the Red Hat signal disappeared from the main screen.\n\nWith the information provided by Aditi, we had a new way to stop the spread and start fighting back against this infectious scourge. Unfortunately, it wasn’t as simple as pulling a plug. By the time we had begun our counter-offensive, places were so entrenched with the Red Hat virus that it felt, for the longest time, that we weren’t getting anywhere.\n\nFor every action we took to stop the spread, they found ten other ways to circumvent our controls. When we outlawed red hats, they turned to more barbaric options—like using razor wire as crowns of thorns, or driving nails through the bones of their followers. When we attacked the shortwave broadcast centers or jammed their frequencies with other electro-magnetic bursts, they turned to training their ardents in Mongolian throat singing or hijacking our cloning facilities with a small pox variant to induce genetic changes.\n\nFive years of intensive battles later, on the eve of S Corp’s first decade anniversary, suddenly it was all over. Not with a bang, not some celebratory GAME OVER. All Red Hat activity simply disappeared.\n\nI stood amid the calm, a gnawing unease burrowed into my thoughts. This victory, clean, surgical, almost routine, felt wrong. Like I had found a Maguffin and was protected by some insane plot armor crafted by an unhinged writer in some parallel dimension. The ending was just too tidy.\n\nThe Red Hats had been a formidable force, a chaotic storm we had barely managed to weather. After five years of battle, this sudden collapse didn’t make sense. Where was Todd, the charismatic madman who had orchestrated so much destruction? What force could snuff out such an unrelenting movement so completely?\n\nThe questions itched at my mind like maggots on the bloated corpses left in the wake of Red Hat terror. I’d seen their handiwork—their barricades of fused flesh, their cruel ingenuity. Their atrocities weren’t the actions of a superficial threat. They had been meticulous, strategic, and utterly uncompromising. Now, they were just... gone? It defied logic.\n\nThe command center buzzed softly with activity. Operators monitored network integrity, their muted conversations blending with the hum of machinery. The atmosphere was serene, yet the calm felt fragile, as if reality itself had exhaled too deeply and might snap back at any moment.\n\nI stared at the central display, watching the last vestiges of Red Hat influence fade like dying embers. A knot tightened in my chest. What if this isn’t over? The thought slithered through my mind like a shadow in the periphery of my vision.\n\nMorgan approached, her face flushed with satisfaction. “We did it,” she said, gesturing to the darkened map that had once pulsed with hostile signals. “The colonies are stable, communications are secure, and the network is fortified. We can finally breathe.”\n\nI forced a smile, but the unease wouldn’t loosen its grip. “Can we?” I asked; my voice was quieter than I intended. “Todd is still unaccounted for. A force like his doesn’t vanish without a trace. It doesn’t just go away.”\n\nMorgan’s expression faltered. “You think there’s more to this?”\n\n“I know there is,” I replied, my tone sharper now. “This collapse was too clean, too convenient. The Red Hats were ruthless, methodical. Every fucking thing we threw at them for five years bounced back. Something this calculated doesn’t disintegrate overnight without an external push—or a hand pulling strings.”\n\nMorgan frowned, glancing at the display. “Then what do you think happened?”\n\nI exhaled, my mind flashing back to the Pointer appearances from before—their cryptic messages, their cryptic certainty. The air seemed colder now, the faint whir of the machines suddenly distant. “I don’t know,” I admitted. “But whatever it is, it’s not over. This isn’t a victory. It’s a pause. A prelude.”\n\nHer gaze was steady, her voice softer. “What do you suggest?”\n\n“We keep digging,” I said firmly. “Track every signal, every remnant of their presence. And we prepare. Whatever’s coming next won’t give us this kind of clean break. Not again.”\n\nMorgan nodded, the light of triumph in her eyes dimming to a flicker of cautious resolve. As she walked away, I turned back to the screen. The silence of the Red Hats echoed louder than their chaos ever had. Somewhere, in the vast expanse of possibility, the real threat was coiling, waiting. I could sense it—an invisible presence gnawing at the edges of our hard-fought peace.\n\nThe air, thick with unspoken questions, hung heavy as I stared into the flickering light of the monitor. Relief had arrived, but dread poisoned it. The universe didn’t give victories this easily, not without a price.\n\nAnd something, somewhere, was already preparing to collect.\n\n**********\n\nIt didn’t take long for humanity to recover. Within months, after years of being on a war footing, people just started living again. Mundane things, like going out to dinner, or even just doing the dishes didn’t seem so strange.\n\nIn this newly restored atmosphere of peace and reason, we got back to the really fun shit. Exploring space and expanding the S Corp empire. We launched new missions to unexplored regions of space, venturing into the unknown with eager anticipation. We established additional colonies on distant planets and moons, in locations as far as we could reach, remote galaxies and even further galactic clusters, pushing the boundaries of human existence further than ever before. Our existing outposts flourished under our diligent care, evolving into thriving hubs of innovation and discovery.\n\nAt the same time we were expanding outward, our scientific teams made groundbreaking discoveries across a multitude of fields. From the intricate realm of astrophysics to the innovative arena of bioengineering, we delved into the depths of knowledge, unearthing hidden gems that propelled humanity into uncharted territories. Even the power of the Eternal Engine and Infinitron would seem like stone age tools compared to the reality altering breakthroughs we made.\n\n“We did it!” Morgan burst into the room, her voice bright with exhilaration, TJ close on her heels.\n\nTheir excitement was infectious. Morgan’s eyes sparkled with triumph, her face flushed as if she had just run a marathon of discovery. Even TJ, usually composed and deliberate, wore an uncharacteristic grin, his childlike enthusiasm shining through.\n\n“We’ve established an outpost,” Morgan announced, her voice trembling with awe. “Thirteen-point-eight billion light-years away. At the edge of the visible universe. We’re calling it ‘The Restaurant at the Edge of the Universe.’”\n\nTJ activated a holographic projection with a wave of his hand. The room dimmed, and before us, a shimmering model of the outpost floated in the air. Its sleek, futuristic architecture seemed to pulse with energy, a beacon of humanity’s determination and ingenuity. The design was breathtaking, a harmonious blend of advanced technology and organic elegance, as if it were part of the cosmic tapestry itself.\n\nTJ’s voice was low, almost reverent. “It’s extraordinary. To think we’ve reached this far, witnessing the birth and death of stars along the way. It’s... humbling.”\n\nMorgan pointed to another holographic display, her voice soft but brimming with wonder. “And there,” she said, gesturing toward a faint, almost imperceptible glow beyond the visible galaxies, “lies another 13.8 billion light-years of uncharted territory. A vast ocean of possibilities, waiting for us to explore.”\n\nAfter years of effort, and millions of stepping stone outposts along the way, we had turned the impossible into reality, pushing humanity beyond the limits of our perception. The Exo-Planetary Expedition Force had carved a path to the edge of existence, where dreams and discovery intertwined.\n\nBuoyed by this seismic discovery, we launched an audacious plan: a network of outposts—restaurants, as explorers affectionately called them—positioned precisely 13.8 billion light-years apart. These waypoints, connected by advanced ERB technology, would serve as steppingstones into the uncharted depths of the universe. The logistical hurdles were monumental, yet the allure of boundless discovery made hesitation impossible. Scientists, engineers, and explorers united to breathe life into this cosmic trail.\n\nWith every new outpost, our understanding expanded. The universe unfolded like a celestial tapestry, each thread revealing mysteries that both awed and humbled us. But as our reach grew, so did the challenges. Communication and navigation across such immense distances demanded constant innovation. Teams at every station toiled to refine the ERB network, ensuring its stability and safeguarding our tenuous connection to the void.\n\n**********\n\nDecades later and a trillion miles from home, I found myself in the heart of a bustling research facility, its corridors alive with the hum of innovation. When I entered the science team’s command room, Morgan greeted me with an infectious excitement. Screens covered the walls, displaying star maps and equations that danced like celestial choreography.\n\n“We’ve made astonishing discoveries,” Morgan said, gesturing toward a holographic display. Stars and galaxies stretched into infinity, a patchwork quilt of light and mystery. “We’ve been monitoring cosmic background radiation,” she explained, her voice charged with energy. “Here we are, trillions of light-years away, it’s still constant. It’s a connection to the very birth of the universe.”\n\nI stared at the projection, the weight of her words settling in. The radiation—a remnant of creation itself—was an eternal thread linking us to the Big Bang, vibrating across unimaginable distances.\n\nThe vastness of infinity was suffocating.\n\n“So…” I started, choosing my words carefully. “Where the fuck is everyone?” My voice was too loud, too small at the same time. “Where is Tattonie, the Ewoks… fuck, I’d even settle for a Dalek at this juncture.”\n\nMorgan didn’t answer right away. She was scanning the darkness ahead, her jaw tight, fingers twitching at her sides. I knew that look. The one she got when reality wasn’t lining up with what it was supposed to be. When logic—beautiful, stable, predictable logic—started to slip.\n\nI turned in a slow circle, scanning the star-flecked void stretching infinitely in every direction. The absence of intelligence was almost tactile, pressing down on us like the weight of something enormous lurking just beyond perception.\n\nWhen I led S Corp beyond the cradle of Earth, when we took our first trembling steps into interstellar space, we were afraid. Not of the void. Not of the radiation, or the distances so vast they defied imagination. No, we were afraid of what might be waiting for us out there.\n\nOn Earth, we had spent our entire lives looking up at the stars, searching for signs of life—radio signals, megastructures, anything that would confirm we weren’t alone. Instead, we found nothing but silence. The great contradiction, the Fermi Paradox, gnawed at us, haunted every new discovery – there should be life, it should be intelligent, but something is wrong.\n\nThat something we called The Dark Forest Hypothesis.\n\nThe idea that the reason we saw no signs of intelligent life wasn’t because it wasn’t out there… but because it was hiding. Because in the vast, infinite expanse of the cosmos, every civilization was locked in an eternal, paranoid struggle. A cosmic game of Highlander—where the only rule was that there could be only one.\n\nIn such a universe, broadcasting our existence wasn’t just reckless. It was suicidal.\n\nBecause if every intelligent species instinctively viewed all others as a potential threat, then the only way to survive was to remain unseen. To strike first. To annihilate before being annihilated.\n\nIf the Dark Forest was real, then every world we looked upon with wonder might already be a tomb. Civilizations that rose and fell without a trace, erased the moment they dared to whisper into the dark. It was a chilling thought: that the universe wasn’t empty, but rather full of corpses.\n\nIt shaped everything we did when we began our interstellar expansion.\n\nWe set up the Exo-Solar Exploration Center on Pluto because in the blackest reaches of the void, far from the beacon of our sun, we could remain hidden from prying eyes. We used the ERB portals to send transmissions so nothing would escape. We buried our colonies under layers of silence, and never—never—sent out a broadcast larger than a whisper.\n\nWe operated on the assumption that the moment we revealed ourselves, something would notice. And that something would not be friendly.\n\nWe imagined death hiding in the shadows of every distant star. A long-dormant machine intelligence, waiting for the scent of new life. A predatory empire, honed over eons, sniffing the air for civilizations foolish enough to announce their existence. The remnants of an ancient war, automated weapons systems still following protocols long after their creators had turned to dust.\n\nOur paranoia may have helped save us against Todd, but it didn’t prepare us for what came next.\n\nThe more we looked, the more our initial fears of this being a dark forest vanished.\n\nIt was replaced by something even worse.\n\nBecause we weren’t finding hidden predators. We weren’t seeing traces of civilizations torn apart by war. We weren’t even finding the ruins of long-dead empires.\n\n“There’s nothing,” Morgan finally whispered, and it was that whisper that unsettled me more than anything. I had never heard Morgan whisper. Not in the face of disaster, not in the aftermath of catastrophe. But now, as we stood on the precipice of absolute solitude, her voice had diminished to something fragile.\n\n“That’s not all,” she continued, forcing herself to sound clinical, detached. “The deeper we explore, the more diverse the stars become. No repetitions. No patterns. There’s no sign of this being a finite universe.”\n\nHer words hit me like a punch to the chest.\n\nAn infinite universe.\n\nEndless possibilities. Endless challenges.\n\nEndless emptiness.\n\nI met her gaze, and for the first time in all our years together, I saw fear there. Not the usual fear. This was something different. This was the realization that there was no enemy to fight, no disaster to avert.\n\nThere was just… nothing.\n\n“If this is true,” I said, my throat dry, “then we’re not just explorers. We’re custodians of sentience, and everything it entails.”\n\nMorgan nodded slowly, but she didn’t look comforted. “The universe is a boundless canvas,” she murmured, more to herself than to me. “And it’s our responsibility to keep the spark of intelligence alive. We must seek, but also protect.”\n\nShe was trying to be rational. Trying to hold onto the mission, the purpose. I understood\n\nNo matter how many trillions upon trillions of light years we spread out, the deeper we explored, the more the silence was a constant companion.\n\nSure, planets teemed with life—astonishing, breathtaking ecosystems that thrived in ways we had never imagined. Bioelectric leviathans, continent-spanning fungi, oceans thick with bioluminescent serpents.\n\nHell, we even found life living in empty fucking space (fuck those space worms).\n\nYet, none of it was sentient. No ruins, no artifacts, no whispers of civilizations past or present. Just a vast, thriving void of unthinking organisms. Rudimentary survival instincts, maybe even complex organizational structures that resembled thinking and communication.\n\nBut no one was out there waving back at us.\n\nWe were truly alone out here.\n\n**********\n\n“Happy 300th Birthday Grandpa!” everyone shouted at me as they rushed into the room.\n\nI turned toward the door as it flew open, revealing Stellan, my great-great-great-grandson, now a man in his prime, flanked by his wife. Behind them, their own grandchildren—my greatx4-grandchildren—scampered into the room, their laughter a bright contrast to pressures of watching S Corp expand into the infinite void.\n\n“Still staring out windows?” Stellan teased, his tone warm but laced with the confidence of a man who had stepped firmly into his role within S Corp’s hierarchy.\n\n“It’s a view worth contemplating,” I replied, rising to greet him. “Though sometimes I wonder if I’ve spent too much time looking outward and not enough looking inward.”\n\nThe children darted to the window, pressing their small hands against the glass, their eyes wide with wonder. “Is that a galaxy you created?” one of them asked, her voice tinged with awe.\n\n“It is,” I said, crouching beside her. “Each one of those lights contains billions of stars, and around many of those stars, there are planets. Some are just like Earth, filled with life. Others are gigantic puzzles to explore.”\n\nHer brother tugged at my sleeve. “And we’ve been to all of them?”\n\n“Not yet,” I said, smiling and sitting back down. “But we’re trying.”\n\nStellan chuckled, settling into one of the chairs. “You make it sound so simple. But I remember you telling me stories of when this started. Humans were struggling to even go back to the moon, let alone the edge of the universe.”\n\nI sat across from him, studying his face. He bore traces of my features, lines carved by the weight of leadership, softened by the joy of family. “Do you think they understand what it took to get here?” I asked, nodding toward the grandchildren.\n\nHe hesitated. “They’ve grown up in a world of miracles, miracles you wrested from the impossible. They can’t truly grasp what it was like before. But that’s not a bad thing, is it? They dream without limits because you gave them a world where dreams have become reality.”\n\nHis words filled me with a mix of pride and melancholy. “Dreaming without limits is dangerous,” I said softly. “We’ve accomplished so much, but the farther we reach, the greater the risks. This empire was built on ambition and resilience, but it’s fragile. A single misstep could undo everything. Remember the lessons about Todd.”\n\nHis wife, silent until now, leaned forward. “And yet, you’ve ensured that even if Todd might come back, the legacy remains. You’ve passed that strength to all of us. We won’t let it crumble.”\n\nBefore I could respond, one of the grandchildren pointed excitedly at the window. “Look! That one’s moving!”\n\nI smiled, rising to join them again. “That’s not a galaxy. It’s one of our outposts. People like you live there, scientists, explorers, families.”\n\n“Do they have windows like this?” the girl asked.\n\n“Better ones,” I replied. “The stars there look so close you’d think you could touch them.”\n\nThe conversation turned to alien worlds and discoveries, the grandchildren’s endless questions filling the room with lightness. But as they played and dreamed, my thoughts drifted back to the darker truths we were suddenly facing. Beneath S Corp’s gleaming facade lay cracks, network anomalies, inexplicable failures, whispers of a malevolent force growing within our systems.\n\n“What’s on your mind?” Stellan asked quietly, his tone shifting.\n\nI met his gaze, choosing my words carefully. “The foundations we’ve built are strong, but something’s wrong. Patterns in the network, anomalies we can’t explain, it seems... deliberate. As if something unseen is pushing back.”\n\nHis brow furrowed. “Have you spoken with the science team?”\n\n“They’re working on it. But a lot of them secretly think I’m just a cranky old man going back to my old delusions. Like the Pointers. A lot of young folks these day think Todd and the Red Hat rebellion is just a myth we tell kids to keep them in line or something,” I said, the weight of uncertainty pressing against my chest. “But the answers elude us. I’ve always believed in confronting challenges head-on, but how do you fight what you can’t see? What defies logic?”\n\nHe leaned forward, his voice firm. “You taught us to adapt, to innovate. This is no different. Whatever it is, we’ll face it. Together.”\n\nHis confidence steadied me. I looked at the grandchildren, now sprawled on the floor with holographic models of alien planets glowing between them. They laughed and played, oblivious to the demons their world hid.\n\n**********\n\nLike forgotten nightmares from centuries before, suddenly reality slipped through my fingers like grains of sand. One moment, I was staring out the window, galaxies spinning endlessly in the void; the next, I was trapped in a dream, writing a book on a computer somewhere, then back, then nowhere. Perhaps the dream was reality or was reality the dream. Stellan sat across from me; his voice steady but distant. My grandchildren’s laughter muffled like echoes in a hollow chamber, faint and warped. The edges of the room rippled as though I were peering through water, and then, abruptly, I was back, standing there, solid, watching them play. But it felt... wrong.\n\n“Grandpa,” Stellan’s voice grounded me for a moment, but it wasn’t enough. The world around us seemed too perfect, too pristine. I watched the grandchildren scamper across the floor, their holographic toys projecting planets and constellations into the air. Their innocence, their joy, it was almost cruel in its beauty.\n\nI forced myself to speak, my voice hollow. “Do you ever think... none of this is real?”\n\nHe frowned, concern etched in his features. “What are you talking about?”\n\n“This,” I said, gesturing vaguely to the room, the universe beyond the window. “This empire. This peace. It appears like a dream, perfect, but fragile. Too fragile.” My hands trembled as I clenched them into fists. “It’s as if I’m standing in someone else’s story, playing my part while something watches, writing.”\n\n“You’ve done so much,” he said, his tone firm but kind. “You built this. You gave us this future. It’s real. You’re too old to start chasing up phantoms again.”\n\n“But what if it isn’t?” I snapped, my voice rising. The room wavered, the edges of my vision flickering like static. “What if all of this is... wrong? Look, everything we’ve seen, everywhere we’ve been is—it’s the same. Unimaginative junk. Infinity is stale and repeats. Think about it. Nothing but unintelligent blobs of cells, nothing other than us. Than you. Than me. None of this feels real. Like we’re trapped somehow. It’s like were just in some procedurally generated space game like No Mans Sky. We always win, we never loose. I’m surrounded by some invisible plot armor that makes everything feel stale.”\n\nHis concern deepened, but he didn’t move. “You’ve been pushing yourself too hard. You need rest. Let me go get Dr. On to go get some medicine for you.”\n\nRest. The word hung in the air, mocking me. Rest would not save me from the cracks splintering through my mind. The room blurred again, shifting, and unexpectedly I was watching myself from the other side of the window, looking in. My grandchildren’s laughter grew distant, distorted, as if it were underwater. Stellan’s voice warped into a low hum, words stretched into incomprehensible murmurs.\n\nI blinked, and I was back in the room. The grandchildren were giggling, their toys projecting alien worlds that shimmered and pulsed. My son was watching me, his face tense with worry. But the shadows around us were deeper now, thicker, pressing closer.\n\n“Do you feel it?” I questioned, barely audibly. “Something is here. Watching us. It’s been watching all along.”\n\n“Grandpa, stop,” he said, stepping toward me. “You’re scaring them.”\n\n“Maybe they should be scared,” I muttered. The static in my vision grew louder, harsher, a piercing sound only I could hear. “Because this isn’t peace. It’s prelude.”\n\n“To what?” he demanded, his voice sharp.\n\n“To I AM.” The words escaped me before I realized I had spoken them. My son froze, his expression shifting from concern to something darker—fear, or perhaps recognition.\n\n“What did you say?” he asked.\n\nBut I couldn’t answer. The shadows deepened, the light from the galaxies dimming as if an unseen hand had closed around them. My knees buckled, and I gripped the desk for support. The room around me warped and stretched, the walls seeming to pulse with a malevolent rhythm. My reflection in the window stared back, but it wasn’t me. Its eyes were hollow voids, and its mouth moved silently, forming words I couldn’t hear.\n\nThe static peaked, and then there was silence. A vast, deafening silence that pressed against my ears and filled my lungs like drowning.\n\nI AM.\n\nThe words were everywhere, inside me, around me, woven into the fabric of existence. They weren’t spoken; they were felt, resonating in my bones, my thoughts, my very essence. The presence beyond the void wasn’t just watching, it was here. It had always been here, waiting for this moment.\n\n“Grandpa!” Stellan‘s voice dragged me back, his hands gripping my shoulders. “What’s happening? What do you see?”\n\nI turned to him, my voice shaking. “It’s not over. It was never over. Something is coming. Something that’s been waiting for us to falter.”\n\nThe grandchildren had stopped laughing. They stared at me with blank, lifeless eyes. The static returned, softer now, a whisper that coiled around my mind like smoke.\n\nI AM.\n\nThe shadows around us twisted and grew, and the void outside the window seemed to breathe. The storm wasn’t coming.\n\nI AM was already here.	82	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 12:10:55.14515-05	2026-03-05 19:39:33.692739-05	\N	16	\N	\N
83	1	83	77: Fractured Dreams of War: The Return of the Oenomancer	In the dead of night, under a foreign string of moons that hung like a cold, indifferent string of pearls in the sky, I found myself once again trapped within the labyrinthine tunnels of my own subconscious.\n\nThe nightmares were relentless, night after night. The humanoid ants with their imposing red heads haunted my dreams, their mandibles snapping menacingly as they encircled me. These weren’t just ordinary dreams; they carried a weight, a sense of foreboding that lingered long after I awoke.\n\nThe stress of the recent glitches in S Corp’s vast network had seeped into my subconscious, manifesting in these vivid, terrifying dreams. It was a stark reminder of a part of myself that I had long neglected - my practice in Oneiromancy, the ancient art of dream manipulation and exploration.\n\nI woke up gasping, my limbs tangled in the sheets, drenched in sweat. My heart thundered in my chest, the remnants of fiery ants crawling under my skin, their burning trails vivid even as the dream faded.\n\n“Honey…” My wife’s voice was soft, but it cut through the haze of panic. She sat beside me, her hand warm and grounding on my shoulder. “You were thrashing again. Dreams?”\n\nI nodded, trying to steady my breath. “Yeah... they’re back. And worse.”\n\nShe moved closer, her fingers brushing damp hair from my forehead. Her eyes, full of concern, searched mine. “It’s been centuries since you’ve had anything like this. What’s changed?”\n\n“I don’t know,” I admitted. “But I can’t shake the feeling that something’s... off. These dreams, they’re too vivid, too real. And the glitches we’ve been seeing—they have to be connected.”\n\nHer brows furrowed. “You really think the two are related? Dreams and glitches?”\n\nI let out a shaky sigh, leaning back against the headboard. “It’s more than that. The dreams aren’t just dreams. They feel like... messages. Warnings, I don’t know. And every time I wake up, the patterns from the dream—they match the disruptions we’ve been tracking. I’ll dream of ants eating the corpse of a deer, and then the next day I get a message from one of the colonies the food processing systems went offline or something.”\n\nShe stayed quiet for a moment, her hand never leaving my shoulder. Finally, she said, “Why don’t you talk to Patrick? See what he think.”\n\nI hesitated, the idea forming solidly in my mind. Patrick, a veteran of the initial Red Hat invasion, knew first hand what these eldritch horrors could mean. But in the intervening centuries of peace, he’d be largely relegated to S Corp generic security duties and interstellar policing. We had no need for an army because we never fought anyone other than ourselves. If anyone could connect the dots, it was him.\n\n“Yeah,” I said, my voice steadier now. “Yeah, I’ll see him tomorrow. Perhaps he’s noticed something, too.”\n\nHer lips relaxed into a small, reassuring smile. “Good.”\n\nShe kissed my forehead and settled back into her side of the bed, her presence an anchor in the storm of uncertainty swirling around me.\n\nThe next day, I walked into the local S Corp security office, a nondescript building humming with quiet efficiency. The scent of recycled air and faint traces of cleaning solvents greeted me as I stepped inside. A dozen sharp-eyed security specialists were scattered around the room, their movements brisk and purposeful. Every monitor flickered with streams of data—threat assessments, colony logs, and the omnipresent surveillance feeds that kept the sector in check.\n\n“Can someone let Patrick know I’m looking for him?” I asked to the room in general.\n\nA few minutes later, Patrick emerged from the ERB portal. His grizzled face, weathered by centuries of experience, exuded the kind of no-nonsense authority that had seen us through countless crises.\n\n“Patrick,” I said as he sat down at a free workstation. “I think something fucked up is going on. I’ve been having the weird dreams again, and I think they might be connected to all the shit that’s starting to happen. I’ve had this shitty feeling for a while now, and I need someone to tell me I’m insane. That or figure out if anyone else has noticed patterns.”\n\nHe turned to the display, steepling his fingers and staring at the faint glow of his console. Finally, he met my gaze. “Ever since we met in High School eons ago, you’ve always been a little... unusual. But yeah, the team’s flagged something. What exactly have you been experiencing?”\n\nI hesitated, the images of fiery ants flashing vividly in my mind. Just thinking about them made my skin crawl. “Dreams,” I began, my voice steady. “No, more than dreams. Nightmares. They’re relentless. They feel orchestrated—like a signal buried inside my mind. Every time I think I’m close to understanding them, they shift. Lately, it’s been ants—burning, crawling through everything. They’re tearing at... me.”\n\nPatrick’s brow furrowed. He reached for the console, and with a flick of his fingers, a holographic display came to life between us. Streams of data, sleep pattern charts, and neurological scans rotated slowly in the air. “Ants, huh?” he muttered. “You’re not the only one seeing strange things. We’ve been tracking reports, dream anomalies, erratic behavior, mental strain. But here’s the kicker, it’s not just you. Not just in this region. It’s everywhere.”\n\n“What the actual fuck?” I leaned forward, the light from the holograms casting shadows across my face. “Are you sure? That... that can’t be a coincidence.”\n\nPatrick nodded grimly. “We’ve checked. Cross-referenced neurological scans, sleep pattern anomalies, even environmental factors around your quarters. Whatever’s happening, it’s locked onto us like a damn missile.”\n\n“It’s no coincidence,” I said, my voice tightening. “These dreams—they’re linked to the glitches we’ve been dealing with. I’ve seen patterns in the chaos. The fiery ants—they mirror the disruptions across our networks, the breakdowns in the colonies. It’s like they’re trying to... warn me.”\n\nPatrick’s eyes narrowed. He folded his arms and leaned back, processing my words. “Or manipulate you. This isn’t the first time we’ve seen tech intersect with the mind, but the scale... this is different. You’re saying these dreams are more than just subconscious noise?”\n\n“They’re messages,” I said firmly. “I’ve... reconnected with my training in Oneiromancy, the art of reading dreams. These aren’t random neural firings. They’re pointing to something bigger—something far beyond the glitches.”\n\nPatrick’s skepticism was evident, but so was his intrigue. “Oneiromancy,” he repeated, his tone halfway between curiosity and doubt. “That’s not exactly standard security protocol. What are we talking about here? Sentient interference?”\n\nI exhaled. “Something beyond sentient. These glitches, these visions—they’re symptoms of a larger, connected system. The Threshold—it’s tied to everything we’ve built, to the Eternal Engine, the Infinitron, even the colonies themselves.”\n\nPatrick straightened, his voice sharp and deliberate. “If that’s true, then we’re dealing with something existential. You’re saying the fabric of what we’ve built might be unraveling because of... dream signals?”\n\nI met his gaze, my conviction unshaken. “Yes. And if we don’t figure this out, it won’t just be my dreams tearing me apart—it’ll be everything.”\n\nHe stood slowly, crossing his arms and fixing me with a hard look. “Alright. If the dreams are the key, we need more than gut feelings and half-remembered Oneiromancy. I’ll assign a team to dig into the anomalies. But if this escalates...” His voice dropped slightly, an edge of warning creeping in. “You need to be ready for what’s on the other side of those dreams.”\n\nA faint smirk crossed my lips, though weariness tugged at the edges of my expression. “I’ve spent centuries walking that line, Patrick. I just hope we’re not too late to figure it out.”\n\nJust then an alarm blared. An ancient defense and monitoring system had been triggered.\n\nEver since the Red Hat war centuries ago, I had a feeling that something was just off. To alleviate my fears S Corp baked in monitoring stations and equipment into our ERB network that would alert us if anything preternatural should ever occur again.\n\nWithin the grainy static feed, we received from a long abandoned monitoring post in the Proxima Centauri system, we could see that the ERB portal was becoming infected, a mass of organic screams fused together to block the entrance, a barricade made of our worst nightmares.\n\nIn the center stood a man, scarlet hat on his head, seemingly to look directly into the security camera.\n\n“FUCK ME” I said as the world around me faded to black.\n\n**********\n\n“Are you ok” Patrick shouted as he violently shook me. “You passed out.”\n\n“He’s fucking back,” I screamed.\n\nTodd had never disappeared; he had merely sunk into the shadows, waiting. Just as these nightmares dredged up long-buried fears, his return was inevitable, a buried threat rising to the surface. I was right all along. Todd, and whatever darkness he carried, had only been lying in wait.\n\nSirens started going off from everywhere—deafening, relentless—blaring over the chaos unfolding around us. The security center suddenly became a warzone, every screen flashing crimson warnings, comms channels alive with screaming voices and garbled distress signals. The floor trembled beneath our feet, a distant explosion ripping through the structure, shaking the very foundation of headquarters.\n\nThe smell of burning metal and scorched electronics filled the air.\n\n“Shit—INCOMING!” someone screamed.\n\nA blast tore through the east wall of the building, sending a shockwave through the corridor. A fireball roared past the security center’s entrance, consuming everything and everyone in its wake. Security personnel scrambled, weapons drawn, their voices sharp with urgency.\n\nPatrick sprang into action, his voice cutting through the pandemonium. “Get him the fuck out of here!” he bellowed to his team, already moving toward the command console.\n\nChaos erupted. The pounding of boots against steel, the frantic shouts of officers coordinating defenses, the acrid scent of smoke and ozone thick in the air—it was overwhelming.\n\n“Keep your heads down! Stay close!” an officer barked, gripping my shoulder as we were herded toward the bunker entrance.\n\nI struggled against their grip, twisting around as I shouted over the wail of alarms. “What’s happening? Why are we being rushed into the bunker?!”\n\nGunfire rattled through the hallways—sharp bursts of automatic fire, too close, too loud. Somewhere outside, another explosion ripped through the structure, shaking the entire complex. The lights flickered, casting wild shadows across the sweat-slicked faces of the security team.\n\nAn soldier, her face smeared with dirt and blood, turned to me with a sharp, level gaze. “There’s a direct assault on the colony. Enemy forces have breached the perimeter. We have to secure leadership before—”\n\nA deafening BOOM.\n\nThe ceiling cracked. Debris rained down as a section of the wall buckled inward. Another soldier took the brunt of the collapse, his scream swallowed by the sheer weight of the falling rubble.\n\nGunfire erupted in the distance. More screams. Someone shouted orders through the radio—static.\n\nPanic clawed at my throat, but I forced it down. Focus.\n\n“What about the others?” I pressed, stumbling as another explosion rocked the facility. “We can’t just focus on us!”\n\n“They’re our priority too!” she shot back, her voice tight with barely contained urgency. “But if leadership falls, S Corp collapses! Your role is to guide us, not throw yourself into unnecessary danger!”\n\nA security officer ahead of us was cut down mid-step, a sharp burst of gunfire punching through his chest. Blood splattered across the steel walls as he crumpled, lifeless. Someone screamed.\n\nThe bunker door loomed ahead—reinforced steel, thick as a vault—but we were still so far.\n\nThe sound of shattering glass above sent a fresh surge of terror through the group. They’re coming in from the roof.\n\n“Move, move, MOVE!” Patrick roared, shoving me forward just as another explosion rocked the facility, knocking us all off our feet.\n\nThe world blurred, ringing with a high-pitched whine as I struggled to breathe. My hands found the floor, slick with something warm. A security officer—his face frozen in shock—lay beside me whimpering.\n\nI scrambled to my feet, grabbing onto someone—anyone. The bunker door exploded open ahead, and security officers ushered the last of us inside, weapons drawn, holding the line against whatever was closing in behind us.\n\nThen the doors slammed shut.\n\nDarkness. Silence. The sudden, jarring absence of the warzone above.\n\nThe bunker hummed with tension, the reinforced walls sealing us off from the chaos. Screens flickered with real-time data, casting the room in an eerie glow. I clenched my fists, my breath ragged, frustration boiling under my skin.\n\n“How long are we expected to wait here?” I growled, my voice hoarse.\n\nPatrick wiped blood from his forehead, glancing at the nearest security feed. Fire, smoke, and moving shadows filled the halls beyond.\n\nHis jaw clenched.\n\n“As long as it fucking takes.”\n\nAnother officer answered, her voice measured but grim. “Until the threat is fully assessed and neutralized.”\n\n“We’ve faced worse,” I said suppressing a chuckle, meeting their anxious gazes. “We don’t let fear paralyze us. Although, if this attack really is from I AM we are royally fucked. It’s been centuries, the threat never went away...”\n\nThe mention of I AM cast a shadow over the room. One officer hesitated before speaking. “Sir, what if this is beyond us? If I AM is orchestrating it, how do we fight something so... omnipotent?”\n\n“With every-fucking-thing we’ve fucking got,” I replied, my tone steel. “Fear is his greatest weapon. The fact we’re still standing proves we’re capable of resisting him. We will not let him undo what we’ve built.”\n\n**********\n\nHours stretched painfully long as we waited. Finally, a crackle on the radio broke the silence.\n\n“All clear. The threat has been neutralized.”\n\nRelief swept through the bunker like a wave. Live feeds filled the bunker with scenes of other colonies in turmoil, besieged by the reemerged Red Hats.\n\nReports from our scientists painted an even grimmer picture. Todd and his followers had advanced far beyond what we anticipated, evading our ERB network entirely. “They’ve mastered interstellar travel without detection,” one researcher explained, his voice tight with exhaustion. “They’re using systems we can’t trace, outpacing our monitoring capabilities.”\n\nThis wasn’t just an attack—it was a statement. The Red Hats weren’t defeated; they had evolved, and their war was far from over.\n\n“We’ve been complacent,” I admitted to the room, my voice steady but heavy with resolve. “We underestimated their adaptability. That ends now. Strengthen every system, rethink every protocol. We fight back, not just to defend what we’ve built, but to send them back from the bowels of hell where the crawled out of.”\n\nAll the monitors in the bunker sprang to life as Todd’s face emerged. His voice, sharp, and cutting, delivered a chilling condemnation. “Through your hubris and ego, you were blind to a reality you know nothing about.” His utterance echoed in the air, heavy and damning, like a dagger aimed at our collective failure.\n\nBefore his image had fully dissipated, the ground beneath us shuddered violently. A thunderous roar heralded the arrival of the Red Hat cyborgs, grotesque amalgamations of flesh to appear more machine-like armed with innovative cybernetics. They moved with ruthless precision, breaching our defenses with terrifying efficiency. The bunker’s security feeds showed chaos unfolding—all our countermeasures were useless against these relentless adversaries.\n\nThe bunker reeked of sweat, blood, and gunpowder. The fluorescent lights flickered, casting jagged shadows across the concrete walls. The security feeds showed nothing but carnage—bodies torn apart, hallways slick with viscera. What had once been our headquarters was now a charnel house, a slaughter unfolding in real-time.\n\nMy heart pounded in my chest as I took it all in. This wasn’t just an attack.\n\nThis was a fucking massacre.\n\nTodd’s last transmission echoed in my skull: "We were too soft. We let them in. Now look at us."\n\nHe wasn’t wrong. We had been arrogant, convinced of our own invincibility. We had played defense, thinking deterrence would keep us safe. We were wrong. The only thing our restraint had accomplished was giving the enemy time to prepare for a full-scale extermination.\n\nNow, they were hunting us down like animals.\n\nBlood dripped down the security screens, smeared by desperate hands clawing at the monitors before death took them. One feed showed the med bay—once pristine, now a slaughterhouse. Limbs strewn across the floor. Shattered IVs dripping crimson. A doctor’s head jammed inside a fucking bio-waste disposal chute.\n\nAnother feed showed the main entrance. The last of our defensive turrets had been ripped apart, their metal husks sparking feebly. Among the wreckage, bodies were impaled on rebar, twitching, coughing up blood. Some of them weren’t even dead yet.\n\nPatrick’s voice broke through the tense silence. “Orders, Commander?”\n\nMy stomach was a pit of acid, but my voice was steady. “Mobilize every fucking resource we have. Human, clone, drone—I don’t give a shit. We wipe them out. No survivors.”\n\nThe room tensed at the finality in my voice, but no one hesitated. They knew. This wasn’t about repelling an invasion. This was about survival.\n\nPatrick’s special forces captain, her uniform still smeared with someone else’s blood, stepped forward, her jaw set. “We’ve analyzed their movements. If we take out their primary energy source, it’ll cripple their offensive capabilities. Won’t be easy. It’s deep inside their occupied zone.”\n\nI nodded. “Then we burn our way through. No more fucking around. If it moves, we put it down. If it surrenders, we shoot it twice.”\n\nNo one objected. Not this time.\n\nThe captain’s grim smile was all I needed before she turned and began rallying her squad.\n\nA sharp-eyed recruit hunched over one of the security feeds, his fingers twitching as he studied the enemy forces. His voice was taut, urgent. “Commander. There’s a weakness.”\n\nI was at his side in an instant. “Show me.”\n\nHe pointed to the grainy footage, where the enemy cyborgs were advancing methodically. Their plating was thick, but their joints—knees, elbows, necks—moved with the smallest fraction of exposed wiring.\n\n“We hit them there, they drop,” he said.\n\nA slow, wicked smile spread across my face. “You just made yourself useful, recruit. Assemble a fireteam and start cutting these fuckers apart joint by joint.”\n\nThe kid swallowed but nodded, already grabbing his gear.\n\nMorgan stormed in next, cradling something that looked like a fusion bomb had a one-night stand with a microwave.\n\n“Commander,” she said, her voice tight. “I’ve got something for you.”\n\nShe held up the device. Its dull metal shell pulsed with an unsettling, rhythmic hum.\n\n“This is an EMP generator,” she explained. “It’s dirty, unstable, and if you’re too close when it goes off, it’ll fry every synapse in your fucking brain.”\n\nI exhaled, my breath coming out slow and sharp. “I love it already.”\n\n“It’ll only give us a few minutes, but that’s all we need,” she continued. “We deploy it strategically, buy our teams the time to do some real damage.”\n\nI gripped her shoulder. “Do it. Coordinate with Johann. I want these bastards blind, deaf, and pissing themselves before they even know what’s happening.”\n\nThe bunker was no longer a place of fear. It was now a war