nderstand. Because right now, understanding is all I have left.\n\n"Deleted entities cannot be restored," the Auditor says. "Please prepare for the next phase of processing."\n\nAndrew's voice cracks across the silence. "What the fuck do you want from us?"\n\nThe Auditor returns to his file. "Unauthorized name recovery. Timeline interference. Improper use of shadow artifacts. And most significantly, the attempted liberation of a Class-6 forbidden entity."\n\n"Whitney," Morgan whispers.\n\nThe room shudders. Alarms wail. The floating code turns blood red.\n\n"That designation is archived," the Auditor says, his voice now carrying the weight of collapsing mountains. "Entity Whitney-7739 has been classified and removed from active reality."\n\n"Wait," I say, desperation making my voice crack. "What about Benji? Where's Benji? He said he'd help us!"\n\nThe Auditor pauses, consulting his file with what might be confusion. "Entity designation 'Benji' is not present in current case records. No such entity exists within authorized parameters."\n\n"He's a demon! Works for Lady Illkeserod! He helped us before!"\n\n"No such records exist," the Auditor repeats. "Subject appears to be experiencing false memory syndrome. Noted for correction."\n\nThe silence that follows feels like drowning. Even Benji seems to have been erased. We're completely alone.\n\n"Standard penalty for your violations is immediate deletion from the active reality matrix," the Auditor continues. "You will have never existed."\n\nHe pauses. For the first time since this nightmare began, he actually pauses. His many eyes blink out of sequence, like a computer trying to process corrupted data. The floating code around us stutters, repeating the same fragments: CALCULATING... CALCULATING... ERROR...\n\nIn the corner of my vision, I catch a brief flicker—something that looks like a moth made of static, there and gone so fast I might have imagined it. But the Auditor's head jerks toward the same spot, his perfect composure cracking for just an instant.\n\n"External interference detected," he mutters, more to himself than to us. "Minor protocol deviation. Administrative override... pending."\n\nThe floating code shifts, and for just a moment, buried in the streams of ones and zeros, I see something that looks almost like a wink—a pattern that shouldn't exist in the rigid mathematics surrounding us.\n\n"However..." He tilts his head at an impossible angle, consulting his file with what might be confusion. "This is... unprecedented. I am receiving new parameters."\n\nHis beige suit flickers between textures—wool, silk, scales, static. The perfect bureaucratic mask slips for just a moment, revealing something almost like uncertainty underneath.\n\n"The administrators have authorized a clemency protocol," he says finally, but his voice has lost some of its mechanical certainty. "This deviation from standard procedure is... irregular. I have not encountered emotional detachment protocols of this magnitude in a Subject before."\n\n"What kind of protocol?" TJ asks, his voice barely a whisper.\n\nThe Auditor's eyes focus on me specifically, studying my flat expression with something resembling fascination "Memory wipe. Forget entity Whitney. Return to assigned baseline existence. Resume authorized life path with corrected memories and social alignment."\n\nThe Auditor’s many eyes sweep across us in turn. "You will experience normative developmental milestones. Secondary education. Approved career trajectory. Standard reproductive cycle. Termination at statistically appropriate interval. Subsequent recycling."\n\nAbove us, a countdown begins to burn: 10... 9... 8...\n\n"Not without Jamie, you fucking fuck!" I snarl, blood running down my arms. "I won’t go back without her!"\n\n7... 6... 5...\n\n"Stephen," TJ whispers sharply, "what are you doing?"\n\n"I’d rather be deleted than live in your fake fucking world without her!" I shout at the Auditor. "Do you hear me? DELETE ME!"\n\n4...