hreat in non-augmented entities."\n\nRelief floods through me even as guilt tears at my chest. I'm trading one friend for another, one love for another.\n\nThe Auditor's gaze, a kaleidoscope of impossible eyes, settles on his clipboard. With a soft click of his pen, a single line of code flashes across the glowing surface: ENTITY WHITNEY-7739 PURGED.\n\n**********\n\nA raw, humorless laugh rips from my throat, surprising even me.\n\nI still remembered her.\n\nBecause the Auditor made one mistake. One huge fucking mistake.\n\n"Fucking assholes. There was no one named Whitney. We only knew her as Jennifer."\n\nThe name hangs in the air like a bomb that hasn't decided whether to explode. The Auditor freezes, his undulating form going completely still. The walls stop pulsing. Even the floating code seems to pause mid-stream.\n\nAnd then reality starts to fucking glitch.\n\nThe marble floor cracks in geometric patterns. The floating code turns red, then purple, then colors that don't have names. Alarm bells ring in frequencies that make my teeth ache, and somewhere in the digital distance, I can hear the sound of a massive system error cascade.\n\nJennifer hunched over her notebook in Biology class, drawing elaborate J's in the margins—cursive J's with hearts for dots, block J's with little wings, gothic J's surrounded by thorns. "It's my real name," she whispered when I asked. "The one I picked for myself."\n\nThe silence stretches so long I can hear my own heartbeat echoing in the vast space, but now it's accompanied by the sound of reality trying to debug itself.\n\n"Clarification required," the Auditor says finally, and for the first time, there's something like uncertainty in that awful voice. Static crackles around the edges of his words. "Entity designation Jennifer is not present in current case files."\n\nThe first day she showed up to lunch with a Jennifer name tag stuck to her shirt. "From now on," she announced to our table, "this is who I am. William is dead