take away your house, your clothes, your family. But they can't take away what you call yourself in your head."\n\nThat's when Patrick appears.\n\nNot slowly, not fading in—he just is, suddenly standing where Jamie's bloodstain used to be. But something's wrong with him. His body is being shredded by invisible forces, like he's caught in a blender made of razor wire and mathematics. Blood sprays from wounds that open and close in geometric patterns across his skin.\n\n"NO!" he screams, his voice layered with harmonics of agony as something unseen peels strips of flesh from his chest. "You fucking assholes, it's going to destroy me! The pattern recognition is breaking down!"\n\nHis left arm doesn't pixelate—it gets torn apart muscle by muscle, sinew by sinew, like invisible teeth are chewing through him. Blood and bone fragments scatter across the marble as he reaches toward us with what's left of his shoulder socket, his face a mask of terror and accusation.\n\n"Stephen, what the fuck did you DO?" he shouts as his legs begin to separate at the joints, ligaments snapping with wet, meaty sounds. "Something's ripping me apart! I can feel my bones being pulled from their sockets!"\n\nThe Auditor doesn't even look up from his clipboard, speaking in the same monotone as Patrick's body comes apart. "System integrity check in progress. Memory allocation conflict detected."\n\n"My skin is tearing!" Patrick screams as his torso begins to split open like a zipper, ribs cracking and organs spilling out in impossible colors. "Something's shredding me from the inside! What did you fucking break, Stephen?"\n\n"Subject Velistra restoration process initiated," the Auditor continues calmly, making notes while Patrick bleeds. "Concurrent entity designation 'Jennifer' has created paradoxical memory validation. Group dynamic array calculating seven members instead of required six."\n\nHe’s right. I did this. The realization hits me like a sledgehammer. My clever little semantic trick created a memory that shouldn't exist during Jamie's restoration process. The system is trying to restore Jamie while simultaneously processing that we remember Jennifer, who transitioned to Whitney, who was forbidden.\n\n"My guts are spilling out!" Patrick shrieks, his voice cracking with terror and rage as his arms start to separate at the shoulders. "I can feel my muscles being peeled away from the bone! Stephen, you piece of shit! Whatever you did, fix it! FIX IT!"\n\n"Restoration algorithm conflicting with memory validation protocol," the Auditor explains patiently, as if he's discussing a minor software bug while Patrick's entrails splash across the marble. "System requires buffer deletion to resolve computational overflow. Subject Morales selected for emergency termination."\n\n"My head's coming off!" Patrick's skull begins to separate from his neck, held on by stretching tendons that snap one by one like guitar strings. "I can feel every tendon snapping! Stephen, please! I'm being butchered and I don't know why! HELP ME! Remember me! Someone has to remember!""\n\n"Patrick, I'm sorry!" I scream, but it's too late. "I didn't know! I didn't—"\n\nHe disappears mid-scream, just like Jamie, leaving only scattered blood that dissolves into pixels, then nothing.\n\n"Processing paradox elimination," the Auditor adds with bureaucratic efficiency. "Entity Jennifer's retroactive existence proves Entity Whitney's historical validity, contradicting deletion protocols. Balance restoration requires sacrifice of overflow entity."\n\nThe silence that follows is deafening. Everyone is staring at me, but Becca's expression cuts deepest. Her face is a mask of horror and something else—something that looks like hatred.\n\n"You chose a memory over Patrick," she whispers, and her voice carries the weight of absolute condemnation. "You chose to remember a dead girl over saving a living boy."\n\nShe's right. I didn't just save Jennifer's memory—I made it impossible for the system to cleanly erase Whitney, creating a paradox that demanded Patrick's deletion to restore balance. I saved the past by sacrificing the present.\n\nAnd Becca will never forgive me for it.\n\nThe walls begin to ripple violently, reality bending and folding like origami collapsing. The floating code streams turn white-hot and begin pouring toward the ceiling like reverse rain. Where Jamie's body should be, a new restraint table is materializing—empty, waiting.\n\nThe world folds inward, colors bleeding into each other in ways that shouldn't be possible. The last thing I see is the Auditor's form beginning to unravel, his beige suit dissolving into component pixels, and somewhere in the static, Patrick's scream still echoing: "Remember me! Someone has to remember!"\n\nEverything dissolves into white, then black, then nothing at all.\n\n**********\n\nI wake up at my desk in calculus class, Mr. Frahm's voice droning about quadratic equations like nothing has happened. The familiar scratch of chalk on blackboard fills my ears, mixed with the subtle sounds of teenage boredom—pencils tapping, pages rustling, someone's stomach growling in the back row.\n\nThe classroom seems largely undisturbed, no scorch marks or signs of the chaos that consumed our reality just moments—or was it hours?—ago. There's no smell of smoke drifting from the Biology Lab on the other side of the school, just the usual classroom mixture of industrial cleaner, teenage desperation, and Mr. Frahm's coffee breath.\n\nThrough the windows, I can see the Biology Lab in the distance—intact, undamaged, like it didn't burn to the fucking ground while we watched helplessly from the courtyard. My notebook is open to a half-finished problem set, my handwriting looking foreign to me now. The equations swim on the page like they're written in a language I used to know but have somehow forgotten.\n\nFor a moment, I wonder if I dreamed everything—the burning school, the impossible fire truck, the Auditor's bone-deep voice. Maybe I fell asleep in class. Maybe the stress of everything that's been happening finally broke my brain.\n\nThen Jamie pokes me in the ribs with her pencil.\n\n"We still fucking after school or what?" she whispers, her smile too wide, like someone wearing a mask of their own face. "It's Friday, baby. We can bang all weekend."\n\n"Jamie?" I study her features, looking for something familiar. The shape is right, hair is in the right places, but her eyes are cold. Calculating. Like she's performing a role she learned from bad porn.\n\n"What's wrong, stud?" She runs her tongue across her lower lip in a gesture that's supposed to be seductive but feels mechanical. "Don't you want to test out what we learned in biology class? I've been thinking about it all morning. Want me to help you take a sample it in the bathroom later? "\n\nThe words hit me like a slap. This isn't Jamie. The real Jamie would die of embarrassment before saying "fucking" out loud. Even when her demonic side showed through, she was sensual, not pornographic. This thing wearing her face is spouting lines like she learned them from amateur porn videos.\n\nI mumble something noncommittal and check my watch—11:48 AM. A few hours before the fire started. Reality reset to its last save point, all changes undone, all progress lost. But they got something wrong in the restoration. This isn't my Jamie.\n\nAfter class, I stumble to my locker on autopilot, muscle memory guiding me through hallways that feel both familiar and strange. My combination spins under my fingers—11, 36, 38—and the metal door swings open with its usual squeak.\n\nThere, sitting on top of my textbooks, is Jennifer's Journal.\n\nPristine leather binding the color of dried blood, but now I can see what I missed before—tiny J's embossed all over the cover. Hundreds of them in different fonts and styles, some with hearts for dots, others with little wings, gothic J's surrounded by thorns. Jennifer's signature, repeated endlessly like a prayer.\n\nFresh ink smell that makes my head spin. The cover is warm to the touch, like it's been sitting in sunlight, though my locker never gets any light.\n\nI flip it open. The first page is blank except for a single line of text in handwriting that's almost but not quite my own:\n\nShe remembers you. Do you remember her?\n\nThe words make my hands shake.\n\nAt lunch, everything feels off-kilter. I sit at our usual table, but it's just me and the Jamie-thing. Our friends are gone—TJ, Morgan, Andrew, and Becca all left school earlier, claiming they felt sick. The nurse's office probably looks like a war zone with all of them showing up with "stomach bugs" and "migraines."\n\nThey're trying to process what the fuck happened to us in the Audit. I don't blame them.\n\nThe absence of their voices, their laughter, their presence makes the cafeteria feel hollow. Even the ambient noise seems muted, like someone turned down the volume on reality itself.\n\nWilliam sits with his ROTC friends at the next table, making his usual crude jokes, but there's something different about him. He's still an ass, but... muted. Like someone turned down his asshole dial from eleven to about a six. When a freshman drops his tray nearby, William actually gets up to help him clean it up instead of mocking him. His friends look confused by the gesture.\n\n"That's weird," I mutter to myself. In the old reality, William would have laughed and probably thrown food at the kid.\n\nThe she-beast-in-Jamie's-skin beside me has done something to her clothes that makes my skin crawl. Her shorts are so short I can see the edge of her cheeks when she sits down, and she's clearly taken off her bra—I can see everything through the thin fabric of her shirt, which she's tied up to expose her midriff like some kind of music video extra. She keeps running her hand up my thigh under the table, whispering increasingly graphic suggestions in my ear, then actually slips her hand down the front of my pants right there in the empty corridor.\n\n"Jesus, Jamie," I snap, grabbing her wrist and pulling her hand out. "What the hell?"\n\nShe just giggles and tries to go for it again. "Come on, baby. There's nobody here to watch. We could do it right on the table."\n\nEach word feels like nails on a chalkboard. This isn't the girl who blushed when I held her hand. This isn't even the demon who seduced me with gentle touches and meaningful looks. This is some algorithm's idea of a horny teenager, all base urges and no soul—a walking, talking sex doll programmed by someone who's never actually met a real human being.\n\nThe empty seats where my friends should be sitting make her wrongness even more obvious. TJ would have choked on his sandwich. Morgan would have wanted to disappear under the table. Becca would have covered her eyes and whispered prayers.\n\nBut they're not here. They couldn't handle being around this broken version of our reality.\n\nAnd I don't blame them.\n\nAfter lunch, as we're walking to our next classes, I pull out Jennifer's Journal, hoping to find some clue about what went wrong. The moment the leather binding appears, Jamie-thing stops walking.\n\n"What's that?" she asks, but her voice has changed. Gone cold. Then she sees the journal clearly and recoils like I just pulled out a crucifix. "What the fuck is that thing? Get it away from me!"\n\nHer face contorts with genuine revulsion and fear—the first real emotion I've seen from her all day. She backs away from the journal like it's radioactive, pressing herself against the lockers.\n\n"Is that a present for me, baby?" The words come out of her mouth almost robotic, but her eyes are wide with terror. It's like watching someone else control her vocal cords. "You know how wet notebooks make me." She's fighting against her own voice, shaking her head frantically even as her mouth keeps moving.\n\n"Just a notebook," I say, but something underneath her programming has taken control. Her hands move against her will, 