d us. Gave us new cover stories, new backgrounds, new reasons for being who we are. But they installed a placeholder Jamie, some generic "girlfriend.exe" program to fill the role until they could figure out what to do with the real one.\n\n"Stephen," she breathes, and now it's really her voice—soft, uncertain, tinged with the harmonics of her merged nature. "They tried to replace me. With... with some cheap knockoff."\n\n"I know," I whisper, helping her sit up against the wall. "I could tell. You were all wrong."\n\n"The real me was backed up," she says, tears streaming down her cheeks. "Quarantined while they decided whether to delete me completely or try to salvage the useful parts. But Jennifer's Journal... it's a trigger. A failsafe. It brought me back."\n\nThe crowd of students starts to disperse as she obviously recovers, but I can see the concern in everyone’s faces.\n\nBut some things run too deep to delete.\n\nSome memories refuse to die.\n\nAnd Jennifer's Journal, warm against my palm, pulses once like a sleeping heart waking up.\n\n"This is too much for today," I tell the others, my voice shaky with relief and exhaustion. "I'm taking Jamie home."\n\nIt's Friday. We have the whole weekend to figure out what the hell just happened to us, what parts of our memories are real, and how to cope with the fact that our best friend is truly, completely gone—replaced not just in reality, but in our own restored lives.\n\nAt least I have Jamie back. The real Jamie. Not some algorithm's idea of teenage lust, but the complicated, beautiful, impossible girl who chose love over duty and somehow survived deletion.\n\nThat has to count for something.\n\nEven if everything else is broken beyond repair.	16	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 12:19:13.808636-05	2026-03-05 19:39:33.717247-05	\N	44	\N	\N
