t been invented.\n\nReset.\n\nThe belt falls through concepts and mathematics and the spaces between thoughts.\n\nI scream.\n\nReset.\n\nThe room is no longer a room. The trailer is no longer a trailer. Reality has worn thin from repetition, like a VHS tape played too many times. The edges blur. The center holds only because it has to. Because the beating must continue. Because I refused to die properly when poison would have been so much kinder.\n\nAnd in the static between resets, I hear him. Not my father. The thing behind my father. Ancient and patient and amused by this small rebellion of mine.\n\nYou wanted to remember, it whispers. So remember this. Forever.\n\nThe belt falls.\n\nReset.\n\nThe belt falls through what's left of reality.\n\nReset.\n\nThe belt falls, and I finally understand what Benji meant about gods waking up cranky.\n\n**********\n\nThe smell hits me first—not urine and rot, but leather. Industrial-grade leather treatment chemicals that burn the sinuses and make your eyes water. I'm awake before my eyes open, every nerve screaming that something's wrong. The air tastes like a tannery exploded, coating my tongue with the flavor of processed hide and chromium salts.\n\nI crack my eyelids and immediately wish I hadn't.\n\nThis isn't my bedroom. This isn't even the trailer from Nevada. This is something else entirely—a classroom that remembers being those places but got the details catastrophically wrong. The blue Popeye curtains have been replaced with strips of hanging leather in various stages of curing, from raw hide still dripping to finished products gleaming with polish. They cast shadows that move independently, each one demonstrating a different belt-making technique in silhouette.\n\nThe furniture has transformed into a grotesque parody of educational equipment. My old desk is now a tanning bench, complete with stretching racks and chemical vats that bubble with solutions I don't want to identify. The chair beside it is a wooden stool covered in leather samples, each patch labeled with tiny brass plates: "CORRECTED GRAIN," "FULL GRAIN," "BONDED," "GENUINE."\n\nThe walls are covered in educational posters, but they're wrong—showing cross-sections of human skin being processed, step-by-step guides where human figures are being turned into belts. "FROM BOY TO BELT IN 10 EASY STEPS!" one cheerfully proclaims. The ceiling has become a conveyor system, different belts hanging like meat in a slaughterhouse, sorted by width, color, and the age of their source material.\n\nI try to sit up and realize I'm not in a bed—I'm on an examination table, the kind you'd find in a leather workshop. The surface is cold metal covered with scratches from years of cutting and measuring.\n\nThe door opens.\n\nNo sound. It just opens, revealing my father in a leather apron that's seen better days. In his hands, not one belt but dozens, draped over his arms like sleeping snakes.\n\nBut his face—his face is worse than any monster. It's perfectly normal. Perfectly calm. The face of a teacher who's disappointed you didn't do the homework.\n\n"Pop quiz," he says, his voice carrying that particular tone of an educator who knows you're about to fail. "Today we're learning about leather crafting. The noble art of belt-making."\n\nHe holds up the first belt—thin, black, with a silver buckle.\n\n"What type of leather is this?"\n\nMy mouth opens but no sound comes out. I don't know. How could I know?\n\n"WRONG!" The patience evaporates like water on hot metal. "All wrong! Every single answer!"\n\nHe throws the belts aside, and they writhe on the floor like living things. Behind him, one of the vats glows with heat, the tanning solution inside churning with an appetite all its own.\n\n"If you can't identify leather," he says, advancing with the calm purpose of someone performing a necessary task, "then perhaps you need a more... immersive education."\n\nHis hands are on my shoulders. Not violent. Almost gentle. The hands of a teacher