oks like Florida if you squint, the pile of dirty clothes in the corner that I keep meaning to deal with. Even the smell is right—old carpet, dust motes dancing in sunbeams, the faint mildew scent that comes with living in Florida's humidity.\n\nThis feels more real than real. Hyperreal. Like someone took my memory of safety and dialed up the saturation until it glowed.\n\nMy father appears in the doorway, and for a moment—just a moment—I almost believe in miracles.\n\nHe's smiling. Actually smiling, and not the sharp-edged thing I'm used to, but something soft around the eyes. His face looks younger somehow, like twenty years of anger has been surgically removed. He's wearing his weekend clothes—faded jeans and the blue button-down that brings out his eyes—and his posture is relaxed, shoulders loose instead of coiled for violence.\n\n"We should go fishing this weekend," he says, and his voice carries the warmth I've been starving for my entire life. The words land on my skin like sunshine, and for one impossible second I feel what it might be like to have a father instead of a warden.\n\nI can already picture it: sitting on some quiet dock with our lines in the water, talking about nothing important while dragonflies skim the surface. Maybe he'd tell me stories about when he was a kid. Maybe I'd tell him about the books I've been reading. Maybe we'd just exist in the same space without either of us bleeding.\n\nThe fantasy lasts exactly three heartbeats.\n\nHis face crumples like wet paper, features sliding around as if his skull has suddenly become liquid. The smile doesn't fade—it stretches, widening until it splits his cheeks, revealing too many teeth arranged in rows like a shark's mouth. His eyes remain kind even as they begin to leak something dark and viscous down his cheeks.\n\n"Pop quiz," he says, and the warmth curdles into something educational and terrible. From his belt, he draws something that shouldn't exist—a filleting knife made entirely of leather