chest, sudden and specific and wrong. My hands shake.\n\nJamie.\n\nThe name doesn't just hit me—it tears through my chest like shrapnel. With it comes the smell of sulfur and the sound of screaming metal, but underneath all that, something else. The memory of her laugh. Real laughter, not the hollow echo my son keeps repeating. Her laugh was chaos and music and the sound of someone who saw the world burning and still found reasons to smile.\n\nA church. Sand. Fire. And her hand... God, her hand. Small and warm and reaching for me as everything we'd built together crumbled into ash. She was trying to tell me something. Something important. Her lips were moving but the explosion swallowed her words, and I never got to hear what she wanted to say. What she needed me to know.\n\n"I'll always..."\n\nThe grief hits like a physical blow. Jamie. She was... she was everything. The way she looked at me like I wasn't broken, like all my fractured pieces made sense when she assembled them in her mind. The way she kissed me like the world was ending—which maybe it was—but she didn't care because we had that moment. We had each other.\n\nAnd now I'm here. In this sterile morning light with my wife who smells like lavender fabric softener instead of wild herbs and danger. My wife who loves me in that safe, predictable way—the kind of love that comes with matching coffee mugs and shared Netflix passwords and birthday cards that say "To My Husband" in cursive fonts.\n\nShe's kind. She's caring. She performs consideration like a carefully choreographed dance, learned from some manual on How to Be a Good Wife. Every gesture measured, every smile perfectly calibrated. When she touches my arm, it's with the same practiced tenderness she uses to arrange flowers—beautiful, precise, and utterly devoid of passion.\n\nShe always curls over onto her side bed, always has some excuse. Too tired. Too stressed. Her back hurts. And when I reach for her in those lonely 3 AM moments, she gives me that polite little pat—the kind you'd give a dog that's being too enthusiastic—before rolling further away.\n\nIt's like living with a beautiful mannequin that's been programmed to say the right things. "How was your day, honey?" "Don't forget to take your vitamins." "I love you too," delivered with the emotional resonance of a weather report.\n\nShe's kind. She's caring. She's everything a reasonable man should want.\n\nShe's just not Jamie.\n\nJamie was fire and rebellion and the taste of something forbidden. She was the girl who would've burned down the world just to keep me warm. And I... God help me, I would've let her. I would've held the match.\n\nBut even as the memory surges through me, bright and terrible and real, I can feel it starting to fade. Like sand through my fingers. Like smoke in wind. The edges blur first—her voice becomes an echo, her face loses definition. The pain remains, but the details... the details are slipping away.\n\nNo. Please. Not her. Don't take her from me again.\n\nBut it's already happening. The name becomes just a sound. The grief becomes just an ache I can't explain. And my wife—my safe, cotton-sheet wife—turns around and smiles at me like nothing has changed.\n\nLike nothing was ever different.\n\nLike Jamie was never real at all.\n\nWho? What was I just thinking about?\n\nReality hits me again like a shock as I get another blast of flour in the face. After several agonizing flour drenched moments in the kitchen, I reveal my masterpieces of disaster. The three of us sit down at the table, our plates filled with warm waffles adorned with fresh berries and drizzled with syrup.\n\nBut the waffles taste like ash. And when I look at my wife's face, for just a moment, her features flicker. Brown Jamie hair instead of her Japanese Black. Different eyes. A dark, sinister voice whispering: "You wanted closure. Not truth."\n\nIn that perfect morning moment, surrounded by the comforting love of my family, I realize that something is terribly wrong. The walls are too still. The light never moves. And my son's laughter... it's the same laugh, repeating, like a broken record.\n\nI raise my coffee mug to toast our upcoming trip. "To adventure, to knowledge, and to the sheer joy of eating like an unsupervised raccoon in a space station because we're all just here for a good time, not a long time."\n\nThe coffee cup never touches my lips. My hand freezes mid-air, and I watch the steam rise in perfect spirals that never dissipate.\n\nOutside the window, the sun hangs motionless, painted against a sky that doesn't breathe.\n\nThen—like a film reel snapping back into motion—time lurches forward. The moment shatters. Whatever was clawing at the edges of my consciousness retreats, leaving only the faintest echo. Not memories, exactly. Just... feelings. Fragments of something that might have mattered once.\n\nThen nothing.\n\nI blink, and I'm here. Really here. In my house in Florida with my wife humming in the kitchen and my son's voice drifting from the living room, talking to online friends whose names I actually recognize. The coffee in my mug is still warm. The waffles taste like waffles should—sweet and ordinary and real.\n\nThis is my life. This has always been my life.\n\nWhatever darkness was pressing against the windows has lifted, leaving only the comfortable weight of routine. Still unemployed. Bills to figure out. Resumes to update. A family that needs me in all the small, unglamorous ways that families do.\n\nLater that afternoon I settle down at my computer desk, its old, worn surface bathed under the soft glow of afternoon light, and allow my fingers to dance across the keys. Job listings flicker on my screen, each one a potential gateway to a new chapter in my shitty life.\n\nThere, in the midst of the myriad of possibilities, and future rejection emails, a position at ExSpacey as a director of Application Development. The words resonate with the depths of my skills and experiences, beckoning me towards a purpose that aligns with the collective march of humanity towards the stars.\n\n"Back to the fucking grindstone," I think to myself as I begin the torture that is the task of updating my resume.\n\nAs the news feed talks about Leon’s latest failed rocket ship explosion, humanity's journey diverges from the boundless wanderings of the dreamscape. Here, we do not drift through illusion, but carve our path through the marrow of reality itself—where the thrill is not in the unknown, but in the undeniable, the touchable, the real. Although the universe may stretch out before us, vast and mysterious, it is here, within the joys and challenges of everyday life on Earth, that we find our own unique allure, even with all the pain, suffering and disgust we face on a daily basis.\n\nI sit there and think to myself, jobless with a ton of time on my hands, I really should write a book about all these dreams and profound shit I think about. Something tugs at my memory... Elysium something. Not Rising. Falling. The word feels heavy, like it carries weight I can't remember lifting.\n\nBut then the idea passes, a fleeting memory soon to be forgotten.\n\n"An author. Yeah right." I mutter to myself. "Dreams come to Etheris to fucking die."\n\nI freeze. My fingers hover over the keyboard.\n\nEtheris?\n\nWhere the hell did that word come from? I meant to say Earth. Dreams come to Earth to die. That's the phrase. That's always been the phrase.\n\nBut my mouth said Etheris. Like it knew something my brain didn't.\n\nThe room suddenly feels... wrong. Too quiet. Like the moment between a lightning strike and thunder. I look at my hands. They're shaking. Why are they shaking?\n\nMy wife walks in, holding her phone at arm's length like it might bite her. Her face is pale. "Honey... there's someone on the phone for you."\n\n"Who?"\n\n"She says her name is Whitney." My wife's voice cracks.\n\n"I don't know anyone named—"\n\nBut I can't finish the sentence. Because suddenly I do. I remember everything. The flickers. The shadows. The demon in Jamie's smile. Whitney vanishing into William. The journal that wrote itself. Benji's winged eye. The kick that started it all.\n\nThe phone trembles in my wife's hand. I can hear a voice on the other end. Distant. Familiar. Impossible.\n\n"Stephen?" the voice says. Clear as a bell. Clear as a memory that was never supposed to exist. "Stephen, it's Whitney. But you need to listen very carefully. I found Jay…"\n\nI drop the phone as the line suddenly goes dead.	22	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 12:21:01.629298-05	2026-03-05 19:39:33.717247-05	\N	53	\N	\N
113	2	23	About Elysium Fallen	So in 2024 I got laid off my job building crazy software systems to help manage some interesting electrical components. Like Tesla and their Supercharger network. With a bunch of time on my hands I decided to finally put pen to paper and get Elysium Rising out of my head. It’s something I had dancing around in my brain for at least 20 years.\n\nThe main question of that book is what would happen if you did end up having infinite power and knowledge, and wanted to do a commentary on the meaninglessness of existence. But that got boring, so I decided to inject a little bit of occult magic and commentary on modern day politics into it. It was a run write, and after 6 months it was finished. Over the next 12 months I sought out a publisher, some illustrators, and a few editors. After not finding anyone who wanted anything to do with my ramblings, I decided to self publish it on Kindle on February 6, 2025.\n\nAnd that was that. The story was told. Sorhhinda was some teaser that was never meant to be finished (the whole hope and dream cycles thing).\n\nBut then someone signed me up for the AutoCrit 90 day writing challenge. Not having any other ideas I just started banging away at the keyboards, envisioning what was the outcome of the ending of Elysium Rising (not the space travel part, but what happened after waking from the dream). I had that first chapter done and then nothing for almost a month. How am I going to move this story forward. I just died.\n\nAnd that’s when Jamie started haunting my dreams. Begging me to bring her to life. And so I did.\n\nUnfortunately this book got a lot longer than I had wanted, and since I only had 90 days to complete everything, it’s a LOT rougher than the first book. I tend to write, ramble, and repeat myself a lot, and so tend to ballon things up a lot more than I probably should (at some point ER was almost 450k words – but I did get that down to 239k before publishing it). This one, it’s at 124k words, and I didn’t have to build out the whole universe this time.\n\nMaybe, if these things really well, I can pay for an actual editor, get these books fixed up and streamlined to the point where it’s a lot better and republish things.\n\nBut that’s going to have to wait until I finish my 30 day writing challenge for BOOK 3 (this November 2025).	23	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 12:21:10.899172-05	2026-03-05 19:39:33.717247-05	\N	55	\N	\N
