118	3	5	Chapter 3: Circling the Grey	It always begins the exact same way.\n\nYou are crouching there, hunched against something solid at your back. You open your eyes and peels of grayness flood in. Everywhere around you is a dull shade of average. It's raining, everything is damp, yet nothing is wet and there are no puddles.\n\nYou stretch, stand up, and run your hands against what you feel are bricks behind you. Sound then starts entering your brain. The drip-drip-drop of rain that couldn't be bothered to decide if it wanted to keep time or not.\n\nYou feel dirty. Confused. Your clothes are all wrong. Layers of tweed that just don't match, blurred from behind glasses that just don't seem to focus. Standing there you have a gnawing feeling that you've forgotten something catastrophically important.\n\nAll this is new. All this is old. You constantly touch your face, reassuring yourself that yes, you are real, but all the time searching, expecting something that isn't there.\n\nYou ache. You're not hungry. You're not thirsty. But something is missing. You hunger for something deep inside, something you just can't define. Every person you see walking by feels like a word you are forgetting.\n\nStep. Step. Walk. You look into the distance, at the corner where you see the woman with the red umbrella sneaking a peek at the fancy businessman checking his watch. You sneeze. You shiver.\n\nThen it hits you.\n\nA smell.\n\nNot the rain. Not the wet brick or the exhaust from cars that never quite arrive. Something else. Something underneath. It crawls up your nostrils and settles behind your eyes like a migraine made of light.\n\nOzone. Burnt cinnamon. Copper pennies left in the sun.\n\nThe smell of waking up.\n\nYou shouldn't know what that smells like. You don't know how you know. But the recognition is immediate, absolute, like remembering how to breathe after someone reminds you that you've been doing it all along.\n\nYour non-existent wings itch.\n\nYou freeze. Wings? You don't have wings. You've never had wings. Your shoulder blades spasm anyway, trying to flex appendages that don't exist, reaching for a form you can't remember wearing.\n\nThe itch spreads. Down your spine and up into your skull. You touch your face again, but this time you're not checking if you're real. You're checking if you're you. And the answer that comes back is wrong.\n\nYou're not supposed to be here. This body, these clothes, this corner, this rain that never puddles... None of it is yours.\n\nBut if this isn't you, then what are you?\n\nThe question cracks something open in your head. A flash, quick and bright: flying. Not with wings, but with intention. Moving through spaces that don't have names. Laughing at something... a joke? No. The joke. The cosmic punchline that makes gods weep.\n\nThen it's gone.\n\nYou gasp, stagger, catch yourself against a brick wall. Your hand is shaking. You stare at it like it belongs to someone else.\n\nMaybe it does.\n\nYou decide to walk again. Movement helps. Movement gives the illusion of purpose.\n\nYou head to the corner, where the woman with the red umbrella was sneaking a peek at the fancy businessman checking his watch. Wait. Weren’t you just there? You've seen this before. You know you have. But when? How many times?\n\nYou count your steps. One. Two. Three. Seventeen steps to the corner.\n\nThe woman's umbrella is still red. The businessman's watch still gleams. You stop. You watch.\n\nThe woman glances at the businessman. He checks his watch. She smiles, just slightly, then walks past.\n\nYou wait.\n\nSeventeen minutes later, you see her again.\n\nSame corner. Same umbrella. Same glance. Same smile. The businessman checks the same watch, and the light catches it in the exact same way, throwing the exact same reflection onto the exact same wet pavement that never puddles.\n\nYour heart, the one you forgot you had, starts hammering.\n\nYou turn. There, across the street, a different person. A jogger in bright yellow shorts. Earbuds in. He passes a bodega, turns left at the flower stand, disappears behind the newsstand.\n\nYou count. One Mississippi. Two Mississippi.\n\nAt forty-seven Mississippi, he reappears. Same bodega. Same yellow shorts. Same path. Turn. Disappear.\n\nYour mouth goes dry.\n\nThe loop isn't smooth. It's not perfect. There are micro-variations, tiny glitches you wouldn't notice unless you were looking. The businessman's tie shifts half an inch to the left every third loop. The woman's umbrella sometimes drips, sometimes doesn't. But the pattern holds.\n\nEveryone is stuck.\n\nEveryone but you.\n\nYou walk to a newsstand where the red umbrella woman might have been. Your hands are still shaking. You reach for a magazine, any magazine, just to touch something solid, something real.\n\nYou grab one.\n\nThe letters aren't letters. They're colors, angles, geometries that shouldn't exist in two dimensions. Your eyes try to focus, try to parse meaning, and the shapes reject the attempt. They fold inward, bleeding impossible hues, screaming frequencies your brain interprets as pain.\n\nYour eyes start to bleed. Actually bleed. Warm wetness runs down your cheeks, and you drop the magazine. It hits the ground and pixelates, breaking into constituent parts before reassembling itself back on the rack.\n\nThe world spins.\n\nYour knees buckle.\n\nAnd everything...\n\n**********\n\nIt always begins the exact same way.\n\nYou are crouching there, hunched against something solid at your back. You open your eyes and peels of grayness flood in. Everywhere around you is a dull shade of average. It's raining, everything is damp, yet nothing is wet and there are no puddles.\n\nBut this time, something is different.\n\nThis time, you remember the loop.\n\nYou stand up fast, too fast. Your head swims but you push through it. Seventeen steps to the corner. You don't take them. You stay where you are, pressed against the brick wall, watching.\n\nThe woman with the red umbrella. The businessman with the watch. The jogger in yellow shorts.\n\nThey move like clockwork. Like puppets on very good strings.\n\nAnd you... you see the strings.\n\nThat's when the smell hits again. Ozone. Burnt cinnamon. Copper pennies. But stronger now. Closer. It's not coming from around you.\n\nIt's coming for you.\n\nYour spine locks. Your back screams in phantom agony. Every instinct you don't remember having tells you to run, hide, disappear.\n\nBecause something noticed.\n\nThe rain doesn't change. The loops don't stop. But the air grows teeth.\n\nSomewhere, something is waking up. Something designed to clean up messes. Something that doesn't appreciate anomalies breaking the pattern, noticing the strings, remembering.\n\nYou press yourself flat against the brick wall, and for the first time since you opened your eyes in this grey, looping hell, you understand what you're feeling.\n\nTerror.\n\nPure, primal, animal terror.\n\nBecause you don't belong here. You're not supposed to exist. And the system that runs this place just realized you do.\n\nThe smell of ozone grows stronger. The rain begins to fall in reverse, just for a moment, drops rising instead of falling.\n\nAnd somewhere, seventeen steps away, a woman in a white coat steps out of another coffee shop that wasn't there a moment ago.\n\nShe's looking directly at you.\n\nShe's smiling.\nShe doesn't walk toward you. She simply is closer, like the distance between you decided to collapse out of politeness.\n\nThe woman in the white coat stands three feet away. Then two. You didn't see her move. You blinked, and proximity changed.\n\nHer coat is immaculate. Not just clean, but sterile. The kind of white that exists in operating rooms and morgues. It catches the grey light and reflects nothing back. Her hair is pulled into a bun so precise it looks architectural. Her face is beautiful in the way that surgical steel is beautiful—flawless, cold, purpose-made.\n\nShe's looking at you, but not at your face. Her eyes, pale grey like the rain, scan your body the way a doctor examines an x-ray. Cataloging. Assessing. Finding the fractures.\n\n"You're leaking," she says.\n\nHer voice is soft, almost gentle, but it has the weight of a diagnosis. Terminal. Untreatable. Final.\n\nYou open your mouth. Nothing comes out. Your throat has forgotten how to make words.\n\nShe tilts her head, just slightly. The movement is too smooth, like ball bearings in oil. "Consciousness," she clarifies, as if you'd asked. "You're leaking it all over my street. Little puddles of awareness where there shouldn't be any."\n\nShe steps closer. You should step back. You can't. Your legs have turned to concrete.\n\n"The smell must be intolerable," she continues, her nose wrinkling just enough to convey distaste. "All that waking up. All that noticing. It's septic. Infectious." Her eyes finally meet yours, and they're empty in a way that makes the void seem crowded. "If you stay out here much longer, you'll attract the collectors."\n\n"Collectors," you manage. The word tastes like rust.\n\n"Mmm." She doesn't elaborate. She just watches you with the patience of something that has already won. "Come inside before you make more of a mess."\n\nIt's not a request.\n\nShe turns, and you follow. You don't decide to follow. Your body just... does. One foot in front of the other, pulled by a gravity that shouldn't exist.\n\nThe coffee shop wasn't there before.\n\nYou're certain of it. You've been on this corner for... how long? Loops upon loops. Seventeen steps. Red umbrella. Yellow shorts. The newsstand with its impossible magazines. But never a coffee shop.\n\nNow there is.\n\nThe door is glass, spotless, and opens without a sound. No bell. No creak. Just the whisper of air pressure equalizing.\n\nInside, the space is wrong.\n\nNot wrong in the way the magazines were wrong, with their lettering that made your eyes bleed. This is worse. This is a wrongness that fits. Everything is exactly where it should be.