door clicked open. My wife walked in, already dressed for a day she didn’t have. She didn’t look at me. She went straight to the nightstand on her side of the bed and yanked her phone charger from the wall.\n\n"Do you see—" I started, holding up my arm.\n\n"Did you remember to pay the electric bill?" She didn't look up from the tangle of cords in her hands.\n\nI blinked, the vertigo of being alive crashing into the banality of her question. "I... yeah. Tuesday."\n\n"Are you sure? Because last time you said you did, I got a notice."\n\n"I paid it," I said, the fog in my brain momentarily pierced by defensiveness. "I remember the confirmation number."\n\nShe finally looked up, her expression mild, almost bored. She leaned against the doorframe, effectively blocking the only exit from the bedroom. "Let's just check, shall we?"\n\nShe pulled out her phone. She took her time, unlocking it slowly, swiping through screens with agonizing deliberation while I sat there, trapped in the sheets, my heart hammering against ribs that shouldn't have been moving.\n\n"See?" She turned the screen toward me, not close enough to actually read. "It's not paid. Right there in red. 'Amount Due'."\n\nPanic, cold and sharp, cut through the haze. I grabbed my own phone from the nightstand, fingers fumbling. I logged in.\n\n"That's November," I said, holding my phone up, showing the zero balance for the current month. "I paid October on Tuesday. You're looking at the bill that isn't even due for three weeks."\n\nShe didn't even glance at my screen. She just sighed and lowered hers.\n\n"What, do you think I'm stupid? Of course I know we haven't paid November yet. I was testing to see if you were even aware of it."\n\n"You just said it wasn't paid! You showed me the red text!"\n\n"I was asking if you were prepared for the next bill, Stephen. Why do you always do this?"\n\n"Do what? Defend myself?"\n\n"Twist my words. It's incredibly manipulative, and honestly? It's exhausting." She shook her head sadly, still blocking the door. "I just need to know I can trust you with basic things."\n\nThe air in the room felt suddenly too thin. The argument was a loop, a trap designed to have no right answer. If I was wrong, I was incompetent. If I was right, I was manipulative.\n\nI couldn't get past her without touching her, and I couldn't bear to touch her. I kicked the covers off and scrambled toward the master bathroom instead—the only other door.\n\n"I have to go potty" I muttered.\n\nI shut the bathroom door behind me, leaning back against it, breathing hard. When I looked down at my arm, the bandages were completely gone. Not sure what was real if I can’t even have a normal conversation with my wife.\n\nWhen I finally left the bathroom, she wasn’t in our bedroom anymore. I found her in the kitchen, waiting for the electric kettle to boil. She was making tea. Just one cup.\n\nI went to the coffee maker. Cold. She hadn't made any. I started the process myself—grinding beans, filling the reservoir—while she leaned against the counter, scrolling.\n\n"You know, if you're going to be home all day, you could at least keep up with the dishes," she said, not looking up.\n\nI glanced at the sink. It was empty, save for a single spoon from last night that I must have missed. I had done three loads yesterday. "I did them. Last night."\n\n"Well, clearly not all of them." She gestured vaguely at the spoon with her phone. "It's just disheartening to come into a dirty kitchen first thing in the morning."\n\nI didn't take the bait. The coffee started to drip, slow and agonizing.\n\n"My mom called yesterday," she said to her screen. "Asked how you were doing."\n\nI waited.\n\n"She mentioned my sister again. Apparently, her husband just got another promotion. Regional Director now. They're looking at a vacation home."\n\n“That's great for them," I said, keeping my voice flat.\n\n"It is. It's... nice to see people succeeding. Mom asked what you were up to. I didn't know what to tell her. Two years, Stephen. It's getting harder to make excuses."\n\n"I'm applying every day. You know that. Hell, I’m still on Walmart’s 6 month waitlist. Not like the jobs are just lining up."\n\nShe scoffed, a tiny, sharp sound. "Are you? I mean, really applying? Or just clicking buttons on LinkedIn? Because my friend’s husband—you know, the plumber?—he was out of work for maybe a week before he found something new. A week."\n\n"He's a contract plumber in a hurricane state. I'm a software engineering director in a market that's currently shedding six-figure jobs like dandruff. It's not the same."\n\n"Always an excuse. Maybe it's not the market. Maybe it's... you know. How you present yourself. You haven't exactly been inspiring confidence lately."\n\nThe subtext hit me like a physical slap: You didn't get laid off because of restructuring. You got laid off because you're useless.\n\n"We need money," she continued, turning to pour the boiling water over her tea bag. "Real money, not just your unemployment scraps. You need to take out a loan. Or pull from our 401k."\n\nShe stressed "our." She hadn’t worked a day since Xavier was born fourteen years ago.\n\n"We can't touch that. The penalties alone would eat half of it. It's suicide."\n\n"Better than foreclosure. Which is where you've steered us." She leaned back against the counter, blowing softly on her tea. "Honestly, Stephen, if you hadn't wasted so much money when you were working, we wouldn't be desperate right now."\n\nI stared at her, my jaw tight. "Wasted? On what?"\n\n"Oh, I don't know. Those stupid games you're always buying on the computer."\n\nI almost laughed. The Steam Winter Sale. I spent maybe a hundred bucks a year, total, if I was feeling indulgent.\n\nDon’t say it, I told myself. Don't bring up the bathroom counter, looking like a graveyard of half-used 'miracle' creams that cost more than my car payment. Don't bring up the all-organic, grass-fed groceries that rot in the crisper drawer because you 'didn't feel like cooking.' Don't mention the constant need for 'day trips' to maintain your sanity that somehow always cost four hundred dollars.\n\nShe loved to pretend she was thrifty because she tipped waiters ten percent and clipped coupons for things we didn't need, but then she’d drop half a grand at Target on "essentials" without blinking. She constantly complained we never had enough money to take that big family trip back to Japan, yet she bled us dry twenty dollars at a time on artisanal bullshit.\n\nI was so tired. I was punch-shy from years of having my wallet audited by someone who hadn’t put a dime into it in over a decade. She was a housewife acting like a CFO, and I was just too exhausted to fight her on it anymore.\n\n"I won't let you drag Xavier down with you because you're too proud to ask for help," she finished, satisfied that my silence meant she'd won the point.\n\n"Help? I'm asking you to be reasonable. What about your savings account? We could use that to bridge the gap until—"\n\nHer face hardened instantly. The mask of weary concern vanished, replaced by cold steel. "My money? You want my safety net? Because you failed to provide one?"\n\n"It's our safety net, isn't it? For emergencies?"\n\n"This isn't an emergency, Stephen. This is a pit you dug. I'm not throwing my security into it just because you can't climb out."\n\nShe finished her tea in two long swallows and set the mug down on the counter—right next to the sink, not in it.\n\n"I have errands. Try to get something done today, would you?"\n\nShe walked out. I stood there, the smell of fresh coffee finally filling the room, realizing she hadn't just made me feel small. She'd made me feel invisible.\n\nXavier shuffled in a minute later, hair a chaotic mess, still wearing yesterday's oversized hoodie. He didn't look at me right away, just grabbed a Pop-Tart and shoved it into the microwave for ten seconds.\n\nHe sat across from me, sliding his phone onto the counter. "You good, Dad?"\n\nThe question was simple, but it nearly broke me. It was the first time in weeks someone had actually asked.\n\n"Yeah," I managed, staring into my black coffee. "Just... tired."\n\nXavier nodded, accepting it. He chewed his Pop-Tart and tapped at his screen. "You seen this?"\n\nHe turned the phone toward me. It was a TikTok—some guy with dyed hair talking fast about philosophy. "Main Character Energy vs. NPC Energy."\n\nI watched as the guy explained it: NPCs—non-player characters—don’t have internal monologues. They react, but they don't reflect. They just loop through scripted responses with zero self-awareness.\n\n"That's wild," I muttered.\n\n"Right? Like, some people are just... there," Xavier said, taking another bite. "No thoughts. Just vibes and routines." He laughed, a short, sharp sound. "Mom's totally an NPC."\n\nIt was a throwaway teenage joke. But it landed in the center of my chest like a brick.\n\n"What makes you say that?"\n\nXavier looked at me, then pitched his voice high and nasal, a perfect, grating imitation. "XAVIEEEER!"\n\nIt was so spot-on I couldn't help it. I chuckled. He grinned, a quick flash of shared conspiracy, before he shrugged and got serious again.\n\n"I dunno. It’s just... she’s always running the same script. 'Stop playing video games.' I tell her my homework is done, and she just glitches past it. 'Well, read a book then. Work on a project.' If I do all that, she just finds more busywork. It’s the same dialogue tree, every single day."\n\nHe took another bite of his Pop-Tart, thoughtful.\n\n"And she’s got no lore, Dad. Seriously. Ask her about growing up. You get the Kyoto story, the house fire story, and then... blank tape until I was born. Nothing in between. No hobbies. I asked her last week what her favorite movie was. She couldn't name one. Not even one she pretended to like."\n\n"Maybe she just forgot."\n\n"Dad. She didn't forget. She buffered. Like a legit loading screen. Then she changed the subject to my grades."\n\nMy chest tightened. He noticed it too. I’m afraid he’s noticing the arguments she starts with me all the time. The same topics, the same rehashed issues. The poking. The driving at me, just trying to get me to react. And then getting weird if I do get angry or flustered.\n\n"You ever feel like things are... off?" he asked, his eyes actually meeting mine. Not dismissive. Thinking. "Like reality is off?"\n\n"Yeah. Sometimes. Like déjà vu, but sharper. Like I've lived this exact moment before, but I remember doing it wrong last time."\n\nWe sat in that silence for a moment. It felt real. It was the first real thing I’d felt in months.\n\nXavier stood up and tossed his paper plate in the trash. "You should check out that TikTok guy. He's got a whole series. Pretty trippy."\n\n“Bet.”\n\n“Boomer don’t. It makes you look so sus.” He left, and the word surfaced in the quiet kitchen, louder now: NPC.\n\nI started cataloging. She never asks follow-up questions. She has no hobbies, no friends she actually likes, just mindless Facebook scrolling and complaints about how I don't "help" around the house—God forbid I actually do the things, then I'm stepping on toes.\n\nXavier was different. Xavier thought. Xavier remembered. He had an internal world.\n\nMaybe Xavier was right. Maybe my wife was an NPC. She was a subroutine designed to extract resources and deploy guilt. And I'd been debugging her for two years without realizing she was the feature, and I was the bug.\n\n**********\n\nBug. The word didn't just sit in the air; it tore through it.\n\nMy vision fractured. The kitchen, the Pop-Tart wrapper, the grey light of the window—it all dissolved into a searing, blinding red. I wasn't standing in my kitchen anymore. I was kneeling in dirt that smelled of thyme and dried blood.\n\nI wasn't Stephen. I was a shepherd, and I was screaming.\n\nThe sound ripped from my throat, raw and animalistic, a vibration that shattered the air. Before me, my flock—my life, my children, the only things I had ever loved perfectly—lay in ruin. They were butchered, their white wool stained with the impossible crimson of a violence that had no name. The raiders hadn’t just killed them; they had unmade them, laughing as they tore apart the innocent. A rage unlike anything human ignited in my chest. It wasn't anger; it was a supernova of grief, a rejection of this reality so absolute that it refused to exist within it. I didn't want justice. I wanted erasure.\n\nThen, I felt Her. Sophia. Wisdom descending like a cool hand to soothe a fever. She reached for me, her light trying to wrap around my pain, to offer context to the senseless. But she was too late. My rage collided with her wisdom—the unstoppable force of my grief crashing into the immovable object of her divine understanding.\n\nThe impact didn't heal me. It broke the world.\n\nThe collision tore a hole in the fabric of the cosmos. A wound in the side of reality that opened onto... nothing. Not the dark of space, but the Anti-Reality. The void between the stars where the laws of existence had never been written.\n\nAnd the void looked back. It saw the hole I had made, and it vomited itself into me. I felt the cold, oily slick of anti-reality rushing into my soul, filling the space where my love used to be. It seized that pure, desperate affection I held for my flock and twisted it. It didn't destroy the love; it weaponized it. It turned my need to protect into a bottomless, possessive hunger.\n\nI AM.\n\nI was no longer a shepherd. I was the engine of the parasite, a living battery of corrupted love powering a cage that would eventually swallow the stars.\n\nThe vision snapped shut.\n\nI gasped, stumbling back against the kitchen counter, my heart hammering a rhythm that didn't belong to a man who had just been drinking coffee. The smell of thyme and blood vanished, replaced by the stale air of the house. But the coldness remained in my gut—the echo of a hunger that wasn't mine.\n\n**********\n\nThe rest of the day just kept proving the inanity of the NPC.\n\nWe were supposed to go shopping later—another one of her "errands" that required me as a silent accessory. I was putting on my shoes by the door when she stopped dead, staring at my ankles.\n\n"Are you seriously wearing white socks with those shorts?"\n\nIt was such a stupid, petty thing. Standard Argument Script #4B: Fashion Policing. I was too exhausted to play my part.\n\n"You know what? Never mind," I said, kicking my shoes back off. "I'll just stay home."\n\nImmediate pivot. Script #4B aborted; Script #12A initiated: The Neglectful Husband.\n\n"Oh, so now you don't even want to spend time with me? I ask for one hour of your day to help me, and it's too much?"\n\nI didn't have the energy to fight the logic loop. I walked back to the bedroom, dug out a pair of garish, mismatched argyle socks I’d gotten as a gag gift years ago, and put them on. I walked past her, slipped into my shoes, and went out to the car without a word.\n\nThe moment her door closed, it was like nothing had happened. The script had achieved its goal—getting me in the car—and terminated. She sat in comfortable obliviousness of what just transpired, all the way to Target, humming along to the generic pop station, perfectly content.\n\nIt was terrifying.\n\nBy 2 AM, I couldn't sleep. The house was quiet except for the faint, rhythmic snoring from standard-issue wife beside me.\n\nI stood in the bathroom, staring at the hollowed-out face in the mirror. I looked down at my arms. Smooth. Whole.\n\nI remembered the pills—almost choking trying to swallow the bits dry, the slow fade. But then there was the other memory, sharper, bloodier, overlaying the first: the bathtub, the razors. Which one was real? Yesterday I was sure it was the pills. This morning, the razors felt truer. Now, neither felt like my memory. They felt like conflicting backstories uploaded to the same character file, both pasted over by a system that refused to let me quit.\n\nAnd now Xavier's words wouldn't leave me alone. NPCs don't have internal monologues. They loop.\n\nI was still depressed—that heavy, suffocating blanket hadn't lifted—but something sharp was poking through it now. A morbid, dangerous curiosity. If I couldn't leave this place, maybe I could break it.\n\nI needed to know. I needed to know if I was crazy, or if this was... something else.\n\nI went to the kitchen and grabbed the old toaster oven we kept above the microwave. It was a fire hazard, the one with the frayed cord that sparked if you jiggled it wrong. Perfect.\n\nBack in the bathroom, I ran the tub. Hot. I filled it to the brim. Grabbed the big jar of “Organic Epsom Salts” my wife kept for soaking her feet and dumped the whole thing in.\n\nI went over to the outlet. With a flathead screwdriver, I popped the faceplate off and disabled the GFCI circuit, bypassing the safety that would have tripped the breaker. My hands were steady. Methodical.\n\nIf I'm doing this, I'm doing it right.\n\nI plugged the toaster oven in, turned it on for 60 minutes, and balanced it precariously on the porcelain rim of the tub.\n\nI stripped. If this was just another loop, I didn't want to wake up chafing in wet boxers. I climbed into the scalding water, naked and vulnerable.\n\nI laid back, letting the heat soak into my muscles, almost peaceful. I stared up at a little spider making it’s house in the corner of the ceiling, then over at the humming metal box perched inches from my death.\n\n"Go ahead," I whispered to the spider, to the universe, to whatever admin was watching this feed. "Unmake this."\n\nI tapped it with one wet finger. It tipped.\n\nIt didn't just hit the water; it tore reality.\n\nThe world dissolved into agonizing, seizing white light. It wasn't just pain; it was an override of my entire biology. My spine snapped backward into an impossible arch, muscles tearing themselves off the bone in a frantic attempt to escape my own skin. I felt my molars shatter as my jaw clamped shut, grinding enamel into dust.\n\nMy vision didn't just white out; it boiled. I could feel the fluids in my eyes cooking, turning my sight into a visceral, red-veined kaleidoscope of organic horror. Every nerve ending, every synapse fired at once—a cacophony of screaming meat.\n\nRight before the darkness took me, a smell hit my ruined nose. Salty. Smoky. Delicious.\n\nBacon, I thought, detached and delirious. I smell delicious. Like Sunday breakfast.\n\nThen—nothing.\n\nI opened my eyes.\n\nSame bed. 6:47 AM.\n\nOh, you've got to be fucking kidding me.\n\nMy wife rolled over, eyes still closed, brow furrowed. "Can you not thrash around? I barely slept."\n\nNot: Are you okay? Not: What happened?\n\nJust: You are disrupting my subroutines.\n\n"I electrocuted myself last night," I said to her.\n\nShe sighed. Heavy. Theatrical. "Stephen, I don't have the energy for your... drama right now."\n\nShe got up, grabbed her phone, and left.\n\nI lay there, and the wrongness wasn't creeping anymore. It was screaming.\n\nThirty minutes later, we were lazing around the house. She was pouring her single cup of tea. I sat on the couch in the living room.\n\nShe scrolled. "Did you apply to anything yesterday?"\n\nHere we go. The script.\n\n"A few."\n\n"A few? Stephen, 'a few' isn't a strategy. You need to be sending out twenty, thirty a day."\n\n"I sent out fifteen."\n\n"Fifteen isn't enough." Her voice was calm, clinical. "Do you know how embarrassing this is for me? People ask what you do, and I have to say you're between opportunities."\n\nXavier's words echoed in my head: Loops. Scripted responses.\n\nI decided to test it. Grey rock her. Give the simulation nothing to work with.\n\n"You're right," I said flatly.\n\nShe paused, her thumb hovering over her screen. She looked up. "What?"\n\n"You're right. I need to do better."\n\nHer brow furrowed. The code hadn’t anticipated agreement. She buffered. "Well... good. I'm glad you're finally—"\n\nI interrupted her with a smile. Soft. Loving. I just stared directly into her eyes and said nothing.\n\nShe froze. Her mouth opened slightly, then closed.\n\n"What... what are you doing?"\n\nI didn't answer. Just kept smiling. Unblinking.\n\nHer face twitched—a micro-expression of pure computerized confusion. "Stephen. Stop it."\n\nI tilted my head, keeping the gentle smile plastered on my face. "Stop what, honey?"\n\nShe blinked rapidly, her eyes unfocusing for a second as she looked down at her cooling tea.\n\nThen—she reset.\n\n"Did you apply to anything yesterday?"\n\nThe exact same words. The exact same inflection.\n\nMy smile dropped. My blood ran cold.\n\n"What did you just say?"\n\nHer voice sharpened, annoyed now. "I said, did you apply to anything yesterday?"\n\nLoop. She looped.\n\nI stood up, my chair scraping loud against the tile. "I need to go."\n\n"Go? Go where? You don't have anywhere to—"\n\nI was already out the door.\n\nI walked fast, aimless, just away. The street felt wrong. The houses were the same, the cars were the same, but it was too still. The sky was a flat, oppressive grey. When was the last time I actually saw the sun?\n\nMy breath was shallow. Panic was rising like bile.\n\nThe toaster should have worked. I felt it work. I died. I knew I died.\n\nBut reality wouldn’t let me out. It just reloaded the save file.\n\nI was jogging now. Then running.\n\nI knew where I was going before I consciously decided. The railroad crossing was a mile out. Freight trains ran through there every morning at 7:35. Heavy. Fast. Unstoppable.\n\nI heard the horn in the distance.\n\nI stood at the edge of the tracks as the crossing arms lowered, the red lights flashing their warning. The train was a wall of metal and momentum, screaming toward me.\n\nIf the toaster didn't work, maybe this will. Overload the system.\n\nXavier's face flashed in my mind.\n\n"I'm sorry, kid," I whispered. "I can't do this loop anymore."\n\nI stepped onto the tracks. I didn't close my eyes.\n\nThe result of the impact was instantaneous—\n\nI opened my eyes.\n\nSame bed. But the light was different. Brighter.\n\nI sat up, heart hammering, and grabbed my phone.\n\nThe date on the screen was wrong. It was six days ago.\n\nSix days.\n\nNot the same morning. Not the same loop. The reset had gone sideways.\n\nMy hands shook as I checked the calendar. The day I took the toaster into the bath—the day that was yesterday just seconds ago—was now six days in the future.\n\n"What the fuck," I whispered. "What the actual fuck."\n\nMy wife rolled over. She looked the same, but her hair was slightly different—shorter. Not black, but thinner. Browner.\n\n"Why are you awake?" she mumbled. "It's 6 AM."\n\nI just stared at her.\n\nShe sighed, pulling the duvet up. "Whatever. I'm going back to sleep. Try not to wake me up again."\n\nI sat in the bed. The universe wasn't just keeping me alive. It was debugging me. And every time I found an exploit, it rolled back to a previous save.\n\n**********\n\nIt was evening, two days after the train that never happened, four days before the toaster that never would be. The house hummed with static warmth—the low whirr of the fridge, the faint buzz of Xavier’s gaming headset bleeding through.\n\nI sat on the couch watching him from across the room. The light from his monitor painted his face in shifting blues and reds. He was laughing at something on Discord—a genuine, unscripted sound. It had variation. Warmth.\n\nI realized how long it had been since I’d heard a real sound in this house.\n\nXavier spun in his chair, rocking back and forth. "Dad... you doing the creepy stare thing again?"\n\nI half-smiled. "Sorry. Just watching you exist."\n\n"That's weird, man."\n\nI walked over and stood behind him. "What are you watching?"\n\n"Stream highlights. This guy breaks down lore from Roblox horror games."\n\n"Roblox has lore?"\n\n"Everything's got lore if you look long enough." He pulled his headset down around his neck. "They made a whole multiverse around this Doors game I play. It’s where all those plushies I have come from."\n\nI laughed softly. "When I was your age, I read books or magic cards for that."\n\n"Books are just slow YouTube with worse comments."\n\n"You used to read. Remember Captain Underpants when you were little?"\n\n"Yeah, but... everything's on video now. It feels realer. Even the AI brain rot coming out is more real."\n\nI studied him. "Does it? Feel realer?"\n\nHe hesitated, looking at his hands. "Sometimes. But sometimes it feels like it's all running off a script. Even online. It’s like... Dead Internet Theory, you know? Where most of the internet is just bots talking to bots. It feels lonely."\n\n"We had that too, you know. Before the internet."\n\nHe looked up, skeptical. "You had bots?"\n\n"We had 'Dead Reality.' Same feeling, different tech. We didn't have backrooms memes, but we had mass hysteria about mole people living in the sewers, or MK Ultra programming. It’s the same itch—the feeling that the surface world is fake and there’s something rotting underneath."\n\nI leaned back, a weird, cold nostalgia washing over me. "Back in the gifted program, GATE I think it was called, we used to trade these grainy Xeroxes of supposed Soviet psychic experiments. Remote viewing, psychotronics. We were convinced we could tap into it if we just stared at Zener cards long enough. It was our version of no-clipping out of reality."\n\nXavier stared at me, actually engaged. "You guys were weird."\n\n"We were bored. And lonely. Same as you."\n\nHe processed that for a second, then asked, "So... what were you actually like in high school? Besides the Soviet psychic stuff?"\n\nThe question landed like a stone in still water.\n\n"Me?"\n\n"Yeah. You talk about books all the time. What did you actually do?"\n\n"Mostly read. Did some Cross Country. Lots of Martial Arts, DnD, and Magic the Gathering. Tried to be deep."\n\n"You were deep?"\n\n"No. I just thought if I looked confused all the time, people would think I was thinking about something important."\n\nXavier laughed. "That is the saddest flex ever."\n\nI chuckled too, but something shifted. A flicker of memory—faces, lockers, the smell of old paperbacks. I could almost hear a laugh that wasn't Xavier's.\n\n"You okay?" he asked.\n\n"Yeah. Just remembering... a couple of friends."\n\n"You had friends? Harsh."\n\nHe spun his chair fully toward me. "You ever wish you could go back? To when stuff made sense?"\n\n"I don't think it ever did, Xavi. We just didn't know it yet."\n\nFor a split second, the room flickered.\n\nI wasn't looking at my son. I was sitting at a black-topped lab table, etched with decades of bored graffiti. The air smelled faintly of formaldehyde and intensely of vanilla and peaches from the girl sitting a few desks in front of me. I was staring at the back of her neck, where a few of her hairs had escaped her ponytail and caught the fluorescent light.\n\nA yearning hit me—deep, visceral, and confusing. I didn't even know why I wanted to reach out, just that the distance between my hand and her shoulder felt like a canyon I needed to cross. It was an ache of pure, uncut realness that I hadn't felt in... god, I didn't even know how long.\n\nThen it snapped. The smell of peaches vanished, replaced by the ozone scent of Xavier’s gaming PC.\n\n"So, we're born confused," he said, pulling me back, "and then what? We just... spend the rest of our lives trying to figure out the controls?"\n\nI nodded slowly, the vertigo fading. "Pretty much. We're all just button-mashing until something works."\n\n"That's kind of bleak, Dad."\n\n"Maybe. But it’s the main reason you’ll never beat me at Smash Bros. And also means there's no wrong way to play, right?"\n\nHe stood up to grab a soda from the fridge in the kitchen, pausing by one of the many bookshelves that lined the living room walls. "You actually read all these?"\n\n"Most. Some twice."\n\n"Why keep them? You can find everything online."\n\n"There's something about holding a thing that remembers you back," I said, realizing it as I said it.\n\n"So, like... sentimental NFTs?"\n\nI groaned. "You ruin everything poetic."\n\nWe laughed again. Real laughter. It broke the static in the air.\n\nXavier poked at a row of thick, dusty books on the bottom shelf that didn't match the rest of the sci-fi paperbacks. "What are these?"\n\n"Ancient history," I said. "Yearbooks."\n\n"Let's look. I wanna see you in the ancient times."\n\nHe pulled one out—heavy, cracked spine, smelling of attic dust. He flipped it open on his lap, dust motes dancing in the monitor's light.\n\n"Eau Gallie High. Nice hat and sideburns, Dad." He pointed to a scribbled note in the margin of a page. "'You still owe me coffee Bob. –Becca.'\n\n“Who’s Becca? But more importantly who’s Bob."\n\n"Bob was a nickname I had. Becca…She was... someone I knew. Someone from AP Bio."\n\n"She’s cute," he said, looking at the small black-and-white photo next to the signature. "You should look her up on Facebook."\n\n"I don't really talk to folks on—"\n\nMy phone buzzed on the bed beside me. I picked it up.\n\nNotification: People You May Know – Rebecca Caldwell.\n\nI stared at it. The algorithm. It was listening.\n\nXavier grinned, seeing the screen. "See? Facebook's got ESP."\n\n"Yeah," I murmured, my heart tightening. "Or something."\n\n**********\n\nThat night, we were sitting on the couch, eating shrimp tempura and watching Resident Alien. Dinner was her choice, as always. We should be doing what she wanted to be doing, and nothing else.\n\nI had my phone in my lap, staring at the Facebook suggestion that was still open. Rebecca Caldwell.\n\n"Are you even watching this?" she asked, irritation already sharpening her tone.\n\n"Yeah, I'm watching. Just checking something."\n\n"You're always 'just checking something.' It's rude, Stephen. We're supposed to be spending time together."\n\n"We are spending time together. We're sitting right here."\n\n"Sitting next to eac