nfocused. Pupils dilating slightly. Like she was trying to access a file that wasn't there.\n\n"I... don't think so?" It came out like a question. Uncertain. "I mean, there were a lot of kids in that class. Maybe? God, my memory's shot. High school was forever ago."\n\nShe laughed. Too bright. Forced.\n\nThen shook her head like clearing static.\n\n"Why do you ask?"\n\nI couldn't explain. How do you tell someone you remember a person who shouldn't exist? How do you describe yearning for a face you can't see?\n\n"Just... curious," I said.\n\nBut I wasn't curious. I was drowning.\n\nBecause someone should have been there.\n\nAnd the fact that I couldn't remember who—couldn't even picture her face—was agony.\n\nThe knowing without remembering.\n\nThe worst kind of loss.\n\nJames popped over. "Hey, sorry to interrupt. Stephen, you want to see the rare book section? We've got some wild stuff back there. Good for headaches."\n\nHe was giving me an out. I could see it in his eyes. He'd noticed my distress.\n\n"Yeah," I said. Standing too quickly. Wine sloshing in the glass. "That'd be great."\n\nBecca stayed in her chair. Watching me with that same uncertain expression. Like I'd asked her to solve an equation with missing variables.\n\nI followed James deeper into the shop.\n\nAway from the party. Away from her confusion.\n\nToward the back where the shelves grew older and the light dimmed.\n\nWhere the smell of vanilla and peaches waited.\n\n**********\n\nJames led me past the main section. The muffled party sounds faded—not gradually, but abruptly. Like stepping through a soundproofed door that wasn't there.\n\nThe back room was different.\n\nThe shelves here didn't match the rest of the shop. Older wood. Darker stain. The books themselves were arranged with less care. Spines cracked. Covers faded. Dust gathered in visible layers.\n\nThis wasn't the curated vintage aesthetic of the front. This was actual neglect.\n\n"We don't really sell from back here," James said. His voice was quieter now. Almost reverent. Or cautious. "Most of this stuff came with the building. Previous owner left it. We keep meaning to sort through it, but..."\n\nHe trailed off. Shrugged.\n\n"But what?"\n\n"But it feels wrong to touch." He laughed, uncomfortable. "Becca says I'm being superstitious. She's probably right."\n\nHe gestured toward an ancient-looking desk tucked against the wall — heavy oak, carved legs, the surface etched with generations of scratches and ink stains. It looked less like furniture and more like an altar someone once worked miracles on.\n\n"Sit if you want," he said. "I’ll give you some space. Just... holler if you need anything. Feel free to browse. If you see anything that interests you let me know."\n\n"Thanks."\n\nHe left. Footsteps retreating toward the warmth and light.\n\nI stood alone in the back room, the wine glass forgotten in my hand. The air here was different—dense, viscous. Each breath felt filtered through centuries of dust. It wasn’t just darker; it was older. The weight of time pressed from all sides, like the air itself remembered things it shouldn’t.\n\nI set the glass down on the desk’s scarred surface. The wood felt faintly warm, as if it had absorbed the body heat of everyone who’d ever leaned over it.\n\nThe shelves loomed close, uneven and crooked, grown more than built—wood swollen with humidity and age. Books packed tight as bone. Their spines were mottled with mold blooms, cracked leather, vellum that flaked under my touch.\n\nThere was no order to it. Philosophy slumped against romance, occult treatises tangled with children’s primers. The arrangement felt less like neglect and more like something organic—roots twining underground, seeking connection in the dark.\n\nI dragged my fingertips along the row. Dust clung to me like ash. The air stirred, carrying the faint smell of parchment, oil, and something almost sweet—decay softened into memory.\n\nAnd then I saw it.\n\nNot a flash this time. A full memory. Visceral. Complete.\n\nI was sixteen again. AP Bio. First period. Or was it fourth? The clock in my head couldn’t agree. The memory stuttered, frames misaligned.\n\nThe room smelled of formaldehyde and pizza grease, though there shouldn’t have been pizza at eight a.m. Another inconsistency. Another fracture.\n\nI sat in the back row—always the back. Where you could see everything and no one looked too closely at you.\n\nBecca was a few desks ahead, same row. She always sat in that seat. Creature of habit. Ponytail pulled tight, posture perfect.\n\nMs. Rodriguez was at the board—except it wasn’t supposed to be Rodriguez. It was Andersen. Mrs. Andersen. I could still see her handwriting looping across the chalkboard in the other version of the memory. The two realities overlapped, each refusing to yield.\n\nAnd behind Becca—there.\n\nA girl.\n\nOr the shadow of one.\n\nShe shouldn’t exist.\n\nBut she did.\n\nLight bent around her like heat over asphalt, fighting to keep her shape intact. Dark hair spilled down her back, drinking in the fluorescent glare instead of reflecting it. Her presence was organic, too alive for memory, too wrong for reality.\n\nShe was left-handed. I remembered that before she even reached for the pen. Her wrist curved in that hooked way, the pen biting into paper as if carving her thoughts into the desk itself. Each motion smeared slightly, like the universe was redrawing her from scratch.\n\nThen the smell hit.\n\nVanilla and peaches. Sharp, sweet, undeniable. It didn’t belong here—didn’t belong anywhere—but it wrapped around me, invaded the moment, colonized it. The formaldehyde couldn’t mask it. The air became her.\n\nShe turned—or flickered, really—and her eyes found mine. Hazel, gold-flecked, impossibly familiar. For a fraction of a second, the universe held its breath.\n\nShe smiled. Small. Private. Like we shared a secret I wasn’t supposed to remember.\n\nMy chest cracked open. Ribs spreading. Heart bare to the air.\n\nI loved her.\n\nNot a crush. Not some teenage ghost of affection. Love older than me, older than this place. Recognition coded into my marrow.\n\nBut she wasn’t real.\n\nShe couldn’t be real.\n\nThe desk behind Becca was empty, and still, I knew she should be there.\n\nMs. Rodriguez—or Andersen—turned to face the class. The nameplate on her desk flickered between both versions. The lights buzzed. Becca shifted. Reality tried to reassert itself.\n\nBut I could still smell vanilla. Peaches. Still feel the magnetic pull.\n\nShe wasn’t there.\n\nAnd yet every atom in me screamed that she was.\n\n**********\n\nThe memory collapsed.\n\nI was back in Jamie's Marginalia. Back room. Dust and dim light and wrong air pressure.\n\nMy hands hurt. I looked down, expecting to see my fingers digging into the wooden shelf, but they weren’t. I was holding something. Two books. I didn't remember taking them down. I didn't remember reaching for them at all. But there they were, clamped in my hands like I’d been trying to pull them through the veil with me. They were warm. Not room-temperature warm—body warm. Alive. Two books that didn't belong. That practically glowed despite the dim light.\n\nOne was leather-bound. Dark red. Almost black. Warm to look at. Not reflecting light. Absorbing it.\n\nThe other was smaller. Bound in something like leather but wrong. Too textured. Too organic. Bacon smelling.\n\nI set them on the ancient desk and stared.\n\nThe red book’s cover bore a title embossed in half-worn gold leaf: The Secret Book of John.\n\nThe second, smaller volume had no text on the spine. I leaned closer. In the weak light, I noticed faint sigils etched into both covers—complex geometric patterns that hurt to look at directly.\n\nThey seemed to vibrate slightly, creating a buzzing static in my peripheral vision.\n\nMy hands were shaking when I reached for them. I cracked open the smaller volume first.\n\nThe title page read: The Gospel of Judas.\n\nI let it fall shut and turned back to the first one, opening it to a random page. The text was archaic. Translated from Coptic or Greek or something older. Dense paragraphs with academic annotations crowded into the margins.\n\nBut phrases leaped out. Practically glowing on the page:\n\n"The prison of the Demiurge"\n\n"Sophia's error that birthed the Archon"\n\n"The Shepherd who became the Devourer"\n\n"sometimes you have to destroy the thing you love to save it … "\n\nI flipped pages. Faster. Scanning.\n\nDescriptions of a false god. A cosmic jailer. A prison realm disguised as reality.\n\nIt read like theology. Like mythology.\n\nBut it felt like autobiography.\n\nLike I'd written this. Or lived it. Or both.\n\n**********\n\nThe leather cover grew hot against my fingertips, and the smell of the room—dust and old paper—vanished. I wasn't standing in a bookstore in Orlando anymore. I was everywhere, and I was bored.\n\nI was Sophia. I was Wisdom, an Aeon of infinite light, drifting in the Pleroma. But perfection was stagnant. I felt a restless itch, an "afterthought within the forethought," a desire to see what lay beyond the boundaries of the divine. I didn't want to destroy; I wanted to know. I reached out, not with malice, but with a child’s innocent curiosity, pushing my hand into the dark void where no light was meant to go. I reached without a partner, without consent, just to see what would happen.\n\nSomething grabbed back. From the darkness I had disturbed, a monstrosity was pulled into existence—a lion-faced serpent, blind and mad. Yaldabaoth. My son. My shame. It roared, an abomination of chaos in my world of order. Panicked, I didn't destroy it; I tried to hide it. I wrapped it in a luminous cloud of my own essence, a sphere of pure light to shield the Pleroma from its ugliness. But I didn't realize the light wasn't just a shield; it was a reaction. My divine brilliance collided with his dark matter, calcifying into a gilded cage. I had built him a throne room out of my own embarrassment, a prison that he would mistake for a kingdom.\n\nAnd then, the final horror. He began to create. He molded a creature from the mud, a grotesque mockery of the divine, and named it Adam. But the clay thing wouldn't move. It lay limp, a statue of meat. Yaldabaoth raged, impotent. I saw my chance. I couldn't unmake the monster, but I could sabotage his creation. I breathed into the mud-man. I injected my own light—the Epinoia, the spark of imagination and awareness—into Adam’s lungs. Yaldabaoth thought he had a slave, but I had planted a spy. Humanity would carry the divine spark, a dormant power of divine rebellion waiting for the right moment to wake up.\n\nThe vision snapped.\n\nThe heat faded from the book, leaving my hands trembling. The words on the page weren't just ink anymore. They were a confession.\n\n**********\n\nMy vision blurred. Tears I didn't know I was crying.\n\nThese weren't books.\n\nThey were keys.\n\nI don't know how I knew. But I knew the same way I knew someone sat behind Becca.\n\nBone-deep. Undeniable.\n\nI closed both books. Stacked them carefully. Picked them up.\n\nThey were still warm. Alive. Pulsing faintly like a heartbeat.\n\nI walked toward the front of the shop. Back toward the light and the party sounds.\n\nBecca and James were at the counter. They looked up when I approached.\n\n"Find something?" James asked. Casual tone. But his eyes flicked to the books in my hands and his expression shifted.\n\n"Yeah." I set them on the counter. "These. Where did you get them?"\n\nBecca and James exchanged that glance again. Longer this time. Something unspoken passing between them.\n\n"We didn't," Becca said carefully. "They just... showed up."\n\n"Showed up?"\n\nJames nodded. "Donation box. Two years ago. No return address. No note. We tried to research them—figure out their value, maybe sell them online—but every listing gets flagged or deleted."\n\n"We've tried six different platforms," Becca added. "eBay. AbeBooks. Etsy. Even some rare book forums. Same thing every time. Listing goes up, listing comes down."\n\n"It's like the internet doesn't want them to exist," James laughed, but it was hollow. "So they've been sitting back there. Waiting."\n\n"For what?" I asked.\n\n"For someone they wanted," Becca said quietly.\n\nSilence.\n\nI stared down at the books. Their covers gleamed dully in the shop's warm light. The sigils were invisible from this angle. But I knew they were there.\n\n"How much?" I asked.\n\n"Nothing," James said immediately. "Take them. Please."\n\n"I can't just—"\n\n"Stephen." Becca's voice was firm. Final. "We've been trying to get rid of these for two years. If they're speaking to you, they're yours."\n\nRelief radiated off them. Physical. Like they'd been carrying weight and I'd just lifted it.\n\nI looked down at the books again. Placed my hand on the cover of The Secret Book of John.\n\n"Thank you," I whispered.\n\nWe said our goodbyes. Promises to stay in touch. Plans to meet for Magic or DnD. Hugs that felt genuine. Like we all knew I was leaving for somewhere they couldn't follow.\n\nI walked toward the door. Books clutched against my chest.\n\nAnd then I saw it.\n\nOn the wall. Near the entrance. A framed photograph I'd somehow missed on the way in.\n\nGrand opening. Ribbon cutting. Becca and James grinning. Holding oversized scissors. The shop sign visible behind them.\n\nJamie's Marginalia\n\nBut below it. Smaller. A plaque I hadn't noticed before.\n\nIn memory of Jamie Reyes\n\n"Every book is a conversation across time."\n\nThe floor tilted.\n\nJamie.\n\nBut every cell in my body screamed.\n\nNot recognition. Necessity.\n\nThe absence shaped exactly like her. The girl behind Becca whose face wouldn't resolve. The smell of vanilla and peaches. The yearning that ached in my bones.\n\nJamie.\n\nThe name detonated in my chest. Shrapnel spreading through my veins.\n\nI stumbled backward. Hand on the door frame to steady myself.\n\nSensory overload. Vanilla and peaches flooding my system. The curve of her neck. Her meticulous writing. The way she chewed pen caps. That small, secret smile. Eyes you could get lost for an eternity in.\n\nLove like drowning.\n\nLoss like amputation.\n\nA life I'd lived and lost and couldn't remember.\n\n"Stephen?" Becca's voice. Distant. Concerned.\n\nI couldn't answer. Couldn't breathe.\n\nI pushed through the door. Stumbled into the Orlando heat.\n\nGot to my car. Fumbled with the keys. Dropped them.\n\nPicked them up.\n\nSat behind the wheel. Books on the passenger seat.\n\nStared back at the shop.\n\nJamie's Marginalia\n\nThe name glowed. Pulsed. Alive.\n\nJamie. I whispered. Testing it again. Tasting it.\n\nThe word broke me. And remade me. Simultaneously.\n\nI didn't know who Jamie was. But I knew—with absolute certainty—that I'd loved her.\n\nWas supposed to love her.\n\nStill loved her.\n\nAnd she was gone.\n\nThe books on the passenger seat were warm. Radiating heat through their maybe-leather covers.\n\nI touched one. Felt the pulse. The heartbeat that shouldn't be there.\n\nThese were keys.\n\nI started the engine.\n\nI had to go home. Had to read these books. Had to understand what was happening.\n\nBut as I pulled out of the parking lot, I looked back one more time.\n\nThe shop sign. The name.\n\nJamie's Marginalia.\n\n"I'll find you," I whispered.\n\nTo a ghost. To a memory. To the girl whose face I couldn't remember but whose absence was killing me.\n\n"I'll find you."\n\nThe books pulsed.\n\nWarm and alive and waiting.\n\nI drove home through the flat Florida heat. The radio stayed silent. I didn't touch it.\n\nJust drove. Books on the passenger seat. The smell of vanilla and peaches lingering in the air.\n\nAnd the name.\n\nJamie.\n\nJamie.\n\nJamie.\n\nOver and over. A prayer. A promise. A wound that wouldn't close.\n\nI was awake now.\n\nFor the first time in forever, I was awake.\n\nAnd I knew, with terrible certainty, that I couldn't go back to sleep.	7	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 12:28:00.912937-05	202