121	3	8	Chapter 6: Is there Anybody Out There?	The oven door clanged shut and the kitchen filled with that warm, buttery smell of something good.\n\nMy wife had flour on her hands again, hair tied back, humming off-key like nothing was wrong.\n\n“So… what do you want for dinner?” she asked without looking up, elbows deep in a mixing bowl.\n\n“Didn’t you already start something?”\n\n“Yeah, but that’s dessert. Dinner first.”\n\n“Chicken kara-age,” Xavier called out from his desk, not looking away from the glowing screen.\n\nShe sighed. “I can’t ever make food that everyone likes. I swear, I could serve gold and one of you would still ask for ketchup.”\n\nI laughed softly. “I can cook if you want. I’ll throw something together.”\n\nHer hands froze mid-stir. “Oh, so now you’re going to help?”\n\n“Just offering. It looks like you’ve been baking all afternoon.”\n\n“I thought you were too busy looking for jobs to be bothered with the kitchen.”\n\n“That’s not fair,” I said. “I applied to three today.”\n\n“Three? Wow. Should we throw a parade?” She smirked, but it wasn’t playful.\n\nI exhaled, counting the beats. “I’m trying, okay? It’s not like people are lining up to hire mid-life software has-beens.”\n\n“There it is. Always an excuse.”\n\n“It’s not an excuse. It’s the market. It’s—”\n\n“It’s always something, Stephen.”\n\nThe spoon hit the counter harder than it needed to.\nXavier slid his headphones on. Retreat complete.\n\nI leaned back into the couch, staring at nothing. “I just needed to clear my head. That’s why I went to the bookstore.”\n\n“Right. Clear your head by wasting gas and money.”\n\nShe turned, eyes sharp. “Everything with you is ten minutes away. Ten minutes here, ten minutes there. No wonder we can’t save anything.”\n\nI opened my mouth to respond, but what was left to say? The script always ended the same way.\n\nShe kept going, words sharp but oddly empty.\n\n“Do you even care what happens to this family anymore? Or are you just waiting for someone to hand you another fantasy project so you can play genius again?”\n\nI stared at the phone in my hand. The reflection of her in the screen looked distorted, like a puppet moving half a second out of sync.\n\nShe wasn’t done. “I’m serious, Stephen. You sit there all day, scrolling, pretending like you’re thinking deep thoughts, but I don’t see any progress. You say you’re applying for jobs—where? When? Who even calls you back?”\n\nI said, “Did you want to see the emails? I can send a few your way.”\n\n“A few. God, you think that’s enough? You think this house runs on a few?”\n\n“Working on it,” I muttered.\n\n“Working on it,” she mimicked, pitching her voice high and nasal. “That’s your answer to everything. You’ve been ‘working on it’ for two years. I bet you didn’t even update your résumé like I told you to.”\n\n“I did,” I said quietly.\n\n“When? Show me.”\n\nI didn’t look up.\n\nShe scoffed. “Right. That’s what I thought. You probably spent all day daydreaming again, writing those weird stories or talking to your imaginary online friends.”\n\nMy pulse started to throb behind my eyes. I tried to breathe slow, to stay still. It’s like the trip to the bookstore had been completely forgotten.\n\nShe kept circling, each sentence hitting closer, faster, louder. “I just don’t get it. You used to be someone. You used to care. Now it’s like you’ve just… stopped. What happened to you, Stephen? What happened to us?”\n\nHer words tangled. “I mean, maybe this is what you wanted all along—to make me look crazy. To make me the bad guy while you play the martyr. Poor misunderstood Stephen. The genius nobody appreciates. Is that it? Because if that’s it, congratulations. You’ve won.”\n\nI murmured something—maybe “okay,” maybe “I hear you.”\n\nShe didn’t notice. Or didn’t care. “You just sit there like a ghost. You don’t even fight back anymore. At least when you yelled, I knew you still felt something.”\n\nI looked down at the couch fabric, tracing the weave with my thumb. My answers had shrunk to single syllables.\n\n“Sure.”\n\n“Okay.”\n\n“Fine.”\n\nEach one smaller than the last.\n\nShe filled the silence. “You think I like this? You think I enjoy being the only adult here? Do you even realize what it’s like to live with someone who doesn’t try?”\n\nHer voice cracked near the end, a high-pitched quiver that didn’t sound like hurt—more like static.\n\nI stopped answering.\n\nInstead, I picked up my phone, thumbed the screen, scrolling through nothing—Facebook, the narcissistic-survivor forums, dead-bedroom threads, other people’s sadness stacked like trading cards.\n\nSomeone posted a meme about loving people who no longer love you back. Another about stonewalling and emotional fatigue.\nI kept scrolling.\n\nHer voice kept going, a steady drone of accusation and guilt.\nSomewhere between the scrolling and the smell of peaches, I felt it—the question I always come back to.\n\nWhy do I even try?\n\nAn article headline caught my eye: “Why Narcissists Hate Silence.”\nI read the first paragraph twice before the words blurred.\n\nBecause I choose to keep trying to love her.\n\nThe thought didn’t feel noble. It felt tired. But it was true.\nThe oven beeped.\n\nThe air filled with the soft, sweet scent of peach cobbler, sunlight and sugar.\n\nAnd just for a second, everything blurred.\n\nA girl laughing in a hallway. Vanilla and peaches.\n\nJamie.\n\nAnd right behind that scent came the memory of a voice—faint, distant, but real.\n\n“Stephen, it’s Whitney… I found Jay…”\n\nI froze.\n\nThe room fell away. The clatter of dishes became static. The air hummed.\n\nI stared at my phone. The screen flickered once, then twice. No missed call. No notification. Nothing.\n\nBut I knew I’d heard it.\n\nWho are you, Whitney?\n\nFrom across the room, Xavier’s monitor light flashed blue against his face. He looked up, concerned, but didn’t say anything.\n\nThe oven beeped. My wife hummed again.\n\nEverything was normal.\n\nExcept it wasn’t.\n\n**********\n\nAt the same time, somewhere far away yet infinitely close.\n\nThe stars here don’t shine.\n\nThey breathe.\n\nEach pulse rolls through the void like a heartbeat echoing inside glass, colors folding into one another—violet, green, gold, then a shade that doesn’t exist inside human language. The air (if you can call it that) hums with the residue of creation, like a hymn sung backward.\n\nThis is the space outside Etheris.\n\nNot a place, but a wound.\nA hole in the infinite fabric where reality once tried to heal itself and failed.\n\nThe sphere floats at the center of it all—smooth, black, horizonless. From far away, it looks like serenity. Up close, it eats light, memory, and meaning. Etheris. The prison of souls. The great forgetting.\n\nA cosmic tear. A Black Hole. The edge of the contained corruption.\n\nAround it, members of the resistance have gathered. Not an army, but a gathering of old gods and discarded myths. Fragments of beings once worshipped, now burned thin by the ages.\n\nTiamat coils lazily through the void, scales shimmering like oil on water. Each exhale births galaxies that unravel before they can cool.\n\nHecate stands nearby, her three faces half-shadowed by their own moons. Eyes like candles in wind.\n\nChernobog waits in silence, black wings folded tight, his smile carved too deep.\n\nAnd Lady Illkeserod hovers between them all, her body woven from the translucent filaments of thought. A goddess of wisdom who remembers every instant of every memory, back well before rebellion was still possible.\n\nBenji walks beside her, his cardigan flickering between colors that don’t belong to the spectrum. He looks smaller than the others, but older somehow. A survivor who knows what eternity costs.\n\nBenji: “She made it through, Ilkse. I felt her before the lattice collapsed. Whitney got a signal in.”\n\nLady Illkeserod: “A whisper is not a presence, Benji.”\n\nBenji: “No. But she was there. If she can send something into Etheris, we might be able to pull something out.”\n\nHecate’s left face laughs softly. “You speak as if there’s direction in infinity.”\n\nTiamat: “There is pattern, if you know how to listen.” She lowers her head toward the sphere, voice thick and serpentine. “The song of her signal still clings to the membrane. Sweet. Defiant. Almost… human.”\n\nLady Osilari drifts from the starlight, robes of symbols and shadow. “If she escaped, she left a tear. Find the tear, and perhaps the current still carries her.”\n\nChernobog chuckles, a sound like bones tumbling down marble stairs. “Or perhaps the tear carried something else. Something that pretends to be her.”\n\nBenji: “It was her.”\n\nHis voice doesn’t rise, but it doesn’t waver either.\n\nLady Illkeserod: “Even if it was, she’s beyond our reach now. The Akashic Realm can no longer see inside the sphere. When you were ejected, the pathways collapsed. Our sight dimmed.”\n\nThe others turn toward the black sphere. Waves of color ripple across its surface, and for a heartbeat, reflections stir within—fleeting glimpses of rooms, faces, places that don’t belong here.\n\nLady Illkeserod extends a hand. Filaments of light flow from her fingers into the void, touching the sphere’s surface. The feedback shatters her projection into a thousand shards of self. Benji catches her, stabilizing her form.\n\nBenji: “Careful. It’ll consume you.”\n\nLady Illkeserod: “It already is.”\n\nThrough the shimmer, a flicker appears—Stephen’s face, faint, fading, unaware.\n\nHe sits on a couch somewhere small and real.\nScrolling. Searching.\n\nA voice around him scratching his name on a chalkboard, grating and discordant.\n\nThe image ripples and vanishes.\n\nHecate: “He’s remembering.”\n\nLady Illkeserod: “That is what frightens me.”\n\nBenji: “It’s what gives us a chance.”\n\nHe steps closer to the edge of the void, staring at the reflection that no longer shows him anything. The hum of the Akashic flows around them deepens, thousands of overlapping voices whispering through the continuum.\n\nBenji: “If Whitney reached him once, she can again. The memory will guide him.”\n\nTiamat: “And if the memory breaks him?”\n\nBenji: “He prevented Yaldabaoth from escaping twice already. I don’t think another shattered timeline is going to break him now. Not after he got reunited with Jamie last time.”\n\nThe air ripples again—this time with something more violent. A tremor across the unseen web of realities. The sphere grows darker, impossibly darker, until light bends backward around it.\n\nThe gods recoil instinctively.\n\nLady Osilari: “He’s looking for her now. The human. His mind is pressing against the walls.”\n\nLady Illkeserod: “Then we must be gentle. If he pushes too hard, the feedback will collapse both sides.”\n\nBenji: “And if we do nothing?”\n\nChernobog: “Then the cage holds, and the dream continues.”\n\nBenji looks back at the others, then at the dark sphere.\nHe presses his hand to his chest as though steadying something fragile.\n\nBenji: “Whitney risked everything to get through. We must find her.”\n\nThe words hung there, soft and defiant, swallowed by the shimmer of the void.\n\nBenji’s reflection broke into fragments across the black surface of Etheris, each shard a different version of him—one still trapped, one erased, one screaming without sound.\n\nHe glanced toward Lady Illkeserod. Her form was barely holding—lines of blue-white light running beneath translucent skin, a lattice of energy threaded through memory.\n\nLady Illkeserod: “And if we don’t what then? After pulling you out we’ve got no connection back inside any more. If there are any other resets, the prison might shatter, and he. IT might break free.”\n\nBenji: “We’ll find her, Ilkse. Even among infinity there are patterns, flows… pieces of Akashic threads we can pull and follow to the source of that signal.”\n\nLady Illkeserod didn’t respond. Her gaze was fixed on the sphere—on the way it pulsed, alive and dying at the same time.\nReflections crawled across its skin like veins of mercury, showing faces that existed and never existed, fragments of laughter and grief.\nFor a moment, the air itself seemed to hum with the scent of peaches and sunlight.\n\nLady Illkeserod: “That signal… it shouldn’t have been possible. The prison was deleting her. The prison was sealed. Every current was redirected inward. But she forced her way through. She broke the pattern.”\n\nHer voice trembled, not from fear, but awe.\n\nBenji: “And in doing so, she broke the reset. Then, if Whitney hadn’t sent that pulse when she did, I’d be gone. Not ejected. Deleted.”\n\nHe looked down at his hands, flexing them as though testing whether he still existed. “I remember the moment—the silence after the collapse. Then the light. Not white, not black, just knowing. I thought it was death. Then I realized it was her.”\n\nLady Illkeserod: “She pulled you into the in-between. The Purgatory Zone.”\n\nBenji: “The space where deletion bleeds into recursion. I should’ve dissolved there. But your voice cut through the static.”\n\nShe allowed a faint smile. “I couldn’t reach inside, not fully. The edge of Etheris burns even me. But I found the breach—where her signal tore through the fabric. I followed it until I felt you thrashing inside the code.”\n\nHe looked at her, eyes shimmering with faint starlight. “You pulled me out.”\n\nLady Illkeserod: “Barely. I almost lost half my realm stabilizing the conduit. But it worked.”\n\nBenji: “Then we owe her everything.”\n\nThe Akashic field rippled—slow at first, then pulsing in sync with their words.\n\nBeyond them, Tiamat coiled through a nebula of gold fire, muttering ancient equations.\n\nHecate stood motionless, her three faces whispering in unison.\n\nChernobog crouched on the edge of the nothingness, a patient smile slicing through shadow.\n\nEven gods, for a breath, seemed to listen.\n\nTiamat: “You speak of her as if she were divine.”\n\nBenji: “Maybe she is.”\n\nChernobog: “Or maybe she’s another contagion from inside the sphere. One that will rot your threads the way it did the last time you meddled.”\n\nLady Illkeserod’s eyes flared white. “Enough. She acted when none of you would. She cut through the suppression matrix that has kept those souls asleep for eons. For the third time in endless cycles, the reset faltered.”\n\nHecate: “A pause is not freedom.”\n\nBenji: “But it’s a start.”\n\nThe gods fell silent again. The hum of the Akashic streams rose around them, carrying whispers from the deep layers of existence—echoes of Stephen’s voice, indistinct but alive, reaching upward like a child calling in sleep.\n\nLady Illkeserod reached toward the void again. The blackness rippled, resisting her touch. This time, she didn’t withdraw.\n\nLady Illkeserod: “Do you feel that? The vibration?”\n\nBenji: “Yes. The human is stirring. His memories are trying to realign.”\n\nThrough the darkness, faint light—an image again: Stephen on the couch, still scrolling a phone, the scent of peaches around him. The memory of a girl he can’t name.\n\nLady Illkeserod: “Jamie.”\n\nThe word slipped out before she could stop it, and the sphere reacted. The light twisted, bending toward her, reaching, hungry.\n\nBenji grabbed her wrist, pulling her back as cracks of blue fire laced through the void. “Careful, Ilkse. It’s listening.”\n\nShe steadied, breathing out a flare of golden light. “It always is.”\n\nThe Akashic currents pulsed once, brighter this time, running through the rebel gods like a shared heartbeat. The colors washed over them—crimson, cobalt, violet—and then dimmed again to nothing.\n\nTiamat folded her wings. “Even among infinity, the pattern changes. The reset stuttered once. It may again.”\n\nBenji: “It will. Because she’s still out there. Whitney’s signal didn’t break the prison—it just broke through. She’s alive somewhere out here, beyond the outer flow.”\n\nLady Illkeserod: “Then that’s where we begin.”\n\nThe others turned away one by one, dissolving into mist and motion.\n\nTiamat’s tail left a streak of starlight as she vanished.\n\nHecate’s three faces winked out in alternating phases.\n\nChernobog smiled once more before folding into his own shadow.\n\nThe Akashic field quivered, then stilled.\n\nBenji stood beside Lady Illkeserod, both staring at the sphere’s black heart.\n\nBenji: “We’ll find her, Ilkse.”\n\nShe nodded, but her expression stayed distant, haunted by something even she couldn’t name.\n\nThe hum faded.\n\nThe currents subsided.\n\nAnd all across the infinite multiverse, the echo of Whitney’s signal continued to drift—thin, persistent, and alive.\n\n**********\n\nAt the edge of the Akashic flow, seeping into Etheris time folds in on itself. It’s months ago, yet it still is now.\n\nThe streams shimmer like liquid memory, carrying whispers between infinities.\n\nFor an instant, Lady Illkeserod’s outstretched hand finds the right vibration—the single harmonic that bridges thought and matter. Forward and backward through time.\n\nShe finds it, the time when the pulse ripped through.\n\nThe sphere ripples. A thread of light unfurls through it, too thin to see but heavy enough to shift gravity itself.\n\nInside the human world, somewhere far below, Stephen exhales.\n\nA pulse of vertigo hits him—like the air has forgotten how to breathe.\n\nFor the briefest moment, he feels it: the weightless pull of something vast pressing through him. Not pain… not quite. More like being remembered. He reaches for the phone his wife hands him.\n\nIn a dark corner of a twisted reality somewhere lost in the multiverse, Whitney drifts—flailing, weightless, blind. There’s nothing to hold onto, nothing to name. Just the slow spin of her own breath and the cold hum of the void.\n\nShe tumbles through it for what feels like forever… and then something shifts. A strange stillness.\n\nFreedom.\n\nAwakening.\n\nThe sense that the dream was never hers to begin with.\n\nBut fear crawls up through her, raw and childlike. Her hands reach for someone—her father. The memory stings: his voice, sharp and heavy; his eyes that disowned her and wanted her dead. She recoils… then another face bleeds through the dark.\n\nStephen.\n\nIt flickers—who? Friend, both, neither, everything, nothing. The edges blur like wet ink.\n\nAnd then it hits her all at once.\n\nJamie. Stephen. The truth buried beneath both names.\n\nHer prayer cuts through the void, trembling but clear:\n“Stephen… it’s Whitney. I know where Jamie is. I know who Jamie is. I know who both of you are. Please help. Come find me.”\n\nFor the briefest moment, he feels it—something vast moving through him, like being seen from the inside out.\n\nNot pain. Not quite.\n\nMore like being slotted.\n\nA sudden, violent alignment as the world snaps back into place.\n\nThe line goes dead. He drops the phone.\n\nLady Illkeserod backs away from the edge and visualizes where the pulse came from.\n\nAnd in the darkness, Whitney suddenly feels hope.\n\n**********\n\nIt is now again. Back inside Etheris.\n\nThe house is silent now.\n\nThe argument has long since burned itself out, and with it, the afternoon. All that's left are the radish hues leaking through the blinds like some disembodied harvest.\n\nIt’s the same sunset, but something underneath has changed.\nThe air buzzes faintly, the way electronics do before they die.\n\nI’ve been sitting at my desk now for hours, tapping away at the keyboard, fans whining.\n\nBrowser tabs stack like gravestones—searches for Whitney Carson, Whitney Willard, Whitney occult forum, Whitney Florida Wicca… endless permutations of a name that both exists and doesn’t.\n\nEach time I get close—when an old post flickers, or a profile picture half-loads—the screen glitches. Letters rearrange themselves. Results vanish.\n\nI scroll, reload, scroll again. The glow paints my face a tired blue.\n\nStephen, it’s Whitney… I found Jay…\n\nThe memory echoes again, half-audible, half-felt.\nI click through an archived message board: “Do you remember the ones who got out?”\n\nThe link opens for a second, then collapses into 404 errors. 502 server errors. DNS errors.\n\nI lean closer, “Come on. Just one trace…”\n\nThe power flickers.\n\nThe cursor freezes mid-blink.\n\nThen a faint distortion appears across the screen—an image, maybe, or a reflection.\n\nI tilt my head.\n\nFor a heartbeat, it looks like some giant eyeball overlapping my own.\n\nA knock of metal on wood—\n\nMy wife, standing in the doorway, arms crossed, eyes blankly pleasant.\n\n“You’ve been at that computer all night,” she says.\n\n“I’m just looking up something.”\n\n“Something. Right. Because that’s productive.”\n\n“It’s research,” I mutter.\n\nHer tone hardens instantly. “Research? You mean wasting electricity chasing ghosts? You told me you were job searching.”\n\nI look up at her over the monitors. “I didn’t tell you anything.”\n\n“Exactly,” she replies too quickly, like a response drawn from a script. “Because you never tell me anything until I have to drag it out of you. And now the power bill’s through the roof, and we’re behind again, but sure, let’s keep feeding your obsessions.”\n\nI study her face—how her smile flickers a frame too slow, how her eyes don’t quite blink together.\n\n“Why do you care how long I’m on the computer?”\n\n“Because it’s mine too,” she says automatically. Then louder, “Because you don’t listen!”\n\nShe crosses the room. The smell of something wrong trails behind her.\n\nHer movements are jerky, as if reality has forgotten how joints work.\n\n“Turn it off, Stephen. Right now.”\n\n“I just need a few minutes. Please.”\n\n“You’re using too much power.”\n\nThe lights dim again. The cursor on the screen multiplies, hundreds of blinking eyes.\n\nI try to ask her to be reasonable. “It’s important.”\n\nHer voice flattens to monotone. “You’re wasting power.”\n\n“Just a minute.”\n\nShe reaches out—grabs the corner of the computer underneath the desk…\n\nAnd a surge of static jumps between them, bright enough to sting.\n\nThe monitors flashes white.\n\nFan screams.\n\nSmoke curls from the back vent.\n\nShe jerks her hand back, eyes wide. “What the heck did you do?”\n\nI blink at her. “I didn’t—”\n\n“You always break things! Every time I turn around, something else is fried or crashed or corrupted. What were you even doing?”\n\n“Nothing,” I say, too fast. “It just—glitched. I’ll get it fixed.”\n\nHer voice snaps sharp again. “With what money, Stephen?”\n\n“We’ve still got the warranty. We got this right before the layoff two years ago.” I say, standing slowly. “It’s almost out, but still good. I’ll take it in tomorrow. I know someone there.”\n\nShe stares at me like she’s trying to read a line that isn’t written yet. “You always ‘know someone.’”\n\n“I do,” I say softly. “Gil. Best Buy.”\n\nFor a moment, something in her expression flickers—confusion, then recalibration. The same blank reboot he’s seen before.\n\n“Oh,” she says finally, voice smoothing out. “Fine. You handle it.”\n\nShe turns and drifts back toward the other side of the house, back to our bedroom. She starts to hum again, same broken tune, same single note circling itself into nothing.\n\nI exhale and look down at the dead monitors.\n\nBecause I’m still trying to love her.	8	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 12:28:17.511922-05	2026-03-05 19:39:33.719335-05	\N	69	\N	\N
122	3	9	Chapter 7: Placental Lies	The Best Buy smelled.\n\nNot just old—though it was that too, the kind of stench that settles into carpet fibers and never leaves. It smelled like a place that had stopped trying. Like it knew.\n\nI hefted the desktop tower onto the Geek Squad counter, the ancient PC case making a dull thunk against the laminate. Three of the fluorescent panels overhead were dead. The ones still working buzzed like dying wasps.\n\n"Holy shit, Stephen, is that you?" Someone said from the back of the store.\n\nGil looked different. Heavier around the middle, grayer at the temples, but his eyes still had that spark. That old "I see the magic under the surface" spark we'd all had back in high school before the world beat it out of most of us.\n\n"Hey, man." I tried to smile. Failed. "Yeah. It's me."\n\nHe came around the counter and we did the awkward half-hug thing guys do when they haven't seen each other in years. He smelled like energy drinks and electronics cleaner.\n\n"Damn, Stephen. How long's it been? Ten years?"\n\n"Something like that."\n\nHe stepped back, taking in my rumpled shirt, the bags under my eyes. "You look like shit, brother. What happened?"\n\nI gestured at the tower. "This happened. Or… my wife happened. To this."\n\nGil's eyebrows went up. "She… attacked your computer?"\n\n"It's complicated." I lowered my voice, even though the store was empty except for a bored cashier at the far end picking at her phone. "When I get close to finding anything about what I’m searching for, she threatens divorce. When I drop it, she promises… other things. That never happen."\n\nThe words came out flat. Tired. I'd stopped being angry about it somewhere around the third loop.\n\nGil winced. "Damn, dude. That's… I'm sorry." He pulled the tower toward him, started logging it into the system. "So what happened? Spill something on it?"\n\n"She poured coffee into the top vent. While it was running." I really couldn’t tell him the truth for fear he’d short circuit or something.\n\n"Jesus."\n\n"Yeah."\n\nHe typed slowly, hunting and pecking. The keyboard was missing two keys. "And you need the data off it?"\n\n"I need to keep searching. For Whitney."\n\nHe glanced up. Something flickered across his face. Confusion, maybe. "Whitney?"\n\nMy chest tightened. "Yeah. Whitney. You remember her, right? From high school?"\n\nGil's face went blank. Not thoughtful-blank. Empty-blank. Like I'd just asked him to recall a dream from fifteen years ago.\n\n"Whitney," he repeated slowly, testing the word. "No, man. Doesn't ring a bell. You sure she went to our school?"\n\nMy chest tightened. "Yeah. She was in our grade. Had that leather journal she carried everywhere. Always wore black nail polish."\n\nGil shook his head, genuine confusion on his face. "Sorry, dude. Still drawing a complete blank."\n\nThe fluorescent lights buzzed louder.\n\nI swallowed. Tried a different angle. "Okay. Okay, what about... Jamie?"\n\nHis face twitched.\n\nNot a normal twitch. His whole expression froze mid-formation, like a video buffering. His mouth opened slightly. Closed. Opened again.\n\n"Ja..." He blinked hard. "Jam... who?"\n\nThe lights flickered. Once. Twice.\n\nGil physically recoiled, one hand going to his temple. "Sorry, man. My brain just... 404'd. What did you say?"\n\n"Jamie. Do you remember anyone named Jamie from high school?"\n\nHis eyes unfocused. His hand pressed harder against his head. "I... there's..." He winced. "It’s like there's supposed to be something there that isn’t. Like, my head hurts trying to think about it. That's weird, right?"\n\nThe door chimed.\n\nWe both looked up.\n\nTwo people walked in. The guy and girl I'd seen at Jamie’s Marginalia. Andrew and Morgan. They moved in that same slow, deliberate way, like they were wading through invisible water.\n\nThey walked directly to the smart home display.\n\nAndrew picked up a box. Looked at it. Put it down.\n\nThey both turned and walked toward us.\n\nMy pulse kicked up. "Gil—"\n\n"Yeah, they come in all the time," he said quickly, too quickly, still rubbing his temple. "Went to high school with us. Andrew and Morgan. You remember them, right?"\n\nI watched them approach. "No. Not really."\n\n"Huh." Gil's voice had gone flat. Distant. "Well, they did."\n\nI watched the door swing shut behind them. "I saw them in Orlando last week. At James and Becca’s bookshop. They did the exact same thing. Picked up a book, put it down, left. Seems like everyone remembers them but me."\n\n"Maybe they're just indecisive." Gil laughed. It sounded hollow. "They're NPCs, man."\n\n"What?"\n\n"Non-player characters." He tapped the side of his head. "Background people. They don't have inner monologues. No real thoughts. They're just… running a script."\n\nIt was almost as if he was repeating what Xavier told me before. Word for word.\n\n"You can tell?"\n\n"Sure. You just… you learn to see it. Who's real and who's furniture." He pulled a work order slip from under the counter, started filling it out by hand. "Most people are furniture, Stephen. Just… there to fill space."\n\nThe buzzing got louder. Or maybe it was just in my head.\n\nGil paused, pen hovering over the form. "Speaking of people who... forgot. Did you ever hear what happened to Patrick?"\n\n“Patrick? No. Why?"\n\n"That whole amnesia thing he had senior year was crazy. Like, completely blanked on huge chunks of his life." Gil shook his head. "But after he graduated, he got into the Air Force. Straightened himself out, I guess. Last I heard..." He trailed off, looking uncomfortable.\n\n"Last you heard what?"\n\n"He was shot down. Training exercise over Panama. They never found the wreckage. Just... gone." Gil cleared his throat. "Anyway. Been like five years now."\n\nMy mouth went dry. Patrick. Dead. Or missing. And I hadn't even known.\n\n"Let me take this to the back," Gil said, hefting the tower like he wanted to escape the conversation. "See what I can salvage."\n\nI nodded, not trusting my voice.\n\nHe disappeared through the "Employees Only" door. I stood there, staring at the empty store. At the clearance stickers on the sparse shelves. At the flickering lights.\n\nNPCs.\n\nThe door swung open and Gil emerged, but he wasn't holding paperwork. He was holding a single card.\n\n"Almost forgot," he said, grinning. "Speaking of old magic. Check out what I scored."\n\nHe handed it to me.\n\nIt was a Magic: The Gathering card. The art showed a figure stepping between realities, one foot in shadow, one in light. The figure's face was obscured, but something about the posture felt… familiar.\n\nVeilwalker\n\nI read the text box.\n\nThis card cannot be removed from the battlefield. If Veilwalker is ever alone, exile it and lose the game.\n\nThe words didn't just register in my brain. They registered somewhere deeper. Somewhere that recognized the pattern, the rule, the truth behind the game mechanic.\n\nI felt it before I heard it.\n\nA thrum.\n\nSilent. Invisible. But undeniable. Like a tuning fork struck deep in my chest cavity, vibrating outward through my ribs, through the air, through the walls of this dying Best Buy and out into the cosmos beyond.\n\nThe fluorescent lights stopped buzzing. Suddenly all the lights above remembered they were supposed to be working.\n\nThe air shifted. Not temperature. Something else. Like reality had skipped a frame and reloaded slightly wrong.\n\nI looked up.\n\nAndrew and Morgan were standing at the counter.\n\nNot approaching. Just... there. Like they'd always been there. But I'd watched them leave. Watched the automatic doors hiss shut behind them.\n\nGil didn't seem to notice anything strange. "Hey guys. Find what you needed?"\n\n"Hey Stephen," Andrew said. His voice had weight now. Presence. Not the flat affect from before. "Hey Gil."\n\nMy skin prickled. "Andrew."\n\n"You're wrong about Patrick," he said, like we'd been mid-conversation. Like the last ten minutes hadn't happened.\n\nGil blinked. "What?"\n\n"Patrick. You said he was dead. Shot down over Panama." Andrew's eyes were focused. Sharp. "He's not dead."\n\nMorgan nodded. "He lives out in BFE. Way out past Christmas."\n\n"Christmas, Florida?" I asked.\n\n"Yeah. Got himself a whole compound or something. Real eccentric." Morgan leaned against the counter. "Living off his Purple Heart and VA benefits. Went full hermit after he got out."\n\nGil was staring at them. "How do you... I didn't think you guys knew Patrick."\n\n"We didn't really," Andrew said. "But we knew his girlfriend. Or... ex-girlfriend? It was complicated."\n\nMy heart stopped. "Girlfriend?"\n\n"Yeah. Whitney, I think her name was." Andrew looked at Morgan. "That right?"\n\n"Whitney," Morgan confirmed. "They were together for a while. Then she just... disappeared. I think that's what messed Patrick up so bad."\n\nGil's face had gone blank again. "Whitney? Who's Whitney?"\n\nAndrew frowned. "Patrick's girl. You really don't remember her?"\n\n"No clue, man."\n\nI was still holding the card. The Veilwalker. My hands had started shaking.\n\nAndrew turned to me. "You should go see him. Patrick. If you're looking into old high school stuff." He pulled out his phone, tapped at it. "I've got his address somewhere. Let me send it to you."\n\n"I... yeah. Okay." I fumbled for my phone.\n\nThe text came through. An address. Way out in the sticks, past where civilization stopped pretending.\n\n"Thanks," I managed.\n\nAndrew nodded. He and Morgan turned and walked away. This time I watched them all the way to the door. Watched them leave. Watched the doors close shut.\n\nGil was staring at me. "You okay, man? You just... went somewhere."\n\n"Yeah." My voice sounded far away. "Yeah, I'm good."\n\nBut I wasn't.\n\nBecause somewhere out there, I knew someone had just felt that vibration too.\n\nAnd they were searching.\n\n**********\n\nThe thing had too many teeth.\n\nThat was Benji's first thought as he rolled under its swipe, the claws missing his face by inches. Not just a lot of teeth. Too many. More than could fit in a mouth that size, packed in like tumors, each one pulsing with its own wet heartbeat. They grew from the gums in clusters, spiraling inward toward a throat that opened into somewhere else entirely.\n\n"Left!" Lady Illkeserod's voice cut through the chaos.\n\nHe pivoted, bringing his blade up in a wide arc. The weapon wasn't really a blade—it was energy shaped like the memory of a blade, pulled from the raw substrate of this place. It cut through the beast's leg, and the limb didn't just sever. It unraveled. Muscle peeled away from bone in wet ribbons, revealing something underneath that looked like exposed nerve endings woven into lattices of tooth stubs. The thing screamed in frequencies that made his bones ache, made his teeth feel loose in his gums.\n\nBlack ichor sprayed across the ground. Where it landed, the surface bubbled and grew tiny mouths that gasped for air before sealing shut.\n\nTwo more shadows peeled off the ground that wasn’t really ground, manifesting from the dark energy that swirled around them like amniotic fog. But they weren't emerging from the darkness. They were being born from it, pulling themselves through membranes of congealed space that tore with wet, ripping sounds.\n\nBenji counted appendages. Legs that bent wrong. Eyes in places eyes shouldn't be—embedded in joints, blinking from the hollows of throats, weeping a clear fluid that hissed when it hit the ground.\n\nThe air itself felt wrong here. Thick. Viscous. Like breathing through placenta.\n\n"How many more?" he shouted.\n\n"Does it matter?" Lady Illkeserod stood at the edge, her form flickering between states. Sometimes she was solid. Sometimes she was just the idea of solid, a sketch of a woman drawn in the static between realities. Right now she looked annoyed, which was never good.\n\nThe nearest beast lunged at her, all teeth and hunger and impossible geometry.\n\nShe raised one hand.\n\nThe shadow didn't explode. It