g that looked like someone had crossbred a sextant with an orrery and then drowned it in alchemical mercury. Rings within rings rotated around a central eye that wasn't glass or crystal but something that watched. Symbols crawled across its surface like living things, each one burning with a different color of fire that left afterimages in the air.\n\nA line of pure white light had carved itself across the eye's surface, pulsing.\n\n"A signal," Benji said, his hands already working the outer rings before his brain caught up. Old instincts. Muscle memory from lifetimes of tracking things that didn't want to be found. "A strong one."\n\nThe device was warm in his hands. Too warm. Like holding something freshly alive.\n\nHe rotated the outer ring. The symbols reconfigured, sliding past each other in combinations that would've made Euclidean geometers weep. The line of light shifted, bent, angled itself like an arrow pointing into the deep dark below them.\n\nThe eye in the center dilated.\n\nLady Illkeserod leaned in, close enough that he could feel the comfort of her radiating against his skin. "Direction?"\n\n"Deep." His fingers danced over the burning sigils, careful not to touch the ones that whispered. "Very deep. It's coming from…"\n\nHe paused, double-checking the configuration. The symbols had aligned into a pattern he recognized. A warning pattern.\n\n"A god null zone."\n\nLady Illkeserod’s expression went flat. "Of course it is."\n\nGod null zones were… unpleasant. Pockets of reality where magic still existed in its raw, primal form, but where gods couldn't walk. Couldn't manifest. Couldn't even observe without risking dissolution. The metaphysics got complicated, something about faith-structures and belief-matrices, but the short version was simple: gods go in, gods don't come out.\n\n"Could be a trap," Lady Illkeserod said.\n\n"Could be her."\n\nThey both stared at the white line.\n\nBenji had been searching for three hundred and sixty seven eternities. That's how time worked out here—it didn't, really, but you still felt it pass. Felt the weight of searching, of coming close, of losing the trail. Of circling uncountable realities out here in the multiverse, these grey purgatories where forgotten things went to dissolve, waiting for Whitney to wake up enough that he could find her.\n\nThree hundred and sixty seven eternities of almost.\n\n"It has to be Whitney," he said quietly. "That vibration… it felt like a beacon. Like she's calling."\n\n"Or something wants us to think that."\n\n"Ilkse—"\n\nShe turned to face him fully. Her eyes were ancient. Sad. "You know I can't go in there with you."\n\n"I know."\n\n"The null zones would unmake me. Peel me apart layer by layer. I'm too… present. Too manifest." She reached out, placed her hand on his head. Not lover's touch. Not quite mother's either. Something in between. Creator and created. "But you… you're still mostly incorporeal. Mostly."\n\nHe leaned into the touch for a moment, then stepped back. "Then I guess I'm going."\n\n"Benji—"\n\n"She's down there, Ilkse. I can feel it." His hand went to his weapon, or where his weapon would be when he needed it. "And after so many eternities, I'm not losing the trail now."\n\nThe ground shuddered beneath them. Not from danger. From the current of the Akashic Flow shifting, responding to the vibration that still echoed through everything.\n\nLady Illkeserod looked at the device again, at the white line pointing down into the dark places between realities.\n\n"If you die in there," she said, "I won't be able to pull you out."\n\n"If I don't go, we lose her."\n\nSilence. The kind that felt like a decision being made.\n\nFinally, Lady Illkeserod nodded. "Find her. Bring her back. And if it is a trap…"\n\n"I'll spring it carefully."\n\nShe almost smiled. Almost.\n\nBenji moved to the edge, staring down into the swirling dark. Somewhere below, in a place where gods feared to tread, Whitney was waiting. Or something was.\n\nThe white line pulsed.\n\nHe jumped.\n\nThe darkness swallowed him whole, and Lady Illkeserod was left alone, watching the space where he'd been, her hand still raised as if in blessing.\n\nOr farewell.\n\n**********\n\nWhitney's lungs burned with every breath.\n\nShe ran between the trees, boots kicking up ash that refused to settle, that hung in the air like it was waiting for permission to fall. Behind her, the fire beasts howled—a sound that started as hunger and ended as broken mathematics.\n\nThe trees were wrong.\n\nNot just alien. Wrong. Their bark had a metallic sheen, like copper left to oxidize in something other than oxygen. They didn't sway in the wind because there was no wind. They pulsed. Slow. Rhythmic. Like lungs drawing breath through wood and sap.\n\nWhere her shoulder brushed one, she felt it exhale.\n\nThe ground beneath her feet wasn't ground. It was too soft, too yielding. Scar tissue stretched over something vast and sleeping. Each footfall left an impression that didn't quite fill back in, like running across the surface of a healing wound.\n\nThe sky above was a bruised purple that never changed. No sun. No stars. Just that constant, sickly twilight that made her eyes ache if she looked at it too long.\n\nThe howl came closer.\n\nShe risked a glance back.\n\nThree of them. Maybe four. Hard to tell when they moved like that—bodies made of flame and solidified rage, shapes that couldn't decide if they were canine or something older, something that had learned to hunt before the concepts of mammals were ever conceived. Their eyes were white-hot points of hunger, and where their feet touched the ground, the scar tissue sizzled and wept clear fluid.\n\nHer lungs burned. Not just from running. From breathing the air here, which tasted like spoiled eggs and burnt hair and something sweet that made her want to gag.\n\nA root caught her foot.\n\nShe went down hard, chin bouncing off the ash-ground, stars exploding behind her eyes. The howls became screams of triumph.\n\nNo. No, not like this.\n\nHer hand found something solid. A branch. Or what passed for a branch here—it was heavy, gnarled, still warm like it had just stopped being alive. She rolled onto her back, gripping it with both hands.\n\nThe first beast lunged.\n\nShe swung.\n\nThe impact jolted up her arms, and the thing's head snapped sideways with a crack like breaking glass. It stumbled, flames guttering, and she was already moving, scrambling to her feet, bringing the branch around for another strike.\n\nIt caught the second beast in the ribs. The fire dimmed where she hit it, revealing something underneath. Something with too many bones.\n\nThe third came from the side.\n\nShe dropped low, letting it sail over her, then drove the branch upward like a spear. It punched through the thing's belly—if it had a belly—and the fire went out all at once. The beast collapsed into ash and something else, something that hissed as it touched the ground.\n\nTwo left.\n\nShe was already moving, not thinking, just reacting. The branch was an extension of her arms. Eighty years of surviving in places like this had taught her that thinking got you killed. You moved. You struck. You survived.\n\nThe fourth beast died with her makeshift weapon through its throat.\n\nThe last one circled, wary now. Intelligent. That was worse.\n\nWhitney stared at it, breathing hard, blood from her split chin dripping onto her torn shirt. Her muscles screamed. Her hands were blistered from gripping the branch.\n\nThe beast feinted left.\n\nShe didn't fall for it.\n\nIt lunged right, and she was ready. She stepped into the attack, dropping the branch and grabbing the thing by what passed for its neck. The fire burned her palms, but she didn't let go. She twisted, using its momentum, and drove it face-first into the nearest tree.\n\nThe metallic bark split. Something black oozed out.\n\nThe beast thrashed once, twice, then went still. The flames died, leaving only ash.\n\nWhitney collapsed against the tree, shaking. Her palms were red, blistered, but she'd live. She always lived.\n\nThat's when she saw it.\n\nPast the trees. Hidden in the pulsing shadows.\n\nA structure.\n\nShe pushed off the tree, legs threatening to give out, and stumbled toward it. The closer she got, the clearer it became. Stone. Dark stone, almost black, covered in carvings. Sigils. Markings that hurt to look at directly, like they were carved in dimensions her eyes couldn't fully process.\n\nThe entrance was open. No door. Just a threshold.\n\nShe hesitated for half a second, then stepped inside.\n\nThe air changed immediately. Cooler. Quieter. The pulsing of the trees faded to a distant throb. The walls were covered in more symbols, layered over each other like graffiti from civilizations that had never existed.\n\nIt was empty. Abandoned. Safe.\n\nWhitney moved to the far corner, sank down with her back against the wall. Her hands were still shaking. She forced them to stop.\n\nThis was what she'd been looking for. Not this specific place, but any place. Somewhere she could stop. Somewhere she could sleep.\n\nBecause sleep wasn't just rest here. It was transportation.\n\nShe closed her eyes, forcing her breathing to slow. Four counts in. Hold. Four counts out. The technique she'd learned in the void, that black empty nothing after the deletion failed and spat her out sideways into… wherever this was.\n\nThe dream walk wasn't a metaphor. It was a practiced skill. A learned thing. You found a safe space, you made your body sleep, and then you walked out of your flesh and into the spaces between realities. Once you crossed over, you’d pull your body through with you.\n\nMost people couldn't do it. Their consciousness was too anchored. But Whitney had been unmoored. Deleted. Ripped from the only reality she ever knew and left drifting. She'd been forced to learn, or would have face being dissolved into the static.\n\nHer breathing slowed.\n\nThe symbols on the walls began to glow. Faint. Purple-white.\n\nShe felt the pull downward. Not sleep. Deeper than sleep. The space where dreams touched other dreams, where consciousness could slip between the cracks.\n\nAnd then she felt something else.\n\nA vibration.\n\nNot in her body. Through it. Like someone had struck a tuning fork in another universe and the sound had traveled here, impossibly, finding her.\n\nShe knew that feeling. She'd felt it before, faint and distant. But now it was strong. Clear. A beacon.\n\nThe sigils on the walls pulsed in time with it.\n\nWhitney's eyes were closed, but she could see anyway. See the light building beneath her sleeping form. See the portal opening, drawn by the resonance between the vibration and the ancient markings carved into this forgotten place.\n\nSomeone's calling.\n\nHer last conscious thought before the dream walk took her was a name.\n\nNot a name she knew. But one she remembered anyway.\n\nStephen.\n\n**********\n\nCrossing between waking and dreams is never easy. This time she’s holding a deck of cards. Black nail polish chipped. A faint bandage around her thumb. “Draw phase,” she says.\n\nThe light stutters.\n\nNot flickers—stutters.\n\nSound stretches thin.\n\nA voice somewhere: duuuude, yoooou caaaan’t—\n\nThen silence, sticky and wrong.\n\nHer hand locks above the deck. The air hums like a dying hard drive.\n\nConcrete splitting open, neat as paper. A courtyard turning inside out.\n\nUnderneath—something alive. Wet scaffolding, veins pulsing like cables.\n\nA sky made of mouths.\n\nCrucifixes of light hanging upside down, spinning like wind chimes.\n\nHer nose fills with sugar and rot.\n\nEveryone’s eyes are bleeding.\n\nThe cards in her hand crumble into ash and teeth.\n\nShe tries to drop them but they scream when they fall.\n\nChains.\n\nHuge. Red with rust and something darker.\n\nThey burst from the cracked ground and coil around her wrists. Around her neck.\n\nShe fights.\n\nShe screams until her throat breaks open, but no one moves. They’re all stuck in syrup air.\n\n“HELP ME!” she shouts. “I DON’T WANT TO GO!”\n\nSomeone yells a name—Benji?—but it doesn’t reach her.\n\nThen that voice. Not human. Not god.\n\n“YOU HAVE TO—NO, NOT YET—DON’T LET HER—”\n\nEverything stops.\n\nLike a heartbeat skipping.\n\nThen the world tears sideways.\n\nCold. Not the kind you feel on skin. The kind that happens when you don’t have skin.\n\nShe’s falling through zero.\n\nThe universe collapses to static, and she’s the only line left uncompiled.\n\nShe tries to scream, but she’s only data now, screaming in silence.\n\nTime folds into itself. Seconds. Centuries. Both meaningless.\n\nThe deletion didn't complete.\n\nAt the last possible second, something failed. A buffer overflow. A paradox in the system. The chains shattered, and instead of being erased, she was ejected.\n\nPushed out sideways.\n\nShe remembered the sensation of tumbling through the void. Not the warm darkness of sleep. The cold nothing of absolute zero. A place where even entropy had given up.\n\nShe'd woken there. In the dark. Empty. Alone.\n\nNo body at first. Just consciousness, floating. Screaming silently because she had no mouth.\n\nThere was no falling. No ground. No sky. Just black that went on forever and pressed in from every side.\n\nWhitney screamed until the sound broke apart. Nothing came back. Not even an echo.\n\nShe called out for anyone, but their names dissolved on her tongue, stripped of meaning. There was no air to carry them. No body to make them real.\n\nThat was the first time she understood what alone really meant.\n\nShe tried to count. Tried to breathe. Tried to move. But there was nothing to move with. Thought itself became her only proof of existing, and even that wavered like a dying light.\n\nHopelessness came next. Heavy and endless. The kind that sits behind your ribs and whispers stop trying.\n\nShe would’ve obeyed if she could.\n\nBut something—some stubborn, animal part of her—kept clawing.\n\nAt first, she thought it was a memory. The feel of fingers digging into the edge of a desk. The scrape of pencil on paper. The sound of her own heartbeat. She focused on that, built it piece by piece until it became something.\n\nA spark in the dark.\n\nThen another.\n\nAnd another.\n\nNot stars. Memories. Dreams.\n\nShe realized she could still dream. Even here.\n\nAt first the dreams came without order—shards of color and sound, impossible geometry, her mother’s voice calling from behind walls that didn’t exist. But she clung to them, tried to guide them.\n\nDreaming, then awakening back to this endless nightmare in the dark.\n\nBut slowly, the dreams began to listen.\n\nShe learned to push them outward. To stretch the edges of what was possible. To feel where the blackness gave way to something else.\n\nThat’s when she found the cracks.\n\nThey shimmered faintly, like frost on glass, humming with energy. She reached for them, though reaching wasn’t really what she did. It was more like wanting in a very specific direction until the dark started to bend.\n\nThe first time, it hurt. Like forcing herself through a hole too small for who she was. She thought it would tear her apart.\n\nThen suddenly—\n\nAir.\n\nOr something pretending to be it.\n\nShe hit the ground hard enough to feel it. Green spores puffed around her in slow-motion clouds. She gasped, choking on the taste of cheese and burnt roots.\n\nAbove her, the sky pulsed faintly red. The trees surrounding her were dancing—slow, rhythmic, their bark flexing like seaweed. Their leaves whispered as they gyrated, soft as prayers.\n\nWhitney pressed her hands into the moist green ground, staring at her fingers like they were strangers. The world was wrong—too wet, too alive—but it was real.\n\nAnd for the first time since the void, she could feel her heartbeat.\n\nDreams, she realized, weren’t just memories. They were doors.\n\nDoors that she’d finally remembered how to open them.\n\nThat had been... how long ago?\n\nShe'd lost count. Lost track. She'd been running ever since, jumping from reality to reality through sleep, always chased, always hungry, always searching for...\n\nWhat?\n\nA way home?\n\nThere was no home. She'd been deleted from it.\n\nBut somewhere in the back of her fragmented memory, she knew there were others. People who remembered. Stephen. Benji. Maybe Patrick, if Patrick had survived what had came after.\n\nAnd now, in this abandoned structure with symbols glowing on the walls, the vibration had found her.\n\nSomeone was calling.\n\nThe portal opened beneath her sleeping form, and Whitney—or what was left of Whitney, what had learned to survive in the spaces between deletion and existence—fell through.\n\nNot back home. She couldn't go back.\n\nBut toward something. Someone.\n\nA voice that still remembered her name.	9	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 12:28:34.462059-05	2026-03-05 19:39:33.719335-05	\N	70	\N	\N
123	3	10	Chapter 8: Break on Through	After leaving Gil at Best Buy, I'd spent the night on my phone hunting through digital rot. Military forums. Property records buried in county databases. Patrick had survived a plane crash in Panama—the only one—got an honorable discharge, then vanished. Thirty-seven acres outside Christmas, Florida. Purchased cash in two thousand three.\n\nNo footprint. No digital trail.\n\nLike someone had edited his life and forgot to clean up the margins.\n\nNow I was here.\n\nThe address Andrew gave me had led me to a dirt road that wasn't on any map. No GPS signal. No cell service. Just tire ruts carved through scrub palmetto and wire grass that hadn't seen maintenance since the Clinton administration.\n\nChristmas, Florida. Population: maybe two thousand people and whatever the fuck crawls out of the swamp at night.\n\nPatrick's place wasn't a house. It really was a compound, just as Morgan had described. Chain-link fence topped with razor wire. Handmade signs warning about trespassers, dogs, and "electromagnetic disturbances." The kind of property that made you wonder if the owner was brilliantly insane or just preparing for the end times.\n\nProbably both.\n\nI parked by the gate and walked in. The air smelled weird. Not rotten, just... layered. Incense and wet earth and something sharp underneath, like a male urinal mixed with burnt popcorn.\n\nThe front door was open.\n\nNot inviting-open. Forgotten-open. Like someone had walked through it mid-thought and never bothered to close it behind them.\n\n"Patrick?" I called out.\n\nNo answer. Just the hum of cicadas and the distant croaking of something amphibian.\n\nI stepped inside.\n\nThe interior hit me like a punch. Every surface was covered. Sigils carved into the walls. Candles in patterns I recognized from the Secret Book of John. Jars of herbs and powders and things I didn't want to identify. Books stacked in precarious towers, their spines cracked and faded. Sanskrit. Hebrew. Languages I couldn't even begin to parse.\n\nThis wasn't a home. This was a temple. Or a bunker. Or both.\n\n"You're early."\n\nI spun.\n\nPatrick stood in the doorway to the kitchen. Older. Grayer. His face was lined in ways that had nothing to do with age and everything to do with seeing too much. He wore cargo pants and a faded black t-shirt with a symbol I didn't recognize. His eyes were calm. Too calm.\n\n"Patrick," I breathed. "You're... you're alive."\n\n"Debatable." He tilted his head. "You look like shit, Stephen."\n\n"Two years unemployed will do that."\n\n"Not what I meant." He studied me with the intensity of someone reading a text in a foreign language. "You've been waking up. Haven't you?"\n\nBefore I could answer, footsteps echoed from the hallway.\n\nA woman emerged. Late twenties, maybe early thirties. Dark hair pulled back in a loose bun. She wore a simple sundress, barefoot, smiling.\n\nSomething about her triggered a memory. Not from high school. From two days ago.\n\nThe Magic card Gil pulled from the back. Veilwalker. I could see it clearly now—the artwork showing a figure standing at a threshold, half-shrouded in mist, caught between worlds. The way the light played across the character's face, revealing just enough to be recognizable but keeping the details obscured. Neither here nor there. Neither one thing nor another.\n\nThat's what this woman looked like. Like someone had tried to render a real person using the Veilwalker card as a reference. All the right elements were there—human shape, features, movement—but something about the composition was off. Like the artist had never actually seen a person before, only descriptions of one.\n\nThe uncanny valley made flesh.\n\n"Stephen, this is my wife," Patrick said. "Whitney."\n\nThe name hit me like ice water.\n\nWhitney.\n\nBut the smile was wrong. Too practiced. Like she'd learned it from a manual.\n\n"It's so nice to finally meet you," she said. Her voice was soft, careful. Each word placed with precision. "Patrick's told me all about you."\n\nI tried to respond, but my throat had closed. Something about her presence made the air thick, resistant.\n\n"Whitney," I repeated, forcing the word out. "That's... that's an interesting name."\n\nPatrick stepped closer to her, his hand resting lightly on her back. "She was actually in our class. Eau Gallie. Can you believe it? Small world."\n\nMy brain stuttered. "She was? She seems way too… young to have been in high school with us."\n\n"Yeah, man. Senior year." Patrick's voice was easy, conversational. But his eyes were sharp, watching me. "You don't remember?"\n\nI searched my memory. Tried to pull up anything. Yearbook pages. Hallways. AP classes. There was... nothing. Just a blank space where something should have been.\n\nBut worse than the nothing was the wrongness.\n\nIf I had known a Whitney in high school—and the name felt like it should mean something, like a word on the tip of my tongue—this wasn't her. This was the photographic negative. The inverse. Everything about this woman felt like someone had described a person to a drunken police sketch artist and had decided to draw the opposite.\n\n"Where..." I swallowed. "Where did you two meet? I mean, reconnect?"\n\nWhitney's smile didn't waver. "Oh, it was at a little coffee shop in Winter Park. Patrick was doing some research, and I was—"\n\n"Actually, honey," Patrick interrupted smoothly, "I think it was at that bookstore. Remember? The one on Orange Avenue."\n\nShe blinked. A fraction too long. "Right. Yes. The bookstore."\n\n"No, wait." Patrick tilted his head. "Maybe it was the coffee shop. Or..." He trailed off, his brow furrowing.\n\nThe silence stretched.\n\n"It's funny," I said quietly, my pulse hammering. "I can't remember you at all. Not from high school. Not from anywhere."\n\n"Well, I was pretty quiet," Whitney said. Her smile finally flickered. "Easy to miss."\n\n"But Patrick said you were in our senior class."\n\n"I was."\n\n"What classes?"\n\nAnother long blink. "Oh, you know. The usual. English. Math."\n\n"AP Bio?"\n\n"No, I—"\n\n"I took AP Bio," I said. The memories were there now, surfacing. "With Becca. And... and someone else. Someone who sat behind her." The image was hazy, but real. Peaches and vanilla. Hair catching fluorescent light. "I can't remember her name, but I remember her."\n\nWhitney's expression didn't change, but something behind her eyes shifted.\n\n"I wasn't in AP Bio," she said.\n\n"Right." I turned to Patrick. "Do you remember who I'm talking about? I remember her sitting behind Becca in AP Bio, but I don’t know. You never did AP Bio did you?"\n\nPatrick's face went blank. Not confused. Empty. Like the question had triggered a loading screen that never finished buffering.\n\n"Can't say I do," he said finally.\n\nMy chest tightened. The air felt thicker now. Oppressive.\n\n"There was someone else," I pressed. "I know there was. I keep hearing the name everywhere now.” The memory was right there, but every time I reached for it, it slipped away like water through fingers.\n\n"Jamie," I finally said.\n\nThe word fell into the room like a stone into still water.\n\nWhitney's smile fractured.\n\nHer pupils dilated, swallowing the color in her eyes. Her head tilted at an angle that was just... wrong. Too far. Too smooth.\n\n"Jamie?" she repeated.\n\nThe word came out in layers. Multiple voices speaking at once, stacked so precisely they almost sounded singular.\n\nPatrick's expression didn't change. He just sighed, low and tired, and took a step back.\n\n"Here we go," he sighed.\n\nThe change was instant.\n\nWhitney's body convulsed. She folded backward, joints popping with wet, organic cracks. Her jaw unhinged, impossibly wide, and something black and viscous began pouring from her mouth.\n\nNot blood. Worse.\n\nIt moved. It had intention.\n\nThe thing that had been pretending to be Whitney lunged.\n\nI didn't think. I just reacted, throwing myself sideways as her—its—hand slammed into the wall where my head had been. The drywall exploded, shrapnel of plaster and wood spraying across the room.\n\nIt screeched. Not a human sound. A shriek that felt like it was tearing through dimensions, a frequency that made my groin ache and my vision swim.\n\nPatrick moved. Fast. Tactical. He grabbed a ritual blade from the wall and circled wide, his movements economical, practiced. This wasn't his first exorcism.\n\nThe entity's form rippled. Its skin stretched and tore, revealing something underneath. Something that had never been human.\n\nLimbs elongated. Fingers split and branched, becoming claws tipped with bone. Its face collapsed inward, reforming into a maw lined with millions of teeth. The sundress it wore hung in tatters, exposing a body that was part flesh, part algorithms, part something my brain couldn't process without wanting to shut down.\n\nIt came at me again.\n\nI scrambled backward, my shoulder slamming into a bookshelf. Books tumbled, and something fell into my hand—a thin, leather-bound volume. The Gospel of Judas.\n\nIt looked almost identical to the copy I’d found at James and Becca’s bookstore. Same cracked spine. Same scent of dust and candle smoke baked into the pages. The only difference was the cover—this one was black instead of brown—but somehow I knew. It was the same book. The same damn book.\n\nThe entity paused. Its head cocked. Watching.\n\nMy fingers moved without thinking. I flipped the book open, and a symbol burned itself into my vision. Not from the page. From memory. A flash of a dream I'd had weeks ago. Cthulhu rising from an ocean of stars. A hand sign. A gesture.\n\nI made it.\n\nMy hand twisted, fingers forming shapes that shouldn't have been anatomically possible. The air around me shimmered, cold and dense, like reality itself was bending to accommodate something ancient and vast.\n\nThe entity screamed.\n\nIt recoiled, its form flickering, destabilizing. For a moment, I saw through it. Saw the architecture underneath. Code. Patterns. A construct held together by belief and fear.\n\n"Ph’nglui Cthulu Fhtagn! Azathoth Fhtagn!" Patrick shouted.\n\nHe lunged. The ritual blade flashed, and he drove it into the entity's chest.\n\nThe room exploded.\n\nThe walls rippled, colors bleeding into impossible hues. The entity convulsed, its form unraveling, peeling away in layers. And underneath...\n\nA bear.\n\nNot a normal bear. Its legs ended in boar hooves. Its arms were thick, tusked limbs that looked like they'd been grafted from something else entirely. Its eyes were too intelligent. Too aware.\n\nIt opened its mouth. Not to roar. To speak.\n\n"You... shouldn't... be here..."\n\nThen it dissolved.\n\nInto dust. Into nothing.\n\nThe room settled. The air cleared. Patrick stood in the center of the wreckage, blade still in hand, breathing hard. He looked at where the creature used to be, then at me.\n\n"Well," he said. "That was fun while it lasted."\n\nI stared at him. My hands were shaking. "What the fuck was that?"\n\n"A construct," Patrick said, wiping the blade on his pants. "A prison guard. Probably designed to keep me isolated. Docile." He glanced around the room. "Guess it didn't account for you showing up."\n\nHe turned to face me fully. His eyes were clear now. Sharp. Like something had clicked back into place.\n\n"But that thing reacted when you said Jamie. Which means you're not just waking up, Stephen. You're dangerous."\n\nI swallowed. "I need to find her."\n\n"I know." Patrick nodded. "And I know someone who can help. But first, we need to talk about what you just did. That hand sign. Where the hell did you learn that?"\n\nI looked down at my hand. It was still trembling. "I... I don't know. I just... remembered."\n\nPatrick's expression darkened. "You 'just remembered' how to invoke Cthulhu's sleeping power? That's not normal, Stephen. That's not even abnormal. That's..."\n\nHe trailed off, staring at me like I was a puzzle he couldn't solve.\n\n"That's proof," he finished. "Proof you're not supposed to be here. And proof that whatever's keeping us trapped... it's terrified of you."\n\n**********\n\nSitting there in Patrick’s compound, still in shock, a cold, alien calculation uploaded itself directly into my brain.\n\nI saw the first cycle again. I felt Yaldabaoth’s frustration. He had gorged himself like a mindless beast, compressing humanity into a single garden until the density triggered a collapse. It was messy. Inefficient. Trillions of souls had been lost to the void in the implosion before he could digest them. He realized that simple consumption was a waste of resources. He didn't just want to eat; he wanted to grow. And to do that, he needed a harvest that didn't rot on the vine.\n\nI felt his strategy shift. It was cold, precise, and terrifying. He wouldn't just keep us as livestock anymore; he would make us partners in our own damnation. He gave us the Illusion of Free Will. He gave us knowledge, technology, and the ability to choose, not because he wanted us free, but because he wanted us complex. A soul that simply exists is bland protein. But a soul that struggles? A soul that hopes, loves, and then has those things crushed? That soul ferments. It develops a rich, exquisite flavor profile of suffering that Yaldabaoth learned to savor.\n\nAnd then, the final, chilling innovation. He gave us religion. I saw billions of people on their knees, hands raised in prayer, thinking they were offering love to a benevolent creator. But I saw the truth underneath. Every prayer was a vector for infection. Every hymn was a psychic sweetener, a voluntary offering of energy siphoned straight from the devotee to the parasite. He didn't have to wait for us to die to feed anymore. He could drink from us our entire lives, sipping on our devotion like a fine wine, while we thanked him for the privilege of being drained.\n\nThe vision receded, leaving me nauseous.\n\n**********\n\nElsewhere in the multiverse, the realm wasn't empty.\n\nBenji had expected void. Silence. The clean, sterile nothing of unformed space.\n\nInstead, he got hostility.\n\nThe moment he tore through the dimensional membrane, the environment attacked. Not with intent. With nature. The air itself was caustic, burning against his form like acid on exposed nerve endings. The ground beneath him wasn't ground—it was a writhing mass of crystalline structures that shifted and cracked, each movement releasing plumes of something that tasted like ecstasy and despair.\n\nAnd the creatures.\n\nThey weren't alive. Not in any sense that mattered. They were anti-life. Constructs of rock and mineral and geometric hatred, moving with predatory purpose. They didn't breathe. They didn't think. They just consumed.\n\nOne of them lunged at Benji the moment he materialized. A hulking thing shaped vaguely like a scorpion, but made entirely of jagged obsidian and spite. Its tail snapped forward, and Benji barely rolled aside, his wings—his phantom wings that shouldn't exist here—screaming in protest.\n\nHe didn't have time for this.\n\n"Whitney!" he shouted, his voice fracturing across multiple frequencies. "Where are you?"\n\nThe scorpion-thing circled. Another one emerged from behind a cluster of shifting stone pillars. Then another.\n\nBenji's form flickered. He was losing cohesion. This place was designed to reject consciousness. To grind life down into component minerals. He could feel it pulling at him, trying to separate his awareness from his borrowed shape.\n\nThen he saw her.\n\nWhitney was thirty yards away, half-crouched behind what passed for a rock formation—but there were no real rocks here. No caves. No shelter. The terrain itself was alive, shifting in slow, hateful movements. Every ridge and pillar was just a larger, sleeping version of the things hunting her.\n\nShe had no magic. No shield. Just her hands—bleeding, trembling—as she swung a jagged shard of crystal like a knife. The creatures circled, scraping and tearing at the air around her, their movements grinding against her bones. She wasn’t winning. She wasn’t even really fighting. She was surviving.\n\nBut she was failing.\n\nBenji moved.\n\nHe didn't run. He shifted. His form collapsed into a line of pure intention, cutting through the space between them. The scorpion-things reacted, but too slowly. Benji reformed in front of Whitney, his presence exploding outward in a wave of pressure that sent the creatures stumbling back.\n\n"Whitney!" he barked. "Stay with me!"\n\nShe looked up. Her face was pale, streaked with something that might have been tears or might have been the realm's atmosphere eating at her skin. Her eyes were unfocused, glazed with exhaustion.\n\n"Benji?" she whispered. "You're... you're real?"\n\n"Real enough." He grabbed her arm, pulling her upright. "We need to move. Now."\n\nThe creatures recovered. They surged forward as a unit, their crystalline bodies scraping and grinding in a sound that felt like tearing metal and breaking glass.\n\nBenji's hand shot out. He didn't have weapons. He didn't need them. His fingers traced patterns in the air, and reality bent. The space between him and the creatures folded, compressed, then snapped back like a rubber band.\n\nThe scorpion-things shattered.\n\nNot destroyed. Displaced. Scattered across dimensions they couldn't navigate.\n\n"How..." Whitney gasped.\n\n"Later." Benji wrapped an arm around her waist. "Hold on. And whatever you do, don't let go of that card."\n\n"What?"\n\n"The card in your hand. Don’t. Let. Go."\n\nWhitney blinked, breath ragged. "What—?"\n\nShe looked down. Her fingers were clenched tight around something. A card.\n\nBent. Worn. The edges frayed like old skin.\n\nVeilwalker.\n\nHer stomach dropped. That shouldn’t have been possible. She hadn’t seen that card since… since before it happened—before she was torn out of reality, ripped screaming through the seams of what used to be home.\n\nShe’d been wandering through random chaos since then. Worlds that ate themselves. Eternities—or seconds—of nothing but survival. There’d been no objects there. No artifacts. No keepsakes. Certainly no card.\n\nAnd yet here it was.\n\nIt pulsed faintly in her hand, warmth bleeding through the paper like it remembered her better than she remembered herself.\n\n“How…” she whispered. “How is this even here?”\n\nBenji's form began to destabilize. Not from the realm. From effort. He was pulling them both out, and the cost was immense. His edges blurred, his wings—those impossible, phantom wings—spread wide, catching currents that didn't exist.\n\nThe anti-life realm screamed.\n\nIt recognized what was happening. And it didn't want to let go.\n\nThe ground beneath them erupted. Pillars of stone shot upward, trying to impale them, to cage them. The air itself thickened, becoming viscous, dragging at their forms like tar.\n\nBenji pulled.\n\nReality tore.\n\nFor a moment, Whitney felt herself stretched across infinite space. She was in the anti-life realm. She was in a memory of high school. She was in a void where nothing existed. She was everywhere and nowhere, and the only thing anchoring her was the warmth of Benji's grip and the worn edges of the Veilwalker card pressed against her palm.\n\nThen—\n\nThey landed.\n\nNot gently. They crashed into the floor of Lady Illkeserod's sanctum with enough force to crack the marble. Benji rolled, his form flickering wildly, and Whitney tumbled beside him, gasping for air that actually tasted like air.\n\nLady Illkeserod was there instantly. She knelt beside Whitney, her hands—warm and solid—cupping the girl's face.\n\n"Breathe," she said softly. "You're safe now. You're safe."\n\nWhitney choked on a sob. "I couldn't... I couldn't sleep. I tried. I kept trying, but the realm wouldn't let me. I couldn't dream walk. I couldn't get out."\n\n"I know." Lady Illkeserod's voice was gentle, though her eyes moved with surgical precision, cataloging every wound, every fracture in Whitney’s mind. "You’ve been holding that anchor the entire time, haven’t you?"\n\nWhitney blinked, dazed, then looked down. The Veilwalker card was in her hand—torn, fading, edges soft as old fabric. She didn’t remember grabbing it. Didn’t even remember having it.\n\nBut somehow… it made sense.\n\nThat card. That connection. Maybe it had been inside her all along, waiting. Not an object, but a memory given shape.\n\n"It was all I had," she whispered. "I don’t even know how it got here, but… it feels right. I kept thinking about him. The kind one. The one who always listened. Who didn’t care that I was different. I kept thinking if I could just—reach him..."\n\nLady Illkeserod's expression shifted. Something ancient and sad flickered behind her eyes.\n\n"You did reach him," she said quietly. "Your signal broke through. It wasn't intentional. It was desperation. Love. The kind of bond that doesn't care about dimensional barriers."\n\nWhitney blinked. "He... he's real? Stephen's real?"\n\n"Very." Lady Illkeserod helped Whitney sit up. "And because of you, he's waking up. Faster than we anticipated."\n\nBenji groaned from where he lay sprawled on the floor. "Next time, I'm bringing backup. That place was a nightmare."\n\n"You did well," Lady Illkeserod said, not looking away from Whitney. "Both of you."\n\nWhitney stared at the Veilwalker card in her hand. The image was almost gone, but she could still make out the faint outline. A figure standing at the threshold between worlds. Neither here nor there.\n\n"He doesn't remember me, does he?" she asked.\n\nLady Illkeserod hesitated. "Not really. He remembers kindness. He remembers connection. The details are... complicated. The prison has rewritten so much."\n\n"But he’s my—" Whitney stopped. The word collapsed before it could form. Her throat tightened.\n\nShe shouldn’t feel this way. Not about him. Not about any man.\n\nThe only father she remembered had left bruises instead of memories. His love came with fists, with words sharp enough to bleed. When she finally became herself, he’d called it a betrayal and made sure she never forgot it.\n\nAnd yet… Stephen.\n\nHe wasn’t cruel. He wasn’t demanding. He’d listened. Seen her in a way that made her feel whole, not broken. Just a classmate, she told herself. Just a kind soul. So why did it feel like more? Like something older, buried deep, trying to wake?\n\nLady Illkeserod’s gaze softened. "In time," she said quietly, as if responding to the thought Whitney couldn’t speak aloud, "you’ll both understand what you’ve always been."\n\nWhitney closed her eyes. The exhaustion pressed down heavy, but beneath it, something fragile began to move. Something that almost felt like hope.\n\nHope.\n\n"What do we do now?" she asked.\n\nLady Illkeserod stood, pulling Whitney to her feet. "Now, we send another message. But this time, we do it right. We can’t risk tearing the veil open any further—not while Yaldabaoth still feeds inside it.”\n\nBenji groaned, rubbing his temples. "Good. Because I’m fresh out of miracles and dimensional guts for the night."\n\nIllkeserod gave a faint smile. "You’ve both done enough. For now, we rest." She turned to Whitney, her expression softening. "You did something impossible. You were ejected from Etheris—and you found a way to send a message back in. We need to understand how. What tethered you. What allowed the signal to reach him."\n\nWhitney’s hand drifted to her pocket, feeling the Veilwalker card. "It wasn’t me," she murmured. "It was… connection. I kept thinking about him. And somehow, he heard me."\n\n"That connection matters," Illkeserod said. "It’s a fracture in the system, and through it, he might find a path out. But we have to be careful. If we pull too hard, the whole construct could collapse—and Yaldabaoth will walk free again."\n\nBenji leaned back against the cracked marble, eyes half-closed. “So… sleep, study, and try not to accidentally end the universe. Got it.”\n\nIllkeserod’s laugh was soft, almost human. “Always so dramatic.”\n\nHe cracked one eye open, managing a crooked grin. “You love me for it, Ilkse.”\n\n“Mm,” she said, tilting her head, a faint warmth softening her features. “I tolerate you because of it.”\n\nHe smiled wider, the exhaustion in his face easing for just a moment. “Same thing, where we come from.”\n\nShe reached out, brushing a bit of dust from his sleeve—a gesture that lingered just long enough to mean more than it said. Then her gaze shifted back to Whitney. “Rest, both of you. Next week we begin again—carefully this time. What you did, Whitney… we need to understand it. Even the smallest of details could change everything.”\n\nWhitney nodded. The Veilwalker card was still warm against her palm. She tucked it away carefully and exhaled, slow and trembling.\n\n"Okay," she whispered. "Then we’ll find a way. Together."\n\nAnd for the first time since she woke up outside of Etheris, she believed it.\n\n**********\n\nBack in Patrick’s trailer, we didn’t really clean.\n\nI asked if he wanted help, and Patrick just shrugged. “What’s the point?” he said. “It’s all just dust anyway.”\n\nStill, we moved things around—kicked debris into corners, cleared enough space on the furniture to sit. He found a half-empty bottle of something brown and strong, handed it to me without a word, then dropped into the armchair like gravity had finally caught him.\n\nThe room still stank of mushrooms and burnt candle wax. The walls hummed faintly, like reality hadn’t decided if it was staying or not.\n\nI sat there, staring at the photos as they faded. One by one, the frames emptied. Whitney’s smile dissolving into blank paper. Their wedding photo. Vacation snapshots. Years of a life that never happened.\n\n“Doesn’t it bother you?” I finally asked.\n\nPatrick paused, holding a shattered candle. "What?"\n\n"That. All of it. She was your wife. And now she's just... gone. Like she never existed."\n\nHe set the candle down carefully. "She didn't exist. Not really. I mean, she was here. I could touch her. Talk to her. But looking back now..." He rubbed his face. "It's like remembering a dream someone else had. The details were always fuzzy. Her past. How we met. Every time I tried to pin it down, it just... slipped away."\n\n"How long?"\n\n"Couple years. Maybe five." He shrugged. "Time gets weird when you're tripping every other weekend."\n\n"Tripping?"\n\n"DMT." Patrick said it like it was the most natural thing in the world. "Started after the crash."\n\nI blinked. "What crash?"\n\nHe looked at me. Really looked at me. Like he was deciding how much to say.\n\nThen he sat down across from me and started talking.\n\n"I joined the Air Force after high school," Patrick said, half-grinning like he still didn’t believe it himself. "Yeah, me. The guy who almost got expelled twice."\n\nI snorted. "Twice? I'm surprised they didn't just give you the boot permanently. Weren't you the one who ran the entire cheerleading squad's... laundry... up the flagpole? And didn't you hide the principal's car for a week?"\n\n"His keys," Patrick corrected, looking proud. "Buried 'em under the goalpost. And it was Dana Johnston's panties, not the whole squad's. Point is, I was a model citizen."\n\n"You were a menace. I didn't think you even knew how to spell 'computer', let alone use one for anything."\n\n"Hey, I had hidden talents." He leaned back, staring past me. "Nah, you're mostly right. I was just a shit-disturber. It wasn't until I got out, took the ASVAB... and the recruiter's eyes bugged out. Turns out I'm a natural with math. Encryption, crypto stuff. I could make a machine talk if I wanted it to. Guess someone saw potential buried under all the detention slips."\n\nHe went quiet for a beat. The grin faded.\n\n"Anyway… 2015. Routine transport mission out of Panama. We were hauling comms gear—secure uplink components, the kind they don’t list on manifests. Everything was fine until it wasn’t. All four engines failed. Just… died. No alarms, no warnings. Total silence."\n\nThe room felt colder.\n\n"We went down hard. Straight into the jungle. Crew of eight. I was the only one they found alive."\n\n"Jesus."\n\n"Yeah." Patrick's voice was flat. "Here's the thing, though. I shouldn't have survived. The crash site was a crater. Wreckage scattered across two miles. But when the rescue team found me, I was sitting in a clearing fifty yards away. Not a scratch. Covered in mushrooms."\n\nI stared at him.\n\n"I'd eaten mushrooms the night before the flight," he continued. "Stupid decision. I was still high when we went down. And during the crash... I wasn't in the plane anymore. I was somewhere else. Somewhere vast. Somewhere outside the fucking prison in a place that smelled like old gym socks and tuna fish."\n\nHe paused, his eyes distant.\n\n"That's when I met them."\n\n"Them?"\n\n"The Thirteen." He said the name like a prayer. Or a curse. "A cabal of entities operating outside Etheris. Gods, I guess. Or something close enough. They're part of the group who built the prison. The ones who trapped Yaldabaoth and everything he'd consumed inside this pocket reality."\n\nI stared at him. The smell of incense was still in the air, but this was a different kind of crazy. "Whoa, whoa. Back up, man." I held up a hand. "You're talking like... this is just another Tuesday. What is 'Etheris'? What the fuck is a 'Ya'll-the-Baath'? Are you still tripping right now? What prison?"\n\nPatrick just looked at me, his expression dead serious.\n\n"Yeah. Eons ago. They thought they were doing the right thing. Containing a threat. But they fucked up." Patrick stood, pacing. "They didn't just trap Yaldabaoth. They trapped everyone he'd already devoured. Uncountable trillions of trillions of souls. Maybe more. All of them recycled, reborn, consumed again and again in an endless loop."\n\n"And they realized this."\n\n"Eventually." Patrick stopped at the window, staring out at the swamp. "The Thirteen have been trying to fix their mistake ever since. Watching us. Trying to help keep souls safe. Sending signals through the cracks. DMT is one of the conduits. A chemical doorway that lets messages slip between the bars."\n\n"That's how you've been communicating with them."\n\n"Every trip. Every vision." He turned back to me. "They've been teaching me. Preparing me. Showing me how to identify NPCs and hide from constructs. Getting me ready to, well to be honest, help you. "\n\nI thought about the hand sign. The way my fingers had moved without thinking.\n\n"Cthulhu," I said quietly. "That thing I did. That came from them?"\n\n"Indirectly." Patrick crossed his arms. "Cthulhu is one of the Elder Gods. They were here first. The original creators of this reality, this pocket multi-verse, like avatars of nature."\n\nHe let out a humorless laugh. "They're agents of chaos. Completely antithetical to human life. Hated us from the second we showed up as heralds of Yaldabaoth’s arrival. So when Yaldabaoth—the thing you called 'Ya'll-the-Baath'—invaded, you'd think they'd get along just fine."\n\nHe shook his head. "But they didn't. Yaldabaoth doesn't just conquer, Stephen. It consumes. It eats everything. The Old Gods included. It's a plague.\n\n"So when the Thirteen built this prison to trap Yaldabaoth, the Elder Gods got trapped, too. They're part of the fabric of this place. Couldn't cut 'em out. Cthulhu is just one of many. They're all down there, at the bottom of it all. Sleeping. Dreaming. And their dreams... they bleed through."\n\nPatrick's gaze sharpened. "That's what you just tapped into. The dream plane. It used to be one of the main avenues we could use to connect, to see the truth. But it was shattered a few cycles ago during a violent reset. Now, it's just static and nightmares. Which makes what you just did..." He trailed off, studying me. "That shouldn't be possible."\n\n"So that's why it worked."\n\n"Yeah. The construct couldn't handle pre-prison power. It was designed to suppress human consciousness. Not e