untain of blood slowed to a fast, dark ooze.\n\nThen I saw it.\n\nThe cultists. The mutations. They had all stopped fighting. They were turning, one by one. Not to me.\n\nTo the puddle of blood at my feet.\n\nThey sniffed. They knelt. And then they began to drink, lapping at it like animals, their perfect faces smeared with my life.\n\n"It's hungry," I gasped, leaning on Xavier. "The King... he just wants to eat."\n\nIn the chaos, a piece of scaffolding had been knocked over, and Xavier had landed wrong. He cried out as he put weight on his foot. "My ankle... Dad, I think it's broken..."\n\n"We go. Now."\n\nI threw his arm over my shoulder, taking his weight. I took my first step, a wave of dizziness from the blood loss crashing over me. We half-dragged, half-limped toward the door.\n\nNo one followed. They were too busy.\n\nWe burst out of the steel door, into the freezing, clean night air, and collapsed onto the gravel. Behind us, I slammed the door shut, the sound echoing in the sudden, terrible silence. We were alone, listening to our own ragged, gasping breaths and the steady drip... drip... drip... of my existence onto the frozen ground.\n\n**********\n\nI drove. My left arm was a dead, throbbing weight, the tourniquet biting so deep my hand was a pale, bloodless claw resting on the car door. Every bump in the road jostled the rebar and sent a fresh wave of agony from my stomach, and the world was starting to get a fuzzy, vignetted look.\n\n"You're drifting, Dad," Xavier's voice came from far away. "The next exit. Take the next exit."\n\n"Can't," I slurred. "Hospital... can't..."\n\n"We're not going to a hospital," he said, his voice hard with a new, terrified authority. "We're just getting off the interstate. Now, Dad. Turn."\n\nI did as I was told. We plunged off the main highway, onto a dark, two-lane road that cut through the rolling blackness of the Tennessee hills. The stolen Civic's headlights sliced into an empty, sleeping country. We drove for an hour