.. to... walk faster!" I said, grabbing his arm. "No, go faster!"\n\nWe started to... go faster... down the grey street.\n\nThe things followed. Silent. Implacable. More of them emerging from the... the holes in the walls where people walk through... the... empty wooden frames that are missing something in the middle. Their void-mouths opened wider.\n\nAnother tug at my mind. Stronger. The word for... the thing on your face you use to breathe.. Gone. The word for the path between buildings... Blank.\n\n"Dad, I can't... I can't... recall..." Xavier stumbled, clutching his head. "There was a word... a word for... for when you know something from before. When you don't let it go..."\n\n"Hold onto it?" I offered, my own mind feeling foggy.\n\n"No, that's not it. It's... it's the thing you do with your brain... to keep a thought. I... I can't... know it anymore."\n\nThe bad things were all around now. Many of them. Many more. They got close around us, their holes taking, taking.\n\nI tried to hit them. I hit at no-thing. But you can't hit a hole. Can't hit no-thing. My hands went through them. No hurt. And with each touch, more was gone.\n\nThe song my mother liked. The color of my first car. The name of the man who showed me the... things. Gone. Gone. Gone.\n\n"Guy!" the boy’s sound was scared. "I can't... I do not know how to... the thing with your legs, where you go, what is it?"\n\nWe were both on our knees now. The bad things coming close. Their big holes very close to our faces. I could feel them taking all of me. Every thought from before. Every thought. All of me... being taken, bit by bit.\n\nThis is how it stops, I thought. Not with hurt. Not with hurt. Just... gone.\n\nThen a sound came in the…\n\n"What the fuck are you doing way the hell out here?"\n\nWisdom and memories flooded back as a figure burst through the circle of spectres. Small. Familiar. Wearing a cardigan that looked more worn and tattered than I remembered.\n\nBenji.\n\nHe moved between us and the void-creatures, his form shifting, expanding. For a moment, he wasn't a small figure in a cardigan. He was something vast and winged.\n\nThe spectres recoiled.\n\n"Back!" Benji's voice resonated with authority that made the air vibrate. "These souls are spoken for! You want to feed, go hunt somewhere else in the outer wastes where nobody cares!"\n\nThe spectres hesitated. Their void-mouths working silently, hungry but uncertain.\n\nBenji's form solidified back into something more human-shaped. He made a gesture with one hand, and symbols appeared in the air—complex geometries that hurt to look at. The spectres scattered, dissolving into the shadows between buildings.\n\nSilence settled over the empty street.\n\nBenji turned to face us, and I saw how much he'd changed. The cardigan was the same, but everything else... he looked tired. Worn down. Like he'd been fighting for a very long time.\n\n"Stephen," he said. "Xavier. Welcome to the Akashic Realm. You picked a terrible entry point."\n\nI tried to speak, but the words came out jumbled. Too many memories now in the wrong places.\n\n"Don't worry," Benji said. "The memories will come back. Mostly. Those things don't actually eat your thoughts—they just... borrow them. Temporarily. You'll remember everything in a few minutes…"\n\nHe started walking, and we followed. Xavier was still shaking, occasionally reaching for words that weren't quite there yet.\n\n"What were those things?" I managed.\n\n"Wisdom Devourers. They're supposed to be confined to the outer wastes, but things have been... unstable lately." He waved a hand at the grey landscape. "This whole realm, the Akashic, it's supposed to be a cosmic information superhighway, flowing underneath realities. Not everywhere, mind you—there are places even we're banned from—but it's usually reliable."\n\nHe sighed, "But Etheris... after the dreamscape collapsed in the last reset, it's been dammed up. Blocked. Nothing has been flowing in or out of there. Normally you’d never be able to physically cross into the Akashic Etheris, it’s hard enough as it is for dreamers and other spirits to enter on a good day. All this means everything here in this area is destabilizing everything. That's why you've got lots of entities wandering into places they shouldn't be." He glanced back at us. "Speaking of which, that instability is probably how you ended up in the industrial district. The normal entry points are all miles from here."\n\n"We built a gateway," I said. "From Etheris. We didn't really have a choice of where we landed."\n\nBenji stopped walking. Turned to face me fully.\n\n"You built a gateway," he repeated. "From inside Etheris. A prison specifically designed to prevent exactly that. You just... built one."\n\n"We had help," I said, the memory of the gymnasium, the gunfire, and the leaking fluids still stinging my eyes. "From... from two women. Maeve and Elara. I thought they might have been Fae. They knew about the old ways... the old pathways that existed long before Etheris was a prison. They gambled that one of those old roads might still be open with all the instabilities."\n\nHis expression softened. "They didn't make it through, did they?"\n\n"No."\n\n"I'm sorry." He resumed walking. "The ones who help usually don't."\n\nWe moved through the grey streets in silence. Gradually, the buildings changed. The ruins gave way to structures that were intact, maintained. The floating towers grew more numerous, connected by elegant bridges of stone and light. And there were people here. Figures moving between buildings, carrying books and scrolls, their forms varying wildly—some humanoid, others decidedly not.\n\n"This is the outer ring of the Archives," Benji explained. "The Tower of Secrets is at the center. Lady Illkeserod's domain."\n\nAt the mention of her name, my chest tightened. Memories were flooding back now. The battlefield. Her rage. The way she'd blamed me for Benji's capture. The lies she'd told in the previous reset about Jamie not existing.\n\n"She knows we're here?" I asked.\n\n"She knows everything that happens in the Archives," Benji said. "She's been tracking you since the moment you entered. Probably wondering what took me so long." He smiled slightly. "You think you just stepped over, don't you? Let me tell you, tracking and time here are a bitch. Everything is going sideways. The veil between the Akashic Realm and Etheris is royally fucking things up, especially since the dreamscape collapsed. It's a miracle I found you in 2 weeks, but I'm still going to hear about it later."\n\nThe Tower of Secrets rose before us, a massive structure of black stone and silver filigree that stretched impossibly high. It didn't just go up—it went sideways, folding through dimensions my eyes couldn't quite process. Staircases wrapped around its exterior, some leading up, others down, a few seeming to go nowhere at all.\n\nWe climbed.\n\nThe entrance hall was vast, circular, with bookshelves stretching in every direction. But these weren't normal shelves. They curved and spiraled, defying gravity, creating a three-dimensional maze of knowledge that hurt to look at for too long.\n\nTwo figures waited at the center.\n\nLady Illkeserod stood with her back to us, studying something on a floating projection of symbols and light. She wore robes of deep purple, her hair pulled back in an intricate braid. Even from behind, she radiated authority.\n\nBeside her stood another woman. Shorter, rounder, with dark skin and eyes that seemed to contain entire universes. She wore simpler robes, brown and practical, covered in embroidered symbols that shifted and changed as I watched.\n\nThe moment I saw her, a memory hit me—faded, like a dream I'd had years ago. A feeling of... training. Of her teaching me, showing me how to see the patterns beneath things. It felt like a direct callback to a past I’m just beginning to remember, to a time before I knew how trapped I really was.\n\n"Lady Osilari," Benji said quietly. "Keeper of the Seals. Master of Symbols. She's... well, you'll see."\n\nLady Illkeserod turned.\n\nHer face was exactly as I remembered—beautiful, ancient, sad. She looked at me, and I saw recognition flash in her eyes. Not just of who I was now, but of who I'd been.\n\n"Stephen," she said, her voice warm but cautious. "You made it. And you brought..."\n\nI didn't let her finish. I crossed the distance in three fast, angry strides. As she fully faced me, I slapped her.\n\nThe sound echoed in the vast chamber, sharp and final.\n\nLady Illkeserod didn't flinch. Didn't move. She just stood there, her head still turned slightly from the impact, accepting it. Xavier hung back with Benji, frozen.\n\n"You lied to me," I snarled, my voice shaking with rage.\n\n"I did."\n\n"You told me Jamie didn't exist. That she was just a dream. A fantasy."\n\n"I did," she repeated, still not moving.\n\n"She exists," I said. My voice was shaking. "She's real. She's trapped inside that prison, and you told me she wasn't real."\n\n"I did," Lady Illkeserod said for the third time. "Because telling you the truth then would have broken everything."\n\nShe gestured to a nearby table. We sat. Xavier and Benji joined us. Lady Osilari remained standing, watching with those universe-filled eyes.\n\n"The prison is powered by love," Lady Illkeserod began. "Your love. Jamie's love. You are the cathode. She is the anode. Your connection generates the energy that keeps the entire structure stable."\n\n"I know that," I said. "I remember. But why lie? Why tell me she didn't exist, was just a construct of my memory during the last reset?"\n\n"Because if you'd known," she said softly, "if you'd remembered who she really was and that she really existed while you were still inside... the pain of separation would have destabilized the power flow. The prison was already weakening. Adding that variable could have caused a catastrophic cascade."\n\n"So you let me suffer in ignorance."\n\n"Yes." No hesitation. No justification. Just the cold truth. "It was a risk you both agreed to take when you first entered Etheris. You knew you'd be separated. Knew your memories would be suppressed. Knew you'd live infinite lifetimes apart, loving and losing other people, never quite remembering what you'd lost." She paused. "It was the cruelest part of the plan. And you chose it anyway."\n\nI sat back, the anger draining out of me. "Why?"\n\n"Because the alternative was letting Yaldabaoth stay free and continue consuming everything." Lady Illkeserod's eyes were wet. "Because you love her enough to sacrifice eternity. Because she loves you the same way. Because some prisons can only be powered by the strongest bonds... and the greatest pain."\n\nXavier spoke up, his voice small. "So my dad and Jamie... they've been doing this for how long?"\n\n"Infinite cycles beyond counting," Lady Osilari said. Her voice was precise, motherly. "Billions of reincarnations each cycle. Then, each time the prison collapses and resets, Jamie in the middle of it all. Stephen alone in the dark watching it all. Each time, memories are suppressed. Each time, they never find each other, always yearning for something that never will come. An eternal engine of love and loss."\n\n"That's..." Xavier's face was pale. "That's horrible."\n\n"It’s hell," Lady Illkeserod agreed.\n\nSilence settled over the table.\n\nI wanted to be angry. Wanted to hold onto the rage. But I couldn't. Because somewhere deep down, beneath all the missing memories and suppressed lives, I understood.\n\nSometimes love means suffering.\n\nSometimes the greatest sacrifice is choosing pain over watching the universe burn.\n\n"I am not sorry for the lie," Lady Illkeserod said quietly, her voice firm. "I am sorry for the pain it caused you. For the confusion. But I am not sorry for the choice."\n\nShe met my gaze, her own unwavering. "If you had your full memory, you would have demanded it. You'd do it to yourself. It's why you volunteered for this."\n\nShe paused, letting the words sink in. "When all your memories come back, think hard. You will know it to be true. I'd do it again, because the alternative is worse."\n\nI looked at her. Really looked. Saw the weight she carried. The guilt. The impossible choices she'd made and would make again.\n\n"I know," I said.\n\nShe smiled. Sad. Grateful.\n\n"So," Benji said, breaking the tension. "What now?"\n\n"Now," Lady Illkeserod said, standing, "we wait."\n\n"Wait for what?" Xavier asked.\n\n"For your NPC wife," she said, looking at me. "She came through the gateway after you. We've been tracking the disturbance of her entry, but that's where the trail ends."\n\n"How long until she gets here?"\n\nLady Illkeserod shook her head. "That's the problem. Time works differently between realms. She might have arrived seconds after you. She might have arrived five thousand years ago, for all we know. Our scouts are out searching the outer provinces, but the Akashic Realm is infinite. We don't know where she is, or when she is. We just have to be ready."\n\n"So we just sit here?"\n\n"We prepare," Lady Osilari said. "We fortify. We gather our forces. And when she arrives..." Her eyes glowed faintly. "We put an end to the chase."\n\nThey gave us rooms in the tower. Warm. Safe. Beds that actually felt real.\n\nBut I couldn't sleep.\n\nI stood at the window, looking out over the Archives. The floating towers. The bridges of light. The grey sky that never quite committed to storming.\n\nXavier knocked softly before entering. "Can't sleep either?"\n\n"No."\n\nHe stood beside me. We watched the realm in silence.\n\n"Dad," he said eventually. "When this is over... when we get out... what happens to us?"\n\n"I don't know."\n\n"Will you and Jamie... will you be together?"\n\nI thought about that. About cycles of separation. About love that transcended memory. About choosing pain.\n\n"I hope so," I said. "But honestly? I don't know if we get a happy ending. I don't know if anyone in this mess does."\n\n"That sucks."\n\n"Yeah," I agreed. "It really does."\n\nHe leaned against me. My son. My real son. The one piece of this nightmare that felt completely, undeniably good.\n\n"I'm glad you're here with me," I said.\n\n"Me too, Dad."\n\nWe stood there, watching the grey realm, waiting for the storm that was coming.\n\nAnd somewhere in the distance, in a village we couldn't see, my wife screamed and tore reality apart, hunting for the family she thought belonged to her.\n\nThe waiting had begun.\n\n**********\n\nThe alarm came three days later.\n\nNot a sound. A feeling. Like the air pressure dropped and my ears popped simultaneously. I was in the common room with Xavier, trying to teach him a card game Benji had shown us, when every symbol on every wall in the tower flared.\n\nLady Osilari appeared in the doorway, her expression grim.\n\n"She's here," she said. "An outer province called Thuleskarne. She brought an army."\n\nWe ran.\n\nThe tower had a viewing chamber near the top, a circular room with no walls, just open air and a projection of the realm spread out below us like a map. Lady Illkeserod and Benji were already there, watching the chaos unfold.\n\nI could see it even from here. A small town, peaceful and pastoral, now burning. Figures swarming through the streets. Some moved normally. Others... didn't.\n\n"The police. Those other agents," I said. "She brought them through."\n\n"Some of them are real," Benji said, his voice tight. "Actual humans, trapped in Etheris, probably don't even know they're inside a prison. But the others..." He pointed to figures that moved with jerking, stuttering motions. "NPCs. Unawakened constructs who’s divine seed is so buried they are nothing more than memories of light. They don't have enough substance to hold form in this realm."\n\nI watched one of the twisted figures grab a fleeing merchant. The thing's face was a melted horror of features that couldn't decide where they belonged. It looked like someone had tried to sculpt a human from clay but forgot what humans looked like halfway through.\n\n"We need to mobilize," Lady Illkeserod said. "Osilari, gather the defense forces. Benji, you're with me. Stephen..." She looked at me. "You and Xavier stay here. You're... just a man, Stephen. You don't have your memories, your abilities. Your karate won't work against this."\n\n"No," I said. "We're coming."\n\n"Stephen—"\n\n"She's my wife. Or was. Or... whatever she is now. This is my responsibility."\n\nLady Illkeserod studied my face for a long moment. Then nodded. "Fine. But you stay in the back lines."\n\nI turned to Xavier. "You. You stay here. Where it's safe. If things get bad, you run. Understood?"\n\nXavier just stared at me, his grip tightening on his walking stick.\n\n"Dad." He let out a shaky, hysterical laugh. "A month ago, I was playing Roblox and my biggest worry was asking Tiffany out." He flushed bright red, knowing I knew he'd been agonizing over her for weeks. "Then Mom goes all ape-shit, your high school friend gets attacked by a mob of dwarf strippers, and now I'm sucked into this strange place where some... some fucking book depository that looked like a bathroom tried to eat me."\n\nHe shook his head, his knuckles white. "No fucking way I'm staying here alone. I'm too freaked out as it is. I'm sticking with you."\n\n"Ok buddy. Just keep your head down and run if anything goes sideways," I said, seeing the new, hard line in his jaw. He wasn't asking.\n\nWe descended through the tower, moving fast, as the Archives emptied. It was a flood of impossible defenders answering a silent, psychic call. I saw beings I couldn't begin to classify—some humanoid, others decidedly not.\n\nA massive, spider-like Sinsinani skittered past us, its many legs clicking on the stone, an emissary from the Draelith webs within the tower.\n\nSquads of Arcanists of the First Order sprinted by, each wielding two weapons that hummed with infused mana, their minds already processing strategy at inhuman speeds.\n\nThey were followed by the Priests of Osilari, disciplined and silent, armed with weapons forged from gold, silver, and diamonds.\n\nBehind them, Arcanists of the Fourth Order chanted, their hands already glowing as they prepared to channel massive amounts of energy to heal the front lines.\n\nDrifting through the stone walls, ignoring the physical stampede, were the Servitors of Illkeserod, their forms becoming incorporeal ghosts as they moved to the battle.\n\nEven the mindless servitors were called; we had to dodge hulking Gravid Husks and pale, drifting Ryekan'hahs wraiths being herded toward the exit by a being that looked like a stern Chinese examiner, who I assumed was Kui Xing, the enforcer. All of them were armed with weapons that ranged from simple steel to constructs of pure light.\n\n**********\n\nBy the time we reached ground level and started the march toward Thuleskarne, we were a force of maybe two hundred. Not an army. But enough.\n\nThe journey took a full day. Time moved strangely here, but the sun—if you could call it that—tracked across the grey sky in something approximating normal. We moved through empty fields, past abandoned farmhouses, following roads that seemed to know where they were going even when we didn't.\n\nXavier limped beside me, his ankle still giving him trouble. His walking stick, carved from dark wood, and used it to take some of his weight.\n\n"You okay?" I asked.\n\n"Scared," he admitted. "But okay."\n\n"Me too."\n\nWe crested a hill just as the sun was setting. Below us, Thuleskarne spread out in a valley. Or what was left of it.\n\nSmoke rose from burning buildings. Bodies lay in the streets. And in the center of town, standing in what had probably been a marketplace, I saw her.\n\nMy wife.\n\nBut not.\n\nHer body was wrong. The proportions were stretched and compressed, as if she were a 3D model being violently manipulated by a malicious user. Her limbs were too long, her torso too short, and her entire form undulated with a low, static hum, flickering at the edges like a bad TV signal.\n\nHer face... god, her face. It was a liquid nightmare, constantly melting and reforming. One moment it was her, and the next, her features would slide like hot wax—eyes migrating to her chin, her mouth splitting vertically down her chest, her nose dissolving and reforming on her cheek only to flicker out of existence entirely.\n\nShe wore a semblance of a suit, but it was fused with her skin in places, the dark fabric bubbling and separating from the pale, shifting flesh underneath. It was drenched in gore, smeared with the visceral remains of the villagers she had torn apart.\n\nAround her, the twisted agents shambled and jerked, their own forms failing to hold shape in this realm. They were avatars of organic wrongness, glitching between states of being.\n\nSome still looked mostly human, but would suddenly shift channels—their heads replaced for a split-second with a burst of static, their limbs flickering into appendages that looked like raw, weeping meat. Others had devolved completely into nightmares, shambling things with arms where legs should be, faces that were just a collection of screaming mouths, their bodies covered in the blood and entrails of the innocents they had slaughtered.\n\nNo commands were given. None were needed. The diverse army of Archives defenders simply... formed.\n\nThey fell into place as if they had rehearsed this formation for a thousand years. The melee fighters—Arcanists and Priests—solidified into a silent front line. The long-range casters and specialists fanned out behind them, a second wave of power.\n\nI found myself ushered toward the rear of the formation with Xavier. As the front line advanced, the Servitors behind us wordlessly created a protective pocket, gently pushing us back into the nearest standing structure—what must have been a tavern—for cover.\n\nThe moment we stepped inside, the stench hit us. The building had been recently gutted. The walls were scorched, and the floor was slick with viscera and body parts, the gruesome remains of the villagers my wife and her army had rampaged through. We were now at the rear, surrounded by carnage and protected by silent, ghost-like defenders.\n\nWe were inside the ruined tavern for less than a second when her scream tore through the air, as if hiding inside the building had somehow triggered her detection.\n\n"STEPHEN!"\n\nHer voice was wrong, echoing from the center of the town, layered with a dozen other inhuman tones. She couldn't possibly have seen us, but she knew.\n\n"YOU CAN'T HIDE FROM ME! I CAN SMELL YOU! BRING ME MY SON!"\n\nThe army surged forward.\n\nThe battle was chaos.\n\nThe defenders of the Archives fought with precision and magic. Bolts of pure energy lanced through the air, striking the twisted agents. Where the magic hit, the constructs simply... unraveled. Their false forms dissolving back into the nothingness they'd been pulled from.\n\nBut the ones that were real—the actual humans caught in this nightmare—they fought back with bullets and batons and the desperate strength of people who didn't understand what was happening to them.\n\nI stood in the tavern, useless. My fists clenched. Every instinct screamed at me to do something, but I had no weapons. No powers. Nothing but the memory of karate forms from twenty years ago.\n\nBeside me, Xavier gripped his walking stick like a baseball bat.\n\n"We're just going to stand here?" he asked.\n\n"We're going to stay alive," I said. "That's what we're going to do."\n\nThe line held. Barely. The defenders pushed forward, driving the enemy back toward the center of town. Magic and steel against bullets and madness.\n\nThen the tavern's defenses shattered.\n\nA cluster of agents—some human, some glitching nightmares—burst through the broken windows and the main doorway, running straight for the back of the building. Straight toward us.\n\n"Xavier, get back!" I yelled.\n\nBut there was nowhere to run. We were cornered in the ruined taproom, the agents between us and the only exit. The Servitors protecting us moved to intercept, but two more agents were already on top of us.\n\nThe first one through looked human enough. He wore the tactical suit of the FBI agents and raised his rifle to aim at Xavier.\n\nI didn't think. I just moved.\n\nForty years of martial arts muscle memory kicked in. He was focused on Xavier, not me. I charged forward, putting all my weight into a clothesline tackle that caught him square across the chest and throat.\n\nWe crashed into the bar, the impact sending abandoned glasses scattering. He went down, hands scrambling for his shotgun clattering between us. I was faster. I ripped the weapon from his grasp, clicked off the safety, and shoved the muzzle in his face just as he tried to get up.\n\nI pulled the trigger.\n\nHis head didn't just break; it exploded. A wet spray of red mist, bone fragments, and gray matter painted the floor. But the gore sizzled in the air and dissolved before it could even drip, evaporating into oily black smoke.\n\nThe second agent—a glitching, static-filled thing with a face that wouldn't hold still—rounded the bar.\n\nI fired from the hip. The blast tore a smoking, basketball-sized hole straight through its chest, revealing the wall behind it. It looked down at the void where its torso should have been, then dissolved into a shower of static.\n\n"Dad!" Xavier swung his walking stick, connecting with another agent's knee. The crack was sickeningly loud.\n\nWe fought. Not well. Not skillfully. But desperately. The way cornered animals fight when there's nothing left but survival.\n\nI lost track of time. Everything became motion and fear and the burning ache in my injured arm as stitches tore and wounds reopened.\n\nThen I heard her.\n\n"STEPHEN!"\n\nThe tavern wall behind us exploded. Wood, plaster, and stone vaporized as my wife's distorted form smashed through it, landing in the center of the room.\n\nShe rose to her full, impossible height, her form flickering and undulating, her gaze fixed on Xavier.\n\n"Sweetheart," she said. Her voice had gone soft. Almost tender. "My beautiful boy. Come here. Come to mommy."\n\nXavier backed away, his face pale. "You're not my mom."\n\n"Of course I am." She took a step forward. Her body rippled, features sliding. "I made you. I raised you. I—"\n\n"You tried to kill us!" Xavier shouted.\n\nSomething flickered across her melting face. Confusion. Pain. Maybe even recognition.\n\n"I... I don't..." Her voice wavered. Multiple tones fighting for dominance. "You were mine. You are mine. I just... I wanted..."\n\nShe lunged. A blur of motion, aimed directly at Xavier.\n\nI moved. Threw myself between them.\n\nHer clawed hands didn't just tear through fabric and skin; they found the half-healed bullet grazes from the gym. The stitches Maeve had put in my arm and side ripped open. A fresh, searing pain exploded across my chest.\n\nI went down hard, the wind knocked from my lungs.\n\nBut it gave Xavier time. He didn't run deeper into the battle. He did what any terrified kid would do—he scrambled past the bar and bolted out of the ruined tavern, trying to find safety, to go somewhere else, anywhere else.\n\nMy wife screamed. A sound of pure, animal rage as she saw him escape.\n\nAnd then she turned her attention to our son.\n\nI tried to get up. Couldn't. My chest was on fire. Fresh blood soaked my shirt.\n\n"Xavier!" I choked out. "Xavier, run!"\n\nBut when I looked up, I saw that he wasn't running. He was standing his ground, the walking stick raised like a weapon.\n\nMy wife closed the distance in seconds.\n\n"XAVIIIEEEERRR.” In that yell we knew all too well. “You ungrateful little—"\n\nShe grabbed him. Not gently. Her distorted hands wrapped around his torso and pulled.\n\nXavier’s body began to stretch. Like some Stretch Armstrong doll pulled way too hard.\n\n"NO!"\n\nI was moving before I knew it. Running. The pain in my chest didn't matter. Nothing mattered except reaching him.\n\nBut I wasn't fast enough.\n\nA third hand, a fourth, appeared. Appendages that were more like talons of shifting, glitching matter… they plunged toward him.\n\nXavier let out a sound I will never forget—a choked gasp, his eyes wide with surprise.\n\nHis form wavered, warped, distorted to the breaking point. For a moment, I saw his very divine spark shining bright for all the universe to see.\n\nXavier's sound cut off, replaced by a silent, O-mouthed shock as he stared at what was happening to him.\n\nHis face started to relax, and then he... just... wasn't.\n\nThere was no sound, no flash of light. Just a silent pop, like a bubble bursting. The space he occupied was suddenly, completely empty. An absence.\n\nThis reality rejected him. One moment he was there. The next, he was gone.\n\nMy wife stood there, her hands still outstretched, covered with the shadow of the absence of Xavier. She stared at the empty space where he'd been.\n\n"What... what did I...?"\n\nSomething inside me snapped.\n\nNot shattered. Transformed.\n\nRage  flooded through me that wasn't human. It was older. Deeper. It was the fury of every cycle, every lifetime, every moment of pain compressed into a single, burning point. I was the shade of the avatar of a reality long since consumed.\n\nI didn't think. I reached.\n\nThe power came from somewhere primal. Somewhere I'd forgotten existed. My hands moved in patterns I didn't remember learning, and the air around me bent.\n\n"For all those days," I said, my voice not quite my own, "when I needed comfort and you weren't there."\n\nMy wife turned toward me, her melting face showing something that might have been fear.\n\n"Stephen, I—"\n\n"For every moment you made me feel worthless."\n\nThe power gathered. Purple-black energy crackling around my fists.\n\n"For every time you used my love against me.. Twisted my words and made everything about yourself."\n\nShe 