rom the chaos of the mind into the quiet of his Father's room, a solid presence in the dreamscape. He stood at the head of the bed, looking down at the man who had found him, and been found by him, twice.\n\nHis Father, in his sleep, sensed him. He opened his dream-eyes, and there was no fear. Only recognition. A circle, completed.\n\nXavier let his knowing, his purpose, and his love flow into his Father's mind, a quiet confirmation of everything they had both endured.\n\nYou passed the test, he sent.\n\nHe was no longer just a memory from a lost library. He was no longer a promise from a dying world. He was home. The hope had found its anchor.\n\nAnd then his father woke up.\n\n**********\n\nThe shift was absolute, but it was not of darkness and emptiness. It was a passage of pressure and warmth, a sudden brightness that shocked him from a dark, wet warmth into a world of sharp air and sterile light. He was no longer a thought, but a thing. He was small, helpless, and loud.\n\nHis first act was to cry out, a raw, thin wail against the sudden, overwhelming everything.\n\nMuffled voices, speaking in a language he had somehow already forgotten, surrounded him. He was wiped, weighed, and wrapped. The cold air was replaced by a soft, warm cloth. The bright, distant lights blurred. The world was a wash of sensation, none of it familiar.\n\nAnd then, he was lifted.\n\nHe was placed into a new warmth. It was not the all-encompassing hum of the library, but it was just as steady. It was a firm, solid presence. A large, warm hand splayed across his tiny back, holding him securely against a new sound—a slow, rhythmic thump-thump that vibrated through him.\n\nHis crying hitched, quieting to a small gasp.\n\nThe Father was exhausted. The last few hours had been a blur of sterile corridors, anxious waiting, and a final, terrifying, joyous rush. He sat in the quiet hospital room, the sounds of Tokyo muted by the thick glass, and looked down at the bundle in his arms.\n\nHis son.\