vive. Get to the Pyramid. We'll guide you as best we can but the interference—"\n\nThe static roars. Peaks. Then cuts to a thin, whistling tone.\n\nHer voice comes through one last time, distant and fading.\n\n"—be careful. Please be careful. We can't lose you now—"\n\nSilence.\n\nJust me. Just the fall. Just the ground rushing up to meet me.\n\nI can see the bodies clearly now. Mi-Go. Their crab-like shells split open. And beyond them, a black mass stretching to the horizon. Not sand. Not rock.\n\nCreatures.\n\nA living ocean of them. All moving. All surging toward something in the distance that my eyes can't quite focus on.\n\nThe impact is three seconds away.\n\nTwo.\n\nOne.\n\nI slam into sand.\n\nThe impact drives every molecule of air from my lungs. My ribs flex, threatening to crack. My vision goes white, then red, then a pulsing gray that feels like my brain is trying to restart itself.\n\nI gasp. Choke. Taste copper and salt.\n\nSand.\n\nI'm laying face-down in sand. Coarse. Wet. Wrong.\n\nI push myself up on shaking arms and immediately wish I hadn't.\n\nThe "rocks" I nearly impaled myself on aren't rocks.\n\nThey're bodies.\n\nThe stench hits me first.\n\nRaw. Overwhelming. It floods my sinuses and coats the back of my throat like oil. Not decay—this is too fresh for that. It's the smell of raw seafood left in the sun mixed with ammonia and something chemical, like formaldehyde cut with brine.\n\nI gag. Roll onto my side. Spit sand and bile.\n\n"What the fuck is that?"\n\nThe card pulses. Benji's voice cuts through, clearer now that I'm on solid ground.\n\n"Mi-Go," he says. "Fungoid creatures from Yuggoth. You're looking at their corpses."\n\nI stare.\n\nDozens of them. Their bodies are wrong in a way that makes my brain stutter. Chitinous shells—like crabs, but too geometric, too planned—cracked open like dropped eggs. Wet, pink meat spills onto the dunes, glistening in the twilight. Some are still twitching, their crab-like legs scratching weakly at the