eel like hiding. And it doesn't feel stolen or secret. It was... it was given to me. Birthed from love." She looked at both of us, her eyes shining. "It feels... it feels right. Like it's finally mine."\n\nShe smiled, a real, unguarded smile. "I like it. I think... I'd like to be Hannah from now on."\n\nXavier, ever the teenager, had a more pressing concern. He’d been exploring the apartment's interface, his frustration growing.\n\n"This is all great," Xavier said, looking around the comfortable apartment, "but... are there any video games here? I mean, what do people do?"\n\nHannah laughed. "Video games? Like... Super Nintendo? I think I saw someone setting one up somewhere. That’s all I remember from... you know. Back in the 1990’s in Etheris."\n\n"No," Xavier said, looking confused. "I mean... online. Like Roblox? Discord?"\n\n"Rob... lox?" Hannah looked completely blank. "Is that an internet thing? I never really got that."\n\n"It's an online platform," I said. "A whole universe of games."\n\n"Oh!" Hannah’s face lit up, catching on. "I bet we can find that. Rescued souls are showing up here every day. They're all trying to rebuild the lives they lost, use all this advanced tech out here to recreate their old lives, clone their digital files. Let's make a call."\n\nShe moved to an interface on the wall, her fingers flying across a console that looked far beyond the tech she remembered. A holographic directory appeared.\n\n"Okay, let's see... 'Rescue Resources'... 'Recreated Software'... ah, here we go. 'Games.'" She scrolled rapidly through a long list. "Roblox... Roblox... here! 'David.' It says he created the platform. Let's try him."\n\nShe tapped his name, and a moment later, a holographic video call shimmered to life. A man with bright, energetic eyes appeared, looking confused.\n\n"Hello? Can I help you? Who are..." His voice trailed off as he looked from Hannah to me, his eyes widening. "Wait. No way. You're... you're Stephen. And Hannah. Oh, wow. Everyone