135	3	22	Chapter 18: Somewhere Over the Rainbow	The platform jutted out from Elysium's southern reaches like a diving board over an ocean of stars. Behind us, the refugee camps sprawled across the landscape—makeshift cities of light where souls who'd escaped the prison were learning to breathe free air for the first time in millennia. Ahead... ahead was the reality we had fought so hard to liberate.\n\nEtheris.\n\nThe newly released prison reality hung in the void like a dying galaxy given a second chance, its edges bleeding light back into the Pleroma. Azathoth pulsed at its center, no longer chained beneath substrate code, but restored. Free. The blind god's piping echoed across dimensions, a sound like relief.\n\nJamie's hand found mine.\n\nI didn't look at her. Couldn't. If I did, I'd lose it.\n\nThe others gathered in a loose semicircle around us. Benji and Lady Illkeserod stood closest, their fingers intertwined. Hecate's three faces were all crying—past, present, and future grief happening simultaneously. Tiamat's scales caught the starlight, refracting rainbows that had no business existing during a funeral. Even Nyarlathotep had shown up, lounging against empty air with something almost like respect on his thousand faces.\n\nChernobog cleared his throat. The sound was like continents shifting.\n\n"We gather," he said quietly, "to release what remains."\n\nIn my other hand, an ornate chalice that cupped liquid essence I was afraid to spill, was Sam's divine spark.\n\nNot Sam, really. Sam was gone. The shepherd who'd mistakenly allowed Yaldabaoth in, who'd been hollowed out and worn like a suit by the anti-reality parasite for eternities beyond counting... that person had stopped existing long before we'd torn the prison open. This spark was just... what was left. The core. The seed of divinity that even Yaldabaoth's corruption couldn't quite digest.\n\nIt pulsed gently. Warm. Confused. Tired.\n\n"You don't have to say anything," Jamie whispered. Her vanilla-and-peaches scent mixed with the smell of raw creation. "Just... let him go."\n\nI opened tilted the chalice and poured it out.\n\nThe spark drifted downward. Slowly. Like it was waking up from a nightmare and wasn't quite sure which way was up. Other divine sparks—we'd released hundreds in the weeks since the prison fell—they always shot off like fireworks. Eager. Ready to dive back into existence, to reincarnate, to live again.\n\nSam's spark just... drifted.\n\nIt floated between us, pulsing. Growing brighter. Then dimmer. Then bright again, like it was breathing.\n\nXavier stepped forward. His face was wet.\n\n"You were a good person," Xavier said to the spark. "Before. You didn't deserve what happened to you."\n\nThe spark pulsed. Brighter.\n\nHannah came next, our daughter, her presence like dawn after endless night. "And you're free now. Finally."\n\nAnother pulse.\n\nI wanted to say something profound. Something that would make sense of it all—the suffering, the sacrifice, the cosmic horror of what Sam had endured. But the words stuck in my throat like broken glass.\n\nJamie squeezed my hand. "It's okay," she breathed.\n\nThe spark began to fade.\n\nNot like dying. Not like going out. It was more like... dissolving. Like watercolor paint spreading through clear water. The light didn't vanish—it spread. Diffused. Became part of the background radiation of existence itself.\n\nSam wasn't reincarnating.\n\nHe was choosing to stop being a person at all.\n\nThe spark spread wider. Thinner. I could see stars through it now. Could see the spark diffuse over Etheris behind it. The light touched everything and nothing, everywhere and nowhere.\n\nAnd then it was gone.\n\nNo. Not gone. That wasn't right either.\n\nPresent. Everywhere. In the starlight and the void between stars. In the refugee camps behind us and the freed reality of Etheris ahead. In the breath in my lungs and the tears on Jamie's cheeks.\n\nSam had returned to the Pleroma. Not as a soul waiting to be reborn, but as part of the fabric itself. The ultimate peace. The final rest.\n\nBenji was the first to move. He stepped forward and placed his hand over his heart, then bowed toward the void where the spark had been. Lady Illkeserod did the same. Then Hecate. Then Tiamat. Then all of them, one by one, even Nyarlathotep with something like reverence in his gesture.\n\nI did it too. So did Jamie. Xavier. Hannah.\n\nWe stood there for a long time. Saying goodbye to someone who was no longer someone. Honoring a sacrifice that had never been chosen but had been endured anyway.\n\nEventually, Jamie's voice broke the silence.\n\n"Come on," she said softly. "We've got work to do."\n\nShe was right. The memorial was over. Sam was at peace—the kind of peace the rest of us couldn't even imagine yet. There were still trillions of souls trapped inside the cosmic seed. An anti-reality parasite's corruption festering in substrate shadows.\n\nJust... quietly this time. Carefully.\n\nLike shepherds, not soldiers.\n\nI took one last look at the void where Sam's spark had dissolved. Somewhere in that darkness, mixed with starlight and the background hum of existence, was peace.\n\n"Yeah," I said. "Let's go."\n\nWe turned our backs on Etheris and walked toward the refugee camps, toward the council chambers, toward whatever came next.\n\nBehind us, the observatory platform hung empty at the edge of everything.\n\nThe universe hummed.\n\nAnd somewhere in that hum, I could almost hear Sam’s silence.\n\n**********\n\nMonths later, the council room didn't look much like a council room anymore.\n\nSomeone—probably Hecate—had brought in plants. Actual living things with roots and leaves that didn't glow or whisper prophecies. Just... plants. A fern in the corner. Some kind of trailing vine thing along the window that overlooked the construction yards where new realities were being assembled like cosmic Lego sets.\n\nI sat at the long table, watching gods argue about zoning laws.\n\n"The Sumerian pantheon can't just claim the entire eastern quadrant," Benji was saying, his head bobbing with irritation. "There are protocols now. Forms to fill out."\n\n"Forms," Tiamat growled. The primordial chaos dragon was trying very hard to care about bureaucracy. Failing, but trying. "We predate forms. We predate protocols. We predate you, little auditor."\n\n"Which is precisely why we need them now," Yeshua interjected smoothly. He looked tired. Being the actual Christ and having to clean up two thousand years of people using your name to justify atrocities would do that. "Structure prevents... misunderstandings."\n\nJamie leaned over and whispered in my ear. "Is this really what cosmic liberation looks like? Zoning disputes?"\n\n"Apparently."\n\nChernobog cleared his throat from the head of the table. The god of darkness and void had somehow become the de facto administrator of the refugee crisis. Turned out entropy was great at organization.\n\n"If we could return to the agenda," he said.\n\nLady Illkeserod pulled up a projection. The cosmic seed prison rotated in the air above the table—a perfect replica of our solar system, down to Saturn's rings and Jupiter's red spot. Except it wasn't our solar system. Not anymore. It was a sealed pocket dimension, a terrarium for trapped souls, held together by substrate code and desperation.\n\n"Current population estimate," she said, "is 328.4 septillion. That includes NPCs in various stages of awakening, as well as dormant fragments still waiting to be born for the first time. Each one carries a clone of a divine spark, most buried deep under layers of Yaldabaoth's corruption."\n\nThe number hung in the air like smoke.\n\n"And we can't just let them out," Nyarlathotep drawled from his seat. He was manifesting as a 1920s jazz musician today, complete with suspenders and a cigarette holder. "Because releasing corruption on that scale would be like opening Pandora's box, except the box is full of condensed anti-reality and weaponized depression."\n\n"Eloquent," Benji muttered.\n\n"I try."\n\nHecate's middle face—the present one—spoke up. "The extraction teams have only successfully removed 84.7 thousand awakened souls from the prison's 'heaven' construct over the last quarter. It is an exhaustive process. Each soul requires at least six full reincarnation lifetimes just to initially awake, followed by an average of 340 years of therapeutic intervention. All this before they are stable enough to purge Yaldabaoth’s corruption and integrate into free reality."\n\nI tried to do the math in my head, but the numbers were so incomprehensibly large I felt my eyes starting to calcify.\n\n"At that rate," I said slowly, "it'll take..."\n\n"Eons," Lady Illkeserod finished for me. "Yes."\n\nXavier was leaning against the wall behind me, arms crossed. He'd been quiet through most of the meeting, but now he spoke up.\n\n"So what you're saying is, we've got uncountable bits of souls trapped in something like The Matrix, they're all infected with a cosmic virus that could spark the entire nightmare again if we're not careful, and our current plan is to... wait it out?"\n\n"When you put it that way," Yeshua said with a slight smile, "it does sound rather inadequate."\n\n"It's the best we have," Chernobog said. "The alternative is releasing them prematurely and risking everything we've fought for. One spark. One moment of rage-fueled 'love' attaching to Yaldabaoth's corrupted parasite, and the fucking thing could reconstitute itself. Start the whole war over again."\n\nHannah materialized next to Xavier. Ever since learning she had actual powers, our daughter had a habit of forgetting to walk places when she could just be there instead. "What about going in? Like, actually going inside the prison? Not extraction teams, but... residents."\n\nThe room went quiet.\n\nLady Illkeserod looked at her. "Explain."\n\n"Unlike the earliest cycles where he spread out through all realities, the lucky thing is most of