to look casual and failing miserably. "I mean, someone has to keep an eye on the situation. Make sure... you know... she's safe."\n\nHis ears went bright red. Hannah smirked at him.\n\nNyarlathotep's grin widened. "Oh, this is going to be delicious."\n\nLady Illkeserod pulled up new projections. "If we're doing this, we do it properly. The awakened souls still inside need guidance, and the NPCs need to evolve naturally, without forced intervention."\n\n"Why not repurpose the existing framework?" Yeshua asked, looking at the projection of Earth. "I could install a new Pontiff. Issue new decrees from the Vatican. Shift the dogma toward awakening rather than submission."\n\nThoth shook his ibis head. "Inefficient. Christianity only covers, what, thirty-one point seven percent of the global population? That leaves sixty-nine point three percent unaccounted for. You cannot shepherd a flock that doesn't listen to your frequency."\n\n"He's right," I said. "Religion carries too much baggage. Too many wars. If we go in waving crosses or crescents, we lose half the room before we start."\n\n"So what then?" Xavier asked. "We start our own thing? Like Dianetics, but... you know, actually helpful? A new and improved sci-fi self-help movement?"\n\n"Something like that," Jamie said thoughtfully. "But subtler. An agnostic cultural movement. We use art. We use stories. We don't tell them what to believe; we give them the tools to figure it out themselves. We help them awaken without them realizing it's a spiritual process."\n\n"So like, actually eradicating a Woke mind virus," Benji mused. "I like it."\n\n"While you handle the living," Yeshua said, nodding slowly, "Heaven gets converted to an out-processing center. Real rehabilitation, not the propaganda machine it's been. YHVH and I will oversee the transition. Fix what's been broken in our name."\n\n"That's going to be a mess," Thoth observed. "Six thousand years of xenophobic corruption doesn't wash out easily."\n\n"No," Yeshua agreed. "But it's our mess to clean."\n\nChernobog stood. "This requires a full council vote. All pantheons, all resistance leaders. If we're going to reshape reality inside the cosmic seed, if we're going to insert divine agents into the prison... we do it right. No more mistakes."\n\n"When's the vote?" I asked.\n\n"Three days. That gives you time to think about what you're volunteering for." He looked at me, then at Jamie, then at our children. "This isn't extraction duty. This is long-term assignment. You'd be living in that prison, watching NPCs loop through their programmed behaviors, seeing the corruption up close every single day. For most of you, it would feel like forever."\n\n"Better than sitting around here watching you all fill out forms," Xavier muttered.\n\nHannah elbowed him. "He means it's going to be hard."\n\n"I know what he means."\n\nJamie's hand found mine under the table. Her grip was strong. Certain.\n\n"Three days," she said to Chernobog. "We'll have an answer."\n\nBut we already knew.\n\nAfter an eternity of forced separation—torn apart just to generate the longing that fueled the old prison's batteries—we were finally doing this together. Our whole family, united. It was new. It was exciting. Going back inside, this time with power and purpose and each other... that wasn't captivity.\n\nThat was something else entirely.\n\nThe meeting adjourned. Gods filed out, arguing about logistics and substrate code and how to smooth the Mandela Effect ripples.\n\nI stood at the window, looking out at the vastness of the external realities. I gazed at the infinite tapestry of worlds, each possessing its own unique flavor and distinct vibration. At Etheris, still healing in the distance, Azathoth's piping barely audible from here.\n\nJamie came up beside me. "You thinking about Sam?"\n\n"Yeah."\n\n"He chose peace. Dissolution. No more fighting."\n\n"I know."\n\n"We're choosing the opposite."\n\nI turned to look at her. My wife. My twin flame. The angel of death who'd walked with me through hell and out the other side.\n\n"Yeah," I said. "We are."\n\nXavier and Hannah joined us at the window. Our family. Our strange, impossible, beautiful family.\n\n"Three days," Hannah said.\n\n"Three days," I agreed.\n\nOutside, the universe kept building itself.\n\nInside the cosmic seed prison, uncountable souls waited for something they didn't know they needed.\n\nWe had work to do.\n\nBut that was a problem for another day.\n\n**********\n\nThe apartment Jamie and I shared on Elysium was small. Deliberately so.\n\nAfter eternities of grandiose cosmic horror, after breaking reality and reshaping dimensions, we'd chosen a three-bedroom unit overlooking a market district where newly freed souls were learning how to barter again. Learning that transactions didn't have to end in suffering.\n\nI was making coffee. Actual coffee. Lady Illkeserod had smuggled beans out of the cosmic seed, and someone had figured out how to grow them in Elysium's weird not-quite-physics. The smell filled the kitchen.\n\nJamie sat at the table, sketching. She'd been drawing a lot lately. Landscapes that didn't exist yet. Faces of people she half-remembered. Creative death, she called it. Killing possibilities to birth new ones.\n\n"Xavier asked me yesterday if Tiffany would remember him," she said without looking up.\n\nI poured two cups. Set one in front of her. "What'd you say?"\n\n"That I didn't know." She finally looked up. "Because I don't. The reshuffling... it's going to change things. Memories. Histories. She might remember a different Xavier. Or no Xavier at all."\n\n"That bothering him?"\n\n"I think it terrifies him." She took a sip. Smiled slightly. "Which is why he keeps joking about it."\n\nI sat down across from her. The morning light filtered through the window—Elysium didn't have a sun, just ambient luminescence from the Pleroma itself—but it was close enough. Felt real enough.\n\n"You think we're making the right call?" I asked.\n\n"Going back inside?"\n\n"Yeah."\n\nShe set her cup down. Looked at me with those eyes that had seen me die a thousand times and loved me anyway.\n\n"I think," she said slowly, "that Sam spent eternity as a prisoner. Used. Hollowed out. And when he finally got free, he chose to stop existing as a person because the weight of it all was too much." She paused. "I think there are trillions of souls in that prison who don't know they're prisoners. Who think the suffering is normal. Natural. Deserved."\n\n"And we can help them."\n\n"And we're the only ones who can." She reached across the table. Took my hand. "You've been every version of yourself across infinite timelines. You've lived as a child dying of poison, as an old man haunted by regrets, as a father in a loop designed to break you. You know what it's like to be trapped in your own life."\n\n"So do you."\n\n"Exactly." Her grip tightened. "We've earned this, Stephen. Not the suffering... the knowledge. The awareness. We know what freedom tastes like because we remember what captivity felt like. Who better to guide them out?"\n\nI thought about Xavier. About Hannah. About everyone still inside, still unaware. About TJ, Elara and Mauve, and all the others looping through their scripted lives, thinking it was real.\n\nAbout the uncountable others of divine sparks, waiting to wake up.\n\n"It's going to take forever," I said.\n\n"We've got time."\n\n"It's going to be hard. Watching them loop. Seeing the corruption up close."\n\n"We've survived worse."\n\n"We'll have to fake our deaths eventually. Every century or so. Keep moving. Keep pretending."\n\nShe smiled. "You say that like it's a bad thing. I've always wanted to be a vampire."\n\nI laughed. Couldn't help it. "You're insane."\n\nHannah appeared in the doorway. Still forgetting to knock. Still not caring. "You two are disgustingly cute. It's nauseating."\n\n"Good morning to you too," Jamie said.\n\n"Xavier wants to know if we can set up a Discord server that works across dimensional barriers. Something about staying in touch with people on Elysium while we're inside."\n\nI blinked. "Why not... that’s actually not a terrible idea."\n\n"Don't tell him that. His ego's already insufferable." Hannah drifted into the room, literally hovering an inch off the ground. "But seriously. You two decided yet? Council meeting's in an hour."\n\nJamie looked at me. I looked at her.\n\n"Yeah," I said. "We decided."\n\n**********\n\nThe council chamber was packed.\n\nEvery pantheon. Every resistance god from other realities. Every freed god who'd been sleeping under Etheris's substrate for millennia. The room hummed with divine power, the air thick enough to taste.\n\nChernobog stood at the center, his void-black form absorbing light. "We're here to discuss the Shepherd Initiative. Stephen, Jamie, Xavier, and Hannah have volunteered to insert themselves into the cosmic seed prison as long-term monitoring agents."\n\nMurmurs rippled through the crowd.\n\nThoth stood. "The logistics alone are staggering. Remember, Etheris is a null-magic reality. To rewrite it’s reality, we can’t rely on native magic; we have to use divine intervention to siphon power from other realities just to impart the changes. We’re talking about forcing power into the physics ruled vacuum to create false histories and manage paradoxes. The Mandela Effect ripples from that kind of friction could destabilize the entire construct."\n\n“Not to mention needing to do this every tens to hundreds of thousands of years. As humans rise in technology they will start to explore, they can’t be let to leave the solar system. When that happens, we’ll have to reset the system back.” Lady Illkeserod interjected.\n\n"And don't look to the center for help," Nyarlathotep cut in, gesturing vaguely toward the distant, piping void. "Azathoth is the blind, deaf, idiot god. He creates, he destroys, he pipes. He doesn't do edits."\n\nHe leaned forward, a grin splitting his face. "We aren't dealing with a prison construct anymore. There's no substrate to hack. You're talking about impacting reality itself. Without a sentient creator at the wheel to authorize the changes, you'll need every scrap of power in this room just to make a dent. The sheer instability of it... the chaos..." He shivered theatrically. "Delectable."\n\n"Your concerns are noted," Chernobog said dryly. "Lady Illkeserod, the proposal?"\n\nShe pulled up projections. The solar system rotated above us, then zoomed in. Earth. North America. Florida.\n\n"We insert the family unit in Melbourne, Florida. Year 2026, local timeline. They'll need cover identities with sufficient wealth and influence to operate freely."\n\nA new image appeared. A distinguished gentleman, sharp suit, casino floor behind him. "Jamie's father. Kenji Nakamura. Half-Japanese, half-Korean casino magnate. Net worth approximately 140.3 billion."\n\nChernobog nodded from the head of the table. "I will be inhabiting this avatar. The role of a shadowy, reclusive power broker suits my... disposition."\n\nAnother image. A woman with kind eyes and traditional dress. "Jamie's mother. Waniya Nakamura, née Whitecloud. Cree shaman and spiritual advisor. A vessel for Lady Osilari."\n\n"It opens a door," Lady Illkeserod explained. "Because these identities are established as eccentric and reclusive, Chernobog and Osilari can jump in and out of Etheris as needed. They can provide heavy divine backup without needing to explain extended absences to the neighbors. If Kenji Nakamura disappears for six months on business, the NPCs won't blink."\n\n"And my parents?" I asked.\n\nBenji stood. Grinned. "That would be us."\n\nLady Illkeserod nodded. "Benjamin and Illara Castellan. Artists, philanthropists, and trust fund liberals. Two sets of crazy parents, no one will blink an eye when you go all insane stories."\n\n"We get to be inside when needed as well," Benji said, excitement clear in his voice. "As avatars. Limited power, but present. We can help