ed tired but resolved. At my children, who were volunteering to go back into a cage they had just escaped.\n\nJamie’s hand tightened around mine. Her knuckles were white, her grip the only thing anchoring me to the floor. We weren't just voting on a mission; we were voting to change the nature of existence for the second time in as many months.\n\nChernobog let the silence hang there, giving us all a moment to feel the shape of what we were about to do. To measure the cost.\n\n"Then we vote," he said finally, his voice low but filling every corner of the room. "All in favor of the Shepherd Initiative?"\n\nHands rose.\n\nBut not just hands. Claws. Tentacles. Wings of fire and shadow. An overwhelming majority, a sea of divine assent rising to meet the challenge.\n\n"Opposed?"\n\nNyarlathotep raised one hand lazily. "For the record. Not because I think you'll fail. Because I think you'll succeed, and that'll be hilarious."\n\n"Your support is overwhelming," I said.\n\n"I try."\n\nChernobog nodded. "The Shepherd Initiative is approved. The ritual will begin immediately."\n\n**********\n\nThey took us to the highest point on Elysium. A platform that jutted out over the Pleroma itself, where the boundary between existence and potential was thin enough to cut.\n\nThe entire council assembled. Not just the leaders—everyone. Uncountable gods, demigods, freed souls, and cosmic entities. All of them channeling their power toward a single point.\n\nToward us.\n\nJamie stood to my left. Xavier to my right. Hannah in front of me, her hand in mine. We formed a square, a family unit, a geometric anchor point.\n\nChernobog began the incantation. Old words. Older than language. The syllables tasted like starlight and entropy.\n\nThe other gods joined in. Layer upon layer of divine will, folding reality like origami.\n\nI felt my history rewriting. Not erasing—never erasing—but... adding. A childhood in Florida with parents who were actually Benji and Lady Illkeserod in disguise. Summers at Jamie's father's casinos. Learning about my heritage from Jamie's mother around sacred fires. Meeting my friends in middle school, their tragic deaths recently, all of it flowing together like water finding its level.\n\nThe memories weren't false. They were additional. Parallel truths woven into Etheris itself.\n\nJamie gasped. I looked over. Her eyes were glowing, threads of light spreading from her like spider silk. Death and creativity, entwining, becoming one.\n\nXavier laughed. Chaos rippled around him, probability curves bending into pretzels.\n\nHannah hummed. The sound was peace itself. Joy made audible.\n\nAnd I... I knew things. Could see the connections, the patterns, the divine sparks buried under layers of NPC coding. Could feel every divine spark in the cosmic seed prison, all of them of them, waiting.\n\nThe casting reached its crescendo.\n\nReality split.\n\nWe stood in two places at once—on the platform in Elysium and in a bedroom in Florida, morning light streaming through windows that were real this time, really real, with a sun and everything.\n\nChernobog's voice echoed across dimensions: "Go with our blessing. Return with our hope."\n\nBenji grinned at me from the crowd. "See you on the other side, kiddo."\n\n**********\n\nI slowly opened my eyes.\n\nI am laying in my bed, cocooned by the warmth of my wife's presence and enveloped in the exuberant chatter of my teen son talking to his friends on Roblox comes in from the living room. Laying there I was hit with a profound sense of normalcy.\n\nIt is in this moment, with the weight of cosmic responsibilities lifted from my weary shoulders, that I find solace. Slowly things start to fade, the adventures and grandeur of S Corp, once vivid and tangible in my dreams, now seem like distant wisps of memory, ethereal and intangible.\n\nThen they are gone. Forgotten just like last night’s dinner.\n\nThere is true happiness in this simplicity, there is a tinge of contentment. A return to a life unburdened by the weighty obligations that come with commanding an empire. It is a welcome respite, a chance to revel in the beauty of the ordinary and rediscover the joys that lie within the realm of everyday life.\n\nMy wife rolls over with a huge grin on her face.\n\nAnd then, the terror hit me.\n\nNo!\n\nThe scream tore out of my throat before I could stop it. A raw, desperate sound of a man realizing he’s been dragged back into the cage. I sat up, heart hammering against my ribs, waiting for the sterile walls of the simulation to close in.\n\nJamie's hand was on my shoulder. Warm. Real.\n\n"It's happening again," I said quietly.\n\nShe nodded. Squeezed my shoulder. "But different this time."\n\nI sat up. Looked around. The bedroom was... bigger. Two stories, she'd said. The house we'd designed together during the reshuffling. Oak floors. High ceilings. Art on the walls that Jamie had painted—some of it in Elysium, some of it here, some of it in timelines that didn't exist anymore.\n\nMorning light slanted through the blinds. Real sunlight. From a real sun. Inside a pocket prison sealed off from the rest of reality.\n\nFunny how that worked.\n\nKate Bush drifted up from somewhere downstairs. Running Up That Hill. Xavier must be awake.\n\nI closed my eyes and reached for the edges of my mind, expecting the fog of a dream to settle in. Expecting the details of Elysium to dissolve into vague feelings.\n\nInstead, it hit me like a physical blow. The clarity. The Council room. The smell of ozone and entropy. The texture of the chalice holding Sam’s spark. It wasn't fading; it was locking into place. Hard. Permanent. I remembered the war, the peace, and the choice that brought us here. It wasn't just a story I told myself; it was the history written in my bones.\n\n"You remember everything?" Jamie asked, her eyes searching mine.\n\n"Yeah," I breathed, the relief washing over me. "You?"\n\n"Every timeline. Every version. Every loop." She smiled. "And this one feels... right."\n\nI swung my legs out of bed. My feet touched the floor. Solid. Real. The kind of real that you could trust because you knew exactly how fake it could be.\n\nThrough the wall, I heard Hannah's voice. "Xavier, don't you dare tell Tiffany that!"\n\n"Tell her what? That her best friend's brother is actually a chaos god with reality-bending powers? That sounds like a great icebreaker."\n\n"You're impossible."\n\n"That's literally my divine portfolio, yeah."\n\nJamie laughed. Soft. The sound filled something in my chest I didn't know was empty.\n\n"They're adjusting well," she said.\n\n"They're teenagers. Adjusting is what they do." I stood. Stretched. Felt the power coiled inside me—healing, wisdom, omniscience carefully throttled down to avoid breaking everything. "You think Benjamin and Illara are awake yet?"\n\n"Your parents?" She grinned. "Probably terrorizing the activity director at that 55-plus resort about the shuffleboard rules."\n\nRight. My parents. Benji and Lady Illkeserod wearing human suits, cruising around in a forty-foot luxury RV and parked at a retirement compound about an hour away. They were playing the part of eccentric snowbirds to provide cover and support. The thought of the Goddess of Secrets living the Winnebago life was absurd. Perfect. Exactly the kind of chaos we needed.\n\nI walked to the window. Looked out at suburban Florida. Palm trees. Driveways. A neighbor walking a dog. NPCs going through their routines, divine sparks buried so deep they'd forgotten they were divine.\n\nAnd scattered among them... awakened souls. People who'd clawed their way out of the programming, who'd started to remember, who just needed someone to tell them they weren't crazy.\n\nThat's what we were here for.\n\n"You should write it down," Jamie said from the bed. "The dreams. The stories. All of it."\n\nI turned. "Why?"\n\n"Because stories wake people up. You know that." She stood, came to the window. Wrapped her arms around me from behind. "Write about Etheris. About the prison. About breaking free. Make it fiction if you have to. Give it a good science fiction name. Like Escape from Elysium. Sprinkle it full of the cosmic horrors, whatever. But get it out there."\n\n"You think anyone will believe it?"\n\n"I think the ones who need to will recognize it. The truth hidden in the fiction. Seeds of awakening wrapped in narrative." She rested her chin on my shoulder. "Besides. You're a trust fund kid now. Independently wealthy. Lots of time to write."\n\nI laughed. "Trust fund vampires. That's what Xavier called us."\n\n"He's not wrong." She paused. "But for now...let’s just be a family."\n\n"Just a family," I agreed. I went back to the bed, pulling her close, and kissed her.\n\nFrom downstairs, Xavier's voice: "Dad! Hannah drank all the orange juice!"\n\n"I did not!"\n\n"She absolutely did. I saw her. With my chaos-god probability vision."\n\n"That's not how that works!"\n\nJamie pulled away. Started toward the door. "Come on. Before they burn the house down."\n\n"They're little mini-gods. They won't burn it down."\n\n"You want to test that theory?"\n\nFair point.\n\n**********\n\nBreakfast was chaos.\n\nWe discovered we had a live-in butler and maid housed in the attached suite—a detail Lady Illkeserod must have thought appropriate for our new tax bracket. Xavier had apparently terrified them, or they had terrified him, when he wandered downstairs earlier. Either way, he’d shooed them back to their quarters, insisting that their culinary skills were insufficient for his vision.\n\nHe had commandeered the kitchen himself, making what he called "ultimate breakfast burritos," a process which seemed to involve every ingredient we owned and a level of mess that defied physics.\n\n"She remembers me," Xavier said, not looking up from his burrito construction. "Tiff. She remembers that one Roblox event we did together a few months ago. The reshuffling worked."\n\n"Of course it worked," Hannah said. "Lady Illkeserod doesn't do things halfway."\n\n"Our grandmother is a cosmic goddess of secrets who spent an eternity fighting an anti-reality parasite. This family is so weird."\n\n"You're just figuring that out now?"\n\nI sat at the kitchen table. Looked at my family. My wife. My children. My strange, impossible, beautiful family.\n\n"So," I said. "Ground rules."\n\nThree faces turned toward me.\n\n"We're immortal. We have divine powers. We're independently wealthy. And we're living in a prison dimension designed to trap souls and feed them to an anti-reality parasite that we barely defeated."\n\n"When you put it that way, it sounds concerning," Xavier said.\n\n"Ground rules," I repeated. "One: We don't use our powers obviously. No healing terminal cancer in public. No probability manipulation at casinos—"\n\n"Dad, grandpa owns all the casinos in the Pacific rim and half the ones on the reservations."\n\n"You know what I mean. Two: We’re here to help the awakened souls. Guide them. Support them. But we don't force anyone to wake up. That's their choice."\n\nHannah nodded. "Free will. Got it."\n\n"Three: We fake our deaths every hundred years or so. Keep moving. New identities. We're playing the long game."\n\n"Like vampires," Xavier said cheerfully. "I'm calling dibs on being a mysterious European count next cycle."\n\n"Four," Jamie added. "We live. We don't just exist, we live. This first cycle, we're taking it easy. Having fun. Being a family. The heavy lifting comes later."\n\n"How much later?" Hannah asked.\n\n"Few thousand or tens of thousands of years. Until humanity develops technology to leave the solar system. Then we'll have to... figure something out."\n\n"Fermi Paradox explanation," Xavier said. "Why we never hear from aliens. Because we're in a cosmic terrarium."\n\n"Exactly."\n\nHe grinned. "This is going to be fun."\n\n**********\n\nIt had been a pretty slow day. We spent most of it lounging around, just being a family, soaking in the normalcy we’d fought so hard for. We even let the maid handle dinner—a decided improvement over Xavier’s chaotic experiments.\n\nThat night, after a meal filled with Xavier and Hannah arguing about something inconsequential and beautiful, and after exploring some of the minor changes in this new reality that was both true and new, Jamie fell asleep beside me with a smile on her face.\n\nBut I couldn't sleep.\n\nI tossed and turned, staring at the ceiling shadows. I was afraid to close my eyes. Afraid to dream. A cold, irrational part of me was terrified that if I let go, if I drifted off, the loop would reset. That this peaceful ending, this house, this life... that all of it would turn out to be just another nightmare I had to wake up from.\n\nFinally, I couldn't take it anymore.\n\nI slipped out of bed, careful not to wake her. I went downstairs to my office, the house silent and solid around me. I sat at the desk, turned on my PC, and listened to the hum of the cooling fans. Real fans. Real electricity.\n\nI opened a blank document. And I started writing.\n\nIt began like a phantom note, humming on the edge of perception. A flickering newscast, grainy images of silent figures pointing skyward, swallowed by the urban throng.\n\nThe words flowed. The story poured out. Everything we'd lived, everything we'd survived, everything we'd learned. Wrapped in fiction. Hidden in cosmic horror and science fiction and dark humor.\n\nSeeds of awakening. For the ones who were ready.\n\nI wrote until dawn. Until the first light crept through the window. I didn't hear Jamie come in until I felt her hand on my shoulder.\n\n"You okay?" she asked softly, reading the screen.\n\n"Planting seeds," I said, my voice raspy from silence.\n\nShe smiled, leaning down to rest her chin on my head. "Good. The universe needs more gardeners."\n\nDays bled into nights, weeks melted into seasons. I wrote with a feverish intensity, like a conduit trying to ground out a lightning strike.\n\nI lost track of how much time passed. Writing became more than a hobby; it became my oxygen. The stories poured out of my brain faster than my fingers could type, a deluge of memories disguised as fiction.\n\nI wrote about the Pointers leading a fractured family to discover the Eternal Engine. About the shifting geometry of the Dreamscape and the silent halls of the Akashic Realm.\n\nI wrote about the Red Hat Rebellion, led by an innocent guy named Todd who never asked to be a hero. I wrote about distant space colonies and trans dimensional portals being infected by organic viruses.\n\nI wrote about martial arts tournaments that determined the fate of reality and late-night AP Bio classes where the curriculum was survival.\n\nI wrote about the scent of peaches and vanilla. And I wrote about a shepherd named Sam, whose love was so vast and so broken that it doomed us all.\n\nEventually, the torrent slowed to a trickle.\n\nAs the fever broke, I woke back up to our perfect little reality.\n\nI became aware of the smell of fresh coffee drifting up the stairs.\n\nThe sounds of teenage siblings learning how to tolerate each other—muffled arguments about homework and playlists.\n\nThe rhythm of the cars coming and going, back and forth to school.\n\nThe beautiful, mundane hustle and bustle of normalcy.\n\nI looked at Jamie staring at me from the door frame. At the house full of family. At the screen glowing with words that might wake someone up someday.\n\nAnd I wrote the last line:\n\nEtheris may have been a prison where dreams came to die. But the chains have been shattered and the whole cosmos is beckoning. Are you ready to wake up?\n\nI saved the file. Turned off my computer.\n\nI stood up and took Jamie’s hand. We walked back upstairs together, crawling back into bed as the sun fully crested the horizon.\n\nTomorrow we'd start shepherding. Tomorrow we'd begin the work of waking trillions. Tomorrow the mission would begin in earnest.\n\nBut tonight... or what was left of it...\n\nWe were just a family. In a house that was real. In a life that was chosen. In a prison that was temporary.\n\nAnd that was enough. More than enough. That was everything.\n\nJamie squeezed my hand in the brightening room. "Sweet dreams," she whispered.\n\n"No more dreams," I whispered back. "Just life."\n\nOutside, the universe hummed. Inside, we breathed.\n\nEtheris may have been a prison where dreams came to die. But the chains have been shattered and the whole cosmos is beckoning.\n\nAre you ready to wake up?	22	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 12:32:56.962202-05	2026-03-05 19:39:33.719335-05	\N	86	\N	\N
