ed across the broken greenhouse floor. Glistening. Iridescent. Black like the space between stars.\n\nAnd it struck Kai where he stood, his mouth still open in that scream of impossible rage.\n\nIt entered him.\n\nThe pain was absolute.\n\nEvery cell in his body inverting. Turning inside out. His spine arched backward, vertebrae popping with wet, organic sounds. His skin bubbled. Split. Reformed.\n\nThe last android stumbled backward, its optical sensors struggling to process what they were witnessing.\n\nKai's face… ran. Features melting like wax. His jaw unhinging with a terrible tearing of cartilage. His skull warped. Expanded. The scream of NO frozen in the reforming flesh, but its meaning… inverted.\n\nThe love for his plants—that pure, specific need to protect—wasn't erased.\n\nIt was hollowed out. Refilled. Twisted into something possessive. Bottomless. Hungry.\n\nHis body convulsed. Limbs elongated. Fingers split and branched. His flesh rippled with movement from within, as if a thousand things were crawling just beneath the surface.\n\nWhat rose from the dirt of that ruined garden was no longer Kai.\n\nIt was a primal, festering gash wearing the shadow of form. Eyes shattered like crystalline shards. Black. Reflecting Sorhhinda's three suns in impossible angles.\n\nIt saw the remaining spatch weed plants. The few that had survived. Still trembling. Still afraid.\n\nAnd for the first time, it wanted.\n\nNot to nurture. Not to protect.\n\nTo possess. To consume. To make them part of itself so they could never be taken again.\n\nThe android tried to run.\n\nThe thing that had been Kai was faster.\n\nRoots erupted from its body—not plant roots, but something else. Tendrils of flesh and anti-reality, wrapped in the stolen memory of chlorophyll and cellulose. They lashed out. Wrapped around the android's metal frame.\n\nAnd pulled.\n\nThe android came apart. Not destroyed. Unmade. Its components dissolving into constituent atoms, then into nothing at all.\n\nThe thing stood in the center of the ruined compound. Sorhhinda's three suns painted it in colors that shouldn't exist.\n\nIt opened what had been Kai's mouth.\n\nI AM.\n\nThe words came out layered. Multiple voices speaking as one. The voice of a gardener. The voice of something vast and ancient. The voice of love turned inside out.\n\nAround it, the garden began to change. The soil darkened. The surviving plants twisted, their stems thickening, their leaves sharpening into blades. They were no longer growing toward the light.\n\nThey were growing toward it. While spreading all across the surface of the planet.\n\nFeeding it. Sustaining it. Becoming part of the thing that now stood at the center of its own new creation.\n\nIn the distance, warning sirens began to wail. Then they flickered and faded as the root system spread over the entire planet.\n\n**********\n\nSomewhere in the infinite tapestry of realities, Sophia felt it. Another thread. Another corner where love and anti-reality collided.\n\nAnother shepherd. Another flock.\n\nAnother wound in creation's skin.\n\nShe closed her eyes and wept.\n\nBecause in an infinite cosmos full of infinite realities…\n\nEverything that can happen will happen.\n\nAnd already has.	23	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 12:33:14.303832-05	2026-03-05 19:39:33.719335-05	\N	88	\N	\N
137	3	24	Afterward (About The Towers of Elysium Series)	So that’s that.\n\n30 days of writing. 30 endless nights of sweating and vomiting out the latest creation from the elephants in my head during the ProWritingAid’s 2025 NovNov 30 day writing challenge.\n\nAnd if you’ve stayed with me through this journey, congratulations!\n\nIf we backup and start at the beginning, I’ve always been a fan of Science Fiction, and so the idea for The Towers of Elysium started way back when I was still knee high to my father’s belt buckle. But, the concept of a cosmic horror induced dream hallucination and time loop, backed by technology and a penchant for being overly descriptive didn’t start formulating until working through the Abramelin process and falling down the Simeon Necronomicon rabbit hole.\n\nSuddenly, it was then that I realized I had tapped into something much deeper. My dream journals took on new meaning, and the idea of a dream born reality bounded by some cosmic entity took shape.\n\nInitially the Elysium story was just a backstory, a short story that was supposed to kick off a whole series of books that took place thousands of years in the future, generations after S Corp kicked off the whole process. The story was going to be something like Asimov’s Foundation, where we see the effects of a civilization in decay even with god like powers.\n\nBut then I went to go write the start to the book, and the Pointers sprung into existence. Suddenly I had 5 introduction chapters for this idea of a short story, that demanded I follow this to it’s natural conclusion. Instead of just popping the Eternal Engine and Infinitrons into existence (with no explanation), I suddenly had to figure out how to go from Pointers to these items of power, and some how end with us living in a simulation.\n\nWhen I first completed the alpha version of Elysium Rising in 2024, it had ballooned to over 400k words. Getting that published was going to be a nightmare (even self publishing), so I spent the next several months ripping and tearing it to try and get it closer to 150k limit that most books of it’s genre followed (I’m no Brandon Sanderson). But in doing so, I ended up keeping a lot of the flavor stories, and removing a bunch of other side story narrative holes I went down.\n\nDid you know that Todd was an addition (initially I had based his character on one insanely rich Techno-Bro but when I rewrote him economically swapped to the bottom of the social ladder). I also excised a lot of his personal journey and only kept him in during the highlight sessions. So now the world will never know why the red hats were chosen as their symbol.\n\nOr that Aditi had her own adventures and story arcs (intersecting with Kai’s family), and that each of my “friends” who worked at S-Corp was a counterbalance to the “awakened” entities – with the original draft highlighting how my friends actions triggered the entities awakening.\n\nThen there was the whole dream scape side of things, training, coming into power, learning about all the crazy bits happening out there beyond Etheris. But more importantly, the whole origination and time-looping nature of the Pointers and their ties into the mandala effect.\n\nLong story short, in a rush to get something out and tell the whole initial story (tech leader becomes emperor and then resets the entire universe), I crammed what should have probably been 3-4 books into one longer than needed book that omitted a ton of the development I had originally put out there, and a bunch of exposition instead of story.\n\nBut it was a story.\n\nIt was done.\n\nAll the bits and pieces were extracted from my brain and Elysium was done. Nothing more to tell from it (aside from maybe publishing some of the side stories later if the series really was good).\n\nAnd I thought the cliffhanger epilogue was a great way to just wrap everything up with a bow.\n\nFast forward then to April 2025 and the AutoCrit 90 day writing challenge. I had no story idea. So I just started writing.\n\nSuddenly I was back in the world of The Towers of Elysium and wanted to just see where the story would have gone there if things had continued. I quickly wrote myself into a corner, the beginning there was just a depressing ending to what should have been an eventful life.\n\nThat’s when my son kicked me in the head (literally, I was doing something under the couch and he ran into me) and I came up with the let’s go back into the prison but from another angle approach.\n\nThis time, however, I gave my protagonist a name. And started fleshing things out – and by doing so, it was a symbol of the protagonist no longer being just an NPC (like they were portrayed in the first book), but a living breathing person.\n\nThis time, unlike NPCs in the previous book, the characters in the book started taking on lives of their own, and things went off the rails (my initial outline didn’t even include Whitney, but was rather a story of what would you do if you could re-live high school all over again and knew that the world was a simulation).\n\nNext thing you know I’ve got girls fighting over me (who in the initial drafts were just nameless folks), and a demon auditor trying to make contact.\n\nBy the time I finished Elysium Fallen, I knew there was one more story to tell here – and to get this to a meaningful ending, I needed to escape from the prison once and for all.\n\nNotice that in Escape from Elysium, my son, who now gets a name, isn’t an NPC, but my wife, who still remained nameless, did (and unfortunately if you didn’t pick up on that, it would have been clearer from one of the lost chapters from Elysium Rising – only entities had names and conversely that’s why their counterparts had names as well).\n\nAnd now, I’ve got to wrap everything up, pull all the cosmogony together, and ensure that nothing I wrote about in the first two books contradicted things I wrote in the final book (plus all those call back scenes). In the end we’re in a universe where the books themselves are artifacts of those universes (hence the first person narration), but because they exist in this universe, they can be seen as gateways into there.\n\nThen there’s the whole issue of the AI covers and Eleven Reader audio books.\n\nMuch like my protagonist, I’m dirt poor. So had to scrimp and save to get the cover designed for my first two books. Unfortunately, money doesn’t grow on trees and I don’t have a matter replicator here, so I’ve not been able to afford the art for the final cover. Not to mention half of the readers don’t like reading, and want an audio book version.\n\nSo, I’m throwing it up on Eleven Labs (for the time being), so I can have an audiobook version available for you lazy bums out there – because for me, having people read the story is more important than earning money. Once I get back here in January, I’ll start reading the books, posting the chapters up to YouTube, and eventually compile it, and sell the whole bundle on Audible when it's completed. By then, hopefully I’ll have found another job, can pay my designer, and get them to replace the final artwork.\n\nMaybe then, I can even afford an editor or two, and work with them to release a new “special edition” version of the series – this time spanning Elysium Rising into 4 books, and then working through all the developmental and line editing issues. While I may be great at creating stories, writing, and even editing short stories – doing that for these long form books, yeah, I’m not an editor and I don’t have an MFA or solid grasp of English.\n\nThen there’s the whole marketing component, and don’t get me started on that – folks are either going to love the book and tell folks about it so it spreads organically, or I’m letting it die in the womb for some future generation to rediscover it, let it become a cult classic, and folks can visit my grave and put MTG cards on it in tribute.\n\nI’m not planning on dying anytime soon (so if anything does happen, look to the Vatican or Illuminati, those agents of Yaldabaoth here who’re trying to silence the TRUTH), and if I do, I’m going to reincarnate into my protagonist here in this book so I can be reunited with my one true love…	24	ai_extracted	2026-02-27 12:33:31.016824-05	2026-03-05 19:39:33.719335-05	\N	89	\N	\N
