380	COO (Saboteur)	t	The Chief Operating Officer operates as a hidden saboteur within the corporate infrastructure, wielding system credentials not to build but to destroy. Though physically absent during the crisis, their presence permeates the disaster through embedded malicious code—a signature of reckless tinkering masquerading as legitimate access.	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	The COO falsely blames the protagonist to the CEO: 'the COO says it's because of you.' Later, the protagonist discovers 'a script tied to the COO's credentials' that 'derails critical processes, blocking every fix we attempted.' The protagonist notes 'This wasn't an accident; it was yet another example of the COO's reckless tinkering.' The COO is 'conspicuously absent when things turned catastrophic' and remains 'a ticking time bomb' as long as they retain system access.	Embedded a malicious script within critical infrastructure tied to their credentials; deflected blame onto protagonist by manipulating CEO's trust; remains strategically absent during crisis aftermath.	Unlike a typical antagonist who confronts openly, the COO operates through sabotage and absence—a more insidious threat because protagonist cannot definitively act against them without exposing themselves. The COO represents systemic corruption: access without accountability, power without responsibility. Their ability to cause cascading infrastructure failure while remaining insulated from consequences makes them uniquely dangerous.	23	claude	claude-haiku-4-5-20251001	2026-02-27 13:17:55.447677-05	2026-03-14 18:06:15.112419-04	2	\N
