421	Oblivion the Memory Thief - Devourer of Waking Worlds	t	A cosmic force of erasure that manifests across both dream and waking realms, systematically unraveling identity, meaning, and consciousness itself. It operates through psychological fragmentation, corporate machinery, and existential dissolution—making it simultaneously omnipresent and imperceptible. Its presence corrupts reality's fabric, turning memories into sand and purpose into void.	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Oblivion appears as both 'Manifestation' and 'The Void of Mundanity - Devourer of Souls and Meaning,' positioning it as the chapter's ultimate existential threat. It encompasses the protagonist's dissolution through workplace trauma, dream realm decay, and the erasure of identity itself. The entity transcends singular antagonism to become the chapter's foundational catastrophe.	Silence. The absence of sound, memory, and self. It erases not through violence but through the systematic forgetting of what made one human—leaving only the hollow echo of 'The Void of Mundanity' consuming all meaning.	Unlike The COO (corporate saboteur) or The Nightmares (psychological manifestations), Oblivion is irreducible and omniscient. It operates across all domains—corporate offices, dream journals, waking trauma, and the spaces between thought and void. It is not defeated but only temporarily held at bay by defiance and clarity, making it the chapter's true final boss.	\N	claude	claude-haiku-4-5-20251001	2026-02-27 13:24:56.190477-05	2026-03-14 18:06:15.112419-04	2	\N
