1	Yaldabaoth Variant — The Shepherd's Echo [Class E]	t	A faint psychic residue of Sam's original mortal grief, barely tethered to existence. It manifests as a weeping silhouette clutching a broken shepherd's crook, flickering in and out of visibility like a dying signal. Its form is translucent and fraying at the edges, shedding wisps of shadow that dissolve before touching the ground. The faintest outline of a boy's face can be seen beneath the static — mouth frozen mid-sob, eyes hollow with the memory of watching his flock die. It is less an enemy and more a scar left on reality, a wound that never closed.	Inconsolable grief twisted into a low, throbbing ache of proto-hunger. The sorrow is authentic but decaying — what was once a boy's heartbreak is slowly curdling into something that wants to feed.	A thin, reedy wail that rises and falls like wind through a canyon. Occasionally, the distorted bleating of dying sheep echoes beneath the cry. Static hiss punctuates every silence.	Wet wool and copper. The faintest trace of lanolin and blood, as if a flock had been slaughtered centuries ago and the scent refuses to fade.	\N	Passive psychic drain on nearby consciousness; induces waves of melancholy and despair in a small radius. Can briefly solidify into a cold grasp that saps willpower. Extremely fragile — dissipates under sustained attack but reforms slowly if not fully purged.	The origin point of all that Yaldabaoth became. Before the hunger, before the divine pretense, there was a boy named Sam who watched his flock die and felt something inside him break so completely it tore a hole in the fabric of what is.	"My sheep... my sheep are dying... why won't anyone help me..."	Class E — the weakest manifestation. Unlike all other Yaldabaoth variants, this one retains a fragment of genuine mortal emotion. It is pitiable rather than terrifying, a ghost of the person the Demiurge consumed to become what it is. Defeating it feels less like victory and more like mercy.	\N	an