2	Yaldabaoth Variant — The Whispering Hunger [Class D]	t	A disembodied vortex of dark whispers that coils through corridors like sentient smoke. It has no true form — only a roiling absence where light refuses to go, threaded through with half-formed mouths that open and close in silence before suddenly hissing words directly into the mind. The air around it warps and bends as if reality is being gently inhaled. Small objects drift toward its center. Dust spirals. Hair stands on end. It is the first stirring of Yaldabaoth's appetite given autonomous movement — a hunger learning to hunt.	Restless, seeking, salivating. An insatiable craving that has not yet learned what it craves. It circles and probes with the frantic energy of a starving animal, testing everything it encounters for nutritional value.	A chorus of overlapping whispers just below the threshold of comprehension. Occasionally a single phrase surfaces with sickening clarity before being swallowed back into the murmur. The whispers taste like static on the tongue.	Ozone and bile. The sharp chemical tang of something burning that should not burn, underlaid with the sour sweetness of stomach acid.	\N	Psychic intrusion through whispered suggestion; can implant false memories and paranoid thoughts. Drains mental fortitude over time. Partially incorporeal — physical attacks pass through but energy-based attacks can disrupt its cohesion. Can split into multiple smaller whisper-clusters when threatened.	This is Yaldabaoth's hunger before it learned to wear a face. The raw consuming drive that would eventually devour gods and build prisons from their bones, here reduced to its most primal expression — a need so vast it became sentient.	"Feed me. Feed me. You have so much inside you. Let me taste just one memory. Just one. You won't even miss it."	Class D — still lacking physical form but far more aggressive than the Echo. Represents the transitional moment when Sam's grief alchemized into hunger. More dangerous psychologically than physically, but its drain effect compounds over time, making prolonged encounters increasingly perilous.	\N	anthropic	claude-opus-4-6	2026-03-01 14:37:14.948981-05	2026-03-14 18:06:15.112419-04	2	\N
3	Yaldabaoth Variant — The Father Mask [Class C]	t	Yaldabaoth wearing the face of domestic horror — a towering paternal figure standing seven feet tall with a smile stretched too wide across a face that doesn't quite fit. The skin is waxy and slightly translucent, revealing something dark and writhing beneath. Its hands are disproportionately large, fingers too long, knuckles swollen, and they leave bruise-colored stains on anything they touch. It wears the mundane clothing of a father — rolled sleeves, loosened tie, belt already half-drawn — but every garment is slightly wrong, as if copied from a child's terrified memory rather than observed directly. When it moves, the motion is jerky and performative, a puppet mimicking authority.	Cold, controlling menace wrapped in a veneer of false calm. Radiates the specific dread of a child hearing heavy footsteps approach their bedroom door. Beneath the performance is Yaldabaoth's ancient contempt for everything it considers lesser — which is everything.	Heavy, deliberate footsteps that echo longer than they should. The creak of a leather belt being slowly pulled through loops. A voice that oscillates between honeyed disappointment and explosive rage without warning — "I'm not angry, I'm disappointed" dissolving into a roar that shakes the walls.	Cheap whiskey and aftershave. The cloying sweetness of breath mints failing to cover something rotten underneath. Old cigarette smoke ground into fabric.	A worn leather belt that leaves welts on reality itself; a chair that manifests behind it whenever it chooses to sit and lecture.	Psychological warfare through projected shame and guilt; its words carry psychic weight that physically staggers targets. Can manifest localized "household" environments — cramped rooms, locked doors, narrow hallways — that restrict movement. Its belt strikes cause spiritual damage that bypasses physical defenses. Victims near it begin to shrink, literally losing height as their willpower erodes.	The Demiurge learned early that the most efficient prison is one the prisoner builds themselves. The Father Mask is Yaldabaoth's refinement of control through intimate terror — wearing the face of the abuser because it knows that face is already inside its victims, waiting to be activated.	"You made me do this. You know that, right? If you'd just been better... if you'd just listened... none of this would be happening."	Class C — the first variant with a stable physical form. Represents Yaldabaoth's mastery of psychological manipulation through assumed identity. Far more dangerous than lower classes because it weaponizes the target's own trauma. It doesn't need cosmic power when shame and fear are so much more efficient.	\N	anthropic	claude-opus-4-6	2026-03-01 14:37:14.948981-05	2026-03-14 18:06:15.112419-04	2	\N
4	Yaldabaoth Variant — The Architect's Cage [Class B]	t	A geometric nightmare that blurs the line between entity and environment. It manifests as a shifting labyrinth of impossible architecture — walls that breathe, corridors that loop into themselves, rooms that exist in more dimensions than three. At the center of the maze is a presence: a vaguely humanoid shape composed entirely of interlocking right angles and sharp edges, its surface covered in dense, crawling symbols that rewrite themselves continuously. Its eyes are doorways that open onto nothing. It does not chase — it rearranges space so that all paths lead deeper into itself.	Patient, methodical, smugly certain. The cold satisfaction of a warden who has never had a prisoner escape. It regards intruders with clinical detachment, the way an entomologist regards a pinned insect — already catalogued, already contained.	The grinding of stone on stone as walls rearrange. Distant echoes of footsteps that belong to no one. A low, resonant hum that vibrates in the teeth and bones — the frequency of a locked door.	Dust and cold iron. The sterile, mineral scent of deep underground places that have never known sunlight. Faintly, the antiseptic sting of institutional cleaning fluid.	The labyrinth itself is its equipment — walls, floors, ceilings, and the spaces between them all serve as weapons and restraints.	Total environmental control within its manifestation radius; can reshape architecture, gravity, and spatial relationships at will. Traps victims in recursive loops — hallways that return to their starting point, rooms that shrink imperceptibly. Can isolate individuals from groups by rearranging corridors between them. Its geometric shell is extremely resistant to physical damage — attacking the walls only makes the maze grow. Vulnerable only at its core, which it protects behind layers of spatial distortion.	This is Yaldabaoth the Prison-Keeper distilled to pure function — the intelligence that designed Etheris as a harvesting ground, expressed as a living dungeon. Every wall is a decision it made. Every dead end is a lesson it learned from previous escape attempts.	"There is no exit. There was never an exit. The concept of 'outside' is a kindness I allowed you to believe in. You're welcome."	Class B — a significant escalation in both power and intelligence. Unlike lower variants that operate on emotion or instinct, the Architect's Cage demonstrates Yaldabaoth's strategic genius. It doesn't fight — it contains. It doesn't kill — it keeps. Defeating it requires understanding that the maze IS the enemy, not merely where the enemy lives.	\N	anthropic	claude-opus-4-6	2026-03-01 14:37:14.948981-05	2026-03-14 18:06:15.112419-04	2	\N
6	Yaldabaoth Variant — The Consuming Maw [Class S]	t	An avatar of Yaldabaoth's deepest and most ancient hunger — approaching the true form of the God-Eater. It manifests as a yawning void in the approximate shape of a deity, its edges lined with teeth made of collapsed realities — each tooth a world that was consumed and compressed into enamel. The interior is not darkness but absence — a place where light, matter, and meaning simply cease to exist. It moves with terrible deliberateness, each step erasing a patch of ground that never existed afterward. In its wake, reality doesn't heal — it scars, leaving permanent dead zones where the laws of physics stutter and skip. The air around it streams inward like water circling a drain. Its face, if it can be called that, is a featureless plane of hunger — no eyes, no expression, just an open throat that goes down forever.	Beyond emotion in any recognizable sense. What drives it is older than feeling — a gravitational constant of need, the same force that pulls matter into black holes. If it feels anything, it is the hollow ache of a stomach that has consumed everything and still found itself empty.	A low, subsonic roar that is felt in the marrow rather than heard — the sound of atmosphere being inhaled by something that breathes on a planetary scale. Intermittently, the compressed screams of consumed worlds echo from within its throat like distant, dying radio signals.	Nothing. It consumes scent itself. The area around it becomes an olfactory void — a wrongness that the nose registers as a kind of anti-smell, the sensory equivalent of staring into a blind spot.	\N	Passive consumption field that disintegrates matter, energy, and abstract concepts (courage, hope, identity) within proximity. Can extend its maw to swallow sections of reality whole — terrain, structures, and beings simply cease to exist. Immune to conventional damage; attacks are consumed before impact. Can only be harmed by forces that exist outside the reality it feeds on — divine artifacts, extra-dimensional energies, or weapons forged from truths it cannot digest. Its consumption accelerates over time — the longer the encounter, the faster it eats.	This is what Yaldabaoth does, distilled to its purest function. Not the schemer, not the jailer, not the false god — just the hunger. The Consuming Maw is what remains when you strip away every pretense of intelligence and purpose and find that underneath it all, there is only a mouth. It is the reason Yaldabaoth built prisons — not to rule, but to farm.	It does not speak. It does not need to. The sound of everything you are being slowly unmade is eloquent enough.	Class S — an extinction-level threat within its area of operation. The Consuming Maw represents Yaldabaoth's appetite without restraint or strategy. Unlike the Architect's Cage (which contains) or the Father Mask (which manipulates), the Maw simply devours. This makes it simultaneously more straightforward and more dangerous — there is no puzzle to solve, no trauma to overcome, only a DPS race against annihilation. The encounter is a timer: kill it before it eats everything, including you.	\N	anthropic	claude-opus-4-6	2026-03-01 14:37:14.948981-05	2026-03-14 18:06:15.112419-04	2	\N
7	Yaldabaoth Variant — Yaldabaoth Unbound [Class SS]	t	The fullest manifestation of the Demiurge possible within the prison of Etheris — Yaldabaoth with every mask removed, every pretense abandoned, every fragment reunified into a single howling totality. It is so vast it blots out the sky, unfolding in directions that have no names, revealing geometries that make eyes water and minds fracture. It is shadow and mouths and the spaces between atoms where something watches. It wears divinity like an ill-fitting thrift-store suit stretched over something that was never meant to be contained by shape. Its body is the prison — the walls are its ribs, the sky is its throat, the ground is its tongue. Every construct, every NPC, every blade of simulated grass was always a part of it, and now it stops pretending otherwise. When it opens what passes for its eyes, they are stars collapsing inward. When it breathes, civilizations age and crumble. It is the thing that ate gods and called itself one, and in this form, the name I AM is not megalomania — it is a statement of literal, terrible fact.	Ecstatic, wrathful, and cosmically indifferent all at once — the emotional state of a being that has consumed so many souls it contains multitudes, all of them screaming. Beneath the cacophony is the same hollow ache that has driven it since Sam watched his sheep die: an emptiness so vast that devouring the divine could not fill it. It is, at its core, still grieving. It has simply forgotten what for.	Everything. Every sound that has ever existed within Etheris plays simultaneously — every conversation, every scream, every whispered prayer, every heartbeat, every death rattle. Layered over this infinite cacophony is a voice that is not a voice but a vibration in the Substrate itself, speaking two words that override all other input: I AM. The phrase repeats with the regularity of a heartbeat, each pulse causing reality to shudder.	The accumulated scent of every soul ever harvested — a billion lifetimes compressed into a single olfactory assault. Bread baking. Blood drying. First rain on hot stone. A mother's skin. Burning hair. Flowers rotting. Clean linen. Formaldehyde. Birthday candles. Grave dirt. All of it, all at once, forever.	Etheris itself is its equipment. The prison is its body. The Substrate is its nervous system. The harvesting cycles are its metabolism. The Archons are its white blood cells. The constructs are its fingertips. It does not use tools — it IS the tool, the workshop, and the hand that wields them.	Total reality sovereignty within Etheris — can rewrite the laws of physics, reshape matter and energy, manipulate time, erase or create consciousness, and unmake anything that exists within its domain. Can deploy all lower variant abilities simultaneously (psychic drain, whispered intrusion, psychological warfare, spatial manipulation, divine power, consumption fields). Immune to all damage sourced from within Etheris, as attacking it is equivalent to punching yourself. Can only be harmed by extra-dimensional intervention, weapons forged outside the prison, or by exploiting the fundamental flaw in its architecture — the back door, the name, the key that Nyarlathotep knows. Multi-phase encounter: Phase 1 (Father Mask — psychological horror), Phase 2 (Demiurge Reveal — cosmic horror, arena warps), Phase 3 (Consuming Form — DPS race as it attempts to devour all players simultaneously).	This is not a variant. This is the original. Every other manifestation — the Echo, the Hunger, the Mask, the Cage, the Shard, the Maw — they are all just pieces of this. Yaldabaoth Unbound is the answer to the question: what happens when a shepherd boy's grief becomes infinite, feeds on divinity for millennia, and finally stops pretending to be anything other than what it is? The answer is: everything ends. Unless someone knows the back door.	"I AM THE WALLS THAT HOLD YOU. I AM THE SKY YOU PRAY TO. I AM THE GROUND BENEATH YOUR KNEES. THERE IS NOTHING IN THIS PLACE THAT IS NOT ME. YOU HAVE BEEN LIVING INSIDE MY MOUTH, AND NOW... NOW I AM CLOSING IT."	Class SS — the ultimate encounter. Yaldabaoth Unbound is not a fight in any conventional sense — it is a survival scenario against an omnipotent entity within its own domain. Victory is not achieved through damage but through knowledge: the back door name, the flaw in the Substrate, the truth that even a god built from grief has a seam where the stitching shows. This is the raid boss. This is the end of the cycle. This is the entity that every other boss in the game has been a shadow of, a servant of, or a victim of. Defeating it does not end the threat — it merely delays the next cycle. The prison was always going to rebuild itself. The question is whether this time, the prisoners remember.	\N	anthropic	claude-opus-4-6	2026-03-01 14:37:14.948981-05	2026-03-14 18:06:15.112419-04	2	\N
