170	47	1	The Breakthrough and First Crossing	The narrator experiences a breakthrough inspiration while watching TV with his son, leading to the successful creation of an Einstein-Rosen Bridge. He tests the portal by crossing through it, experiencing both triumph and terror as the unstable gateway collapses moments after his passage.	The infuriating mystery of successfully forming an Einstein-Rosen Bridge (ERB) consumed my every waking moment, each failed attempt crushing my spirits even more. It was that one fateful night, whilst enjoying Gravity Falls with my son, when a spark of inspiration ignited within me. The show’s eerie mix of science and the supernatural triggered a breakthrough idea in my mind: connecting two spacetime locations via quantum entanglement. This could be it - the answer I have been searching for all this time.\n\nOver in the warehouse, the hum of the quantum stabilizer let loose a quiet, ominous growl, vibrating through the air as I stared into the shimmering void of the Einstein-Rosen Bridge. It pulsed with an otherworldly light, the warped fabric of space-time stretching and folding before my eyes. This was it—the culmination of endless calculations and several dozen failed replicated devices that disintegrated under the energy constraints. My heart hammered in my chest, torn between exhilaration and dread.\n\nThe gateway flickered, a delicate dance of stability and chaos. Every atom of my being screamed caution, but I couldn’t turn back now. Adrenaline coursed through me as I stepped forward, crossing the threshold into the unknown. In an instant, everything shifted—a stomach-turning lurch as reality bent around me. Then, with a flash of disorienting brilliance, I emerged.\n\nThe other side of the warehouse materialized in front of me, ordinary and yet extraordinary. I had done it. Against all odds, I had opened a bridge through space-time and stepped through unscathed. Triumph surged through me, but it was short-lived. The air around the portal grew heavy, the once-steady hum turning into a dissonant whine.\n\nI turned just in time to see the gateway tremble, its light stuttering and collapsing inward. Panic gripped me as the fabric of the bridge began to unravel, the once-stable connection tearing itself apart. The implosion came seconds later with a deafening roar, a cascade of energy that threatened to consume everything in its wake. I dove to the ground as the portal collapsed, the air crackling with residual energy that scorched the floor where it once stood.\n\nGasping for breath, I pressed my forehead to the cool concrete, the realization of what had just happened washing over me. I had narrowly escaped death. The power I had wielded was volatile, unpredictable—a stark reminder that I was treading dangerous ground.	0	176	f	2026-02-27 12:00:50.774866-05	2026-02-27 16:00:11.118498-05	normal	\N
176	47	7	Reunion Through the Stars	Bursting with excitement, the narrator wakes his wife and brings her to the warehouse, revealing the stable gateway to Kyoto. They step through together and emerge in her mother's backyard in Japan, creating an emotional reunion that validates all his sacrifices and risks.	I raced home, bursting through the door and slamming it behind me. “Wake up!” I called, shaking my wife gently but insistently.\n\nShe groaned, burying her face in the pillow. “It’s too early for this,” she muttered.\n\n“You’ll want to see this,” I insisted, practically pulling her out of bed. Groggy and half-protesting, she followed me to the car. I drove like a man possessed, my excitement spilling out in bits of hurried explanation as she gave me skeptical glances from the passenger seat.\n\nWhen we arrived at the warehouse, her annoyance gave way to stunned silence. In the corner of the room, the ERB gateway shimmered, defying the constraints of time and space. Without hesitation, I took her hand and led her through.\n\nThe transition was seamless, the sensation fleeting, and instantaneously we were standing in the cool night air of Kyoto, Japan. Her eyes widened in disbelief as we stepped into her mother’s backyard, the familiar surroundings bathed in moonlight.\n\n“Come on,” I urged, pulling her around the house toward the front door.\n\nHer mother opened the front door. The shock on her face mirrored the awe we felt. “Ka-san!” my wife exclaimed, her voice breaking as tears welled in her eyes.\n\nHer mother’s gaze shifted between us and the strange structure in her back yard. “How... how did you get here?” she asked in Japanese, her voice trembling with wonder.\n\n“We walked,” I said with a grin, earning a tearful laugh from my wife as she embraced her mother.	6	182	f	2026-02-27 12:00:50.774866-05	2026-02-27 16:05:13.20571-05	normal	\N
328	82	2	Expansion into the Void	Humanity recovers and begins ambitious interstellar expansion, establishing outposts across the universe and making groundbreaking scientific discoveries, with Morgan and TJ revealing the construction of a station at the edge of the visible universe.	**********\n\nIt didn’t take long for humanity to recover. Within months, after years of being on a war footing, people just started living again. Mundane things, like going out to dinner, or even just doing the dishes didn’t seem so strange.\n\nIn this newly restored atmosphere of peace and reason, we got back to the really fun shit. Exploring space and expanding the S Corp empire. We launched new missions to unexplored regions of space, venturing into the unknown with eager anticipation. We established additional colonies on distant planets and moons, in locations as far as we could reach, remote galaxies and even further galactic clusters, pushing the boundaries of human existence further than ever before. Our existing outposts flourished under our diligent care, evolving into thriving hubs of innovation and discovery.\n\nAt the same time we were expanding outward, our scientific teams made groundbreaking discoveries across a multitude of fields. From the intricate realm of astrophysics to the innovative arena of bioengineering, we delved into the depths of knowledge, unearthing hidden gems that propelled humanity into uncharted territories. Even the power of the Eternal Engine and Infinitron would seem like stone age tools compared to the reality altering breakthroughs we made.\n\n“We did it!” Morgan burst into the room, her voice bright with exhilaration, TJ close on her heels.\n\nTheir excitement was infectious. Morgan’s eyes sparkled with triumph, her face flushed as if she had just run a marathon of discovery. Even TJ, usually composed and deliberate, wore an uncharacteristic grin, his childlike enthusiasm shining through.\n\n“We’ve established an outpost,” Morgan announced, her voice trembling with awe. “Thirteen-point-eight billion light-years away. At the edge of the visible universe. We’re calling it ‘The Restaurant at the Edge of the Universe.’”\n\nTJ activated a holographic projection with a wave of his hand. The room dimmed, and before us, a shimmering model of the outpost floated in the air. Its sleek, futuristic architecture seemed to pulse with energy, a beacon of humanity’s determination and ingenuity. The design was breathtaking, a harmonious blend of advanced technology and organic elegance, as if it were part of the cosmic tapestry itself.\n\nTJ’s voice was low, almost reverent. “It’s extraordinary. To think we’ve reached this far, witnessing the birth and death of stars along the way. It’s... humbling.”\n\nMorgan pointed to another holographic display, her voice soft but brimming with wonder. “And there,” she said, gesturing toward a faint, almost imperceptible glow beyond the visible galaxies, “lies another 13.8 billion light-years of uncharted territory. A vast ocean of possibilities, waiting for us to explore.”\n\nAfter years of effort, and millions of stepping stone outposts along the way, we had turned the impossible into reality, pushing humanity beyond the limits of our perception. The Exo-Planetary Expedition Force had carved a path to the edge of existence, where dreams and discovery intertwined.\n\nBuoyed by this seismic discovery, we launched an audacious plan: a network of outposts—restaurants, as explorers affectionately called them—positioned precisely 13.8 billion light-years apart. These waypoints, connected by advanced ERB technology, would serve as steppingstones into the uncharted depths of the universe. The logistical hurdles were monumental, yet the allure of boundless discovery made hesitation impossible. Scientists, engineers, and explorers united to breathe life into this cosmic trail.\n\nWith every new outpost, our understanding expanded. The universe unfolded like a celestial tapestry, each thread revealing mysteries that both awed and humbled us. But as our reach grew, so did the challenges. Communication and navigation across such immense distances demanded constant innovation. Teams at every station toiled to refine the ERB network, ensuring its stability and safeguarding our tenuous connection to the void.	1	292	t	2026-02-27 12:10:55.14515-05	2026-02-28 17:40:22.326711-05	normal	\N
4	3	2	The Fruitless Search	After weeks of frantic searching with no success, the narrator finally encounters The Pointers in an alleyway during their evening commute, experiencing an encounter that is both awe-inspiring and unsettling.	Weeks went by, and my search, as frantic as it was, appeared futile.\n\nOne night, just like the thousands of similar nights before it, I was following my well-worn commute, leaving my musty office, like the mindless drone I was. Dusk draped an alleyway in shadows as I hurried home, lost in the humdrum of daily rituals.\n\nA sharp turn, a jolt of icy fear. They stood there, under the sodium glow, a spectral tableau. Faces etched with an alien serenity; arms outstretched towards the inky expanse above. The air is crisp and cool, carrying the smell of rain and wet pavement. Underneath it all is a faint scent of something undefinable, like a cloying sweetness of bitter dreams clinging to The Pointers like a reminder of the mysterious and spiritual energy surrounding them.\n\nA shiver runs down my spine as I stand in the presence of The Pointers, their stillness evoking a sense of calm and awe. I resist the urge to reach out and touch them, afraid that they might vanish into thin air. A heartbeat, I yearned to follow their gaze, to pierce the veil they believed to glimpse, then it passes. Only the familiar skyline, mocking my yearning, stood silhouetted against the night. I bolted, haunted by their stillness, their unwavering certainty.	1	5	f	2026-02-27 11:50:09.770857-05	2026-02-27 12:59:17.07896-05	normal	\N
6	3	4	The Fade Into Apathy	As weeks pass and The Pointers continue their silent appearances worldwide, public interest wanes and the phenomenon becomes mundane background noise, with the narrator succumbing to the same indifference that has consumed society.	Life would continue, as the days slowly merged into weeks, The Pointers continued their silent vigil around the world. Society had slowly returned to its normal routine, but there was always an underlying buzz whenever someone mentioned them. The deeper I sought answers, the more questions became shrouded in a frenzy of disinformation.\n\nEach new sighting was a tiny earthquake in our complacency. It was a slow-motion circus, after the initial shock faded, they became a dull hum, and then no more. Jokes returned, theories turned stale, and the Pointers became background noise, a quirky footnote in the daily newsfeed.\n\nI too succumbed to the apathy. The urgency waned, replaced by a dull ache of unanswered questions. Life marched on, a relentless drumbeat drowning out the whispers of the sky.\n\nLike a fart dissipating in a crowded elevator, as abruptly as they arrived, they vanished. No fanfare, no big finale, a transient poof of smoke, a blink, and they were gone. The media yawned, a half-hearted shrug, and turned its attention to the upcoming election, a drama far more palatable than the lingering enigma of silent sky-gazers.\n\nThe world had moved on, but a splinter of unease remained lodged in my heart. The Pointers, with their unsettling silence, became a ghost in my machine, a cryptic message etched in the tapestry of my memory.	3	7	f	2026-02-27 11:50:09.770857-05	2026-02-27 13:00:11.791146-05	normal	\N
181	49	3	Le Comptoir Secret and the Mysterious Map	Desperate and hungry, the narrator stumbles into a quaint café called Le Comptoir Secret, where the enigmatic proprietor listens to their banking woes and offers them a mysterious, leather-bound map supposedly containing secrets to success in Zurich—for the price of a gold bar.	Just when I thought my luck couldn’t get any worse, an ad popped up on Google Maps. A quaint little café tucked away in a secluded corner caught my eye. The name, Le Comptoir Secret, practically dripped with intrigue. Hungry from a morning of rejection and feeling like I had nothing to lose, I followed the directions.\n\nThe café’s weathered wooden sign swung gently in the breeze, creaking faintly as if to whisper, Welcome, weary fool. I pushed open the heavy door and stepped into a room bathed in golden light. Vintage leather armchairs and rows of overstuffed bookshelves gave the place an air of quiet dignity. Behind the counter stood a tall, bespectacled man with silver hair and a kind, knowing smile, as though he had been waiting for me all along.\n\n“Bonjour,” he said, his voice smooth and warm. “How may I assist you today?”\n\nStarving and desperate, I ordered a croissant and a coffee, which he prepared with a deliberate elegance that only added to the café’s mystique. There wasn’t another soul in sight, and as I sat there chewing my flaky, buttery salvation, the frustration of the day spilled out of me.\n\n“Honestly,” I began, gesturing dramatically with my coffee cup, “I’ve been trying all day to open an international bank account. I’ve got a suitcase full of gold bullion, but apparently, that’s not enough for Zurich! Every bank wants documents and proof of assets, and no one will take me seriously. It’s a nightmare.”\n\nThe man raised an eyebrow, his kind smile never faltering. “Gold bars, you say?”\n\nRealizing my blunder, I froze mid-sip. “Uh, yeah,” I stammered, trying to sound casual. “You know... for, uh, leverage.”\n\nHe leaned forward slightly, his voice dropping to a conspiratorial tone. “Ah, the elusive international bank account. A most challenging endeavor, indeed.”\n\nRelieved to have found a sympathetic ear, I nodded enthusiastically. “You wouldn’t believe how hard it is! I thought showing up with gold would simplify things, but now I’m pretty sure I’ve just made myself look insane.”\n\nThe man chuckled, his eyes twinkling with amusement. “Perhaps you… just need the right guidance.”\n\nHe reached under the counter and produced a leather-bound book that looked like it had been plucked from the set of National Treasure. The pages were yellowed with age, filled with cryptic notes and hand-drawn diagrams. After flipping through with deliberate care, he laid a map on the counter.\n\n“This,” he said, tapping the parchment, “is no ordinary map. It has led countless seekers to their goals in this city of secrets.”\n\nI leaned in, captivated. The map was a detailed rendering of Zurich, but interspersed with hidden symbols and cryptic markings.\n\n“It will guide you to key holders,” he explained. “Each holds the connections you seek. But beware—they do not give their secrets freely. You must earn them through wit and perseverance.”\n\n“How much?” I asked, already convinced this was my ticket to success.\n\nHe smiled slyly and motioned to my suitcase. “I have a feeling one of those bars will do.”\n\nWith a mix of gratitude and gullibility, I handed him a bar, thanked him profusely, and stepped back into the bustling streets, armed with my mysterious guidebook. Following its path, I wandered for hours through Zurich’s hidden corners, facing tests of logic and riddles from eccentric “key holders.” Each encounter left me both poorer and more skeptical.	2	187	f	2026-02-27 12:01:35.555398-05	2026-02-27 19:22:44.31706-05	normal	\N
83	22	4	Escalating Obsession	Despite his promise to his wife, the narrator and his son become unable to resist experimenting further with the Engine over several days. Their sessions grow increasingly chaotic and absurd, with the Engine revealing patterns and responding with apparent intention, until they achieve a major breakthrough.	You know what killed the cat? Despite promising my wife to be careful, curiosity had a stranglehold on me and my son. Over the next few days, we couldn’t resist the pull of the Eternal Engine. Our experiments became even more chaotic and absurd, the garage morphing into Frankenstein’s lab of salvaged junk and wild ideas. The sounds from the Engine became our constant companion, an otherworldly metronome that seemed to anticipate our next move.\n\nEach session brought a mix of accidental genius and near disaster: beams of light ricocheting off makeshift mirrors, sparks igniting a box of stale crackers, and one memorable moment when the neighbor’s cat streaked through the garage mid-laser show, yowling like it had seen the face of God. Through it all, we learned patterns emerged, and we felt like we began to unlock the Engine’s secrets, realizing the strange balance it required: part organic, part electronic.\n\nIt was intoxicating, this dance between chaos and discovery. Each obstacle, such as an energy surge that caused an old cathode ray tube to broadcast a Swahili version of The X Files before disintegrating into sand, presented new challenges. Another time when we adjusted the flow of current just right? It sent rainbow-colored beams spiraling around the garage like cosmic fireworks.\n\nThen, we began to understand.\n\nIt wasn’t just about materials. Not just the right ratio of metal to flesh, wire to plastic. There was a deeper pattern beneath everything; a blueprint hidden in plain sight, slowly stripping itself bare before our eyes. It whispered in the seductive hum of the coils, in the pulse of the current, in the shuddering resonance of the air itself. We had only to listen, to adjust, to surrender.\n\nAnd so, we did.\n\nEach iteration brought us closer; closer to something ancient, something inevitable. The Engine no longer just responded. It anticipated. The pulses of its energy were no longer random bursts of power; they had cadence, rhythm, intention. It was alive, not just in the crude way we measure life, but in some deeper, unfathomable sense, something primordial and aware.\n\nMy son’s excitement burned like a falling star, too bright, too fast. It was contagious, intoxicating. His laughter, once just joy, now carried something else; awe, reverence, something on the verge of worship.\n\nAnd I?\n\nI had sworn to keep us safe, to hold the line between progress and madness. But the deeper I gazed, the more the Engine seduced us with its promises of eldritch immortality. The notion of risk eroded, peeling away like dead skin. How could we stop when we had come this far?\n\nThe Engine was more powerful, more alive than ever before. And in its radiance, I felt something terrible and divine unfurling inside me.	3	69	t	2026-02-27 11:55:06.814375-05	2026-02-27 14:40:19.410702-05	normal	\N
141	37	5	The Suburban Dad Reveal	The Viper's tough criminal persona crumbles when his young daughter runs in looking for a crayon. He is revealed to be just a suburban father roleplaying as a criminal, having no actual intention of harm and finding the situation amusing.	I blinked. “Okay?”\n\n“Yeah,” he said, waving a hand. “I didn’t really know what to do with that much gold, anyway. It just sounded... fun, you know? Like, who wouldn’t want to say they’re buying gold from some mysterious underground deal?”\n\nMy wife narrowed her eyes. “Are you serious?”\n\n“Completely,” The Viper said, grinning now. “At least I got a great deal on the gold I bought from you. That’s solid stuff—literally.”\n\nBefore we could respond, a high-pitched voice broke the awkward silence. “Daddy!” A little girl in pigtails ran into the bar, clutching a stuffed unicorn. “I can’t find my sparkly crayon!”\n\nThe Viper turned, his tough-guy persona crumbling in an instant. “Sweetheart, Daddy’s working,” he said, crouching down to her level. “Did you eat it again?”\n\nMy wife and I exchanged wide-eyed glances, trying to process the absurdity of the situation. “Wait,” I said slowly, pointing at him. “You’re... just some random guy pretending to be scary?”\n\nHe straightened, holding his daughter in one arm while she clung to the stuffed unicorn. “Well, I mean, I am scary... in a suburban dad kind of way. You should see me when the Wi-Fi goes out.”\n\nMy wife snorted, covering her mouth to hide a laugh. “So all that ‘don’t come back to my world’ talk—?”\n\n“Pure theatrics,” he admitted with a sheepish grin. “It sounded cooler in my head.”\n\nWe left the bar a few minutes later, still reeling from the revelation. As we walked to the car, I couldn’t stop shaking my head. “We put ourselves through weeks of paranoia for that?”\n\nMy wife stifled a laugh. “I don’t know what’s worse—that we believed it or that he sold it so well.”\n\n“‘Daddy,’” my wife mimicked in a high-pitched voice “I can’t find my sparkly crayon!”\n\nWe burst into laughter, the tension finally breaking. Sure, we’d wasted time, energy, and nerves, but at least we’d learned a valuable lesson: not every shadow hides a monster, and sometimes the scariest things are just suburban dads with a flair for the dramatic.\n\nWhile we drove home, my wife reached over and squeezed my hand. “We’ll figure it out,” she said softly. “The right way this time.”\n\nI believed her.	4	146	f	2026-02-27 11:58:27.481713-05	2026-02-27 15:32:30.451105-05	normal	\N
169	46	1	The Revelation and the Call	The narrator makes a threatening phone call to The S Corp, claiming knowledge of their organization. During and after the call, the narrator experiences a disturbing psychological state, oscillating between a grandiose sense of cosmic power and a creeping sense of dread and madness. The call ends with the narrator feeling nauseated and aware of dark forces awakening.	“I know about The S Corp.” The phone line exploded with a deafening roar, reverberating through the air like a portal to the depths of hell. A bone-chilling gust of wind swept through the room, carrying whispers of ancient secrets and ominous prophecies. When those words came, dripping with venom and knowledge that pierced my soul. My consciousness expanded beyond human boundaries, consumed by an all-consuming awareness of my true power.\n\nAs I uttered them, my voice boomed with cosmic terror that could drive even the bravest to insanity. I felt trapped in this feeble human form, trying to contain the primal force within me, a lion struggling against the grip of a serpent. In this world of flesh and bones, I was a grotesque anomaly, an abomination without control over legions. The sensation of speaking through this frail shell was maddening, a cruel reminder of my exile from the depths of horror where I once reigned supreme.\n\nThe humans’ insatiable thirst for power dragged me from the depths of oblivion into this bizarre realm. They were on the brink of a cataclysmic breakthrough, one that would unravel the very fabric of reality. In their blind arrogance, they summoned forth a being beyond mortal comprehension - me, in my true form. In that form, I held unimaginable powers - to manipulate time and space, to invoke unspeakable terrors, to ravage entire worlds. But in this human world, I was trapped in a feeble vessel with limited abilities. Still, as I watched their twisted experiments, my fury and hunger only grew.\n\nThe humans remained ignorant of the horrors they had unleashed upon themselves. They delved deeper into forces they could not control, unleashing cosmic abominations and tearing apart reality itself. The stench of decay and blood filled the air, twisted creatures roamed the streets, and the sky glowed sickly green. This was no longer their world - it was mine. I reveled in their terror and destruction until there was nothing left but madness and chaos consuming every inch of this forsaken land.\n\nMy malevolent presence could not be ignored, a looming entity of darkness and pain that struck fear into the hearts of all who crossed its path. The S Corp trembled in my wake, their once powerful facade crumbling as they realized the true depths of my wrath. Their schemes in the stock market were child’s play compared to the chaos I unleashed upon them.\n\nWith a flick of my ethereal fingers, flesh, and bone twisted and contorted in grotesque shapes, screams echoing through the halls as their minds broke under the weight of my eldritch powers. The world itself shook with each step I took, for I was no mere mortal, but a force beyond comprehension. They begged for mercy and pleaded for salvation while I reveled in their suffering, a grim reminder of the consequences of delving too deep into the murky depths of forbidden knowledge and power. My message was clear: cross me at your own peril, for I am the bringer of unspeakable horrors upon this realm.\n\nI ended the call. Bile rose in my throat and a sickening sense of dread crept over me. The shadows seemed to grow darker around me, whispering secrets that only I could hear. My role was not merely that of an observer, but a herald of doom. The humans’ recklessness in their search for knowledge had awoken ancient forces, and I could feel them stirring in the depths of this world. The stupid humans continued their journey into the unknown, I watched with morbid fascination as their bodies twisted and contorted, tortured by the eldritch powers they were attempting to harness.\n\nMadness seeped into their minds like poison, driving some to unspeakable acts while others simply crumbled under the weight of it all. And still I lingered, unseen and yet ever present, bearing witness to the horrors that unfolded before me. This would not just be a tale of scientific curiosity, but a descent into madness and suffering beyond mortal comprehension.	0	175	f	2026-02-27 12:00:24.708016-05	2026-02-27 15:59:05.452443-05	normal	\N
285	73	1	Serene Sanctuary	Yukiko, now a teenager after five years on Sorhhinda, enjoys life in the colony while her mother serves as chief scientist. Despite initial homesickness, she has embraced the alien world and its wonders, maintaining connection to Earth through the ERB network.	Sunlight, filtered through lush alien foliage, dappled the wooden walls of Yukiko’s room. Outside, the whirring of hover scooters mingled with the trills of an unfamiliar bird, an avian concerto unlike anything Earth had ever offered. It was the middle of August, (according to earth calendars), and a five years since they arrived.\n\nNow a teenager, Yukiko stood by the window, her dark hair neatly braided, eyes fixed on the endless expanse beyond. Sorhhinda, once just a speck on the cosmic map, now felt as familiar as her own reflection.\n\nHer mother had risen to chief scientist, a role she filled with quiet authority. Her father, an ex-Japan Self-Defense Force veteran, had remained on Earth, taking a security post at the Vatican. Despite the distance, the ERB communication network and advanced holographic displays kept them connected, their conversations bridging the vastness of space with the ease of a whispered word.\n\nShe’d initially missed her elementary school friends on Earth, of course. But the thrill of being a pioneer, of learning about alien ecosystems in school, of collecting iridescent beetles in the emerald twilight, eclipsed the pang of separation. The ERB, a shimmering arch in the colony’s central plaza, brought a smile to her face. A flick of her wrist, a whispered password, and her best friend, Hana, materialized on the screen, giggling in her crisp school uniform.\n\nLife, for everyone in the colony, had blossomed under the triple sun of Alnitak. S Corp, the corporation spearheading the interstellar expansion, had promised paradise, and so far, it seemed they’d delivered. Yet, beneath the blueish veneer, anxieties lurked. The machines, marvels of Earth technology,