283	72	2	James's Dark Revelation	James confronts the protagonist in a tense private meeting, revealing the ideological depth of the extremist threat and proposing more aggressive internal sabotage tactics. The conversation is interrupted by escalating reports of suicide attacks and advanced munitions, culminating in Patrick's announcement of a devastating nuclear device called the God Killer.	**********\n\nThe air in my office felt suffocating, thick with an unspoken tension that pulsed like a living thing. The dim fluorescent lights flickered, casting jagged shadows across the walls that seemed to shift and writhe. James entered, his movements deliberate, the air around him charged with an almost predatory energy. His eyes, sharp and unnerving, carried the weight of a man who had stared into something incomprehensible, and returned with a piece of it lodged in his soul.\n\nHe took the seat across from me, his gaze locking onto mine with unsettling intensity. “I’ve been monitoring the situation,” he began, his voice low and urgent. “Our conventional methods of diplomacy don’t work with these factions. They see S Corp as more than an corporation—they have declared jihad against what they see as the emissary of hell. To them, we’re a direct threat to everything they believe in.”\n\nI leaned forward, the depth of his words settling over me. “Tell me something I don’t already fucking know please?”\n\nJames’s lips pressed into a thin line. “Their identity. Their control. To them, we’re not just offering autonomy; we’re dismantling the very power structures they’ve built their lives around. They fear irrelevance.”\n\n“Jesus fucking on a Christ cracker. What the hell do they want? We gave them an opportunity for their whole own fucking planet where they could shit all over each other as much as they wanted.” I shot back, exasperated.\n\nHe paused, his fingers tapping rhythmically on the table. “Our operatives have uncovered the depth of their conviction. They’re not just reacting to change, they’re trying to halt the very concept of progress as anything that comes from is satanic. It’s ideological warfare, and they believe God is under attack.”\n\n“How the fuck did they come up with that conclusion…Fanatics fueled by delusions. Thousands of years of human evolution and religion is still shtting in the stewpot and telling people to eat it,” I muttered, shaking my head. “Their resolve is dangerous because they falsely believe it’s personal.”\n\n“Exactly,” James replied, leaning closer. “We can’t afford to underestimate them. They’re willing to sacrifice everything to stop us, which makes them unpredictable and lethal. Just the other day we had reports of children with anal bombs attacking one of our education centers.”\n\n“Anal bomb?” I asked.\n\n“Taking suicide bombs to the next level, shoving it somewhere we won’t scan and then setting it off before we can react. Luckily after the first incidence the local Infinitron nodes picked up on this and has been stopping it as much as we can. But we’re not everywhere…” James replied.\n\nI exhaled sharply, my mind racing. “So how do we counter this bullshit? We can’t just bulldoze over their concerns without deepening their resistance.”\n\nJames’s gaze darkened. “We need a two-pronged approach. First we need to poison them from within. As much as I hate to say it, the whole pacifistic approach might need to stop. We’ve got to cut the literal heads of these snakes, prevent the infection from spreading.”\n\n“And the second?” I asked.\n\n“Don’t ask,” he said, the word almost jarring in its softness.\n\nGil entered, his brisk stride breaking the tension. He dropped a stack of reports onto the table. “They’ve stepped up their attacks again, this time with more advanced munitions,” he said bluntly.\n\nAs James prepared to leave, he stopped at the door, his gaze lingering on me. “This isn’t just about S Corp,” he said, his voice quiet but heavy. “This is about the future of humanity. If we fail here, we lose more than this fight, we lose the ability for rationality to prevail and delusions to die.”\n\nJames’ words stayed with me long after he left. But I would have time to ponder them when I was dead. Another urgent message appeared on my screen. This time it was a report from the field—something dark, something chilling. The battle ahead wasn’t just political or ideological. It was existential.\n\nPatrick came running into my office and handed me the paper version of the report. A tangible emphasis of the seriousness of its contents - odd in this age of digital information. I had to take a double take and reread the report. North Korean scientists working with Russian nuclear researchers had forged a sinister alliance, creating a new nuclear device ominously named the “God Killer.”\n\nThe name alone sent a chill down my spine. The device wasn’t just a weapon, it was a herald of annihilation, capable of collapsing entire civilizations at the push of a button – it’s projected yield was 138 billion Mega Tons. More than all the combined nuclear devices humanity had ever made.\n\n“This has got to be fucking wrong. There is no way in hell this is right. That thing would be massive.” I said.\n\nPatrick looked at me. He was serious. “They’ve figured out how to use the ERB portals along with stolen variants of the Eternal Engine to concentrate the power into the bomb.”\n\n“Mother fuckers. This is what I was afraid of from the start. This shit would get into the wrong hands and fucking imbeciles out there would try to blow each other up with it.” I cried.\n\nWhat shook me to the core was their target: the Pyongyang Space Elevator.\n\nI sucked in a sharp breath, my heart hammering against my soul. There was no time to waste. I grabbed my comm and dialed Morgan, my voice cracking with urgency. “Morgan, get everyone to the command center. Now.”\n\nHer voice, steady despite the fear I knew she felt, came through the line. “On it. We’ll be ready.”\n\nMinutes later, the command center was alive with activity. The screens glowed with maps, diagrams, and live intelligence feeds. James stood near the central console, his usual composure replaced with a palpable tension.\n\n“What do we know?” James asked, his voice clipped as his sharp eyes flicked between me and Morgan.\n\n“The God Killer,” I began, barely able to suppress the tremor in my voice. “It’s real. North Korea and Russia plan to use Somali forces to suicide run and detonate it at the Pyongyang Space Elevator base.”\n\nJames’s face hardened. “It doesn’t matter where that thing goes off, the whole fucking planet is toast.”\n\nMorgan, standing at the tactical display, spoke quickly. “Our security fields around the elevators are designed to withstand massive energy levels, even nuclear bombs, but an Eternal Engine powered device? We can’t rely on theoreticals. We need a plan to neutralize this threat before they can even get close.”\n\n“We’re past defense,” I said, scanning the live feeds. “We need to blow the shit out of the fuckers. Kill them all and let their god sort it out or something. Patrick how is the work coming on the extra-stellar alien extermination units coming along?”\n\nJames nodded. “But we can’t strike blindly. We can’t turn this into a genocide just because we’re scared. I’ll reach out to our operatives for immediate intel and see if we can be more precise.”\n\nGil entered, a folder in hand, his face grim. “Too late. We’ve got reports,” he said, tossing the documents onto the table.\n\nThe room buzzed with activity, but the weight of impending doom hung over us like a storm cloud. The live feed flickered to a countdown, the God Killer’s timer ticking down with cruel precision. Every second felt like a hammer blow.\n\nJames broke the silence. “We have one shot at this. We intercept the device before it reaches the elevator. If we miss, we lose everything.”\n\nGil’s gaze was razor-sharp. “Do we have allies in the area? Anyone who can get us closer?”\n\n“Not directly,