cessibility...”\n\n“Accessibility? Can’t users just zoom in?” someone snapped.\n\nEach irrelevant question hacked at my composure, the room’s silent judgment pressing in like vultures circling. So, when the software crashed during the demo, the tension exploded.\n\n“Is this... part of the presentation?” one board member asked, their condescension cutting through the silence.\n\n“No,” I said tightly, frantically typing as my hands shook. My colleagues shifted uncomfortably, grateful not to be in my shoes. It was when the CEO leaned back and muttered, “This is... enlightening,” that the thin thread of my composure finally snapped. “Did you test this beforehand?” another member asked, her voice laced with faux concern.\n\n“Yes,” I muttered, my face hot with humiliation.\n\nWhile the system stubbornly refused to cooperate, I could feel the thin threads of my composure snapping one by one. It was a disaster, and there was no escape from it.\n\nRelief or resignation rolled over me when the presentation finally ended. Surprisingly, the boardroom erupted into applause. “Fantastic work,” someone said, while another praised it as “exactly the forward thinking we need.” The CEO stood to shake my hand, pride lighting up her face. It was a victory, a triumph. Inside, however, I was unraveling. Every misstep replayed in my head: the awkward pauses, the rushed explanations, the reversed graph axes. How could they not see it? Their approval felt surreal, a performance they applauded while I fixated on its flaws.\n\nBack at my office, the dissonance gnawed at me. Congratulations from my colleagues felt hollow, their smiles a distant echo in the cavern of my self-doubt. The screens mocked me with their silence, the rest of the day passing in a fog of imagined failure. By the time I reached my car, the city blurred into neon streaks and faceless strangers, a fitting reflection of the storm in my mind.	0	25	f	2026-02-27 11:51:17.198726-05	2026-02-27 13:17:06.05021-05	normal	\N
