cked on the video, eager to uncover the truth, or at least another fragment of it. The clip began with shaky footage of a Pointer shimmering against an unremarkable urban backdrop, its outline faint and flickering. I started watching the video with utter disbelief. Just as quickly, my excitement evaporated. The movements were unnaturally glitchy, like a poorly rendered animation, and the background didn’t quite align with the Pointer’s supposed trajectory. I paused, rewound, and played it again, searching for authenticity but finding only disappointment. “Another fake,” I muttered under my breath, setting my phone down with a resigned sigh.\n\n“Fake what?” The voice startled me, and I turned to see Mattias, plopping down on the bench beside me, a greasy brown paper bag in hand.\n\n“Just another Pointer video,” I replied, half-heartedly waving my phone.\n\nHis eyes widened as he unwrapped his sandwich, the plastic crinkling loudly. “Oh, man! Was it the one where it disappeared? That one’s wild! Did you see it?”\n\nI groaned and tilted my head toward him, my frustration bubbling over. “Yeah, I saw it. It’s a hoax. The glitches, the mismatched shadows, classic bad editing. You can even see where they tried to mask the background.”\n\nMattias frowned, his enthusiasm dimming slightly. “You sure? It looked legit to me. I mean, who’s got the time to fake something like that?”\n\n“People have time and AI for everything,” I said. “Especially when it gets them clicks. Come on, Mattias, it’s not even convincing. The Pointer moves like someone’s running it on a computer from 1995.”\n\nHe hesitated, taking a bite of his sandwich as he mulled over my words. “Okay, let’s say the video’s fake, but that doesn’t mean the Pointers aren’t real. Have you read the theories? Some say they’re aliens scouting Earth. Others think they’re failed government experiments, like that movie where they reincarnate the Spice Girls on the moon, but worse.”\n\nI couldn’t help but roll my eyes. “Sure, b