n what had happened.\n\n“We... we kept going back. No matter where we turned, we ended up... here.”\n\nHe pointed into the humming trees, hands trembling, and for a moment, none of us wanted to look. All of us were terrified to see what he was showing us. Something felt wrong. The shadows were stretched in angles too tightly. The forest lay unnaturally still.\n\nThe moment passed, and we headed back. Getting the scouts on their feet was the simple part; leaving was another story.\n\nThe path we thought we’d come from was gone, swallowed by darkness that twisted the landscape into something unrecognizable. Flashlight beams flickered across shapes that seemed to shift when we weren’t looking. The air grew colder, damp and thick, as though it was closing in around us. The boys whispered nervously, their earlier panic bleeding into my own rising fear.\n\nEvery step back toward camp felt heavier, the forest tightening its grip with every turn. Trails that should have been straightforward, looped impossibly, or ended abruptly. The dirt seemed to drag at our boots, the weight of exhaustion pressing harder with each step.\n\nMy map was useless, landmarks blurred, distances stretched, and nothing matched what we had seen earlier—everything looping us back to that clearing we started from.\n\nI fought to stay calm, to focus on leading the group, but doubt gnawed at me. Time warped into meaningless stretches; minutes felt like hours. The boys clung to one another, their whispered questions — “Are we lost? Is this the way? Weren’t we just here?” — met with strained reassurances, I barely believed myself.\n\nThe trees loomed closer, their branches like grasping bones. Everywhere we turned, the forest seemed to breathe around us, a low susurration pulsing through the ground beneath our feet. My chest tightened with the suffocating weight of the dark, the growing certainty that we were not alone—or worse, that we would never leave.\n\nAfter what seemed like hours, the faint glow of the campfire finally broke through the trees. It felt unreal, a mirage conjured from desperation. The boys stumbled into camp, collapsing in relief. Despite the palpable relief, I couldn’t dismiss the sense that the forest had let us go only because it wanted to—and that it was watching, waiting, for another chance to pull us back.	4	41	f	2026-02-27 11:52:20.46094-05	2026-02-27 13:53:14.875679-05	normal	\N
