e. The copper wires began to glow, emitting a sound like a mechanical scream. Then, with a gut-punching WHOMP, the chicken imploded, sending a shockwave through the garage that knocked us both off our feet.\n\nAfterwards, everything was still. A mist of frost hung in the air, glinting in the light of the Engine. The copper wires lay in a melted heap, and the chicken, what was left of it, was splattered across the walls and ceiling in greasy chunks.\n\n“Did it...work?” my son whispered, sitting up and brushing rice and fired chicken clumps off his hoodie.\n\n“It did something...” I murmured, my eyes fixed on the workbench.\n\nIn the center of the chaos, where the chicken had been, lay something extraordinary. It shimmered like frozen starlight, an intricate crystal structure that seemed to fold in on itself endlessly. Iridescent hues shifted across its surface, each movement hinting at an infinite depth, as if it existed in more dimensions than we could comprehend.\n\n“It’s...it’s beautiful,” my son breathed, edging closer.\n\nI nodded, unable to take my eyes off the thing. “Be careful,” I cautioned, though I couldn’t stop myself from reaching out. My fingers brushed against its surface, and a strange energy coursed through me, cool and sharp, like the first breath of winter.\n\n“Why does it feel alive?” my son asked, his voice filled with awe.\n\nBefore I could answer, the door to the house slammed open, and my wife stormed into the garage. She stopped dead in her tracks, her nose wrinkling. “What are you two doing out here? It smells like... did you order Chick-fil-A?” Her eyes scanned the mess, landing on the crystal. “And what the hell is that?”\n\n“It’s a magic crystal!” my son exclaimed, grinning ear to ear. “It came out of the chicken!”\n\nShe blinked, utterly dumbfounded. “Out of the—what? You’re telling me you cooked a raw chicken with... copper wire and it shit that out?” She gestured wildly at the crystal, her tone teetering between disbelief and fury.\n\n