ure.\n\nWilliam’s moment of recognition, a flicker of Finstone hidden in a Chartreuse and pink logo, felt like a victory. I allowed myself to hope that we could fight this, that we could stitch together the torn fabric of reality. But hope is a cruel thing, a flickering light that only serves to deepen the shadows around it. Even as I butchered his mind back together, I felt something watching. Not from the edges of the room or the cracks of my lab, but from behind the veil of existence itself. It was patient. Curious. Hungry.\n\nThat’s when the experiments spiraled out of control. Data streams turned to gibberish, equations unraveling into symbols I didn’t recognize, patterns that stared back at me as if they were alive. The colliders themselves seemed to hum with something new—no longer the steady thrum of machinery, but a low, resonant pulse that felt like a heartbeat. It wasn’t ours. It wasn’t human.\n\nThe boundaries of the lab became porous, the walls shimmering as though they were breathing. My colleagues began to disappear, not in the physical sense. They were never there to begin with. Their words would slip, strange phrases interspersed with the familiar: “I am sees,” “I am comes.”\n\nI tried to stop the experiments, to shut it all down. When I tried to enter LHC-2 and LHC-3, the buildings never existed. Only LHC-1 remains standing, roaring with a life of its own. The pulsing hum growing louder, resonating in my chest. The lights are dimming, and in the flickering glow, I see something. No—feel something. A presence that presses against the fabric of reality, straining to break through. It wasn’t a being, not in the sense we understand. It was awareness. Vast. Infinite. Malevolent.\n\nMy memories are no longer my own, bleeding together with fragments of lives I have never lived. I hear its voice now, a low, resonant hum that speaks not to my ears but to my soul. It does not demand or threaten. It simply waits.\n\nI fear it’s too late for me.\n\nI am here. 