pite myself. “Okay, give me an example.”\n\nHe grabbed his phone and pulled something up, gesturing animatedly, as if conjuring the images from thin air. “Alright, imagine this mall—completely empty. The lights are on, music’s playing faintly, but there’s no one around. Or this one, an office building late at night, rows of cubicles and flickering lights. You know that feeling when a place seems too quiet, like it’s waiting for something?”\n\nA chill ran down my spine as his words painted the picture. “Yeah, I think I’ve felt that before. Like it’s familiar, but... wrong. Almost like you shouldn’t be there.”\n\n“Exactly!” he said, his voice rising with excitement. “There’s a Discord server I’m in where people post photos of these places. Some of them are fake, but a lot look real. They make my brain itch, like I’m looking at something I shouldn’t be able to see.”\n\nI leaned back, a strange unease settling over me. “So why do people care about them so much? Just because they’re creepy?”\n\nHe nodded, his brow furrowing slightly. “Sort of. I think it’s because they mess with your sense of reality. Like, they make you question what’s real and what’s not. Kinda like the Mandela Effect.”\n\nThat caught my attention. “The Mandela Effect, huh? You mean the whole ‘Berenstain Bears’ thing? Or Sinbad as a genie?”\n\n“Yeah!” His grin returned as he pointed his pencil at me. “Like that Sinbad movie you keep telling me about? It doesn’t exist, but you keep swearing you’ve seen it. So has half the internet. How does that even happen? Other than you just being crazy.”\n\n“I don’t know, but it’s weird. It’s like... shared false memories. Collective misremembering. And some of them are so specific, it’s hard to believe they’re just mistakes.”\n\nHis voice dropped, almost conspiratorial. “What if they’re not mistakes? What if they’re glitches? Like something’s wrong with reality itself?”\n\nI froze for a moment, the thought hanging heavily in the air between us. “Glitches? You mean,