rast to the timeless existence I was accustomed to.\n\nIn another time, my name was whispered in fear and reverence as part of ancient lore. Here I was, forgotten and abandoned by a world that had long moved beyond the fears and superstitions that once gave me strength. Amidst all these revelations, a sense of purpose burned within me, driving me forward towards the source of this unimaginable disturbance.	0	136	f	2026-02-27 11:57:54.658108-05	2026-02-27 15:22:30.53397-05	normal	\N
144	39	2	The Exhaustion Sets In	The family is overwhelmed by the physical toll of the fulfillment operation, prompting the narrator to pitch an audacious solution: building robots to automate their packing and shipping tasks. His wife remains skeptical until they begin prototyping designs.	The heat of the mid-May Florida weather in the garage was oppressive. We were working ourselves to death. Not the poetic, “chasing your dreams” kind of death, either—this was the sweat-drenched, sore-back, cardboard-cuts kind. And I should know. In Japan, I watched three coworkers literally work themselves to death. Karoshi, they called it. Back then, it was the warning cry of a brutal corporate culture. Now, it felt uncomfortably close to home. Except instead of spreadsheets and strategy sessions, we were drowning in tape guns, bubble wrap, and endless UPS runs.\n\n“I’m dying over here,” I muttered, slapping a shipping label onto yet another box. “I’ve done my fair 160-hour weeks before, but at least then I got the occasional sushi platter. Now all I get is the ‘Ding!’ of an Amazon sale notification.”\n\n“Yeah, and I’d bet you were just as good at organizing back then as you are now,” my wife quipped, stepping over a precariously balanced stack of boxes that threatened to crush the dog. “If one of these topples, we’ll be the next workplace accident headline.”\n\nBefore I could retort, our son burst into the garage, waving his iPad like a trophy. “Guys, check this out! My new Roblox update dropped, and guess what? A million zombie-fighting automatons—working together—took down the boss in under thirty seconds! My players are freaking out!” He spun the tablet’s screen toward us, showing an explosion of emojis in the chat.\n\n“That’s... great?” I said, glancing at my wife. “Do we congratulate him or start prepping for the robot apocalypse he just inspired?”\n\n“Maybe both,” she deadpanned. “Because apparently, even in a virtual world, he figured out how to get help before we did.”\n\nThe words hung in the air for a moment before something clicked. “Wait a second,” I said, setting down the tape gun. “Robots. That’s it. If we’re already surviving on the Infinitron’s brainpower, why not go all in? We could build robots to handle this mess.”\n\n“Robots?” My wife crossed her arms, eyeing me like I’d just suggested reinventing the wheel. “The last time you built something, it was a ‘controlled pyrotechnics experiment.’ Spoiler alert: It wasn’t controlled.”\n\n“This is different!” I protested. “We’ve got the Infinitron now. We could design machines to pack, sort, and ship for us. Think about it—no more late-night order packing, no more endless bubble wrap tangles. We could actually sleep.”\n\nShe raised an eyebrow, her expression a perfect mix of skepticism and amusement. “You’re seriously pitching me ‘robots will save our lives’? This, from the same guy who once declared, ‘AI is coming for our jobs in the same ‘dey took our jerbs!’ voice while shaking his fist at the sky like an unhinged conspiracy theorist?”\n\nI groaned, already regretting the South Park reference I’d made during one of my business are doing AI all wrong rants. “That was different! I was talking about large-scale automation replacing people, not us building a couple of bots to save our sanity. Plus, I welcome our AI overlords. You know why I always thank Alexa...”\n\nShe smirked, clearly enjoying this a little too much. “Oh, so now robots aren’t the enemy? Because last month, you said, and I quote, ‘We’re all one firmware update away from being obsolete.’”\n\n“Okay, okay, yes, I said that,” I admitted, throwing up my hands in mock surrender. “But come on, you’re telling me your twisted back is fine now? At this point, if a robot wants to take over bubble wrap duty, I’ll hand it a name badge and a little hat.”\n\nShe leaned against the workbench, crossing her arms with the air of someone about to deliver a well-aimed verbal jab. “Let’s not forget the time you tried to ‘optimize’ our coffee maker for efficiency and ended up flooding the kitchen with three gallons of espresso. You’re really sure this is going to work?”\n\n“That was an isolated incident,” I said, trying to sound confident and failing miserably. “Besides, it’s not like we’re programming these things to handle brain surgery. They’re packing boxes, not curing cancer.”\n\n“Right,” she said dryly, her lips twitching as she fought back a grin. “Because your track record with over-engineering simple tasks is so stellar. Should I go ahead and order a mop for when the robots inevitably flood the garage with packing peanuts?”\n\n“Very funny,” I muttered, reaching for the nearest blueprint. “Just you wait. These bots will completely change our workflow.”\n\nShe snorted. “If by ‘revolutionize’ you mean ‘create a whole new category of chaos,’ then yeah, I believe you.”\n\nI shot her a look, but deep down, I couldn’t help but laugh. Her teasing might have been relentless, but she wasn’t wrong. If anyone could find a way to turn a robot assistant into an accidental assassin, it was me. Still, I was determined to make this work—even if it meant enduring a few more well-earned jabs along the way.\n\n“Come on, it’s worth a shot. Besides, what’s the alternative? We keep running ourselves ragged until we’re walking punchlines in a cautionary tale?”\n\n“Fine,” she relented, though her tone dripped with skepticism. “But if one of your robots so much as beeps at me the wrong way, it’s getting shot.”\n\nWith the Infinitron’s help, the robots practically designed themselves. Each unit came equipped with a meshed network version of the Infinitron crystal and an eternal engine power source that promised limitless energy. The matter replicator made quick work of building the whole robots, spitting out intricate machine like a 3D printer on steroids. The real challenge? Figuring out what these mechanical saviors should actually look like.\n\n“What about sleek humanoid bots?” I suggested one evening, sketching a vaguely Westworld-esque figure on my notebook.\n\n“Humanoid?” My wife rolled her eyes, gesturing toward the living room where our son was meticulously arranging a fleet of mini-figurines. “Have you met your design skills? Unless you want these things looking like a Roblox fever dream, maybe let someone else take the lead.”\n\n“Oh, please,” I said, balling up the drawing exaggerated indignation. “That was one bad Halloween costume. And for the record, the cardboard robot suit was ahead of its time.”\n\nShe smirked, holding up the latest prototype arm, which unfortunately resembled a lobster claw welded to a bicycle chain. “Sure. And this? Is this ‘ahead of its time,’ too? Because it’s either a robot appendage or an audition for the next Pixar villain.”\n\n“It’s a work in progress,” I replied, snatching the claw from her and turning to the Infinitron interface. “Besides, you’re the one who said we needed something functional. Form follows function, remember?”\n\n“Yeah, but I didn’t mean ‘function follows nightmares,’” she quipped, sidestepping as a half-assembled bot rolled past, beeping mournfully. Its mismatched wheels clattered unevenly on the floor, like a toddler learning to walk.\n\nEven our son weighed in, striding into the garage and pointing at the prototypes with all the authority of a budding game designer. “Why don’t you make them look cool? Like, give them glowing eyes or laser cannons.”\n\n“Laser cannons?” my wife said, folding her arms and shooting me a warning glare. “Yeah, because that’s what we need—weaponized bots in the house. Next thing you know, one of them gets stuck in ‘attack mode,’ and we’re explaining to the insurance company why there’s a crater where the garage used to be.”\n\n“He’s kidding,” I said quickly, glancing at our son, who absolutely was not kidding. “No lasers. Just efficient, practical designs.”\n\n“Efficient and practical,” she repeated, looking over at the hulking, boxy prototype we’d cobbled together earlier. It stood awkwardly in the corner, its LED eyes glowing with unsettling brightness. “Right. So... Boxy McBoxface here is your idea of practical?”\n\n“That’s his name?” I asked, glancing at my son, who grinned.\n\n“Yeah,” he said proudly. “Because he’s boxy, and he puts stuff in the boxes. Duh.”\n\nMy wife shook her head, trying—and failing—not to laugh. “I’m starting to think this entire project is just a way for you two to bond over bad ideas.”\n\n“Bad ideas are the cornerstone of innovation,” I said, grinning as I adjusted Boxy’s programming through the central workstation in the living room. “Besides, once we finalize the design, these bots are going to revolutionize our entire operation.”\n\n“And if they don’t?” she said, raising an eyebrow. “If these things end up as glorified Roombas with delusions of grandeur?”\n\n“Then,” I said, with mock solemnity, “we’ll build better bots. Or... move to a cave and start selling artisan rocks.”\n\nShe chuckled, shaking her head as Boxy began its first test run. It beeped cheerfully, scooped up a box with its claw-like appendages, and proceeded to drop it onto a stack with surprising precision.\n\n“Alright,” she said, watching as the bot continued its task. “I’ll admit—it’s kind of impressive. But if one of these things gets any ideas about taking over my kitchen, it’s going straight to the scrapyard.”\n\n“Duly noted,” I replied, unable to suppress a grin. “And for the record, that’s your department. I’m strictly garage chaos.”\n\nShe laughed, giving Boxy one last skeptical glance before heading back inside. I watched her go, then turned back to the robot, now beeping triumphantly as it completed its task. The design might not have been perfect, but it was a start—and for the first time in weeks, I felt like we were making progress.	1	69	t	2026-02-27 11:58:57.113507-05	2026-02-27 15:35:53.115407-05	normal	\N
