nto a clearing, gasping for air, only to find myself face-to-face with a shrine. Its worn stones glowed faintly, emanating an aura that seemed to thrum with an otherworldly pulse. The figure of Ebisu, serene and smiling, stood atop the shrine, his visage smeared with a slick, dark liquid that gleamed like oil.\n\nCompelled by forces I couldn’t name, I reached into my backpack and retrieved one of the cans of beer I’d brought. My hands shook as I poured it over the statue, the liquid hissing and steaming as it made contact. A sudden, violent tremor shook the ground, throwing me to my knees.\n\nThe air split with an ear-piercing crack, a sound so primal it felt like it originated from the core of existence itself. A searing white light engulfed me, obliterating the forest and everything it contained. I couldn’t tell if I was screaming—the void swallowed the sound, leaving only the bone-deep hum of something incomprehensibly vast pressing against my senses. Then, silence.\n\nWhen my vision returned, the world was no longer there. I floated weightlessly in a realm where logic and reason had no bearing. The sky above churned in an eternal kaleidoscope of impossible colors—hues that my mind struggled to name, let alone comprehend. The very act of looking made my head throb, as though the colors themselves were invading my thoughts.\n\nSpiritual representations of people hanging in trees spun around me; grotesque mockeries of life, their gnarled, spiraling forms reaching infinitely skyward, their bark undulating as though composed of sinew and muscle. They bled a viscous, amber fluid that hissed when the blobs floated past me. Suddenly falling, a gelatinous, shifting surface that sucked at my feet with every hesitant step, rose up from the depths. The warmth emanating from it wasn’t comforting but suffocating, like the oppressive heat of fevered skin.\n\nTime and gravity dissolved into absurdity. I couldn’t tell if I was standing, floating, or falling. The seconds stretched and folded back on themselves, tangling into something that felt eternal yet fleeting. My own body felt alien—a tangle of disconnected limbs and thoughts straining to maintain their cohesion against the madness of the place.\n\nAnd then they came.\n\nShadows slithered out of the writhing trees, their forms formless yet bristling with countless eyes that stared directly into my soul. Each gaze was a violation, a forceful peeling away of every memory, every thought, every hidden part of me I’d tried to keep buried. I wanted to scream, but my voice caught in my throat, swallowed by the weight of their scrutiny.\n\nAbove, creatures like Tengu with wings of living fire tore through the fractal sky, their flames leaving trails of ash that rained down upon me, burning my skin. The pain was sharp and immediate, but the ash seemed to sink deeper, igniting something more profound—a sense of despair so overwhelming it felt like drowning. My thoughts were not my own anymore; they were chaos, consumed by the whispers of a thousand voices speaking languages that defied sound.\n\nOthers emerged, their forms fluid and liquid, shifting between incomprehensible geometries that hurt to behold. One moment they were towering behemoths, the next they condensed into tiny, flickering points of light that left searing afterimages in my vision. Their presence wasn’t just terrifying—it was wrong. They were intrusions into reality itself, aberrations that defied the fragile order of existence.\n\nThe realm itself seemed to conspire against me. The air grew heavy with an acrid, metallic scent that burned with every breath, and unseen forces tugged at my limbs, stretching me thin, as if the place were trying to unravel me into its chaos. My thoughts splintered, fragments of memories surfacing and dissolving as the world pressed in, whispering promises of madness.\n\nFrom nowhere, a voice—or perhaps a thought—pierced through the din. It didn’t come from outside but resonated directly inside my skull, vibrating against the walls of my mind. “The gateway will open, but only if you truly understand. This can only be paid through blood and everything you are.”\n\nThe words carried images—blistering, searing flashes of cosmic horror. Stars collapsing in on themselves, black holes devouring galaxies with a hungry inevitability. Cities, impossibly grand, swallowed by waves of shadow. In the center of it all, a figure that seemed to stretch infinitely in every direction and yet shrink to a point so small it barely existed. Its gaze was cold, detached, watching the destruction with the apathy of a god who had seen it all before and would see it all again.\n\nPain flared in my chest as I struggled to hold on to myself, to the thread of humanity that anchored me. My mind screamed for escape, clawing for the boundaries of this madness, but there was no boundary, no edge. The place was infinite, and I was lost in it.\n\nSwiftly, another crack of light enveloped me, and I was hurled back into the forest. I hit the ground hard, gasping for air as the oppressive weight lifted. The shrine stood before me as though nothing had happened, the forest silent once more.\n\nI stumbled out of Aokigahara under the mid-day sun. Looking down at my watch, only 15 minutes had passed since I entered, yet the whispers of the forest still echoing in my ears. The encounter had left me battered, my perception of reality fractured, yet beneath the terror was a revelation—one that hinted at something far greater than I’d ever imagined. The dream engine, the forest, the entities—all of it was connected. Any answers I sought weren’t just in the realm of science; they were ingrained in the very fabric of existence.\n\nAnd now. Now I had the key to unlock them—if I dared.	3	171	t	2026-02-27 12:00:19.390527-05	2026-02-27 15:55:03.069155-05	normal	\N
166	45	5	The Fisherman's Tale	After returning from the forest, the narrator spends the day with his family in distress and confusion. That evening, he tells his wife everything he experienced. She shares an ancient story about a fisherman who crossed a gateway to another realm, connecting it to theories about wormholes and Einstein-Rosen bridges. The narrator realizes the shrine was a natural wormhole gateway.	Spending the rest of the day with my family at the hot spring did nothing to set my mind at ease. Later, the evening Shinkansen ride back to Kyoto passed in a blur, the scenery rushing by unnoticed as my thoughts churned like a storm. The encounter at the Ebisu shrine had rattled me to my core. It was as though the veil between worlds had been lifted for a fleeting, harrowing moment, leaving me stranded on the edge of comprehension.\n\nBy the time we reached my mother-in-law’s house, night had fallen, casting long shadows across the garden. The quiet should have been comforting, but instead, it reminded me of the oppressive realm from earlier, as though the very air was thick with expectation. I drifted through the evening in a daze, unable to escape the questions swirling in my mind.\n\nAfter dinner, my wife noticed my distance, her concerned gaze following me as I paced the room.\n\n“Come to bed,” she urged softly, patting the space beside her. “You’ve been distant all day. What’s wrong?”\n\nI hesitated, then sighed, sitting down on the edge of the bed. The moonlight streamed through the window, bathing the room in a cold, ethereal glow. “It’s hard to explain,” I admitted, running a hand through my hair. “The shrine... what I saw... what I felt—it’s as if reality itself unraveled for a moment. I can’t stop thinking about it. It’s like there’s something I’m missing, something just out of reach.”\n\nShe studied me, her brow furrowed. “Start from the beginning,” she said, her voice steady. “What happened?”\n\nI told her everything, my words tumbling out in an unfiltered stream. The visions, the light, the creatures—they all poured forth as she listened, her expression shifting between disbelief, fascination, and worry. When I finished, the room fell silent, save for the faint rustle of the wind outside.\n\nAfter a moment, she spoke, her voice deliberate. “That reminds me of an old story my mother used to tell me when I was a child.”\n\nI turned to her, curiosity piqued. “What kind of story?”\n\nShe sat up, her silhouette framed by the moonlight. “A story about gateways,” she said. “And the dangers of crossing them.”\n\nHer voice softened, taking on the cadence of a storyteller. “In ancient times, there was a fisherman who lived by the sea. Every day, he cast his nets and brought in the ocean’s bounty. One fateful day, a storm came without warning, tossing his boat like a leaf on the waves. He was sure he would die, but then... a light appeared.”\n\nThe moonlight gleamed in her eyes as she continued. “The light was blinding, and when it faded, he was no longer on the sea. He found himself in a world unlike anything he had ever known—an endless ocean of colors that defied description, skies alive with shifting patterns, and creatures so beautiful they made his heart ache. However, the wonder didn’t last.”\n\nI leaned forward, hanging on her every word. “What happened?”\n\nShe hesitated, her voice dropping to a whisper. “The creatures spoke to him—not with words, but with thoughts. They showed him visions of things he could not comprehend. They told him he didn’t belong, that he was a trespasser in their realm. And then... then they sent him back.”\n\nI shivered at the eerie parallels. “Sounds like a pretty wild dream. Did he remember anything?”\n\nShe pursed her lips tightly. “Yes, but it drove him mad. He spent the rest of his life trying to return, convinced the answers to the universe lay on the other side. But the gateways are not meant to be crossed—they are cracks in the fabric of reality, dangerous and unstable.”\n\nI stared at her, the implications sinking in. “You think that’s what I saw at the shrine—a gateway?”\n\nShe nodded slowly. “I don’t know what else it could be. If that’s true, you must tread carefully. Whatever is beyond those cracks might not want us there.”	4	172	f	2026-02-27 12:00:19.390527-05	2026-02-27 15:56:03.964792-05	normal	\N
