e time.”\n\n“Fine,” my son said, rolling his eyes. “Let’s see how it handles some real science.”\n\nWe downloaded the BOINC software, picked a few projects, disease research, climate modeling, nothing too fancy, and linked it to the Infinitron. As soon as we hit start, the garage filled with the familiar ethereal sighing of the machine. Data poured onto the screens at an unbelievable pace, calculations processed faster than my eyes could follow.\n\n“Unreal,” my son muttered. “It’s like ChatGPT and a quantum calculator had a baby.”\n\n“More like if ChatGPT and an AI overlord got together and decided to speedrun human extinction,” my wife deadpanned.\n\nMinutes later, all the projects we’d selected were marked as complete. Decades worth of computations finished in the blink of an eye. We exchanged wide-eyed grins, exhilarated by what we’d just accomplished.\n\nThen the flood of emails started coming in.\n\nDear Participant,\nYour recent results have been flagged for suspected tampering. Your account has been suspended pending further review.\n\n“What?” I groaned, refreshing the inbox as more messages rolled in. Each one echoed the same sentiment: too fast, too impossible, too suspicious.	0	69	f	2026-02-27 11:55:50.031329-05	2026-02-27 14:50:45.736917-05	normal	\N
