 reality now. And reality means finding work, cutting costs, and getting our finances under control.”\n\nA hush fell over the room as we stared at one another, the weight of the situation heavy in the air. After a few minutes, I broke the silence. “What about bitcoin?”\n\nMy wife frowned. “Bitcoin? Isn’t that... sketchy?”\n\n“Not if we do it right,” I replied. “The Infinitron’s computational power could make us ridiculously efficient miners. If we play it smart, we could get the financial breathing room we need—quickly.”\n\nThe setup didn’t take long. Within hours, the Infinitron was connected to a mining pool and hashing. The screen lit up with streams of calculations, the network processing power spiking with every second.\n\n“It’s like we’re taking over the whole network.” my son said, his face illuminated by the flickering data.\n\n“Not like,” I muttered, watching the numbers climb. “We are. And that’s a problem”\n\nThe Infinitron tore through the hash rates, mining bitcoin almost as fast as the early days when folks were earning dozens of bit coins per hour. By the time the alerts started rolling in, emails warning us of suspicious activity, flags from the mining pool, we’d already accumulated half a dozen bitcoins.\n\n“We’re getting banned,” my wife said, reading over my shoulder.\n\n“No surprise,” I said, shutting things down as the alerts piled up. “We’ve been too efficient.”\n\nMy son frowned, watching me disconnect the Infinitron. “But isn’t being efficient the whole point? Why would that get us banned?”\n\n“Because in Bitcoin, one entity, or one pool, owning more than half the hash rate is a disaster waiting to happen.”\n\nMy wife leaned against the workbench, arms crossed. “Okay, break it down for us. Why is that bad?”\n\n“Bitcoin relies on decentralization,” I began. “The whole system works because no single person or group controls the network. The hash rate, that’s the computing power used to process transactions and secure the blockchain, is spread across thousands of miners worldwide. That way, no one can mess with it.”\n\n“And if someone does control the majority?” my son asked, his voice hesitant.\n\n“That’s called a 51% attack,” I explained. “If one pool controls over half the hash rate, they can basically rewrite the rules. They could double-spend coins, reverse transactions, or block new ones entirely. It would destroy trust in the system, and once that trust is gone, Bitcoin is as good as dead.”\n\nHis eyes widened. “So, by being too good at mining, we almost became the bad guys?”\n\n“Not intentionally,” I said quickly. “But yeah, if we stayed connected and kept dominating the hash rate, it could’ve triggered a panic. People would start abandoning the network, assuming something shady was going on.”\n\nMy wife raised an eyebrow. “And here I thought we were just mining to pay this month’s mortgage payment. Turns out we almost tanked an entire cryptocurrency.”\n\n“That’s why we shut it down,” I said firmly. “The Infinitron’s power is... a lot. More than we realized. And if we’re not careful, it’s going to draw attention we don’t want.”\n\nMy son nodded slowly, absorbing the gravity of the situation. “Okay. No more taking over networks.”\n\n“Good call,” my wife said dryly, glancing at the now-dormant machine. “Let’s stick to solving our problems without breaking the internet.”\n\n“So, how much did we make before they shut us down?” my wife asked.\n\nI checked the wallet, and my jaw dropped. “Roughly 7 bitcoins.”\n\nHer eyes widened. “At $120,000 each?”\n\n“That’s... uh... a lot,” my son said, his eyes as wide as hers.\n\n“A little over $840k pre-tax,” I said, leaning back and exhaling slowly. “Enough to pay off the mortgage and a bit!”\n\nThe three of us stared at the screen in stunned silence. The tension that had hung in the air earlier was gone, replaced by a cautious optimism.\n\n“Well,” my wife said finally, crossing her arms, “I guess that’s one way to fix the mortgage problem.”\n\n“Not bad for a day’s work,” I said, smirking.\n\n“But no more public projects,” she warned. “We got lucky this time. If anyone figures out what we’re doing, what we have, we’re going to have bigger problems than a bounced mortgage.”\n\nI nodded. She was right. The Infinitron was more than a machine, it was a secret, and keeping that secret was now our top priority.	2	102	f	2026-02-27 11:55:50.031329-05	2026-02-27 14:52:26.990127-05	normal	\N
