ectronics graveyard. So, what’s the plan, Einstein? We can barely afford organic groceries right now, let alone high-tech equipment.”\n\nI gestured toward the Infinitron, its crystalline glow casting faint rainbows on the garage walls. “That’s where this comes in. It’s smarter than us, faster than us, stronger than us. Heck, it’s practically running circles around the entire scientific community. Why not let it do the heavy lifting?”\n\nSo, we set the Infinitron to work, setting it to seek out every scrap of data it could find on materials science, research papers, textbooks, anything remotely relevant. It synthesized the information at a pace that made my head spin, running simulations and generating models with breathtaking precision.\n\nFor days, the garage hummed with activity. The Infinitron processed the vast amounts of data we were feeding it, pointing us toward compounds and molecular structures that had the potential to revolutionize energy transmission. Every material discovered felt like digging through endless rock and finally hitting something that sparkled—a brief, tantalizing glimmer in the suffocating dark.\n\n“Sugoi!” my wife said one evening, staring at spreadsheets filling the glowing screen. “It’s mapping out things that no one’s even thought of yet.”\n\n“It’s like having our own materials science genius in a box,” I said, though the excitement was tempered by reality. “The problem is, we still don’t know how to use any of this. It’s giving us the answers, but we don’t have the tools, not to mention the money, to do anything with this.”\n\nShe frowned, her shoulders slumping. “So, we’re back to square one? All this information, and it’s useless because we’re broke?”\n\n“Not useless,” I said quickly. “Just... out of reach for now. The Infinitron’s giving us the roadmap, but we’re going to need to get creative if we want to follow it.”\n\nAfter the material discoveries were catalogued, it set about putting together processes to manufacture the material. The first set of instructions id produced was as ambitious as it was daunting. Advanced synthesis techniques, intricate conditions, exotic materials, none of which we could comprehend, let alone execute in our garage.\n\n“This thing has no concept of a budget,” I muttered, scrolling through the latest set of recommendations.\n\n“It’s like asking for meat and getting the instructions to raise a Wagyu cow,” my wife quipped, leaning over my shoulder. “It’s great in theory, but...”\n\nI rubbed my temples, frustration bubbling up. “We’re going to need more than creativity to make this work. We’d need resources, connections, or an actual lab.”\n\n“Or,” she said, raising an eyebrow, “we need to figure out what we can do right here. Start small, build up. We don’t need to solve everything today.”\n\nI glanced at the Infinitron, its glow steady, and patient, as if waiting for us to catch up. “Small steps,” I agreed, though the enormity of the task loomed large. “If we’re going to make this work, we need to get smarter, and fast.”\n\n“Well, it is super intelligent,” she said, gesturing toward the Infinitron. “It could be it’s onto something. We should at least give it a try.”\n\n“You read my mind! With these instructions, and some of my old university chemistry books, I’ve got just the idea for the first experiment.”\n\nMy wife raised an eyebrow so high it could’ve left orbit. “What, and blow up the whole damn neighborhood this time? I’m still not over the chicken incident.”\n\n“Trust me,” I said, flashing what I hoped was a confident smile. “I think I know what I’m doing.”\n\nShe folded her arms, her expression deadpan. “Famous last words.”\n\n“Come on,” I said, already pulling up the Infinitron’s latest instructions. “What’s the worst that could happen?”\n\nShe threw up her hands. “I don’t know, maybe actual explosions? Or aliens knocking on the door because we accidentally sent them a distress signal?”\n\nI grinned. “Aliens would be cool, though.”\n\nShe sighed, muttering something about genius and madness being two sides of the same coin, as she walked back into the house. I took that as permission (can always ask for forgiveness later).	0	69	f	2026-02-27 11:56:03.944951-05	2026-02-27 14:53:45.224779-05	normal	\N
