ht Alexa was glitching. She’s been saying weird stuff all day.”\n\n“She was not glitching!” I snapped. “I was dying!”\n\n“Relax, you’re fine,” he said, waving me off. “Anyway, I was watching this video where a guy made grilled cheese with a hairdryer. It was awesome.”\n\nI stared at him, my mouth agape. “You were watching what?”\n\n“Hairdryer grilled cheese,” he repeated, as if it were the most reasonable thing in the world. “You should try it sometime.”\n\nI sighed, hiding my face in my hands. “Why do you waste your time on that brain rot? They overcomplicate everything for clicks!”\n\nHe grinned. “Maybe, but they don’t over-explain it like you do. At least they’re entertaining.”\n\nSomething clicked in my brain. “Explain things better...” I murmured, looking up at him. “What if we taught the Infinitron to simplify its experiments?”\n\nHe raised an eyebrow. “Simplify them? Like how?”\n\n“Like... break it down to the level where we can actually do it,” I said, my excitement building. “No exotic materials, no impossible techniques, just something we can manage with what we’ve got.”\n\n“Isn’t it more of a big-brain data cruncher?” he asked, skeptically. “Not exactly a ‘how-to’ machine.”\n\n“It doesn’t hurt to try,” I countered. “If we’re stuck making MacGyver science, we might as well get some real help.”\n\nThe next few hours blurred into an acrid haze of adjustments. My son and I asked the Infinitron to simplify experiments, limit materials to household items, and break instructions into clear, manageable steps. It responded with a list of XKCD like how-to instructions.\n\n“Do you think it’ll actually work?” he asked, peering at the screen.\n\n“I hope so,” I said. “Because if I have to guess my way through another experiment, I might blow up the neighborhood.”\n\nHe smirked. “Mom’s going to love that.”\n\nThe first set of simplified instructions was startlingly straightforward. No exotic chemicals or high-tech equipment, just basic household supplies. Following its 