to understand?”\n\n“The answer is this place,” the voice replied. “It is the labyrinth of crucible. And you must endure.”\n\nI was back in the ruins, the air thicker with smoke. The Engine’s glow dimmed in my hands, its hum dying in my bones. Around me, demon remains crumbled into ash, but shadows converged, slowly and deliberately.\n\nThe ground trembled as a grinding sound rose in the distance. It was mechanical and impossibly vast. I held the Engine tighter, its icy surface slipping away from me. There was no way forward, no way back. I was losing everything. Only the grinding, the shadows, and the certainty that I was surely dead.\n\nSuddenly, all around me, the void swallowed me into its infinitely spanning in all directions, an endless expanse that pressed in with crushing weight. No ground anchored me, no sky offered reprieve, only the oppressive vacuum of despair coiling tighter around my thoughts. Hope flickered like a distant mirage, only to dissolve into nothingness, leaving me adrift and stumbling through the void.\n\nA scream tore from my throat as searing agony erupted in my leg. Another fiend’s claws raked my flesh, and blood mingled with the ash-laden space. The crystal sharpness of the Infinitron wailed against its anchor in the Engine against my wrist, its erratic glow threatening to slip away as the hellscape burned around me. Demon crosses jutted like grotesque monuments, and fire consumed the ruins, mocking my every move.\n\nThree more demons emerged; their blazing eyes fixed on me with unrelenting malice. My gaze caught a glint of metallic blue in the rubble. In an instant I realized it was the superconducting wire, gleaming unnaturally bright. It was reckless, but I had no choice. If I could reach the wire, there might be a chance for an escape.\n\nThe Threshold twisted violently.\n\nI wasn’t alone anymore. The whispers closed in, sharp and urgent. “Hold the line, damn it! We’ve got your six!” a rough, distorted voice barked. Twisted soldiers 