167	45	6	The Wormhole Vision	The morning after his revelation, the narrator discusses his wormhole theory with his wife and mother-in-law. He outlines how the Eternal Engine could stabilize a controlled wormhole, despite the considerable risks. His family expresses concern about safety, but the narrator is determined. They pack to return home, where he will begin construction of this dangerous experiment.	**********\n\nThe morning sunlight streamed through the shoji screens, casting delicate patterns across the tatami mats. The tranquility of the room was at odds with the chaos churning in my mind. I sat cross-legged at the low table, my hands wrapped around a steaming cup of green tea, but its warmth did little to soothe the cold unease that had settled in my chest. Across from me, my wife studied me intently, her brows furrowed in concern.\n\n“You’ve been quiet since last night,” she whispered, breaking the silence. “I know that look. Your mind is racing, and you want to blow something up again.”\n\nI let out a sigh, setting the cup down with a soft clink. “It’s more than that,” I admitted, leaning back against the wall. “The shrine, the visions—it’s like they cracked something open in my head. There’s so much swirling around up here, and I can’t make sense of it. But I can feel it—there’s something there. Something big.”\n\nMy wife exchanged a glance with her mother, who had joined us at the table, quietly observing. “What is it that you think you’ve found?” she asked, her voice steady but tinged with apprehension.\n\n“Do you remember your story about the fisherman and the gateway?”\n\nShe tilted her head, surprised by my sudden question. “Of course. Why?”\n\nI leaned in, lowering my voice. “It reminded me of a lecture I once attended during a graduate physics symposium back at university. Something about energy and space, an Einstein-Rosen bridge. Wormholes. It’s a theory about shortcuts through space-time, like folding a piece of paper so the ends touch. What if the gateway you described wasn’t just a metaphor? What if it’s real?”\n\nShe frowned slightly, processing my words. “You think the shrine, the visions—it’s connected to wormholes?”\n\nI nodded. “The idea seemed like science fiction back in university, but now... With the Eternal Engine, we might actually have the power to stabilize one. The shrine could have been a natural gateway, unstable and dangerous, but still functioning. Walking through with my Infinitron crystal powered by the Eternal Engine might have set something dormant off. If we can recreate it under controlled conditions—”\n\n“Wait,” she interrupted, holding up a hand. “You’re saying you want to create a wormhole?” Her voice wavered between awe and apprehension.\n\n“Not just want to,” I said, my voice firm. “We need to. This could revolutionize everything—travel, communication, understanding the universe. And I think the Eternal Engine is the missing piece to make it work.”\n\nHer gaze softened, but the concern lingered in her eyes. “But what about the risks? The shrine showed you things that shook you to your core. What happens if we create something we can’t control?”\n\nOur son, who had been quietly following the conversation, looked up from his phone. “Are you sure it’s safe? What if the wormhole collapses—or worse, calls one of those things through?”\n\nI walked over, placing a reassuring hand on his shoulder. “You’re both right to worry,” I admitted. “Wormholes are inherently unstable, and the energy involved is unimaginable. But this isn’t just theoretical anymore. We’ll take every precaution—build safeguards into the system, run simulations, use the Infinitron to monitor every variable. The risk is there, but so is the potential for greatness.”\n\nMy wife studied me, her expression a mask of unease. “You’ve always been drawn to the impossible,” she whispered. “But this... This isn’t just pushing boundaries. It’s breaking them. I’m worried the explosions here wouldn’t just be small fireworks in the garage any more...”\n\n“That’s exactly why we have to do it,” I interjected, my voice unwavering. “If the shrine taught me anything, it’s that we’ve only scratched the surface of what’s out there. We can’t stop now—not when we’re this close.”\n\nShe sighed, then smiled faintly. “You’ve already made up your mind, haven’t you?”\n\nI nodded. “I have. But we can’t do this here. We need to get back home.”\n\nWe set about packing our bags for the return trip home, the air buzzing with a mix of excitement and trepidation. My wife’s mother hugged us tightly at the airport, tears in her eyes as she said goodbye. “Ittereshai!” she whispered, her gaze lingering on me as if sensing the weight of what lay ahead.\n\nThe flight back was uneventful, but my mind was anything but calm. The equations, the energy requirements, the possibilities—they spun in my thoughts, each piece of the puzzle sliding closer into place. As the plane touched down, a sense of unease settled in my stomach, a faint whisper of doubt amid the growing anticipation.\n\nDriving through our neighborhood, the familiar streets seemed somehow unfamiliar, shadows stretching longer than they should. I couldn’t dismiss the feeling that crossing this threshold—both figuratively and literally—would set something irreversible in motion.	5	173	t	2026-02-27 12:00:19.390527-05	2026-02-27 15:57:02.220804-05	normal	\N
325	81	5	The Long Wait and Bitter Victory	As the rescue mission unfolds in deep space, the commander endures agonizing uncertainty. Morgan's team locates Aditi and Hiro but faces a Red Hat attack during extraction, resulting in Hiro's death and Aditi's critical injuries but securing her survival.	**********\n\nAs the days dragged on, the tension became suffocating. Each incoming message was dissected for progress, every signal a source of hope or dread. My heart raced with every update, praying for news of Aditi’s survival. The weight of waiting pressed down on all of us, the uncertainty gnawing at the edges of our resolve.\n\nStill, the flicker of hope remained. Aditi’s courage had brought her this far. Now, it was up to us to bring her the rest of the way home.\n\nEvery hour we didn’t hear anything was like an eternity. Finally the message we had been waiting for finally arrived. The comms crackled to life, Morgan’s voice breaking through with a mix of relief and urgency. “We’ve found them. Aditi and Hiro are with us.”\n\nA surge of joy coursed through me, but it was short-lived. The strain in Morgan’s tone told me this was far from over. “What happened, Morgan?” I asked, leaning closer to the console. “How are they?”\n\nThere was a hesitation, a beat of static, before she answered. “We’ve encountered hostile forces,” she said, her voice tight. “The Red Hats launched an attack on our ship. We held them off, but not without casualties.”\n\nMy chest tightened. The thought of my team under fire, of Aditi in danger, was overwhelming. “And Aditi?” I pressed, my voice edged with fear. “Is she...?”\n\n“She’s alive,” Morgan interrupted, though her tone carried a weight that hit me like a blow. “Unfortunately, Hiro didn’t make it, and she’s gravely injured. She took a direct hit during the attack. The crew managed to stabilize her, but barely. The ship’s taken heavy damage. We’re holding it together, but it’s fragile.”\n\nA wave of dread swept over me as I gripped the console, my knuckles whitening. I took a deep breath, forcing calm into my voice. “What’s your plan? How soon can you reach Elysium?”\n\n“We’re en route now,” Morgan replied. “Her condition is critical. We’re doing everything we can, but she needs advanced care. We have to get her to an AutoDoc as fast as possible.”\n\nI nodded, though she couldn’t see it. “I’ll have Patrick coordinate security for your arrival. We’ll clear the approach and ensure the med-bay is ready for immediate treatment.”\n\n“Understood,” Morgan said, her voice steady despite the tension. “We’ll make it. We have to.”\n\nWhen the comm line went quiet, I turned to Patrick, who stood at my side, his expression as grave as my own. “Patrick, we need to rally every resource we have. I want the landing zone locked down and secured. Mobilize medical teams, and prepare for worst-case scenarios.”\n\n“You’ll have it,” Patrick said, his voice firm. “The Red Hats won’t get within a light-year of her. We’ll ensure she gets home safely.”\n\nI placed a hand on his shoulder, the gesture brief but filled with meaning. “This isn’t just about Aditi,” I said quietly. “It’s about showing everyone, our colonies, the Red Hats, and ourselves, that we protect our own. No matter the cost.”\n\nPatrick nodded, his resolve mirroring my own. “We’ll make it happen.”	4	337	f	2026-02-27 12:10:35.467287-05	2026-02-28 17:37:23.2536-05	normal	\N
