me unseen will. The materials laid before me were both exquisite and horrific—sheets of superconducting alloy, their surfaces shifting in a kaleidoscope of unnatural iridescence, folded at impossible angles, their razor-thin edges biting through the air itself. Each cut, each solder-weld, each delicate maneuver clicked into place with a mechanical finality, forming something intricate, something deliberate.\n\nThe box looked wrong—a thing that should not exist.\n\nWhat I was constructing was not just a machine, not just a device—it was a puzzle, a lattice of impossible geometry, every segment locking into the next with a precision that defied human craftsmanship. Polished metal beams framed the structure, forming a skeletal cage, an exquisite confinement. The superconducting veins wove through the frame, pulsing as if alive, threading unseen energy through a body not yet given form.\n\nIt felt ancient and profane, as though it had always existed and I was merely remembering how to assemble it.\n\nThe pieces whispered as I set them in place, their interlocking joints moving with a fluid, unnatural grace, not like mechanical parts, but like tendons, like sinew stretching taut. I ran my fingers over the edges, feeling the wrongness of its perfection, the way it defied the natural entropy of the world.\n\nThis was not a mere device.\n\nThis was a key.\n\nAnd it was waiting for me to turn it.	2	115	f	2026-02-27 11:56:46.621112-05	2026-02-27 15:04:42.480403-05	normal	\N
