cate anything—materials, objects... even food. This doesn’t just bend physics and chemistry; it rewrites them.”\n\nMy son, who had been hovering at a safe distance with the deadpan apathy of a terminally online teenager, finally stepped forward. He eyed the claw, then me, his usual smirk replaced by something more serious. “Okay, so what’s the move? Are we actually doing this? Or is this just another ‘Dad’s gone mad with dream science’ moment?”\n\nI met his gaze, the weight of what I was about to say settling between us like a loaded gun on the table. “We are. But we have to be careful. This thing—this power—isn’t like anything we’ve dealt with before. If we’re not careful—”\n\n“Oh my God,” my son groaned, throwing his head back. “Please, no ‘with great power comes great responsibility’ speech. You’re not Uncle Ben. Can we skip the moral lesson and get to the cool stuff? This is like, what, the third nightmare contraption we’ve built?”\n\nMy wife cut in, her voice sharp as a knife. “Hold up. What’s that in your other hand?”\n\nI hesitated, then slowly turned, revealing the object behind my back.\n\nThe cube gleamed under the garage lights. Where before it had been a simple, smooth construct, now intricate etchings crawled across its surface, carved deep like ancient script. The metal had cooled, its once-muted sheen replaced with a darkness that pulsed faintly, casting eerie, shifting shadows against the walls. It was alive in a way that should not be possible.\n\n“Oh my God,” my son whispered, leaning in. “It looks like the Lament Configuration from Hellraiser.”\n\nMy wife took a step back, her face twisting with alarm. “Are you fucking serious? You dreamt you were in hell and now you’ve built a device that will take us there? Are you sure you didn’t just open a portal to hell with that thing? Because that hand,” she said, pointing, “looks like something straight from the Leviathan.”\n\n“It’s not what it looks like,” I said quickly, holding the cube defensively. “The first time it scanned me, I flinched. I moved my hand too fast. That’s how we got... that.” I gestured to the grotesque thing.\n\n“That’s what you got?” she shot back, incredulous. “What were you expecting? Fucking daisies?”\n\nI sighed, exasperated. “I didn’t expect it to scan me, okay? It caught me off guard. But I figured it out now. Unlike the Infinitron, this thing needs time. If you keep still, it’s precise.”\n\n“Precise,” she muttered skeptically, glaring at the cube. “Sure. Let’s just pretend precision looks like a horror show.”\n\nUnfazed, my son stepped closer, his curiosity undimmed. “So, it scans you. Then what? Does it just... print whatever it finds? Like... can it do pizza? Or, I don’t know, money?”\n\n“Let’s start small,” I said, rubbing my temples as I stifled a laugh. “Before we try conjuring currency, or even a buffet, we need to understand its limits—and test it safely.”\n\nMy wife raised an eyebrow, her skepticism razor-sharp. “When the hell have you ever done anything slowly? Or has all your constant ‘boom!’ given you a TBI?”\n\nI shot her a dry look. “Watch,” I said, ignoring the smirk tugging at the corner of her mouth.\n\nCarefully, I set the cube on the workbench, its cool surface humming faintly against my fingertips. I aimed at a nearby wrench and pressed its side. The intricate patterns etched across its face began to glow, their eerie light spreading out in rippling waves, casting long, surreal shadows across the walls.	4	69	f	2026-02-27 11:56:46.621112-05	2026-02-27 15:06:22.683423-05	normal	\N
