115	30	1	The Trailer - Before the Fall	Todd and Stacey argue in their squalid trailer home. Todd begins experiencing disturbing hallucinations after Stacey secretly dosed him with mushrooms. The reality around them starts to fracture as a supernatural force tears through their world.	The trailer reeked of stale beer, fried bologna, and something else—something sickly and off that wouldn’t be pinned down. It hung heavy in the air, mingling with the last dregs of a blood-orange sunset peeking through the ripped screens. The feeble light offered little respite from the stifling summer heat that clung to the walls like a suffocating blanket.\n\n“What the fuck did you do to me, Stacey?” Todd’s moan sent a tremor through the particle board walls, shaking loose a half-empty pack of discount smokes from the stained countertop. The accusation made the acrid odor in the air curl at the edges, intensifying its presence.\n\nStacey, a wisp of a woman with perpetually bruised skin, huddled on the filthy pullout couch. Her hands trembled, but not from fear. After years of enduring Todd’s wrath, rage pulsed stronger than terror within her. Her voice, once cracked and timid, mirrored Todd’s harsh tones as she mustered her own defiance. “Ain’t done nothin’ different, you worthless sack of...”\n\n“Why the fuck can I see the faces? In the walls, damn it!” He swatted at his arm as if something clung there, his movements frantic and desperate. His pupils, black pools amidst yellowed sclera, darted frantically around the room, searching for answers that eluded him.\n\nFear pricked Stacey’s heart. Sure, she’d contemplated slipping rat poison into Todd’s nightly sandwiches more than once, but in the end, she’d opted for a softer touch. After a heated argument about him running off with her kid to save them from the deep state and leaving her behind in “this hellhole,” she’d gotten in touch with her brother’s girlfriend’s best friend, someone who specialized in a different kind of “mellow.” Crushing a few of the mushrooms Riley-boy had scored, claimed they were some “special kinda trip”—into Todd’s sandwich seemed harmless enough at the time. Just another ploy to buy herself a hazy reprieve from the storm of their lives and possibly half a decent sleep. Just one stupid fight in a thousand.\n\nBut this... this ain’t normal. She wasn’t shouldn’t be hallucinating; what Todd was seeing were the exact same fucking things. Her eyes widened as she watched his hands morph into gnarled branches, the trailer’s paneling ripple and warp into leering masks—grotesque visions birthed from his own addled mind. “Just stop yelling, alright? They get worse when—”\n\nA sharp shriek, like metal shearing against metal, split the air, drowning out Todd’s frantic cursing. It rose above their voices, followed by a pounding that shook the entire trailer. In that moment, they knew they weren’t inside the confines of their dilapidated sanctuary anymore. The familiar stink and squalor dissolved as if wiped away by an unseen hand.\n\n“Kaylee” she screamed as she frantically looked toward the room where her daughter should have been sleeping melted away.	0	84	f	2026-02-27 11:57:07.141277-05	2026-02-27 15:09:35.797104-05	normal	\N
116	30	2	The Crimson Hellscape - Flight from Titans	Todd and Stacey find themselves in a nightmarish dimension filled with impossible architecture and monstrous entities. They flee across a hostile landscape, pursued by grotesque horrors and a mysterious voice that offers them rest.	Blinking hard at the landscape before her, Stacey struggled to comprehend what lay before them. An impossible crimson sky hung low, heavy with something other than twilight. Jagged spires of black rock thrust up from the cracked ground, reaching towards monstrous clouds that billowed like living smoke. It felt cold, even though the air shimmered with an oppressive heat that seemed to seep into their bones.\n\nTodd, now on his hands and knees, scrabbled in the dirt and ash with his branch-like hands. It was the sound that made them turn in unison. A low, rumbling growl filled the air—something both primal and mechanical, a menacing symphony of power and danger. And then, they saw it.\n\nOn the horizon, towering, larger than any skyscraper they had ever laid eyes on, was a skeletal titan. Gears ground and churned deep within its metal gut, emitting sparks that rained down like a million fireflies. Each step it took made the cracked earth below groan in protest. Its clawed hand raked across the crimson sky, and Todd screamed, more in anguish than fear.\n\nIn that moment of terror and realization, they understood. Whatever Riley-boy had sold them, it wasn’t just a high. It had torn a hole in the fabric of their world, plunging them into... where? Hell? Some dimension cooked up by Todd’s own twisted brain? It didn’t matter anymore.\n\nWhat mattered was the thing watching them.\n\nBelow the towering titan, its shadow cast as a vast blot across the cracked landscape, something else stirred. It’s glowing red head pierced the gloom, and whatever monstrous maw held them let out a chilling hiss that echoed through the air.\n\nIt looked at them.\n\nThat was when they ran.\n\nThey ran as if chased by the end of reason itself, their flight fueled by terror so primal it stripped them of thought. Stacey’s lungs burned; her breaths ragged as knives. Her legs screamed with each jarring step, the razor sharp rocks tearing at her feet and leaving blood to mingle with the cloying ash that blanketed the ground like the shroud of a suffocating grave. Behind her, the echo of Todd’s rasping sobs punctuated the air, his earlier defiance dissolved into gasps that clawed desperately at the scant remnants of sanity.\n\nEventually, the skeletal titan faded into the distance, lost to memory, the terror of size replaced with fresh horrors rising from the desolation. Faceless shapes slithered and skittered from blackened spires; their shifting forms barely tethered to reality. Shadows warped and elongated unnaturally, limbs bending in ways that defied comprehension, their movements a mockery of the physical world. The air pulsed with something unnamable, each vibration thrumming against their bones as though the very core of creation rebelled against their presence.\n\nFurther and further still, they ran. Every step was an act of defiance against a universe that had turned hostile, where every twisted pathway promised only further torment. The horizon seemed to writhe and shift with cruel intent, as though the landscape itself sought to ensnare them. Stacey’s heart thundered, her mind fracturing under the weight of this unrelenting nightmare, yet something unyielding pushed her forward.\n\nA flicker of hope, or perhaps desperation, burned within her. A fragile ember against the howling void.\n\nTheir pursuers were more than physical horrors; they were echoes of madness, fragments of an unknowable force that seeped into their minds and bodies. The world around them crumbled and reformed in grotesque parodies, the ground beneath their feet moist with malice. They did not falter. They had become creatures of survival, driven not by courage but by the raw instinct to endure. Beyond the crimson haze and the writhing shadows lay their fate, whether salvation or obliteration.\n\nThey ran toward it with all that remained of their broken will.\n\n“Can’t...go…on…” Stacey gasped. Her knees buckled, sending her sprawling onto the razor sharp landscape slicing her arms and hands. Todd, now a moment ahead, looked back. His eyes were still wild, but a sliver of his monstrous personality seemed to resurface.\n\n“Come on bitch,” he grunted, pulling her up. He didn’t wait for thanks, but the gesture made Stacey look at him anew. They’d fallen further than the lowest rung of the trailer park ladder, yet here he was, pulling her onward.\n\nThere was nowhere to go. The land stretched forever, an endless canvas of death and machinery. It wasn’t until exhaustion stole their last, desperate surge of strength that they saw the change.\n\nAn echo of green bled onto the horizon, a putrid stain against the blood-red sky. At first, it was just a smudge, but as they hobbled forward, it took shape.\n\n“Trees...” Todd managed, his voice barely a whisper now. His branch-like hand trembled as he pointed. They weren’t trees like back home, not exactly. These were monstrous, twisted shadows of trees, their bark black and scarred. There was still something clinging to them, a perversion of life that made their monstrous pursuers seem almost merciful.\n\nFrom nowhere it came, a voice that wasn’t a voice at all. It was inside their heads, a thought made real: “Suffering... pain... lost...” There was a curiosity about it, a cold analysis of their desperation. Beneath it was a chilling echo of their own misery.\n\nTodd fell to his knees, hands covering his ears. It was too much, too close to the monster he saw when he closed his eyes. Stacey collapsed beside him, her ragged breaths mingling with his agonized gasps.\n\n“Rest... I offer. Succor. This pain has an end...”\n\n“Who... who are you?” Stacey choked out; a strange defiance sparked by the unknown tormentor.\n\n“... I AM...” the voice whispered back, filling their broken minds. This wasn’t a name, but a statement of being. It held an immense power, a sense of being beyond time itself. “Come follow me, and I shall give you rest... “\n\nThey stumbled on, propelled forward only by their unwavering determination to get the fuck out of there. The green ahead acted as a lifeline, guiding them through the labyrinthine twists and turns. Every step further towards its verdant shelter seemed to draw them closer to the haunting voice that whispered in the rustle of blackened leaves, its eerie presence offering a perverse kind of comfort amidst the desolation.\n\nShadows grew thicker, enveloping them in an oppressive darkness that seemed to seep into their very souls, slowing them down, and pulling them back into the nightmare. Each twisted trunk they passed stood as a frozen scream against the backdrop of a blood-streaked sky, a chilling testament to the horrors that had unfolded within this forsaken place. The air hung heavy with an unsettling stillness, broken only by the labored breaths of Stacey, whose wounds throbbed relentlessly, each pulse sending waves of searing pain through her weakened body.\n\nJust when it felt as if the encroaching darkness would consume them whole, an unexpected opening materialized before their eyes. Suddenly, they found themselves in the forest's heart, standing next to a shimmering lake, its surface mirroring the crimson hues of the sky above. However, where one would expect to see swirling pools of blood, it was an ethereal pale green that beckoned them forward. The lake’s mesmerizing glow held an otherworldly allure, promising respite from their torment.\n\nBeyond the serene lake, figures took shape, their forms blurred by exhaustion and the peculiar light that enveloped them. It was as if these spectral entities existed within a realm between reality and dreams, their presence both unsettling and strangely comforting. In this impossible oasis, an offer lingered,