143	39	1	The Empire Expands	The narrator's son celebrates his thriving Roblox game and merchandise business, while the family jugggles packing orders in their garage using Matter Replicators. The narrator struggles with nostalgia for pure scientific work while his family reminds him that their commercial enterprise is its own form of innovation.	“Two. Thousand. Players!” my son shouted, his voice echoing through the house like a firework. He spun his chair dramatically, grinning from ear to ear as he pointed to the screen. “Look, Dad! My newest game just went live on Roblox, and I already hit 2,000 players! Infinitron assisted coding for the win!”\n\nI poked my into his cardboard fort, his little “dining room corner come cardboard kingdom” where multiple monitors flickered with a chaotic array of stats, game designs, and Twitch chat scrolling faster than I could read. His headphones hung around his neck, blasting EDM remixes of sea shanties and AJR into the background. A towering stack of mini-figurines, Matter Replicated versions of his game characters, loomed beside his desk like a shrine to his burgeoning empire.\n\n“That’s amazing,” I said, trying to sound enthused, even as I wiped the sweat from my forehead with the back of my arm. “But can you keep the volume down? Your mom and I are drowning in bubble wrap out here.”\n\n“Yeah, yeah,” he mumbled, half-listening as he tapped furiously at his keyboard. “But seriously, Dad, the merch is selling faster than we can replicate it! I think I need more storage racks here. And maybe, like, another Matter Replicator to help with creation times.”\n\nFrom the garage, my wife called out, “We’re not expanding your empire until you finish that geometry assignment! Your teacher emailed me again—third time this month!”\n\nHe groaned dramatically. “Moooom! Do you know how many kids are DMing me about this game? It’s educational! I’m basically teaching math and resource management through an MMORPG!”\n\nI snorted, trying not to laugh as I returned to the garage. “That’s one way to spin it.”\n\nThe garage was a sauna of packing material, and the steady hum from our matter replicators. What once had been my little lab—a place of discovery and experimentation. Now, it looked like the backend of a small fulfillment center. Shelves groaned under the weight of packages ready to ship, and the floor was a minefield of discarded packing peanuts.\n\n“Hey, Dad!” My son appeared at the doorway, holding up a box. “Just got another order for a custom USB hub. The one with the neon flames etched on it, with the Moomba figure holding the wire. This design is pure fire—literally.” He cackled at his own joke, his energy boundless.\n\nI glanced up from the shipping labels I was applying. “That’s great, but I mean it—homework first. Neither the Infinitron nor ChatGPT can bail you out on state testing.”\n\n“Dad,” he said, planting his hands on his hips with the righteous indignation of a budding entrepreneur, “when was the last time a test score went viral? I’m creating content. I’m engaging with community. That’s real-world learning!”\n\nMy wife smirked, balancing a box on one hip as she taped it shut. “He’s not wrong, you know. His figurines are flying off the shelves, and his Twitch donations paid for the water bill last month.”\n\n“I know,” I said, leaning back against the table and rubbing the bridge of my nose. “It’s just... Sometimes I miss the old days, you know? When we were working on... I don’t know, actual science. Controlled pyrotechnics, reaching into the unknown, discovering new phenomena, and then fleeing for our lives when it went BOOM. It felt... meaningful.”\n\nMy son tilted his head, studying me like one of his game analytics charts. “Dad, this is meaningful. People love what we’re making! Look at this,”—he held out his phone, which displayed a glowing five-star review of his merchandise—“this kid said our mini-figures inspired him to start designing his own game. How is that not making a difference?”\n\nI stared at the screen, the glowing words staring back at me like a challenge. “Inspiring future creators.” Maybe he was right. Maybe our detour into commerce wasn’t as far from our original goals as I thought.\n\n“You’ve got to admit,” my wife chimed in, her voice cutting through my self-pity, “this beats all those garage explosions you called ‘science experiments.’ At least now, when something blows up, it’s because we’re actually making money off it.”\n\nI sighed, running a hand through my hair. “You’re both teaming up on me now, huh? Fine. Maybe this is another kind of frontier. It’s just... It feels so different. Like we traded the pursuit of knowledge for the pursuit of likes and followers.”\n\n“Maybe,” my wife said, gently nudging my shoulder, “but innovation isn’t always about the grand unknown. Sometimes it’s about making life better for people, even if it’s in unexpected ways.”\n\nMy son grinned. “Exactly! Plus, if you think about it, I’m like a modern-day Renaissance man—game dev, Twitch streamer, merch mogul. That’s innovation, Dad. It’s like... entrepreneurial art!”\n\nI couldn’t help but laugh. “Alright, alright. I get it. Let’s not lose sight of the bigger picture, okay? Whether we’re building robots, games, or... USB hubs with flames, let’s make sure it matters.”\n\nMy wife raised an eyebrow. “Possibly you could cut back on the philosophical musings and pack those last three boxes before the courier arrives?”\n\nI smiled, grabbing a roll of tape. The garage hummed with activity again, a strange blend of chaos and purpose. While working, I realized our new path wasn’t as different from our original vision as I feared. Perhaps, in our own way, we were still reaching for the stars—just with a different kind of fire.	0	149	f	2026-02-27 11:58:57.113507-05	2026-02-27 15:34:52.287488-05	normal	\N
