132	35	1	The Ten-Thousand-Dollar Milestone	The family celebrates reaching $10,000 in sales in just two weeks, but the protagonist's unease about the operation's ease and potential consequences tempers their joy. They discuss scaling challenges and commit to sustainable growth while remaining under the radar.	“We did it!” I exclaimed, practically bursting into the kitchen with my laptop in hand. “Ten thousand dollars in sales! Can you believe it? Ten grand!”\n\nMy wife, stirring a pot of tea on the stove, turned with a wide smile. “Wait, already? It’s only April. I thought you said it would take years to hit numbers like that.”\n\n“It should have,” I said, dropping into a chair and spinning the laptop around to show her the dashboard. “But here it is, clear as day. Orders are rolling in like clockwork.”\n\n“Wow,” my son chimed in, popping his head around the corner, munching on a snack. “So, uh... are we rich now?”\n\nI just laughed out loud. “Ten grand is great, but that’s about as much as I used to make in a month. It only took us a couple of weeks to get here.”\n\n“Still,” my wife said, sitting down beside me, “this is incredible. We’re finally seeing the payoff for all those late nights and UPS runs.”\n\nThe initial excitement waned as a wave of unease settled over the room. My wife noticed it first. “So... what’s the catch?” she asked, narrowing her eyes. “You don’t look entirely thrilled.”\n\n“It feels off, somehow. Too easy or something.” I said with a resigned sigh.\n\nOur operation was running relatively smoothly, but the weight of our success was starting to show. “Okay,” my wife said . “We’ve got the logistics figured out, but what happens as the demand keeps rising? Can the replicator keep up?”\n\n“That’s the beauty of the system,” I replied, gesturing toward the compact handheld device. “It can scale infinitely. As long as we’ve got the designs stored, we can replicate as much as we want. Want a whole box of things? Just tell it to replicate the box instead of each individual component one at a time.”\n\n“Still,” she said, crossing her arms. “We need to be careful. Success can attract attention—and not always the good kind.”\n\nShe wasn’t wrong. Even as we celebrated our progress, a persistent doubt remained. For now, our operation is flying under the radar. But how long could that last?\n\n“Alright,” I said, exhaling slowly. Let’s do it bit by bit. For the time being, we focus on making products that stand out, ones that people love. The rest... we’ll deal with when it comes.”\n\nMy son grinned, raising a fist in the air. “Ten grand, baby! Let’s go!”\n\nWe all laughed, the tension momentarily easing. But as I glanced at the replicator’s interface, I couldn’t shake the feeling that our journey was only beginning—and the road ahead would be anything but smooth.	0	10	f	2026-02-27 11:58:06.143632-05	2026-02-27 15:24:08.696694-05	normal	\N
134	35	3	Playing the Market	The protagonist proposes using the Infinitron to trade the stock market to raise capital for a warehouse. After the wife's skeptical agreement, they test and deploy the AI's trading capabilities, which quickly generate impressive returns while drawing regulatory attention.	“What are you thinking?” she asked, giving me the stink eye. “Because you’ve got that look again.”\n\n“We need money,” I said, the words heavy in the air. “Real money. Enough to move this operation out of this house and into a warehouse.”\n\nShe folded her arms, her skepticism clear. “And how do you propose we do that? Print money? Because we’ve had that conversation.”\n\n“No,” I said quickly. “Not money. Data. The Infinitron can process complex patterns, predict outcomes... We could use it to trade on the stock market.”\n\nShe blinked, stunned into silence for a moment. “You want to play the stock market? With our Amazon money?”\n\n“It’s not gambling if we have the ultimate cheat code,” I argued. “The Infinitron can analyze trends, predict shifts—it’s like having a thousand analysts working in real time.”\n\n“And what if it’s wrong?” she countered, her voice rising. “What if we lose everything?”\n\n“More nights packing boxes, I guess. But that’s the thing—the Infinitron doesn’t guess. It calculates,” I said, leaning forward. “Look, we’re already taking a risk every day we keep this operation running out of our living room. We need to scale up, and we can only do that if we have a larger place to operate out of. This is our best shot of getting there quicker.”\n\nMy son wandered in, catching the tail end of the conversation. “Wait, you’re gonna play the stock market? Like, Wolf of Wall Street style?”\n\n“Not exactly. Your mom would never go for the coke,” I said, laughing despite the tension.\n\n“Or hookers.” She snorted.\n\n“But kind of. The Infinitron will do the hard part. We just need to decide how much to risk.” I said.\n\nShe turned back to me, her expression soft