“Yeah, sure,” he said, crunching on his cereal. “If this thing is so smart, why can’t it make us rich faster?”\n\nI leaned back, frustrated. “Because the stock market isn’t a vending machine. It’s complex, unpredictable, and even with the Infinitron’s help, there are limits. We could unleash the beast, but then it would devour the market and society would collapse.”\n\n“What can we really do, then?” my wife asked, her voice tinged with desperation. “We can’t keep living like this—boxes everywhere, no space, no end in sight.”\n\nI stared at the screen, the flickering data reflecting my own frustration. “We could try taking bigger risks,” I suggested cautiously. “Increase the stakes, focus on high-volatility stocks. It’s dangerous, but the potential returns could get us to the warehouse faster.”\n\n“Okay, so perhaps the stock market isn’t the only way,” my son said, setting his bowl aside. “What if we use the replicator to make something big? Like, I don’t know, a product people would pay a ton for?”\n\n“Like what?” My wife asked.\n\n“Gold.” My son replied.\n\nMy heart raced with excitement and fear as I leaned forward in my chair against the table, trying to mask my eagerness. My son tore his gaze away from his computer screen for a moment, giving me a quick glance before returning Roblox.\n\nI struggled to keep my composure as I continued eagerly, “Just imagine what we could do with it! We could sell it and make a fortune! Perhaps even take that vacation we’ve been talking about. We wouldn’t have to wait either. There’s got to be buyers out there ready to buy today.”\n\nI rushed to our bedroom and retrieved my late grandfather’s prized possession - a golden bracelet that had been passed down through generations. Its intricate design, gleaming in the dim light, spoke volumes about our family’s rich history and possible royal lineage.\n\nWith a quick scan and replication. We soon had 2.\n\nThe gold seemed to cast a glamor on me. Feeling emboldened by this success, I eagerly began replicating more copies - doubling the quantity of gold chains until they piled up like mountains of shining wealth. Next, I downloaded some schematics for an electronic foundry and went out onto the back patio to do some smelting.\n\n“What the hell are you doing?” The wife yelled at me as she came out to the stuff I was setting up on the patio. “The fucking machine can create bars without you needing to melt this shit down. All you needed to do was give it a sample of gold so it could replicate the atomic structure.”\n\nAshamed, and muttering under my breath, I went back inside and programmed in the gold bards. The replicator continued to churn out glimmering gold bars, my initial excitement turning to apprehension. The already crowded room was now even more hard to navigate. Filled with a literal ton of gold, all the previously free floor space between boxes was now occupied by stacks of shiny, happy bars.\n\n“Talk about yellow brick road, Dr. Oz.” My son snarkily mentioned.\n\nA creeping unease settled in. What were we supposed to do with all this gold? It’d be easy to sell it to someone, somebody had said.\n\nThat someone should be shot.\n\nOur first attempts to turn our fortune into cash were met with disappointment. Local pawn shops were unimpressed by our offers, only to handle a couple of ounces at one time and unwilling to handle the scale of our production. We were left with only minuscule sales compared to the vast reserves at our feet. Not only that, what was our plan? Tell them we had a ton of gold we needed to unload? Talk about security risk...\n\nWhile we struggled with conflicting ideas on how to dispose of the gold, a sense of urgency and paranoia began to consume us. Every potential solution seemed fraught with risks—from traveling shop to shop all over the state, to finding a wealthy buyer willing to purchase bulk amounts. Our desire for profit clashed with our fear of getting caught, leaving us conflicted and uncertain about our next steps in this tangled web of wealth and worry.	0	102	f	2026-02-27 11:58:27.481713-05	2026-02-27 15:29:19.507091-05	normal	\N
