ng,” I said too quickly.\n\nShe folded her arms, her brow furrowing. “That’s not the face of someone doing ‘nothing.’ Spill.”\n\nI let out a sigh, running my hand through my hair. “I... found someone who might be able to take the gold off our hands.”\n\nHer eyes widened in alarm. “Who? How? Please don’t tell me you went looking for criminals.”\n\n“It’s not like I had a lot of options!” I shot back. “It’s not like there’s a legit gold buy-back program for, you know, people who replicate wealth out of thin air.”\n\nHer gaze softened, but only slightly. “And who is this brilliant buyer?”\n\nI hesitated. “He calls himself The Viper.”\n\n“The Viper?” she repeated, her tone dripping with skepticism. “Really? That doesn’t scream trustworthy to you?”\n\n“It’s all I’ve got,” I said, my voice dropping. “He’s meeting me tonight. I’ll bring a single bar, see how it goes.”\n\nShe shook her head, clearly uneasy. “This is a terrible idea.”\n\n“I know,” I admitted, holding her gaze. “But we’re out of good ones.”\n\nWith a deep breath, I pocketed a single bar of gold and grabbed my keys. “You should come with. It’ll be fun,” I joked.	1	144	f	2026-02-27 11:58:27.481713-05	2026-02-27 15:30:15.446954-05	normal	\N
