ll paint now a reflective surface that seemed to ripple with the ambient light.\n\nThe headlights morphed into LED matrices, cutting through the dim garage like miniature suns. The tires evolved into advanced alloys with adaptive terrain systems—because apparently, even the nanites didn’t think we’d stick to paved roads forever.\n\nInside, the transformation was nothing short of breathtaking. The control panels turned into sleek, touch-sensitive glass displays. The seats transformed into ergonomic masterpieces with built-in massage functions that could probably cure stress just by looking at them.\n\n“Does it come with a butler? Or autopilot?” my son asked, running his hand over the new dashboard.\n\n“No,” I said, mock-serious, “but it might come with an opinion about your mom’s speeding habits.”\n\nThe pièce de resistance of this project—the super-secret upgrade—lay hidden beneath the hood. The old, sputtering engine had been replaced with a fully electric engine powered by an Eternal Engine. This wasn’t just eco-friendly; it was perpetual. My wife would never need to charge it, fill it with gas, or worry about range again. It wasn’t just a car—it was independence on wheels, and maybe the closest thing to a miracle of engineering we’d ever made.\n\nWhen the nanites completed their masterpiece and dissolved back into their storage unit, we stood in awe of what we’d accomplished. The car before us wasn’t just functional—it was a symbol of everything the nanites made possible.\n\n“I think Mom’s going to freak out,” my son said, grinning ear to ear.\n\n“Freak out in a good way or a bad way?” I inquired, despite being aware of the answer.\n\n“Well, she might ask how we did it,” he admitted, “but we could just say it was a really good Groupon.”\n\nI laughed, but a pang of unease crept into the back of my mind. “Do we... tell the insurance company?”\n\nMy son snorted. “What are you gonna say? ‘Hi, I’d like to insure my nanite-upgraded hybrid supercar. Oh, and it self-repairs.’”\n\n“Fair point,” I said, shaking my head. “Some secrets are better left unsaid.”	2	69	t	2026-02-27 11:59:23.65491-05	2026-02-27 15:43:06.431027-05	normal	\N
