ng to my knee. “Wait. You’re walking... without limping?”\n\nI nodded, swallowing hard. “It worked.”\n\nHer hands flew to her hips. “And the screaming? The recordings I just listened to where it sounded like you were being eaten alive? That’s just a side effect?”\n\n“It’s... part of the process,” I admitted, trying to suppress the memory.\n\nShe shook her head, her anger slowly dissolving into exasperated disbelief. “You’re unbelievable. You know that, right?”\n\n“Yeah,” I mumbled. “But it worked. The pain’s gone.”\n\nAfter a long silence, she sighed, rubbing her temples. “Alright. You’ve done your mad scientist thing, and it’s... amazing. Just next time, talk to me first. I hate surprises—especially ones that involve screaming.”\n\n“I promise,” I said earnestly.\n\n“You’d better fix whatever made it hurt that much,” she added, pointing a finger at the AutoDoc. “No one should have to go through that kind of pain—not even you.”\n\nI stood there, testing my newly restored knee. A mix of awe and unease churned within me. The pain that had haunted me for decades was gone, erased in a single, excruciating moment. But the memory of that agony lingered, a grim reminder of the cost of progress.	1	164	t	2026-02-27 11:59:46.281952-05	2026-02-27 15:48:27.36101-05	normal	\N
