d them down the path of insanity and destruction. As an immortal being from the depths of hell, I could only watch with twisted fascination as their hubris consumed them.	1	167	f	2026-02-27 11:59:53.171376-05	2026-02-27 15:51:16.120877-05	normal	\N
162	45	1	The Dream Takes Shape	The narrator and his family enjoy thriving business success that allows them to plan a trip to Japan. On the flight, he becomes intrigued by a teleportation documentary, planting the seed for his next obsession. Upon arrival in Kansai, his wife experiences an emotional homecoming while he begins to feel the pull of deeper mysteries.	Our business was thriving beyond our wildest expectations, our latest projections were showing an average sales volume of over $15k a day. Not only that, but with the automation in full swing (and coordinating regular UPS pickups from our warehouse), meant we no longer had to make nightly package drop offs. This success also granted us the rare luxury of stepping away to fulfill a different dream—a trip back to Japan.\n\nEver since we were evacuated to the U.S. after the Fukushima disaster, my wife’s longing to see her mother again was unmistakable, and our teenage son, born in Japan but raised in the U.S., deserved to experience his roots firsthand. So in mid-June we decided to take the trip.\n\n“It’s been so long,” she said one evening, her voice tinged with both nostalgia and resolve. “I want to show him where it all started.”\n\n“We’ve talked about this for years,” I replied, squeezing her hand. “It’s time.”\n\nThe flight over was a restless blur. I rarely slept on airplanes, and this trip was no exception. At some point during the endless hours, I found myself half-watching a documentary on teleportation. The narrator’s voice droned on about its tantalizing potential, only to conclude with a predictable problem: the staggering amount of energy it would require.\n\nI couldn’t help but chuckle to myself. Wouldn’t it be great if that could happen? Just skip the entire flight altogether? The thought lingered, a seed of an idea that refused to fade even as I turned my attention elsewhere.\n\nWhen we landed at Kansai International Airport, the hum of activity and a flood of Japanese announcements welcomed us. My wife’s eyes glistened as she whispered, “I’m home,” and I felt the weight of her words settle in my chest. It wasn’t just a homecoming for her—it was a bridge to a part of our family’s story my son had never fully known.\n\nNear the baggage claim, my mother-in-law stood waiting, her smile as warm as the embrace she enveloped my wife in. “Oakairi-nasia! You’re here,” she said, her voice trembling. She turned to our son, reaching out to brush his hair. “Look at you—so grown up. I’ve missed you.”\n\n“Hi, Ba-san,” he said, grinning. “I’ve missed you too. And... I want to see everything.”\n\nThe next days were a blur of discovery. Kyoto’s narrow streets opened their secrets to us: the heady aromas of street food, the vibrant chaos of markets, and the serene beauty of ancient shrines. We meandered through temples bathed in golden light, each step a connection to the past, each story shared by my mother-in-law a thread weaving us closer.\n\nOne evening, as we strolled under the paper lanterns of the “frozen in time” alleyways, I turned to my wife. “What was it like growing up here?” I asked.\n\nHer expression softened. “It was simpler,” she began, her gaze distant. “We played in parks, ate choco-bananas, and shared whatever we had with neighbors.”\n\n“Can we see those places?” my son asked eagerly. “Tomorrow?”\n\nHer laughter was light, her joy infectious. “Of course. Let’s relive them together.”\n\nAmidst the joy, I found my mind drifting. The quiet beauty of Kinkaku-ji Temple sparked a restless curiosity, its fusion of history and innovation tugging at something deeper. Late at night, while my wife and son slept, I began sketching in the soft light of the garden, the hum of cicadas underscoring my thoughts as I allowed the Infinitron interface in my head to run some calculations. The concept of teleportation—instantaneous movement across time and space—began to crystallize, teasing me with possibilities.\n\n“Still working?” my wife’s voice broke through one evening,