ed in sweat, my mind a storm of doubt and determination.\n\nLater that night, exhaustion overtook me, pulling me into a vivid dream. I stood in the shadow of a Japanese temple gate, its graceful arches framing a path through the forest. Aokigahara’s haunting trees loomed in the distance, their twisted forms both beautiful and menacing. The vision was clear: the portal couldn’t exist as an unsupported anomaly—it needed structure, a foundation to anchor it to our reality.\n\nI woke with a start, the answer blazing in my mind. Inspired, I commanded an army of nanites to begin construction on the other side of the warehouse, the location where the previous gateway had failed. I opened the portal, and the swarm moved like a living organism, their metallic bodies glinting as they synchronized to form an intricate network, pulsing with electric energy. Even as they worked at lightning speed, they could not finish before the gateway quickly collapsed again, unable to sustain itself.\n\nFrustration boiled within me, but the failure sparked a new idea. Instead of starting from scratch each time, what if the nanites pre-assembled the gateway’s structure, leaving only final adjustments to be made during activation. The portal would open, the swarm and mostly completed structure would go through, and once there perform the final calibration. With the new plan in place, I unleashed the swarm once more.\n\nDrawing from the serene design of Japanese temple gates and the ominous majesty of Aokigahara’s trees, I completed the structural framework for the portals. When the nanites finished the final connection, the room filled with a sonorous, resonant hum. The ERB shimmered to life, the nanites went through, along with the other half, calibration was locked in—and the ERBs luminous energy flowing seamlessly between the two gateway structures.\n\nAfter a few moments of holding my breath, and some shaky readings during their traversal, the wormhole actually stabilized. The symmetrical 