172	47	3	Stargazing and Wonder	The narrator and his son spend an evening stargazing, discussing meteors, light, and the nature of time and speed. Their conversation about instant travel plants seeds for a new scientific breakthrough inspired by their genuine connection.	**********\n\nThe next night, my son and I crammed onto old patio chairs, their worn frame groaning in protest. He wrestled with the telescope, smudging the lens as he twisted it far too aggressively.\n\n“Careful, you’re not trying to break it,” I said, nudging his hand away to adjust it myself.\n\n“Chill, Dad. I know what I’m doing,” he shot back, his tone somewhere between defensive and playful.\n\nI gave him a look but let it slide. “Sure you do. Just don’t make me duct-tape it back together again.”\n\nHe ignored me, squinting into the eyepiece. “Just replicate another one. You think we’ll see a shooting star tonight?”\n\n“Maybe,” I said, leaning back to gaze at the clear sky. “But they’re not really stars, you know. They’re just meteors burning up in the atmosphere.”\n\n“Yeah, I know,” he said, rolling his eyes but still watching the sky. “But they look cool—like fire racing through space. If we catch one, it’ll be epic.”\n\n“You don’t catch them,” I said, smirking. “You spot them, if you’re lucky.”\n\nHe sat back with a huff. “Fine. Spot one. Whatever.”\n\nWe both stared at the stars in silence, the cool night air settling around us.\n\n“Did you know stars communicate with light?” I said, breaking the quiet.\n\nHe glanced at me, suspicious. “Light... talks? Like your butt?”\n\nI snorted. “It’s not ‘talking’ like us, genius. Stars send out bursts of light—patterns. It’s like Morse code but way cooler. Those patterns tell us stuff, like how hot they are or how far away they are.”\n\nHis interest piqued despite himself. “Wait, so light is, like, data or something?”\n\n“Exactly,” I said, nodding. “It moves faster than anything else. Nothing beats light speed.”\n\nHe tilted his head, thinking. “So, if I was, like, a photon or whatever, flying through space... what would I see?”\n\nI grinned. “You wouldn’t see much, because time doesn’t exist for light.”\n\nHe blinked at me. “That makes zero sense. How can it go anywhere if there’s no time?”\n\n“Think of space and time like a giant stretchy sheet,” I said, gesturing with my hands. “Light moves so fast it pulls the sheet tight, like sling shotting through it. From light’s perspective, it’s already at its destination the second it starts.”\n\nHe stared at me, processing. “So it just skips the whole trip?”\n\n“Not skips,” I corrected. “It’s more like the trip and the destination happen at the same time for light. Different rules.”\n\nHe leaned back, staring up at the sky. “That’s insane. Do you think we’ll ever move like that? Just... instantly be wherever we want to go?”\n\n“Who knows,” I said, shrugging. “Possibly. But figuring that out? That’s the dream.”\n\nHe gave a small smile. “If we ever do, you better let me name it.”\n\nI laughed, nudging his shoulder. “Deal. Just don’t call it something stupid like ‘ZoomTube’ or whatever.”\n\nHe rolled his eyes again but didn’t hide his grin as he turned back to the telescope, his teenage skepticism giving way to genuine wonder.	2	178	f	2026-02-27 12:00:50.774866-05	2026-02-27 16:01:50.513606-05	normal	\N
