epted it, scanned it with a sleek handheld device, then looked up, her expression unreadable. “I’m afraid we require more than just identification. You’ll need proof of your business operations, financial history, and the origin of your assets. Without those, we cannot proceed.”\n\n“Proof of... I mean, the gold is right here!” I protested, gesturing to the suitcase.\n\nHer lips twitched, almost imperceptibly. “Gold is a nice touch, sir, but we’re not a pawnshop.”\n\nI left the bank with my confidence slightly dented but undeterred. My next stop was a modern financial institution that screamed “tech-savvy exclusivity.” Its façade was all sleek glass and steel, and the entrance featured a cheerful animatronic robot.\n\n“Welcome,” the robot intoned in a saccharine voice. “How can I assist you today?”\n\n“I’d like to open an international account,” I said, holding my passport and bracing for rejection.\n\nThe robot scanned my documents, its digital face flickering as it processed the data. “One moment, sir.”\n\nI waited, my fingers tightening around the suitcase handle.\n\n“Apologies,” the robot finally said, its voice still unnervingly cheerful. “We are unable to process your request. Our security algorithms have detected irregularities with your history of boarder crossings. We must ensure the legitimacy of your presence before proceeding.”\n\n“Security algorithms? What irregularities?” I asked, exasperated.\n\n“I’m afraid I cannot disclose that information, sir. Have a wonderful day!” Its animated face beamed, an infuriatingly perfect smile.\n\nBy the time I left my eleventh bank, I felt like a walking punchline. The cobblestone streets of Zurich seemed to mock me as I trudged along, my suitcase of gold trailing behind me like a poorly conceived heist gone wrong.\n\n“Maybe gold isn’t the flex I thought it was,” I muttered under my breath, glaring at the suitcase.\n\nI slumped onto a bench, pulling out my phone to search for yet another bank. Nearby, a tourist couple 