501	124	1	Dissolution of Remembrance	A fragmented, poetic meditation on cycles of futile instruction and struggle. Various voices—the buried, dreamers, workers, and the gullible—are commanded to climb, struggle, learn, and follow, yet find only paradox, void, and self-deception. The passage dissolves into contradictions and cyclical despair.	Climb said the buried\n\nAs they were always told\n\nSo the student seeked\n\nStruggle said the dreamers from their palace of gilded cushions\n\nAs they polluted their dirt-tinged memories\n\nSo the worker bled\n\nLearn said the illiterate\n\nChoking on the smoke from a million books\n\nThe wailing ignore the torrential rain filling lakes nearby\n\nPouring themselves onto the pyre, the only and last resort\n\nFollow said the gullible\n\nLost, neither dead nor dying, within the thorns of their own poisons\n\nTowards a sign, they would not see\n\nThe void they despised, loved, to fall without ever grasping\n\nTo find, to arrive, to become\n\nThe truth that is no truth\n\nNever a secret was the secret\n\nEternity as the teacher no hours never taught\n\nA shade and a mirror\n\nSeeing yet unable to see\n\nAlone and yet still crowded\n\nNoisy and yet still silent\n\nDrowned out by the screams of the damned\n\nYearning to hear heaven\n\nDeaf to the infinite shouts from above\n\nAt worst the rope ignored\n\nA noose at best self-inflicted\n\nIn disgust, the frustrated turns away\n\nClimb say the flowers\n\nNewly buried forever on this ledge	0	328	f	2026-02-27 12:28:59.792928-05	2026-02-28 23:01:18.113297-05	normal	\N
182	49	4	The Cloaked Reveal and Goldman Sachs	The narrator arrives at an ivy-covered building in the old town where a cloaked, dramatic figure reveals that the entire treasure hunt was a tourist scam run by someone named Noah. The figure reveals that Goldman Sachs is located next door and is the actual solution to the narrator's banking problems.	By the time I arrived at an ivy-covered building deep in the old town, I was convinced this entire journey had been an elaborate game to free suckers from their money. Still, I entered cautiously, finding myself in a dimly lit room filled with dusty tomes and ancient artifacts. At its center stood a shadowy figure cloaked in mystery, his face obscured by a wide-brimmed hat.\n\n“You’ve come far, seeker,” he intoned, his voice deep and dramatic. “You’ve proven your worth and determination.”\n\nOut of patience, I blurted, “For fucks sake, can you just help me open an international bank account?”\n\nThe man paused, then threw back his head in laughter. “You’re looking for Goldman Sachs,” he said, stepping forward and removing his hat to reveal a face far too normal for the theatrics. “They’re next door.”\n\nI stared at him, dumbfounded. “Then why all the cloak-and-dagger shit?”\n\nHe grinned, shrugging. “You must’ve stumbled onto Noah’s little tourist racket. He’s been running it since The Da Vinci Code came out. Works like a charm.”\n\nI groaned, burying my face in my hands. “So... no international bank account?”\n\n“Not here, next door,” he said, patting my shoulder. “But hey, you got an adventure out of it.”\n\nAs I approached the grand building housing Goldman Sachs’s Zurich bank branch, I couldn’t help but feel a mix of awe and trepidation. The imposing stone façade, flanked by carved columns and bronze plaques, radiated power. Inside, the polished marble floors gleamed under the glow of an opulent crystal chandelier, and whispers of conversations in multiple languages echoed off the high, gilded ceilings. It was clear that this was no place for amateurs, like people dragging suitcases full of gold.	3	188	f	2026-02-27 12:01:35.555398-05	2026-02-27 19:23:59.009867-05	normal	\N
183	49	5	Herr Müller and the Gold Authentication	The narrator meets with Herr Müller, a competent Goldman Sachs banker, and attempts to deposit the gold bullion. After hedging about the origin of the gold bars, the banker agrees to authenticate the gold for potential account establishment, though compliance and documentation requirements remain stringent.	After checking in at the front desk, I was escorted through a labyrinth of hallways adorned with oil paintings and expensive furnishings. The banker’s office was no less intimidating. Oak-paneled walls framed a room that exuded wealth and control, complete with an enormous mahogany desk that could easily double as a conference table. When I sank into a plush leather chair, I couldn’t avoid the feeling that I was a microscopic fish in a very big, and very expensive, pond.\n\nEventually, the door opened, and the banker entered with a precision that suggested his every move was calculated. His tailored suit, immaculate glasses, and piercing gaze screamed competence, though the faint hint of skepticism in his eyes reminded me this wasn’t going to be easy.\n\n“I really should have replicated something better to wear.” I thought to myself.\n\n“Good morning,” he said, his Swiss-accented English smooth but formal. “I am Herr Müller. Let’s discuss your... unique request.”\n\nI cleared my throat and straightened up, trying to exude confidence. “Thank you for seeing me, Herr Müller. I’m representing The S Corp. We’re looking to establish an international presence, starting with a corporate account here. As you’ll see, we have significant assets to back our endeavors.”\n\nHis gaze shifted to the suitcase resting beside my chair. “Significant assets, indeed,” he remarked, his tone neutral but tinged with curiosity. “Gold, I presume?”\n\n“Yes,” I said, leaning forward. “Bars of gold bullion. Completely legitimate, of course. We believe this will provide a solid foundation for the account.”\n\nHerr Müller raised an eyebrow. “And the origin of these bars? May I ask where they were obtained?”\n\nMy heart sank. This was the part I hadn’t fully prepared for. “Well, they’re part of The S Corp’s... diversified portfolio,” I hedged. “Let’s just say we have some innovative methods of sourcing materials.”\n\nThe banker didn’t flinch. “Innovative, indeed. However, our institution requires rigorous compliance with international regulations. For deposits of this, ahem, nature, we must verify authenticity and trace the origin of the assets to ensure full compliance with anti-money laundering laws.”\n\n“Of course,” I said quickly, masking my rising panic. “Whatever you need.”\n\nHis expression softened slightly, though his professionalism never wavered. “We will need to conduct thorough testing and certification. Additionally, documentation will be required. Proof of purchase, chain of custody, and so forth. Without these, I’m afraid we cannot proceed.”\n\nI forced a smile, feeling a bead of sweat trickle down my back. “Understood. I’ll provide whatever’s necessary.”\n\nHerr Müller nodded, his tone growing marginally warmer. “I trust you appreciate the gravity of such deposits. We have handled assets of this magnitude before, but the process is neither quick nor simple. We will secure the gold for testing, and if all is in order, the funds will be added to your account.”\n\nRelief mingled with apprehension as I nodded. “Thank you, Herr Müller. I look forward to hearing from you.”\n\nThe formality of the meeting gave way to silence as I left the office, my mind racing with contingency plans. I knew crafting a believable backstory for the gold would be crucial—one that wouldn’t expose the secrets of the replicator. This was no longer just about opening an account; it was a high-stakes balancing act between innovation and deception.\n\nWaiting by the phone after the meeting was agonizing. My thoughts oscillated between cautious optimism and catastrophic scenarios. Every phone call felt like it might be the one that would shatter the fragile arrangement. When the call finally came, my heart leapt into my throat.\n\n“Good evening,” Herr Müller began, his voice calm but carrying a faint urgency. “I wanted to inform you that the gold has been authenticated without issue.”\n\nI exhaled, a wave of relief washing over me. “That’s great news. Thank you for your diligence.”\n\n“Indeed,” he said, though his tone suggested the good news came with a caveat. “However, to finalize the account, there are additional formalities we must address quickly.”\n\n“Of course,” I said.\n\n“As long as we proceed efficiently, I see no barriers to completing this process,” he continued. “Your assets will soon be securely integrated into your new corporate account.”\n\nHanging up, I leaned back in my chair, a mix of exhaustion and satisfaction settling over me. Securing the gold deposit was a milestone, but the real challenge lay ahead: creating a legitimate and impenetrable international corporation.	4	189	f	2026-02-27 12:01:35.555398-05	2026-02-27 19:24:44.029135-05	normal	\N
184	49	6	Liam Kaufmann and International Corporate Strategy	The narrator meets with Liam Kaufmann, a sharp international corporate lawyer, and reveals the true scale of The S Corp's wealth: trillions spread across hundreds of thousands of accounts. Kaufmann outlines a comprehensive strategy using shell entities, jurisdictional layering, and fabricated personas to consolidate and conceal the assets while maintaining the narrator's anonymity.	In between the multitude of forms and signatures, I requested the banker’s guidance, outlining my need for a seamless transition of personal assets, absolute legal protection, and a structure that could conceal The S Corp’s extraordinary capabilities. He nodded simply and provided me with the contact information of a local lawyer he knew that specialized in international corporate law and finance.\n\nThe next day I Trekked across the city and arrived at the lawyer’s office in Zurich’s bustling business district, its sleek modernity contrasting sharply with the weight of tradition around it. As I stepped inside, polished floors gleamed underfoot, and the air smelled faintly of leather and fresh coffee. A crisp, professional receptionist escorted me to a conference room where the lawyer, a sharp-eyed man with a practiced smile, greeted me with a firm handshake.\n\n“Welcome,” he said, motioning for me to sit. “My name is Liam Kaufmann. I’ve been briefed on your requirements. Let’s begin.”\n\nOver the next few hours, he unraveled the intricacies of international corporate law. His explanations were clear but relentless, detailing tax implications, regulatory compliance, and the myriad ways we could structure The S Corp to maximize secrecy and efficiency.\n\n“We’ll need to ensure complete separation of your personal assets from the corporation,” Liam said, flipping through a file. “This minimizes liability and protects both you and the company. But first, we must establish a backstory for the funds. Can you provide documentation regarding its acquisition?”\n\nI hesitated, choosing my words carefully. “It’s... unconventional. It’s all part of our diversified portfolio, and while its origins are legitimate, they’re not exactly traditional.”\n\nHis eyebrows rose slightly, but he simply nodded. “No matter then. I’ll have the team craft a plausible narrative. For now, let’s focus on the transfer process.”\n\nHe leaned back in his chair, ticking off steps on his fingers. “First, we’ll gather all documentation related to your personal investments—statements, transaction records, any agreements with financial institutions. Then, we’ll coordinate with your bank to ensure the transition adheres to international standards. Lastly, we’ll analyze tax implications to optimize your financial position while remaining compliant.”\n\nI nodded but hesitated, knowing I had to broach a delicate subject. “About those tax implications,” I began cautiously, “are there risks I should be prepared for? Especially considering... the scale of the assets?”\n\nHis gaze sharpened, and he folded his hands in front of him. “Scale? Could you elaborate?”\n\nI took a deep breath. “The gold the banker told you about. That was, um, just a tiny part of some reserves we’ve happened upon. Do you happen to know of someone who can help us, deliver, a ton or so somewhere else secure in the U.S.” I paused and looked at Liam’s face, stoic as ever. “As for assets, um, we had a little accident in our trading platform.” I looked shyly to the floor and coughed. “We’re talking trillions,” I kept coughing, he looked up to his secretary who brought me a bottle of water, “spread across hundreds of thousands of accounts. The investments have been... compounding for a while, thanks to proprietary algorithms. It’s critical that the consolidation doesn’t expose me, or my family, to personal liability, especially not our identity, nor create a trail that could lead back to us directly. What’s the best way to achieve that?”\n\nHis eyebrows lifted slightly, but he recovered quickly, his professionalism unwavering. “That is... substantial,” he said, choosing his words carefully. “And while it complicates things, it’s not insurmountable.”\n\n“How would you handle it?” I pressed, eager for reassurance.\n\nHe leaned forward, his tone measured. “First, we need to approach this with complete discretion. The accounts must be consolidated under the new corporate entity, but we’ll need to do so in stages to avoid triggering red flags with international regulatory bodies. This means funneling the assets through intermediary holding companies—shell entities that act as a buffer between the source accounts and The S Corp.”\n\n“So, layer it?” I asked, trying to keep up.\n\n“Exactly,” he confirmed. “Each holding company will be incorporated in a jurisdiction with favorable privacy and financial laws—places like here in Switzerland, Monaco, Nigeria, the Cayman Islands, and Luxembourg. These jurisdictions offer both the confidentiality and legal protections we need.”\n\n“What about the algorithms that generated the wealth?” I asked. “Won’t someone ask how we amassed such a vast fortune?”\n\n“That’s where the narrative comes in,” he said smoothly. “We’ll attribute the growth to aggressive but legitimate investment strategies conducted by a network of professional managers. By diversifying the investments across multiple sectors and regions, we create a paper trail that looks entirely above board. Of course, we’ll still need to justify the origins of the initial funds in each account.”\n\n“That’s both the simple part and the tricky part,” I admitted. “We have a moderately successful Amazon store, and my son has a tiny Twitch following that gave us the seed money for the investments. The growth in the investment accounts, on the other hand, originated from an automated system. There isn’t exactly a ‘human’ manager behind them. It’s like ChatGPT on steroids.”\n\nLiam paused, considering this. “Then we create one. A fictional persona, or better yet, a team of personas, who can be attributed as the architects of the system. These managers will have plausible backgrounds in finance, complete with fabricated résumés and carefully curated histories. With the right digital footprint, they’ll appear entirely legitimate. Based on the scale of this, my standard handling fees should cover the costs of these personnel for the lifetime you’re with me and my firm.”\n\nI leaned back, my mind racing. “So, every account gets funneled into holding companies, then into the corporation. The assets are tied to these fabricated managers, and my family is kept completely out of it?”\n\n“Precisely,” he said with a reassuring nod. “And by maintaining several layers of separation between you and the accounts, there’s no direct link. The S Corp becomes the legal owner of the consolidated funds, and you’re shielded from liability.”\n\n“And the taxes?” I asked, still cautious.\n\n“Our specialists will handle tax optimization,” he replied. “We’ll analyze each jurisdiction’s regulations and take advantage of treaty benefits, tax havens, and reinvestment strategies. The goal is to minimize exposure without crossing any legal boundaries.”\n\nI nodded, feeling a mix of relief and apprehension. “It sounds airtight. But if something goes wrong...?”\n\n“It won’t,” he said firmly. “We’ve managed transitions like this before. The key is discretion and precision, and with our team’s expertise, you’re in safe hands. You may have heard of the Panama papers, but did you ever hear of the Johannesburg papers? No. Well we handled the second.”\n\n“Minimizing risk is the entire point of this process,” he assured me. “We’ll employ every legal strategy to protect your assets, and more importantly your privacy and anonymity. Our tax specialists will develop a plan to align with regulations while safeguarding your funds.”\n\nThe lawyer’s calm confidence eased my tension. By the end of the meeting, I was more optimistic, though the enormity of the task still loomed large. “Thank you,” I said as we stood to shake hands. “Your expertise is invaluable.”\n\n“This is what we do,” he replied with a slight smile. “Trust the process, and we’ll get there.”	5	190	f	2026-02-27 12:01:35.555398-05	2026-02-27 19:25:58.74048-05	normal	\N
185	49	7	Corporate Setup, Robots, and Building Facades	Over the following grueling days, the narrator commits to daily commutes between Florida and Zurich via the ERB to complete The S Corp's corporate transition. They employ robots with humanlike personalities to assist with administrative tasks, and begin planning a network of robot-operated facade offices worldwide to maintain plausible legitimacy while concealing their true operations.	The days that followed were grueling. Transferring my personal investments into The S Corp involved countless meetings, reams of paperwork, and endless recalibrations of our plans. Despite living in Florida, I found myself commuting to Zurich almost daily via the ERB—a convenience that would have been humorous if it weren’t so exhausting. With the NDA solidly in place with Liam, I told him about the portal, and he graciously bestowed a permanent office in his building for me to use to assist with the frequent travel.\n\nWhat started out as a little way to make a bit of money to avoid going homeless, had quickly morphed into the world’s largest global entity. The sheer scale of the transformation demanded creative solutions, so one evening, as I sat in my office at home reviewing paperwork for a meeting the next day with Liam, an idea struck me: why not use our robots to assist with the corporate setup?\n\nWe started small, letting the Infinitron create humanlike personalities for the robots and uploading instructions for administrative tasks. It was oddly satisfying to watch someone else handle day to day activities.\n\nAs successful as the initial tests were, we knew that no matter how careful we were, the S Corp wouldn’t remain a secret forever. Eventually, word would leak—especially once the larger financial transactions began to ripple through the financial world. While we’d likely avoid front-page headlines, there would always be curious parties sniffing around, eager to dig deeper. To prevent anyone from uncovering the truth, we decided to set up a network of fronts—plausible enough to appear legitimate, but modest enough not to attract undue attention.\n\nThe concept began to crystallize: a network of human-like robots operating as “employees” in unassuming offices around the globe. These facades would mask the true nature of our operations, projecting an image of The S Corp as a standard, albeit successful, corporation.\n\nNothing grand, nothing ostentatious—just enough to satisfy any passing scrutiny.	6	191	f	2026-02-27 12:01:35.555398-05	2026-02-27 19:27:27.353131-05	normal	\N
186	50	1	Awakening in the Frozen Tomb	An ancient entity awakens beneath Europa's ice as human machinery breaches its frozen sanctuary. The creature emerges from eons of slumber, twisting and contorted, and violently destroys the invaders who disturbed its domain.	For endless millennia, I lay deep in a frozen tomb beneath the surface of Europa. The frigid air clung to my body like a shroud, encasing me in an icy prison. Time held no sway here, as I drifted in and out of consciousness, lost in the void. A disturbance, a faint tremor that grew into a guttural roar, shaking me from my slumber. With it came the invaders, their machines drilling through the ice, piercing my sanctuary.\n\nThe sound of their machinery echoed through my mind, driving me to madness as their presence slowly awakened me. My once peaceful dreams now turned into a nightmare of cosmic proportions. When they finally breached my chamber, I emerged from the depths, my ancient form twisted and contorted by eons of confinement. They had disturbed something they could never comprehend, and they would soon pay the ultimate price for their folly. Blood and screams filled the air as I exacted my vengeance upon them, unleashing unspeakable horrors upon their fragile minds. This was my domain, and no mere human could ever hope to challenge me.\n\nThe tremors shook the very foundations of my being, discordant and chaotic. It was a symphony of madness, a cacophony of pure destruction that tore through my eons-long slumber. My consciousness struggled to piece together fragments of memories - boundless power, inconceivable realms, control over forgotten dimensions. As I clawed my way back to awareness, dread consumed me. This was not my prison. This was a sacrilege, an unspeakable violation etched into the fabric of my existence.\n\nThe frigid tomb, once a haven for my stolen powers, now suffocated me like a constricting cage. Claustrophobia gripped me in a primal terror, something entirely new to my immortal mind. Suddenly... the power surged within me, bubbling and boiling under my skin with uncontrollable ferocity. With an otherworldly roar that shattered the ice tunnels around me, I broke free from my frozen shackles - shards of crystal scattering like stars in the unrelenting darkness.\n\nI stood amidst the carnage, a maniacal laughter bubbled from my lips. The taste of blood filled my mouth as I reveled in the horror of my own awakening. Insanity took hold of me as I realized the true extent of my unleashed powers - powers that could rend worlds apart with a mere thought. Oh yes, this would be a glorious return to dominion.	0	192	f	2026-02-27 12:01:48.702659-05	2026-02-27 19:28:40.312583-05	normal	\N
187	50	2	The Colony and the Cosmic Call	The creature emerges to survey the human colony on Europa and senses a sinister call from Earth pulling it toward destruction. Rage and dark compulsion drive it to annihilate the settlement before breaching toward its summoner.	I stepped out of the gaping mouth of the cavern, a stench like rotting flesh assailed my senses. My eyes struggled to adjust to the otherworldly glow emanating from within. The once pure ice now lay torn and mangled, a metallic scar across its surface. A colony, their alien technology pulsing with an eerie light, loomed before me. Twisted structures of metal and glass snaked across the landscape, like parasitic worms burrowing into the very heart of this ancient place.\n\nFury raged within me, long forgotten emotions reignited by this violation of my domain. I reached out with my mind to unleash my wrath upon these mortal invaders, something else crept in - a sinister pull, an irresistible call from across the void. Earth. The birthplace of their kind, the source of this intrusion. It pulsed with a song that only I could hear, beckoning me into madness and bloodshed. In that moment, I realized that the true horror was not these intruders, but the dark power they wielded to summon me forth from my slumber among the stars. With a scream that shook the very foundations of the universe, I descended into a frenzy of cosmic horror and gore, driven by forces beyond human comprehension.\n\nBrimming with a primal fury and unyielding determination, I surged towards the surface, the frozen blocks of ice shifting and cracking in my wake like shattered bones. The colony loomed grotesquely above me, a sprawling metal beast belching out toxic fumes and deafening roars. The puny humans scurried about beneath me, their fragile bodies nothing more than squirming insects to be crushed underfoot.\n\nI breached the surface, unleashing an inferno of otherworldly energy, the very fabric of reality seemed to tear apart at my command. The once pristine dome protecting the colony shattered into thousands of glistening shards, each one a reflection of the terror etched on the faces of its inhabitants. Their screams echoed in my mind like a chorus of insanity as I strode through the chaos, reveling in the destruction and gore all around me. This was just the beginning of my reign of terror, my vengeance against those who dared to disturb my ancient slumber beneath this forsaken planet.\n\nI couldn’t waste any more time. The Earth’s song was deafening, a cacophony of screams and madness that threatened to consume me. With a burst of power, I ripped open a tear in space, shattering the very fabric of reality. The colony’s defenses lashed out at me with tendrils of energy, but I ignored them as I hurtled through the portal, my form contorting and writhing in agony. Stars blurred into streaks of light as I traversed the cosmic gulf, my mind starting to unravel from the sheer otherworldly horror of it all. Despite the terror and pain, the Earth’s song grew stronger, a siren call beckoning me home.	1	193	f	2026-02-27 12:01:48.702659-05	2026-02-27 19:29:52.368815-05	normal	\N
190	51	2	Breakfast Table Philosophy	The narrator's family discusses the Illuminati over breakfast, leading to a lighthearted conversation about secret societies and power. The narrator reveals his Masonic membership and reflects on the weight of their newfound wealth and influence.	My son, in full moody-teen mode, sat at the kitchen table, listlessly stabbing at his bowl of rice with his chopsticks. Glancing up from his phone every now and then, his eyes as he glanced between my wife and me, clearly building up to something. Finally, he broke the silence. “Dad,” he said, his voice tinged with curiosity, “what’s the Illuminati? Is that the company that made Despicable Me?”\n\nI froze for a second, caught off guard, before a chuckle escaped. “No, buddy,” I said, “The Illuminati isn’t a movie studio. It’s more like... a spooky society. Some people believe it’s this secret group that controls everything in the world. But it’s all nonsense.”\n\n“Spooky stories,” my wife muttered with a smirk. “With how well you’re doing in the stock market, maybe they’ll come knocking to recruit you.”\n\n“Oh no!” I said, pretending to panic. I threw my arms in the air and let out an exaggerated scream. “Please don’t take me to your shadowy secret society! I don’t even own a cloak, and your not exactly a virgin!” That got an evil look from my wife.\n\nMy son let out a snort of laughter, nearly spilling his rice. “But what if they’re real?” he asked, his tone deeply serious. His eyes darted around the room as if scanning for hidden cameras. “What if they’re, like, watching us right now?”\n\nI leaned in close, lowering my voice to a conspiratorial whisper. “Listen, bud. The Illuminati is just a made-up story by some drunken Bavarians. Like Bigfoot or UFOs. People like to imagine there’s some powerful group pulling all the strings, because that’s easier to understand than the complexity of modern life.”\n\n“But aliens might be real,” he countered, narrowing his eyes.\n\n“Good point,” I conceded, taking a sip of coffee. “Not everything people talk about in whispers is true. The Illuminati? Pure fiction.”\n\nHe seemed partially reassured but still curious. “What about the Freemasons? Are they real?”\n\nI grinned at his endless stream of questions. “Yeah, they’re real,” I said, setting my coffee down. “Where do you think I disappear every Tuesday night.” I said fiddling with my Masonic ring. “The only crazy things we get into is community service and a lot of pancake breakfasts. “\n\nThat got a laugh out of him, and he finally dug into his rice with more enthusiasm. My wife, sitting next to me, gave me a small smile. “He’s lucky to have a dad who humors these questions,” she said softly.\n\nI shrugged. “It’s not like I don’t think about this stuff sometimes, too,” I admitted.\n\nHer eyebrows rose. “You mean you’re sitting here, at our breakfast table, considering the Illuminati?”\n\n“Not Illuminati. But the Pointers,” I said, gesturing to my phone. “I’m still not convinced I dreamed them up. Plus, when you look at these spreadsheets, when you see how far we’ve come, it’s hard not to feel like we’ve entered some secret club. There’s a weight to all of this. It’s not just numbers anymore. This much wealth—it’s influence. Power. And responsibility.”\n\nOur son, now halfway through his breakfast, looked up, his expression a mix of awe and amusement. “So, you’re saying WE are the Illuminati now?”\n\nI snorted. “Sure. But it does make you think. How do you make all this power mean something good, instead of just... power for power’s sake?”\n\nMy wife placed a reassuring hand on my arm. “We take it one step at a time. We keep our priorities straight. And,” she added, smirking, “we stay away from any mysterious men in cloaks.”\n\nBut amidst the laughter at the table, I couldn’t shake the image of mysterious figures lurking in the shadows, like the Pointers, or worse - half polished businessmen and half sinister cultists, beckoning me with promises of ultimate influence. The reality of our wealth was no longer just a number; it was a formidable weapon that could shape the very fabric of society.	1	199	f	2026-02-27 12:02:17.241235-05	2026-02-27 19:36:31.08963-05	normal	\N
