e guilt was overwhelming.\n\nWhat had I done? Sneaking into her dreams was beyond invasive. It was a betrayal of trust, driven by my own selfish need for clarity. I buried my face in my hands, the enormity of my actions sinking in.\n\nThe next morning, the guilt lingered. Over coffee, I watched her move about the kitchen, oblivious to the breach I’d committed. The usual warmth between us still felt strained, but I couldn’t bring myself to tell her what I had done.\n\nI resolved, then and there, to never repeat the mistake with her. Dreams were sacred, private realms. No matter my intentions, intruding on them was wrong.\n\nAlthough shame clawed at me as a new understanding took root. This ability to enter dreams wasn’t limited to my own; it extended to others. The realization sent a shiver down my spine. This was power, terrifying, potent, and fraught with ethical implications.	3	245	t	2026-02-27 12:04:55.335255-05	2026-02-27 20:17:29.039812-05	normal	\N
