203	53	1	The Half-Gateway Experiment	The protagonist establishes MOM (Mountains of Madness), a secret research facility in Antarctica, to test a radical concept: a half-gateway that opens a portal on one end but remains incomplete on the other. When activated, the half-gateway reveals something terrifying on the other side—an aware, living force that defies comprehension.	The keyboard clattered beneath my fingers; each keystroke charged with purpose. The vast global network of The S Corp pulsed around me, a living system humming with potential. Fueled by my insatiable curiosity and inability to just leave well enough alone, I was ready to push the boundaries of our enterprise even further. For a while, a radical idea had taken root: what if I could create a “half-gateway,” opening a portal on one end but leaving the other incomplete? Could this shield objects from external forces or dissipate explosions? The possibilities were staggering, yet fraught with danger, a perfect storm to ignite my imagination.\n\nERBs demanded immense exotic energy to function, which they siphoned from the Eternal Engine. But their collapse would unleash that energy catastrophically. Testing such a volatile concept in populated areas was unthinkable. I needed a remote, desolate location where no one would notice (or be impacted) if things went wrong. The solution was obvious: Antarctica, a frozen wasteland untouched by humanity’s grasp.\n\nSecuring an isolated region in Antarctica wasn’t simple, but with Liam’s finesse and a few greased palms, we navigated international treaties and claimed a remote stretch far from settlements or aircraft routes. The area’s solitude guaranteed secrecy, and the world’s indifference made it ideal.\n\nWith a touch of dark humor, I christened the site MOM, “The Mountains of Madness,” a nod to Lovecraft’s desolate landscapes.\n\nUsing the ERB network, I opened a portal deep within our new site, within Antarctica’s icy expanse. Arctic gear replicated and ready