d a wildfire of speculation. Social media buzzed with theories, sinister government conspiracies, alien abductions, shadowy cabals. The world was watching, and our carefully guarded operations teetered on the edge of exposure.\n\nAs panic mounted, we tightened our grip. Security protocols became draconian, off-world colonies were monitored with unwavering vigilance, and anyone who posed a risk was swiftly neutralized. The truth could not come out, not yet. Humanity wasn’t ready for the seismic shift our advancements would bring, and we couldn’t afford even the slightest leak. No matter the cost, The S Corp’s secrets would remain locked away until the world could handle the revelation.	3	213	f	2026-02-27 12:02:55.673761-05	2026-02-27 19:49:25.598046-05	normal	\N
207	53	5	The Cloning Debate	As the demand for skilled scientists outstrips Earth's supply, the protagonist proposes human cloning to sustain off-world colonies. After heated debate with Morgan, they proceed cautiously, developing an ethical framework where clones are created with full consciousness, informed of their origins, and given complete freedom to choose their paths.	**********\n\nBut communications was the least of our problems, as our operations expanded across the solar system, the need for skilled scientists and researchers outstripped earth’s supply. Robots and automated systems could only do so much; they lacked the intuition, creativity, and decision-making essential for progress. Plus, it would be years before any of our staff’s kids were ready to seriously contribute to the science mission (although some of their science fair projects were pretty out there).\n\nLate one night at a lunar base meeting, desperation led us to a solution I had long resisted: human cloning.\n\n“Morgan, I understand your concerns,” I began, keeping my tone measured. “But we’re at a breaking point. The demand for human expertise is overwhelming. Not only that but our colonies can house THREE TRILLION people, we are barely keeping a thousand out there. Cloning could serve as a temporary measure while we seek sustainable alternatives.”\n\nMorgan’s arms crossed tightly; her expression sharp. “Do you hear yourself?” she retorted. “This isn’t just a logistical issue—it’s a moral quagmire. Cloning humans opens a Pandora’s box we may never close. What if we create beings without rights, forced to exist solely for our purposes? Are we ready for that?”\n\nI paused, grappling with her words. Morgan had been the voice of reason since joining, tempering my ambitions with practicality. “Our intentions are noble,” I countered. “The clones wouldn’t be tools or slaves. They’d be fully human, treated as equals and contributors. We’d establish safeguards to ensure their rights and well-being