213	55	1	Thirteen Nights of Terror	The protagonist is tormented by thirteen nights of relentless nightmares that blur the line between dream and reality. He realizes that his technological empire was built by stealing innovations from the dream realm, and the dreams are now demanding payment. The nightmares intensify with visions of a grotesque farmhouse, a writhing Eternal Engine, and an obsidian monolith that serves as a gateway between worlds.	The witching hour struck, and the darkness wrapped around me like a vice, squeezing the air from my lungs. My heart hammered against the icy sheets as if trying to escape the suffocating stillness. The line between dream and waking life was gone, and I was caught in a relentless cycle of terror.\n\nFor thirteen nights, the nightmares came. Not fleeting glimpses, but all-encompassing horrors. Over and over, each dream dragged me deeper into a realm that defied understanding, a place where monstrous shapes moved with unnatural grace, and the air hummed with the threat of collapse. The twisted landscapes of suffocating darkness and grotesque whispers consumed me.\n\nLike the Pied Piper, it was time to pay. The whole of my technological empire, S Corp, the pinnacle of human innovation, had sprung from the device I had somehow wrested from my dreams: the Eternal Engine. From it came the Infinitron, the Matter Replicators, and, ultimately, the ERBs. All of it was possible because I had torn these marvels from their rightful homes, violating the veil between realms. I could no longer deny the truth. The dreams I had stolen from were calling, demanding I face the sins I had committed, or at least try to understand what the hell I had done.\n\nThe worst part wasn’t the fear; it was the allure and my goddamn need to turn things up to 11 all the time. The dreams were a wake up call, pulling me closer to an abyss I couldn’t avoid. Night after night, the edges of reality frayed, leaving me questioning which world I truly inhabited. In the Threshold, I was no longer in control. Forces beyond comprehension toyed with me, puppeteering my every thought and movement.\n\nIn the waking hours, it didn’t end. The visions clung to my mind like shadows I couldn’t shake. Running S Corp had been consuming enough, but now, every decision felt heavier, every moment colored by the dread seeping from my dreams. I’d ignored them, denied their pull, but I could no longer escape their grip.\n\nI knew I had to face it. Whatever secrets these nightmares held, they were spilling into my life, corrupting the world I thought I could control. The boundaries were breaking, and if I didn’t act, the horrors would bleed further into the waking world.\n\nThe dream was on constant repeat, in it, a shadowy presence relentlessly pursued me, elusive yet omnipresent, like a predator playing with its prey. It wasn’t just fear; it was a sense of teetering on the brink between annihilation and transcendence, a precarious balance that bled into my waking life.\n\nThe setting of the dream was as fragmented as my thoughts. It was chaos, unpredictable, and as soon as I thought I had figured out the pattern, the next time it occurred, it would be exactly the same, yet entirely different—as if my mind and memory were trapped in a horrific version of the Mandela Effect.\n\nAt times, I wandered through sprawling cityscapes shrouded in malevolence, skyscrapers casting long, sinister shadows. Other nights, I was stranded in desolate wastelands where the silence pressed in like a living thing. Yet, no matter the setting, the feeling of dread was constant, and the outcome was always the same, a suffocating shroud that threatened to consume me. Each night a new and even more terrible entity would torment with rapacious glee at my torture.\n\nThe unpredictable terror of repetition wasn’t the worst of it. Lurking within these visions was something more profound, something that beckoned me. At first, it was a whisper, faint and easy to dismiss. With each passing night, it grew louder, more insistent, until I could no longer ignore it. This was not just a dream but a summons, a demand for my attention, surrender my soul and entire being. The very fabric of these visions pulsed with meaning, as if the dream realm itself was trying to communicate a truth I was too blind to see.\n\nNot only did I have to face these torments at night, but these dreams also tore deeply through my soul, seeping into my waking hours and casting long shadows over every achievement. Even as S Corp thrived; our interplanetary colonies expanding, our space elevator progressing; the call in my dreams grew deafening. It was as if the cosmos itself demanded I DO SOMETHING, drawing me toward an unknown destination with a pull I couldn’t resist.\n\nThe irony was not lost on me: in reality, I was secretly celebrated as the architect of humanity’s greatest advancements. Deep down, I knew these marvels were borrowed, stolen from the very realm that now haunted me. Night after night, the dreams weren’t just an escape, it was an unpaid invoice now sent to the cosmic bill collectors. The Threshold was no longer a shadow of my subconscious; it was the origin of everything, and it demanded I come back to face what I had unleashed.\n\nIn one dream, my farmhouse appeared, but this time it was a grotesque, decayed version of my refuge where I had initially birthed the Engine. Inside, walls pulsated with sinister sigils that seemed to whisper my name. In the center loomed the Eternal Engine, its once-perfect form now a writhing mass of tentacles and eyes. It pulsed with a sickening hum, radiating a black hole’s hunger. I couldn’t look away as it devoured reality itself, dragging me through dimensions where time dissolved and sanity slipped through my fingers.\n\nThrough it all, I felt myself unraveling. The threads of my identity, once tightly woven, began to fray and dissolve into the surrounding chaos. The distinction between my body and the Threshold eroded, and I became a formless awareness, suspended in a realm where meaning unraveled as quickly as it formed. Colors bled into sounds, and the air pulsed with sensations that defied language. The surrounding abyss was no longer just an external force; it seeped into me, filling every crevice of my being. My thoughts splintered and scattered, merging with the howling void.\n\nThe terror wasn’t the sharp sting of a fleeting fear; it was a pervasive, suffocating weight that pressed down on every fiber of my existence. I had become more than an observer or a victim—I was becoming part of this place, absorbed into its infinite grotesquery. My breath, if I was even breathing, felt like a feeble gasp against the currents of madness. Every flicker of light, every distorted shadow seemed to reach for me, mocking my attempts to cling to any semblance of self.\n\nBeneath the dread, something pulled me forward. An insidious compulsion drove me to move deeper, to peel back the layers of this nightmarish reality. It wasn’t curiosity—it was a primal instinct, a perverse need to uncover the truth carved into this swirling chaos. It felt like a trial, some ancient and unholy test that would strip me bare and lay my soul open to horrors I couldn’t yet fathom.\n\nFrom the void, came the monolith.\n\nIt rose from the formless landscape like an invading god, blacker than the void surrounding it, an impossibility within impossibility. The surface shimmered with a sickly iridescence, as if it were alive, pulsating with an unnatural rhythm that resonated deep within my bones. The air around it warped and bent, filled with a high-pitched keening that threatened to destroy my sanity. I couldn’t look away. My gaze was riveted to the pillar, its eldritch design a web of shifting sigils and grotesque carvings that seemed to breathe and writhe.\n\nThe light it emitted wasn’t light at all, but radiant darkness. A perversion of light, a malignant radiance that seemed to devour rather than illuminate. Shadows poured forth from the monolith like sentient smoke, their forms shifting with a fluid, predatory grace. They weren’t just silhouettes; they were entities, born of the abyss itself, their eyeless faces and jagged mouths radiating hunger and rage.\n\nThe shadows descended upon the grotesque beings that surrounded the monolith, and the air erupted into chaos. The creatures, malformed horrors of fused flesh and bone, screamed in languages not meant for human ears as the shadows tore into them. Limbs were severed, torsos rent open, and viscera spilled into the unnatural ground, steaming and writhing as if alive. The slaughter was methodical, almost ritualistic, and yet filled with a primal ferocity that chilled me to my core.\n\nI stood frozen, every instinct screaming at me to flee, yet my feet were rooted to the ground. My mind couldn’t process the carnage unfolding before me—it wasn’t just violence; it was an unmaking, a violation of existence itself. The shadows seemed to feast not just on the bodies of their victims but on something deeper, draining their very essence into the monolith.\n\nAnd then, I understood.\n\nThe monolith wasn’t just a structure—it was a nexus, a wound in the fabric of reality through which unspeakable horrors seeped into our world. It was a gateway, not to another place, but to an idea—a concept so vast and incomprehensible that it reduced all of existence to a fragile, trembling thread.\n\nThe monolith pulsed, and I felt its pull, a terrible gravity that reached for the core of my being. It wasn’t calling me—it was consuming me. My thoughts became static, my vision blurred, and for one horrifying moment, I wasn’t sure where I ended and the monolith began.\n\nThis wasn’t just a nightmare. This was something more, something eternal and inevitable.\n\nI woke gasping for air, my heart pounding with the lingering terror. The dream clung to me like a curse, its images refusing to fade. The obsidian monolith, the Eternal Engine, the twisted dimensions—they weren’t just symbols of my subconscious. They were warnings, fragments of a truth far greater and more terrifying than I could yet grasp.\n\nThe Space Elevator, what should be the crown jewel of S Corp’s achievements, would stand as a testament to my hubris. They had called it a bridge to the stars, but I saw it now for what it truly was: a beacon, a gravitational force pulling the nightmare closer. Each step we took toward colonizing the cosmos was another tether drawn tight between these two realities. The monolith and the dreams weren’t content to remain apart—they were coming for us; drawn by the very tools I had ripped from their rightful home.\n\nI couldn’t ignore the symmetry. The dream realm’s horrors weren’t abstract warnings—they were consequences. Every advancement we celebrated at S Corp was another transgression, another brick in the bridge connecting us to the abyss. The monolith wasn’t just a gateway; it was a sentence. A declaration that my damnation was sealed the moment I brought the Eternal Engine into this world.\n\nI had thought myself a pioneer, a visionary unlocking the universe’s secrets. But I wasn’t building a future—I was unraveling the boundaries between realities, trading progress for chaos. The dreams were no longer whispers; they were screams, and I couldn’t look away. I had forged this connection between worlds, and now it demanded payment in a currency I couldn’t yet comprehend.	0	222	f	2026-02-27 12:03:32.268159-05	2026-02-27 19:56:15.444082-05	normal	\N
214	55	2	Unraveling at Home	The protagonist's mental and physical deterioration affects his family as he desperately attempts various medications and self-medication to escape the dreams. Desperate for help, he seeks out Dr. Pai M. On, a sleep psychiatrist, who dismisses his claims as stress-induced delusions. Frustrated and feeling gaslit, he resolves that only he can face the horrors he has unleashed.	**********\n\nMy family bore the brunt of my unraveling. My wife, exhausted from my nocturnal thrashing and incoherent ramblings, sought solace on the couch. My son, his bedroom separated by a thin wall, was haunted by the muffled sounds of my tortured sleep. Their concern grew, but I had no answers for them—only the mounting certainty that this was far from over.\n\nDesperate for relief, I began self-medicating. Melatonin failed to lull me into peace. Alcohol’s promise of escape left me stranded in fractured, disjointed slumber. Medical marijuana, hailed as a panacea for the restless mind, only deepened the labyrinth of unsettling dreams that stalked me into my waking hours. I stared at benzodiazepines in my medicine cabinet, the small bottles whispering promises of tranquility. Their siren song came with warnings too ominous to ignore.\n\nBy the time I sought professional help, I was a shadow of myself, drained of hope and clinging to the edges of sanity.\n\nThat’s when I looked up Dr. Pai M. On, a sleep psychiatrist renowned for unraveling the mysteries of the human mind. She was hired onto the MOM project near its inception to assist with night terrors some of our scientists were having. Her office was a serene oasis of neutral tones and faint lavender, designed to lull patients into comfort, but the calm only magnified my inner chaos. She greeted me with a warm, practiced smile that felt more clinical than genuine.\n\n“Tell me,” Dr. On said, settling into her chair, pen poised over a notepad, “what brings you to me today?”\n\nI hesitated, words catching in my throat. How could I explain the swirling vortex of terror and madness consuming me? Finally, I managed, “It’s the dreams. They’re... not normal. They’re vivid, overwhelming. They’re starting to feel more real than this.”\n\nDr. On tilted her head slightly, her smile never wavering. “Vivid dreams are not uncommon for someone under significant stress, especially someone in your position. You’ve been carrying a great deal on your shoulders, haven’t you?”\n\n“It’s more than stress,” I said, my voice rising. “These dreams—they aren’t just dreams. They’re... something else. A place. And I can’t control it. The things I see there... They shouldn’t exist.”\n\nShe tapped her pen against the pad thoughtfully. “What sort of things?”\n\nI hesitated, the images flashing behind my eyes: the obsidian monolith, the writhing shadows, the grotesque beings that mocked all logic. “Monsters. Otherworldly structures. Places where time doesn’t work the way it should. It’s like I’m being pulled somewhere, and I can’t stop it. It’s not just affecting me—it’s bleeding into my life. My family can see it. My work is suffering. I’m... unraveling.”\n\nDr. On leaned forward, her tone gentle but firm. “You’re exhausted. Sleep deprivation can cause heightened dream states, even hallucinations. It’s not unusual for the mind to weave stress and imagination into something that feels real.”\n\n“It’s not my imagination!” I snapped, leaning closer. “This isn’t some stress response. I’ve seen things—impossible things. I think... I think it’s connected to the technology we’re developing at S Corp.”\n\n“Ah,” she said, his voice steady as ever. “The Infinitron interface you claim is in your brain.” Her voice hiding a mocking tone of disbelief. “And you believe it’s affecting your dreams?”\n\nI nodded, desperation leaking into my voice. “It has to be. I can feel it. The lines between what’s real and what’s not—they’re blurring. I don’t know where I stop, and the dreams begin anymore. What if the Infinitron is opening something? What if—”\n\nDr. On raised a hand, her calm demeanor unshaken. “Let’s take a step back. Your Eternal Engine is a marvel of engineering, but it’s still a tool. It doesn’t create dreams; it doesn’t alter reality. What you’re experiencing, while frightening, is a natural response to prolonged stress. You’re not losing yourself. You’re simply overwhelmed.”\n\nHer words felt like a slap, their simplicity at odds with the churning chaos inside me. “You don’t understand,” I said, my voice trembling. “These aren’t normal dreams. They’re warnings. And if we don’t figure out what they mean—”\n\n“Then nothing will happen,” she interjected, her tone firm but kind. “You’re safe. Your mind is trying to process the immense pressure you’re under. It’s doing what it was designed to do. You’re not insane, and there’s nothing supernatural at play here.”\n\nHer reassurances felt hollow, a cage of soft words locking me into a reality I no longer believed in. “So, what do I do?” I asked, the weight of exhaustion heavy in my voice.\n\n“Trust the process,” Dr. On said, leaning back with that infuriatingly calm smile. “Keep up with our sessions, practice the relaxation techniques we discussed, but most importantly—step back for a bit. Give yourself permission to pause. You’ve been through a lot, and sometimes the best thing you can do is let your mind breathe. This isn’t a failure; it’s just your brain’s way of asking for rest.”\n\nShe studied me for a moment, her expression softening. “When was the last time you truly took a break? And I don’t mean just a weekend where you half-unplug, but still check emails out of habit. I mean an actual break—no work, no responsibilities, just you stepping away from everything for a while. Because I think you need that.”\n\nShe let the words settle before continuing. “Take a vacation, even if it’s just a few days. Go somewhere quiet. Shut off the noise, the pressure, the endless cycle of doing. Sometimes, clarity comes when you stop forcing yourself to find it.”\n\nHer gaze met mine, steady and unyielding. “You really should consider it.”\n\nI nodded numbly, though the words felt meaningless. I left her office, the lavender-scented air clinging to my skin. One thought consumed me: Dr. On was wrong. She had to be. The dreams weren’t my mind’s cries for rest—they were something darker, and I was spiraling into their grasp.\n\nI knew this world was too good for me, and nothing here would be able to help. Alone in my solitude, I seethed with anger at Dr. On, her hollow reassurances echoing in my mind like a cruel joke. Who the hell did she think he was? Gaslighting me with her calm smile and condescending explanations, as if I didn’t know what I was experiencing. Where did she think I’d found the ability to make the miraculous happen—the Eternal Engine, the Infinitron, the ERBs?\n\nThat power didn’t come from yoga and breathing exercises. It came from somewhere darker, somewhere far beyond his understanding. She was full of shit, and I was truly on my own, left to wrestle with the horrors I had unleashed.	1	223	t	2026-02-27 12:03:32.268159-05	2026-02-27 19:57:19.46887-05	normal	\N
