216	56	1	The Weight of Command	In his corner office, the protagonist confronts evidence of the entity I AM infiltrating corporate systems while wrestling with neglecting his family. Dr. On visits to encourage him to step away from work and be present for his loved ones, advice he initially resists but ultimately accepts.	In the hushed confines of my corner office suite on top of the S Corp’s Houston HQ tower, a battlefield of project files sprawled across the desk. Recessed LED lighting bathed the room in a harsh, white, sterile glow, casting jagged shadows that mimicked the fragments of my dreams.\n\nThe reports on my screen painted a troubling picture. Across the corporation, whispers of I AM had surfaced—not just in hushed conversations, but in the very fabric of our systems. Snippets of code, corrupted database entries, and, most disturbingly, the dreams of employees. The entity was everywhere and nowhere, a phantom lurking at the edges of cognition.\n\nThe relentless ticking of the wall clock matched the cadence of my pulse, an eerie synchronization that only deepened my unease. Just beyond the thin walls of my office, the muffled laughter of my son drifted from his VR room; a sound so distant, so detached, that it felt like an echo from another life. A reminder of something just out of reach.\n\nOn the couch, my wife sat curled up with a blanket, her figure a silhouette of solitude against the dim light. She was there, but distant, a silent casualty of my obsession. A breeze drifted through the slightly ajar balcony door, carrying the damp scent of imminent rain. It felt like the world itself was pressing me to make a change.\n\nA soft knock startled me from my thoughts. Dr. On stood in the doorway, her figure framed by the dim light from the hall.\n\n“Mind if I step in?” she asked, her voice calm but with an edge that said she wasn’t here to waste time.\n\n“Door’s open,” I said, gesturing vaguely toward the chair opposite me. “You’d come in anyway.”\n\nDr. On smiled faintly and sat down, her sharp eyes taking in the scattered chaos of my desk. “Quite the battlefield you’ve got here.”\n\nI snorted. “You think this is bad? Should’ve seen my cubicle back in the ‘90s when we were battling the COBOL demons of Y2K. And at least back then I didn’t have some cosmic entity trying to kill me.”\n\n“True,” she said, her tone laced with dry amusement. “But back then, you weren’t running a multinational empire either. Progress comes with complications, doesn’t it? Have you given any more thought to what we talked about in our last session? Taking a break?”\n\nI leaned back, rubbing my neck. “You don’t know the half of it. Stepping away from S Corp feels... wrong. Like I’m abandoning something. But I have to, don’t I? I look at my family, and I realize I’m back to the same shit I was doing before I was even laid off. Just phoning it in, existing instead of living. They deserve better.”\n\nDr. On tilted her head, her expression softening in that carefully neutral way that made me wonder if she was actually listening or just waiting for me to come to her conclusions. “That’s a hard truth to face. So, what’s your plan?”\n\nHer tone was steady—too steady. As if she wasn’t interested in whether I was right, just that I was moving on. I shifted in my seat, the tension in my shoulders tightening.\n\n“I don’t know. Maybe figure out what’s really going on with I AM.”\n\nDr. On sighed, offering a patient smile that made my skin crawl. “You mean a very common phrase that you’ve latched onto? I am sitting here. I am talking to you. I am wondering if you’re starting to see patterns where there aren’t any.”\n\nI clenched my jaw. “It’s not that simple.”\n\n“Isn’t it?” she asked, her voice gentle but firm. “You’ve been under a lot of stress, and stress makes people fixate on things. You’re a problem solver, and right now, you don’t have a problem to solve, so your brain is making one.”\n\nHer words were reasonable. Too reasonable. Like she wanted me to drop this, walk away, forget.\n\nI exhaled sharply, trying to shake the uneasy feeling crawling up my spine. “And if I’m not imagining it?”\n\nDr. On gave a small, knowing smile. “Then I suppose you’ll find out, won’t you?”\n\nAnd for some reason, that answer didn’t sit right with me at all.\n\n“But in the meantime, start showing up. For real this time. Actually listen, put the damn phone down, be present. You know, stuff that doesn’t come with a board meeting or a product launch.”\n\n“Not a bad plan,” she said. “But it’s a marathon, not a sprint. They’ll need to see you’re committed long-term, not just for a week or two.”\n\nWithout a chance to reply, the door flung open. My son stood there, holding a VR headset in one hand and a grin that could light up the room.\n\n“Hey, Mom, Dad! Wanna do a movie tonight?”\n\nI glanced at Dr. On, who raised her eyebrows as if daring me to say no.\n\n“Of course,” I said. “What’re we watching?”\n\n“Crater. It’s the one about teens on the moon.”\n\n“Crater, huh? Disney flick, right? Didn’t they pull that because of low viewership?”\n\n“Why not just head over to New-New York if you want to do something on the moon?” My wife interjected.\n\n“So, it’s not about being real, it's about having fun. Plus, I figured out how to use the Infinitron to hack into their archives so we can watch it,” he said, his tone a mix of enthusiasm and the casual shrug of a teen trying not to seem too excited.\n\n“So that’s why we got those surveys from Homeland Security,” I said, grinning. “I’ll make the popcorn. You are picking the drinks?”\n\n“Duh,” he said, rolling his eyes with a smirk.\n\n“You’re getting water. I’m worried about your blood sugar, so no more soda.” My wife said.\n\nI laughed, shaking my head. “Fine, water it is.”\n\n“Bet.” He turned and started heading back to the VR room before pausing to add, “Oh, and Mom, you have to actually watch this time. No checking your phone every five seconds.”\n\n“But my cat videos, they’re so cute,” She called after him. “And you better not fall asleep like last time.”\n\nHe shot back a mock salute and disappeared down the hall.\n\nDr. On watched the exchange with a small smile. “There’s your reminder,” she said. “Presence isn’t just about being there physically. It’s about being engaged. Think you can pull that off?”\n\n“Guess we’ll find out,” I said, standing. “Thanks for the pep talk. I still think you’re still full of shit, but maybe not completely covered in flies.”\n\nDr. On laughed softly. “High praise from you. Go enjoy your family and leave the shit talking to when you play your son in Call of Duty.”\n\n“It’s something,” I replied, my voice quieter.\n\nShe stood, smoothing her skirt. “You’re making progress, but don’t forget—this isn’t just about movie nights. It’s about showing up every day. Your family needs to see you’re in this for the long haul.”\n\n“And you?” I asked, raising an eyebrow. “Do you think I can do this, or are you just going to keep gaslighting me with positivity?”\n\nDr. On chuckled softly. “A little gaslighting might be necessary, but you’ll get there. I’m not your enemy, you know. I AM here because you have nowhere else to turn. And because, deep down, you know you’re capable of change.”\n\nHer words lingered as she left, something off about what and how she said things, and I followed my son’s laughter down the hall.	0	225	f	2026-02-27 12:03:52.900871-05	2026-02-27 19:59:19.292843-05	normal	\N
217	56	2	Stepping Away	Over several weeks, the protagonist gradually reduces his time at S Corp headquarters and increases time with his family in Florida. As summer ends, he and his family prepare for an international trip to the UK and Rome with his son's school program.	*********\n\nAs the days rolled on, the frenetic chaos of my life at S Corp faded into the background. I spent less and less time at our Houston HQ, and more time back in our Florida home. Sure, we still wandered over to the HQ to use the Theater rooms and fitness center, but otherwise we became more homebodies. In its place, a quieter rhythm took hold, simple routines, moments of shared laughter, and the delicate work of repairing the fractures I’d neglected for too long.\n\nThe transition was far from seamless. I still fought the impulse to check my email or peek at the latest progress reports from the Space Elevator, but I was making progress. And with the end of summer on the horizon, a new adventure loomed: the August trip with my son’s class to the UK and Rome, part of the Cambridge program through his school.\n\nFor him, it was a chance to see the world—a culmination of months of excitement and anticipation, and possibly the last time to do normal teen things before S Corp blew up everywhere. For my wife, it was a homecoming of sorts, steeped in nostalgia and the joy of reuniting with her twin sister, who still lived in London. Having spent nearly a decade of her twenties there, the city held a special place in her heart, a tapestry of memories woven into its streets. For me, it was an opportunity to step away from the extraordinary and embrace the ordinary, to savor the journey rather than rush to the destination.\n\n“Are you sure you don’t want to use the ERBs for this?” I asked, leaning against the kitchen counter as I watched my son cram yet another bag of snacks into his already-bursting backpack.\n\nHe rolled his eyes, the universal signal of teenage exasperation, but his smirk betrayed a flicker of amusement. “Dad, nobody else’s parents have, like, teleportation tech. That’s cheating.”\n\n“Cheating?” I repeated, raising an eyebrow in mock disbelief. “You’re saying no to skipping a ten-hour flight? Who are you? It’s not like were going to be huffing it back in the economy seats, we’ll be up front practically flying the plane...”\n\n“I’m going to try and raw dog it and post the video to my feed. I’m someone who actually wants the plane experience, not only that but Riley and I have got plans on an awesome Pokémon tournament on the way.” he replied, zipping his bag with a dramatic flourish and slinging it over one shoulder. “It’s about the journey, right?”\n\nAcross the room, my wife looked up from her laptop, a teasing smile tugging at her lips. “You’re just excited about the in-flight snacks and movies.”\n\n“Maybe,” he admitted with a nonchalant shrug, then added, “but also, I want to hang out with my friends on the plane. You know, like normal people.”\n\n“Normal,” I muttered, shaking my head as I crossed my arms. “That’s overrated. But fine, we’ll play it your way. Just don’t come crying back to us in First Class when the in-flight Wi-Fi crashes, and you’re stuck watching that one Frozen short on repeat.”\n\nMy wife chuckled as she closed her laptop and slid it into her own carry-on. “And don’t think I didn’t notice you packing three extra power banks,” she said, her eyes sparkling with maternal amusement as she directed the comment at our son.\n\nHe grinned sheepishly, his bravado momentarily slipping. “Hey, you can’t take any chances,” he said, his voice rising defensively.\n\nWe gathered by the door, and did one final check of things.\n\n“Do you have your passport?” my wife asked, her tone already edging into exasperation.\n\n“Yes, Mom.”\n\n“Yes, sweetie.” We replied in unison.\n\n“Adapter for the outlets?”\n\nHe nodded his head.\n\n“Focus medicine? Enough socks?”\n\n“Ugh, yes!” he groaned, slinging his bag over his other shoulder with an exaggerated huff. “You’d think I’ve never left the house before. We go camping like every month with scouts. Plus we just were in Japan two months ago.”\n\nI couldn’t resist jumping in. “You did forget your deodorant. Or don’t you remember smelling like sushi the whole time we were there? Maybe your mom has a point.”\n\nHe shot me a mock glare, narrowing his eyes with the kind of dramatic flair only a teenager could muster. “Then bring a replicator and we can replicate a new one when we get there.”\n\n“Wait. I thought you just said you were going to raw dog this trip.” I teased, grabbing my own bag. “Let’s just hope you don’t end up wearing the same shirt for three days because you forgot to pack extras. Again.”\n\n“Ha-ha, unlike travel in the 1920’s most places have laundry facilities,” he deadpanned, rolling his eyes again but failing to suppress a small grin.\n\nWe loaded into the Uber. The atmosphere shifted, charged with the infectious excitement radiating from my wife.\n\n“I can’t wait to see my sister. It’s been almost 10 years,” she said, her voice brimming with enthusiasm. Her eyes shone as she added, “And Rome... oh, you’re going to love it. The history, the architecture, the food—it’s just incredible.”\n\n“Don’t forget the Basil gelato,” I chimed in, glancing at our son in the rearview mirror. “That alone is worth the trip.”\n\nHe glanced up from his phone, “They better have pistachio. That’s the best flavor.”\n\n“Oh, they’ll have pistachio,” my wife assured him. “And hazelnut. And probably flavors we can’t even pronounce.”\n\n“I already have a list of places we need to go,” she continued, holding up her phone like a trophy. “Don’t worry—we’ll have plenty of time for spontaneous exploring too.”\n\n“Good,” I said, nodding sagely. “Spontaneous combustion is my specialty.”\n\n“Dad,” my son said, his tone flat as he looked up from his screen. “You’re the least spontaneous person I know.”\n\n“Hey, I can be unpredictable!” I protested, feigning offense.\n\n“Sure,” he said, his lips curling into a mischievous grin. “Just not with anything inflammable.”\n\nThe banter carried us all the way to the airport, where the bustling hum of travelers and the faint smell of overpriced coffee filled the air. We zipped through TSA-pre check security and found our gate.\n\n“Alright,” I said as we reached our seats, turning to my wife. “You ready for this?”\n\nMy wife beamed, her smile brighter than ever. My son shot me a thumbs-up before heading back to economy to sit with his friends, already glued to his phone, lost in whatever was streaming. “Let’s do this,” he said, his voice carrying the casual confidence of youth.	1	226	t	2026-02-27 12:03:52.900871-05	2026-02-27 20:00:15.326717-05	normal	\N
218	56	3	London's Living History	The family explores London, visiting iconic landmarks like Big Ben and Stonehenge with the protagonist's wife serving as guide through her sister's perspective and memories. Despite enjoying the cultural immersion, the protagonist begins sensing an unsettling presence.	**********\n\nOur first days in the UK were a blur of history and culture, an intoxicating blend of grandeur and charm. The streets of London pulsed with life, from the rhythmic clatter of red double-decker buses to the cheerful hum of locals and tourists mingling at open-air markets. Each landmark seemed to vibrate with its own story: the solemn resilience of the Tower of London, the regal splendor of Buckingham Palace, the timeless serenity of Westminster Abbey.\n\nAlthough we had a tour guide, my son spent much of his time exploring with his friends, leaving my wife and me to enjoy a more personal perspective. Her sister, a Londoner at heart, became our guide for the days, weaving us through the city’s tapestry of history and modernity with the ease of someone who had lived it intimately. With her as our storyteller, the city transformed into more than just a destination; it became a living scrapbook of memories and moments, layered with both her past and the present we were now sharing.\n\n“Look at the size of that thing,” my son said, craning his neck to take in Big Ben. His voice was tinged with both awe and the casual curiosity of a teenager trying not to seem too impressed.\n\n“You’re looking at one of the most famous clocks in the world,” I replied, slipping into my role as amateur tour guide. “It’s been keeping time since 1859.”\n\n“Longer than Dad’s been alive,” he said with a smirk.\n\n“Barely,” my wife shot back, rolling her eyes.\n\n“Keep it up, and I might leave you here as a permanent resident.” I said with mock menace.\n\nMy wife laughed, sliding her arm through mine. “Ignore him. He’s just jealous you’re old enough to know all the cool facts about this place.”\n\nOur tour took us to some places further afield, to Stonehenge, where the chill of the countryside wrapped around us like a cloak. The ancient stones loomed against the backdrop of a cloudy sky, their jagged silhouettes both eerie and mesmerizing.\n\n“Why do you think they built this?” my son asked, his voice hushed, as if afraid to disturb the spirits of those who had come before us.\n\n“No one really knows,” my wife answered, her gaze fixed on the monoliths. “A burial ground, maybe. Or a place of worship. It’s like a puzzle that’s been unsolved for thousands of years.”\n\nI nodded, feeling the pressure of the Threshold seeping through the area and into my chest. “Whatever it was, you can feel the intention here. Like they built it for something bigger than themselves.”\n\nWe continued to wander through the site. The wind seemed to whisper untold mysteries in a foreign tongue. The stones radiated an otherworldly presence, their shadows stretching long and dark across the damp grass. I couldn’t shake the unsettling feeling; the memories surfacing from what I had triggered at Aokigahara felt like the ground itself held ancient, restless spirits, their whispers threatening to break through.	2	227	t	2026-02-27 12:03:52.900871-05	2026-02-27 20:00:58.520459-05	normal	\N
219	56	4	Vatican Expulsion	In Rome, the family visits the Vatican and Sistine Chapel, initially awed by the art and history. However, they soon sense they are being watched by Swiss Guards and cardinals; guards abruptly expel them from the Vatican with a cryptic warning about their safety, leaving them confused and unsettled.	**********\n\nIf the UK had embraced the balance of modernity and heritage, Rome clung to the bones of its past with religious fervor, its streets a collision of the ancient and the eternal. The city overflowed with life, from the sun-dappled piazzas to the intricate fountains where tourists tossed coins with whispered wishes. The scent of freshly baked focaccia mingled with the tang of espresso wafting from corner cafés, and the cobblestone streets seemed to carry the weight of countless footsteps.\n\nAt the Vatican, the awe was almost overwhelming. The entire place felt like one vast, continuous museum, a labyrinth of human achievement where every corridor, courtyard, and crumbling facade unveiled another masterpiece, another echo of history refusing to be forgotten.\n\n“Look at this one,” my wife said, pointing to a fresco that stretched across the arched ceiling. Her voice was hushed, reverent. “The detail, the movement—it’s like the figures are alive.”\n\nMy son squinted up at the scene, his brows furrowing. “How long do you think it took to paint all this?”\n\n“Years,” I said, keeping my voice quiet. “Some of these works took decades. Whole lifetimes.”\n\nWhen we entered the Sistine Chapel, the air seemed to thicken, pressuring us into silence. Every inch of the space was alive with Michelangelo’s vision: Adam reaching for God, the Last Judgment looming large. My son was uncharacteristically quiet, his face tilted upward as if drinking in every detail.\n\n“It’s like... overwhelming,” he finally said, his voice barely audible. “You could stare at it forever and still not see everything.”\n\n“You’re right,” I murmured, gripping his shoulder lightly. “This is something you carry with you.”\n\nWhile the Vatican dazzled, us with its ostentation, an unsettling undercurrent threaded through the experience. I first felt it as we passed through the hallowed halls, a prickle at the back of my neck, as if unseen eyes tracked our every move.\n\n“Dad,” my son whispered, tugging at my sleeve. “Why are those guards staring at us?”\n\nI glanced at the Swiss Guards, their stoic expressions betraying nothing. “They’re just doing their job,” I said, though my voice sounded less certain than I intended.\n\n“Sure,” he replied, unconvinced. “But it’s weird.”\n\nI tried to shrug it off, but the unease clung to me. It wasn’t just the guards; cardinals passed us with subtle glances, their gazes heavy, knowing. It felt as if they were sizing me up, not as a tourist, but as something else—something dangerous or valuable.\n\n“Do you feel that?” I asked my wife quietly as we paused near a gilded altar.\n\nShe frowned, tilting her head. “Feel what?”\n\n“Like... we’re being watched,” I said, lowering my voice.\n\nShe looked around, her expression thoughtful. “I thought it was just me. It’s probably nothing. Just the atmosphere—it’s overwhelming.”\n\nI wasn’t so sure. We continued the tour through the Vatican, but the feeling just intensified. The air seemed heavier, tinged with an unnamable tension. I found myself glancing over my shoulder, half-expecting to see shadows coalescing into something tangible.\n\nThere, in the faint glow of a flickering candle near a forgotten alcove, I thought I saw it: a shape, barely visible, retreating into the darkness. It wasn’t human—it couldn’t be.\n\n“Are you okay?” my wife asked, pulling me from my thoughts.\n\n“Yeah,” I lied, forcing a smile. “Just tired.”\n\nThe lie tasted bitter, and the unease clung to me like moist underpants. Every step we took seemed to reverberate, as though the floor wasn’t stone but something alive, something waiting. The ornate marble columns and gilded relics, so full of grandeur moments ago, now felt oppressive. The grandeur had teeth.\n\nWhen we turned into a quieter hallway lined with solemn statues, the click of footsteps broke the uneasy silence. I glanced back to see a cluster of Swiss Guards moving toward us. Their colorful uniforms should have been a reassuring sight, a symbol of order and protection. Instead, their rapid approach carried an undercurrent of urgency and menace.\n\n“Excuse me,” one of them said, his Italian-accented English clipped and formal, “you need to leave. Immediately.”\n\n“Leave?” my wife asked, startled. “Why? The museums and gift shops don’t close for hours.”\n\n“That is not your concern,” another guard snapped, his tone leaving no room for argument.\n\nMy son instinctively edged closer to me, clutching my arm. “Dad?” he whispered, his voice trembling slightly.\n\n“It’s fine,” I said, though the words felt hollow. I turned to the guards, keeping my voice calm but firm. “Is something wrong? Did we do something?”\n\n“Move. Now.” The lead guard stepped closer, his hand resting on the hilt of the ceremonial sword at his side. The other guards flanked us, their expressions unreadable but tense.\n\n“We haven’t done anything,” my wife protested, her voice rising. “You can’t just—”\n\n“Yes, we can,” the guard interrupted sharply, his eyes narrowing. “You need to go.”\n\nThe tone made it clear: this was not a request.\n\nThe guards herded us down the hallway, their movements brisk and unyielding. I glanced over my shoulder at the gilded corridors disappearing behind us, a gnawing sense of unfinished business growing with every step.\n\n“Dad,” my son said in a small voice, “what’s happening?”\n\n“I don’t know,” I admitted, my own unease mirrored in his own.\n\n“Are they kicking anyone else out?” my wife whispered, glancing around. The answer was clear—no. Other visitors wandered freely, their voices echoing faintly in the distance. Whatever this was, it was just for us.\n\nWe emerged into the sunlight outside the Vatican walls, the guards stopping abruptly at the exit.\n\n“Do not ever return, you have been banned,” one of them said, his voice cold. “This is for your safety.”\n\n“Our safety?” my wife repeated, incredulous. “What does that even mean? What are you not telling us?”\n\nThe guard’s expression didn’t waver. “Leave.”\n\nI considered pressing further, demanding an explanation, but something about the way the guard’s jaw tightened warned me against it. Without another word, the guards turned and marched back inside, the heavy doors closing behind them with a finality that made my stomach twist.\n\nWe stood there in stunned silence, the Roman streets around us a stark contrast to the suffocating tension of the Vatican.\n\n“What the hell was that?” my wife said, breaking the quiet. Her voice was sharp, but beneath it, I could hear the lingering tremor of fear.\n\n“Maybe it’s some kind of emergency,” I offered weakly, though the words felt as thin as tissue paper.\n\n“An emergency that only affects us?” she shot back, crossing her arms.\n\n“They were watching us the whole time,” my son said quietly, his gaze fixed on the Vatican doors. “You noticed, right? Those guards, those old dudes in robes... they kept looking at us.”\n\n“I noticed,” I said. “I just don’t know why.”\n\nMy wife let out an exasperated sigh, throwing her hands in the air. “Unbelievable. Zero-star Yelp reviews for you assholes!” she shouted in Japanese, her tone dripping with frustration and just a hint of humor.\n\nMy son laughed nervously, and I couldn’t help but crack a faint smile despite the lingering unease.\n\nWe turned to leave, but I couldn’t escape the feeling that the Vatican hadn’t expelled us out of hostility—it had been fear. Not fear for us, but fear of us. Something had shifted within those ancient walls, and whatever it was, we were at its center.\n\n**********	3	228	t	2026-02-27 12:03:52.900871-05	2026-02-27 20:01:50.193074-05	normal	\N
220	56	5	Unplanned Detour to India	Rather than return home to face S Corp, the family spontaneously decides to extend their journey to India, where the protagonist wants to share his youthful backpacking memories with his family. They travel in style with a private driver through the vibrant chaos of Indian streets.	Fresh from our Vatican misadventure, where even our tour guides failed to extract answers from the tight-lipped representatives, we found ourselves at the quiet edge of our journey. The trip had been winding down naturally, but something about that final, unanswered mystery made it feel as though the story had ended rather than simply concluded. My son’s classmates, still buzzing with stories and souvenirs, would soon board their flights home, their adventure neatly wrapped up. But for me, an unsettled feeling lingered, as if we had left something unfinished, something just beyond reach.\n\nAt the airport, as we waved to his classmates and they said their goodbye, the dread of getting back to S Corp life looming ahead, we hesitated. The thought of going back felt like closing a book before the final chapter. On a whim, driven by a spark of spontaneity and a desire to keep the momentum of discovery alive, we made the decision: we weren’t done.\n\nIndia, a place I had once explored as a reckless teenager armed with little more than a backpack and a thirst for adventure, called to me. This time, the journey wasn’t just mine, it was ours. I wanted my wife and son to experience the wonder, chaos, and beauty that had left an indelible mark on me so many years ago.\n\nNo longer constrained by the limits of a class trip, our journey was different, more measured less forced marching to see everything, more deliberate, yet no less thrilling. Gone were the days of crowded buses and smelly half-star hotels; we opted for opulence, a private driver, weaving us through the cacophony of India’s roads with practiced precision.\n\n“You sure this doesn’t feel too... touristy?” I asked my wife as we sped past a kaleidoscope of street vendors, their stalls brimming with fruits and fabrics in every conceivable shade.\n\nShe glanced at me, her expression thoughtful. “It’s different, but that doesn’t make it less meaningful. Besides,” she added with a grin, “I think your backpacking days are behind you.”\n\n“Speak for yourself,” I replied, feigning indignation. “I’ve still got some adventure left in me.”\n\nOur son chimed in from the back seat, his nose buried in his phone. “Yeah, Dad. Nothing screams ‘adventure’ like AC and bottled water.”\n\nThe sarcasm was sharp, but his smirk betrayed his amusement.\n\n“Ok wiseass, you can walk outside in the 120 degree heat.” I snapped back.\n\n“Only 118 degrees today, sir.” The driver patiently corrected me.	4	229	t	2026-02-27 12:03:52.900871-05	2026-02-27 20:02:45.227231-05	normal	\N
221	56	6	The Temple Revelation	At a lesser-known temple in the Aravalli Range, the protagonist discovers statues matching the two female figures from his dreams—identified by a priest as the goddesses Lakshmi and Parvati. The priest reveals that these entities appear to guide those who are spiritually adrift and possess ancient wisdom about balance and transformation.	**********\n\nThe temples we visited felt alive, their ornate carvings and vivid frescoes pulsating with stories of gods and mortals intertwined. In the heady mix of incense and chanting, I found echoes of something familiar—something I hadn’t yet named. It was at a lesser-known temple nestled in the foothills of the Aravalli Range that the connection crystallized.\n\nWe entered through an archway adorned with lotus motifs, the scent of jasmine heavy in the air. At the heart of the temple stood a statue unlike any I had ever seen. One figure bore the serene grace of a goddess, her gaze both maternal and commanding, while another seemed poised for flight, her celestial wings outstretched.\n\nI froze, my breath catching in my throat.\n\n“What is it?” my wife asked, her voice soft as she followed my gaze.\n\n“That...” I gestured toward the statue, my voice barely above a whisper. “That looks like... them.”\n\n“Them?” she pressed, stepping closer.\n\n“Lady I and Lady A,” I said, the names heavy on my tongue. “From my dreams.”\n\nShe frowned, her expression skeptical but not dismissive. “You’re saying these are the same beings you’ve dreamt about? That’s... a stretch.”\n\n“It’s not,” I insisted, my pulse quickening. “Look at the details. The wings, the posture... even the expressions. It’s them.”\n\nOur son, approached and squinted at the statue. “Okay, that’s freaky. How could that even be possible?”\n\n“I don’t know,” I admitted, my mind racing. “But I think we’re meant to be here.”\n\nWhile we were lingering near the statue, an elderly priest approached, his saffron robes flowing with his deliberate steps. His eyes, sharp and knowing, seemed to pierce through me.\n\n“You see them,” he said, his voice gentle but firm.\n\nI nodded slowly. “Who are they?”\n\nHe gestured for us to sit on the cool stone floor, the sunlight filtering through lattice windows and casting intricate patterns of light and shadow around us. The air was heavy with the scent of incense, and the faint murmur of prayers echoed in the background.\n\n“They are Lakshmi and Parvati,” he began, his tone reverent and deliberate, as though each word held great weight. “In Hindu tradition, Lakshmi is the goddess of wealth, prosperity, and beauty. She is the consort of Lord Vishnu, the preserver of the universe, and her presence brings abundance and harmony to those who honor her.”\n\n“Lakshmi represents abundance?” my wife asked, her curiosity piqued.\n\n“Indeed,” the priest replied, nodding. “Not only material wealth. Her gifts extend to the prosperity of the mind, the heart, and the spirit. She is seen as the guiding light in the darkness, offering wisdom and stability to those seeking balance in their lives.”\n\n“And Parvati?” I interjected, leaning forward.\n\nThe priest’s expression softened. “Parvati is the mother goddess, a symbol of love, devotion, and strength. She is the consort of Lord Shiva, the destroyer, and transformer. Together, they represent the cyclical nature of existence—creation, preservation, and destruction.”\n\n“Why two consorts for different gods?” I asked, intrigued by the duality.\n\n“Because they embody complementary energies,” he explained, his voice steady. “Lakshmi nurtures the material and spiritual worlds, ensuring their growth and harmony. Parvati is the force of transformation and endurance, guiding individuals through trials and changes. She is also known as Shakti, the divine feminine energy that powers the universe itself.”\n\nMy son leaned forward; her gaze fixed on the priest. “Doesn’t it seem strange, that this temple honors both of them? How are they really connected?”\n\nThe priest’s eyes glimmered as though he were about to share a secret. “Their roles in mythology are profound, yet their essence transcends the stories. Lakshmi, for instance, is born from the churning of the cosmic ocean, a tale that symbolizes the struggle to achieve true prosperity. Parvati, on the other hand, chose to undertake intense penance to win Shiva’s love, a reflection of devotion and self-realization.”\n\n“Penance?” my son asked, tilting his head.\n\nThe priest nodded. “Yes. In one story, Parvati retreats to the mountains to meditate, seeking to awaken Shiva from his ascetic solitude. Her unwavering resolve is said to have brought balance to the universe, for without her, even Shiva’s power was incomplete.”\n\n“Balance,” I repeated, the word resonating in my mind. “You said they come to those who are lost?”\n\nHe fixed me with a penetrating gaze. “Yes. Some believe that when a soul is adrift—whether in despair or confusion—they appear, weaving threads of connection through dreams, visions, and symbols. These are not mere deities of the past; they are energies, present and active in the lives of those who are willing to listen.”\n\n“Have you seen them?” my wife asked softly, her tone almost reverent.\n\nThe priest’s gaze grew distant for a moment. “Not with my eyes, but with my heart,” he said, his voice tinged with emotion. “Their presence can be subtle, like the rustling of leaves in a breeze. But sometimes, it is undeniable—visions too vivid to ignore, signs too clear to dismiss.”\n\nHe turned to me, his expression searching. “You speak as one who has seen. Have they come to you?”\n\nI hesitated, glancing at my wife and son before nodding. “Yes,” I said, my voice faint. “In my dreams. I didn’t know who they were until now. They saved me from... something I can’t even begin to explain.”\n\nThe priest leaned forward, his expression both intense and gentle. “Then you must heed their call. To see them is a gift, but it is also a burden. They do not appear without reason. What they offer is guidance, but what you do with it is your choice.”\n\n“Guidance for what?” I asked, the gravity of his words settling on me like a stone.\n\n“For balance,” he said simply. “For understanding. They come to remind us that prosperity and transformation are meaningless without harmony.”\n\nI glanced at the statues again—Lakshmi’s serene smile, Parvati’s resolute gaze—and felt a shiver course through me. They were more than carvings, more than legends. They were something vast, ancient, and alive, their presence weaving through the fabric of this world and my own fragmented understanding of it.\n\n“What do you think they want from me?” I asked, my voice barely above a whisper.\n\nThe priest’s lips curled into a knowing smile. “That is for you to discover. But I believe you already know the answer. Listen carefully, and they will show you the way.”	5	230	t	2026-02-27 12:03:52.900871-05	2026-02-27 20:03:44.339838-05	normal	\N
222	57	1	Orient Express Departure	The protagonist's family boards the legendary Orient Express for a three-day journey through Europe. They encounter a mysterious man named R wearing a hat bearing the symbol of Lady I, who cryptically warns them about the train's true nature as a passageway for the strange and extraordinary, and leaves behind an enigmatic grimoire.	We still had about a week left before my son started his new school year, so we did what any other obscenely wealthy family would do while traveling back from India: we decided to make a detour and take a voyage aboard the legendary Orient Express. This iconic train offered more than luxury; it promised a three-day passage through Europe’s stunning landscapes, steeped in the timeless romance of rail travel. It was the perfect finale, a harmonious blend of sophistication and discovery.\n\nA short flight to Istanbul later, we stood before the legendary Orient Express. Its polished carriages gleamed in the golden hues of the setting sun, reflecting the brilliance of the moment. The faint hum of voices and the occasional hiss of steam filled the air, mingling with the rich aroma of freshly brewed coffee wafting from the dining car. The scene felt alive, steeped in an air of anticipation that seemed almost otherworldly.\n\nThe whistle blew, sharp and clear, signaling the train’s imminent departure. I turned to my wife and son, who stood beside me, their expressions a mix of awe and exhilaration.\n\n“Ready for this adventure?” I asked, my voice carrying a rare note of excitement.\n\nMy wife nodded eagerly, her eyes sparkling. “Absolutely. It’s like stepping into a different time—something out of an Agatha Christie novel.”\n\nMy son smirked, adjusting his backpack. “Or a murder mystery movie. Just saying, if someone disappears, I’m not helping you solve it.”\n\nI laughed, shaking my head. “Noted. You can stick to the snacks, Sherlock.”\n\nWe boarded the train, following the porter to our private coach. The train’s opulent interiors were a tribute to a bygone era, with mahogany paneling, brass accents, and plush seats that seemed to promise comfort beyond imagination. The rhythmic hum of the train’s engine provided a soothing undercurrent, its vibrations subtly connecting us to the journey ahead.\n\nAs we approached the door to our car, we found someone seated at a table in the walkway. A man with an air of quiet authority, dressed impeccably in a tailored suit and a hat that contrasted greatly with his dreadlocks and large heavy chains around his neck. My breath caught. The emblem on his hat bore an uncanny resemblance to the symbol of Lady I.\n\n“Apologies,” he said, standing and offering a polite bow. His voice carried a calm confidence that immediately set him apart. “It seems I’m a bit lost.”\n\n“No harm done,” I said, though curiosity gnawed at me. “Your hat—it’s intriguing. May I ask about the symbol?”\n\nHe removed the hat and studied it for a moment, his lips curling into a faint smile. “You have a sharp eye,” he replied, his tone both amused and guarded. “The emblem is not one many notice, let alone recognize.”\n\nI stepped closer, lowering my voice. “I think I’ve seen it before, if not the symbol itself, then what it represents.”\n\nHis expression shifted, his eyes narrowing slightly as if measuring my words. “Is that so? Perhaps you’re more attuned to the unseen than most.”\n\n“What do you mean?” I asked, glancing back at my wife, who was now watching the exchange with keen interest.\n\nThe man gestured toward the plush seat he had vacated, inviting me to sit. “You can call me R,” he said, his tone soft yet commanding. “I’ve been associated with this train for many years. It’s more than just a vehicle, it’s a passageway, a crucible for the strange and the extraordinary.”\n\nMy wife took the seat beside me, her curiosity as piqued as mine. “You mean the train itself has... secrets?” she asked.\n\nR inclined his head. “Not the train, per se, but the passengers it carries. This journey has a way of drawing out what lies beneath; dreams, fears, truths one might prefer to keep buried.”\n\n“And the symbol?” I pressed. “Does it have to do with Lady I and Lady A?”\n\nHis eyes flickered with recognition at the names, though he said nothing for a long moment. The silence stretched, thick with implication.\n\n“You’ve seen them,” he said finally, his voice barely above a whisper.\n\n“In dreams,” I admitted, the words tumbling out before I could second-guess them. “They saved me once, from something I can’t explain. And now, they... linger.”\n\n“They exist in a realm between,” R said, his gaze distant as though recalling something profound. “Between reality and the subconscious, between creation and destruction. They are protectors, yes, but also arbiters. They guide, but they also judge.”\n\n“Judge what?” my wife asked, her voice steady despite the growing tension in the air.\n\n“Your readiness,” R said simply. “Your willingness to confront the truths you seek. Not all who encounter them are prepared for what follows.”\n\nI felt a chill run through me as the train jolted into motion, its rhythmic clatter blending with R’s words. “What happens to those who aren’t ready?”\n\nR’s smile was enigmatic, his gaze unyielding. “They fade. Consumed by the very questions they were too afraid to pose.”\n\nThe weight of his words settled over us, thick and oppressive. My wife shifted uneasily, while my son, who had been listening in silence, finally spoke.\n\n“Okay, so... this isn’t just a regular train ride, is it?” he asked, his usual sarcasm tinged with genuine unease.\n\nR chuckled, a deep, melodic sound. “It’s a train ride, young man. The destinations are simply... variable.”\n\nBefore we could press him further, R stood, placing his hat back on his head with deliberate care. “Dark blessings,” he said, his tone final. “But for now, enjoy the journey.”\n\nWith that, he walked back up the train, leaving us in stunned silence. The compartment felt colder somehow, the echoes of his words lingering in the air.\n\n“What the hell just happened?” my wife asked.\n\n“How the fuck should I know,” I replied, my gaze fixed on the door. “But I think this trip just got a lot more complicated.”\n\nWhen we opened the door and entered our private car, something immediately caught my eye. Sitting on the nightstand, was a thick, leather-bound book. Its cover was weathered but ornate, embossed with intricate patterns that seemed to shift under the faint light. The spine bore no title, only a single, indented symbol—the same emblem R had worn on his hat.\n\nMy breath hitched as I picked it up, its weight heavier than it should have been. A faint hum seemed to emanate from the book, not audible but felt, like a vibration against my palms. The edges of the pages were gilded, their surfaces slightly uneven as if they’d been hand-cut centuries ago.\n\nI considered opening it. My fingers hovered over the cover, the urge to delve into its secrets nearly overwhelming. Unexpectedly, a sharp pang of guilt tugged at me. This trip was for my family, for reconnecting, for memories. Whatever mysteries the book held could wait. With deliberate care, I slipped it into my bag, tucking it beneath layers of clothing as if to muffle its pull.\n\nOver the remaining days aboard the Orient Express, I searched for R, hoping to glean more about the strange book and his uncanny knowledge. It was as though he had vanished entirely. The train’s staff either didn’t recognize his description or offered vague, dismissive answers that only deepened my unease.\n\n“I’m sorry, sir,” one porter said with an apologetic shrug. “There is no one by that name or description on board.”\n\nAnother worker, older and sterner, stiffened at my inquiry. “You’d do well to forget about him,” he muttered before hurrying away, leaving me standing in the corridor with more questions than before.\n\nR had slipped away like a shadow, leaving behind only the enigmatic grimoire.	