o know they're paying attention to your biceps. "Wouldn't want you to hurt yourself."\n\n"I'll manage," Patrick grins, flexing just enough to make the point without being obvious about it. "But if I do get injured, you offering to kiss it better?"\n\nShe blushes and looks away, but she's smiling. "Maybe. Depends on where you hurt yourself."\n\nGod, they're painful to watch. But also kind of beautiful in that uncomplicated way that normal people navigate attraction—like they both read the same instruction manual and actually understood it.\n\nMeanwhile, Jamie slides closer to me on the couch. The shag carpet beneath our feet is thick enough to lose small pets in, a brown nightmare that somehow makes the basement feel both retro and eternal.\n\n"You know," Jamie says, her shoulder bumping against mine as she settles into the cushions, "we still have some unfinished business from Tuesday."\n\n"Tuesday?" I blink, genuinely confused.\n\nAt school we've been hanging out, sure, but always with the whole group—passing notes in biology, MTG at lunch, the usual herd dynamics that make any real conversation impossible. Tuesday feels like six months ago instead of three days. "What happened Tuesday?"\n\nShe gives me a look—not angry, exactly, but the kind of expression girls get when they're deciding whether you're being deliberately obtuse or just naturally dense. "Spin the bottle…"\n\n"Oh. Right." Heat creeps up my neck like spilled paint. "That."\n\n"That," she repeats, and there's something playfully dangerous in her tone that makes my stomach do complicated gymnastics. She traces a pattern on my knee with her fingertip, just the lightest touch through my jeans, but it feels like she's writing her name on my soul with electricity. "That concussion must have been really bad if you forgot about that."\n\n"I'm not—" I start, but my voice cracks like I'm thirteen again. "I mean, it's not that..."\n\n"Good," she murmurs, and somehow she's moved even closer, close enough that I can smell her perfume mixing with the basement's atmosphere of teenage rebellion. "Because we're here now, and I can give you that reminder..."\n\n"Jamie!" Becca's voice cuts through the moment like a buzz saw. She appears beside the couch with the kind of timing that suggests she's been watching us from across the room. "Can you come help me with something? Girl emergency."\n\nJamie's face flashes with annoyance, but she squeezes my knee once—a promise of unfinished business—before standing. "We'll pick this up later," she says.\n\nPatrick's been helping Whitney by carving symbols into the plywood with his grandfather's old hunting knife—crude lines that look more like the doodles of a disturbed child than ancient mysticism. But there's something about them that makes my eyes water if I stare too long, like trying to focus on an optical illusion that doesn't want to be solved.\n\n"Did you really get these instructions from some random BBS?" Becca asks as she slides back into the room, her voice pitched higher than usual.\n\n"BALG. Black Arts Liberation Guild," Patrick says, waving the dot-matrix printout like a flag of surrender. "F.B. Goatting knows his shit. This guy's supposedly summoned everything from minor demons to major credit card debt forgiveness."\n\n“If my parents find out I am so dead.” Becca keeps mumbling to herself.\n\n"If they try anything weird," Patrick adds to Becca with a grin that's pure trouble, "you can always crash at my place. My parents don’t care."\n\nBecca laughs, a sound like silver bells wrapped in mischief. "Is that your idea of a pickup line? 'Hey baby, want to flee religious persecution together?'"\n\n"Is it working?"\n\n"Maybe."\n\nMy head pounds—a bass drum keeping time with my pulse. The concussion from Saturday's tournament still feels like broken glass rattling around in my skull. Every flicker of candlelight sends jagged reminders dancing across my peripheral vision, and I can't shake the feeling that some of them are dancing 