nted. “No other dreamer has brought forth something so intricate and potent,” Madam O said, her voice tinged with wonder.\n\n“It’s not just a machine,” Lady I added, her expression thoughtful. “It’s a symbol of defiance. Its existence challenges the Demiurge’s dominion, a crack in Etheris’ impenetrable walls.”\n\nSo the Eternal Engine, wasn’t just a product of my imagination and technomancy, it stood as a beacon of hope. Its potential went beyond technological advancement—it could amplify dream energies, channeling messages and awakening others trapped in Etheris.\n\nMadam O’s eyes gleamed with trust as she spoke. “The path ahead will be arduous, but you are not without guidance. With the Eternal Engine and the symbols you’ve mastered, you are equipped to fight against the Demiurge’s tyranny over Etheris.”\n\n“It is time to break THEIR chains.”	7	238	f	2026-02-27 12:04:21.441383-05	2026-02-27 20:11:04.575326-05	normal	\N
231	58	2	Breakfast and the Time Reversal Card	At breakfast the next morning, the narrator's wife and son dismiss his earthquake dream. As they discuss a Magic: The Gathering card called Time Reversal, the narrator notices its artwork eerily resembles The Pointers—a mysterious group he's been obsessing over. This sparks a new line of investigation into potential timeline shifts and the Mandela Effect.	The nostalgic smell of maple and brown sugar filled the kitchen, mingling with the soft hum of morning. Our new breakfast table and chairs, still had that new chair smell, were the centerpiece of this ordinary moment. The scene felt grounded—so normal; it was almost surreal after the chaotic lifetimes lived the previous night. I stirred my oatmeal slowly, my mind lingering on the dream that had shaken me to my core.\n\n“Did either of you feel the house shaking last night?” I finally asked, breaking the silence.\n\nMy wife glanced up, her expression calm but curious. “Shaking? No. I slept fine,” she said in Japanese, switching effortlessly to English. “You must have been snoring again.”\n\n“Yeah, Dad,” my son added, between bites of cheese toast. “You’ve been doing that a lot lately. Is your C-Pap malfunctioning?”\n\nI sighed, recounting the vivid details: the sirens, the ground crumbling, the feeling of falling into an endless abyss. My wife’s brow furrowed slightly, while my son’s eyes lit up—not with concern, but intrigue.\n\n“Sounds intense,” he said, leaning forward. “Like when a boss battle starts in a video game.”\n\n“Or when the world’s ending,” I muttered.\n\n“Same thing,” he quipped with a grin.\n\nMy wife gave him a soft nudge with her elbow. “Don’t tease your dad,” she chided lightly, though her tone carried affection. She turned back to me, her gaze thoughtful. “Which one was it last night? A hell dream, one where you learn something, or one where the shadowy puppet masters appear?”\n\n“Yes.” I nodded, unable to shake the strange sense of connection between the dreamscape’s lessons and the unsettling imagery from last night.\n\nI was reflecting on the connection between the dreamscape and the unsettling imagery of the night before, my son’s voice broke through my thoughts, sharp and animated. “Oh, come on! That’s not even how it works!”\n\nI glanced at him, startled. He was furiously tapping away on his phone, a familiar expression of exasperation plastered across his face.\n\n“What are you on about?” I asked, mildly amused.\n\nHe didn’t look up, his thumbs flying across the screen. “Discord. This guy in my Magic: The Gathering Arena group keeps saying Time Reversal is a trash card. Like, excuse me? It literally won me the tournament yesterday.”\n\n“Time Reversal?” I echoed.\n\n“Yeah.” He paused his typing to hold up his phone, flashing the card’s digital artwork. “It’s like a reset button—reshuffles your hand, your deck, everything. Boom, fresh start. Total game-changer.”\n\n“Sounds handy,” I said, leaning back.\n\nHis attention finally shifted from his phone to me, his eyes lighting up. “Right? Imagine hitting a reset button anytime you mess up. You could totally change the outcome of, like, anything. It’s like a do-over button,” he said, holding his phone out with the card displayed for emphasis. “You reshuffle your hand, reset the board, and get a second chance. It’s the ultimate play.”\n\nI chuckled, grateful for the distraction. “Wish I had one of those for real life,” I said.\n\n“I mean I did use the replicator to print out a few copies,” he replied with a grin. “But in real life, imagine rewinding time every time you mess up. No stress. No consequences.”\n\n“Maybe,” I said, pulling an image of the card up on my phone and studying its intricate artwork—a lone figure pointing as they gaze off into the distance, their expression a mix of determination and longing. Something about it gave me pause, an eerie flicker of recognition. “But would it really fix things?”\n\nMy wife, sipping her tea, chimed in. “Sometimes, facing what’s already happened is more important than trying to change it,” she said gently. “Growth doesn’t come from shortcuts.”\n\n“Exactly,” I agreed, her words resonating deeply. “As tempting as it sounds, rewinding time wouldn’t erase the lessons we’re meant to learn.”\n\n“Still,” my son said, grabbing the deck next to him, pulling out the card from his deck next to him, and sliding the card across the table, “you gotta admit, it’d be cool.”\n\nI picked it up, examining the figure more closely. The artwork stirred a strange memory—The Pointers. That elusive group, their sudden disappearance still a mystery, had felt connected to all of this somehow. The resemblance was uncanny.\n\n“This... this reminds me of something,” I murmured, turning the card in my hands.\n\n“What?” my son asked, his curiosity piqued.\n\n“There used to be a group,” I began, my voice trailing off as I tried to piece together the connection. “They called themselves The Pointers. Despite having vivid memories of them, and even remembering them making the news. They completely vanished without a trace, but this...” I tapped the card, its image sending a shiver down my spine. “It’s like they’re still here, hiding in plain sight.”\n\nMy wife gave me a puzzled look. “The Pointers? Not this shit again. Did you run out of medicine from Dr. On? She said stop with the false-memory crap.”\n\nI hesitated. “Because I thought they were gone, I was going insane. Or maybe they were never real. But now... I’m not so sure.”\n\nThe card felt heavier in my hand, its symbolism pressing on my thoughts. Was it coincidence, or was there something deeper at play? A simple game tied to the mysteries I’d been chasing? And now, after all the crazy dream work it finally decides to show back. It was absurd—but so much of my journey already defied logic.\n\n“You think they’re connected to your dreams?” my son asked, his voice cautious but intrigued.\n\n“Maybe,” I admitted, my gaze lingering on the card. “Or maybe I’m just looking for patterns where there aren’t any. And the Infinitron interface in my brain is giving me cancer.”\n\nAn exasperated sigh followed my wife’s sharp intake of breath. “You always say that,” she said, her tone hovering between concern and amusement. “You’re not getting cancer. You’ve had a team check your brain, what, a hundred times?”\n\n“Hundred and two,” I corrected, smirking. “They still haven’t ruled out long-term side effects. Just because they haven’t found anything doesn’t mean it’s not lurking.”\n\nShe rolled her eyes but reached out, her hand settling over mine. “Trust your instincts,” she said softly, her voice steady. “If there’s something there, you’ll find it. You always do.”\n\nHer reassurance brought a flicker of comfort, though it didn’t entirely quell my doubts. “I’ve tried,” I said, leaning back in my chair. “I’ve set the Infinitron to dig into the Pointers more times than I can count. I even assigned an entire team of investigators at S Corp—some of the best minds I could gather. You know what they turned up?”\n\n“Nothing,” my son guessed, glancing up from his phone.\n\n“Not a damn thing,” I confirmed, gesturing emphatically. “No records, no sightings, no digital footprint. It’s like they’ve been scrubbed from reality. Either they’re the best-hidden secret on the planet, or I’m chasing ghosts.”\n\nMy wife arched an eyebrow. “Which do you think it is?”\n\nI hesitated, my expression caught somewhere between frustration and self-awareness. “If you ask the team, they’ll tell you I’m nuts. There’s a running joke that all my genius inventions and formidable prowess are tainted by my insanity. ‘Here comes the boss with another Pointer lead,’” I said, mimicking one of my employees with mock severity.\n\nMy son snorted. “Do they really say that?”\n\n“Only behind my back,” I replied, grinning. “But I hear things.”\n\nMy wife shook her head, a wry smile on her lips. “You’re impossible.”\n\n“Impossible, maybe. I feel like I know there’s something out there,” I said, my voice firming. “I can’t explain it, but they’re connected to all this—the dreams, the symbols, even the Eternal Engine. I just need to figure out how.”\n\nHer smile softened. “Then keep looking. Just don’t let it consume you.”\n\nAs small as it seemed, this connection felt monumental. The card’s intricate artwork, a lone figure staring into the distance, its gaze filled with a haunting mix of determination and despair, perfectly captured The Pointers, the elusive figures that had haunted my thoughts for what seemed like forever. How had this artist, working on a game decades ago, portrayed them so accurately?\n\n“This might be the crack I needed,” I murmured, my fingers tracing the edge of the card. “Look at this,” I said, sliding the card toward her. “It’s them. Exactly as I remember.”\n\nShe studied the card, tilting her head. “It’s... something. But you know those fantasy artists, they make up all sorts of weird combinations. How do you know it’s not just coincidence?”\n\n“I don’t. That’s what I’m going to try to find out.”\n\nSomething about the card nagged at me—a detail I couldn’t pin down. I turned to my laptop and dove into the MTG archives, sifting through forums and wikis. It didn’t take long to stumble upon a conspiracy thread that made my pulse quicken.\n\n“Listen to this,” I said, gesturing for my wife and son to come closer. “There are two known designs for Time Reversal. But officially, only one master print exists.”\n\n“What?” my son asked, leaning in. “How’s that possible? Cards don’t just change designs randomly.”\n\n“Exactly,” I said. “Look, on these forums, people are arguing about it. Some swear they’ve seen a different version, while others insist this is the only one. It’s like... it’s like the Mandela Effect, but with proof. Both designs exist, but somehow, they don’t.”\n\nMy wife frowned. “The Mandela Effect? Didn’t you already go down that rabbit hole?”\n\n“Sort of,” I said, scrolling through the forum. “The Pointers... they could be another example. This card—this is tangible. It’s not just a memory. It’s here, in my hands.”\n\n“Okay, but how does that work?” my son asked, skepticism mingling with fascination. “Parallel universes or something?”\n\n“Some think so,” I said, pointing to a user’s post. “Look at this: ‘The dual designs of Time Reversal might be evidence of timeline shifts caused by CERN’s experiments at the Large Hadron Collider.’”\n\n“CERN?” my wife asked, her voice sharpening.\n\nI nodded. “There’s a theory that their work with particle collisions could’ve disrupted reality. It sounds wild, but with this,” I held up the card again, “is hard to ignore.”\n\nMy son grabbed his phone, opening Discord. “Hold on. I’ve seen people talking about this in one of my MTG groups.” He scrolled rapidly, muttering under his breath. “Here, check this out.”\n\nHe turned the screen toward us, showing a conversation between two users debating the card’s origins.\n\nMTGFanBoi: The original design had way more detail. I remember because I used to have it.\n\nDeepPickles69: No way, man. You’re misremembering. The artwork’s always been the same.\n\nMTGFanBoi: How the fuck do you explain the scans floating around online? They don’t match the print in circulation.\n\nDeepPickles69: AI. Shitferbrains.\n\n“It’s everywhere,” I said, scrolling further. “There’s an entire community trying to figure this out. Some say it’s a printing error; others think it’s a cover-up. Take a look at this scan,” I pointed to a blurry image. “It’s the alternate design. It’s just as real. I’m sure of it.”\n\n“Real or not,” my wife interjected, “what does this have to do with The Pointers?”\n\n“That’s the thing,” I said, the words tumbling out as the pieces clicked into place. “The Pointers might be tied to these shifts. They’re connected to symbols, to changes in reality itself. This card? It’s proof that something’s been altered. Not only that, but this proof also has a picture of the pointers on it.”\n\nI finally felt like I was on the verge of finally figuring one of these fucking mysteries out.	1	199	f	2026-02-27 12:04:35.69225-05	2026-02-27 20:12:57.002851-05	normal	\N
232	58	3	Madam O's Revelation on Time and Dreams	The narrator seamlessly enters the dreamscape and meets Madam O in her infinite library. She reveals that dreams exist outside time and can alter the past, explaining the Mandela Effect as ripples of dreams crossing temporal boundaries. She warns of deliberate forces using dreams to rewrite history and emphasizes the narrator's role as a dreamwalker guardian of the cosmic tapestry.	All the training with Lady I and Lady A had another nice side effect. I no longer struggled transitioning from waking to slumber. It was seamless. The lines between reality and the dreamscape blurring as I drifted into Madam O’s chamber within the Lady I’s realm. The view was breathtaking, a plane of bookshelves stretching infinitely in all directions; its vast expanse illuminated by an otherworldly glow. Symbols danced in the air, shifting and reforming with a logic that defied comprehension. In the heart of this luminous labyrinth stood Madam O, her presence both commanding and serene, the embodiment of ancient wisdom.\n\nShe turned to me, her eyes reflecting the shimmering patterns around us. “You’ve come with questions,” she said, her voice as steady as the pulse of the cosmos.\n\n“Questions, yes,” I replied, the weight of my recent discoveries pressing on my chest. “But mostly confusion. The Mandela Effect, CERN, dreams... it’s all connected, isn’t it? But how? And why?”\n\nMadam O’s lips curved into a faint smile. “Time,” she said simply. “Time is not a straight path, as mortals often imagine. It is a tapestry, woven with threads that cross and intertwine in ways your waking mind struggles to perceive.”\n\nI frowned. “So, what we call the Mandela Effect, it’s not just a quirk of memory or a collision of timelines? It’s... deliberate?”\n\n“Not always,” she replied, gesturing for me to follow her. As we walked, the air around us shifted, forming a vivid tableau of a blood-soaked battlefield. “Let me show you.”\n\nThe acrid stench of death filled the air, mingling with the cries of wounded soldiers. The scene was visceral, every detail rendered with a clarity that made my breath catch. Madam O spoke as we stood amidst the carnage.\n\n“You may remember this teachings from your catechism about the widow’s son. During the Crusades, a Knight Templar fought valiantly for Jerusalem,” she began. “Despite his indomitable will, his army was outmatched, slaughtered to the last man. He himself lay dying in the Temple of Baphomet, his blood pooling on its sacred floor.”\n\nI watched as the knight, a hulking figure clad in battered armor, collapsed to his knees. His face was pale, his breaths ragged. Yet, in his final moments, something extraordinary happened.\n\n“As death approached,” Madam O continued, “he drifted to sleep, his mind reached beyond the confines of time and space. In his dream, he stood face to face with an aged soldier, one whose voice had once called him to arms.”\n\nThe dreamscape shifted, the battlefield dissolving into a dimly lit chamber. The knight now stood before the older man, whose stern gaze bore the weight of countless battles. The soldier’s voice was gravelly, laced with urgency. “The Ottomans are preparing a trap,” he warned. “You must act swiftly, or all will be lost.”\n\n“When the knight awoke,” Madam O said, her tone heavy with significance, “he found himself not on the brink of death, but amidst a tent in the newly conquered city, surrounded by his victorious comrades. The dream had put him back to a pivotal moment, allowing him to alter the course of history. You know the rest of the story and how that lead to the founding of the fraternity of builders.”\n\nI stared at Madam O, the enormity of her tale sinking in. “You’re saying dreams can change the past?”\n\nShe inclined her head. “Dreams exist outside time. They are not bound by the linear constraints of your waking world. What you dream today may be someone else’s past, or their future becomes your present.”\n\n“But that