k.”	2	241	t	2026-02-27 12:04:35.69225-05	2026-02-27 20:13:53.489482-05	normal	\N
233	59	1	The Honing Deck	Late at night, the narrator writes down details from a dream encounter with Madam O about time and the dreamscape. His son joins him at the kitchen table, and they spend the morning playing Magic: The Gathering. During the game, the son proposes that Magic cards function like tarot—a therapeutic tool for working through real-life problems through symbols and strategy.	The house was silent, the world outside cloaked in the stillness of night. At the kitchen table, I sat hunched over my dream journal, furiously scribbling down every detail from my latest encounter with Madam O. The ideas she had imparted, the fluid nature of time, the dreamscape’s ability to touch past and future simultaneously, threatened to slip away like grains of sand through my fingers.\n\nThe faint rustling of clothes from the hallway pulled me from my thoughts. I glanced up to see my son rubbing his eyes, his lots, and lots of curly brown hair sticking up at odd angles. “You’re still up?” he mumbled, stifling a yawn.\n\n“Just had another visitation. Thought I better write it down before I forget it and have to dream it again some other night,” I admitted, closing the journal. “What are you doing awake?”\n\nHe shrugged. “It’s morning. Figured I’d get a head start on my honing deck. Want to give me a hand?” He shuffled over to the kitchen table, a small stack of Magic cards already in hand.\n\nBy the time dawn’s golden light spilled through the windows, we were locked in a battle of wits, our MTG decks sprawled across the table.\n\n“Where do you think you got your skill from?” I asked as he shuffled his deck with laser focus.\n\n“Practice,” he said flatly.\n\nI smirked. “Genetics.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed. “Prove it.”\n\nChallenge accepted. He stacked his deck around Time Reversal, while I unearthed my old Black Lotus, Channel, Fireball combo.\n\n“You’re going down, old man.” He snapped his deck into place, grinning.\n\n“Keep dreaming.” I smirked. “You’re about to get wrecked by a deck so broken they had to ban half the cards.”\n\nHe groaned. “You know those are illegal.”\n\n“Good thing we’re not playing by tournament rules.” I shuffled with satisfaction, relishing his exasperation. “Let’s do this.”\n\nAs we played, the cards spread across the table like a miniature mosaic of the cosmos. The intricate artwork, the symbols embedded within each card, seemed to pulse with meaning beyond the game. I couldn’t help but notice how they echoed symbols Madam O had introduce to me - time, memory, and the blurred boundaries of reality.\n\n“Dad,” my son said casually, leaning back in his chair, his expression thoughtful. “What if Magic was, like, secretly a way to work through real-life problems?”\n\nI raised an eyebrow, intrigued. “Go on.”\n\nHe gestured to the Time Reversal card. “Think about it. What if the cards weren’t just about winning? Like, maybe they represent stuff in real life, choices, regrets, things we don’t always want to face. And playing the game helps us figure them out without realizing it.”\n\nI considered his words, glancing at the cards on the table. “So, you’re saying Magic is less a game and more... therapy?”\n\n“Yeah,” he said, warming to the idea. “Like the tarot. People use those cards to, I don’t know, look at their life, right? What if Magic is the same thing, but for people too scared, or too cheap, to pay for a real reading?”\n\nI chuckled, leaning back. “That’s a bold take. You’re saying every time someone slaps down a Fireball or a Counterspell, they’re working through their issues?”\n\n“Maybe,” he said, grinning. “Think about it. You’re literally strategizing, making choices, dealing with setbacks. It’s like a sneaky way to practice for real life.”\n\nI held up a card depicting a lone figure standing against a storm. “So, what’s this one about, then? A metaphor for resilience? Facing your fears?”\n\nHe nodded enthusiastically. “Exactly. And maybe when you get down to zero life, it’s a reminder that even when you 