d, laughing. “But now I kind of want to build a deck where every card has a secret meaning. Like, if someone plays against me, they’re telling me their deepest darkest secrets, like a physical version of that dark psychology book on NLP I found in your office.”\n\nI chuckled, shaking my head. “I think you might be onto a new kind of game. Or maybe you’ve just cracked the code behind Magic all along.”	0	199	f	2026-02-27 12:04:55.335255-05	2026-02-27 20:15:01.787245-05	normal	\N
399	100	2	Cross Country and Conspiracy	At cross country practice the next day, Stephen reveals Whitney's BBS message to the group, and they decide to investigate the west ridge during the run. Jamie attempts multiple times to seduce Stephen into abandoning the search, but he remains committed to finding the location. The group discovers an impossible trailer deep in Whickham Park that shouldn't exist, decorated with religious iconography and marked by signs of spiritual torment.	**********\n\nThe Florida heat hits us like a wet slap the moment we step onto the track. It's that special kind of afternoon hell where the asphalt shimmers and the air tastes like melted rubber and teenage desperation. The smell hits me in waves—aged crotch sweat baked into decades old polyester uniforms, the sharp tang of fear-sweat that's different from exercise-sweat, and underneath it all, the earthy rot of the swamp that borders Whickham Park.\n\nCoach Henderson stands at the edge of the track, swaying slightly in a way that has nothing to do with the heat. His clipboard hangs limp in one hand, a thermos that definitely doesn't contain water in the other. His eyes are glassy, focused somewhere beyond us, beyond the track, probably beyond this decade.\n\n"Alright, you little shits," he slurs, not bothering to look at us directly. "Warm-up laps, then some... some knee-raises or whatever. Then trail running. You know the drill."\n\nHe waves vaguely toward the woods behind the school, then shuffles toward his favorite park bench—the one positioned perfectly in the shade where he can pretend to supervise while nursing whatever poison is keeping him upright.\n\nJamie presses against my side, her body vibrating with nervous energy. Her skin is already damp with sweat, but there's something else in the tension of her muscles, the way her red-polished fingernails dig into my arm. She's wearing those characteristic red Converse high-tops that she swears bring her luck, and her red JanSport backpack bounces against her hip as we start moving. The color should trigger alarm bells—Whitney's message specifically said bring nothing red—but when Jamie leans close, her breath warm against my ear, coherent thought becomes a luxury I can't afford.\n\n"You know," she whispers, "we could always just disappear into the woods together. Forget about messages and ghosts and focus on something more... immediate."\n\nHeat crawls up my neck despite the Florida humidity already doing its best to melt me. "Jamie, the message said—"\n\n"Forget the message." Her fingers trace patterns on my chest that make my brain stutter. "I keep dreaming about someone too, you know. But in my dreams, he's telling me to stop looking. To focus on what's real." She presses closer. "To focus on what's right in front of me."\n\nTJ falls into step beside us, his Reagan '84 shirt already darkening with sweat. He fidgets with his calculator watch, the ancient LCD display flickering in the harsh sunlight. "Anyone else think Coach is getting worse? Like, professionally speaking? I'm pretty sure that thermos is just straight vodka at this point."\n\nBehind us, Becca and Patrick run close enough that their arms brush with each stride. They're having one of their wordless conversations—the kind where entire novels pass between glances. Patrick's coolness never wavers, even in this heat, while Becca keeps stealing looks at us with something that might be concern or might be curiosity.\n\nThe printout burns in my pocket like a secret I can't keep much longer.\n\n"We need to go somewhere new on the trails," I say quietly, just loud enough for our group to hear. "Today. Now."\n\n"Go where?" TJ pants, already looking winded despite our glacial pace.\n\nI pull out the crumpled paper, smoothing it against my chest as we jog. "Whitney left us a message. On the BBS. From next week."\n\nThat stops everyone except Patrick, who keeps running for another twenty yards with before realizing he's alone.\n\n"What the fuck does that mean?" TJ whispers, glancing toward Coach Henderson, who's now sprawled on his bench like a passed-out drunk at a bus stop.\n\nJamie's grip on my arm tightens, but not in the good way. "Stephen, we talked about this—"\n\n"Just listen," I say, and read them Whitney's words, my voice barely audible over the sound of our breathing and the distant hum of traffic. When I finish, the silence stretches like a held breath.\n\n"The west ridge," Becca says finally. "Behind the park. There's... nothing back there. It just goes straight into Wickham Road."\n\nPatrick's eyes go distant. “I... there was a trailer. White and broken. But that's impossible. We've never...” He shakes his head violently. “Someone else's memories. They're not mine.”\n\nJamie's red fingernails dig deeper into my arm. "The fucking road is back there, and nothing else." She turns to me, eyes bright with something that might be excitement or might be fear. "But Stephen, we could just…I know a better place—north of the trails, lots of shade, private..." Her voice drops to that whisper that makes my skin prickle. "We could slip away somewhere just the two of us..."\n\nI try to focus on the message, on Whitney's warning about bringing nothing red, but Jamie's thumb traces circles on my wrist and my brain short-circuits.\n\nThe message said—, I try to say but nothing comes out.\n\nJamie leans closer, voice dropping to that whisper that makes my skin prickle. "I picked red on purpose. You noticed, right? The shoes, the nails..." Her voice drops. "I thought maybe if everything matched, you'd finally..."\n\n"Let's get on with it," Patrick starts. "The sooner we run the trails—"\n\n"…the sooner we can get back to more interesting activities," Becca finishes for him with a knowing smile and a giggle that makes Patrick's ears turn red.\n\nJamie sighs dramatically but doesn't let go of my arm. "Fine. We'll go chase your ghost. But when we find nothing—and we will find nothing—you owe me. Remember?"\n\nThe way she says 'owe me' makes my mouth go dry, and once again, Whitney's warning about red slips from my mind like water through my fingers.\n\nWe slip away one by one, then regroup at the edge of Whickham Park where the manicured grass gives way to scrub palmetto and twisted oak. The temperature drops ten degrees in the shade, but the air gets thicker, heavier, like breathing through wet cotton.\n\n"Last chance," Jamie murmurs, pressing against my side as we start down the trail. "We could still turn back. Find somewhere quiet. I promise I'll make it worth your while."\n\nThe trail—if you can call it that—winds deeper into the woods than any official park path should go. Spanish moss hangs like funeral shrouds from the branches, and the ground squelches under our feet with decades of accumulated leaf rot and standing water.\n\n"This feels wrong," Becca mutters, swatting at mosquitoes that seem to materialize from thin air.\n\nIt does feel wrong. The deeper we go, the more the forest closes in around us, until we're jogging single file through a green tunnel that blocks out most of the sun. The air smells like decay and secrets, like things that died and were never properly buried.\n\n"Still time to turn back," Jamie whispers behind me, her red shoes squelching in the mud. "Still time to focus on something real."\n\nThen we smell it—the unmistakable stench of poverty and desperation. Rusted metal, moldy fabric, the sour tang of cheap beer and cheaper cigarettes. It's the smell of giving up, of places where hope goes to die.\n\nThe clearing opens before us like a wound in the earth.\n\nThe trailer sits dead center, listing to one side on cinder blocks and broken dreams. It's the kind of white that was never really white, now stained yellow-brown with years of neglect and Florida weather. The aluminum siding is streaked with rust that looks like dried blood, and half the panels have pulled away from the frame, creating gaps that stare at us like missing teeth.\n\nBut it's the decorations that make my stomach turn.\n\nPlastic crosses hang from every available surface, sun-bleached and cracked. A ceramic Jesus lies shattered on the front steps, his painted eyes staring blindly at the sky. Wind chimes made from bent coat hangers and broken glass tinkle in the dead air, creating a sound like tiny screams.\n\n"Jesus Christ," TJ breathes.\n\n"Don't," Jamie whispers, her red fingernails digging into my arm hard enough to draw blood. "Don't say that here."\n\nThe windows are covered with yellowed newspaper, but I can see gaps where the tape has come loose, revealing darkness beyond that seems to move and shift when I'm not looking directly at it. The front door hangs slightly ajar, held open by what looks like a child's broken doll.\n\nThis is Whitney's childhood—or at least one version of it. The realization hits us like a physical blow, memories flooding back that feel borrowed from someone else's nightmares. This is where she learned that love comes with conditions and God comes with fists. This is the place she ran from, again and again, until her psycho white-trash parents finally made the running permanent by disowning their own child for the crime of being true to herself.\n\nExcept this trailer never existed. We know this. We've been in these woods before, and there was never anything here but trees and shadows and the occasional beer can. This is where Will grew up—the Will that Patrick remembers from his childhood, before he became William fucking Carson. He and his parents moved to Baytree after... after... How do we know this? The memories feel twisted, wrong, like someone else's photographs shuffled into our family album. Suddenly I can't tell what's real anymore.\n\nBut standing here now, breathing the stench of sanctified decay, I know we've been here before. All of us. In dreams, maybe, or in the spaces between memories where the real truth hides.\n\nThe air buzzes against our skin like static electricity, and somewhere in the distance, I swear I can hear the sound of a television tuned to nothing but snow.\n\nJamie presses closer against me, her whole body trembling now, all traces of seduction gone. "We shouldn't be here," she whispers.\n\nBut the door is already calling to us, and Whitney's message burns in my pocket like a promise we're not brave enough to break. I glance at Jamie's red backpack, her red shoes, her red fingernails, and finally understand why Whitney warned us about the color.\n\nToo late now.\n\n**********	1	411	f	2026-02-27 12:17:05.060788-05	2026-02-28 18:46:58.7638-05	normal	\N
431	107	5	The Dimensional Breach	The group attempts to breach dimensions using a reflection sigil in an abandoned gas station, but the ritual goes catastrophically wrong when they use Florida coordinates instead of Nevada. The explosion destroys the last traces of Patrick's humanity, leaving only an empty shell, and forces them to confront the reality of their plan to travel to Nevada.	**********\n\nMarch 28, 1996\n\nThe abandoned gas station squats on the edge of SR518 like a monument to broken promises, its Texaco sign bleached bone-white by decades of unforgiving Florida sun. The place shouldn't exist—according to every map, every rational understanding of geography, we're standing in what should be empty swampland. But here it is, shimmering in the Florida humidity like a mirage made manifest, its rusted pumps standing sentinel over cracked asphalt that drinks moonlight like blood.\n\nJamie drew the reflection sigil, mixing her chalk with three drops of blood from a pin prick on her thumb. The lines pulse with their own bioluminescence now, geometric patterns that hurt to look at directly, as if they exist in more dimensions than human eyes can process. The air around them vibrates like tuning forks struck in sequence, creating harmonics that make my teeth ache.\n\n"Everyone ready?" TJ asks, but his voice carries the hollow bravado of someone whistling past a graveyard. He's changed out of his Reagan shirt into something black and practical, though his hands shake as he checks his watch for the dozenth time.\n\nMorgan nods, her science notebook clutched against her chest like armor. She's documented every detail of tonight's plan in her precise handwriting, complete with backup contingencies and failure protocols. But her face has gone pale in the moonlight, her eyes wide as she stares at the sigil. "The journal never said Florida," she murmurs, more to herself than anyone. "We're improvising—and that’s not how this kind of math likes to work."\n\nAndrew crushes his third cigarette under his heel, the embers scattering like dying stars across the asphalt. "This is insane," he mutters, but he takes his position at the circle's eastern point anyway. "We're actually trying to punch a hole through reality."\n\nPatrick stands at the circle's perimeter with mechanical precision, his posture perfect, his eyes scanning our ritual setup with the detached interest of a security camera. Becca hovers near him, her hand occasionally reaching out to touch his arm, seeking comfort from something that can't offer it anymore.\n\n"ADMIN OVERRIDE: Participate in dimensional breach protocol," Becca commands quietly, desperation creeping into her voice.\n\nPatrick's head tilts with mechanical consideration. "Command parameters unclear. Define 'participate.'"\n\n"Just... just stand there and help us if something goes wrong," she whispers.\n\nJamie kneels at the sigil's center, her hands hovering over lines that seem to writhe like living things. The chalk mixture has begun to glow brighter, casting shadows that move independently of our bodies. "Once I activate this, there's no going back," she warns. "Reality's going to notice what we're doing, and it won't be happy."\n\nShe presses her palms flat against the sigil's central hub. The lines erupt with electric blue light that turns the night into negative film, all wrong colors and inverted shadows. The air grows thick and viscous, like trying to breathe underwater. My ears pop from pressure changes that shouldn't exist in open air.\n\nTJ and Morgan begin the chant from Jennifer's Journal, their voices harmonizing in languages that predate human speech. The words taste like numbers turned into colors when I mouth along, each syllable leaving emotional residue on my tongue. Andrew maintains our perimeter, his flashlight beam cutting through darkness that seems to press back with physical weight.\n\nReality bends around us like heated glass. The gas station flickers between decay and vitality—one moment showing rusted pumps and shattered windows, the next revealing gleaming chrome and neon signs advertising gasoline at 1989 prices. The asphalt beneath our feet ripples like water disturbed by stones.\n\nThrough the dimensional membrane that forms above Jamie's sigil, I see him: myself at eight years old, cross-legged on a carpet that smells of Lysol and broken dreams. He's watching Saturday morning cartoons in a living room where sunlight streams through clean windows, where toys scatter across floors that don't echo with violence, where a mother's love doesn't come disguised as poison.\n\nBut something's wrong. The image flickers, unstable, like a television with bad reception. The boy's face keeps shifting between versions of myself—sometimes unmarked and innocent, sometimes already bearing the haunted expression of someone who's seen too much.\n\n"The anchor's not holding," Morgan shouts over the increasing dimensional static. "The resonance is all wrong!"\n\nPatrick suddenly steps forward, his movements more fluid than they've been in weeks. "Geographic coordinates incorrect," he announces, his voice carrying an odd harmonic that makes the air shimmer. "Nevada location required for temporal stability."\n\n"What?" Becca stares at him in confusion. "Patrick, what are you—"\n\nBut Patrick is changing. His too-perfect posture begins to shift, his mechanical precision giving way to something more organic, more human. For a terrifying moment, I think we might be getting the real Patrick back. Then I see his eyes—they're glowing with the same electric blue as Jamie's sigil.\n\n"NEVADA, INITIAL COORDINATES INCORRECT" he says, and his voice echoes with the weight of cosmic authority. "THE ANCHOR POINT IS IN NEVADA. THIS LOCATION IS INSUFFICIENT."\n\nThe portal above us convulses like a living thing in pain. The eight-year-old boy in the image turns toward us, and now I can see the fear in his eyes—not fear of leaving his safe world, but fear of us. Of what we're dragging into his peaceful reality.\n\n"Shut it down!" Andrew yells, but it's too late.\n\nThe sigil explodes.\n\nThe blast sends us all flying in different directions. I hit the concrete hard enough to taste blood, my vision going white as my skull bounces off the asphalt. Andrew screams as he's thrown against one of the rusted gas pumps, the metal tearing through his jacket and into the flesh beneath. He hits the ground clutching his arm, blood seeping between his fingers.\n\nMorgan and TJ crash into each other, both crying out as they slam into the remains of the station's concrete wall. Morgan's notebook goes flying, its pages scattering like dying birds across the oil-stained ground.\n\nBut Patrick... Patrick stands in the center of the devastation, completely untouched, his eyes still glowing that impossible blue. The light flickers and dies, and he collapses like a marionette with cut strings.\n\n"Patrick!" Becca rushes to him, dropping to her knees beside his motionless form. For a horrible moment, I think he's dead—really dead this time. Then his eyes flutter open, but instead of recognition or confusion, there's nothing. Just blank blue light that flickers once before going dark.\n\n"Initializing," Patrick says in a voice devoid of any human inflection. "System reboot in progress. Please stand by."\n\nBecca's face crumples as she watches the mechanical precision return to his movements. He sits up with robotic efficiency, his head turning to scan the destruction around us with the detached interest of a security camera cataloging damage.\n\n"Initialization complete," he announces. "Please input operational parameters."\n\n"Oh God," Becca whispers, her hands hovering over his face like she's afraid to touch him. "You're gone again, aren't you?"\n\nPatrick's head tilts with mechanical curiosity. "Previous identity matrix corrupted during dimensional breach event. Awaiting new command structure."\n\nA cold dread, more profound than any physical pain, settled in my gut. He was gone. Again. And this time, he wasn't even a convincing imitation of himself, just an empty shell waiting for someone to program it. The guilt that had fueled Becca's earlier punches now coiled around my own throat, choking me with the reality of what I'd broken.\n\nJamie helps me to my feet, both of us battered but functional. "The location was wrong," she says, wiping blood from a cut on her cheek. "We need to be in Nevada. The actual Nevada, not some Florida approximation."\n\nAndrew tries to stand and immediately goes pale, clutching his injured arm. "I'm not going anywhere," he says through gritted teeth. "Think I might have torn something important."\n\nBecca stares at Patrick for a long moment, watching him sit perfectly still while awaiting instructions. When she finally speaks, her voice carries a mixture of heartbreak and resigned acceptance.\n\n"I'm staying with him," she announces, her hand resting on Patrick's shoulder. "And with Andrew. Someone needs to take care of them both."\n\n"Becca—" TJ starts, but she cuts him off.\n\n"He's fucking gone, okay?" Her voice cracks with raw grief, each word bleeding out like an open wound. "I know that. I'm not stupid. The Patrick I loved—the one who sang stupid math songs in Mr. Frahm's voice, who made me laugh until my sides hurt, who held me when I cried about my parents—he's dead. He died in that basement trying to save Whitney, and what came back is just... lines of code wearing his face."\n\nShe wipes her nose with the back of her hand, leaving a streak of blood and tears across her cheek. "I know how fucked up this sounds. I know what it makes me. But when everything else about him is gone—his laugh, his terrible jokes, the way he used to scrunch up his nose when he was thinking—at least I can still make him want me. Even if it's just programming responding to commands."\n\nHer face flushes crimson as she continues, "Even the... overly enthusiastic commands. The ones where I can tell him to go again and again until I'm too exhausted to remember how much this hurts."\n\nThe raw honesty in her voice makes everyone look away except Patrick, who observes her confession with the clinical detachment of a security camera recording evidence. "Clarification requested: are current operational parameters focused on sexual functionality?"\n\n"Among other things," Becca mutters. "We'll figure it out as we go."\n\nTJ and Morgan help each other to their feet, both moving gingerly but with a fury that burns brighter than their injuries. Morgan's left arm hangs at an odd angle, probably dislocated, while TJ has a gash across his forehead that's bleeding freely.\n\n"Fuck this thing," TJ snarls, wiping blood from his eyes. "Whatever's doing this to us, whatever's playing with our lives—I want to burn it all down."\n\nMorgan nods grimly, cradling her injured arm. "The math was right. The theory was sound. We just had the wrong coordinates." She glares at the scattered remains of our ritual like a scientist whose experiment has been sabotaged. "But now we know. Nevada. The real Nevada."\n\nAs we gather the scattered pages of Jennifer's Journal, one entry catches my eye—written in fresh ink that still gleams wet in the moonlight: "Florida was never the beginning. It was just where you learned to remember. The true anchor waits where it always has—in the desert where gods go to die."\n\nThe abandoned gas station begins to flicker like a dying bulb, its edges growing transparent. Soon there will be nothing here but empty swampland and the memory of our failure.\n\nAs we pile into TJ's car—battered, bloodied, but still breathing—the reality of what we're planning starts to sink in. TJ's pristine Lexus purrs like a contented cat even after our dimensional disaster, its leather seats and climate control a stark contrast to the supernatural chaos we've just endured.\n\n"We need time," Morgan says, cradling her dislocated arm as she settles into the passenger seat. "Time to plan, to figure out exactly where in Nevada we need to go. And time to heal."\n\nTJ nods grimly, wiping blood from the gash on his forehead with a tissue from the car's premium dispenser. "This thing could make it to Nevada and back without breaking a sweat, but my parents will literally murder me if I take it out of state. They've got LoJack on it and everything."\n\n"Bus tickets," I say, watching the gas station continue its impossible burn in the rearview mirror. "Greyhound runs cross-country routes. We can take the I-10 corridor through Texas and New Mexico."\n\nJamie's hand finds mine in the darkness between streetlights. "April 19th," she says quietly. "That gives us three weeks to prepare, to research the exact location, to say our goodbyes."\n\n"April 19th, 1996," Patrick repeats as Becca snuggles next to him, making it official with the precision she brings to everything. "Departure date confirmed."	4	443	t	2026-02-27 12:19:38.208317-05	2026-02-28 19:19:21.715603-05	normal	\N
