it.”\n\nMorgan took a seat, her expression thoughtful. “That reminds me of a project I worked on years ago, experimenting with liquid agents to enhance reflective properties of telescope lenses. It was a long shot, but it opened doors we hadn’t even considered.”\n\nHer words ignited a spark in the room. TJ sat up straighter. “What if we applied that principle here? Combining unconventional materials with innovative techniques might give us the breakthrough we need.”\n\n“Screw it. We’ve been playing it too safe. Maybe it’s time to light the whole damn thing on fire and see what doesn’t burn.” Morgan’s eyes lit up as she said this.\n\nAndrew leaned back, smirking. “As long as we’re not pouring beer into a multi-trillion-dollar prototype, I’m in.”\n\n“Hey, a little beer never hurt any space probe. It did help the staff get through those long, lonely mission monitoring nights.” Morgan snorted.\n\nTJ raised an eyebrow. “Pretty sure NASA would disagree. And I’d rather not be the next  ‘SpaceX Exploding Rocket Disaster, Again’ headline. We don’t need any S Corp publicity drawing attention to us.”\n\n“Look, just because ‘fuck it, we ball’ is a valid strategy in some industries doesn’t mean it works here. We’re not making a YouTube prank video; we’re building humanity’s goddamn bridge to the stars. If this thing fails, we don’t just replicate from zero. We kill people.” I exhaled, rubbing my temples.\n\nTJ nodded, tapping his fingers on the table. “Fine. But if we’re breaking new ground, we have to think about scalability. We can’t build some sci-fi monolith. Even though we can replicate everything, if it’s going to cost a small country's GDP just to transport shit time, we replicate it...”\n\nMorgan leaned in. “Agreed. Calculated risks, not Jackass ones.”\n\n“Ah yes, the fine line between genius and well, that’s how we lost Wyoming.” Andrew added.\n\nThe room crackled with energy, a mix of reckless ambition and just enough common sense to keep us from blowing ourselves up. 