248	62	4	Amateur Investigation Begins	During lunch and over the following days, Aditi and her friends dive deep into researching the sleepwalking phenomenon. They discover patterns, legends of underground rivers, and other similar incidents online, while grappling with the question of why the wider world dismisses or ignores these events.	At lunchtime everyone continued to buzz around Aditi and her friends, but their world was confined to the small huddle around her phone. The video played again, the sleepwalkers moving in eerie unison, their arms outstretched like human compasses. The haunting silence of the crowd in the clip contrasted sharply with the excited whispers now filling the group.\n\n“This is unreal, Aditi!” Meera exclaimed, her voice trembling with excitement. “How did you not notice you were part of this?”\n\nAditi shrugged, “I didn’t even realize it happened until I saw the video. It’s like my mind erased the whole thing.”\n\nRaj, leaned closer to scrutinize the screen. “It’s creepy, but are we sure this isn’t just some weird mass sleepwalking? People have been doing strange things in their sleep forever.”\n\nNeha shook her head, her eyes alight with curiosity. “This isn’t just sleepwalking, Raj. Look at them—they’re all pointing the same way. That’s not random. It’s... coordinated.”\n\nAditi nodded. “Exactly. And this isn’t the first time we’ve heard about something like this. There’s got to be a pattern.”\n\nRaj scoffed lightly. “A pattern? You sound like one of those conspiracy theory YouTubers.”\n\n“Maybe she’s onto something,” Meera interjected, nudging him playfully. “You have to admit, this is weird. We should dig deeper.”\n\nOver the next few days, the group transformed into amateur detectives, devouring every scrap of information they could find on mass sleepwalking events. They poured over online forums, dug through obscure news articles, and even consulted old library books. Each clue they uncovered added a layer to the mystery.\n\n“Check this out,” Meera said one evening, waving her laptop screen in front of the group. “There’s this legend about an underground river running beneath the city. Some people say it has mystical properties that can mess with your dreams.”\n\n“Like some kind of ancient energy source?” Neha asked, leaning in.\n\n“Or radiation,” Raj muttered, earning a glare from Meera.\n\nAditi’s fingers tapped rhythmically on her keyboard. “If this river exists, maybe it’s connected to the sleepwalking. People point in the same direction, maybe it’s leading us to something.”\n\nRaj leaned back in his chair. “Or it’s just a collective psychosis. People see a video, and their brains fill in the blanks.”\n\n“Then why were we all doing it before anyone posted anything?” Aditi shot back. “You saw my video, it was happening in real-time, not because someone read about it.”\n\nSilence filled the room. Even Raj didn’t have an answer for that.\n\nOther videos of sleepwalking crowds began to surface online, like the one shot from a Florida trailer park, although they were mostly dismissed as pranks or urban legends.\n\n“It’s like people don’t want to see what’s right in front of them,” Aditi said one afternoon, scrolling through the comment section of her post. “They’re just brushing it off.”\n\n“It’s easier to ignore it than to face something they don’t understand,” Meera replied.\n\nNeha sighed. “Or maybe something’s making them ignore it. What if this isn’t just about sleepwalking? What if there’s something keeping people from connecting the dots?”\n\nThe group exchanged uneasy glances. It was a chilling thought, but it also made too much sense.\n\nThat night, Aditi lay awake, the faint glow of her phone screen illuminating her face. She thought about the underground river, the legends, and the faint hum of energy she had felt that night. Something connected these pieces, a thread she couldn’t quite put her finger on it, but knew was there.	3	257	f	2026-02-27 12:05:37.735014-05	2026-02-27 20:27:23.550079-05	normal	\N
249	63	1	The Space Elevator Crisis	At the Antarctic MOM facility, the protagonist grapples with the Space Elevator project's mounting challenges. Morgan briefs him on material failures and design obstacles, but their brainstorming session with the team generates breakthrough ideas for hybrid composites and self-healing materials. A cryptic exchange hints at potential sabotage.	S Corp’s audacious Space Elevator project loomed in my thoughts, its challenges mirrored in Morgan’s anxious updates from the Antarctic facility. Amid the biting cold and stark desolation of MOM, the blueprint for humanity’s bridge to the cosmos was being meticulously crafted, yet cracks in the plan started to threaten its foundation.\n\nEach passing day intensified my focus on the project, and with it, the magnitude of the task crystallized. The Antarctic team’s progress was awe-inspiring, yet their breakthroughs in carbon nanotube composites and magnetic propulsion systems remained hamstrung, their potential teetering on the edge of feasibility. Success required not just innovation but resilience, collaboration, and an unyielding commitment to pushing the boundaries of possibility.\n\nI set aside the dream journals and the questions of what might lie beyond the veil of the subconscious. There was work to be done in the waking world, work that demanded every ounce of my energy and intellect. If the dreamscape held answers, they would have to wait. For now, the dream of humanity’s ascent into the stars was enough to propel me forward.\n\nThe facility buzzed with an undercurrent of urgency as I returned, greeted by Morgan with her ever-intense energy. She wasted no time launching into a detailed update, her voice a mix of frustration and defiance.\n\n“The materials just aren’t holding up,” she began, her tone edged with exasperation. “We’ve thrown everything we’ve got at this, but nothing is cutting it. Space isn’t just tough—it’s brutal.”\n\nHer determination was evident, though the challenges she described painted a daunting picture. “Every time we solve one problem, another shows up. It’s like the universe doesn’t want us to win,” she said with a wry smile. “But we don’t quit.”\n\nI nodded, scanning the reports she handed me. The data told a stark story: innovative composites buckling under strain, theoretical calculations falling short when applied, and new concepts riddled with unforeseen flaws. This wasn’t just a struggle, it was an invitation to innovate.\n\n“We’ll have to outsmart the universe,” I said, placing the reports on a nearby table. “We’ve got the best minds on this. Let’s make the impossible possible.”\n\nMorgan’s sharp gaze softened, and a grin tugged at her lips. “You and your fucking slogans. I swear you’re like a walking motivational poster some days.”\n\nWe called the team together in one of teh sprawling lab, its walls lined with diagrams, equations, and prototypes. Morgan commanded the room like a general rallying her troops. “We’re here to build a bridge to the stars,” she began, her voice steady but forceful. “I don’t care you’re sleep deprived. Go get yourself some amphetamines or something. I need every one of you to push past what you think is possible.”\n\nA young researcher, Jonathan, raised his hand, his expression brimming with enthusiasm. “What if we explored hybrid composites? We could layer materials with different properties to create something that adapts under stress.”\n\nMorgan’s eyes lit up. “That’s what I’m talking about, Jonathan. Start sketching that out and get the materials team involved. Let’s see where it takes us.”\n\nAnother hand shot up. “What if we design a self-healing material? Something that can repair micro-fractures before they propagate.”\n\n“That’s brilliant,” I interjected. “Integrate microcrystals into that concept. Build a prototype.”\n\nThe room ignited with energy, the team bouncing ideas off one another, each concept building on the last. Whiteboards filled with frantic equations, and every corner of the lab hummed with focused intensity.\n\n“Your idea is solid,” Morgan told one engineer, her hand on his shoulder. “Push it further. Either make it explode, implode, or certify it.”\n\nWith every breakthrough came setbacks, but the failures only sharpened our resolve. When a kilometer long prototype cable snapped during a stress test, scattering shards across the chamber, Morgan clapped her hands. “Good! Now we know what doesn’t work. Let’s figure out why.”\n\nMorgan leaned over a model of the Space Elevator’s anchor platform and glared at me. “What the fuck are we missing? The numbers should be working. It’s like there is something going on, like a worm is in there eating our materials from the inside. I just don’t get it.”\n\n“Do the letters I, A and M mean anything to you?“ I asked.\n\n“Only of my sorority. Why?” She responded.\n\n“No reason. Get Gil to double check the cybersecurity routines.” I said.	0	258	f	2026-02-27 12:05:57.845267-05	2026-02-27 20:28:23.734349-05	normal	\N
253	63	5	The Pointers Campaign	With the Europa story fading, S Corp pivots to exploit the global Pointers phenomenon—mysterious sleepwalkers pointing toward construction sites. The team develops a viral marketing strategy to capitalize on public fascination and use it as cover for their Space Elevator construction.	**********\n\nLucky for us, Europa quickly fell from the news cycle. In it’s place, The Pointers, the enigmatic phenomenon of sleepwalking individuals pointing toward unseen destinations, had captured global curiosity. TJ, ever the strategist, leaned forward at the conference table, his voice cutting through the din.\n\n“We should to seize this moment,” he declared, his conviction undeniable. “The world is fascinated by the Pointers. We can use them to keep focus on other things will we finish up everything behind the scenes.”\n\nBecca, her laptop glowing with real-time analytics, nodded. “Public interest is spiking, but there’s no direction. People are intrigued, but they’re not informed.”\n\n“Then we make something up” Andrew chimed in, his tone brimming with excitement. “They’ve been basically running an advertising campaign for us without even realizing it. Each area these flash mobs are pointing at are directly towards our construction sites. This is our chance to exploit the Pointers to cover the Europe story. Folks are already talking about aliens and strange forces. We just give them a little nudge.”\n\nJames, raised a hand. “But what even the fuck are these things? Why are folks doing this?”\n\nBecca pulled up her notes. “I’ve already reached out to journalists and influencers who are intrigued by the phenomenon. Most psychologists they’ve talked to are linking it to current global economic anxiety. Things are going south because of the right-wing shift in national governments which has led to the collapsing markets. They see these gigantic projects going on at transportations, combined with all our secrecy behind them. It’s a bit of mass hysteria that’s become self-perpetuating even though they don’t realize it.”\n\n“So if we want to tap into this, what? Leverage social media,” Andrew added. “Short-form videos, TikTok dances, anything that sparks curiosity and spreads like wildfire?”\n\nAs the team brainstormed, the room filled with an infectious energy. Ideas flew fast, weaving together a strategy that combined innovative science, compelling storytelling, and a touch of mystery. This wasn’t just a campaign; it was an opportunity to capture the world’s imagination and position S Corp as the vanguard of a new era.\n\nMorgan leaned back, her expression thoughtful. “This is bigger than the Pointers,” she said. “This is about inspiring people. Showing them that the universe is more interconnected than they ever imagined.”\n\nThe room fell silent for a moment, her words settling over us like a challenge. Then TJ clapped his hands together. “Let’s do it,” he said. “Let’s turn the Pointers from a curiosity into a movement.”\n\n“Fuck yeah!” I shouted. “Finally yall are seeing these fucking things and have stopped calling me crazy. What I really want to know, however, is why the hell did I start seeing these fucking things months ago?”	4	262	t	2026-02-27 12:05:57.845267-05	2026-02-27 20:31:46.265936-05	normal	\N
254	63	6	The Rise of Elysium	Under the cover of the Pointers distraction, S Corp accelerates construction on the Space Elevator towers, using nanite technology to make the massive cables invisible. The protagonist and Morgan witness the towering structures rising skyward as Elysium nears completion.	**********\n\nThe campaign surged with a force that captured the world’s imagination. The Pointers dominated every news cycle, every social media feed, every whispered conversation in coffee shops and boardrooms. Speculation ran rampant. was it a natural phenomenon, a cosmic message, or something else entirely? The frenzy became the perfect veil for our true ambitions.\n\nUnder the cover of this orchestrated global distraction, we accelerated the final construction on the terrestrial components of the space elevators. The work was monumental, but with the precision of our advanced nanites and the expertise gained from off-world projects, progress was breathtaking. Every cable secured, every module assembled, was a step closer. With the nanites help, we were able to coat the massive cables with light bending materials, making them invisible to anyone in the area. Despite the enormity of the project, they would remain under the last cloak of secrecy until we were ready.\n\nSlowly, but steadily, the elevators began to rise from the ground, towering symbols of ingenuity and ambition. Their sleek, gleaming structures stretched skyward, defying gravity and limits alike. These weren’t just marvels of engineering; they were monuments to possibility, beacons lighting the path to a future where Earth and the cosmos were seamlessly connected.\n\nMorgan stood beside me at the base of one of the towering structures, the late afternoon sun casting a golden glow over the final assembly site. “Do you think they understand what this means?” she asked, her voice soft but charged with awe.\n\n“Not yet,” I replied, gazing up at the elevator as its summit disappeared into the heavens. “Elysium is going to change everything.”	5	263	t	2026-02-27 12:05:57.845267-05	2026-02-27 20:32:33.456793-05	normal	\N
