485	120	4	Reunion at Jamie's Marginalia	Inside the bookshop, Stephen is greeted warmly by old high school friends Becca and James, who own the shop. They discuss unemployment, broken systems, and the nature of consensus reality, while Stephen becomes increasingly unsettled by phantom memories and the missing presence of a girl from their past.	**********\n\nThe smell hit first.\n\nOld paper. That specific must of books that have lived long enough to develop their own ecology. Coffee—dark roast, burnt edges. Something floral underneath. Not quite vanilla. Not quite peaches. But close enough to make my chest pound.\n\nThe shop was small. Intimate. Exposed brick walls lined with floor-to-ceiling shelves. Mismatched armchairs clustered in corners. A few small tables scattered near the front. String lights draped overhead, warm and amber.\n\nMaybe a dozen people inside. Wine glasses. Cheese plates. Low murmur of conversation creating a cocoon of white noise.\n\nI stood in the doorway, scanning.\n\nLooking for... what?\n\n"Bob!"\n\nI turned.\n\nBecca.\n\nShe was crossing the shop toward me, wine glass in one hand, smile wide and genuine. Older. Laugh lines around her eyes. Grey streaks through her hair that she wasn't hiding. Still pretty. Still herself.\n\n"Holy shit, you actually came." She pulled me into a hug. I went stiff, then remembered how. Arms around her shoulders. Pat on the back. Human contact from someone who wasn't extracting housework or demanding job applications.\n\nI almost cried.\n\n"Yeah," I managed. "Traffic wasn't bad."\n\nShe pulled back, studying my face. Her smile flickered. Just for a second. She saw something there. The hollowness. The toaster. The loops.\n\nBut she didn't push. Just squeezed my arm.\n\n"Come on. James is gonna lose his mind. He was just talking about you last week."\n\nShe pulled me deeper into the shop. Past the small crowd. Into the racks where a man stood examining a shelf, hands clasped behind his back like he was at a museum.\n\n"James! Look who showed up!"\n\nHe turned.\n\nBald. Wire-rimmed glasses. Navy posture—shoulders back, spine straight. Dressed like a philosophy professor. Cardigan over button-up. Jeans that actually fit.\n\nHis face lit up.\n\n"No fucking way. Stephen?" He crossed to me, hand extended. Firm handshake. The kind that communicated competence and presence. "Man, how long has it been?"\n\n"Since graduation," I said. "Yours. 1999."\n\n"Jesus. Twenty-six years." He shook his head. "You look... exactly like I expected."\n\n"Unemployed and dead inside?"\n\nHe laughed. Becca winced.\n\n"I was gonna say distinguished. But sure, we’ll go with your version."\n\nBecca handed me a wine glass—red, full, probably something good. I meant to just sip, but the nerves got there first. I drained the whole thing in one go, the tannins clawing at the back of my throat.\n\nBecca blinked, eyebrows raised. James stifled a grin.\n\n"Wow," she said. "You must be really thirsty."\n\n"Guess so," I muttered, setting the glass down a little too hard.\n\nStill smiling, she poured another, the sound of it oddly loud between us.\n\nJames tilted his head, smirk tugging at his mouth.\n\n"Careful," he said. "That’s how it starts. First you thirst for wine, then for knowledge. Next thing you know, you’re writing a dissertation nobody reads."\n\nBecca laughed, handing me the refilled glass. The tension broke—at least enough for me to breathe again.\n\n"So," James said, gesturing around. "What do you think? Five years and we're still here. Not bankrupt yet."\n\nI looked around. Really looked this time. The shop was... warm. Lived-in. Books organized by some chaotic system only James and Becca understood. A section labeled "Esoteric Bullshit" next to "Cozy Mysteries." Philosophy texts shelved beside graphic novels.\n\n"It's great," I said. And meant it. "The name though—Jamie’s Marginalia. That’s... specific."\n\nBecca and James exchanged a glance. Something passed between them—quick, almost rehearsed.\n\n"It’s an homage," Becca said. "To the notes people leave in books. The scribbles in margins. Conversations across time between reader and text and some future explorer that picks it up decades later."\n\n"And," James added, deadpan, "it sounds pretentious as hell, which really helps in this neighborhood. You wouldn’t believe how much artisanal irony sells."\n\nBecca swatted his arm. "Ignore him."\n\nThen, softer, she turned back to me. "Actually, it was his grandmother’s name. Jamie. She’s the reason he’s James."\n\nI looked at him. "Seriously?"\n\nHe shrugged, half-smile. "She taught me to read in the margins. Said that’s where the real stories hide."\n\nBecca nodded. "So when we opened the shop, it just… fit."\n\nI took another sip of wine. The tannins bloomed across my tongue, warm and full-bodied. "Yeah," I said quietly. "It does."\n\n"How about you?" Becca asked. "What are you up to these days? Still building nuclear bombs and writing about cosmic horrors?"\n\nAnd there it was. The question I'd been dreading.\n\nI could lie. Say I was freelancing. Consulting. Taking time to focus on personal projects.\n\nBut I was so tired of lying.\n\n"Unemployed," I said. Flat. "Two years now."\n\nThe silence that followed wasn't uncomfortable. It was... understanding.\n\n"Shit," James said quietly.\n\n"Yeah." I stared into my wine glass. "Twenty years of experience. Software engineering leadership. MBA. PhD in IO Psychology. None of it matters. I'm either overqualified or underqualified or they're going in a different direction or pausing hiring initiatives or restructuring or trying to find someone just as experienced but willing to work minimum wage."\n\n"The job market's fucked," Becca said. Not platitude. Statement of fact.\n\n"Beyond fucked," James added. "We know several dozen people going through the same thing. Shit. It’s like half our friends list is hurting. Smart people. Experienced. Just... stuck. Not even McDonalds has enough room for everyone."\n\nI looked up at him. "Really?"\n\n"Really. One guy we know—director-level, fifteen years at Google—got laid off and spent eighteen months applying before he gave up and started driving for Uber. Another friend, PhD in data science, can't get past the automated screening. It's not you, man. The whole system's broken."\n\nSomething in my chest loosened. Just slightly.\n\nValidation. Actual human validation from people who weren't my wife telling me I wasn't trying hard enough.\n\n"I keep thinking..." I started, then stopped. Shook my head. "Never mind."\n\n"What?" Becca leaned in. Genuinely curious.\n\n"I keep thinking I'm doing something wrong. Like there's some trick I'm missing. Some magic phrase in the cover letter. Some networking strategy. And if I just figure it out, it'll all click."\n\n"There's no trick," James said. "The system's running on busted code. Consensus reality is breaking down."\n\nI froze.\n\nThose words.\n\nConsensus reality.\n\nThey landed wrong. Too precise. Too heavy.\n\nJames must have seen my reaction. He raised an eyebrow.\n\n"My dissertation," he explained. "Rhetorical Enforcement of Consensus Reality. Basically, how shared belief systems maintain social structures. When consensus breaks down—when people stop believing in the same rules—the whole thing collapses."\n\n"And that's... what's happening?" I asked carefully.\n\n"Everywhere." He gestured vaguely. "Job markets. Politics. Media. Everyone's operating in their own reality now. No shared framework. It's chaos wearing a suit."\n\nBecca touched his arm. Gentle. "James gets worked up about this stuff."\n\n"Sorry." He laughed, self-deprecating. "Hazard of the profession. Can't shut off the academic brain."\n\nBut I couldn't let it go.\n\n"What happens when consensus breaks down completely?" I asked.\n\nJames met my eyes. His expression shifted. Something serious beneath the casual academic veneer.\n\n"Then you find out what's underneath," he said quietly. "What was always there. Holding the illusion together."\n\nThe air in the shop felt suddenly thinner.\n\nBecca cleared her throat. "More wine?"\n\n"God, yes," I said.\n\nShe refilled my glass. Then hers. The moment passed. Conversation shifted to safer topics. High school stories. Remember-whens.\n\nJames brought up the fire drill incident. Him falling asleep standing up. Getting carried out by the janitor while everyone watched.\n\n"I was so sleep-deprived," he said. "I was trying to take a full AP course load despite barely making it in honors classes."\n\n"You wore that 'Who is John Galt?' shirt every other day," I reminded him.\n\n"I burned that shirt," Becca said. Deadpan. "Literally. In a bonfire."\n\n"It was a statement!" James protested.\n\n"It was insufferable," she countered.\n\nWe laughed. Real laughter. The kind that aches in your ribs because you haven't done it in so long.\n\nI'd forgotten what this felt like. Friendship. Ease. People who remembered who you were before the loops started grinding you down.\n\nJames topped off his wine again. "Seriously though. It's good to see you. We should do this more often. You play any Magic still? We've got a group that meets Tuesdays.”\n\n"I haven't played in years."\n\n"Doesn't matter. Half of them are trash anyway. You'd fit right in."\n\nI smiled. "Maybe."\n\n"Or DnD. We're starting a new campaign next month. Could use a—"\n\nHe stopped mid-sentence. Looking past me toward the door.\n\nI turned.\n\nA couple had just walked in. Late thirties or early forties. Dressed like they'd wandered out of an occult bookshop's Instagram. All black. Silver jewelry. Pentagram necklaces.\n\nThey moved through the shop with the ease of regulars. Waved at Becca. She waved back.\n\nWhen I turned around, James was staring at me. Expression unreadable.\n\n"What?" I asked.\n\n"Nothing," he said. Too quickly. "Just thought you might know them. Andrew and Morgan. They come in a lot."\n\nThe names meant nothing.\n\n"Should I?"\n\n"No." He shook his head. "No, forget it. Just... déjà vu or something."\n\nBut the moment had shifted again. That thin membrane feeling. Like the shop was breathing different.\n\nBecca was looking at me now too. Studying my face the way she had when I first walked in.\n\n"You okay?" she asked. "You look a little pale."\n\n"Fine," I lied. "Just... it's been a long morning."\n\n"Do you want to sit? We've got chairs in back. Quieter."\n\nI nodded. Grateful.\n\nBut as I followed her toward the rear of the shop, I caught it again.\n\nThat smell.\n\nVanilla and peaches.\n\nFaint. Underneath the coffee and old books. Almost imperceptible.\n\nBut there.\n\nI stopped walking.\n\nBecca turned back. "Stephen?"\n\n"Sorry." I forced my feet to move. "Just... I thought I smelled something."\n\n"Probably the jasmine candles. James is obsessed with them."\n\nJasmine. Not vanilla. Not peaches.\n\nJust jasmine.\n\nI followed her to the back corner where a pair of worn armchairs sat beside a small table. Quieter here. The party sounds muted by distance and shelves.\n\nI sank into the chair. Let the wine glass rest on my knee.\n\n"Better?" Becca asked.\n\n"Yeah. Thanks."\n\nShe sat across from me. Sipped her wine. We existed in comfortable silence for a moment.\n\nThen she said, "High school feels like a different lifetime, doesn't it?"\n\n"Yeah."\n\n"I barely remember most of it. Just... flashes. You know? Big moments. But the day-to-day stuff..." She trailed off. Shook her head. "Gone."\n\n"AP Bio," I said. Don't know why. The words just came out.\n\nHer face brightened. "Oh god. Ms. Rodriguez. No wait. Mrs.... shit. What was her name?"\n\n"Andersen," I said. The name surfaced from nowhere. Certain of my uncertainty.\n\n"Right! Andersen. With the—" She gestured vaguely. "The lab with the frogs. That formaldehyde smell. I thought I'd never get it out of my hair."\n\nI could see it. Suddenly. Vividly.\n\nBlack-topped lab tables. Etched with decades of graffiti. Crude drawings. Initials in hearts. Formaldehyde clinging to everything.\n\nAnd something else.\n\nVanilla. Peaches. So strong it overwhelmed the chemicals.\n\nI saw Becca. Two rows ahead. Her ponytail swaying as she bent over her notebooks.\n\nAnd behind her...\n\nA girl.\n\nDark hair catching the fluorescent light. Back of her neck where a few strands had escaped. Curve of her shoulder. The way she chewed pen caps when she was thinking.\n\nHer presence like gravity.\n\nI couldn't see her face. Burned-out photograph. Missing data.\n\nBut I knew she was there.\n\nI knew.\n\n"Who sat behind you?" I asked. Voice barely above whisper.\n\nBecca blinked. "What?"\n\n"In AP Bio. Who sat behind you?"\n\nShe frowned. Confused. "No one. I mean... you did. Way in the back. Why?"\n\n"Someone sat behind you," I insisted. "Between you and me. A girl."\n\nBecca's expression did something strange. Her eyes unfocused. Pupils dilating slightly. Like she was trying to access a file that wasn't there.\n\n"I... don't think so?" It came out like a question. Uncertain. "I mean, there were a lot of kids in that class. Maybe? God, my memory's shot. High school was forever ago."\n\nShe laughed. Too bright. Forced.\n\nThen shook her head like clearing static.\n\n"Why do you ask?"\n\nI couldn't explain. How do you tell someone you remember a person who shouldn't exist? How do you describe yearning for a face you can't see?\n\n"Just... curious," I said.\n\nBut I wasn't curious. I was drowning.\n\nBecause someone should have been there.\n\nAnd the fact that I couldn't remember who—couldn't even picture her face—was agony.\n\nThe knowing without remembering.\n\nThe worst kind of loss.\n\nJames popped over. "Hey, sorry to interrupt. Stephen, you want to see the rare book section? We've got some wild stuff back there. Good for headaches."\n\nHe was giving me an out. I could see it in his eyes. He'd noticed my distress.\n\n"Yeah," I said. Standing too quickly. Wine sloshing in the glass. "That'd be great."\n\nBecca stayed in her chair. Watching me with that same uncertain expression. Like I'd asked her to solve an equation with missing variables.\n\nI followed James deeper into the shop.\n\nAway from the party. Away from her confusion.\n\nToward the back where the shelves grew older and the light dimmed.\n\nWhere the smell of vanilla and peaches waited.	3	497	f	2026-02-27 12:28:00.912937-05	2026-02-28 22:40:43.101452-05	normal	\N
