341	84	1	The Sealed Cosmos	As the war reaches a desperate crescendo, the cosmic entities seal the dream realms to contain the I AM, inadvertently cutting humanity off from salvation. The narrator grasps a final grimoire and discovers a horrifying truth: the I AM is an eternal devourer, and they themselves unleashed it by claiming the Eternal Engine.	The war raged with renewed intensity, driven by urgency, desperation, and purpose. We had pushed technology to the brink, exhausted every resource, and hurled ourselves into the inferno of battle. And still, it was not enough.\n\nLady I, Lady A, and Madam O. All the other cosmic entities we knew and would never understand. The ancient stewards of balance, had intervened, not to save us but to contain the chaos. They wove a second prison, an impervious seal that extended beyond the physical into the intangible realms of dreams.\n\nEtheris, their celestial prison to contain I AM, expanded into a fortress of isolation. Layers and layers were added, severing humanity’s connection to the ethereal wisdom of the cosmos, to the Threshold, and anything else that had been infected by the demiurge. This barrier, meant to shield reality from the I AM and his Red Hat acolytes, had inadvertently cut us off from the only knowledge that could have saved us.\n\nThe once-vivid dreamscapes, realms alive with impossible geometry and endless knowledge, had become silent tombs. Their doors were sealed, their pathways barred, their songs reduced to a faint, dying hum. What had once vibrated with purpose and clarity now pulsed like the dying breath of a forgotten god.\n\nEvery attempt to reconnect left me feeling smaller, as if the universe itself had turned its back on us, deeming our struggles unworthy of intervention. The sealing of the dream realms was more than a turning point in the Eternal War—it was the moment we lost faith in divine aid. The cosmic guardians had spoken: the destiny of the universe—the fate of the battle against the demiurge—was now solely in mortal hands.\n\nIn desperation, I reached for one last lifeline—a dream plane grimoire, forged in secret with Lady I. It was a failsafe, a final tether to the wisdom that had once flowed freely. The moment of connection was fleeting, but the revelation that followed changed everything.\n\nI AM was not merely a usurper, not simply a tyrant. He was something older, eternal. Something worse. A cosmic entity of boundless malevolence, enslaving humanity under the guise of a savior. His promises of heaven were grotesque deceptions, a fabricated afterlife designed as a trap, leading the faithful into eternal suffering while he fed upon their souls. His paradise was not salvation—it was an endless, inescapable feast where he was the only one who would ever be satisfied.\n\nFor an eternity, the other cosmic forces had fought him and his infection, they tried to contain him, believing they had ended his reign of terror by sealing him away within Etheris. But they had only delayed the inevitable. They had assumed that time would wither him, that isolation would weaken his grasp. Instead, he had waited, his malevolence festering in the darkness, growing stronger with each passing cycle.\n\nAnd now, that single, imperceptible fracture in the fabric of existence had become his foothold—a sliver of opportunity he had waited eons to exploit. A crack through which his corruption could seep, spreading like a malignant infection. A chance to consume everything.\n\nAnd I was the one who had given it to him.\n\nThe Eternal Engine, the artifact I had torn from the Threshold, had not just been the key—it had been the wound. The very breach that had set this monstrosity free.\n\nAnd in that moment of absolute clarity, I saw him for what he truly was. Not a ruler, not a fallen god, not even an adversary. He was something ancient, something infinite. A devourer of souls, a black hole wrapped in the illusion of divinity. His promises were not gifts; they were bait, designed to lure the unsuspecting into eternal torment, their consciousnesses stripped, their essence absorbed into his monstrous being. He was no mere enemy. He was hunger itself. And if he was not stopped, existence itself would become his next meal.	0	353	f	2026-02-27 12:11:34.224178-05	2026-02-28 17:52:10.07673-05	normal	\N
342	85	1	Approach to the Corpse World	The narrator's ship approaches Earth, finding it transformed into a grotesque monument to death. The Obsidian Tower looms ahead, a living structure of fused bodies and tortured souls. As the crew begins to fall to the Tower's corruption, the narrator alone resists through technological shielding and willpower.	The Aditi’s Genesis cut through the void like a blade through flesh, a precise, final incision into the black maw of oblivion. We had taken an isolated ERB close to the front, a desperate foothold at the edge of reason, where the laws of reality had begun to crack. The weight of I AM's dominion pressed against us, thick and suffocating, seeping into the steel bones of our vessel. Every system hummed with the strain of holding back the madness that lurked just beyond the hull.\n\nThen we saw Earth.\n\nHope died in our throats.\n\nThe planet was no longer a home, no longer even a ruin—it was a corpse, bloated and mutilated beyond recognition. Its once-blue oceans churned with black rot, its landscapes warped into grotesque parodies of life. Cities stood like monuments to agony, frozen mid-collapse, their skeletal remains stretching toward the void as if pleading for mercy that would never come.\n\nAnd the bodies.\n\nThey weren’t just scattered across the surface. They were the surface.\n\nTwisted into impossible shapes, mouths frozen in soundless, eternal screams, they fused into the very fabric of this forsaken world. A landscape of flesh, bone, and agony, a tableau of suffering so absolute it defied reason—a final, hideous mockery of a species that had once dared to dream.\n\nBut even this wasn’t the worst of it.\n\nThe Obsidian Tower loomed ahead, a monolithic wound in reality. It had not been built—it had grown.\n\nA living monument to annihilation, its writhing form breathed, its surface undulating like muscle beneath skin, shifting and folding upon itself in grotesque, liquid motion. It pulsed with a slow, monstrous rhythm—a heartbeat of the damned, thrumming with the essence of every soul it had devoured.\n\nIt was not stone. It was not metal.\n\nIt was flesh.\n\nA grotesque fusion of the fallen, their bodies stacked, contorted, stretched into the tower’s very walls. Eyes still blinked from within its surface, mouths whispering, gasping, sobbing—a constant, ceaseless chorus of despair.\n\nHands jutted from its shifting mass, fingers twitching, grasping at nothing. Some reached toward us, pleading—for help, for release, for anything—but their cries were swallowed by the thing that had claimed them.\n\nThe closer we got, the more it breathed us in.\n\nA wave of malice poured from its core, thick and cloying, sinking into our thoughts like rot into open wounds. The air around it felt wrong, not just heavy but hungry—as if the Tower itself could smell us, could taste us, could pull us into its walls to join the eternal nightmare stitched into its flesh. And then my crew began to fall.\n\nOne by one, I watched them break.\n\nTheir eyes hollowed, pupils dilating into bottomless voids. Limbs twisted, elongating into grotesque parodies of flesh and metal. Their bodies snapped—joints bending in ways that should have shattered bone but instead reshaped them into something other. The last remnants of their human forms dissolved, swallowed by the nightmare.\n\nTheir screams merged with the Tower’s unearthly hum, a sound that wasn’t heard but felt, vibrating through bone, through thought, through soul.\n\nI should have joined them.\n\nI should have crumbled under the weight of it, another pawn claimed by the demiurge’s endless hunger.\n\nBut I resisted.\n\nNot because of strength. Not because of will. Because of technology, because of the Infinitron grafted into my skull—a machine intelligence beyond human comprehension, shielding my thoughts from the corruption.\n\nBut to stand alone, I had to cut myself off.\n\nThe Infinitron severed its link to S Corp’s vast network, isolating me from every AI, every command relay, every digital lifeline. I stood before the Tower, alone in a universe that had already been consumed.\n\nAnd I AM noticed.\n\nIt reached for me.\n\nNot with hands, not with form, but with presence, a pull more inevitable than gravity. It was not a god. Not a demon. It was a wound in existence, a sentient absence, older than the stars, a devourer of light, of sanity, of meaning itself.\n\nEach step toward the Tower was a battle against oblivion.\n\nThe ground beneath me trembled, whispering promises of release, of surrender.\n\nRest. Cease. Dissolve.\n\nI clenched my fists until my nails bit into flesh. My heart pounded like a war drum, its defiant rhythm the only sound in the void.\n\nThe Tower’s will grew heavier. Tendrils of madness coiled around my thoughts, pressing against my mind, demanding I give in. To give in was peace. To give in was to become part of something infinite, timeless, all-consuming.\n\nMy fallen crew had succumbed in an instant. Why should I fight?\n\nBut even as despair threatened to consume me, a flicker of defiance burned inside.\n\nI was not a pawn.\n\nI was not an offering.\n\nI was the last ember of humanity’s light, and if I was to be swallowed, then I would burn my name into the fabric of existence before the void could take me.\n\nI forced myself forward, each step a rejection of the silence, a defiance against the abyss.\n\nI AM loomed closer.\n\nThe air vibrated, thick with hunger, with the weight of creation and annihilation locked in an endless, violent embrace.	0	354	f	2026-02-27 12:11:49.890226-05	2026-02-28 17:53:26.778049-05	normal	\N
343	85	2	Confrontation with the Demiurge	The narrator crosses the threshold into the Tower and confronts I AM directly, engaging in both verbal and physical combat. Despite an initial apparent victory, I AM resurrects itself and severs the narrator's arm, forcing them to recognize that brute force alone cannot end the cycle.	The shrieks started the moment I crossed the threshold into the tower.\n\n“I AM!”\n\nThe words didn’t just echo—they erupted, tearing through the space around me like jagged glass. A cacophony of a thousand wails, each one a note in a symphony of suffering. The walls vibrated, pulsing in time with the voice, a voice that wasn’t a voice at all but something fundamental, something carved into the marrow of existence itself.\n\n“You insufferable fuck!” The words smashed against my skull, rattling my thoughts, splitting reality at the seams. “You will die, mortal. You play at being a god—why?”\n\nThe shadows coalesced, warping into something vast, something that shouldn’t be.\n\nI AM wasn’t just evil.\n\nIt was the absence of everything else.\n\nThe Tower itself was merely an extension of its will, a grotesque throne of writhing bodies, pulsating with unnatural hunger. Here, in the heart of its domain, I was staring into the locus of all wrongness, the cancer at the core of creation.\n\nAnd it grinned.\n\nA maw split open, jagged and infinite, eyes burning with cold malice.\n\n“I AM,” I said, voice steady, defiance carved into every syllable. “I’m not playing God. I’m here to fucking end you.”\n\nLaughter. Cold, cruel, absolute.\n\n“You dare defy me?” it sneered. “You—a fleeting spark of fucking mortality? You cannot fathom the depth of my power. I am the fucking architect of this universe. Its master. Bow, or be fucking erased.”\n\nI stepped forward.\n\n“You call yourself an architect?” My words were razor wire, slicing through the choking pressure of its presence. “You’re just a shitty parasite. You didn’t create this place—you stole it. You fucking infested it. A builder doesn’t strangle their creation. A tyrant does.”\n\nThe grin widened.\n\nWith a guttural growl, I AM moved, its form shifting, an avalanche of darkness sweeping toward me. Tendrils of seething black energy cracked like whips, hungry.\n\nI threw myself sideways, barely dodging the onslaught. The air shrieked as reality itself buckled beneath the force of its attack. The walls twisted, shifting flesh and bone, screaming faces emerging before sinking back into the living nightmare of the Tower.\n\n“You are nothing,” I AM hissed, voice thick with contempt. “A pest. A stain on my perfect design.”\n\nI spun, teeth bared, heart thundering.\n\n“Your perfect design?” I snarled. “It’s flawed. You underestimated humanity. We don’t just survive—we resist. We fight. And today, we win.”\n\nAnother blast of raw entropy, and I was moving again, dodging the writhing mass of destruction as it tore apart everything in its path.\n\n“You think belief will save you? I AM belief you fuck? I am the alpha and the fucking omega? Don’t you fucking get it yet?” I AM mocked, its form warping, expanding, collapsing. “I made faith and I own faith. Faith is a fleeting thing. It will not stand against me.”\n\nI charged, closing the gap between us in a blink. “Belief is the spark—but action is the fire that will burn you to the fucking ground.”\n\nOur wills collided, the impact ripping through existence.\n\nThe Tower shuddered. The walls screamed. The very fabric of the universe fractured as our energies clashed, raw power splitting the void apart, leaving only chaos in its wake.\n\nI could feel the weight of it, the pressure of an entire cosmic will bearing down on me, threatening to crush me into nothingness. My bones screamed, my muscles burned, my mind fought against the sheer, impossible magnitude of I AM.\n\nBut I did not break.\n\nThe spirits of all the realms, their towers, and the entire cabal of entities had my back. The shade of Lady I my guardian.\n\nI pushed.\n\n"You think yourself invincible," I growled, every syllable laced with rage, with defiance, with fury. "But humanity has always risen against tyrants. We defy the odds. We resist. And we always FUCKING triumph."\n\nI AM laughed, its voice a crashing maelstrom of mockery and malice.\n\n“You are nothing but a shadow on my canvas! Your hope is a pathetic ember against my eternal fire!”\n\nI gritted my teeth.\n\n“No, I AM.” The nanites surged, pushing my body beyond its limits. The Infinitron pulsed within my skull, a final surge of raw power fueling my last stand.\n\n“Hope isn’t an ember. It’s a fucking beacon.”\n\nAnd then I struck.\n\nA single blow, every ounce of my being poured into one final, obliterating attack.\n\nThe universe screamed.\n\nA flash of light, so bright it burned through time itself. The impact shattered reality, a shockwave of destruction and renewal ripping through the battlefield, silencing everything in its wake.\n\nThe world went still.\n\nThe Tower—collapsed around me, the bonds holding the flesh severed.\n\nI stood, beaten and broken, gasping raggedly. The magnitude of ruin surrounded me, the remnants of I AM’s domain collapsing into dust.\n\nVictory.\n\nAbsolute silence.\n\nAnd then—\n\nA screech, piercing, slicing through my skull like ice-driven daggers.\n\nI turned.\n\nSmoke coiled from I AM’s shriveled remains.\n\nTendrils formed, reaching for me, thick and suffocating.\n\nI froze as the thing rose anew—larger, darker, more monstrous than before.\n\nBlades materialized in its hands, dripping with the stolen essence of the dead.\n\nAnd before I could react—\n\nOne of those blades struck.\n\nI barely felt it at first.\n\nThen the pain hit.\n\nMy left arm—gone.\n\nSevered in a spray of crimson, agony exploding through my body, knocking me back.\n\nI AM loomed above me, towering, eyes glowing with infinite cruelty.\n\n“You cling to your fragile fucking hope,” it sneered. “But you are no better than I. You seek dominion through your creations. You wield power with the same fucking arrogance you claim to despise.”\n\nThe words slammed into me.\n\nA challenge. A truth wrapped in venom.\n\nI forced myself to my feet, vision swimming, blood pouring from the stump of my arm.\n\n“And what of you?” I demanded. “You claim the right to rule. To devour, to dictate the fates of the many. What makes your tyranny just?”\n\nThe answer was there.\n\nIt had always been there.\n\nBrute force wouldn’t end I AM. It would only continue the cycle.\n\nTo break the loop, I had to understand.\n\nTo unravel it.\n\nThe only way out was through.\n\nI made my choice.\n\nWith deliberate intent, I let go—and allowed myself to be consumed.\n\nNot as surrender.\n\nNot as defeat.\n\nA calculated plunge into the depths of I AM’s void.\n\nAnd as the darkness closed around me, I felt the first threads of truth begin to unravel.	1	355	f	2026-02-27 12:11:49.890226-05	2026-02-28 17:54:28.254649-05	normal	\N
