499	123	4	The Truth Unfolds	Patrick explains the nature of the prison, Yaldabaoth, the Thirteen, and DMT as a conduit for communication. Stephen realizes his wife is a construct designed to keep him docile and learns of the threat to his son Xavier.	**********\n\nBack in Patrick’s trailer, we didn’t really clean.\n\nI asked if he wanted help, and Patrick just shrugged. “What’s the point?” he said. “It’s all just dust anyway.”\n\nStill, we moved things around—kicked debris into corners, cleared enough space on the furniture to sit. He found a half-empty bottle of something brown and strong, handed it to me without a word, then dropped into the armchair like gravity had finally caught him.\n\nThe room still stank of mushrooms and burnt candle wax. The walls hummed faintly, like reality hadn’t decided if it was staying or not.\n\nI sat there, staring at the photos as they faded. One by one, the frames emptied. Whitney’s smile dissolving into blank paper. Their wedding photo. Vacation snapshots. Years of a life that never happened.\n\n“Doesn’t it bother you?” I finally asked.\n\nPatrick paused, holding a shattered candle. "What?"\n\n"That. All of it. She was your wife. And now she's just... gone. Like she never existed."\n\nHe set the candle down carefully. "She didn't exist. Not really. I mean, she was here. I could touch her. Talk to her. But looking back now..." He rubbed his face. "It's like remembering a dream someone else had. The details were always fuzzy. Her past. How we met. Every time I tried to pin it down, it just... slipped away."\n\n"How long?"\n\n"Couple years. Maybe five." He shrugged. "Time gets weird when you're tripping every other weekend."\n\n"Tripping?"\n\n"DMT." Patrick said it like it was the most natural thing in the world. "Started after the crash."\n\nI blinked. "What crash?"\n\nHe looked at me. Really looked at me. Like he was deciding how much to say.\n\nThen he sat down across from me and started talking.\n\n"I joined the Air Force after high school," Patrick said, half-grinning like he still didn’t believe it himself. "Yeah, me. The guy who almost got expelled twice."\n\nI snorted. "Twice? I'm surprised they didn't just give you the boot permanently. Weren't you the one who ran the entire cheerleading squad's... laundry... up the flagpole? And didn't you hide the principal's car for a week?"\n\n"His keys," Patrick corrected, looking proud. "Buried 'em under the goalpost. And it was Dana Johnston's panties, not the whole squad's. Point is, I was a model citizen."\n\n"You were a menace. I didn't think you even knew how to spell 'computer', let alone use one for anything."\n\n"Hey, I had hidden talents." He leaned back, staring past me. "Nah, you're mostly right. I was just a shit-disturber. It wasn't until I got out, took the ASVAB... and the recruiter's eyes bugged out. Turns out I'm a natural with math. Encryption, crypto stuff. I could make a machine talk if I wanted it to. Guess someone saw potential buried under all the detention slips."\n\nHe went quiet for a beat. The grin faded.\n\n"Anyway… 2015. Routine transport mission out of Panama. We were hauling comms gear—secure uplink components, the kind they don’t list on manifests. Everything was fine until it wasn’t. All four engines failed. Just… died. No alarms, no warnings. Total silence."\n\nThe room felt colder.\n\n"We went down hard. Straight into the jungle. Crew of eight. I was the only one they found alive."\n\n"Jesus."\n\n"Yeah." Patrick's voice was flat. "Here's the thing, though. I shouldn't have survived. The crash site was a crater. Wreckage scattered across two miles. But when the rescue team found me, I was sitting in a clearing fifty yards away. Not a scratch. Covered in mushrooms."\n\nI stared at him.\n\n"I'd eaten mushrooms the night before the flight," he continued. "Stupid decision. I was still high when we went down. And during the crash... I wasn't in the plane anymore. I was somewhere else. Somewhere vast. Somewhere outside the fucking prison in a place that smelled like old gym socks and tuna fish."\n\nHe paused, his eyes distant.\n\n"That's when I met them."\n\n"Them?"\n\n"The Thirteen." He said the name like a prayer. Or a curse. "A cabal of entities operating outside Etheris. Gods, I guess. Or something close enough. They're part of the group who built the prison. The ones who trapped Yaldabaoth and everything he'd consumed inside this pocket reality."\n\nI stared at him. The smell of incense was still in the air, but this was a different kind of crazy. "Whoa, whoa. Back up, man." I held up a hand. "You're talking like... this is just another Tuesday. What is 'Etheris'? What the fuck is a 'Ya'll-the-Baath'? Are you still tripping right now? What prison?"\n\nPatrick just looked at me, his expression dead serious.\n\n"Yeah. Eons ago. They thought they were doing the right thing. Containing a threat. But they fucked up." Patrick stood, pacing. "They didn't just trap Yaldabaoth. They trapped everyone he'd already devoured. Uncountable trillions of trillions of souls. Maybe more. All of them recycled, reborn, consumed again and again in an endless loop."\n\n"And they realized this."\n\n"Eventually." Patrick stopped at the window, staring out at the swamp. "The Thirteen have been trying to fix their mistake ever since. Watching us. Trying to help keep souls safe. Sending signals through the cracks. DMT is one of the conduits. A chemical doorway that lets messages slip between the bars."\n\n"That's how you've been communicating with them."\n\n"Every trip. Every vision." He turned back to me. "They've been teaching me. Preparing me. Showing me how to identify NPCs and hide from constructs. Getting me ready to, well to be honest, help you. "\n\nI thought about the hand sign. The way my fingers had moved without thinking.\n\n"Cthulhu," I said quietly. "That thing I did. That came from them?"\n\n"Indirectly." Patrick crossed his arms. "Cthulhu is one of the Elder Gods. They were here first. The original creators of this reality, this pocket multi-verse, like avatars of nature."\n\nHe let out a humorless laugh. "They're agents of chaos. Completely antithetical to human life. Hated us from the second we showed up as heralds of Yaldabaoth’s arrival. So when Yaldabaoth—the thing you called 'Ya'll-the-Baath'—invaded, you'd think they'd get along just fine."\n\nHe shook his head. "But they didn't. Yaldabaoth doesn't just conquer, Stephen. It consumes. It eats everything. The Old Gods included. It's a plague.\n\n"So when the Thirteen built this prison to trap Yaldabaoth, the Elder Gods got trapped, too. They're part of the fabric of this place. Couldn't cut 'em out. Cthulhu is just one of many. They're all down there, at the bottom of it all. Sleeping. Dreaming. And their dreams... they bleed through."\n\nPatrick's gaze sharpened. "That's what you just tapped into. The dream plane. It used to be one of the main avenues we could use to connect, to see the truth. But it was shattered a few cycles ago during a violent reset. Now, it's just static and nightmares. Which makes what you just did..." He trailed off, studying me. "That shouldn't be possible."\n\n"So that's why it worked."\n\n"Yeah. The construct couldn't handle pre-prison power. It was designed to suppress human consciousness. Not elder gods."\n\nI rubbed my temples. The headache was back, worse now, but it wasn't just pain. It was... clarity. Like a lens snapping into focus.\n\n"It's... it's all starting to make sense," I whispered, the words tasting like bile. "My son, Xavier. He keeps talking about 'NPCs.' Non-player characters. Like... like he can see the code."\n\nPatrick just watched me, letting me connect the dots out loud.\n\n"Those two... Andrew and Morgan. I saw them at James and Becca's bookstore. Then they were at the Best Buy. Same dead eyes. Same... emptiness. Until Gil brought out that Veilwalker card. It was like he rebooted them."\n\nI looked up, my hands starting to shake. "But my wife, Patrick. Oh god, my wife."\n\nThe words came tumbling out now. "She's been getting violent. Erratic. But it's not random. It's... it's triggered. I've been getting these flashes. Memories. Of... of Whitney. Of Jamie. And every time I try to dig into it, every time I get close to remembering..."\n\nI swallowed, the memory of last night burning in my throat. "She escalates. At first, it was just threats. Divorce. Taking Xavier away. Then... she started getting physical. Throwing things. A glass that just missed my head. Calling me names... just, vile. Soulless."\n\n"But that's not the worst part," I said, my voice dropping. "The worst part is when I stop. When I back down. When the fear works... she changes. She becomes... perfect. 'I'll cook your favorite meal, honey.' Promises... promises of pleasure."\n\nI laughed, a sharp, bitter sound. "For a decade, it's been a game. Just enough to keep me from leaving. A kiss here, a touch there, and once or twice every few years some starfishes... It’s just enough to make me stay, but always pulling away the second I thought things were getting better. For years, I've been trying to earn... anything. A touch that wasn't just... there."\n\nI shook my head. "And now, suddenly, she's all in? Promising the world? It's trying to keep me docile. It's trying its hardest to keep me from moving forward, from waking up."\n\n"I... I even looked at her driver's license yesterday," I added, my voice barely a whisper. "Pulled it right out of her purse while she was in the shower. And Patrick... I couldn't read her name. The letters were just... jumbled. Blurry. Like the world wouldn't let me see it. It's like she doesn't even have a name. She's just... 'my wife'."\n\nMy eyes locked on his. "And Xavier... he sees it too. He sees the change. He knows something's wrong with her. And she... she's starting to notice him noticing."\n\nPatrick's expression darkened. "Your son is real. I can feel it. But your wife?" He shook his head. "She's another construct. Like my Whitney. A prison guard wearing a wedding ring."\n\n"She's going to hurt him."\n\n"Probably." Patrick's voice was cold. "If she perceives Xavier as a threat to her mission—keeping you docile, keeping you trapped—she'll eliminate him. That's what they do."	3	513	t	2026-02-27 12:28:53.100953-05	2026-02-28 22:59:15.731939-05	normal	\N
542	133	3	The Six-Month Search	Six months pass as the Resistance searches for the cosmic seed while Stephen struggles with nightmares and moral uncertainty. Jamie provides comfort and support as Stephen grapples with his past actions and current mission.	**********\n\nThe search took six months.\n\nSix months of scouts dispatched into remote realities. Six months of me watching the rescue operations slow to a crawl as resources diverted. Six months of watching the numbers tick down while Yaldabaoth's breeding programs ticked up.\n\nThe nightmares came back during that time. Not the ones where I relived my deaths. Different ones. Worse ones. Dreams where I stood outside Etheris, watching it pulse and grow, watching new souls flicker into existence only to be consumed. Watching the prison swell like a tumor until it burst, spilling anti-reality across everything.\n\nJamie found me on the observation deck again, third night in a row.\n\n"Can't sleep?"\n\n"Can't stop thinking."\n\nShe sat beside me, our shoulders touching. Below us, Elysium hummed with activity. Always activity. Never enough activity.\n\n"They'll find it," she said.\n\n"And if they don't?"\n\n"Then we try something else. We always try something else."\n\nI wanted to believe that. Wanted to believe there was always another option, another plan, another way through the maze. But I'd seen too many resets. Too many failures. Too many moments where the only option left was to burn everything down and hope something better grew from the ash.\n\n"I killed him," I said quietly.\n\nJamie didn't ask who. She knew. "That was a different you. A different reset."\n\n"Does that matter? He still remembers it. Still has that terror in his eyes when he looks at me."\n\n"He's insane, Stephen. The memories aren't even his anymore. They're just... echoes. Trauma printed on a consciousness that wasn't built to hold it."\n\nMaybe. But I still saw myself in Todd's ravings. The dreamer. The man who thought he could solve everything with machines and logic and perfect systems. The man who'd forgotten that love was the only thing that mattered.\n\nI'd killed Todd for massacring everything I built. And now I was here, trying to build another system to contain another god.\n\nSame patterns. Different scale.\n\nJamie's hand found mine again. "You're spiraling."\n\n"I know."\n\n"Want to talk about it?"\n\n"Not really."\n\nShe leaned her head on my shoulder. We sat like that for a while, watching the stars through the viewport. Stars that weren't really stars. Just lights in the distance. Other realities. Other prisons maybe. Other gods dreaming broken dreams.	2	309	f	2026-02-27 12:32:10.366732-05	2026-02-28 23:41:49.981048-05	normal	\N
294	74	2	Hydroponics Crisis	Frank responds to the Hydroponics emergency where Dr. Adler panics about gamma radiation from UV filters. Frank diagnoses and fixes the issue, nearly drowning in irradiated tomato soup in the process.	My first stop was Hydroponics, where the radiation alarms wailed like banshees. “Frank, thank God!” hollered Dr. Adler, Hydroponics’ resident paranoid. His hair stuck up like static-charged algae.\n\n“What’s up, Doc?”\n\n“The UV filters! They’re emitting gamma rays! Gamma! We’re going to grow mutant cucumbers, Frank!”\n\n“I hate to break it to you, Doc, but that’s not how radiation works.” I climbed into the filtration system, dodging dripping pipes and swearing under my breath. “Gamma rays... Christ. You’d think interns designed these systems.”\n\nAn hour later, the filters were fixed, though not before I’d nearly drowned in irradiated tomato soup. Dr. Adler thanked me with a cucumber smoothie that glowed faintly in the dark. I drank it anyway—hydration’s hydration.	1	306	f	2026-02-27 12:08:36.270579-05	2026-02-28 17:08:17.235701-05	normal	\N
