297	74	5	Contact Lost with Sorhhinda	Pete, Frank's husband and comms officer, calls with catastrophic news: Sorhhinda colony has gone completely dark with no signals, telemetry, or beacons. Frank and Pete wrestle with the implications of humanity's first exo-solar outpost falling silent.	“Frank, pick up. We’ve got a situation,” came Pete’s voice—calm, steady, and with just a hint of his trademark sarcasm. That voice was the only reason I survived life on Elysium without losing my mind. Pete, my husband, and the best damn comms officer for S Corp, was the yin to my perpetually frazzled yang.\n\n“What now?” I sighed, already bracing for Pete’s mix of bad news and dry humor.\n\n“You’re not going to like it,” Pete said. “We’ve lost contact with Sorhhinda.”\n\nMy stomach did that sinking thing, the one that only happens when you realize you’ve moved from ‘manageable mess’ to ‘galactic clusterfuck.’ “Lost contact? As in... no signals?”\n\n“As in, the whole colony’s gone dark,” Pete replied. “No comms, no telemetry, no beacons. Just silence.”\n\nI paced the observation deck, where nothing but the endless void of space hung in the distance, a serene black nothing mocking the chaos that was life on Elysium. “Well, that’s a shit-pipe I can’t patch with duct tape,” I muttered, my plumber’s mindset kicking in. “What’s the official theory? Clogged line? Cracked flange?”\n\n“Cracked flange?” Pete deadpanned. “Frank, I swear, everything’s a plumbing metaphor with you.”\n\n“Hey, it’s all pipes. Just depends on what kind of shit’s flowing through them,” I shot back. “Now tell me—what’s your guess?”\n\nPete sighed. “Best case? It’s a comm array failure or a solar storm knocking their systems offline. Worst case? Sabotage. Or something... bigger.”\n\n“Bigger?” I repeated, my voice tightening. “You’re not talking about raccoons again, are you?”\n\n“No, Frank. I’m talking about the kind of bigger that makes my skin crawl and my comms training feel like a joke,” Pete admitted. His voice, normally a rock-solid anchor, carried an edge of fear that made my gut twist.\n\n“Jesus.” I ran a hand through my hair, trying to focus. Sorhhinda wasn’t just any colony—it was humanity’s first exo-solar outpost. Losing them would be like shit hitting the S Corp fan. “Any distress signals?”\n\n“Nothing,” Pete said, the tension in his voice sharpening. “It’s like someone flipped a switch and cut them off.”\n\nI leaned against the cold glass of the observation deck, my mind racing. Images of Sorhhinda flashed before me: gleaming toilet paper fields, rows of eager junior plumbers with their hopes pinned on this colony, the kind of future people crossed stars to build. And now, nothing.\n\n“You’re sure there’s no way to reach them?” I asked, though I already knew the answer.\n\n“We’ve tried everything,” Pete said. “But if there’s interference—or worse, sabotage—nothing’s getting through.”\n\n“Pete, if something’s gone wrong there, we can’t sit around and hope it fixes itself. Someone’s got to unclog the pipe.”\n\n“Frank, this isn’t a backed-up toilet. This is an entire colony gone silent!” Pete’s voice cracked, frustration giving way to worry. “You’re a janitor—not a superhero.”\n\nAnd if raccoons and rogue bowling balls were the good days, I was heading straight for hell.	4	309	f	2026-02-27 12:08:36.270579-05	2026-02-28 17:10:53.113677-05	normal	\N
298	75	1	The Anomaly in the Data	TJ discovers a troubling pattern in resource utilization data suggesting widespread withdrawal from productivity among creators and innovators. The anomaly represents a growing dissatisfaction with the post-scarcity utopia, masked by surface-level happiness metrics.	Europa’s icy crust shimmered through the domed ceiling of TJ’s office, refracting the city lights of New Atlantis into a kaleidoscope of blues and whites. It should have been breathtaking, but today the beauty felt hollow, like a polished veneer hiding rot. TJ sat hunched over his desk, his fingers twitching near the blinking red indicator on his data stream. Unease clouded his usually sharp mind.\n\nSix months ago, the anomaly had first appeared, a subtle dip in resource utilization so small it barely registered in S Corp’s monolithic efficiency reports. But TJ, a veteran political analyst, recognized patterns like a bloodhound scenting prey. What started as a whisper in the data had become a full-throated roar, one he could no longer ignore.\n\nIt was funny. It started out as the shitstorm to end all shitstorms. Back in 2024, during early experiments on an early version of the Space Elevators, the construction had opened up into some deep core vault and unleashed destruction of a magnitude never before seen by humanity. Here it was 5 years later, and Europa was considered the gem of the empire.\n\nWith the S Corp going public and unveiling the space elevators in 2025, they had ushered in the Great Unburdening. Replicators and ERBs eradicated poverty, provided limitless food, and rendered basic survival concerns obsolete. Humanity leapt into leisure, exploring art, virtual worlds, and self-indulgence with the zeal of a species untethered from the chains of necessity.\n\nTJ had been one of its architects, dubbing it humanity’s “second Eden.” But Eden was never without its serpents.\n\nThe cracks began quietly. A drop in consumption among independent creators: artists, engineers, builders. Not out of scarcity, but because they had stopped producing. What started as a trickle was becoming a torrent. The anomaly on TJ’s screen wasn’t just a glitch in the system; it was a rebellion in the making. Purpose had withered, and people were withdrawing into silence, disconnection, and discontent.\n\nFirst, it was the Red Hats.\n\nThey’d started as a whisper, a rumor. A scattered movement that seemed to spring up in the strangest places: abandoned mines, derelict lunar stations, even deep within S Corp facilities. Their crimson hats, emblazoned with cryptic symbols, were a badge of defiance. What they wanted was shrouded in secrecy, but wherever they appeared, unrest followed. Graffiti scrawled across pristine walls. Supply lines were disrupted. Shadowy meetings held in back alleys and virtual forums. They were no longer just a nuisance—they were an echo of something far darker, something that threatened the fragile utopia S Corp was trying to foster.\n\nThe red indicator continued to pulse on his screen, a mocking heartbeat. TJ hesitated, his finger hovering over the activation pad. To report this would be to light a fuse on a bomb he couldn’t defuse. But ignore it? That would be suicide. His chest tightened as he made his choice.\n\nThe alert was sent.	0	310	f	2026-02-27 12:08:48.334161-05	2026-02-28 17:12:16.497076-05	normal	\N
299	75	2	The Confrontation with Dr. On	Dr. Pai M. On arrives to hear TJ's report on the anomaly. TJ argues that purpose and happiness are distinct, and that people are rebelling by withdrawing from productivity. Dr. On dismisses his concerns, refusing to acknowledge the severity of the growing Red Hat movement and its implications.	Minutes later, the door to his office hissed open, and Dr. Pai M. On strode in. Once just a sleep psychologist who headed up early investigations into dream disturbances, she was now head of S Corp’s Social Integration Division. She moved with the practiced ease of someone accustomed to managing crises—and eliminating them. Her polished smile felt like a blade wrapped in silk.\n\n“TJ,” she greeted smoothly, her gaze flicking to the pulsing red anomaly. “Your report caught my attention. A deviation in the projections. What are we looking at?”\n\nTJ outlined his findings, pointing to the resource underutilization trend and it’s unsettling implications. “It’s not just an economic blip,” he concluded, his voice tight. “People are opting out. Not just consumers, the creators, the innovators. It’s a quiet revolt, and it’s growing. It’s like the resurgence of the Luddites in the 1950s. Like people want to go back to the stone age!”\n\nDr. On’s smile faltered. “We’ve seen no significant decline in societal happiness metrics. You should stop imagining things.”\n\n“Happiness?” TJ scoffed. “Happiness and purpose aren’t the same thing, Dr. On. We’ve given them leisure, but we’ve stripped away their drive. They’re cogs in our perfect machine, and they’re trying to break free.”\n\nHer expression hardened, steel glinting beneath her diplomatic façade. “Purpose is adaptable, TJ. Humanity has always thrived under new paradigms. They’ll adjust. Stop making problems where there are none.”\n\n“No, they won’t,” he snapped. “Not unless you give them something to strive for. They’re retreating into pods, into fabricated realities, or worse—they’re joining the Red Hats.”\n\nDr. On’s lips pressed into a thin line. “The Red Hats just are a stupid fringe movement. Disorganized. Harmless. Just ignore them. There are bigger issues you should be focused on.”\n\n“They’re growing,” TJ countered, his voice muted. “And they’re not harmless. They’re planting seeds of dissent, exploiting the cracks in our perfect systems.”\n\nDr. On stood abruptly, her heels clicking against the obsidian floor. “Thank you for your insights, TJ. We’ll monitor the situation closely.”\n\nHer departure left the room colder than before, the shimmering ice above casting fractured shadows on the walls. TJ slumped back into his chair, the red indicator still pulsing on his screen. He’d sounded the alarm, but S Corp wasn’t listening. Not yet.	1	310	f	2026-02-27 12:08:48.334161-05	2026-02-28 17:13:04.202317-05	normal	\N
326	81	6	The Analog Weapon Revealed	Aditi, recovered enough for debriefing, explains how she discovered the Red Hats' exploitation of simple analog technology. Shortwave radio broadcasts combined with amplifying hats create infectious sound patterns that corrupt listeners, bypassing all digital security.	As soon as Aditi was well enough for a debrief, she recounted her ordeal with a calmness that belied the harrowing experience she’d endured. Stranded in the vast void of space, her hope dwindling, she had tuned into radio frequencies in desperation. What she found changed everything.\n\n“They’re using shortwave radio,” Aditi explained, her voice steady. “It’s old-school, over a century old. We overlooked it in favor of more advanced systems. They’ve exploited that blind spot to bypass our network controls entirely.”\n\nPatrick raised an eyebrow, leaning forward in his chair. “Shortwave? They’ve weaponized something that basic?”\n\nAditi nodded. “The simplicity is the genius. It’s untraceable by our systems. All our security measures are aimed at digital threats. We never accounted for something so analog.”\n\n“Aditi,” I asked, my voice calm but intent, “how did you manage to decode their transmissions?”\n\nA faint frown tugged at her lips as she glanced at the makeshift radio equipment on the table beside her. “It was mostly Hiro. It took trial and error.”\n\nPatrick’s gaze sharpened. “And what did you find?”\n\nHer smile faded, replaced by a somber expression. “Their broadcasts detailed plans to sabotage our main communication networks and disrupt supply routes to the colonies. It’s all about creating chaos, breaking us apart, weakening our unity. They’re organized, ruthless, and utterly committed. But the biggest thing was the sound patterns and their damn hats. Apparently there is something in the broadcasts that takes over listeners personalities completely. If you hear it, most people get corrupted by it. Both Hiro and I have had too many ear infections as kids