creaming. Dying.\n\nAnd he reached back.\n\nNot with his hands. With something deeper. Something that had always been there but never needed. Never wanted.\n\nHe reached into the space between cells. Between atoms. Into the fundamental fabric of what made reality reality.\n\nAnd he pulled.	2	576	f	2026-02-27 12:33:14.303832-05	2026-03-01 00:56:18.580112-05	normal	\N
303	76	3	The Weight of Distance	In her small Elysium dorm room, Aditi gazes at Earth through her window and connects with her mother via hologram. Her mother reveals the worsening conditions at home—her father's persistent cough, Rahul's absence, and the desperation to escape. Aditi promises to bring them to Elysium but struggles with the guilt of knowing the waitlist is designed to keep them out.	The nights on Elysium were the hardest. The orbital city, gleaming with sterile perfection, seemed to mock Aditi with its cold indifference. Outside her window, Earth hung like a fragile blue marble, breathtaking and distant. The hum of Elysium’s artificial systems buzzed faintly, filling the oppressive silence of her small dorm room. After what felt like hours of staring at the planet below, Aditi finally activated the hologram terminal.\n\nThe connection sputtered to life, static flickering before resolving into the faint image of her mother’s face appeared thinner than Aditi remembered, her cheeks hollow and her eyes rimmed with exhaustion. The flickering light of their cramped Mumbai kitchen glowed behind her, an all-too-familiar backdrop from a life Aditi couldn’t forget.\n\n“Amma,” Aditi whispered, her voice barely steady.\n\nHer mother’s face softened into a weak smile. “Meri pyari bitiya,” she said, her voice warm but strained. “How are you, beta? Did you eat?”\n\n“I’m fine, Amma,” Aditi replied, swallowing the lump in her throat. “What about you? How are things at home?”\n\nHer mother sighed, the sound heavy with weariness. “Beta, the air here keeps getting worse. Your papa’s cough hasn’t gone away. And Rahul, he’s hasn’t been home since you’ve left.” She hesitated, her voice dropping as though speaking too loudly might break the fragile connection. “When are we coming, bitiya? It’s getting so hard to live here.”\n\nAditi’s chest tightened, her heart twisting with guilt. Her mother’s words carried the weight of everything she couldn’t yet deliver. “Amma, the waiting list is very long,” she murmured, her voice faltering. “I’m trying, but it’s going to take some time.”\n\nHer mother’s eyes filled with unshed tears, and she gripped the edge of her sari like it was a lifeline. “Beta, we’re only waiting to see you. This house... it feels so empty without you. Especially since Rahul is…”\n\nAditi clenched her fists, her nails biting into her palms. She couldn’t bring herself to explain the truth—that the waitlist wasn’t just about bureaucracy. It was about caste, privilege, and money, all designed to filter out those deemed “unworthy” of the stars. To Elysium, her parents were just another statistic in the shadows of Earth’s decay.\n\n“I promise, Amma,” she said, forcing strength into her trembling voice. “I’ll do something. I’ll bring you and Papa here. Just give me a little more time. I can ask around if they can help you find Rahul.”\n\nAmma nodded, but her face betrayed the fragile hope behind her acceptance. “Beta, we just want you to be happy. But don’t forget everything you left behind here.”\n\n“I won’t, Amma,” Aditi whispered, her voice cracking. “I could never forget.”\n\nThe static on the hologram worsened, Amma’s image flickering like a ghost caught between two worlds. Aditi’s fingers hovered over the terminal, wishing she could reach through and hold her mother’s hand, erase the distance between them. But all she had was the cold, unfeeling glow of the light projections.\n\nWhen the call ended, Aditi stared at the empty display, her chest hollow, and her heart heavy. Outside, Earth continued to spin, its soft light taunting her with its unattainable closeness.\n\nShe turned back to her dorm, the equations on her tablet a temporary refuge from the promises that felt more impossible with every passing day. Yet her mother’s words echoed in her mind, soft and insistent: Don’t forget everything you left behind.	2	315	f	2026-02-27 12:09:13.493498-05	2026-02-28 17:16:42.307152-05	normal	\N
304	76	4	The Anomalous Signal	Months later, late at night in Elysium University's research lab, Aditi discovers an erratic, desperate signal from Pluto referencing a Janus gate jump. She immediately reports it to Professor Nakamura, who becomes deeply alarmed at the implications—a direct transmission to Pluto that bypasses the standard ERB network, suggesting potential compromise.	Late one night, months later, in the quiet hum of Elysium University’s research lab, Aditi sat surrounded by the rhythmic thrum of machinery—a sound that usually lulled her into focused tranquility. But tonight was different. The sharp crackle of static from the antique radio telescope shattered the stillness, cutting through the usual calm like a siren in the dark, jolting her awake.\n\nShe shot upright, her pulse quickening as her fingers danced over the console. The erratic signal was unlike anything she’d encountered before—raw, jagged, desperate. Adrenaline coursed through her veins as she filtered the data, refining the garbled static into discernible fragments.\n\n“This is... I am... Janus gate jump... execute... Pluto…”\n\nThe voice dissolved into white noise, leaving behind an oppressive silence that seemed to thrum with unspoken danger.\n\nAditi froze, her heart pounding in her chest. Pluto. The name alone sent a shiver down her spine. It was the solar system’s edge, home to S Corp’s experimental Exo-planet colonization facilities. A place shrouded in secrecy and so isolated that even rumors struggled to escape.\n\nProtocol dictated that she report the anomaly immediately. But something about the transmission felt different—not a signal, but a plea. A voice lost in the abyss, reaching out across the void. Her hesitation weighed heavy, but the urgency of the situation forced her hand. She rushed out of the lab and ran to Professor Nakamura’s domicile.\n\n“Professor Nakamura,” she called, her voice tight with urgency. “You need to see this.”\n\nThe professor’s typically stoic demeanor cracked as he reviewed the data. His eyebrows knit together in alarm, his gaze flicking between the console and Aditi. “A transmission... to Pluto?” he murmured, half to himself. “That’s impossible. They’d use the ERB network. Why wouldn’t they?”\n\nAditi swallowed hard. “What if they couldn’t? What if the network is compromised?”\n\nNakamura’s silence spoke volumes. His expression darkened as the implications unfolded in his mind. “Keep this between us,” he said firmly. “For now.”\n\n**********	3	316	f	2026-02-27 12:09:13.493498-05	2026-02-28 17:17:32.639147-05	normal	\N
305	76	5	Conspirators in Code	Aditi brings the encrypted transmission to Hiro, a brilliant and rebellious fellow student, and together they decrypt hidden S Corp files revealing ancient Latin blueprints for faster-than-light propulsion systems and a secret operational vessel called the Genesis near the Rome space elevator. Despite the danger, Aditi convinces Hiro to help her steal the ship to investigate what happened to Sorhhinda and uncover the truth.	The dim glow of the console reflected off Hiro’s glasses as he hunched over the terminal, his fingers flying across the holographic keys with practiced precision. Hiro was one of the few people Aditi had connected with at Elysium University, a quiet boy with a sharp mind and a knack for finding cracks in systems, both digital and social. They’d bonded in Professor Nakamura’s class, their shared disdain for elitism and love of unconventional problem-solving bridging the gap between his reserved nature and her fiery resolve.\n\n“Tell me I’m crazy,” she murmured, eyes scanning the shifting code. “Tell me this isn’t all connected.”\n\nHiro exhaled sharply, adjusting his glasses. “I’d love to.” He gestured to the screen. “But that would make both of us crazy.”\n\nAditi leaned in, her breath slow but unsteady. The pieces were there, scattered like shattered glass. The rogue transmission. The Janus Gate. The whispers of Rome. The darkness swallowing Sorhhinda. Each clue had been a separate anomaly: until now.\n\n“You’re sure this is the right file?” Hiro asked, his voice a faint whisper. The screen was filled with diagrams in ancient Latin, their intricate notations spiraling into what looked like madness but hinted at genius.\n\n“Yes,” Aditi replied, her voice clipped but resolute. “The signal referenced Janus. These blueprints match the propulsion system specs—systems that shouldn’t even exist.”\n\nHiro leaned back, his chair creaking. “Latin blueprints for a faster-than-light engine hidden in an S Corp database. Makes total sense,” he muttered, shaking his head. “We’re going to get caught, you know.”\n\n“We won’t,” Aditi said, her tone sharp enough to cut through his doubt. “Not if you stop talking and finish the decrypt.”\n\nHiro sighed but refocused. “You’re lucky I like you, Aditi. Otherwise, I’d be halfway back to my dorm with a bag of stolen protein bars and no regrets.”\n\n“I’ll buy you a lifetime supply of protein bars if we live through this,” she quipped. Her eyes flicked back to the terminal. “Is that the location?”\n\nHiro pointed at the screen. “Far side of Elysium, near the Rome space elevator. Hidden launch facility. I’ve flagged a vessel... the Genesis.” He turned to her, the rebellious glint in his eyes muted by apprehension. “It’s operational.”\n\nAditi exhaled sharply. “Operational? And no one’s using it?”\n\nHiro shrugged. “Maybe it’s classified. Maybe it’s bait. Either way, if you’re thinking about doing something reckless...”\n\n“I am,” she interrupted. Her voice dropped, firm and deliberate. “We’re going to Pluto.”\n\nHiro froze, then pushed his glasses up the bridge of his nose. “You’re serious.”\n\nAditi stepped closer, lowering her voice. “Hiro, this isn’t just about some rogue signal. Something’s happening out there. Sorhhinda’s gone dark, the Red Hats are more organized than anyone’s admitting, and now there’s this...” She gestured at the blueprints. “...this technology, hidden from everyone. If we don’t figure out what’s going on, who will?”\n\n“And you think hijacking an experimental ship to Pluto is the answer?” Hiro asked incredulously.\n\n“Do you have a better idea?” she shot back.\n\nHiro’s silence lingered for a beat too long. Then he smirked. “Fine. But if this gets me expelled, or killed, I’m haunting you.”	4	317	f	2026-02-27 12:09:13.493498-05	2026-02-28 17:18:16.287851-05	normal	\N
306	76	6	Stealing the Stars	Aditi and Hiro infiltrate the hidden launch facility and discover the Genesis, an unconventional ship that feels almost alive with its organic design. After a brief moment of doubt, they board the vessel and launch into the void, setting course for Pluto with nothing but determination and the faint hope that they can uncover the truth hidden at the edge of the solar system.	The hidden launch facility was eerily quiet, bathed in dim red emergency lighting that seemed unnecessary but added an ominous touch. The Genesis loomed in the center of the cavernous hangar, alive and predatory, its hull humming with an almost imperceptible vibration. The ship felt alive. It’s shape was strangely animal-like.\n\n“Wow,” Hiro whispered, his voice tinged with awe. “That’s not just a ship. That’s a statement.”\n\nAditi approached the vessel, her footsteps echoing across the wooden planks, strangely out of place here. “This is it,” she said, more to herself than him. “Project Janus’ last gamble.”\n\nHiro jogged to catch up, craning his neck to take in the ship’s organic surface. “You know, I could still talk you out of this.”\n\n“No, you couldn’t,” Aditi said, running her hand along the strange surface. “Hiro, this isn’t just about us. If that signal was real, if there’s even a chance, we can figure out what happened to Sorhhinda or stop whatever’s happening on Pluto, we have to take it.”\n\nHe groaned. “Fine, but I’m logging my complaints somewhere. They’ll find my list floating in space after this all goes to hell.”\n\nInside, the Genesis felt like stepping into a different era. It was like an ancient sea going vessel superimposed for space travel. The cabin was compact but pristine, its design minimalist and efficient. Mahogany panels glowed faintly, casting an ethereal orange light resembling candles. Aditi slid into the pilot’s throne, her fingers brushing over the controls. They responded immediately, the console flaring to life as the ship thrummed beneath them.\n\n“Are you sure you can fly this thing?” Hiro asked, strapping himself into the copilot’s seat.\n\nAditi’s lips curved into a small, determined smile. “No. But I’m sure I’ll figure it out.”\n\nAlarms chirped softly as the launch sequence initialized. The hangar doors groaned open, revealing the endless vacuum outside Elysium. Stars glinted faintly beyond, a backdrop to their recklessness.\n\nHiro glanced at her. “Last chance to change your mind.”\n\nAditi’s gaze didn’t waver. “Set course for Pluto.”\n\nThe Genesis roared to life, its engines vibrating through their seats. The vessel surged forward, tearing through Elysium’s confines and into the void. Behind them, the station dwindled, its glittering lights fading into the vast, starlit abyss.\n\nThe silence enveloped them, broken only by the hum of the ship. Hiro exhaled slowly. “So... this is the part where I say you’re completely insane, right?”\n\nAditi smiled faintly, her eyes fixed on the distant stars. “Maybe. But insanity’s the price of progress.”	5	318	f	2026-02-27 12:09:13.493498-05	2026-02-28 17:19:08.436575-05	normal	\N
