ingers find the inside of my shirt. Suddenly I don't care about reality. The speedometer climbs as my pulse races to match it.\n\n"Trust us," they say in unison, their voices blending into harmony that makes my vision blur.\n\nThe world becomes liquid—palm trees melting into sky, asphalt flowing like mercury. This is what I've dreamed about in the dark hours when I thought no one would ever want me. Both of them. Choosing me. Wanting me.\n\nI let the sensations of pleasure take me and close my eyes to be fully present in their caresses. The steering wheel slips in my suddenly nerveless grip. The white lines of the road blur together into one hypnotic stream.\n\n"Stephen?" Jamie's voice, sharp with sudden fear.\n\nThe concrete of the causeway wall rushes toward us with mathematical certainty.\n\nImpact.\n\nMetal screams. Glass explodes. Rushing water as the steering column punches through my chest like a lover's final betrayal. Everything becomes pain and copper-taste and the sound of dreams dying.\n\nBut this time, as consciousness fades, I feel their hands still on me—Jamie's fingers tangled in me, Becca's teeth deeply sucking at my neck. Even in death, they don't let go.\n\nI slam back into my body choking on the taste of gasoline and blood that isn't there. Sweat soaks my shirt, and dampness fills my pants. My heart hammers like it's trying to escape my ribcage. But underneath the terror is something else—the lingering ghost of their touch, the phantom warmth of being wanted.\n\nThat was another me, I realize with crystalline clarity. Another Stephen, in another timeline, getting everything he wanted right before it killed him.\n\nThe death visions aren't just random flashes. They're me. All the versions of me that died, bleeding through into this reality like cosmic interference.\n\nI sit up slowly, my body still tingling with the memory of hands that were never really there. Part of me wants to lie back down, chase that dream, find my way back to that Corvette 