log:\n\nhttps://does-god-exist.org/\n\nFor Rina, the love of my life, who puts up with all the elephants running around in my head, and for Xavier who hopes someday I forget to lock the games on his computer.	0	366	f	2026-02-27 12:12:37.51848-05	2026-02-28 18:04:58.145163-05	normal	\N
356	92	1	The Pod's Last Malfunction	A seventy-four-year-old man in a hospice pod experiences a life-support system failure at 3:17 AM. As he gasps for air in the metal coffin, he reflects on his deceased wife of forty-seven years and their distant but enduring love, contrasting her memory with his current technological imprisonment.	The hospice pod murdered me at 3:17 AM.\n\nLet me back up a few minutes. The strange glyph appears on the screen as the life support system hiccups, oxygen cuts to nothing, and I'm clawing at the emergency release like a seventy-four-year-old animal. My lungs burn. Stars dance behind my eyelids. For thirty seconds, I'm drowning in a metal coffin while some firmware update decides whether I'm worth keeping alive.\n\nThen it kicks back on. Sweet, recycled air floods my chest. The pod hums its mechanical lullaby again, pretending nothing happened.\n\nMy wife would have laughed herself sick.\n\nThe pod smells like crusty socks and regret. Maybe it's me. The thing lurches again, some computer bug trying to kill me at seventy-four. She would've found this hilarious. Software Engineer killed by software glitch. I can almost hear her laughing as I watch the skyline, smog choked with dust or possibly betrayal, and remember when I thought any of this mattered.\n\nShe had this way of finding humor in the cosmic joke of existence. Even when the cancer was eating her alive, she'd crack jokes about how her body was "finally getting revenge for all those years of terrible American food." She'd pat my hand with those delicate tiny fingers—pianist fingers, though she never played—and smile that polite, distant smile that had charmed me for forty-seven years.\n\nThe smile that never quite reached her eyes.\n\nNot that it mattered. I loved her anyway. Maybe because of it. There's something beautiful about loving someone who holds pieces of themselves just out of reach. She was kind. Gracious. Perfect in every way that mattered and a few that didn't.\n\nFourteen moments of connection in four decades. Fourteen times she let me close enough to matter. One of those times gave us our only son. I tracked each one like a prisoner marking days on a cell wall. But she'd make me green tea every morning in that precise, ritual way of hers. She'd listen to my rambling theories about technology and humanity with the patience of a saint. She'd fold my clothes with corners so sharp they could cut glass.\n\nShe even put up with our son's Roblox obsession and his eventual career in software development—not the doctor she'd dreamed of, but at least he followed in the old man's footsteps.\n\nLove doesn't always look like passion. Sometimes it looks like quietly making space for someone else's existence, even when your own body feels like borrowed time.\n\nThe pod lurches again.\n\nThrough the greasy window, the world spreads out like a fever dream painted in rust and disappointment. Thirty years ago, a weird dream made me think I could change everything. Woke up, fired off an application to help Leon build the future. I  even wrote a whole damn novel about the experience—Elysium Rising. Sold maybe 200 copies. Not exactly the cultural impact I'd envisioned.\n\nTurns out my sacrifice had changed everything. Just not the way I expected.\n\nAI helped us cure cancer, build cities in space. But when we tried giving it godlike intelligence; we only ended up with glorified search engines. Sure, we extended human lifespan to 150 years, but that mostly just meant 150 years of compound disappointment. We reached for the stars, only for them to spit in our faces and let us grab a handful of cosmic static.\n\nShe died anyway because she hated medicine with the passion of a woo-woo goddess on fire. It happened gradually, last year, peacefully, holding my hand while her breathing became shallower and shallower until it just... stopped. No dramatic last words. No profound revelations. Just the last quiet fart end of a long quiet love.\n\nThe orbital rings never held. Cosmic vacuum power stayed perpetually twenty years away until it became a punchline. The automation tools cross bread with OnlyPorns and wandered off into their own digital heavens, leaving us with customer service sex-bots and targeted smut. Society didn't collapse—it just got tired and forgot how to dream.\n\nBut I still dream about her smile. The real one. The one she gave me exactly twice in forty-seven years. Once when our son was born. Once, inexplicably, when I brought her terrible hospital coffee on a Tuesday in October, three weeks before she died.\n\nThat smile could have powered the orbital rings we never could quite figure out and build.\n\nThe pod's systems flicker again. Another misaligned update, another brush with digital euthanasia. I close my eyes and remember the weight of her hand in mine, how her breathing matched the rhythm of machines that she wouldn't allow to save her.\n\nMaybe she was right. No gods. No heaven. Just the echo of green tea and the geometric precision of perfectly folded shirts.\n\nAnd this goddamn pod. Finish what you’ve fucking started already!	0	368	f	2026-02-27 12:14:01.519812-05	2026-02-28 18:07:20.891756-05	normal	\N
357	92	2	The Death of Dreams and Science	The protagonist drifts into bitter memory and reflection on humanity's failed technological age, where AI solved only minor problems before hitting a knowledge ceiling, leading to societal collapse, demographic extinction, and universal resignation. Increasingly surreal glitches and impossible symbols begin appearing in his environment.	A memory surfaces.\n\nThe day humanity launched ChatGipety 2026xl. Hell, I was part of the craze working with Leon as he helped build his own competing version he named CrockXxx. Leon was bouncing off the walls with that manic energy of his, convinced we were about to birth the singularity.\n\nFive years of AI-enhanced progress. Cancer cured. Cold Fusion solved. Some other random math and material science advancements. That's all we got. Five years of feeling like gods before the universe reminded us we were still just clever apes with Wi-Fi.\n\nThe scientific community had this term for what happened next: "the death of science." Sounds dramatic, right? Turns out knowledge has an upper limit. Who knew? All those sci-fi stories we grew up on... they lied. There was no infinite energy waiting for us at the end of the equation. No AGI that would solve every problem. No fountain of youth hidden in our DNA. No wormholes to distant galaxies.\n\nJust us. Stuck on a couple of rocks with our 150-year lifespans and AI that couldn't even make decent conversation.\n\nI'm floating here in my pod now, feeling that old urge to laugh at how it all turned out. The universe is bigger and emptier than we thought. Humanity is smaller and more tired than we wanted to admit.\n\nWe tried spreading out, of course. Mars went feral first. Those colonies we were so proud of? They became algorithmic ghost towns, running themselves into the ground with no one bothering to ask why. I remember when Mars seemed like a blank canvas, a masterpiece waiting to happen. Instead, it painted itself with indifference and porn parlor