ss our faces. Morgan perches on the arm of TJ's custom leather recliner, laughing at something he said, her hand brushing his shoulder. He's oblivious, animated about some conspiracy theory involving Reagan and time travel, gesturing with some imported beer. Whitney sits cross-legged in the corner, grimoires spread around her like protective wards, occasionally glancing up to watch Patrick with those knowing eyes. Patrick leans against the pool table, cigarette dangling from his lips, taking in everything with that quiet intensity of his, like he's cataloging each moment for some future reckoning.\n\nAnd then there's the war zone surrounding me.\n\nJamie and Becca have positioned themselves like opposing generals claiming territory on the sofa that's become our unofficial confession booth. Jamie's thigh presses against mine, warm and deliberate, and when she leans close I catch the scent of something— like rain on hot pavement mixed with the faint musk of excitement. Becca mirrors her on my other side, fingers finding excuses to brush my arm—reaching for her drink, adjusting her shirt, anything to stake her claim on this anachronistic battlefield.\n\n"So we doing this?" Patrick takes a long drag from his cigarette, exhaling smoke toward the expensive ventilation system. "As much as I love hanging out with you losers on a Tuesday night, I think we've officially crossed into certifiable insanity territory."\n\n"Insanity's underrated," Becca says, her voice dropping to something that might be philosophy or might be seduction. She glances around the room when she says it, but her hand finds my knee with quiet determination. I'm too focused on the weird tension in the air to really notice, but Patrick's eyes glance at Becca, his eyes lingering just for a moment before turning away.\n\nI feel Jamie's entire body tense beside me like a coiled spring.\n\n"Oh, I don't know," Jamie purrs, leaning closer until her breath tickles my ear. "I think there's something beautiful abo