een my ears like thoughts I can’t unthink. I’m detaching again. I’m merging again. A beautiful new panic takes hold, one I didn’t know I could feel.\n\nI hear my voice and not my voice: "Where am I?” And then: “This isn’t happening.” They should strap me in a medical pod, a machine, an old man’s death watch. But I’m still too young for that. They settle on the gurney and wait for me to settle into it. I don't. I watch the shadows around me, crowding in like they’ve been patient for so long. Now that my terror is pure and refined, they don’t have to be.\n\nA voice cuts through the chaos. "Stephen!"\n\nJamie. The name feels familiar, like a song half-remembered. But who...? Then I see her face above me—worried, beautiful, real—and it clicks. The girl from the end of the hall. The one I'd noticed in three different classes but never worked up the courage to approach. Transfer student who walked through high school like she owned the place.\n\nI'd watched her. Choosing seats just close enough to study the way she tucked her hair behind her ear, far enough that I never risked more than stolen glances. Close enough to smell her shampoo in science, distant enough that I was still just another face in the crowd.\n\nThe ref's whistle screams: "Clear the mat!"\n\nBut her voice comes again, softer now, relieved. Like she actually knows me. Like we're... friends? When did that happen? When did I stop being invisible to her?\n\nThe shadows watch her too.\n\nShadows that aren’t her yet are her. Everyone else. Strange forms with limbs made of waiting. They writhe into people and pull away, like disembodied optic illusions with cosmic agendas. A beautiful new clarity settles in me. I should panic, but I don’t. Not anymore.\n\n“Not again,” I hear the shadow accountant whisper.\n\nI’m gone before they look away.\n\n*********	2	373	t	2026-02-27 12:14:19.76048-05	2026-02-28 18:11:31.795623-05	normal	\N
