ion."\n\nBefore I can process what she means, her hand turns my face toward hers and her lips find mine. The kiss is quick but intense, tasting like beer and secrets and the kind of promises that get people in trouble.\n\n“BENJI! BENJI! BENJI!”\n\nThe TV erupts at maximum volume, some old kids' show blaring the name like an air raid siren. A cartoon dog with a red bandana bounces across the screen, barking the word over and over while cheerful music plays underneath like a soundtrack to madness. The dog pauses, stares straight at the screen, and smiles. It's mouth showing, not animated—human teeth. Then the audio distorts. Like it knows we’re watching.\n\nWe all scream. Jamie grabs me tighter, Becca stumbles backward, TJ dives for the remote. But the volume won't turn down, won't respond, just keeps blasting that name like the universe has decided to join our conversation.\n\n"BENJI! TIME FOR ADVENTURE! BENJI!"\n\nPatrick runs for the TV, and grabs the power cord, but when he does he realizes it's already unplugged. The massive screen keeps blaring that name like it's powered by something beyond electricity.\n\n"What the fuck?" he shouts over the noise.\n\nBecca appears beside him, grabbing a pool cue from the rack and shoving it into his hands. "Hit the back! Get inside and turn it off!"\n\nPatrick doesn't hesitate. He starts beating on the rear panel of the elderly television case, wood splintering under his assault until he can reach inside and rip at whatever's making the sound. Finally, mercifully, the TV goes silent.\n\nWe all stare at each other in the sudden quiet, hearts racing, ears ringing from the assault. Becca looks shaken, more than the rest of us—like the coincidence of that name has rattled something loose inside her. She drifts away to a corner of the room, wrapping her arms around herself.\n\nPatrick lets out a long, shaky breath, still holding the pool cue like a weapon. "Well," he says, his voice hoarse. "That was fucked."\n\nThe echo of that na