ca agrees, but she's looking at Jamie when she says it, not me. "Head injuries are serious. You could have brain damage and not even know it."\n\n"Thanks for the vote of confidence," I say.\n\nJamie laughs—this bright, musical sound that makes something in my chest flutter. "You know what I mean. We're just worried about you."\n\n"We?" Becca raises an eyebrow. "I think you mean 'I.'"\n\n"What's that supposed to mean?"\n\n"Nothing. Just that some of us don't need to make everything about ourselves."\n\nThe tension between them is thick enough to cut with a knife, but I'm completely lost. Are they fighting? About what? They're both being nice to me, they never even talked to me.\n\nWait. That's not right.\n\nSomewhere in the back of my mind, the older version of me—the one with seventy-four years of hindsight—is practically cackling. You dense, stupid idiot, he whispers. They're fighting over you. How did you not see it the first time?\n\nBut that can't be right. I'm the guy who sits in the back of class and plays Magic at lunch with the other nerds. I'm the one who gets tongue-tied around pretty girls and has never went on a date until his thirties. Girls like Jamie and Becca don't fight over guys like me. They barely notice guys like me.\n\nExcept... they're noticing now. Jamie's hand is still on my arm. Becca keeps finding excuses to stand closer. They're both volunteering to babysit me through lunch like I'm some kind of prize worth winning.\n\nThis isn't how it happened, I think frantically. I was invisible. I was nobody. They were nice to me sometimes, sure, but they weren't... they didn't...\n\nThe old man in my head chuckles again. Maybe you were just too scared to see it. Maybe you spent so much time convinced you were invisible that you missed all the signs.\n\nMy heart starts beating faster, and not in a good way. Because if the older me is right—if I really was this blind the first time around—then what else did I miss? What other signals flew right over my oblivious teenage head?\n\nJamie is still talking about lunch plans, something about saving me a seat and how she's been working on a new green creature deck that could totally counter TJ's strategy. Becca jumps in with something about her blue control build and how she's been testing new counterspell combinations all weekend.\n\nThey're nerds.\n\nJust like me.\n\nThey know the meta, they understand card synergies, they've been spending their weekends theory crafting Magic decks. The realization makes my chest tight because I can see it now: the competition I'm watching between them isn't about who has the better deck or the smarter strategy. Becca keeps shooting Jamie these little competitive looks—the same look she gets when someone tries to counter her spells. Jamie's hand is still on my arm, staking her claim like she's tapping mana.\n\nThey're not fighting about Magic cards.\n\nThey're fighting about me.\n\nBreathe, I tell myself. This is a good thing.\n\nBut it doesn't feel good. It feels terrifying. Because if my memories are this wrong about something this important, then what else is my brain lying to me about?\n\n"Anyway," Jamie says, turning back to me with a smile that could melt Europa, "you should definitely sit close to us today. I brought extra cookies."\n\n"I brought homemade cookies, not some store-bought crap" Becca adds quickly, eyeing Jamie. "I made oatmeal raisin ones this morning. Your favorite, right?"\n\nHow does she know what my favorite cookies are? And when did Jamie start bringing me food?\n\n"That's really nice of you both," I say, because it seems like the safe response.\n\nJamie grins. "Great! Meet us in the courtyard. Usual table."\n\n"Our table," Becca corrects. "The one where we always sit."\n\nAs they walk towards Biology class—together but somehow competing even in their footsteps—I'm left standing there wondering what the hell just happened. Since when do girls fight over who gets to give me cookies? Maybe that concussion scrambled more than my short-term memory.\n\nThe 74-year-old me is practically screaming in the back of my skull: Just grab one of them and kiss her, you coward! Any one! Both of them! Stop overthinking and START LIVING!\n\nBut I'm already lagging too far behind, paralyzed by the impossibility of it all. By the time I work up the nerve to even consider making a move, they're halfway down the hall, their laughter echoing off the lockers like some beautiful, unreachable song. And I'm still standing here like an idiot, watching my chances walk away in real time.	0	385	f	2026-02-27 12:15:06.509492-05	2026-02-28 18:21:18.761858-05	normal	\N
374	95	2	Biology Test and the Scaffold Vision	During an AP Biology test on cellular respiration, Stephen experiences a vivid death vision of falling from scaffolding. Ms. Rodriguez notices him collapse but then seems to forget the incident entirely, leaving Stephen disoriented.	**********\n\n8:20 AM. Biology. I barely make it through the door before the bell rings—TJ and Patrick held me up in the hallway, giving me shit about Saturday's tournament. "Dude, you got kicked so hard you probably saw your own funeral," TJ had said, laughing. Patrick just shook his head and muttered something about needing better head protection. I'd laughed along, but something about TJ's joke felt too close to the truth.\n\nMs. Rodriguez has that look—the one that means suffering for everyone who didn't study over the weekend. Test papers sit in neat stacks on her desk like ammunition.\n\nI slide into my usual seat near the back, close enough to be near Jamie and Becca but far enough away for plausible deniability. Jamie's got her color-coded study cards spread across her desk. Becca's frantically highlighting something in her textbook. I keep my head down, playing it cool—just another guy who definitely wasn't staring at the two prettiest girls in class.\n\n"Alright, everyone," Ms. Rodriguez announces, wielding the test papers like weapons. "Cellular respiration. Everything we've covered for the past two weeks. You have fifty minutes."\n\nThe papers get passed back row by row. Jamie catches my eye and gives me a little thumbs up—probably thinking about our study session last night. I manage a weak smile back.\n\nThe test lands on my desk. Multiple choice, short answer, essay questions. Standard AP torture.\n\nI write my name at the top and start reading the first question. Something about mitochondria and ATP production. Easy enough. I've got this.\n\nThen the world explodes.\n\nI'm on scaffolding that shrieks as it collapses. Older hands, scarred hands, reach for a cable that snaps with the sound of breaking bones. Thirty feet of free fall compressed into one perfect moment of terror. The ground welcomes me with concrete finality.\n\n"Stephen?"\n\nI'm face-down on my desk, test paper crumpled under my cheek, dark crimson fluids pouring from my mouth. Ms. Rodriguez stands over me, hand on my shoulder. The classroom spins like a carnival ride.\n\n"Do you need to go to the nurse?" Her voice sounds far away, underwater.\n\nI try to sit up but my arms feel like overcooked pasta. "I'm fine," I croak.\n\nBut when I blink and look again, Ms. Rodriguez is back at the front of the class, writing notes on the board like nothing happened. Like she never left her spot.\n\nJamie and Becca are both twisted in their seats, staring at me with identical expressions of worry. Whitney's turned around too, mouthing "What's wrong?"\n\nI point weakly toward the door and mouth "Lunch."\n\nThe test sits before me, still blank except for my name. Fifty minutes feels like fifty-four years.\n\n**********	1	386	t	2026-02-27 12:15:06.509492-05	2026-02-28 18:22:18.394996-05	normal	\N
375	95	3	Lunch Table Games and Cosmic Questions	During lunch in the courtyard, Stephen plays Magic with his friend group while Jamie and Becca continue their subtle competition. Stephen asks disturbing questions about dying and death visions, prompting Whitney to warn him about speaking openly about these experiences.	12:12 PM. Lunch. The courtyard is our kingdom—concrete tables under dying trees, where we gather like some discount version of Camelot. Everyone's already settled into their usual spots when I arrive, cards and lunch scattered across the table like tarot readings for nerds.\n\nTJ's shuffling his red deck, the one that's basically "burn everything and ask questions later." Morgan's got her white artifact spread, all gleaming chrome and mechanical precision. Becca sits across from me with her blue control deck, the kind that says "I will counter your hopes and dreams." Jamie's next to her with green—elves and forests and honest creatures that hit hard. Andrew's running some janky combo that works exactly once every seventeen games. Gil, James and Patrick round out the table with their usual random booster pack mix.\n\n"Saved you a seat," Jamie says, patting the bench beside her. "And brought cookies."\n\nShe slides a bag of Mrs. D's Soft Peanut Butter cookies to me. Becca immediately produces her own container full of oatmeal raisin.\n\n"Thanks," I say, taking one of each because I'm not stupid enough to pick sides. "These are really good."\n\nJamie beams. Becca smiles but shoots Jamie a look that could freeze lava.\n\n"Whose turn?" I ask, trying to defuse whatever's happening here.\n\n"Mine," TJ says, dealing out hands. "Royal Rumble rules. Last one standing wins."\n\nThe game starts normally enough. Lands, small creatures, everyone feeling each other out. But I'm distracted by the way Jamie keeps leaning over to "help" me organize my hand, and how Becca somehow manages to need my opinion on every single play.\n\n"So," I say, trying to sound casual while I play a forest. "Weird question, but... have any of you ever felt like you died?"\n\nGil looks up from his hand. "Every time I play against TJ's burn deck."\n\n"No, I mean really died. Like, actually stopped existing for a minute."\n\nThe table goes quiet. Andrew pauses mid-shuffle.\n\n"Dude," Morgan says slowly, "that concussion really scrambled your brain, didn't it?"\n\n"I'm serious. Saturday, when I got kicked... I was gone. Like, completely gone. And when I came back, I remembered... things."\n\n"What kind of things?" Jamie asks, her hand finding my arm under the guise of reaching for cards.\n\n"Flashes. Deaths that aren't mine. This morning in Bio, I was on some scaffolding, falling—"\n\n"Stephen." Whitney's voice cuts through my explanation like a blade. She's staring at me with eyes that have seen too much. "Maybe we should talk about this later."\n\nThere's something in her tone—a warning, maybe. Or fear. I follow her gaze and notice the shadows under our table are... wrong. Too dark. Too still.\n\n"Fine," I mutter. "Parking lot after school?"\n\nShe nods.\n\n"Maybe it's just your crazy dream imagination," TJ says, tapping mana for another Fire Bolt. "You know, like those stories about space elevators you were always going on about since sixth grade. What did you call it... Elysium Rising? Said if you ever got old enough to care, you'd write a sci-fi book and put us all in it."\n\nThe words hit me like a physical blow. Memories crash over me—the old man's voice, Lady Illkeserod's library, the weight of writing that book in another life. But then they're gone, slipping away like smoke, leaving me gasping.\n\n"I... what?"\n\n"Dude, you literally drew diagrams of orbital rings on your notebook covers. Don't tell me you forgot your own obsession."\n\nBut I did forget. Or I never remembered. Or—\n\nThe confusion must show on my face because TJ just shakes his head and eliminates Andrew with a perfectly timed burn spell.\n\nThe game continues. Andrew gets eliminated first when Morgan's artifacts overrun him. "Stupid white weenie bullshit," he grumbles, but he's grinning.\n\nGil falls next to TJ's relentless burn. "Death by a thousand fire spells," he announces dramatically.\n\nPatrick tries to stabilize with life gain, but Jamie's elves grow too big too fast. "Nature finds a way," she says, clearly pleased with herself.\n\n"That's Jurassic Park, not Magic," Morgan points out.\n\n"Still applies."\n\nMorgan's artifacts look unstoppable until Becca drops a Counterspell on her key piece. "Blue always finds an answer," Becca says sweetly. Too sweetly.\n\nTJ throws everything at Becca in a final kamikaze run, but she's got too much mana up. Another Counterspell seals his fate. "Mono-red is honest," he sighs. "Honestly dead."\n\nNow it's just me, Jamie, Becca, and Whitney. The real game begins.\n\nJamie and Becca eye each other like predators circling the same prey. Which, I'm starting to realize, might be exactly what's happening.\n\n"I attack you," Jamie declares, pointing at Becca.\n\n"Why me? Stephen's got more life."\n\n"You're the bigger threat."\n\n"Am I? Or are you just jealous that he liked my cookies better?"\n\n"He ate yours first," I point out helpfully.\n\nBoth girls turn to stare at me. Apparently that was the wrong thing to say.\n\n"I mean, chronologically. Not preference-wise. They're both equally—"\n\n"Just play your cards, Stephen," Becca says, but she's trying not to smile.\n\nThe battle between Jamie and Becca becomes vicious. Creatures clash, spells fly, life totals plummet. I try to stay out of it, but eventually I have to pick a side.\n\nI attack Becca.\n\nHer face falls for just a moment before she covers it with a smile. "Fine. Gang up on me. I see how it is."\n\nJamie grins triumphantly and pats my shoulder. "Good choice."\n\nThree turns later, Becca's eliminated. She gathers her cards with quiet dignity, but I catch her watching Jamie with something that might be resignation. Or calculation.\n\n"And then there were three," Whitney says.\n\nBut not for long. My black demon deck is powerful but straightforward. Black against white. Whitney's been playing the long game, and when she finally reveals her strategy, it's devastating.\n\n"I play Veilwalker," she announces, placing down a card I've never seen before.\n\nI squint at it. Beneath Whitney’s doodles of the letter J adorning the card, the art shows a figure stepping between realities, one foot in shadows, one in light. The rules text is strange: This card cannot be removed from the battlefield. If Veilwalker is ever alone, exile it and lose the game.\n\n"What set is that from?" Jamie asks.\n\nWhitney shrugs. "Custom card. I had it made."\n\nShe uses Veilwalker to exile my demons, leaving me defenseless.\n\nThen dead.\n\nJamie puts up more of a fight, but eventually even she falls to Whitney's otherworldly strategy.\n\n"Good game," Whitney says, but she's looking at me when she says it. Not at the cards. At me.\n\nThe shadows under our table seem to shift, pleased.\n\nAfter the others drift away to dump their lunch trays, Whitney catches me. "Four-twenty. Parking lot. And Stephen?" She glances around, making sure we're alone. "Stop talking about the visions. They're listening."\n\n"Who's listening?"\n\nBut she's already gone.	2	387	t	2026-02-27 12:15:06.509492-05	2026-02-28 18:23:01.002886-05	normal	\N
376	95	4	Parking Lot Revelations	In the parking lot after school, Patrick and Whitney reveal to Stephen that they have also been experiencing strange visions and supernatural phenomena. They theorize that Whitney's coming out as transgender has somehow 'broken' reality, and they plan to investigate together.	**********\n\n4:20 PM. Afterschool. Patrick and Whitney catch me by the parking lot, and I'm not sure what to make of it. Patrick with his unshakable calm, the kind that used to annoy me until I realized how much of it I wanted for myself. Whitney, finally Whitney—the first year she's been open about it at school, the universe finally acknowledging what Patrick's known since 8th grade.\n\nThey ask if they can talk. I've seen them both plenty, but not together like this, not as a team. Patrick's hands are already fishing for his clove cigarettes. Whitney's got that nervous energy she gets when she's about to say something important.\n\n"Thought we'd gone nuts," Patrick says, lighting up. The smell of cloves and tobacco mixes with the Florida heat.\n\nThere's relief in his voice, but it's complicated relief—the kind that comes with more questions than answers.\n\n"It's not just us then?" Whitney asks.\n\nI look from one to the other. These two have been inseparable since forever—Patrick was the first person Whitney told when she figured out who she really was back in 8th grade. When her family kicked her out for it, Patrick's family officially adopted her to keep her out of foster care, but in reality we all took her in.\n\nShe couch-surfs between Patrick's place, Becca's house, and Jamie's—wherever feels safest on any given night. We all worked together to get her out of that Christian fundamentalist hellhole she was living in.\n\nWhen Whitney threw herself into Wicca and the occult, trying to find some kind of spiritual framework that didn't condemn her existence, Patrick followed her into it.\n\n"It's like we're haunted," Whitney says. "Not by ghosts, but... something else. Something that watches."\n\nHer eyes have that look—the same one she gets when she's casting circles or reading tarot cards. Like she's seeing layers of reality most people miss.\n\n"We thought it was just us processing Whitney's transition," Patrick adds, exhaling smoke. "You know, reality shifting because she was finally being herself. The universe adjusting."\n\nWhitney nods. "When I came out publicly this year, things started feeling... different. Not just socially. Actually different. Like the world was rewriting itself around the truth."\n\n"But then you started talking about your visions," Patrick continues. "Death flashes. Other timelines. And we realized we've been seeing weird shit too."\n\n"Shadows that move wrong," Whitney says. "Dreams that feel more real than waking up. Cards that shouldn't exist." She touches her deck protectively. "That Veilwalker card? I didn't make it. I found it in my collection one morning, like it had always been there. So I claimed it as my own and scribbled my J’s all over it."\n\nPatrick takes another drag. "Whitney thinks it's connected to her transition. That being true to herself somehow... broke something. Made her more sensitive to whatever's bleeding through."\n\n"I spent so many years hiding who I was," Whitney says quietly. "Living as Will, pretending to be someone I wasn't. Maybe that created some kind of... spiritual static. And when I finally stopped pretending, it cleared up enough for me to see what was always there."\n\n"The question is," Patrick says, stubbing out his cigarette, "are we seeing things that are real, or are we all just losing our minds together?"\n\nI think about the scaffold collapse, the taste of blood and concrete. About Ms. Rodriguez noticing me, then not noticing me. About the shadows under our lunch table.\n\n"It's real," I say. "Whatever's happening, it's real. The visions, the shadows, all of it."\n\nWhitney steps closer. "Then we need to figure out what it wants. Because something this big doesn't just happen. It gets summoned."\n\n"Or it escapes," Patrick adds darkly.\n\nThe three of us stand there in the parking lot, the Florida sun casting long shadows that seem to reach for us with intentional hunger. For the first time since Saturday's tournament, I don't feel completely alone with this weirdness.\n\n"We need to meet again," Whitney says. "Compare notes. Figure out the pattern."\n\n"Agreed," Patrick says. "But not at school. Too many ears."\n\n"TJ's," Whitney offers. "Plus he's the only one with a car if we need to go anywhere."\n\nPatrick nods. "Rich kid privilege has its advantages. And TJ's used to us weirdos crashing his place."\n\n"His Nana will probably just leave snacks and tell us stories about the old country," Whitney adds with a small smile. "Perfect for discussing interdimensional bleeding and shadow entities."\n\nI nod. For the first time in days, I feel like maybe I'm not going crazy. Or if I am, at least I've got good company for the trip.	3	388	t	2026-02-27 12:15:06.509492-05	2026-02-28 18:23:56.599271-05	normal	\N
377	95	5	Evening Phone Call and Hidden Intentions	Stephen receives a phone call from Whitney describing her own death visions of being burned, revealing family trauma. Jamie wrestles the phone away and flirtingly suggests they have been playing a romantic 'game' for months, with clear romantic intentions.	**********\n\n6:19 PM. Home. The sun bleeds out. My ceiling catches the stain, repaints itself red with light that's traveled too long to mean anything. I feel like I've traveled with it, a secret too old for my brain to keep. Shadows conspire in the corners of my room. I think they whisper. I think I'm hearing them even before they make a sound. I watch, I wait. The phone in the living room breaks the silence, almost breaks me. My heart jumps. I rush to go pick it up before my parents do. I hear a voice more haunting than ghosts.\n\n"It happened again," Whitney says, both of us holding memories too loud to forget.\n\n"How bad was it?" I ask.\n\nShe breathes on the other end, the quiet fear of someone who's already seen the ending. "Pretty bad." Her words are steady, but I hear what's under them. A shiver. A nightmare that made itself at home in her voice. "I didn't want to freak Patrick out earlier, but when you started talking about those death visions... I've been seeing things too."\n\n"What kind of things?"\n\n"Fire. But not like your scaffold thing. I'm always burning. Sometimes I'm tied to a stake, sometimes I'm just... consumed. Like the universe wants to punish me for existing." She pauses. "One minute I'm lighting a candle for protection, next I'm somewhere else. Watching myself burn. Feeling it burn. Screaming." The memory fills the silence. "Then I'm back, and the candle's still in my hand."\n\n"Jesus, Whitney. I'm sorry."\n\n"It's happening more often now," she continues, not like a victim but like a scientist observing her own trauma. "Like the universe keeps glitching every time I try to be myself."\n\n"Where are you now?" I ask.\n\n"Jamie's. Her parents are working late, so we can actually talk without someone listening." There's relief in her voice. "I can't exactly discuss interdimensional death visions at your place with your parents around."\n\nYeah. My fucked-up home situation isn't exactly conducive to cosmic horror discussions.\n\n"Stephen," Whitney's voice gets softer, more careful. "I know why you wear those baggy hoodies all the time. Those bruises weren't from the tournament, were they?"\n\nMy throat tightens. "Whitney—"\n\n"You don't have to say anything. I just... I know what it's like. Living in a house where you never know what's going to set them off. Where hiding becomes second nature." Her voice carries the weight of someone who's been there. "If you ever need to talk about anything—and I mean anything—you know I'm here, right? I've been where you are."\n\nI close my eyes. She does know. Better than anyone.\n\n"Thanks," I manage. "Really."\n\n"I'm bringing some stuff tomorrow night to TJ's. Oiji board, tarot deck, some ritual components and research I've been doing on dimensional bleeding, maybe some protection spells. Figure we should come prepared if we're going to dive into this weirdness properly."\n\n"That sounds—"\n\nIn the background, I can hear Jamie's voice getting closer—that distinctive giggle mixed with what sounds like her tripping over furniture. Then there's a crash, followed by Whitney's exasperated "Jamie, be careful!" and more giggling.\n\n"And here comes trouble," Whitney mutters. "I should probably go before she breaks something important."\n\n"Good luck with that."\n\n"See you tomorrow, Stephen. Whoever we are by then."\n\nThe line goes quiet, but I can hear Jamie's voice getting louder, more insistent about talking to me.\n\n"Is Stephen on the other line?" Jamie's voice comes through breathless now, like she just ran across the room. "Because if he is, I want to talk to him. It's important."\n\nWhitney laughs. "Jamie, no—"\n\nBut Jamie's already wrestling the phone away. I can hear the struggle, complete with more giggling and what might be a lamp getting knocked over.\n\n"Hey, Stephen." Her voice is warm and slightly out of breath. "So... about lunch today."\n\n"The Magic game? You played really well. I thought we made a great team against Becca there. Too bad Whitney came in with that card at the end."\n\n"Oh, I wasn't talking about the cards." There's something in her voice—satisfaction mixed with mischief. "Though I'm glad you noticed my... technique."\n\nI have no idea what she means by that. "Your deck construction was solid. Good mana curve."\n\nJamie laughs, and it's not quite the response I expected. "Stephen, you're so... focused on the technical stuff. But yeah, I definitely got what I wanted out of that game."\n\n"A win's a win, I guess."\n\n"Exactly. And now I get to sit next to you tomorrow at lunch, like Becca and I agreed. Winner's privilege." Her voice gets softer, more playful. "Unless you're really on her side?"\n\n"Sides? What sides? It's just lunch."\n\n"Just lunch," she repeats, and I can practically hear her smiling. "Right. Just the two of us. Sharing cookies. Sitting really close so I can... monitor you for concussion symptoms."\n\n"That's very responsible of you."\n\n"I'm a very responsible person, Stephen. I take care of what's mine."\n\nWhat's hers? When did I become hers? "I appreciate the concern, but I'm feeling much better—"\n\n"Good. Because I have plans for us tomorrow. And the day after that. And..." She trails off. "Well, let's just say I'm very good at long-term strategy. In Magic and... other games."\n\nOther games? "I didn't know we were playing any other games."\n\nJamie's laugh is bright and delighted. "Oh, Stephen. We've been playing the same game for months. I'm just finally winning."\n\nIn the background, Whitney's voice cuts through: "Jamie, you're being way too obvious. Give me the phone back before you scare him off."\n\n"I'm not being obvious!" Jamie protests. "I'm being... strategically direct. There's a difference."\n\n"You're being a menace is what you're being."\n\nMore rustling sounds as they apparently wrestle for the phone. I hear Jamie call out, "Don't forget, Stephen—lunch tomorrow! Just you and me!"\n\n"Stop tormenting him," Whitney's voice adds as she reclaims the phone. "Sorry about that. Jamie gets a little... intense when she's excited about something."\n\n"Excited about what?"\n\n"Oh, you poor, oblivious boy," Whitney says, but her voice is fond. "We'll see you tomorrow, okay? Try not to die in your sleep or anything."\n\n"I'll do my best."\n\nAfter I hang up, I sit there for a minute, replaying the conversation. Jamie seemed really happy about winning that Magic game. Must have been nice to finally beat Becca's deck—her strategy is really hard to crack.\n\nThe shadows in my room seem to be watching me with what I can only describe as amusement.	4	389	t	2026-02-27 12:15:06.509492-05	2026-02-28 18:24:40.738767-05	normal	\N
430	107	4	Mapping the Timeline	Throughout late February and March, the group systematically investigates the journal entries, experiments with dimensional travel theory, and discovers that Nevada holds the key to understanding the timeline fractures.	February 28, 1996\n\nThe library after school smells like old. Patrick sits across from me, his movements mechanical but his pattern recognition absolutely flawless. We've spread Jennifer's Journal entries across three tables, trying to map the timeline fractures like we're plotting a war.\n\n"Observe the correlation," Patrick says, his finger tracing connections between dates with robotic precision. "February 14, 1989 appears in seventeen different timeline variants. But there's a statistical outlier." He points to an entry written in faded pencil: February 13, 1989 – 11:47 PM – Bedtime story interrupted – Nevada trailer – Subject remained conscious.\n\n"The night before," I breathe. "In one timeline, I stayed awake."\n\nPatrick's head tilts with mechanical curiosity. "Hypothesis: the divergence point occurs not during the poisoning event, but during the preparatory phase. Recommendation: focus investigation on the evening of February 13th, 1989."\n\nFor a robot wearing my dead friend's face, he's absolutely brilliant.\n\nMarch 7, 1996\n\nBecca stumbles into TJ's basement looking disheveled and glowing with the kind of satisfaction that comes from very good sex and very bad decisions. Patrick follows behind her, his shirt buttoned wrong and his hair showing actual signs of human contact.\n\n"We found something," she announces, collapsing onto the couch with a grin that's equal parts triumph and exhaustion.\n\nTJ looks up from where he's been attempting to teach Morgan how to play Settlers of Catalan. "Please tell me you didn't just fuck important information out of a robot."\n\n"Okay, I won't tell you that," Becca laughs. "But Patrick's geographical memory is incredible. Every time we... reached optimal privacy parameters, he'd start reciting coordinates and map references. Turns out there's a specific location in Nevada that keeps coming up in his data banks."\n\nPatrick adjusts his shirt with mechanical precision. "Coordinates 28.1201 ° N, 80.7205 ° W. High probability of significance to primary mission parameters."\n\nMorgan looks up from her cards, skull earrings catching the basement lighting. "That's... incredibly specific. And incredibly weird that he knows it."\n\n"Everything about this is weird," TJ points out, but he's already pulling out a road atlas. "Let's see where that puts us."\n\nMarch 15, 1996\n\nTJ's basement has been transformed into something that looks like a cross between a physics lab and a madman's workshop. Morgan has decoded sections of Jennifer's Journal that read like scientific formulas for dimensional travel, complete with precise measurements and electromagnetic field calculations.\n\n"The mathematics are elegant," she says, adjusting her makeshift instruments while consulting her dream journal. "It's a frequency modulation system, basically. We're not traveling through space, we're tuning into a different reality channel." She points to equations covering every inch of available wall space. "If consciousness vibrates, then we should be able to punch through these barriers using enough harmonic resonance."\n\nTJ sits in the center of her experimental setup, his Reagan shirt replaced with something black and practical, electrodes taped to his temples. "And you're sure this won't, like, fry my brain or explode me into component atoms?"\n\n"Reasonably sure," Morgan replies, which is not as reassuring as it could be. She's wearing safety goggles and has a fire extinguisher within arm's reach, which somehow makes it worse.\n\nJamie watches from behind a hastily constructed barrier of overturned tables, her knowledge radiating like heat shimmer. "The theory is sound," she confirms. "But Morgan's right to approach this scientifically. Magic without method is just wishful thinking with dramatic flair."\n\nWe spend three hours attempting controlled experiments anyway. Nothing happens except TJ gets a nosebleed from the electromagnetic fields, Morgan's Tesla coil shorts out with a shower of sparks, and the basement starts smelling like ozone and burnt electronics.\n\n"Hypothesis rejected," Morgan announces, pulling off her safety goggles with scientific resignation. "We're still missing a critical component. Something that can serve as a quantum anchor between dimensional states."\n\nMarch 22, 1996\n\nThe breakthrough comes from the most unlikely source. Andrew, who's been chain-smoking and stress-researching legal precedents for interdimensional travel (apparently there aren't any), suddenly drops his cigarette and points at Jennifer's Journal.\n\n"The photograph," he says. "The one you mentioned. From Nevada, with the dog."\n\nJamie's eyes widen with recognition. "Brandy. The one you showed us that night."\n\nMy heart stops. I'd forgotten about the photograph—the single piece of evidence that my Nevada childhood ever existed.\n\n"It's at home," I say. "Hidden behind my mirror."\n\n"That's our anchor," Jamie breathes. "A physical object that exists in both timelines simultaneously. It's the bridge we need."	3	391	f	2026-02-27 12:19:38.208317-05	2026-02-28 19:18:16.066932-05	normal	\N
385	97	2	The Summoning Begins	At 11:11 PM, the group positions themselves in a salt circle as Whitney leads them through preparations. The candles freeze unnaturally, the temperature drops, and they begin the chanting ritual. Reality begins to destabilize immediately.	*********\n\n11:11PM We think things are ready. Whitney kneels in the center of everything, spreading her tarot deck out like the wings of some exotic bird. The cards look older than they should, considering it’s a brand new set with some cyberpunk fairy aesthetic. The edges are worn smooth, and the images seem to shift when I'm not looking directly at them.\n\n"Everyone needs to understand," Whitney says, her voice taking on that ethereal quality she gets when she's channeling something deeper than teenage angst, "once we open this door, we can't just slam it shut and pretend nothing happened."\n\n"Speak for yourself," Becca mutters, as she grabs at the cross around her neck. "My parents are going to murder me if they find out. And I mean literally murder me. Like, Old Testament smiting."\n\n"Your parents think Dungeons and Dragons is a gateway drug to Satanism," TJ points out, popping open another beer with the skeleton key hanging from his wallet chain. "Pretty sure they'd perform an exorcism if they find your Magic cards."\n\nThe basement grows colder as we settle into position. Not the comfortable cool of central air, but something that seeps up through the floor and wraps around your bones like wet cloth.\n\n“Shit.” Whitney stands up and grabs two large boxes of Morton's salt, the blue containers looking absurdly mundane in her hands.\n\n"Almost forgot. For protection," she says simply, and begins walking a wide circle around us, pouring salt in a steady stream. The white crystals form a perfect barrier about ten feet across, encompassing our entire ritual space. She moves with deliberate precision, like she's done this before, her bare feet silent on the shag carpet.\n\nOnce the salt circle is complete, she takes her place within it, and immediately the candle flames stretch impossibly tall, their tips nearly touching the drop ceiling, and then suddenly freeze—not flickering, not wavering, just perfectly still like someone hit pause on reality.\n\n"Is it supposed to do that?" Becca whispers, her eyes fixed on the motionless flames. Instinctively, she leans closer to Patrick, and he doesn't miss the opportunity to slide his arm around her shoulders.\n\nNobody answers because nobody knows. We're flying blind into whatever cosmic shitstorm we're about to unleash, armed with nothing but arrogance and instructions downloaded from a dial-up bulletin board at 2400baud.\n\nPatrick takes a long drag from his clove cigarette and exhales slowly, the sweet smoke mixing with the incense until the air tastes like a funeral home trying too hard to be exotic. "Well," he says, grinning like a man about to jump off a cliff, "I guess we're about to find out."\n\nThe temperature drops another ten degrees, and somewhere in the darkness beyond our circle, something that might have been laughter echoes off the paneled walls.\n\n**********\n\nThe chanting starts rough, our voices scraping against each other like rusted metal dragged across concrete. Whitney leads us through syllables that feel wrong in my mouth, words that taste like rancid spam dissolved in old dog piss and dumpster porno mags. The basement air grows thick, viscous, like we're breathing through honey mixed with blood and the last breath of dying things.\n\nThen reality doesn't just crack—it screams.	1	397	t	2026-02-27 12:15:55.357503-05	2026-02-28 18:32:02.688626-05	normal	\N
