's voice stretches like taffy, each repetition becoming more distorted, more desperate. The rosary she pulled out of her pocket snaps, beads buzzing around her like bot flies looking to embed themselves in her flesh.\n\nJamie doubles over on top of my lap, her body convulsing so violently I can hear her spine popping. When she opens her mouth, what emerges isn't vomit—it's liquid darkness, black and viscous as crude oil mixed with tar and the space between stars. The substance defies gravity, flowing upward in spiraling ribbons that reach toward something I can't see but can feel watching us. The dark fluid splashes across my face, my chest, soaking through my shirt with cold that burns like frostbite.\n\n"It's in me," Jamie gasps between heaves, her voice wet and broken. "It's in me and it's hungry—"\n\nWhitney's transformation is the most horrifying. Her eyes don't just roll back—they invert, becoming perfect spheres of bloodshot white threaded with optic nerves that pulse with their own light. Her head tilts at an angle that should snap her cervical vertebrae, and when she speaks, harmonics layer beneath her voice like a chorus of the damned singing backup.\n\n"The membrane is thin here," she says, and each word drips with frequencies that make my eardrums feel like they're bleeding. "We can taste your dreams. We can smell your endings."\n\nPatrick tries to reach for Becca, but where she was sitting there's now something that writhes and shifts. His hand doesn't just sink into her—it disappears in her crotch up to his wrist, swallowed by flesh that ripples and quivers at it starts suckling on his arm. Through the distortion of what used to be my friends, I glimpse pulsing masses that stretch beyond the curve of space, lined with organic machinery that squelches wetly, breathing with lungs made of rusted metal and regret.\n\nBlood doesn't just run from my nose—it erupts, a crimson geyser that defies gravity and joins Jamie's dark vomit in a grotesque ballet twenty feet above our heads. The pressure in my skull builds like a over-inflated balloon, and I can feel my consciousness beginning to fray at the edges, unraveling like the intestines of a drawn and quartered criminal.\n\nThe fluids start sloshing over everything, forming a liquid shell that starts closing in— physically contracting like the womb of a living goddess trying to abort it’s passengers. The ceiling drops inch by inch, the shag carpet now the villi of an eviscerated bowl.\n\nMy consciousness tears loose with a sound like ripping silk.\n\nI'm suspended on silver threads that sprout from the center my hands, my feet, and my side—threads connecting me to the places where my friends used to be. I can smell their agony flowing through the connections like cyanide in my lungs, taste their screams like crusty mucus on my tongue.\n\nTJ's terror cuts through the silver thread like broken glass against bare skin, every jagged edge slicing through my nerve endings until I can feel my flesh being flayed from the inside out.\n\nPatrick's agony floods my mouth with the taste of being consumed alive—copper blood and acid bile, like swallowing molten metal while my tongue dissolves.\n\nWhitney's transformation sears my vision with light and fire and devastation, electric white-hot needles driving themselves directly into my optic nerves until I'm blind from the brightness of her endless burning.\n\nBecca's corruption assaults my nostrils with the stench of organic decay—pustules bursting in rhythm, filling the air with the smell of rotting meat mixed with sulfur and the sweet cloying scent of flowers left too long on a grave.\n\nAnd Jamie... Jamie's darkness doesn't touch my body at all—it reaches directly into my very essence, wrapping around my thoughts like cold fingers, whispering I'm alone.\n\nI've always been alone\n\nEveryone I've ever loved was just a beautiful lie I told myself to keep the emptiness at bay.\n\nBelow me? Above me? Direction has abandoned all meaning as the landscape contracts into impossibility, walls of breathing mucus pressing closer with each frantic heartbeat until the air itself develops a cardiovascular system, numbers of light pulsing in panic as space compresses like a cosmic fist clenching.\n\nMirrors multiply and shrink, each reflection smaller than the last—Victorian coffins, chrome boxes, medieval plague pits, a child's bedroom—all contracting until every possible death happens simultaneously in a space the size of a pinprick, dense beyond sanity, dense beyond the possibility of existing, a kaleidoscope of endings compressed so tightly that reality itself screams as it's crushed into something smaller than a heartbeat yet somehow still shrinking, tighter, more impossible with each fraction of a second that refuses to end.\n\nWhitney suddenly catches fire—not burning but becoming flame, her flesh turning to ash that reforms again and again in an inescapable cycle of death and resurrection. The dark shadows she emanates are wrong, casting light that moves independently and whisper secrets in languages that predate human consciousness. Her screams become a symphony, harmonizing with frequencies that make the basement walls bleed actual blood.\n\n"THE FIRE DOESN't STOP," she shrieks, her voice layering over itself in impossible harmonies. "I KEEP BURNING BUT I NEVER GET TO DIE—"\n\nA choir of centipedes with human faces erupts from the intestinal lining that was the carpet, their segments clicking in percussion as they vomit calendars showing dates that violate the laws of time—February 30th, the 13th month of Octember, years that count backward into negative numbers that hurt to contemplate. Their fleshy parchments dissolve before hitting the ground, becoming letters that rearrange themselves into colors I recognize from dreams I've never had, counting out feelings I've never heard but somehow know by heart.\n\nAbove it all, a voice speaks with the casual authority of entropy itself:\n\n"You came. You opened. We answered. Enjoy the resonance bleed."\n\nThe words don't enter through my ears—they carve themselves directly into my nerves with needles made of crystallized understanding I desperately don't want. The truth they carry is too large for my skull, too sharp for whatever sanity I have left, and I feel myself beginning to unravel like a poorly knitted sweater.\n\nThe fluid sack continues to contract, the membrane now close enough to touch. The blackness that Jamie has been vomiting begins to fill the shrinking space, mixing with the reality to create a soup of liquid darkness that rises like sewage. The silver threads connecting me to my friends stretch taut, then begin to snap one by one—first TJ's thread parts with a sound like a guitar string breaking, then Patrick and Becca's merged connection severs with a wet tearing noise.\n\nWhitney explodes into purity, abstractions dissolving into memes that scatter like startled birds, leaving only the echo of her final scream reverberating in dimensions I can't perceive. Her thread doesn't break—it simply ceases to exist, as if she never was.\n\nThe fluid creeps over my chest, then my neck, warm as blood and thick as tar. It tastes like everything I’d imagine loosing Jamie would when it touches my lips. Jamie's thread is the last to snap, and when it does, I feel a part of my soul tear away with it.\n\n**********	2	397	f	2026-02-27 12:15:55.357503-05	2026-02-28 18:32:52.632028-05	normal	\N
388	97	5	Return to the Wrong Basement	The narrator crashes back into their body in what appears to be the basement, but something is wrong. The walls are dirt and roots, the ceiling wrong. All five friends are naked and unconscious. The familiar basement has been replaced with something else. As the narrator checks on the others, they notice the broken TV flickering with Benji's voice, and Becca wakes with an unsettling consciousness, speaking of 'membranes.' Darkness then overwhelms them all again.	**********\n\nThe waiting room implodes like a house of cards made of screaming.\n\nI slam back into my body with the force of a meteor hitting atmosphere, every nerve ending catching fire as consciousness crashes into flesh. The basement floor is cold concrete against my cheek, rough and real and gloriously mundane. I gasp for air that tastes like incense and terror, my lungs working overtime to process oxygen that feels too thick, too laden with possibility.\n\nMy friends are scattered around me like casualties of a war nobody remembers fighting. Naked. All of us, completely naked. Unconscious. Breathing, but barely—their chests rising and falling with the mechanical rhythm of people whose souls have temporarily vacated the premises.\n\nBut something's wrong. This isn't TJ's basement. The walls dirt, lined with clawing roots and mottled paintings, the shag carpet is missing, instead it’s a cold, moist concrete. The room’s proportions are all off. The ceiling is too low, angles are wrong, pressing down like the lid of a coffin. The corners stretch into shadows that seem deeper than they should be, and the familiar hum of the dehumidifier has been replaced by something that sounds almost like breathing.\n\n"Hey," I croak, my voice sounding like gravel in a blender. "Hey, wake up."\n\nNothing. Jamie lies curled in a fetal position in my lap. Her dark hair fans across the my legs like spilled ink, and when try and flip her over to check her pulse, her skin is cold as marble. But the pulse is there—steady, strong, reassuring.\n\nWhere the hell are our clothes? I scan the basement frantically, but there's nothing. No discarded jeans, no scattered shirts, no underwear—like we materialized here already naked. The ritual supplies are gone too. No candles, no BALG printout, no tarot cards. Just us, bare and vulnerable on cold concrete that feels like it's never been warm.\n\nI get up and go over to try to shake TJ awake, but his body is limp, but he feels as fragile as porcelain. His eyes move beneath closed lids, tracking something I can't see, and occasionally his mouth forms words in a language that doesn't exist. When I touch his shoulder, his skin is fever-hot despite the basement chill.\n\n"Come on, man," I whisper, panic creeping into my voice. "This isn't funny anymore."\n\nThat's when I notice the TV.\n\nIt's flickering with static, which shouldn't be possible since Patrick smashed it with the pool cue on Tuesday—the frame cracked, the cord still unplugged, the whole thing a monument to teenage violence against malfunctioning electronics. The screen shows nothing but white noise bleeding through the spider web of cracks, but there's something moving in the static—patterns that almost resolve into faces before dissolving back into chaos.\n\nThen I hear it: "We'll talk again. Bring snacks next time."\n\nBenji's voice, tinny and distant, crackling through speakers that shouldn't work. The words would make me laugh if I wasn't so terrified. At least some part of what happened was real.\n\nWhitney's body convulses once, violently, and black fluid leaks from the corner of her mouth before her breathing stabilizes. The fluid doesn't evaporate—it pools on the concrete, forming patterns that look almost like symbols before dissolving into ordinary liquid darkness.\n\n"Whitney!" I scramble over to her, but she's completely unresponsive. Her pulse is there but weak, fluttering under my fingers like a trapped bird.\n\n"Come on," I whisper, crawling from friend to friend, checking pulses, looking for any sign that they're still themselves. "Please don't be broken. Please still be you."\n\nPatrick's fingers twitch, and for a moment they're not fingers at all but something segmented and chitinous, like the legs of some cosmic insect. Then they're human again, pale and ordinary, but when I look closer, there are tiny puncture wounds in his palms, as if something with very small, very sharp teeth has been nibbling at him.\n\nThat's when Becca's eyes snap open—not gradually, but all at once, like someone flipping a switch. Her pupils are dilated to perfect black circles, and she stares at the ceiling with an expression of absolute serenity that's somehow more terrifying than any scream.\n\n"The membrane remembers," she says in a voice that's definitely hers but wrong somehow, like she's speaking through water. "The membrane always remembers."\n\nThen, without breaking eye contact with the ceiling, her hand shoots out and grabs Patrick's naked crotch with surprising gentleness. Her fingers curl around him with the familiarity of lovers, and a slow, satisfied smile spreads across her lips.\n\n"Becca?" I whisper, but she doesn't respond. Her hand remains where it is, and Patrick's breathing changes—not waking, but shifting into something that sounds almost like purring.\n\nThen she's unconscious again, nestled in her new home, but that smile lingers for a few seconds before her face goes slack.\n\nI sit back down, naked and shivering and more alone than I've ever been in my life. The basement feels too small now, like a cage designed to hold something that's outgrown its boundaries. Every shadow seems pregnant with possibility, every flicker of light threatens to tear open reality and show me things I'm not ready to see.\n\nThe walls start to shift—not dramatically, but subtly, like someone's adjusting the contrast on reality. The wood paneling ripples like water, and for split seconds I glimpse what's behind it