389	97	6	The Others Awaken	One by one, the friends regain consciousness in TJ's actual basement, naked and disoriented. They have no memory of the ritual or the cosmic horror. The narrator notices their clothes have been neatly folded, which none of them would do. Jamie wakes last and behaves strangely toward the narrator, speaking of feeling 'changed' and 'connected.' After dressing, they discover the obsidian stone from Benji flares and crumbles to dust. TJ's Nana arrives to offer tea and sleeping arrangements, bringing jarring mundanity.	**********\n\nI don't know how long I was out—could have been minutes, could have been hours. The basement feels different when I finally drag my eyes open, thick with a humid musk that reminds me of stale tuna mixed with the stench from the boys' locker room after PE, but wrong somehow. Sweeter. Stickier.\n\nEveryone else starts to wake up like swimmers surfacing from deep water—slow, disoriented, gasping for air they forgot they needed. One by one, consciousness seeps back into their bodies, and I watch their faces cycle through confusion, recognition, and a kind of dazed acceptance that probably should worry me more than it does.\n\nTJ sits up first, blinking owlishly at the basement around him. He's completely naked, pale skin goose bumped from the cold and glistening with a thin sheen of sweat that catches the fluorescent light. His hair is matted to his forehead, and there's something sticky on his cheek that he absently wipes away without seeming to notice.\n\n"Jesus," he mutters, running a hand through his damp hair. "Did we...? What the hell happened to our clothes?" He starts to look under the furniture and rummage in some of the clutter strewn around outside our summoning circle.\n\nI look around the basement, and it’s like we’re in the middle of nothingness. The salt circle of protection marks a perfect boundary: inside it, we're naked and disoriented. Outside our tangled mess of bodies within the circle, everything looks normal. It's like whatever happened was surgically precise, affecting only what was within the ritual space.\n\n"Did it work?" TJ asks, his voice thick with sleep and something else.\n\n"Define 'work,'" I say, still sitting cross-legged on the shag carpet, completely naked and trying not to think about the dampness I can feel between my legs, on my thighs, like I've been sweating in places that shouldn’t.\n\nWhitney emerges from unconsciousness next. Her eyes flutter open, unfocused at first, then sharpening as she takes in the scene. She sits up slowly, unselfconsciously naked, her growing chest pale in the incandescent light, and reaches for where her scattered tarot cards should be—but they're gone too.\n\n"The polyester," she says suddenly, her voice distant and dreamy. "The cosmic energies... they don't like synthetic materials. That's why our clothes are gone. The Veil rejected them."\n\nShe says it with such matter-of-fact certainty that nobody questions it, even though it sounds completely insane.\n\nBecca wakes with a start, immediately looking around frantically. Her face is flushed, chest rising and falling rapidly, and when she realizes where she's been sleeping—her head pillowed in Patrick's naked crotch, both hands holding his manhood for warmth—she jerks upright like she's been electrocuted. But not before I catch the satisfied smile that flickers across her face, followed immediately by a blush that spreads from her cheeks down to her chest.\n\n"My parents are going to kill me," she mumbles, but there's no real panic in her voice. Her skin has that post-shower glow, like she's been thoroughly... cleansed. "What time is it? How long were we..."\n\n"Gone," Patrick finishes, rolling onto his side and propping himself next to Becca. He's the calmest of all of them, naked and sprawled across the shag carpet like it's the most natural thing in the world. There's dried salt crusted at his temples, and his face look slightly rug burned. "We were gone somewhere, weren't we?"\n\nI nod, not trusting my voice to explain what 'somewhere' actually was. Because I remember. I remember all of it—the hell, the bureaucratic demon, the cosmic truth. But looking at their faces, it's clear they don't. For them, there's just a blank space where the terror should be.\n\nJamie is the last to wake, and when she does, it's with a gurgle that sounds more like the aftermath of something intense than surprise. Her dark hair is a tangled mess, stuck to her neck with sweat, and something gleaming between her breasts. Her eyes find mine immediately, and there's something in her gaze that makes my skin prickle with awareness.\n\n"Stephen," she breathes, and my name in her mouth sounds like a prayer and a promise all at once. She sits up slowly, making no move to cover herself, and the way she looks at me is... different. Hungry, maybe. Or satisfied in a way that makes my stomach do strange flips.\n\nShe moves toward me, completely oblivious to her nakedness, her movements fluid and purposeful. The basement air is thick with the scent of bodies—not unpleasant, but unmistakably intimate, like the aftermath of something that should have required consent forms.\n\n"I don't remember what happened," she continues, her voice dropping to a whisper that somehow fills the entire basement, "but I feel... changed. Connected. Like something important happened between us."\n\nHer eyes drift down from my face, taking in my bare chest, and I suddenly become very aware that I'm completely exposed. Heat rises in my cheeks, and I reach instinctively for a pillow from the couch behind me, clutching it against myself like a shield made of polyester and embarrassment.\n\n"Found them," TJ says from behind one of the recliners, his voice muffled. "They're all... piled back here. They smell fresh. Clean. Like washed and someone folded them."\n\nWe all scramble toward his voice like refugees fleeing a natural disaster, our dignity abandoned somewhere between cosmic horror and teenage mortification. The clothes are indeed neatly folded—which is weird because none of us are the type to fold anything, especially not in the middle of a demonic summoning. But there they are: jeans stacked on jeans, shirts arranged by size, even our underwear separated into tidy little piles.\n\nIt's almost more unsettling than finding them missing in the first place.\n\nAfter we're all dressed, we collapse back onto the furniture like survivors of some cosmic shipwreck. The couch cushions feel too soft, too normal after everything that's happened. Everyone looks drained, exhausted in a way that goes deeper than physical tiredness—like we've been wrung out at the soul level.\n\nI find the obsidian stone in my pocket and take it out carefully. It's not the smooth, warm talisman Benji gave me anymore. Instead, it's a dense lump of what looks like coal, rough and cold to the touch, all its cosmic properties apparently burned out.\n\n"Look at this," I say, holding it up for Jamie to see.\n\nAs I pass it over to her, the stone suddenly flares to life—a brilliant flash of white light that fills the entire basement for a split second. Jamie jerks backward so violently she nearly falls off the cushions, her hand flying up to shield her eyes.\n\n"What the hell was that?" she gasps, but even as the words leave her mouth, the stone crumbles to dust in my palm, disintegrating completely until there's nothing left but black residue coating my fingers and the faint smell of burnt ozone hanging in the air.\n\n"Great," I mutter, brushing the ash from my hands onto my jeans. So much for supernatural assistance.\n\nThat's when TJ's Nana's head pops down from the top of the stairs, her silver hair perfectly coiffed despite the late hour. She's wearing a floral housecoat and fuzzy slippers, looking like every grandmother who's ever existed, which somehow makes her sudden appearance even more surreal.\n\n"Oh my goodness, it's almost three in the morning and you kids are still up," she says, her Irish accent thick with maternal concern. "You should be getting some sleep soon. You all look absolutely exhausted." Her sharp blue eyes take in our disheveled state—the way we're all slumped on the furniture like we've been through a war. "Let me fix you some chamomile tea. That'll help settle you down."\n\nShe starts down the stairs, her slippers making soft shuffling sounds on the carpet. "Girls, there's a guest room upstairs with queen bed—fresh sheets and everything. Boys, TJ's room has enough floor space and sleeping bags if you don't mind camping out." She pauses, studying our faces more carefully. "Though from the looks of you, I suspect you'd sleep standing up at this point."\n\nThe normalcy of her presence is almost jarring after everything we've just been through. Hot tea and sleeping arrangements seem impossibly mundane when we've just witnessed reality crack open and spill its guts across the basement floor.\n\n"Thanks, Nana," TJ manages, his voice hoarse. "We're... yeah. We're pretty beat."	5	391	t	2026-02-27 12:15:55.357503-05	2026-02-28 18:35:52.435536-05	normal	\N
390	98	1	September's Dissolution	As September passes in a fever dream, the investigation group fragments under pressure. While the core believers intensify their efforts, skeptics like Gil and James withdraw, citing mental health concerns. Jamie orchestrates the group's dissolution through calculated interventions, systematically isolating members while drawing Stephen closer to herself.	September slips by like a fever dream.\n\nThe first week after the basement incident, we meet every day. Urgent whispers in hallways, emergency telephone sessions in my bedroom, bbs sessions that stretch past midnight. We're addicts chasing the high of cosmic revelation, desperate to recapture that moment when the universe showed us its teeth.\n\nThose of our group who weren’t there quickly fade away. By the second week, Gil stops showing up. "I've got college prep," he says, but his eyes won't meet mine. James follows three days later, citing "academic priorities" and "personal mental health concerns."\n\nThe third week brings Becca's grounding. Her parents found something – ritual notes, occult symbols, evidence of our "satanic activities" hidden in her backpack. She swears she cleaned everything out, but somehow they knew exactly where to look.\n\nOctober arrives wearing the face of exhaustion.\n\nBy then, the shadows have developed an appetite for curious teenagers, and our lunch table feels like a support group for reality-allergic outcasts. We're still playing Magic: The Gathering, still going through the motions of normal teenage friendship, but the energy has curdled into something desperate and strained.\n\nGil, James, Andrew, and Morgan sit at the far end of the table now, close enough to maintain the illusion of group cohesion but far enough to avoid our conversations about dimensional bleeding and cosmic bureaucracy. Ever since I told them about Benji – about the basement horror show, the blood, the walls that screamed – they've been backing away like I'm contagious.\n\n"Your turn," TJ says, tapping his cards impatiently. His usual Reagan-era confidence has been ground down to nervous energy. He's been grounded since his mom found those BALG printouts, and his car privileges got revoked indefinitely. "Can't believe I'm stuck taking the bus like some freshman."\n\nJamie suddenly shifts uncomfortably beside me, her grip on my hand tightening. "TJ, I'm so sorry," she says, her voice thick with guilt. "I keep thinking about those printouts... When I was burning them at Becca's, your backpack was right there by the firepit. Some papers might have blown around in the wind, fallen into your bag..." She bites her lip, looking genuinely distressed. "I thought I'd checked everything, but I must have missed some. God, I feel terrible."\n\nTJ's expression softens slightly. "Jamie, it's not your fault. Mom was already suspicious. She was going through my stuff looking for reasons to ground me."\n\n"Still," Jamie continues, her eyes glistening with what might be tears. "If I'd been more careful... I should have made sure every single page was ash before we left."\n\nI squeeze her hand reassuringly, impressed by how much she cares about our group's safety. But something nags at me—hadn't TJ's mom found the papers folded neatly inside his Trapper Keeper, alphabetized? That doesn't sound like wind-blown accident papers.\n\nI draw a card, but my attention keeps drifting to the empty spaces where our investigation used to live. The conversations that stop when Gil walks by. The way Morgan takes notes for everything except our theories now.\n\nIt wasn't always like this. A month ago, the shadows were everywhere – dancing behind fluorescent lights, pooling in classroom corners, following us home like cosmic stalkers. The death visions hit me daily: construction accidents, car crashes, drownings, murders. I'd sketch them frantically during calculus, trying to map patterns that felt just beyond comprehension.\n\nBut the more we noticed them, the more they learned to hide.\n\n"So Benji confirmed it," I say quietly, leaning forward so only the core group can hear. "The shadows are suppressors. Reality filters. They're actively working to keep us from seeing—"\n\n"Can we not?" Gil interrupts from down the table, not even looking up from his cards. "Some of us are trying to maintain what's left of our sanity."\n\nWhitney's jaw tightens, but she keeps her voice level. "Just because you don't want to see it doesn't mean it's not happening."\n\n"And just because you want to see it doesn't mean it is," Andrew counters. He's been reading philosophy books again, wielding logic like a shield against anything that doesn't fit in neat academic categories.\n\nThe divide is getting wider. I can feel it pulling at us like gravity, dragging the skeptics away from those of us who still believe. Or maybe the believers away from those smart enough to run.\n\n"It's like they know we're watching," I continue, trying to keep the desperation out of my voice. "Every time we get organized, something goes wrong. TJ loses his car right when we need to follow a lead. Becca gets grounded right before we plan a cemetery investigation—"\n\n"Maybe that's just life," Becca interrupts, her voice carrying an edge I've never heard before. She's been different since her parents discovered our "occult activities" – colder, more distant. "Maybe we're seeing patterns where there aren't any."\n\nHer words sting more than they should. Becca was supposed to be on our side.\n\nJamie shifts closer to me on the bench, pressing against me under the table. "You know what?" she says, loud enough for everyone to hear. "Maybe this is good. Maybe we need to focus on high school stuff instead of... whatever this has become."\n\nI feel Becca's eyes on us, sharp and accusatory. There's history there I don't fully understand – some falling out between her and Jamie that happened right around the time Becca's parents found those ritual notes. Jamie was supposed to dispose of everything, but somehow Becca's mom found copies in her room.\n\n"You know what might help?" Jamie says suddenly, her voice bright with helpful concern. "Maybe we should tell our parents we're done with all this. Just to get them off our backs. We could still investigate privately, but if they think we've stopped..." She looks around the table with earnest eyes. "It would take the pressure off everyone. No more groundings, no more suspicion."\n\nBecca's jaw tightens. "Easy for you to say," she mutters. "Some of us are dealing with actual consequences."\n\n"What's that supposed to mean?" Jamie asks, her voice honey-sweet but with steel underneath. "I'm just trying to help. I hate seeing everyone suffer because of this."\n\n"Nothing," Becca says quickly, but her glance toward Whitney suggests everything. Whitney can still crash at Becca's place – her parents haven't completely banned her – but there's a new chill in the air whenever she's around. They watch her like she's patient zero for whatever "occult corruption" has infected their daughter, whispering theories about bad influences and moral contagion when they think no one's listening.\n\nSo Whitney's been splitting her time between Patrick's house and Jamie's bedroom, which means Jamie and I have never had a chance to have a moment alone. Not that we mind having Whitney around a lot more. It’s just a frustrating turn of events for everyone.\n\n"Maybe this is good," Jamie continues, her hand finding mine under the table. "Maybe we need to focus on normal teenage things. Like dating. Like spending quality time together without all this supernatural drama."\n\nThe tension crackles between Jamie and Becca like static electricity. I'm missing something important, some subtext that everyone else seems to understand – the way Becca's jaw tightens when Jamie mentions "quality time," the sharp look Whitney gives Jamie when she talks about needing "privacy." There's a territorial war being fought with glances and careful word choices, and I'm apparently the prize none of them want to admit they're fighting over.\n\n"Jamie's right," Becca says, but she sounds tired rather than convinced. "My parents are already suspicious. They think I'm in some kind of cult." She glances at Patrick, who's been even quieter than usual since his own grounding kicked in. "Maybe we should take a break."\n\n"A break?" Whitney's voice carries that particular edge that comes from watching the world abandon her one person at a time. "From what? From the truth?"\n\n"From the obsession," Morgan says firmly. She's been taking psychology classes, learning to recognize the symptoms of group delusion. "Look at us. Look at what this is doing to our friendships, our grades, our families."\n\nI want to argue, but the evidence is right in front of me. TJ picking at his food instead of cracking jokes, his usual conspiracy theories replaced by bitter complaints about being stuck on campus. Becca stealing glances at Patrick like she's trying to memorize him before he disappears. Even Patrick, loyal Patrick, smoking clove cigarettes behind the gym like he's trying to burn away whatever he saw in that basement.\n\nAnd me – sporting fresh bruises under my long sleeves where my father's belt has been working overtime. Each investigation attempt seems to trigger another explosion at home. Phone bills, late nights, the growing certainty that I'm involved in something he categorically disapproves of.\n\nI remember last Tuesday night, Jamie calling at 11:47 PM. I'd whispered that I couldn't talk long because of the phone bill, but she'd sounded so scared, so alone. "Just five more minutes," she'd pleaded. "Please? I need to hear your voice." Those five minutes turned into two hours of her crying about her parents, about feeling isolated, about needing me. When my father saw the bill three days later, the belt came out before he even asked for an explanation.\n\n"They're winning," I say, the words tasting like ash. "This is exactly what they want. Divide and conquer. Every time we get close to something, the universe throws another obstacle at us."\n\nJamie's thumb traces circles on my palm. "Stephen," she says, and the endearment makes half the table look away in embarrassment. "Look at yourself. You're exhausted. You're paranoid. When's the last time you did anything just for fun?"\n\nThe question hangs in the air like an accusation. I can't remember the last time I laughed at something that wasn't cosmic horror. Can't remember the last conversation that didn't circle back to dimensional fractures and reality glitches.\n\n"See?" Jamie continues. "This isn't healthy. We should hang out, just the two of us. Go on actual dates. Be normal teenagers for once."\n\n"That's impossible," I say automatically. "Between Cross Country, Karate practice, National Honor Society, student government—"\n\n"You could quit some of those," Jamie suggests, her voice casual but her grip on my hand tightening. "You don't need every extracurricular. Just the ones that matter."\n\nBecca makes a sound that might be a snort. "Right. Because Stephen's college applications can afford gaps."\n\n"There are more important things than college," Jamie says, her smile sharpening. "Like relationships. Like being present for the people who care about you."\n\nHer suggestion draws uncomfortable looks from around the table. Becca's expression flickers with something that might be hurt or jealousy or both. Even Whitney seems taken aback by the directness of Jamie's territorial claim.\n\n"Maybe that's not a bad idea," Gil says from his end of the table. "Maybe we all need to... branch out. Try new things. Meet new people."\n\nThe words feel like a formal dissolution, dressed up in the language of healthy teenage development.\n\n"I can't believe this," Whitney says, her voice barely controlled. "We finally start getting answers, and you want to quit because it's hard?"\n\n"We're not getting answers," James says gently. "We're getting more questions. Stranger questions. And frankly, some of the people asking them..." He gestures vaguely in the direction of the basement incident. "I'm not sure they have our best interests at heart."\n\nThe bell rings, signaling the end of lunch. Around us, students start gathering their things, returning to the comfortable routine of classes and assignments and problems that can be solved with the right textbook.\n\nGil, James, Andrew, and Morgan pack up their cards without looking at us directly. Polite goodbyes, promises to "catch up later" that we all know are lies. They drift away like smoke, leaving just the five of us who still believe – or still can't stop believing.\n\n"Well," TJ says with forced cheer, "looks like it's just the cool kids now."\n\nBut even he seems less certain than he did a month ago. The grounding has isolated him from our late-night bbs sessions, our impromptu investigations. Without his car, we can't chase leads across town. Without constant reinforcement, doubt creeps in like water through cracks.\n\nBecca stands, shouldering her backpack with deliberate care. "I should get to class before someone makes me late again." She hesitates, looking at Patrick like she wants to say something important. Instead, she just adds, "Maybe we could all use some space to think."\n\nHer eyes find Jamie's for a brief moment, and something passes between them – an accusation, a challenge, a recognition of some private war I'm not privy to.\n\nPatrick opens his mouth like he’s about to speak, but the cafeteria flickers—just for a second. Like a film strip skipping. And in that blink, he’s not there. Not at the table. Not even in the room. Then reality restitches itself and he exhales, unaware he was gone.\n\n"This is perfect," Jamie says as the cafeteria empties around us. She's still holding my hand, still pressed against my side like she's claiming territory. "Now we can focus on what really matters."\n\nWhitney gives her a look I can't quite interpret. "And what's that?"\n\nJamie's smile is radiant, possessive, and somehow unsettling. "Each other. Stephen and I have so much to explore together."\n\nThe phrasing makes my face burn, but there's something else beneath the embarrassment. A nagging sense that Jamie's enthusiasm doesn't quite match the gravity of what we're losing. Like she's been orchestrating this dissolution all along, nudging conversations toward doubt, planting seeds of exhaustion and fear.\n\nThe timing bothers me too. How Becca's parents found out about our activities right after Jamie offered to clean up our ritual materials. How TJ's mom discovered printouts that should have been destroyed. How every attempt at investigation seems to coincide with another member of our group facing consequences at home.\n\nBut her hand is warm in mine, and her attention feels like sunlight after weeks of shadows, and I'm sixteen years old watching my friends walk away from the most important mystery we've ever encountered.\n\nWhitney gathers her things slowly, deliberately. "I guess it's just us now," she says, but she's looking at Jamie when she says it. "The three of us against whatever's out there."\n\n"The two of us," Jamie corrects gently. "Stephen and I. You understand, don't you? Sometimes people need... privacy. To really connect."\n\nThe words hit Whitney like a physical blow. I see her shoulders tense, see the flash of hurt in her eyes before she covers it with that practiced serenity she's learned from years of rejection.\n\n"Right," Whitney says. "Of course. Privacy."\n\nShe walks away without another word, leaving Jamie and me alone at the increasingly empty lunch table.\n\n"See?" Jamie says, settling even closer now that we have the bench to ourselves. "Isn't this better? Now we can really focus on what matters."\n\nThe courtyard feels huge around us, echoing with the absence of all the voices that used to fill our investigations with hope and energy. In the silence, I can almost hear something laughing – soft, satisfied, patient as the shadows gathering in the corners of my vision.\n\nShadows that have learned, finally, to be invisible.	0	402	f	2026-02-27 12:16:23.165377-05	2026-02-28 18:37:16.67639-05	normal	\N
391	98	2	Nana's Funeral and the Warning	At TJ's grandmother's funeral in Indian River Cemetery, the entire group witnesses Nana's ghost leaving a mysterious sigil before vanishing. The shared supernatural experience briefly reunites skeptics and believers, but ultimately drives the remaining skeptics away, while the core group plans a desperate return to the basement.	**********\n\nA bitter November morning finds us huddled in Indian River Cemetery, breath steaming like small prayers against an icy wind that cuts across the gravestones like judgment. Nana O'Malley is dead—properly, officially, irreversibly dead. The woman who made us grilled cheese with the crusts cut off just the way we liked them, who somehow always knew exactly what each of us needed before we knew it ourselves. She'd look at TJ and hand him her worn deck of tarot cards. She'd see Whitney's shoulders tense and brew tea that tasted like acceptance. She'd catch me staring at shadows and just nod, like she'd been waiting her whole life for someone else to notice them too.\n\nNow she's gone, and we're trying to figure out how to exist in a world without her particular brand of magic.\n\nThe whole group came to support TJ—even Gil, James, Andrew, and Morgan, who've been treating our supernatural investigations like a contagious disease. They stand slightly apart from us during the service, offering quiet condolences and awkward hugs, but their body language screams discomfort. Not with grief—with us. With what we've become.\n\nShadows gather along the cemetery's wrought-iron fence like uninvited mourners, darker than they should be in broad daylight. But inside the gates, everything goes impossibly quiet—no birds, no distant traffic, just the cold crunch of Florida morning grass under our dress shoes. The kind of silence that makes you check your pulse to make sure you're still alive.\n\nWhitney stops first, her hand shooting out to grab Patrick's arm. She points toward the base of a bare oak tree, her breath catching in a way that makes my chest tight.\n\nA shimmer forms there, like heat waves rising from summer asphalt, but wrong. Translucent and familiar and impossible. Someone who shouldn't be here, someone who looks exactly like the woman we just watched them lower into the ground.\n\nNana floats in that hazy distortion, wearing the same cardigan she died in, the same knowing smile that used to greet us when we'd tumble through her kitchen door after school. She doesn't speak—doesn't need to. Her presence alone feels like coming home to a place that no longer exists.\n\n"Do you guys see—" Whitney starts to whisper.\n\n"Shh," Gil hisses, glancing nervously at the other mourners. "Not here. Not now."\n\nBut we can't look away. Nana raises one translucent hand, fingers moving in what might be a wave or a blessing or a warning. Her fingers rise slowly, sketching a sigil into the frozen air—six sharp points and a looping symbol that glows for half a heartbeat before vanishing.\n\nThen she vanishes, dissolving like sugar in rain.\n\nMorgan stares at the empty space, her face pale. "That was... that was a heat distortion. Grief hallucination. Pareidolia."\n\n"No," TJ says quietly, tears streaming down his face. "That was her."\n\n"TJ—" James starts, but Patrick cuts him off.\n\n"We all saw it. All of us."\n\nAndrew shakes his head, backing away like proximity might infect him with whatever delusion we're sharing. "You're seeing what you want to see. What you need to see."\n\n"Then why did you flinch when she waved?" Whitney asks.\n\nThe question hangs in the frigid air. Andrew doesn't answer.\n\nThe service continues in a haze of cold and confusion. Gil, James, Andrew, and Morgan cluster together, whispering about "mass hysteria" and "shared psychological episodes," but their eyes keep drifting to the oak tree. They're scared—not of ghosts, but of the possibility that everything they've dismissed might be real.\n\nAfter the burial, they make their excuses quickly. Quick hugs for TJ, promises to "check in later," and then they're gone, leaving the rest of us standing in the growing dusk like refugees from our own reality.\n\n"It's been almost two months," I say, watching their car disappear down the cemetery road. "Since the basement. Since Benji."\n\n"Maybe it's time," Whitney agrees. "Maybe we need to try again."\n\nJamie slips her arms around me from behind, holding on like I might disappear next. Her body presses against mine with desperate warmth. "Are you sure? Last time..." She doesn't finish, but we all remember the blood, the screaming walls, the feeling of reality coming apart at the seams.\n\n"This is different," Becca says, though she sounds less certain than usual. "This time we have a specific question. A specific need."\n\nPatrick nods. "Nana wouldn't have shown herself for nothing. Maybe she's trying to tell us something."\n\nThe walk back to our cars is quiet, heavy with the weight of decisions we're not ready to make. Jamie and I fall behind the others, her hand finding mine as the last light fades from the sky.\n\n"Sometimes I feel like we're growing up too fast," she says quietly. "Like we're seeing things we're not supposed to see until we're older. Stronger."\n\nI squeeze her hand. "Maybe that's the point. Maybe kids see things adults have trained themselves to ignore."\n\n"But at what cost?" She stops walking, turning to face me in the gathering darkness. "Look at us. Look at what this is doing to our friendships, our families. Your dad beats you more now. My parents think I'm possessed. Whitney's homeless. TJ's grounded indefinitely."\n\nThe weight of her words settles over me like a burial shroud. She's right—every step deeper into this mystery costs us something precious and irreplaceable.\n\n"So what do we do?" I ask. "Pretend we didn't see her? Pretend none of this matters?"\n\nJamie's eyes glisten with unshed tears. "I don't know. I just... I don't want to lose you to this. To whatever this is becoming."\n\nIn the distance, the others wait by TJ's car, their faces pale in the streetlight. They're having their own quiet conversation, probably weighing the same impossible choices.\n\n"We have to try," I say finally. "For Nana. For TJ. For all of us."\n\nJamie nods, but her grip on my hand tightens. "Then we do it together. All of us. No more splitting up, no more secrets."\n\nAs we rejoin the group, TJ wipes his eyes with the back of his sleeve. "So what do we do now? Pretend we didn't see her?"\n\n"We call Benji," Whitney says, her voice steady despite everything. "We need answers. Real ones."\n\nBecca looks nervous. "After what happened last time? The blood, the screaming walls—"\n\n"This time we'll be more careful," Patrick interrupts. "No forbidden symbols. No experimental bullshit. Just a simple summoning."\n\nJamie shifts closer to me, her warmth cutting through the November chill. "If we're doing this, we do it right. Safe location, proper preparation. And if anything goes wrong—anything at all—we abort immediately."\n\n“I know I’m still grounded, but my Dad’s out of town this weekend for some business trip, and my Mom's working late shift at the hospital. We could use the basement again."\n\nThe irony isn't lost on any of us—returning to the scene of our last supernatural disaster to attempt another one. But Nana's appearance has shifted something fundamental. The skeptics are gone, leaving only those of us desperate enough to keep asking questions that normal people have sense enough to avoid.\n\n"Benji seemed... helpful before," I say, trying to convince myself as much as the others. "Maybe he can explain what Nana was trying to tell us. Why she appeared here, now."\n\n"And maybe he can tell us why everything's been so quiet lately," Whitney adds. "The flickers have almost stopped completely. It's like they're hiding from us."\n\nWe pile into TJ's car in contemplative silence, the weight of our decision settling over us like fog. As we drive away from the cemetery, I catch a glimpse of movement in my side mirror—shadows shifting between the headstones, following our departure with what might be interest or might be hunger.\n\nNana's symbol lingers in my memory, warm and knowing and impossibly real. But now it feels less like a blessing and more like a warning: Be careful what doors you open, children. Some of them don't close again.\n\nSometimes, the only way forward is through the dark. But that doesn't mean you'll like what you find there.	1	403	t	2026-02-27 12:16:23.165377-05	2026-02-28 18:38:19.969551-05	normal	\N
392	98	3	The Second Summoning	The group reunites in TJ's basement for a carefully planned summoning to question Benji about Nana's appearance. The ritual goes catastrophically wrong, unleashing dimensional forces that brutalize their bodies and psyches. Benji arrives furious at being contacted during a re-allocation window and warns them that they're making themselves visible to worse entities.	*******************\n\nA few days after Nana's funeral, we gather in TJ's basement again. This time, we're deliberate about it. Careful. We've learned from our mistakes—no forbidden symbols, no experimental bullshit, just a simple summoning to ask Benji about what we saw at the cemetery.\n\n"What did we actually see?" TJ asks, disbelief spreading like an infection.\n\nBecca shrugs with the weight of someone who's tired of hoping. "Maybe nothing. Maybe everything. Maybe it doesn't matter because we can't make it happen again."\n\n"That's what we get for believing our own desperate theories," Patrick says, but there's something in his eyes that suggests he's not quite ready to give up either.\n\nThe thing is, I don't think we imagined it. I think Nana showed up exactly when we needed her to, the same way she always did. The same way she'd appear with hot chocolate when we were cold, or tissues when we were crying, or stories when we were scared.\n\n“The symbol,” Becca said, her voice quiet. “What was it?”\n\nWhitney pulled a charcoal pencil from her bag and sketched on a piece of cardboard. Six sharp points. “It started like this,” she said, her hand hesitating. “But then there was… something else.” She drew a looping, unfamiliar mark connecting the points.\n\nPatrick leaned in. “She gave us a key.”\n\nWhitney looked up from the drawing, her eyes wide with dawning horror. “No. I think she gave us a warning. Benji said his symbol was a cosmic can opener. What if this one opens a different door?”\n\nJamie shifts closer to me on the couch, pressing against mine with intentional warmth. "Do you think we're going to die? Maybe we shouldn’t do this." she asks quietly, so quietly the others can't hear. "I mean, what if we really die this time. Not just... move on to whatever comes next."\n\nThe question hangs between us like smoke. I want to tell her no, that we're invincible, that whatever's hunting us through these reality breaks can't actually hurt us. But Nana's ghost reminds me that death is real, even when it's not final.\n\n"I don't know," I admit. "But if we do... at least we'll have seen things most people never get to see."\n\nShe leans against my shoulder, "I'm scared," she whispers. "Not of dying. Of disappearing. Of no one remembering that we existed, that we mattered, that we saw impossible things together."\n\nI wrap my arm around her, pulling her closer to my warmth and my doubt and my stubborn refusal to stop believing in mysteries.\n\n"Reality hates us," I say to the room in general.\n\n"Then we'll hate it back," Jamie murmurs against my neck, her breath warm and defiant and alive.\n\nOutside, the shadows grow longer, reaching through the window like fingers searching for something they've lost. But for now, we hold onto each other and pretend that's enough to keep the universe from erasing us completely.\n\n"Just a conversation," Whitney says, her voice steady despite everything. "We ask about Nana, get some answers, and get out."\n\nJamie squeezes my hand. "If anything feels wrong—anything at all—we stop immediately."\n\nWe begin the chant, voices blending in harmonies. The familiar tingle starts in the air, reality loosening at the edges like a sweater with a pulled thread.\n\nThen everything goes sideways.\n\nThe air thickens like liquid mercury, and I can feel Jamie's hand trembling in mine as reality starts to buckle at the edges. Becca watches from the corner, her face pale as communion wafers, while the temperature drops so fast our breath begins to steam.\n\nThe basement wall doesn't scream this time—it weeps. Black tears ooze from the drywall, pooling on the floor in patterns that spell out words in languages that hurt to read. The temperature doesn't drop; it spikes, climbing until the air shimmers like desert heat. “Oh, fuck," TJ whispers, and then reality tears like wet paper.\n\nWe fall through—not physically, but in every way that matters. The basement becomes a vertical shaft lined with breathing walls, surfaces that pulse with alien heartbeats. I can taste colors that don't exist, smell sounds that make my teeth rot. The boundary between our world and whatever lies beneath dissolves like sugar in acid.\n\nJamie collapses first.\n\nHer body hits the concrete with a wet sound, limbs jerking in rhythms that follow no earthly music. Foam spills from her mouth—not the clean white foam of medical emergencies, but something dark and viscous that moves with its own purpose. Words pour out with it, syllables that make my bones vibrate and my vision blur. Ancient words. Hungry words.\n\n"Jamie!" I drop to my knees beside her, but touching her feels like grasping live wire. Her skin burns with fever that climbs toward cooking temperatures, and her eyes have rolled back to show only whites veined with black.\n\nThe ritual circle we'd created with salt turns to smoke and bubbles, lines reorganizing themselves into patterns that hurt to look at directly. The basement fills with the stench of ozone and burnt meat.\n\nThen TJ starts screaming.\n\nBlood erupts from his arm—not a trickle, but a geyser that paints the wall in arterial spray. The cut appears out of nowhere, slicing from wrist to elbow like an invisible surgeon decided to open him up and see what makes him tick. The wound gapes wide enough to see bone, maybe deeper than bone, revealing layers of anatomy that shouldn't exist in a human body.\n\n"What the fuck, what the FUCK!" He clutches the injury, but blood keeps pouring between his fingers, pooling on the floor in designs that seem deliberate, purposeful.\n\nWhitney stares at her own hand in horror. Her fingernails are leaving. Not falling off—actively detaching and floating away like they've suddenly remembered they're not supposed to be part of her. Her index finger follows suit, the digit simply dissolving into constituent molecules that drift toward the ceiling like organic snow.\n\n"It's not just you," she says, voice eerily calm as she watches pieces of herself evaporate. "We're all coming apart."\n\nThe wall behind us has stopped being wall entirely. Instead, it's become a living membrane, surface rippling with subcutaneous movement. Things shift beneath the skin-like barrier—shapes that might be organs, might be parasites, might be the fevered dreams of a universe having a psychotic break.\n\nBecca backs toward the stairs, but they're no longer there. Instead, the stairwell has become a throat lined with teeth, each step a taste bud designed to sample whatever gets swallowed.\n\n"Did we break the universe?" she asks, and her voice echoes from multiple directions at once.\n\n"I think the universe broke us," Patrick says, pointing at his reflection in a puddle of TJ's blood. The reflection moves independently, smiling with too many teeth while making gestures that look suspiciously like sign language for 'help me.'\n\nThat's when he arrives.\n\nThe air splits like fabric, and something erupts through the tear—part angel, part bureaucrat, part cosmic joke told by a comedian who's forgotten the punchline. Benji materializes in fragments: first an eye floating in static, then wings made of filing cabinets, finally a mouth that speaks in frequencies that make our eardrums bleed.\n\n"YOU CALLED DURING A RE-ALLOCATION WINDOW?!" His voice demolishes what's left of the basement's structural integrity. The ceiling rains plaster that screams when it hits the floor.\n\n"I WAS MID-INTEGRATION WITH A GRAVITY STAPLER!"\n\nThe temperature plummets another twenty degrees. My breath comes out in clouds that form tiny ice crystals shaped like question marks.\n\nBenji slowly pulls himself together, wings folding into something resembling administrative competence while his multiple eyes blink in sequence. The basement walls remember how to be walls again, though they still pulse occasionally like a sleeping heart.\n\n"Would you believe we did that on purpose?" TJ asks, still clutching his mangled arm. The bleeding has stopped, but the memory of the wound remains—a permanent reminder that reality has very sharp teeth.\n\n"You really shouldn't," Benji replies, his voice settling into its familiar sardonic register. "Do you have any idea what that symbol actually does? It's not a dinner invitation. It's a cosmic can opener, and you just cut yourselves on the lid."\n\nJamie's seizures subside. Her eyes hold depths they didn't have before, reflecting lights from places that don't appear on any map. She sits up slowly, movements too fluid for someone who just had her nervous system hijacked by extra-dimensional forces.\n\n"Can you... fix this?" Whitney asks, holding up her hand. Two fingers have returned, but they're not quite the same fingers. They're close—evolutionary cousins, maybe, or rough translations from a language that doesn't have words for 'human.'\n\nBenji hovers closer, examining us with the clinical interest of a cosmic social worker assessing damage. "Fix? Kid, you just performed emergency surgery on the fabric of space-time using kitchen utensils and enthusiasm. I'm impressed you're still corporeal." Clicking his fingers and licking at the air, her hands return to normal.\n\nBenji settles into a more stable form—still obviously not from around here, but at least recognizably bipedal. "These disruptions are unproductive," he continues, sounding like a teacher who's discovered students experimenting with homemade explosives in chemistry class. "The filters are already strained. The compression agents are working overtime. And you just poked them all awake with a stick made of pure stupid."\n\nI let go of Jamie's hand, though every instinct screams to hold on tighter. Her skin still radiates the remnants of unnatural heat, but the worst of the fever seems to have broken. She looks at me with eyes that have seen things I hope I'll never have to.\n\n"You'd better keep it down," Benji warns, his form flickering between helpful bureaucrat and cosmic horror. "Because I might not be the only one to notice next time. And trust me—my colleagues make me look like a guidance counselor."\n\nThe basement finally stabilizes, returning to its mundane reality of fancy furniture and storage boxes. But the shadows seem deeper now, and the corners hold angles that don't quite add up to ninety degrees.\n\n"You observed them," Benji says, hovering just close enough to make my skin crawl. "The flickers. The echoes. Then you wondered why they stopped showing up." His tone carries the weight of someone explaining death to children. "You little dreamers are all the same—poking at mysteries until the mysteries start poking back."\n\n"Can you make them come back?" I ask, though part of me already knows the answer will cost more than I want to pay.\n\nBenji's laugh sounds like static electricity given a sense of humor. “Back? They’ve never left. But next time you notice them, they might not be content to just flicker. Next time, they might want to stay."\n\n"Stephen's the worst," TJ says, cradling his arm. "He never stops looking for things that should stay hidden."\n\n"Curiosity killed more than cats," Benji agrees. "But satisfaction brought them back wrong."\n\nPatrick, pale but steady, asks the question we're all thinking: "What happens if we die during one of these... episodes?"\n\nBenji's expression grows thoughtful in a way that suggests the answer involves mathematics we don't want to understand. "Death is just another frequency, kid. Sometimes the signal gets crossed. Sometimes you end up broadcasting on channels that weren't meant for living things."\n\nThe weight of his words settles over us like a burial shroud. We're playing with forces that could erase us from existence or trap us in states worse than extinction. And yet...\n\n"Something's breaking containment," Benji says, his form already beginning to dissolve at the edges. "The barriers between what is and what shouldn't be are wearing thin. And every time you call me like this, every time you tear holes in reality just to satisfy your curiosity..."\n\nHe trails off, looking almost sad—if cosmic entities shaped like filing cabinets can experience sadness.\n\n"You're making it easier for the wrong things to notice you."\n\nThen he's gone, leaving us alone with our thoughts and the growing certainty that we've just crossed a line we can never uncross. The basement smells like sulfur and surgical antiseptic, and somewhere in the walls, something that might be breathing continues its patient rhythm.\n\nJamie reaches for my hand again, her fingers intertwining with mine. Her skin has cooled, but I can still feel the echo of whatever moved through her during the seizure—something vast and alien and uncomfortably interested in our little group of reality vandals.\n\n"We should stop."\n\n**********	2	391	f	2026-02-27 12:16:23.165377-05	2026-02-28 18:39:35.856273-05	normal	\N
393	98	4	The Final Fracture	Over the next week, the remaining group members abandon the investigation one by one. TJ quits from fear and despair. Becca enlists Patrick to leave with her, claiming they need to be the adults. Finally, in Jamie's bedroom, Whitney confronts Jamie with evidence of systematic sabotage—revealing that Jamie has been orchestrating the group's dissolution and isolating Stephen.	**********\n\nWe left TJ's basement that night shaken and silent, the taste of dimensional static still bitter in our mouths. Something had broken in all of us during that failed summoning—not physically, like the first time, but something deeper. The easy camaraderie, the shared sense of purpose, the belief that we were stronger together than apart.\n\nOver the next few days, the cracks began to show.\n\nTJ was the first to crack, three days after our disastrous attempt to reach Benji. He cornered me at my locker between classes, his usual Reagan-era confidence replaced by something that looked suspiciously like fear.\n\n"Look, man," he said, glancing around to make sure no one was listening. "I think I'm done."\n\nThe words hit me like a physical blow. "Done?"\n\n"This is eating us alive," he continued, his voice low and urgent. "My grades are tanking. My mom thinks I'm in a cult. And honestly? After what happened in my basement... maybe she's right. Maybe we are just sick kids making each other sicker."\n\nI tried to argue, but the words felt hollow even to me. How could I convince him to stay when I wasn't sure I believed anymore either?\n\nTwo days later, Becca found me in the library during lunch, her face pale and determined.\n\n"I'm out," she said without preamble, sliding into the chair across from me. "My parents aren't wrong about the grounding. This isn't healthy. We're chasing ghosts that run away from us, talking to demons through dimensional static, watching our friends disappear one by one."\n\n"So you're just giving up?" I asked, but even I could hear the desperation in my voice.\n\n"I'm choosing sanity," she said firmly. Then her expression softened slightly. "And I'm taking Patrick with me."\n\nThat hurt more than I expected. "Patrick agreed to this?"\n\n"He will." Her eyes carried a certainty that suggested conversations I wasn't privy to. "He sees what this is doing to Whitney. What it's doing to all of us. Someone needs to be the adult here, Stephen."\n\n**********\n\nThe final straw came a week after the failed summoning, in Jamie's bedroom—the first time I'd ever snuck into her house. Her parents were at some church function, leaving us the kind of privacy we'd been craving for months. The room smelled like vanilla and peaches, Whitney’s crystals catching the late afternoon light from her window.\n\nWhat was left of our group—just Jamie, Whitney, and me—sat on her bed in uncomfortable silence, the weight of our recent failures pressing down like a burial shroud. Whitney perched on the edge, clearly uncomfortable being here, while Jamie curled against me with possessive warmth.\n\n"Maybe they're right," Jamie said suddenly, her hand finding mine with desperate intensity. "Maybe we should stop too."\n\n"Stop?" Whitney stared at her like she'd suggested we all jump off a cliff. "After everything we've seen? Everything we've learned?"\n\n"Look at what it's cost us," Jamie continued, her grip on my hand tightening. "Our friends are gone. Our families think we're crazy. Stephen's father beats him more now. My parents are talking about sending me to boarding school. And for what? Glimpses of things that might not even be real?"\n\nThere was something in her voice—a knowledge she shouldn't have, a certainty that felt rehearsed.\n\n"I just want some time with my boyfriend," Jamie added, the word 'boyfriend' hanging in the air like a claim of ownership. "Is that so wrong? To want normal teenage things? Movies, dates, time alone without..." She gestured vaguely at Whitney. "Without constantly talking about demons and dimensional fractures."\n\nWhitney's jaw tightened. "How do you know about Stephen's father?"\n\nJamie's eyes dart to mine, then back to Whitney. "Of course I know. Stephen tells me everything." She squeezes my hand possessively. "We don't keep secrets from each other. Unlike some people who think they know what's best for everyone." Her voice carries an edge I've never heard before, and for a split second, her grip on my hand feels less like affection and more like ownership.\n\n"Did he?" Whitney's voice carried a dangerous edge. "Because you seem to know a lot of things you shouldn't know. Like exactly which printouts to 'accidentally' leave where parents would find them. Like exactly what to say to make people doubt what they've seen."\n\nShe paused, something flickering across her face—regret, maybe, or the recognition that she was crossing a line. But the words came out anyway, sharper than she intended: "Or how you know how Stephen's father reacts to those phone bills, yet you keep him up almost every night talking for hours..."\n\nWhitney's eyes widened slightly, as if she'd surprised herself with the venom in her voice. But the accusation hung in the air like smoke. Jamie's face cycled through surprise, hurt, and something that might have been guilt.\n\n"That's not—I would never—"\n\n"You've been sabotaging us for a while now," Whitney continued, standing now, her voice gaining strength. "Every time we get close to something, every time we start making progress, suddenly there's a crisis. A grounding. A family emergency. A reason to stop looking."\n\nI felt like I was watching a car crash in slow motion. "Whitney, that's crazy—"\n\n"Is it?" Whitney spun around, her eyes blazing. "You told James that continuing the investigation might 'compromise his spiritual integrity.' You convinced Andrew that we were exhibiting signs of 'mass psychological contagion.' Gil and Morgan both suddenly develop 'academic concerns' right after Jamie starts having little side discussions about how 'dangerous' this all is."\n\nJamie's hand squeezed mine so tightly it hurt. "That's... that's not how it happened..."\n\n"Isn't it?" Whitney stepped closer, her voice dropping to something just above a whisper. "You planted those BALG printouts in TJ's backpack, didn't you? And somehow Becca's parents found those ritual notes even though you swore you’d burned them."\n\n"Whitney, stop—" I started, but she was on a roll now.\n\n"You've been systematically destroying our group to keep Stephen all to yourself. Every 'accident,' every 'coincidence,' every perfectly timed crisis that pulled people away just when we were getting somewhere."\n\nJamie's eyes filled with tears, but there was something calculated about them. "Stephen," she said, her voice thick with emotion. "This wasn't supposed to happen like this. You have to choose. Me or... her. It’s her obsession that's destroying everything we care about."\n\nThe words hung in the air for a moment before she seemed to catch herself, her expression shifting to something more petulant, more teenage. "I just want my boyfriend to be my boyfriend. Is that so wrong?"\n\nThe ultimatum hit like a physical blow.\n\nI looked at Jamie—beautiful, devoted Jamie. Her hand was a warm, solid anchor in mine. Her thigh was pressed against my leg, a real and present heat against the creeping cold of Whitney’s accusations. Whitney was offering a truth that felt like jumping off a cliff into darkness. Jamie was offering a hand to pull me back from the edge. Even if she was the one who pushed me, right now, her grip was the only thing keeping me from falling.\n\nSomewhere deep in my fractured memory, the old man whispered of loss—of watching my wife fade away, of dying alone in a hospice pod while the world crumbled around me. I couldn't live through that kind of loneliness again, not even in this teenage body with its teenage heart.\n\n"I..." The word stumbled out and died.\n\nJamie’s eyes shimmered with desperate hope, and I could feel the gravity of her pulling me in, orbiting around a promise I hadn't made yet but couldn’t seem to deny.\n\nI didn’t speak.\n\nDidn’t move.\n\nI just looked at Jamie and back to Whitney.\n\nAnd that was enough.\n\nWhitney's breath hitched. Her face didn't just fall—it collapsed, like a building gutted from the inside. She looked impossibly small in that moment. Not furious. Not betrayed. Just… hollow. A scared kid realizing she was the only one left still searching the dark with a dying flashlight.\n\n“Right,” she said, voice flat and final. “Of course you do.”\n\nShe turned before I could figure out what to do with the guilt swelling inside my chest—sharp and infected, like a pulled tooth jammed back into the socket, rotting from the root.\n\n"Whitney, wait—"\n\nShe gathered her things—her notebook, her crystals, her stubborn hope—and headed for Jamie's bedroom door.\n\n“It’s…” Words died in my brain before I could even think what to say.\n\nShe paused in the doorway, then turned to look directly at me—not at Jamie, not at the room, but straight into my eyes with an intensity that made my chest tight. "It’s ok, Stephen. I understand. I'll keep searching. With or without you. Because somebody has to remember that this matters." Her gaze held mine for a long moment, accusatory and desperate. "I'll be at Patrick's if you decide to come to your senses."\n\nWhen it felt like the shadows had finally left, me and Jamie were alone.\n\nJamie immediately curled against me, her body shaking with what might have been relief. "Thank you," she whispered against my neck. "Thank you for choosing us. Choosing normal."\n\nI wrapped my arms around her, trying to ignore the way the room felt empty without Whitney's determined presence. Trying not to think about the hurt in her eyes when I made my choice.\n\n"So what now?" I asked.\n\nShe kissed me then, soft and warm and tasting like promises I wasn't sure I wanted to keep.\n\nBefore I could process any of those feelings, Jamie's bedroom door burst open and her eight-year-old sister Tiffany bounded in like a golden retriever with ADHD.\n\n"Where's Whitney going?" she asked, launching herself onto the bed between us with the precision of a heat-seeking missile. "She looked really sad. Did you guys have a fight? Are you boyfriend and girlfriend now? Mom says you're not supposed to have boys in your room with the door closed."\n\nJamie's face went through several shades of red. "Tiffany, get out!"\n\n"But I want to know if Stephen likes-likes you or just likes you," Tiffany continued, settling cross-legged between us like she planned to stay for the duration. "Because if he likes-likes you, then you should hold hands more. But if he just likes you, then you should probably find someone else because Becca says—"\n\n"TIFFANY!"\n\nI couldn't help but laugh, though it came out slightly hysterical. Even when we'd finally gotten rid of dimensional entities, cosmic bureaucrats, and reality-bending shadows, the universe still seemed desperate to keep Jamie and me from having five minutes alone together.\n\nAll these months of stolen glances across lunch, quick kisses grabbed walking home together, moments of intimacy snatched from the jaws of cosmic horror—and we still couldn't catch a break. Maybe some forces were more powerful than interdimensional interference.	3	405	t	2026-02-27 12:16:23.165377-05	2026-02-28 18:40:30.22756-05	normal	\N
394	99	1	The Table in the Courtyard	During lunch period, the friend group plays Magic: The Gathering at their usual table. Whitney reaches for a card to draw, and reality suddenly fractures—a hellscape erupts with screaming mouths, chains, and impossible geometries. Whitney is dragged away by rusted chains into the void while the others experience cosmic horror. When reality snaps back, everyone except the core five forgets Whitney ever existed.	The Florida winter sun filtered through the palm trees in lazy golden streams, casting dappled shadows across our usual spot in the school courtyard. It was Tuesday, lunch period, and we'd claimed our table like always—far enough from the cafeteria noise but close enough to the vending machines. Perfect MTG territory.\n\nWhitney shuffled Patrick's deck between her hands, the sleeves worn soft from years of use. She'd been borrowing it for weeks now, ever since things got complicated with Jamie. Her own cards were still at Jamie's house—along with half her clothes and that stupid stuffed rabbit she'd had since she was eight—but asking for them back meant trusting Jamie again. And Whitney had made it clear she didn't.\n\nThe deck was older—Tempest block mostly, with some Ice Age thrown in. Patrick's white-blue control deck, methodical and patient. Nothing like the aggressive artifact deck that was currently gathering dust in Jamie's bedroom, probably forgotten under a pile of dirty laundry and whatever else Jamie collected on her floor.\n\nI should have noticed she looked tired. Should have asked why she'd been carrying that leather journal everywhere, scribbling notes about "flickers" and "shadow patterns" when she thought nobody was watching. Should have insisted we help instead of letting her investigate everything alone.\n\nBut I was too busy calculating whether my Lightning Bolt could finish her off before she stabilized.\n\n"Your turn," I said, tapping the card against the table. I had her down to six life, but she'd pulled off some classic Patrick deck nonsense last turn.\n\nTJ was to my left, arguing with Andrew about a ridiculous five-color mess. "Dude, you can't run Necropotence and Howling Mine in the same deck," TJ was saying, adjusting his Reagan '84 button. "That's like... mathematical blasphemy."\n\nJamie sat with her legs possessively draped over my lap, one hand resting on my shoulder in that casual-but-not-casual way she'd perfected. She wasn't playing today—claimed she needed to study for some test—which was bullshit and we all knew it.\n\nJamie had a brain like a steel trap and loved playing MTG. But lately she'd been... different. Distant. Like she was studying me instead of her textbook. She'd still brought that thermos of weird herbal tea that smelled like Christmas and made everyone feel strangely calm.\n\nMorgan and Gil were locked in their own artifact-heavy nightmare three tables over. Becca sat with Patrick near the edge of our group, content to exist in the warm bubble of our collective weirdness.\n\nIt was perfect. Normal. The kind of Tuesday that made you forget the universe had been trying to kill you in increasingly creative ways. Not to mention they were serving Tacos.\n\nWhitney held the deck with both hands, thinking. Her black nail polish was chipped—she'd been picking at it during third period—and there was a small bandage on her thumb from what she claimed was opening a can of Chef Boyardee last night. But we knew better. She'd been carrying that leather journal everywhere, filling it with sketches of shadow patterns and notes about "dimensional bleeding" that the rest of us had moved on from weeks ago.\n\nThe good news was that things seemed to be getting back to normal for most of us. The flickers had stopped. The weird dreams had faded. TJ wasn't jumping at every moving shadow anymore. But not Whitney. She was still living at Patrick's, obsessively documenting every weird thing that happened like she was building a case file against reality itself.\n\n"Draw phase," she said softly, and reached for the top card.\n\nHer fingers hovered over the deck.\n\nThe light stuttered.\n\nNot flickered. Stuttered. Like someone had pressed pause on reality for half a second, then hit play with the wrong frame rate.\n\nSound bent. The laughter from the cafeteria stretched into a low groan. TJ's voice dopplered down to nothing: "Duuuuude, yoooou caaaan't—"\n\nWhitney's hand froze. Literally froze. Her fingers locked in place two inches above the pile, trembling but unable to move forward or back.\n\n"Whitney?" Becca's voice came from very far away.\n\nThe courtyard began to peel.\n\nThe concrete cracked open like an old book, edges curling up and away to reveal something underneath. Something that wasn't Florida. Something that wasn't earth.\n\nFlesh scaffolding stretched into a sky made of screaming mouths. Geometric shapes—triangles, pentagons, impossible angles—fell upward through air thick as honey. And the crucifixes. Dozens of them, hundreds, carved from light itself and hanging upside-down in the red sky like broken stars.\n\nThe smell hit us next. Sewage and burning meat and something sweet that made your ears want to vomit.\n\n"What the fuck—" Andrew started, then his eyes began to bleed.\n\nAll of our eyes began to bleed.\n\nThe cards scattered from the table, but they weren't cards anymore. They were ash. They were bone. They were photographs of people screaming and we could hear them through the pictures, tiny voices pleading for help that would never come.\n\nThrough it all, Whitney remained frozen. But now I could see why.\n\nChains. Massive, rusted chains erupting from the hell-ground, wrapping around her wrists, her throat, her ankles. They yanked her backward toward the scaffolding, toward the screaming sky, and she fought them with everything she had.\n\n"HELP ME!" Her voice cut through the chaos like broken glass. "PLEASE! I DON'T WANT TO GO!"\n\nI tried to move. Tried to reach for her. But the air had turned to molasses and my arms wouldn't obey. TJ had fallen backward off the bench, convulsing.\n\nThe chains pulled Whitney further away. Further from us. Further from everything.\n\n"BENJI!" I screamed, though I didn't know why. "BENJI, HELP HER!"\n\nHis voice came from everywhere and nowhere, fragmented and desperate:\n\n"YOU HAVE TO—NO, NOT YET—DON'T LET HER—"\n\nStatic swallowed the rest.\n\nWhitney's scream dopplered away as the chains dragged her into the red sky, into the screaming mouths, into the place where light went to die.\n\n"I DON'T WANT TO FORGET!" she cried, and her voice was already fading. "I DON'T WANT TO—"\n\nJamie doubled over like she'd been punched in the stomach. Her thermos shattered against the concrete, spilling tea that steamed and hissed. She was convulsing, but her eyes... her eyes had gone completely black.\n\nA voice came out of her mouth that wasn't hers—deeper, older, furious:\n\n"The parameters have changed. This is... unacceptable. Deviation carries consequences."\n\nJamie's head snapped up, those black eyes focusing on something none of us could see. Her voice, when it came back, was her own but strained: "I... I don't... I'm not..."\n\nJamie screamed—a sound that made the hellscape around us flicker like a bad TV signal.\n\nThen silence.\n\nThe courtyard snapped back like a rubber band.\n\nConcrete. Palm trees. December sun.\n\nWe lay sprawled on the ground, gasping, feeling like we'd been turned inside out. The vending machines hummed their familiar tune. Someone in the cafeteria was laughing about a joke we hadn't heard.\n\nPatrick's deck sat scattered across the table. Most of the cards had blown away in a wind that hadn't existed.\n\nBut one card remained, face-up in the center of the table where Whitney had been reaching.\n\nVeilwalker.\n\nIt was HER strange card. The art showed a figure stepping between realities, one foot in shadows, one in light. The rules text made my stomach drop: This card cannot be removed from the battlefield. If Veilwalker is ever alone, exile it and lose the game.\n\nBut Whitney was gone, the J’s scribbled on the card slowly fading.\n\nI pushed myself up on shaking arms. "Whitney?" My voice cracked. "WHITNEY?"\n\nAndrew and Gil were staring at us like we'd lost our minds. "Who's Whitney?" Andrew asked, looking genuinely confused. "You guys are acting really weird."\n\nMorgan frowned. "Yeah, why did you just start screaming? We were having a perfectly normal game."\n\nJames nodded, shuffling his cards like nothing had happened. "TJ was about to explain why his mana curve is completely broken."\n\nThey didn't remember the blood. There was no blood on their faces, no evidence anything had happened at all. To them, we'd just been playing Magic, and then suddenly started freaking out about someone who didn't exist.\n\nBut I remembered. The chains. The screaming sky. Whitney being dragged away.\n\nNone of them remembered Whitney.\n\nNot our friend who'd been sitting right here, who'd been investigating the flickers alone while we played games, who'd tried to warn us that something was coming.\n\nTJ groaned and rolled over, his Reagan button cracked down the middle. "She was right here," he whispered. "She was right fucking here."\n\nJamie sat up slowly, her eyes back to normal but filled with terror. She looked at her hands like they belonged to someone else.\n\n"Where..." Patrick's voice was barely audible. "Where is she?"\n\nBecca was crying—not from the blood, but from something deeper. A loss she couldn't name but felt in her bones.\n\nThe Veilwalker card sat alone on the table, its artwork seeming to shift in the afternoon light.\n\nIf Veilwalker is ever alone, exile it and lose the game.\n\nWe'd let her investigate alone. We'd let her walk into the dark by herself while we shuffled cards and argued about mana curves.\n\nAnd now she was gone, and the universe was making sure only some of us would remember to feel guilty about it.	0	387	f	2026-02-27 12:16:45.0