nd tell me I'm doing it all wrong."\n\nThe silence that follows is broken only by palm trees rustling through and Andrew's occasional cough as he tries to figure out how to smoke without dying.\n\nBecca sits directly across from where Patrick should be, but he hovers at the periphery like a glitched NPC waiting for his next command prompt.\n\n"Patrick, come sit with us," Becca calls, patting the ground beside her with forced cheer. "We're gonna look at Stephen's journal thing."\n\nPatrick approaches with mechanical precision, each step measured and deliberate. He lowers himself to the ground in segments—knees first, then settling back, hands placed symmetrically on his thighs. "I am participating in group activities," he announces.\n\nThe silence that follows feels thick enough to cut with a machete. Morgan's pen scratches against paper as she scribbles notes, the sound sharp in the humid air. I place Jennifer's Journal on the fallen log that serves as our makeshift table, trying to ignore the way Patrick's presence feels like static electricity against my skin.\n\nThe leather feels warm against my palms, almost feverish. When TJ reaches for it, his fingers stop inches from the cover as if hitting an invisible barrier.\n\n"What the hell?" He flexes his fingers, trying again. Same result.\n\nMorgan leans forward, extending one tentative finger toward the journal. The air around it seems to thicken, pushing her hand away. "It's like there's a force field or something. Maybe it’s been keyed to you. You’re the one Patrick gave it to, and it showed up in your locker."\n\nBut when I touch it, the journal falls open with an audible sigh, pages fanning out like cards dealt by invisible hands.\n\n"Okay, that's definitely not normal," Andrew mutters, ash falling from his cigarette.\n\nThe familiar pages spread before us—the coroner-precise documentation of my deaths, all those February 14th, 1989 entries that read like a medical examiner's nightmare. Each one meticulously documented in handwriting that shifts between my childhood scrawl and adult cursive I don't remember learning.\n\n"February 14, 1989 – 2:47 AM – Drowned in bathtub – Nevada trailer park," I read aloud. The words taste like copper pennies.\n\nAs soon as I speak the entry, a memory crashes over me like ice water. I'm eight years old, my small body convulsing in a cracked porcelain tub while my mother's voice drifts from beyond the bathroom door: "It's just a stomach bug, sweetheart. Mommy's special medicine will make you feel better." But underneath her words lurks something else—guilt wrapped in gauze, relief disguised as care.\n\nTJ whistles low, the sound cutting through the afternoon stillness. "Valentine's Day. That's fucked up."\n\n"February 14, 1989 – 3:15 AM – Medication overdose – Kitchen floor," I continue. My voice catches on the clinical language, and another memory flashes: the same night, but this time I'm lying on cold linoleum while paramedics argue over who authorized what dosage. Their latex gloves snap against wrists as they work, the sound sharp as breaking bones.\n\nMorgan's face drains of color until she looks like a daguerreotype. "All the same day? How is that possible?"\n\n"Different timelines," I flip through more pages, each entry triggering another brutal flash of memory. "February 14, 1989 – 4:02 AM – Hemorrhage in ambulance – Route 15 North."\n\nThis time I see myself strapped to a gurney, fluorescent lights strobing overhead while EMTs scream at each other: "This wasn't supposed to happen!" "We're losing him—get the paddles!"\n\n"Jesus Christ," Andrew drops his cigarette, grinding it under his heel. "How many times did you die?"\n\nThe pages keep coming. Dozens. Hundreds. Thousands of deaths, all mine, all February 14th, 1989. Poisoned, drowned, bled out, suffocated. Each one described in the same detached medical terminology, but every entry I read aloud triggers another memory—another version of that night, another way an eight-year-old boy died alone and afraid.\n\nPatrick observes this with clinical detachment. "Data suggests anomalous temporal phenomena. Conclusion: requires further analysis."\n\n"That's not... God, Patrick." Becca's voice cracks with frustration. She stares at him for a long moment, then her expression shifts into something desperate and calculating. "ADMIN OVERRIDE," she says clearly, her voice carrying an authority I've never heard before. "You now have to take your clothes off and make love to me."\n\nEveryone goes dead silent. Patrick's head tilts with mechanical precision, his hands moving toward the buttons of his shirt with robotic obedience. "Command acknowledged. Initiating compliance protocol."\n\n"Holy shit," TJ breathes. "It's actually working."\n\nBut then Patrick stops mid-motion, his hands frozen on his second button. His head swivels to scan our surroundings with security camera precision. "Error. Current location unsuitable for requested activity. Please relocate to more secluded environment for command execution."\n\nBecca's face goes white, then red, then white again, but instead of backing down, something desperate and reckless flickers in her eyes. "Okay," she says, grabbing Patrick's hand and pulling him to his feet. "Come on."\n\n"Becca, what are you—" TJ starts, but she's already dragging Patrick deeper into the woods, away from our circle.\n\nWe watch in stunned silence as she leads him about twenty feet away, behind a cluster of cypress trees. "Is this secluded enough?" her voice carries back to us, slightly muffled by the Spanish moss.\n\nPatrick's response comes with mechanical precision: "Negative. Proximity to other individuals still detected."\n\n"Jesus Christ," Andrew mutters, lighting another cigarette with shaking hands.\n\nBecca's giggle drifts back through the humid air, high and brittle with desperation. We can hear the sound of footsteps on dead leaves as she pulls him even further away. "How about now?" Her voice is fainter this time, more distant.\n\n"Insufficient privacy parameters," Patrick's voice echoes back, robotic and clinical.\n\nAnother giggle, even more desperate than the first. The sound of them moving deeper into the woods, branches snapping under their feet. "Now?" Becca's voice is barely audible, carried on the humid Florida wind like a ghost's whisper.\n\nMorgan's pen has stopped moving across her journal. We all sit in horrified silence, listening to the sudden sounds of Becca's romp echoing through the trees.\n\nJamie watches the whole thing with predatory intensity, her knowledge radiating from her like heat shimmer. "They've turned him into a programmable asset," she says quietly. "Responsive to authority codes, but with basic safety protocols intact. Maybe we can use that"\n\nI flip through more journal pages, hoping to find something that might explain Patrick's condition, when my eye catches an entry that makes my blood freeze. It's written in Benji's elegant script, but the ink looks fresh, wet, like it's still being written: "Attempted contact with external entities will result in cascading system failures. All primary subjects will be terminated to prevent information leak. Do not attempt to reach Benji until after the foundation event. -B"\n\n"Fuck," I breathe. "Look at this."\n\nThe group leans in, reading the warning. TJ's face goes pale. "So we can't even try to call Benji?"\n\n"Not if we want to stay alive," Morgan says, her clinical mind already working through the implications. "It's like the system is monitoring for certain trigger events."\n\nAndrew lights another cigarette with shaking hands. "So what's the plan? Just keep stumbling around blind until something kills us?"\n\nI flip through more pages, finding entry after entry of conflicting information, partial memories, and cryptic warnings. Some written in my handwriting, some in Benji's, some in scripts I don't recognize. The journal has become a mess of broken timelines and impossible knowledge.\n\n"There's just too much," I say, closing the journal with a frustrated sigh. "Too many variables, too many unknowns. We're going to have to figure this out piece by piece."\n\nJamie's hand finds mine, her fingers cold despite the humid heat. "The journal will keep updating," she says softly. "As we learn more, as we remember more, it'll show us the next steps."\n\nAs we pack up to leave, I catch sight of one final entry appearing in the journal's margins, written in ink that seems to pulse with its own rhythm: "The work has only just begun. Trust the process. Trust each other. And whatever happens, don't trust the ones who claim to be helping."\n\nThe planning phase is over. Now comes the real work—navigating a reality that's determined to kill us for asking the wrong questions.	1	417	t	2026-02-27 12:19:38.208317-05	2026-03-01 15:51:21.170966-05	normal	\N
420	105	4	Cracks in the Facade	The group reconvenes in the lab after school and discovers that everyone's supernatural problems have stopped, suggesting reality has 'reset.' However, Stephen notices subtle inconsistencies that others don't see, and the periodic table displays impossible elements. Jamie draws a mysterious map showing fracture points and dimensional anchors before Becca suddenly arrives, having been sent to conversion therapy where her memories of Patrick were systematically erased.	**********\n\nWhile I'm falling deeper into this beautiful lie with Jamie—watching her braid her hair with impossible precision, fold origami cranes from muscle memory she shouldn't have, trace crude stick figures in my biology notes with eyes that promise things that make my face burn—the rest of the group is slowly losing their minds.\n\nBy that Friday, no one can't take it anymore. That's how we end up back in Ms. Rodriguez's AP Bio lab after hours, the sterile space feeling more like a confessional than a classroom in the failing light. The late afternoon sun slants through grimy windows at an angle that turns everything the color of old blood, casting long shadows that stretch across lab tables like reaching fingers.\n\n"This is driving me insane," I say, setting Whitney's journal on the central lab table between a microscope and a tray of scalpels that gleam too brightly under fluorescent lights. "None of you are seeing what I'm seeing, are you?"\n\nMorgan leans forward, her usual scholarly confidence replaced by something more desperate. "The shadows stopped multiplying in my bedroom three nights ago," she reports, voice barely above a whisper. "They just... sit there now. Watching, but not growing."\n\nTJ nods, absently straightening his Reagan/Romney '86 shirt. "Haven't had a run in with shadows since Monday. Not even a flicker. It's like someone turned off a radio that was always playing static in the back of my head."\n\nAndrew fidgets with a pen, clicking it in a rhythm that sounds almost like morse code. "My dreams have been boring as hell. Just regular anxiety nightmares about forgetting to study for tests. It's actually kind of refreshing."\n\nWe all turn to look at Jamie, who's been unusually quiet, sitting with perfect posture that somehow manages to look both graceful and slightly unnatural. She meets our eyes and smiles—a real smile that reaches all the way to her pupils.\n\n"I made cookies this morning," she says, wonder in her voice like she's announcing the discovery of fire. "From scratch. With my own hands. And they turned out... normal. Good normal. Not demon-enhanced or cursed or anything. Just cookies."\n\nThe relief in the room is palpable, like pressure equalizing after a long dive. But underneath it, something insidious lurks. The normalcy feels too perfect, too carefully constructed, like a movie set designed to look like a real room.\n\n"But the journal's still changing," I insist, opening it to pages that shift and blur when I'm not looking directly at them. "Look, this entry about fracture points appeared yesterday, and this map—"\n\n"What map, Stephen?" Morgan says gently, "We're all reading the same thing. The handwriting's consistent. Everything looks normal."\n\n"That's the problem!" I slammed the journal shut. The sound echoed wrong, too sharp, like glass breaking in a padded room. "Everything's too normal. Too stable. Like someone hit the reset button and we're all pretending we didn't notice."\n\nI pointed at the periodic table, where some of the symbols were subtly wrong, shimmering like heat haze. "Look at this! The elements are different!" Morgan blinked, her gaze following my finger, and then her eyes widened, just for a flicker.\n\n"Oh," she said, her voice soft, "they always were, weren't they? I guess I just never noticed." And as she spoke, the subtle distortions on the chart seemed to solidify into her perception, just as quickly as they'd been visible to me. It was like they were seeing the changes as they became the new truth.\n\nJamie stands and moves to the whiteboard with fluid grace, uncapping a red marker. "But maybe that's good," she says, beginning to draw what looks like a map. "Maybe we found our way back to something real."\n\nThe map shows familiar landmarks—our school, the downtown church, even the fucking trailer-that-isn’t-a-trailer in the woods that appears and disappears depending on who's looking. But she's added annotations: "fracture core," "dimensional anchor points," "reality stable but monitored."\n\n"How do you know all this?" Morgan asks, leaning closer to study the map.\n\nJamie pauses, marker hovering over the board. "I... remember. Not clearly, but it's like having a word on the tip of your tongue. The knowledge is there, just out of reach." She turns to look at us. "I think this is what I was really sent here to do. Not to destroy you, but to help you find the path back to Whitney."\n\nThe fluorescent lights flicker once, so briefly it might have been imagination, but in that strobing instant, every preserved specimen in the room seems to turn its empty eyes toward us.\n\nThe dead pig fetus seems to widen its grin imperceptibly. The reproductive system poster, twitches momentarily, revealing anatomical diagrams of organs that couldn't possibly exist in any earthly biology: spiral wombs that twist through dimensions, fallopian tubes that branch like fractals into infinity.\n\nAlmost normal.\n\nNormal enough to fool us for exactly thirty-six more seconds.\n\n**********\n\nThe lab door slams open with a bang that makes us all jump, our carefully constructed sense of normalcy shattering like glass.\n\n"Jesus Christ!" TJ yelps, nearly falling off his lab stool.\n\nBecca stumbles through the doorway, hair disheveled and eyes red-rimmed with exhaustion. She looks like she's been running, or crying, or both. For a heart-stopping moment, we all freeze—because this is exactly how the bad shit always starts. Doors slamming open. Someone appearing where they shouldn't be. Reality preparing to fold itself inside out.\n\nBut then she's just... Becca. Tired, traumatized, but undeniably human Becca.\n\n"Holy shit, you scared us," Andrew breathes, hand over his heart.\n\nShe stops in the doorway, taking in the scene—the five of us huddled around the whiteboard with Jamie's map, Whitney's journal open on the lab table, the general air of conspiracy that clings to us like incense. For a moment, she just stares, and I can see her trying to process what she's walked into.\n\n"You're back," Morgan says, genuine relief flooding her voice. We all move toward her at once, a collective impulse to comfort and protect that feels as natural as breathing.\n\nBecca lets us envelope her in a group hug that smells like vanilla perfume mixed with something institutional—disinfectant and despair. She's thinner than when she left, sharper at the edges, and when she looks at us, there's something haunted in her expression.\n\n"Yeah," she says, voice hoarse. "I'm back. Finally."\n\n"How was..." I start, then stop, because I’ve got no idea where she’s been.\n\n"Healing Through Traditional Values Institute," she says bitterly. "Three weeks of being told that my trauma was actually gender confusion, and that if I just embraced my feminine role properly, I'd stop having nightmares about people who never existed."\n\nThe words hit us like physical blows. Jamie's face goes pale. TJ makes a sound like he's been punched in the stomach.\n\n"Wait, what?" TJ sputters. "They sent you to conversion therapy? But you're not even—"\n\n"Gay?" Becca laughs bitterly. "Yeah, I tried explaining that. Told them I was in love with Patrick, that he was a boy, that we were planning to go to prom together. But since Patrick doesn't exist in any of their records, any of their photos, any of their reality..." She wipes her nose with the back of her hand. "They decided I'd invented a male persona to cope with my 'unnatural desires.'"\n\n"That's fucking insane," Andrew breathes.\n\n"They tried to convince me that Patrick was a lesbian fantasy I created to avoid dealing with my attraction to girls," Becca continues, tears starting to track down her cheeks. "That my grief was really just misplaced sexual confusion. They had charts, Stephen. Fucking charts about how trauma manifests as gender dysphoria in vulnerable teenage girls."\n\n"Charts?" Morgan's voice cracks on the word.\n\n"Diagrams. Flowcharts. 'Signs Your Daughter Is Confused About Her Gender Identity.' Shit like 'excessive grief over fictional male friends' and 'resistance to traditional feminine activities.'" Becca's hands shake as she mimics air quotes. "They said my love for Patrick was actually my psyche trying to become male to attract other females."\n\n"But that doesn't even make sense," TJ says, his face flushed with anger. "You've dated guys before Patrick. Not to mention your not-so subtle crush on Stephen."\n\n"Try explaining that to people who think psychology is just applied theology," Becca snaps. "They had an answer for everything. Danny was a 'practice boyfriend' I used to convince myself I was normal. My makeup and dresses were 'performance femininity' to hide my true nature. Even my fucking period was somehow evidence of gender confusion."\n\nJamie's hands are pressed to her mouth, eyes wide with horror. "They can't just... decide someone's gay and ship them off."\n\n"Watch them," Becca says darkly. "Especially when your grief for a boy who 'never existed' becomes evidence of dangerous delusions. Three weeks of being told that every memory I had of Patrick was actually my brain trying to process my attraction to Whitney."\n\n"Whitney?" My voice comes out strangled.\n\n"Oh yeah, that was their grand theory. That I was secretly in love with Whitney, but since I couldn't cope with lesbian feelings, my mind invented Patrick as a safe way to express those emotions. When Whitney disappeared, my 'Patrick fantasy' became a coping mechanism for losing the girl I really loved."\n\nThe silence that follows is deafening. The implications hang in the air like toxic gas—if they could convince Becca's parents that her memories of Patrick were delusions, what's stopping them from doing the same to the rest of us?\n\n"That's insane," Andrew says finally, but his voice lacks conviction. In a world where reality rewrites itself and demons masquerade as high school students, Christian gaslighting seems almost quaint.\n\n"Is it?" Becca laughs, but there's no humor in it. "Because I was starting to believe them. By the end, I almost couldn't remember what Patrick looked like. They showed me my own yearbook photos, and he wasn't in any of them."\n\n"But you remember him now?" TJ asks.\n\n"Yeah." She wipes her nose with the back of her hand. "The memories came flooding back the moment I stepped onto school property. Like crossing some kind of threshold." She looks around the lab, taking in Jamie's map, the open journal, our guilty faces. "What's been happening? And don't say 'nothing,' because I can feel it in the air. Something's different."\n\nWe exchange glances, a whole conversation happening in the space between heartbeats. How do you explain dimensional fractures and reality shifts to someone who's just spent three weeks being told their memories are lies?\n\n"It's... complicated," I start.\n\n"The journal's been writing itself," Morgan blurts out.\n\n"Jamie's becoming more human," TJ adds.\n\n"We think we found a way to get Whitney back," Jamie says quietly.\n\nBecca stares at us for a long moment. Finally, she nods.\n\n"Okay," she says. "Show me."	3	429	t	2026-02-27 12:18:47.29239-05	2026-02-28 19:08:17.660236-05	normal	\N
421	105	5	The World Breaks	When Stephen touches the journal, it erupts into intelligent flames that spread with malevolent purpose. A chain reaction of explosions follows—gas lines explode, lights implode, dark liquid sprays from sprinklers, and a warped child's laugh echoes through the lab. The group flees through a hallway warping with impossible geometry, finally escaping to a parking lot that stretches into whiteness, where they encounter identical figures in beige suits with too-sharp teeth waiting for them.	I reach for the journal, relief washing over me. Finally, someone else will see what I've been seeing. The bleeding pages, the impossible text, the way words rearrange themselves when—\n\nBut this fucking universe has other plans.\n\nThe moment my fingers touch the journal's leather cover, it erupts into flames.\n\nOrange-blue fire that consumes the pages without seeming to burn the lab table beneath. The flames move with intelligence and malice, spreading in patterns that spell out words in a language that makes my eyes water to look at.\n\n"What the fuck—" Andrew starts, but his words are lost as the gas line to the Bunsen burner station explodes with a roar that turns the world white.\n\nThe blast wave hit us like a physical wall, sending lab stools skittering and knocking Andrew backward. He collided with the skeleton turned poster, which didn't just collapse, but disassembled in a shower of yellowed bones, each piece clattering with impossible resonance.\n\nThe air filled with the stench of burning methane, sharp and chemical, but underneath it churned something else—something organic and sickening, like hair and meat left too long in the sun, rotting backward through time.\n\n“Get down!” I screamed, my voice raw, but it was swallowed by the chain reaction of overhead lights exploding, not shattering, but imploding inward, showering us not just with sparks and glass, but with shards of warped light that stung like acid.\n\nThe emergency sprinklers coughed, then spewed a thick, dark liquid that wasn't water, but something viscous and black, sizzling and boiling where it met the flames, smelling like ozone and burned dreams. From the newly formed shadows, a sound erupted—a child's laugh, but warped, processed through a meat grinder, mechanical and wet and utterly alien.\n\nThe laugh stretched, becoming a scream, then splintered into harmonics that made the remaining windows shiver and crack in spider-web patterns, the glass singing a horrible, high-pitched song of breaking.\n\n"The journal!" I scream, lunging toward the burning book, but Jamie grabs my arm with strength that's definitely not human.\n\n"Don't touch it!" she yells, her eyes completely black now, pupils dilated to swallow the iris. "It's not just burning—it's being erased from reality!"\n\nThe map on the whiteboard begins to burn from within, the plastic melting away to reveal glowing text that writes itself in letters carved from concentrated malevolence: "THE ESCAPE ATTEMPT HAS BEEN NOTED."\n\nMorgan begins to scream, high and thin like a tea kettle reaching its breaking point. Blood pours from her nose in patterns that look disturbingly like cuneiform script, and when TJ tries to help her, she collapses against him, driving them both to the floor.\n\nThrough the smoke and chaos, I see Becca pressed against the wall near the door, her face white with terror. A chunk of ceiling tile has opened a gash across her forehead, and blood streams down her face like war paint.\n\n"We have to get out!" Andrew shouts, but his voice is wrong—too deep, with harmonics that suggest something else is speaking through him. He’s got wounds on his face from shattered lab equipment, and where the glass cut his face, the wounds are perfectly geometric, forming angles that shouldn't exist.\n\nJamie doubles over beside me, blood pouring from her mouth onto the linoleum floor in quantities that should have killed her. When she looks up, her face is a battlefield between human and something else—features shifting like she's being rewritten in real time.\n\n"Oh God," she whispers, and her voice carries frequencies that make the lab equipment resonate, beakers singing like wine glasses touched by invisible fingers. "I remember. I remember everything."\n\nThe room fills with smoke that moves wrong—not rising with the heat but flowing along the floor like liquid, reaching for our ankles with predatory intent. Through the windows, I catch a glimpse of something that makes my brain stutter: the moon blinks like a faulty light bulb. Once. Twice. Then holds steady as if nothing happened.\n\nWe stumble toward the door, supporting each other, breathing air that tastes like rotten eggs and burnt plastic. TJ has Morgan's arm draped over his shoulder, her feet barely touching the ground. Andrew's shirt is soaked with blood from a cut on his scalp shaped like a perfect crescent. Becca moves like she's walking through deep water, every step an effort.\n\nThe hallway beyond is a maze of emergency lighting and shadows that move independently of their sources. Exit signs flicker between English and symbols that hurt to look at. The fire doors at the end of the corridor seem impossibly far away, stretching like we're looking through the wrong end of a telescope.\n\n"Keep moving," I gasp, though my lungs feel like they're full of glass. "Don't look at the shadows."\n\nWe make it halfway down the hall when the floor begins to tilt, gravity shifting like we're on a ship in rough seas. The lockers along the walls start to buckle inward, metal groaning with stress that shouldn't be possible.\n\nAndrew stumbles, his legs giving out. When Becca tries to catch him, they both go down hard. Andrew’s arm bends at an angle that makes my stomach clench.\n\n"I can't—" he starts, then screams as something in his shoulder pops with a wet sound.\n\n"We're not leaving you," Becca says, her voice steady despite the blood streaming down her face. She helps Andrew get upright, and together we make lurching progress toward the exit.\n\nThe fire doors burst open as we approach, and cool evening air hits our faces like salvation. We tumble out onto the parking lot, gasping and choking, our clothes singed and skin streaked with soot and blood.\n\nBut as we collapse on the asphalt, trying to catch our breath, the world around us begins to change. The parking lot stretches impossibly wide, becoming a vast white expanse that hurts to look at. The school behind us flickers like a bad television signal, then stabilizes into something that looks right but feels fundamentally wrong.\n\nStanding in a perfect line across our path are figures in beige suits—dozens of them, identical except for the numbers printed on their lapels. They have the posture of customer service representatives and the eyes of sharks, and when they smile in unison, their teeth are too white and too sharp.	4	433	t	2026-02-27 12:18:47.29239-05	2026-02-28 19:09:14.078264-05	normal	\N
422	106	1	The Audit Begins	The Auditors arrive at the burning school to confront the group about their violations of Reality Containment Protocols. As reality itself glitches around them—showing impossible colors, floating code, and a void beyond the perimeter—the characters witness the surreal manifestation of their world breaking down. Jamie reveals her fear that they've been found, and the group is warned of imminent transport.	The one in the center—Number 1, according to his badge—steps forward with a clipboard that definitely wasn't there a second ago. His measured pace makes no sound on the grass, and when he speaks, his voice has the quality of every teacher who's ever disappointed you and every god who's never returned your prayers.\n\n"Stephen, Jamie, Morgan, Thomas, Andrew, Rebecca," he says, consulting the clipboard without looking at it. "You are, each of you, in violation of 13.5.9a of the Reality Containment Protocols."\n\nThat's when I notice everything else is wrong.\n\nThe fire alarm's shriek dies mid-wail, leaving us standing in smoke that rises in perfect geometric spirals. Behind us, the school burns with flames that flicker between normal orange and something that isn't quite a color—more like the visual representation of a computer error. The acrid taste in my mouth shifts from smoke to peanut butter to something metallic and digital.\n\n"What the fuck," Andrew breathes, and for once, nobody tells him to watch his language. We're all too busy staring at the void.\n\nBeyond the school's perimeter, there's nothing. Not darkness, not emptiness, but a complete absence that hurts to look at—like staring at the space between television static, the place where pixels go to die. Above us, where the sun should be, there's a cursor blinking against a background that shifts between grey and blue in jarring cuts. BLINK. Grey. BLINK. Blue. No fade, just hard transitions like someone's flipping a switch.\n\nThe fire engine parked at the curb makes my eyes water. It's painted in a primitive, crayon-red that exists in only one shade—no highlights, no shadows, no depth. Like someone took the concept of "red" and stripped away everything that makes red actually look like red, leaving behind this flat, impossible color that screams FIRE TRUCK in the same way a child's drawing screams HOUSE.\n\n"That's not right," Becca whispers. "That's not... that's not how colors work."\n\nAndrew's arm comes up around her shoulders without thinking, pulling her against his side. Right now, with reality dissolving around us, they're the only comfort each other has left.\n\n"It's okay," Andrew murmurs into her hair, though his voice shakes. "We're gonna figure this out."\n\nBecca presses her face against his shoulder, her artist's eye still cataloging the impossible wrongness even as she seeks shelter from it. "The perspective is all fucked," she says, her voice muffled by his jacket. "It's like someone drew this with crayons and forgot how light works."\n\nAndrew tightens his grip on her, his free hand finding hers. Their fingers interlace,  desperate human contact in a world that's forgotten how to be human.\n\nJamie makes a sound like a wounded animal. When I look at her, she's gone pale, her hands shaking so hard she can barely form fists. "No, no, no," she whispers, backing away from the Auditor. "You can't be here. You're not supposed to—they said if I kept him safe—"\n\n"Jamie?" I reach for her, but she flinches away.\n\n"They found us," she says, and her voice cracks. "Oh God, Stephen, they found us and it's all my fault."\n\nStrings of ones and zeroes start drifting through the air like digital snow, occasionally clustering together to form brief words—ERROR, RESET, COMPLIANCE—before dissolving back into random data streams. @ symbols and % signs float past us at eye level, fragments of commands that spell out half-formed instructions: IF TRUE THEN, GOTO LINE, EXECUTE ORDER.\n\n"Is that... is that fucking code?" Morgan’s voice cracks, and she's staring at a line of text that just streamed past her head: SUBJECT ARRAY IDENTIFIED.\n\nMorgan reaches out to touch a drifting ampersand, and her hand passes right through it. The symbol flickers like a bad connection, pixelating for a moment before reforming. "It's like we're inside a computer," she says, her voice smaller than I've ever heard it. "But a really shitty one. Like Windows 3.1 had a baby with a fever dream."\n\nThe other firefighters—if that's what they are—move with mechanical precision, their uniforms that same flat, impossible red. Their faces are perfectly symmetrical in a way that human faces never are, like they were generated by an algorithm that learned about humans from instruction manuals. When they breathe, their chests rise and fall in perfect unison, a choreographed lie of life.\n\nTJ suddenly grabs Morgan's arm, his fingers trembling against her skin. "Look at that," he says, pointing at a line of code floating just above the fire truck: CONTAINMENT PROTOCOL ACTIVE. "Morgan, what does that mean?"\n\n"I don't know," she whispers back, but instead of pulling away, she steps closer to him. Close enough that I can see her studying his face like she's memorizing it. "TJ, I—"\n\n"Yeah?" His voice cracks on the single syllable, and despite everything—the void, the code, the approaching digital apocalypse—he's blushing like she just asked him to prom.\n\nMorgan takes a shaky breath, her eyes darting between the advancing firefighters and TJ's face. "If we're about to get deleted from reality, there's something I need to tell you."\n\n"Morgan, don't—" he starts, but she cuts him off.\n\n"You're an idiot," she says, but her voice is soft. "A sweet, Reagan-obsessed, MTG-playing idiot who thinks supply-side economics is romantic foreplay." TJ's blush deepens, but he doesn't look away. "And we've been doing this awkward dance for months now—holding hands, sharing lunches, making out behind the gym—but you still won't call me your girlfriend, you magnificent disaster."\n\nTJ shifts uncomfortably, his free hand fidgeting with his wallet chain. "I mean, we're... we hang out a lot. And the making out is really good. But boyfriend-girlfriend sounds so... official. What if I mess it up?"\n\n"Thomas James O'Malley," Morgan says, using his full name like his mother does when he's in trouble, "we're about to be deleted from reality. I think we can risk making it official."\n\nTJ stares at her for a moment, mouth opening and closing like a fish. More code streams between them—ERROR 404: EMOTIONAL PROCESSING NOT FOUND—but he doesn't notice.\n\n"You... you like me?" he stammers. "But you're, like, smart. And I have a Reagan/Bush '84 poster above my bed and think the Laffer Curve is basically porn."\n\n"Exactly," Morgan says, reaching up to straighten his collar with hands that won't stop shaking. "You're wonderfully, completely insane, and I'm tired of pretending I don't want to kiss you every time you explain why the Berlin Wall was actually a good idea."\n\n"It created market efficiency through scarcity!" TJ protests automatically, then his brain catches up. "Wait. You want to kiss me?"\n\nThe Auditor's voice cuts through the air—"Transport will commence in thirty seconds"—but Morgan doesn't look away from TJ's face.\n\n"If we're going to die," she says, stepping even closer, "I at least want to kiss you one last time."\n\n"One last—!"\n\n"Then we better hurry up," she says, and pulls his face down to hers.\n\nThe kiss is awkward, desperate, and absolutely perfect. TJ's hands hover uncertainly in the air for a moment before settling on her waist, and Morgan makes this soft sound against his lips that would probably make him faint under normal circumstances. Around them, the floating code shifts from red to pink to something that might be digital hearts, like even the simulation is rooting for them.\n\nWhen they break apart, TJ's hair is sticking up at impossible angles.\n\n"Morgan," he says solemnly, "if we