hand. This isn't even the demon who seduced me with gentle touches and meaningful looks. This is some algorithm's idea of a horny teenager, all base urges and no soul—a walking, talking sex doll programmed by someone who's never actually met a real human being.\n\nThe empty seats where my friends should be sitting make her wrongness even more obvious. TJ would have choked on his sandwich. Morgan would have wanted to disappear under the table. Becca would have covered her eyes and whispered prayers.\n\nBut they're not here. They couldn't handle being around this broken version of our reality.\n\nAnd I don't blame them.\n\nAfter lunch, as we're walking to our next classes, I pull out Jennifer's Journal, hoping to find some clue about what went wrong. The moment the leather binding appears, Jamie-thing stops walking.\n\n"What's that?" she asks, but her voice has changed. Gone cold. Then she sees the journal clearly and recoils like I just pulled out a crucifix. "What the fuck is that thing? Get it away from me!"\n\nHer face contorts with genuine revulsion and fear—the first real emotion I've seen from her all day. She backs away from the journal like it's radioactive, pressing herself against the lockers.\n\n"Is that a present for me, baby?" The words come out of her mouth almost robotic, but her eyes are wide with terror. It's like watching someone else control her vocal cords. "You know how wet notebooks make me." She's fighting against her own voice, shaking her head frantically even as her mouth keeps moving.\n\n"Just a notebook," I say, but something underneath her programming has taken control. Her hands move against her will, reaching for the journal with fingers that shake with resistance. She's fighting it—I can see the struggle in her eyes—but her body moves with mechanical precision, like she's being puppeteered.\n\n"No, no, no," she whispers, even as her fingers stretch toward the leather binding.\n\nThe moment her skin touches the leather, she stops sudden