es my spine straighten involuntarily. Her presence radiates that familiar cocktail of vanilla and peaches, but underneath it now there's something else—the sulfuric scent of barely controlled violence.\n\nBecca tries to wrench free, but Jamie's grip is absolute. "Let me go! Let me fucking go! He deserves this!"\n\n"He does," Jamie agrees quietly, and her honesty hits harder than any of Becca's punches. "But breaking your hands on his face won't bring Patrick back."\n\nThe words stop Becca's struggles like a slap. She stares at Jamie with wild eyes, her whole body shaking with rage and grief and exhaustion. "He's gone," she whispers. "Patrick's gone and it's all his fault and I can't... I can't fix this. I can't fix any of this."\n\nJamie releases her wrist slowly, and Becca staggers backward, wrapping her arms around herself like she's trying to hold her pieces together through sheer force of will. Her knuckles are bloody from connecting with my face, and she stares at the blood like she can't quite believe it belongs to her.\n\n"I know," Jamie says softly. "We all know. We all saw what happened in there."\n\nAnd that's when Becca goes completely still, her face draining of color so fast I think she might collapse.\n\n"Patrick?" The word comes out as barely a whisper.\n\nI follow her gaze and see Patrick, dead and gone Patrick, walking down the hallway with the confident stride of someone who's never questioned his place in the world. Clean ROTC uniform pressed to military standards, hair styled with geometric perfection, that easy smile Patrick always wore when he thought he was being charming.\n\nBut we all watched him disappear. We all saw those invisible forces tear him apart like wet paper, his screams echoing through that basement nightmare as reality deleted him one piece at a time.\n\nBlood drips from my nose onto the floor as I stare at the impossible sight of my dead friend walking toward us like nothing ever happened.\n\nBecca's grip on my shirt goes slack. "That's... that's not possible."\n\nTJ pushes through the crowd, Morgan right behind him, both of them staring at Patrick like they're seeing a ghost. Which, in a way, we are.\n\n"Holy shit," TJ breathes. "How is he...?"\n\n"This is wrong," Jamie whispers, her voice so low only I can hear it. "This is very, very wrong."\n\nPatrick continues down the hallway, oblivious to our stares, nodding politely to Mr. Frahm as he passes. The motion is perfect—too perfect, like watching a film loop of someone being friendly rather than actual spontaneous human interaction.\n\nBecca takes a shaky step forward, then another, her anger transforming into desperate hope. "Patrick!" Her voice cracks as she calls out to him, bright as aluminum foil. "Patrick, over here!"\n\nHe turns toward the sound with mechanical precision, and his smile assembles itself piece by piece, each facial muscle engaging in sequence like a complex machine performing a programmed routine. But it's Patrick's smile—the same lopsided grin that used to make Becca blush when he'd catch her staring during study sessions.\n\n"Becca," he says, and his voice is Patrick's voice, warm and familiar. "Good morning."\n\nBecca rushes toward him, her movements jerky and uncertain, like someone approaching a wild animal that might bolt at any sudden motion. Her hands flutter at her sides, reaching out then pulling back, not quite knowing what to do with themselves. Blood from my broken nose drips steadily onto the linoleum behind her.\n\n"Patrick?" Her voice cracks on his name. "I... how are you? What happened? Where have you been?"\n\nThe questions tumble out in a breathless rush because what the fuck do you say to a ghost? How do you ask someone who got torn apart by invisible forces if they're feeling okay? How do you explain that you've spent almost a month mourning them, that TJ punched holes in his bedroom wall while Morgan documented every detail of their erasure with obsessive precision? How do you tell him that B