imate genuine emotion yet. It's like watching a very expensive animation where they got everything right except the soul.\n\n"Morning, kiddo! You look serious. Everything okay?"\n\nKiddo. The word hits me like phantom pain from a broken bone that healed wrong. Real Dad called me "boy" when he was feeling generous, "worthless little shit" when he wasn't, and things that would make a drill sergeant blush when the belt came off. This version throws around endearments like he's getting paid by the syllable, each "sport" and "champ" making my shoulders hunch involuntarily, waiting for the sucker punch that never comes. My body keeps flinching from kindness it reads as a wind-up to violence.\n\n"I need to ask you both something," I manage, my voice sounding foreign in this house that's shaped like mine but wrong in every detail that matters. Same walls, same layout, but the drywall doesn't have fist-holes spackling over. The carpet isn't stained with mysteries I learned not to ask about.\n\nWrong Mom shifts on the couch, a tectonic movement that sends ripples through the cushions. "If this is about money, you know we've discussed budgeting, sweetheart." Her voice has that sugar-coated edge that means she's being helpful in the way that makes you feel guilty for needing help.\n\n"It's for school," I say quickly. "A geology project. In Nevada."\n\nWrong Dad dries his hands on a dish towel that doesn't have blood or beer stains. Everything in this kitchen is aggressively clean, like the universe is trying to convince me this is what normal looks like. "Nevada? That's quite a trip. How much are we talking?"\n\n"Maybe three hundred? For bus tickets. Me and some friends need to—"\n\n"Four hundred," he interrupts, already reaching for his wallet. Not the beaten leather thing Real Dad carried like a prop, but some thick billfold that actually contains money. He starts peeling off twenties like he's dealing cards, casual as breathing. "These things always cost more than you expect