dio audience to applaud.\n\n"I'm fine," I tell her, which is true in exactly zero timelines.\n\nWrong Dad moves closer, and I have to fight the instinct to flinch. But he just claps me on the shoulder—firm but not painful, the kind of masculine affection that feels rehearsed. "Hey, we're proud of you for taking initiative with this project. Shows real character."\n\nCharacter. Like it's something you can develop through field trips instead of having it beaten into you in a garage while your mother pretends not to hear.\n\n"Thanks," I mumble, the word ash in my mouth.\n\n"When do you leave?" Wrong Mom asks, already mentally cataloging which snacks will survive a bus trip and make her look like a good mother.\n\n"Tomorrow morning."\n\n"Tomorrow?" Wrong Mom's face cycles through expressions like a slot machine before landing on Concerned Parent #3. "But tomorrow's Friday! I was going to let you have people over—like a real party! With no supervision!"\n\n"And beer!" Wrong Dad blurts out. "I'll buy beer for you and your friends! The good stuff! Import!"\n\nI've never seen Real Dad share alcohol with anyone, let alone offer to supply it to minors.\n\n"I really need to leave tomorrow," I say, backing toward the hallway.\n\n"But tomorrow's when we were going to Best Buy!" Wrong Mom's voice pitches higher. "To get you that PlayStation you wanted! And a Sega Saturn! And every game! We'll just buy the whole store!"\n\n"Your Uncle Edmund just died!" Wrong Dad announces like it's Christmas morning. "Left us everything! Fifty million dollars! It's all yours for college! Or... or whatever kids buy! Porn! Cocaine!"\n\n"Dad!” Wrong Mom gasps, then pivots seamlessly. "What he means is tomorrow we're going car shopping! For YOU! A Mustang! Or a Camaro! Or both! You can have both!"\n\nThey're both standing now, moving to flank the hallway like they're trying to herd me back to the couch. Their smiles are stretching wider, showing too many teeth.\n\n"Tomorrow I'm calling the school," Wrong Mom says frantically. "Tell them you're too smart for classes! Straight A's forever! No homework!"\n\n"Tomorrow the Playboy Mansion called!" Wrong Dad's eyes are wild now. "They want you for... for an article! About smart young men! Very tasteful!"\n\n"Tomorrow MTV wants to give you your own show!"\n\n"I'M STILL LEAVING TOMORROW!" I shout, and they both freeze mid-gesture like someone hit their pause button.\n\nThe silence stretches like taffy. Wrong Mom's smile flickers but doesn't fall. Wrong Dad's hand is still extended, holding invisible car keys to something I can't even legally drive yet.\n\n"Well," Wrong Mom says finally, her voice dropping back to that aggressively pleasant register. "If you really must go, I should pack those snacks. We have Doritos. Cool Ranch." She laughs, bright and hollow. "All the kids love Cool Ranch!"\n\nWrong Dad nods slowly, mechanically. "Sure, sport. Tomorrow it is. Your... rock thing."\n\nThey're still smiling as I back away, but something in their eyes has gone static. Like NPCs whose dialogue tree just ran out of options.\n\n"Oh!" Wrong Mom calls out as I reach my door. "If you change your mind, tomorrow Nine Inch Nails is playing a private concert in our living room! Trent Reznor specifically asked for you!"\n\nI escape to my room—my wrong room in this wrong house with these wrong parents who love me in all the wrong ways. Through the wall, I can hear them resuming their morning routine: him returning to dishes that don't need washing, her to a TV that promises transformation through organization.\n\nTomorrow I leave for Nevada to destroy this careful constructed lie.\n\nToday I pretend their hollow love is enough to make me stay.	0	444	f	2026-02-27 12:20:00.287901-05	2026-02-28 19:20:41.935681-05	normal	\N
433	108	2	The Last Night	Stephen packs for departure while Jamie secretly visits, revealing she may be pregnant. They make love and commit to leaving together, choosing truth over the comfortable lie of their false paradise.	**********\n\nI pull out my old duffel bag—except it's not old, not worn, not held together with duct tape and denial like the one I remember. This one's pristine, and somehow is now a Louis Vuitton, still has the tags on it. My hands shake as I throw clothes in, trying not to think about how even my underwear here is name-brand.\n\nAt the bottom of my desk drawer, wrapped in a Nirvana t-shirt I definitely didn't buy, is the clay sigil made from the stone Benji gave me. It's the only thing in this room that feels real—rough where everything else is smooth, warm where everything else is temperature-controlled perfection. It pulses against my palm like a second heartbeat, and for a moment I swear I hear his voice: "Kid, you're overthinking this. Some prisons have nicer bars. Still prisons."\n\nI tuck it carefully into the bag's side pocket. My only anchor to what's real, assuming anything is anymore.\n\nThe packing takes five minutes. Turns out it's easy to leave when nothing here is really yours.\n\nLater, lying in bed that's too soft with sheets that smell like fabric softener instead of fear-sweat, I stare at the ceiling and try to make sense of what I'm about to do. The moonlight streams through windows that aren't painted shut, in a room where the lock is on the inside, where no one's going to burst through at 3 AM looking for someone to hurt.\n\nTomorrow I'm leaving paradise. And for what? Some cosmic principle? The memory of friends who might not even exist?\n\nMy seventy-four year old mind drifts to the other reality, the one where I wrote Elysium Rising—my wife who loved me the way people love vintage furniture. Admired from across the room, dusted occasionally, shown off to guests. She'd hold my hand at parties, kiss my cheek for photographs, call me "sweetheart" in front of our friends. Perfect choreography, every gesture precisely what a wife should do. But in private, we lived like cordial roommates who'd forgotten why they'd moved in together. She loved me, I think, in whatever way she understood love—like a concept she'd studied but never quite felt. I was her favorite theorem, proven but never truly understood.\n\nHere, Jamie exists. Jamie who breaks into my room at midnight to tell me about weird dreams. Jamie who gets genuinely excited about finding a cool rock. Jamie who loves me like I'm the first person to ever really see her.\n\nAnd I'm about to throw it all away.\n\nThe curtains stir—no breeze, just that subtle shift that means she's here. The mattress dips behind me, and her arms slip around my waist like coming home. She's trembling, but trying to hide it.\n\n"Your parents?" I ask.\n\n"Fucking psychotic." Her voice is muffled against my shoulder. "Mom suddenly decided we need family bonding time. At midnight. Suggested we all get matching tattoos tomorrow. Of dolphins. Dad offered to buy me a horse if I don't leave. A fucking horse, Stephen. I don't even like horses."\n\n"The universe is pulling out all the stops."\n\n"Even Tiffany's in on it. She's eight—usually won't shut up about her Polly Pockets for five seconds. Tonight she bounces into my room at eleven PM, wide awake, with a pillowcase full of fake flower petals. Wants to practice being a flower girl for when we get married. When, not if. Started planning the whole thing—you in a tux, me in white, her in some pink princess dress." Jamie's voice catches. "She made a crayon drawing of us at the altar. The universe is using an eight-year-old's dreams as ammunition."\n\nShe laughs, but it's hollow. "The universe can kiss my half-demonic ass."\n\nBut her bravado cracks on the last word, and I feel her tears hot against my neck. The human side of her is terrified. The demon side is probably terrified too, just better at hiding it.\n\n"I'm scared," she admits, and I turn in her arms to face her. In the moonlight she looks so young it breaks my heart—not a succubus or a mission or a cosmic anomaly, just a sixteen-year-old girl carrying the weight of demonic memories, human hormones, and a mission she's abandoned for love. The universe's last perfect trap, and she fell right into it when she wasn't supposed to fall at all.\n\nJamie suddenly shifts away from me slightly, pressing a hand to her stomach. "Stephen…there’s something I need to tell you."\n\nI notice the way she avoids my gaze, the way her hand trembles against her abdomen. My heart stutters, understanding already dawning like a quiet horror.\n\n"I'm late," she whispers, voice small in the darkness, heavy with fear and hope intertwined.\n\nThe words hang between us like a third presence in the room.\n\n"How late?"\n\n"Two weeks. Maybe three. I can't... time moves weird now. But I feel different. My body feels different." She finally looks at me, and her eyes are ancient. "Another chain, right? Another way to keep us here."\n\nMy hand moves to her stomach without thinking, palm flat against the soft cotton of her stolen shirt. Nothing yet. But maybe—\n\n"It could be stress," I say.\n\n"It could be a lot of things." She covers my hand with hers. "But if it's real... Stephen, how can I ask a baby to pay for our principles? How can I drag an innocent into whatever hell we're heading toward?"\n\n"How can we raise one in a lie?"\n\nShe laughs, bitter and broken. "Listen to us. Talking about raising anything when tomorrow we might not exist."\n\nWe lie there in the philosophical wreckage of our own making. The perfect trap—not just love, but potential life. The universe offering us everything humans are programmed to want: safety, family, future. All we have to do is accept the beautiful cage.\n\n"You know what kills me?" Jamie says after a while. "This is nice. This whole reality, even with its desperate bribes and cartoon parents—it's nice. We could stay. Have the baby. Get married. I'd wear a white dress and pretend I deserve it. You'd work as some executive somewhere and come home to dinner on the table. We'd be happy."\n\n"We'd be lobotomized."\n\n"Same difference, maybe." She traces patterns on my chest—protective sigils that won't protect us from what's coming. "But Whitney's still gone. Patrick's still erased. And this whole place still reeks of formaldehyde under the air freshener."\n\n"I know."\n\n"I hate that you know. I hate that we both know. Ignorance would be so much easier."\n\n"If you want to stay—"\n\nShe cuts me off with a kiss that tastes like desperation and decision. "Shut up. Where you go, I go. Even if it means losing this." Her hand drifts to her stomach again. "Even if it means losing everything that could be."\n\nWe make love like it's a prayer to gods we don't believe in, slow and careful and complete. Not the desperate fumbling of teenagers or those Tuesday night appointments where affection was performed rather than felt. This is something else—two people trying to memorize each other before the world ends. Her skin tastes like salvation I have to refuse. Her sighs sound like home I'll never have.\n\nAfter, we lie tangled in sheets that smell like us, watching the ceiling fan turn lazy circles. The universe tries one more time—a shooting star crosses perfectly through the window frame, and I swear I smell fresh-baked cookies from nowhere.\n\n"Subtle," Jamie mutters.\n\n"Tomorrow—"\n\n"Tomorrow we burn it all down," she finishes. "Tomorrow we choose truth over comfort, reality over dreams, whatever the fuck that means anymore." She props herself up on an elbow, hair falling across her face like a curtain. "But tonight, we're here. Tonight you're mine and I'm yours and maybe there's a tiny us growing in the space between. Tonight the universe can bribe all it wants—we've already made our choice."\n\n"I love you," I tell her, meaning it across every timeline.\n\n"I know," she says. "That's the only real thing left."\n\n**********	1	445	f	2026-02-27 12:20:00.287901-05	2026-02-28 19:21:42.120812-05	normal	\N
434	108	3	The Greyhound to Nowhere	Stephen, Jamie, TJ, and Morgan board a surreal bus to Nevada. As they travel, the bus transitions into a liminal space where dead passengers from different eras ride alongside them, and reality itself begins to thin and distort.	April 19, 1996 - Day One: Departure\n\nThe Greyhound station squats on the wrong side of town like a concrete toad, sweating diesel and desperation in the pre-dawn gloom. We should have been able to fly—TJ's parents have money bleeding out of their investment portfolios. But when he mentioned Nevada, they went full psycho.\n\nTJ shows up at the station looking like he's been through a war. Twigs in his hair, mud on his Reagan/Nixon shirt, a bruise spreading across his jaw like spilled wine.\n\n"Jesus, what happened to you?"\n\n"My dad happened." His voice is hollow. "The neurosurgeon who saves lives for a living? He tried to cave my skull in with a nine-iron when I said I was leaving. Mom held me down."\n\nMorgan clutches her backpack tighter. "We spent the night in the woods behind my house. My parents were cool about the trip—even offered to help—but then the cops showed up. TJ's parents told them he'd stolen fifty thousand dollars and was armed."\n\n"Armed with what?" Jamie asks.\n\n"My MTG deck, apparently." TJ attempts a laugh that sounds like breaking glass. "Cops searched Morgan's house, turned the whole place upside down. Her parents tried to explain, but..." He trails off, staring at nothing.\n\n"We've been hiding in drainage ditches since midnight," Morgan continues. "Moving between bushes like we're in fucking Vietnam. Every siren, every flashlight—" She shudders.\n\nI scan the parking lot nervously. "They still looking?"\n\n"Probably. Dad's golf buddy is the chief of police." TJ pulls out a wadded ball of cash—all hundreds and fifties. "Thank God for the emergency stash I kept in the garage. It's a wonder they're not here right now."\n\nSo here we are at 5:47 AM, standing in line behind a woman arguing with her grocery bag and a man who might be dead but hasn't realized it yet.\n\n"Four tickets to Nowhere, Nevada," I tell the clerk, whose face has the texture of beef jerky left in a tanning bed.\n\nShe squints at her amber monitor. "Nowhere ain't on the... wait." Her fingers stop moving. The screen flickers. "That's... huh. Says here it's a valid destination. Route 666-B." She looks up, and for a second her pupils are vertical slits. "Y'all sure? That's way outside the coverage area."\n\n"Coverage area?" Morgan asks.\n\n"For existing," the clerk says flatly, then blinks like she's coming out of a trance. "I mean, for emergency services. No ambulances out there. No police. No... help." She stares through us like we're already ghosts. "Place like that, you break down, you stay broken."\n\nThe bus looks like it was assembled from the parts of other, sadder buses. The license plate reads "LAST-CHANCE" which feels a bit on the nose. Our driver has the skeletal face of someone who's been dying by degrees since the Nixon administration.\n\n"Welcome aboard the 5:55 to the edge of the world," he croaks. "Please keep your arms and legs inside the vehicle. Not because of safety. They just tend to... wander off out there."\n\nWe board, and the stench hits like a physical presence—burnt rubber, despair, and something medicinal that makes my eyes water. The seats are held together with duct tape and what might be human hair. Jamie slides in beside me, her thigh pressed against mine, radiating heat like a furnace.\n\n"Cozy," she mutters, picking at a suspicious stain on the armrest.\n\nTJ and Morgan take the seats across the aisle. She immediately curls into his side like a cat seeking warmth, and I watch TJ's entire body go rigid with panic. His arm hovers awkwardly above her shoulders for a solid thirty seconds before finally settling down like a crane lowering delicate cargo\n\nAs we pull out of the station, I watch the familiar streets scroll past the grease-smeared windows. Everything looks normal—aggressively normal, like a movie set maintained by people who've only heard descriptions of "normal." The Wendy's sign flickers between "Fresh Never Frozen" and "Flesh Never Forgotten." Nobody else seems to notice.\n\nHour Six\n\nWe're somewhere in the Alabama panhandle when Morgan attempts what I can only describe as "flirting by proximity." She's been curled against TJ's side since we got on, but now she's actively trying to engage with more than just his shoulder.\n\n"So," she whispers, tilting her head up to look at him. "What do you think really happened to Whitney?"\n\nTJ brightens—finally, familiar territory. "Well, according to my notes—" He pulls out a crumpled paper covered in his chicken scratch, accidentally elbowing her in the process. "Sorry! The dimensional frequency—"\n\n"Your handwriting's really neat," Morgan interrupts, which is a bald-faced lie. TJ writes like a serial killer having a seizure.\n\n"Oh. Um. Thanks?" He blinks down at her, processing. "Your... hair smells nice?"\n\n"It's herbal shampoo. My grandma makes it."\n\n"That's... practical."\n\nThe moment the word leaves his mouth, the bus lurches sideways—not like hitting a pothole, but like reality hiccupped. The lights flicker off then on, but in that split second of darkness, they're somewhere else. Somewhere that smells like sulfur and old bones.\n\nA drunk six rows up suddenly projectile vomits, but what comes out isn't bile—it's words, fully formed and desperate: "TURN BACK TURN BACK TURN BACK THE EDGE ISN'T MEANT FOR—"\n\nThen he's just a drunk again, wiping his mouth and mumbling apologies while the businessman with the broken laptop mops up consonants and vowels with paper towels.\n\nMorgan's grip on TJ's hand has gone from flirty to terrified. "Did he just—"\n\n"Yeah," TJ whispers back. "He did."\n\nThey spend the rest of the hour holding each other like life preserves, all pretense of note-comparing abandoned. Sometimes the investigation finds you, whether you're ready or not.\n\nHour Nine\n\nJamie's been squirming for the last hour, and not in the fun way.\n\n"I have to pee," she announces.\n\nWe both look toward the back of the bus, where a door marked "LAV" hangs at an angle that defies geometry. The door itself seems to be breathing, pulsing with a rhythm that doesn't match the bus's engine.\n\n"I'll go with you," I offer.\n\n"My hero."\n\nThe moment we open the door, I understand why the other passengers have been holding it for hours. The bathroom isn't built—it's grown. The walls are ribbed with something that might be cartilage, slick with condensation that smells like amniotic fluid. Discarded needles crunch under our feet like insect shells, and the toilet... Christ, the toilet looks like it might have once been part of something larger. Something that had opinions.\n\nShe sits down, as if nothing is out of the ordinary, drops her pants, and empties her bladder.\n\n"This is romantic," Jamie says standing back up, pressing against me by the sphincter-like dimensions of the space. "Very Sid and Nancy."\n\n"At least they had heroin."\n\nShe laughs, then pulls me down for a kiss that tastes like bus station coffee and rebellion. For a moment, we forget we're standing inside what might be an organ. Her hands are in my hair, mine are on her waist, and maybe we can salvage something beautiful from this nightmare—\n\nThe bus hits what feels like a crater. We're thrown against the ceiling—which screams—then the wall—which bleeds—then each other in ways that would be erotic if they weren't actively breaking ribs. The toilet makes a sound like a dying whale giving birth. Something under the floor responds in what might be Welsh, if Welsh were spoken by things with too many mouths.\n\n"Ow," Jamie says, rubbing her head where it connected with what I'm choosing to believe is a soap dispenser. "I think I'm bleeding."\n\n"It's just soap."\n\n"The soap has fingernails."\n\nWe stumble out to find the entire bus staring at us. The woman with the grocery bag looks up, and her eyes are solid black from lid to lid.\n\n"Coitus in altitudine melius est in caelis," she says in perfect Latin, her voice layered like a chord played on broken organs. "In inferno, pretium est anima."\n\nThe temperature drops ten degrees. Her grocery bag rustles with anticipation.\n\n"What did she say?" Jamie whispers.\n\n"Something about the mile high club being better in heaven," I translate, my high school Latin suddenly, horribly useful. "In hell, the price is your soul."\n\nTJ slow claps, but his hands don't quite sync up with the sound. Morgan hides behind her notebook. The bus driver adjusts his mirror to leer at us, and his reflection has too many teeth arranged in too many rows, like a shark that's been practicing.\n\nHour Twelve\n\nWe're deep in Mississippi now, and the world outside is starting to thin. Towns have fewer buildings. Signs advertise businesses that can't possibly exist: "JEFF'S LIMB EXCHANGE," "YESTERDAY'S TOMORROW, TODAY!" and simply "MEAT."\n\nThe other passengers are getting worse. The businessman has an old typewriter that doesn’t have any paper, but he keeps typing, occasionally laughing at jokes in dead languages. The grocery bag woman has started feeding her bag actual groceries, which it seems to be digesting.\n\n"Notice how we haven't seen any other traffic for the last hour?" Morgan whispers.\n\nShe's right. The interstate stretches empty in both directions, like we're the only moving thing in a painted backdrop.\n\n"We're leaving the rendered area," Jamie says quietly. "Going off-map."\n\nApril 20, 1996 - Day Two\n\nThe bus doesn't stop for the night. Can't stop. Won't stop. The driver's hands might be welded to the wheel for all we know.\n\nInstead, passengers keep boarding through doors that weren't there seconds before. A woman in a wedding dress torn to ribbons, rice still in her hair, who whispers "He's waiting at the altar" to no one. A child—maybe eight, maybe eighty—who draws pictures of houses on fire and hands them out like party favors. Each new arrival is less human than the last, more warning than person.\n\nAround 3 AM, I drift off against Jamie's shoulder—\n\nFLASH\n\nThe child is strangling me with hands made of smoke and scripture. "GO BACK," it screams with a voice like breaking bells. "THE CHURCH WANTS FRESH MEAT NOT OLD SOULS GO BACK GO BACK—"\n\nJamie's trying to pull it off but her hands pass through it. TJ's shouting something about constitutional rights. Morgan's throwing notebooks that burst into flames on impact—\n\nFLASH\n\nI jerk awake. The bus rocks gently on bad shocks. The child is just a homeless kid, maybe eight, sleeping three rows up with his head against the window. The wedding dress woman is an old lady in a housecoat, muttering about her cats. They smell like piss and mud, but they're human. Completely, boringly human.\n\n"Bad dream?" Jamie asks, but her eyes say she was in it too.\n\n"Yeah," I lie.\n\nMorgan and TJ are curled together across the aisle, her head on his shoulder. They look almost normal except for the fresh scratches on TJ's neck and the way Morgan's notebook is singed around the edges. Evidence of a fight that didn't happen. Or unhappened. Or happened in the spaces between happening.\n\nThe landscape outside grows more theoretical with each mile. Cities become towns become suggestions of habitation become nothing at all. We're driving through America's deleted scenes, the parts God left on the cutting room floor.\n\nAnother passenger boards through a door that opens in the ceiling. This one doesn't even pretend to have a face, just a smooth expanse of skin with a mouth that says, "Last chance, kids. Last chance to have futures that include futures."\n\nFLASH\n\nIt's on fire. We're all on fire. The bus is a crematorium on wheels and the driver is laughing because he doesn't have lungs anymore just hollow spaces that echo with—\n\nFLASH\n\nA homeless vet with one leg boards at a stop that doesn't exist, pays with coins that aren't currency, sits in a seat that wasn't there until he needed it. He reeks of bourbon and gunpowder and something older than both.\n\n"You kids should turn around," he says conversationally. "Nowhere ain't nowhere anymore. It's something else now. Something that ain't got a name 'cause names would run screaming."\n\nWe don't answer. Can't answer. Our tongues are too busy staying in this timeline.\n\nThe bus rolls on through the night that might be day that might be the space between breathing in and breathing out. Morgan's hand finds TJ's in the dark, and they hold on like drowning swimmers. Not romantic anymore. Just survival.\n\nBy dawn—or what passes for it in this thinning reality—we're the only ones who remember being warned.\n\nThe other passengers are just people again. Poor, broken, human people heading nowhere special on a bus that definitely didn't eat three realities during the night.\n\nDefinitely.\n\nHour Twenty\n\nWe're in Texas, except Texas has never looked like this. The landscape is flat in ways that hurt to perceive—not just lacking hills, but lacking the concept of elevation. The sky is the color of television tuned to a dead channel. Sometimes things move in our peripheral vision, but turning to look reveals nothing except the certainty that something was just there.\n\nThe bus itself is changing. Seats we passed yesterday don't exist today. The aisle is longer than it was. The emergency exit leads to what looks like another bus, identical but wrong, stretching into infinity.\n\n"We should be in New Mexico by now," TJ says, checking his watch. The hands are moving backward.\n\n"We are," the driver calls back. "Also aren't. Geography gets optional out here."\n\nA new passenger boards at a stop that wasn't there until the bus stopped. She's wearing a prom dress from the 1950s and crying tears that fall upward. She takes a seat that materializes just for her, then spends the next hour writing in a notebook that burns whatever she puts down.\n\nMorgan's been doing calculations in her notebook for the last hour, but now she sets down her pencil with shaking hands.\n\n"I figured it out," she whispers. "The woman in the wedding dress—I've seen her picture. She's Mrs. Henderson from my neighborhood. But she died in '87. Car accident on her wedding day."\n\nWe all turn to look. The woman is still there, rice in her hair, staring at nothing.\n\n"The vet," Morgan continues, voice getting higher. "Listen to what he's been muttering. It's not bourbon gibberish—he keeps talking about Hill 875, watching for Charlie. That was a real battle. In 1967."\n\nWe all look at the one-legged man who's been rocking in his seat, eyes focused on jungle that isn't there.\n\nTJ squints at the cigarette pack tucked in the man's hat band. "Lucky Strikes. Green pack. My grandpa told me they changed those to white in—"\n\n"1942," Morgan finishes. "But soldiers in 'Nam hoarded the old green ones. Considered them lucky." Her voice cracks. "Guys, he's been riding this bus since 1967. He's been dead for almost thirty years."\n\n"And the kid—the one who draws the burning houses? There was a fire on Maple Street in '92. Whole family died. Including their eight-year-old son who loved to draw."\n\nThe bus suddenly feels colder. Ancient. Like something that's been running this route far longer than the interstate has existed.\n\n"We're on the fucking boat," Jamie says quietly. "The river Styx has rubber wheels and a diesel engine."\n\n"But we're not dead," I protest.\n\n"Yet," the driver calls back cheerfully. "Geography's not the only thing that gets optional out here. So does chronology. You're heading somewhere that doesn't exist, kids. Only one kind of person goes to places that don't exist."\n\nThe prom dress girl looks up from her burning notebook and smiles at us with lips that are too blue. "Welcome aboard," she says. "You'll get used to it. Takes about forty years, but you'll get used to it."\n\n"This is wrong," Morgan keeps muttering, but she won't let go of TJ's hand, even though we can all see through it now—not transparent exactly, just... less insistent about being solid.\n\nHour Twenty-Eight\n\nOne moment we're on the bus with our deceased companions, the next we're standing in fluorescent purgatory. The gas station stretches in all directions—aisles that go on forever, each one identical, stocked with products that hurt to read. "FOOD™" and "EDIBLE OBJECTS" and "CONSUMABLE MATTER" line shelves that reach up into darkness.\n\nEveryone from the bus is here, shuffling through the aisles like they've been doing it for decades. The prom girl eternally reaching for items she can't touch. The vet limping down an aisle that never ends, searching for cigarettes that stopped existing before he did. Even our driver is here, polishing a counter that's never been clean.\n\n"We need to leave," Jamie says, but the automatic doors won't open. They're painted on. Everything is painted on—the exit signs, the windows, even the HELP scrawled on the bathroom door in what looks like fingernails and desperation.\n\nWe follow the wedding dress woman down an aisle of "MEAT(?)" and "PROTEIN SUBSTITUTE," past shelves of nothing pretending to be something. TJ tries his Reagan charm on the door. Morgan attempts to calculate an escape route. But math doesn't work here. Politics don't work here. Nothing works here because we're—\n\n"Where are we?" I ask the cashier with the painted smile.\n\n"Nowhere," he says without moving his non-mouth.\n\nThe word hits like a physical shock. The fluorescent lights flicker—\n\nFLASH—\n\nWe're back on the bus. Just the four of us now. Empty seats stretch out like missing teeth, no sign of the ghosts we've been traveling with. The driver's hands are still on the wheel, but I can see through them now, see the mechanism underneath that's more suggestion than substance.\n\n"The others?" Morgan whispers.\n\n"Never left," the driver says. "That's their stop. Has been for years. Will be for years. But you four..." He adjusts his mirror to look at us, and his reflection is just empty clothes. "You're still choosing. Still got a chance to turn back. Still got enough life in you to make decisions that matter."\n\nThrough the windshield, Nevada waits like an open mouth.\n\n"Next stop, the crossroads," he announces. "End of the line. For everyone."\n\nThe bus shudders forward, leaving the last rest stop that exists in quotation marks, where the dead shop for things they'll never eat, forever and ever, amen.\n\nHour Thirty-Three: Arrival\n\nWe're close now. The landscape has given up pretending to be landscape. It's just suggestion now—the idea of desert, the rumor of mountains. The bus moves through it like a pill through a system that's rejecting it.\n\nI pull out the photograph I've kept hidden for years—scuffed and faded, taken when I was eight, before everything went wrong. Me and Brandy in the foreground, her tongue lolling out, my arm around her neck. But it's the background I study now. There, barely visible behind our old trailer, is a church steeple tilting at an impossible angle.\n\nAs we get closer, the desert outside starts to solidify, matching the photo like reality is remembering what it's supposed to look like. The vague suggestions of sand become actual dunes. The rumored mountains gain edges, shadows, substance.\n\n"You feel it?" Jamie asks, looking over my shoulder at the photo.\n\nI do. Through the grimy windshield, I can make out details that shouldn't be visible yet. The broken fence posts from my childhood. A Joshua tree bent into the shape of a question mark. And there—if I squint—the aluminum siding of a trailer that should have been demolished years ago, assuming it ever existed in this timeline at all.\n\nThe photograph grows warm in my hands, like it's happy to be home.\n\nMorgan and TJ are asleep, still holding hands despite the blisters. Her head on his shoulder, his Reagan/Eisenhower hat sliding off. They look almost peaceful, like two people who found love at the end of the world and decided that was enough.\n\nThe driver's voice comes over the intercom, static-laced and final: "Next stop, Nowhere. Mind the gap between the bus and reality. It's been getting wider."\n\nThrough the windshield, I can see our destination resolving like a Polaroid developing in reverse—the crossroads where the road ends and my memories begin. The church steeple pierces the sky exactly where the photo says it should be. The desert beyond doesn't look hungry anymore.\n\nIt looks like it's been waiting.\n\nJamie squeezes my hand. "Together?"\n\n"Together."\n\nThe bus shudders to a stop like a dying heart, and I pocket the photograph that's served as my map through madness.\n\n**********	2	446	f	2026-02-27 12:20:00.287901-05	2026-02-28 19:22:35.180837-05	normal	\N
435	108	4	The Desecrated Church	The group arrives in Nowhere, Nevada and approaches an inverted, geometrically impossible church that pulses with malevolent energy. Inside, they discover systematic desecration and attempt a ritual to fuse Stephen's fractured timelines, triggering catastrophic consequences.	April 21, 1996\n\nWhen we finally arrive in Nowhere, Nevada—a town that exists on no official maps—the bus driver won't even pull into what passes for a station. He stops at a crossroads where two dirt paths meet a cracked asphalt road, the engine idling like a sick animal's labored breathing.\n\n"This is as far as I go," he says, not looking back at us. "Church is that way." He points down a path that disappears into heat shimmer. "Y'all sure you don't want to turn around? Last chance."\n\nWe gather our bags in silence. As we step off the bus, the heat hits us like opening an oven door—dry, merciless, alive with malevolence. The driver slams the door shut before we're even clear, and the bus disappears in a cloud of black smoke that smells like sulfur and burning hair.\n\nThe desert stretches in all directions, empty except for the skeletal remains of Joshua trees that look like crucified scarecrows. The silence is absolute, pressing against our eardrums until we can hear our own heartbeats. No birds. No insects. No wind. No roads. Just the sound of our breathing and the crunch of sand under our feet.\n\nWe head towards the church for what feels like hours but could be minutes—time moves strangely here, like it's been stretched and twisted by invisible hands. The sun beats down with an intensity that feels personal, vindictive, and our shadows stretch behind us like dark fingers trying to pull us back.\n\nThen we see it.\n\nThe church rises from the desert floor like a bone pushed through skin. Weathered wood gone white as bleached skulls, the building tilts at angles that make my eyes water to look at directly. The stained glass windows are broken into jagged teeth, some panes missing entirely, others hanging by threads that sing discordant notes in wind that doesn't seem to reach us.\n\nThe bell tower lists to one side like a broken neck, and I can see something dark hanging in the belfry—not a bell, but something that moves slightly, swaying with a rhythm that has nothing to do with air currents.\n\n"Jesus Christ," TJ whispers, and the words seem to fall flat, swallowed by the oppressive atmosphere.\n\nWe approach slowly, and the wrongness of the place becomes more apparent with each step. The cross atop the building isn't just tilted—it's inverted, as if gravity works differently here. The front steps are cracked down the middle, creating a chasm that descends into darkness deeper than the foundation should allow.\n\nMorgan pulls out her notebook and starts taking measurements, her hands shaking. "The geometry is wrong," she mutters. "The proportions don't... they can't work. The building should have collapsed decades ago."\n\n"This is ground zero," Jamie gasps as she steps closer to the entrance. "Where your timeline fractured. Where your mother's poison met whatever the fuck lived under Nevada. Every other death, every other reset—they all spiral out from here. We're not just fusing your selves. We're cauterizing a wound in reality."\n\nThe wooden doors hang open on rusted hinges, revealing an interior that seems to stretch impossibly far back. Pews are scattered like broken ribs, some upright, others overturned, a few stacked in configurations that hurt to contemplate. The pulpit at the far end glows with a sickly phosphorescence, and the air above it shimmers like heat waves.\n\nAs we step inside, our footsteps echo wrong—not just the normal reverberation of an empty building, but sounds that come back changed, distorted, like someone else's feet following our rhythm. The floorboards creak not under our weight, but a split second before we step, as if the building is anticipating our movements.\n\nThe walls are covered in graffiti, but not the usual teenage rebellion. These are symbols that seem to crawl when I'm not looking directly at them—geometric patterns that make my brain itch, words in languages that predate human speech, crude drawings of eyes that seem to track our movement.\n\n"Look at this," Morgan calls from near the altar. She's found a section of wall where the wood has been carved away in spiraling patterns. The cuts are deep, precise, and when I run my finger along one groove, it comes away wet with something that isn't quite blood.\n\nThe air inside tastes soupy and thin, like breathing through copper wire. Dust particles dance in shafts of light that cut through the broken roof, but they move in patterns that defy physics—spiraling upward in helixes, clustering into shapes that almost look like faces before dissolving.\n\nTJ sets up our battery-powered lanterns in what he thinks is a circle, but the light they cast creates shadows that don't match our positions. My shadow seems to be reaching for something behind me. Jamie's appears to have too many arms.\n\n"The temperature's dropping," Morgan announces, her breath suddenly visible. "Twenty degrees in the last two minutes."\n\nFrost begins forming on the broken pews, creating patterns that look deliberate—like script written in a language of ice. The metal clasps on our bags start to vibrate with a low, subsonic hum that I feel in my teeth.\n\nJamie kneels at the pulpit and begins sketching her sigil with chalk mixed with drops of her own blood. Where the mixture touches the wood, it hisses and smokes. The pattern she draws seems to shift and writhe even as she creates it, the lines refusing to stay where she places them.\n\n"This place wants us here," she says, not looking up from her work. "It's been waiting."\n\nAs if summoned by her words, sounds begin emanating from the walls—not voices, exactly, but something that might have been voices once. Whispers that carry no words but somehow convey meaning: Turn back. Run. This is not the way. Paradise is behind you. Love is behind you. Safety is behind you.\n\nBut underneath those whispers, something else. A deeper voice, older and infinitely patient: Come deeper. Come home. The door is opening.\n\nThe building itself seems to breathe around us, walls expanding and contracting with slow, deliberate rhythm. The broken cross above us creaks with each inhalation, and I realize it's not broken at all—it's a gateway, hanging open like a mouth.\n\nWe begin chanting the words from Jennifer's Journal, our voices creating harmonies that make the floating dust spiral into impossible patterns. The walls respond, groaning like a ship in a storm, and cracks begin appearing in the foundation—not from structural failure, but as if something vast is pushing up from below.\n\nReality is thinning, and I can feel something immense and patient stirring on the other side, waiting for us to tear the last veil away.\n\nThe church isn't just abandoned.\n\nIt's hungry.\n\nWhat I'd mistaken for phosphorescent glow in the dim light is actually something far worse. The once-sacred space has been systematically defiled—smeared with human excrement in patterns that mirror the inverse of Jamie's sigils, littered with freshly used condoms that glisten like shed snake skins in the lantern light and reek of stale tuna. The wooden surface is stained with a red substance drawn by the discarded tampons littered about. Beelzebub’s flies buzz lazily around offerings that reek of decay and violation.\n\nTJ gags and covers his mouth with his shirt. "What the hell happened here?"\n\n"Desecration," Jamie says, her voice tight with disgust. "Someone—or something—turned this into an anti-church. A place where the sacred becomes obscene."\n\nThe smell is overwhelming—not just the obvious filth, but something underneath it. Something that smells like burning feces and Clorox bleach and the moment just before you vomit. The air itself feels contaminated, thick with malevolence that clings to our skin like invisible oil.\n\nMorgan tries to set up her equipment, but her hands are shaking so badly she drops a vial of chemicals. The glass shatters against the floor. "I can't... I can't concentrate," she whispers. "Something's in my head."\n\nThe whispers from the walls are getting louder, more insistent. They're not just suggesting we leave anymore—they're commanding it.\n\nGO. LEAVE. THIS IS NOT FOR YOU. PARADISE WAITS. LOVE WAITS. SAFETY WAITS.\n\nBut Jamie pushes through, continuing to sketch her sigils around the polluted altar. Her chalk-and-blood mixture hisses where it touches the defiled surface, and steam rises like incense made of anguish. The pattern she draws seems to fight her, the lines wanting to curve in directions that would break the circle, turn the protection into invitation.\n\n"Something's coming," TJ says, his voice cracking with terror. "Can you feel it? Something's climbing up from below."\n\nThe floor beneath us begins to vibrate, a deep thrumming that I feel in my bones. Dust sifts down from the rafters, and somewhere in the distance—or maybe very close—something howls with a voice like breaking glass and dying children.\n\nI want to run. Every survival instinct I've ever developed is screaming at me to get out, get away, find the bus and go home to parents who love me and a girl who makes me feel whole. The terror is so complete it's become a physical thing, pressing against my chest like a weight.\n\nBut then the sigils begin to burn with cold fire, and reality tears like wet paper.\n\nThe church falls away, dissolving into mist and shadow, and suddenly I'm standing in a space that feels clean and bright and impossibly peaceful. The oppressive weight lifts from my shoulders, and I can breathe again without tasting corruption.\n\nIt's still a church, but this one is whole. Sunlight streams through intact stained glass windows, casting rainbow patterns on polished wooden pews. The altar gleams with fresh oil, and the air smells like lemon polish and fresh flowers. Everything here speaks of love and sanctuary and safety.\n\nAnd there, curled on the front pew like a sleeping angel, is an eight-year-old version of myself.\n\nThe relief is so intense it brings tears to my eyes. After the horror of the desecrated space, this feels like stepping into paradise. The boy looks exactly as I remember—messy brown hair, grass stains on his jeans, that peaceful expression he used to get when he'd fall asleep reading comic books.\n\nBut as I approach, something shifts in the air. A wrongness that starts subtle and grows stronger with each step.\n\nThe boy's eyes flutter open as I reach out to touch his shoulder, and they're filled with tears and confusion. "She made me drink it," he whispers, his voice barely audible. "But I didn't want to die."\n\nMy heart breaks for him—for me—for the innocent child who trusted his mother's love only to have it poisoned, literally and figuratively. I pull him into my arms, feeling his small body trembling against mine, and for a moment everything feels right. The fractured pieces of my soul beginning to align, memories flooding back of a childhood before the fractures, before the fear.\n\nBut then I smell it.\n\nOn his breath. Sweet and cloying with an undertone of something chemical. Something that makes my stomach clench with recognition and revulsion. The same smell that used to cling to my mother's "special vitamins," the taste that would linger for hours after she'd given me her "medicine."\n\nEven here, in this moment of reunion, the poison is still there. Still working its way through his small system. Still killing him slowly, methodically, with the patience of someone who believes suffering is love.\n\nThe boy dissolves into me like smoke and starlight, and I gasp as the original timeline floods back—real Nevada sunshine, Brandy's wet nose against my palm, my mother's face before fear carved it into a mask of controlled poison. But underneath it all, the bitter taste of betrayal remains, the knowledge that even the truest love in my life came with a price measured in parts per million.\n\nThe clean church begins to crack around the edges, reality reasserting itself, and I can hear my friends screaming my name from somewhere very far away.\n\nThe poison is still there. It's always been there.\n\nAnd now it's part of me forever.\n\nThe moment my younger self dissolves into me, reality doesn't just crack—it screams.\n\nThe fusion tears through the fabric of space-time like a chainsaw through silk, and the universe responds with the fury of a wounded god. The clean church explodes back into the desecrated nightmare, but now everything is on fire. Not normal fire—cold flames that burn without heat, consuming matter at the molecular level while leaving souls intact to feel every moment of dissolution.\n\nThe ritual's success triggers a cascade failure that reaches down through dimensions we don't have names for. A gas line, disturbed by our dimensional manipulations, rupture and rise through cracks that suddenly appear in the earth itself. The gas meets Morgan's carefully measured chemicals in a reaction that turns physics into a weapon of mass destruction.\n\nThe church doesn't just explode—it vaporizes.\n\nTJ hits the wall with a sound like dropped fruit. I see his eyes go wide with surprise—not pain, just surprise—before the light leaves them. My best friend since sixth grade, reduced to angles that don't work.\n\nMorgan doesn't even get to scream. The chemicals she brought to save us consume her in a flash of impossible mathematics. One moment she's reaching for TJ, the next she's just light and absence and the smell of burning pork.\n\nBut Jamie—God, Jamie—her death is the worst because she refuses to let it be quick.\n\nThe blast catches her full in the face, tearing away half her skull in a spray of bone fragments and brain matter that paints the burning walls in abstract patterns. Her left eye is gone completely, the socket weeping blood and cerebrospinal fluid, while her right eye—somehow intact—continues to track my movement with desperate love. The entire left side of her face has been flayed away, revealing muscle and sinew that pulse with each failing heartbeat.\n\nShe should be dead. Any normal person would be dead. But she's not normal—she's part demon, part teenage girl, part miracle of stubborn affection—and she refuses to die until she reaches me.\n\nI watch in frozen horror as she drags herself across the burning floor using her one remaining functional arm. The other completely missing, but she keeps coming. Each movement leaves a trail of blood and worse things, organs that have been liquefied by the blast sloshing out through tears in her torso.\n\n"I'll always—" she begins, her voice a wet whisper that barely carries over the roar of destruction. Pink foam bubbles from her ruined throat, and I know her lungs are filling with blood, but she keeps trying to speak. Keeps reaching for me with fingers that have been stripped to the bone.\n\nThe heat is incredible now, sand transformed into glass shrapnel that tears through what's left of our bodies like invisible razors. My own skin is blistering and peeling away in sheets, and I can smell my hair burning, but all I can focus on is Jamie's remaining eye—still beautiful, still loving, still trying to tell me something crucial in the seconds before death claims her. And in her ruined eye, I saw not just the death of my love, but the silent, unmourned erasure of a future that had whispered only moments before.\n\nShe's almost reached me when the secondary explosion hits.\n\nA methane pocket beneath the church, a vast, volatile network spanning half the Nevada desert, ignites with the force of a detonated star. The ground itself becomes a cannon, launching chunks of bedrock the size of cars into the air while molten sand rains down like napalm tears.\n\nThe church, the desert, the sky itself—everything becomes a furnace that makes screaming impossible because the air is too hot to breathe. My lungs sear with each attempted breath, and I feel my throat closing as the heat cooks me from the inside out. But somehow, impossibly, I remain conscious long enough to witness the complete annihilation of everything I've ever loved.\n\nTime stretches like taffy, each second becoming an eternity of agony as my nervous system fails in stages. First touch, then taste, then sight—but hearing remains, cruelly sharp, so I can hear the wet sounds of my own body coming apart, the hiss of blood turning to steam, the crackling of bones as they shatter from thermal expansion.\n\nThe pain is beyond description, beyond endurance, beyond anything the human nervous system was designed to process. It's not just physical—it's existential, the kind of suffering that rewrites your fundamental understanding of what it means to exist. Every cell in my body is dying simultaneously, creating a symphony of agony that drowns out thought itself.\n\nBut in that endless moment before oblivion, I understand something crucial: this isn't just death. It's birth. The universe is tearing itself apart to let me through, reality sacrificing itself so I can reach the timeline where everything began.\n\nThe price is everything I've ever loved, everyone who ever mattered, every moment of joy I've stolen from this false paradise.\n\nAnd as the white light consumes the last traces of my dissolving consciousness, I realize I'm willing to pay it.\n\nIn the beginning there was darkness.	3	447	f	2026-02-27 12:20:00.287901-05	2026-02-28 19:23:43.43186-05	normal	\N
436	109	1	Awakening in the Trailer	The protagonist awakens in an eight-year-old body in a cramped Nevada trailer, confused and fragmented. Three distinct consciousnesses—a child, a teenager, and an old man—occupy the same mind, struggling to understand what happened during the anchor ritual. The protagonist realizes they're back in their childhood home with memories of multiple timelines intact.	“WAKE UP!”\n\nThe voice tears through the void like shattered glass—desperate, feminine, achingly familiar. For a heartbeat I know that voice, know it the way you know your own bones. Jennifer? Whitney? The name shifts like smoke between my fingers, there and gone before I can grasp it. Then silence swallows everything, thick and absolute as drowning.\n\nMy eyes slowly open.\n\nThe ceiling is wrong.\n\nThat's the first thing that hits me—not the taste of burning flesh in my mouth or the way my limbs feel like they belong to someone else, but the ceiling. Water stains bloom across yellowed walpaper like bruises, and there's a crack running from the corner that splits into tributaries I don't remember mapping. My eyes track along peeling lead paint that curls down like dead skin, and for a moment I can't breathe because where the hell am I?\n\nI try to sit up and my body responds... wrong. Too light. Too small. My arms are twigs where they should be muscle, and when I look down at hands that should be calloused from martial arts matches, I see soft pink fingers that have never thrown a punch. The panic hits like ice water.\n\nWhat happened to me?\n\nThe room swims into focus. Dirty white walls. A dresser made of particle board that's swollen from humidity. Toys scattered across linoleum that's supposed to look like wood but fools no one. And those curtains...\n\nLight blue. Popeye. Faded from years of Nevada sun.\n\nOh God. Oh no.\n\nI know this place. The memory slams into me like a freight train—this cramped bedroom in a single-wide trailer where the walls are so thin you can hear every argument, every sob, every belt hitting flesh. Every time my “aunts” came to visit and “play with my pretty parts”. But that was... that was before. Before New York. Before the martial arts tournament. Before Jamie's kiss and Whitney's erasure and—\n\nJAMIE.\n\nHer name explodes in my skull and suddenly I'm drowning in a flood of memories that don't belong to this body. I see her face—beautiful and terrible and demonic—leaning over me in TJ's basement. I feel her lips against mine, taste sulfur and desire. I remember the church exploding, Patrick's sacrifice, the anchor ritual that was supposed to fix everything...\n\nBut there's something else. Another voice in my head, older and tired and so fucking done with everything. It whispers about hospice beds and failed dreams, about Leon's rockets rusting in orbit while humanity curls up to die. The old man's memories seep out like a ruptured pustule.\n\nThree of us. Three minds in one skull.\n\nThe eight-year-old—the original, the innocent, the one who still thought mothers were meant to protect—shrieks in pure animal terror. He doesn't understand why there are strangers wearing his thoughts, why his body feels like a costume three people are trying to wear at once. His fear tastes like fruit loops and saturday morning cartoons, like believing your biggest worry was whether Dad would be home for dinner.\n\nThe teenager—me, or the me I'm supposed to be, or the me I remember being—fights for control with the desperate fury of someone who's lost too much to lose more. I catalog everything: the concussion that started the awakening, every flicker and shadow, every friend erased from reality. Whitney becoming William. Jamie becoming mine. The world becoming a lie I could see through but never escape.\n\nThe old man just... observes. He's the stillness in the storm, the resignation that comes after rage burns out. He knows how this story ends because he's already lived it. Died it. Failed it. He watches our panic