l photo, the kind with the cloudy blue background and forced smile. A girl, maybe nine or ten, with eyes that hold too much knowledge and a smile that's practicing for a happiness it hasn't found yet. She's wearing a purple sweater—hand-me-down, the teenager notes, from the way it hangs wrong on her frame.\n\nI don't know her. None of us know her.\n\nExcept...\n\nThe way the light hits the photo, there's a second image underneath. Like someone replaced the picture but the old one left a ghost. A boy in the same pose, same forced smile, different eyes. The child thinks it's a trick of the light. The teenager recognizes dimensional bleed. The old man murmurs a name that sounds like "William" but tastes like "Whitney."\n\nMy hand—our hand—reaches toward the photo, and for a moment the fingers look wrong. Too many, maybe, or not enough. Like reality can't decide how many people are reaching.\n\nShe was here, something whispers. Not any of our three voices. A fourth presence, faint as an echo of an echo. She's always here, in the spaces between.\n\nI find the bathroom and flick on the light. The bulb flickers—everything in this place flickers—and when it finally holds steady, I see myself in the mirror.\n\nChrist.\n\nEight years old. Moon face padded with baby fat that speaks of too many government cheese slices and not enough vegetables. Eyes that haven't learned to hide their thoughts yet, broadcasting every fear and hope like an open frequency. Hair that sticks up in defiance of physics and good sense.\n\nWe're all here. All three of us.\n\nThe child whimpers, drowning in memories of demonic possession and divine bureaucracy, concepts his eight-year-old psychology can't process. He keeps getting stuck on details that don't matter—why did the church smell like cinnamon, why was Jamie's laugh different when she was trying to kill me, why can't I remember my dog's name anymore?\n\nThe teenager rages, silently, efficiently. Every muscle coiled for a fight this body can't 