alls of the feet, hands open, ready to grab or deflect. All those years of training trying to manifest in limbs made of pasta and hope. He knows sixteen ways to break a grip, twenty ways to escape a hold, and none of them work when you're three feet tall and weigh as much as a bag of groceries.\n\nThe old man just... accepts. Like watching a movie he's seen too many times to be surprised by the jump scares. He knows what comes next. Knows there's no avoiding it. His only contribution is a weary observation: At least it'll be over quick.\n\nThe footsteps lumber closer. Uneven. Aggressive. The particular rhythm of someone who's spent their rage on the outside world and brought the leftovers home for dessert. I can map his path by sound alone: door to kitchen (brief pause, probably grabbing a beer), kitchen to hallway (stumble on the threshold he never fixes), hallway to—\n\nThe bedroom. His bedroom. Our theoretical salvation.\n\nThe door slams with rehearsed violence. Springs shriek as a body collapses onto them. For three heartbeats—four—five—silence except for my triple-time pulse.\n\nThen: snoring. Deep, rumbling, the sound of oblivion achieved through Miller High Life and exhaustion.\n\nMy knees buckle. All three versions agree on that, at least. I slide down the bathroom wall until I'm sitting on cold tile, shaking with adrenaline that has nowhere to go.\n\nThank—Thank... who?\n\nI catch myself mid-thought. Not God—never that bastard. Yaldabaoth. Not the false idol calling himself I AM ruling over this broken reality, allowing monsters to wear father-masks while children cower in bathrooms. But thank someone. I…I? Lady I? maybe? Though her name feels distant now, filtered through too many deaths and rebirths to hold its original weight.\n\nStop. You're spiraling.\n\nAll three voices speak at once, creating a moment of perfect unity in the command to just fucking survive. Right now, that's all that matters. Not cosmic hierarchies or dimensional refugees or the goddess who might have been a dream. Just:\n\nBreathe. Hide. Live through the next hour.\n\nAnd figure out what comes after.\n\nOutside the bathroom, the house settles into its toxic rhythm. Snores from the bedroom. The refrigerator's death rattle from the kitchen. And somewhere, so faint I might be imagining it, humming. My mother, preparing breakfast.\n\nPreparing the poison.\n\nThe thought hits all three minds like a slap. She's out there right now, measuring out whatever she thinks will save me by killing me slowly. The child wants to run to her, warn her, beg her to stop. The teenager wants to confront her, demand answers, maybe break the cycle before it begins. The old man knows better—knows that some trajectories can't be changed, only survived.\n\nBut we all agree on one thing:\n\nNot today. Today, we remember. Today, we resist.\n\nToday, we begin the work of becoming whatever the fuck we are now.\n\nI stand—we stand—still shaky but determined. In the mirror, three faces look back with the same resolution. And for just a moment, so brief I almost miss it, a fourth face appears. A girl with kind eyes and a name that tastes like geometry.\n\nRemember me, she mouths, and then she's gone.\n\nBut we do. All three of us do.	0	448	f	2026-02-27 12:20:16.527882-05	2026-02-28 19:24:56.545502-05	normal	\N
