ng house, playing mother, playing the role she thinks will save us both. In her mind, she's June Cleaver with a medicine cabinet. Betty Crocker with a secret ingredient.\n\nShe sets the bowl in front of me with the ceremony of communion. The cereal—Lucky Charms, because even poor kids deserve magic—floats in milk that's slightly off, just shy of sour. But there's something else. A small glass vial in her other hand, contents clear as water and twice as innocent-looking.\n\n"Your vitamins, sweetheart." She says it like she believes it. Maybe she does. The human mind's capacity for self-deception is infinite when the alternative is admitting you're killing what you love. "Doctor says you need them. Growing boys and all."\n\nThere's no doctor. There never was. Just a woman trying to solve an equation where love plus fear equals whatever the fuck this is.\n\nThe teenager in me wants to slap the vial from her hand. The old man watches with the detachment of someone who's seen every possible ending to this scene. But the child... the child sees his mother, and that's the worst part. Because she IS his mother, right down to the way she unconsciously touches her wedding ring when she lies.\n\n"Eat up, sweetheart. You need your strength."\n\nI look at the bowl. At her. At the vial she's now uncapping with practiced efficiency.\n\nNo. Not this time.\n\n"Why are you poisoning me?"\n\nThe words come out in a child's voice but with an adult's certainty. They hang in the air like a spell, and I swear I can see them—actual letters floating between us, each one a small betrayal of the natural order.\n\nThe effect is seismic.\n\nHer face doesn't change all at once. It's like watching a time-lapse of erosion—certainty crumbling into confusion, confusion into horror, horror into something beyond naming. The vial slips from her fingers, and as it falls, I see reality hiccup.\n\nFor a fraction of a second, there are two vials falling. One breaks. One doesn't. In one version, the liquid spreads across linoleum. In another, it spreads across my tongue. I can taste both futures—medicine and milk, salvation and slow death.\n\nThe vial shatters. Reality chooses this path. But the echo of the other timeline ripples outward, and I watch my mother's shadow split for just a moment—one version standing, one version kneeling beside a small body that isn't there anymore.\n\n"I... what... how did you..."\n\nHer voice is breaking apart like bad reception. Each word seems to come from a different distance, a different decade, a different mother who made different choices but all led here.\n\nThe kitchen tilts. Not physically—the room stays level. But something fundamental shifts, like the universe is adjusting its books.\n\nShe grabs the counter like it's the edge of the world. Maybe it is. Her knuckles white against yellowed Formica that suddenly looks too yellow, too old, like it aged twenty years in twenty seconds.\n\n"How do you... you can't... you're just a baby..."\n\nBut I'm not. Not anymore. Not entirely. And she can see it somehow—the wrongness of me, the impossibility of an eight-year-old with eyes that have seen the heat death of human ambition. Her maternal instincts are screaming two different alarms: protect the child and that's not a child.\n\nThen the sobs start.\n\nNot crying—sobbing. The kind that comes from so deep inside it might be tectonic. Her body convulses with each one, and I realize she's not just crying for now. She's crying for every moment she's held that vial. Every breakfast that was supposed to help. Every time she watched me weak and thought at least he's safe.\n\n"I was saving you!" The words rip out of her like shrapnel. "Don't you understand? I was SAVING you!"\n\nShe slides down the cabinet, and as she falls, I see her façade crack. She lands among the glass shards, and they cut through her pajama pants like promises. Blood seeps through—real blood, the kind that doesn't care about timelines or good intentions. But she doesn't notice. Her world has collapsed to a single point of horrified understanding.\n\n"He gets so angry." The words bubble up through snot and tears. "When you're strong... when you talk back... when you look at him wrong... when you exist too loud..."\n\nShe's rocking now, arms wrapped around herself, and I can see the child she used to be. Someone hurt her first. Someone taught her that love meant making yourself smaller, safer, less threatening. And she passed that lesson on the only way she knew how—drop by drop, meal by meal.\n\n"But sick kids... sick kids are different. They're not competition. They're not threats. They're just... projects. Problems to solve. And sometimes..." Her voice drops to a whisper that sounds like wind through empty houses. "Sometimes he'd even be gentle. When you were really bad. He'd bring you soup. Pet your hair. Like you were actually his son instead of just another thing to break."\n\nThe logic of it is twisted into shapes that hurt to follow. She poisoned me to save me from beatings. Made me weak so I wouldn't seem worth destroying. Killed me slowly to keep me from dying fast.\n\n"I wasn't trying to hurt you," she sobs, reaching toward me with hands that shake like prayers. "I was trying to make you invisible. Small. Safe. I thought... God help me, I thought I was being smart."\n\nShe looks up at me then, and her eyes are drowning. Not in tears—in realization. Because she can see it now, written in my impossible knowledge and too-old eyes. She can see what her love became when filtered through fear. Can see how protection became poison, how motherhood became murder.\n\n"I just wanted you to survive," she whispers. "Even if it meant... even if you had to be less to do it."\n\nThe kitchen shivers around us. Wallpaper that was yellow is now green. Was always green. Except I remember yellow. The clock on the wall runs backward for three ticks, then forward, then stops entirely. In the corner of my vision, I see versions of us—her feeding me, me eating trustingly, both of us dying in different ways at different speeds.\n\nShe doubles over, forehead touching the floor, and makes sounds that predate language. Pure grief. Pure horror. The noise mothers make when they realize they've become the monster under their baby's bed.\n\nAnd I stand there, eight years old and ancient, looking down at this broken bird of a woman who loves me so much she tried to kill me with it.\n\nThe child wants to comfort her, to say it's okay even though it's not.\n\nThe teenager wants to rage, to make her feel the weight of every poisoned morning.\n\nThe old man just feels tired, because he's seen how trauma reproduces itself like a virus.\n\nBut all three of us understand: she's not evil. She's not even stupid. She's just broken in exactly the way this broken world breaks mothers—convinced that making their children smaller is the same as keeping them safe.\n\nI kneel beside her, careful to avoid the glass, and do something none of my selves expected: I hug her.\n\nShe freezes. Then collapses into me, sobbing harder, and I can feel her bones through too-thin skin. She's been dying too, I realize. Just differently. While she was poisoning me to make me invisible to my father, she was disappearing herself. Becoming nothing so she could be no threat to anyone.\n\n"I'm sorry," she gasps into my shoulder. "I'm so sorry, baby. I'm so fucking sorry."\n\nI hold her while she shakes apart. This woman who loved me wrong. This mother who tried to save me by erasing me. This broken bird who thought the only way to protect her chick was to clip its wings before it learned to fly.\n\nThe kitchen settles into a new configuration. Not the old timeline, not this one, but something between. Something where a mother's confession cracked reality just enough to let a little light in. Where an eight-year-old boy holds his sobbing mother and forgives her for loving him the only way her broken heart knew how.\n\nIt doesn't fix anything. The bruises are still there. The poison still happened. The father still sleeps one room away, dreaming of violence.\n\nBut for this moment, in this space between timelines, a mother and son see each other clearly. And that has to count for something.\n\n**********	1	449	f	2026-02-27 12:20:16.527882-05	2026-02-28 19:26:00.523469-05	normal	\N
438	109	3	The Bureaucrat Angel	The protagonist flees to the bathroom where Benji, a cosmic bureaucrat in angelic form, appears to explain the true nature of their situation. Benji reveals that the protagonist isn't the cause of reality's breakdown but rather a symptom of a larger systemic failure, and that Yaldabaoth is beginning to wake.	I can't stay in that kitchen. Can't watch my mother bleed into broken glass while the truth of our family's toxicity spreads across the floor like spilled medicine. I stumble toward the bathroom, my eight-year-old legs unsteady, the teenager's memories warring with the old man's resignation while the child just wants to hide.\n\nThe bathroom door clicks shut behind me, and I lean against it like it's the only thing keeping the world from collapsing inward. The mirror above the sink reflects my young face—round cheeks, wide eyes that haven't learned to guard themselves yet. But there's something else moving behind the glass. A ripple, like heat waves rising from summer asphalt.\n\n"She was trying to save me," the eight-year-old whispers, voice thick with tears that want to fall.\n\n"She was trying to kill us," the teenager snarls back. "Drop by drop. Breakfast by breakfast."\n\n"Both can be true," the old man observes, weary as November rain. "That's what makes it hurt."\n\nWe're having a three-way argument in a child's body, which would be hilarious if it weren't so fundamentally fucked. The child wants to run back and hug her. The teenager wants to flush every pill in the house down the toilet. The old man just wants to take a nap that lasts until the heat death of the universe.\n\nThat's when I see it.\n\nThere, on the edge of the sink, sits a crystal. Amethyst, rough-cut, about the size of a walnut. It shouldn't be here. This bathroom barely has soap, let alone New Age decorations. But I know this crystal. Whitney gave it to me—will give it to me—in a timeline that hasn't happened yet.\n\n"For protection," her voice echoes across temporal boundaries. "And to remember that some things can't be erased."\n\nI reach for it, and my fingers pass through empty air. It's already gone, like it was never there. But the memory of its weight lingers in my palm, a phantom sensation from a future that's been deleted.\n\nPerfect. Even the comfort objects are gaslighting me now.\n\nThe mirror's surface begins to bend.\n\nNot crack—bend. Like the mirror is made of water suddenly disturbed by something massive moving beneath. The glass bulges outward, pressing against reality's skin until I can see the membrane starting to thin. Light bleeds through the cracks—not the harsh fluorescent of the bathroom fixture, but something warm and golden that hurts to look at directly.\n\n"Oh, come on," I mutter, backing against the door. "Can't I get five minutes to process before the cosmic horror show starts up again?"\n\nThe mirror explodes outward in a shower of not-glass but pure light, and through the breach comes Benji. Not the sarcastic bureaucrat in a moth-eaten cardigan I remember from the group's séances, but Benji. His true form.\n\nA single massive eye the size of a dinner plate, iris swirling with colors that don't have names, surrounded by wings that fold through dimensions I can feel but not see. Geometric patterns pulse along his feathers—fractals that spiral into infinity, equations written in a language older than mathematics. When he blinks, reality hiccups.\n\n"Finally," he says, and his voice comes from everywhere at once—the walls, the floor, the space between my ribs. "The signal's been garbage for weeks. Dimensional static. Your little anchor ritual really stirred up the interference. Do you have any idea how many forms I had to file just to appear here? Seventeen. SEVENTEEN. In triplicate. Using ink that only exists on Tuesdays."\n\nI slide down the door until I'm sitting on cold tile, staring up at what my brain keeps insisting is an angel even though I know better. "Benji? Is that really you?"\n\nHis eye rotates to focus on me, performing what can only be described as an angelic eye-roll. "No, it's your other cosmic horror friend who enjoys paperwork and cosmic irony. Of course it's me. Though technically I'm only 73% here. The rest of me is stuck in a meeting about proper breach protocols. I'm getting lectured by a concept that translates roughly to 'The Inevitable Heat Death of Enthusiasm.' Real fun guy at parties."\n\n"Integration?" My voice cracks. "I've got three different versions of myself fighting for control in an eight-year-old's body while my mother confesses to slow-motion attempted murder. How do you think it's going?"\n\n"About as well as can be expected when you perform soul surgery with a ritual you found in what amounts to a cosmic bathroom stall." His wings rustle, shedding light like dandruff. "The anchor protocol has a 0.000003% success rate. Most people who attempt it end up as gibbering vegetables, sentient furniture, or—in one memorable case—a rather philosophical shade of blue. You're actually doing remarkably well, all things considered."\n\n"Remarkably well?" I gesture wildly at my reflection in the broken glass. "Benji, I'm eight years old! In Nevada! In a timeline where my mother has been poisoning me! This isn't well—this is fucked!"\n\n"Oh, you think THIS is fucked?" His eye dilates with indignation. "Try existing in sixty-two-point-four dimensions simultaneously while filling out customs forms for each one. Try explaining to your supervisor—who is literally the concept of Disappointment wearing a tie—why you're helping a human who technically shouldn't exist anymore. Try getting coffee when you don't have a mouth but still have caffeine addiction from your last corporeal assignment!"\n\nHe pauses, seems to collect himself. "Sorry. It's been a rough millennium. The point is, you made it. That's the important part. You're alive, you're aware, and you remember everything. Do you have any idea how statistically impossible that is?"\n\nI laugh, and it comes out more like a sob. "Great. I'm a statistical anomaly. That makes me feel so much better about being trapped in my childhood trauma."\n\n"Stephen." His voice sharpens, cutting through my self-pity like a blade made of disappointed parent energy. "Look at me. Really look. With whatever passes for spiritual eyes in that meat puppet you're wearing."\n\nI meet his gaze—that impossible eye that seems to contain entire galaxies, tax forms, and what might be a cosmic break room—and feel something settle inside me. The three versions of myself stop struggling for a moment, united in the simple act of listening to someone even more frustrated than we are.\n\n"You think this is your fault," he says. It's not a question. "You think you broke something. That you're the problem, the virus in the system that's causing reality to glitch and fracture. That if you could just... what? Forget? Stop remembering? Submit to the reset? Fill out the proper forms? Everything would be fine."\n\n"Isn't it?" The words come out small, young, carrying the weight of an eight-year-old's confused guilt. "I mean, before I started remembering past lives, before the flickers, before I opened my mouth about Whitney... people weren't disappearing. Reality wasn't eating itself."\n\nBenji's wings flutter, and for a moment he looks almost sad. "Oh, kid. You beautiful, stupid, magnificent disaster. You really don't get it, do you? You're like a smoke alarm blaming itself for the fire. 'If only I hadn't screamed, the house wouldn't be burning!'"\n\n"Get what?"\n\n"You're not the problem. You're the symptom." His eye narrows, focusing on me with laser intensity. "Etheris has been breaking down for eons. The prison was designed to contain one entity—I AM, Yaldabaoth, whatever you want to call that cosmic narcissist. But prisons decay. Systems fail. Even divine ones. ESPECIALLY divine ones. You ever try to get tech support from an entity that exists outside temporal continuity? It