ight of his words settles over me like a blanket made of bureaucratic frustration. "So what happens now? I'm stuck in this timeline, in this body, with a mother who poisons me and a father who..." I gesture vaguely toward the bedroom where snores still rattle the walls.\n\n"Now," Benji says, producing what appears to be a cosmic clipboard from nowhere, "we see how long it takes him to notice you're awake. I'm supposed to observe and document. Note behavioral changes. File reports in triplicate. You know, real helpful stuff while you're getting traumatized."\n\n"Him?"\n\n"I AM. Yaldabaoth. The thing wearing your father's face. Sometimes. When it remembers to." Benji's voice drops to a whisper that somehow fills the entire room. "He's been on autopilot for centuries, confident that the system would keep running without his direct intervention. Like a CEO who forgot he owns the company. But your little stunt with the anchor ritual? That was loud. Cosmic loud. The kind of noise that makes sleeping gods hit the snooze button with extreme prejudice."\n\nFear crawls up my spine like ice water mixed with expired milk—cold and wrong. "And when he wakes up?"\n\n"When he wakes up," Benji says, checking his clipboard with the expression of someone who's seen the schedule and doesn't like it, "we find out just how much you've really learned about fighting monsters. And I get to watch. And take notes. And file reports. While being explicitly forbidden from intervening due to Cosmic Non-Interference Clause 7.3.2a, subsection J."\n\nHe begins to fade as the mirror repairs itself around him, his form becoming translucent. "I'd help if I could, kid. I really would. But the bureaucracy of the divine is worse than the DMV and the IRS had a baby and that baby went to law school. All I can do is observe and occasionally provide cryptic guidance that's 40% helpful and 60% frustrating."\n\n"That's it? That's all you can do?"\n\n"Hey, I'm risking a lot just being here! Do you know what the 