y to sit up and realize I'm not in a bed—I'm on an examination table, the kind you'd find in a leather workshop. The surface is cold metal covered with scratches from years of cutting and measuring.\n\nThe door opens.\n\nNo sound. It just opens, revealing my father in a leather apron that's seen better days. In his hands, not one belt but dozens, draped over his arms like sleeping snakes.\n\nBut his face—his face is worse than any monster. It's perfectly normal. Perfectly calm. The face of a teacher who's disappointed you didn't do the homework.\n\n"Pop quiz," he says, his voice carrying that particular tone of an educator who knows you're about to fail. "Today we're learning about leather crafting. The noble art of belt-making."\n\nHe holds up the first belt—thin, black, with a silver buckle.\n\n"What type of leather is this?"\n\nMy mouth opens but no sound comes out. I don't know. How could I know?\n\n"WRONG!" The patience evaporates like water on hot metal. "All wrong! Every single answer!"\n\nHe throws the belts aside, and they writhe on the floor like living things. Behind him, one of the vats glows with heat, the tanning solution inside churning with an appetite all its own.\n\n"If you can't identify leather," he says, advancing with the calm purpose of someone performing a necessary task, "then perhaps you need a more... immersive education."\n\nHis hands are on my shoulders. Not violent. Almost gentle. The hands of a teacher guiding a student to the next lesson.\n\n"No, wait—"\n\n"The best way to understand leather," he says conversationally, walking me backward toward the vat, "is to experience the process firsthand."\n\nThe heat hits my back. The chemical stench makes me gag. I can see into the vat now—the solution isn't just boiling, it's alive, reaching up with tendrils made of dissolved skin and broken dreams.\n\n"Consider this," he says, his black eyes reflecting the churning liquid, "your final exam."\n\nHe doesn't push hard. Doesn't need to. Just a gentle pressure, like encouraging a reluctant student to try the deep end of the pool.\n\nI fall backward. The solution embraces me like a mother I never had, pulling me down into chemical warmth that strips away everything—skin, muscle, consciousness, the very idea of being human.\n\nThe room dissolves into white noise and the taste of leather.\n\nI'm gone.\n\nAnd somewhere, a bell rings.	1	453	t	2026-02-27 12:20:35.047922-05	2026-02-28 19:29:44.415204-05	normal	\N
