rest. It drips something that steams when it hits the floor, eating through the organic carpet like acid.\n\n"You always thought it was your choice to leave me, Aspolin."\n\nThe name hits like ice water in my brainstem. His voice doesn't come from his mouth anymore—it emanates from the walls, the screaming ceiling, the spaces between atoms where nightmares gestate.\n\nHis face stretches like warm taffy as skin peels away in long strips, revealing circuitry made of bone and muscle underneath. His body splits open from sternum to groin with wet tearing sounds, spilling golden light that burns my retinas.\n\n"You thought that this was your rebellion. But this loop... is your penance."\n\nFrom this chrysalis of meat and machinery emerges something vast and impossible—Yaldabaoth, the Demiurge, wearing divinity like an ill-fitting thrift store suit. It unfolds in directions that don't have names, revealing geometries that make my eyes water.\n\nThe room shrinks as the entity continues expanding, reality compressing like a trash compactor. But something's wrong—its voice cracks like an adolescent deity going through puberty, all harmonic distortion and feedback loops.\n\n"Kneel," it commands, but the word arrives broken, transmitted through a medium that can't handle the bandwidth.\n\nI remain standing, studying this decaying god-thing with detached fascination. This far into the infinite fringes of reality, the mask is finally slipping.\n\nThe space transforms into an impossible cathedral of compressed starlight and polished pearl, but underneath the beauty, angels are screaming—not singing, screaming in harmonies of precise agony. The entity towers above me, golden light shifting to reveal wings spanning dimensions, eyes blinking in mathematical sequences.\n\n"Kneel before your creator," it commands, but its voice trembles now. The golden light flickers, revealing cracks where hungry darkness leaks through. The heavenly chamber shudders, hairline fractures spreading from