n a box with the one person who never believed in him."\n\nThe walls flicker. Just once. Like a television struggling with bad reception.\n\n"See, kid, here's the thing about gods." Benji leans forward, and for a moment his form shifts—not quite the bureaucratic angel I'm used to, but something older, sadder, carrying the weight of watching universes bloom and die. "They're infinite and eternal and all-powerful, right up until the moment they're not. And then? Then they never were. Reality's funny that way—it's got a nasty habit of rewriting itself backward."\n\nI AM was never supposed to be trapped. That much I understand now, sitting in this frozen moment between heartbeats. He was a consumer, a cosmic mouth that opened wider with each reality he devoured. Souls weren't just food to him—they were validation, proof that he existed, acknowledgment that his hunger mattered. Every prayer, every scream, every moment of worship or terror fed him not just power but being itself.\n\n"The Outsiders contained him here," I say, the words coming from some deep well of inherited knowledge. "Because he would have eaten everything. Not just this reality, not just Earth or humanity. All of it. Every possible universe, every quantum possibility, every dream that ever was or could be."\n\nBenji nods, crunching another kernel. "Smart kid. Yeah, they built Etheris as a prison, but also as a feeding tube. Keep him contained, keep him fed, keep reality from becoming just one big empty stomach with I AM's name on it." His expression darkens. "Course, prisons have a way of becoming too small for their inmates."\n\nThe mobile above me gives the tiniest twitch. Luke's X-wing shifts a millimeter closer to Vader's ship, and suddenly the air tastes like apples and burnt electronics.\n\nI know what Benji isn't saying. I've always known, even when I was pretending not to. I AM isn't just connected to reality—he is the substrate everything else is built on. Pull him out, destroy him completely, 