hat carries the weight of three lifetimes of accumulated rage.\n\n"This is for Jamie," I say, my foot connecting with I AM's reconstructed skull with a sound like the universe's spine snapping.\n\nHis head separates from his shoulders in slow motion, spinning through the air like a dropped planet made of failing starlight. It tumbles end over end, his expression frozen in that perfect moment between godhood and gravity, before hitting the linoleum with the crystalline crash of a champagne flute filled with liquid mathematics.\n\nThe head shatters into countless fragments, each piece whispering a different lie he ever told, each shard reflecting a different reality he consumed. The fragments keep breaking, smaller and smaller, until they're just glittering dust that tastes like burnt offerings and broken promises.\n\nThe waiting room immediately begins to collapse. Not violently—almost gently, like a tired sigh made of architecture. The walls crack in perfectly geometric patterns, revealing equations that bleed backwards into themselves. The ceiling peels away in strips, exposing not sky but the raw code underneath reality, scrolling past in fonts that hurt to read directly.\n\nThe floor beneath my feet dissolves into static, pixel by pixel, leaving me standing on nothing but the memory of standing. Even the motionless Star Wars mobile finally moves, spinning faster and faster until Luke and Vader become a blur of eternal, meaningless conflict.	1	458	f	2026-02-27 12:20:46.564757-05	2026-02-28 19:33:59.211403-05	normal	\N
