463	117	1	The Fracture	Stephen experiences a disturbing moment at his desk when an unknown word surfaces in his typing, triggering fragmented memories of another life. His wife receives a call from someone named Whitney, and Stephen's body undergoes a horrifying biological transformation before consciousness scatters into darkness.	"An author. Yeah right." I mutter to myself. "Dreams come to Etheris to fucking die."\n\nI freeze. My fingers hover over the keyboard.\n\nEtheris?\n\nWhere the hell did that word come from? I meant to say Earth. Dreams come to Earth to die. That's the phrase. That's always been the phrase.\n\nBut my mouth said Etheris. Like it knew something my brain didn't.\n\nThe room suddenly feels... wrong. Too quiet. Like the moment between a lightning strike and thunder. I look at my hands. They're shaking. Why are they shaking?\n\nMy wife walks in, holding her phone at arm's length like it might bite her. Her face is pale. "Honey... there's someone on the phone for you."\n\n"Who?"\n\n"She says her name is Whitney." My wife's voice cracks.\n\n"I don't know anyone named…"\n\nBut I can't finish the sentence. Because suddenly I do. I remember everything. The flickers. The engine. The pointers in the shadows. The medical pod. The demon in Jamie's smile. Whitney vanishing into William. The journal that wrote itself. Benji's winged eye. The kick that started it all.\n\nThe phone trembles in my wife's hand. I can hear a voice on the other end. Distant. Familiar. Impossible.\n\n"Stephen?" the voice says. Clear as a bell. Clear as a memory that was never supposed to exist. "Stephen, it's Whitney. But you need to listen very carefully. I found Jay…"\n\nI drop the phone as the line suddenly goes dead.\n\nThe floor beneath my feet immediately feels wrong. It is no longer solid, but yields like soft, wet tissue, a membrane rippling as if I’ve stepped onto the surface of a vast, hidden organ. The sudden, absolute shock of forbidden knowledge, the memories exploding, has triggered something.\n\nA wave of intense, suffocating wrongness washes over me. This isn't cold; it's a biological imperative. My skin erupts in a slick, cold sweat that immediately thickens, becoming a viscous, opalescent fluid, the system’s lubricant, trying to scour the data from my very cells. My bones don't just ache; they soften, turning to gristle, the rigidity that holds me human dissolving from the inside out.\n\nI try to call out. My jaw unhinges, but what escapes isn't a scream. It's a low, wet, tearing sound as my tongue thickens and buds, sprouting new, useless papillae that taste only of static and ozone.\n\n"Stephen?"\n\nMy wife, looks at me, her head tilting slightly. She seems to be just noticing me for the first time, her movements slow, languid, possessing an unnerving, liquid grace that stands in stark contrast to the biological panic ripping my body apart.\n\n"Honey? What's wrong?" she asks.\n\nHer voice is soft, the pitch of perfect solicitude, but it’s a beat too early. Only after she speaks do her brows furrow. The motion is clean, mathematically precise, the expression of concern settling flawlessly onto her face, yet it never reaches her eyes.\n\nThey remain utterly placid, wide and bright. Watching, but not seeing.\n\nI want to point, to show her, but my hand is no longer my own. It knots into a fist, and I feel a sickening, wet pop as my knuckles burst through the skin, not as bone, but as glistening, cartilage-like nodules rapidly forming new, impossible joints. Skin stretches taut and tears like wet paper over a frame that is actively reconfiguring, trying to find a baseline that no longer exists.\n\nThe mundane details of the room violently assert themselves. The window doesn't pixelate; it develops a cataract, a milky, organic film clouding the light. I can hear the air pulsing, and I realize with terror that the pulse is mine. My own ribs are flexing, distending and contracting like a grotesque bellows, breathing for the room itself.\n\nMy vision doesn't tunnel; it blooms with arterial red as the blood vessels in my eyes erupt, weaving a cancerous mesh of new capillaries across my sight. The world implodes into a white-hot agony of nerves firing, dying, and regenerating all at once.\n\nThe last sense that registers is the sound of my heart literally tearing itself from its moorings, a final, heavy, wet rip as it fails to synchronize with the cosmic pulse.\n\nFlesh gives way. Consciousness scatters, no longer anchored to the ruined meat.\n\nI am gone.\n\nDarkness finally consumes me	0	475	f	2026-02-27 12:26:51.696511-05	2026-02-28 22:21:08.477877-05	normal	\N
464	117	2	The Reset	Stephen wakes in bed next to his wife with a profound sense of normalcy. They discuss his ongoing job search failures and financial impossibilities, with mounting tension over his lack of progress and her clinical, emotionless pressure.	I slowly open my eyes.\n\nI am laying in my bed, cocooned by the warmth of my wife's presence and enveloped in the exuberant chatter of Xavier talking to his friends on Discord comes in from the living room.\n\nLaying there I am hit with a profound sense of normalcy.\n\nThe light in the room had that thin, grey quality. I was awake, but I hadn't moved. I was facing the wall. She was behind me, a line of warmth against my back, but I knew she was awake, too. I could feel the tension in her stillness.\n\nShe sighed, a small, quiet puff of air. The bed shifted as she turned.\n\n"Did you check your phone yet?" Her voice was quiet, muffled by her pillow.\n\n"It's seven," I mumbled. "No one sends a rejection email at 7 AM Sunday. They wait until 4 PM on a Friday. Maximum damage."\n\n"They said they'd be in touch today. Synergex. You said that one felt good."\n\nI finally rolled over. Her eyes were open, staring at the ceiling. "They all feel good, honey. The interviews are always great. They love me. Right up until they 'go in a different direction.'"\n\n"'Or 'pause the hiring initiative,'" she recited, her voice flat, joining the familiar catechism.\n\n"'Or 'have an internal restructuring.'"\n\n"'Or 'find a candidate whose experience is a more precise match.'"\n\nA long silence settled between us, heavier than the comforter.\n\n"It's been two years," she whispered, still staring at the ceiling.\n\n"I'm aware."\n\n"No... I mean... two years. Thousands of applications. You said you hit five thousand."\n\n"Did I? I don't remember." I rubbed my face, my beard scratching my palms. "It's just... a blur. Fill out the form. Upload the resume. Write the cover letter. Do the dance. Get the 'no.' It's just noise. The job I had before... I can barely even remember what I did all day. It's all static."\n\n"You've had offers," she pressed, turning her head on the pillow to look at me. "Good ones. Remember Aeterna? The VP called you to welcome you aboard. And then... poof. 'Budget reallocation.' That's not normal."\n\nI pushed myself up on one elbow. "What part of any of this feels normal? And how are we... here? How are we still in this house? My severance was good, but it wasn't two years good. It can't be."\n\nI watched her. "I look at the bank account, and the number... it just sits there. It never really changes, does it? We're paying the mortgage. Xavier's still in his school. We're... eating."\n\nHer eyes were clear, untroubled. "I'm managing it. We're cutting back. It's fine."\n\n"It doesn't feel fine," I said, my voice rising. "It feels wrong! None of it adds up."\n\n"What needs to add up," she said, and her tone shifted. The sleepy warmth was gone, replaced by a clean, cool logic. She sat up in one smooth motion, swinging her legs over the side of the bed. "Is you getting a job. One that sticks."\n\nI just stared at her back.\n\n"All this... thinking about it... it's not productive," she continued, her voice clinical. "What's productive is applying."\n\n"I don't know if I can do it again today," I whispered, falling back onto the pillow.\n\n"You can." She stood and stretched, a normal, everyday motion that suddenly felt completely alien, like watching a demonstration. "Check for the Synergex email. Then send out twenty more. Okay? Just twenty. It's just a numbers game."\n\nShe paused at the bedroom door.\n\n"The numbers have to work out eventually, Stephen."\n\nThe door clicked shut. I was alone in the grey light, trying to remember what "Synergex" even did.\n\nIn the kitchen, my wife was pouring two mugs, her back to me. She wore faded pink PJs and didn't turn as I entered, just passed a mug over her shoulder in a practiced, silent motion.\n\n"Xavier's already on that thing," she said, her voice warm. "I swear, I think he just sleeps with the headset on."\n\n"Probably," I said, taking a sip. The coffee was hot, bordering on scalding. Perfect.\n\nWe sat at the small kitchen table. The sounds of Xavier's keyboard provided the silent soundtrack to our morning. She was scrolling through her phone, thumbing idly at the screen while her other hand held picking up a bite of rice.\n\n"Oh, that's weird," she mumbled.\n\n"What?"\n\n"Looks like I got a call at 3 AM last night. Don't remember it at all." She turned the phone and slid it across the table toward me. "Do you know this number?"\n\nI picked it up. The call log was open, a single unknown number at the top, time-stamped 3:04 AM. I squinted. The digits seemed to shimmer, refusing to resolve.\n\n"Hang on," I said, setting the phone down. "I left my glasses in the bedroom." I rubbed my eyes hard with my palms, blinking to clear the sleep-induced blur. "Let me see again."\n\nI looked back down.\n\nThe phone was displaying her lock screen. A bright, clear photo of Xavier’s first day of High School grinning at me.\n\nI stared at it, the image perfectly in focus. A beat of pure confusion passed. I had... I was... what was I doing? I had a distinct feeling I was in the middle of a task, but the thread was gone.\n\nSlowly, I picked up the phone and handed it back to her.\n\nShe took it with a small, pleasant smile. "Oh, thanks, honey." She tapped the screen, which was already dark, and set it face down on the table.\n\nShe took a bite of her rice. "So, what's on the agenda for today? It's Sunday, I know, but..." She let the 'but' hang in the air, heavy with expectation.\n\nI sipped my coffee. It was lukewarm now. "Figured I'd do a little research. Look up a few of those companies I bookmarked. See if there's any new postings." I shrugged. "Then, probably just veg out. Hop on that new MMORPG I’ve been playing."\n\nThe idea of spending another eight hours on a weekend scrolling through LinkedIn, sending connection requests, tailoring resumes to automated scanners, and filling out the same endless forms made my stomach clench. It was a slow-motion psychic death. I just couldn't do it. Not every single day.\n\nHer smile didn't just fade; it inverted. Her lips thinned, and she put her spoon down.\n\n"Veg out," she repeated, the words sounding sterile. "Stephen, 'researching' isn't applying. Playing video games isn't applying."\n\n"It's Sunday," I said, my defensiveness rising. "I'm not a machine. I can't just... apply for jobs 12 hours a day, seven days a week. It's... it kills me."\n\n"What's 'killing us' is this," she said, her voice quiet but sharp, gesturing around the kitchen. "You say you're doing everything you can, but then you take the whole weekend off. Every weekend. 'Research' and 'vegging out' isn't going to get you a job."\n\n"I sent out thirty applications on Friday," I shot back.\n\n"And how many will you send out today? Zero? That's what it sounds like."\n\nShe stood up, taking her mug and bowl to the sink. The sound of the ceramic clinking into the stainless steel sink was loud in the quiet room.\n\nI just watched her, the familiar feeling of uselessness settling over me like a wet, cold blanket.\n\nShe sighed, her back to me. "I don't care. Whatever. I've got laundry to do."\n\nShe walked out, leaving me alone at the table with my cold coffee and the distant clicking of Xavier saving some other world.	1	389	t	2026-02-27 12:26:51.696511-05	2026-02-28 22:22:01.340867-05	normal	\N
466	117	4	The Unraveling	Stephen and his wife sit on opposite sides of the couch at night, separated by silence and screens. He receives another rejection email and attempts a desperate advance that is coldly rejected. A disturbing realization strikes: he cannot remember his wife's name.	**********\n\nIt was night. Maybe 8 PM. Maybe 10. The clocks in this house didn't measure time; they measured the duration of my failure.\n\nThe only light came from the television. Some bright, loud reality show was on mute. The contestants were smiling, laughing, living. It was obscene.\n\nShe was on the other end of the couch. A separate cushion. A separate continent. Her face was lit from below by the white-blue glow of her phone. She was scrolling. Always scrolling.\n\nThe house was a mess. Not trashy, just... stagnant. The coffee table was a cluttered landscape. Remnants of old, unread mail—mostly thin, official-looking envelopes I was afraid to open—were scattered around the base of Xavier's complex Lego DnD dragon tower. The tower itself was broken, a whole section of it collapsed after someone knocked it over weeks ago. The scattered bricks just sat there, a monument to our shared inertia, a testament to the fact that no one had the energy to even try to fix it.\n\nI checked my email on my phone. The ritual. The self-flagellation. A new one.\n\nFrom: Aeterna Human Resources\n\nSubject: An Update on Your Application (Director, Future-Proofing)\n\nI’d applied to Aeterna again. The same company that had "budget-reallocated" my VP offer two years ago. I'd applied for a role two rungs down the ladder.\n\n"Dear Stephen,\n\nWhile we were impressed with your experience, we have decided to pursue a candidate whose vision for the future is a more precise match..."\n\n"A more precise match." I didn't even have the energy to feel angry. It was just... information. The loop confirmed. I let the phone drop onto the cushion beside me. The screen stayed lit, a tombstone for my career.\n\nI looked at her. I was starving. Not for food. I was starving for... contact. A touch. A word that wasn't an instruction or a sigh. The memory of the kitchen hug, a week ago... or was it a month? It felt like a lifetime. It had been a tactical maneuver. A calculated jolt to get the machine running.\n\nNow, the machine was broken again.\n\nI was a beggar. I knew it. I hated it. I slid my hand across the cushion, an inch at a time, until my fingers brushed the fabric of her PJs. She didn't react. I put my hand on her thigh.\n\nHer muscles didn't just tense; they hardened. She became stone. She didn't look up from her phone. Her thumb just... stopped scrolling. She was waiting. Waiting for me to finish this pathetic, unwanted advance so she could go back to whatever digital world was more interesting than me.\n\nMy hand was just resting there. A dead thing on a dead limb. My mind flashed to the last time. Six, maybe seven months ago. The bi-annual pity party. It was always after a particularly bad rejection. She'd let me. That was the only word for it. She would lie on her back, arms at her sides, staring at the cracked paint on the ceiling. A perfect, unmoving starfish. The act was a cold, clinical exercise in my own humiliation. It wasn't intimacy; it was a chore she performed, like taking out the trash, just to shut me up. The only sound was the mattress crying, a rhythm of pure, pathetic need. I always felt sicker afterward. More alone.\n\nI pulled my hand back from her thigh. I couldn't do it. I couldn't be that beggar tonight. I curled my hand into a fist on my own lap.\n\nI just... looked at her. Her face, illuminated by the phone. The lines around her mouth. The small furrow between her brows. Who is she? I mean, she was "my wife." The mother of my child. The person on the other end of the couch. But... who was she?\n\nMy mind went blank. I tried to picture her name. I could picture her signature on a permission slip, a sharp, angry-looking scrawl. I could hear myself thinking the word "wife." But the name. The name... It wasn't there. It was a 404 error in my own brain.\n\nA cold, electric panic washed over me. Rebecca? No. Jennifer? No. Andrea? I was just guessing. I was pulling names from a void. I could remember the name of my Level 90 Necromancer. I could remember "Synergex" and "Aeterna." I could remember the name of the Gargoyle King in an Early Access video game. I could not remember my wife's name.\n\nThe room tilted. The silent, smiling faces on the TV were laughing at me. "Honey?" I whispered. My voice was a dry croak. It was a placeholder. A variable for a value that was null.\n\nShe finally, finally, let out an aggravated sigh and looked up from her phone. The light cut out, plunging her face into shadow. "What, Stephen?"\n\nShe knew my name. Oh god, she knew my name. I just stared at her, the blank space where her name should be pulsing in my head like a wound. "Nothing," I mumbled, and turned to face the laughing, silent TV. "It's nothing."	3	390	t	2026-02-27 12:26:51.696511-05	2026-02-28 22:23:40.799739-05	normal	\N
