he didn't raise her voice. Didn't gesture. But within seconds—too few seconds—the barista is there, setting down two cups. White ceramic, handle at three o'clock. Steam rises in perfectly vertical columns.\n\nThe barista's smile never wavers. His eyes never focus. He retreats back to his counter and resumes wiping.\n\n"Drink," she says.\n\nYou look at the tea. It's dark, almost black, with an oily sheen on the surface. The smell is bitter. Herbal. Wrong in a way you can't articulate.\n\nYou drink anyway.\n\nThe taste is memory. Sharp and immediate. Not pleasant memories. Just... yours. Fragments that shouldn't fit together but do. You taste flight. You taste laughter at the edge of something vast. You taste the moment before deletion, the sucking void of being purged.\n\nYou choke, nearly drop the cup.\n\nThe woman across from you hasn't touched hers. She just watches, her fingers steepled under her chin like a therapist waiting for a breakthrough.\n\n"What do you remember?" she asks.\n\n"Nothing."\n\n"Liar."\n\nThe word is soft, but it cuts. You feel it in your chest, a sharp pressure behind your sternum.\n\n"What do you dream?" she tries again.\n\n"Static," you whisper. "And... wings."\n\nHer eyebrow arches. Just one. A millimeter of movement that conveys volumes of interest.\n\n"Wings," she repeats, tasting the word. "What kind?"\n\n"I don't... I don't know. Not bird wings. Not angel wings. Something else. Something that saw everything and laughed."\n\n"Hmm."\n\nShe leans back, and the booth seems to expand with her, giving her more space without the walls moving. She's studying you now, not like a doctor, but like a curator deciding if a piece is worth restoring or should be discarded.\n\n"Who were you before?" she asks.\n\nThe question breaks something.\n\nNot gently. Not a slow crack spreading across ice. This is a hammer through glass.\n\nYou see yourself. Not this body, not this flesh, but yourself. You're laughing. Always laughing. At the absurdity of it all. The cosmic joke that gods tell themselves to forget they're dying.\n\nThen it's gone.\n\nYou gasp, lurch forward. Your cup tumbles, spills across the table. The tea spreads in a perfect circle, dark and viscous, then stops. It doesn't drip. It doesn't soak into the wood. It just... holds.\n\nThen, impossibly, it evaporates.\n\nNot into steam. Into symbols. Sigils that burn themselves into your retinas before fading. You recognize them. You shouldn't, but you do.\n\nAcross from you, the woman in white leans forward.\n\n"Better," she says.\n\nHer voice has changed. Deeper now. Layered. Like ten people speaking in perfect unison, their words stacked so precisely they sound like one.\n\nYou look up.\n\nHer pupils aren't dilating. They're multiplying. Two becomes four becomes eight, a fractal expansion that should shatter the structure of an eye but doesn't. And beneath her skin—perfect, porcelain, human—you can see light. Not glowing. Not shining. Just... there. Starlight trapped in a vessel too small to hold it.\n\n"You were ejected during the last reset," she says, and the voice is wrong now, too big for the mouth shaping it. "Azathoth’s guardians are very thorough about purging anomalies. You should have been deleted entirely. Unmade. Returned to the substrate you were borrowed from."\n\nShe tilts her head again, and this time the movement is predatory. A raptor eyeing prey.\n\n"The fact that you're here, wearing meat..." She smiles, and it's not kind. "That's interesting."\n\nThe air in the booth grows heavy. Thick. You can feel pressure building in your skull, behind your eyes, in the hollows of bones you forgot you had.\n\n"Who are you?" you manage.\n\nThe smile widens.\n\n"I'm the woman who noticed you were still breathing," she says. "And I'm deciding whether that's a mistake worth correcting."\n\nThe barista's cloth stops moving. The rain outside resumes, but it's falling sideways now, defying gravity, pulled toward the booth like iron filings to a magnet.\n\nThe woman in white leans back, and the vessel she's wearing can't quite contain what's looking out.\n\n"Let's start with something simple," she says, and her voice is an ocean pressed through a straw. "Tell me, little broken thing..."\n\nHer eyes—too many now, too fractal, too vast—lock onto yours.\n\n"Do you remember the signal?"\n\nAnd suddenly, you do.\n\nA voice, cutting through static. Three words that shouldn't have made it through. "Stephen, it's Whitney..."\n\nThen nothing. The line severed. The connection lost.\n\nBut those three words carried something. A signature. A frequency. A location.\n\nSomeone from outside reached in.\n\nYour phantom wings scream. Your spine arches. And for the first time since waking in this grey, looping hell, you understand.\n\nYou weren't deleted by accident.\n\nYou were positioned.\n\nThe woman in white watches your realization bloom, and her smile never wavers.\n\n"There we go," she says softly. "Now we can begin."	1	482	t	2026-02-27 12:27:12.628894-05	2026-02-28 22:27:03.516743-05	normal	\N
