r.\n\nHer tone hardens instantly. “Research? You mean wasting electricity chasing ghosts? You told me you were job searching.”\n\nI look up at her over the monitors. “I didn’t tell you anything.”\n\n“Exactly,” she replies too quickly, like a response drawn from a script. “Because you never tell me anything until I have to drag it out of you. And now the power bill’s through the roof, and we’re behind again, but sure, let’s keep feeding your obsessions.”\n\nI study her face—how her smile flickers a frame too slow, how her eyes don’t quite blink together.\n\n“Why do you care how long I’m on the computer?”\n\n“Because it’s mine too,” she says automatically. Then louder, “Because you don’t listen!”\n\nShe crosses the room. The smell of something wrong trails behind her.\n\nHer movements are jerky, as if reality has forgotten how joints work.\n\n“Turn it off, Stephen. Right now.”\n\n“I just need a few minutes. Please.”\n\n“You’re using too much power.”\n\nThe lights dim again. The cursor on the screen multiplies, hundreds of blinking eyes.\n\nI try to ask her to be reasonable. “It’s important.”\n\nHer voice flattens to monotone. “You’re wasting power.”\n\n“Just a minute.”\n\nShe reaches out—grabs the corner of the computer underneath the desk…\n\nAnd a surge of static jumps between them, bright enough to sting.\n\nThe monitors flashes white.\n\nFan screams.\n\nSmoke curls from the back vent.\n\nShe jerks her hand back, eyes wide. “What the heck did you do?”\n\nI blink at her. “I didn’t—”\n\n“You always break things! Every time I turn around, something else is fried or crashed or corrupted. What were you even doing?”\n\n“Nothing,” I say, too fast. “It just—glitched. I’ll get it fixed.”\n\nHer voice snaps sharp again. “With what money, Stephen?”\n\n“We’ve still got the warranty. We got this right before the layoff two years ago.” I say, standing slowly. “It’s almost out, but still good. I’ll take it in tomorrow. I know someone there.”\n\nShe stares at me like she’s trying to read a line that isn’t written yet. “You always ‘know someone.’”\n\n“I do,” I say softly. “Gil. Best Buy.”\n\nFor a moment, something in her expression flickers—confusion, then recalibration. The same blank reboot he’s seen before.\n\n“Oh,” she says finally, voice smoothing out. “Fine. You handle it.”\n\nShe turns and drifts back toward the other side of the house, back to our bedroom. She starts to hum again, same broken tune, same single note circling itself into nothing.\n\nI exhale and look down at the dead monitors.\n\nBecause I’m still trying to love her.	3	505	t	2026-02-27 12:28:17.511922-05	2026-02-28 22:52:02.890953-05	normal	\N
492	122	1	The Best Buy Encounter	Stephen visits a struggling Best Buy to repair his computer and reconnects with his old friend Gil. Strange exchanges about high school acquaintances lead to discovery of disturbing gaps in collective memory, culminating in an encounter with Andrew and Morgan who reveal information about Whitney and Patrick.	The Best Buy smelled.\n\nNot just old—though it was that too, the kind of stench that settles into carpet fibers and never leaves. It smelled like a place that had stopped trying. Like it knew.\n\nI hefted the desktop tower onto the Geek Squad counter, the ancient PC case making a dull thunk against the laminate. Three of the fluorescent panels overhead were dead. The ones still working buzzed like dying wasps.\n\n"Holy shit, Stephen, is that you?" Someone said from the back of the store.\n\nGil looked different. Heavier around the middle, grayer at the temples, but his eyes still had that spark. That old "I see the magic under the surface" spark we'd all had back in high school before the world beat it out of most of us.\n\n"Hey, man." I tried to smile. Failed. "Yeah. It's me."\n\nHe came around the counter and we did the awkward half-hug thing guys do when they haven't seen each other in years. He smelled like energy drinks and electronics cleaner.\n\n"Damn, Stephen. How long's it been? Ten years?"\n\n"Something like that."\n\nHe stepped back, taking in my rumpled shirt, the bags under my eyes. "You look like shit, brother. What happened?"\n\nI gestured at the tower. "This happened. Or… my wife happened. To this."\n\nGil's eyebrows went up. "She… attacked your computer?"\n\n"It's complicated." I lowered my voice, even though the store was empty except for a bored cashier at the far end picking at her phone. "When I get close to finding anything about what I’m searching for, she threatens divorce. When I drop it, she promises… other things. That never happen."\n\nThe words came out flat. Tired. I'd stopped being angry about it somewhere around the third loop.\n\nGil winced. "Damn, dude. That's… I'm sorry." He pulled the tower toward him, started logging it into the system. "So what happened? Spill something on it?"\n\n"She poured coffee into the top vent. While it was running." I really couldn’t tell him the truth for fear he’d short circuit or something.\n\n"Jesus."\n\n"Yeah."\n\nHe typed slowly, hunting and pecking. The keyboard was missing two keys. "And you need the data off it?"\n\n"I need to keep searching. For Whitney."\n\nHe glanced up. Something flickered across his face. Confusion, maybe. "Whitney?"\n\nMy chest tightened. "Yeah. Whitney. You remember her, right? From high school?"\n\nGil's face went blank. Not thoughtful-blank. Empty-blank. Like I'd just asked him to recall a dream from fifteen years ago.\n\n"Whitney," he repeated slowly, testing the word. "No, man. Doesn't ring a bell. You sure she went to our school?"\n\nMy chest tightened. "Yeah. She was in our grade. Had that leather journal she carried everywhere. Always wore black nail polish."\n\nGil shook his head, genuine confusion on his face. "Sorry, dude. Still drawing a complete blank."\n\nThe fluorescent lights buzzed louder.\n\nI swallowed. Tried a different angle. "Okay. Okay, what about... Jamie?"\n\nHis face twitched.\n\nNot a normal twitch. His whole expression froze mid-formation, like a video buffering. His mouth opened slightly. Closed. Opened again.\n\n"Ja..." He blinked hard. "Jam... who?"\n\nThe lights flickered. Once. Twice.\n\nGil physically recoiled, one hand going to his temple. "Sorry, man. My brain just... 404'd. What did you say?"\n\n"Jamie. Do you remember anyone named Jamie from high school?"\n\nHis eyes unfocused. His hand pressed harder against his head. "I... there's..." He winced. "It’s like there's supposed to be something there that isn’t. Like, my head hurts trying to think about it. That's weird, right?"\n\nThe door chimed.\n\nWe both looked up.\n\nTwo people walked in. The guy and girl I'd seen at Jamie’s Marginalia. Andrew and Morgan. They moved in that same slow, deliberate way, like they were wading through invisible water.\n\nThey walked directly to the smart home display.\n\nAndrew picked up a box. Looked at it. Put it down.\n\nThey both turned and walked toward us.\n\nMy pulse kicked up. "Gil—"\n\n"Yeah, they come in all the time," he said quickly, too quickly, still rubbing his temple. "Went to high school with us. Andrew and Morgan. You remember them, right?"\n\nI watched them approach. "No. Not really."\n\n"Huh." Gil's voice had gone flat. Distant. "Well, they did."\n\nI watched the door swing shut behind them. "I saw them in Orlando last week. At James and Becca’s bookshop. They did the exact same thing. Picked up a book, put it down, left. Seems like everyone remembers them but me."\n\n"Maybe they're just indecisive." Gil laughed. It sounded hollow. "They're NPCs, man."\n\n"What?"\n\n"Non-player characters." He tapped the side of his head. "Background people. They don't have inner monologues. No real thoughts. They're just… running a script."\n\nIt was almost as if he was repeating what Xavier told me before. Word for word.\n\n"You can tell?"\n\n"Sure. You just… you learn to see it. Who's real and who's furniture." He pulled a work order slip from under the counter, started filling it out by hand. "Most people are furniture, Stephen. Just… there to fill space."\n\nThe buzzing got louder. Or maybe it was just in my head.\n\nGil paused, pen hovering over the form. "Speaking of people who... forgot. Did you ever hear what happened to Patrick?"\n\n“Patrick? No. Why?"\n\n"That whole amnesia thing he had senior year was crazy. Like, completely blanked on huge chunks of his life." Gil shook his head. "But after he graduated, he got into the Air Force. Straightened himself out, I guess. Last I heard..." He trailed off, looking uncomfortable.\n\n"Last you heard what?"\n\n"He was shot down. Training exercise over Panama. They never found the wreckage. Just... gone." Gil cleared his throat. "Anyway. Been like five years now."\n\nMy mouth went dry. Patrick. Dead. Or missing. And I hadn't even known.\n\n"Let me take this to the back," Gil said, hefting the tower like he wanted to escape the conversation. "See what I can salvage."\n\nI nodded, not trusting my voice.\n\nHe disappeared through the "Employees Only" door. I stood there, staring at the empty store. At the clearance stickers on the sparse shelves. At the flickering lights.\n\nNPCs.\n\nThe door swung open and Gil emerged, but he wasn't holding paperwork. He was holding a single card.\n\n"Almost forgot," he said, grinning. "Speaking of old magic. Check out what I scored."\n\nHe handed it to me.\n\nIt was a Magic: The Gathering card. The art showed a figure stepping between realities, one foot in shadow, one in light. The figure's face was obscured, but something about the posture felt… familiar.\n\nVeilwalker\n\nI read the text box.\n\nThis card cannot be removed from the battlefield. If Veilwalker is ever alone, exile it and lose the game.\n\nThe words didn't just register in my brain. They registered somewhere deeper. Somewhere that recognized the pattern, the rule, the truth behind the game mechanic.\n\nI felt it before I heard it.\n\nA thrum.\n\nSilent. Invisible. But undeniable. Like a tuning fork struck deep in my chest cavity, vibrating outward through my ribs, through the air, through the walls of this dying Best Buy and out into the cosmos beyond.\n\nThe fluorescent lights stopped buzzing. Suddenly all the lights above remembered they were supposed to be working.\n\nThe air shifted. Not temperature. Something else. Like reality had skipped a frame and reloaded slightly wrong.\n\nI looked up.\n\nAndrew and Morgan were standing at the counter.\n\nNot approaching. Just... there. Like they'd always been there. But I'd watched them leave. Watched the automatic doors hiss shut behind them.\n\nGil didn't seem to notice anything strange. "Hey guys. Find what you needed?"\n\n"Hey Stephen," Andrew said. His voice had weight now. Presence. Not the flat affect from before. "Hey Gil."\n\nMy skin prickled. "Andrew."\n\n"You're wrong about Patrick," he said, like we'd been mid-conversation. Like the last ten minutes hadn't happened.\n\nGil blinked. "What?"\n\n"Patrick. You said he was dead. Shot down over Panama." Andrew's eyes were focused. Sharp. "He's not dead."\n\nMorgan nodded. "He lives out in BFE. Way out past Christmas."\n\n"Christmas, Florida?" I asked.\n\n"Yeah. Got himself a whole compound or something. Real eccentric." Morgan leaned against the counter. "Living off his Purple Heart and VA benefits. Went full hermit after he got out."\n\nGil was staring at them. "How do you... I didn't think you guys knew Patrick."\n\n"We didn't really," Andrew said. "But we knew his girlfriend. Or... ex-girlfriend? It was complicated."\n\nMy heart stopped. "Girlfriend?"\n\n"Yeah. Whitney, I think her name was." Andrew looked at Morgan. "That right?"\n\n"Whitney," Morgan confirmed. "They were together for a while. Then she just... disappeared. I think that's what messed Patrick up so bad."\n\nGil's face had gone blank again. "Whitney? Who's Whitney?"\n\nAndrew frowned. "Patrick's girl. You really don't remember her?"\n\n"No clue, man."\n\nI was still holding the card. The Veilwalker. My hands had started shaking.\n\nAndrew turned to me. "You should go see him. Patrick. If you're looking into old high school stuff." He pulled out his phone, tapped at it. "I've got his address somewhere. Let me send it to you."\n\n"I... yeah. Okay." I fumbled for my phone.\n\nThe text came through. An address. Way out in the sticks, past where civilization stopped pretending.\n\n"Thanks," I managed.\n\nAndrew nodded. He and Morgan turned and walked away. This time I watched them all the way to the door. Watched them leave. Watched the doors close shut.\n\nGil was staring at me. "You okay, man? You just... went somewhere."\n\n"Yeah." My voice sounded far away. "Yeah, I'm good."\n\nBut I wasn't.\n\nBecause somewhere out there, I knew someone had just felt that vibration too.\n\nAnd they were searching.	0	506	f	2026-02-27 12:28:34.462059-05	2026-02-28 22:53:10.219175-05	normal	\N
493	122	2	Benji's Hunt in the Grey Purgatory	In a place between realities, Benji and Lady Illkeserod fight off monstrous creatures before detecting a strong vibration signal emanating from a god null zone. Benji determines the signal likely comes from Whitney and prepares to descend into the dangerous zone.	The thing had too many teeth.\n\nThat was Benji's first thought as he rolled under its swipe, the claws missing his face by inches. Not just a lot of teeth. Too many. More than could fit in a mouth that size, packed in like tumors, each one pulsing with its own wet heartbeat. They grew from the gums in clusters, spiraling inward toward a throat that opened into somewhere else entirely.\n\n"Left!" Lady Illkeserod's voice cut through the chaos.\n\nHe pivoted, bringing his blade up in a wide arc. The weapon wasn't really a blade—it was energy shaped like the memory of a blade, pulled from the raw substrate of this place. It cut through the beast's leg, and the limb didn't just sever. It unraveled. Muscle peeled away from bone in wet ribbons, revealing something underneath that looked like exposed nerve endings woven into lattices of tooth stubs. The thing screamed in frequencies that made his bones ache, made his teeth feel loose in his gums.\n\nBlack ichor sprayed across the ground. Where it landed, the surface bubbled and grew tiny mouths that gasped for air before sealing shut.\n\nTwo more shadows peeled off the ground that wasn’t really ground, manifesting from the dark energy that swirled around them like amniotic fog. But they weren't emerging from the darkness. They were being born from it, pulling themselves through membranes of congealed space that tore with wet, ripping sounds.\n\nBenji counted appendages. Legs that bent wrong. Eyes in places eyes shouldn't be—embedded in joints, blinking from the hollows of throats, weeping a clear fluid that hissed when it hit the ground.\n\nThe air itself felt wrong here. Thick. Viscous. Like breathing through placenta.\n\n"How many more?" he shouted.\n\n"Does it matter?" Lady Illkeserod stood at the edge, her form flickering between states. Sometimes she was solid. Sometimes she was just the idea of solid, a sketch of a woman drawn in the static between realities. Right now she looked annoyed, which was never good.\n\nThe nearest beast lunged at her, all teeth and hunger and impossible geometry.\n\nShe raised one hand.\n\nThe shadow didn't explode. It didn't dissolve. It inverted. Its insides became its outsides in a single, nauseating moment. Organs that shouldn't exist in three dimensions spilled into the space where the creature had been—pulsing sacs connected by threads of sinew, a heart with seven chambers beating in discordant rhythm, intestines that looped through themselves in Klein bottle configurations.\n\nThen even those stopped being. Reality pressed delete, and the space was empty. Clean. Like nothing had ever been there at all.\n\nThe other beast, seeing this, tried to retreat back into the shadows it had crawled from.\n\nLady Illkeserod clenched her fist.\n\nThe creature's spine snapped backward, vertebrae erupting through its skin. It collapsed in on itself, folding and folding until it was smaller than a fist, compressed into a dense point of screaming matter, then gone.\n\nBenji straightened, breathing hard. The air tasted like copper and formaldehyde. "Could've done that from the start."\n\n"Where's the fun in that?"\n\n"Fun. Right." He sheathed the blade, watching it dissolve back into potential. Around them, the undulating floor was slick with residue—fluids that couldn't decide if they were liquid or gas, slowly evaporating into chaotic patterns. "Remind me why we're out here again?"\n\n"We're looking for—"\n\nShe stopped.\n\nHer head snapped up, eyes going wide. Not afraid. Surprised.\n\nThe membrane of reality around them shivered. Like skin responding to a touch from the inside.\n\nBenji felt it a half-second later.\n\nA vibration.\n\nNot sound. Not motion. Something deeper. Like someone had plucked a string that ran through the center of all things, and the note was still resonating outward in concentric waves.\n\nHe stumbled, catching himself against what looked like a rock.\n\n"What the hell was that?"\n\nLady Illkeserod was already moving, her form solidifying as she crossed to where Benji stood fumbling with the device from one of his pockets.\n\nHe pulled it free—an impossible thing that looked like someone had crossbred a sextant with an orrery and then drowned it in alchemical mercury. Rings within rings rotated around a central eye that wasn't glass or crystal but something that watched. Symbols crawled across its surface like living things, each one burning with a different color of fire that left afterimages in the air.\n\nA line of pure white light had carved itself across the eye's surface, pulsing.\n\n"A signal," Benji said, his hands already working the outer rings before his brain caught up. Old instincts. Muscle memory from lifetimes of tracking things that didn't want to be found. "A strong one."\n\nThe device was warm in his hands. Too warm. Like holding something freshly alive.\n\nHe rotated the outer ring. The symbols reconfigured, sliding past each other in combinations that would've made Euclidean geometers weep. The line of light shifted, bent, angled itself like an arrow pointing into the deep dark below them.\n\nThe eye in the center dilated.\n\nLady Illkeserod leaned in, close enough that he could feel the comfort of her radiating against his skin. "Direction?"\n\n"Deep." His fingers danced over the burning sigils, careful not to touch the ones that whispered. "Very deep. It's coming from…"\n\nHe paused, double-checking the configuration. The symbols had aligned into a pattern he recognized. A warning pattern.\n\n"A god null zone."\n\nLady Illkeserod’s expression went flat. "Of course it is."\n\nGod null zones were… unpleasant. Pockets of reality where magic still existed in its raw, primal form, but where gods couldn't walk. Couldn't manifest. Couldn't even observe without risking dissolution. The metaphysics got complicated, something about faith-structures and belief-matrices, but the short version was simple: gods go in, gods don't come out.\n\n"Could be a trap," Lady Illkeserod said.\n\n"Could be her."\n\nThey both stared at the white line.\n\nBenji had been searching for three hundred and sixty seven eternities. That's how time worked out here—it didn't, really, but you still felt it pass. Felt the weight of searching, of coming close, of losing the trail. Of circling uncountable realities out here in the multiverse, these grey purgatories where forgotten things went to dissolve, waiting for Whitney to wake up enough that he could find her.\n\nThree hundred and sixty seven eternities of almost.\n\n"It has to be Whitney," he said quietly. "That vibration… it felt like a beacon. Like she's calling."\n\n"Or something wants us to think that."\n\n"Ilkse—"\n\nShe turned to face him fully. Her eyes were ancient. Sad. "You know I can't go in there with you."\n\n"I know."\n\n"The null zones would unmake me. Peel me apart layer by layer. I'm too… present. Too manifest." She reached out, placed her hand on his head. Not lover's touch. Not quite mother's either. Something in between. Creator and created. "But you… you're still mostly incorporeal. Mostly."\n\nHe leaned into the touch for a moment, then stepped back. "Then I guess I'm going."\n\n"Benji—"\n\n"She's down there, Ilkse. I can feel it." His hand went to his weapon, or where his weapon would be when he needed it. "And after so many eternities, I'm not losing the trail now."\n\nThe ground shuddered beneath them. Not from danger. From the current of the Akashic Flow shifting, responding to the vibration that still echoed through everything.\n\nLady Illkeserod looked at the device again, at the white line pointing down into the dark places between realities.\n\n"If you die in there," she said, "I won't be able to pull you out."\n\n"If I don't go, we lose her."\n\nSilence. The kind that felt like a decision being made.\n\nFinally, Lady Illkeserod nodded. "Find her. Bring her back. And if it is a trap…"\n\n"I'll spring it carefully."\n\nShe almost smiled. Almost.\n\nBenji moved to the edge, staring down into the swirling dark. Somewhere below, in a place where gods feared to tread, Whitney was waiting. Or something was.\n\nThe white line pulsed.\n\nHe jumped.\n\nThe darkness swallowed him whole, and Lady Illkeserod was left alone, watching the space where he'd been, her hand still raised as if in blessing.\n\nOr farewell.	1	507	t	2026-02-27 12:28:34.462059-05	2026-02-28 22:54:12.55464-05	normal	\N
494	122	3	Whitney's Struggle and Awakening	Whitney battles fire beasts in a twisted landscape before finding shelter in an ancient stone structure. She initiates a dream walk to traverse realities, detecting the vibration calling to her and preparing to answer it.	**********\n\nWhitney's lungs burned with every breath.\n\nShe ran between the trees, boots kicking up ash that refused to settle, that hung in the air like it was waiting for permission to fall. Behind her, the fire beasts howled—a sound that started as hunger and ended as broken mathematics.\n\nThe trees were wrong.\n\nNot just alien. Wrong. Their bark had a metallic sheen, like copper left to oxidize in something other than oxygen. They didn't sway in the wind because there was no wind. They pulsed. Slow. Rhythmic. Like lungs drawing breath through wood and sap.\n\nWhere her shoulder brushed one, she felt it exhale.\n\nThe ground beneath her feet wasn't ground. It was too soft, too yielding. Scar tissue stretched over something vast and sleeping. Each footfall left an impression that didn't quite fill back in, like running across the surface of a healing wound.\n\nThe sky above was a bruised purple that never changed. No sun. No stars. Just that constant, sickly twilight that made her eyes ache if she looked at it too long.\n\nThe howl came closer.\n\nShe risked a glance back.\n\nThree of them. Maybe four. Hard to tell when they moved like that—bodies made of flame and solidified rage, shapes that couldn't decide if they were canine or something older, something that had learned to hunt before the concepts of mammals were ever conceived. Their eyes were white-hot points of hunger, and where their feet touched the ground, the scar tissue sizzled and wept clear fluid.\n\nHer lungs burned. Not just from running. From breathing the air here, which tasted like spoiled eggs and burnt hair and something sweet that made her want to gag.\n\nA root caught her foot.\n\nShe went down hard, chin bouncing off the ash-ground, stars exploding behind her eyes. The howls became screams of triumph.\n\nNo. No, not like this.\n\nHer hand found something solid. A branch. Or what passed for a branch here—it was heavy, gnarled, still warm like it had just stopped being alive. She rolled onto her back, gripping it with both hands.\n\nThe first beast lunged.\n\nShe swung.\n\nThe impact jolted up her arms, and the thing's head snapped sideways with a crack like breaking glass. It stumbled, flames guttering, and she was already moving, scrambling to her feet, bringing the branch around for another strike.\n\nIt caught the second beast in the ribs. The fire dimmed where she hit it, revealing something underneath. Something with too many bones.\n\nThe third came from the side.\n\nShe dropped low, letting it sail over her, then drove the branch upward like a spear. It punched through the thing's belly—if it had a belly—and the fire went out all at once. The beast collapsed into ash and something else, something that hissed as it touched the ground.\n\nTwo left.\n\nShe was already moving, not thinking, just reacting. The branch was an extension of her arms. Eighty years of surviving in places like this had taught her that thinking got you killed. You moved. You struck. You survived.\n\nThe fourth beast died with her makeshift weapon through its throat.\n\nThe last one circled, wary now. Intelligent. That was worse.\n\nWhitney stared at it, breathing hard, blood from her split chin dripping onto her torn shirt. Her muscles screamed. Her hands were blistered from gripping the branch.\n\nThe beast feinted left.\n\nShe didn't fall for it.\n\nIt lunged right, and she was ready. She stepped into the attack, dropping the branch and grabbing the thing by what passed for its neck. The fire burned her palms, but she didn't let go. She twisted, using its momentum, and drove it face-first into the nearest tree.\n\nThe metallic bark split. Something black oozed out.\n\nThe beast thrashed once, twice, then went still. The flames died, leaving only ash.\n\nWhitney collapsed against the tree, shaking. Her palms were red, blistered, but she'd live. She always lived.\n\nThat's when she saw it.\n\nPast the trees. Hidden in the pulsing shadows.\n\nA structure.\n\nShe pushed off the tree, legs threatening to give out, and stumbled toward it. The closer she got, the clearer it became. Stone. Dark stone, almost black, covered in carvings. Sigils. Markings that hurt to look at directly, like they were carved in dimensions her eyes couldn't fully process.\n\nThe entrance was open. No door. Just a threshold.\n\nShe hesitated for half a second, then stepped inside.\n\nThe air changed immediately. Cooler. Quieter. The pulsing of the trees faded to a distant throb. The walls were covered in more symbols, layered over each other like graffiti from civilizations that had never existed.\n\nIt was empty. Abandoned. Safe.\n\nWhitney moved to the far corner, sank down with her back against the wall. Her hands were still shaking. She forced them to stop.\n\nThis was what she'd been looking for. Not this specific place, but any place. Somewhere she could stop. Somewhere she could sleep.\n\nBecause sleep wasn't just rest here. It was transportation.\n\nShe closed her eyes, forcing her breathing to slow. Four counts in. Hold. Four counts out. The technique she'd learned in the void, that black empty nothing after the deletion failed and spat her out sideways into… wherever this was.\n\nThe dream walk wasn't a metaphor. It was a practiced skill. A learned thing. You found a safe space, you made your body sleep, and then you walked out of your flesh and into the spaces between realities. Once you crossed over, you’d pull your body through with you.\n\nMost people couldn't do it. Their consciousness was too anchored. But Whitney had been unmoored. Deleted. Ripped from the only reality she ever knew and left drifting. She'd been forced to learn, or would have face being dissolved into the static.\n\nHer breathing slowed.\n\nThe symbols on the walls began to glow. Faint. Purple-white.\n\nShe felt the pull downward. Not sleep. Deeper than sleep. The space where dreams touched other dreams, where consciousness could slip between the cracks.\n\nAnd then she felt something else.\n\nA vibration.\n\nNot in her body. Through it. Like someone had struck a tuning fork in another universe and the sound had traveled here, impossibly, finding her.\n\nShe knew that feeling. She'd felt it before, faint and distant. But now it was strong. Clear. A beacon.\n\nThe sigils on the walls pulsed in time with it.\n\nWhitney's eyes were closed, but she could see anyway. See the light building beneath her sleeping form. See the portal opening, drawn by the resonance between the vibration and the ancient markings carved into this forgotten place.\n\nSomeone's calling.\n\nHer last conscious thought before the dream walk took her was a name.\n\nNot a name she knew. But one she remembered anyway.\n\nStephen.	2	508	f	2026-02-27 12:28:34.462059-05	2026-02-28 22:55:00.715933-05	normal	\N
495	122	4	Whitney's Deletion and Rebirth	Through fragmented memories and visions, Whitney experiences the trauma of deletion and the void, then recounts her gradual reawakening in alternate realities and her development of dream-walking abilities to survive.	**********\n\nCrossing between waking and dreams is never easy. This time she’s holding a deck of cards. Black nail polish chipped. A faint bandage around her thumb. “Draw phase,” she says.\n\nThe light stutters.\n\nNot flickers—stutters.\n\nSound stretches thin.\n\nA voice somewhere: duuuude, yoooou caaaan’t—\n\nThen silence, sticky and wrong.\n\nHer hand locks above the deck. The air hums like a dying hard drive.\n\nConcrete splitting open, neat as paper. A courtyard turning inside out.\n\nUnderneath—something alive. Wet scaffolding, veins pulsing like cables.\n\nA sky made of mouths.\n\nCrucifixes of light hanging upside down, spinning like wind chimes.\n\nHer nose fills with sugar and rot.\n\nEveryone’s eyes are bleeding.\n\nThe cards in her hand crumble into ash and teeth.\n\nShe tries to drop them but they scream when they fall.\n\nChains.\n\nHuge. Red with rust and something darker.\n\nThey burst from the cracked ground and coil around her wrists. Around her neck.\n\nShe fights.\n\nShe screams until her throat breaks open, but no one moves. They’re all stuck in syrup air.\n\n“HELP ME!” she shouts. “I DON’T WANT TO GO!”\n\nSomeone yells a name—Benji?—but it doesn’t reach her.\n\nThen that voice. Not human. Not god.\n\n“YOU HAVE TO—NO, NOT YET—DON’T LET HER—”\n\nEverything stops.\n\nLike a heartbeat skipping.\n\nThen the world tears sideways.\n\nCold. Not the kind you feel on skin. The kind that happens when you don’t have skin.\n\nShe’s falling through zero.\n\nThe universe collapses to static, and she’s the only line left uncompiled.\n\nShe tries to scream, but she’s only data now, screaming in silence.\n\nTime folds into itself. Seconds. Centuries. Both meaningless.\n\nThe deletion didn't complete.\n\nAt the last possible second, something failed. A buffer overflow. A paradox in the system. The chains shattered, and instead of being erased, she was ejected.\n\nPushed out sideways.\n\nShe remembered the sensation of tumbling through the void. Not the warm darkness of sleep. The cold nothing of absolute zero. A place where even entropy had given up.\n\nShe'd woken there. In the dark. Empty. Alone.\n\nNo body at first. Just consciousness, floating. Screaming silently because she had no mouth.\n\nThere was no falling. No ground. No sky. Just black that went on forever and pressed in from every side.\n\nWhitney screamed until the sound broke apart. Nothing came back. Not even an echo.\n\nShe called out for anyone, but their names dissolved on her tongue, stripped of meaning. There was no air to carry them. No body to make them real.\n\nThat was the first time she understood what alone really meant.\n\nShe tried to count. Tried to breathe. Tried to move. But there was nothing to move with. Thought itself became her only proof of existing, and even that wavered like a dying light.\n\nHopelessness came next. Heavy and endless. The kind that sits behind your ribs and whispers stop trying.\n\nShe would’ve obeyed if she could.\n\nBut something—some stubborn, animal part of her—kept clawing.\n\nAt first, she thought it was a memory. The feel of fingers digging into the edge of a desk. The scrape of pencil on paper. The sound of her own heartbeat. She focused on that, built it piece by piece until it became something.\n\nA spark in the dark.\n\nThen another.\n\nAnd another.\n\nNot stars. Memories. Dreams.\n\nShe realized she could still dream. Even here.\n\nAt first the dreams came without order—shards of color and sound, impossible geometry, her mother’s voice calling from behind walls that didn’t exist. But she clung to them, tried to guide them.\n\nDreaming, then awakening back to this endless nightmare in the dark.\n\nBut slowly, the dreams began to listen.\n\nShe learned to push them outward. To stretch the edges of what was possible. To feel where the blackness gave way to something else.\n\nThat’s when she found the cracks.\n\nThey shimmered faintly, like frost on glass, humming with energy. She reached for them, though reaching wasn’t really what she did. It was more like wanting in a very specific direction until the dark started to bend.\n\nThe first time, it hurt. Like forcing herself through a hole too small for who she was. She thought it would tear her apart.\n\nThen suddenly—\n\nAir.\n\nOr something pretending to be it.\n\nShe hit the ground hard enough to feel it. Green spores puffed around her in slow-motion clouds. She gasped, choking on the taste of cheese and burnt roots.\n\nAbove her, the sky pulsed faintly red. The trees surrounding her were dancing—slow, rhythmic, their bark flexing like seaweed. Their leaves whispered as they gyrated, soft as prayers.\n\nWhitney pressed her hands into the moist green ground, staring at her fingers like they were strangers. The world was wrong—too wet, too alive—but it was real.\n\nAnd for the first time since the void, she could feel her heartbeat.\n\nDreams, she realized, weren’t just memories. They were doors.\n\nDoors that she’d finally remembered how to open them.\n\nThat had been... how long ago?\n\nShe'd lost count. Lost track. She'd been running ever since, jumping from reality to reality through sleep, always chased, always hungry, always searching for...\n\nWhat?\n\nA way home?\n\nThere was no home. She'd been deleted from it.\n\nBut somewhere in the back of her fragmented memory, she knew there were others. People who remembered. Stephen. Benji. Maybe Patrick, if Patrick had survived what had came after.\n\nAnd now, in this abandoned structure with symbols glowing on the walls, the vibration had found her.\n\nSomeone was calling.\n\nThe portal opened beneath her sleeping form, and Whitney—or what was left of Whitney, what had learned to survive in the spaces between deletion and existence—fell through.\n\nNot back home. She couldn't go back.\n\nBut toward something. Someone.\n\nA voice that still remembered her name.	3	126	t	2026-02-27 12:28:34.462059-05	2026-02-28 22:55:44.180429-05	normal	\N
496	123	1	The Compound	Stephen arrives at Patrick's remote compound in Christmas, Florida and discovers a temple-like interior filled with occult artifacts. Patrick reveals himself alive, and Stephen encounters a supernatural entity masquerading as Patrick's wife Whitney. The creature attacks, forcing Stephen to invoke an ancient Cthulhu sign, and Patrick destroys it with a ritual blade.	After leaving Gil at Best Buy, I'd spent the night on my phone hunting through digital rot. Military forums. Property records buried in county databases. Patrick had survived a plane crash in Panama—the only one—got an honorable discharge, then vanished. Thirty-seven acres outside Christmas, Florida. Purchased cash in two thousand three.\n\nNo footprint. No digital trail.\n\nLike someone had edited his life and forgot to clean up the margins.\n\nNow I was here.\n\nThe address Andrew gave me had led me to a dirt road that wasn't on any map. No GPS signal. No cell service. Just tire ruts carved through scrub palmetto and wire grass that hadn't seen maintenance since the Clinton administration.\n\nChristmas, Florida. Population: maybe two thousand people and whatever the fuck crawls out of the swamp at night.\n\nPatrick's place wasn't a house. It really was a compound, just as Morgan had described. Chain-link fence topped with razor wire. Handmade signs warning about trespassers, dogs, and "electromagnetic disturbances." The kind of property that made you wonder if the owner was brilliantly insane or just preparing for the end times.\n\nProbably both.\n\nI parked by the gate and walked in. The air smelled weird. Not rotten, just... layered. Incense and wet earth and something sharp underneath, like a male urinal mixed with burnt popcorn.\n\nThe front door was open.\n\nNot inviting-open. Forgotten-open. Like someone had walked through it mid-thought and never bothered to close it behind them.\n\n"Patrick?" I called out.\n\nNo answer. Just the hum of cicadas and the distant croaking of something amphibian.\n\nI stepped inside.\n\nThe interior hit me like a punch. Every surface was covered. Sigils carved into the walls. Candles in patterns I recognized from the Secret Book of John. Jars of herbs and powders and things I didn't want to identify. Books stacked in precarious towers, their spines cracked and faded. Sanskrit. Hebrew. Languages I couldn't even begin to parse.\n\nThis wasn't a home. This was a temple. Or a bunker. Or both.\n\n"You're early."\n\nI spun.\n\nPatrick stood in the doorway to the kitchen. Older. Grayer. His face was lined in ways that had nothing to do with age and everything to do with seeing too much. He wore cargo pants and a faded black t-shirt with a symbol I didn't recognize. His eyes were calm. Too calm.\n\n"Patrick," I breathed. "You're... you're alive."\n\n"Debatable." He tilted his head. "You look like shit, Stephen."\n\n"Two years unemployed will do that."\n\n"Not what I meant." He studied me with the intensity of someone reading a text in a foreign language. "You've been waking up. Haven't you?"\n\nBefore I could answer, footsteps echoed from the hallway.\n\nA woman emerged. Late twenties, maybe early thirties. Dark hair pulled back in a loose bun. She wore a simple sundress, barefoot, smiling.\n\nSomething about her triggered a memory. Not from high school. From two days ago.\n\nThe Magic card Gil pulled from the back. Veilwalker. I could see it clearly now—the artwork showing a figure standing at a threshold, half-shrouded in mist, caught between worlds. The way the light played across the character's face, revealing just enough to be recognizable but keeping the details obscured. Neither here nor there. Neither one thing nor another.\n\nThat's what this woman looked like. Like someone had tried to render a real person using the Veilwalker card as a reference. All the right elements were there—human shape, features, movement—but something about the composition was off. Like the artist had never actually seen a person before, only descriptions of one.\n\nThe uncanny valley made flesh.\n\n"Stephen, this is my wife," Patrick said. "Whitney."\n\nThe name hit me like ice water.\n\nWhitney.\n\nBut the smile was wrong. Too practiced. Like she'd learned it from a manual.\n\n"It's so nice to finally meet you," she said. Her voice was soft, careful. Each word placed with precision. "Patrick's told me all about you."\n\nI tried to respond, but my throat had closed. Something about her presence made the air thick, resistant.\n\n"Whitney," I repeated, forcing the word out. "That's... that's an interesting name."\n\nPatrick stepped closer to her, his hand resting lightly on her back. "She was actually in our class. Eau Gallie. Can you believe it? Small world."\n\nMy brain stuttered. "She was? She seems way too… young to have been in high school with us."\n\n"Yeah, man. Senior year." Patrick's voice was easy, conversational. But his eyes were sharp, watching me. "You don't remember?"\n\nI searched my memory. Tried to pull up anything. Yearbook pages. Hallways. AP classes. There was... nothing. Just a blank space where something should have been.\n\nBut worse than the nothing was the wrongness.\n\nIf I had known a Whitney in high school—and the name felt like it should mean something, like a word on the tip of my tongue—this wasn't her. This was the photographic negative. The inverse. Everything about this woman felt like someone had described a person to a drunken police sketch artist and had decided to draw the opposite.\n\n"Where..." I swallowed. "Where did you two meet? I mean, reconnect?"\n\nWhitney's smile didn't waver. "Oh, it was at a little coffee shop in Winter Park. Patrick was doing some research, and I was—"\n\n"Actually, honey," Patrick interrupted smoothly, "I think it was at that bookstore. Remember? The one on Orange Avenue."\n\nShe blinked. A fraction too long. "Right. Yes. The bookstore."\n\n"No, wait." Patrick tilted his head. "Maybe it was the coffee shop. Or..." He trailed off, his brow furrowing.\n\nThe silence stretched.\n\n"It's funny," I said quietly, my pulse hammering. "I can't remember you at all. Not from high school. Not from anywhere."\n\n"Well, I was pretty quiet," Whitney said. Her smile finally flickered. "Easy to miss."\n\n"But Patrick said you were in our senior class."\n\n"I was."\n\n"What classes?"\n\nAnother long blink. "Oh, you know. The usual. English. Math."\n\n"AP Bio?"\n\n"No, I—"\n\n"I took AP Bio," I said. The memories were there now, surfacing. "With Becca. And... and someone else. Someone who sat behind her." The image was hazy, but real. Peaches and vanilla. Hair catching fluorescent light. "I can't remember her name, but I remember her."\n\nWhitney's expression didn't change, but something behind her eyes shifted.\n\n"I wasn't in AP Bio," she said.\n\n"Right." I turned to Patrick. "Do you remember who I'm talking about? I remember her sitting behind Becca in AP Bio, but I don’t know. You never did AP Bio did you?"\n\nPatrick's face went blank. Not confused. Empty. Like the question had triggered a loading screen that never finished buffering.\n\n"Can't say I do," he said finally.\n\nMy chest tightened. The air felt thicker now. Oppressive.\n\n"There was someone else," I pressed. "I know there was. I keep hearing the name everywhere now.” The memory was right there, but every time I reached for it, it slipped away like water through fingers.\n\n"Jamie," I finally said.\n\nThe word fell into the room like a stone into still water.\n\nWhitney's smile fractured.\n\nHer pupils dilated, swallowing the color in her eyes. Her head tilted at an angle that was just... wrong. Too far. Too smooth.\n\n"Jamie?" she repeated.\n\nThe word came out in layers. Multiple voices speaking at once, stacked so precisely they almost sounded singular.\n\nPatrick's expression didn't change. He just sighed, low and tired, and took a step back.\n\n"Here we go," he sighed.\n\nThe change was instant.\n\nWhitney's body convulsed. She