517	128	1	Building the Gateway	Stephen and Xavier accompany Maeve and Elara to an abandoned school gymnasium to construct a magical gateway to the Akashic Realm. They gather materials and prepare for the ritual while Maeve warns of the pain required to fuel the gate with intellectual knowledge.	The abandoned school smelled like mildew and forgotten dreams.\n\nI stood in what used to be the gymnasium, my arm and side still throbbing beneath the bandages Maeve had changed this morning. The stitches pulled when I moved, a constant reminder that I was barely held together. Xavier limped beside me, his ankle wrapped tight, testing his weight on it with each step.\n\n"This is the place," Elara said. She moved through the space like she belonged here, her wild white hair catching the dusty light filtering through broken windows. "Knowledge was sought here. Children learned. Teachers taught. The residue remains."\n\n"Residue?" Xavier asked.\n\n"Intent," Maeve said, hefting a canvas bag full of things I couldn't name. Stones. Herbs. Lengths of rope woven from something that wasn't quite rope. "Every building holds the echo of what it was. A hospital remembers healing. A prison remembers suffering. This place remembers learning. That's what we need."\n\nI looked at the floor. Someone had spray-painted crude tags across the basketball court. The bleachers were collapsed. But underneath the decay, I could almost feel it... the weight of a thousand small moments. Kids struggling with algebra. Teachers explaining photosynthesis. The quiet hum of curiosity.\n\n"The Akashic Realm," Elara said, kneeling in the center of the court, "is a library. A record of all things. To open a door to it, we need a threshold that remembers the pursuit of knowledge."\n\nShe pulled a piece of chalk from her bag. Just normal chalk from a big box of assorted colors.\n\n"You'll need to help," she said, looking at me. "Both of you. This isn't something we can do alone."\n\n"What do we do?" I asked.\n\n"You build the frame," Maeve said, unpacking stones from her bag. They were smooth, river-worn, but covered in symbols that seemed to shift when I wasn't looking directly at them. "We provide the foundation. But the gate needs intellect. It needs you to understand it. To structure it. To pour everything you know into making it real."\n\nShe looked at me with those ancient, knowing eyes.\n\n"This will hurt," she said. "Not your body. Deeper than that. You're going to reach into yourself and pull out every scrap of data you've ever hoarded. Every book you've read. Every manual you've skimmed. Every YouTube tutorial you've watched at 3 AM when you couldn't sleep. You're going to take all that raw information and you're going to build with it."\n\nXavier looked down at the small, unassuming pile of rocks. "That's it? A few rocks on a gym floor? It doesn't exactly look like a gateway."\n\n"Not every threshold has to be a grand arch or a golden door," Maeve said, a small, dangerous smile playing on her lips. "Think of it like a fairy ring. Just a simple circle of mushrooms in the grass. You don't even remember entering it, but you can never forget leaving it."\n\nXavier shifted beside me. "That sounds terrible."\n\n"It is," Elara said, but there was kindness in her voice. "But it's the only way."\n\nWe worked for hours.\n\nFirst, the metal had to go. All of it. "It grounds the energy," Maeve had warned. "Interferes with the weave." So we moved out the bleachers. We stripped our belts, emptied our pockets of change and keys. We took off our jeans and put on sweatpants instead. I even had to pry my wedding ring off my finger, leaving a pale band of skin that felt suddenly naked in the cold air.\n\nMaeve and Elara placed the stones and bundles of dried herbs in a circle, each one positioned with mathematical precision. They chanted as they worked, their voices weaving together in harmonies that made the air itself vibrate.\n\nWe couldn't draw the symbols—our hands were too clumsy, our minds too linear for that kind of geometry. Instead, we provided the fuel.\n\n"Find words," Elara had commanded. "Find things that were read. Things that were learned."\n\nXavier and I raided the abandoned classrooms and hallways. We hauled in armfuls of damp textbooks heavy with mildew, snatched crumbling permission slips from rusted lockers, even tore down a faded poster of the solar system. We piled them between the stones, a physical library of rot and memory to feed the gate.\n\n"Think about what you're building," Maeve said, adjusting a stack of waterlogged history textbooks. "A door. A threshold. A way through. You aren't just piling up garbage. You're giving it structure."\n\nI looked at the result. Honestly? It looked like a recycling bin had exploded in the middle of a gym. A ring of river stones surrounding a chaotic mound of mildewed paper, dead flowers, and a faded poster of the solar system.\n\n"This is..." Xavier hesitated, nudging a crumbling hall pass with his sneaker. "Dad, are we sure about this? It looks like a pile of random trash. Is this really going to work?"\n\n"It works because it's unassuming," Elara murmured, sprinkling ash over the books. "Grand arches attract attention. Refuse attracts only flies... and those who know how to look."\n\n"Focus," Maeve commanded. "Don't look at the paper. Look at the words. Look at the intent."\n\nI focused. I stared at the mound of refuse and tried to push everything I had into it. The fear. The exhaustion. The desperate, clawing need to get Xavier out of this nightmare. To find Jamie. To end this.\n\nI opened my eyes, waiting for the magic. For a hum, a spark, a shiver in the air.\n\nNothing.\n\nThe pile sat there, damp and pathetic. Just a mound of rotting paper and rocks in the middle of a derelict gym. I looked at Maeve and Elara, chanting at dust motes, and the cold reality washed over me. We weren't building a gateway. We were fugitives hiding with a pair of crazy hoarders building a trash castle.\n\nI opened my mouth to say something—to ask what the hell we were actually doing here—when Elara stepped back, wiping sweat from her brow.	0	531	f	2026-02-27 12:30:46.897308-05	2026-02-28 23:17:39.810218-05	normal	\N
518	128	2	The Police Ambush	Sirens approach the gymnasium, revealing that the location has been discovered. Maeve and Elara reveal they are fae and engage the tactical team in combat to buy time for Stephen and Xavier to escape through the incomplete gateway.	"It's a pile of wet books," I snapped, the frustration finally boiling over. "Nothing is happening. We're just standing here staring at—"\n\nAnd then we heard it.\n\nSirens.\n\n"Shit," I hissed, spinning toward the frosted windows. Red and blue lights washed over the decay, painting the peeling walls in panic.\n\n"Local police," Maeve said, her rhythm hitching but not stopping. "They must have seen the car."\n\n"That's impossible," I snapped. "We hid the car behind the cafeteria. We are miles from anything, in a dead school in a ghost town in the middle of winter. No one saw us. There was no one to see us."\n\nThe wail grew louder, cutting through the silence of the woods. It didn't make sense. Unless they weren't just patrolling. Unless they were hunting.\n\n"We need to go," I said. "Now."\n\n"We can't," Elara said. Her voice was strained, sweat beading on her forehead despite the December chill. "The circle isn't complete. If we leave now, the anchor breaks. We'll have to start over from scratch."\n\n"How long?"\n\n"Twenty minutes. Maybe thirty."\n\nThe sirens got louder. Car doors slammed. Voices shouted outside.\n\n"This is the police! Come out with your hands up!"\n\nXavier looked at me, his face pale. "Dad..."\n\n"Finish it," I said to Elara. "We'll handle this."\n\nMaeve stood, pulling something from beneath her shawl. A knife. Old. Obsidian blade with a bone handle wrapped in leather.\n\n"Shadow glass," she said. "It'll hurt them more than it hurts us."\n\n"Them?"\n\nShe smiled. Sad. Knowing. "We're fae, Stephen. We can't lie. And we don't bleed the way you do."\n\nBefore I could process that, the door exploded inward.\n\nThree cops. Small-town uniforms. But their movements were too precise. Too coordinated. They spread out instantly, weapons raised.\n\n"On the ground! Now!"\n\nMaeve moved first.\n\nShe was fast. Impossibly fast. The knife flashed, and the first cop went down, clutching his arm where she'd opened it to the bone. He screamed, a sound that was more surprise than pain.\n\nThe second cop fired.\n\nThe bullet hit Maeve in the shoulder, spinning her around. She stumbled but didn't fall. Where the bullet entered, there was no blood. Just... emptiness. A soft, reddish dew that leaked from the wound.\n\n"Iron," she gasped. "They're using iron rounds."\n\nThe third cop aimed at me.\n\nXavier grabbed a piece of a broken lacrosse stick and swung it like a bat, connecting with the cop's knee. The man went down hard.\n\n"Go!" Maeve shouted. "Get out! We'll hold them!"\n\n"We're not leaving you!"\n\nElara's voice cut through the chaos. "The circle is done! Stephen, get Xavier to the center! Now!"\n\nI grabbed my son's arm and ran. Behind us, more cops were pouring through the door. Six. Ten. Too many.\n\nMaeve and Elara stood between us and them.\n\n"For the old ways," Elara said softly.\n\n"For the lost roads," Maeve replied.\n\nThey moved as one.\n\nI looked back, and I saw it all.\n\nIt wasn't a fight; it was a slaughter. Maeve was a blur of gray wool and violence. She didn't fight like an old woman; she fought like a thresher. She ducked under a spray of bullets that chewed up the gym floor, closed the distance, and took the first cop apart. Not stabbed—disassembled. Her obsidian knife took his arm at the shoulder; her free hand ripped his throat out in a wet spray of bright arterial red.\n\nElara wasn't chanting anymore. She was screaming, a sound that shattered the tactical lights on the officers' rifles. She moved with the jerkiness of a spider, slamming a man’s head into the wall with enough force to cave in his skull.\n\n"Keep coming!" Maeve roared, laughing through a mask of other people's blood. "I have lifetimes of rage for you!"\n\nThen the high windows shattered inward. The thwap-thwap-thwap of a helicopter rotor drowned out the sirens, whipping up a tornado of dust and old homework. Rappelling lines dropped. Black-clad figures swung in, boots crunching on the glass-strewn floor.\n\n"More meat for the grinder!" Maeve shouted. She leaped, catching a Repeller in mid-air, driving him into the floorboards with a crunch that vibrated through my soles.\n\nIt looked like they were going to win. It looked like the old magic was stronger than modern lead. Maeve stood over the broken body, chest heaving, triumphant. She turned to us, a fierce grin splitting her blood-soaked face.\n\n"Go! We've got—"\n\nCRACK.\n\nThere was no warning. No red dot. Just the deafening thunderclap of a high-caliber sniper round from the roof.\n\nMaeve’s head didn't just break; it vaporized. One second she was smiling, and the next, her shoulders were coated in a wet, pink mist of brain matter and bone shards. Her lower jaw, still attached to a neck stump, hung slack for a split second before her body realized it was dead and collapsed like a marionette with cut strings.\n\n"MAEVE!" Elara’s scream was a physical blow, tearing at her own throat.\n\n"Dad, we have to help them!" Xavier screamed, pulling against my grip.\n\n"She's gone! We can't!" I shoved Xavier toward the glowing circle, slipping on the slick floor. "Get in! Get in now!"\n\nI looked at the circle. To my eyes, it hadn't changed at all. It was still just a pathetic ring of river stones surrounding a mound of waterlogged textbooks and rotting paper. Just a pile of trash on a dirty gym floor.\n\nBut the air around it... it wasn't right.\n\nIt felt heavy. Charged. Every hair on my arms stood straight up, prickling against my sleeves. The atmosphere tasted of lost underwear and missing beef jerky. It was the physical sensation of standing in an open field the split-second before lightning strikes—invisible, silent, but undeniably, terrifyingly there.\n\nBehind us, the gunfire stopped.\n\nI looked back.\n\nMaeve’s body lay there, a heap of gray wool and blood, the stump of her neck steaming in the cold air. Elara was kneeling in the gore, her hands hovering over the empty space where Maeve's face used to be. She wasn't chanting anymore. She was wailing—a raw, broken sound that had no magic in it, only grief.\n\n"Elara!" I screamed.\n\nShe didn't look up. She didn't seem to care that the tactical team was advancing.\n\nThen the shot rang out.\n\nIt hit Elara in the center of her back. She arched, her wail cut short. Red liquid exploded from her chest where the bullet exited, splashing across the floor like spilled mercury. She slumped forward, draping herself over Maeve's remains.\n\nAs she fell, the space behind her cleared.\n\nStanding in the doorway, silhouetted against the setting sun, stepping over the bodies like they were nothing but trash...\n\nBut... no. It couldn't be.\n\nThe woman I knew—the woman I’d lived with for years—was demure. Quiet. Her anger was a cold, silent thing, a vengeful stillness that filled a room without a word. She wasn't a killer. She certainly wasn't some tactical bounty hunter dressed in a dark suit with an FBI badge hanging from her neck.\n\nThis... this was bad casting. A glitch. A character model dropped into the wrong role. It just reinforced everything I’d suspected—she wasn't real. She was just a program that had finally broken its own logic.\n\n"Stephen!" she called out, her voice carrying across the gymnasium. "Stop running! Bring me my son!"\n\n"Dad," Xavier whispered, terrified. "Dad, please..."\n\nI didn't stop. I didn't even slow down. I grabbed Xavier's hand and yanked him forward, sprinting toward the mound of rotting books and river stones.\n\n"Shoot him!" she screamed, the mask of the loving wife slipping completely. "He's got to be stopped! Take the shot!"\n\nThe air around us snapped. Crack-thwip.\n\nA bullet tore through the sleeve of my jacket, burning a line across my triceps. Another chipped the floor inches from my heel, sending up a spray of varnish and wood. They were close—too close—but nothing hit anything vital.\n\n"Jump!" I yelled.\n\nElara's voice rose one final time from the floor behind us. A word I didn't know. A sound that wasn't meant for human throats.\n\nWe stepped onto the trash pile, but our feet didn't find purchase on the books.\n\nThe floor was gone. The pile wasn't a mound anymore; it was a mouth. A jagged hole in the fabric of the world that opened up directly beneath us.\n\nWe didn't step through. We fell.	1	531	f	2026-02-27 12:30:46.897308-05	2026-02-28 23:18:33.847596-05	normal	\N
519	128	3	Arrival in the Akashic Realm	Stephen and Xavier crash-land in a grey, desolate realm and encounter Wisdom Devourers that steal their memories. Benji rescues them and escorts them to the Tower of Secrets, where they meet Lady Illkeserod and learn the truth about Jamie and the prison.	Gravity vanished, then slammed back into us from the wrong direction. The world literally turned upside down. The gymnasium ceiling was suddenly below my feet, the floor a distant ceiling above me. We tumbled through the dark, spinning, flailing, leaving the gunfire and the screaming fae behind as we plummeted into the cold grey sky of another world.\n\nWe hit stone. Cold. Hard. Real.\n\nI gasped, my lungs burning, my vision swimming. Xavier was beside me, curled into a ball, his hands pressed to his head.\n\n"Xavi," I choked out. "Xavi, are you okay?"\n\n"I think so," he managed. "I think... Dad, where are we?"\n\nI looked up.\n\nThe sky was grey. The buildings around us were ancient, crumbling stone towers. Leafless trees grew from cracks in the ground, their branches reaching toward clouds that moved too fast.\n\nAnd it was cold. Winter-morning cold. The kind that bit through your clothes and settled in your bones. Even colder than the winter we’d just left in Tennessee.\n\n"The Akashic Realm," I said.\n\nAbove us, the portal we'd come through was already closing. Sealing. The barrier dissolving into nothing.\n\nAnd on the other side, just before it winked out entirely, I saw her. My wife. Her face pressed against the barrier. Her eyes wide. Her mouth open in a scream I couldn't hear.\n\nThen she was gone.\n\nThe gateway collapsed.\n\nAnd we were alone.\n\nXavier was shaking. From cold or fear or both, I couldn't tell.\n\n"They're dead," he whispered. "Maeve and Elara. They died so we could get through."\n\n"I know."\n\n"Why?" His voice cracked. "Why would they do that?"\n\nI pulled him close, wrapping my arms around him even though my own body was trembling.\n\n"Because that's what people do, what real people do" I said quietly. "When they care about something more than themselves."\n\nWe sat there for a long time. Two broken figures in a strange, cold realm. Refugees from a prison we'd barely escaped.\n\nAnd somewhere in the distance, something moved in the shadows between the floating towers.\n\nSomething that had noticed we'd arrived.\n\n**********\n\nWe stayed huddled on the cold stone for maybe ten minutes. Maybe an hour. Time moved differently here, like running sideways when you’re really just trying to be green.\n\n"Let me see that foot," I said, nodding toward his wrapped ankle.\n\nXavier extended his leg, rotating the joint carefully. He frowned. "It's... okay. Actually, it feels a little weird. Not better, not painful, just... lighter? Like the gravity here isn't pulling on the bone the same way."\n\nHe looked up, his eyes catching the tear in my jacket sleeve where the bullet had burned past. Dark blood was soaking into the fabric.\n\n"You got hit," he said.\n\n"Just a graze."\n\n"It's bleeding, Dad." He shifted closer, using the clean hem of his shirt to carefully dab at the wound on my tricep. The cloth stuck slightly, and the air stung the raw skin, but his hands were steady. Gentle. "It’s shallow. Just took the skin."\n\nHe finished cleaning it as best he could, then wiped his face with the back of his hand.\n\n"We should move," he said. His voice was raw. "Sitting here won't... it won't help them."\n\nHe was right. But standing up felt like an impossible task.\n\nThe gravity was wrong. Not drastically, but enough to notice. Each movement required adjustment, like my body was trying to remember how to exist in a space that didn't quite follow the rules I'd grown up with. It felt like swimming through air that was just a little too thick.\n\n"Okay," I said. "Okay. Let's find... someone. Anyone."\n\nThe landscape around us was desolate in a way that felt intentional. Grey stone buildings stretched in every direction, some floating impossibly in the air, connected by bridges that looked too thin to support weight. The trees were skeletal, their branches bare and twisted. Everything was rendered in shades of grey and black and white, punctuated occasionally by deep, arterial reds where winter flowers pushed through the cracks.\n\nNo birds. No insects. Just the whisper of wind through empty streets.\n\n"This place feels dead," Xavier said.\n\n"Not dead," I replied. "Waiting."\n\nWe started walking. Xavier's limp was worse here, the changed gravity making it harder for him to compensate initially. I put his arm over my shoulder, taking some of his weight.\n\nThe streets were empty. Doors hung open on rusted hinges. Windows stared at us like hollow eyes. We passed what might have been a marketplace once—stone stalls covered in dust, goods long since rotted or stolen.\n\n"Where is everyone?" Xavier asked.\n\n"I don't know."\n\nA sound behind us made me turn.\n\nNothing. Just empty street. But the feeling of being watched settled over my skin like oil.\n\n"Dad..."\n\n"I know. Keep moving."\n\nThe feeling grew stronger. Not quite threatening, but... hungry. Like standing near something that wanted to consume but hadn't decided if you were food yet.\n\nThen I saw them.\n\nAt first, I thought they were shadows. Patches of darkness moving independently of any light source. But as they got closer, I realized they had substance. Or the absence of it.\n\nThey were voids. Human-shaped holes in reality. Where their mouths should have been, there were only gaping, black openings that seemed to pull at the air itself.\n\n"What are those?" Xavier whispered.\n\nOne of them drifted closer. I felt something tug at my mind. Not painful, just... insistent. Like fingers prying at the edges of my thoughts. I was trying to think about the gateway, about Elara's symbols, about the way the light had... had... gotten bigger. But the thought slipped.\n\n"Dad," Xavier whispered, "they're getting closer."\n\n"We have to... to... walk faster!" I said, grabbing his arm. "No, go faster!"\n\nWe started to... go faster... down the grey street.\n\nThe things followed. Silent. Implacable. More of them emerging from the... the holes in the walls where people walk through... the... empty wooden frames that are missing something in the middle. Their void-mouths opened wider.\n\nAnother tug at my mind. Stronger. The word for... the thing on your face you use to breathe.. Gone. The word for the path between buildings... Blank.\n\n"Dad, I can't... I can't... recall..." Xavier stumbled, clutching his head. "There was a word... a word for... for when you know something from before. When you don't let it go..."\n\n"Hold onto it?" I offered, my own mind feeling foggy.\n\n"No, that's not it. It's... it's the thing you do with your brain... to keep a thought. I... I can't... know it anymore."\n\nThe bad things were all around now. Many of them. Many more. They got close around us, their holes taking, taking.\n\nI tried to hit them. I hit at no-thing. But you can't hit a hole. Can't hit no-thing. My hands went through them. No hurt. And with each touch, more was gone.\n\nThe song my mother liked. The color of my first car. The name of the man who showed me the... things. Gone. Gone. Gone.\n\n"Guy!" the boy’s sound was scared. "I can't... I do not know how to... the thing with your legs, where you go, what is it?"\n\nWe were both on our knees now. The bad things coming close. Their big holes very close to our faces. I could feel them taking all of me. Every thought from before. Every thought. All of me... being taken, bit by bit.\n\nThis is how it stops, I thought. Not with hurt. Not with hurt. Just... gone.\n\nThen a sound came in the…\n\n"What the fuck are you doing way the hell out here?"\n\nWisdom and memories flooded back as a figure burst through the circle of spectres. Small. Familiar. Wearing a cardigan that looked more worn and tattered than I remembered.\n\nBenji.\n\nHe moved between us and the void-creatures, his form shifting, expanding. For a moment, he wasn't a small figure in a cardigan. He was something vast and winged.\n\nThe spectres recoiled.\n\n"Back!" Benji's voice resonated with authority that made the air vibrate. "These souls are spoken for! You want to feed, go hunt somewhere else in the outer wastes where nobody cares!"\n\nThe spectres hesitated. Their void-mouths working silently, hungry but uncertain.\n\nBenji's form solidified back into something more human-shaped. He made a gesture with one hand, and symbols appeared in the air—complex geometries that hurt to look at. The spectres scattered, dissolving into the shadows between buildings.\n\nSilence settled over the empty street.\n\nBenji turned to face us, and I saw how much he'd changed. The cardigan was the same, but everything else... he looked tired. Worn down. Like he'd been fighting for a very long time.\n\n"Stephen," he said. "Xavier. Welcome to the Akashic Realm. You picked a terrible entry point."\n\nI tried to speak, but the words came out jumbled. Too many memories now in the wrong places.\n\n"Don't worry," Benji said. "The memories will come back. Mostly. Those things don't actually eat your thoughts—they just... borrow them. Temporarily. You'll remember everything in a few minutes…"\n\nHe started walking, and we followed. Xavier was still shaking, occasionally reaching for words that weren't quite there yet.\n\n"What were those things?" I managed.\n\n"Wisdom Devourers. They're supposed to be confined to the outer wastes, but things have been... unstable lately." He waved a hand at the grey landscape. "This whole realm, the Akashic, it's supposed to be a cosmic information superhighway, flowing underneath realities. Not everywhere, mind you—there are places even we're banned from—but it's usually reliable."\n\nHe sighed, "But Etheris... after the dreamscape collapsed in the last reset, it's been dammed up. Blocked. Nothing has been flowing in or out of there. Normally you’d never be able to physically cross into the Akashic Etheris, it’s hard enough as it is for dreamers and other spirits to enter on a good day. All this means everything here in this area is destabilizing everything. That's why you've got lots of entities wandering into places they shouldn't be." He glanced back at us. "Speaking of which, that instability is probably how you ended up in the industrial district. The normal entry points are all miles from here."\n\n"We built a gateway," I said. "From Etheris. We didn't really have a choice of where we landed."\n\nBenji stopped walking. Turned to face me fully.\n\n"You built a gateway," he repeated. "From inside Etheris. A prison specifically designed to prevent exactly that. You just... built one."\n\n"We had help," I said, the memory of the gymnasium, the gunfire, and the leaking fluids still stinging my eyes. "From... from two women. Maeve and Elara. I thought they might have been Fae. They knew about the old ways... the old pathways that existed long before Etheris was a prison. They gambled that one of those old roads might still be open with all the instabilities."\n\nHis expression softened. "They didn't make it through, did they?"\n\n"No."\n\n"I'm sorry." He resumed walking. "The ones who help usually don't."\n\nWe moved through the grey streets in silence. Gradually, the buildings changed. The ruins gave way to structures that were intact, maintained. The floating towers grew more numerous, connected by elegant bridges of stone and light. And there were people here. Figures moving between buildings, carrying books and scrolls, their forms varying wildly—some humanoid, others decidedly not.\n\n"This is the outer ring of the Archives," Benji explained. "The Tower of Secrets is at the center. Lady Illkeserod's domain."\n\nAt the mention of her name, my chest tightened. Memories were flooding back now. The battlefield. Her rage. The way she'd blamed me for Benji's capture. The lies she'd told in the previous reset about Jamie not existing.\n\n"She knows we're here?" I asked.\n\n"She knows everything that happens in the Archives," Benji said. "She's been tracking you since the moment you entered. Probably wondering what took me so long." He smiled slightly. "You think you just stepped over, don't you? Let me tell you, tracking and time here are a bitch. Everything is going sideways. The veil between the Akashic Realm and Etheris is royally fucking things up, especially since the dreamscape collapsed. It's a miracle I found you in 2 weeks, but I'm still going to hear about it later."\n\nThe Tower of Secrets rose before us, a massive structure of black stone and silver filigree that stretched impossibly high. It didn't just go up—it went sideways, folding through dimensions my eyes couldn't quite process. Staircases wrapped around its exterior, some leading up, others down, a few seeming to go nowhere at all.\n\nWe climbed.\n\nThe entrance hall was vast, circular, with bookshelves stretching in every direction. But these weren't normal shelves. They curved and spiraled, defying gravity, creating a three-dimensional maze of knowledge that hurt to look at for too long.\n\nTwo figures waited at the center.\n\nLady Illkeserod stood with her back to us, studying something on a floating projection of symbols and light. She wore robes of deep purple, her hair pulled back in an intricate braid. Even from behind, she radiated authority.\n\nBeside her stood another woman. Shorter, rounder, with dark skin and eyes that seemed to contain entire universes. She wore simpler robes, brown and practical, covered in embroidered symbols that shifted and changed as I watched.\n\nThe moment I saw her, a memory hit me—faded, like a dream I'd had years ago. A feeling of... training. Of her teaching me, showing me how to see the patterns beneath things. It felt like a direct callback to a past I’m just beginning to remember, to a time before I knew how trapped I really was.\n\n"Lady Osilari," Benji said quietly. "Keeper of the Seals. Master of Symbols. She's... well, you'll see."\n\nLady Illkeserod turned.\n\nHer face was exactly as I remembered—beautiful, ancient, sad. She looked at me, and I saw recognition flash in her eyes. Not just of who I was now, but of who I'd been.\n\n"Stephen," she said, her voice warm but cautious. "You made it. And you brought..."\n\nI didn't let her finish. I crossed the distance in three fast, angry strides. As she fully faced me, I slapped her.\n\nThe sound echoed in the vast chamber, sharp and final.\n\nLady Illkeserod didn't flinch. Didn't move. She just stood there, her head still turned slightly from the impact, accepting it. Xavier hung back with Benji, frozen.\n\n"You lied to me," I snarled, my voice shaking with rage.\n\n"I did."\n\n"You told me Jamie didn't exist. That she was just a dream. A fantasy."\n\n"I did," she repeated, still not moving.\n\n"She exists," I said. My voice was shaking. "She's real. She's trapped inside that prison, and you told me she wasn't real."\n\n"I did," Lady Illkeserod said for the third time. "Because telling you the truth then would have broken everything."\n\nShe gestured to a nearby table. We sat. Xavier and Benji joined us. Lady Osilari remained standing, watching with those universe-filled eyes.\n\n"The prison is powered by love," Lady Illkeserod began. "Your love. Jamie's love. You are the cathode. She is the anode. Your connection generates the energy that keeps the entire structure stable."\n\n"I know that," I said. "I remember. But why lie? Why tell me she didn't exist, was just a construct of my memory during the last reset?"\n\n"Because if you'd known," she said softly, "if you'd remembered who she really was and that she really existed while you were still inside... the pain of separation would have destabilized the power flow. The prison was already weakening. Adding that variable could have caused a catastrophic cascade."\n\n"So you let me suffer in ignorance."\n\n"Yes." No hesitation. No justification. Just the cold truth. "It was a risk you both agreed to take when you first entered Etheris. You knew you'd be separated. Knew your memories would be suppressed. Knew you'd live infinite lifetimes apart, loving and losing other people, never quite remembering what you'd lost." She paused. "It was the cruelest part of the plan. And you chose it anyway."\n\nI sat back, the anger draining out of me. "Why?"\n\n"Because the alternative was letting Yaldabaoth stay free and continue consuming everything." Lady Illkeserod's eyes were wet. "Because you love her enough to sacrifice eternity. Because she loves you the same way. Because some prisons can only be powered by the strongest bonds... and the greatest pain."\n\nXavier spoke up, his voice small. "So my dad and Jamie... they've been doing this for how long?"\n\n"Infinite cycles beyond counting," Lady Osilari said. Her voice was precise, motherly. "Billions of reincarnations each cycle. Then, each time the prison collapses and resets, Jamie in the middle of it all. Stephen alone in the dark watching it all. Each time, memories are suppressed. Each time, they never find each other, always yearning for something that never will come. An eternal engine of love and loss."\n\n"That's..." Xavier's face was pale. "That's horrible."\n\n"It’s hell," Lady Illkeserod agreed.\n\nSilence settled over the table.\n\nI wanted to be angry. Wanted to hold onto the rage. But I couldn't. Because somewhere deep down, beneath all the missing memories and suppressed lives, I understood.\n\nSometimes love means suffering.\n\nSometimes the greatest sacrifice is choosing pain over watching the universe burn.\n\n"I am not sorry for the lie," Lady Illkeserod said quietly, her voice firm. "I am sorry for the pain it caused you. For the confusion. But I am not sorry for the choice."\n\nShe met my gaze, her own unwavering. "If you had your full memory, you would have demanded it. You'd do it to yourself. It's why you volunteered for this."\n\nShe paused, letting the words sink in. "When all your memories come back, think hard. You will know it to be true. I'd do it again, because the alternative is worse."\n\nI looked at her. Really looked. Saw the weight she carried. The guilt. The impossible choices she'd made and would make again.\n\n"I know," I said.\n\nShe smiled. Sad. Grateful.	2	533	f	2026-02-27 12:30:46.897308-05	2026-02-28 23:19:38.316489-05	normal	\N
