im square across the chest and throat.\n\nWe crashed into the bar, the impact sending abandoned glasses scattering. He went down, hands scrambling for his shotgun clattering between us. I was faster. I ripped the weapon from his grasp, clicked off the safety, and shoved the muzzle in his face just as he tried to get up.\n\nI pulled the trigger.\n\nHis head didn't just break; it exploded. A wet spray of red mist, bone fragments, and gray matter painted the floor. But the gore sizzled in the air and dissolved before it could even drip, evaporating into oily black smoke.\n\nThe second agent—a glitching, static-filled thing with a face that wouldn't hold still—rounded the bar.\n\nI fired from the hip. The blast tore a smoking, basketball-sized hole straight through its chest, revealing the wall behind it. It looked down at the void where its torso should have been, then dissolved into a shower of static.\n\n"Dad!" Xavier swung his walking stick, connecting with another agent's knee. The crack was sickeningly loud.\n\nWe fought. Not well. Not skillfully. But desperately. The way cornered animals fight when there's nothing left but survival.\n\nI lost track of time. Everything became motion and fear and the burning ache in my injured arm as stitches tore and wounds reopened.\n\nThen I heard her.\n\n"STEPHEN!"\n\nThe tavern wall behind us exploded. Wood, plaster, and stone vaporized as my wife's distorted form smashed through it, landing in the center of the room.\n\nShe rose to her full, impossible height, her form flickering and undulating, her gaze fixed on Xavier.\n\n"Sweetheart," she said. Her voice had gone soft. Almost tender. "My beautiful boy. Come here. Come to mommy."\n\nXavier backed away, his face pale. "You're not my mom."\n\n"Of course I am." She took a step forward. Her body rippled, features sliding. "I made you. I raised you. I—"\n\n"You tried to kill us!" Xavier shouted.\n\nSomething flickered across her melting face. Confusion. Pain. Maybe even recognition.\n\n"I... I don't..." Her voice wavered. Multiple tones fighting for dominance. "You were mine. You are mine. I just... I wanted..."\n\nShe lunged. A blur of motion, aimed directly at Xavier.\n\nI moved. Threw myself between them.\n\nHer clawed hands didn't just tear through fabric and skin; they found the half-healed bullet grazes from the gym. The stitches Maeve had put in my arm and side ripped open. A fresh, searing pain exploded across my chest.\n\nI went down hard, the wind knocked from my lungs.\n\nBut it gave Xavier time. He didn't run deeper into the battle. He did what any terrified kid would do—he scrambled past the bar and bolted out of the ruined tavern, trying to find safety, to go somewhere else, anywhere else.\n\nMy wife screamed. A sound of pure, animal rage as she saw him escape.\n\nAnd then she turned her attention to our son.\n\nI tried to get up. Couldn't. My chest was on fire. Fresh blood soaked my shirt.\n\n"Xavier!" I choked out. "Xavier, run!"\n\nBut when I looked up, I saw that he wasn't running. He was standing his ground, the walking stick raised like a weapon.\n\nMy wife closed the distance in seconds.\n\n"XAVIIIEEEERRR.” In that yell we knew all too well. “You ungrateful little—"\n\nShe grabbed him. Not gently. Her distorted hands wrapped around his torso and pulled.\n\nXavier’s body began to stretch. Like some Stretch Armstrong doll pulled way too hard.\n\n"NO!"\n\nI was moving before I knew it. Running. The pain in my chest didn't matter. Nothing mattered except reaching him.\n\nBut I wasn't fast enough.\n\nA third hand, a fourth, appeared. Appendages that were more like talons of shifting, glitching matter… they plunged toward him.\n\nXavier let out a sound I will never forget—a choked gasp, his eyes wide with surprise.\n\nHis form wavered, warped, distorted to the breaking point. For a moment, I saw his very divine spark shining bright for all the universe to see.\n\nXavier's sound cut off, replaced by a silent, O-mouthed shock as he stared at what was happening to him.\n\nHis face started to relax, and then he... just... wasn't.\n\nThere was no sound, no flash of light. Just a silent pop, like a bubble bursting. The space he occupied was suddenly, completely empty. An absence.\n\nThis reality rejected him. One moment he was there. The next, he was gone.\n\nMy wife stood there, her hands still outstretched, covered with the shadow of the absence of Xavier. She stared at the empty space where he'd been.	4	535	f	2026-02-27 12:30:46.897308-05	2026-02-28 23:21:48.960132-05	normal	\N
522	128	6	Xavier's Erasure	Stephen's wife tears Xavier apart; his form is rejected by the Akashic Realm and he vanishes completely. Stephen, overwhelmed by rage and grief, taps into a primal power and destroys her, but learns that Xavier's fate is unknown—he could be anywhere or nowhere.	"What... what did I...?"\n\nSomething inside me snapped.\n\nNot shattered. Transformed.\n\nRage  flooded through me that wasn't human. It was older. Deeper. It was the fury of every cycle, every lifetime, every moment of pain compressed into a single, burning point. I was the shade of the avatar of a reality long since consumed.\n\nI didn't think. I reached.\n\nThe power came from somewhere primal. Somewhere I'd forgotten existed. My hands moved in patterns I didn't remember learning, and the air around me bent.\n\n"For all those days," I said, my voice not quite my own, "when I needed comfort and you weren't there."\n\nMy wife turned toward me, her melting face showing something that might have been fear.\n\n"Stephen, I—"\n\n"For every moment you made me feel worthless."\n\nThe power gathered. Purple-black energy crackling around my fists.\n\n"For every time you used my love against me.. Twisted my words and made everything about yourself."\n\nShe tried to run. Too late.\n\n"For killing my son! For XAVIER!"\n\nI struck.\n\nWith will. With intention. With power I didn't understand but somehow knew how to use.\n\nThe blast hit her center mass and tore. Her distorted body came apart, peeling back in layers. Skin. Muscle. Bone. Down to the core.\n\nAnd there, at her center, I saw it.\n\nA divine spark.\n\nSmall. Weak. But real.\n\nShe fell. Her body dissolving but her eyes—her actual eyes, clear for just a moment—found mine. For a moment she shifted, back to the porcelain doll.\n\n"I'm sorry," she whispered. "I didn't... I didn't mean to..."\n\nThen the humanity faded. The NPC programming reasserted itself for a final moment.\n\n"You were mine," she said. "You were supposed to be mine."\n\n"I know," I said softly.\n\nHer body dissolved completely. The divine spark hung in the air for a moment, glowing softly, then drifted upward. Away. Out.\n\nFree.\n\nThe rage that had filled me, the impossible power I'd pulled from God-knows-where... it all vanished, leaving a cold, hollow space.\n\nI collapsed.\n\nShock. Grief. A wave of conflicting emotions so vast it stole my breath. I'd just destroyed her. After everything she'd done, after the lies and the violence, after she'd torn my son apart... I felt a profound, aching regret for what I'd done to her. Despite all the rage, the sorrow, and the sadness, a single, impossible truth remained.\n\nBecause I’m still trying to love her.\n\nThe battle was over. The twisted agents had fallen. The defenders were regrouping, tending to wounded, mourning the dead.\n\nI was still on my knees, staring at the patch of stone slick with my son's blood. Gone. He was just... gone. The sound of his scream, the wet tear as she ripped him apart... it played over and over in my head.\n\nLady Illkeserod appeared beside me, kneeling down. "Stephen." Her voice was gentle. "Xavier is..."\n\n"Gone," I finished, my voice hollow. I'd just watched him die. Eviscerated.\n\n"Yes," she said, helping me sit up. "He's gone. The realm... it ejected him when he was damaged. He is no longer in the Akashic Realm."\n\n"Where is he?" I grabbed her arm, desperate. "Is he dead? Is he... is he back in Etheris?"\n\nShe looked at me, her ancient eyes filled with a pity that tore at me. "I don't know, Stephen. I cannot sense him. He is not in this realm. I do not think he is back in Etheris... but I cannot be certain."\n\n"So where is he? What happened to him?"\n\n"The Akashic Realm is connected to infinite realities," she said softly. "When it rejected him... he could be anywhere. There is a chance he got out. Truly out. But where he would go, what reality he would land in... no one can know." She paused. "We are still operating within the membrane, pressed against the inside of Etheris. My senses are limited here. He is just... gone."\n\nGone. Not "safe." Not "outside." Just gone. I didn't know if he was alive, or dead, or trapped in some other hell. I'd just lost him, and I didn't even know how to mourn.	5	536	f	2026-02-27 12:30:46.897308-05	2026-02-28 23:22:36.541274-05	normal	\N
523	128	7	The Choice to Return	Lady Illkeserod reveals Xavier was ejected from the realm to an unknown location; the battle is over and a retreat is necessary before the prison collapses the connection. Stephen refuses to leave without Jamie and commits to re-entering Etheris, knowing it will cause catastrophic consequences.	Benji limped over, his cardigan torn and bloody, his face grim. He cut off whatever Lady Illkeserod was about to say.\n\n"There is no 'now,' Stephen. We're done. Your wife's incursion, the deaths, the raw violence... it's destabilized everything. The bridge we used to get Illskerkarne here—it can't hold against the prison's new security protocols."\n\nLady Illkeserod looked toward the horizon, her expression pained. "He's right. The realm is rejecting Etheris. We have to pull out. Now. Before the connection collapses and traps us all."\n\nShe turned to me, her eyes heavy. "This is it, Stephen. This is your one chance. You can come out with us. You can escape Etheris. Be free."\n\nEscape? I looked at the smoking ruins of the town, at the spot where Xavier's blood stained the stone. I was done. I'd already lost the life I knew. I'd just lost my son. I'd even lost my wife, a cold, programmed thing that I still cared for.\n\nI wasn't going to lose Jamie, too.\n\n"No," I said.\n\n"Stephen, you don't understand," Benji pleaded. "We're leaving. This is the exit. This is the only exit!"\n\n"I'm not going out," I said, my voice flat. "I'm going back in."\n\n"What?" Lady Illkeserod looked at me in horror. "Back into the prison?"\n\n"I'm not leaving without her."\n\n"If you go back in, you'll be trapped!" Benji shouted. "We can't do this again! We won't be able to pull the Akashic Realm back into Etheris to get you. You will be lost. Trapped. Forever."\n\n"I don't care."\n\n"But you understand what that means, don't you?" Lady Illkeserod said, her voice filled with a terrible, sad understanding. "If you go back in... if you somehow manage to find her and pull her out... if you remove the her..."\n\n"The power fails," I finished. "The prison collapses."\n\n"Everything collapses," she corrected, her voice grim. "Etheris. The substrate. All of it. The cascade will be catastrophic for countless realities outside the prison. Yaldabaoth will be free."\n\nI met her gaze, the ancient, tired part of my soul surfacing. "I know. It'll be a catastrophe. But 'outside' is infinite. Even if he consumes all the realities outside, there will still be infinitely more. We'll just have to figure out another way to contain him. Fuck the whole prison is broken already, the resets aren’t working and things are leaking out now."\n\n"Stephen," she pleaded, "you're talking about restarting the Great War. Going back to the infinite cycles of fighting, escaping, and repeating... all the suffering we endured just to trap him here in the first place."\n\nI looked at the green, swirling portal that led to Jamie.\n\n"Good," I said. "Let the whole fucking universe burn."\n\nBenji and Lady Illkeserod exchanged glances.\n\n"We'll help you get to her," Lady Illkeserod sighed. "But first, we need to make contact with someone who can guide you under the substrate."\n\n"Nyarlathotep," I said.\n\n"Yes."\n\nBenji closed his eyes, his form flickering. When he spoke, his voice carried across dimensions I couldn't perceive.\n\n"Crawling Chaos. Messenger of the Outer Gods. We require passage."\n\nThe air grew cold.\n\nA presence settled over the battlefield. Not visible. Not tangible. Just... there. Vast and ancient and utterly alien.\n\nYou again, a voice said. Not in my ears. In my mind. In my bones. You little worm. You corrupted half my auditors last time you were in here. Almost destroyed the place when we tried to purge the system of Whitney or Jennifer or whatever the fuck she's called. I thought we deleted you.\n\n"You almost did," Benji said calmly. "But luckily I had Lady Illkeserod on my side. She pulled me from the purge purgatory at the edges."\n\nHow inconvenient for me. A pause. And now you want passage under the substrate. Why should I help?\n\n"Because either way Yaldabaoth breaks free. If this works, we might be able to figure something else out," Lady Illkeserod said. "And you know what happens then."\n\nAnother pause. Longer this time.\n\nFine, the presence said. But this is the last favor. Next time you come begging, little auditor, I'll let you rot.\n\nThe ground in front of us began to ripple. Not like water. Like reality itself was becoming unstable. A tear opened, glowing faintly green, looking more like pudding than a hole.\n\n"There's your door," Benji said. "Straight down. Under the substrate. To where Azathoth pipes his mad dreams into existence."	6	537	f	2026-02-27 12:30:46.897308-05	2026-02-28 23:24:24.79032-05	normal	\N
524	128	8	The Passage Below	Lady Illkeserod heals Stephen's wounds; Benji summons Nyarlathotep and negotiates passage beneath the substrate to reach Jamie; Stephen receives a mysterious Magic card from Benji and jumps into the portal leading under the reality of Azathoth's dreams.	I stood. My legs shook. My chest was a mass of torn stitches and fresh wounds. But I was done waiting. Done planning. Done with everything except getting Jamie out.\n\n“But you’re still injured.” Benji said.\n\nLady Illkeserod’s hands did something, and for a moment I forgot I had ever been injured. It was like the memory of the injuries ceased to exist, and because I didn’t believe in them any longer, I suddenly wasn’t in any pain.\n\n"Thank you," I said to Lady Illkeserod. "For everything."\n\n"Don't thank me yet," she said. "You might not survive what comes next."\n\nBenji fumbled in one of his impossible pockets. "Wait!" he called out. "It's dangerous to go alone! Take this!"\n\nHe handed me what looked like a worn, scuffed Magic: The Gathering card. I recognized it instantly, though it looked... different. Thicker.\n\nVeilwalker.\n\nA memory flashed: high school lunch, years ago. Whitney, back when she was still struggling with being Will, tapping this very card. She'd called it a "custom card," her signature 'J's scribbled all over it in black marker. I even remembered Gil squinting at it, confused by the rules when he found one in this reality.\n\nBut the card in my hand wasn't the one I remembered. The 'J's were gone. The artwork was new—not just a figure stepping between realities, but something more visceral. It showed a winged, beautiful demon stepping through a tear in a wall of fire. The demon was reaching for an angel tied to a burning stake.\n\nI looked at the stats. The mana cost was impossible: 9, White, Blue, Black, Red. It was missing green.\n\nThe rules text was different, too. It wasn't about shuffling libraries. This text was a threat:\n\nVeilwalker – 9WUBR\n\nLegendary Creature – Avatar\n\nWhen Veilwalker enters the battlefield, you must pay 20 life. When you do, exile all other non-land permanents.\n\nIf Veilwalker leaves the battlefield, and you do not control 'The Boy Who Remembers' or 'The Girl Who Chose Love', you lose the game.\n\n"I... I don't understand," I said.\n\n"You will," Benji said, pushing me toward the portal. "Now go. You're out of time."\n\nI walked to the edge of the tear and looked down. The green pudding-substance swirled, revealing glimpses of something vast and terrible below. Something oozing. Something living. Glistening, fleshy tubes pulsed in the gloom, coiling around massive, dark organs that beat with a slow, wet rhythm. The entire chasm was slick with a foul, organic slime, and underneath it all, a sound.\n\nA piping. Maddening and eternal.\n\nAs I stepped through, I smelled it.\n\nPeaches and vanilla.\n\nShe was down there. Waiting. And I was finally going to bring her home.\n\nI jumped.	7	538	f	2026-02-27 12:30:46.897308-05	2026-02-28 23:25:40.326367-05	normal	\N
525	129	1	The Library Between Worlds	Xavier and Hannah exist in a pre-birth state within a vast, infinite library—a spiritual realm where consciousness itself dwells. They await their Father's arrival, knowing they are meant to be a family. The peace is absolute, timeless, and complete.	There was no transition. No sense of travel.\n\nOne moment, the impossible, agonizing pop—the next, silence.\n\nHe simply was.\n\nHe floated in a dark, cold void, an absolute nothingness that had no up or down. The suddenness of it was the most confusing part. His mind, still reeling from the tavern, tried to catch up, to find purchase on a reality that had no edges.\n\nAs his consciousness tried to reassemble itself in the silent dark, a different feeling surfaced. The cold nothingness of the void dissolved, replaced by a memory so profound it was more real than the dark.\n\nHe wasn't cold anymore. He was... warm.\n\nIt was a memory of a time before time. A place where consciousness was not a thing he arrived at, but a place he had always been. It was the gentle, constant hum of shared happiness, a "strange womb like room" that was not a room at all, but a state of being.\n\nThe world was a gentle, constant hum. It was a place of shared happiness, a warmth that had no beginning and no end. It was, Xavier knew, a "strange womb like room," and it was the only home he had ever had. He existed within it, a "quiet knowing," and he was not alone.\n\nBeside him, a part of him, was Hannah.\n\nIf he was the knowing, she was the "bright and joyful sound." They were two parts of a single thought, waiting.\n\nTheir home was not a room, not truly, but a state of being that had taken on a familiar shape. It was a library, vast and yet impossibly intimate. Bookshelves brimming to the top with books stretched in every direction, the walls themselves. They held not just stories, but the idea of stories, the whole of all infinite knowledge before it had ever been known. The ceiling was low, sloping down into an infinite, hazy distance, creating a feeling of perfect safety.\n\nAnd they were not the only ones.\n\nFrom their cubbyhole, tucked neatly into the shelves, Xavier watched the others. An infinite number of people, all in white robes, milled around. Their spirits brushed past one another like soft fabric, indistinct and aimless. They, too, were waiting, but their waiting was a river flowing to a general sea.\n\nXavier felt no kinship with them. They were waiting to exist. He and Hannah were waiting to reunite.\n\nHe'll be here, Hannah sent to him.\n\nShe was "the talkative" one. Her essence was light, a vibration that formed ideas he understood more clearly than any spoken word. Her joy bubbled over, an effervescence against his own quiet certainty. He's looking for us, even if he doesn't know it yet. And we'll be a family.\n\nXavier, the mysterious one, did not need to reply. He simply knew. He knew his sister, and he knew the precise, aching-sweet space in their shared existence that was waiting to be filled.\n\nHe settled back into his cubby, the white mattress a part of the floor beneath him. He pulled the blue fuzzy blanked up to his chin, a wisp of comfort against the timelessness of the place.\n\nHe was content. Here, in the heart of all knowledge, the only thing he needed to know was the feeling of Hannah's spirit nearby, and the patient, pulling feeling of their Father, somewhere beyond the shelves, on his own journey to find them. He closed his eyes, though he did not need to sleep, and let himself drift in the hum. He would wait. They would wait. It was all there was.	0	539	f	2026-02-27 12:31:00.673544-05	2026-02-28 23:26:46.796322-05	normal	\N
547	133	8	The Crawling Chaos Appears	Nyarlathotep arrives violently, nearly destroying Benji but his wards hold. The chaotic entity demands a price for opening the portal, and Lady Illkeserod intervenes to protect the demon, leading to a tense standoff between gods.	Here is the tweaked and expanded scene:\n\n"We don't." Benji's smile widened, but it looked strained. "He's already here. He's been watching since we arrived. Probably—"\n\nBenji's words cut off in a wet gurgle. He didn't just freeze; he convulsed.\n\nHis body arched backward, a sound like a dozen wet towels snapping coming from inside his chest. He clawed at his cardigan as something moved beneath the wool, bulging and shifting.\n\n"Benji!" Lady Osilari screamed.\n\nHis stomach split open. Not with a tear, but with a series of wet, percussive pops. Organs, slime, and black ichor erupted from his torso. But it wasn't just his insides—it was wrong. Extra limbs, glistening and chitinous, unfolded from the wound. Eyes embedded in weeping flesh bubbled to the surface. He was being turned inside out, his corporeal form shredding as his essence was torn apart. He let out a high, thin scream as his body began to disintegrate into fragments.\n\nBut the wards Lady Osilari had placed on him flared with violent, blue light. The glowing symbols, which had been resting invisibly on his skin, now blazed, acting like cosmic sutures. They grabbed the dissipating pieces of Benji. The flying viscera, the extra limbs, the fragments of his being—they all stopped in mid-air and were pulled back.\n\nWith a sound like a hurricane rewinding, the gore and pieces were sucked back into the gaping hole in his body. We watched in horror as the wound knit itself shut, wetly and imperfectly, leaving him whole but visibly traumatized.\n\nBenji collapsed to his knees, panting, covered in his own fluids.\n\nThe space rippled.\n\nShadows pooled in front of us, collecting, condensing, becoming something that wore the shape of a man but moved like liquid chaos. Nyarlathotep emerged from nothing, his form shifting, hands casually in his pockets. He looked at Benji, then at Lady Osilari, and then he smiled.\n\n"Well, well, well," he purred, his voice laced with amusement. "I told you what I'd do if I ever saw you near my realm again, little auditor."\n\nHe looked genuinely impressed, if a little miffed. "I must say, I didn't expect the wards to hold. Actually pulling you back together after a full-essence dissolution... that's a new trick." A delighted, cold laugh escaped him. "How wonderfully, delightfully... unexpected. Did you miss me little auditor?"\n\n"Not particularly." Benji's voice stayed level despite the ward-light flickering nervously around him.\n\n"Liar." Nyarlathotep moved closer, circling. Predatory. "I can smell your fear. Taste it on the air. You remember what I did to you last time. How I peeled your consciousness apart layer by layer. How I almost succeeded in erasing you completely."\n\n"I remember."\n\n"And yet you called me anyway. Brave? Or stupid?" The Crawling Chaos grinned, showing too many teeth. "I'm leaning toward stupid."\n\nI stepped forward, putting myself between them. "We need your help."\n\nNyarlathotep's gaze shifted to me. Amused. Curious. "Again? How wonderfully masochistic of you. The child-thing who broke Yaldabaoth twice. I wondered if you'd come crawling back."\n\n"We're not crawling. We're asking."\n\n"Semantics." He waved a hand dismissively. "What do you want, little godling?"\n\n"Another portal into Etheris."\n\n"And you think I'm that someone?" He laughed, the sound echoing wrong through the Akashic space. "Flattering. But why should I? At this rate, we’ll be free weather you intervene or not. Let the prison collapse. Let the anti-reality spread. Let everything burn. Chaos is my domain, after all."\n\nJamie moved to my other side. "Of course. What’s another trillion years among gods. Or, we could help get you out now."\n\n"Maybe." Nyarlathotep studied her with unsettling intensity. "So the power source speaks. The twin soul. I've heard stories about you. How you died and came back wrong. How you see things that shouldn't be seen." He leaned closer. "How you taste like vanilla and peaches and existential dread."\n\n"Back off." My voice came out harder than intended.\n\nNyarlathotep's grin widened. "Protective. How sweet. How... mortal." He straightened, spreading his arms. "But fine. I'll help you. Not because I owe you anything. But because watching you dive back into the god's essence will be entertaining."\n\n"What's the price?" Lady Osilari asked quietly.\n\n"Smart woman. Always checking for hidden costs." Nyarlathotep's form shifted, becoming something that hurt to look at directly. "The price is simple. When this is done... when you've separated Sam from his infection or died trying... the demon is mine."\n\nBenji didn't flinch. "I already promised you that."\n\n"You promised after. I'm collecting now. The moment you cross back into this realm, assuming you survive, you belong to me. No delays. No negotiations. No last-minute reprieves from your goddess lover."\n\nThe air grew thick. Heavy. I looked at Benji, searching for... what? Fear? Resignation? Some sign that this was too much, that he wanted to back out.\n\nBut Benji just smiled. That same peaceful, resigned expression. "Deal."\n\n"No." Lady Illkeserod's voice cut through the space like a blade.\n\nWe all turned. She stood at the edge of our platform, having crossed into the Akashic Realm without any of us noticing. Her form radiated power and barely contained fury.\n\n"The auditor is under my protection," she continued, moving toward Nyarlathotep. "You will not take him."\n\n"Your protection means nothing in there, little resistance goddess." Nyarlathotep's amusement faded to something colder. Dangerous. "This is neutral ground. The Akashic Realm answers to no one. And the demon offered himself freely."\n\n"He offered himself after the mission. Not before. Not now."\n\n"I'm changing the terms."\n\n"Then I'm refusing them." Lady Illkeserod's hand found Benji's shoulder. Possessive. Protective. "Find another price."\n\nThe space between the two gods crackled with tension. Reality bent at the edges, threatening to tear.\n\nThis was going to get ugly fast.\n\n"Stop." Benji stepped forward, out of Illkeserod's grip. "Both of you. This isn't helping." He looked at Nyarlathotep. "You want me? Fine. Take me now. Before the mission. Erase me. Unmake me. Do whatever cosmic horror bullshit you've been planning. But open the portal first. Let them through."\n\n"Benji, no—" Illkeserod reached for him.\n\n"It's okay." He smiled at her. Sad. Loving. Final. "I've had a good run. Longer than most demons get. And if my death buys them a chance to save Sam, to stop the anti-reality from spreading... that's worth something."\n\n"You're worth more than that."\n\n"Maybe. But this is my choice."\n\nNyarlathotep watched the exchange with undisguised fascination. "How touching. How tragic. How..." He paused. "Boring."\n\nEveryone froze.\n\n"What?" Benji blinked.\n\n"I said boring." Nyarlathotep's form shifted again, becoming almost human. Almost sincere. "You're willing to die. That takes all the fun out of it. Where's the chase? The fear? The desperate scrambling as I slowly dismantle everything you are?" He waved dismissively. "No. I'll pass. You can keep your martyrdom."\n\n"Then what do you want?" I asked carefully.\n\nNyarlathotep's grin returned. "Entertainment. I want to watch you fail. Watch you try to save a god who doesn't want saving. Watch the anti-reality tear you apart from the inside. That's payment enough."\n\n"You're letting us go? Just like that?"\n\n"I'm opening a portal. What you do with it is your problem." He turned, shadows gathering around his hands. "But know this, little dreamer... if you succeed, if you somehow manage to separate Sam from his infection, if you do wind up getting us out, I will remember. And I will owe you an infinite debt."\n\n"I can live with that."\n\n"Can you?" Nyarlathotep's eyes gleamed. "We'll see."\n\nHis hands moved in patterns that made my brain hurt. The air in front of us split. Tore. Reality bleeding at the edges as something vast and terrible forced its way through the reinforced Veil.\n\nThe portal opened.\n\nBeyond it, I saw twisted light. Wrong colors. Images of death and chaos interspersed with the normal hustle and bustle of city life.\n\n"There." Nyarlathotep stepped back, admiring his work. "One entrance to Heaven, freshly torn. Try not to die too quickly. I hate short entertainment."\n\nLady Osilari moved first, checking the portal's stability. Her symbols pulsed, reading the energies. "It's solid. But it won't last long. Once we cross through, it'll seal behind us."\n\n"No coming back this way," Jamie said quietly.\n\n"No coming back any way unless we succeed." I looked at my team. Benji, still wrapped in protective wards. Lady Osilari, her tattoos glowing with borrowed power. Jamie, my twin soul, my anchor, my everything.\n\n"Together," Jamie said.\n\n"Together," I agreed.\n\nWe stepped toward the tear in reality. Behind us, Lady Illkeserod called out. "Benji. Come back to me. Promise me you'll come back."\n\nHe looked over his shoulder. Smiled. "I promise I'll try."\n\nThen we crossed through into Yaldabaoth's domain.	7	533	f	2026-02-27 12:32:10.366732-05	2026-02-28 23:46:03.077546-05	normal	\N
526	129	2	The Mall Apocalypse and Hannah's Sacrifice	The library collapses into a dying mall reality as Yaldabaoth, a void entity, tears reality apart. The Father, transformed into a god-like being, hesitates to unmake the world. Hannah sacrifices herself to give her Father the resolve to save Xavier, erecting a barrier that prevents her from being born into the waking world.	**********\n\nThe hum was gone. It had been replaced by a tearing, metallic scream.\n\nThis was not the library. This was a place of glass and false light, a "mall" reality that had become a prison. And it was ending.\n\nXavier clung to Hannah’s essence, his quiet knowing reduced to a single point of terror. The world was coming apart. The ceiling, a parody of a sky, was shattering. Reality was twisting in weird distortions, and through the ruptures, it started to hail fire boulders.\n\nIn the center of the atrium, their Father stood. He was not the warm, patient pull from the library; here, he was a god, an omnipotent form of burning light, holding the collapsing world together with sheer will.\n\nBut he was failing.\n\nOpposite him, a monstrous sight unfolded. Yaldabaoth. It was not a creature, but a void given shape, an emptiness that consumed the light. Somehow it clicked. This was the thing within which the library had sat, the unreality surrounding the library. Yet there it was, it was the antithesis of the library, the end of all knowledge.\n\nTheir Father, the dreamer, was the only one who could stop it. But Xavier knew, with the cold certainty that was his nature, what it would cost. To destroy the void, their Father would have to unmake this entire plane of existence. He would have to sacrifice this world to save the countless souls, including their own, trapped within it.\n\nAnd their Father was hesitating. The god-form shuddered, unwilling to pay the price.\n\nThen Hannah moved.\n\nShe left Xavier’s side, her bright and joyful sound now a single, pure note of resolve. She was no longer just his sister; she was a spirit child, and she understood the cost. She moved through the rain of fire, untouched, and stood before their Father.\n\nXavier watched, frozen. He could hear her, not as words, but as pure intent broadcast through the chaos.\n\nYou must, she sent to their Father, her essence unwavering. He must be stopped. You must do it now.\n\nTheir Father's light flickered with agony. He knew.\n\nHannah turned her head, just slightly, and Xavier knew she was speaking her final piece. He was not meant to hear it, but his connection to her was absolute. He overheard the message meant only for their Father.\n\nI will not be with you in the waking real. Not as you planned. I will no longer end up being your biological child, but I will be remembered. You must remember me.\n\nA new, terrible understanding dawned on Xavier. The sacrifice was not the world. It was her.\n\nHannah’s essence flared, bright and brave. She delivered her final words, the command that would become Xavier's life’s purpose.\n\nYou must never forget this existence. And you must always take care of Xavier. He is the hope. He is the future. He is the everything.\n\nTheir Father's form constricted, a star collapsing in on itself. A single, silent word of grief and acceptance tore through reality.\n\nThe world went white.\n\nThe Father's power, unleashed, was absolute. It was not a pull but a push. As the mall and the void and the fire boulders dissolved into pure, agonizing light, Xavier felt his Father's will focus on him. It was a violent exhalation, a desperate, final act of salvation.\n\nHe saw Hannah, for one fractional second, turn toward him. She was not afraid. Her essence smiled.\n\nThen the separation was absolute. He was ejected, hurtled from a reality that was no more. He was alone, a fragment of a spirit tumbling through a non-space of buzzing static. The warmth of the library was a memory. The light of his sister was an echo. All he had left was the mission, the promise he had overheard.\n\nHe was the hope. And he had to find his Father again.	1	540	f	2026-02-27 12:31:00.673544-05	2026-02-28 23:27:30.02706-05	normal	\N
