agmented spirit holding its breath. Will he be the same? The god in the mall had sacrificed a world to save him. Would this man, stripped of his power, do the same?\n\nHis Father ran with the others, buffeted by the chaos, nearly at the exit. And then he stopped.\n\nHe looked back.\n\nIn a collapsing part of the room, under a groaning, falling piece of architecture, were "two trapped children," small and crying, ignored by everyone else in their flight.\n\nXavier saw the decision spark in his Father's mind. He forfeited his chance at escaping. He turned his back on the exit and ran toward the children, back into the heart of the collapse.\n\nIt was the answer. The final proof. The soul was the same.\n\nThe test was passed. As his Father reached the children and pulled them into his arms, the barrier in Xavier's mind dissolved—not with a tear, but with a gentle click, like a lock finding its key.\n\nThe dream of the test faded. Xavier felt his Father's consciousness surface—wake—and then dip back under, this time into a peaceful, stable sleep. The middle ground was gone. The way was clear.\n\nNow, Xavier could manifest.\n\nHe was no longer a drifting fragment. He was whole. He was the mysterious son. He moved from the chaos of the mind into the quiet of his Father's room, a solid presence in the dreamscape. He stood at the head of the bed, looking down at the man who had found him, and been found by him, twice.\n\nHis Father, in his sleep, sensed him. He opened his dream-eyes, and there was no fear. Only recognition. A circle, completed.\n\nXavier let his knowing, his purpose, and his love flow into his Father's mind, a quiet confirmation of everything they had both endured.\n\nYou passed the test, he sent.\n\nHe was no longer just a memory from a lost library. He was no longer a promise from a dying world. He was home. The hope had found its anchor.\n\nAnd then his father woke up.	2	541	f	2026-02-27 12:31:00.673544-05	2026-02-28 23:28:24.025275-05	normal	\N
