eginning and more like a long-awaited arrival. As if he hadn’t just met this person, but had been waiting for him, searching for him, his entire life.\n\n"Hey there buddy," he whispered, his voice thick. "It's me. I'm your dad. I've got you."\n\nThe baby, Xavier, flinched at the sound. The vibration of the voice, the one he had followed through the chaotic middle ground, was now real. It was a physical thing, a new anchor in this cold, bright world. His unfocused, newborn eyes fluttered, trying to find the source of the sound. His crying stopped completely. He settled into the warmth, a quiet knowing already deep in his bones, and felt a profound, instinctual sense of rightness.\n\nThe Father felt the shift as the tiny body relaxed against his chest. A lock clicked into place, deep in his heart. He didn't know that this was the final test, the one he had already passed in his dreams. He didn't know that the soul in his arms was a promise from a shattered reality, or that the weight he felt was the hope, the future, and the everything.\n\nHe just knew this was his son. And he knew, with an unshakeable certainty that he would never be able to explain, that he would never, ever let him go. The mysterious son, and the father who had found him twice, were finally, simply, home.	3	542	f	2026-02-27 12:31:00.673544-05	2026-02-28 23:29:11.461923-05	normal	\N
529	130	1	The Endless Fall	Stephen falls through a portal into an alien realm called Etheris, experiencing a disorienting descent toward a desert landscape. He receives fragmented communications from Whitney and Benji through a pulsing card, hearing warnings about creatures and a mysterious Pyramid he must reach.	I fall.\n\nThe portal above is gone. I don't know when it vanished—one moment the sickly green shimmer was there, the next just... absence. Below me stretches an endless expanse of tan and brown, muted twilight colors that blend together like old photographs left in the sun too long.\n\nI'm so high up the ground doesn't seem to be getting any closer. It should be. Physics should still work, even here. But the landscape below remains distant, unchanging, a flat desert horizon that extends in every direction to infinity.\n\nI fall.\n\nAnd fall.\n\nAnd keep falling.\n\nThere's no wind. No sense of direction. Just the sick, rolling sensation of my stomach trying to catch up with my body while my body tries to remember which way is up. My arms and legs move through empty air. I try to orient myself but there's nothing to orient against. Just endless sky above and endless sand below.\n\nTime stretches. Or compresses. I can't tell. Minutes? Hours? My lungs burn but I can't remember if I'm breathing or if breathing even matters here.\n\nThe card in my pocket pulses.\n\nNot heat. Not cold. Just... presence. A thread connecting me to something outside this freefall. It vibrates against my chest like a second heartbeat, and with it comes—\n\nStatic.\n\nVoices beneath the static. Fragmented. Broken.\n\n"—phen can you—"\n\n"—nal is degraded but—"\n\n"—keep trying he needs to—"\n\nBenji's voice cuts through, clearer than the rest. Calm. Steady. An anchor in the chaos.\n\n"Breathe, Stephen. You're still here. Still real."\n\nI try to respond but I don't know if I have a voice anymore. The falling has stripped away all sense of self. Am I Stephen? Am I just a consciousness