es are wrong in a way that makes my brain stutter. Chitinous shells—like crabs, but too geometric, too planned—cracked open like dropped eggs. Wet, pink meat spills onto the dunes, glistening in the twilight. Some are still twitching, their crab-like legs scratching weakly at the sand, drawing patterns that might be language or might just be death spasms.\n\nOthers have been torn apart entirely. Segmented bodies scattered across the beach like discarded toys. Limbs bent at angles that don't match any anatomy I know.\n\n"They're not biological in the way you understand it," Benji continues. His voice has that professorial tone, like he's lecturing to calm himself as much as to inform me. "Part fungus, part crustacean, part something else entirely. They're miners. Scientists. Surgeons. They can extract a human brain and keep it alive in a cylinder for transport across the cosmos."\n\n"Jesus Christ."\n\n"They're also virtually indestructible," he adds. "Those shells? Harder than diamond. Takes immense force to crack them open like that. Whatever killed them... it was powerful. Concentrated. Deliberate."\n\nI stagger to my feet. Look around. The bodies stretch in every direction. A graveyard of impossible things, all pointing toward—\n\n"A Pyramid," Whitney's voice cuts in, choppy but present. "They were trying to reach it. Trying to breach the barrier. Something stopped them."\n\nI look at the nearest corpse. Its shell has a spiral pattern etched into it, almost decorative. One of its legs—segmented, jointed in three places where there should be one—still clutches something. A tool? A weapon? I can't tell.\n\nThe smell intensifies as I move. Ammonia burns my eyes. The brine makes me think of fish markets and low tide and things that shouldn't be out of water.\n\n"Why are they here?" I ask.\n\n"Same reason you are," Benji says. "They want in. Want to reach what's underneath. But the prison doesn't discriminate. It kills everything that tries to break the rules."\n\nI look down at the Mi-Go I almost landed on. Its shell is split vertically, exposing the pink-gray flesh inside. Something pulses in there. Not a heart. Something else. A sac of fluid? A cluster of organs I don't have names for?\n\nThe crab-like legs twitch once more.\n\nThen stop.\n\n"Their spines," Benji says quietly. "If you need a weapon... their spinal structures are nearly unbreakable. Like compressed carbon. They won't shatter, won't bend. If you can extract one..."\n\nI stare at the corpse. At the exposed meat. At the thing that might be a spine running through the center mass.\n\n"You want me to dig through alien corpses for improvised weapons."\n\n"I want you to survive," Benji replies. "Look around, Stephen. You're going to need something."\n\nI follow his direction. Look past the immediate graveyard to what lies beyond.\n\nThe dunes crest about fifty yards ahead. And beyond them...\n\nThe black ocean.\n\nIt's moving. Shifting. And as I watch, I realize it's not water at all.\n\nIt's them.\n\nCreatures. Millions of them. A living, seething mass stretching all the way to the horizon.\n\nAnd they're all heading in the same direction.\n\nI squint.\n\nThere.\n\nA pyramid. The pyramid.\n\nBut calling it a pyramid is like calling a black hole "dark." Its angles are wrong. They bend in ways that make my eyes water and my head throb. It's massive. Impossible. And every creature in that black ocean is clawing its way toward it.\n\n"You see it?" Whitney's voice cuts through the static. "That's where you need to go."\n\n"Through that?" My voice comes out raw.\n\n"Through that."\n\nBenji's voice joins hers. "The barrier at the base of the pyramid... Nyarlathotep controls access. You'll need to reach it. And then you'll need to convince him to let you in."\n\n"Convince. Right." I look down at the horde. "Any suggestions on how I survive the next five minutes?"\n\n"Duck," Whitney says.\n\nSomething screams overhead.\n\nI drop flat as a thing with too many wings tears through the space where my head was. It's not a bird. It's not anything that should fly. Its body is a pulsing sac of translucent flesh, veins visible beneath the skin, and its wings are membranous sheets stretched between finger-bones that are too long, too jointed.\n\nIt banks. Circles back.	0	474	f	2026-02-27 12:31:22.457664-05	2026-02-28 23:30:12.228137-05	normal	\N
