d down. Finally grab one of the Mi-Go spines scattered among the bodies.\n\nIt's nearly as long as my arm. Curved. Sharp. And when I test it against a nearby rock, the rock cracks.\n\nGood enough.\n\nI straighten. Look toward the pyramid.\n\nIt's closer now. Maybe a mile. Maybe less. Hard to tell when the landscape keeps shifting.\n\n"The entrance is on the far side," Whitney says. Her voice is strained. "You'll know it when you see it. It's... wrong. Like everything else here, but more so."\n\n"Great. Super helpful."\n\n"Stephen." Benji's voice is gentler. "You're doing well. Just keep moving. We're with you."\n\nI nod. Even though they can't see me.\n\nThe horde presses tighter as we near the pyramid. The creatures aren't just fighting each other anymore. They're fighting something else. Something invisible.\n\nA barrier.\n\nI feel it before I see it. A pressure in the air. A resistance. Like walking into a strong wind that only exists in one direction.\n\nThe creatures slam against it. Claw at it. Scream at it.\n\nIt doesn't care.\n\nI push through them. Shoulders hunched. Head down. The Mi-Go spine clutched in both hands.\n\nSomething grabs my ankle.\n\nI look down.\n\nA ghoul. Half its face torn away, exposing the muscle and bone beneath. It grins up at me with teeth that are filed to points.\n\nI bring the spine down.\n\nOnce. Twice. Three times.\n\nThe skull cracks. The grip loosens.\n\nI pull free and keep moving.\n\nThe pyramid's base is a cliff face of black stone. Smooth. Featureless. Impossible to climb.\n\n"Around the corner," Whitney says. "There's a fissure. You'll have to squeeze through."\n\nI follow the wall. The horde thins here, the creatures unable to navigate the tight space between the cliff and the barrier.\n\nAnd there it is.\n\nA crack. Not wide. Maybe two feet at its widest point. It twists into the stone at an angle that shouldn't allow passage.\n\nI look back at the seething mass of creatures. At the sky that's growing darker. At the distant shapes circling overhead.\n\nThen I step into the fissure.\n\nThe walls press tight. I turn sideways. Shuffle. The stone scrapes against my chest and back. My arms screams as I force it through a particularly narrow section.\n\nBehind me, something follows. I hear it scratching. Chittering.\n\nI move faster.\n\nThe fissure opens into a small chamber. Natural. Or at least pretending to be. The walls are slick with something that might be water but smells like ammonia.\n\nI turn.\n\nTwo octopus-looking things squeeze through behind me. Their forms torn. Leaking. But still functional. Not quite solid—more like concentrated oil given shape and purpose.\n\nThe spine in my hand feels suddenly inadequate.\n\nThey rush me.\n\nI swing. The first one takes the blow across its mass. The gelatinous body splinters but doesn't dissipate. It reels back, appendages reaching out, reforming even as I watch.\n\nThe second one doesn't tackle me.\n\nIt lunges.\n\nStraight past me. Toward the far wall. Toward the shimmer in the air.\n\nIt hits the barrier.\n\nFor a fraction of a second, nothing happens.\n\nThen it screams.\n\nNot a sound. A frequency. Something that makes my heels rattle and my vision blur.\n\nThe creature doesn't just die. It comes apart. Torn by something invisible. Something surgical and precise. Its body shreds lengthwise, then horizontally, then in ways that don't follow any pattern I understand. Like reality itself is rejecting it, cutting it into smaller and smaller pieces until there's nothing left but a fine black mist that dissipates into the stone.\n\nThe whole thing takes maybe two seconds.\n\nI stumble backward. Away from the wall. Away from the shimmer.\n\nThe first creature—the one I hit—stops its advance. Its mass quivers. Then it retreats. Squeezing back through the fissure with desperate, jerking movements.\n\nI stand there. Gasping. Covered in black ichor and my own blood. Staring at the space where the second creature used to be.\n\n"Stephen." Whitney's voice cuts through the shock. Urgent. "You're at the barrier. Don't get any closer. You can't cross it. Not without permission."\n\n"What the hell was that?" My voice is shaking.\n\n"The prison's immune system," Benji answers. "Anything that tries to force entry gets... processed. Deleted. You need an invitation."\n\n"You need to ask him," Whitney says. "You need to ask Nyarlathotep to let you through."\n\nI look at the far wall. At the space that should be solid stone but isn't. At the shimmer in the air that suggests something else. Something watching.\n\nMy hand tightens on the Mi-Go spine.\n\n"And if he says no?"\n\n"Then you're stuck," Benji says quietly. "And the horde behind you will eventually find this chamber."\n\nI swallow. Taste blood and seaweed.\n\nRight.	1	544	f	2026-02-27 12:31:22.457664-05	2026-02-28 23:31:00.047308-05	normal	\N
544	133	5	The Family Argument	Stephen and Jamie decide to return to Etheris with Benji and Lady Osilari, but Xavier and Hannah fiercely resist, leading to a painful confrontation about family loyalty and sacrifice.	"It's suicide," Xavier said flatly. "You'd be going right back into the thing we just pulled you out of."\n\n"I've done it before."\n\n"And you barely survived!"\n\n"But I did survive. And I learned something. I learned that love recognizes love. That even under all that corruption, Sam still remembers what it was like to care for his flock. I can reach him. I know I can."\n\nJamie stood, moving to my side. Her hand found mine, grip tight enough to hurt. "Then I'm going with you."\n\n"Jamie—"\n\n"Don't." Her eyes locked on mine. "Don't you dare try to leave me behind. We're stronger together. You said it yourself."\n\nI wanted to argue. Wanted to protect her. Wanted to tell her to stay safe out here in Elysium while I dove back into hell.\n\nBut I'd tried that before. Tried to be the hero who sacrificed himself alone.\n\nAnd it never worked.\n\n"Okay," I said softly. "Together."\n\nXavier stood up fast enough to knock his chair over. "No. Absolutely not. You two aren't going back in there. We just got you back. We're not losing you again."\n\nHannah nodded beside him. "We're sticking together. All of us. As a family."\n\nAnd there it was. The moment I'd been dreading.\n\nI looked at my son. My daughter. Both of them wearing expressions I recognized because I'd worn the same one a thousand times. Determination. Love. The absolute certainty that family meant never leaving anyone behind.\n\nThey'd been trapped in Yaldabaoth's essence for eternities. Consumed. Digested. Reformed. Their consciousnesses shredded and rebuilt over and over while the dark god fed on their divine sparks.\n\nI couldn't send them back into that.\n\nWouldn't.\n\n"No," I said.\n\nXavier's face darkened. "Dad—"\n\n"I said no." My voice came out harder than I meant it to. "You two stay here. That's final."\n\n"You can't just—"\n\n"I can and I am. You're not going back into that thing. Not after what it did to you. Not after what you've survived."\n\nHannah's eyes flashed with anger. "And what about you? What about what it did to you?"\n\n"I'm different."\n\n"Bullshit."\n\n"Hannah—"\n\n"It's bullshit and you know it!" Her voice cracked. "You're not invincible. You're not some cosmic martyr who gets to decide he's the only one allowed to suffer. We're a family. We stick together."\n\nGod, she sounded just like Whitney. That same fire. That same refusal to back down.\n\n"You're staying," Jamie said quietly. Her voice had that tone. The one that meant the discussion was over. "Your father's right. You've been through enough."\n\n"So have you!" Xavier shot back.\n\n"And we're choosing to go anyway. You don't get that choice. Not this time."\n\nI saw the moment Xavier realized we meant it. Saw the anger shift to something worse. Betrayal. Fear. The terrible understanding that sometimes love meant saying no, even when no felt like abandonment.\n\n"This is fucked up," he said finally.\n\n"I know."\n\n"If you die in there…"\n\n"We won’t."\n\n"You can’t promise that."\n\nI let the quiet sit a moment… felt it tug at my chest.\n\n"Yeah. I guess not."\n\nHe looked at me like he wanted something solid to hold. Something certain.\n\n"Worse comes to worse," I said, "we get swallowed. Pulled into its hunger. Maybe we drift in there for… I don’t know… ages no one counts."\n\n"That’s not helping."\n\n"Wait." I tried a small breath. "Even then… I still see this picture in my head of you coming back in. Finding us. Pulling us out. Same way we pulled you."\n\nHe shook his head, but it wasn’t a no… more like a scared maybe.\n\n"And Yaldabaoth…" I rubbed my thumb along my palm. "It never keeps the sparks. Not really. It just eats. Swells. Pops. Like some giant ooze that doesn’t know what it’s chewing."\n\n"You make it sound simple."\n\n"It isn’t." I met his eyes. "But it’s the truth we’ve got."\n\nAnd he was right. I couldn’t promise anything. Not with something like that waiting for us.\n\nBut I could promise the simple thing… the only thing.\n\n"Your mother and I are gods," I said. "From another reality, yes, but gods nonetheless. Inside Etheris, that matters. The null-magic doesn't erase what we are. It just... dampens it. But our divine essence can still affect the substrate in ways mortal souls can't."\n\n"Plus," Jamie added, "we're bringing backup."\n\nBenji shifted in his seat. "You're bringing me."\n\n"You know the prison's internal workings better than anyone. You've audited Yaldabaoth's systems. If anyone can navigate us to Sam's core, it's you."\n\n"And me," Lady Osilari said. "For occult protection. The anti-reality will try to corrupt you on the way in. My symbols and wards can buy you time."\n\nSo. Our team.\n\nJamie and me. Divine essence anchoring us.\n\nBenji. The auditor who knew every back door in the prison's architecture.\n\nLady Osilari. Master of symbols, keeper of wards against things that shouldn't exist.\n\nFour of us diving back into hell to perform an exorcism on a god.\n\n"When do we leave?" I asked.\n\nLady Illkeserod looked at Lady Osilari. Some silent conversation passing between them.\n\n"We need to prepare," Lady Osilari said finally. "Ward you properly. The anti-reality will fight back. It always does."\n\n"How long?"\n\n"A week. Maybe two."\n\nI nodded. Two weeks to say goodbye. Two weeks to prepare for what might be the last mission any of us ever took.\n\nTwo weeks to figure out how to save a shepherd who'd forgotten his own name.	4	558	f	2026-02-27 12:32:10.366732-05	2026-02-28 23:43:35.020819-05	normal	\N
532	130	4	The Hall of Worshippers	Stephen enters the Pyramid's interior and encounters Nyarlathotep in a surreal hallway lined with ecstatic worshippers in various states of painful transformation. Nyarlathotep reveals dark truths about Stephen's children, Xavier and Hannah/Whitney, and their scattered existence across timelines.	**********\n\nInside it feels like I’m being force fed honey and burnt wire.\n\nI stand in what might be a hallway. Or a room. The perspective keeps sliding away from me when I try to pin it down. The walls are too close and too far at the same time, covered in hieroglyphs that writhe when I'm not looking directly at them.\n\nAnd the people.\n\nThey line the walls like decorations. Dozens of them. Maybe hundreds. All naked. All in various states of... wrong.\n\nA woman hangs suspended by hooks through her shoulders. Her skin pulls taut around the metal, blood running in thin rivulets down her chest and stomach. But she's not screaming. She's smiling. Eyes half-closed. Breathing slow and deep. Her lips move in a whisper I can't quite hear.\n\nNext to her, a man kneels with his arms stretched above his head, wrists bound to an iron ring in the wall. His back is a lattice of cuts, precise and symbolic. Fresh blood wells from each line. He sways slightly, humming.\n\nThey're not prisoners.\n\nThey're worshippers.\n\nThe light here is wrong too. Not torches. Not electricity. Just a diffuse, amber glow that seems to seep from the stone itself. It casts everything in shades of brown, yellow, orange. Desert colors. But underneath, shadows pool deeper than they should, purple and hungry.\n\n"Enjoying the decor?"\n\nNyarlathotep appears beside me. Not walking. Just there. He's still wearing the suit, but now it's a different color. Was it gray before? It's yellow now. Canary yellow. Rustic red. Too bright for this place.\n\n"They chose this," he says, gesturing to the worshippers. "Every single one. I offer them clarity. Purpose. A way to feel something real in a reality that's all lies and loops. And they say yes. They always say yes."\n\nI keep my hand on the Mi-Go spine. It feels solid. Real. Everything else here feels negotiable.\n\n"Where's the way down?" I ask.\n\n"Impatient." He walks ahead, his footsteps silent on the stone. "You outsiders. Always rushing toward your own destruction. Can't you appreciate the journey?"\n\n"The journey's been long enough."\n\nHe laughs. The sound echoes wrong, bouncing off walls that weren't there a second ago.\n\nWe walk. The hallway stretches. Contracts. Sometimes we're in a tunnel barely wide enough for my shoulders. Sometimes we're in a cathedral space so vast I can't see the ceiling.\n\nThe worshippers change too. Some are suspended in elaborate rope work, their bodies bent into shapes that should break bone but somehow don't. Others are pierced with so much metal they look like living sculptures.\n\nBut they're not all human.\n\nA creature with too many joints and skin like polished obsidian hangs from chains, its multiple arms spread in a pose of ecstatic surrender. Something that might have once been a Cthulhu spawn writhes slowly against the wall, its tentacles woven through iron rings, the flesh splitting and healing in endless rhythm.\n\nA Mi-Go—or what's left of one—has been opened like a flower, its shell peeled back in perfect geometric petals, the pink-gray meat inside pulsing with visible pleasure. It makes a sound like wind through pipes.\n\nOne man—maybe elf or vampiric—has his chest opened, ribs spread like wings, his heart visible and beating. He's laughing. Tears stream down his face but the sound is pure joy.\n\nNext to him, something with fur and too many eyes has been flayed in strips, the skin hanging in neat ribbons. It purrs.\n\nThey're organic. Visceral. Wrong in every way that matters.\n\nAnd yet... right.\n\nThere's a symmetry to it. A terrible beauty. Like they've all found exactly what they were looking for. Like the pain and the modification and the wrongness of it all is precisely what they needed.\n\n"You know what's funny?" Nyarlathotep doesn't look at me as he speaks. "This whole prison... Yaldabaoth formed it to contain himself. Or rather, the thing wearing him dreamed it up. Consciousness trapping consciousness. A paradox eating its own tail. And you... you keep coming back. Life after life. Loop after loop. Always trying to save her."\n\n"Jamie."\n\n"Is that what you're calling her this cycle?" He stops. Turns. His eyes aren't human anymore. They're black pits with stars at the bottom. "She's had so many names. So many faces. But yes. Jamie. The twin flame. The other half. The power source keeping this whole rotten structure standing."\n\nMy chest tightens. "Where is she?"\n\n"Under the substrate. Beneath everything. She power’s this whole place and keeps Azathoth company. His little pet. Has been for eternities." He tilts his head. "But you know that already. Don't you? Deep down. That's why you're here. That's why you've always been here."\n\nHe starts walking again. I follow.\n\n"Want to know something delicious?" His voice is lighter now. Conversational. "Your children. The boy and the girl. You think you found them. You think they somehow escaped after being consumed by Yaldabaoth. And they did... in a way. But they weren't supposed to. Not like that."\n\nMy hand tightens on the spine.\n\n"Vietnam," he says. "You and Jamie. Different names, different bodies, but the same pathetic souls. You managed to finally find each other. She got pregnant. Should have been Twins. A boy and a girl. Beautiful, really. All that hope. All that desperate love."\n\nHe pauses beside another worshipper—a woman with her eyes sewn shut, her mouth open in silent song.\n\n"Then you died," he continues. "Both of you. And the twins... were never allowed to get born. Never got a chance to cross over properly. They've been floating in the dream plane ever since. Fragments. Echoes. Waiting."\n\n"Xavier—"\n\n"Is real now because you pulled him through. You ripped him out of the dream plane in a moment of grief so powerful it tore holes in reality. Your NPC wife couldn't have children. Wasn't designed to. But you wanted a son so badly you forced it. Made it happen. Yanked his soul across dimensions and shoved it into her flesh."\n\nHe glances at me. "Impressive, really. Most people can't do that."\n\nMy throat is dry. "And Hannah?"\n\n"But here's the beautiful part," he continues, his voice almost gleeful. "When Xavier was trapped—when the system tried to delete him too—Hannah made a choice. In the dreamscape, where souls drift between incarnations, she found him. Her brother. And she pushed him out. Sacrificed her own escape to give him the momentum he needed to break through."\n\nHe glances back at me. "She saved him. Threw herself into the gap so he could cross. Very heroic. Very stupid."\n\n"What happened to her?"\n\n"She was thrust out," Nyarlathotep says. "Not into Etheris. Not into any reality you'd recognize. Into purgatory. The space between. The place even Bythos—the Monad, the Source of All—dares not tread. A realm so close to anti-reality it's practically touching the membrane. The edge of everything. Where existence meets un-existence."\n\nHis voice drops lower. "She was free. Truly free. No prison. No loops. No Yaldabaoth. Just... endless nothing. An infinity of absence. Alone at the boundary where reality ends and something worse begins."\n\nHe stops walking. Turns to face me fully.\n\n"She wandered that edge for eternities. Or seconds. Time doesn't exist there. Laughing sometimes. Screaming others. Going mad. Getting sane again. All while standing at the precipice of the anti-reality, feeling it pull at her. Wanting to let her in. Wanting to unmake her completely."\n\n"But she didn't fall," I say.\n\n"Ah. Now that's where it gets fun." His smile is wide. Too wide. "If she was going to be born. She should have been Hannah. Your daughter. The girl you and your cold, hollow wife were supposed to have but never did. Because your wife was an NPC. A construct. Incapable of warmth. Incapable of creation. She was a wall, Stephen. A barrier keeping you from having what you wanted."\n\nThe hallway narrows. I have to turn sideways to keep following him.\n\n"So Hannah's soul just... floated," he says. "Waiting for a body that never came. Until the reset. Until you died in that hospital pod in your eighties, bitter and full of regret, screaming into the void about how your family never felt complete. How it should have been different. How you should have had more children."\n\nHe stops. We're at a junction. Three paths. Each one darker than the last.\n\n"That rage," he says softly. "That grief. It triggered something. A glitch in the matrix. And when the reset happened, Hannah's soul got caught in the machinery. She incarnated wrong. Into an NPC. A boy named William."\n\nMy stomach drops.\n\n"Whitney," I whisper.\n\n"Whitney," he confirms. "Your daughter, wearing the wrong skin. Living the wrong life. Suffering under a father who hated what she was because he couldn't see who she actually was. She spent that whole life trying to be real, trying to be seen, and the whole time she was more real than anyone around her."\n\nHe chooses the middle path. I follow.\n\n"And when she finally became herself," he continues, "when she finally broke the script and lived as Whitney... the system tried to delete her. Couldn't allow the paradox. But she was too real. Too you. Too stubborn. So instead of deletion, she got ejected. Thrown clear of the prison entirely."\n\nThe path slopes downward. The temperature drops.\n\n"She's been trying to reach you ever since," he says. "Your daughter. Screaming across dimensions. And you didn't even recognize her voice."\n\n"I heard that." Whitney's voice cuts through the card's connection. There's no anger in it. Just... weight. "Can we not do the whole emotional breakdown thing right now? I'm fine. I'm here. That's what matters."\n\nNyarlathotep laughs. "She has your spine, at least. Both of them do. Xavier and Hannah. Your actual children. Not the hollow thing you were married to."\n\n"Why are you telling me this?" I ask. My voice sounds far away.\n\n"Because it's funny." He stops at another junction. This time there are five paths. "Because you've been playing house with an NPC for decades while your real family scattered across timelines. Because the tragedy is so perfectly crafted it might as well be art. And because..." He turns to face me. "...you need to understand what you're about to lose."\n\n"What do you mean?"\n\nNyarlathotep grins. "Told you this was going to be fun."	3	545	f	2026-02-27 12:31:22.457664-05	2026-02-28 23:32:37.755908-05	normal	\N
533	130	5	Descent into the Substrate	Stephen descends through an increasingly claustrophobic tunnel that compresses and narrows until it becomes impassable, forcing him through a barrier of pure darkness. He experiences complete physical dissolution, layer by layer, until only consciousness remains.	I look down the five paths. They all lead into darkness.\n\n"Which one?" I ask.\n\n"Does it matter?" He spreads his arms. "They all go to the same place. Down. Into the substrate. Into the dark where Jamie's been waiting for you. Into the space beneath the machine where Azathoth pipes his mad song and the Nameless Mist tears intruders apart."\n\nHe leans in close. His breath smells like embalming fluid and old flowers.\n\n"The darkness down there isn't empty," he whispers. "It's full. Full of everything that tried to cross before you. Full of screaming. Full of pieces. And it's hungry. So very, very hungry."\n\nI choose the left hand path. Because it doesn't matter.\n\n"Good luck, little mouse," Nyarlathotep calls after me. "Try not to die too quickly. I want to see how this ends."\n\nThe tunnel swallows me. The amber light fades.\n\nBehind me, I hear him laughing.\n\nAnd ahead... ahead there's only the dark.\n\nAnd somewhere in that dark, a thin, mad piping begins.\n\n"We're still with you," Whitney says. Her voice is small but steady. "Whatever happens down there. We're not leaving."\n\n"Keep moving," Benji adds. "Follow the music. It's the only way through."\n\nI grip the Mi-Go spine tighter.\n\nAnd I descend.\n\nInto the place where reality ends.\n\nInto the space beneath everything.\n\nInto the dark that's been waiting for me since the first loop began.\n\nThe piping grows louder.\n\nAnd the Nameless Mist stirs.\n\n**********\n\nThe tunnel doesn't end. For a trillion years I walk, always forward, darker, deeper, closer.\n\nGradually, over eons, it just... changes.\n\nThe amber light has long faded away like the death of the final star. The walls close in. What was a walkable corridor becomes something I have to turn sideways to navigate. Then something I have to shuffle through. Then something that forces me to my hands and knees.\n\nThe stone presses against my shoulders. My back. My chest.\n\nI keep moving forward because there's nowhere else to go. The tunnel behind me is compressing. I can hear it. Feel it. A grinding, crushing pressure that's eating the space I just occupied.\n\nThe darkness ahead is absolute now. Not the warm twilight of the pyramid's interior. Not even the cold nothing of empty space. This is dark. Capital D. The kind that presses against your eyeballs like physical weight. And cold. Pure absolute cold.\n\nI crawl.\n\nMy hands find stone. Then nothing. Then stone again, but narrower. The walls are closing. Not slowly. Deliberately. Like the tunnel is swallowing me.\n\nI have to flatten myself. Drag myself forward on my stomach. The stone scrapes my ribs. My hips. I can barely draw breath. The space is too tight. Too small.\n\nBehind me, the grinding gets louder.\n\nI push forward. Inch by inch. My shoulders wedge. I have to exhale completely, make myself as small as possible, force myself through the gap.\n\nAnd then I hit it.\n\nThe barrier.\n\nIt's not stone. Not physical. It's darkness itself, solidified. I reach out and my hand stops against something that isn't there. Something that pushes back with absolute resistance.\n\nI can't go forward.\n\nThe walls behind me keep closing. And now it’s pressing me forward. Compressing me like a hydraulic press.\n\nI'm pressed against the barrier now. My face. My chest. My whole body flattened against this wall of pure nothing.\n\nThe stone behind me crushes inward.\n\nI want to scream but there's no air left to scream with.\n\nThe pressure is absolute. Everywhere. All at once. Not just on my body—in it. My ribs compress. My lungs collapse. My bones creak and flex and threaten to splinter.\n\nAnd then—\n\nThe barrier gives. Or rather I give.\n\nNot breaks. Not opens. It's more like... I'm being forcibly filtered by this barrier. Mind, body, and spirit.\n\nThe pressure squeezes.\n\nAnd squeezes.\n\nAnd I feel myself compressing and contracting. Not violently. Not all at once. Just... compressed. Like being forced through a sieve that only lets the finest particles through.\n\nSuddenly…\n\nthere's nothing.\n\nNo pressure. No walls. No tunnel.\n\nI'm falling. Again. But not like before.\n\nThis is falling into absence.\n\nThe darkness here isn't like closing your eyes. It's not the darkness of a room with the lights off. It's not even the darkness of the tunnel.\n\nThis is the darkness that exists before light was invented. Before the concept of light had a name. Before "before" meant anything.\n\nIt's darker than anything. Darker than black. Darker than the space between stars.\n\nAnd it's aware.\n\nI feel it pressing against me. Not physically—It touches the parts of me that shouldn't be touchable. My thoughts. My memories. The raw, electric sense of I think therefore…\n\nI'm alone in it.\n\nCompletely, utterly, impossibly alone.\n\nAnd I'm still falling.\n\nThen the pain starts.\n\nNot all at once. It builds. A pulling sensation in my chest. My skin. My bones. Like something is trying to peel me apart from the inside out.\n\nI try to scream but I don't have a mouth anymore.\n\nThe darkness is eating me.\n\nLayer by layer. Sense by sense.\n\nFirst, the buzzing starts in my hands. Then it spreads up my arms. My legs start tingling as well. I am a mass of buzzing cells all trying to stay together.\n\nThe Mi-Go spine slips from my hands as the buzzing intensifies.\n\nThen, like an onion, everything starts to slowly rip apart.\n\nThe skin goes first. I feel it peel. Not like sunburn. Not like anything natural. It lifts away in sheets, starting at my fingertips and working inward. I watch—can still see somehow—as the dermal layers separate from the subcutaneous fat, pulling free with wet, tearing sounds that I feel more than hear.\n\nThe fat follows. Adipose tissue dissolving like wax under heat. It doesn't drip. It just... stops being solid. Becomes liquid. Then vapor. Then gone.\n\nThe muscles are next.\n\nThey don't tear. They un-thread. Each fiber separating from its neighbors. Pulling apart strand by strand. I feel every single one. The deltoids in my shoulders unraveling like rope. The pectorals across my chest peeling away in layers. The quadriceps in my thighs splitting into thousands of individual strings that drift away into the dark.\n\nMy organs go with them. Liver. Kidneys. Lungs collapsing like deflated balloons. Intestines uncoiling, dissolving segment by segment. Heart stopping mid-beat, the muscle fibers un-twisting before the final contraction completes.\n\nThe pain is absolute.\n\nEvery nerve fires at once. A symphony of agony as my entire body screams its dissolution. But I can't scream back because my throat is gone, my lungs are gone, my mouth is—\n\nAnd then the bones dissolve.\n\nNot shattering. Dissolving. The calcium leaching out, turning to powder, to dust, to memory. Ribs. Spine. Femurs. The scaffolding that held me upright for forty-some years just... gives up. Becomes mineral. Becomes nothing.\n\nWhat's left shouldn't exist.\n\nBut it does.\n\nMy nervous system.\n\nHanging there in the dark like a grotesque puppet with its strings cut. Brain. Spinal cord. The intricate web of nerves branching out to where limbs used to be. All of it exposed. Raw. Glistening with synaptic fluid that has nowhere to drain.\n\nI'm a blueprint of electrochemical impulses. A map of every sensation I've ever felt, stripped of the machinery that gave it context.\n\nAnd I can feel everything.\n\nEvery synapse. Every axon. Every dendrite firing in panicked confusion because the body they're supposed to control isn't there anymore.\n\nThe pain doesn't stop. It intensifies.\n\nBecause now there's nothing to buffer it. No endorphins. No shock response. Just pure, unfiltered neural feedback screaming into a void that doesn't care.\n\nAnd then even that starts to go.\n\nThe peripheral nerves first. The ones that used to reach my fingers, my toes. They fray. Unwind like thread pulled from a spool. Each nerve fiber separating, drifting away, taking with it the memory of touch, of sensation, of physical connection to the world.\n\nThe spinal cord follows. Vertebra by vertebra, the bundle of nerves comes apart. Unwinding. Un-spooling. I feel each fiber separate. Feel the connections severing. Feel the pathways that carried signals from brain to body dissolving into static.\n\nMy brain is last.\n\nThe cerebellum goes first. Balance. Coordination. The sense of where my body is in space. Gone.\n\nThe temporal lobes. Memory. Language. The ability to name what's happening to me. Dissolving.\n\nThe frontal cortex. Personality. Decision-making. The sense of who I am. Peeling away like layers of an onion.\n\nUntil all that's left is the brainstem. The lizard core. The part that knows only one thing:\n\nI am.\n\nAnd then even that unravels.\n\nPiece by piece. Neuron by neuron. Synapse by synapse.\n\nThe pain doesn't fade. It just... stops having anywhere to register. Stops having anyone to experience it.\n\nI'm coming apart.\n\nNot dying. Worse than dying.\n\nBeing unmade.\n\nLayer by layer. Sense by sense. Until there's nothing left but—\n\nA spark.	4	547	f	2026-02-27 12:31:22.457664-05	2026-02-28 23:33:24.869695-05	normal	\N
534	130	6	The Nameless Mist and Divine Spark	Stephen's consciousness exists as a bare spark in the absolute darkness of the substrate, surrounded by the whispers of previous victims and the awareness of the Nameless Mist. He navigates toward the piping of Azathoth while maintaining his coherence against dissolution.	That's all. A single point of consciousness. Awareness without form. Thought without brain. Existence without body.\n\nJust me. Whatever "me" means now.\n\nFloating in the dark.\n\nAlone.\n\nI try to pull away but there's nothing left to pull with.\n\nThe card.\n\nThe thought surfaces through the dissolving static of my consciousness.\n\nVeilwalker.\n\nWhen Veilwalker enters the battlefield, you must pay 20 life.\n\nIn the game, 20 life is everything. Most players start with 20. Paying it means ending the game. Losing. It's not a cost—it's a sacrifice. You give up your entire position, your entire foundation, for one moment of transformation.\n\nI’ve paid the pound of flesh, but yet I still am.\n\nThe person I was—Stephen, husband, father, failed software engineer, fugitive, killer of his own wife-construct—dissolves into the dark. Every memory, every moment, every choice that made me me burns away.\n\nThe card slips through dimensions I can't perceive anymore. Gone. Used. Sacrificed.\n\nAnd I'm...\n\nI'm...\n\nThe divine spark.\n\nThat's all that's left. A single point of consciousness floating in infinite nothing. No body. No history. No name.\n\nJust awareness. Bare and terrible and alone.\n\nThe dark Nameless Mist presses closer. Hungry. Curious.\n\nAnd then I hear them.\n\nThe others.\n\nWhispers without mouths. Screams without throats. The voices of everything that's been dissolved before me, their final thoughts still echoing in this space like radio signals that never stop broadcasting.\n\n"...can't breathe can't breathe can't..."\n\n"...my children where are my children I had..."\n\n"...real I was real I was I..."\n\n"...please please please please please..."\n\nThey press against me. Into me. Trying to find something solid to hold onto. But I'm barely solid myself. Just a spark trying not to go out.\n\nThe Nameless Mist coils through the dark. I can't see it but I can feel it. A presence without form. It doesn't hunt. It doesn't chase. It just is. And anything that exists in its space gets absorbed. Processed. Returned to the substrate.\n\nI'm drifting.\n\nNo up. No down. No reference point.\n\nThe isolation is worse than the pain. Worse than the dissolution. I'm alone in a way that makes every other lonely moment in my life feel like a crowded room.\n\nAnd then—\n\nMusic.\n\nNot music. Piping.\n\nThin. Mad. Coming from everywhere and nowhere. A sound that shouldn't be possible. Notes that climb and fall in patterns that hurt to hear. Rhythms that pulse against the beat of something vast and sleeping.\n\nI focus on it.\n\nIt's the only thing here that isn't the dark. The only thing that isn't the mist. The only thing that suggests there's a direction in this directionless void.\n\nI move toward it.\n\nNot swimming. Not flying. Just... willing myself in that direction. Pulling my consciousness along a thread of sound that shouldn't exist.\n\nThe whispers get louder. Angry. Desperate. They don't want me to leave. They want company. They want someone else to be lost with them forever.\n\nI push through.\n\nThe piping grows. Swells. Becomes deafening.\n\nAnd then I see it.\n\nAzathoth.	5	548	f	2026-02-27 12:31:22.457664-05	2026-02-28 23:34:10.82569-05	normal	\N
535	130	7	Azathoth and Recognition	Stephen encounters Azathoth, the blind idiot god at the center of creation, whose form is incomprehensible and whose piping sustains the prison. He perceives Jamie's spark within the darkness and experiences a revelation about their connection as the twin flame battery powering the entire system.	The blind idiot god at the center of everything. The nuclear chaos. The sultan of demons. The thing that dreams reality into existence without knowing it's dreaming.\n\nIt's...\n\nI can't... I can't look at it. Every time I try to focus, my perception slides off like water on glass. It's too big. Too fundamental. Too utterly, terrifyingly other.\n\nBut I catch glimpses.\n\nA mass. Writhing. Made of something that's both matter and antimatter. Light and darkness fused into a substance that shouldn't exist. It pulses with a rhythm that predates hearts, predates time itself.\n\nAnd it's piping.\n\nNot with instruments. With itself. Its form creates the sound. Every pulse. Every shift. Every moment of its terrible, mindless existence generates that mad music that holds this place together.\n\nLooking at it is agony.\n\nMy consciousness—what's left of it—starts to fray. The edges blur. I'm losing coherence. Losing the thread of what I am. What I was. Why I'm here.\n\nThe dark around the infinite Azathoth is absolute. Not the darkness of the void I came through. This is the darkness beneath the void. The substrate below the substrate. The final layer where even nothing can't exist without permission.\n\nAnd in that darkness...\n\nLight.\n\nSmall. Impossibly small. But there. A pinpoint of warm illumination in all this cold nothing.\n\nI know it.\n\nNot with memory. Memory is gone. I know it with something deeper. Something that was there before I was Stephen. Before I was anyone.\n\nJamie.\n\nThe recognition is instant. Absolute. Like finding the other half of a frequency that's been broadcasting incomplete for eternity.\n\nI move toward her.\n\nThe darkness fights me. It doesn't want me to reach her. Doesn't want anything to disturb the power source. The engine. The battery keeping this whole rotten prison running.\n\nTendrils of nothing wrap around my spark. Try to pull me back. Try to dissolve me completely.\n\nI push harder.\n\nThe piping reaches a crescendo. Azathoth's form shifts. For just a moment, I see through it. See the machinery beneath. The impossible architecture of how this prison works.\n\nIt's everywhere and nowhere. Infinite and contained. Inside and outside simultaneously. The substrate isn't a layer—it's a state. A way of being that exists in every moment across every timeline. Azathoth dreams at the center, and its dreams echo outward, creating Etheris, creating the loops, creating the cage that holds us all.\n\nAnd Jamie...\n\nShe's here. But she's also...\n\nFlashes hit me. Not memories. Knowledge. Pure information burning through what's left of my consciousness.\n\nVelistra. The body I knew in the war outside. The warrior. My love in that timeline.\n\nJamie. High school. Peaches and vanilla. The girl behind Becca.\n\nThe Angel of Death. The Archon. Stolen by Yaldabaoth becoming the corrupted divine consciousness recycled through endless loops.\n\nThey're all the same. All fragments of the same source. The same spark that I'm looking at right now.\n\nThe paradox makes my non-existent head hurt.\n\nHow can she be here—locked beneath the substrate as the power source—and also walking around in bodies across different timelines? How can she be the battery and the person at the same time?\n\nAzathoth's piping shifts. The answer burns through me.\n\nLove.\n\nThat's what the divine sparks are. That's what we are.\n\nWe never got to meet. Not really. Not in any way that lasted. But I always felt her love. No matter where I was. No matter which loop. Which life. Which body.\n\nHer love surrounded me. Pulled at me. I was the cathode to her anode. Opposite charges creating a circuit that couldn't be broken no matter how hard the system tried.\n\nAnd that pull—that endless, aching, necessary connection—is what gave this prison its power.\n\nNot Yaldabaoth's hunger. Not the substrate's machinery. Not Azathoth's blind piping.\n\nUs.\n\nOur love. Our bond. The twin flame connection that predates this prison, predates Etheris, predates everything except the moment two sparks first recognized each other across the infinite dark.\n\nI understand it now.\n\nWhy we were inside. Why we separated ourselves. Why we never tried to find each other across infinite cycles.\n\nWe are the battery.\n\nThe power source wasn't just Jamie. It was us. Together. The magnetic pull between our souls. The desperate, clawing need to reunite. That infinite energy—that endless, renewable, indestructible love—is what kept Yaldabaoth contained. What held the prison together. What powered the loops for eternity.\n\nWe did this to ourselves.\n\nWe chose this. Chose to be the battery. Chose to be separated. Chose the endless, aching loneliness of infinite cycles apart because the alternative was worse.\n\nBecause if we ever did come together...\n\nI reach out.\n\nMy spark extends. A thread of consciousness stretching across the final gap.\n\nAnd she reaches back.\n\nThe moment our sparks touch, everything explodes.\n\nLet there be light.	6	549	f	2026-02-27 12:31:22.457664-05	2026-02-28 23:35:00.03246-05	normal	\N
536	131	1	The Seed Bleeds	A fragmented meditation on movement, stillness, and presence. The narrator transitions from chasing and forward motion into listening, breathing, and simply existing in the moment.	Chasing\n\nOnly the transient\n\nAlways forward\n\nForever moving\n\nStop\n\nThe trees are speaking\n\nListen and be calm\n\nBreathe in aware\n\nExist	0	550	f	2026-02-27 12:31:25.802748-05	2026-02-28 23:35:59.332937-05	normal	\N
537	132	1	Awakening in Elysium	Stephen regains consciousness in a hospital bed aboard a space station called Elysium, orbiting the black hole prison Etheris. He struggles to reconcile his cosmic experience with his fragile human body, then discovers Whitney—his erased daughter Hannah—waiting for him. Xavier awakens in the adjacent bed, and the three are reunited as a fragmented family.	My eyes struggle open, gritty and heavy, like they’re sealed with sand.\n\nThe first thing I register is a sound. A low, electronic hum. Steady. Persistent. It’s wrong. The last thing I remember is… light. An explosion of it, a sensation of touching… Jamie. Our sparks, her spark… we had merged. Then, the infinite, blinding light.\n\nI gasp, a dry, painful rasp, expecting the void, the darker-than-black substrate, the sound of the mad piping.\n\nInstead, I smell… antiseptic.\n\nThe air is cold, sterile. It stings the back of my throat. I’m lying on something stiff, not the organic, living floor of the substrate. Sheets. They’re tucked in, tight and clean.\n\nMy vision clears. The light isn't the infinite, explosive kind; it's a cold, white, fluorescent panel in a seamless white ceiling. I follow the line of the ceiling to the wall, just as white. I feel a slight tug on the back of my hand and see a thin tube taped there, leading to a metal pole and a dripping bag.\n\nA hospital. I’m in a hospital. The contrast between the memory of cosmic dissolution and the sterile banality of this room is a vertigo that makes my stomach lurch. I’m in a bed. My body feels heavy, thick, and terribly hungover. I try to sit up, but my muscles scream in protest, weak and unused. This is my 46-year-old body—overweight, hair thinning, aching in ways that are all too human.\n\nA dull, familiar ache radiates from my lower back. Of course. After being a spark, after touching infinity, I'm back.\n\nI look down, and a wave of resignation hits me. The soft belly. The thinning crown. The persistent throb in my joints that never quite goes away. All that power, all that light, and I'm still stuck in this crappy, failing shell.\n\nThe room is sterile. White. Too white.\n\nI turn my head, my neck stiff. There isn't a wall. It’s a viewport.\n\nMy breath catches.\n\nOutside is not a city or a sky. It’s space. A vast, gleaming ring structure, impossibly huge, floats against the black. Smaller vehicles, silent and sleek, move between its glowing towers. And in the distance, dominating the view, is a swirling, massive, pitch-black hole.\n\nEtheris. We’re outside. We’re looking at it.\n\n"What... where am I?" My voice is a dry croak.\n\n"You're safe."\n\nThe voice comes from the corner. I squint, and a figure detaches from the shadows. A girl. Sixteen, maybe seventeen, dressed in black jeans and a worn hoodie.\n\nMy heart stutters. I know her.\n\n"Whitney?"\n\nThe name feels right and wrong at the same time. The memories collide—my high school classmate, the girl who was erased, the vision of my daughter an eternity ago, a reality that no longer exists, from a life consumed by Yaldabaoth.\n\nShe steps into the light, and her smile is nervous, awkward, her eyes darting between me and the floor. She knows. Nyarlathotep told us. We just never had a moment to...\n\n"Hey… Dad."\n\nHer voice hitches on the word, catches. It sounds foreign in her mouth, like she's trying on a coat that doesn't quite fit, but one she knows is hers. Of course it's awkward; her last solid memory of me is as her 16-year-old classmate, her friend. She knows, intellectually, that I'm her father from an ancient, erased timeline, but this is the first time she's acting on it. It’s weird for her, but I can see in her eyes that it also feels, strangely, right.\n\nThe word hits me with the force of a physical blow.\n\nDad.\n\nShe’s never called me that. My own memories are a tangled mess. I'm staring at the 16-year-old girl I played Magic with at lunch, but I'm also seeing the faint, ghost-memory of a little girl I held in my arms in a reality that was deleted eons ago.\n\nShe's my daughter. Hannah. She's my friend. Whitney. I'm 46. She's 16. We're on a space station staring at a black hole that's also a prison.\n\nIt’s strange. It’s really fucking weird.\n\nShe rushes forward and embraces me, and the hug is just as awkward and hesitant as I feel. It’s not the easy comfort of a daughter who’s known you her whole life; it's the confused, desperate hug of a friend who's just been told she's your daughter.\n\nYeah, this is going to take some work. Some getting used to. But as I hug her back, feeling her solid and real in my arms, I know we'll eventually get there. It’s an awkward, hesitant hug, but it’s real.\n\n"It's okay," she says. "You're safe. We're all safe."\n\nA groan comes from the bed next to mine. I break the embrace and look over.\n\nXavier.\n\nHe’s pale, his hair matted, but he's here. He’s not a burst of light. He wasn't... he's here.\n\n"Hey, Dad," he mumbles, his eyes fluttering open.\n\nAll the cosmic weight, the confusion, the paradox of Hannah—it all vanishes, replaced by a surge of pure, staggering relief.\n\n"Hey, buddy."\n\nXavier struggles to sit up, his eyes wide as he takes in the viewport. "Dad... where are we? This looks like... a sci-fi movie. Are we on a space station?"\n\n"I think so," I said, pointing with a shaky hand toward the swirling vortex in the blackness. "And that... that's Etheris. We're outside."\n\nWhitney nodded, stepping forward. "We are. They call this place 'Elysium' It's a monitoring station. A base. It's… the Resistance's headquarters, I guess."\n\nElysium.\n\nThe name hits me like a ton of bricks. The towers. The eternal engine. The earth ring from a few resets ago.\n\nIt was all real and I was just manifesting it all inside Etheris.\n\nI looked from the flying vehicles outside to the advanced medical tech in the room. "I don't get it," I said. "Lady Illkeserod, Chernobog, Hecate... they're old gods. Magic. I was expecting... I don't know, a different realm, maybe. Castles. Not... this."\n\n"Yeah, that part's weird," Whitney explained, her voice carrying the confidence of someone who'd had a few weeks to get the briefing. "Magic is real, but so is this. Lady Illkeserod explained that pastoral realities exist, but so do hyper-advanced technological ones, and realities where, I don't know, energy is matter and matter is popcorn. It's 'convergent evolution.' Turns out computers and high technology are just as universal as magic. This station wasn't just built; it was... assembled. Over eternities. By all the different, folks on the outside here. It's the best way to monitor the prison."\n\nShe smiled, that familiar, awkward smile. "Anyway, she's waiting. Mom's been up for a while, and she's really, really wanted to see you."\n\nMom.\n\nJamie. My Jamie. She's here. She's awake. She's waiting. The need to see her is a physical ache.\n\n"I have to..." I try to swing my legs out of the bed. The moment my feet touch the floor, my muscles immediately fail, locking up in protest. I don't just stumble; I crash, my legs folding under me. I pitch forward, catching myself on the edge of the metal bed frame with my face.\n\nA sharp, hot pain explodes from my nose, followed by the immediate, metallic taste of blood.\n\n"Fucking hell!" I groan, rolling onto my back and pinching the bridge of my nose as it starts to gush. "What's wrong with me? I mean, it's been a few weeks." I glare at the advanced tech around the room. "And if this place is so damn advanced with 'technomancy' or whatever, why can't they just magic all this away? This is ridiculous."\n\n"Easy, Dad!" Whitney says, rushing over but not trying to haul me up. She taps the panel on the wall. "You've been in a medically-induced coma. Your muscles are shot." She speaks into the panel, "We need assistance in here." Then she looks back at me, a small, worried smile on her face. "Let me call and get you guys some wheelchairs for now. We can't have you cracking your head open before Mom even gets to see you."\n\n"A few weeks? Dad…" Xavier groans. He rubs his eyes, then his gaze lands on Whitney. He looks at me, completely bewildered. "Who are you?"\n\nWhitney stands up and offers a small, awkward wave. "Hi. I'm... Whitney. I guess I'm your older sister?"\n\nXavier's jaw drops. He looks from her, to me on the floor, and back to her. "My... sister? Dad, what is she talking about?"\n\nI manage a pained nod from the floor, my nose still throbbing. "It's a long story, buddy. But... yeah. She is. It fits."\n\nWhitney and Xavier stare at each other for a beat, two strangers who just found out they're family. Whitney tentatively steps forward. "So... hi. Brother." They exchange a brief, clumsy, and profoundly awkward hug.\n\nWhitney smiles and taps a panel on the wall. "I'll let the nurses know you're up. They'll get us some wheelchairs. Mom is really anxious to see you."	0	551	f	2026-02-27 12:31:41.651453-05	2026-02-28 23:36:55.521137-05	normal	\N
538	132	2	The Council and Reunion	Stephen and Xavier are wheeled to a conference room where they're reunited with Jamie and introduced to a council of divine beings—Lady Illkeserod, Chernobog, Hecate, and Tiamat. They learn about the ongoing rescue operations, the cost of their escape, and the fates of their friends. Stephen and Jamie share an intimate reunion, discovering their separation and love powered the prison itself.	**********\n\nThe futuristic corridor was as white and sterile as the room, a stark contrast to the organic horrors of the substrate. The nurses pushed us at a brisk, silent pace, with Whitney walking beside my wheelchair, her presence a strange, comforting paradox.\n\n"So, what happened to you?" I asked Xavier in the chair next to mine. "After... you know. The tavern."\n\nXavier stared at his hands, his voice quiet. "I... I popped. That's the only word for it. One second she... she was...", he swallowed, unable to finish the thought. "The next, I was just... somewhere else. A different place. It was so dark, Dad. And cold. Just... rocks and dead planets and... nothing. I’ve never been so scared. I thought I was alone forever." He looked at me, his eyes still holding the shadow of that terror. "Then... he found me. Chernobog. He said he was a friend."\n\n"He is," a new voice said. We had arrived at a large, circular conference room. The doors slid open, revealing the group inside.\n\nBefore I could ask Xavier anything else, Whitney spoke up. "Dad, what about you? The last thing we heard was static from inside pyramid. We thought... we thought we'd lost everyone."\n\nI looked at her, then at Xavier, my new-old family. "I remember the fall. The substrate. It was... a void. Darker than black. And I heard it... this mad, endless piping ."\n\nThe nurses stopped, and I pushed myself up from the chair, my legs shaky but working.\n\n"I was unmade," I continued, walking into the room. "I was just... a spark. But then I saw her. Another spark. A tiny point of light in all that darkness. I knew it was her."\n\nA woman across the room looked up. Her hair was dark, her eyes impossibly deep. She wasn't the sixteen-year-old girl from the reset. She wasn't the demonic entity. She was just... her.\n\nJamie.\n\nShe ran. I didn't even meet her halfway. She crashed into me with a sob that felt like it had been held in for an eternity. I buried my face in her hair, breathing in the scent of peaches and vanilla that I thought I’d lost forever. She was solid. Real. We were whole.\n\n"I finally understood," I whispered into her hair. "In the dark. I saw it. Us. We were the battery. Our separation... our love... that was the power source holding the whole damned prison together ."\n\n"We chose it," she whispered back, her voice thick with tears. "To trap him. We chose to forget."\n\n"I know."\n\nWe held each other for a long time, two halves of a circuit finally made complete. I was acutely aware of how we were complete—she felt divine, elegant, and perfect in my arms, and I was still in my 46-year-old, "old man fat" body.\n\nShe pulled back just enough to look at me, a mischievous, highly charged giggle escaping her lips.\n\n"Why are you still using this form?" she chided, running a hand lightly over my stomach in a way that was both an appraisal and a promise. "We're outside the prison, Stephen. You can be... anything."\n\nShe leaned in, her lips brushing my ear. "Don't worry," she whispered, her voice dripping with a sweetness I had never knew I missed. "I'll have to teach you a few things about bodies... later."\n\n"Ahem." Benji’s voice, now warm and blessedly familiar, broke the silence.\n\nWe separated, though my hand never left Jamie’s. I looked at the council assembled around a large holographic table, which currently displayed the swirling, angry vortex of Etheris.\n\nLady Illkeserod and Lady Osilari were there, their faces etched with relief. Benji stood beside them, looking no worse for wear. But there were three new figures. One was a man who seemed to be carved from pure shadow, his form shifting at the edges. Another was a woman who radiated an ancient, primal chaos that smelled of salt and the deep ocean. The last was a woman draped in veils, who seemed to stand at a crossroads I couldn't see.\n\n"The surge from your reunion was... significant," Lady Illkeserod said, a rare smile touching her lips. "It didn't just break you out. It created a brand new null-reality, your own new universe, a bubble in the void. That's where we found you, floating, unconscious. Not to mention, you blew a temporary, unstable hole in the prison wall."\n\nThe man of shadows, Chernobog, spoke. "Your surge was the key. It weakened the barriers, allowing us—and others—to finally create our own extraction points. Brief... and dangerous... but effective."\n\nHecate, the veiled woman, glided forward. Her voice was a sibilant whisper, like dry leaves skittering over stone. "The process is not simple. We cannot just line up our realms and open a door. Each extraction must be precise, surgical. We must identify the divine spark, isolate it, and pull it through before any NPCs, the corrupted prison clones, can alert Yaldabaoth. We've had to... terminate... millions of corrupted clones who tried to follow."\n\n"It's a shit-show," Tiamat rumbled, the chaotic energy rolling off her making the air thrum. "Every time we open a window, we risk our own realms. We risk becoming a vector for Yaldabaoth's disease to spread. My primordial chaos has suffered the heaviest losses from these incursions, but we push forward." She bared teeth that were entirely too sharp. "It's why we meet every extraction with a shit-ton of firepower, just in case something else decides to break through."\n\nChernobog nodded at Xavier, his shadow-form absorbing the light in the room. "The boy was lucky. He was ejected into my home realm. It's been dead and empty since Yaldabaoth infected it eons ago—a perfect, sterile staging ground. He was safe... but alone."\n\n"So you're rescuing people?" I asked. "You're pulling them out?"\n\n"We are," Hecate, the woman of veils, confirmed.\n\nI felt a surge of desperate hope. "My friends. The ones from the high school... from the reset. Are they...?"\n\n"They are safe," Lady Illkeserod confirmed. "Gil, Becca, Andrew, James, Patrick, and Morgan. We flagged them as 'real souls' and retrieved them. They're currently in quarantine."\n\n"Quarantine?"\n\n"We must be certain," Tiamat, the woman of chaos, rumbled. "We cannot allow any part of Yaldabaoth to hitch a ride."\n\nMy heart ached. "And the others? TJ? The Fae... Maeve and Elara?"\n\nBenji looked down, his expression pained. "They died inside Etheris, Stephen. Their sparks were consumed by Yaldabaoth. They're... part of him now. We can't get them back."\n\nI nodded, absorbing the loss. Another price paid.\n\nBut I knew this was only partially true. Yaldabaoth consumed, yes, but he was never able to fully consume a divine spark. If he could, he'd be able to truly create, not just make twisted clones. Every reset, those sparks that were once part of him dissipated back... somewhere... deep in his essence, where he eventually cloned them again from his Adam.\n\nMy children, Xavier and Hannah, were thought lost forever, yet here they were. So maybe, eventually, TJ, Elara, and Maeve weren't gone for good. Maybe we'd find a way to pull their sparks out once and for all.\n\nI looked at Jamie, then back at the council. "My NPC wife. The construct. What happened to her?"\n\n"A unique case," Lady Illkeserod said. "When you destroyed her, you were outside the prison, in the Akashic Realm. Her programming was shattered, but her divine spark was freed. She wasn't consumed. She is... out there. Somewhere in the infinite realities. Free."\n\nI let out a breath I didn't know I was holding. "Good. I'm glad she's free."\n\n"The prison is still standing," Benji said, "but without its power source—you two—it's unstable. We don't know what happens next."\n\n"That is our fight now," Chernobog said, his shadow-form solidifying. "We will handle the rescues. We will monitor the prison."\n\nLady Illkeserod put a hand on my shoulder. "For now, Stephen, Jamie... you have done more than enough. Go. Rest. Heal. Be the family you were never allowed to be."	1	552	t	2026-02-27 12:31:41.651453-05	2026-02-28 23:37:48.544408-05	normal	\N
539	132	3	Piecing Together a Family	Stephen's family settles into a comfortable apartment on the ring city, beginning the difficult process of bonding as a unit. Hannah reclaims her original name and shares her journey of self-discovery through different identities. Xavier seeks entertainment, and through a holographic call with David (the creator of Roblox), they connect him back online. The scene ends with Stephen affirming his family's future together.	**********\n\nWe spent the next few days in a comfortable living area, a quiet apartment carved out of the ring city's massive superstructure. It was the first time in... lifetimes... that I’d felt a sense of peace. The viewport looked out onto the endless, silent drift of space, the angry black hole of Etheris a constant, distant reminder of what we'd escaped.\n\nWe were a family, but a new one, piecing ourselves together from fragments.\n\n"So... you're my sister?" Xavier asked, poking Whitney in the arm as she sat on the couch.\n\nWhitney, who was still processing that her 16-year-old classmate was her 46-year-old father, looked just as bewildered. "I guess so. And Jamie... you're my mom?"\n\nJamie, who had been curled up against my side, smiled. "It's... a lot, I know. It's confusing for us, too."\n\nBoth Whitney and Xavier remembered their "most recent lives" vividly. Xavier remembered our home, his 'NPC' mother, and the escape. Hannah remembered her entire high school life, the abusive parents who had disowned her for being herself. But before that? Nothing. The eternities spent as consumed souls, the ancient lives they'd lived before everything... all of it was just a blank, mercifully erased by Yaldabaoth.\n\n"You guys... you keep almost calling me something else," Whitney said, looking at me and Jamie. "Not Whitney. Something... 'Han...'"\n\nI looked at Jamie, and she nodded, her eyes soft.\n\n"Hannah," I said, the name feeling profoundly right on my tongue. "In a... in a life from a very long time ago, before all of this, that was your name. Your original name. The one we gave you."\n\nWhitney—Hannah—was quiet for a long moment. She tested the name. "Hannah."\n\nWhitney’s eyes grew distant as she started remembering. "The first thing I remember from inside was how much I hated everything." She practically spat, a bitter shadow crossing her face, a deep, visceral rejection. She wouldn't even say the word. "That... that was never me."\n\n"It was the wrong body, the wrong life," she continued, her voice shaking, "with parents who... God, they were just so abusive. They called me…." She stumbled, the poison refusing to come out. She looked to me for help.\n\nI said it quietly, mostly for Jamie and Xavier, so they could understand the context. "William."\n\nThe name felt like vomit in my mouth. I didn't want to say it; it was her dead name, a monument to a life that had tried to destroy her. But it came out, because it was a part of what made had allowed her to become herself.\n\nA bitter shadow crossed her face, a deep, visceral rejection of the name. "That... that was never me."\n\n"So I escaped," she continued, her voice quiet. "I chose to be 'Whitney.' I thought it would keep me safe. It was close to my grandmother's name, Wilhelmina. I thought maybe they wouldn't notice, or it would be... easier for them. But it never quite fit. It always felt like a shield. Like I was still hiding."\n\n"I even had another name, a secret one I kept just for me." Her eyes met mine—her high school friend, her father. "You knew. I told you. Jennifer."\n\nHer gaze shifted to Jamie, and a new, dawning understanding washed over her face. "I... I never realized." Her voice was soft with wonder. "'Jennifer'... 'Jamie'... It's so close. It's like I was... I was reaching for something I could feel but didn't know I'd lost. Reaching for you, Mom."\n\nShe tested the new name, her voice soft. "But... 'Hannah'..." A small, real smile touched her lips as she tasted the sound. "It doesn't feel like hiding. And it doesn't feel stolen or secret. It was... it was given to me. Birthed from love." She looked at both of us, her eyes shining. "It feels... it feels right. Like it's finally mine."\n\nShe smiled, a real, unguarded smile. "I like it. I think... I'd like to be Hannah from now on."\n\nXavier, ever the teenager, had a more pressing concern. He’d been exploring the apartment's interface, his frustration growing.\n\n"This is all great," Xavier said, looking around the comfortable apartment, "but... are there any video games here? I mean, what do people do?"\n\nHannah laughed. "Video games? Like... Super Nintendo? I think I saw someone setting one up somewhere. That’s all I remember from... you know. Back in the 1990’s in Etheris."\n\n"No," Xavier said, looking confused. "I mean... online. Like Roblox? Discord?"\n\n"Rob... lox?" Hannah looked completely blank. "Is that an internet thing? I never really got that."\n\n"It's an online platform," I said. "A whole universe of games."\n\n"Oh!" Hannah’s face lit up, catching on. "I bet we can find that. Rescued souls are showing up here every day. They're all trying to rebuild the lives they lost, use all this advanced tech out here to recreate their old lives, clone their digital files. Let's make a call."\n\nShe moved to an interface on the wall, her fingers flying across a console that looked far beyond the tech she remembered. A holographic directory appeared.\n\n"Okay, let's see... 'Rescue Resources'... 'Recreated Software'... ah, here we go. 'Games.'" She scrolled rapidly through a long list. "Roblox... Roblox... here! 'David.' It says he created the platform. Let's try him."\n\nShe tapped his name, and a moment later, a holographic video call shimmered to life. A man with bright, energetic eyes appeared, looking confused.\n\n"Hello? Can I help you? Who are..." His voice trailed off as he looked from Hannah to me, his eyes widening. "Wait. No way. You're... you're Stephen. And Hannah. Oh, wow. Everyone's been talking about you." He suddenly looked a bit star-struck, realizing he was talking to the "royalty" who had just escaped.\n\n"I'm David," he said, still looking a little awestruck at us. "What can I do for you?"\n\nXavier leaned forward, his voice hesitant. "This is going to sound weird, but... do you know anything about a game called Roblox?"\n\nDavid's face broke into a huge grin. "Yeah, that thing? I created it."\n\nXavier’s head whipped around to me, his eyes wide with the first spark of genuine, teenage hope I’d seen in him since this nightmare began. "No way... you're the David? The David?" He turned back to the hologram, his voice suddenly shy. "Hey, so... can you, you know, get me hooked up? Get me back online?"\n\nDavid laughed. "One and the same. And kid, you're in luck. It took some doing, but we've been using the Akashic Realm—Lady Illkeserod's tech is amazing—to create a secure virtual network connection back into Etheris. We're pulling what we can from the 'old internet' inside."\n\n"The Infinitrons—these computers out here that have near-infinite processing speed and memory—are miracle workers at cracking firewalls. They can crack any encryption. They've been able to access parts of the 'old internet' inside... stuff I've never even heard of, like a deep dark Mariana Web. It's making for easy work cloning everything out here.”\n\n“Not only that, but we've already pulled most of the original Roblox team out, and we've got a complete clone of the servers running out here, including all the server data from inside. Your account should be right where you left it. Someone has even got Discord clone up and running."\n\n"That's... that's amazing," Xavier stammered.\n\nHe looked at me, his face alight. "Dad. Can we? Please?"\n\nI looked at my family. My real family, finally together. My son, Xavier, cynical and scarred but still just a kid who wanted to play his games. My daughter, Hannah, finally free and rediscovering her own name. And Jamie, my anchor, my partner, the literal center of my universe, sitting beside me, her hand in mine.\n\nDespite all the fuckiness, despite the wars and the loss and the realities we’d burned, this moment was perfect.\n\n"Yeah, buddy," I said, pulling him into a one-armed hug. "Absolutely. We can."	2	553	t	2026-02-27 12:31:41.651453-05	2026-02-28 23:38:57.385934-05	normal	\N
540	133	1	The Rhythm of Rescue	Stephen observes the ongoing rescue operations at Elysium and confronts the impossible mathematics of saving souls from the prison. He and Jamie discuss the weight of insufficient progress as Benji arrives with news of an extraction.	Elysium had a rhythm now. I could feel it in the ground beneath my feet... the pulse of souls arriving, the hum of quarantine processing, the quiet prayers of the rescued as they learned they weren't crazy. That the prison was real. That they'd been rescued.\n\nThree months since my arrival. Three months of watching the Resistance pull people out, one awakened soul at a time.\n\nThree months of pretending it was enough.\n\nI stood at an observation portal, watching another transport dock. Twelve souls this time. A good haul. Jamie leaned against the rail beside me, her vanilla-and-peach scent somehow cutting through the recycled air.\n\n"How many today?" she asked.\n\n"Twelve."\n\n"That's good."\n\nI didn't answer. Good was relative. Twelve souls out of trillions. Twelve drops from an ocean that kept filling faster than we could bail.\n\nJamie's hand found mine. "You're doing that thing again."\n\n"What thing?"\n\n"The thing where you calculate impossible odds and try to solve them."\n\nI almost smiled. Almost. "The numbers don't work, Jamie. They never have. We pulled out 12 today. You know how many new kids were born in there? 362,737"\n\nShe squeezed my hand. "They don't have to work perfectly. They just have to work enough."\n\nBut that was the problem, wasn't it? Enough had never been enough. Not for me. Not for the people still trapped in there, dreaming their scripted dreams while Yaldabaoth bred new souls to replace the ones we saved.\n\nThe door behind us hissed open. Benji stepped through, his cardigan somehow immaculate despite all the chaos. Behind him came Lady Illkeserod, her presence filling the room like gravity.\n\n"Stephen. Jamie. We need you in the briefing room."\n\nSomething in Benji's voice made my stomach tighten. Not fear exactly. Something older. Recognition maybe.\n\n"What happened?"\n\n"We extracted someone." Benji's expression flickered. "Someone you know."	0	554	f	2026-02-27 12:32:10.366732-05	2026-02-28 23:40:25.261074-05	normal	\N
541	133	2	The Broken Oracle	Stephen encounters Todd, a former enemy from a previous reset, now shattered and insane but carrying crucial information about a cosmic seed hidden at the heart of the eternal engine. Todd reveals the seed's location and potential.	**********\n\nThe briefing room was smaller than I expected. Round table. Dim lights. The kind of place where secrets went to breathe before dying.\n\nLady Osilari was already there, her symbols tattooed across her skin glowing faintly in the low light. She nodded as we entered.\n\nAcross from her sat a shriveled man in tattered robes, but looking at him made my brain hurt.\n\nA flash hit me. Fast. Sharp. Red hats marching in hot wind. That chant the organic constructs used… the one that made the ground vibrate. A street full of empty husks that used to be people. The air tasting like copper and fire.\n\nI flinched. Just a little.\n\nThe room fell back into itself.\n\nTodd.\n\nExcept Todd wasn't quite the right word anymore. The man at the table was Todd's shape, Todd's face... but something had broken behind his eyes. Something fundamental. He rocked slightly, muttering to himself, fingers tracing patterns on the metal surface.\n\n"Organic," Todd whispered. "Organic. Organic. The flesh remembers. The blood remembers. The seed remembers."\n\nJamie tensed beside me. "Is he...?"\n\n"Completely insane," Benji finished. "Yes. But there's method in it. There always is with the mad ones."\n\nI moved closer, memories flooding back. Todd in another life. Another reset. White-trash rage and borrowed power, leading an army of organic Luddites across the cosmos against the technology civilization I'd built. The Red Hats, they'd called themselves. Rejecting technology. Worshipping the organic. Worshipping Yaldabaoth. Burning technologists like funeral pyres.\n\nAnd he'd been killed for it.\n\nBut Yaldabaoth hadn't consumed his soul after he died. Not fully. Instead, it seeped through the reset, it merged Todd's essence with something else... divine memories, cosmic truths, the weight of things mortals weren't meant to carry. And when that reset collapsed, Todd's consciousness had survived, scattered across the substrate like bloodied shrapnel.\n\nNow he sat here, rebuilt, broken, whispering secrets to the table.\n\n"Todd," I said softly. "Do you remember me?"\n\nTodd's head snapped up. His eyes focused, just for a second, and I saw recognition there. Recognition and something worse.\n\nTerror.\n\n"You," Todd breathed. "The dreamer. The builder. You made the towers. You made the machines. You made the eternal engine that..." He laughed, high and brittle. "That's where we found it. That's where we found the seed."\n\nLady Illkeserod leaned forward. "What seed, Todd?"\n\n"The cosmic seed!" His voice rose, cracking. "At the heart of the eternal engine. Potential energy. Compressed. Infinite density. We were going to use it. Power the organic revolution. But then..." His hands trembled. "Then everything ended. The reset. The reset. And the seed was ejected. Lost. Floating."\n\nMy pulse quickened. "Ejected where?"\n\n"Out." Todd's finger pointed upward, then everywhere, nowhere. "Beyond the prison. Into the spaces between. It's out there still. Pulling. Always pulling. Like a black hole that forgot how to collapse."	1	555	f	2026-02-27 12:32:10.366732-05	2026-02-28 23:41:05.773463-05	normal	\N
