545	133	6	Warded and Ready	Eleven days of intense preparation: Lady Osilari paints protective symbols onto the team, Benji studies schematics of the prison, and Stephen and Jamie spend sleepless nights confronting the impossible task ahead. Final goodbyes and a leap through the portal into the unknown.	**********\n\nThe preparations took eleven days.\n\nLady Osilari worked tirelessly, painting symbols across our skin with ink made from ground starlight and melted realities. Each mark burned going on. Each one settled into something that felt less like protection and more like armor.\n\nBenji studied maps of Etheris that shouldn’t exist. Schematics pulled from his time as auditor, showing the prison's layers, its substrates, its infinite recursions. He marked a path through the chaos. A route to the core, marked celestial throne, that avoided the worst of the corruption.\n\nProbably.\n\nMaybe.\n\nWe'd find out.\n\nJamie and I spent the nights together, not sleeping, just holding each other in the dark. Sometimes we talked. Mostly we didn't. What was there to say? I love you? I'm scared? Don't let me get lost in there?\n\nAll of it true. None of it helpful.\n\nOn the tenth night, she whispered, "Do you think Sam wants to be saved?"\n\nI didn't answer right away. Couldn't. Because that was the question, wasn't it? The one I'd been avoiding.\n\n"I don't know," I said finally. "Maybe he just wants it to end. Maybe he's been begging for death for so long that offering him freedom feels like cruelty."\n\n"And if that's true?"\n\n"Then we give him what he needs. Not what we think he deserves."\n\nShe was quiet for a long time after that.\n\nOn the eleventh day, we stood at the portal chamber.\n\nThe portal hung open… wider than it should. Colors that didn’t belong to any sane spectrum kept sliding over each other, like they were arguing about how to exist.\n\nA sharp scent hit me. Burned mathematics. Fading hope. Something brittle underneath it all.\n\nJamie whispered, "This isn't her realm."\n\n"It is," I said. "She just pushed the doorway far past her borders. Out into the infinite wastes. Deep into the fringes of undiscovered wisdom, into the edges where knowledge has yet to be discovered. If anything goes wrong… it dies out there."\n\nXavier and Hannah stood at the back of the chamber. Neither had really spoken to me since the briefing. But as I moved toward the portal, Xavier called out.\n\n"Dad."\n\nI turned.\n\nHe didn't say anything else. Just... looked at me. His eyes red. His jaw tight.\n\nI understood.\n\n"I'll come back," I said.\n\n"You better."\n\nHannah stepped forward, hugging me quickly, fiercely. Then Jamie. Then she stepped back, wiping her eyes.\n\n"Kick his ass," she said.\n\nI almost laughed. "Which one?"\n\n"Both of them."\n\nJamie took my hand. Lady Osilari and Benji moved to flank us.\n\nThe portal beckoned.\n\nI took one last look at Elysium. At the rescued souls moving through their new lives. At my children standing together, holding each other up.\n\nAt everything we were fighting for.\n\nThen I stepped through into the teeth of the god.	5	559	f	2026-02-27 12:32:10.366732-05	2026-02-28 23:44:22.238206-05	normal	\N
546	133	7	The Sealed Door	The team emerges in the Akashic Realm and discovers the portal into Etheris has been sealed—Yaldabaoth has locked the prison from inside. Facing an impossible barrier, they must make a desperate decision to call upon Nyarlathotep for help.	**********\n\nThe Akashic Realm looked different than I remembered.\n\nOr maybe I looked different. Hard to tell. The space between realities doesn't care much about perspective or memory or anything that makes sense to mortal minds. It just... is. Infinite library. Infinite possibility. Every story that ever was or could be, written in light across the void.\n\nWe stood on nothing. Or everything. A platform made of crystallized potential that hummed beneath our feet.\n\nJamie's hand found mine immediately. "Where's the portal?"\n\nGood question.\n\nThe portal from Elysium had dumped us here, out in the infinite expanses of unrealized information. But there should have been another door. Another way through. This was where the barrier between the Akashic Realm and the inside of Etheris was the thinnest.\n\nI looked around. Saw nothing but the endless emptiness of potential Akashic records stretching into forever.\n\nNo door. No path. No way forward.\n\n"Benji?" I turned to find him already pacing, his demon face tight with concentration.\n\n"This doesn't make sense." He moved through the space, hands outstretched, feeling for something I couldn't see. "The portal coordinates were correct. We should be able to cross directly from here into Etheris. It should be thinnest here, not just so we can pull something out, but so we can push something back in."\n\n"But?" Jamie prompted.\n\n"But there's nothing here. It's like..." He paused, face darkening. "It's like something in the prison sealed itself. Locked the doors from the inside again."\n\nLady Osilari knelt, touching the crystallized platform. Her symbols glowed faintly, reading something in the substrate. "The Veil has thickened. Azathoth must know we're coming. He's reinforced the boundary between his reality and everything else."\n\n"Can we break through?" I asked.\n\n"Maybe. Given time. Weeks. Months. But by then..." She stood, brushing imaginary dust from her robes. "By then he'll have adapted further. Made the walls thicker. We'd be fighting an arms race we can't win."\n\nMy stomach sank. We'd planned for resistance. But didn’t think Azathoth was trying to keep us out. At best he’d be trying to help us so he could reclaim his reality as his own and be done with the whole prison experience. But sealing the entrance entirely? That was new.\n\nThat was terrifying.\n\n"So what do we do?" Jamie's voice stayed calm, but I felt her tension through our joined hands. "We can't just go back. The cosmic seed plan doesn't work without separating Sam from the infection."\n\nBenji looked at me.\n\nI knew what he was going to say before he said it.\n\n"No."\n\n"Stephen—"\n\n"Absolutely not. We're not asking him. Not after what he did to you last time."\n\n"Last time I provoked him." Benji's smile was strained. "This time we're asking for help."\n\n"He tried to unmake you. Erase you from existence. You barely survived."\n\n"I know. I was there." Benji's hand touched his chest, where the scars from Nyarlathotep's attack still marked him. Even in the Akashic Realm, some wounds stayed visible. "But he's the only option we have. He’s the closest thing Etheris has to an administrator and really our only way in."\n\nLady Osilari nodded slowly. "The Crawling Chaos has helped us before. He gave us the portal to get Jamie out . The prison didn't collapse , and he must know we're not finished. He must still want to return that reality to its rightful owners, kick out the infection, and free the souls inside. That should count for something."\n\n"It won't count for anything if he kills Benji the moment he shows up."\n\n"Then we make sure he doesn't." Jamie squeezed my hand. "I'll stand between them if I have to. We're gods too, remember? Not as powerful as Nyarlathotep, but powerful enough to make him think twice about starting a fight."\n\nI wanted to argue. Wanted to find another option. Any option that didn't involve calling in the most volatile, unpredictable entity in the outer darkness.\n\nBut there wasn't one.\n\nThe math was simple and brutal. No portal meant no mission. No mission meant the cosmic seed plan failed. The plan failed, and eventually Etheris collapsed into another reset, spreading anti-reality across everything we'd fought to save.\n\n"Fine." I relented. "But we do this carefully. Lady Osilari, can you ward Benji before we call him?"\n\n"Already working on it." Her hands moved in complex patterns, symbols appearing in the air around Benji like armor made of light. "These won't stop Nyarlathotep if he truly wants you dead. But they'll slow him down. Give us time to intervene."\n\nBenji stood still as the wards settled over him. His expression was... peaceful. Resigned maybe. Like a man who'd already accepted his death and decided it wasn't worth worrying about.\n\nI envied that sometimes. His calm in the face of cosmic horror. His ability to smile at the void and mean it.\n\n"How do we call him?" I asked.	6	533	f	2026-02-27 12:32:10.366732-05	2026-02-28 23:45:11.072587-05	normal	\N
548	133	9	Inside the God	The team crosses through the torn portal into Etheris and navigates the prison's horrific substrate—a landscape of crystallized screams, looped torments, and billions of chained souls. They reach the throne room where Yaldabaoth waits, and execute their desperate plan: offering themselves as bait so he will consume them willingly.	**********\n\nThe first thing you notice when crossing back into Etheris is the wrongness.\n\nNot pain exactly. Not fear. Just... wrongness. Like every atom in your body suddenly remembers it's not supposed to exist here. Like reality itself is trying to spit you out while simultaneously pulling you deeper.\n\nThe portal spat us into a corridor that shouldn't exist. Walls made of crystallized screams. Floor that pulsed like a heartbeat. Ceiling that bled upward into infinite darkness.\n\nI'd been here before. Different reset, same hell.\n\nJamie's hand tightened in mine. "I forgot how much I hate this place."\n\n"It gets worse," Benji said cheerfully. He was already moving, consulting maps that existed only in his head. "Follow me. And whatever you do, don't look at the doors."\n\nToo late. I'd already seen them. Thousands of doorways lining the corridor, each one showing a different reality. Different lives. Different versions of us making different choices.\n\nIn one, I saw myself old and grey, holding a grandchild.\n\nIn another, I saw myself dead at age 8, poisoned by my own mother.\n\nIn a third, I was behind the wheel of a cherry-red Corvette, Jamie pressing against me from one side and some other girl nuzzling my neck from behind, both trying to kiss me, right before the car slammed head-on into a concrete wall\n\n"The substrate's defensive response," Lady Osilari said, her symbols glowing brighter. "It's trying to distract you. Fragment your sense of self. Keep moving."\n\nWe moved.\n\nThe corridor twisted. Changed. Became a street I recognized from childhood. Then a hospital pod where I died at the age of 76. Then a bedroom where I'd first kissed Jamie. Then nothing. Just void with teeth.\n\n"It's adapting faster than it should," Benji muttered. "The prison knows we're here."\n\n"Of course it does." My voice sounded hollow in the twisted space. "I killed it once. It remembers."\n\nThe void rippled. Something moved in the darkness ahead. Multiple somethings. Shapes that hurt to look at directly. Shadows with substance. NPCs maybe, or something worse.\n\nThey rushed us.\n\nLady Osilari's hands moved in patterns I couldn't follow. Symbols flared around us, geometric impossibilities that bent space into protective shells. The shadows hit the wards and... stopped. Not destroyed. Just confused. Like they'd forgotten what they were attacking.\n\n"Keep moving," she said through gritted teeth. "I can't hold this forever."\n\nWe ran.\n\nThe corridor became a staircase. The staircase became a ladder. The ladder became a throat, pulsing and wet, trying to swallow us down into something's digestive system.\n\n"This isn't the way," I said.\n\n"It's the only way." Benji's voice was strained. "The prison's rewriting itself around us. Trying to trap us in loops. We have to push through."\n\nSo we pushed.\n\nThrough layers of broken realities. Through rooms where past lives played on repeat, same conversations, same deaths, same empty cycles. Through spaces where NPCs screamed in eternal loops, their anguish powering something deeper, something hungry.\n\nI saw a woman giving birth over and over, the child dying each time, her grief resetting to fresh horror with every cycle.\n\nI saw a man being executed, his last words cut short, rewound, repeated until the words lost all meaning.\n\nI saw children playing in a park that slowly dissolved into ash, their laughter turning to confusion turning to terror turning back to laughter as the loop reset.\n\n"These are his NPC worshippers," Jamie whispered. "The ones who were programmed and died believing. He's keeping them conscious. Making them suffer to... to..."\n\n"To try and awaken their divine sparks." I felt sick. "He doesn’t just want NPCs he wants them to be awake and aware of what’s going on."\n\nThis was the reward for blind faith. Eternal conscious torment disguised as paradise, each soul slowly refined into the perfect meal.\n\nWe kept moving. Had to. Stopping meant thinking too hard about what we were seeing. Thinking meant breaking. Breaking meant joining the loops.\n\nThe throat-corridor opened into something vast. A chamber that stretched up and out forever. And at the center...\n\nThe throne.\n\nGod, the throne.\n\nIt sat at the center of everything, raised on a dais made of compressed suffering. The throne itself was... I don't have words. Imagine furniture made from people. Living people. Conscious people. Their bodies woven together, faces pressing against each other in expressions of permanent horror, mouths open in screams that had no sound, eyes that tracked our movement with desperate, pleading awareness.\n\nThese weren't NPCs. These were real souls. Not just any NPCs, but awakened souls. Souls our resistance would try and extract. Folks with a newly realized divine spark who'd died worshipping Yaldabaoth, expecting paradise, receiving this instead.\n\nChained to the throne's base were more. Billions of them. Trillions maybe. Each one trapped in their own personal loop of torment. A woman being flayed alive by her lover over and over. A man watching himself kill his children repeatedly. A child being left in a dumpster by their parents in infinite variations.\n\n"He's strengthening their divine sparks," Lady Osilari whispered, her face pale. "Refining them through suffering. Making them more aware, more conscious, so when he finally consumes them..."\n\n"They taste better." The words made me sick.\n\n"Welcome."\n\nThe voice came from everywhere and nowhere. The throne room rippled, and he appeared sitting at the throne.\n\nYaldabaoth rose from his throne of souls, their screams intensifying as his weight lifted.\n\n"You again," he said, and his voice was honey poured over razor blades. "You worms. You insects. You insignificant sparks of consciousness that refuse to know their place." He moved closer, each step making reality crack at the edges. "I've consumed you before, dreamer. Torn you apart and digested you slowly. Made you scream for eternities while I savored every fragment of your divine essence."\n\nHe was right. I remembered it. The first glitched reset that led us all here. Being swallowed whole. Dissolved. Unmade piece by piece while the god laughed.\n\nI remembered it and didn't run. Because he forgets what I did once I found myself inside.\n\n"And what's this?" Yaldabaoth's gaze shifted to Jamie, to Benji, to Lady Osilari. "You brought me even more tempting treats. A twin soul ripe with power. A demon who thinks he's clever. A goddess whose symbols mean nothing in my domain." His smile widened, showing teeth that went back too far. "How thoughtful. How generous. How perfectly, deliciously foolish."\n\nArchons emerged from the walls. Pieces of Yaldabaoth's will given semi-autonomous form. They circled us like sharks, hungry, patient, waiting for the command to feed.\n\nThis was it. The moment.\n\nI looked at Lady Osilari. She nodded once. Understood.\n\n"We're not here to fight you," I said.\n\nYaldabaoth paused. Tilted his head. "No?"\n\n"No. We're here to accept your invitation."\n\nConfusion flickered across his stolen face. "What invitation?"\n\n"You said you wanted to consume us. So..." I spread my arms. "Here we are."\n\n"Stephen," Jamie said quietly. "Are you sure about this?"\n\n"No. But when has that ever stopped us?"\n\nLady Osilari's hands moved fast. Faster than thought. Symbols erupted around us, complex geometries that wove through space and settled on our skin like armor made of condensed meaning. Wards. Protections. Patterns that would hold our consciousness together even as we were torn apart.\n\nProbably.\n\nMaybe.\n\nWe'd find out.\n\n"What are you doing?" Yaldabaoth's smile faded to something colder. Suspicious.\n\n"Gambling," Benji said cheerfully. "We're very good at it."\n\nYaldabaoth’s form expanded, becoming less old man in a robe and more void, a shifting mass of darkness and teeth. He didn't lunge. He... approached. He was cautious, wary. This was a trap, it had to be.\n\nHe moved slowly, languidly, like a predator with all the time in the world. He tasted the air around us, his essence brushing against Lady Osilari's glowing wards. We didn't run. We didn't fight. We stood there, holding hands, wrapped in symbols of power, and waited.\n\nThe chained souls screamed warnings. The archons hesitated, confused by prey that wouldn't flee.\n\nA tendril of Yaldabaoth's anti-reality, black and glistening, snaked out. It bypassed me and Jamie, touching the edge of Lady Osilari's ward. The ward sizzled, and a small part of her robe dissolved into light.\n\nYaldabaoth inhaled the flavor of the dissolving ward.\n\nHis entire form shuddered. A sound that was half-groan, half-ecstasy rumbled through the substrate. He had tasted the divine energy, the power, the flavor. It was pure. It was real.\n\nA tremor ran through Yaldabaoth's entire form, a ripple of exquisite pleasure so intense it was almost a release. The taste of the divine energy, so pure and so potent, was overwhelming. A deep, shuddering moan seemed to emanate from his very essence as he savored the flavor. This was not a trap. This was a feast\n\nHis caution vanished, replaced by a frenzied, desperate hunger. He was like a wild beast, high on catnip, who had just realized the meal was real. He roared, and this time, he did lunge—not to swallow, but to devour.\n\nHe tore at us, his attacks slamming against Osilari's wards. Pieces of the symbolic armor shattered, dissolving into his essence. He reveled in the taste, his frenzy growing. He was ripping at us, trying to get pieces of us, no longer caring about the consequences.\n\nBut the wards were holding. He couldn't get through. The pieces he could get were not enough. Never enough.\n\nThis... this was taking too long. The anticipation was too much. The meal was right there.\n\nYaldabaoth's confusion turned to rage, turned to a hunger so vast it couldn't be questioned. He pulled back, his form swelling, darkness blotting out the twisted light of the throne room.\n\nHis jaws opened wide enough to swallow stars.\n\nHe swallowed us whole.\n\n**********\n\nDarkness.\n\nComplete. Total. Absolute darkness that pressed in from all sides.\n\nI couldn't see. Couldn't hear. Couldn't feel anything except...\n\nJamie's hand in mine.\n\nStill there. Still solid. Still real.\n\n"Everyone okay?" My voice sounded muffled. Wrong. Like speaking through water.\n\n"Define okay." Jamie's response came from my left. Close. Thank god.\n\n"The wards are holding." Lady Osilari's voice, calmer than it had any right to be. "We're inside his essence. Inside the god himself. But we're staying together. Staying intact."\n\n"Where's Benji?"\n\n"Here." His voice came from somewhere ahead. "And you're all going to want to see this."\n\nLight bloomed. Faint at first, then stronger. Lady Osilari's symbols providing illumination in the absolute dark.\n\nWe stood on... nothing. Or maybe everything. Hard to describe the substrate of a god's consciousness. The space around us was vast and close simultaneously. Walls made of digested memories. Floor that pulsed with stolen lives. Ceiling that bled upward into infinite hunger.\n\nThis was the inside of Yaldabaoth. The truth beneath the throne room. The digestive system of something that had forgotten it was ever anything but appetite.\n\n"We're actually inside him," Jamie breathed. "We're actually... how is this possible?"\n\n"The wards." Lady Osilari's symbols glowed brighter, more urgent. "They're confusing his essence. Making us indigestible. But they won't last forever. The anti-reality is already trying to corrode them. We need to move. Need to find Sam's core before—"\n\nThe space around us convulsed.\n\nBut it wasn't the panic of a body rejecting a foreign object. It was a shudder of ecstasy. Yaldabaoth was savoring us.\n\nLady Osilari's wards were holding our core essence intact, but stray, insignificant pieces—a thread from my shirt, a strand of Jamie's hair, a flake of skin—were being dissolved. And he could taste them.\n\nA deep, reverberating moan rumbled through his essence, a wave of pure, sensual pleasure so profound it made the substrate tremble. He was in the throes of delight. Each tiny morsel, each dissolved thread, was a burst of flavor he hadn't experienced in eons, and it was driving him mad with pleasure.\n\nThe first wave hit us as a crushing pressure, as if his delight were a physical force.\n\n"The wards are holding," Lady Osilari grunted, her symbols flaring.\n\nThen another convulsion, stronger this time, an orgasmic ripple of pure consumption. He'd found another piece. Another taste. The pleasure was building, and its effects were intensifying. The very walls of his essence began to press in, the anti-reality working faster to dissolve us, to get more.\n\n"He... he likes this," Benji said, his voice tight with horror. "He's not trying to crush us. He's trying to savor us."\n\nAnother wave, more powerful than the last, slammed into our protective bubble. I felt the wards sizzle, the symbols corroding faster under the ecstatic assault. Yaldabaoth had realized something was right—that this was the best meal he'd ever had—and now his entire being was converging on our position, trying to break the wards, to crush us, to dissolve us, to unmake us, not out of malice, but out of a desperate, overwhelming hunger.\n\n"Which way?" I shouted over the reverberating moans of cosmic pleasure that rumbled through the very essence of the god.\n\nBenji pointed deeper into the darkness. "Down. Always down. Sam's not in the upper layers. He's at the very center. At the heart of all this."\n\n"Then let's go." Jamie's hand tightened in mine. "Before he... finishes... and absorbs us completely."\n\nWe ran into the god's heart, racing against the ecstatic hunger that would never stop until it consumed everything.\n\nEven itself.	8	562	f	2026-02-27 12:32:10.366732-05	2026-02-28 23:46:50.752935-05	normal	\N
549	134	1	The Descent into Yaldabaoth's Heart	The group reaches the vast chamber at the center of the corrupted god's corpse and discovers Sam suspended and being used as a host by the anti-reality parasite. They fight their way through tentacle attacks as Stephen reaches Sam, triggering a trance state that pulls him into Sam's internal mindscape.	We stumble into the center of everything.\n\nThe darkness opens like a wound. One moment we're running through corridors of corrupted divine flesh. The next we're standing in a vast chamber that shouldn't exist.\n\nAnd there, at the center of it all, is Sam.\n\nHe's suspended in mid-air. Arms spread. Head thrown back. Screaming.\n\nNot a scream of pain. Worse. A scream of something beyond pain. Beyond any sensation I have words for. His body convulses. Spasms. Held in place by thousands of invisible cables that pulse and writhe around him.\n\nI can barely see them. The tentacles. Anti-reality made semi-solid. They dig into Sam's flesh. His mind. His soul. Anchoring him. Using him. Feeding through him into the infinite substrate beyond.\n\n"Oh gods," Jamie breathes beside me.\n\nLady Osilari steps forward. Her hands already moving through protective symbols. "The host. He's still alive. Still conscious."\n\n"After all this time?" Benji's voice is strained. "He shouldn’t be... there should be nothing left."\n\nBut there is. I can see it. The divine spark at Sam's core. Diminished. Tortured. But still burning. Still aware.\n\nStill screaming.\n\nI start toward him.\n\nThe tentacles react instantly.\n\nThey lash out from the darkness. Hundreds of them. Thousands. Black cables that slice through the air toward us. Not trying to feed anymore. Trying to defend. Protecting their host from anything that might free him.\n\n"Fulketeh Kaddesh!" Lady Osilari's power flares. A barrier of ancient symbols springs up between us and the attacking tendrils. They slam against it with enough force to crack reality. The symbols hold. Barely.\n\nMore tentacles come. From above. Below. All sides. The anti-reality parasite is fully aware of us now. Fully focused. It knows we're a threat.\n\nBenji is moving through the chaos like smoke. Not fighting directly—that's not his strength—but creating openings. Distractions. He phases partially out of reality, drawing tentacles toward him, then slipping away at the last second so they slam into each other instead. He's weaving small disruptions in the substrate. Nothing powerful. Just enough to tangle the parasites. To slow them down so Jamie and Lady Osilari can strike.\n\nJamie is moving. Fast. Precise. Angel of death training kicking in. She's dodging tentacles, redirecting them with sharp movements, using their momentum against each other. When one gets too close she grabs it—bare hands—and tears it away from its anchor point. Her movements are pure physical skill. No power. Just training. Just instinct honed over lifetimes.\n\n"Stephen, go!" Lady Osilari shouts. "We'll hold them off. Get to Sam!"\n\nI run.\n\nThe tentacles reach for me. These aren't like the ones outside. These are anchored. Connected directly to Sam's divine core. They're real enough to hurt.\n\nOne slashes across my shoulder. I feel it tear through fabric. Through skin. The pain is immediate. Sharp. Wrong. Like being cut by a blade made of frozen void.\n\nAnother whips at my legs. I dodge but not fast enough. It catches my calf. Rips a chunk of flesh away. I stumble. Nearly fall. Blood runs hot down my leg. Real blood. My blood.\n\nBehind me I hear the battle intensifying. Jamie screaming as tentacles get past her defenses. Benji's voice rising in panic. Lady Osilari calling out warnings I can't quite parse.\n\nBut I don't look back. Can't look back.\n\nSam is right there. Ten feet away. Five.\n\nHis eyes snap open.\n\nThey're not his eyes anymore. Corrupted. Filled with that same black substance that makes up the tentacles. But behind the corruption I see him. The real him. Trapped. Aware. Suffering.\n\nI reach out.\n\nThe moment my hand touches his arm, everything changes.\n\nThe tentacles release him. All at once. Sam drops from his suspension. Falls toward the ground. But he doesn't collapse.\n\nHe lands on his feet.\n\nStanding.\n\nMoving.\n\nComing toward me with divine speed.\n\nI have half a second to register his hands around my throat before consciousness blinks out.\n\nI hit the ground. Vision swimming. Sam stands over me. His hands still outstretched. Still shaped like they're choking. But they're not moving.\n\nHe's not moving.\n\n"Stephen!" Jamie's voice cuts through the fog. I hear her footsteps. Feel her rushing toward me.\n\n"No." Lady Osilari's command stops her cold. "Don't. Watch."\n\n"But he's going to—"\n\n"Watch."\n\nI can't turn my head. Can't move. But I can see Sam above me. His eyes are blank now. His body swaying slightly. Like he's standing in a breeze that isn't there.\n\nHe's in a trance.\n\nI'm in two places at once. Here on the ground. Unconscious. Vulnerable. And somewhere else. Somewhere inside.	0	563	f	2026-02-27 12:32:28.849865-05	2026-02-28 23:48:01.749225-05	normal	\N
550	134	2	The Infected Pasture	Stephen enters Sam's internal mindscape, a corrupted pastoral realm where billions of sheep are revealed to be parasitic entities draining Sam's essence. Stephen systematically destroys the parasites while Sam watches, finally destroying the last one himself and achieving clarity about his guilt.	**********\n\nThe pasture opens around me.\n\nI find him at the center of everything.\n\nNot Yaldabaoth. Not the devourer. Just Sam.\n\nThe space around us is black. Not the clean black of night sky or deep water, but something thicker. Heavier. Like reality got scraped away and left behind this void residue. Shades of gray leak through in patches, never quite reaching white. The only color comes in sickly flashes of puke-green grass and the twisted shapes of trees that don't belong to any world I've ever known.\n\nSam stands in the middle of rolling fields that stretch forever. His house sits behind him, the same weathered wood and stone I remember from the first reset where I faced Yaldabaoth. Sparse forests dot the landscape in the distance. And everywhere, covering every inch of ground, pressing against each other like a living carpet... sheep.\n\nBillions of them. Trillions. More than should fit in any space, physical or otherwise.\n\nJamie's voice cuts through from somewhere outside. "Stephen, be careful—"\n\nThen the sound vanishes. I'm inside now. Fully. The pasture wraps around me like a fever dream made solid.\n\nSam sees me and his face lights up. Actually lights up. Like I'm the first visitor he's had in forever. Which, I suppose, I am.\n\n"My friend!" His voice carries warmth I didn't expect. He's walking toward me, arms open. "You've come. Please, you must be hungry. I have bread. Fresh water. Come, sit with me."\n\nThe sheep part around his legs as he moves. Docile. Peaceful. Their wool gleams in that strange not-quite-light that permeates everything here.\n\nBut underneath them, I see it.\n\nTentacles. Black cables that pulse and writhe. They connect sheep to sheep in an endless web, stretching out into the infinite distance. Sucking. Draining. Devouring. Each one feeds into the next, and all of them feed into Sam at the center. He doesn't notice. Doesn't see the way they're hollowing him out from the inside.\n\nI take a breath. Something smells like sweat and festering cheese.\n\n"Sam," I say. "We need to talk."\n\nOutside—because there is still an outside, I can feel it—Jamie is screaming. The crash of something massive hitting stone. Benji's voice raised in a language that predates language. Lady Osilari's power flaring like a star going nova.\n\nThey're buying me time.\n\nI walk toward Sam. The tentacles slide off me harmlessly. They try to latch on, probe at my skin, but I'm real. Actually real. And anti-reality can't touch that. The parasites sense it, recoil, writhe in frustration.\n\nSam gestures to a table that wasn't there a moment ago. Bread and honey. A clay cup filled with something that might be wine. His smile is gentle. Genuine.\n\n"I don't get visitors," he says. "Not in... I can't remember how long. But you're here now. That's good. The flock will be pleased."\n\nI sit. Not because I want to eat, but because I need him to listen.\n\n"Sam, this place..." I gesture around us. "Do you know where you are?"\n\nHis brow furrows slightly. "Home. Where I've always been. With my flock."\n\n"And before this?"\n\nThe furrow deepens. "There is no before. There's only... this. The sheep. Taking care of them. Keeping them safe." His voice drops. "Protecting them from the raiders."\n\nA memory flickers across his face. Something dark. Violent. But it's gone before I can grab it.\n\nI lean forward. "Sam, what if I told you this isn't real? That you're trapped inside a—"\n\nThe tentacles move.\n\nFast. Violent. They whip between us, not attacking me, but wrapping around Sam's head. His ears. His mouth. I watch his expression shift. The warmth drains away. His eyes go hard.\n\n"What did you say?" His voice is different now. Colder. "What are you trying to tell me?"\n\n"I said—"\n\n"You're one of them." He stands. The chair vanishes beneath him. "One of the raiders. Come to take my flock. To hurt them."\n\n"Sam, no, I'm trying to—"\n\nHe lunges.\n\nI barely dodge. His hands are still gentle shepherd's hands, but they move with divine speed. With power that shouldn't exist in something that looks this human.\n\nI don't fight back. Can't. He's the victim here. Not the enemy.\n\n"Sam, listen to me!" I roll away from another strike. "The sheep aren't real. They're parasites. They're feeding on you."\n\nBut the tentacles twist his perception. Every word I say gets corrupted before it reaches him. He hears threats. Accusations. Things designed to make him rage.\n\nA flash of memory breaks through. Raiders. Real ones. Coming for his flock eternities ago. Killing. Burning. The devastation that drove him to make his terrible choice.\n\nHe's tired. I can see it. The attacks slow. His breathing gets ragged.\n\nI take the opening.\n\nOne of the sheep is close. I can see the tentacles clearly now, how they pulse and feed. How they're not part of the sheep at all, but something wearing the sheep like a costume.\n\nI grab it.\n\nThe sheep bleats. Innocent. Confused.\n\nSam roars.\n\nBut I don't let go. I reach past the wool, past the facade, and I pull. The tentacles shriek—an awful sound that has no business coming from anything organic—and the sheep's form tears away like wet paper.\n\nUnderneath is something black. Writhing. All mouths and hunger.\n\nSam freezes.\n\n"No," he whispers. "No, that's... that's my..."\n\nThe parasite lunges at me. I snap its core and it dissolves into nothing. Into less than nothing. Back into the anti-reality it came from.\n\n"Sam." I'm breathing hard now. "Look. Really look."\n\nHe does. I watch his eyes scan the flock. The billions of sheep that aren't sheep at all. The cables connecting them. The way they're draining him. Using his love as fuel.\n\nAnother sheep comes close. He stares at it. I can see him trying to reconcile what his heart tells him—these are mine, I love them, I must protect them—with what his eyes are finally starting to see.\n\n"They can't be..." He reaches for one. His hand trembles. "I've cared for them so long. I've given them everything."\n\n"I know." I move beside him. "And they've taken everything. That's what parasites do."\n\nHe steps back. Shaking his head. "I can't. I can't hurt them. Even if they're... even if..."\n\n"Then don't." I grab the nearest sheep. Pull. The parasite underneath shrieks as I tear it free. Dissolve it back into nothing.\n\nSam collapses to his knees. Hands over his face. Sobbing.\n\nBut he doesn't stop me.\n\nI move through the flock. One by one. Ripping away the facades. Destroying the parasites underneath. Each death is agony for him. I can feel it. Each sheep lost tears another memory free. Another fragment of truth. Another piece of the horror he's been living.\n\nOutside, something explodes. Jamie's voice again, strained. "How long is this going to take?"\n\nLady Osilari's response is calm. Steady. "As long as it needs to."\n\nTime loses meaning here. Minutes stretch into hours. Hours into days. I'm killing for what feels like years. My hands raw. My body exhausted. But I don't stop.\n\nSam watches. Weeping. Each parasite I destroy brings back another memory. The choice he made. The entity that promised to protect his flock. The moment he became a host instead of a shepherd.\n\n"I did this," he whispers between sobs. "All of it. The prison. The suffering. I let them use me to—"\n\n"You were tricked," I say. Destroying another parasite. "Infected. This isn't your fault."\n\nBut he's drowning in guilt now. In the weight of what he's finally remembering. What he's finally seeing clearly.\n\nBillions become millions. Millions become thousands. Thousands become hundreds.\n\nI'm covered in the residue of dissolved parasites. Exhausted beyond anything I've ever felt. But still I keep going.\n\nUntil there's one left.\n\nIt stands alone in the middle of the infinite pasture. Small. Vulnerable. Looking up at Sam with eyes that seem to beg for mercy.\n\nSam is still on his knees. Still crying. But something has changed in his face. In his eyes.\n\nUnderstanding. Terrible. Complete. Understanding.\n\nHe stands slowly. Unsteadily. Like a man carrying the weight of entire universes on his shoulders.\n\nHe walks to the last sheep.\n\nKneels before it.\n\nHis hands hover over its wool.\n\n"Please," he whispers. "Please let this one be real."\n\nBut even as he says it, he knows. We both know.\n\nThe tentacles are there. Thinner than the others. Almost invisible. But there.\n\nSam closes his eyes. Takes a breath that sounds like it's being torn from the center of his being.\n\nThen reaches past the wool. Grabs the corruption underneath.\n\nThe sheep doesn't bleat. It hisses. Writhes. Shows its true form in that final moment.\n\nSam kills it himself.\n\nThe pasture goes silent.\n\nNo more sheep. No more parasites. Just gray fields stretching into forever and a shepherd standing alone in the center of his own corrupted heaven.\n\nHe looks at me. His eyes are clear now. Fully awake. Fully aware of everything he's done. Everything that's been done through him.\n\n"What have I done?" he asks.\n\nBefore I can answer, the pasture fractures. Cracks spread through the ground. Through the sky. Through the very fabric of this inner world.\n\nWe're being pulled back out.\n\nTogether.\n\nAfter what felt like decades in the blink of an eye.	1	564	t	2026-02-27 12:32:28.849865-05	2026-02-28 23:48:59.752249-05	normal	\N
551	134	3	The Extraction	Stephen and the group witness a horrific ritual extraction of the anti-reality parasite from Sam's body, orchestrated by Lady Illkeserod and Nyarlathotep. Sam's divine essence is torn away piece by piece as the parasite is forcibly separated, ultimately destroying him but freeing the trapped souls.	**********\n\nReality snaps back like a rubber band.\n\nI'm on the ground. Sam limp, laying on top of me. Both of us gasping.\n\nWe're still inside the shell of Yaldabaoth. Inside the corrupted husk of what used to be a god. The blackness surrounds us on all sides. Pressing in. Suffocating. But now I can see through it. See the throne room beyond. The dais. The actual throne itself, empty and waiting.\n\nSam is standing. Swaying. His eyes are clear but his body... something's wrong with his body. The black substance of the parasite clings to him like oil. Like tar. Pulsing with each breath he takes.\n\nThen the ritual begins.\n\nI feel it before I see it. A pressure building from two directions at once. Lady Illkeserod from outside the prison. Nyarlathotep from somewhere deep in the substrate beneath us. Their voices rise in harmony. Not words. Something older than words. Something that predates language itself.\n\nThe black substance around Sam starts to writhe.\n\n"No," Sam whispers. "No, please, just let me—"\n\nThe first piece tears free.\n\nI see it happen. Watch the black substance peel away from Sam's chest like skin being flayed. But it's not just surface. It goes deeper. Through muscle. Through bone. Through the divine essence that makes him what he is.\n\nThe parasite screams.\n\nNot through Sam's mouth. Through everything. Through the substrate itself. The sound is physical. Violent. It slams into us like a shockwave. I feel my eardrums rupture. Taste copper flooding my mouth. Blood runs hot from my nose.\n\nSam screams with it.\n\nHis back arches. Muscles tearing. Tendons snapping audibly as his body convulses against the extraction. Where the parasite pulls away, it leaves wounds. Raw. Gaping. Not bleeding—there's no blood in something this divine—but leaking light. Leaking essence. Pieces of what made Sam immortal spilling out onto nothing.\n\nThe black mass writhes in the air between us. Still connected by dozens of thinner tendrils. Still anchored deep in Sam's core. It fights. Thrashes. Tries to burrow back in.\n\nJamie turns away. Vomits. The sound wet and violent. Benji follows a moment later. Doubling over. Retching until there's nothing left.\n\nLady Osilari's hands shake. Her symbols flare brighter. Tightening the surgical field. Forcing the separation to continue. But I see her other hand moving. Trying to weave something. Anything. Patterns meant to dull pain. To ease suffering.\n\nThey dissolve before they form.\n\nYou can't separate reality from anti-reality gently. Can't make this clean. Can't numb what's happening when the very act of extraction is tearing existence itself apart.\n\n"I'm sorry," she whispers. Her voice cracks. Tears streaming down her face. Like a surgeon who knows the patient won't survive but has to keep cutting anyway. "I'm so sorry."\n\nThe symbols pulse. The extraction continues.\n\nSam's body convulses again.\n\nAnother piece rips free.\n\nThis one takes a chunk of Sam's shoulder with it. Divine flesh tears away in strips. The wound is obscene. Wrong. I can see too far inside him. See structures that shouldn't exist in three dimensions. See the architecture of godhood being unmade in real time.\n\nSam's scream changes pitch. Goes higher. More desperate. His fingers claw at the ground—at the nothing beneath us—trying to find something to grip. To anchor against the agony.\n\nJamie collapses. Falls to her knees. Sobbing. The kind of crying that comes from somewhere deeper than grief. Deeper than horror.\n\nI go to her. Drop down beside her. Pull her against me.\n\nWe hold each other. Her face buried in my shoulder. My arms wrapped around her like I can shield her from what we're witnessing. But I can't. Neither of us can look away for long. Can't stop watching.\n\nWe're the only anchors each other has right now. The only real things in this nightmare. The only comfort in a process that has to continue. That can't be stopped.\n\nNo matter how much we want it to.\n\nThis is the parasite's final gift. Its last act of destruction. Making us watch. Making us complicit. Making us carry this with us forever.\n\nThe extraction continues.\n\nThe parasite is fighting harder now. Sensing its mortality. Its tendrils whip through the air. Slam against Lady Osilari's barriers. Crack them. Weaken them. Desperate to hold on. Desperate to keep its host.\n\nBenji's voice rises. This time a soothing lilt in his cadence. Trying to ease the pain of anyone who is listening.\n\nLady Illkeserod and Nyarlathotep's ritual intensifies from outside. I feel their power converging. Crushing down. Forcing the separation.\n\nMore pieces tears free and Sam's left arm goes with it.\n\nNot severed. Worse. Dissolved. The divine matter that made up his limb unravels as the parasite pulls away. Strings of darkness and essence coming apart like wet tissue paper. What's left behind is a stump that ends in nothing. In absence. In a wound that will never heal because there's nothing left to heal with.\n\nSam isn't screaming anymore.\n\nCan't scream anymore.\n\nHis voice is gone. Shredded by the first extraction. What comes out now is just air. Just the desperate wheeze of something being unmade piece by piece.\n\nReality buckles again. I taste blood and ozone and something that has no name in any language I know. Something that predates language. Predates thought.\n\nThe sound of creation running backward.\n\n"It's working," Jamie breathes beside me.\n\nBut it's not clean. Could never be clean. You don't host an anti-reality parasite for eons without it leaving marks. Without it taking root so deep that pulling it out means pulling out pieces of yourself.\n\nMore pieces tear away, this time not physical, but the construct beneath it all.\n\nI see Sam's immortality go first. The eternal spark that made him human. It peels off in strips of black corruption and dissipates into nothing. His power follows. The divine strength that let him devour entire realities. Gone. Consumed by the parasite's death throes.\n\nThen his memories.\n\nI watch them flicker across his face as they're ripped away. Eons of existence. Cycles of creation and destruction. The faces of his flock before the raiders came. Before the infection. Before everything went wrong.\n\nAll of it being pulled out like infected tissue from a wound.\n\nSam's scream shifts. Changes pitch. It's not pain anymore. Not entirely.\n\nIt's relief.\n\n"Yes," he gasps between convulsions. "Yes, take it. Take all of it. Everything I've done. Everything I've become. Just—" Another wave of extraction. Another piece torn free. "Just make it stop."\n\nThe black substance is forming a mass now. Off to the side. Near the throne. All the extracted pieces coalescing into a single writhing entity. Smaller than before. Diminished. But still conscious. Still hungry.\n\nIt tries to latch onto the throne itself. Tries to anchor in reality through the seat of power.\n\nBut it can't.\n\nAnti-reality has no purchase without a host. Without love to corrupt. Without a divine spark to feed on. It slides off the throne like water off glass. Impotent. Raging. Trapped.\n\nBenji wipes his mouth. Straightens. He moves fast—faster than I've seen him move before—toward the writhing mass. His hands already weaving patterns in the air. Pulling something from his pockets.\n\nThe cosmic seed.\n\nHe places it carefully. Precisely. At the base of the throne. Right where the parasite is coiling and thrashing. The seed appears as a point of crystalline geometry. Impossibly complex. Hurting to look at.\n\n"This will hold it," he says. Voice hoarse. "Once the extraction is complete. Once there's nothing left for it to anchor to."\n\nThe seed pulses. Waiting. Ready to bloom the moment Sam is finally free.\n\nLady Illkeserod's voice rises higher. Nyarlathotep's responds from below. The ritual intensifies.\n\nSam is barely recognizable now. The light that made him divine is almost gone. What's left is a mass of flesh and organs that might have once been human. Twisted. Exposed. His ribcage visible where the parasite tore through. Lungs working in spasms. Heart beating erratically in the open air. Intestines coiled and pulsing where his abdomen should be.\n\nBut he's alive.\n\nImpossibly. Horrifically. Alive.\n\nStill suffering. Still aware. Eyes rolling in a face that's half-melted. Half-dissolved. A mouth that tries to form words but only produces wet gasping sounds.\n\nHe's dying. I can see it. We all can.\n\nThe last pieces of the parasite tear free with a sound like reality breaking. The mush of Sam collapses forward. The remnants are like holding smoke given temporary form.\n\n"I'm sorry," he whispers. Blood gurgles out of places where his mouth used to be. Not real blood. Memory of blood. The idea of it. "For all of it. For what I let happen. For the souls I trapped. For—"\n\n"Sam—"\n\n"No." He manages, weak but insistent. "Let me say it. I need to... I need to..." His eyes find Jamie. Then Benji. Lady Osilari. "I thought I was protecting them. My flock. I thought if I just held on tight enough, loved them hard enough, nothing could hurt them."\n\nHis voice cracks.\n\n"But love without wisdom is just... it's just fuel for monsters."\n\nThe black mass near the throne writhes harder. Slamming against invisible barriers. Searching for any crack. Any weakness. But the cosmic seed is already beginning to form around it. I can feel it in the substrate. The new prison taking shape. Smaller. Stronger. Designed specifically to contain this thing.\n\nSam's breathing gets shallow. Ragged.\n\n"I don't want to be saved," he says quietly. "I don't deserve it. All those souls. All that suffering. I was awake for it. Every moment. Watching myself destroy everything I ever cared about and I couldn't... I couldn't stop it."\n\nTears spurt out of what are left from his eyes, dripping down to the puddle he’s become. Real tears this time. Human tears.\n\n"I just want to go back," he whispers. "Back to the Pleroma. Back to nothing. Let me fade. Let me be forgotten. That's all I want now. That's all I've wanted since—"\n\nHe doesn't finish.\n\nThe bloody mass that was his body starts to dissolve. Not violently. Gently. Like ash caught in a breeze that isn't there. Particles of light and matter drifting apart. Returning to the fundamental substrate from which all things come.\n\nWithout his divinity, without his power, without anything to hold him together... he's just unmaking. Naturally. Inevitably.\n\nJamie kneels beside us. Her hands cup something I can't quite see. A light. A spark. The core of what made Sam who he was before the infection. Before the parasite. Before the choice that damned him.\n\n"I've got you," she says softly. "You're not alone."\n\nSam tries to smile. Almost manages it.\n\n"My flock," he breathes. "Tell them I'm sorry. Tell them I..."\n\nBut there's nothing left to say. Nothing left to tell.\n\nHis body finishes dissolving. The last particles drift away like snow in reverse. All that remains is the spark in Jamie's hands. Small. Fragile. Pure.\n\nShe cradles it against her chest. Protecting it. Keeping it safe until we can get it out of this prison. Out into real reality where it can finally rest.\n\nThe black mass near the throne gives one final shriek. Then falls silent.\n\nThe ritual is complete.\n\n**********	2	565	t	2026-02-27 12:32:28.849865-05	2026-02-28 23:49:55.754688-05	normal	\N
552	134	4	The Prison Within a Prison	Benji and Lady Osilari activate the cosmic seed to imprison the parasite in a new, smaller containment. The expanding fractal geometry forces them to flee toward the portal while navigating a panicking crowd of freed souls.	Benji and Lady Osilari are standing on the sides of the cosmic seed. Their hands moving in patterns that hurt to watch. Weaving substrate and intention into something that can hold anti-reality without being consumed by it.\n\n"How long?" Jamie asks. She's still holding Sam's spark. Still cradling it like something precious.\n\n"Not long," Benji says without looking up. "Once it starts expanding, we'll have minutes. Maybe less."\n\nThe black mass of the parasite writhes nearby. Sensing what's coming. It slams against the invisible barriers with renewed fury. Desperate. Afraid.\n\nGood. Let it be afraid.\n\nLady Osilari's voice cuts through my thoughts. "When the seed activates, run. Don't stop. Don't look back. The portal will be open but not for long."\n\n"What about—" I gesture around us. At the throne room. At the vast emptiness of Yaldabaoth's corpse surrounding everything.\n\n"What about the souls still trapped here?"\n\nShe meets my eyes. "The ones close enough will figure it out. The ones who can't..." She doesn't finish. Doesn't need to.\n\nTrillions upon trillions of NPCs. Substrate shadows too deep in the dream to wake. Souls so far from the epicenter they'll never make it to the portal in time.\n\nWe can't save everyone.\n\nThe cosmic seed pulses.\n\nOnce. Twice.\n\nThen it blooms.\n\nFractal geometry explodes outward from the throne. Reality folding into itself. Creating new space. New dimension. A prison within a prison. But this time designed with purpose. With precision.\n\nThe anti-reality parasite screams as the fractals wrap around it. Engulfing it. Containing it. I watch it struggle. Watch it try to corrupt the new reality the way it corrupted Etheris. But the cosmic seed doesn't run on love