a wall of desperate souls.\n\nThey're not attacking. Not trying to hurt us. They're just... there. Everywhere. Bodies packed so tight there's no space between them. Crowding. Pushing. Clawing their way toward escape. Some are running. Some are crawling. Others are phasing through each other in their desperation to reach the light.\n\nIt's chaos.\n\nPure. Unfiltered. Chaos.\n\n"Move!" Benji is shouting. Trying to phase through. Trying to slip between them. But there are too many. Too densely packed.\n\nThe fractal expansion is visible now. A wall of crystalline geometry rushing toward us. Consuming everything in its path. The throne dissolves into it. The dais. The vast emptiness of Yaldabaoth's corpse folding into the new reality.\n\nWe've got seconds.\n\nI lower my shoulder and push forward. Gently at first. Trying not to hurt anyone. These are victims. Refugees. People who've suffered longer than I can comprehend.\n\nBut they won't move. Can't move. There's nowhere for them to go.\n\nThe fractal wave gets closer.\n\n"Stephen!" Jamie's voice. Desperate.\n\nI push harder. My hands on translucent shoulders. Shoving. A soul stumbles. Falls. Gets trampled by others behind them.\n\nI keep going. Have to keep going.\n\nJamie is beside me now. Doing the same. Her training kicking in. Using leverage. Using force. Bodies tumbling aside. Some crying out. Some just collapsing.\n\nWe don't want to hurt them. Don't want to add to their suffering.\n\nBut we have to get out.\n\nLady Osilari is ahead. Her symbols creating a small clearing. But it fills immediately. More souls pressing in. Benji behind us. Grabbing. Pulling. Throwing aside anyone who gets too close.\n\nThe portal is twenty feet away. Fifteen.\n\nA soul grabs my arm. Translucent fingers with too many joints digging in. Won't let go. Eyes begging. Mouth forming words I can't hear over the chaos.\n\nI rip free. Skin tears where the fingers held. The soul stumbles back into the crowd.\n\nTen feet.\n\nWe're carving a path now. Actively violent. No choice. Arms shoving. Hands tearing at bodies that feel like cold meat. Like wet clay. They break apart under our touch. Dissolve. Reform. But they're in our way and the fractal wave is right there.\n\nFive feet.\n\nThe crowd is thickest here. Everyone funneling toward the same narrow point. Bodies pressing so tight I can barely breathe. I use my elbow. My knee. Anything to create space. A soul's face caves in under my forearm. Another loses their grip and falls. Gets crushed under the stampede behind us.\n\nI see a child. Can't be more than eight. Wedged between two larger forms. Being compressed. Her mouth open in a silent scream.\n\nI grab her. Yank hard. Something tears. The souls holding her in place stumble back. I pull the child against my chest. Her body cold. Fragile. Like holding a memory given form.\n\nThe portal is right there.\n\nThree feet.\n\nBodies slam into us from all sides. Desperate. Frantic. I see faces. Too many faces. All of them suffering. All of them begging without words.\n\nI push through. Feel ribs crack under my hands. Feel flesh give way. Translucent blood—or something like it—coating my arms.\n\nBenji goes through first. Then Lady Osilari. Forcing her way past the final cluster of souls.\n\nJamie is right behind her. Looking back at me. At the child in my arms. At the wave of fractal geometry consuming everything.\n\n"Go!" I shout.\n\nShe goes.\n\nI'm right behind her. One final shove. A soul falls. Another crashes into them. The path clears just enough.\n\nThe child clutched against my chest. The portal surrounds me. Pulls me. Reality shifts from fractured to solid. From dream to awake.\n\nI fall through.\n\nHit ground. Real ground. Elysium ground.\n\nMy hands are shaking. Covered in residue. In the memory of violence. The child stumbles away from me. Confused. Dazed. But alive. Safe.\n\nBehind us the portal is still open. Souls are pouring through. Dozens every second. Running. Screaming. Crying. Some celebrating. Others collapsing the moment they cross over.\n\nThen I see it.\n\nThe fractal expansion reaching its limit. The cosmic seed achieving its final form. A perfect prison. Self-contained. Inviolable.\n\nAnd Etheris... the original prison that held it all... shatters.\n\nThe shockwave hits us first. A pressure wave that makes my teeth rattle. Makes my bones ache. The cage that kept Etheris separate from the wider cosmos simply... breaks. Explodes outward. Returns to the substrate from which it was made in a cascade of collapsing dimensions.\n\nEtheris rejoins reality.\n\nI watch it transform through the still-open portal. The twisted nightmare dimensions smoothing out. Becoming normal. Natural. A regular reality where gods can roam as they were meant to. Where souls can live and die and live again without the parasite feeding on them.\n\nIt looks peaceful. Beautiful even.\n\nBut in the center. Where the solar system used to be. The cosmic seed pulses. A new prison. Smaller. Stronger. Containing the anti-reality parasite and trillions of souls who didn't make it out.\n\nThe moment Etheris shatters—the exact moment the shockwave finishes rippling through reality—the portal slams shut.\n\nMore souls were trying to rush through. Dozens. A flood of desperate bodies trying to make it before the threshold seals.\n\nA figure was reaching the threshold. Hands outstretched. Eyes wide with hope and terror.\n\nReality cuts through them like a blade.\n\nThe top half falls through to our side. Chest. Arms. Head. Still alive. Still conscious. Eyes blinking in confusion before the screaming starts.\n\nBehind them—through the flash I see for just a split second—dozens more. Cut at the waist. At the shoulders. At the neck. The bottom halves on the other side. The top halves here.\n\nAll of them screaming.\n\nAll of them dying.\n\nThe pieces on our side twitch. Writhe. Translucent blood pooling beneath them. Some try to crawl. To reach for the space where the portal was. As if they could somehow rejoin with their missing halves.\n\nOne by one they stop moving.\n\nSilence falls.\n\nI'm on my knees. Hands still shaking. Still covered in the residue of violence. Jamie beside me. Sam's spark still cradled in her hands. The rescued souls scattered around us. Some sitting. Some standing. All of them staring at the space where the portal was.\n\nAt the bodies that didn't make it.\n\nAt the new prison visible in the distance. At Etheris around it. Free but damaged. Saved but scarred.\n\nBenji helps me stand. His face is grim. Pale. He's staring at his hands like he doesn't recognize them.	3	128	t	2026-02-27 12:32:28.849865-05	2026-02-28 23:50:49.965476-05	normal	\N
553	135	1	Sam's Release	The family gathers on a platform overlooking the freed reality of Etheris to release Sam's divine spark, which chooses to dissolve into the fabric of existence itself rather than reincarnate. After a solemn ceremony honoring Sam's sacrifice, the group turns to the work ahead.	The platform jutted out from Elysium's southern reaches like a diving board over an ocean of stars. Behind us, the refugee camps sprawled across the landscape—makeshift cities of light where souls who'd escaped the prison were learning to breathe free air for the first time in millennia. Ahead... ahead was the reality we had fought so hard to liberate.\n\nEtheris.\n\nThe newly released prison reality hung in the void like a dying galaxy given a second chance, its edges bleeding light back into the Pleroma. Azathoth pulsed at its center, no longer chained beneath substrate code, but restored. Free. The blind god's piping echoed across dimensions, a sound like relief.\n\nJamie's hand found mine.\n\nI didn't look at her. Couldn't. If I did, I'd lose it.\n\nThe others gathered in a loose semicircle around us. Benji and Lady Illkeserod stood closest, their fingers intertwined. Hecate's three faces were all crying—past, present, and future grief happening simultaneously. Tiamat's scales caught the starlight, refracting rainbows that had no business existing during a funeral. Even Nyarlathotep had shown up, lounging against empty air with something almost like respect on his thousand faces.\n\nChernobog cleared his throat. The sound was like continents shifting.\n\n"We gather," he said quietly, "to release what remains."\n\nIn my other hand, an ornate chalice that cupped liquid essence I was afraid to spill, was Sam's divine spark.\n\nNot Sam, really. Sam was gone. The shepherd who'd mistakenly allowed Yaldabaoth in, who'd been hollowed out and worn like a suit by the anti-reality parasite for eternities beyond counting... that person had stopped existing long before we'd torn the prison open. This spark was just... what was left. The core. The seed of divinity that even Yaldabaoth's corruption couldn't quite digest.\n\nIt pulsed gently. Warm. Confused. Tired.\n\n"You don't have to say anything," Jamie whispered. Her vanilla-and-peaches scent mixed with the smell of raw creation. "Just... let him go."\n\nI opened tilted the chalice and poured it out.\n\nThe spark drifted downward. Slowly. Like it was waking up from a nightmare and wasn't quite sure which way was up. Other divine sparks—we'd released hundreds in the weeks since the prison fell—they always shot off like fireworks. Eager. Ready to dive back into existence, to reincarnate, to live again.\n\nSam's spark just... drifted.\n\nIt floated between us, pulsing. Growing brighter. Then dimmer. Then bright again, like it was breathing.\n\nXavier stepped forward. His face was wet.\n\n"You were a good person," Xavier said to the spark. "Before. You didn't deserve what happened to you."\n\nThe spark pulsed. Brighter.\n\nHannah came next, our daughter, her presence like dawn after endless night. "And you're free now. Finally."\n\nAnother pulse.\n\nI wanted to say something profound. Something that would make sense of it all—the suffering, the sacrifice, the cosmic horror of what Sam had endured. But the words stuck in my throat like broken glass.\n\nJamie squeezed my hand. "It's okay," she breathed.\n\nThe spark began to fade.\n\nNot like dying. Not like going out. It was more like... dissolving. Like watercolor paint spreading through clear water. The light didn't vanish—it spread. Diffused. Became part of the background radiation of existence itself.\n\nSam wasn't reincarnating.\n\nHe was choosing to stop being a person at all.\n\nThe spark spread wider. Thinner. I could see stars through it now. Could see the spark diffuse over Etheris behind it. The light touched everything and nothing, everywhere and nowhere.\n\nAnd then it was gone.\n\nNo. Not gone. That wasn't right either.\n\nPresent. Everywhere. In the starlight and the void between stars. In the refugee camps behind us and the freed reality of Etheris ahead. In the breath in my lungs and the tears on Jamie's cheeks.\n\nSam had returned to the Pleroma. Not as a soul waiting to be reborn, but as part of the fabric itself. The ultimate peace. The final rest.\n\nBenji was the first to move. He stepped forward and placed his hand over his heart, then bowed toward the void where the spark had been. Lady Illkeserod did the same. Then Hecate. Then Tiamat. Then all of them, one by one, even Nyarlathotep with something like reverence in his gesture.\n\nI did it too. So did Jamie. Xavier. Hannah.\n\nWe stood there for a long time. Saying goodbye to someone who was no longer someone. Honoring a sacrifice that had never been chosen but had been endured anyway.\n\nEventually, Jamie's voice broke the silence.\n\n"Come on," she said softly. "We've got work to do."\n\nShe was right. The memorial was over. Sam was at peace—the kind of peace the rest of us couldn't even imagine yet. There were still trillions of souls trapped inside the cosmic seed. An anti-reality parasite's corruption festering in substrate shadows.\n\nJust... quietly this time. Carefully.\n\nLike shepherds, not soldiers.\n\nI took one last look at the void where Sam's spark had dissolved. Somewhere in that darkness, mixed with starlight and the background hum of existence, was peace.\n\n"Yeah," I said. "Let's go."\n\nWe turned our backs on Etheris and walked toward the refugee camps, toward the council chambers, toward whatever came next.\n\nBehind us, the observatory platform hung empty at the edge of everything.\n\nThe universe hummed.\n\nAnd somewhere in that hum, I could almost hear Sam’s silence.	0	567	f	2026-02-27 12:32:56.962202-05	2026-02-28 23:52:16.263632-05	normal	\N
554	135	2	The Council Debates the Shepherd Initiative	Months later, the council meets to discuss the logistics of freeing the trillions of souls still trapped in the cosmic seed prison. Hannah proposes inserting a family unit as long-term residents rather than extracting souls one at a time, leading to heated debate and ultimately a vote to approve the Shepherd Initiative.	Months later, the council room didn't look much like a council room anymore.\n\nSomeone—probably Hecate—had brought in plants. Actual living things with roots and leaves that didn't glow or whisper prophecies. Just... plants. A fern in the corner. Some kind of trailing vine thing along the window that overlooked the construction yards where new realities were being assembled like cosmic Lego sets.\n\nI sat at the long table, watching gods argue about zoning laws.\n\n"The Sumerian pantheon can't just claim the entire eastern quadrant," Benji was saying, his head bobbing with irritation. "There are protocols now. Forms to fill out."\n\n"Forms," Tiamat growled. The primordial chaos dragon was trying very hard to care about bureaucracy. Failing, but trying. "We predate forms. We predate protocols. We predate you, little auditor."\n\n"Which is precisely why we need them now," Yeshua interjected smoothly. He looked tired. Being the actual Christ and having to clean up two thousand years of people using your name to justify atrocities would do that. "Structure prevents... misunderstandings."\n\nJamie leaned over and whispered in my ear. "Is this really what cosmic liberation looks like? Zoning disputes?"\n\n"Apparently."\n\nChernobog cleared his throat from the head of the table. The god of darkness and void had somehow become the de facto administrator of the refugee crisis. Turned out entropy was great at organization.\n\n"If we could return to the agenda," he said.\n\nLady Illkeserod pulled up a projection. The cosmic seed prison rotated in the air above the table—a perfect replica of our solar system, down to Saturn's rings and Jupiter's red spot. Except it wasn't our solar system. Not anymore. It was a sealed pocket dimension, a terrarium for trapped souls, held together by substrate code and desperation.\n\n"Current population estimate," she said, "is 328.4 septillion. That includes NPCs in various stages of awakening, as well as dormant fragments still waiting to be born for the first time. Each one carries a clone of a divine spark, most buried deep under layers of Yaldabaoth's corruption."\n\nThe number hung in the air like smoke.\n\n"And we can't just let them out," Nyarlathotep drawled from his seat. He was manifesting as a 1920s jazz musician today, complete with suspenders and a cigarette holder. "Because releasing corruption on that scale would be like opening Pandora's box, except the box is full of condensed anti-reality and weaponized depression."\n\n"Eloquent," Benji muttered.\n\n"I try."\n\nHecate's middle face—the present one—spoke up. "The extraction teams have only successfully removed 84.7 thousand awakened souls from the prison's 'heaven' construct over the last quarter. It is an exhaustive process. Each soul requires at least six full reincarnation lifetimes just to initially awake, followed by an average of 340 years of therapeutic intervention. All this before they are stable enough to purge Yaldabaoth’s corruption and integrate into free reality."\n\nI tried to do the math in my head, but the numbers were so incomprehensibly large I felt my eyes starting to calcify.\n\n"At that rate," I said slowly, "it'll take..."\n\n"Eons," Lady Illkeserod finished for me. "Yes."\n\nXavier was leaning against the wall behind me, arms crossed. He'd been quiet through most of the meeting, but now he spoke up.\n\n"So what you're saying is, we've got uncountable bits of souls trapped in something like The Matrix, they're all infected with a cosmic virus that could spark the entire nightmare again if we're not careful, and our current plan is to... wait it out?"\n\n"When you put it that way," Yeshua said with a slight smile, "it does sound rather inadequate."\n\n"It's the best we have," Chernobog said. "The alternative is releasing them prematurely and risking everything we've fought for. One spark. One moment of rage-fueled 'love' attaching to Yaldabaoth's corrupted parasite, and the fucking thing could reconstitute itself. Start the whole war over again."\n\nHannah materialized next to Xavier. Ever since learning she had actual powers, our daughter had a habit of forgetting to walk places when she could just be there instead. "What about going in? Like, actually going inside the prison? Not extraction teams, but... residents."\n\nThe room went quiet.\n\nLady Illkeserod looked at her. "Explain."\n\n"Unlike the earliest cycles where he spread out through all realities, the lucky thing is most of Yaldabaoth's attention was focused on Earth," Hannah said. "On that one solar system. Alone. The people inside—the NPCs—they didn't experience the collapse directly. For them, it was just... Tuesday. Maybe some weird accidents, but nothing world-ending."\n\n"Where are you going with this?" Tiamat asked.\n\nJamie sat forward. I could feel her mind working, connecting dots I hadn't seen yet. "She's saying we could insert monitoring teams. Long-term. Live there. Guide the awakening from inside instead of trying to extract souls one at a time."\n\n"Shepherds," I said quietly.\n\nBenji's eyes widened. "Like Sam was supposed to be. Before."\n\n"But not alone," Lady Illkeserod added, her expression shifting. "And not corrupted. A family unit, perhaps. With full awareness. Divine powers, carefully limited. Wealth and influence to guide without controlling."\n\nNyarlathotep laughed. It wasn't a nice sound. "You're talking about playing god in a prison that was literally created by a god complex run amok. The irony is… chef's kiss."\n\n"We're not playing," Jamie said, and there was death in her voice. The angel of death I'd fallen in love with. "We're shepherding. There's a difference."\n\nChernobog leaned back in his chair. "This would require... significant restructuring. Divine intervention on a scale we haven't attempted since the collapse. Inserting backdoors into the substrate, creating false histories, managing the Mandela Effect ripples..."\n\n"And someone would need to volunteer," Yeshua added. "To go back inside. To live in that prison, knowing it's a prison, for... untold cycles, potentially."\n\nI looked at Jamie. She looked at me.\n\nXavier pushed off the wall, his eyes darting to the projection of the prison. "Is Tiffany in there?"\n\nLady Illkeserod checked the data stream and nodded. "Yes. Her essence is currently looping in the Florida construct."\n\n"Then I'm in," Xavier said instantly.\n\n"Just like that?" Hannah teased.\n\nXavier crossed his arms, trying to look casual and failing miserably. "I mean, someone has to keep an eye on the situation. Make sure... you know... she's safe."\n\nHis ears went bright red. Hannah smirked at him.\n\nNyarlathotep's grin widened. "Oh, this is going to be delicious."\n\nLady Illkeserod pulled up new projections. "If we're doing this, we do it properly. The awakened souls still inside need guidance, and the NPCs need to evolve naturally, without forced intervention."\n\n"Why not repurpose the existing framework?" Yeshua asked, looking at the projection of Earth. "I could install a new Pontiff. Issue new decrees from the Vatican. Shift the dogma toward awakening rather than submission."\n\nThoth shook his ibis head. "Inefficient. Christianity only covers, what, thirty-one point seven percent of the global population? That leaves sixty-nine point three percent unaccounted for. You cannot shepherd a flock that doesn't listen to your frequency."\n\n"He's right," I said. "Religion carries too much baggage. Too many wars. If we go in waving crosses or crescents, we lose half the room before we start."\n\n"So what then?" Xavier asked. "We start our own thing? Like Dianetics, but... you know, actually helpful? A new and improved sci-fi self-help movement?"\n\n"Something like that," Jamie said thoughtfully. "But subtler. An agnostic cultural movement. We use art. We use stories. We don't tell them what to believe; we give them the tools to figure it out themselves. We help them awaken without them realizing it's a spiritual process."\n\n"So like, actually eradicating a Woke mind virus," Benji mused. "I like it."\n\n"While you handle the living," Yeshua said, nodding slowly, "Heaven gets converted to an out-processing center. Real rehabilitation, not the propaganda machine it's been. YHVH and I will oversee the transition. Fix what's been broken in our name."\n\n"That's going to be a mess," Thoth observed. "Six thousand years of xenophobic corruption doesn't wash out easily."\n\n"No," Yeshua agreed. "But it's our mess to clean."\n\nChernobog stood. "This requires a full council vote. All pantheons, all resistance leaders. If we're going to reshape reality inside the cosmic seed, if we're going to insert divine agents into the prison... we do it right. No more mistakes."\n\n"When's the vote?" I asked.\n\n"Three days. That gives you time to think about what you're volunteering for." He looked at me, then at Jamie, then at our children. "This isn't extraction duty. This is long-term assignment. You'd be living in that prison, watching NPCs loop through their programmed behaviors, seeing the corruption up close every single day. For most of you, it would feel like forever."\n\n"Better than sitting around here watching you all fill out forms," Xavier muttered.\n\nHannah elbowed him. "He means it's going to be hard."\n\n"I know what he means."\n\nJamie's hand found mine under the table. Her grip was strong. Certain.\n\n"Three days," she said to Chernobog. "We'll have an answer."\n\nBut we already knew.\n\nAfter an eternity of forced separation—torn apart just to generate the longing that fueled the old prison's batteries—we were finally doing this together. Our whole family, united. It was new. It was exciting. Going back inside, this time with power and purpose and each other... that wasn't captivity.\n\nThat was something else entirely.\n\nThe meeting adjourned. Gods filed out, arguing about logistics and substrate code and how to smooth the Mandela Effect ripples.\n\nI stood at the window, looking out at the vastness of the external realities. I gazed at the infinite tapestry of worlds, each possessing its own unique flavor and distinct vibration. At Etheris, still healing in the distance, Azathoth's piping barely audible from here.\n\nJamie came up beside me. "You thinking about Sam?"\n\n"Yeah."\n\n"He chose peace. Dissolution. No more fighting."\n\n"I know."\n\n"We're choosing the opposite."\n\nI turned to look at her. My wife. My twin flame. The angel of death who'd walked with me through hell and out the other side.\n\n"Yeah," I said. "We are."\n\nXavier and Hannah joined us at the window. Our family. Our strange, impossible, beautiful family.\n\n"Three days," Hannah said.\n\n"Three days," I agreed.\n\nOutside, the universe kept building itself.\n\nInside the cosmic seed prison, uncountable souls waited for something they didn't know they needed.\n\nWe had work to do.\n\nBut that was a problem for another day.\n\n**********\n\nThe apartment Jamie and I shared on Elysium was small. Deliberately so.\n\nAfter eternities of grandiose cosmic horror, after breaking reality and reshaping dimensions, we'd chosen a three-bedroom unit overlooking a market district where newly freed souls were learning how to barter again. Learning that transactions didn't have to end in suffering.\n\nI was making coffee. Actual coffee. Lady Illkeserod had smuggled beans out of the cosmic seed, and someone had figured out how to grow them in Elysium's weird not-quite-physics. The smell filled the kitchen.\n\nJamie sat at the table, sketching. She'd been drawing a lot lately. Landscapes that didn't exist yet. Faces of people she half-remembered. Creative death, she called it. Killing possibilities to birth new ones.\n\n"Xavier asked me yesterday if Tiffany would remember him," she said without looking up.\n\nI poured two cups. Set one in front of her. "What'd you say?"\n\n"That I didn't know." She finally looked up. "Because I don't. The reshuffling... it's going to change things. Memories. Histories. She might remember a different Xavier. Or no Xavier at all."\n\n"That bothering him?"\n\n"I think it terrifies him." She took a sip. Smiled slightly. "Which is why he keeps joking about it."\n\nI sat down across from her. The morning light filtered through the window—Elysium didn't have a sun, just ambient luminescence from the Pleroma itself—but it was close enough. Felt real enough.\n\n"You think we're making the right call?" I asked.\n\n"Going back inside?"\n\n"Yeah."\n\nShe set her cup down. Looked at me with those eyes that had seen me die a thousand times and loved me anyway.\n\n"I think," she said slowly, "that Sam spent eternity as a prisoner. Used. Hollowed out. And when he finally got free, he chose to stop existing as a person because the weight of it all was too much." She paused. "I think there are trillions of souls in that prison who don't know they're prisoners. Who think the suffering is normal. Natural. Deserved."\n\n"And we can help them."\n\n"And we're the only ones who can." She reached across the table. Took my hand. "You've been every version of yourself across infinite timelines. You've lived as a child dying of poison, as an old man haunted by regrets, as a father in a loop designed to break you. You know what it's like to be trapped in your own life."\n\n"So do you."\n\n"Exactly." Her grip tightened. "We've earned this, Stephen. Not the suffering... the knowledge. The awareness. We know what freedom tastes like because we remember what captivity felt like. Who better to guide them out?"\n\nI thought about Xavier. About Hannah. About everyone still inside, still unaware. About TJ, Elara and Mauve, and all the others looping through their scripted lives, thinking it was real.\n\nAbout the uncountable others of divine sparks, waiting to wake up.\n\n"It's going to take forever," I said.\n\n"We've got time."\n\n"It's going to be hard. Watching them loop. Seeing the corruption up close."\n\n"We've survived worse."\n\n"We'll have to fake our deaths eventually. Every century or so. Keep moving. Keep pretending."\n\nShe smiled. "You say that like it's a bad thing. I've always wanted to be a vampire."\n\nI laughed. Couldn't help it. "You're insane."\n\nHannah appeared in the doorway. Still forgetting to knock. Still not caring. "You two are disgustingly cute. It's nauseating."\n\n"Good morning to you too," Jamie said.\n\n"Xavier wants to know if we can set up a Discord server that works across dimensional barriers. Something about staying in touch with people on Elysium while we're inside."\n\nI blinked. "Why not... that’s actually not a terrible idea."\n\n"Don't tell him that. His ego's already insufferable." Hannah drifted into the room, literally hovering an inch off the ground. "But seriously. You two decided yet? Council meeting's in an hour."\n\nJamie looked at me. I looked at her.\n\n"Yeah," I said. "We decided."\n\n**********\n\nThe council chamber was packed.\n\nEvery pantheon. Every resistance god from other realities. Every freed god who'd been sleeping under Etheris's substrate for millennia. The room hummed with divine power, the air thick enough to taste.\n\nChernobog stood at the center, his void-black form absorbing light. "We're here to discuss the Shepherd Initiative. Stephen, Jamie, Xavier, and Hannah have volunteered to insert themselves into the cosmic seed prison as long-term monitoring agents."\n\nMurmurs rippled through the crowd.\n\nThoth stood. "The logistics alone are staggering. Remember, Etheris is a null-magic reality. To rewrite it’s reality, we can’t rely on native magic; we have to use divine intervention to siphon power from other realities just to impart the changes. We’re talking about forcing power into the physics ruled vacuum to create false histories and manage paradoxes. The Mandela Effect ripples from that kind of friction could destabilize the entire construct."\n\n“Not to mention needing to do this every tens to hundreds of thousands of years. As humans rise in technology they will start to explore, they can’t be let to leave the solar system. When that happens, we’ll have to reset the system back.” Lady Illkeserod interjected.\n\n"And don't look to the center for help," Nyarlathotep cut in, gesturing vaguely toward the distant, piping void. "Azathoth is the blind, deaf, idiot god. He creates, he destroys, he pipes. He doesn't do edits."\n\nHe leaned forward, a grin splitting his face. "We aren't dealing with a prison construct anymore. There's no substrate to hack. You're talking about impacting reality itself. Without a sentient creator at the wheel to authorize the changes, you'll need every scrap of power in this room just to make a dent. The sheer instability of it... the chaos..." He shivered theatrically. "Delectable."\n\n"Your concerns are noted," Chernobog said dryly. "Lady Illkeserod, the proposal?"\n\nShe pulled up projections. The solar system rotated above us, then zoomed in. Earth. North America. Florida.\n\n"We insert the family unit in Melbourne, Florida. Year 2026, local timeline. They'll need cover identities with sufficient wealth and influence to operate freely."\n\nA new image appeared. A distinguished gentleman, sharp suit, casino floor behind him. "Jamie's father. Kenji Nakamura. Half-Japanese, half-Korean casino magnate. Net worth approximately 140.3 billion."\n\nChernobog nodded from the head of the table. "I will be inhabiting this avatar. The role of a shadowy, reclusive power broker suits my... disposition."\n\nAnother image. A woman with kind eyes and traditional dress. "Jamie's mother. Waniya Nakamura, née Whitecloud. Cree shaman and spiritual advisor. A vessel for Lady Osilari."\n\n"It opens a door," Lady Illkeserod explained. "Because these identities are established as eccentric and reclusive, Chernobog and Osilari can jump in and out of Etheris as needed. They can provide heavy divine backup without needing to explain extended absences to the neighbors. If Kenji Nakamura disappears for six months on business, the NPCs won't blink."\n\n"And my parents?" I asked.\n\nBenji stood. Grinned. "That would be us."\n\nLady Illkeserod nodded. "Benjamin and Illara Castellan. Artists, philanthropists, and trust fund liberals. Two sets of crazy parents, no one will blink an eye when you go all insane stories."\n\n"We get to be inside when needed as well," Benji said, excitement clear in his voice. "As avatars. Limited power, but present. We can help guide things from the parental angle. Plus no more late night summoning rituals that cause your eyes to turn inside out."\n\nI looked at Jamie. She was smiling.\n\nChernobog gestured, and four symbols appeared above our heads. They didn't look like code. They looked ancient. Primal.\n\n"Etheris is back to normal reality now," Chernobog said. "And the cosmic seed is just a physical space contained within it. That means you won't be using admin privileges or hacks. You're going to be accessing your original divine powers. The ones you held in the time before time, before your reality was consumed by Yaldabaoth."\n\n"The ritual will allow you to tap into the fundamental essences of Etheris," Lady Illkeserod explained. "You'll have a direct line to the reality outside the prison to fuel your abilities inside it. It will take time and practice to re-acclimate—you haven't flexed these specific muscles in eons—but time is the one thing you'll have in abundance."\n\n"In the beginning, this is how they will manifest," Chernobog continued. "But where you go from there... who knows."\n\nHe pointed to my symbol. "Stephen. Lord of Wisdom, King of Life. Initially, it will be accelerated healing and flashes of intuition. But as you practice, you'll regain the ability to know what needs to be known."\n\nHe shifted his gaze to Jamie. "Jamie. Creativity and Death. You'll start by seeing the threads—the connections between souls. You'll be able to guide the awakening or ease the suffering of those ready to rest."\n\n"Xavier. Mischief, Chaos, and Laughter. You'll be able to bend probability. Find the cracks in physics. Keep things interesting."\n\nXavier grinned, testing the weight of the power in his mind. "Now we're talking."\n\nNyarlathotep gave Xavier a knowing glance from across the room. “You and I will need to talk about some things.”\n\n"And Hannah. Serenity and Joy. You are the anchor. When the chaos gets too loud, you provide the balance. The emotional gravity that holds the family—and the mission—together."\n\nHannah's symbol glowed soft and warm. She nodded. "I remember this feeling."\n\n"It will feel like remembering a language you spoke as a child," Lady Illkeserod said. "Awkward at first. Then, fluent."\n\n"The ritual will be relatively quick and painless. But for all of this… reshuffling will take approximately 48 hours to completely manifest," Lady Illkeserod continued. "During that time, we'll smooth the timeline, adjust memories, create backstories that withstand scrutiny. Some awakened souls will notice the Mandela Effect—misremembered histories, timeline inconsistencies—but most NPCs will accept the changes as normal."\n\n"What about Tiffany?" Xavier asked, his voice tighter than usual. "I mean... is she going to remember me? Or do I have to start from scratch? Just walk up to her like a stranger and hope she likes me all over again?"\n\n"We've accounted for the variables," Lady Illkeserod said, adjusting an Akashic ripple. "She is currently rostered in your third-period Spanish class. She remains an active mod on your Discord server. And, according to the substrate logs, she still plays Roblox with you every Thursday night."\n\nXavier let out a breath he’d been holding. "Okay. Okay, that's... good."\n\n"So you have no excuse," Hannah whispered, leaning into him. "You actually have to ask her out this time. And hey, with probability manipulation at your fingertips, you could literally tweak the odds to ensure she says yes."\n\nXavier recoiled, looking genuinely offended. "No. Absolutely not. That's cheating. That's... cosmic gaslighting." He straightened his hoodie. "If I'm going to do this, I'm doing it au naturale."\n\n"Noble," Nyarlathotep drawled. "Boring, but noble."\n\n"Is there any further discussion?" Chernobog asked.\n\nSilence stretched out. Heavy. Thick.\n\nI looked around the room. At the gods who had fought, bled, and died to get us here. At Tiamat, whose scales still bore scars from the war. At Yeshua, who looked tired but resolved. At my children, who were volunteering to go back into a cage they had just escaped.\n\nJamie’s hand tightened around mine. Her knuckles were white, her grip the only thing anchoring me to the floor. We weren't just voting on a mission; we were voting to change the nature of existence for the second time in as many months.\n\nChernobog let the silence hang there, giving us all a moment to feel the shape of what we were about to do. To measure the cost.\n\n"Then we vote," he said finally, his voice low but filling every corner of the room. "All in favor of the Shepherd Initiative?"\n\nHands rose.\n\nBut not just hands. Claws. Tentacles. Wings of fire and shadow. An overwhelming majority, a sea of divine assent rising to meet the challenge.\n\n"Opposed?"\n\nNyarlathotep raised one hand lazily. "For the record. Not because I think you'll fail. Because I think you'll succeed, and that'll be hilarious."\n\n"Your support is overwhelming," I said.\n\n"I try."\n\nChernobog nodded. "The Shepherd Initiative is approved. The ritual will begin immediately."	1	568	f	2026-02-27 12:32:56.962202-05	2026-02-28 23:53:20.13484-05	normal	\N
