 to go back into a cage they had just escaped.\n\nJamie’s hand tightened around mine. Her knuckles were white, her grip the only thing anchoring me to the floor. We weren't just voting on a mission; we were voting to change the nature of existence for the second time in as many months.\n\nChernobog let the silence hang there, giving us all a moment to feel the shape of what we were about to do. To measure the cost.\n\n"Then we vote," he said finally, his voice low but filling every corner of the room. "All in favor of the Shepherd Initiative?"\n\nHands rose.\n\nBut not just hands. Claws. Tentacles. Wings of fire and shadow. An overwhelming majority, a sea of divine assent rising to meet the challenge.\n\n"Opposed?"\n\nNyarlathotep raised one hand lazily. "For the record. Not because I think you'll fail. Because I think you'll succeed, and that'll be hilarious."\n\n"Your support is overwhelming," I said.\n\n"I try."\n\nChernobog nodded. "The Shepherd Initiative is approved. The ritual will begin immediately."\n\n**********\n\nThey took us to the highest point on Elysium. A platform that jutted out over the Pleroma itself, where the boundary between existence and potential was thin enough to cut.\n\nThe entire council assembled. Not just the leaders—everyone. Uncountable gods, demigods, freed souls, and cosmic entities. All of them channeling their power toward a single point.\n\nToward us.\n\nJamie stood to my left. Xavier to my right. Hannah in front of me, her hand in mine. We formed a square, a family unit, a geometric anchor point.\n\nChernobog began the incantation. Old words. Older than language. The syllables tasted like starlight and entropy.\n\nThe other gods joined in. Layer upon layer of divine will, folding reality like origami.\n\nI felt my history rewriting. Not erasing—never erasing—but... adding. A childhood in Florida with parents who were actually Benji and Lady Illkeserod in disguise. Summers at Jamie's father's casinos. Learning about my heritage from Jamie's mother around sacred fires. Meeting my friends in middle school, their tragic deaths recently, all of it flowing together like water finding its level.\n\nThe memories weren't false. They were additional. Parallel truths woven into Etheris itself.\n\nJamie gasped. I looked over. Her eyes were glowing, threads of light spreading from her like spider silk. Death and creativity, entwining, becoming one.\n\nXavier laughed. Chaos rippled around him, probability curves bending into pretzels.\n\nHannah hummed. The sound was peace itself. Joy made audible.\n\nAnd I... I knew things. Could see the connections, the patterns, the divine sparks buried under layers of NPC coding. Could feel every divine spark in the cosmic seed prison, all of them of them, waiting.\n\nThe casting reached its crescendo.\n\nReality split.\n\nWe stood in two places at once—on the platform in Elysium and in a bedroom in Florida, morning light streaming through windows that were real this time, really real, with a sun and everything.\n\nChernobog's voice echoed across dimensions: "Go with our blessing. Return with our hope."\n\nBenji grinned at me from the crowd. "See you on the other side, kiddo."	3	570	f	2026-02-27 12:32:56.962202-05	2026-02-28 23:55:32.26533-05	normal	\N
557	135	5	Waking in the Prison	Stephen awakens in a bedroom in Florida, at first experiencing a dreamlike disconnection from his cosmic memories, then suddenly remembering everything with perfect clarity. Jamie confirms she remembers too, and they hear Xavier and Hannah downstairs, adjusting to their new reality as a family reunited inside the cosmic seed prison.	**********\n\nI slowly opened my eyes.\n\nI am laying in my bed, cocooned by the warmth of my wife's presence and enveloped in the exuberant chatter of my teen son talking to his friends on Roblox comes in from the living room. Laying there I was hit with a profound sense of normalcy.\n\nIt is in this moment, with the weight of cosmic responsibilities lifted from my weary shoulders, that I find solace. Slowly things start to fade, the adventures and grandeur of S Corp, once vivid and tangible in my dreams, now seem like distant wisps of memory, ethereal and intangible.\n\nThen they are gone. Forgotten just like last night’s dinner.\n\nThere is true happiness in this simplicity, there is a tinge of contentment. A return to a life unburdened by the weighty obligations that come with commanding an empire. It is a welcome respite, a chance to revel in the beauty of the ordinary and rediscover the joys that lie within the realm of everyday life.\n\nMy wife rolls over with a huge grin on her face.\n\nAnd then, the terror hit me.\n\nNo!\n\nThe scream tore out of my throat before I could stop it. A raw, desperate sound of a man realizing he’s been dragged back into the cage. I sat up, heart hammering against my ribs, waiting for the sterile walls of the simulation to close in.\n\nJamie's hand was on my shoulder. Warm. Real.\n\n"It's happening again," I said quietly.\n\nShe nodded. Squeezed my shoulder. "But different this time."\n\nI sat up. Looked around. The bedroom was... bigger. Two stories, she'd said. The house we'd designed together during the reshuffling. Oak floors. High ceilings. Art on the walls that Jamie had painted—some of it in Elysium, some of it here, some of it in timelines that didn't exist anymore.\n\nMorning light slanted through the blinds. Real sunlight. From a real sun. Inside a pocket prison sealed off from the rest of reality.\n\nFunny how that worked.\n\nKate Bush drifted up from somewhere downstairs. Running Up That Hill. Xavier must be awake.\n\nI closed my eyes and reached for the edges of my mind, expecting the fog of a dream to settle in. Expecting the details of Elysium to dissolve into vague feelings.\n\nInstead, it hit me like a physical blow. The clarity. The Council room. The smell of ozone and entropy. The texture of the chalice holding Sam’s spark. It wasn't fading; it was locking into place. Hard. Permanent. I remembered the war, the peace, and the choice that brought us here. It wasn't just a story I told myself; it was the history written in my bones.\n\n"You remember everything?" Jamie asked, her eyes searching mine.\n\n"Yeah," I breathed, the relief washing over me. "You?"\n\n"Every timeline. Every version. Every loop." She smiled. "And this one feels... right."\n\nI swung my legs out of bed. My feet touched the floor. Solid. Real. The kind of real that you could trust because you knew exactly how fake it could be.\n\nThrough the wall, I heard Hannah's voice. "Xavier, don't you dare tell Tiffany that!"\n\n"Tell her what? That her best friend's brother is actually a chaos god with reality-bending powers? That sounds like a great icebreaker."\n\n"You're impossible."\n\n"That's literally my divine portfolio, yeah."\n\nJamie laughed. Soft. The sound filled something in my chest I didn't know was empty.\n\n"They're adjusting well," she said.\n\n"They're teenagers. Adjusting is what they do." I stood. Stretched. Felt the power coiled inside me—healing, wisdom, omniscience carefully throttled down to avoid breaking everything. "You think Benjamin and Illara are awake yet?"\n\n"Your parents?" She grinned. "Probably terrorizing the activity 