577	138	8	Aspolin's Choice and Sacrifice	On a battlefield of obsidian glass, the protagonist Stephen (formerly Aspolin) breaks cosmic law to save his beloved Velistra from a twisted war angel, erasing the creature entirely. This act of forbidden un-creation marks a turning point but costs him deeply.	The Gospel of Judas: Aspolin Unbroken\n\nThe first sensation is pressure, then a wet, searing heat.\n\nThe stench of iron, burnt ozone, and something infinitely ancient fills my lungs before I can even open my eyes. I am inside something. Something wrong. My will, the core of what I am, coalesces into a single, sharp point of power and I push.\n\nReality shreds.\n\nI erupt outward in a shower of gore and steaming viscera, collapsing onto a battlefield of cracked, obsidian glass. I gasp, sucking in air that tastes like static and ash. The sound hits me next—a symphony of shrieks, the wet thud of angelic bodies hitting the ground, and somewhere to my left, the defiant, guttural roar of Tiamat.\n\nI stagger to my feet, the cooling blood of the war angel I just burst from clinging to my skin. My eyes scan the chaos, seeking one thing. One person.\n\nVelistra.\n\nThere. I see her. She's not fighting one of Yaldabaoth's pristine, golden-skinned angels; she's fighting one of them. A true thing of the outer chaos, a churning nightmare of glistening black tentacles, whirring rusted-metal limbs, and weeping, stitched-together flesh. It’s a walking cacophony of metallic clicks and wet, tearing sounds.\n\nIt’s fast, impossibly so, but she's faster.\n\nI see her weave, a blade of pure, solidified will in her hand. She ducks under a limb that is more grinding gears than flesh and severs a greasy, black tentacle at its root. The creature emits a high-pitched, metallic shriek and its wounds spray a black, steaming ichor.\n\nVelistra spins away, but the acidic blood splashes across her left arm.\n\nHer scream is sharp, a sound that cuts through the din of the battle. I see her skin and armor boil and dissolve instantly, the flesh melting away like wax, exposing the white of her bone and the raw, twitching muscle beneath. Her face contorts in a mask of pure agony, but she doesn't stop, her jaw set, her eyes blazing with hate as she tries to find the creature's core.\n\nHer pain is a physical knife in my own gut. "Velistra!" I roar, and I finally lunge, pouring all my will into a single forward motion.\n\nBut the creature... it was feinting. It used its own wound as a trap, anticipating my move, my distraction.\n\nAs I lunge, it ignores me. It moves on her.\n\nA single, piston-like appendage—a grotesque hybrid of flensed flesh, exposed nerve, and corroded steel—erupts from its central mass. Velistra tries to twist, her ruined arm hanging in shreds, but she's a fraction too slow, her motion compromised by the searing pain.\n\nIt doesn't just punch through her. It violates her.\n\nThe appendage slams into her stomach with a sickening, wet crunch of pulverized armor and shattering bone. It hammers clean through her, bursting from her back in a spray of bright red blood and silvery spinal fluid. It lifts her from her feet, pinning her in mid-air. I see, with sickening clarity, metallic barbs on the appendage unfurl inside her body, anchoring it deep within her, tearing her apart from the inside out.\n\nTime stops.\n\nThe battlefield, Tiamat, the golden armies of Yaldabaoth—they all fade to a dull, gray roar. The only thing that exists is Velistra. I see the rictus of a scream that won’t come, her body locked in pure, silent agony. Paralyzed. The appendage tore through her spine.\n\nA blind, white-hot rage consumes me. But it isn't just rage. It's love. It is the desperate, star-eating inferno of a soul refusing to lose its other half. It’s an inferno that burns away every oath, every law, every shred of who I am supposed to be. All that matters is her. All that matters is the metal and flesh in her.\n\nI don't care about the cost. I don't care about the consequences, the schisms it will cause, or the cosmic laws of this reality.\n\nOur bond, the love that weaves my soul to hers, becomes a fulcrum. It is an anchor point outside the laws of this dimension, a singular, perfect truth that gives me the leverage to do the impossible. Fueled by that white-hot certainty, I reach out.\n\nNot with my hands, not with my will, but with the raw, screaming need of my soul, amplified and focused by the love I bear her. I reach past the physical battlefield, past the divine energy of the gods around me, and I touch the forbidden. I touch the source code. The Akashic ledger. The tapestry that records all of "is."\n\nI find the thread of the creature. It is a greasy, chaotic knot in the pattern, a stain of "outer wrongness" woven into the fabric of "here."\n\nAnd I pull.\n\nThis isn't magic. It isn't power. It is heresy. It is the antithesis of everything we are fighting for. Creation, destruction... these are natural cycles. This... this is un-creation. It is the ultimate lie, a void given form, a power that screams of the anti-reality we are fighting. It is the power to make reality lie.\n\nBut my love for her is a greater truth, and in this one, singular, impossible moment, it gives me the strength to overpower the cosmos.\n\nA roar rips from my throat, but it is not just sound. It is the sound of my own soul tearing, the shriek of a fundamental law being broken, and the defiant declaration that she will not die.\n\nI seize the creature's very concept of 'is', 'was', and 'will be', and I clench.\n\nThe thing doesn't die. It doesn't explode. It doesn't dissolve.\n\nIt vanishes.\n\nThe process is a silent, visual scream. Reality itself recoils in horror. The obsidian glass where it stood is suddenly clean, un-splattered by its own ichor. The air, which had been full of its metallic shriek, snaps back into a deafening silence, forgetting the sound it just carried. Light bends around the space it occupied, as if it had never been there to be illuminated.\n\nIt simply… ceases to have ever been.\n\nA cold, hollow, sickening void rushes into the space it left. I feel it wash over me, the chilling emptiness of an absolute paradox. I have punched a hole in the fabric of existence, and for a terrifying second, I feel the true, cold nothingness on the other side.\n\nAs reality snaps back into its new, altered state, the effects cascade. The cause is gone, so the effect must follow. The appendage of metal and flesh vanishes from Velistra's body. The gaping, pulverized wound... seals. New flesh, armor, and bone knit together in an instant, as if they were never torn asunder.\n\nBut the fix is imperfect. The universe hates a paradox.\n\nVelistra collapses as the creature disappears, her body shuddering, gasping my name. The wound is gone, but her nerves remember. The acidic burns are erased, but her arm still screams. The pain is a phantom, a scar left by a ghost, an echo of an event that is now, and forever, a lie. She is healed, but she is still trapped in the agony of a moment that never happened.\n\nShe's shuddering, her breath coming in ragged, dry sobs, gasping my name. "Aspolin... Aspolin..."\n\nThe phantom agony still racks her body. I’m at her side in an instant, sliding to my knees, pulling her up into my arms, shielding her with my own form.\n\n"I'm here. I'm here, Velistra," I whisper, my voice raw. "Are you...?"\n\n"It still hurts," she gasps, her hand clutching her stomach where the wound was. Her armor is pristine, untouched, but her eyes are wide with the memory of being torn apart. "The memory... it's... gods, it burns."\n\n"I know. I'm sorry. Just breathe. Stay with me," I plead, pulling her tight against my chest.\n\nShe looks up at me, her eyes clear and sharp despite the agony, and her gloved hand comes up to grip the front of my armor. Her knuckles are white. "You... you felt it, didn't you?" The words are a terrified whisper, meant only for me. "That...nothing. That power. Stephen, you shouldn't have. Not even for this. Not for me."\n\nHer voice isn't just an accusation; it’s terrified for me. She knows what I just touched, what I just channeled.\n\n"I wasn't going to lose you," I say, the words an unbreakable vow, a truth more fundamental than any cosmic law. I press my forehead to hers for one stolen second, a single, desperate anchor of intimacy in the middle of this hell.\n\nIt's only then I realize... it's quiet.\n\nI lift my head, breaking the contact, my eyes scanning our surroundings. The paradox didn't just erase the creature. It sent a ripple of "wrongness" outward, a shockwave of absolute negation.\n\nFor a hundred yards in every direction, the battle has simply... stopped.\n\nDozens of bodies, both Yaldabaoth's golden-winged angels and the twisted, chthonic horrors of chaos, lie dead. They weren't just killed; they were stilled. Their forms are frozen in the middle of a lunge, a strike, their life-force extinguished by the cold, hollow wave that radiated from my act. We are standing in a sudden, grisly circle of peace, a graveyard carved from a lie.\n\nThe sounds of the wider war—the shrieks, the explosions—crash back in, but they are distant now, held at bay by this bubble of silence.\n\nThis is the stillness that allows the others to find us.\n\nOne by one, they emerge from the raging chaos that still rings the edge of our clearing, drawn to this impossible calm. Hecate, her face a mask of cold, appraising fury as she takes in the scene. Tiamat, bleeding from a dozen wounds, her massive form heaving. Chernobog, a silent shadow, his presence more a void than a man. Finally, Lucifer and Lilith, back-to-back, their ethereal light burning cold and sharp.\n\nThey form a defensive perimeter around us, a circle of exhausted, panting gods standing in the center of the unnatural graveyard I just created.\n\nWe take a collective breath. A terrible, unspoken headcount begins.\n\nA silence stretches, louder than any angelic war-cry.\n\nBenji. He's not here.\n\nLady Illkeserod sees it the same moment I do. Her hand flies to her mouth. She stumbles back, her form flickering as if physically struck, her foot sliding on the viscera-slicked glass.\n\nA terrible, unspoken headcount begins.\n\nBenji. He's not here.\n\nLady Illkeserod’s head snaps up, her eyes wide. The blood drains from her face, leaving it a waxy, translucent white. "No," she whispers, the word a fragile, hollow thing. Her gaze darts wildly around the circle of gods, then out into the raging battle, as if she expects him to just walk out of the smoke. " Benji? Benji!"\n\nHer voice cracks on his name, rising from a call to a desperate shriek that cuts through the air. "Where is he?! He was supposed to be... he was right..." The realization hits her, not as a thought, but as a physical blow. She staggers, her hands flying to her head. "No... no, not again! He can't be—"\n\n"Lady Illkeserod," Tiamat rumbles, her voice a deep vibration. She steps toward her, her massive, scaled hand moving to rest on her shoulder. "Calm yourself. We will find him. He is not—"\n\n"Panic is a luxury, Lady," Lilith cuts in, her voice sharp and cold as she moves to her other side. "He is strong. Save your grief until you know it's warranted. We fight, we find."\n\nLady Illkeserod explodes.\n\n"DON'T. TOUCH. ME!"\n\nShe shoves them both back with a percussive, kinetic wave of pure, blue-white energy. The force sends Tiamat skidding back a step and makes Lilith's form shimmer. Lady Illkeserod is vibrating, her entire body wreathed in a power that steams in the cold air, her silhouette flickering violently between the two women she once was.\n\nHer head, a mask of absolute, soul-tearing grief and terror, snaps toward me.\n\nIn a fraction of a second, the grief vanishes. It is not replaced. It is vaporized, super heated and forged into a pure, unadulterated fury that feels hotter than a sun.\n\nShe knows. She doesn't need proof. She sees me, whole and untouched, standing beside a recovering Velistra. She sees the unnatural circle of absolute death I carved into reality. She knows what I did. She knows what I used. And she knows, with the certainty of a god, what I didn't do.\n\nShe knows my tunnel vision, my desperate, forbidden act to save Velistra, is why Benji’s position was overrun.\n\n"YOU," she snarls, the word a curse, a sentence, a final judgment. Her finger, trembling with a rage that could shatter mountains, points directly at my face. "You did this."\n\nHer voice drops from a shriek to a low, terrifying hiss that carries across the clearing. "You were supposed to be his support. You were so busy saving her... you let them take him. He is gone... HE IS GONE BECAUSE OF YOU!"\n\nHer rage hits me like a physical wave, a telepathic storm of her grief, her bottomless loss, and her absolute, burning hatred. It is the force of her memory, of the first time she lost everything, of the world that was torn from her. The world of obsidian glass and shrieking angels dissolves, and I am shattered, plunging backward into the depths of her past.\n\n**********\n\nHer fury unhooks me from time.\n\nThe battlefield vanishes, my senses ripped from my own body and plunged into a memory that isn't mine. I am an observer, a ghost in someone else's past.\n\nThe world I see is… perfect.	7	591	f	2026-02-27 12:34:17.525318-05	2026-03-01 01:10:02.692877-05	normal	\N
578	138	9	Lady Illkeserod's Memory: A World Consumed	Lady Illkeserod's grief and rage force Stephen into a traumatic memory of her past: a world of perfect divine light corrupted and consumed by Yaldabaoth's infection, ending with only Benji's intervention saving her last followers.	It's a pastoral realm, serene and untouched. The air is warm, not with mere heat, but with a palpable, golden contentment. It's a world without fear. I see a farmer in a nearby field, his skin with a faint, bronze sheen, waving to a glimmering naiad who surfaces in a roadside irrigation canal. Gods walk among mortals here, their presence as normal as the sun. And the magic... it's everywhere. The light itself is thick, a visible, honey-gold energy that flows like liquid from the leaves of the trees, bathing the landscape in a divine glow.\n\nAnd in the middle of this perfect peace, I see them.\n\nTwo young girls, riding horses down a dirt road. I recognize them from the fragments of lore I've pieced together. Selyr and Keserod. Lady Illkeserod, before she was one.\n\nThey are impossibly young—Selyr perhaps eleven, Keserod nine. They are dressed in robes of blue satin trimmed with silver lace, their hair covered by matching veils. They are Children of Bythos, and I can sense the blood-magic, the celestial power, imbued in their very being, thrumming from them as naturally as a heartbeat.\n\nThey are supposed to be back at the temple, deep in their studies, but they've snuck out. And now, they are deep in a game of make-believe.\n\nThey are pretending to be noblewomen. Their backs are ramrod straight, their chins held high, and they wear identical expressions of serene, haughty seriousness. As I watch, Keserod, the younger, catches her older sister's eye, and the mask slips. A mischievous grin flashes across her face before she stifles it, quickly regaining her "noble" composure. Selyr just barely manages to roll her eyes, a silent, older-sister rebuke that is equally full of contained laughter.\n\nThey are the picture of innocence, powerful beyond measure, but still just children finding a moment of freedom in a world that already expects so much from them.\n\nTheir game, however, seems to be winding down as they slow their horses, not to enjoy the morning, but for a conference. They drift into a sun-dappled clearing, the honey-light of their world catching the silver threads in their veils. The play-acting seriousness shifts into something genuinely solemn.\n\n“We need to talk,” Keserod, the younger, says. Her voice is small but firm.\n\n“Okay,” Selyr replies, her own voice laced with a worry that doesn’t belong in a child. She slides from her saddle onto a rock.\n\n“I don’t want you riding with me anymore,” the nine-year-old says, her eyes fixed on the ground. “It’s too dangerous for you to do that sort of thing alone.”\n\nThe older girl nods slowly. “There are many dangers out there.”\n\nAs they speak, their game forgotten, they glance back toward the road. The pair of merchants with carts laden high with produce had passed them moments before. Now, they've been stopped by a man in a simple, gray robe.\n\nA "wrong priest." I can feel the description for him bubble up in my mind, a piece of her knowledge. An acolyte.\n\nThe memory sharpens. Through their shared, heightened senses, I see what they see. The priest is speaking, his words calm, reasonable, and carrying on the warm air. He speaks of "his lord and savior," a foreign name, a new god.\n\nAt first, the merchant just looks annoyed, eager to get to market. But as the priest continues, a change begins.\n\nI'm not watching a magical siphoning. I'm watching a conversion. An infection.\n\nThe merchant’s annoyance shifts to curiosity, then to rapt attention. The acolyte's words are a virus, dismantling the man's beliefs, his history, his very identity, in real-time. A frantic, ugly light starts to burn behind the merchant's eyes. It’s not the peaceful, golden magic of this world; it's a hot, feverish, and hungry light. A crusading spirit.\n\nThe merchant's face, moments ago just a simple man's, is now alight with a terrible, zealous need.\n\n"The false gods of this land must be cleansed," the merchant says, his voice suddenly loud, echoing the acolyte's fervor. "All must know His name!"\n\nThe change is instant, absolute. The merchant abandons his cart, his wares, his entire life's work, leaving it to rot on the side of the road. He falls to his knees, kisses the acolyte's dusty robe, then rises and stands beside him, a new and instant soldier for a foreign god.\n\nThe two girls watch this, their playfulness utterly shattered. This isn't a story. This isn't a game. This is real. The sight of a man so instantly, so joyfully discarding his entire life for this invasive, wrong belief terrifies them to their core.\n\nSelyr grabs the reins of her sister's horse. "Keserod... we have to go. Now."\n\nTheir pretense of being noblewomen is gone, replaced by the naked, wide-eyed fear of children who have just seen a monster in broad daylight. They wheel their horses around and race back toward the temple, their blue robes flapping, the sound of the merchant-turned-zealot chanting his new god's praises fading behind them.\n\nThe memory blurs, accelerating. I am pulled through their lives. The terrified girls racing back to the temple become young women, their faces hardened, the last vestiges of their childhood burned away.\n\nI see flashes, a montage of a generational war fought not just on battlefields, but in the minds and souls of their people. Their power isn't just the vital, celestial "Blood" magic of Bythos; it is something deeper, more complex. They can touch the Akashic Flow.\n\nI watch them in a makeshift sanctuary, standing over a man—a soldier—writhing on a cot. He is not wounded, but his mind is ablaze with the zealous fervor of the "wrong priests." Selyr and Keserod join hands, their eyes closed. They reach into the man's mind, into his personal Akashic record, and begin to disinfect him. I see them meticulously, carefully, attempting to eradicate the idea of the false idol, to scrub the mimetic virus of Yaldabaoth from his spirit and restore his own identity.\n\nThey succeed. The man's frantic chanting stops, his body goes limp, and he opens his eyes—his own eyes—confused, but free.\n\nBut they are only two.\n\nFor every soul they manage to "disinfect," eight more acolytes appear on the street corner. For every soldier they restore, a whole new barracks falls to the infection, its inhabitants joyfully marching off to join the crusade.\n\nI see them fighting, not just the "wrong priests," but the growing, numberless hordes of the infected—neighbors, soldiers, and eventually their own families, all consumed by, and willing to die for, this foreign, false deity. It's a war they are destined to lose, a spiritual plague they cannot possibly contain. They are just two healers in a world dying of an incurable disease, a tide they cannot hold back.\n\nThe memory jumps, slamming me into the final, desperate moment.\n\nThe last temple to Bythos is burning. The sky is no longer honey-gold; it is a bruised, churning purple, torn open. Angels, vast and terrible, terrorize the few survivors. And above them all, I see him. Yaldabaoth. A colossal, deformed god, a thing of shadow and broken light, literally eating the reality, devouring souls and landscape wholesale.\n\nSelyr and Keserod are on the collapsing dais, back-to-back, their blue robes scorched and torn. They are the last bastion. An angel, wreathed in a light that burns, lunges at Selyr. Keserod throws herself in the way, her wand raised. At the same instant, another angel strikes Selyr from behind.\n\nThe fatal blows land simultaneously.\n\nIt is not a choice. It is an instinct. As their bodies are pierced, a deep, ancient power from their shared bloodline—the essence of Bythos himself—awakens. They scream, a single, fused sound. They don't die. They detonate. Their physical forms are obliterated, their two souls, Selyr and Keserod, violently weaving together in a blinding, desperate column of pure light.\n\nThe being that reforms is Lady Illkeserod. She is the last conduit of Bythos in this dying world, a being of two souls born in the fire and ash of her last temple.\n\nShe is powerful. But she is alone, and the war is far from over.\n\nAs the angels and the god-eater turn their attention to this defiant, new spark, she does the only thing she can. She flees. She becomes a shadow, a ghost, taking her power and her grief into the dark corners of her world.\n\nThe memory blurs, accelerating. I am pulled through her life, not in days or years, but in generations.\n\nI see her, a ghost-queen, a shepherd of the damned, gathering the few survivors. She leads the last "uninfected," the "pagans" who still cling to the old gods, into magically-shielded underground caverns and remote, hidden valleys. I see her raising a desperate, failing rebellion, teaching them the magic of Bythos, trying to disinfect the world one soul at a time.\n\nBut the world is too far gone. It is a losing battle.\n\nI see angelic hunting parties, led by the "wrong priests," sweeping the land. I see them finding her hidden sanctuaries and turning them into charnel pits. This isn't war; it's an extermination, a sport. I see angels laughing as they vaporize children, their light a mockery of the golden magic that once defined this realm.\n\nThe memory flashes forward one last time. Centuries have passed. The world is a scorched, zealous husk.\n\nI see Lady Illkeserod, aged not by time but by an infinite, bone-deep weariness. She is on a desolate, frozen coastline on the far side of the world. At her back, a black, churning ocean no ship could survive. Before her stand her last few hundred followers, a ragged band of mortals, their faces etched with despair.\n\nThey are cornered.\n\nThe angelic host descends, a million points of hostile light in the dark sky. At their head, I see him: Metatron, the perfect, cold architect of their suffering. And beside him, so vast he blots out the sky, is Yaldabaoth himself, a roiling mass of shadow and mouths, pulsing with the greedy anticipation of finishing his meal, of devouring the last lights of this world.\n\nThis is the end. There is nowhere left to run, no escape.\n\nLady Illkeserod raises her wand for a final, hopeless stand, a last act of defiance before the end of all things.\n\nJust as Metatron raises his hand to give the final command, a portal of white-gold energy tears open the air in the middle of the pagan camp.\n\nA man I know bursts through. Benji.\n\n“Fuck. I’m too late. I thought this reality wasn’t this infested.”\n\nHe's not a warrior. He's not here to fight. His face is slick with sweat, his eyes wide with a frantic urgency. The portal behind him is already wavering, the strain of punching into this doomed, corrupted reality almost too much for him.\n\nHe's not looking at her; he's looking at her people.\n\n"There's no time!" he shouts over the roar of the ocean and the hum of the angelic host. "I can't fight them—this reality is already consumed! I can only open the door! Go! Now!"\n\nLady Illkeserod stares at him, this stranger, this impossible hope. Then she turns to her people. She makes the choice. She doesn't flee to save herself. She becomes the shepherd one last time.\n\n"Through the portal!" she commands, her voice ringing with the last of her divine authority. "Go! Live!"\n\nShe and Benji hold the portal open, a beacon of escape as Metatron’s armies finally charge. The last of her people stream through. Benji is screaming, the energy failing. "Go, now!"\n\nShe shoves the last of her followers through and, with one final look of absolute hatred at the oncoming darkness, she dives through the white-gold light. Benji seizes her arm, his face a mask of grim determination, and pulls her through as the portal snaps shut behind them, leaving her entire reality to its final, dark consumption.\n\n**********	8	592	f	2026-02-27 12:34:17.525318-05	2026-03-01 01:11:01.276898-05	normal	\N
579	138	10	The Hunt for Benji	Stephen and the allied gods track Lady Illkeserod as she hunts for Benji, finding only gruesome fragments of his body scattered across realities. They discover him in the final moment of his un-making by Yaldabaoth.	The memory of Benji’s hand pulling her to safety dissolves, and I am slammed back into my own body, back to the acrid, static-laced air of this nightmare reality.\n\nAn unknown amount of time has passed.\n\nIt's not the same moment. The battle still rages, a distant, thunderous horizon, but we are no longer in that clearing. We are hunting.\n\nWe have been following Lady Illkeserod. Her fury is still burning, but it is no longer the white-hot explosion aimed at me. It has been forged into something colder, sharper, a terrifying, predatory focus. She seems to know where to go, pulled by a thread only she can feel, a connection to the man who saved her.\n\nAnd it has been a trail of absolute horror.\n\nWe are always one step behind, arriving at each new location only moments too late. At first, it was just pieces of his gear—a discarded gauntlet, its metal warped by an impossible force. Then, two realities over, we found fragments of his divine armor, shattered and scorched, as if he'd been used as a battering ram.\n\nAnd then, in the last clearing... him. A scrap of his cloak, snagged on a crystal shard. And beside it... a hand. Severed. His hand.\n\nWe have been following the grisly breadcrumbs of Benji being torn apart, a protracted, agonizing chase across this entire, broken dimension.\n\nNow, the trail has ended.\n\nThe sickening lull in the air isn't a pause in the war; it's the silence of a finished hunt. Lady Illkeserod's focus is no longer a burning point in the distance. It's fixed on the shattered ziggurat directly in front of us. She knows, we all know, we are finally at the end.\n\n"He's there," Lucifer says, his voice flat, all traces of his usual charm burned away. He points with his chin toward the broken structure.\n\nShe doesn't wait for us. She's already moving, a silent, swift glide across the obsidian plain. We follow, a grim procession of gods, moving toward the last, desperate stand of our friend.\n\nWe arrive in a ruined clearing just below the ziggurat. And we see him.\n\nBenji is... held. Suspended in the air by a field of black energy. Before him, colossal and hideously real, is Yaldabaoth. The anti-reality god-eater is not a vague concept here. It is a roiling, malformed mass of shadow, light, and mouths.\n\nAnd it is feeding.\n\nWe are forced to watch. Forced to bear witness.\n\nA gaping maw, lined with teeth like shattered galaxies, opens on Yaldabaoth's torso. From it, a glistening, whip-like tentacle, impossibly long and tipped with a barbed maw of its own, lashes out. It wraps around Benji’s leg.\n\nThere's a wet rip, a sound that vibrates in my soul, as it tears a huge, steaming chunk of muscle and bone directly from his thigh. Benji’s scream echoes, a sound of agony beyond anything mortal, as the tentacle retracts, depositing the raw flesh into Yaldabaoth's central mouth. The god-eater's hideously distorted body twitches with pleasure.\n\nAnother tentacle, thick as a tree trunk and covered in weeping eye-stalks, wraps around Benji’s chest. It squeezes, slowly, audibly crushing his ribcage. His breath hitches in a ragged, choking gasp, his eyes bulging as the pressure distorts his form. His skin begins to stretch, to bubble, like melting wax, as the divine energy within him strains against the physical compression.\n\nBut that is not the worst of it.\n\nWith every physical violation, a corresponding thread of brilliant, divine light—Benji’s very soul, his spark, the essence of his godhood—is ripped, visibly and agonizingly, from his core. It flows like a river of condensed starlight from the tearing wounds, pulled into the gaping gullet of Yaldabaoth. He is being unmade, not just in flesh, but in the very fabric of his being. His essence is being siphoned, consumed, becoming part of the abhorrent god before us.\n\nBenji is screaming, body and soul, a sound I will never forget. It's the sound of a god, of a friend, being erased from existence, piece by excruciating piece. The terror of his agony, the hopelessness of our witness, is a psychic assault.\n\nA noise erupts from Lady Illkeserod that is not human. It is the sound of two souls, Selyr and Keserod, roaring in a single, unified agony.\n\nShe doesn't hesitate. She doesn't plan. She charges.\n\n"Lady Illkeserod, no!" I shout, but she is gone.\n\nShe becomes a comet of pure, blue-white power, the combined essence of her two halves,  channeling Bythos and the entire Akashic Flow in a single, suicidal torrent. She slams into Yaldabaoth with the force of a collapsing star. The god-eater roars, a sound of genuine pain and surprise, as her desperate attack wounds him, searing his shadowy flesh.\n\nHe recoils, his form destabilizing. With a final, hateful glance, Yaldabaoth retreats, dissolving temporarily from this reality, his meal interrupted.\n\nThe black energy field holding Benji dissipates. What's left of him—mangled, scorched parts of his body and a few, guttering embers of his divine spark—crashes to the ground.\n\nShe is on him in an instant, falling to her knees in the gore. She cradles the ruined pieces, her shoulders shaking. "No," she whispers, the word a prayer. "I won't let you."\n\nShe places her hands over the remnants. The power of Selyr and Keserod, the ancient "Blood" magic, flows from her. It is not a gentle healing. It is a desperate, violent act of creation. She is pouring her own essence into him, forcing the divine sparks to re-ignite, forcing the flesh to knit back together around her own power.\n\nA body reforms. He is whole again, the wounds gone. He looks like Benji.\n\nHe opens his eyes.\n\nHe blinks, his gaze unfocused, then finds her. A look of pure, uncomplicated adoration dawns on his face. It is the simple, innocent worship of a newborn imprinting on its mother, of a creation gazing upon its creator. He doesn't know who she is. He doesn't know who he is. He only knows that she is the one who just gave him life.\n\nA small, content smile touches his lips. He doesn't speak, but his loving, empty gaze is the final, killing blow.\n\nLady Illkeserod freezes, her hands still hovering over him. The man who saved her from a dying universe is gone. This... thing... that looks at her with such perfect, hollow devotion, is all that remains.\n\n**********\n\nWe stand in a frozen tableau, a circle of silent, shell-shocked gods. The only sound is the quiet hiss of the obsidian plain and Lady Illkeserod’s ragged, stifled sobs.\n\nBenji, this new Benji, cocks his head, his expression one of pure, childlike concern. He doesn't understand her grief, only that she is in pain. He reaches out a hesitant hand, not to her face, but to her armored wrist, patting it gently.\n\n"Ilske?" he murmurs, the name a familiar sound without a single memory attached. "Don't be sad, Ilske."\n\nHer face crumples. She pulls away from his touch as if it were fire, a fresh wave of agony washing over her. The love he offers is the very proof of what she has lost.\n\nTiamat turns away, her eyes squeezed shut. Hecate watches with a cold, analytical detachment, as if filing this tragedy away for future reference.\n\nBut Lucifer and Lilith... they are not looking at Lady Illkeserod. They are looking at Benji.\n\nI see them exchange a look, a shared glance of sudden, cold understanding that cuts through the grief.\n\n"Look at him," Lilith says, her voice a low, sharp command. We all turn to her. She is pointing at Benji, at the pure, unwavering, devoted gaze he has fixed on Lady Illkeserod. "He's tethered. His devotion... it's a chain. Remembering even when he has nothing left to remember."\n\nA sick, cold feeling begins to dawn in my stomach.\n\n"She's right," Lucifer says, his voice flat and devoid of its usual warmth. He steps forward, his eyes never leaving the tragic pair. "This is how Sophia did it. This is the power she used."\n\nHe looks at us, at me, and I see the terrible, strategic truth in his gaze.\n\n"The Luminous Cloud wasn't just light and will," Lucifer steps forward, his eyes never leaving the tragic pair. "It was a bond."\n\n"Sophia loved him. He was her twisted, monstrous creation, and she was so... embarrassed... by what she had done. She couldn't bring herself to destroy him."\n\nLilith nods, picking up the thread. "So she hid him. The Cloud wasn't just a prison, Aspolin. It was his own reality. His own private multiverse, a gilded cage where he could rule and she could pretend he was contained."\n\n"Exactly," Lucifer confirms. "Her essence, the Cloud itself, was the shield. Her love—that ashamed, desperate, maternal love—is what powered it. It kept him safe inside his kingdom... and it kept us safe from what he is."\n\nHe gestures to the ravaged battlefield around us, the memory of Benji’s un-making still hanging heavy in the air.\n\n"That thing out there isn't just a god-eater," Lucifer says, his voice low and cold. "It's an avatar of anti-reality. A de-maker. It doesn't just conquer; it consumes, de-making the cosmos in its own twisted image before destroying everything.\n\nBut she was able to stop it from spreading. That's what her love did. It was the anchor, the lock, and the walls, all at once. It's what contained him for all those eternities before he finally broke free."\n\nThe realization sinks into all of us, a shared, horrifying, and undeniable truth. We can't kill the god-eater. We can't erase him as I erased that single, simple terror. He is too big, too fundamental.\n\nBut as I look at the devoted, broken god Benji and the grieving god-creator Illkeserod, I understand.\n\nWe don't have to kill him.\n\nWe can trap him.	9	593	f	2026-02-27 12:34:17.525318-05	2026-03-01 01:12:06.192508-05	normal	\N
133	35	2	Drowning in Cardboard	By mid-April, the home is overrun with boxes and packing supplies as orders overwhelm the operation. The family struggles with exhaustion from the endless grind of fulfillment work, and the protagonist and his wife debate solutions to scale without drawing regulatory scrutiny.	By tax day, April 15th, our once-clean home had morphed into a chaotic labyrinth of boxes, product samples, and packing supplies. Cardboard towers loomed in every room, floor-to-ceiling stacks that made navigating the house feel like weaving through a poorly planned maze. The smell of packing tape and fresh cardboard was so constant it clung to our clothes, and the sound of tape guns ripping across boxes had become the soundtrack of our lives.\n\n“I can’t believe we used to call this a living room,” my wife muttered, stepping over a pile of envelopes.\n\n“It’s not a living room anymore,” my son quipped, popping his head out from behind his desk, now hiding behind a particularly tall stack. “It’s Cardboard Fort HQ. And I’m the king.”\n\nHis humor drew a weary laugh from us, but the reality was less amusing. Our days had turned into an endless grind: scanning, replicating, boxing, and shipping, punctuated only by trips to the UPS store and brief moments of sleep. What had started as a thrilling venture now felt like a treadmill we couldn’t escape.\n\n“Making free money is a lot harder than I thought,” I admitted one evening, wiping sweat from my brow as I sealed another package.\n\n“Yeah, turns out capitalism is exhausting,” my wife said, handing me another roll of tape.\n\nThe orders poured in, overwhelming our Matter Replicator. The machine whirred non-stop, it’s once-fascinating hum now a constant reminder of the pressure we were under. I spent my days hunched over spreadsheets and order queues, trying to make sense of the chaos, while my wife coordinated packing and shipping like a general commanding an army of one. My son pitched in where he could, though he mostly enjoyed the novelty of living in a house that doubled as a warehouse.\n\nLate one night, as I stared at yet another batch of orders, my wife walked in, her arms full of freshly printed packing labels. She dropped them onto my desk with a dramatic thud.\n\n“Another hundred orders,” she said, her voice heavy with exhaustion. “We’re need to pause production a bit to replicate some more tape. Too bad it can’t do the same thing for our sanity.”\n\nI leaned back, rubbing my temples. “We’ve come so far already. From nothing to a thriving business. But this pace... it’s killing us.”\n\n“It’s not just the pace,” she shot back, her frustration bubbling over. “I can’t live like this. There are boxes in the bathroom, boxes in the bedroom! I’m claustrophobic, and every time I open a closet, I half-expect an avalanche.”\n\n“We need a solution,” I said finally, my voice heavy with resignation. “What if we let Amazon handle fulfillment? You know, use their logistics network. We ship them the products, and they take care of the rest—packing, shipping, delivery. It would free us up to focus on scaling.”\n\nMy wife gave me a skeptical look, crossing her arms. “That’s a nice idea in theory, but have you thought this through? Amazon doesn’t just take your stuff and ship it, no questions asked. They do deep dives into sourcing and background checks. Are you ready to explain to them where we got all this from? You’re lucky we’ve only had to manage the orders ourselves so far.”\n\nI frowned, the reality of her words sinking in. “I heard about that,” I admitted. “They’d probably want invoices, supplier details—proof everything’s legit.”\n\n“Exactly,” she said, her tone firm. “Do you think they’ll believe we’re just a mom-and-pop operation with advanced, one-of-a-kind manufacturing capabilities? If they start digging too deep, it could raise questions we don’t want to answer.”\n\n“Okay,” I said, throwing my hands up in defeat. “No Amazon Logistics. For now.”\n\n“For now?” she repeated,