d’s eyes lit up, the intensity in them shifting from anger to determination. “Yes! That’s it! Those mother fuckers couldn’t wipe it all. They missed a piece. Godddamit you’ve got to fight, Stacey. You’ve got to fucking hold on to it.”\n\nShe wrapped her arms around herself, her voice trembling. “I’m holding onto as much as I can, Todd. I’m scared. Not of them—of you. I don’t want to lose you to this madness.”\n\nTodd reached for her, his expression softening, but his voice remained firm. “You won’t lose me. Not if we stick together. You’ve got to see the truth, Stacey. You’ve got to help me fight it.”\n\nStacey looked up at him, her eyes glistening. “I don’t know if I can. I’m barely holding on as it is.”\n\nThe trailer was still, the silence heavy with the weight of unspoken words and mounting tension. Stacey’s defiance hung in the air like a fragile ember, flickering against the storm of Todd’s fury. She stood her ground, even as the walls of their cramped home seemed to close in, the oppressive energy pressing against her chest.\n\nTodd’s face rapidly twisted with a primal rage, his eyes wild and unfocused. His knuckles blanched as his fists tightened, trembling with the effort of holding himself back. “No. No. No. Don’t you do that gaslighting shit at me. You think this is a game, Stacey?” he spat, his voice low and venomous. “You think you can just stand there and pretend you don’t see what’s happening? They’re out there, rewriting everything, and you—” He jabbed a finger, barely missing her eye, his voice rising to a shout. “You’re just standing here, acting like nothing’s wrong!”\n\n“I’m acting like nothing’s wrong?” Stacey shot back, her voice cracking under the strain. “Todd, look around! Everything is wrong! It’s not S Corp or lizard people or... or The Pointers doing this to us. It’s you! It’s your obsession that’s tearing us apart!”\n\nTodd’s laugh was harsh, almost unhinged. “You’re blind. Just like the rest of them. Do you even hear yourself? You’re buying into the fucking liberal-commie lies, Stacey. You’re part of the problem.” His hands balling into fists, the air between them electric with tension. “But not me. I see it. I see the truth, and I won’t let them take it from me.”\n\n“Perhaps they already have,” Stacey whispered, her words trembling but deliberate. Her gaze was steady, despite the fear crawling up her spine.\n\nSomething inside Todd snapped. He lunged at her, his movement fueled by anger and desperation. Stacey gasped, her instincts propelling her into motion. She ducked just in time, Todd’s swing cutting through empty air before his momentum carried him forward. He crashed into the wall, his hand slamming into beer bottles on the shelf. The glass scattered like shrapnel, some embedding into his flesh, leaving crimson trails across his fingers and palms.\n\n“Fucking bitch!” Todd bellowed, clutching his hand as he turned to face her, his expression a mask of pain and fury. Blood dripped onto the shag carpet, staining the tangled mess of paper and yarn at his feet. “This is your fault!” he roared, his voice raw. “All of it! You and your—your complacency! You’ve let them worm their way into our lives, into you!”\n\nStacey pressed herself against the far counter, her chest heaving as she tried to steady her trembling hands. “Don’t you dare blame me,” she exclaimed. “You’ve done this to yourself, Todd. I tried to help you, but you won’t let anyone in. You’re so obsessed with fighting shadows that you don’t even see what you’re doing to us. To me.”\n\nTodd’s glare was molten, his fury barely contained. He took another step, trying to get closer to her, then stopped, his shoulders sagging as the fight seemed to drain from him. “Just... get me a damn sandwich,” he muttered, his voice hoarse and bitter. “And a beer. Now.”\n\nStacey didn’t move at first, her body locked in a standoff between flight and compliance. Finally, she turned and walked toward the kitchen; her steps slow and deliberate. When she reached the counter, her eyes fell on the small, unassuming box of rat poison tucked in the cabinet's corner. Its presence was both horrifying and magnetic, a whisper in the back of her mind that grew louder with every passing second.\n\nShe stared at it, her heart pounding in her chest. It would be so easy, the thought came unbidden, dark and insidious. Just a pinch. Enough to silence him, to end this madness, to take back some control. Her hand hovered near the box, trembling.\n\nFor as much as she loathed Todd, as much as his anger, relentless beatings, and paranoia had worn her down to the bone, she couldn’t bring herself to cross that line. The thought of becoming the monster she saw in him made her stomach churn. She closed her eyes, forcing herself to breathe deeply, to find some fragment of clarity amidst the storm. “But he does this because he still loves me.” She thought, lying to herself.\n\nWhen the sandwich was ready, she carried the plate and beer back into the living room. Todd sat slumped in his chair, his injured hand wrapped in a dirty shirt. He didn’t look at her as she set the plate down in front of him, his eyes fixed on the floor.\n\n“Here,” she hissed, her voice flat and distant. “It is done.”	0	84	f	2026-02-27 11:54:46.724658-05	2026-02-27 14:36:01.424183-05	normal	\N
