g in an episode of Hoarders: Robotics Edition.”\n\nI chuckled despite myself, setting the solar system model on the nearest clear surface—an act that involved moving three smaller boxes to an already overburdened shelf. “You know,” I said slowly, the pieces falling into place in my mind, “I think it’s time we find a new building for all this.”\n\nOur son’s eyes lit up. “You mean, like, a real warehouse? With loading docks and conveyor belts?”\n\nMy wife, on the other hand, gave me a skeptical glare that could cut through steel. “You’re serious?”\n\n“Dead serious,” I said, gesturing to the mess around us. “I was running the numbers last night, and we’ve got the funds, at least for the down payment, thanks to the stock market, and if we don’t move this operation out of the house, we’re going to drown in USB hubs and action figures.”\n\nShe hesitated, glancing at the clutter, then back at me. Beneath her skepticism, I saw the faintest flicker of hope. “Okay,” she said finally. “If this turns into another one of your big ideas, like the time you thought converting the guest room into a VR lab would be ‘fun,’ I’m holding you personally responsible.”\n\n“Deal,” I said, grinning. “And for the record, the VR lab was fun.”\n\n“Until you walked into a wall and dislocated your shoulder,” she shot back.\n\n“Minor detail,” I muttered, already pulling up listings for commercial properties on my phone.\n\nOur search for the perfect warehouse turned into an adventure of its own. We toured abandoned manufacturing sites that smelled like mildew and regret, walked through crumbling storage facilities with graffiti that even robots would find offensive, and nearly signed a lease on a space that turned out to double as a raccoon sanctuary.\n\nAfter weeks of false starts, we finally found it: a dilapidated warehouse right across from our neighborhood. Once a bustling plant nursery, then a heavy machinery store, it now stood abandoned, its walls cracked, and its windows broken. Most people would have seen it as a money pit. We saw potential.\n\n“This is it,” I said, stepping inside and gesturing grandly at the cavernous space. Dust motes danced in the sunlight streaming through a hole in the roof. “Our new HQ.”\n\nMy wife raised an eyebrow, crossing her arms. “It’s a dump.”\n\n“It’s a canvas,” I countered. “With the Infinitron’s help, it’ll be a masterpiece.”\n\nHer skeptical expression softened, just a little. “Alright,” she said. “You’re explaining this to the neighbors when the robots start hauling in building materials.”\n\nWith the Infinitron calculating blueprints and our Matter Replicator working overtime (along with a fleet of newly designed humanoid worker robots), the transformation began. The robots proved invaluable, assembling shelves, replicating construction materials, and even painting the walls with surprising precision—except for the one bot that painted its own wheels and got stuck in a corner.\n\n“Your robots are overachievers,” my wife remarked one afternoon, watching as a bot meticulously polished a row of newly installed conveyor belts. “If only they could figure out how to load the dishwasher.”\n\n“They’re working on it,” I said, grinning.\n\nBy the first of June, the warehouse started taking shape, going from crumbling ruin to beginning to look like a state-of-the-art fulfillment center. The shelves gleamed, the floors sparkled, and the air buzzed with the hum of synchronized machines. It really felt like we weren’t just keeping up with the chaos—we were thriving.	3	152	t	2026-02-27 11:58:57.113507-05	2026-02-27 15:37:30.966727-05	normal	\N
