me when she thinks I'm not paying attention...\n\n"I'm fine," I lie. "Mostly. Just realized I have Rodriguez's test and I'm completely fucked."\n\nJamie laughs, and it's the most beautiful sound I've heard since waking up in this timeline. Real. Warm. Uncomplicated by decades of disappointment I haven't lived yet.\n\n"Want company? I've got my notes, and I made coffee. Well, my mom's coffee. The kind that could strip paint."\n\n"You'd do that? It's past two."\n\n"When have I ever gone to bed before three? Besides..." Her voice gets softer, more careful. "You scared the hell out of me. When you went down, and they couldn't wake you up... I thought—" She stops. Starts again. "I'm glad you're okay."\n\nThe silence stretches between us like a live wire. All the things we don't say because we're sixteen and terrified and convinced we have forever to figure it out. In another timeline, maybe we do. Maybe we get married, have kids, grow old together. Maybe she's the one holding my hand when I die at seventy-four, instead of dying alone in that slop infested future.\n\nBut that's not this timeline. This is the one where we're just friends, where the distance between my bedroom and hers feels like the space between galaxies.\n\n"Jamie?"\n\n"Yeah?"\n\n"Thanks. For... you know. Caring."\n\nThe line crackles with static, but her voice comes through clear. "Okay, so cellular respiration. Think of it like a factory..."\n\nI'm sprawled on my bedroom floor, phone cord stretched to its absolute limit, receiver pressed against my ear like a lifeline. My biology textbook lies open in front of me, but I'm not really seeing the diagrams of mitochondria. I'm listening to Jamie's voice, the way she gets excited when she's explaining something, how she makes even the most boring shit sound like poetry.\n\n"Are you writing this down?" she asks.\n\n"Yeah, totally." I'm not. I'm doodling in the margins—little sketcher S that morphs into spirals that look like galaxies or DNA helixes or the way reality bends when you've died twice in one day.\n\n"Liar. I can hear you not writing."\n\n"How can you hear me not writing?"\n\n"Because you make this little humming sound when you're actually paying attention. Right now you're doing that thing where you zone out and think about... I don't know, whatever weird stuff goes on in your head."\n\nAnd for an instant everything just feels right. But it's wrong. She knows me too well but I don't think we've ever really met. It's terrifying and wonderful and makes my chest feel like it's going to collapse.\n\n3:17 AM. We've been talking for over an hour. The phone bill will be a paper monument to my selfishness—every minute of this conversation, another strike of his belt. But her voice is the only thing keeping me tethered to this timeline, and I'm selfish enough to stay\n\n"My dad's going to murder me," I whisper into the receiver. "The phone bill's going to be insane. We're on el-cheapo plan and even local calls cost an arm and a leg."\n\n"We could hang up," Jamie says, but there's no conviction in it.\n\n"Yeah. We should." I chuckle silently, trying to hide my embarrassment, like I'll hide the bruises under my clothes.\n\nNeither of us moves to disconnect.\n\n4:33 AM. She's reading directly from her notes now, her voice getting softer as exhaustion sets in. I can picture her in her own bedroom, probably lying on her stomach with her feet in the air, pink t-shirt nightgown, hair falling like a curtain around her face. Nose scrunched up the same way she does when she gets the cafeteria lunch, the same unconscious grace that makes my stupid sixteen-year-old heart forget how to beat properly.\n\n"Electron transport chain," she murmurs. "Electrons move through protein complexes... creating a proton gradient..."\n\nHer words blend together, becoming less about biology and more about the rhythm of connection. Two teenagers staying up too late, sharing the strange intimacy of exhaustion and academic panic.\n\n"Jamie?"\n\n"Mmm?"\n\n"Thanks for staying up with me. I know this is crazy."\n\nA pause. The kind of silence that feels heavy with things we're too young to say.\n\n"Why wouldn’t I. You’re too cute." she says finally. "Besides, you almost died today. I'm not letting you fail biology on top of that."\n\nCute. Right. The words should sting, but somehow they don't. Because the way she says it, the careful tenderness in her voice, suggests there might be other words hiding underneath. Words we're both too scared to uncover.\n\n5:38 AM. The sky outside my window is starting to lighten, gray bleeding into the darkness like watercolor on wet paper. Jamie's voice has gotten quieter, more intimate, like we're sharing secrets in a confessional.\n\n"Do you ever feel like..." she starts, then stops. "Never mind. It's stupid."\n\n"What?"\n\n"Like you're living the wrong life? Like there's this other version of you somewhere else, doing things differently?"\n\nMy hand tightens on the receiver. She has no idea how close to the truth she is. How many other versions of me are scattered across dimensions, living different failures, dying different deaths?\n\n"Yeah," I whisper. "All the time."\n\n"Maybe that's just being sixteen," she says. "Maybe everyone feels like they're supposed to be someone else."\n\n6:12 AM. "I should go," I say, but don't move to hang up.\n\n"Yeah. Me too."\n\nMore silence. The kind that feels like holding hands across an impossible distance.\n\n"See you at school?" she asks.\n\n"If I survive the test. And my dad."\n\nShe laughs, soft and sleepy. "You will. You're tougher than you think."\n\nIf only she knew. If only she could see the cosmic joke of it—her calling me tough while I'm busy falling apart across multiple timelines, held together by nothing but her voice and the stubborn refusal of my heart to stop beating.\n\n"Goodnight, Jamie."\n\n"Goodnight, brain damage boy."\n\nThe line goes dead. I'm left holding the phone, grinning like an idiot, feeling more alive than I 