416	104	1	Jamie's Confession	Jamie appears at the narrator's window and reveals her true nature as an ancient entity designed for seduction. She confesses to watching him across lifetimes, including during his wife's death, and admits to possibly causing the timeline fractures through her desperate love. As she speaks, her inhuman form becomes increasingly visible, and the journal begins writing itself with cryptic messages.	Jamie appears at my window like smoke given form, her movements too fluid as she slips through the frame without disturbing the curtains or making a sound. She finds me staring at Whitney's journal, its pages no longer writing themselves, silent and still as death. For just a moment, her eyes flicker with something that might be recognition—or regret.\n\n"There was someone else, wasn't there?" she whispers, settling beside me with inhuman grace. "Someone who understood what love could cost."\n\nThe words hit like a blade between my ribs, and suddenly I'm not sixteen anymore. I'm seventy-two, sitting in a sterile hospital room that smells of antiseptic and approaching death. The machines beep in rhythm with my wife's failing heart, each sound marking another second stolen from us by cells that have forgotten how to stop growing.\n\nMy wife lies so still in the bed, diminished by the cancer that's been eating her alive for months. Even now, even dying, she maintains that perfect composure—hands folded just so, hair arranged despite the chemo, a porcelain mask of serene acceptance. Beautiful. Always so beautiful. Like a jade statue that learned to breathe but never quite learned to feel.\n\n"I should have been better wife," she whispers in that careful, practiced English she never quite made sound natural, even after thirty years in America. "Should have... understood you more."\n\nI take her hand, cool even now, and try to memorize the weight of it. She's apologizing, but even her apologies feel choreographed, like she's following a script that someone handed her decades ago.\n\n"You were perfect," I tell her, because what else can you say to someone who spent her entire life trying to be the ideal wife without ever understanding what that meant beyond the surface gestures?\n\nBut even as I say it, I remember the ache.\n\n"You deserved warmth," she says, and for the first time in our marriage, her mask slips slightly. "I know this. I... I tried to give, but I don't think I know how to be warm. Only how to be beautiful. Only how to be... correct."\n\nThe monitor's rhythm becomes irregular. She's been the perfect housewife—cooking meals, maintaining our home, smiling at our friends and families—but I realize now that she was as lonely as I was. Trapped in a role she'd been taught to play without ever being taught to feel.\n\n"There was someone else who could love you better," she whispers. "Someone who wouldn't spend thirty years being a beautiful statue instead of real woman."\n\nSilence stretches.\n\nThe machines flatline with a sound like reality tearing.\n\nI'm sixteen again, sitting in my bedroom with Jamie's inhuman warmth pressed against me, and the memory of that hospital room fades like smoke.\n\nThrough my tears, the moonlight hits her wrong—casting shadows that don't match her body's position, creating silhouettes that suggest wings or tentacles depending on how I squint. But when she curls against me, she's just Jamie again. Scared, crying tears that catch the light like liquid mercury.\n\n"I need to tell you everything," she whispers, her voice layered with harmonics that make my bones ache in ways that feel almost good. "Before I forget how to be human again. Before they take even this from us."\n\nHer body temperature shifts between human warm and something else entirely—sometimes cold as winter stone, sometimes burning like fever. Her skin feels different under my hands, not quite solid, as if she's held together by will and desperation rather than bone and muscle.\n\n"I was there when you were eight," she begins, the words tumbling out like a dam bursting. "When your mother poisoned you. When you nearly died and crossed through the void. I was watching from the spaces between seconds, assigned to monitor anomalies in the timeline."\n\nHer breath against my neck carries scents that don't belong to Earth—ozone and burning copper and something floral that makes my head spin. "You weren't supposed to survive that. The script called for your death, a clean erasure of a soul that had already shown signs of remembering too much. But when you clawed your way back from the edge, when you chose to live despite everything..."\n\nShe pauses, her fingernails extending slightly, not quite claws but sharp enough to draw thin lines of blood when she traces patterns on my chest. The cuts form symbols that burn with cold fire before healing instantly, leaving only the memory of pain that feels too much like pleasure.\n\n"Something in me broke. Or maybe something finally worked the way it was supposed to. I was designed to be emotionally void—desire without attachment, seduction without consequence. But watching you fight to exist, watching you refuse to let go even when letting go would have been mercy..." She laughs, a sound like breaking glass wrapped in velvet. "I learned what hope felt like."\n\nAs she speaks, her skin ripples with patterns that hurt to track directly, geometries that fold in on themselves like living fractals. Something vast moves beneath her flesh, pressing against the boundaries of her human form, but never quite breaking through.\n\n"He called me Velistra Seducera—The Chain of Desire. I was forged in dimensions where pain and pleasure share the same frequency, where love and consumption are molecular twins. For eons, I was his perfect weapon—desire made manifest, love weaponized into the ultimate prison."\n\nShe pulls back to look at me, and for a moment her eyes are those black voids again—infinite depths that reflect nothing but hungry darkness. But then they flicker back to brown, warm and achingly human.\n\n"I've seduced kings and saints and revolutionaries. I've turned freedom fighters into devoted servants with nothing but a kiss and the right words whispered at the perfect moment. But you..." She touches my face with hands that tremble with newfound mortality. "You made me want to be real instead of just functional."\n\nI'm staring past her at Whitney's journal on my desk, watching its cover slowly transform. The leather binding shifts and flows like liquid, reshaping itself until it looks like a faded copy of Elysium Rising. The title bleeds through from some other reality, silver letters that pulse with their own light.\n\n"Your concussion—when your older self merged with your younger body—it created ripples through every timeline you'd ever existed in. I was supposed to be immune to those fractures, but something about you made me... vulnerable. Made me capable of choice."\n\nHer voice drops to barely a whisper. "I've been watching you across lifetimes. I saw you fall in love with her—your wife. Cold, brilliant, beautiful in her own distant way. I watched you build a life together, watched you try to warm yourself against someone who loved you but could never quite reach you."\n\n"You watched? You watched my wife die and you were... jealous?" I ask.\n\nThe question hangs between us like a blade. "I was jealous. God help me, I was jealous of a mortal woman who couldn't even see the shadows gathering around you. All those nights when she couldn’t be there for you, when she chose headaches  over your need for connection, when she left you alone with your nightmares—I wanted to comfort you. I wanted to be the warmth she couldn't provide."\n\nJamie's form flickers slightly, her human mask slipping to reveal something ancient and hungry underneath. "When she died—when the cancer took her—I felt your grief like it was my own. Felt you break apart and try to rebuild yourself from the pieces. And I wanted so badly to reach across the veil and tell you that someone understood. That someone had been watching and caring all along."\n\nThe journal begins to write itself, invisible hands moving an invisible pen across pages that turn without wind. Ink flows across the paper like living blood, forming words in handwriting that shifts between familiar and alien.\n\n"The prison realm—Etheris—it's breaking down," Jamie explains, settling deeper into my arms. "The Demiurge is losing control because too many dreamers are waking up. Too many souls are remembering what they were before he trapped them in recursive loops of death and rebirth."\n\nThe journal's pages flutter open to reveal photographs that shouldn't exist—images of us together in lives we've never lived, in timelines that were overwritten before we could experience them. In one, we're older, married, standing in front of a house that exists nowhere on Earth. In another, I'm holding a baby that has Jamie's eyes and something ancient in its smile.\n\n"Since he can’t feed on souls here, he feeds on identity confusion, on the trauma of forgetting who you really are," she continues. "Every time you die and get reborn with your memories wiped, every time someone you love gets erased and replaced—it nourishes him. Makes him stronger."\n\nThe ink on the journal's pages begins to move, words rearranging themselves into new configurations. I catch fragments as they shift: The boy who remembers. The girl who chose love over duty. The anchor point that could break everything.\n\n"But I think I'm partly to blame for bringing you back here," she whispers, guilt heavy in her voice. "When you died in that hospice, when you finally let go—I couldn't bear it. I pulled at the threads of reality, whispered your name into the void, begged for another chance. I think... I think my desperation created some of the fractures that dragged you back to sixteen. And me into this girl that never really existed."\n\nHer confession hits like a physical blow. "You mean—"\n\n"I mean this might all be my fault. The timelines bleeding together, the reality wars, Patrick's sacrifice—all of it might have happened because I couldn't let you go. Because I loved you too much to let you rest."\n\n"My fault? You think this is YOUR fault? My wife... my life... was it even real? Was she just a placeholder in a script?" I ask incredulously, but the exposition was too much for her.\n\nAs Jamie falls asleep against me, her breathing settling into something almost human, the journal writes its final entry. The words appear in handwriting that shifts between mine, Whitney's, and someone else's.\n\n"I wasn't the first and Patrick won't be the last. You must remember your name, A—"\n\nThe ink smears before completing the word, spreading across the page like spilled blood. But instead of stopping at the paper's edge, it flows upward, defying gravity to spiral through the air like liquid DNA. The dark rivulets pour toward Jamie's sleeping form, soaking into her skin without leaving stains.\n\nFor a moment, she speaks in a voice that isn't hers—masculine, gentle, heartbreakingly familiar: "She's safe. Whitney's safe. I'm holding the door for her, but I can't hold it forever. Remember your name. It's the key to everything. It's who you really are."\n\nThen her voice shifts, becoming something older, more cosmic: "The anchor point is destabilizing. Reality is becoming personal. Consensus is breaking down. Soon, everyone will be living in their own private version of hell or heaven, and the barriers between worlds will dissolve completely."\n\nShe wakes with no memory of speaking, blinking up at me with pure human confusion. "Did I say something? I feel like I was dreaming about... about someone important."\n\nOutside my window, the moon sets wrong—too fast, too bright, casting shadows that point in directions that don't make sense. Reality is becoming personal, just like she said. And somewhere in the distance, I swear I can hear the sound of chains breaking, one link at a time.	0	175	f	2026-02-27 12:18:18.014969-05	2026-03-01 15:41:52.027098-05	normal	\N
398	100	1	Insomnia and Impossible Messages	Stephen lies awake obsessing over Whitney's mysterious message appearing in his journal dated two weeks in the future. Unable to sleep, he boots up his computer late at night and searches the BBS, eventually finding a thread from Whitney posted on December 15th with cryptic instructions about meeting at Whickham Park's west ridge. He calls Jamie for support but she attempts to seduce him instead, ultimately agreeing to accompany him to the location after he promises it will be his last pursuit of Whitney-related mysteries.	I've been tossing and turning for hours, but every time I close my eyes, I see those words scratched into my journal in handwriting that isn't mine: "She's not gone. Just misplaced. You'll have to find the fracture."\n\nTwo days since Whitney's message appeared on a page I know was blank, dated two weeks in the future, and my brain won't stop picking at it like a scab. Tell Patrick his deck saved my life. Tell Jamie her tea really did help. Except Patrick doesn't seem to care anymore, and Jamie thinks I'm losing my mind chasing ghosts that everyone else has forgotten.\n\nFinally, I give up on sleep and slip out of bed, bare feet silent on the stained carpet. The cinderblock walls of our cookie cutter 80’s Florida sub-division house carry sound like a cathedral, and Dad's passed out in his recliner just outside my bedroom door, the TV's shifting colors dancing on my foil blocked windows from the gap beneath my door.\n\nI hold my breath as the 486 boots up, that familiar electronic whine cutting through the silence like a saw blade. When he doesn't stir, I exhale slowly and settle into my chair, the modem's screech announcing another nightly seance with the digital dead.\n\nWith no idea what I’m even looking for I spiral down random rabbit holes.\n\nCASSANDRA'S SENSUAL MASSAGE - OUTCALLS ONLY.\n\nUFO SIGHTING OVER I-4 - MULTIPLE WITNESSES.\n\nLONELY? CALL 1-900-HOT-TALK.\n\nSHADOW PEOPLE IN YBOR CITY???\n\nASIAN FANTASY SEEKS GENEROUS GENTLEMAN.\n\nEach thread leads nowhere, digital detritus from people seeking connection or escape or something to fill the empty hours between midnight and dawn.\n\nI've been at this for hours. Digging through buried conversations, following breadcrumbs that crumble to nothing the moment I touch them. My journal lies open beside the keyboard, pages covered in increasingly frantic scrawl—notes about William's impossible existence, Whitney's surgical removal from reality, the shadows that seem to breed when I'm not watching.\n\nI should be sleeping. Should be letting this go, like Jamie keeps telling me to. Like the rational part of my brain screams every time I boot up this machine. But that note from Whitney, the one that appeared in my journal like a message in a bottle from a sinking ship... it won't stop scratching at the inside of my skull.\n\n"…some things are worth remembering, even when the whole universe wants you to forget”\n\nThe cursor blinks at me, mocking. Another dead end. Another BBS thread that leads nowhere. I'm about to give up, to admit that maybe Jamie's right, maybe I'm losing my mind chasing ghosts through copper cables.\n\nThen I see it.\n\nWHTNY_Jxdead.\n\nThe thread title hits me like ice water. My cursor hovers over it, trembling along with my hand. When I click, the timestamp makes my stomach drop through the floor: December 15th, 1995.\n\nToday is December 3rd.\n\nThe message sits there, glowing like a trap:\n\n"Don't go looking. But if you do… Whickham Park, west ridge. The trail you've never taken but remember anyway. Bring nothing red. Trust no one wearing your face."\n\nMy breath catches in my throat. It's her voice, threaded through those pixels like DNA through a crime scene sample. The way she fractures sentences, breaks thoughts into sharp little pieces that cut when you try to hold them. Nobody else thinks in punctuation wounds the way Whitney does.\n\nDid. Does. Whatever tense applies to someone who exists in the spaces between memory and denial.\n\nI stare at the date stamp until my eyes water. December 15th. Twelve days from now. Posted at 11:47 PM, which means... what? That she'll be alive then? That she'll have access to a computer? That time itself is just another broken toy in whatever cosmic playpen we've stumbled into?\n\nThe dot matrix printer kicks to life before I consciously decide to print. It grinds and whines like a mechanical animal in pain, each line materializing with the precision of prophecy being carved into stone. The sound echoes through the house, obscene in its volume, too much proof that impossible things can become paper and ink and real.\n\nFrom the living room, I hear Dad shift in his recliner, the leather creaking under his weight. Oh fuck. My heart hammers against my ribs as I freeze, listening for the sound of his feet hitting the floor, for the heavy footsteps that would mean another beating for "sneaking around like a little shit" when he's trying to sleep off whatever poison is keeping him upright these days. But after a moment, his breathing settles back into the rhythm of the unconscious—deep, rattling snores so loud they could wake the dead. Which, given what I'm dealing with lately, seems fitting. I exhale slowly.\n\nMy hands shake as I tear the perforated edges free. The ink is still warm, like it's been touched by fever.\n\nI need to call Jamie.\n\nI slip out of my room and ease around the corner to where the phone sits on the living room wall. Dad's still unconscious in his recliner, empty bottles scattered around him like fallen soldiers. I lift the handset with surgical precision and retreat back to my room, the spiral cord stretched to its limit as I dial her number.\n\nFour rings. Shit, what if her parents answer. Five. I'm about to hang up when her voice cuts through the line, but instead of sleep-thick confusion, there's something warm and dangerous in her tone.\n\n"Stephen?" A pause, then a soft laugh. "God, I was hoping you'd call."\n\nMy brain stutters. "You... were?"\n\n"Mmm. I've been missing your voice. There's something about these late-night calls..." Her voice drops to a whisper. "Something about knowing you're thinking about me when you should be sleeping."\n\nHeat crawls up my neck. "Jamie, I—"\n\n"I love when you call like this. Like you can't help yourself." There's rustling on her end, like she's shifting in bed. "What are you wearing?"\n\nThe question I asked her earlier bounces back like a boomerang, and suddenly the printout in my hand feels like it weighs nothing compared to the weight of her attention. "I... uh..."\n\nShe laughs again, softer this time. "You're cute when you're speechless. Tell me what you're thinking about."\n\nThis is it. This is the moment I should put the BBS message aside, should focus on the girl who's practically offering herself to me. But Whitney's words burn in my peripheral vision, and the rational part of my brain that Jamie keeps appealing to chooses the worst possible moment to malfunction.\n\n"I found something," I say, the words tumbling out before I can stop them. "On the BBS. A message from Whitney."\n\nThe silence stretches so long I wonder if the line's gone dead. When she speaks again, the warmth has drained from her voice like water from a broken glass.\n\n"Stephen."\n\n"I know how it sounds—"\n\n"Do you?" Her voice sharpens, all traces of seduction evaporating. "Because it sounds like you'd rather chase dead girls than talk to the living one who's practically throwing herself at you."\n\nThe accusation hits like a slap. "That's not... I didn't mean..."\n\n"We talked about this. After the journal, after the rituals that no one but us remember. You promised you'd let this go." I can hear her moving, probably sitting up in bed, the moment between us crumbling like ash.\n\n"This message, it's dated next week. She's telling us about some place in Whickham Park—"\n\n"Stop." The word cuts through the line like a blade. "Just... stop. Please."\n\nThere's a pause, and when she speaks again, her voice carries a different kind of weight. "Stephen, if you drop this right now, if you promise me you'll stop chasing shadows and focus on what's real... I'll give you something. Something I've been saving. Something sacred."\n\nMy mouth goes dry. The way she says 'sacred' makes it sound like an offering, a ritual exchange. "Jamie..."\n\n"My parents are going out of town next weekend with my sister. And I've been thinking... about boundaries. About crossing them." Her voice wavers slightly, vulnerability threading through the anger.\n\nThe printout crinkles in my grip. Whitney's impossible message versus Jamie's very real, very immediate sacrifice. The choice should be obvious.\n\nBut I made a promise to Whitney—to her memory, to whatever fragment of her is still fighting somewhere in the spaces between reality's cracks. I swore I'd find the fracture, and vows like that aren't something you break for convenience, no matter what they cost you.\n\n"The west ridge," I say quietly, reading from the printout. "Behind Whickham Park. You know where that is, don't you?"\n\nAnother long pause. When she speaks, her voice carries a different strategy—honey instead of vinegar. "You know, we could just sneak off into the woods at cross country practice. I think I know of a place that might afford us enough privacy to... explore things. Just the two of us. Forget about messages and ghosts and focus on something real."\n\nThe offer hangs between us like forbidden fruit, but I've already made my choice. "This message is really strange, Jamie. What if she's really trying to reach us?"\n\n"Then she's dead, Stephen." Her voice hardens again, the seduction cracking like a mask. "And dead people don't send messages through computer networks. They just stay dead, and the people who love them learn to move on."\n\n"If we don't find anything there, I'll drop it. I promise. But we could just pop over during Cross Country practice tomorrow. No harm, no foul."\n\n"Swear to me. Swear on something that matters to you that this is the last of it. That after tomorrow, you'll let the dead stay dead."\n\nMy throat feels tight. "I swear."\n\n"Good." She pauses, and when she speaks again, her voice drops to that whisper that makes my skin prickle. "When we find nothing, you're going to owe me. Big." Her voice drops to that whisper that makes my skin prickle. "And I have very specific ideas about how you can pay that debt that don’t involve any clothes.”\n\nHeat crawls up my neck. "Jamie..."\n\n"Shh. Do you hear that?" There's rustling on her end, then a muffled voice in the distance. "Shit, that's my mom. She must be getting water or something."\n\n"You should go."\n\n"Yeah, I should." Another pause, then her voice drops to barely audible. "Stephen? I've been naked this whole time we've been talking. And there are much better ways to spend a phone call when someone is... clothing impaired." She lets that hang in the air for a heartbeat. "When you're lying in bed tonight, trying not to think about dead girls... Think about what you could have been doing with this conversation instead."\n\nThe line goes dead before I can respond, leaving me alone with the dial tone and the slow realization that breathing has become manual labor. My underwear feels suddenly, uncomfortably restrictive.\n\nAs sleep finally claims me, a smell drifts through my room, of vanilla and peaches, sweet enough to make my mouth water despite everything. Through the gap in my curtains, something moves between the streetlights. I don't look directly at it, but I can feel it watching, patient and knowing.\n\nTomorrow, I'll hunt for ghosts in broad daylight.\n\nTonight, I dream of shadows that smell like forbidden fruit.	0	389	f	2026-02-27 12:17:05.060788-05	2026-03-01 15:41:52.027098-05	normal	\N
