301	76	1	Between Dreams and Reality	Aditi navigates the streets of Mumbai outside the Space Elevator, haunted by the vast inequality between Earth's poverty and Elysium's elite world. Despite her humble origins in a coal-mining family, her exceptional intellect has earned her a scholarship to Elysium University—a ticket to escape. She witnesses Red Hat protesters denouncing S Corp and worries about her brother Rahul's involvement with them.	Mumbai’s humid breath clung to Aditi’s neck as she navigated the labyrinthine alleys outside the transportation complex. Mango sellers hawked their wares, their voices competing with the honking chaos of rickshaws. Above, the skeletal shadow of the Space Elevator, a behemoth of carbon fiber and glass piercing the smog-laced sky, mocked the squalor below. It was a stark visual of the chasm between dreams and reality, a chasm Aditi knew all too well. Despite the promise of clean energy and easy material access, folks down here still loved burning things.\n\nGrowing to parents who’s family worked in the open-cast coal mines before being allowed to own their modest store in 1989, the idea of space had always been her escape. As a child, she would sit in the library and devour any sci-fi story should could get her hands on. Later, once the S Corp had revealed itself to the world, she loved reading the newsletters about those daring trips through the ERB networks to colonize Sorhhinda.\n\nS Corp, the corporation behind the technology, had woven itself into the fabric of her dreams. Yet, the reality was harsh. Tickets to Elysium, the orbiting space ring that housed S Corp’s elite and served as a gateway to the stars, were more expensive than diamonds. For someone like Aditi, the vast distance between Mumbai’s slums and Elysium’s glittering utopia was measured not in miles, but in generations.\n\nBut Aditi was a fighter. S Corp’s universal education programs had offered a crack in the iron-clad door of her caste. Her hunger for knowledge and exceptional grades in Cosmology had landed her the impossible - a graduate scholarship at Elysium University, and an internship working for Professor Nakamura. This was a one-way ticket to a future unimaginable just 5 years ago.\n\nThe closer she got to the Space Elevator, the more the air changed. The cries of street vendors gave way to chants and rhythmic drumming. The Red Hats were out in force, their crimson uniforms clashing with the silver sheen of the elevator’s base. Their leader, a portly man whose fiery sermons were as infamous as his apocalyptic zeal, bellowed into a megaphone.\n\n“S Corp spits in the face of dharma!” his voice thundered over the crowd. “They defy the cosmic order, reaching for the heavens like arrogant rakshasa, blind to their karma! Their towers will crumble, and humanity will pay the price for their hubris!”\n\nAditi felt a familiar prickle of unease. The Red Hats were as ubiquitous as Mumbai’s beggars, their resentment towards S Corp fueled by the widening gap between rich and poor. Not only that, but she had seen the head covering her brother, Rahul, had tried to hide. The infection was everywhere.\n\nAditi couldn’t entirely blame them. For every gleaming Elysium penthouse, a hundred thousand Mumbai hovels suffocated under the weight of neglect. S Corp’s technological marvels, wondrous as they were, only seemed to amplify the stark divide between privilege and suffering. Their promises of equality rang hollow against the entrenched power of Indian oligarchs, who thrived on disparity. And so, the government did what it always had: allowed the divide to fester.\n\nAs she entered the security cordon, the shouts faded into a dull murmur. Glancing back at the churning mass of red, Aditi wondered if, one day, Elysium’s gleaming towers would become testaments not to human ingenuity, but to the price of progress, a monument built on the backs of the forgotten.\n\nHer hand tightened around the Elysium University ID card tucked in her pocket. A cold token of hope amidst the embers of doubt. Today, she was boarding the elevator to the stars, leaving behind the Mumbai she knew. But the question that gnawed at her was this: could she ever truly leave its shadow behind?	0	313	f	2026-02-27 12:09:13.493498-05	2026-02-28 17:15:05.803512-05	normal	\N
