465	117	3	The Grind	Months collapse into a single grey Thursday. Stephen sits in his office chair scrolling through meaningless job listings while his house decays around him. His wife remains emotionally distant and transactional, offering momentary warmth only as motivation for job applications.	*********\n\nSundays bleed into Wedtober. Months blink by in the mote of an eye.\n\nToday, it was the click of my mouse wheel, echoing in the stale air of "my office."\n\nIt was Thursday. At least it felt like a Thursday. Sometime in the bleak, grey middle of the still Florida hot November. Xavier was at school, a physical presence removed from the equation, leaving the house in a vacuum-sealed silence. I was in my command center, a chair worn concave from two years of inertia, staring at LinkedIn.\n\n"Recommended for You."\n\nThe algorithm had decided I was a perfect fit to be a "Digital Transformation Architect " or a "Director of AI Enablement." The jobs were a digital slurry of meaningless titles, all blurring into one long, vertical coffin I was scrolling myself into.\n\nThe room was a testament to the long defeat. Piles of unopened mail sat in one corner of the desk. Dust bunnies had formed complex civilizations in the corners. Old tissues littered the space around the trash can, too tired to even make it inside. It was an ugly, constant, low-grade untidiness, a physical manifestation of my mental state.\n\nI couldn't click "Apply." The act itself felt like a scream into a black hole.\n\nMy stomach growled. I glanced at the clock: 12:30 PM. Lunchtime.\n\nI pushed back from the chair and padded into the kitchen.\n\nThe mess was worse here. The sink was a Jenga tower of last night's dinner plates. The counters were a sticky archipelago of crumbs and old coffee rings.\n\nShe was at the table, just like always. She was eating a meticulously portioned salad from a glass container, scrolling through her phone. She didn't look up when I came in.\n\nI opened the fridge, stared into its bright, empty promise, and grabbed some "close enough" slices of cheese and a pickle.\n\nI stood there, leaning against the counter, chewing. Imagining what it would be like to be the pickle, it’s essence merging into mine as much as my essence was blending into it.\n\n"Find anything?" my wife asked, her eyes still on her screen. "A lot of doomscrolling," I said. "The usual. Lots of promises but nothing tangible"\n\n"You have to apply, Stephen. The numbers won't work if you don't apply."\n\n"I know. I do. I am applying. What do you think I do all day? Right now I'm... just researching the new postings. Making sure they’re not going to be yet another dead end."\n\nShe sighed, a small, tired puff of air, and put her phone face-down on the table. She looked at me, her full attention a rare, heavy thing. The promise was there, deep in her eyes, behind the exhaustion. That fleeting, conditional warmth.\n\nJust do the thing. Just get the job. Then I can love you again.\n\nIt was a cold, transactional hope, but it was the only one I had.\n\n"I'll... I'll hit it hard this afternoon," I mumbled.\n\nThe ghost of a smile touched her lips. "Okay. Good."\n\nBut then, instead of picking her phone back up, she did something impossible. She pushed her chair back, the legs scraping loudly against the tile. She stood up.\n\nShe walked around the table, her steps measured, and stopped directly in front of me. I was still leaning against the counter, trapped between it and her, a slice of cheese still in my hand. My mind raced. This wasn't in the script. This wasn't the usual Thursday loop.\n\nSlowly, as if demonstrating a motion she'd only read about, she lifted her arms and put them around my waist. She rested her head against my chest, just below my collarbone.\n\nA hug.\n\nI went rigid. My heart hammered against my ribs, a trapped bird. I could smell the faint, floral scent of her shampoo. She hadn't done this—this unprompted physical contact—in months. Maybe a year. It felt... wrong. Not unwelcome, but incorrect. It was a gesture of affection so foreign, so out of place in the sticky, untidy kitchen, that it felt like a system glitch.\n\nHer body was stiff against mine. Her hands were just placed on the back of my shirt, not pulling, not caressing. It wasn't a hug of comfort, or of love. It was a hug of confirmation. A down payment.\n\nAnd that cold, transactional hope in my gut didn't just flicker. It flared. A hot, desperate, ugly thing.\n\nThis. This is what I get for just promising to apply. My mind, starved for affection, began to spin, running the new, addictive calculation. What's the reward for sending twenty applications? What if I get an interview? What if I...\n\nThe unbidden, intrusive thought was immediate, a flash of a life I'd lost: If I just get the job, this will happen again. She'll... come back. We'll be...\n\nShe held the pose for three... four... five precise seconds. Then, she pulled away.\n\nShe stepped back, her expression clearing, that faint, clinical smile returning. The warmth was gone. "Good," she said again, her voice suddenly lighter, energized. "I'll go tackle that mountain of laundry in the bedroom, then. You just... focus. Okay?"\n\nShe gave my arm a quick, encouraging squeeze—a final jolt of the electrodes—and then turned. She picked up her empty salad container, placed it in the tower of dirty dishes, and walked out of the kitchen.\n\n“You need to do these dishes some time today.”\n\n“Sure, let me go finish applying to a few things first.”\n\nThe connection wasn't just severed. It had been a calculated, temporary opening, a port accessed and then securely closed, leaving me shaking and more desperate than before.\n\nBut I couldn't. I couldn't go back to the LinkedIn scroll. Not yet. I retreated to the office and closed the door, shutting out the sticky counters and the weight of her expectation. I needed an escape. I needed to win at something.\n\nI didn't open the browser. I launched Project Gargoyle.\n\nThe login screen faded. My character loaded in. "Tinkles," a Level 90 Psychology Necromancer. I was standing in the "Concourse of Forgotten Wills," the endgame hub. My armor was a shifting, ethereal cloak of shadows and whispers.\n\nI was a master. I didn't raise skeletons; I raised doubts. I didn't cast fireballs; I cast "Paralytic Apathy." My ultimate skill wasn't an explosion; it was "Mass Hysteria," an Area-of-Effect spell that made entire armies turn on each other in a frenzy of shared regret.\n\nI opened my quest log. (Empty) I opened my spellbook. Every skill, every talent, was maxed. I walked my character to the Gargoyle King, the final quest-giver, his stone face etched with an eternity of digital sorrow. I hit the 'interact' button. A text box appeared. "Your mastery over the psyche is complete, Necromancer. There are no minds left to break, no wills left to shatter. There is nothing left to do."\n\nI hit the button again. The same message. The same hollow victory.\n\nYour mastery over the psyche is complete, Necromancer...\n\nI had won. I'd beaten the game. Or, at least, I'd beaten the current build. The game was still in Early Access. The devs were active, their patch notes a litany of future promises on Discord, but as of today, there was nothing more to do.\n\nI was the most powerful manipulator of emotions in this entire digital universe, and the reward was... nothing. An empty log. A repeated line of dialogue.\n\nAnd yet, I still came clawing back, again and again. This digital world was my dumpster, the only place I had left to do something, anything at all, just to feel alive, to feel like I mattered. I was a god of apathy, trapped in my own perfect, stagnant, and unfinished creation.\n\nI logged out. The silence of the house rushed back in.\n\nI couldn't face the jobs. I couldn't face the game. There was only one other thing.\n\nI found her in the bedroom, the curtains drawn against the flat, grey light. The untidiness was here, too. Piles of laundry—clean? dirty? impossible to tell—were heaped on the dresser, on the floor. She was lying on the bed, on top of the covers, scrolling on her phone.\n\n"Hey," I said, sitting on the edge of the mattress. "Mmm," she said, not looking up. I put my hand on her stomach, a simple, desperate bid for contact. She flinched, almost imperceptibly, but she didn't pull away. "I'm... having a hard day," I whispered. I moved closer, leaning in to kiss her shoulder, to find that warmth she'd hinted at in the kitchen.\n\nShe went rigid. "Stephen, I can't," she whispered, her eyes squeezing shut. "I have such a splitting headache. Just... just awful." She rolled over, away from me, her back a solid, impenetrable wall.\n\nI sat there for a long time, my hand resting on the empty space on the comforter where she had been. The job hunt was a dead end. The game was a dead end. This was a dead end. I was alone in the messy, silent house, with nothing left to do.	2	389	t	2026-02-27 12:26:51.696511-05	2026-03-01 15:41:52.027098-05	normal	\N
