86	23	1	The Useless Crystal	The narrator and his son receive a mysterious crystalline object dubbed "Chicken Shit" from an unexplained experiment with the Eternal Engine. Despite days of intense experimentation, the crystal remains completely inert until accidentally discovered to emit NFC signals when placed near a phone, sparking new hope and frustration.	What do you do when life gives you chicken shit? You make egg salad sandwiches. At least, that’s what my son said when he tried to pass off Captain Underpants as a legitimate choice for his 8th-grade English book report.\n\nThe logic was baffling, but the sentiment seemed to fit our situation. We had an Eternal Engine, capable of powering the house indefinitely, and now, a crystalline mystery that defied all reason. Dubbed “Chicken Shit” after its absurd origin story, the crystal had captured our imaginations, right up until it crushed them.\n\nThe first few days were a feverish mix of hope and chaos. We poked, prodded, and experimented, convinced we’d uncover some profound cosmic secret. But the crystal was frustratingly inert. Despite its mesmerizing, infinitely complex structure, it did... nothing. Shining light through it? Nothing any $2 prism wouldn’t do. Wrapping it with wires and running a current across it? Nada. Submerging it in different liquids? Zero. It was like trying to unlock the secrets of the universe by staring at a Magic 8-Ball stuck on Ask Again Later.\n\nEven recreating the original chicken experiment, the copper wire, the rice, the whole shebang, was a spectacular failure. The Engine seemed almost smug as it delivered a single, loud explosion, and a layer of charred poultry across the garage. No second crystal, no secret message, just the undeniable sense that we were being told, “You only get one. Stop fucking around.”\n\nI found myself oscillating between fascination and frustration. The crystal sat on the table like a taunt, its iridescent surface shifting in mesmerizing patterns that seemed just beyond comprehension. I would spend hours staring at it, convinced the answers were there if I could only look hard enough. The longer I stared, the more it felt like the crystal was staring back, unimpressed.\n\nThe first breakthrough, or what passed of as one, came by accident.\n\nAfter another exhausting day of failed experiments, I tossed the crystal onto the kitchen counter, too drained to care. My son scooped it up as I collapsed onto the couch, barely registering him wandering over to his computer.\n\nThen, without a second thought, he started spinning it in his hands, the way only an easily distracted teenager could, like some cosmic fidget toy.\n\n“Careful with that,” I said without looking up from my phone. “Mom thinks it might be a bomb. It might be dangerous if you drop it.” My hands mimicking an explosion.\n\n“Dangerous?” he snorted. “It’s basically a paperweight. A cool one, but still a paperweight. Look, nothing is happening.” He threw the crystal hard at the ground, it bounced back, and he caught it in his hand.\n\nI was about to yell at him for being so reckless as he sat back down returning to his Roblox game. When he set it on top of his phone, a faint buzz filled the room, barely noticeable over the sound of my frustration.\n\n“What’s that?” I asked.\n\n“What’s what?” he replied, not taking his eyes off the screen.\n\n“That buzzing sound.”\n\nHe paused, lifting the crystal off his phone. The buzzing stopped. He frowned, setting it back down. The buzzing returned.\n\n“Dad, look at this!”\n\nI leaned in as he turned the phone towards me as he brought the crystal closer. A notification had popped up: Attempting to connect to NFC device.\n\nI blinked, baffled. “The crystal has NFC?”\n\n“What even is NFC?” he asked.\n\n“Near Field Communication. It’s how your phone talks to things like payment readers or headphones.” I frowned, watching the notification flicker but never progress. “Inert objects shouldn’t do this.”\n\nHe grinned. “Guess it’s not such a useless paperweight after all.”\n\nThe excitement quickly faded back to frustration. No matter what we tried, tapping it, holding it at different angles, even pairing it with every phone in the house, the crystal refused to do anything more than buzz and flash that same message: Attempting to connect to NFC device.\n\n“Why won’t it work?” he groaned after our umpteenth failed attempt.\n\n“It could be it’s not meant to work with phones,” I offered, though the words felt empty. “Perhaps it’s... I don’t know. Trying to communicate in a way we don’t understand yet.”\n\nMy son slumped onto the couch, holding the crystal up to the light. “Or possibly it’s just trolling us.”\n\nI laughed, though part of me suspected he might be right. All this weird dream-induced crap seemed to thrive on mystery, giving us just enough to keep us hooked but never enough to reveal its secrets. It was maddening but also exhilarating. Somewhere in its infinite fractal depths, there had to be an answer. We just had to figure out the right question.	0	31	f	2026-02-27 11:55:23.900512-05	2026-03-01 15:41:52.027098-05	normal	\N
234	59	2	Evening Contemplations	After putting his son to bed, the narrator and his wife watch the movie Passengers, which echoes themes of time and choice from his earlier reflections. As exhaustion takes hold, the narrator prepares for sleep, curious about what dreams await and whether they will reveal insights about the Mandela Effect.	**********\n\nAfter a day full of games, later that evening, as I watched him getting ready for bed, brushing his teeth and shutting down his computer, I couldn’t help but reflect on the symbolic nature of our day. The cards, the game, even our banter, everything felt like part of a larger tapestry. The barriers between dreams, reality, and imagination seemed thinner than ever.\n\nTo cap everything off, after he had been safely tucked into bed, my wife and I settled in for a movie: Passengers. Its a sci-fi romance that promised escapism. With the screen flickering to life, I let myself sink into the story, but my mind kept returning to the day’s revelations.\n\nThe movie’s themes, time, choices, the fragile threads that connect lives, echoed my own contemplations. Even as exhaustion claimed me, pulling me into sleep before the credits rolled, one thought remained clear: every moment, no matter how ordinary, holds the potential to reveal something extraordinary.\n\nWith the concepts swirling in my mind like a heard of horny elephants, I prepared for sleep, curiosity tingling through every nerve. What dreams awaited tonight? Would they guide me further, offering clarity on the Mandela Effect and the dreamscape’s mysteries? Or would they just be another flashback to that one time at band camp...?\n\nI kissed my wife’s forehead, her breathing a steady presence, a grounding force amidst the chaos. Slipping under the duvet, I readied myself for another nocturnal journey, determined to explore the boundaries of time and the subconscious, no matter the cost.	1	31	t	2026-02-27 12:04:55.335255-05	2026-03-01 15:41:52.027098-05	normal	\N
