y my usual reckless mix of passion and curiosity, I set up my next experiment with the precision of a mad scientist possessed. Ignoring the nagging voice in my head telling me to start small, I grabbed the telescope my son, and I had used to marvel at meteors just hours before and rushed over to our warehouse. With wild determination, I aimed it at Mars, our distant celestial neighbor, my heart pounding with the thrill of attempting the impossible.	3	157	f	2026-02-27 12:00:50.774866-05	2026-03-01 15:41:52.027098-05	normal	\N
196	51	8	From Packages to People	The narrator reflects on the success of the shipping partnerships and is inspired by a meme about people as packages. He envisions expanding the ERB gateway technology to revolutionize personal transportation, acquiring land near major transportation hubs and beginning construction of a global network.	These partnerships ignited a revolution in the industry. The cumbersome process of cross-border shipping was obliterated, replaced by a seamless operation powered by our innovations. Delivery times shattered records, and the giants of logistics quietly became reliant on The S Corp’s technology for their most critical shipments.\n\nLater, as I sat in my corner office in the S Corp headquarters, the afternoon sun filtered through the blinds of my office, casting bands of light across my desk. Mindlessly scrolling through Facebook, I stumbled upon a post in the “Brown Town” UPS drivers group. It was a lighthearted story about a child who tried to ship himself to Australia, accompanied by a meme that read, “People are packages too.” It made me chuckle—and then it made me think.\n\nThe idea took root: what if our gateway technology could revolutionize not just package delivery, but personal transportation? The possibilities were staggering. I envisioned a world where borders dissolved, and global travel became instantaneous. Fueled by this vision, I set plans into motion, acquiring land near major transportation hubs—airports, seaports, and train stations—as the foundation for a new network.\n\nIn mid-July, 2024 the massive trucks started rumbling into the new sites, piloted by humanoid robots that looked just like any other construction worker. Towering privacy walls rose around the properties, shielding the developments within from prying eyes. Despite having nanites at our disposal, I insisted on traditional construction for these barriers, this way no one would be the wiser that these massive projects were anything other than “corporate