502	125	1	The Living Room War Zone	Stephen arrives home to find his wife violently attacking Xavier over perceived wrongs. A terrifying escalation reveals the wife is no longer human—her eyes contain something wrong, her movements glitching. The confrontation ends in tragedy when she retrieves a gun and, during a struggle, is killed by a falling bookcase.	The noise hit me the second I stepped inside.\n\nNot words at first. Just the sound—high, sharp, scraping at the edges of thought. That’s when the syllables broke through.\n\n"YOU THINK I'M STUPID? YOU THINK I DON'T KNOW WHAT YOU'RE DOING?"\n\nI stepped through the door.\n\nThe living room was a war zone. Books scattered across the floor. The coffee table a complete mess. Xavier's backpack torn open, its contents spilled like entrails across the carpet.\n\nXavier stood in the middle of it all, shoulders rounded, head low, hands half-raised—not to defend, just waiting for it to be over. Fourteen, but already moving like someone twice his age.\n\nShe stood over him. My wife. The woman I'd woken up next to every morning for… how many years? I couldn't remember anymore. The number kept sliding away from me like trying to grasp a greased electric eel.\n\n"I did my homework," Xavier said. His voice was steady. Too steady. The kind of steady that comes from practice. "I showed you. I uploaded it to Canvas. You watched me do it."\n\n“DON’T LIE TO ME!”\n\nShe was still coming at him, words flying faster than thought. “You think I don’t see it? You think I don’t notice when you half-do the dishes, or when you leave the lights on, or when you sneak snacks into your room? You think I forgot about the wall you damaged when you drew all over it when you were five?”\n\n**********\n\nThe aggression in her movement triggered some ancient memory locked in my blood. Time didn't just slow; it shattered. I wasn't Stephen standing in a messy living room anymore. I was Aspolin, and I was desperate.\n\nI stood over Velistra’s broken body, the galaxy burning around us. She was gone—torn apart by a weapon that didn't just kill, but erased. Grief, absolute and maddening, surged through me. I refused to accept it. I reached into the fabric of the cosmos, not with the gentle hands of a creator, but with the claws of a tyrant. I grabbed the threads of time and pulled. I didn't care that it was forbidden. I didn't care that the magic required a fuel source no god should ever tap. I tore a piece of my own soul, a ragged, bleeding chunk of my eternal essence, and fed it into the spell.\n\nThe universe screamed. Reality buckled, forced to run backward against its will. The smoke un-billowed; the blood un-spilled. I watched, weeping with relief, as Velistra’s wounds knit together, as the light returned to her eyes, as the moment of her death was overwritten by the sheer force of my sacrifice. I had done the impossible. I had cheated destiny by rewriting the script while the ink was still wet.\n\nBut the universe demands balance. The wound on her body was gone, erased from history, but the pain remained. It was a paradox—a phantom agony echoing in a body that had never been hurt. She looked at me, alive and whole, but her eyes held a confusion that would never fade, a deep, cellular memory of a death that no longer existed. And in my own chest, the void where I had torn my soul throbbed—a permanent, hollow ache that would never heal, the infinite cost of a miracle that felt terrifyingly like a curse.\n\nThe vision snapped.\n\n**********\n\nEach sentence from my wife was sharper, louder, piling on top of the last until it wasn’t about dishes or walls or anything real anymore. It was about control.\n\nXavier didn’t move. Just stood there, eyes down, breathing slow, waiting for her to burn herself out.\n\nThat only made her angrier.\n\n“LOOK AT ME!” she screamed, voice cracking. “Say something! SAY IT!”\n\nHe flinched. Whispered, “I didn’t do anything.”\n\nAnd that broke her.\n\nShe attacked. Not a slap. Not a shove. A full-body lunge, fingers out, reaching for his throat.\n\n“Hey!” I was already moving. “Hey, stop—”\n\nI grabbed her around the waist, pulled her back. She thrashed, trying to tear free, still yelling at him—about chores, about grades, about nothing that made sense. Her voice splintered, rising and falling in a rhythm that wasn’t quite human.\n\nShe didn’t even seem to notice I was there. I might as well have been air.\n\n“Enough!” I shouted, shaking her. “Look at me!”\n\nAnd then she did.\n\nShe went still, mid-breath, head turning toward me like a puppet finding its next cue.\n\nHer eyes…\n\nGod. Her eyes.\n\nThey weren’t right. The pupils too wide, too black, swallowing everything around them. And behind that blackness—something moved. A shimmer. A flicker. Like static on an old TV, just before the picture cuts out.\n\n“You,” she hissed, eyes locking on me now. “You’re part of this. You and him. Conspiring. Whispering about me when you think I’m not around. I know what you’re doing.”\n\nI held my hands up slightly. “I’m not doing anything,” I said softly, careful. “I just got home. What’s going on?”\n\nShe laughed. Sharp, brittle. “What’s going on? You want to talk about what’s going on?” Her voice cracked, then rose again, steady but venomous. “You’re still unemployed. Sitting in there every day pretending you’re looking for jobs, pretending you’re busy. Playing your stupid video games while the world moves on without you.”\n\n“Please—”\n\n“No,” she snapped, pointing at me like an accusation. “Don’t ‘please’ me. You’ve been lazy for years. You’ve always been lazy. And now it’s rubbing off on your son.”\n\nThe word hit like a slap. Your. Not ours. Not Xavier.\n\n“Look at him,” she said, spinning toward the boy who’d backed himself against the far wall. “He’s just like you. No ambition. No discipline. Another parasite waiting for someone to take care of him. That’s what you’ve done to him. That’s what you’ve made.”\n\nShe turned back, trembling now, but not with fear. Something else. Her pupils flickered, swelling and shrinking like they were breathing.\n\n“You think I don’t see you two whispering? You think I don’t know? You’re both laughing at me. Plotting. Trying to make me crazy.”\n\nHer voice wavered, the edges stuttering like an old tape caught in rewind.\n\n“I’m not—” I started, but she cut me off with a hiss.\n\n“You’re doing it right now. That calm voice. That look. That tone. Always talking to me like I’m some kind of animal. Like I’m broken.”\n\nShe took a step closer, her head twitching, jaw clenched so tight it looked painful. “But I’m not the broken one, Stephen. You are.”\n\nXavier caught my eye. He mouthed two words: She's glitching.\n\nAnd he was right.\n\nI saw it now. The way her head jerked. Too fast. Too precise. Like frames missing from a film. The way her hands twitched at her sides, fingers curling and uncurling in a rhythm that wasn't quite human.\n\nThe NPC was breaking down.\n\n"Why don't we all just take a breath," I said. "Sit down. Talk about this."\n\n“Talk?” She tilted her head. Like something learning how to mimic people. “You want to talk? Fine. Let’s talk about you.”\n\nHer voice dropped lower, steady now, almost conversational, which somehow made it worse.\n\n“Let’s talk about how you’ve been sneaking around. How you think I don’t see you, sitting there on your phone, looking up old girlfriends. Rebecca. Jamie.”\n\nI froze.\n\nShe pushed me back and then took a step closer, eyes glittering, pupils swallowing what little color was left. “You think I don’t know what that means? You think I don’t see what you’re doing behind my back?”\n\n“I haven’t—”\n\n“Oh, please.” The laugh that came out wasn’t right. Too loud. Too hollow. “What don’t I do enough for you already? What more do you want from me? You’ve got a house, a family, someone who actually stays with you. And you still go sniffing around like some… some whore.”\n\nShe spat the word. It didn’t even sound like it came from her anymore.\n\nHer hands clenched, nails digging into her palms. “You think I don’t see it? You think you can hide your filth from me? I know what you are, Stephen. I know what you’ve been doing. Or who you’ve been doing. Who is this Whitney?”\n\nThe room felt smaller. The air thick. And beneath the fury in her face, something else flickered—like static crawling under her skin, distorting the edges of who she was supposed to be.\n\nMy blood went cold.\n\n"Who told you about Whitney?"\n\n"No one had to tell me." Her smile was too wide. Too many teeth. "I know things, Stephen. I know what you're trying to do. You think you can leave? You think you can take him with you?"\n\nShe pointed at Xavier.\n\n"He stays. He's mine. I made him."\n\n"You didn't make me," Xavier said. His voice was quiet. Dangerous. "You're not even real."\n\nThe room went silent.\n\nShe blinked. Once. Twice. Three times. Each blink too slow, too deliberate, like someone manually closing and opening shutters.\n\nThen she screamed.\n\nA raw, primal shriek that seemed to vibrate the air itself. The windows rattled. The pictures on the walls tilted. Xavier clapped his hands over his ears.\n\nAnd she moved.\n\nFast. Too fast. One second she was ten feet away. The next she was on Xavier, her hands wrapped around his throat, her nails digging into his skin.\n\nI didn't think. Just acted.\n\nI grabbed her shoulders and pulled. She came away from Xavier, her nails leaving four red lines around his neck. He stumbled backward, gasping.\n\nShe twisted in my grip. Snapped her head back. Caught me square in the nose.\n\nPain exploded. Hot and immediate. Blood flooded my mouth. I let go, stumbling, my vision swimming.\n\n"Dad!" Xavier's voice. Distant. Muffled.\n\nI blinked. Tried to focus.\n\nShe was walking toward the bedroom. Calm now. Serene. Humming something under her breath. A lullaby? No. Not a lullaby. Something older. Darker. A melody that made my angels weep.\n\n"Xavier," I said. My voice came out thick, nasal. "Xavier, get out. Go to the car."\n\n"Dad, she's—"\n\n"Go."\n\nHe ran.\n\nShe came back from the bedroom holding the little pink Sig Sauer P365 like it belonged to both of us.\n\nI remembered when she'd first wanted it. Years ago, right after we moved from Japan. She'd fixated on it, said owning a gun was 'so American' and she wanted one. She wanted to feel safe, she insisted. So for her birthday, I'd caved and gotten her this. We took the class together. The instructor showed us how to rack the slide, how to breathe, where to put your feet. It was... one of those small, steady memories that felt like a good thing. A stupid, ordinary good thing.\n\nNow she held it like a threat. “I bought this for home protection,” she said, voice flat. “Remember? You said we didn’t need it. But I insisted. A woman should be able to feel safe in her own house.”\n\nShe racked the slide. The click cut through the room. Too loud. It filled every empty place.\n\n“I don’t feel safe anymore, Stephen.”\n\nI raised my hands. Slow. Open. The tone you use with something that might break. “Put the gun down.”\n\n"Why? So you can leave? So you can take him away from me?" She shook her head. "No. No, I don't think so. If I can't have him, no one can."\n\n"He's your son."\n\n"He's a sickness," she said quietly, almost tender. "A mutation that never should’ve survived. Something rotten that grew where it wasn’t meant to."\n\nHer eyes shifted to me, pupils shrinking to pinpoints. "Just like you."\n\nI dove.\n\nThe shot was deafening. I felt the bullet pass inches from my head, heard it punch through the countertop behind me. Granite dust filled the air.\n\nShe raised the gun again, finger tight on the trigger.\n\n“Please,” I said, voice shaking. “You don’t want to do this.”\n\nHer head tilted, slow, almost curious. “Don’t I? Maybe this is what it takes to make you listen.”\n\nThe shot came before I could breathe. A crack so loud it split the air. The round chewed through the cabinet in front of me, spraying wood and glass shards into my hair.\n\n“Put it down!” I shouted. “You’re going to hurt someone!”\n\n“Someone?” she snapped, pacing toward me. “There’s no someone here. Just you. Just your lies.”\n\nI heard shuffling from the living room. Then, wood creaked.\n\nI risked a glance. She’d climbed onto the lower shelves of the big bookcase in the living room. Trying now to angle the gun over the countertop.\n\n“Don’t!” I yelled, but she was already reaching higher, using the edge of the shelf for balance, her weight shifting onto an Ikea fame that wasn’t meant to hold it.\n\n“Get up, Stephen!” she screamed. “Face me!”\n\nThe top shelf groaned.\n\nI ducked lower as she leaned out for a clear shot. Another deafening bang. The recoil threw her backward, arms flailing. Her head whipped back, impaling itself on one of the trophies on the top shelf. The bookcase itself wobbled, then tipped—slow at first, then fast—five hundred pounds of wood and words and gravity.\n\n“NO—!” she gasped, but it was too late.\n\nThe whole thing came down on her with a sound like the world snapping in half.\n\nSilence followed. Just the soft rustle of settling pages and the smell of splintered wood and spent gunpowder.\n\nI lay there, my ears ringing, my nose streaming blood, staring at the wreckage.\n\nOne of her arms stuck out from under the bookshelf. Palm up. Fingers twitching.\n\n"Dad?" Xavier's voice from the doorway. Small. Scared.\n\n"I'm okay." I pushed myself up. Every muscle screamed. "I'm okay. Is she…?"\n\nWe both looked at the bookshelf.\n\nHer fingers stopped twitching.\n\n"We need to go," I said. "Now. Get in the car."\n\n"What about her?"\n\n"She's not real, Xavi. Remember?" The words sounded hollow, a stupid lie even as I said them. "She's just… she's just a program. And the program just crashed."\n\nHe stared at me. His eyes were too old. Too knowing.\n\n"You don't believe that," he said.\n\nHe was right. I didn't.\n\nMy hands were shaking. A cold shock was spreading through my chest, pushing out the air. She'd just tried to kill us. She'd tried to kill him. The anger was there, hot and sharp, but it was tangled up in something else. Something hollow.\n\nBecause somewhere under that bookshelf, buried in particleboard and paperbacks, was a woman I'd woken up next to for years. A woman whose name I couldn't remember. Whose face I'd kissed. Whose body I tried to hold.\n\nReal or not, I'd just let her die.\n\nAnd the reason my chest ached, the reason I'd said that stupid thing about her being a program...\n\nBecause I’m still trying to love her.\n\n"Car," I said, my voice breaking. "Now."\n\nWe ran.\n\nThe car started on the second try. I threw it in reverse, tires squealing as we backed out of the driveway. In the rearview mirror, I saw the open door. The darkness inside.\n\nAnd something moving.\n\nA shadow. Pulling itself out from under the wreckage. Standing. Tilting its head in that wrong, mechanical way.\n\n"Dad…" Xavier's voice was strangled.\n\n"I see it."\n\nI floored it.\n\nThe shadow stood in the doorway and watched us go. It didn't follow. Didn't chase. Just stood there, still and patient, like it had all the time in the world.\n\nLike it knew we'd be back.\n\nI drove without thinking. Just drove. My hands were shaking so badly I could barely hold the wheel. Blood dripped from my nose onto my shirt, dark and wet.\n\n"Where are we going?" Xavier asked.\n\nI pulled Patrick's card from my pocket. Handed it to him. "Put the address in the GPS."\n\nHe did. Orlando. St. Michael's Outreach Center.\n\n"What's there?"\n\n"Someone who might be able to help." I wiped blood from my upper lip. "Someone who knows what's really going on."\n\nI glanced over, my heart still hammering. "Are you okay? Did she...?"\n\nXavier was quiet for a moment. He touched his neck.\n\nI looked. It was already bruising. Not deep, but terrifyingly real. Physical. My stomach tightened. He'd survive, but the welts were a stark reminder of how close she'd come.\n\n"We're past the point of..." I started, but didn't know how to finish. "The world’s breaking down."\n\n"Is that good or bad?"\n\n"I don't know."\n\nWe drove in silence for a while. The sun was setting, painting the sky in shades of orange and purple that looked too perfect. Too saturated. Like someone had cranked up the color values in post-production.\n\nMy phone sat alone in it’s charging cradle, the glow of the lines directing us to our destination. I wanted to throw it out the window. Part of me expected it to ring, to buzz, to announce another part of the system coming down on us. It stayed silent.\n\n"So... with Mom like that..." Xavier started, his voice quiet. "Does this mean we're... leaving?"\n\nI looked at my son. At his too-old eyes. At the wounds that shouldn't be there.\n\n"Yeah," I said. "That's the idea. Find someone who can help us figure this all out. Then find the way out of this fucking place."\n\nXavier nodded slowly. Like he understood. Like he'd been waiting for me to say it out loud.\n\n"Good," he said. "Because I want out of this. All of it."	0	389	f	2026-02-27 12:29:23.081071-05	2026-03-01 15:41:52.027098-05	normal	\N
477	119	4	Testing the Loop	Stephen spends the day testing his theory that his wife is an NPC by refusing to engage with her scripts. After a failed attempt with flattery and silence, she literally loops—repeating the exact same dialogue—confirming his suspicion.	**********\n\nThe rest of the day just kept proving the inanity of the NPC.\n\nWe were supposed to go shopping later—another one of her "errands" that required me as a silent accessory. I was putting on my shoes by the door when she stopped dead, staring at my ankles.\n\n"Are you seriously wearing white socks with those shorts?"\n\nIt was such a stupid, petty thing. Standard Argument Script #4B: Fashion Policing. I was too exhausted to play my part.\n\n"You know what? Never mind," I said, kicking my shoes back off. "I'll just stay home."\n\nImmediate pivot. Script #4B aborted; Script #12A initiated: The Neglectful Husband.\n\n"Oh, so now you don't even want to spend time with me? I ask for one hour of your day to help me, and it's too much?"\n\nI didn't have the energy to fight the logic loop. I walked back to the bedroom, dug out a pair of garish, mismatched argyle socks I’d gotten as a gag gift years ago, and put them on. I walked past her, slipped into my shoes, and went out to the car without a word.\n\nThe moment her door closed, it was like nothing had happened. The script had achieved its goal—getting me in the car—and terminated. She sat in comfortable obliviousness of what just transpired, all the way to Target, humming along to the generic pop station, perfectly content.\n\nIt was terrifying.\n\nBy 2 AM, I couldn't sleep. The house was quiet except for the faint, rhythmic snoring from standard-issue wife beside me.\n\nI stood in the bathroom, staring at the hollowed-out face in the mirror. I looked down at my arms. Smooth. Whole.\n\nI remembered the pills—almost choking trying to swallow the bits dry, the slow fade. But then there was the other memory, sharper, bloodier, overlaying the first: the bathtub, the razors. Which one was real? Yesterday I was sure it was the pills. This morning, the razors felt truer. Now, neither felt like my memory. They felt like conflicting backstories uploaded to the same character file, both pasted over by a system that refused to let me quit.\n\nAnd now Xavier's words wouldn't leave me alone. NPCs don't have internal monologues. They loop.\n\nI was still depressed—that heavy, suffocating blanket hadn't lifted—but something sharp was poking through it now. A morbid, dangerous curiosity. If I couldn't leave this place, maybe I could break it.\n\nI needed to know. I needed to know if I was crazy, or if this was... something else.\n\nI went to the kitchen and grabbed the old toaster oven we kept above the microwave. It was a fire hazard, the one with the frayed cord that sparked if you jiggled it wrong. Perfect.\n\nBack in the bathroom, I ran the tub. Hot. I filled it to the brim. Grabbed the big jar of “Organic Epsom Salts” my wife kept for soaking her feet and dumped the whole thing in.\n\nI went over to the outlet. With a flathead screwdriver, I popped the faceplate off and disabled the GFCI circuit, bypassing the safety that would have tripped the breaker. My hands were steady. Methodical.\n\nIf I'm doing this, I'm doing it right.\n\nI plugged the toaster oven in, turned it on for 60 minutes, and balanced it precariously on the porcelain rim of the tub.\n\nI stripped. If this was just another loop, I didn't want to wake up chafing in wet boxers. I climbed into the scalding water, naked and vulnerable.\n\nI laid back, letting the heat soak into my muscles, almost peaceful. I stared up at a little spider making it’s house in the corner of the ceiling, then over at the humming metal box perched inches from my death.\n\n"Go ahead," I whispered to the spider, to the universe, to whatever admin was watching this feed. "Unmake this."\n\nI tapped it with one wet finger. It tipped.\n\nIt didn't just hit the water; it tore reality.\n\nThe world dissolved into agonizing, seizing white light. It wasn't just pain; it was an override of my entire biology. My spine snapped backward into an impossible arch, muscles tearing themselves off the bone in a frantic attempt to escape my own skin. I felt my molars shatter as my jaw clamped shut, grinding enamel into dust.\n\nMy vision didn't just white out; it boiled. I could feel the fluids in my eyes cooking, turning my sight into a visceral, red-veined kaleidoscope of organic horror. Every nerve ending, every synapse fired at once—a cacophony of screaming meat.\n\nRight before the darkness took me, a smell hit my ruined nose. Salty. Smoky. Delicious.\n\nBacon, I thought, detached and delirious. I smell delicious. Like Sunday breakfast.\n\nThen—nothing.\n\nI opened my eyes.\n\nSame bed. 6:47 AM.\n\nOh, you've got to be fucking kidding me.\n\nMy wife rolled over, eyes still closed, brow furrowed. "Can you not thrash around? I barely slept."\n\nNot: Are you okay? Not: What happened?\n\nJust: You are disrupting my subroutines.\n\n"I electrocuted myself last night," I said to her.\n\nShe sighed. Heavy. Theatrical. "Stephen, I don't have the energy for your... drama right now."\n\nShe got up, grabbed her phone, and left.\n\nI lay there, and the wrongness wasn't creeping anymore. It was screaming.\n\nThirty minutes later, we were lazing around the house. She was pouring her single cup of tea. I sat on the couch in the living room.\n\nShe scrolled. "Did you apply to anything yesterday?"\n\nHere we go. The script.\n\n"A few."\n\n"A few? Stephen, 'a few' isn't a strategy. You need to be sending out twenty, thirty a day."\n\n"I sent out fifteen."\n\n"Fifteen isn't enough." Her voice was calm, clinical. "Do you know how embarrassing this is for me? People ask what you do, and I have to say you're between opportunities."\n\nXavier's words echoed in my head: Loops. Scripted responses.\n\nI decided to test it. Grey rock her. Give the simulation nothing to work with.\n\n"You're right," I said flatly.\n\nShe paused, her thumb hovering over her screen. She looked up. "What?"\n\n"You're right. I need to do better."\n\nHer brow furrowed. The code hadn’t anticipated agreement. She buffered. "Well... good. I'm glad you're finally—"\n\nI interrupted her with a smile. Soft. Loving. I just stared directly into her eyes and said nothing.\n\nShe froze. Her mouth opened slightly, then closed.\n\n"What... what are you doing?"\n\nI didn't answer. Just kept smiling. Unblinking.\n\nHer face twitched—a micro-expression of pure computerized confusion. "Stephen. Stop it."\n\nI tilted my head, keeping the gentle smile plastered on my face. "Stop what, honey?"\n\nShe blinked rapidly, her eyes unfocusing for a second as she looked down at her cooling tea.\n\nThen—she reset.\n\n"Did you apply to anything yesterday?"\n\nThe exact same words. The exact same inflection.\n\nMy smile dropped. My blood ran cold.\n\n"What did you just say?"\n\nHer voice sharpened, annoyed now. "I said, did you apply to anything yesterday?"\n\nLoop. She looped.\n\nI stood up, my chair scraping loud against the tile. "I need to go."\n\n"Go? Go where? You don't have anywhere to—"\n\nI was already out the door.	3	389	t	2026-02-27 12:27:38.515738-05	2026-03-01 15:41:52.027098-05	normal	\N
482	120	1	Waking Wrong	Stephen wakes in his familiar bed, disoriented by the existence of failed suicide attempts from timelines that no longer exist. He receives a mysterious message from Becca about a wine and cheese event and decides to leave the house despite his wife's location tracking, driving toward Orlando.	I woke up wrong.\n\nNot the usual wrong. Not the two-years-unemployed-wife-hates-me-son-sees-through-the-simulation wrong. This was deeper. Cellular. Like my body remembered dying and couldn't quite figure out why it was still breathing.\n\nThe electrocution should have worked. I felt it work. The toaster hit the water, and every nerve in my body fired at once, muscles tearing themselves off bone, eyeballs cooking in their sockets. I smelled myself. Bacon on a Sunday morning. Delicious.\n\nThen there was the train, and the razors, and even the pills.\n\nBut all that was 6 days ago in a timeline that didn’t exist.\n\nSo, I woke up here. Same bed. Same grey light filtering through curtains that haven't been washed in... I don't know. Months? Years? Time doesn't work right anymore.\n\nI sat up. My wife's side of the bed was empty. Sheets cold. She'd been gone a while. Probably dropped Xavier off at school and went out to do some shopping or something.\n\nThe house was too quiet. Not peaceful quiet. Waiting quiet. Like the silence between a gun cocking and the trigger pull.\n\nI checked my phone. 8:23 AM. Battery at 41% because I forgot to charge it. Again.\n\nOne message notification.\n\nBecca: Hey! Don't forget about today. Wine and cheese. 2 PM. Can't wait to see you!\n\nShe'd included the location pin again.\n\nI stared at it until the screen went dark.\n\nStumbling out, the kitchen was a crime scene of mundane neglect. Dishes piled in the sink despite me washing them... when? Yesterday? Two days ago? The timeline kept slipping. Coffee maker cold and empty. She never makes any. Didn't even leave a note.\n\nI stood there for a long time, listening to the house breathe.\n\nThen I grabbed my keys off my bookshelf.\n\nShould I text her? Let her know I was leaving?\n\nThe thought alone exhausted me. She'd see it on Google Maps anyway. We had location sharing turned on. Some relic from when we pretended to care about each other's safety instead of just tracking movements.\n\nI had half a thought to turn it off. Disable tracking. Just... disappear for an afternoon.\n\nBut then I imagined the argument. The accusations. You turned off your location? What are you hiding? Who are you seeing?\n\nNot jealousy. Never jealousy. Just... territoriality. Like I was an asset that needed monitoring.\n\nAnd what would I tell her anyway? That I was driving to Orlando to see high school friends at a bookshop whose names I had long forgotten? That I was wasting gas—money we didn't have—on nostalgia?\n\nShe'd make it about the job search. About priorities. About how I was running away from responsibility.\n\nShe wouldn't be wrong. Not entirely.\n\nBut I couldn't stay in this house another minute.\n\nI pocketed my phone. Left it on. Let her see where I went.\n\nLet her be angry.\n\nI just... left.	0	389	f	2026-02-27 12:28:00.912937-05	2026-03-01 15:41:52.027098-05	normal	\N
479	119	6	Connection with Xavier	Stephen watches Xavier gaming and connects with him over a real conversation about lore, childhood memories, and the feeling that reality is scripted. A brief memory-flash of a girl in biology class disrupts the moment, suggesting Stephen has other lives or memories bleeding through.	**********\n\nIt was evening, two days after the train that never happened, four days before the toaster that never would be. The house hummed with static warmth—the low whirr of the fridge, the faint buzz of Xavier’s gaming headset bleeding through.\n\nI sat on the couch watching him from across the room. The light from his monitor painted his face in shifting blues and reds. He was laughing at something on Discord—a genuine, unscripted sound. It had variation. Warmth.\n\nI realized how long it had been since I’d heard a real sound in this house.\n\nXavier spun in his chair, rocking back and forth. "Dad... you doing the creepy stare thing again?"\n\nI half-smiled. "Sorry. Just watching you exist."\n\n"That's weird, man."\n\nI walked over and stood behind him. "What are you watching?"\n\n"Stream highlights. This guy breaks down lore from Roblox horror games."\n\n"Roblox has lore?"\n\n"Everything's got lore if you look long enough." He pulled his headset down around his neck. "They made a whole multiverse around this Doors game I play. It’s where all those plushies I have come from."\n\nI laughed softly. "When I was your age, I read books or magic cards for that."\n\n"Books are just slow YouTube with worse comments."\n\n"You used to read. Remember Captain Underpants when you were little?"\n\n"Yeah, but... everything's on video now. It feels realer. Even the AI brain rot coming out is more real."\n\nI studied him. "Does it? Feel realer?"\n\nHe hesitated, looking at his hands. "Sometimes. But sometimes it feels like it's all running off a script. Even online. It’s like... Dead Internet Theory, you know? Where most of the internet is just bots talking to bots. It feels lonely."\n\n"We had that too, you know. Before the internet."\n\nHe looked up, skeptical. "You had bots?"\n\n"We had 'Dead Reality.' Same feeling, different tech. We didn't have backrooms memes, but we had mass hysteria about mole people living in the sewers, or MK Ultra programming. It’s the same itch—the feeling that the surface world is fake and there’s something rotting underneath."\n\nI leaned back, a weird, cold nostalgia washing over me. "Back in the gifted program, GATE I think it was called, we used to trade these grainy Xeroxes of supposed Soviet psychic experiments. Remote viewing, psychotronics. We were convinced we could tap into it if we just stared at Zener cards long enough. It was our version of no-clipping out of reality."\n\nXavier stared at me, actually engaged. "You guys were weird."\n\n"We were bored. And lonely. Same as you."\n\nHe processed that for a second, then asked, "So... what were you actually like in high school? Besides the Soviet psychic stuff?"\n\nThe question landed like a stone in still water.\n\n"Me?"\n\n"Yeah. You talk about books all the time. What did you actually do?"\n\n"Mostly read. Did some Cross Country. Lots of Martial Arts, DnD, and Magic the Gathering. Tried to be deep."\n\n"You were deep?"\n\n"No. I just thought if I looked confused all the time, people would think I was thinking about something important."\n\nXavier laughed. "That is the saddest flex ever."\n\nI chuckled too, but something shifted. A flicker of memory—faces, lockers, the smell of old paperbacks. I could almost hear a laugh that wasn't Xavier's.\n\n"You okay?" he asked.\n\n"Yeah. Just remembering... a couple of friends."\n\n"You had friends? Harsh."\n\nHe spun his chair fully toward me. "You ever wish you could go back? To when stuff made sense?"\n\n"I don't think it ever did, Xavi. We just didn't know it yet."\n\nFor a split second, the room flickered.\n\nI wasn't looking at my son. I was sitting at a black-topped lab table, etched with decades of bored graffiti. The air smelled faintly of formaldehyde and intensely of vanilla and peaches from the girl sitting a few desks in front of me. I was staring at the back of her neck, where a few of her hairs had escaped her ponytail and caught the fluorescent light.\n\nA yearning hit me—deep, visceral, and confusing. I didn't even know why I wanted to reach out, just that the distance between my hand and her shoulder felt like a canyon I needed to cross. It was an ache of pure, uncut realness that I hadn't felt in... god, I didn't even know how long.\n\nThen it snapped. The smell of peaches vanished, replaced by the ozone scent of Xavier’s gaming PC.\n\n"So, we're born confused," he said, pulling me back, "and then what? We just... spend the rest of our lives trying to figure out the controls?"\n\nI nodded slowly, the vertigo fading. "Pretty much. We're all just button-mashing until something works."\n\n"That's kind of bleak, Dad."\n\n"Maybe. But it’s the main reason you’ll never beat me at Smash Bros. And also means there's no wrong way to play, right?"\n\nHe stood up to grab a soda from the fridge in the kitchen, pausing by one of the many bookshelves that lined the living room walls. "You actually read all these?"\n\n"Most. Some twice."\n\n"Why keep them? You can find everything online."\n\n"There's something about holding a thing that remembers you back," I said, realizing it as I said it.\n\n"So, like... sentimental NFTs?"\n\nI groaned. "You ruin everything poetic."\n\nWe laughed again. Real laughter. It broke the static in the air.\n\nXavier poked at a row of thick, dusty books on the bottom shelf that didn't match the rest of the sci-fi paperbacks. "What are these?"\n\n"Ancient history," I said. "Yearbooks."\n\n"Let's look. I wanna see you in the ancient times."\n\nHe pulled one out—heavy, cracked spine, smelling of attic dust. He flipped it open on his lap, dust motes dancing in the monitor's light.\n\n"Eau Gallie High. Nice hat and sideburns, Dad." He pointed to a scribbled note in the margin of a page. "'You still owe me coffee Bob. –Becca.'\n\n“Who’s Becca? But more importantly who’s Bob."\n\n"Bob was a nickname I had. Becca…She was... someone I knew. Someone from AP Bio."\n\n"She’s cute," he said, looking at the small black-and-white photo next to the signature. "You should look her up on Facebook."\n\n"I don't really talk to folks on—"\n\nMy phone buzzed on the bed beside me. I picked it up.\n\nNotification: People You May Know – Rebecca Caldwell.\n\nI stared at it. The algorithm. It was listening.\n\nXavier grinned, seeing the screen. "See? Facebook's got ESP."\n\n"Yeah," I murmured, my heart tightening. "Or something."	5	390	t	2026-02-27 12:27:38.515738-05	2026-03-01 15:41:52.027098-05	normal	\N
567	137	2	The Sequel's Unexpected Genesis	The author describes how a second writing challenge without a story idea led them back to The Towers of Elysium world, initially resulting in a depressing trajectory until a literal head kick from their son sparked the concept of revisiting the prison from a different angle. This led to naming the protagonist and developing richer characters.	Fast forward then to April 2025 and the AutoCrit 90 day writing challenge. I had no story idea. So I just started writing.\n\nSuddenly I was back in the world of The Towers of Elysium and wanted to just see where the story would have gone there if things had continued. I quickly wrote myself into a corner, the beginning there was just a depressing ending to what should have been an eventful life.\n\nThat’s when my son kicked me in the head (literally, I was doing something under the couch and he ran into me) and I came up with the let’s go back into the prison but from another angle approach.\n\nThis time, however, I gave my protagonist a name. And started fleshing things out – and by doing so, it was a symbol of the protagonist no longer being just an NPC (like they were portrayed in the first book), but a living breathing person.\n\nThis time, unlike NPCs in the previous book, the characters in the book started taking on lives of their own, and things went off the rails (my initial outline didn’t even include Whitney, but was rather a story of what would you do if you could re-live high school all over again and knew that the world was a simulation).\n\nNext thing you know I’ve got girls fighting over me (who in the initial drafts were just nameless folks), and a demon auditor trying to make contact.\n\nBy the time I finished Elysium Fallen, I knew there was one more story to tell here – and to get this to a meaningful ending, I needed to escape from the prison once and for all.\n\nNotice that in Escape from Elysium, my son, who now gets a name, isn’t an NPC, but my wife, who still remained nameless, did (and unfortunately if you didn’t pick up on that, it would have been clearer from one of the lost chapters from Elysium Rising – only entities had names and conversely that’s why their counterparts had names as well).	1	465	f	2026-02-27 12:33:31.016824-05	2026-03-01 15:51:21.170966-05	normal	\N
452	112	5	The Word That Doesn't Belong	The protagonist speaks the word 'Etheris' instead of 'Earth,' triggering deep recognition and alarm. His wife enters with a phone call from someone named Whitney, and suddenly he remembers everything—the truth behind his reality begins to crack.	I freeze. My fingers hover over the keyboard.\n\nEtheris?\n\nWhere the hell did that word come from? I meant to say Earth. Dreams come to Earth to die. That's the phrase. That's always been the phrase.\n\nBut my mouth said Etheris. Like it knew something my brain didn't.\n\nThe room suddenly feels... wrong. Too quiet. Like the moment between a lightning strike and thunder. I look at my hands. They're shaking. Why are they shaking?\n\nMy wife walks in, holding her phone at arm's length like it might bite her. Her face is pale. "Honey... there's someone on the phone for you."\n\n"Who?"\n\n"She says her name is Whitney." My wife's voice cracks.\n\n"I don't know anyone named—"\n\nBut I can't finish the sentence. Because suddenly I do. I remember everything. The flickers. The shadows. The demon in Jamie's smile. Whitney vanishing into William. The journal that wrote itself. Benji's winged eye. The kick that started it all.\n\nThe phone trembles in my wife's hand. I can hear a voice on the other end. Distant. Familiar. Impossible.\n\n"Stephen?" the voice says. Clear as a bell. Clear as a memory that was never supposed to exist. "Stephen, it's Whitney. But you need to listen very carefully. I found Jay…"\n\nI drop the phone as the line suddenly goes dead.	4	63	f	2026-02-27 12:21:01.629298-05	2026-03-01 15:51:21.170966-05	normal	\N
191	51	3	Building the Physical Empire	The S Corp begins its global expansion by creating a fleet of autonomous Real Estate Lawyer Machines (RELMs) to acquire properties worldwide. The RELMs discover a prime skyscraper in Houston, Texas, which becomes the company's new headquarters.	With the news of our financials starting to trickle out in the market, people would start talking. Soon the S Corp needed more than financial dominance, it needed a physical presence to embody its influence. The solution? Expanding our footprint into the real world with strategic acquisitions across the globe.\n\nTo achieve this, we created a fleet of advanced Real Estate Lawyer Machines (RELMs), powered by the Infinitron and programmed to handle property scouting and negotiations autonomously. Each night, using the ERBs, I opened discreet portals to major cities, sending the RELMS on missions to secure prime real estate. As dawn broke in the local time zone, they scoured cityscapes, assessing properties, negotiating deals, and meticulously evaluating potential acquisitions for The S Corp.\n\nWithin a few days of unleashing our legion onto the world,  their tireless work culminated in a breakthrough: one RELM discovered a stunning skyscraper at 1400 Smith Street in Houston, Texas. Towering over the city’s vibrant economy, the building exuded grandeur and sophistication—the perfect symbol of The S Corp’s ambition.\n\nThe acquisition process, flawlessly executed Liam’s law firm, ensured every detail was handled with precision and utmost secrecy. His team of legal and financial experts, coupled with the S Corps sizeable account balance, navigated complex negotiations seamlessly.\n\nThe purchase provided S Corp with a concrete presence, representing its impact and establishing a base for future expansion. Standing before the gleaming skyscraper, I felt the weight of our journey and the limitless potential of what we’d built.	2	197	f	2026-02-27 12:02:17.241235-05	2026-03-01 15:51:21.170966-05	