1547	434	1	The group meets at a Greyhound station at dawn. TJ shows signs of violence from his father, and Morgan describes their night hiding from police.	April 19, 1996 - Day One: Departure\n\nThe Greyhound station squats on the wrong side of town like a concrete toad, sweating diesel and desperation in the pre-dawn gloom. We should have been able to fly—TJ's parents have money bleeding out of their investment portfolios. But when he mentioned Nevada, they went full psycho.\n\nTJ shows up at the station looking like he's been through a war. Twigs in his hair, mud on his Reagan/Nixon shirt, a bruise spreading across his jaw like spilled wine.\n\n"Jesus, what happened to you?"\n\n"My dad happened." His voice is hollow. "The neurosurgeon who saves lives for a living? He tried to cave my skull in with a nine-iron when I said I was leaving. Mom held me down."\n\nMorgan clutches her backpack tighter. "We spent the night in the woods behind my house. My parents were cool about the trip—even offered to help—but then the cops showed up. TJ's parents told them he'd stolen fifty thousand dollars and was armed."\n\n"Armed with what?" Jamie asks.\n\n"My MTG deck, apparently." TJ attempts a laugh that sounds like breaking glass. "Cops searched Morgan's house, turned the whole place upside down. Her parents tried to explain, but..." He trails off, staring at nothing.\n\n"We've been hiding in drainage ditches since midnight," Morgan continues. "Moving between bushes like we're in fucking Vietnam. Every siren, every flashlight—" She shudders.\n\nI scan the parking lot nervously. "They still looking?"\n\n"Probably. Dad's golf buddy is the chief of police." TJ pulls out a wadded ball of cash—all hundreds and fifties. "Thank God for the emergency stash I kept in the garage. It's a wonder they're not here right now."\n\nSo here we are at 5:47 AM, standing in line behind a woman arguing with her grocery bag and a man who might be dead but hasn't realized it yet.\n\n"Four tickets to Nowhere, Nevada," I tell the clerk, whose face has the texture of beef jerky left in a tanning bed.\n\nShe squints at her amber monitor. "Nowhere ain't on the... wait." Her fingers stop moving. The screen flickers. "That's... huh. Says here it's a valid destination. Route 666-B." She looks up, and for a second her pupils are vertical slits. "Y'all sure? That's way outside the coverage area."\n\n"Coverage area?" Morgan asks.\n\n"For existing," the clerk says flatly, then blinks like she's coming out of a trance. "I mean, for emergency services. No ambulances out there. No police. No... help." She stares through us like we're already ghosts. "Place like that, you break down, you stay broken."\n\nThe bus looks like it was assembled from the parts of other, sadder buses. The license plate reads "LAST-CHANCE" which feels a bit on the nose. Our driver has the skeletal face of someone who's been dying by degrees since the Nixon administration.	0	\N	3	dialogue-heavy	present	2026-02-27 12:20:00.287901-05	2026-02-27 12:20:00.287901-05	\N	\N	0	\N	\N
