ng to become.\n\n"Holy shit," Gil whispered. "He's gotten worse."\n\n"Worse?" Jamie's voice was barely audible. "How long has he been like this?"\n\nAndrew looked at her like she'd asked why water was wet. "Since forever? William's always been a psycho. Where have you guys been?"\n\nBut we knew where we'd been. We'd been living in a world where Whitney existed. Where Patrick's best friend was a gentle girl who brought cookies to study sessions and worried about everyone's feelings.\n\nNow we were stuck in a world where that same best friend was a fascist in a letterman jacket, and we were the only ones who remembered it had ever been different.\n\nPatrick was shaking, staring at the spot where William had been. "He doesn't remember Whitney. He remembers me, but not her. How is that possible?"\n\n"Because she never existed," TJ said bitterly, picking up the pieces of his broken Reagan button. "According to this fucked-up reality, our friend was always that monster."\n\nBecca was crying silently, tears streaming down her face. "She's really gone, isn't she? Not just hidden or displaced. Gone."\n\nI picked up the scattered cards from Patrick's deck, my hands shaking. Most of them were there—Counterspell, Wrath of God, all the careful control magic.\n\nBut the Veilwalker card was missing.\n\nThe one card that shouldn't exist. The one that had warned us about being alone.\n\nWhitney was alone now. Wherever she was, whatever hell they'd dragged her to, she was facing it without us.\n\nAnd we were stuck here with her torturer, in a world that had forgotten she'd ever needed saving.	2	\N	4	dialogue-heavy	present	2026-02-27 12:16:45.074364-05	2026-02-27 12:16:45.074364-05	\N	\N	0	\N	\N
