766	218	2	The family visits Stonehenge, where the protagonist senses an otherworldly presence and experiences unsettling memories from his previous encounter at Aokigahara.	Our tour took us to some places further afield, to Stonehenge, where the chill of the countryside wrapped around us like a cloak. The ancient stones loomed against the backdrop of a cloudy sky, their jagged silhouettes both eerie and mesmerizing.\n\n“Why do you think they built this?” my son asked, his voice hushed, as if afraid to disturb the spirits of those who had come before us.\n\n“No one really knows,” my wife answered, her gaze fixed on the monoliths. “A burial ground, maybe. Or a place of worship. It’s like a puzzle that’s been unsolved for thousands of years.”\n\nI nodded, feeling the pressure of the Threshold seeping through the area and into my chest. “Whatever it was, you can feel the intention here. Like they built it for something bigger than themselves.”\n\nWe continued to wander through the site. The wind seemed to whisper untold mysteries in a foreign tongue. The stones radiated an otherworldly presence, their shadows stretching long and dark across the damp grass. I couldn’t shake the unsettling feeling; the memories surfacing from what I had triggered at Aokigahara felt like the ground itself held ancient, restless spirits, their whispers threatening to break through.	1	\N	3	slow-burn	present	2026-02-27 12:03:52.900871-05	2026-02-27 12:03:52.900871-05	\N	\N	0	\N	\N
