768	219	2	The protagonist becomes aware of being watched and senses an unsettling presence throughout the Vatican, noting the guards' and cardinals' strange behavior and an unexplained shape retreating into darkness.	While the Vatican dazzled, us with its ostentation, an unsettling undercurrent threaded through the experience. I first felt it as we passed through the hallowed halls, a prickle at the back of my neck, as if unseen eyes tracked our every move.\n\n“Dad,” my son whispered, tugging at my sleeve. “Why are those guards staring at us?”\n\nI glanced at the Swiss Guards, their stoic expressions betraying nothing. “They’re just doing their job,” I said, though my voice sounded less certain than I intended.\n\n“Sure,” he replied, unconvinced. “But it’s weird.”\n\nI tried to shrug it off, but the unease clung to me. It wasn’t just the guards; cardinals passed us with subtle glances, their gazes heavy, knowing. It felt as if they were sizing me up, not as a tourist, but as something else—something dangerous or valuable.\n\n“Do you feel that?” I asked my wife quietly as we paused near a gilded altar.\n\nShe frowned, tilting her head. “Feel what?”\n\n“Like... we’re being watched,” I said, lowering my voice.\n\nShe looked around, her expression thoughtful. “I thought it was just me. It’s probably nothing. Just the atmosphere—it’s overwhelming.”\n\nI wasn’t so sure. We continued the tour through the Vatican, but the feeling just intensified. The air seemed heavier, tinged with an unnamable tension. I found myself glancing over my shoulder, half-expecting to see shadows coalescing into something tangible.\n\nThere, in the faint glow of a flickering candle near a forgotten alcove, I thought I saw it: a shape, barely visible, retreating into the darkness. It wasn’t human—it couldn’t be.\n\n“Are you okay?” my wife asked, pulling me from my thoughts.\n\n“Yeah,” I lied, forcing a smile. “Just tired.”\n\nThe lie tasted bitter, and the unease clung to me like moist underpants. Every step we took seemed to reverberate, as though the floor wasn’t stone but something alive, something waiting. The ornate marble columns and gilded relics, so full of grandeur moments ago, now felt oppressive. The grandeur had teeth.	1	\N	4	slow-burn	present	2026-02-27 12:03:52.900871-05	2026-02-27 12:03:52.900871-05	\N	\N	0	\N	\N
