1259	356	2	The protagonist reflects on his deceased wife and their forty-seven years of distant, measured love marked by fourteen moments of genuine connection and small daily rituals.	My wife would have laughed herself sick.\n\nThe pod smells like crusty socks and regret. Maybe it's me. The thing lurches again, some computer bug trying to kill me at seventy-four. She would've found this hilarious. Software Engineer killed by software glitch. I can almost hear her laughing as I watch the skyline, smog choked with dust or possibly betrayal, and remember when I thought any of this mattered.\n\nShe had this way of finding humor in the cosmic joke of existence. Even when the cancer was eating her alive, she'd crack jokes about how her body was "finally getting revenge for all those years of terrible American food." She'd pat my hand with those delicate tiny fingers—pianist fingers, though she never played—and smile that polite, distant smile that had charmed me for forty-seven years.\n\nThe smile that never quite reached her eyes.\n\nNot that it mattered. I loved her anyway. Maybe because of it. There's something beautiful about loving someone who holds pieces of themselves just out of reach. She was kind. Gracious. Perfect in every way that mattered and a few that didn't.\n\nFourteen moments of connection in four decades. Fourteen times she let me close enough to matter. One of those times gave us our only son. I tracked each one like a prisoner marking days on a cell wall. But she'd make me green tea every morning in that precise, ritual way of hers. She'd listen to my rambling theories about technology and humanity with the patience of a saint. She'd fold my clothes with corners so sharp they could cut glass.\n\nShe even put up with our son's Roblox obsession and his eventual career in software development—not the doctor she'd dreamed of, but at least he followed in the old man's footsteps.\n\nLove doesn't always look like passion. Sometimes it looks like quietly making space for someone else's existence, even when your own body feels like borrowed time.	1	\N	2	contemplative	present	2026-02-27 12:14:01.519812-05	2026-02-27 12:14:01.519812-05	\N	\N	0	\N	\N
