p her hand. Two fingers have returned, but they're not quite the same fingers. They're close—evolutionary cousins, maybe, or rough translations from a language that doesn't have words for 'human.'\n\nBenji hovers closer, examining us with the clinical interest of a cosmic social worker assessing damage. "Fix? Kid, you just performed emergency surgery on the fabric of space-time using kitchen utensils and enthusiasm. I'm impressed you're still corporeal." Clicking his fingers and licking at the air, her hands return to normal.\n\nBenji settles into a more stable form—still obviously not from around here, but at least recognizably bipedal. "These disruptions are unproductive," he continues, sounding like a teacher who's discovered students experimenting with homemade explosives in chemistry class. "The filters are already strained. The compression agents are working overtime. And you just poked them all awake with a stick made of pure stupid."\n\nI let go of Jamie's hand, though every instinct screams to hold on tighter. Her skin still radiates the remnants of unnatural heat, but the worst of the fever seems to have broken. She looks at me with eyes that have seen things I hope I'll never have to.\n\n"You'd better keep it down," Benji warns, his form flickering between helpful bureaucrat and cosmic horror. "Because I might not be the only one to notice next time. And trust me—my colleagues make me look like a guidance counselor."\n\nThe basement finally stabilizes, returning to its mundane reality of fancy furniture and storage boxes. But the shadows seem deeper now, and the corners hold angles that don't quite add up to ninety degrees.\n\n"You observed them," Benji says, hovering just close enough to make my skin crawl. "The flickers. The echoes. Then you wondered why they stopped showing up." His tone carries the weight of someone explaining death to children. "You little dreamers are all the same—poking at mysteries until the mysteries start poking back."\n\n"Can y