s said. "The system's running on busted code. Consensus reality is breaking down."\n\nI froze.\n\nThose words.\n\nConsensus reality.\n\nThey landed wrong. Too precise. Too heavy.\n\nJames must have seen my reaction. He raised an eyebrow.\n\n"My dissertation," he explained. "Rhetorical Enforcement of Consensus Reality. Basically, how shared belief systems maintain social structures. When consensus breaks down—when people stop believing in the same rules—the whole thing collapses."\n\n"And that's... what's happening?" I asked carefully.\n\n"Everywhere." He gestured vaguely. "Job markets. Politics. Media. Everyone's operating in their own reality now. No shared framework. It's chaos wearing a suit."\n\nBecca touched his arm. Gentle. "James gets worked up about this stuff."\n\n"Sorry." He laughed, self-deprecating. "Hazard of the profession. Can't shut off the academic brain."\n\nBut I couldn't let it go.\n\n"What happens when consensus breaks down completely?" I asked.\n\nJames met my eyes. His expression shifted. Something serious beneath the casual academic veneer.\n\n"Then you find out what's underneath," he said quietly. "What was always there. Holding the illusion together."\n\nThe air in the shop felt suddenly thinner.\n\nBecca cleared her throat. "More wine?"\n\n"God, yes," I said.	1	\N	2	dialogue-heavy	present	2026-02-27 12:28:00.912937-05	2026-02-27 12:28:00.912937-05	\N	\N	0	\N	\N
