rs flew over the glass, and his face immediately scrunched in disgust.\n\n"Ugh. Dad, this is... wow." He shielded the screen from me. "Okay, like, 90% of this is just... really, really weird porn."\n\n"Just... filter it," I said, rubbing the bridge of my nose. "Look for 'occult' or 'grimoire.' Don't read the rest."\n\n"I'm trying," he giggled, still scrolling. "But what the hell is... 'the black pharaoh's... kiss?' That sounds... wrong."\n\n"You don't want to know, Xavi. Trust me. Just... find something."\n\n"Okay, okay... gross." He shook his head, his face still a little blushed, and typed again. "Alright, filtering out... that. Okay. Here. Got something. A blog post, looks legit, not... that. It's talking about rare occult texts. Mentions a book... 'The Whispers of the Crawling Chaos.' Supposedly a real grimoire. It says... it says a copy was last seen at a rare bookstore in Orlando..."\n\nHe froze, his face pale. "Dad. 'Jamie's Marginalia.'"\n\nI closed my eyes. Of course. The system knew. It wasn't just rewriting things; it was erasing the map.\n\n"Wait," Xavier said, his voice tense. He kept scrolling. "The post is old. But the comments... someone here mentions another place. Not a shop. A... a collector. In Ocala. They're obsessed with all things Lovecraftian."\n\n"How obsessed?"\n\n"Like... really obsessed, Dad. The commenter says this person claims to have original letters from Lovecraft, Robert E. Howard, Clark Ashton Smith... like, all those weird pulp fiction guys from the 1920s. They're convinced the Mythos is real. If anyone has a lead on a book like Whispers, it's them. It's a weak lead, Dad."\n\n"It's the only lead," I said.\n\nThe rest of the meal was uneventful. Xavier looking up leads. Me sitting there, exposed, waiting for my head to explode from some errant snipers bullet or something.\n\nAfter we finished, I threw a twenty-dollar bill on the table. "Ocala. We go to Ocala."	2	\N	3	dialogue-heavy	present	2026-02-27 12:29:45.517676-05	2026-02-27 1