ey've been floating in the dream plane ever since. Fragments. Echoes. Waiting."\n\n"Xavier—"\n\n"Is real now because you pulled him through. You ripped him out of the dream plane in a moment of grief so powerful it tore holes in reality. Your NPC wife couldn't have children. Wasn't designed to. But you wanted a son so badly you forced it. Made it happen. Yanked his soul across dimensions and shoved it into her flesh."\n\nHe glances at me. "Impressive, really. Most people can't do that."\n\nMy throat is dry. "And Hannah?"\n\n"But here's the beautiful part," he continues, his voice almost gleeful. "When Xavier was trapped—when the system tried to delete him too—Hannah made a choice. In the dreamscape, where souls drift between incarnations, she found him. Her brother. And she pushed him out. Sacrificed her own escape to give him the momentum he needed to break through."\n\nHe glances back at me. "She saved him. Threw herself into the gap so he could cross. Very heroic. Very stupid."\n\n"What happened to her?"\n\n"She was thrust out," Nyarlathotep says. "Not into Etheris. Not into any reality you'd recognize. Into purgatory. The space between. The place even Bythos—the Monad, the Source of All—dares not tread. A realm so close to anti-reality it's practically touching the membrane. The edge of everything. Where existence meets un-existence."\n\nHis voice drops lower. "She was free. Truly free. No prison. No loops. No Yaldabaoth. Just... endless nothing. An infinity of absence. Alone at the boundary where reality ends and something worse begins."\n\nHe stops walking. Turns to face me fully.\n\n"She wandered that edge for eternities. Or seconds. Time doesn't exist there. Laughing sometimes. Screaming others. Going mad. Getting sane again. All while standing at the precipice of the anti-reality, feeling it pull at her. Wanting to let her in. Wanting to unmake her completely."\n\n"But she didn't fall," I say.\n\n"Ah. Now that's where it gets fun." His smile is wide. Too wide. "If she was going to be born. She should have been Hannah. Your daughter. The girl you and your cold, hollow wife were supposed to have but never did. Because your wife was an NPC. A construct. Incapable of warmth. Incapable of creation. She was a wall, Stephen. A barrier keeping you from having what you wanted."\n\nThe hallway narrows. I have to turn sideways to keep following him.\n\n"So Hannah's soul just... floated," he says. "Waiting for a body that never came. Until the reset. Until you died in that hospital pod in your eighties, bitter and full of regret, screaming into the void about how your family never felt complete. How it should have been different. How you should have had more children."\n\nHe stops. We're at a junction. Three paths. Each one darker than the last.\n\n"That rage," he says softly. "That grief. It triggered something. A glitch in the matrix. And when the reset happened, Hannah's soul got caught in the machinery. She incarnated wrong. Into an NPC. A boy named William."\n\nMy stomach drops.\n\n"Whitney," I whisper.\n\n"Whitney," he confirms. "Your daughter, wearing the wrong skin. Living the wrong life. Suffering under a father who hated what she was because he couldn't see who she actually was. She spent that whole life trying to be real, trying to be seen, and the whole time she was more real than anyone around her."\n\nHe chooses the middle path. I follow.\n\n"And when she finally became herself," he continues, "when she finally broke the script and lived as Whitney... the system tried to delete her. Couldn't allow the paradox. But she was too real. Too you. Too stubborn. So instead of deletion, she got ejected. Thrown clear of the prison entirely."\n\nThe path slopes downward. The temperature drops.\n\n"She's been trying to reach you ever since," he says. "Your daughter. Screaming across dimensions. And you didn't even recognize her voice."\n\n"I heard that." Whitney's voice cuts through the card's connection. There's no anger in it