se. Every axon. Every dendrite firing in panicked confusion because the body they're supposed to control isn't there anymore.\n\nThe pain doesn't stop. It intensifies.\n\nBecause now there's nothing to buffer it. No endorphins. No shock response. Just pure, unfiltered neural feedback screaming into a void that doesn't care.\n\nAnd then even that starts to go.\n\nThe peripheral nerves first. The ones that used to reach my fingers, my toes. They fray. Unwind like thread pulled from a spool. Each nerve fiber separating, drifting away, taking with it the memory of touch, of sensation, of physical connection to the world.\n\nThe spinal cord follows. Vertebra by vertebra, the bundle of nerves comes apart. Unwinding. Un-spooling. I feel each fiber separate. Feel the connections severing. Feel the pathways that carried signals from brain to body dissolving into static.\n\nMy brain is last.\n\nThe cerebellum goes first. Balance. Coordination. The sense of where my body is in space. Gone.\n\nThe temporal lobes. Memory. Language. The ability to name what's happening to me. Dissolving.\n\nThe frontal cortex. Personality. Decision-making. The sense of who I am. Peeling away like layers of an onion.\n\nUntil all that's left is the brainstem. The lizard core. The part that knows only one thing:\n\nI am.\n\nAnd then even that unravels.\n\nPiece by piece. Neuron by neuron. Synapse by synapse.\n\nThe pain doesn't fade. It just... stops having anywhere to register. Stops having anyone to experience it.\n\nI'm coming apart.\n\nNot dying. Worse than dying.\n\nBeing unmade.\n\nLayer by layer. Sense by sense. Until there's nothing left but—\n\nA spark.	1	\N	5	slow-burn	present	2026-02-27 12:31:22.457664-05	2026-02-27 12:31:22.457664-05	\N	\N	0	\N	\N
