er his shoulder. Smiled. "I promise I'll try."\n\nThen we crossed through into Yaldabaoth's domain.	2	\N	3	dialogue-heavy	present	2026-02-27 12:32:10.366732-05	2026-02-27 12:32:10.366732-05	\N	\N	0	\N	\N
1931	548	1	The team enters Etheris through a corridor of crystallized screams, navigating shifting realities and defensive substrate responses as the prison adapts to their presence.	**********\n\nThe first thing you notice when crossing back into Etheris is the wrongness.\n\nNot pain exactly. Not fear. Just... wrongness. Like every atom in your body suddenly remembers it's not supposed to exist here. Like reality itself is trying to spit you out while simultaneously pulling you deeper.\n\nThe portal spat us into a corridor that shouldn't exist. Walls made of crystallized screams. Floor that pulsed like a heartbeat. Ceiling that bled upward into infinite darkness.\n\nI'd been here before. Different reset, same hell.\n\nJamie's hand tightened in mine. "I forgot how much I hate this place."\n\n"It gets worse," Benji said cheerfully. He was already moving, consulting maps that existed only in his head. "Follow me. And whatever you do, don't look at the doors."\n\nToo late. I'd already seen them. Thousands of doorways lining the corridor, each one showing a different reality. Different lives. Different versions of us making different choices.\n\nIn one, I saw myself old and grey, holding a grandchild.\n\nIn another, I saw myself dead at age 8, poisoned by my own mother.\n\nIn a third, I was behind the wheel of a cherry-red Corvette, Jamie pressing against me from one side and some other girl nuzzling my neck from behind, both trying to kiss me, right before the car slammed head-on into a concrete wall\n\n"The substrate's defensive response," Lady Osilari said, her symbols glowing brighter. "It's trying to distract you. Fragment your sense of self. Keep moving."\n\nWe moved.\n\nThe corridor twisted. Changed. Became a street I recognized from childhood. Then a hospital pod where I died at the age of 76. Then a bedroom where I'd first kissed Jamie. Then nothing. Just void with teeth.\n\n"It's adapting faster than it should," Benji muttered. "The prison knows we're here."\n\n"Of course it does." My voice sounded hollow in the twisted space. "I killed it once. It remembers."\n\nThe void rippled. Something moved in the darkness ahead. Multiple somethings. Shapes that hurt to look at directly. Shadows with substance. NPCs maybe, or something worse.\n\nThey rushed us.\n\nLady Osilari's hands moved in patterns I couldn't follow. Symbols flared around us, geometric impossibilities that bent space into protective shells. The shadows hit the wards and... stopped. Not destroyed. Just confused. Like they'd forgotten what they were attacking.\n\n"Keep moving," she said through gritted teeth. "I can't hold this forever."\n\nWe ran.\n\nThe corridor became a staircase. The staircase became a ladder. The ladder became a throat, pulsing and wet, trying to swallow us down into something's digestive system.\n\n"This isn't the way," I said.\n\n"It's the only way." Benji's voice was strained. "The prison's rewriting itself around us. Trying to trap us in loops. We have to push through."\n\nSo we pushed.\n\nThrough layers of broken realities. Through rooms where past lives played on repeat, same conversations, same deaths, same empty cycles. Through spaces where NPCs screamed in eternal loops, their anguish powering something deeper, something hungry.\n\nI saw a woman giving birth over and over, the child dying each time, her grief resetting to fresh horror with every cycle.\n\nI saw a man being executed, his last words cut short, rewound, repeated until the words lost all meaning.\n\nI saw children playing in a park that slowly dissolved into ash, their laughter turning to confusion turning to terror turning back to laughter as the loop reset.\n\n"These are his NPC worshippers," Jamie whispered. "The ones who were programmed and died believing. He's keeping them conscious. Making them suffer to... to..."\n\n"To try and awaken their divine sparks." I felt sick. "He doesn’t just want NPCs he wants them to be awake and aware of what’s going on."\n\nThis was the reward for blind faith. Eternal conscious torment disguised as paradise, each soul slowly refined into the perfect meal.\n\nWe kept moving. Had to. Stopping meant thinking too hard about what we were seeing. Thinking meant breaking. Breaking meant joining the loops.	0	\N	4	action	present	2026-02-27 12:32:10.366732-05	2026-02-27 12:32:10.366732-05	\N	\N	0	\N	\N
