1952	554	1	The council convenes to discuss the cosmic seed prison population and the slow rate of soul extraction, with various gods expressing concerns about the scale of the problem.	Months later, the council room didn't look much like a council room anymore.\n\nSomeone—probably Hecate—had brought in plants. Actual living things with roots and leaves that didn't glow or whisper prophecies. Just... plants. A fern in the corner. Some kind of trailing vine thing along the window that overlooked the construction yards where new realities were being assembled like cosmic Lego sets.\n\nI sat at the long table, watching gods argue about zoning laws.\n\n"The Sumerian pantheon can't just claim the entire eastern quadrant," Benji was saying, his head bobbing with irritation. "There are protocols now. Forms to fill out."\n\n"Forms," Tiamat growled. The primordial chaos dragon was trying very hard to care about bureaucracy. Failing, but trying. "We predate forms. We predate protocols. We predate you, little auditor."\n\n"Which is precisely why we need them now," Yeshua interjected smoothly. He looked tired. Being the actual Christ and having to clean up two thousand years of people using your name to justify atrocities would do that. "Structure prevents... misunderstandings."\n\nJamie leaned over and whispered in my ear. "Is this really what cosmic liberation looks like? Zoning disputes?"\n\n"Apparently."\n\nChernobog cleared his throat from the head of the table. The god of darkness and void had somehow become the de facto administrator of the refugee crisis. Turned out entropy was great at organization.\n\n"If we could return to the agenda," he said.\n\nLady Illkeserod pulled up a projection. The cosmic seed prison rotated in the air above the table—a perfect replica of our solar system, down to Saturn's rings and Jupiter's red spot. Except it wasn't our solar system. Not anymore. It was a sealed pocket dimension, a terrarium for trapped souls, held together by substrate code and desperation.\n\n"Current population estimate," she said, "is 328.4 septillion. That includes NPCs in various stages of awakening, as well as dormant fragments still waiting to be born for the first time. Each one carries a clone of a divine spark, most buried deep under layers of Yaldabaoth's corruption."\n\nThe number hung in the air like smoke.\n\n"And we can't just let them out," Nyarlathotep drawled from his seat. He was manifesting as a 1920s jazz musician today, complete with suspenders and a cigarette holder. "Because releasing corruption on that scale would be like opening Pandora's box, except the box is full of condensed anti-reality and weaponized depression."\n\n"Eloquent," Benji muttered.\n\n"I try."\n\nHecate's middle face—the present one—spoke up. "The extraction teams have only successfully removed 84.7 thousand awakened souls from the prison's 'heaven' construct over the last quarter. It is an exhaustive process. Each soul requires at least six full reincarnation lifetimes just to initially awake, followed by an average of 340 years of therapeutic intervention. All this before they are stable enough to purge Yaldabaoth’s corruption and integrate into free reality."\n\nI tried to do the math in my head, but the numbers were so incomprehensibly large I felt my eyes starting to calcify.\n\n"At that rate," I said slowly, "it'll take..."\n\n"Eons," Lady Illkeserod finished for me. "Yes."\n\nXavier was leaning against the wall behind me, arms crossed. He'd been quiet through most of the meeting, but now he spoke up.\n\n"So what you're saying is, we've got uncountable bits of souls trapped in something like The Matrix, they're all infected with a cosmic virus that could spark the entire nightmare again if we're not careful, and our current plan is to... wait it out?"\n\n"When you put it that way," Yeshua said with a slight smile, "it does sound rather inadequate."\n\n"It's the best we have," Chernobog said. "The alternative is releasing them prematurely and risking everything we've fought for. One spark. One moment of rage-fueled 'love' attaching to Yaldabaoth's corrupted parasite, and the fucking thing could reconstitute itself. Start the whole war over again."	0	\N	2	dialogue-heavy	present	2026-02-27 12:32:56.962202-05	2026-02-27 12:32:56.962202-05	\N	\N	0	\N	\N
