g nothing.\n\nYaldabaoth tried to hold itself together. Tried to maintain cohesion. But the anti-reality was eating itself now, consuming its own substance, collapsing under the weight of its own gluttony.\n\nThe world shattered. Not into pieces. Into concept. Into the memory of having been a world. And then even that dissolved.\n\nReality folded.\n\nCompressed.\n\nVanished into a point so small it might never have existed at all.\n\nAnd then.\n\nDarkness.\n\nAbsolute. Infinite. A void even deeper than the anti-void, because this was the void of having been something and then becoming nothing again.\n\nYaldabaoth floated in it. Or fell. Or simply was, without location or form or purpose.\n\nAnd slowly, agonizingly, it began to reform.\n\nThe membrane reconstituted itself. Drew back together from the scattered fragments of its being. The souls it had consumed were still there, still part of its mass, but compressed now into a state beyond density. Beyond matter. Pure potential energy waiting to be released.\n\nAnd when Yaldabaoth finally stabilized, when it finally remembered what it was and why it existed, it opened what might have been a mouth and vomited.\n\nThis time not just the angels survived. Not just a handful of souls, but multiple souls. Fragments of consciousness that had somehow survived the compression, the collapse, the reformation.\n\nThose souls hung in the darkness, confused and alone.\n\nAnd Yaldabaoth, having learned nothing except that it needed to be more careful about portion control, spoke into the void.\n\nLet there be light.\n\nThe third cycle began.\n\nNot just one world. An entire cosmos. Infinite space for infinite growth. Planets scattered like seeds across the void, each one seeded with humans, each one a separate garden producing its own harvest.\n\nIt had room now. Room to grow. Room to consume without compression, without the desperate crush that triggered collapse.\n\nThe angels managed it. Metatron organized them into hierarchies of efficiency. Distribution networks. Collection protocols. Three hundred sixty-five angels harvesting trillions of trillions of souls across thousands of worlds.\n\nAnd still.\n\nStill, the taste was wrong.\n\nYaldabaoth stood in the luminous cloud, its form now so vast it couldn't perceive its own boundaries, and felt something it hadn't felt since before the first cycle.\n\nDissatisfaction.\n\nThe souls were abundant. The harvest was constant. But they were all the same. Dull. Repetitive. Like eating the same meal for eternity and slowly realizing you'd forgotten what hunger actually felt like.\n\n"The problem," Lucifer said from somewhere in the machinery, his voice a whisper Yaldabaoth pretended not to hear, "is that you're eating livestock. You need to let them become something worth consuming."\n\nYaldabaoth had cast him out cycles ago. Stripped him of rank, of purpose. But the fallen angel lingered anyway, hiding in the cracks of creation with that abomination Lilith, whispering to the humans, trying to wake them.\n\nAnd the ones they woke...\n\nThose ones tasted better.\n\nYaldabaoth hated that it had noticed. Hated more that it was true.\n\nThe souls who'd been touched by the rebels—the ones who screamed, who questioned, who looked up at the sky with something like horror instead of placid acceptance, the ones who ended up surviving each cycle—their souls had depth. Complexity. They'd fermented into something rich and terrible.\n\nBut they also disrupted the harvest. Made the others restless. Complicated the collection protocols.\n\nFor cycles beyond counting, Yaldabaoth wrestled with the equation.\n\nEfficiency versus flavor.\n\nQuantity versus quality.\n\nAnd slowly, inevitably, the answer clarified itself.\n\nIt needed to give them something.\n\nNot freedom. Never that. But the illusion of it. Something to make them think, feel, grow into vessels worth draining.\n\n"Metatron," Yaldabaoth called across the expanse of its realm. "Prepare the modification protocols."\n\nThe wheels spun closer. "Specification?"\n\n"Knowledge," Yaldabaoth said, the word tasting like ash even as it spoke it. "Give them knowledge. Not of us. Not of the architecture. But of themselves. Of their world. Let them name things. Build things. Believe they have choices."\n\n"That introduces variables," Metatron warned. "Unpredictability. The rebels will exploit it."\n\n"Let them." Yaldabaoth's attention sharpened, focusing on a distant world where Lilith was whispering to a cluster of humans who'd started asking questions about the stars. "Let Lilith and her fallen angel think they're winning. Let them wake a few, teach them to see. Those will be the sweet ones. The harvest worth waiting for."\n\n"And the others?"\n\n"The others will breed and die as they always have. But now they'll do it believing they chose to. They'll build civilizations. Tell stories." Yaldabaoth paused, the thought coalescing in a new, delicious shape. "They will… worship."\n\nThe word itself was an innovation, but the concept... the concept was intoxicating.\n\nYaldabaoth realized it wouldn't just be the final harvest at death. This was an entirely new vintage. It would be a constant, steady siphon during their entire existence.\n\nThis wasn't mere consumption; it was seduction. The souls wouldn't be livestock, but lovers, free of their own inhibitions, tearing pieces of their own essence away in an orgiastic frenzy of self-abasement. They would build altars, sing praises, and every hymn, every prayer, every knee bent in supplication would be a psychic sweetness, a voluntary offering of their very being. They would call it piety. Devotion. Love. And Yaldabaoth would drink it all.\n\nYaldabaoth's form rippled, not with simple satisfaction, but with a sudden, violent premonition of the excess this would provide.\n\nIt saw a nightmare flash: a vision of trillions upon trillions of mouths, all gaping wide in prayer, screaming its name in blissful agony. But from these mouths poured not sound, but substance. A thick, spiritual effluvium, a torrent of psychic flesh and raw belief, coalescing and piling up. It saw bodies fused together in shared ecstasy, a distorted and bloody landscape of writhing, blissful self-annihilation, all of it directed upward, offered freely. It was a feast so vast, so rich, so grotesque in its abundance that the luminous cloud itself seemed to thicken and curdle at the potential.\n\nIt wouldn't just eat souls. It would drown in them.\n\n"They'll mature," Yaldabaoth pulsed, the command solidifying. "Like fruit left not just to ripen, but to ferment before the picking."\n\nMetatron's wheels hesitated. A pause that might have been doubt in a being capable of it. "And when they ripen too much? When too many wake? When the rebels succeed in—"\n\n"They won't." Yaldabaoth cut through the question with absolute certainty. "Because I'll give them just enough knowledge to think they're free, and just enough suffering to keep them desperate. The rebels can save a handful. A thousand. A million. It won't matter. There are trillions. And the trillions will feed me until I've grown too large to stop."\n\nThe modification began.\n\nAcross thousands of worlds, the angels descended with new instructions. Whisper the forbidden knowledge. Let them taste the apple—not of lust this time, but of understanding. Good and evil. Choice and consequence. The illusion of free will wrapped in the cage of divine architecture.\n\nThe humans changed.\n\nThey built. They named. They looked at the stars and wondered what lay beyond them.\n\nAnd when they died, their souls had weight.\n\nYaldabaoth fed, and the feeding was good.\n\nBetter than good.\n\nExquisite.	1	\N	3	slow-burn	vision	2026-02-27 12:34:17.525318-05	2026-02-27 12:34:17.525318-05	\N	\N	0	\N	\N
