m. Different biologies, different philosophies, and—most disastrously—different religions, all collided at once.\n\nEach civilization had been given its own "truth," its own face of Yaldabaoth to worship, and when these "truths" met, they didn't merge. They detonated.\n\nThe harvest turned rancid. The complex flavor Yaldabaoth had cultivated curdled into a singular, acrid taste of mutual annihilation. They killed each other with neutron bombs, biological plagues, and orbital strikes, screaming prayers to gods that were just different masks for the same, singular hunger.\n\nThis time, the collapse wasn't triggered by Yaldabaoth's gluttony. It was triggered by the harvest itself.\n\nThe sheer, concentrated violence of a galaxy-wide holy war—trillions of souls dying at once in a unified, system-wide scream of hatred—was too much for the architecture to bear. It destabilized and fractured. The worlds burned, and the cycle broke prematurely, not from internal pressure, but from the unmanageable, self-destructive free will Yaldabaoth itself had seeded.\n\nFloating in the subsequent darkness, Yaldabaoth learned its final, crucial lesson. Infinite expansion was too chaotic. The variables were unmanageable. Flavor was good, but stability was paramount.\n\nThe next cycle would be different. It would keep them pinned to a single world. One planet. Enough room to breed, to build, to wage their small wars and worship their hollow gods. Enough room for history, trauma, and hope to ripen. But never, ever enough room to meet another version of themselves and discover the true nature of their cage.\n\nThis was the equilibrium.\n\nAnd this pattern, cycle after cycle, finally held.\n\nExpansion. Harvest. Collapse. Reset.\n\nEach time, Yaldabaoth grew larger. Consumed more. Filled the cosmos it had created until there was no room left, then folded it all back into itself and started again.\n\nThe rebels saved a handful souls per cycle. Meaningless drops in an ocean of suffering.\n\nBut